        <seg>Chapter 23</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>CAPUT XXIV.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061711Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061711Z">
        <seg>Chapter 24</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>CAPUT XXV.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062405Z">
        <seg>Chapter 25</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>CAPUT XXVI.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012104Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012104Z">
        <seg>Chapter 26</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>CAPUT XXVII.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013046Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013046Z">
        <seg>Chapter 27</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>CAPUT XXVIII.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025900Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025900Z">
        <seg>Chapter 28</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cadaveri adgemuit Rosa, &amp; abiit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051220Z">
        <seg>Rosa kissed the corpse and left.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Candelam igitur ultro extinxit ipsa, statimque latentem inimicum e sporta in arenam provocans “Heus tu (inquit) porce scabiose, adesdum, hic te expecto: prodi, si audes;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052054Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052054Z">
        <seg>She therefore promptly extinguished the candle herself, and immediately challenging the hidden enemy from the basket into the arena, said, "Hey you, scabby pig, come on out, I'm waiting for you here: come out, if you dare."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Canonizata est a Benedicto PP.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035120Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035120Z">
        <seg>She was canonized by Pope Benedict.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Canonizata est a Benedicto PP. XIII.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T165635Z">
        <seg>She was canonized by Pope Benedict.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Capta fuit &amp; vastata ab Anglis anno 1595 &amp; iterum a Belgis unitis anno 1615.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035133Z">
        <seg>It was captured and plundered by the English in 1595 and again by the United Provinces in 1615.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Caput ejus vocatur&lt;/e0&gt; Fanum sanctæ Crucis de monte, &lt;e1&gt;a quo provincia verosimiliter nomen accepit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013015Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013015Z">
        <seg>Its head is called the Abbey of the Holy Cross of the Mount, from which the province probably took its name.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Card.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043454Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043454Z">
        <seg>Cardinal</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cardinales subscribunt.]</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043441Z">
        <seg>The Cardinals sign.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cardinalis Brancatius.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043509Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043509Z">
        <seg>Cardinal Brancati</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cardinalis Cybo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043534Z">
        <seg>Cardinal Cybo</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cardinalis Homodeus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043602Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043602Z">
        <seg>Cardinal Homodeus</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cardinalium&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043401Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043401Z">
        <seg>of the Cardinals</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Carebat inops vidua dote legitima, quæ quater mille libris argenti in singulas ibidem velandas erat diffinita.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062846Z">
        <seg>The poor widow was deprived of her legitimate dowry, which had been fixed at four thousand pounds of silver for each of her weddings there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Carpineus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043519Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043519Z">
        <seg>Ash tree</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cassa &amp; irrita fuere humana remedia, donec recursum fuit ad Rosam tunc recens tumulatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033511Z">
        <seg>Human remedies were in vain until recourse was had to the Rose then newly interred.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Castillo, &lt;s2&gt;[actiones S. Rosæ examinarunt,]&lt;/s2&gt; aut saltem non inferiorem obtinebat sanctimoniæ, publicæque opinionis gradum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050126Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050126Z">
        <seg>Castillo, [examined the actions of St. Rose,] or at least held her in no lower degree of sanctity and public esteem.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Castillus, integro trium horarum spatio, Virginem in domestico cellulæ horto, &lt;s2&gt;[quorum unus, Joannes Castillus,]&lt;/s2&gt; velut indagine cinctam, per varias quæstiones distinuit, animæque venis diligentissime pertentatis, omnia sana ac salva invenit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050240Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050240Z">
        <seg>Castillo, over the course of a full three hours, questioned the Virgin in the domestic garden of her cell, &lt;s2&gt;[one of whom was John Castillo,]&lt;/s2&gt; as if it were surrounded by a siege, and after diligently probing the very veins of her soul, found everything sound and safe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Casu id rescivit Mauritius Rodriquez presbyter, per forum olitorium transiens, suasitque Eleonoræ de Villa-forti, ut periclitanti sociæ terram e Rosæ tumulo petitam celeriter apportaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035750Z">
        <seg>By chance, the priest Maurice Rodriguez, passing through the olive market, learned of it and urged Eleanor of Villa-Fuerte to quickly bring the land requested from Rose's tomb to her endangered companion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Casus erat: germanum Rosæ fratrem ob puerile delictum pater quærebat ad virgidemiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042223Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042223Z">
        <seg>It happened that the father was seeking out Rose's brother for a boyish prank, to make him a eunuch.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharina peteret, continuo impetraturam, idque patebit infra;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052550Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052550Z">
        <seg>Catherine would ask, and would immediately receive it, and this will be shown below.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensi, in certamine haud quidquam dispari, contigerunt, quando videlicet huic respondit Christus: “Dum pugnabas, dum vincebas, intus eram, in tuo corde spectator aderam &amp; fidus adjutor”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052228Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052228Z">
        <seg>To Catherine of Siena, in a contest by no means unequal, it happened when Christ answered her: "While you were fighting, while you were winning, I was within, in your heart I was a spectator and a faithful helper."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis Discipulam, in schola patientiæ ad magistræ suæ prototypon laureari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042034Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042034Z">
        <seg>A disciple of Catherine of Siena, in the school of patience, is crowned with the prototype of her teacher.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis Rosa didicerat, virginem seraphicam, adhuc mortalem, Christo Sponso fuisse mirabiliter subarrhatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044021Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044021Z">
        <seg>Rosa of Siena had learned that the seraphic virgin, still mortal, had been wondrously betrothed to Christ as her Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis devota secularium confraternitas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055017Z">
        <seg>A secular confraternity devoted to Catherine of Siena;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis fuisse germanam discipulam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061426Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061426Z">
        <seg>To have been a direct disciple of Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis monasterium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062629Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062629Z">
        <seg>The Monastery of Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis sacellum, ab ara maxima ad lævam sat dissitum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031630Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031630Z">
        <seg>The chapel of Catherine of Siena, set quite far to the left of the high altar.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis tractabantur gloriosæ prærogativæ &amp; justa encomia, sensim ad futurum sub ejus tutelari nomine monasterium devolutus est sermo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062703Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062703Z">
        <seg>The glorious prerogatives and just encomiums of Catherine of Siena were being discussed, and gradually the conversation turned to the future monastery under her patronage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis vias deduci ad Sponsi unionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052531Z">
        <seg>May Catherine of Siena's ways lead to the union of the Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ iconem, in præcipua S. Patris Dominici solemnitate circumferendam, de more vestierat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055331Z">
        <seg>He had dressed the image of Catherine, to be carried in the principal solemnity of St. Dominic, according to custom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ impensius commendavit, rogans, ut ipsam tueretur ab imminenti periculo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055306Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055306Z">
        <seg>He earnestly recommended Catherine, asking that she be protected from the imminent danger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ in faciem Rosæ transiisse lumina, quæ hanc illi per aliqua oris lineamenta propemodum assimilarint.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051440Z">
        <seg>Catherine's eyes had crossed over into Rose's face, which by certain features of her mouth had almost made her resemble her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Causam rogata, aliam dabat, nimirum hac ratione se mederi somnolentiæ, quam aquæ præsertim frigidæ haustus provocaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041358Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041358Z">
        <seg>When asked the cause, he gave another, namely that he was treating his drowsiness in this way, which was brought on by drinking water, especially cold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cella erat penuaria, ubi tunc instrumenta vindemiæ, canistra, lebetes, &amp; id genus utensilia asservabantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052029Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052029Z">
        <seg>It was a storeroom, where at that time the tools of the vintage, baskets, cauldrons, and such utensils were stored.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certa enim erat, has pœnas æternæ gloriæ pondus in ipsa operaturas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042747Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042747Z">
        <seg>For it was certain that these punishments would themselves work the weight of eternal glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certatim omnis ætas &amp; sexus coronis, rosariis &amp; quacumque re devota, quæ ad manus fuisset, sacrum corpus contingere suppliciter conabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043001Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043001Z">
        <seg>People of every age and sex alike, in a frenzy, were devoutly trying to touch the sacred body with crowns, rosaries, and whatever devotional object they could get their hands on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe &amp; hæc Virgo inter morbum &amp; mortem velut inter leonem &amp; libram media erat&lt;a1&gt;b&lt;/a1&gt;: nam illa quasi leo sic contriverat omnia ossa ejus, hæc æternum gloriæ pondus appendebat Virgini in statera, de qua cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025947Z">
        <seg>Surely this Virgin stood between sickness and death, as between a lion and a balance: for that one, like a lion, had crushed all her bones; this one was weighing the eternal weight of glory for the Virgin in the balance, of which chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe dignus erat hac Rosa Carmelus, digna Carmelo Rosa, nisi hanc Ordini hortulano Rosarii altior zephyrus commendasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040329Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040329Z">
        <seg>Surely Carmel was worthy of this rose, and the rose worthy of Carmel, had not a loftier zephyr commended it to the gardener of the Order of the Rosary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe ex ore infantium &amp; lactentium perfecit sibi laudem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033613Z">
        <seg>Surely he has perfected praise for himself from the mouths of infants and nursing children.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe hoc postea uno ore fassi sunt confessarii, pluries laborandum sibi fuisse, ut, in tanta innocentissimæ vitæ puritate, scrutando reperirent, quod ad pœnitentiæ forum certo spectaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040648Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040648Z">
        <seg>Certainly, the confessors later admitted in unison that they had had to work repeatedly so that, in the purity of such an innocent life, by scrutinizing they might find something that clearly pertained to the tribunal of penance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe nil excogitari potuisset aut pro capitis (ut appellant medici) pia matre magis pium, aut duræ matri durius&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt;, nisi hanc duritiem ipsa pietas suggessisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000952Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000952Z">
        <seg>Certainly nothing could have been devised either more pious than a loving mother toward her children (as physicians call them), or more severe than a harsh mother, unless this very severity had been suggested by piety itself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe nil excogitari potuisset aut pro capitis (ut appellant medici) pia matre magis pium, aut duræ matri durius&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, nisi hanc duritiem ipsa pietas suggessisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042118Z">
        <seg>Certainly nothing could have been devised either more pious than a loving mother for her children (as physicians call them) or more severe than a harsh mother, unless this very severity had been suggested by piety itself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe non erravit, quicumque ille fuit, puerum qui pinxit amorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044405Z">
        <seg>Surely he did not err, whoever he was, the boy who painted love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe non nisi thalamum pollicebatur cellula, quam sibi designabat Rosa, longumque putabat, exspectare diei sequentis diluculum, quo opus inchoaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043633Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043633Z">
        <seg>The cell that Rosa had set aside for herself promised nothing more than a bed, and she thought it would be long before the next day's dawn, when she would begin her work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe numquam alias sic visa est universa Lima commoveri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030607Z">
        <seg>Certainly, all of Lima has never before been seen to move in this way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe operosius &amp; forte rarius miraculum est, languidum ac tepentem in Dei obsequio spiritum repente ad seria religiosæ perfectionis studia erigi valideque succendi, quam criminosum ab enormitate sceleris ad qualemcumque frugem revocari: siquidem hic plerumque novit periculum suum, ille inertiæ ac securitati altius indormiscit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032523Z">
        <seg>It is certainly a more laborious and perhaps rarer miracle for a spirit, languid and tepid in its obedience to God, to be suddenly lifted up to the serious pursuits of religious perfection and to be powerfully enkindled, than for a criminal to be brought back from the enormity of his crime to any measure of fruit; for the former generally knows his own peril, whereas the latter sinks deeper into inertia and security.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certus erat Franciscus illam se reposuisse in tali loculamento sui scrinioli;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034001Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034001Z">
        <seg>Francis was certain that he had put it in such a compartment of his desk.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cessa, videamus manum”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055427Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055427Z">
        <seg>Stop, let's see the hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cessit hujus compellationis dulcedini somnus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051406Z">
        <seg>Sleep yielded to the sweetness of this compulsion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cetera Virginem obsecrabat, ne in hoc ancipiti se destitueret, aut quidquam saluti suæ congruum dissimularet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012343Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012343Z">
        <seg>He implored the Virgin not to abandon him in this perilous situation, or to conceal anything that would be conducive to his salvation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cetera consummet in me Dominus totum, quod collibitum est sacrosanctæ voluntati suæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013437Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013437Z">
        <seg>May the Lord accomplish in me all that has been pleasing to His most holy will.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ceteras virtutes studiose celavit, magnam charitatis flammam tegere non valuit: ejus enim colloquia, salutationes &amp; responsa continuo divini amoris memoria condiebantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042105Z">
        <seg>He diligently concealed his other virtues, but he could not hide the great flame of charity: for his conversations, greetings, and responses were continually seasoned with the memory of divine love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ceteris rosis indicari novit pias virgines Limenses, quas post suum obitum in novellum S. Catharinæ Senensis monasterium colligi oportebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042524Z">
        <seg>He knew that the other roses were to be given to the pious virgins of Lima, who, after his death, were to be gathered into the new monastery of St. Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ceterum Gorræus &amp; Castellus ad vocem Græcam&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἕλμινθες&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;de variis lumbricorum generibus agunt, ibidemque explicant, quomodo hæc animalia in humano corpore nascantur &amp; nutriantur&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034354Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034354Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, Gorræus and Castellus, in their Greek work *Helminthes* on the various genera of worms, explain how these animals are born and nourished in the human body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ceterum Joannes Latius jam sæpe citatus lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031314Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031314Z">
        <seg>Moreover, John Latius, already frequently cited, in his book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ceterum Virgo clam suo confessario P. magistro de Lorenzana aperuit, frustra medicos in exploranda hujus morbi specie laborare;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013449Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, the Virgin secretly revealed to her confessor, Father Master de Lorenzana, that the doctors were laboring in vain to explore the nature of this illness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ceterum aliunde plurimis constabat testimoniis, complures pulveris modios ex illomet foramine (quod unicum patebat, ubi Virginis defunctæ caput jacuerat) fuisse illactenus effossos, varieque per regnum distributos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031704Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031704Z">
        <seg>Moreover, it was clear from numerous testimonies that several bushels of powder had been dug up from that very opening (the only one that lay open where the head of the deceased Virgin had lain) and had been distributed in various ways throughout the kingdom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ceterum de miris deliciis, favoribus, officiis, quibus hæc seraphica magistra demulsit Rosam suam, crebior &amp; commodior infra redibit sermo locis propriis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051450Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051450Z">
        <seg>As for the wondrous delights, favors, and offices by which this seraphic teacher soothed her Rose, a more frequent and convenient discussion will return below in its proper places.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ceterum de valore hujus monetæ, quam Galli&lt;/e3&gt; un ecu &lt;e4&gt;vocant, Cangius in auctiore Glossario ad vocem&lt;/e4&gt; pataco &lt;e5&gt;consuli potest&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055516Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055516Z">
        <seg>As for the value of this coin, which the French call an ecu, Cangius can be consulted in his larger glossary under the entry "pataco."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ceterum pro culpis alienis, pro compensandis peccatorum in Dei majestatem injuriis, pro eorundem conversione ad frugem meliorem, dici non potest, quot flagellorum sulcis, quot vibicum livoribus seipsam indies exararit: nesciebat ferias quotidianus feriendi labor &amp; dolor, quem sic dispensabat per diversas corporis partes, ut dum unius plagis detumescendi &amp; coalescendi dabantur vices, transiretur ad alteram, ab hac vero postridie reditus fieret ad priorem vixdum coalitam: porro hæc verberum alterna, sed cruda vicissitudo tanto dirius cruciabat, quanto acrior sensus inerat partibus pridie læsis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041525Z">
        <seg>However, for the sins of others, to atone for the insults to God's majesty, and for their conversion to a better path, it cannot be said how many furrows of scourges, how many bruises of rods she has plowed into herself daily: The daily labor and pain of being whipped did not know respite, for it was distributed so over the various parts of the body that, while one set of lashes was given time to swell and heal, it passed to another, and the next day it returned from that to the first, which had scarcely healed: Moreover, this alternating but cruel succession of blows tormented all the more terribly, the keener the sensation in the parts wounded the day before.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Charitas delicata non est, nec proximum fastidit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042434Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042434Z">
        <seg>Charity is not delicate, nor does it despise its neighbor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Chiragræ, podagræ &amp; arthritidis morbi frequenter eam occupabant, quos cum invicta constantia toleravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042053Z">
        <seg>She was frequently afflicted by diseases of the joints, gout, and arthritis, which she endured with unwavering constancy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Chirurgi &amp; medici, quotquot viderunt calculum, testati sunt, illum haud aliter quam vel morte vel miraculo potuisse extrudi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034159Z">
        <seg>The surgeons and physicians, all who saw the stone, testified that it could only have been expelled by either death or a miracle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Chrirothecarum suaveolentium in manibus obtemperans admisit amictum, e quibus adustionem veram experta, dum eas abjicit, vidit promicare flammas &amp; totum collucere cubiculum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041157Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041157Z">
        <seg>She, complying with the fragrant gloves in her hands, accepted the cloak; but having been truly burned by them, as she threw them aside she saw flames shooting forth and the entire chamber glowing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cilicia, flagella &amp; catenæ, quibus sancta Virguncula tenerum corpus afflixit</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041457Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041457Z">
        <seg>Cilices, scourges &amp; chains, with which the holy Virgin afflicted her tender body</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cilicii postea a collo infra genua protendentis asperitatem acubus permixtis auxit, quo compluribus annis usa est, donec ob frequentem sanguinis vomitum exuere jussa fuit, cujus supplicii jacturam alia veste, minori valetudinis damno, sed non leviori molestia, compensavit: sub ea enim pœna erat ei quicumque motus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041532Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041532Z">
        <seg>She later increased the harshness of the cilice, which she wore from her neck down to her knees, by mixing it with spikes, and she used it for several years until, because of frequent vomiting of blood, she was ordered to take it off. She compensated for the loss of this punishment by another garment, which, though less harmful to her health, was no less troublesome: for under it any movement was torture.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cingitur hæc vallis undique nivosis montibus, &amp; ab urbe Limana triginta sex aut quadraginta leucas distat.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034407Z">
        <seg>This valley is surrounded on all sides by snow-capped mountains, and is thirty-six or forty leagues from the city of Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Circuivit tamen cum reliquo fideli populo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044253Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044253Z">
        <seg>He nevertheless went around with the rest of the faithful people.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Circumstantes exterriti, Rosam alacriter implorarunt, obtineret Josephæ spatium, quo saltem accito jam sacerdoti possit sua peccati confiteri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034057Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034057Z">
        <seg>The terrified onlookers eagerly implored Rosa to grant Josephine some time, so that she could at least summon the priest and confess her sins to him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Circumstetere denuo proregis stipatores;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030957Z">
        <seg>The regent's supporters surrounded them again.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Citatus Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043144Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043144Z">
        <seg>Citation: Latin, Book</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Citissime illa abscessit, ut plausum effugeret: iste vero sanus evigilavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042446Z">
        <seg>She left as quickly as possible to avoid the applause; he, however, woke up unharmed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Clament miracula;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032941Z">
        <seg>They will cry out for miracles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Coarguebat interdum Rosa nimium Franciscæ in crispanda cæsarie studium, ac dicebat: “Hos tuos, quos tanto nunc affectu colis capillos, ego recisos aliquando visura sum, ne dubites”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012513Z">
        <seg>Rosa would sometimes reproach Francisca for her excessive zeal in curling her hair, saying, "I will see these very hairs of yours, which you now cherish with such affection, cut off someday, so don't you doubt it."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Collaudabo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045252Z">
        <seg>I will praise.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Collaudemus Dominum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045239Z">
        <seg>Let us praise the Lord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Collegit illa, quas potuit, sinuque se pleno stitit divino Puerulo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061900Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061900Z">
        <seg>She gathered what she could and, with her arms full, stopped the divine little boy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Colligito, lector, ex tam atroci remedio, quam fuerit juvenculæ importuna somni vexatio: in lecto, muricibus ac tribulis, foris autem clavis pugnandum fuit, &amp; Rosæ seu dormire vellet, seu vigilare, tormenta subeunda erant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043106Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043106Z">
        <seg>You, reader, can gather from such a dreadful remedy how severe the young woman's unrelenting sleep disturbance must have been: in bed she was beset by mice and thorns, and outside she had to contend with keys; whether Rose wished to sleep or to stay awake, she had to endure torments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Communicato demum cum Rosa consilio, responsum ab ea retulit, quod postmodum eventus comprobavit: nimirum, ipsam nec S. Dominici, nec alterius Ordinis monialem, sed legitimi mariti conjugem fore.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012535Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012535Z">
        <seg>Finally, after sharing the plan with Rosa, he received her reply, which was later confirmed by events: namely, that she would be neither a nun of St. Dominic nor of any other order, but the wife of a legitimate husband.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Comparebat velut altera in civitate peccatrix, verecundabatur, tundebat præcordia, trepidabat quasi jamjam erebi rictu absorbenda;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040642Z">
        <seg>She appeared like another sinner in the city, was ashamed, beat her breast, and trembled as if about to be swallowed by the gaping maw of hell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Compassa Virgo, ancillam bene de Dei misericordia sperare jussit, fore, quod Leonardus rite ipsam uxorem duceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012702Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012702Z">
        <seg>The compassionate Virgin commanded the handmaid to hope confidently in God's mercy that Leonard would duly take her as his wife.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Compatiebatur Rosa communi tot capitum necessitati, &amp; secretissimo instinctu jussa sperare optime de Dei providentia, dixit matri: “Si mandas, descendam ego in nomine Domini, visura num quid residui mellis inveniam”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062229Z">
        <seg>Compatient with the common need for so many heads, and by a most secret instinct commanded to hope for the best in God's providence, she said to her mother, "If you command, I will go down in the name of the Lord to see if I can find any honey left."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Compendium id fuit, quo Rosa celeri adjuta miraculo, nec deesset operi misericordiæ, nec in alienis ædibus præter Virginum morem diutius hæreret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061510Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061510Z">
        <seg>It was a shortcut by which Rosa, swiftly aided by a miracle, would neither be lacking in the work of charity nor linger longer in a stranger's house than is the custom of virgins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Compertum est, sub hoc densissimo pungentis metalli vepreto, ipsam loquelam Virgini fuisse dolorificam: quid si tussi concutiebatur pectus?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042133Z">
        <seg>It is known that, under this densest thicket of piercing metal, the Virgin's very speech was painful: what if her chest was shaken by a cough?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Componitur hoc nomen ex voce&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;φάρμακον&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;quæ&lt;/e4&gt; medicamentum &lt;e5&gt;vel&lt;/e5&gt; venenum, &lt;e6&gt;&amp; verbo&lt;/e6&gt; &lt;s7&gt;ἀλέξω&lt;/s7&gt;, &lt;e8&gt;quod&lt;/e8&gt; auxilior, &lt;e9&gt;aut&lt;/e9&gt; arceo &lt;e10&gt;significat, ita ut&lt;/e10&gt; alexipharmacum &lt;e11&gt;Latine vocari possit&lt;/e11&gt; medicamentum auxiliare, &lt;e12&gt;vel&lt;/e12&gt; venenum arcens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034515Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034515Z">
        <seg>This name is composed of the word&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;pharmakon&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;which&lt;/e4&gt; means medicine &lt;e5&gt;or&lt;/e5&gt; poison, &lt;e6&gt;and the verb&lt;/e6&gt; &lt;s7&gt;aléxō&lt;/s7&gt;, which means to help or to repel, so that alexipharmacum can be called in Latin a helpful medicine, or a poison repeller.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Compuncta demum, suæque animæ salubrius prospectura, cum modum non videret, quo aut periculoso contubernio se explicaret, aut, servili conditione pressa, ingenui viri legitimas assequeretur nuptias, Rosæ calamitatem suam fidenter aperuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012659Z">
        <seg>At last, deeply troubled and seeking a healthier path for her soul, since she saw no way either to free herself from a dangerous association or, oppressed by her servile condition, to obtain a legitimate marriage as a free woman, she confidently revealed her calamity to Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conabantur pulte liquidiori supplere lactis inopiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052432Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052432Z">
        <seg>They tried to make up for the lack of milk with thinner porridge.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conabatur ad amussim parere Virgo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035712Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035712Z">
        <seg>The Virgin tried to obey to the letter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conabatur inde venari tristissimum solatium, quod putabat sub his cruciatibus se sensim interituram: neque enim videbantur tales, qui sine totali destructione fragilis creaturæ subsistere diu possent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045555Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045555Z">
        <seg>She tried from this to draw the saddest consolation, thinking that under these torments she would slowly perish: for they did not seem to be of a kind that could long endure without the total destruction of the frail creature.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conabatur intellectus vel minimas Deitatis arripere stricturas&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt;, sed omne lumen diffugerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010319Z">
        <seg>He tried to grasp, even in the smallest degree, the strictures of the Deity, but all light had fled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conabatur intellectus vel minimas Deitatis arripere stricturas&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, sed omne lumen diffugerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045509Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045509Z">
        <seg>He tried to grasp, even in the smallest degree, the strictures of the Deity, but all light had fled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conata quidem hæc fuit semel iterumque Filiam a tantæ austeritatis excessu divertere, &amp; lectum immanem frustatim dissipare, sed immisso divinitus conscientiæ stimulo, nec manum ausa est adhibere, nec imperium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042706Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042706Z">
        <seg>She indeed tried again and again to turn her daughter away from such an excess of austerity and to tear the enormous bed to pieces, but, divinely stirred by a promptings of conscience, she dared neither to lay a hand on it nor to command it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Concepta exinde fiducia, supplex nonnullas Rosæ ad se reliquias deferri petiit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034903Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034903Z">
        <seg>Confident of this, the supplicant humbly asked that some relics of Rose be brought to him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Concionatores ipsos vehementer hortabatur, ut flagitiosas animas ab ima vitiorum charybdi&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt;, ne perirent, ad securum pœnitentiæ littus toto conatu evocarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042347Z">
        <seg>He vehemently urged the preachers themselves to summon the most depraved souls from the very depths of the whirlpool of vice, so that they might not perish, to the safe shore of repentance with all their might.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conclamatum erat de vita hominis, cessatum a pharmacis, &amp; ab omnibus medicis desertus infirmus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032048Z">
        <seg>It was proclaimed that the man's life was at stake, that he had stopped taking his medicine, and that the sick man had been abandoned by all the doctors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Concremer ego, deficiam ego, consumar ego igne tui divini amoris, o mi benignissime Jesu”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053117Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053117Z">
        <seg>I am consumed, I shall fail, I shall be consumed by the fire of your divine love, O my most benign Jesus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conditio autem hæc erat, quod Rosa interea sui confessarii febres in se reciperet, prout olim sanctæ Catharinæ Senensi contigit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053511Z">
        <seg>The condition, however, was that Rosa should in the meantime contract her confessor's fevers, just as once happened to Saint Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confessarii coram feretro in voces jubilationis &amp; benedictionum eruperunt, testantes cunctis, baptismalem gratiam nulla umquam lethali noxa ab ea interpunctam fuisse, sed immaculatam totius vitæ innocentiam, &amp; virginei candoris infantilem puritatem cælo ad sequendum Agnum, quocumque ierit, intulisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042939Z">
        <seg>The confessors, standing before the bier, broke out into cries of rejoicing and blessings, testifying to all that the baptismal grace had never been interrupted by any lethal stain, but had preserved an immaculate innocence throughout her life and brought the childlike purity of virginal whiteness into heaven to follow the Lamb, wherever he might go.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confessarii, quo penitus animæ statum nosse cœperant, facilius hanc ei frequentiam indulgebant, unanimiter attestantes in processu, tam limpidam se invenisse in ejus spiritu munditiem, tamque nitidam innocentiæ puritatem, tantam insuper Eucharistiæ famem, tam robustum devotionis calorem, ut exinde numquam ausi fuerint a participatione tanti mysterii Virginem distinere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055705Z">
        <seg>The confessors, having thus come to know the state of the soul thoroughly, more readily indulged this frequency for her, unanimously testifying in the process, that they had found in her spirit such limpid purity, such radiant innocence, such a hunger for the Eucharist, and such a robust fervor of devotion, that from then on they had never dared to withhold from the Virgin participation in so great a mystery.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confessarios exorabat, ut ad eam omni arte pœnitentes inducerent, ipsosque divini verbi præcones frequentissime hortabatur, ut ad ejus studium, veluti ad magnam adversus omnia peccata pharmacopœiam, suos auditores inflammarent, ac piarum meditationum usum populariter inveherent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041727Z">
        <seg>He urged the confessors to bring penitents to her by every means, and he most frequently exhorted the preachers of the divine word to inflame their hearers with zeal for her, as for a great panacea against all sins, and to popularize the practice of pious meditations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confessarius nil differendum esse edixit, iret, properaret, gossipium apponeret, seseque divino favori submitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054004Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054004Z">
        <seg>The confessor decreed that nothing was to be postponed: go, hurry, prepare the gossiper, and submit yourself to divine favor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confestim Virgo accivit vicinum S. Sebastiani parochum licentiatum Quixanum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012929Z">
        <seg>Immediately the Virgin called for the neighboring parish priest of St. Sebastian, Quixanus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confestim Virgo, arrepta occasione, gravem ac nervosum de tranquillitate animi sermonem instituit, breves ac salubres acquirendæ mansuetudinis aphorismos&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt; præscripsit, æquanimitatis ac patientiæ apta consilia ingessit, sicque instructam a se dimisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011528Z">
        <seg>Immediately the Virgin, seizing the opportunity, launched into a serious and nerve-racking discourse on the tranquility of the mind, prescribed brief and wholesome aphorisms for acquiring gentleness, offered fitting counsel on equanimity and patience, and thus dismissed her, having instructed her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confestim Virgo, arrepta occasione, gravem ac nervosum de tranquillitate animi sermonem instituit, breves ac salubres acquirendæ mansuetudinis aphorismos&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; præscripsit, æquanimitatis ac patientiæ apta consilia ingessit, sicque instructam a se dimisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060732Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060732Z">
        <seg>Immediately the Virgin, seizing the opportunity, launched into a serious and nerve-racking discourse on the tranquility of the mind, prescribed brief and salutary aphorisms for acquiring gentleness, offered fitting counsel on equanimity and patience, and thus dismissed her, having been instructed by them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confestim igitur ad Virginem Matrem obsecrando conversa rogavit anxie, periculo succurreret, ipsumque instrumentum in altero locorum, quos ipsa mente designabat, reconderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040909Z">
        <seg>Immediately, therefore, she turned in supplication to the Virgin Mother and anxiously begged her to come to the rescue in the peril and to conceal the very instrument in another of the places she had in mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confestim jacitur alea&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;, favet sors Virgini, lucratur, vincit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010545Z">
        <seg>Immediately the die is cast, fortune favors the Virgin, she gains, she wins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confestim jacitur alea&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, favet sors Virgini, lucratur, vincit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051015Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051015Z">
        <seg>Immediately the die is cast, fortune favors the Virgin, she gains, she wins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confestim tota domus in spem erigitur, parturientis germana soror Maria de la Sierra, susceptis reliquiis, periculo advolat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035120Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035120Z">
        <seg>Immediately the whole household is filled with hope; the sister of the laboring woman, Maria de la Sierra, takes up the relics and flies to the danger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confidit illa in homine mortali, didicitque ad illius fragilem assistentiam tantisper solitos metus ponere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062105Z">
        <seg>She trusted in a mortal man, and learned to set aside her usual fears for the time being for his fragile assistance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confirmat ista (sed breviter) laudatus supra P. Antonius de la Vega Societatis Jesu, aliique.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032752Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032752Z">
        <seg>The above-mentioned Father Anthony de la Vega of the Society of Jesus, and others, confirm these things (but briefly).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confugit Rosa familiari fiducia ad cælestem Sponsum adjutorem in opportunitatibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051111Z">
        <seg>Rose, with familiar trust, fled to the heavenly Spouse for help in times of need.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Congredere, cornuta bellua, eia congredere”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052107Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052107Z">
        <seg>Come on, you horned beast, come on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conjectabat se a variegato papilione non solum ad concolorem Senensis Catharinæ habitum, verum etiam insigniri eodem emblemate cordis, quod olim cum sponsa Senensi Sponsus æternus permutarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044129Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044129Z">
        <seg>He conjectured that he would be distinguished not only by the multicolored butterfly but also by the same emblem of the heart that the Eternal Spouse had once exchanged with his Sienese bride.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conjectarunt subito, quod erat, permagnum aliquid actitari, velut sub iride Rosarii, cujus mysteria triplici hac tinctura distinguuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040357Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040357Z">
        <seg>They suddenly conjectured that something very great was being enacted, as if under the rainbow of the Rosary, whose mysteries are distinguished by this triple tint.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conjicio, te nunc in exornando gloriosæ matris Senensis simulachro occupari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055049Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055049Z">
        <seg>I suppose you are now busy decorating the statue of the glorious mother of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Consederat solitaria in horto domestico colligendis floribus intenta Virgo, jamque effrondibus rosis gremium impleverat, cum, arrectis in cælum oculis, &amp; arcano intus dictante spiritu, cœpit non sine devotis suspiriis jactare in altum singulas rosas, velut superno eas hortulano commendans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062514Z">
        <seg>A solitary virgin sat in the home garden, intent on gathering flowers, and had already filled her lap with freshly picked roses, when, raising her eyes to heaven and moved by a secret inner spirit, she began, with devout sighs, to toss each rose high into the air, as if commending them to a heavenly gardener.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Constat etiam, duas probrosæ vitæ peccatrices, ad Rosæ hujus odoriferam famam compunctas, jugum impuritatis feliciter excussisse, &amp; in calle virtutis constanter fixisse pedem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043057Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043057Z">
        <seg>It is also known that two women of infamous and sinful lives, moved by the fragrant fame of Rose, happily cast off the yoke of impurity and firmly set their foot on the path of virtue.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Constat, ex devotæ Rosæ mystica officina pluries hoc vestimentorum genus prodiisse: nam subinde amicissimis personis eadem liberali dono cedebat offerenda, applicanda, circumponenda Jesulo nudo, amori suo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053613Z">
        <seg>It is clear that from the devout workshop of the Mystical Rose this kind of garment emerged on multiple occasions: for she would from time to time present the same as a generous gift to her dearest friends, to be applied and wrapped around the naked Jesus, her beloved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Consurgebat illis diebus Limæ novellum S. Claræ monasterium, totius sanctitatis, rigoris, atque observantiæ sacrarium, auspicante illustrissima D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040016Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040016Z">
        <seg>In those days the new monastery of St. Clare was being erected in Lima, a sanctuary of all holiness, rigor, and observance, under the auspices of the most illustrious D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Consurgebat illis diebus Limæ novellum S. Claræ monasterium, totius sanctitatis, rigoris, atque observantiæ sacrarium, auspicante illustrissima D. Maria de Quinnones, cui avunculus erat ille ipse famigeratissimus Dei servus, Limensium archiepiscopus tunc superstes, D. Turibius Alphonsus Mogroveio, de quo supra.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000514Z">
        <seg>In those days the new monastery of St. Clare was being erected in Lima, a sanctuary of all holiness, rigor, and observance, under the auspices of the most illustrious Lady Mary of Quinnones, whose uncle was that very renowned servant of God, the then surviving Archbishop of Lima, Don Turibius Alphonsus Mogrovejo, of whom mention has been made above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Contigit aliquando, citharæ omnes deesse chordas, nihilominus Rosa citharizabat, intenta magis interius sonanti decachordo psalterio cum cantico.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053229Z">
        <seg>It happened once that all the harps were out of strings; nevertheless, Rosa played the harp, her attention more intent on the inner sound of the ten-string psaltery with its song.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Contigit hoc anno salutis MDCVI, ipsa S. Laurentii martyris festiva die, cui haud absurde aptaveris illud Tibulli lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040214Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040214Z">
        <seg>This happened in the year 1606, on the very day of the feast of St. Lawrence the Martyr, to which you might not inappropriately apply that passage from Tibullus, Book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Contigit, Joannem ex cruciatibus stomachi periculose ægrotare, ita ut accito confessario, tamquam vitæ incertus animam per exomologesim duxerit expiandam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061438Z">
        <seg>It happened that John fell dangerously ill from stomach pains, so that, having summoned his confessor, he led his soul to be purified by confession, as if uncertain of his life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Contigit, ex medicinæ consilio ægrotanti phlebotomiam adhiberi, extractumque vena sanguinem in angulo seponi, dum medicus ad eum inspiciendum rediret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061339Z">
        <seg>It happened that, on the doctor's advice, the patient was subjected to a bloodletting, and the blood drawn from the vein was set aside in a corner while the doctor returned to examine it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Contigit, huic ad fugam patuisse fortuito posticum cœnobii, quo, ceu elapsus nassa pisciculus, tacite se proripuit, &amp; foris in urbe se occultavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033930Z">
        <seg>It happened that by chance the monastery's back door was open for his escape, through which, like a small fish slipping from a net, he quietly slipped out and hid himself in the city.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Contigit, prout Virgo prædixerat: Franciscam ter culex adrosit, exinde illo die semperque in posterum libera ab his fuit, præsente Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043934Z">
        <seg>It happened, just as the Virgin had foretold: a mosquito stung Francisca three times; from that day forward, in the presence of Rosa, she was always free from them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conveniebant ad eam e vicinis domibus parvulæ, at lusitandi gratia, quod illius ætatis primum studium: afferebant singulæ suas pupas, quibus vestiendis, comendis, ornandis, femellarum pueritia communissimo sexus genio addicitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043221Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043221Z">
        <seg>Little girls from the neighboring houses would gather at her home to play, for that is the first passion of that age; each would bring her own dolls, to whom dressing, feeding, and adorning are the common occupations of girlhood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Convocati testes arte &amp; doctrina celebres, qui, omnibus serio expensis, censuere, supernaturale esse, quod in icone gerebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042133Z">
        <seg>The summoned witnesses, renowned for their art and learning, after careful consideration, concluded that what was depicted in the icon was supernatural.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Coram his D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050237Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050237Z">
        <seg>In the presence of these, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Coram his D. Castillus, integro trium horarum spatio, Virginem in domestico cellulæ horto, [quorum unus, Joannes Castillus,] velut indagine cinctam, per varias quæstiones distinuit, animæque venis diligentissime pertentatis, omnia sana ac salva invenit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010437Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010437Z">
        <seg>In the presence of these, Don Castille, over the course of a full three hours, questioned the Virgin in the domestic garden of the cell—one of them being John Castille—as if she were surrounded by an investigation, and after most diligently probing the veins of her soul, found everything sound and safe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cordatissima est, planeque extrema hæc sui abnegatio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050727Z">
        <seg>This self-denial is most complete and, indeed, extreme.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Corporalem vero ex Eucharistico pastu saturitatem ipsamet Rosa tum confessariis, tum domesticis fassa est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055941Z">
        <seg>Rosa herself confessed to both her confessors and her household that she was sated in body by the Eucharistic meal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Costam Stephano de Cabrera fortuitus lapsus perfregerat: tumebat infirmo quassatum latus, furebat per incrementa, somnumque adimebat horribilis dolor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035533Z">
        <seg>A chance fall had broken Stephen de Cabrera's rib: his side swelled from the bruised wound, the pain raged with each heartbeat, and a dreadful ache robbed him of sleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Costarum tormenta accreverant, dolores ischiadum, cor quotidie septies vel octies suis cruciatibus laborabat, stomachus bis per diem flaccescebat in deliquium, ut subito ad auroram festinato jentaculo fuerit instaurandus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035545Z">
        <seg>His side pains had increased, his sciatic pains, and his heart, daily tormented seven or eight times by its own pangs, his stomach would twice a day give way into a fainting fit, so that he had to be quickly restored with breakfast at dawn.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Crebrum hoc ei in ore erat: “Domine, ecquis te non amaret?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053034Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053034Z">
        <seg>He often had this in his mouth: "Lord, who would not love you?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Credebat innocens Virgo, se suis peccatis has debere vindicias.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041513Z">
        <seg>The innocent virgin believed that she deserved these punishments for her own sins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Credebat, se nec dimidium horæ quadrantem in aviculæ musica hæsisse attentam, &amp; forsan horæ integræ effluxerant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045047Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045047Z">
        <seg>She believed she had not been attentive to the little bird's music for even half an hour, and perhaps a full hour had already passed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Credibile est, ipsammet, statim ut cum Quadragesima expirabat utendi facultas, ultro solvisse catastam, dispersisse testarum fragmina, projecisse paxillos &amp; ligna, ne quid innotesceret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042945Z">
        <seg>It is credible that she herself, as soon as the permission to use it expired with the Lenten fast, would have willingly paid the tax, scattered the fragments of shells, thrown away the pegs and the wood, so that nothing would be discovered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Credidit Rosæ vaticinio Ludovicus, medicum devote coluit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012402Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012402Z">
        <seg>Louis believed Rose's prophecy, and he devoutly honored the doctor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Credidit afflicta mulier in spem contra spem, felicique experimento comperit, Rosam non ex vano promisisse solatium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012705Z">
        <seg>The afflicted woman believed in hope against hope, and by a happy experiment discovered that Rosa had not promised comfort in vain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Credidit mater audiens lusus esse metricos, sed Calendis Augusti prophetice ea prænuntiata fuisse cognovit: sub noctis enim medium audita fuit misere ejulare, &amp; corpusculo super solum extenso, rigentibus membris inventa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042722Z">
        <seg>The mother, hearing it, believed it was a metrical game, but on August Kalends she realized it had been prophetically foretold: for in the middle of the night she was heard wailing miserably, and her little body was found stretched out on the ground with stiffened limbs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Creditur, S. Catharinam Senensem aliquando idem, quod Rosa, passam esse: de B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045831Z">
        <seg>It is believed that St. Catherine of Siena once suffered the same thing as Rosa, concerning the Blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Credëbat firmiter, &amp; ita ab omnibus credi satagebat, omnium se creaturarum esse abjectissimam, &amp; pestilentissimam, nævum execrabilem infelicis sæculi, orbis maculam, humani generis ultimum carcinoma.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041244Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041244Z">
        <seg>She firmly believed, and strove to have everyone believe, that she was the most abject and pestilent of all creatures, an execrable blemish on an unhappy age, a stain on the world, the final cancer of the human race.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Crescebat cum ætate religio, cum statura corporis elevatio mentis in Deum: nam &amp; pueriles ipsius annos exercebat seria meditandi frequentia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044805Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044805Z">
        <seg>As he grew in years, so did his religion grow, and with his stature, the elevation of his mind to God: for even in his boyhood he practiced a serious and frequent meditation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Creverat puella, jamque suimet per ætatem compos, ut rescivit, se neutiquam in baptisterio sed in cunis traxisse hoc succedaneum nomen, suspicata est pro solo vanitatis lenocinio forsitan hoc sibi inditum fuisse nomen, quo vultus sui commendaretur forma ac venustas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034724Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034724Z">
        <seg>The girl had grown up, and now, of age, she learned that she had never been given this substitute name at baptism but in her cradle; she suspected that it had perhaps been bestowed on her for the mere lure of vanity, to commend the beauty and charm of her face.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Crevit puer, at nullo religiosæ inclinationis indicio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012548Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012548Z">
        <seg>The boy grew up, but showed no sign of a religious inclination.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Crevit pullus, sed ea inerat ventricoso pigritia, ut jugiter cubarit humi, &amp; vix umquam visus sit ultro in pedes attolli, numquam auditus cucurire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061531Z">
        <seg>The chick grew, but it was so sluggish and belly-heavy that it lay on the ground all the time, and was scarcely ever seen to rise to its feet of its own accord, nor was it ever heard to chirp.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Crevit subitis ac prodigiosis augmentis recens parthenon, ita quod hodie ultra ducentas numeret sanctimoniales, sponso Deo, sub inviolabilis clausuræ religiosa custodia, militantes.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062421Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062421Z">
        <seg>The newly founded convent has grown by sudden and prodigious increases, so that today it numbers over two hundred nuns, who, with God as their spouse, serve under the inviolable religious enclosure in strict custody.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Crevit ætas, sed pariter cum ætate afflictio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052445Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052445Z">
        <seg>Age has grown, but with it, affliction;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Crucem ex densiori charta, flosculis, plumisque versicolorem imposuit, &amp; quidquid sacrarum imaginum conquirere poterat, in hanc suam basilicam solicite comportabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043252Z">
        <seg>He placed a cross of thicker paper, with little flowers and multicolored feathers, and diligently gathered whatever sacred images he could find and brought them into his basilica.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cruciabatur ingenti scrupulo Rosa, quod exacte ad præscriptam horam evigilare non poterat, ut summe cupiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051329Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051329Z">
        <seg>Rosa was tormented by a great pang of conscience because she could not wake up at the exact prescribed hour, as she had so dearly wished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Crucis archipincerna sitis imiranissi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025739Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025739Z">
        <seg>May the arch-caterer of the Cross be your most wondrous thirst.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cui Rosa: “Quid ni curam tui peculiarem haberet seraphica nostra mater per totum hunc diem, posteaquam tu integram hanc noctem in ejus obsequio mecum laboriosam exegisti”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055318Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055318Z">
        <seg>Rosa said to him, "Why shouldn't our seraphic mother take special care of you this entire day, now that you have spent the whole of last night laboriously in her service alongside me?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cui Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035558Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035558Z">
        <seg>To whom does Rosa belong?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cui modesta Virgo levi subrisu aiebat: “Continuo affabilis illa cæli Regina favoribus cumulat miserrimam hanc peccatricem”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054345Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054345Z">
        <seg>To whom the modest Virgin said with a slight smile, "Immediately that affable Queen of Heaven showers her favors upon this wretched sinner."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cuidam, ob rigidos paralysis stupores sibi familiariter compatienti, mira vultus animique serenitate respondit, aliquando se tractasse cum Deo de adoptan lo mendico puerulo, quem ipsa educaret in spem Euangelii inter barbaros propagandi (ut supra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025658Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025658Z">
        <seg>To a certain person, who was familiarly sympathetic toward her because of the severe stupors of her paralysis, she responded with a marvelous serenity of face and spirit, sometimes saying that she had been negotiating with God about adopting the beggar boy, whom she herself would raise in the hope of spreading the Gospel among the barbarians (as above in the chapter).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cum enim esset duodecim annorum in quodam pago obvium habuit ferocissimum taurum illuc ruentem, &amp; fugam meditantibus his, qui cum ea erant, fixis in cælum oculis dehortata est a fuga.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042530Z">
        <seg>For when she was twelve years old, in a certain village she encountered a most ferocious bull rushing toward her, and she discouraged those who were with her from fleeing, fixing her eyes on the sky.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cum sol hunc attingit (quod bis evenit in anno) dies in toto terrarum orbe fiunt nocti æquales, unde&lt;/e0&gt; æquatoris &lt;e1&gt;nomen ei inditum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041352Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041352Z">
        <seg>When the sun reaches this point (which happens twice a year), the days throughout the entire world become equal to the nights, from which the equator gets its name.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cum tamen locus ille dicatur hic&lt;/e2&gt; metalli fodinis percelebris, &lt;e3&gt;verosimilius indicatur&lt;/e3&gt; Guanca &lt;e4&gt;cognomento&lt;/e4&gt; Velica, &lt;e5&gt;e cujus fodinis decies centena millia pondo hydrargyri vel argenti vivi quotannis eruuntur, &amp; terrestri itinere Limam deducuntur, ut proxime citatus Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T214718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035858Z">
        <seg>Although that place is said to be here&lt;/e2&gt;, it is more likely indicated by Guanca, nicknamed Velica, from whose mines a million pounds of mercury or quicksilver are extracted each year, and transported by land to Lima, as the recently cited Latius reports in book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cum tu Creator sis, &amp; ego creatura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045335Z">
        <seg>Since you are the Creator, and I am a creature.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cum vero in vicinum inde cubiculum vocaretur, sive a cadaveris tristi dimovenda spectaculo, sive ut cum aliis videret Aloysiam tunc forte rigentem in extasi, ire distulit, ne angulum suum tanti jubili conscium præmature desereret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035833Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035833Z">
        <seg>But when he was summoned into the adjoining room—whether to remove himself from the sad spectacle of the corpse or to go with others to see Aloysia, who at that moment was perhaps rigid in ecstasy—he delayed, so as not to desert his corner, aware of so great a jubilation, too soon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cum vos admoneo ad contemptum seculi, invitare vos venio ad cœnam Dei.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041411Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041411Z">
        <seg>As I admonish you to despise the world, I come to invite you to the supper of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cumque nec sic proficeret, imperiosa præcepit, quotidie secum deinceps accumberet communi mensæ cum familia, visura demum, quid, quantumque manducaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041157Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041157Z">
        <seg>And when even that failed to help, she imperiously ordered that from then on she be seated at the family table with them every day, so that she could finally see what and how much she ate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cumque nonnemo præsentium dixisset, prægrandem &amp; omnino rarissimam esse hanc gratiam, sat prospere agi cum animabus hinc migrantibus, dum pro residua mortalis vitæ scoria detergenda ad purgatrices flammas mittuntur, se sibi id optare animitus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061838Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061838Z">
        <seg>And when one of those present had said that this grace was exceedingly great and altogether rare, and that souls departing from here fare well enough while they are sent to the purifying flames to have the remaining scum of mortal life washed away, he earnestly wished that for himself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cunctabatur Virgo, metuens, præpropere sibi dolores eripi, quos pro Sponsi amore diutius tolerare optabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053954Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053954Z">
        <seg>The Virgin hesitated, fearing that her pains would be prematurely snatched from her, which she wished to endure longer for the love of her Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cur ille tardet, Rosa dum ardet,</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051620Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051620Z">
        <seg>Why does he tarry while the rose is in bloom?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Currebat &amp; ipsa per hujus sponsæ vestigia in odorem Sponsi, sed inter adolescentulas, ut sibi videbatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044024Z">
        <seg>She too ran along the bride's trail, following the scent of the Bridegroom, but among the young women, as it seemed to her;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cælestem, respondit, &amp; obtinuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013338Z">
        <seg>He answered, "Heavenly," and obtained it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cælestis Paterfamilias, cujus natura bonitas, voluntas potentia, &amp; opus misericordia est, ubi ad magnam divinitatis cœnam invitatos Judæos, seipsos ut indignos eximentes, conspexit, ad vicos, &amp; plateas civitatis accersentem hinc inde alios atque alios convivas servum fidelem transmisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041030Z">
        <seg>The Heavenly Head of the household, whose nature is goodness, whose will is power, and whose work is mercy, when he saw the Jews invited to the great feast of divinity excusing themselves as unworthy, sent his faithful servant here and there into the villages and streets of the city to summon other and other guests.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cælitus data fuit ei fiduciæ certitudo assequendi tria maxima munera, æternam videlicet beatitudinem, perpetuam &amp; numquam interpolandam Dei amicitiam, &amp; infallibilem ex alto succursum in quibusvis tum necessitatibus, tum repentinis periculis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042502Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042502Z">
        <seg>He was divinely granted the certainty of faith to attain the three greatest gifts: eternal beatitude, the perpetual and never-to-be-interrupted friendship of God, and unfailing help from on high in whatever needs or sudden dangers might arise.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cœpit ergo per otium lectitare, auscultante altera;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032702Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032702Z">
        <seg>She began, then, to read aloud leisurely, while the other listened.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Datum Romæ apud S. Petrum anno Incarnationis Dominicæ MDCLXXI, pridie Idus Aprilis, Pontificatus nostri anno primo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043435Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043435Z">
        <seg>Given at Rome at St. Peter's on the 13th day before the Ides of April, in the first year of our Pontificate, in the Lord's year 1671.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De Christi serto spineo canit Ecclesia: “Felix spina, cujus aculei vires frangunt regis tartarei”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042338Z">
        <seg>Of Christ's thorny crown the Church sings: "Blessed thorn, whose spikes break the power of the Tartarean king."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De aliis ejusdem vocis significationibus auctius Cangii Glossarium ad nomen&lt;/e2&gt; audientia &lt;e3&gt;consuli potest.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030933Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030933Z">
        <seg>For other meanings of the same word, Cangii's Glossary can be consulted under the name 'audientia'.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De apparitionibus hujusmodi consule Papebrochium nostrum tomo 6 Maii pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051508Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051508Z">
        <seg>For apparitions of this kind, consult our Papebroch in the sixth volume of May, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De apparitionibus hujusmodi consule Papebrochium nostrum tomo 6 Maii pag. 246 &amp; sequentibus, ubi habet&lt;/e1&gt; Parergon de Sanctarum exstaticarum secundum species naturaliter præhabitas durante raptu quandoque motarum dictis factisque ad historicarum quæstronum decisiones non transferendis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010626Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010626Z">
        <seg>For apparitions of this kind, consult our Papebroch in the sixth volume of May, page 246 and following, where he includes a Parergon on the natural movements of the ecstatics during their rapture, sometimes made by words and deeds, which are not to be transferred to the decisions of historical questions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De hac Virginis in divinam providentiam admiranda fiducia seorsim capitulo sequenti.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061103Z">
        <seg>On the Virgin's admirable trust in divine providence, separately in the following chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De hujus remedii laudibus ac necessitate Gorræus &amp; Castellus ad vocem Græcam&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;φλεβοτομία&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;consuli possunt&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045952Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045952Z">
        <seg>For the praises and necessity of this remedy, Gorræus and Castellus can be consulted on the Greek word φλεβοτομία.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De igne hoc nostro elementali aiebat ridendam esse comparationem, quia is nonnisi pœnam sensus, quam vocant, infligere aptus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045806Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045806Z">
        <seg>He said that the comparison of this elemental fire of ours was laughable, because it is only capable of inflicting what they call the punishment of sensation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De primis duobus, pauca hic præfari necessum est, ut ex horum sanctitate &amp; peritia, vis, pondus, fides maturissimi examinis aptius innotescat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050033Z">
        <seg>Regarding the first two, it is necessary here to say a few things so that, from their holiness and expertise, the force, weight, and credibility of the most mature examination may be more aptly recognized.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De reliquis rosis subitaneum sertum contexens tremendo Jesu capiti imposuit, qui virgini arridens, eamque benedicens disparuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042521Z">
        <seg>Weaving a sudden garland from the remaining roses, she placed it on the head of the awesome Jesus, who, smiling at the virgin and blessing her, vanished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De situ &amp; etymologia ipsius inter astrologosdisputatur.&lt;/e2&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054800Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054800Z">
        <seg>Its location and etymology are disputed among astrologers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De variis ejus speciebus medici jam sæpe laudati ad hanc vocem consuli possunt.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053002Z">
        <seg>On its various species, physicians who have already been frequently praised on this subject may be consulted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De vario morbi istius effectu plura apud Gorræum &amp; Castellum invenies.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041002Z">
        <seg>You will find more about the various effects of this disease in Gorræus and Castellus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Debebatur enim tam simili Filiæ sinus seraphicæ suæ matris ad quietem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043020Z">
        <seg>For she owed so much to her mother's seraphic daughter, who had brought her to rest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Decumbebat agoni proxima, cum in ultimo vitæ suæ horario quadrante offertur ei pro confortatione cordis pretiosus liquor sorbendus e cyatho;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040713Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040713Z">
        <seg>She lay near death, when in the final quarter-hour of her life a precious liquid was offered to her from a chalice to comfort her heart, to be sipped.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Decumbebat in ædibus viduæ D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061330Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061330Z">
        <seg>He was lying down in the house of the widow D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Decumbebat in ædibus viduæ D. Isabellæ de Mexia quædam ex domesticis famulabus, putri humore, febriumque æstu prætumida;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011646Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011646Z">
        <seg>A certain domestic servant of the widow, Mrs. Isabella de Mexia, was lying ill in her house, overcome by phlegm and a feverish heat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deficiens quadam nocte viribus misit eum ad Mariam de Usategui, ut nuntiaret ei, se egere remedio, quod per famulum statim illa transmisit, vidente &amp; agnoscente hoc a Filia per obedientiam matre.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041941Z">
        <seg>One night, growing weak, he sent him to Mary of Usategui to tell her he needed a remedy, which she immediately sent by way of a servant, as the Mother saw and recognized this being done by her daughter through obedience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deficiente fellis copia, succedebant in vicem folia quædam amarissimi fruticis, quorum suctu &amp; masticatione, Christi in morte sitibundi hyssopum, male jurulentum, saucia cogitatione recolebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041240Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041240Z">
        <seg>When the supply of gall failed, certain leaves of a most bitter shrub took its place; by sucking and chewing them, the thirsty for Christ in death recalled, with wounded thought, the ill-tasting hyssop.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Degebat in quodam Indorum pago cum parentibus duodennis Filiola, ubi die quadam cum matre ac fratribus domum rediens, in patenti area obvium vidit taurum ferocissimum, qui elapsus stabulo, vinculisque partim ruptis, partim raptatis, cursu præcipiti illuc ruebat, unde veniebat cum comitibus Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062117Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062117Z">
        <seg>She was living in a certain Indian village with her parents, the twelve-year-old daughter, when one day, as she was returning home with her mother and brothers, she saw in a clearing a most ferocious bull, which, having escaped from the stable and with its ties partly broken and partly torn away, was rushing at full speed toward the very spot where she was with her companions, Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dehinc Anna in Virginem animæquior, pristinumque errorem deprecata, illius se protectioni religiosius commisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040726Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040726Z">
        <seg>From then on, Anna, more at peace in spirit, repented of her former error and more devoutly committed herself to her protection.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dehinc chartam adhibuit, visurus, an forte ex coloribus oleo temperatis, unctuosa aliqua pinguedo humori inesset: commaduit charta, sed paulo post siccabatur: unde pure aqueum esse collegit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053849Z">
        <seg>Then he applied a sheet of paper to see whether, perhaps, some oily, greasy substance was present in the moisture: the paper adhered, but shortly afterward it dried; from this he concluded that it was purely aqueous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinceps nemo (opinor) dubitabit, Rosam B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061423Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061423Z">
        <seg>Henceforth, no one (I think) will doubt, Rosa B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinde ad adstantes socias lepide conversa, subdebat:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045338Z">
        <seg>Then, turning gracefully to the women standing by, she added:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinde antistes e throno progressus ad feretrum, thuris &amp; lustralium aspersionum cæremonias inter consuetos psalmorum &amp; responsoriorum modulos obibat decenti pompa, velut mox ad ultimam efferendi humandique sacri corpusculi processionem accingendus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031038Z">
        <seg>Then the high priest, stepping down from the throne and advancing to the bier, performed the ceremonies of incense and lustral sprinkling amid the customary psalm and responsorial chants with fitting pomp, as if about to prepare for the final procession to carry forth and lay to rest the sacred little body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinde fidentius ad specialia descendens, monuit hominem de arcanis, ipsi soli notis, quæque a tanta distantia non nisi divinitus, aut angelo internuntio, Virgini potuerant innotuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051908Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051908Z">
        <seg>Then, descending more confidently to particulars, he warned the man of the secrets known only to himself, which from such a distance could have been made known to the Virgin only by divine means or through an angelic messenger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinde ibidem breviter virtutes ejus &amp; lucubrationes recenset, quibus hæc subjungit:&lt;/e2&gt; Vitam ejus testis oculatus in litteras misit Franciscus Losa parochus cathedralis Mexicanæ ecclesiæ, quem secuti sunt peculiaribus suis historiis Alphonsus Ramon Mercinariorum sodalis, &amp; Ludovicus Muños causarum procurator in Matritensi curia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042015Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042015Z">
        <seg>Then, in the same place, he briefly reviews his virtues and writings, to which he adds the following: His life was committed to writing by Francis Losa, parish priest of the cathedral church of Mexico City, whom followed, with their own particular histories, Alphonsus Ramon, a member of the Mercedarian Order, and Louis Muños, a lawyer in the Court of Madrid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinde in vultum augustissimæ Dei Parentis intenta, ut vidit hunc sibi serenum &amp; solito dulcius adblandientem, resumpsit animum, jamque complacebat sibi in eo, quo fuerat contristata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044303Z">
        <seg>Then, gazing at the face of the most august Mother of God, when she saw it serene and smiling on her more sweetly than usual, she recovered her spirits, and was now pleased with that very thing which had before saddened her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinde laxatis templi foribus, admissus populus undatim irruit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030945Z">
        <seg>Then, the temple doors being opened, the people rushed in in waves.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinde pag. 936 &amp; sequentibus ejusdem Operis varia magisteria chirurgica vel chymica exponuntur, &amp;&lt;/e3&gt; magisteria corallorum, perlarum, &lt;e4&gt;aliaque hujusmodi memorantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034457Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034457Z">
        <seg>Then on page 936 and the following of the same work various surgical or chemical masteries are set forth, and the masteries of corals, pearls, and other such things are mentioned.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinde ponderibus denuo impositis, cumulabantur ex adverso gratiæ super gratias;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052801Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052801Z">
        <seg>Then, with the weights once again imposed, graces were piled upon graces from the opposite side.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinde propius accitis, qui aderant, duobus suis germanis fratribus, seriam, gravem, ac sententiosam parænesim dixit, ac inter alia solicitam parentum curam, honorem, obsequium efficacissime commendavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030149Z">
        <seg>Then, having summoned his two brothers who were present closer, he gave them a serious, grave, and sententious exhortation, and among other things, he most effectively commended the careful care, honor, and obedience of parents.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Demum annulo osseo, quem a Rosa acceperat, salutem debuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035008Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035008Z">
        <seg>In the end, she owed her life to the bone ring she had received from Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Demum etiam inde ultimo simplicium novitiorum oratorio illatum est, ibique sub excubitoribus a Priore constitutis pernoctavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030731Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030731Z">
        <seg>Finally, he was also brought there at last to the novices' simple oratory, and there he spent the night under the watch of the guards appointed by the Prior.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Demum excitus fama prodigiorum, quæ ad Rosæ sepulchrum quotidie coruscabant, eo accessit, fusisque de genu devotis precibus, tegmen cum fasciis a capite resolvit, detracto malagmate&lt;a1&gt;h&lt;/a1&gt;, terram ibidem excavatam vulneri inspersit, meliusque ab eo momento se habere sentiens, domum alacer properavit, ac post biduum solide vulnus coaluisse, sanamque induisse cutem deprehendit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035852Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035852Z">
        <seg>Finally, stirred up by the fame of the miracles that were daily shining forth at the tomb of Rose, he went there, and kneeling down with devout prayers, he loosened the covering with the bandages from her head, removed the malagma&lt;a1&gt;h&lt;/a1&gt;, sprinkled the earth that had been dug up there into the wound, and feeling that he was better from that moment, he hurried home cheerfully, and after two days he found that the wound had solidly closed and had grown over with healthy skin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Demum nocte quadam recordatus, sibi in domo esse rosarium, quod paulo ante in feretro exanime Rosæ corpus attigerat, illud, ubi acutior doloris sensus erat, devote applicuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033716Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033716Z">
        <seg>Finally, one night, remembering that he had at home the rosary that had shortly before touched the lifeless body of Rose in her coffin, he devoutly applied it to the spot where the sharpest pang of pain was felt.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Demum oranti in templo S. Dominici Rosæ innotuere omnia, sibique hanc desolatam &amp; perplexam reservari mox intelligens, modum, quo subveniret, prompte invenit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061209Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061209Z">
        <seg>Finally, while praying in the church of Saint Dominic, Rose perceived everything, and, understanding that this desolate and perplexed case was soon to be entrusted to her, she promptly found a way to help.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Demum prædixit apertius, Franciscam (etsi tunc repugnantem) una cum germana sorore Catharina tertii Ordinis sancti Dominici monialem fore, at Philippam, quæ tunc ejusdem instituti flagrabat desiderio, in oblatas tandem honestas nuptias consensuram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012516Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012516Z">
        <seg>Finally, he more clearly foretold that Francisca (though then reluctant) would, together with her sister Catherine, become a nun of the Third Order of Saint Dominic, while Philippa, who at that time was aflame with desire for the same institute, would eventually consent to the honorable marriages offered to her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Demum surrexit sponte, sanitatis indiciis lætus, neque ex eo tempore usque ad ætatis suæ annum decimum septimum (quo una cum matre hæc apud judices apostolicos testificatus est) hac aut alia quacumque infirmitate impetitus fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033524Z">
        <seg>Finally he recovered of his own accord, rejoicing at the signs of his health, and from that time until the seventeenth year of his age (when he and his mother testified to these facts before the apostolic judges) he was not afflicted by this or any other infirmity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Demum ut vestimenta (quibus pene desueverat) impetravit, domo cum honesto comitatu exiit, rectaque ad sancti Dominici ædem solidis gressibus properavit, ubi visitato Rosæ sepulchro, domum inde post mensem ad suos in patriam, alia, quam exierat, rediit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034931Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034931Z">
        <seg>Finally, having obtained clothes (which she had almost grown accustomed to), she left home with an honorable escort and made her way in firm strides straight to the church of Saint Dominic. There, after visiting Rose's tomb, she returned a month later to her own country and to her people by a different route than the one she had taken.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Demum uterque parens, Novena ad Rosæ sepulchrum promissa, filiolam eo faciunt deportari, ac virgineo monumento pronam insterni.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034131Z">
        <seg>Finally, each parent, having promised a novena at the sepulchre of the Rose, has their little daughter carried there and laid prone upon the virgin tomb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique abbatissa servatam ex Rosæ tunica particulam de scrinio protulit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034733Z">
        <seg>Finally, the abbess took from the reliquary a preserved particle of the Tunic of the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique biduo postquam Rosam vita functam terra exceperat, ad illius sepulchrum delatus, fere per duas horas quietus insedit, dum mater cum cognatis feminis in templo hæsit, Deo &amp; virgini Rosæ sobolem commendans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033545Z">
        <seg>Finally, two days after Rosa had passed away and been laid to rest, he went to her tomb and sat there quietly for nearly two hours, while his mother and female relatives remained in the church, entrusting the child to God and to the Virgin Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique ejusdem materiæ totidem ulnas, quæ pallio muliebri sufficerent, devota persona obtulerat Limano Fratrum Prædicatorum Conventui;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061141Z">
        <seg>Finally, a devout person had offered to the Convent of the Friars Preachers of Lima the same number of yards of the same material, which would have been sufficient for a woman's cloak;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique fidem silentii obsecravit, &amp; abiit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044712Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044712Z">
        <seg>Finally, he swore the oath of silence and left.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique hera utilissimi mancipii jacturam non ferens, Helenæ suasit ac persuasit, ad Rosæ, biduum ante sepultæ, monumentum Novenam exigeret (ita novendialem cum certis precibus stationem vulgo appellant.) Infirma, vitæ ac valetudinis percupida, majori animo, quam viribus, aggressa est opus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033741Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033741Z">
        <seg>Finally, unable to bear the loss of her most useful servant, she urged and persuaded Helena to perform a novena at the tomb of Rosa, who had been buried two days earlier (the people call such a nine-day station with specific prayers a novendial). Weak in body but eager for life and health, she undertook the task with more spirit than strength.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique huc respexisse Davidem cum gemeret: “Dicitur mihi quotidie, ubi est Deus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050426Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050426Z">
        <seg>Finally, David looked back here as he groaned: "I am told every day, 'Where is God?'"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique in his Dei attributis pene tota movebatur sui cordis systole atque diastole, tantumque in iis profecerat, ut modeste aliquando fassa sit, etiam inter nendum, ad singulas acus puncturas, novo se Deum encomio compellare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045000Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045000Z">
        <seg>Finally, her heart's very systole and diastole were driven almost entirely by these attributes of God, and she had advanced so far in them that she once modestly confessed that even while sewing, at each puncture of the needle, she compelled herself to utter a new encomium of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique in sacratissimo divini Natalis pervigilio, dum matrona confessura patrem adibat, per ipsam Rosa eidem significari jussit, deponeret jam tandem inanissimam vicini sui obitus persuasionem, sciretque se grandioribus Dei obsequiis reservari, non moriturum prius, quam foris complures animas operosa conversione in viam salutis adduxerit, interque illas peculiariter eminere quinque præcipuas, suo apostolatu dignas, &amp; vero Numini adquirendas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012256Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012256Z">
        <seg>Finally, on the most sacred vigil of the divine Nativity, while a matron was approaching the father to confess, Rosa had him signaled through her to lay aside at last the most vain persuasion of his neighbor's death, and that she should know she was reserved for greater services of God, that she would not die until she had brought several souls outside to the way of salvation through her laborious conversion, and that among them five in particular stood out, worthy of her apostolate and to be won over to the true God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique ingemiscebat querula, tam raros &amp; paucos esse, qui Dominum tam suavem toto ac puro affectu redamarent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054140Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054140Z">
        <seg>Finally, the querulous one sighed, that there were so few who would love the Lord with a whole and pure affection.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique invaluit eo usque prodigiosa lætandi sympathia, quod, dum exspectabatur diei adradiantis diluculum, nonnullæ ibidem devotæ feminæ sacrorum hymnorum sonoro cantu per vices liberrime se oblectarint, protestantes non lesso illic, sed jubilo esse locum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030328Z">
        <seg>At last a marvelous sympathy for rejoicing grew so strong that, while the dawn of the approaching day was awaited, several devout women there freely delighted themselves in turn with the sonorous singing of sacred hymns, proclaiming that it was not a place of mourning but of jubilation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique jam mutatus ab illo, qui paulo ante fuerat, sic Virginem affatus est: “Sentio in te loqui Christum, qui solus me tibi sic detegere potuit, ut intima pravi cordis mei penetrares;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060707Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060707Z">
        <seg>Finally, now changed from the one he had been a little while before, he thus addressed the Virgin: "I feel Christ speaking in you, who alone could have revealed me to you in this way, so that you might penetrate the inmost depths of my wicked heart."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique jam satis omnia terroribus impleverat taurus, quando venientem eminus rhedam conspicatus, recta in illam libravit cursum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062145Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062145Z">
        <seg>At last the bull had already filled everything with terror, when, spotting a chariot approaching from a distance, it charged straight at it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique jucundissimum ipsi erat, quod eodem die ac loco, &amp; palmam &amp; Rosam amiserat, utpote post hoc connubium non amplius sua.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044427Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044427Z">
        <seg>Finally, it was most painful to him that on the same day and in the same place he had lost both the palm and the rose, since after this marriage they were no longer his.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique lætemur, &amp; exultemus, quia cælestis illa curia ex nobis habet, cui sit cura nostri, quæ suis nos protegat meritis, quos informavit exemplis, miraculis confirmavit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040949Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040949Z">
        <seg>Finally, let us rejoice and exult, for we have that heavenly court which cares for us, which protects us by its merits, which it has informed us with its examples and confirmed with its miracles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique materna licentia propius os naresque applicuit, odorem sensit, exertaque lingua mordacitatem sævissimi collyrii lambendo deprehendit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043416Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043416Z">
        <seg>Finally, with her mother's permission, she brought her mouth and nostrils closer, caught the scent, and with her tongue protruding, detected the sharp bite of the most savage collyrium.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique metuebat simul ac sperabat conversæ peccatricis constantiæ ac stabilitati: verum in solatii cumulum adfuit superna revelatio, qua Rosam Christus ipse securam reddidit de seria reductæ monialis pœnitudine, adjungens, eam non modo perseveraturam, sed insignem quoque sanctimoniæ gradum adepturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053433Z">
        <seg>Finally, she feared as much as she hoped for the constancy and stability of the penitent's conversion; but to the abundance of her consolation came a heavenly revelation, by which Christ himself assured Rosa of the sincerity of her monastic repentance, adding that she would not only persevere but also attain an eminent degree of holiness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique post ingentes sumptus frustra in remedia erogatos, e Chincha Limam in ædes Petri de Vega se fecit transportari, quod illic speraret medicos peritiores.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034857Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034857Z">
        <seg>Finally, after having spent vast sums in vain on remedies, he had himself transported from Chincha to Lima, to the house of Peter de Vega, because he hoped for more skilled physicians there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique post multa ultro citroque de sui diffidentia, de ardore fidei, de securitate spei, de stimulis superni amoris ventilata &amp; sagacissime explorata, conclusit doctor, sinceram ac tutam esse viam, qua Virgo ambulabat, nullam hic subesse vaferrimi dæmonis imposturam, tales affectus, effectus, lumina, ab illusione aut principe tenebrarum nequaquam esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050619Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050619Z">
        <seg>Finally, after much back-and-forth about self-doubt, the ardor of faith, and the security of hope, after many things had been tossed back and forth about self-doubt, the ardor of faith, the security of hope, and the stirrings of divine love, which were most shrewdly examined, the doctor concluded that the path the Virgin was treading was sincere and safe, and that no crafty imposture of the devil lay beneath it, and that such affections, effects, and illuminations could in no way be from illusion or the prince of darkness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique pro crepundiis puerulo offeram meas lachrymas, suspiria, actus amoris, cumque his animam &amp; cor meum, ut nihil mihi remaneat, quia convenit nihil me possidere”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053610Z">
        <seg>Finally, in place of a cradle, I will offer my boy my tears, sighs, and acts of love, along with my soul and heart, so that nothing remains for me, for it is fitting that I possess nothing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique sentiebat Rosa, se compellari primo illius diei Responsorio: &lt;b2&gt;Adorna thalamum tuum Sion&lt;/b2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T005947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T005947Z">
        <seg>At last, Rosa felt compelled to the first Responsory of that day: "Adorn your chamber, O Zion."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique sentiebat Rosa, se compellari primo illius diei Responsorio: &lt;b5&gt;Adorna thalamum tuum Sion&lt;/b5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043630Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043630Z">
        <seg>At last, Rosa felt compelled by the first Responsory of the day: "Adorn your bridal chamber, O Zion."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique solemni die Paschatis, hic annulus ex triduano Dominici sepulchri contubernio jam sacratior, gratiorque, ad Rosam rediit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044511Z">
        <seg>Finally, on the solemn day of Easter, this ring, now made more sacred and dear by its three-day companionship with the Lord's sepulchre, returned to Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique subsannent, rideant, cogitent de me, ut lubuerit, ego quoad potero, non cessabo a cura, ne crux Domini mei, vel in abjecta, vilissimaque stipula usquam conculcetur”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054924Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054924Z">
        <seg>"Finally, let them mock, let them laugh, let them think of me as they please; as long as I am able, I will not cease from my care, lest the cross of my Lord be trampled anywhere, even upon the most abject and vile stubble."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique tam publice notum, tamque populariter crebrum fuit hoc prodigium, ut prænominatus Martinez vir gravissimus &amp; religionis pietatisque fama inter eximios clarus, neutiquam in dicto tribunali dubitarit juratus asserere, videri sibi, quod a tempore, quo Peruvia nostris detecta patuit, nullus umquam claruerit prædicator, qui tot ac tantis stimulis gentes illas ad virtutem impulerit, tamque universalem pœnitentiæ spiritum, tam luculentum devotionis incendium excitarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032650Z">
        <seg>Finally, this prodigy was so publicly known and so frequently spoken of by the people, that the aforementioned Martinez, a man of the greatest gravity and renowned among the most eminent for his fame of religion and piety, did not hesitate in the said tribunal, under oath, to affirm, that in his opinion, since the time when Peru was discovered and opened up to us, no preacher had ever shone forth who had urged those peoples to virtue with so many and such great incentives, or who had kindled such a universal spirit of penance and such a brilliant fire of devotion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique umbraticus ille ductor sequentem Rosam ante genitricis cubiculum stitit, &amp; disparuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051821Z">
        <seg>Finally, that shadowy guide stopped the following Rosa before her mother's bedroom and vanished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique una feminarum, terræ illius pugillum fronti Catharinæ illigavit, &amp; illo momento, ad primum sacri pulveris attactum stetit sanguis, exaruit scatebra, nec deinceps hoc malum rediit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035700Z">
        <seg>Finally, one of the women bound a handful of that earth to Catherine's forehead, and at that moment, upon the first touch of the sacred powder, the bleeding stopped, the festering wound dried up, and the evil did not return thereafter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique universa hæc scena non tam funeris, quam triumphi videbatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031512Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031512Z">
        <seg>Finally, the whole scene seemed less like a funeral than a triumph.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique vel in simplicis aquæ potu adinvenit, quo, sive biberet, sive abstineret, gustum fraudaret: nam ut faucium ardori levamen ac refrigerium subtraheret, integras hebdomadas transigebat sine potu;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041352Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041352Z">
        <seg>Finally, he even found a way to ruin the taste of plain water: whether he drank it or abstained, he would cheat the palate; for, in order to deprive his throat of relief and refreshment, he would go for entire weeks without a drink.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique vivacius hunc Rosæ cruciabilem horrorem effigiebat Christi in supremo judicio vultus asperatus, rugosus, ipsisque dæmonibus terribilis, dum cogitatur velut jamjam dicturus horrendum illud: &lt;b1&gt;Ite maledicti&lt;/b1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010353Z">
        <seg>Finally, it depicted with greater vividness the agonizing horror of this Rose, the face of Christ in the Last Judgment, hardened and wrinkled, and terrible even to the demons themselves, as He is imagined on the point of uttering that dreadful word: "Depart, you cursed."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique vivacius hunc Rosæ cruciabilem horrorem effigiebat Christi in supremo judicio vultus asperatus, rugosus, ipsisque dæmonibus terribilis, dum cogitatur velut jamjam dicturus horrendum illud: &lt;b5&gt;Ite maledicti&lt;/b5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045841Z">
        <seg>Finally, it depicted with greater vividness this crucifying horror of the Rose—the face of Christ in the Last Judgment, hardened, wrinkled, and terrifying even to the demons themselves—as if about to utter that dreadful word: "Depart, you cursed."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique, cum aliud non poterat, oblectabat se imagine ac meditatione suppliciorum, quæ sigillatim omnia pro Sponsi amore desiderabat in se experiri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060316Z">
        <seg>Finally, since she could do nothing else, she delighted herself with the image and meditation of torments, all of which she desired to experience in herself for the love of her Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique, ut ægrotanti modica ex Rosæ indumento laciniola fuit applicata, desedit tumor, tepuere infesti calores, ut infirma mox somnum capere potuerit, e quo sana, extinctis cum erysipela febribus, evigilavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034751Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034751Z">
        <seg>Finally, when a small scrap of Rose's garment was applied to the sick woman, the swelling subsided, the burning heat abated, and she was soon able to fall asleep. She awoke healthy, with the erysipelas and fever both gone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denuo supplex obstitit Rosa, certo asseverans, quæstoris mancipium jamjam cum præparato medicamento adfuturum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051710Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051710Z">
        <seg>Rosa again pleaded earnestly, firmly asserting that the questioner's slave would soon arrive with the prepared medicine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deplorabat inconsolabiliter vicinum Chili&lt;a2&gt;b&lt;/a2&gt; regnum, ubi dietim per myriades peribant animæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011447Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011447Z">
        <seg>He lamented inconsolably the neighboring kingdom of Chili&lt;a2&gt;b&lt;/a2&gt;, where souls perished daily by the myriads;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deplorabat inconsolabiliter vicinum Chili&lt;s4&gt;&lt;a5&gt;b&lt;/a5&gt;&lt;/s4&gt; regnum, ubi dietim per myriades peribant animæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060354Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060354Z">
        <seg>He lamented the neighboring kingdom of Chili&lt;s4&gt;&lt;a5&gt;b&lt;/a5&gt;&lt;/s4&gt; unconsolably, where souls perished by the myriads every day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Depositum fuit decentissime corpus a dextera aræ majoris, verum ob copiosam multitudinem offerentium ibi vota, alius locus sacris exuviis collocandis eligendus fuit, ipsum videlicet sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis sacellum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043017Z">
        <seg>The body was most fittingly deposited to the right of the high altar, but because of the great multitude of votaries there, another place had to be chosen for the sacred relics, namely the chapel of Saint Catherine of Siena itself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Descendit, ipsamque cupam invenit usque ad supremum labrum recenti melle plenam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062232Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062232Z">
        <seg>He descended and found the honeycomb itself full of fresh honey up to the very top.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Describebat brevi linearum ductu murorum ambitum, qui sub prima futuri cœnobii exordia minus spatii complexurus erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062731Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062731Z">
        <seg>He was sketching out the outline of the walls with a few strokes, which, at the outset of the future monastery, was to encompass less space.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Desertum erat obscuritatis, languoris, hebetudinis, regio mortis, nox deliquii, antrum ultimæ calamitatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045459Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045459Z">
        <seg>It was a desert of darkness, languor, and dullness; a region of death, a night of swoon, a cavern of the ultimate calamity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Desperata a medicis muniebatur ultimis morientium Sacramentis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034106Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034106Z">
        <seg>Desperate, she was being fortified by the doctors with the last sacraments of the dying.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Desperata a medico jussa est e communi valetudinario in aliud conclave deportari, ut a frequentiori alloquio libera, ultimis sacramentis percipiendis (si fieri posset) quietius aptaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035458Z">
        <seg>Desperate, she was ordered by the doctor to be moved from the common infirmary to another room, so that, freed from more frequent conversation, she might more quietly prepare to receive the last sacraments, if it could be done.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Detumescente animi procella, dolebat interdum ac damnabat ipsamet suæ ferocitatis effrenem impotentiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060722Z">
        <seg>When the storm of her spirit subsided, she would sometimes ache and condemn the unbridled impotence of her own ferocity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deum utriusque nostrum</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045249Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045249Z">
        <seg>God of us both</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deus sit amor noster.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053142Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053142Z">
        <seg>May God be our love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deus, delicium meum, amare te velim, quantum te amat sanctissima Mater tua, Domina mea, Virgo purissima;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053111Z">
        <seg>God, my delight, I would love you as much as your most holy Mother, my Lady, the purest Virgin, loves you.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Devotæ feminæ sacrorum hymnorum sonoro cantu per vices exultabant protestantes, non næniis, sed festis ibi locum esse debere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042933Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042933Z">
        <seg>Devout women, in turn, exulted with the sonorous singing of sacred hymns, protesting that there ought to be room there not for laments but for feasts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dextera manus, quæ anulum implicuerat, sedis costæ laterali innitebatur, at fixe, velut acclavata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044604Z">
        <seg>The right hand, which had entwined the ring, rested on the side of the seat's rib, but it lay fixed, as if nailed in place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dici non potest, ex Virginis spiritu quos quantosque mox spiritus hauserit felix matrona: diriguit, sensitque Rosæ tum affatu, tum afflatu mutari se medullitus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062939Z">
        <seg>It cannot be said how many spirits the fortunate matron soon drew from the Virgin's spirit; she shuddered, and felt herself being changed to the very marrow by Rose's words and breath.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dici non potest, quam sublimem, invictam, solidam fiduciam hæc paucula cordi Virginis impresserint: plus enim ipso proferendi modo, quam verbis sibi significari intellexit, nec deinceps eam permisit Sponsus in æternæ salutis anchora fixam, ullo incertitudinis fluctu turbari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061816Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061816Z">
        <seg>It cannot be said how sublime, invincible, and steadfast a confidence these few things had imprinted on the heart of the Virgin: for she understood that they signified to her more by the very manner of their utterance than by words, and thereafter the Spouse did not allow her, fixed as she was in the anchor of eternal salvation, to be troubled by any fluctuation of uncertainty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dicitur&lt;/e0&gt; cancer &lt;e1&gt;a similitudine cancri piscis, eo quod paulatim serpat, vel pertinaciter hæreat instar cancri, qui aliquid chelis suis apprehendit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061615Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061615Z">
        <seg>Cancer is said to derive its name from the resemblance of the crab of the zodiac, because it creeps gradually or clings tenaciously like a crab that seizes something with its claws.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dictum ibi de Virginis baptismali innocentia nullo umquam mortali piaculo violata, de illibato mundissimæ pudicitiæ flore, de pœnalium exercitiorum austera duritie, de miris illustrationibus, de seraphicis ardoribus, &amp; quidquid intra horarii sermonis compendium substringi potuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031531Z">
        <seg>There was spoken of the Virgin's baptismal innocence, never violated by any mortal sin; of the immaculate flower of most pure chastity; of the austere severity of penitential exercises; of wondrous illuminations; of seraphic ardors; and of whatever could be compressed within the limits of an hourly discourse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dictum illic pro concione de felicis Rosæ mirabili vita &amp; encomiis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031236Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031236Z">
        <seg>A speech was delivered there before the assembly on the blessed Rose's wonderful life and praises.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Didaci conjux Apollonia non alio, quam isthoc simplicissimæ imaginis cataplasmate stitit mordacissimum capitis stillicidium, quod sibi dentium fere radices utrimque eroserat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040543Z">
        <seg>Apollonia, the wife of Didacus, stopped the most painful headache with nothing other than this very simple poultice.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Didaco de Requena domi æger decumbebat filius, æstu febrium &amp; continuo sincipitis dolore pene exanimatus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035014Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035014Z">
        <seg>At home, the son of Diego de Requena lay sick, nearly senseless from the heat of the fever and a constant headache.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Didacus Martinez Societatis Jesu, Patres magistri F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050216Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050216Z">
        <seg>Didacus Martinez of the Society of Jesus, Fathers teachers F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Didacus de Arrasia Ordinis Prædicatorum e Panamensi ad Limanum Conventum in summo æstu proficiscens, [Didacus de Arrasia Dominicanus,] ex mutatione climatis, duplicem tertianam contraxerat, quæ ipsum per compendia brevi stitit in mortis vestibulo, jubente medico accelerari Sacramenta, maxime, quia &amp; venter infirmi ex nimio aquarum haustu altum extumuerat, &amp; quidquid medelæ seu ventri, seu febri adhibebatur, morbum augebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034029Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034029Z">
        <seg>Didacus de Arrasia of the Order of Preachers, from the Convent of Panama to that of Lima, setting out in the height of the heat, [Didacus de Arrasia the Dominican,] from the change of climate, he had contracted a double tertian fever, which in a short time brought him to the very brink of death, the physician ordering the Sacraments to be administered quickly, especially because the sick man's stomach was greatly distended from drinking too much water, and whatever remedy was applied to either his stomach or his fever, it aggravated the illness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Didacus de Moreno Costilla Quitensis olim in bellis Chilensibus ab impacta ferrea clava immane vulnus capite exceperat, cui paulatim ex cura imperita spasmus&lt;a0&gt;g&lt;/a0&gt;, suppuratio, stupor innascebantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035846Z">
        <seg>Didacus de Moreno Costilla, a native of Quito, had once received a monstrous wound to the head from a steel club in the Chilean wars, from which spasms, suppuration, and stupor gradually arose due to unskilled care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Didicit Rosa, vel ad zelotypiam usque amari se a Sponso, &amp; pro tantæ gratiæ crebriori experimento facile exposuisset in prædam, quidquid florum sibi residuum erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051245Z">
        <seg>Rosa learned that she was loved by her Spouse even to jealousy, and for the more frequent experience of such grace she would have readily laid out as a prey whatever flowers remained to her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Die quadam in paternis ædibus defecit numerosæ familiæ panis, neque vel tempus coquendi supererat, vel nummi ad manum erant, ut aliunde procuraretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062204Z">
        <seg>One day, the bread ran out for the numerous family in the father's house, and there was neither time to bake nor money on hand to procure it elsewhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Die quadam, inter alias adfuit prænobilis, nec virtute minus, quam parentela &amp; opibus clara, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062904Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062904Z">
        <seg>On a certain day, among others, was present a most noble lady, distinguished not less for virtue than for her lineage and wealth, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Die quadam, inter alias adfuit prænobilis, nec virtute minus, quam parentela &amp; opibus clara, D. Lucia Guerra de la Daga.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011925Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011925Z">
        <seg>On a certain day, among others, was present the most noble Lady Lucia Guerra de la Daga, who was no less distinguished for her virtue than for her lineage and wealth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Digressæ moniales, post horæ dimidium redierunt, videruntque Mariam dulci sopore vinctam, at simul copiosissimo sudore madidam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040847Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040847Z">
        <seg>The nuns who had gone off returned after half an hour and saw Mary bound by a sweet slumber, yet at the same time drenched in profuse sweat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dilatabatur crescens malum, angebatur languens Joanna, donec recordata, particulam ex virginis Rosæ lineo velo se habere, eam ori applicuit, &amp; subito omnis abscessit dolor, successit somnus, &amp; evigilans mane Joanna se sanam demirata est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034658Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034658Z">
        <seg>The swelling grew larger and larger, and poor Joanna was in agony until she remembered that she had a scrap of the Virgin Rose's linen veil. She applied it to her mouth, and at once all the pain vanished, sleep came over her, and when she awoke the next morning Joanna was amazed to find herself healed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dilectum quis detinet?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050954Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050954Z">
        <seg>Who detains the draft?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Discruciebatur suspicioso angore, quasi non posset appropinquare felix hora, quæ ipsam voti compotem efficeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032922Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032922Z">
        <seg>She was tormented by a suspicious anxiety, as if the happy hour that would make her wish come true could not draw near.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dissimulavit, ut potuit, famulumque propere cum pelvi abeuntem rogavit, hanc sibi ferendam tradi, se non tantum vacuaturam, quod inerat, sed insuper vas ipsum nitide mundaturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061352Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061352Z">
        <seg>She concealed it as best she could and, calling to the servant who was hurrying away with the basin, asked that it be brought to her, saying she would not only empty its contents but also clean the vessel itself until it was spotless.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Diu assueverat gratias ac quotidiana prodigia, quæ ad Virginis monumentum crebrescebant, aut contradicendo eludere, aut spernendo exsufflare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040717Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040717Z">
        <seg>He had long been accustomed either to dismiss with objections or to scorn the thanks and daily miracles that were multiplying at the Virgin's monument.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Diu parvifecit dictum Virginis, imo aversabatur eventum hujus oraculi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043125Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043125Z">
        <seg>He long belittled the Virgin's words; indeed, he spurned the fulfillment of this oracle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Diu placuit ferventi Rosæ crudelis hæc supinæ torsionis catasta, placuissetque diutius, si fuisset atrocior, hoc est, si acutius &amp; omni ex parte sæviisset: nam inter lignorum vacua spatia nil erat quod incubuam satis cruciaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042601Z">
        <seg>For a long time, this backbending rack of cruel Rose's pleased the fervent one, and would have pleased him longer, had it been more atrocious—that is, had it been sharper and more savage in every respect. For between the empty spaces of the wood, there was nothing that could sufficiently torment the incubus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Divinitus admonita, Joannam de Bobadilia virginem nobilem, sed derelictam esse, quam vorax sub mamilla cancer torquebat, &amp; tardante remedio exitium minabatur, domi recepit, menstruum locationis sumptum &amp; medicaminum pretium ei providit, &amp; post aliquot menses integre sanam restituit, hoc unum dolentem, quod non posset, obstante fide data, Virginis beneficium in grati animi obsequium, ea vivente, palam referre.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042415Z">
        <seg>Divinely admonished, she took into her home Joanna de Bobadilla, a noble but abandoned virgin, whom a voracious cancer under her breast was tormenting, and who, with the remedy delayed, was threatened with destruction; she provided for her monthly room rent and the cost of her medicines, and after several months restored her completely healthy, save for one complaint: that she could not, because of a vow she had made, To publicly acknowledge the virgin's kindness as a token of gratitude while she was still living.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Divinitus prospectum fuit, ut ibi residens complures distantes Missas audiret, videretque non secus ac si corpore præsens fuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041622Z">
        <seg>It was divinely ordained that, while residing there, he should hear several distant Masses and see them as if he had been physically present.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dixerat, &amp; respondit eventus, stupentibus universis non tam reditum tauri, quam Virginis animum omni metu superiorem, quæ in summis periculis cælesti Sponso occinebat: “Non timebo mala, quoniam tu mecum es”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042539Z">
        <seg>He had spoken, and the event responded, to the astonishment of all, not so much at the bull's return as at the Virgin's spirit, superior to all fear, who in the greatest dangers sang to her heavenly Spouse: "I will not fear any evil, for you are with me."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dixisses hanc indolem uni patientiæ natam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034925Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034925Z">
        <seg>You would say that this disposition was born of patience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dixisses, non Virgini chirothecas, sed victimæ sartagines &amp; ignem apparari, adeo exterrita diriguit, ingemuit, hæsitavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035334Z">
        <seg>You would have said that not a virgin's gloves, but a victim's frying pan and fire were being prepared; so terrified, she stiffened, groaned, and hesitated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dixit, facie in serenum risum festive diducta, ac niveo Dominicani habitus sui nitore admodum speciosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031952Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031952Z">
        <seg>He said, his face stretched into a festive, clear-skied smile, and looking exceedingly handsome in the snowy whiteness of his Dominican habit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dixit, quod, attentis rationibus in ea relatis, universalique &amp; publica acclamatione, qua in tota hac civitate &amp; regno Rosa de S. Maria, monialis tertii Ordinis S. Dominici, proclamatur &lt;b0&gt;Sancta&lt;/b0&gt;, dabat facultatem, illius corpus e sepulchro, quod nunc occupabat, transferendi in alium decentem locum, intra S. Dominici ecclesiam, ubi magis videbitur P. magistro F. Augustino de Vega dicti Ordinis Provinciali.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031404Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031404Z">
        <seg>He said that, considering the reasons stated in it, and by the universal and public acclamation by which throughout this city and kingdom Rosa of St. Mary, a nun of the Third Order of St. Dominic, is proclaimed &lt;b0&gt;Saint&lt;/b0&gt;, he granted permission to transfer her body from the tomb which it now occupied to another suitable place, within S. Dominic's church, where it will seem more fitting to the Father Master F. Augustin de Vega, Provincial of the said Order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dixit, viduamque consolata, bene sperare jussit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055253Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055253Z">
        <seg>He said, and having comforted the widow, he bade her hope for the best.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Doctori medico N.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040742Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040742Z">
        <seg>To Doctor N.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Docuerat mater filiolam Rosam utcumque singulas nosse alphabeti literas, jamque in eo erat, ut de iisdem in syllabas combinandis puellam instrueret, simulque in pagella rude exemplar dederat, ut inde calamo characteres formare disceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012944Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012944Z">
        <seg>The mother had taught her little daughter Rosa to recognize each letter of the alphabet, and was now about to instruct her in combining them into syllables, while at the same time she had given her a crude model on a small slate so that she could learn to form the characters with a pen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dole etiam tu, ac resipisce.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060648Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060648Z">
        <seg>Grieve also, and repent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dolores sui gutturis mitigari poscit infirma, &amp; impetrat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051025Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051025Z">
        <seg>The sick woman asks for her throat pain to be eased, and she gets it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Doluerunt Patres, integro fere sexennii spatio nullum penitus exemplar, imo nec tenuissimam famam hujuscemodi Constitutionis in illum usque diem Limensibus innotuisse: sed quid mirum in tanta regionum distantia, perque tot hostium classes, pirataram insidias, &amp; naufragiorum crebra discrimina?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031714Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031714Z">
        <seg>The Fathers were distressed that for nearly six years no copy whatsoever, nor even the slightest rumor of such a Constitution, had become known to the Limeños until that day; but what wonder, given the vast distance of the regions and the many enemy fleets, pirate ambushes, and frequent perils of shipwreck?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Doluerunt religiosi, nondum se illo ordine insignitos esse, cui absolvendi potestas annectitur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035805Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035805Z">
        <seg>The religious were grieved that they had not yet been ordained to that order to which the power of absolution is attached.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Doluit Virgo, illic præter consuetudinem apparere hominem, ubi supra erat sine teste, sine comite.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052203Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052203Z">
        <seg>The virgin was distressed that a man appeared there out of the ordinary, where before there had been no witness, no companion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Doluit primum de sacrilega injuria, qua divinus honor facinore tam fœdo ac publico fuerat violatus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053424Z">
        <seg>He was first pained by the sacrilegious insult, by which divine honor had been violated by such a foul and public outrage;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Domestici cavebant, ne coram ea ineptias proferrent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042115Z">
        <seg>The household servants were careful not to utter foolishness in her presence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Domi in cubiculo sola in oratione pernoctabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041603Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041603Z">
        <seg>At home, she spent the night alone in her room, praying.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Domi, quotquot de familia erant, cavebant, ne quas coram Virgine proferrent ineptias;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053312Z">
        <seg>At home, all the family members were careful not to utter any foolishness in the Virgin's presence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dominica erat palmarum, cum in ipso Rosarii sacello in sui nihili abysso demersam sic affatus est: &lt;b0&gt;Rosa cordis mei, tu mihi sponsa esto.&lt;/b0&gt; Contremuit virgo addulces tam divini Sponsi voces, &amp; simul audivit gratulantem sibi Deiparam, &amp; dicentem: En Rosa, quali te dignatur honore meus hic Filius.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041513Z">
        <seg>It was Palm Sunday when, in the very chapel of the Rosary, immersed in the abyss of her own nothingness, He thus addressed her: "Rose of my heart, be thou my bride." The virgin trembled at the sweet words of so divine a Spouse, and at the same time she heard the Mother of God congratulating her and saying, "Behold, O Rose, what honor my Son here bestows upon you."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dominici, &lt;s2&gt;[sicut experientia compertum est,]&lt;/s2&gt; ac D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043847Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043847Z">
        <seg>Dominic, &lt;s2&gt;[as experience has shown,]&lt;/s2&gt; and D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Donec correptam Rosæ imaginem devotis osculis dissuaviatam, eamdem arcto complexu adstrinxit cordi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040357Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040357Z">
        <seg>He pressed the torn image of Rose to his heart, sweetening it with devoted kisses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Donec tandem audivit a Virgine, hunc sibi diem aliquando fore nuptialem, quo ad Sponsi thalamum esset evocanda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013111Z">
        <seg>Until at last she heard from the Virgin that this day would someday be her wedding day, on which she would be summoned to the Bridegroom's chamber.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Donec tandem ultima nonæ diei statio ad Rosæ sepulchrum, in momento, simul universam tot morborum cacochymiam&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; funditus abstersit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033753Z">
        <seg>Until at last the final station on the ninth day at the tomb of Rose, in an instant, completely cleansed the entire foulness of so many diseases.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dono dedit Montoyæ pauperrimæ virgini alterum ex palliis, quæ mater possidebat, prædicens ei, fore ut aliis novis loco veteris provideretur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042409Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042409Z">
        <seg>He gave to the very poor virgin, Montoya, one of the cloaks that her mother possessed, predicting that she would be provided with others, new in place of the old.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dormiebat procul suis in ædibus Christophorus de Ortega, homo secularis, ac fortunæ infra mediocrem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032055Z">
        <seg>Far from his home, Christopher de Ortega, a layman of below-average fortune, was sleeping.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dulce prome canticum:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045233Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045233Z">
        <seg>Bring forth a sweet song:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dulcescebat exinde in corculo Virginis hic precandi modus, ut ab ea frequentanda nec dormiens cessaret, usque adeo nimirum Dei præsentia jam sensus Rosæ interiores undique occuparat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035024Z">
        <seg>From that time on, this manner of praying before the Virgin became so sweet to Rose that she did not cease visiting her, even in her sleep, for the presence of God had by then completely filled her inmost senses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum Rosa adhuc domi propriæ, suum, de quo infra, reclusorium inhabitabat, Gundisalvi quæstoris uxor ei hebdomadatim mittebat rudioris panis octo placentulas, ut diætim una ad minus necessario victui tota impenderetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041324Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041324Z">
        <seg>While Rosa was still at home, living in her own little cloister (as mentioned below), the wife of the treasurer Gundisalvus used to send her every week eight small loaves of coarser bread, so that one less daily meal would be necessary for her upkeep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum Rosa nendo occupabatur in telæ consutura, en iterum dilectum Jesulum nentis pulvillo infantiliter &amp; quietissime insidentem: illic nutibus taciturnis loquebatur ad cor Dilectæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050916Z">
        <seg>While Rosa was busy spinning at the loom, behold, she again saw the beloved Jesus sitting like a child on the spinning-wheel, spinning most quietly; there he spoke with silent nods to the beloved's heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum Rosa oblongam trabeam (scapulare dicimus) iconi amiciendæ consuit, mittit in conclave, ubi hæc stabat, Philippam de Montoya concivem suam, ut inde serici fili glomerem sibi apportaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055104Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055104Z">
        <seg>While Rosa was sewing the oblong plank (which we call a scapular) for the icon's vesting, she sent into the room where it stood her fellow townswoman Philippa de Montoya to fetch her a skein of silk thread.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum adstantes horror ac frigidus stupor defigit immobiles, jam Magdalena suis insistebat pedibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034925Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034925Z">
        <seg>While horror and a cold stupor fixed the onlookers motionless, Mary Magdalene was already standing on her own feet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum defunctæ cadaver prostabat in feretro, disposuerat cælestis illa (cui nihil fortuitum) providentia, ut deesset florida corolla, quæ de more caput virgineum circumdaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042409Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042409Z">
        <seg>While the corpse of the deceased lay in the coffin, that heavenly providence (to which nothing is accidental) had arranged that the florid wreath, which by custom encircled the virgin's head, was missing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum equus (ut erat generosus &amp; juvenis) terram nititur excutere, dum magnis conatibus tentat surgere, Christophorum gravius premit relapsu, &amp; nonnumquam calcibus improvide ferit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034255Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034255Z">
        <seg>While the horse (being noble and young) tries to shake the earth from its hooves and with great effort attempts to rise, it presses Christopher down more heavily with its backslide and sometimes strikes him unwittingly with its hooves.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum ergo se vidit frustra exspectare, dum hæsitat, quid agendum, prospiciens per fenestellam, vidit cominus umbram candidam, motu decoro agilem, aspectuque non injucundam, quæ Virginem, ut se versus domum sequeretur, invitabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051809Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051809Z">
        <seg>So, seeing that he was waiting in vain and hesitating over what to do, he looked through the little window and saw close by a white shadow, graceful in movement and not unpleasant to behold, which beckoned the Virgin to follow it home.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum his cælestium decretorum arcanis avidissime inhiat auscultatque Rosa, videbatur interim suis manibus floridum hoc sertum paulatim connectere, Sponsoque imponere, qui vicissim ostendit, illud sibi gratissimum, &amp; intime charum esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062604Z">
        <seg>While Rosa hungrily absorbed and listened to the mysteries of these heavenly decrees, she meanwhile seemed to be gradually weaving this flourishing garland with her own hands and placing it upon the Bridegroom, who in turn showed that it was most pleasing and deeply dear to him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum igitur examen peragitur, apparet viduæ fulgida Rosa vultu hilari ac familiariter jucundo, gestu vero ac nutu hortatorio, velut mulieri animum faciens ad dicenda, quæ poscebantur, &amp; singula approbando ratificans, quæ de suis encomiis ad tabulas referebantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032157Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032157Z">
        <seg>Thus, as the examination proceeded, the widow's bright Rose appeared, her face cheerful and familiarly pleasant, her gestures and encouraging nods seeming to embolden the woman to speak the things that were asked, and to ratify each item by approving the praises that were inscribed on the tablets.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum igitur supradicti congerrones inde ad proprias ædes cum matre redeuntem vident, roseo vultu instar auroræ subvividam, aspectuque vegetam, amœnam, &amp; minime decolorem, temere suspicati sunt, Virginem venire ab epulis ac lautitie;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040754Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040754Z">
        <seg>Therefore, when the above-mentioned comrades saw her returning home with her mother, her face rosy like the dawn, her appearance lively, charming, and by no means pale, they rashly suspected that the Virgin was coming from a feast and a sumptuous banquet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum in templi limite (quod Rosa adhuc mortalis tam sedulo triverat) mortua sistebatur, ad aspersionem lustralis aquæ, quibusdam videbatur nova hilarescere jucunditate virginea facies, in niveo veli candore rosea, inque purissimi amictus nitida serenitate formosior, collo totoque corpore flexilis, ut pene exclamaretur: “Non est mortua puella, sed dormit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030629Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030629Z">
        <seg>While she lay dead on the threshold of the temple (which Rosa, still mortal, had so diligently swept), at the sprinkling of lustral water her face seemed to some to be newly enlivened with a virginal gladness, rosy in the snow-white purity of her veil, and made more beautiful by the bright serenity of her purest robe; her neck and whole body supple, so that one might almost exclaim: "The maiden is not dead, but sleeps."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum inter ea discrimina ac pavores Blanca in quintum usque diem miserrime distabescit, opportune marito gubernatori adferuntur e Lima nonnullæ ex Rosæ vestimentis reliquiæ, quas veteri amico Didacus de Cantoral mox ab obitu Virginis dono miserat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035039Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035039Z">
        <seg>While amid those trials and terrors Blanca was miserably adrift until the fifth day, some remnants of Rosa's garments were opportunely brought from Lima to her husband the governor, which Diego de Cantoral, an old friend, had soon after the Virgin's death sent as a gift.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum revertitur, audit e pergula canoram aviculam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045022Z">
        <seg>As he returns, he hears a songbird on the pergola.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum sarcophagus coram principe ara in sublimem tumulum huic usui sumptuose adornatum attollitur, archiepiscopus ad latus Euangelii consedit in throno;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031453Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031453Z">
        <seg>While the sarcophagus is being lifted before the altar, which has been sumptuously adorned on the high mound for this purpose, the archbishop sits on the throne at the side of the Gospel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Duobus germanis suis fratribus inter salutaria monimenta, parentum curam &amp; reverentiam serio commendavit, acsi diceret, unicuique illorum ad imitationem Crucifixi: &lt;b0&gt;Ecce mater tua&lt;/b0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042846Z">
        <seg>To his two younger brothers, among salutary admonitions, he earnestly commended the care and reverence of their parents, as if to say to each of them in imitation of the Crucified One: "Behold your mother."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Duodecima sonuit, &amp; nondum adest?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050938Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050938Z">
        <seg>It's twelve, and he's not here yet?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Duplex est hujus medicamenti genus, unum liquidem, alterum siccum, de cujus utriusque usu ac diverso nomine laudatum Castellum ibidem consule&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035217Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035217Z">
        <seg>This medicine comes in two forms, one liquid and the other dry, and for the use of each and their different names, consult the praised Castellus there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Duplicabat angorem, quod alba vestis humano plausui videbatur ansam aditumque ultro concedere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040338Z">
        <seg>It doubled the anxiety, that the white garment seemed, to human applause, to offer a handle and an entrance of its own accord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Durissimum id erat frugali Rosæ, quam vel odor carnium offendebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041030Z">
        <seg>That was the hardest thing for frugal Rosa, who was even offended by the smell of meat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Durum sane effugium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043345Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043345Z">
        <seg>A hard escape indeed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>E.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041045Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041045Z">
        <seg>And</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ea fuit in teneris annis constantia, ut diros chirurgi cruciatus in pollice, aure, &amp; naribus sine querela, &amp; capitis pœnalem corrosionem ex impositis ad medicamen pulveribus ortam, sine querela invicto animo sustineret, ut ejus indoles jam ex tunc patientiæ nata esse crederetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041113Z">
        <seg>She displayed such steadfastness in her early years that she endured the dreadful tortures of surgery on her thumb, ear, and nose without complaint, and the punitive corrosion of her head caused by the powders applied as medicine, all with an unconquerable spirit, so that her disposition was already believed to have been born of patience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ea occasione honestæ feminæ tum Isabellam, tum ægram invisuræ frequentes accedebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062901Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062901Z">
        <seg>On that occasion, respectable women frequently came to visit both Isabella and the sick woman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eadem Rosa anno Domini MDCXVI mense Augusti, cui post processionem delata fuerat imago devestienda, horrenda arthritide in dextera laborabat, quæ immani cruciatu invaserat articulos omnes, ita ut prædicto operi prorsus redderetur inutilis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042248Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042248Z">
        <seg>That same Rosa, in the year of the Lord 1616 in the month of August, to whom the image had been brought for veneration after the procession, was suffering from a dreadful arthritis in her right arm, which had invaded all her joints with excruciating pain, so that she was rendered entirely useless for the aforementioned task.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eadem infanti Rosæ fuit indoles quieta, suavis, lepidula, &amp; continuo serena, ut commune dumtaxat gaudium totius domus videretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034817Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034817Z">
        <seg>The infant Rose had the same quiet, sweet, charming, and consistently serene disposition, so that she seemed to be the common joy of the entire household.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eadem longo post tempore sic alteri injuncta fuit, acsi de illa numquam tractatum fuisset cum Gundisalvo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012811Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012811Z">
        <seg>The same thing was later imposed on the other, as if it had never been discussed with Gundisalvus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eadem matronarum, ceterique feminei sexus ambitiosa pietas, ac visendi ardor: quacumque funebris processio ducenda erat, utrimque palatiorum, ac domuum fenestras, pergulas, arcus occuparant numerosæ prospectantium facies, undequaque ex porticibus, muris, transennis eminus venturo spectaculo inhiabant centeni, milleni oculi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030558Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030558Z">
        <seg>The same ambitious piety and eagerness to see of the matrons and the rest of the female sex: wherever the funeral procession was to pass, the windows of the palaces and houses on both sides were filled with the faces of numerous onlookers; from every portico, wall, and parapet, hundreds and thousands of eyes gazed from afar at the approaching spectacle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eadem moniali Annæ Mariæ a Jesu adversus diuturnos &amp; acutissimos dentium dolores medicina fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040546Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040546Z">
        <seg>The same remedy was used by the nun Anne Mary of Jesus against long-standing and extremely severe toothaches.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eadem quæstoris conjugi clam fassa est, aulam peramplam, in qua prostabat expositum Rosæ cadaver, undique totam micuisse luminoso illapsu cælestis gloriæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030319Z">
        <seg>She secretly confided to the questor's wife that the vast hall, in which lay the exposed corpse of Rosa, had been all aglow from every side with a luminous outpouring of heavenly glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eadem sub noctis conticinio quondam repentinis stomachi vulsionibus torta ac pene exanimis, subito, ut dictam particulam pectori affricuit, tormina sedavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034553Z">
        <seg>She, who at nightfall had long been wracked by sudden stomach cramps and was nearly faint, suddenly, as she rubbed the said particle on her chest, her colic subsided.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eadem vi charitatis acta, ut vera sponsa, Domini zelavit honorem: irreverentiam enim ecclesiarum detestabatur, loquentes ibi otiose, humiliter &amp; suaviter corripiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042111Z">
        <seg>Acting with the same force of charity, as a true bride, she zealously defended the Lord's honor: for she detested the irreverence of the churches, and would gently and kindly rebuke those who spoke there idly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eadem, ubi festiva processione effertur in publicum (quod annue fit in die octava SS. Rosarii mense Octobri) a dispositis per forum chiliadibus crebra scloporum displosione salutatur, fulgente in singularum cohortium explicitis vexillis nomine, tessera, figura imaginis Rosarianæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054304Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054304Z">
        <seg>Likewise, when she is carried in a festive procession into public (which is done annually on the eighth day of the Holy Rosary in October), she is greeted by the thousands of onlookers in the square with repeated blasts of gunfire, while the banners unfurled by each cohort gleam with the name, the emblem, and the image of the Rosarian.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eam hic, prout ipsamet Virgo paucis ante obitum diebus doctori Castillo ex fide pacta narravit, nudo simplicique adtexam filo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052720Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052720Z">
        <seg>I will present it here, in the same way the Virgin herself, a few days before her death, faithfully recounted it to Doctor Castillo, with a bare and simple thread.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eam namque severioris instituti S. Claræ primæ virgines monasterii Limensis in fundationis sodalem habere totis viribus conabantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041318Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041318Z">
        <seg>For they were striving with all their might to have the first virgins of the more austere monastery of St. Clare in Lima as fellow members of the foundation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eamdem foris muniebat ferrea crates itidem inaurata, &amp; inclusi pignoris tam patula prospectui, quam raptui arcta &amp; adversa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031549Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031549Z">
        <seg>Outside, it was fortified with iron grates similarly gilded, and the enclosed pledge was as open to view as it was narrow and impassable to seize.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eamdem opem puer Joannes Ascensius, virgo Beatrix de Zuniga, adultus Antonius de Umblea effectu uniformi, at locis diversis comprobarunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040044Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040044Z">
        <seg>The same aid was confirmed by the boy John Ascensius, the virgin Beatrix de Zuniga, and the adult Anthony de Umblea with uniform effect, but in different places.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ecce (inquit) ancillam, ecce mancipium tuum, o Rex majestatis æternæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044350Z">
        <seg>Behold (she said) a handmaid, behold your handmaid, O King of eternal majesty;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ecce me pallii tui furem domesticam, sed innoxiam, quia plus tibi proderit amissa palla, quam si intactam possideres.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061119Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061119Z">
        <seg>Behold me, a thief of your cloak—a domestic one, but harmless, for a lost cloak will serve you better than one kept intact.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ecce me”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051242Z">
        <seg>Behold me.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ecce moratur:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050957Z">
        <seg>Behold, he delays:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ecclesiam S. Dominici de more precatura adierat, cum illic existentem subita consternavit recordatio unius ex variis instrumentis domando corpusculo adaptatis, quod ipsa domi fortuito reliquerat loco patulo, ubi facile ab aliquo domesticorum absentis cubiculum ingressuro videri poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040902Z">
        <seg>She had gone to the Church of St. Dominic to pray as was her custom when a sudden recollection of one of the various devices she had adapted to subdue the little body—which she had inadvertently left at home in a conspicuous spot—struck her, where it could easily be seen by any of the household entering the absent one's chamber.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eclipsis hæc docet æstimare divinæ familiaritatis præstantiam, &amp; dignationem pure gratuitam, sufflaminat timoris donum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050414Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050414Z">
        <seg>This eclipse teaches us to appreciate the excellence of divine familiarity and the purely gratuitous dignity, and it bestows the gift of fear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Econtra ille experimentum quærens ejus, qui in Virgine loquebatur Christus, velut ambigenti similis extenuabat vires suas ceu tanto ministerio parum idoneas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060418Z">
        <seg>On the contrary, he, seeking an experiment of the one who spoke in the Virgin, Christ, like a doubter, belittled his own powers as if they were insufficient for such a ministry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Econtra non segnius laborabat Rosa, ut minimos quosque defectus in immensum exaggeraret, serioque ploratu ac gemitu posceret medicinam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040652Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040652Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, Rosa was no less hard at work, magnifying every little flaw to an enormous degree and earnestly demanding a remedy with serious tears and groans.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ecquis vidit umquam, a rosis suppleri apum officia?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062238Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062238Z">
        <seg>Has anyone ever seen the duties of bees be supplanted by roses?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ego Uldericus episcopus Tusculanus Cardin.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043516Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043516Z">
        <seg>I, Uldericus, Bishop of Tusculum, Cardinal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ego in Domino confido non quod ita præservari merear, sed quia ille fidelis est in omnibus verbis suis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062027Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062027Z">
        <seg>I trust in the Lord, not because I deserve to be preserved in this way, but because he is faithful in all his words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ego vero, mi bone Jesu, quando te digne amare incipiam?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053037Z">
        <seg>But I, O good Jesus, when will I begin to love you worthily?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ego, ad quam regularis vitæ ac status nulla umquam cogitatio aditum invenit, ego necessariæ dotis inops, cantandi ac psallendi ignara, curis sæcularibus innutrita, spiritu vacua, clausuræ impatiens, onusta pluribus, ego in claustrum me abdam?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062833Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062833Z">
        <seg>I, to whom no thought of a regular life or state has ever found access, I, lacking the necessary gift, ignorant of singing and psalming, nurtured in worldly cares, empty of spirit, impatient of enclosure, burdened with many things—should I enter a cloister?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Egregium sane stimulandæ orexi proritamentum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041228Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041228Z">
        <seg>Truly an excellent appetizer to whet the appetite.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eia fac Domine, fac te amari, ut decet, excute pharetram, vibra quaquaversum ignita purissima tui amoris jacula, erumpant ubique luculenta cordium incendia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053715Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053715Z">
        <seg>Come, do it, Lord, make yourself loved as is fitting; shake your quiver, hurl on every side the purest fiery arrows of your love, so that brilliant fires of the heart may break forth everywhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eia itote, dicite infirmo, certissime ipsum statuta die prædicaturum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053505Z">
        <seg>Go, tell the sick man that he will most certainly preach on the appointed day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eidem Aloysiæ tertiana febris aliquando diros ac vehementissimos stomachi dolores invexerat, adeo ut præ convulsionum angore, mortem sibi crederet imminere: sed confestim, ubi, alio quocumque omisso medicamine, ad Rosam confugit, dictamque particulam stomacho applicuit, simul cum dolore febris abscessit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034742Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034742Z">
        <seg>At one time, a tertian fever had brought on terrible and most violent stomach pains for the same Aloysia, so that from the anguish of the convulsions she believed death was imminent; but immediately, having abandoned all other medicine and fled to the Rose, she applied the said particle to her stomach, and the pain of the fever departed at once.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eidem Mariæ de Oliva jam velatæ, angusta inter reliquas novitias obtigerat cellula, [quæ vidit, per iconem Filiæ suæ] &amp; hanc insuper sat incommode occupabat ingens lecticæ ligneæ catasta ad modum armarii, quam optabat a pavimento altius sublevari, &amp; quatuor hypostyliis imponi, ut subtus caperet tres grandiores arcas, quæ cellulam alias reddebant multo angustiorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040903Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040903Z">
        <seg>To the same Mary of Oliva, already a nun, a narrow cell had been assigned among the other novices, [which she saw, through an icon of her Daughter] and this was made all the more inconvenient by a huge wooden bedstead, built like a wardrobe, which she wished to have raised off the floor and set on four pillars, so that it could hold three larger chests underneath, which otherwise made the cell much more cramped.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eidem alias fluida a capite distillatio scapulam immensis cruciatibus vellicabat, nec segnius in occipite sæviebat cephalæa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034841Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, other fluids, flowing from the head, tore at the shoulder with immense agony, and the cephalæa raged no less fiercely in the occiput.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eidem coaptatæ lodices, at cilicinæ, setosæ, instarque subcerniculi aut cribri, ut simul &amp; ipsæ pungerent, &amp; testarum puncturas non impedirent, sed obiter absconderent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042629Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042629Z">
        <seg>Mattresses were fastened to the same frame, but they were made of Cilician wool, bristly and like the bottom of a sieve, so that they would prick at the same time and not hinder the punctures of the shells, but would instead conceal them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eidem relationi diserte in illo tribunali subscripsere P. Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032609Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032609Z">
        <seg>The same report was formally signed in that tribunal by P. Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ejus confessario Rosa, dum vixit, aliquando prædixerat, certoque asseverarat, Ludovicam omnino illomet habitu fore amiciendam, imo hunc ardentissime ambituram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032848Z">
        <seg>Rosa, while she was alive, had at times foretold to her confessor and had firmly asserted that Ludovica would be entirely bound to that habit, indeed that she would ardently pursue it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ejus hera (nam puella servilis conditionis erat) sæpius protestabatur, Margaritulæ suæ mortem, quam vitam utiliorem fore: siquidem ab anno &amp; amplius, claræ lucis impatiens, clausis plerumque oculis per ædes aberrabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034636Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034636Z">
        <seg>Her mistress (for the girl was of servile status) often protested that her little Margaret's death would be more useful than her life; for more than a year, intolerant of bright light, she had been wandering through the house with her eyes mostly closed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ejus pulverem ex vino sumptum nephriticum dolorem a flatibus aut frigida causa provenientem dicunt tollere &lt;e4&gt;&amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043156Z">
        <seg>They say that its powder, taken with wine, relieves kidney pain caused by gas or cold, etc.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ejusdem ancilla dentium acutissimos dolores fugavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040019Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040019Z">
        <seg>His same maid drove away the sharpest toothaches.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ejusdem anni mense Novembri, aliam Mariam, decem mensium infantem orphanam, Hieronymus de Soto Alvarado in suis alebat ædibus, deformi lepra toto corpusculo horride maculosam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040326Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040326Z">
        <seg>In November of the same year, another Mary, an orphaned ten-month-old infant, was being supported by Jerome de Soto Alvarado in his almshouses, hideously spotted all over her little body by leprosy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Elegans fructu suo onusta propter ejus amplitudinem: florem fert similem albæ rosæ, in cujus foliis aliquæ velut Passionis Christi notæ (inquit Monardes) delineatæ conspiciuntur, quas magna diligentia istic pictas existimes &lt;e1&gt;&amp;c.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041451Z">
        <seg>Elegant, laden with its fruit because of its size: it bears a flower like that of the white rose, on whose petals certain marks resembling the Passion of Christ (as Monardes says) are discernible, which you would think were painted there with great care, &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eleonora Ruiz de Saudosa discruciabatur atroci cephalæa&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034528Z">
        <seg>Eleonora Ruiz de Saudosa was tormented by a severe headache.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eleonoræ de Castro annosa contubernalis, Rosam in sua eremo visitabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043850Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043850Z">
        <seg>Eleanor of Castile's long-time companion visited Rose in her hermitage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eminebant in ipso, certabantque de primatu, excelsa vitæ perfectio &amp; scholasticæ theologiæ profunditas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050129Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050129Z">
        <seg>They shone in him, and contended for the primacy: the exalted perfection of life and the profundity of scholastic theology.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>En vultum Salvatoris totum sudor operuit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053740Z">
        <seg>Behold, the face of the Savior was completely covered with sweat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>En, inter flores pulchrescis &amp; inter olivas</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053241Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053241Z">
        <seg>Behold, among the flowers you grow more beautiful &amp; among the olives</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eodem modo plura, quæ hoc capite de familiaritate&lt;/e1&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e2&gt;cum Christo narrantur, spiritualiter intelligi velim&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051527Z">
        <seg>In the same way, I would like the many things narrated in this chapter about St. Rose's familiarity with Christ to be understood spiritually.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eodem momento cessavit dolor, consopita quievit Agnes, quæ diu neque dormire potuerat, nec jacere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034156Z">
        <seg>At that very moment the pain ceased, and Agnes, who for a long time had been unable to sleep or lie down, fell asleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eodem zelo precibus transtulit in se confessarii sui febrim, ut concionatoris officio in percelebri solemnitate fungeretur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042121Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042121Z">
        <seg>With the same zeal, he transferred to himself his confessor's fever, so that he might perform the duties of a preacher at the very celebrated solemnity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Epithalamium id fuit, quod non Davidis plectro, sed ore proprio, Sponsi Mater accinuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044418Z">
        <seg>It was a nuptial song that the Bridegroom's Mother sang, not with David's harp, but with her own mouth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Erat Mariæ de Oliva in suo domestico gallinario miræ pulchritudinis pullus gallinaceus, ludebat in ejus tergore &amp; alis versicolor &amp; amœna striatarum plumarum varietas, collum quædam velut purpura decore incinxerat, postrema corporis pro pennarum arcuata venustate in iridem videbantur desinere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061525Z">
        <seg>There was in Mary of Oliva's own domestic hen-house a pullet of marvelous beauty; it played upon its back, and its wings displayed a delightful variety of striped feathers. A certain band encircled its neck with a purple-like beauty, and the hind part of its body, instead of feathers, seemed to end in an arc of charming iridescence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Erat huic Basilio collybistes Didacus de Burguinas, natione Cantaber&lt;a1&gt;n&lt;/a1&gt;, qui anno MDCXIX in eodem vico Potosii quadam ignoti defluxus intemperie sensit sibi guttur intus adstringi, &amp; foris extumescere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035145Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035145Z">
        <seg>This Basil had as his servant Diego de Burguinas, a Cantabrian by birth, who in 1619 in the same street of Potosí felt his throat tighten inside and swell outward from some unknown disorder of the flow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Erat in ejus spiritu limpida mundities, nitida innocentiæ puritas, ac robustus devotionis calor, &amp; nihilominus cum tanto immaculatæ conscientiæ candore, quoties ad divinum Epulum accedendum erat, toties summe gemebunda &amp; lachrymis perfusa sacramentalem confessionem præmittebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042152Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042152Z">
        <seg>There was in her spirit a clear purity, a shining innocence, and a strong fervor of devotion; yet with such immaculate brightness of conscience, whenever she was to approach the divine banquet, she would each time, in deep anguish and bathed in tears, precede it with sacramental confession.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Erga imagines beatissimæ Virginis miro fervebat devotionis affectu, &amp; maxime circa eam, quæ augustissimam rosarii Reginam exprimit, in cujus sacello habitum sui Ordinis suscipiens, ploranti matri visa fuit blando favore Deiparæ in cælum elevari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042217Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042217Z">
        <seg>He burned with a marvelous devotion to the images of the Blessed Virgin, and especially toward that one which depicts the most august Queen of the Rosary; in whose oratory, while receiving the habit of his Order, he was seen, as a weeping mother, to be lifted up to heaven by the gentle favor of the Mother of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo a Basilio pugillum lanæ (de qua supra) petiit, collo circumligavit, &amp; invocato Rosæ auxilio reliquum noctis quievit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035152Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035152Z">
        <seg>Therefore he asked Basil for a handful of wool (about which mentioned above), wrapped it around his neck, and, with Rose's help invoked, rested for the rest of the night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo ab iis derelicta, ad Rosam pro vita ac valetudine appellavit, &amp; illo temporis puncto integerrime convaluit, diuque incolumis supervixit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034051Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034051Z">
        <seg>Therefore, abandoned by them, she appealed to Rosa for life and health, and at that very moment she recovered completely and lived on in good health for a long time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo cedat Æsculapius&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt;, quiescite, o medici;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000525Z">
        <seg>Therefore, let Aesculapius give way, rest, O doctors;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo cedat Æsculapius&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, quiescite, o medici;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041141Z">
        <seg>Therefore, let Aesculapius give way, rest, O doctors;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo collocandis Rosæ exuviis alius demum commodior electus est locus, ipsum videlicet B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031627Z">
        <seg>Therefore, another, more suitable place was finally chosen for the burial of Rosa's remains, namely B itself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo in confusa illa trepidaque hinc urgentium, inde cunctantium perplexitate, recurrendum denique fuit (non sine instinctu Numinis) ad coronatam S. Catharinæ Senensis imaginem, quam Rosa, dum viveret, ornare consueverat: hujus itaque sertum proprium, omnibusque publice notum, tantisper transfertur in Discipulam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042418Z">
        <seg>Therefore, in that confused and anxious perplexity of those pressing on from one side and those hesitating from the other, recourse was finally had (not without a divine prompting) to the crowned image of St. Catherine of Siena, which Rosa had been accustomed to adorn during her lifetime: and so her own proper crown, publicly known to all, is transferred for the time being to the Disciple.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo inops consilii, penetravit cum reliqua turba in ædes quæstoris Gundisalvi, ubi tunc Rosa prostabat in feretro, jamjam efferenda;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033818Z">
        <seg>So, at a loss for what to do, he pushed his way with the rest of the crowd into the house of the quaestor Gundisalvi, where Rosa lay in a coffin, about to be carried out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo luctam &amp; afflictionem suam Deiparæ, ceu veræ Stellæ matutinæ questa est, ut subveniret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051332Z">
        <seg>Therefore, she appealed to the Virgin Mary, as the true Morning Star, for help in her struggle and affliction.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo ne perrexeris meos interimere culices, &amp; ego vicissim tibi spondeo, quod æque tecum uti mecum pacem colent”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043906Z">
        <seg>Therefore, do not go on killing my mosquitoes, and I in turn promise you that they will treat you as they treat me and keep the peace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo notum Religiosum adiit, coronam porrexit, collo imaginis quantocyus immittendam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043525Z">
        <seg>Therefore he approached the known religious man, offered him the crown, and hastily placed it around the image's neck.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo prudens antistes ulterioris violentiæ proclivi metu conversus ad Priorem &amp; graviores Patres, monuit, divertendum esse præsens periculum hac altera dilatione sepulturæ, sibi nequaquam id tempus videri idoneum, quo possit sacrum illud cadaver, per tot hominum globos ac manus, integrum deduci in fossam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031057Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031057Z">
        <seg>Therefore the prudent prelate, fearful of further violence, turned to the Prior and the more senior Fathers and warned that the present danger must be averted by this second postponement of the burial, saying that he by no means considered that time suitable for the sacred corpse to be carried intact to the grave through such crowds and throngs of people.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo puta exosos Deo capillos, quos tu amare videris” Hæc ludibundus puer gestu velut concionatorio profatus, repetebat ludum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034956Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034956Z">
        <seg>"So suppose the hair you seem to love is hateful to God." The boy, jesting, having spoken this with a sort of oratorical gesture, repeated the game.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo recursum ad eosdem pulveres, &amp; statim puella etiam secundas enixa est, sibi &amp; infanti gratulata incolumitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035744Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035744Z">
        <seg>Therefore, she returned to those same powders, and immediately the girl also gave birth to twins, congratulating herself and the infant on their safety.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo rogavit obnixe Gundisalvi cojugem, sibi permitterent saltem per aliquot dies sic jacere absque humano alloquio, forte ut sub solitario hoc silentio quietius intimos &amp; inexplicabiles suos dolores intingeret ultimis doloribus crucifixi Amoris, cui se membratim confixam in cruce sentiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013459Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013459Z">
        <seg>Therefore she earnestly begged Gundisalvi's wife to allow her, at least for a few days, to lie thus without human speech, so that under this solitary silence she might more quietly probe her inmost and inexplicable pains in the final torments of the crucified Love, to which she felt herself nailed limb by limb upon the cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo, proxima Dominica die ad pium hoc sui furtum electa, in viam se conjicit, unico germano suo lætissimi itineris assumpto comite, ac pone juxta S. Dominici vicinam ecclesiam transiens, in eam divertit cum fratre, ut illic in divæ Virginis sacello (cui a Rosario nomen &amp; cultus est) maternam augustissimæ Deiparentis benedictionem, velut opportunum suæ profectionis viaticum præsumeret, exinde reliquum viæ alacrius decursura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040109Z">
        <seg>Therefore, having chosen the next Sunday for this pious theft of herself, she sets out on the road, taking her only brother as her most joyful companion, and makes for St. Passing by the neighboring church of St. Dominic, she turned into it with her brother, so that there in the chapel of the Virgin (which is named and venerated as the Rosary) she might presume to receive the maternal blessing of the most august Mother of God as a fitting viaticum for her journey, and from there would more eagerly complete the rest of the way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Erubuit virgo in sponsi reditu omnino impar suis viribus opificium adhuc infectum inveniri, cui dolenti reseravit ille amplam lapidariam officinam, in qua innumeræ electæ virgines cædendis, &amp; poliendis marmoribus viriliter insudabant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041501Z">
        <seg>The virgin blushed when her groom returned to find the work still unfinished, a task beyond her strength; to her distress, he opened a vast stonecutter's workshop, where countless chosen virgins were laboring manfully to cut and polish marbles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Erupit tandem puerulus Jesus in hoc canticum dramatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044337Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044337Z">
        <seg>The little boy Jesus finally burst into this song of the drama.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Est autem promulsis cibus aut potus, quo orexis excitatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025804Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025804Z">
        <seg>A promulsis is a food or drink that stimulates the appetite.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Est autem&lt;/e0&gt; philtrum, &lt;e1&gt;Græce&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;φίλτρον&lt;/s2&gt;, pharmacum concilians amorem, &lt;e3&gt;quod derivatur a verbo&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;φιλέω&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;hoc est&lt;/e5&gt;, amo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033053Z">
        <seg>It is, however, a philter, from the Greek φίλτρον, a drug that conciliates love, which is derived from the verb φιλέω, that is, "I love."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Est autem&lt;/e3&gt; antiperistasis, &lt;e4&gt;teste Calepino&lt;/e4&gt;, reciprocatio, compressio undique circumfusa, per quam, ubi calidum est, inde frigus expellitur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025829Z">
        <seg>Antiperistasis, however, is, according to Calepinus, a reciprocation, a compression diffused from all sides, by which, where it is hot, cold is expelled from there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Est herba passim nota, cujus varias species apud Dioscoridem explicatas curiosus lector inveniet.&lt;/e5&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055510Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055510Z">
        <seg>It is a herb commonly known, whose various species the curious reader will find explained by Dioscorides.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et ecce post paululum, sociabus nil minus cogitantibus, adfuit illustris feminæ Hieronymæ de Gama ancilla Æthiopissa, cumque pecunia litterulas attulit, quarum hic tenor: “Salve Rosa soror.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055045Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055045Z">
        <seg>And behold, after a little while, while the companions were thinking nothing less, the illustrious woman Jerome de Gama's Ethiopian slave girl appeared, and she brought letters with money, the contents of which were: "Greetings, sister Rosa."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et ecce, a Virginis alloquio vix quinque dies effluxerant, quando virum invasit omnimoda tabaci nausea, mox &amp; horror, ac vehemens abominatio, ita ut deinceps non modo facillime sese abstinuerit sorbitione tubi cretacei&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt;, sed neque amplius sufferre potuerit odorem hujus infaustæ fornaculæ, quam deliciis &amp; cupediis humanæ fatuitatis accensere is solus potuit, qui Job 41 sic depingitur: “De naribus ejus procedit fumus, sicut ollæ succensæ:” &amp; denique hic etiam per Europam invenit, qui secum fumos helluentur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011537Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011537Z">
        <seg>And behold, scarcely five days had passed since the Virgin's address, when the man was seized by a complete nausea for tobacco, soon followed by horror, and a vehement abhorrence, so that from then on he not only most easily abstained from the smoking of the clay pipe, but could no longer endure the smell of this ill-omened little furnace, which could be kindled by the delights and appetites of human folly only by him who is thus described in Job 41: "From his nostrils comes forth smoke, like the smoke of a cauldron that boils over:" and finally, even here in Europe, he found those who would smoke with him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et ecce, a Virginis alloquio vix quinque dies effluxerant, quando virum invasit omnimoda tabaci nausea, mox &amp; horror, ac vehemens abominatio, ita ut deinceps non modo facillime sese abstinuerit sorbitione tubi cretacei&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, sed neque amplius sufferre potuerit odorem hujus infaustæ fornaculæ, quam deliciis &amp; cupediis humanæ fatuitatis accensere is solus potuit, qui Job 41 sic depingitur: “De naribus ejus procedit fumus, sicut ollæ succensæ:” &amp; denique hic etiam per Europam invenit, qui secum fumos helluentur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060803Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060803Z">
        <seg>And behold, scarcely five days had passed since the Virgin's address, when the man was seized by a complete nausea of tobacco, soon followed by horror and a vehement abhorrence, so that from then on he not only abstained most easily from the smoke of the chalky pipe, but could no longer bear the smell of this ill-omened little furnace, which could be kindled by the delights and cravings of human folly only by him who is thus described in Job 41: "From his nostrils comes forth smoke, like the smoke of a cauldron that boils over:" and finally, even here in Europe, he found those who would smoke with him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et ecce, imago, quæ pallentem Rosæ vultum semper retulerat, illo momento venuste in genis rubuit, velut publico civitatis tripudio serene congaudens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040252Z">
        <seg>And behold, the image that had always reflected Rosa's pale face at that moment blushed charmingly on its cheeks, as if serenely rejoicing in the public triumph of the city.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et ecce, postridie cum ingenti fœtidi cruoris profluvio, una prodiere secundæ jam putrefactæ, sensitque Blanca, iterum se morti ereptam, &amp; Limam profecta votum exsolvit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035101Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035101Z">
        <seg>And behold, the next day, with a great flow of foul, bloody matter, the second womb, already putrefied, was expelled; and Blanca, realizing she had once again been snatched from death, set out for Lima and fulfilled her vow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et ecce, quarta circiter post meridiem hora quidam ex incolis præcipiti cursu adfuit, ac sonoro ejulatu rogabat Fratres (quos credebat jam esse sacerdotes) cito accurrerent Cacique&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt; Indæ, vicinis in ædibus animam agenti, ut moribundam saltem pœnitentiæ sacramento expiarent: neque enim alius tunc in oppido sacerdos aderat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035802Z">
        <seg>And behold, at about the fourth hour after noon, a certain one of the townspeople came running up in a great hurry and, with a loud cry, begged the Brothers (whom he believed to be priests) to hurry to the house of the neighboring Cacique, who was dying, so that they might at least absolve her with the sacrament of penance: for there was no other priest in the town at that time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et ecce, sub noctis medium auditur miserrime ejulare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013313Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013313Z">
        <seg>And behold, in the middle of the night, a most wretched wailing is heard.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et ecce, vidit juxta Virginem puerulum Jesum amictu polymito cærulei purpureique coloris decentissime tunicatum, quem ut conspexit fulgida claritate circumdatum, ac radiis undique coruscantem, non ausa conversationem interrumpere, substitit eminus, contenta permissione dulcissimi spectaculi, cujus arcana pro ætate nondum capiebat, nec, antequam Rosa migrasset e vivis, rem detexit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051143Z">
        <seg>And behold, she saw beside the Virgin a little boy, Jesus, most decently dressed in a multicolored tunic of blue and purple, whom, as soon as she saw him, she perceived to be surrounded by a shining brightness, and shining with rays from every side, she, not daring to interrupt the conversation, stood back from a distance, content with the permission of the most sweet sight, whose mysteries her age could not yet grasp, nor did she discover the matter before Rosa had passed from this life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et en, confestim surrexit equus in pedes, homine intacto: descendunt in fossam vernæ, extrahitur Perez incolumis, quem alii intra horam moriturum libere clamitabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034308Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034308Z">
        <seg>And behold, the horse immediately rose to its feet, the man unhurt: they descend into the spring's ditch, Perez is pulled out unharmed, whom others were freely proclaiming would die within an hour.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et en, quidquid sentire ex attactu coronæ cœperat, longe validius sensit ab anulo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044529Z">
        <seg>And behold, whatever he had begun to feel from the touch of the crown, he felt far more strongly from the ring.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et en, subito conquievit pedum manuumque distorta agitatio, surrexit reddita sibi puella, comedit, totamque morbi vim evanuisse indicavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040534Z">
        <seg>And behold, suddenly the distorted agitation of her feet and hands ceased; the girl, restored to herself, rose, ate, and indicated that the entire force of the disease had vanished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et forsitan ob id Virgini datum fuit divinitus, quod plerumque species sacramentales in ejus stomacho ad septem vel octo horas perdurarent, antequam ventriculi calore dissolverentur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055926Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055926Z">
        <seg>And perhaps for this reason it was divinely given to the Virgin that the sacramental species would usually last in her stomach for seven or eight hours before being dissolved by the heat of her stomach.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et nonne sub tanta gratia dignum erat, Reginæ nostræ laudibus diutius immorari”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054546Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054546Z">
        <seg>And was it not fitting, under such grace, to linger longer in praising our Queen?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et paulo infra: “Jam hoc vestimentum confectum est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054630Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054630Z">
        <seg>And a little below: "This garment is now finished;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et quamvis sermo nonnisi de Deo esset, protestabatur utilius sibi ac jucundius semper fore, cum Deo, quam de Deo loqui.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043500Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043500Z">
        <seg>And although the conversation was only about God, he maintained that it would always be more useful and more pleasant for him to speak with God than about God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et quid non egit, ut is ab omnibus diligeretur?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053616Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053616Z">
        <seg>And what did he not do to be loved by all?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et quotiescumque hoc suave ostentum suis referebat, una testabatur, florituras in illo monasterio præclaras Dei famulas, quæ virtutum odore ac sanctitatis fama gloriose excellant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062610Z">
        <seg>And whenever she presented this sweet spectacle to her own people, she bore witness to the splendid blossoms in that monastery—the devoted handmaidens of God who gloriously excelled in the fragrance of virtue and the renown of holiness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et repente conquievit horrida commotio, siluit tempestas, rediit serena tranquillitas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033006Z">
        <seg>And suddenly the dreadful commotion ceased, the storm fell silent, and serene tranquility returned.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et revera is erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044142Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044142Z">
        <seg>And indeed, it was he.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et revera proprium Rosæ centrum sacra Hostia erat, ubicumque eam poterat visu assequi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060022Z">
        <seg>And truly, the sacred Host was the very center of Rose's being, wherever she could see it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et revera ubi medium noctis adfuit, Rosa hoc sese clamore sensit accersiri, candelam benedictam placidissimo nutu petiit, crucis signaculo, velut rem magnam aggressura, frontem, os, pectus communiit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030229Z">
        <seg>And indeed, when midnight came, Rosa, roused by this cry, was summoned; she asked for the blessed candle with the gentlest nod, and, as if about to undertake something great, she marked her forehead, lips, and breast with the sign of the cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et revera, ut durum sit, animam separari a corpore, durius revelli a semetipsa, durissimum fuit revelli a Dilecto post experimentum unionis, ac suavissimorum amplexuum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045642Z">
        <seg>And truly, as hard as it was for the soul to be separated from the body, harder still to be torn away from itself, but hardest of all to be torn away from the Beloved after the experience of union and the sweetest embraces.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et sane profuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034714Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034714Z">
        <seg>And indeed, it was helpful.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et tamen in Rosæ thesauris eminebant virginea puritas &amp; abyssalis humilitas, quæ propriam his nuptiis dispositionem parant, dotemque constituunt: quo fit, quod raris ejusmodi hymenæis peculiari jure præsideat augustissima angelorum Imperatrix, utpote supremæ tum puritatis, tum humilitatis privilegio Virgo singularis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044030Z">
        <seg>And yet in the treasures of the Rose there shone virgin purity and abyssal humility, which prepare the proper disposition for these nuptials and constitute the dowry: whereby it comes to pass that the augustest Empress of the angels presides by a special right over such rare weddings, since the singular Virgin is privileged with the supreme purity and humility.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et te quidem æterno suo decreto selegit Deus, qui primus ibidem sacrosanctam litabis Hostiam in solemnitate: quia primus illic fabricandæ ecclesiæ lapis ritu celeberrimo collocabitur in fundamentis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062650Z">
        <seg>And indeed God has chosen you by His eternal decree, you who will first offer the most sacred Host there in solemnity: for the first stone of the church to be built there will be laid in the foundations with the most solemn rite.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et vere sic erat: nam Rosa sibi, ac genuino vultus colori demum reddita, erupit in jubilum victotiæ: “Eia, (inquit) Sorores, laudandus est nobis Deus, qui nos Tertiarias, communi solidæ charitatis vinculo, sibi dignatur adstringere, ac perpetim sociare”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040400Z">
        <seg>And indeed it was so: for Rosa, her own color and the genuine hue of her face at last restored, broke into a cry of victory: "Come, sisters," she said, "we must praise God, who deigns to bind us Tertiary Sisters to Himself by the common, steadfast bond of charity, and to unite us forever."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et verius &amp; venustius hoc vulnere pedis rubuit Rosa nostra Peruana, quam quod de Rosis Cypriis fabulata est antiquitas, nimirum has non ante rubuisse, quam ex Cypriæ deæ sauciato calcaneo fortuitus eas cruor aspersisset&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003542Z">
        <seg>And our Peruvian Rose blushed more truly and more charmingly with this wound to its foot than the legend tells of the Cyprian Roses, namely that they did not blush until the chance blood from the wounded heel of the goddess of Cyprus had sprinkled them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et verius &amp; venustius hoc vulnere pedis rubuit Rosa nostra Peruana, quam quod de Rosis Cypriis fabulata est antiquitas, nimirum has non ante rubuisse, quam ex Cypriæ deæ sauciato calcaneo fortuitus eas cruor aspersisset&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043330Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043330Z">
        <seg>And our Peruvian Rose blushed more truly and more charmingly with this wound to its foot than the legend tells of the Cyprian Roses, namely that they did not blush until the chance blood from the wounded heel of the goddess of Cyprus had sprinkled them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et videbat tamen, quia sic mulcebant hi favores, ut ab exteriore opere attentionem, nec totam avocarent, nec partem exdividerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050932Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050932Z">
        <seg>And yet he saw that these favors soothed him in such a way that they neither wholly diverted his attention from the external work nor even parted it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et, quod omnem tum rationem, tum admirationem supergreditur, occupabat divina hæc præsentia interiores Rosæ potentias, sine notabili abstractione a sensibus, ita quod, dum Deo loquebatur intus, absque impedimento simul alia quæque necessaria cum domesticis tractaret foris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044836Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044836Z">
        <seg>And, what surpasses all reason and admiration, this divine presence occupied Rose's interior faculties without any notable abstraction from the senses, so that, while she was speaking to God inwardly, she would simultaneously, and without impediment, attend to other necessary matters with her household outside.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Etiam hoc nomen metaphorice pro vili vel fœdo homine sumitur, quo sensu humilis Virgo nostra superius num.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061627Z">
        <seg>This name is also taken metaphorically for a vile or foul person, in which sense our humble Virgin is referred to above, no.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eumdem Petrum alia vice invaserat ischiadum atrox tormentum ac simul dolorifica inflatione tumuerat altera tibiarum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033722Z">
        <seg>The same Peter was struck again by a severe case of sciatica, and at the same time the other of his shins was swollen with painful inflammation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Evacuta utcumque ecclesia, de more adductæ pessulisque firmatæ sunt fores, nec alia sperari poterat tumulandæ Rosæ opportunior hora, quam qua totus religiosorum conventus regulariter pransurus existimabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031143Z">
        <seg>Once the church had been cleared, the doors were, as was customary, drawn to and fastened, and no more opportune hour for Ros's burial could be hoped for than the one when the entire religious congregation was expected to be regularly at lunch.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Evenit, uti Virgo prædixerat: paucis ante concionem horis prædicator convaluit, officioque præclare functus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053517Z">
        <seg>It happened, as the Virgin had foretold: a few hours before the sermon the preacher recovered and performed his duty splendidly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Evicit tandem illa, accedente confessarii nutu, ut Virgo quæstoris uxori se comitem fore promitteret ituræ ad ægrum invisendum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061445Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061445Z">
        <seg>At last, with the confessor's nod, she prevailed upon the Virgin to promise the questor's wife that she would accompany her on her visit to the sick man.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Evidenter agnovit, ab unica sua incredulitate hæc provenire: supplex protestatur, se credere, &amp; toto corde fateri, quod Rosa vere sancta sit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043138Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043138Z">
        <seg>He clearly recognized that this stemmed from his own single act of unbelief; he humbly protests that he believes, and with all his heart confesses, that Rosa is truly holy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Evigilans Mariana prosiliit, ægram sublevatura accessit, obsequium quodcumque in rem foret obtulit, simulque de morbo ac morbi causa perplexe interrogavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041724Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041724Z">
        <seg>Waking, Mariana sprang up, approached to lift up the sick woman, offered whatever assistance was needed, and at the same time asked perplexedly about the illness and its cause.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Evigilans Pacecus, ac mysterii ignarus, volvebat animo, quid sibi voluerit hæc dulcis apparitio, quid brachii apprehensio, quid illa diuturna &amp; tenax constrictio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032333Z">
        <seg>Waking up, Pacecus, ignorant of the mystery, pondered in his mind what this sweet apparition had meant for him, what the grasp of the arm, what that prolonged and tenacious constriction.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Evigilans, hilari vociferatione accitis domesticis, pedem fasciis evolutum ostendit, podagra, tumore, rubore vacuum, sinistroque pedi nihil dissimilem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040611Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040611Z">
        <seg>Upon waking, he called his household servants with a cheerful shout and showed them his foot, freed from its bandages, completely free of gout, swelling, or redness, and in no way unlike his left foot.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Evigilans, non solis doloribus capitis, sed &amp; tota febrium vexa se reperit penitus liberatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034543Z">
        <seg>Upon waking, she found herself completely freed not only from her headaches but also from the entire fever.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Evigilans, tametsi vegetas in redivivis partibus sentiret vires, dimovere tamen reliquias noluit, velut salutiferi effectus plenitudini quietem reliquæ illius diei perlubenter indulgens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034918Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034918Z">
        <seg>Upon waking, though he felt strength in the revived parts, he nevertheless refused to move the remnants, as if gladly granting the rest of that day to the fullness of the healing effect.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex Limano S. Catharinæ Senensis monasterio soror Angela de Haro continuis tribus mensibus lancinabatur cardiogmo, seu convulsione stomachi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040404Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040404Z">
        <seg>Sister Angela de Haro, from the Monastery of St. Catherine of Siena in Lima, was afflicted for three consecutive months with a heartburn, or stomach convulsion;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex Virginis spiritu hausit felix matrona divinos sensus, &amp; religiosi status cæleste desiderium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042617Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042617Z">
        <seg>From the spirit of the Virgin, the blessed matron drew divine senses, and a heavenly desire for the religious state.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex adjunctis arbitror, hic significari tuberculum in naribus exortum, quod forsan metaphorice&lt;/e2&gt; phaseolus &lt;e3&gt;a chirurgis vocatur, quia similitudinem pisi vel fabæ refert.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T025753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035235Z">
        <seg>From the accompanying notes, I take it that this refers to a growth in the nose, which is perhaps metaphorically called a&lt;/e2&gt; "bean" &lt;e3&gt;by surgeons, because it resembles a pea or a bean.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex adverso contrarias his impressiones, tumorem, confusionem divisi cordis, complacentiam sui, &amp; inquietudinem erga Deum, nonnisi picem improbi authoris redolere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052246Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052246Z">
        <seg>On the contrary, these impressions—pride, confusion of a divided heart, self-complacency, and restlessness toward God—smell of nothing but the pitch of an impious author.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex epitheto&lt;/e0&gt; mortiferi, &lt;e1&gt;quod hic additur, satis patet, quale fuerit istud&lt;/e1&gt; promulsidarium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025807Z">
        <seg>From the epithet "mortiferous," which is added here, it is clear enough what sort of promulsidarium that was.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex hac significatione periodus illa satis intelligitur.&lt;/e4&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054723Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054723Z">
        <seg>From this meaning, that period is sufficiently understood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex his Philippa &amp; Catharina desiderabant, spreto seculo, religiosum sancti Dominici habitum, ac tertii Ordinis amplecti institutum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012501Z">
        <seg>Of these, Philippa and Catherine desired, having spurned the world, to embrace the religious habit of Saint Dominic and the institute of the Third Order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex his ad Rosam, celebrem peccatorum apud Deum causidicam, ut convertantur &amp; vivant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032507Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032507Z">
        <seg>From these, to the Rose, the celebrated advocate of sinners before God, that they may be converted and live.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex his duo referam, quæ victrici ejus constantiæ validius incubuerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040245Z">
        <seg>Of these, I will relate two that most heavily weighed upon her steadfastness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex his omnibus conjicio, hic&lt;/e5&gt; unctione magistrali &lt;e6&gt;significari remedium quoddam vel unguentum pretiosum, quod huic morbo pellendo adhibendum fuisset, &amp; cui tenues istius sartoris facultates non sufficiebant&lt;/e6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034503Z">
        <seg>From all this I conclude that by this masterful anointing a certain remedy or precious ointment is meant, which would have had to be applied to drive away this disease, and for which the meager abilities of that tailor were not sufficient.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex hoc loco colligo, istud embamma frigidum sumi, &amp; alibi reperi, eo vulgus &amp; rusticos in Hispania vesci.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041439Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041439Z">
        <seg>From this passage I gather that this embamma is eaten cold, and I found elsewhere that the common people and country folk in Spain eat it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex ipsa divini amoris ignita pharetra jaculatorias collegerat in formam precatiunculas, quæ legentibus placuere, &amp; multis usu profuerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041637Z">
        <seg>He had gathered from the very fiery quiver of divine love short prayers, which pleased readers and benefited many in their use.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex solo pondere sentiebat ægra, adhuc se brachium pedemque alterum habere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025602Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025602Z">
        <seg>She felt ill from the mere weight, still having one arm and one leg.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exanimis subito pavore mater cum filiis, circumspiciebat fugam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062120Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062120Z">
        <seg>Struck suddenly with terror, the mother with her children looked around for a way to escape.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Excitata Virgo respondit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051350Z">
        <seg>The excited virgin answered;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Excitata, consedit in lectulo, totoque sopore facile discusso, tacita &amp; attenta mirabatur, ad quid, unde, &amp; a quo illa hora fuisset in vigiliam revocata: dumque sic omnia versans animo, auscultat &amp; hæsitat, ecce vox lenis ac dulcissima sedenti allabitur, ita inquiens: “Nihil dubites, Maria, neque angaris: etenim Rosa vere sancta est”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032253Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032253Z">
        <seg>Awaken, she sat up in her bed, and with her slumber easily shaken off, she sat in silence and with attention, wondering to what, from where, and by whom she had been roused from her sleep at that hour. And as she thus turned all things over in her mind, listening and hesitating, behold a gentle and most sweet voice floated to her, saying: "Doubt not, Mary, nor be troubled: for Rosa is truly holy."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exclamavit cum Paulo: “Domine quid me vis facere”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032907Z">
        <seg>He cried out with Paul, "Lord, what do you want me to do?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exclamavit denuo matrona: “Quid agis Rosa?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055411Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055411Z">
        <seg>The matron exclaimed again, "What are you doing, Rosa?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exclamet hic præ stupore Rex citharœdus: “Numquid confitebitur tibi pulvis, aut annuntiabit veritatem tuam?” Aio &amp; pergo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040100Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040100Z">
        <seg>Let the kitharædus king here exclaim in astonishment: "Will the dust confess to you, or will it announce your truth?" I say and proceed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Excusabat puella, se ipsam non satis habere compertum, quid in anima sua pateretur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045738Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045738Z">
        <seg>The girl excused herself, saying she did not fully understand what was going on in her own soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exemit, viditque luculentis e chirothecis promicare flammas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035356Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035356Z">
        <seg>He drew it out, and saw flames shooting from the bright sheaths.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exhorruit Gundisalvi uxor;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012225Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012225Z">
        <seg>Gundisalvi's wife shuddered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exhorruit Virgo ad improvisam tantilli præconii vanitatem, arreptaque viva calce cruciabiliter manum utramque deformavit, usque dum ardore mordacissimo pellis undique finderetur in rugas, bullulisque sparsim tumesceret, eo quidem dolore ac damno, ut per dies triginta &amp; amplius vestire seipsam Rosa nequierit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040726Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040726Z">
        <seg>The Virgin recoiled at the unexpected vanity of the little herald, and, seizing a live coal, horribly disfigured both her hands, until the most biting heat split the skin on every side into wrinkles, and it swelled in scattered blisters, with such pain and damage that for thirty days and more Rosa could not manage to dress herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exhorruit infelix, ubi se circumspexit immersum labyrintho tam cænoso &amp; ineluctabili;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032547Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032547Z">
        <seg>The unfortunate one shuddered when he looked around and saw himself immersed in such a filthy and inescapable labyrinth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exin, quasi tessera data, invitabat aviculam metro, quod hunc in usum composuerat, fere hoc sensu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045227Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045227Z">
        <seg>Then, as if a signal had been given, he invited the little bird with a meter he had composed for that purpose, in almost these words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exinde crevit sacræ imagini popularis veneratio, cumque fama tanta miraculi, regnum ac universam regionem pervasit mirifica salutiferæ devotionis propago.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054246Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054246Z">
        <seg>From then on, popular veneration of the sacred image grew, and with the fame of such a miracle, a marvelous propagation of salutary devotion spread throughout the kingdom and the entire region.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exinde mox conquisitis, &amp; in sui cubiculi capaci angulo compaginatis tabulis rude stragulum injecit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042527Z">
        <seg>He soon threw a rough mat of planks, which he had just gathered, into the corner of his spacious room.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exinde sensit, aliis sibi remediis opus non esse: nam unica Rosæ effigies &amp; detergebat inveteratam mœstitiam, &amp; dimovebat cardialgiæ&lt;a1&gt;d&lt;/a1&gt; cruciatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040400Z">
        <seg>From that point on he realized he needed no other remedies: for the single image of Rose both wiped away his long-standing sorrow and eased the torment of his heartache.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exinde sæpius per diem sonoris cantibus implevit viciniam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061553Z">
        <seg>From then on, he often filled the neighborhood with sonorous songs throughout the day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Existimo rarum hoc divortium sublimioribus Rosæ operibus posse accenseri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041950Z">
        <seg>I think this divorce can be counted among Rose's more sublime works.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exitus e domo pœnis etiam sibi, vel in pede saxo, vel in oculis mordacissimo pipere Indico, inflictis vitabat, tolerabilius judicans excæcari, quam vanitates sæculi prospicere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041606Z">
        <seg>He avoided leaving the house by inflicting punishments on himself—either a stone in his shoe or the most biting Indian pepper in his eyes—judging it more tolerable to be blinded than to behold the vanities of the world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Expalluerunt, quotquot cum Rosa erant feminæ, prosiliere foras, quæ citius poterant, spemque salutis in fuga collocabant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062148Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062148Z">
        <seg>The women who were with Rosa, pale with fear, rushed outside as fast as they could, placing their hope of salvation in flight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Expalluit confusa Virgo, timens hoc indicio deprehensum iri, quantum exercitiis pœnitentiæ soleret incumbere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040906Z">
        <seg>The Virgin paled with confusion, fearing that this sign would reveal how devoted she was to penitential exercises.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Expavit ad Rosæ implorationem uxor venefica, jamque veneni sui effectum desperans, seipsam lethali vulnere sauciavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034342Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034342Z">
        <seg>The witch-wife, enraged by Rosa's plea, and now despairing of her poison's effect, wounded herself with a fatal wound.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Expavit insperato gaudio, remque trepida ad Rosam in proximo cubiculo nentem retulit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055110Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055110Z">
        <seg>She burst out with unexpected joy and, trembling, ran to Rosa, who was spinning in the next room.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Expavit obstetrix, nullumque omisit remedium, ut membranam eliceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034227Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034227Z">
        <seg>The midwife panicked and spared no remedy to draw out the membrane.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Expavit, &amp; contremuit exanguis Virgo, angore, fletu, &amp; mœrore contabuit, ac seipsam durissime objurgans, ter, quater illisit pugnum aculeatæ capitis sui coronæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041253Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041253Z">
        <seg>She gasped and trembled, the color drained from her face; she wasted away with anguish, weeping and sorrow, and, reproaching herself most harshly, struck her fist three or four times against the spiky crown of her head.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Expergefactus hac vociferatione filius Thomas de Mesa, qui prope dormiebat, a patre totam rei gestæ seriem percepit, sed incredulus, multis obtestatus est patrem, vanitati insomniorum temere ne crederet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032116Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032116Z">
        <seg>Woken by this outcry, Thomas de Mesa's son, who was sleeping nearby, learned the whole story from his father. But, disbelieving, he earnestly implored his father not to rashly believe the vanity of his dreams.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Expleto hoc pietatis officio, blande ac suaviter Patri gratias egit, quod in opere tam pretioso ac serio aptam sibi methodum viamque recitando præiverat, qua utcumque imitaretur divinum in cruce Agnum pro suis adversariis extreme deprecantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030024Z">
        <seg>Having completed this pious duty, he thanked the Father gently and sweetly, because he had, in so precious and solemn a work, shown him a fitting method and path by reciting it, by which he might in some way imitate the divine Lamb on the cross, who was earnestly interceding for his adversaries.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Expleto opere, disparuit, &amp; ecce cordis effigiem plene ac scitissime formatum in veste Rosæ, quam a papilione ibi depictam, relictamque, conspicue observarunt puellæ, quotquot in eodem conclavi laborabant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044120Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044120Z">
        <seg>When the work was finished, she vanished, and behold, the image of a heart, fully and most skillfully formed, was seen on Rose's garment, which, left there by the butterfly depicted on it, was clearly observed by the girls who were working in the same room.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exsiliit deinde sanus, mediisque se turbis fidenter immiscuit, Rosam identidem suam liberatricem altis vocibus inclamans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033434Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033434Z">
        <seg>He then sprang out, sound in mind, and confidently mingled with the crowds, repeatedly crying out in a loud voice that Rosa was his savior.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exterrita domina lecto exsiliit, (nam juste media nox erat) cumque D. Ludovica Baretta sorore sua germana ad Antonii cubile properavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033158Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033158Z">
        <seg>The terrified lady leapt out of bed (for it was indeed midnight), and, with her sister, Lady Louisa Baretta, she hurried to Anthony's bedchamber.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exterruit circumstantes atrocitas vulneris: nam ubertim manabat cruor, &amp; ex hiatu ambiguum erat spectantibus, num quid forte nervorum aut venarum una truncatum fuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034819Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034819Z">
        <seg>The atrocity of the wound terrified those standing by: for blood gushed out in abundance, and through the gash it was unclear to the onlookers whether some nerves or veins had been severed along with it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Extimuit mater;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035433Z">
        <seg>The mother was terrified.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Extimuit, adactisque calcaribus fugam iniit Joannes, magnis vocibus periculum suum Deo &amp; B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033834Z">
        <seg>He was terrified, and spurred on by the spurs, John took to flight, crying out loudly his danger to God and the B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211730Z">
        <seg>Father</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>F. Gabriëlis de Zarate: nam huic per sortem (ut ibi moris est) uno mense ante hoc sermocinandi onus obtigerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012411Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012411Z">
        <seg>F. Gabriel of Zarate: for to him, by lot (as is the custom there), the burden of speaking had fallen one month before this.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>F. Joannes de Lorenzana, conceptoque repentinæ fiduciæ instinctu, jussit infirmum bene sperare, eo quod illo momento in ecclesia viderat, coram SS. Hostiæ tabernaculo pro salute ægri luculentum ardere cereum, Rosam intelligens, quam illic ardentissimas fundere preces eminus observarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012318Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012318Z">
        <seg>Father John of Lorenzana, moved by a sudden instinct of confidence, told the sick man to hope for the best, for at that moment he had seen in the church, before the tabernacle of the Blessed Sacrament, a bright candle burning for the patient's health—Rosam, whom he had observed from afar there pouring out the most ardent prayers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Facies Virginis gratia ac splendore micabat infinito;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031912Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031912Z">
        <seg>The face of the Virgin shone with infinite grace and splendor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Facile consensit ac subscripsit Rosa, existimans agi de solo humilitatis exercitio, eumque Ordinis sui morem esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030053Z">
        <seg>Rosa readily agreed and signed, believing it was merely an exercise in humility and that it was the custom of her Order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Facile ex internis criteriis, quæ a sancta Catharina Senensi didicerat, agnovit Rosa latentem sub umbra tutelarem angelum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051812Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051812Z">
        <seg>Rosa easily recognized, by the internal criteria she had learned from Saint Catherine of Siena, the guardian angel hidden in the shade.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Factum est, &amp; en, Grimaneca repente sibi reddita, primo sensit aruisse penitus cruenta profluvia, inde &amp; febres defervescere, sicque, nullo alio tentato remedio, post placidum soporem postridie mente &amp; corpore sana fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035452Z">
        <seg>It happened that, behold, Grimanus, suddenly regaining consciousness, first felt that the deep, bloody flow had ceased, then that his fever was subsiding; and so, having tried no other remedy, after a peaceful slumber he was, the next day, sound in mind and body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Factum est, statimque ut parientis ventrem terra imposita attigit, fœtus brevi ac facillimo nixu erupit, matremque periculo simul &amp; onere liberavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035753Z">
        <seg>It happened, and as soon as the earth was laid upon the mother's womb, the fetus burst forth with a brief and very easy effort, and freed the mother from both danger and burden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Factum est: cum archiepiscopo prorex, &amp; uterque, tum ecclesiasticus, tum secularis regni senatus, inito consilio, collatisque suffragiis, Limanam D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054255Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054255Z">
        <seg>It was done: with the archbishop as pro-regent, and both the ecclesiastical and secular senates of the kingdom, having held a council and cast their votes, appointed D. to Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Factum est: cum archiepiscopo prorex, &amp; uterque, tum ecclesiasticus, tum secularis regni senatus, inito consilio, collatisque suffragiis, Limanam D. Virginis sanctissimo Rosario præstantis imaginem elegerunt, &amp; quotannis feria secunda post Dominicam, &lt;b0&gt;Quasi modo geniti&lt;/b0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;d&lt;/a1&gt;, instituta publica supplicatione (cui tum ipsi, tum singulorum Ordinum Regulares, &amp; universa tribunalium dicasteria&lt;a2&gt;e&lt;/a2&gt; interveniunt) eamdem in suo apud FF. Prædicatores sacello invisunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011054Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011054Z">
        <seg>It was decreed: the Viceroy, together with the Archbishop and both the ecclesiastical and secular members of the kingdom's council, after deliberation and a vote, chose the most holy image of Our Lady of the Most Holy Rosary of Lima, and ordered that it be celebrated annually on the second Monday after the Sunday of the Rosary, &lt;b0&gt;As newborns&lt;/b0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;d&lt;/a1&gt;, with a public procession established (in which both the civil and religious authorities, the Regulars of each Order, and all the dicasteries of the tribunals participate), they visit the same image in its chapel at the Friars Preachers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Factum est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043909Z">
        <seg>It is done.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Factum, ut ille imperaverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054007Z">
        <seg>It was done, as he had commanded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Factus est deinde voti compos, dum minime cogitabat: nam superna providentia hunc postea Rosæ non modo confessarium dedit, sed &amp; præceptorem, cui Virgo obedientiam usque ad mortem velut regulari suo superiori exhibuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055830Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055830Z">
        <seg>Thus his vow was fulfilled when he least expected it: for by divine providence he was later given to Rose not only as a confessor but also as a teacher, to whom the Virgin showed obedience unto death, just as she did to her regular superior.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fama erat Limæ, Rosam impetrare, quæcumque apud sanctam illam imaginem postularet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042220Z">
        <seg>It was rumored in Lima that Rosa could obtain whatever she asked of that holy image.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fama erat, Rosam impetrare omnia, quæcumque apud sacram imaginem ab augustissima Rosarii Regina serio flagitaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054407Z">
        <seg>It was said that Rosa could obtain whatever she earnestly requested from the sacred image of the most august Queen of the Rosary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Familiare quoque sibi effecit quoddam orandi genus altissimum, luminosum, ignitum, quo nominatim gloriosa Dei attributa mente pervolvens, singulis proprium latriæ cultum exhibebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044926Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044926Z">
        <seg>He also established for himself a certain highest, luminous, and fiery mode of prayer, by which, mentally reviewing the glorious attributes of God one by one, he offered to each its proper worship of latria.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Familiares ipsius sermones vim orationis ut plurimum continebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041718Z">
        <seg>His familiar conversations contained as much force as speech could.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Familiaribus suis ingenue profitebatur humilis Rosa, plectendis suis criminibus haud sufficere communem gehennæ rogum, alium sibi deberi singularem infernum, ac veteri profundiorem, longeque deteriorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041259Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041259Z">
        <seg>The humble Rosa frankly confessed to her family that the common pyre of hell was not sufficient for her crimes; she deserved a separate hell of her own, one deeper than the old one and far more dreadful.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fasciam præbento quinque coronæ Domini, 5 dies inediæ, totidem stationes in reverentiam suæ circumcisionis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053601Z">
        <seg>I offer the Lord's cincture: five crowns, five days of fasting, and as many stations in reverence of His circumcision.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fassa est aliquando seraphica Catharina beato Raymundo&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt; confessori suo, cum nullo hostium æque ut cum somno, operosius sibi fuisse confligendum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003517Z">
        <seg>Saint Catherine once confessed to her blessed confessor Raymond that she had had to fight more earnestly against no enemy than against sleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fassa est aliquando seraphica Catharina beato Raymundo&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; confessori suo, cum nullo hostium æque ut cum somno, operosius sibi fuisse confligendum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043016Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043016Z">
        <seg>Seraphic Catherine once confessed to her blessed confessor Raymond, that she had to struggle more diligently against no enemy than against sleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fassus est postea, multoties inter arcana illius fori alloquia se obmutuisse ad profunditatem &amp; eruditionem solidorum discursuum, quibus Rosa prompte, facillime, &amp; ex ordine satisfaciebat quæstionibus præarduis &amp; abstrusis, ac subito se agnovisse spiritum altiorem, qui in Virgine loquebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050705Z">
        <seg>He later confessed that, many times during the secret colloquies of that forum, he had been silenced by the depth and erudition of the solid discourses, by which Rosa promptly, most easily, and in order satisfied the most arduous and abstruse questions, and suddenly recognized a higher spirit speaking in the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fatebatur ingenua Rosa, ad omnem illius picturæ intuitum novo se perfundi calore &amp; gaudio, subsultare præcordia, inflammari spiritum, eo quod divinus ille Puerulus videbatur sibi nunc blando arrisu salutem dicere, nunc suavi radiorum effluxu animam transfigere, aliquando etiam curta brachiola foras protendere, velut in amplexum Rosæ jamjam ultro procursurus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054125Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054125Z">
        <seg>The ingenuous Rosa confessed that at every glance of that painting she was filled with a new warmth and joy, her heart leaping for delight, her spirit was inflamed, because that divine Little Boy seemed now to greet her with a gentle smile, now to pierce her soul with a sweet effulgence of rays, and at times even to stretch out his short little arms, as if about to rush into Rosa's embrace of his own accord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fatebatur, sibi permolestum, ac grave esse, inter feminarum colloquia cernere pomposas vestes, pallia, fasciasque pretiosas, interesse otiosis fabulis, distineri reciprocis tot inanium urbanitatum ritibus: præcipue tamen dolebat, se neque in domestico sui recessus asylo tutam a visitationibus, liberamque esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043444Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043444Z">
        <seg>She admitted that it was exceedingly troublesome and burdensome to her to see, amid women's conversations, pompous garments, cloaks, and precious headbands, to be drawn into idle tales, to be distracted by so many reciprocal, vain urbanities and rituals. Above all, she lamented that she was neither safe from visits nor free in her own private domestic retreat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Febres ephimeras, ac etiam reliquas contumaciores ipsa consuetudine jam pene in jocum verterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042050Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042050Z">
        <seg>She had by now almost turned the occasional fevers, and even the more stubborn ones, into a joke through sheer habit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fecit confestim Rosa, ut sibi permissum erat, summæque curæ summum adjunxit secretum, adeo ut neque post suum obitum (nam tunc primum res innotuit) secundus iste pœnarum lectulus usquam potuerit inveniri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042942Z">
        <seg>Rosa immediately did as she had been permitted and added the utmost secrecy to the utmost care, so that not even after her death (for it was only then that the matter became known) could this second bed of punishment be found anywhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fecit ille bona fide, quod jussus fuerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012954Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012954Z">
        <seg>He did what he had been ordered to do in good faith.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fecit, jecit, ac subito bocarum &amp; armadillorum&lt;a2&gt;f&lt;/a2&gt; tantam vim traxit, ut pondus impleti retis vix potuerit sustinere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033920Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033920Z">
        <seg>He made, threw, and suddenly drew such a swarm of locusts and armadillos that the weight of the full net could barely be supported.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Felices spinæ, quibus de tali manu tale successit in cælis diadema!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042437Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042437Z">
        <seg>Blessed are the spines, for such a hand has bestowed upon them a diadem in the heavens!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Festus erat dies beatissimæ Virginis in templo purificatæ, quando internuntia hæc trias, Mariæ de Oliva (gratulando magis, quam flagitando) exposuit Rosæ suæ constans desiderium fabricandi sibi cellulam angustam ac solitariam, in qua sine confessarii permissu nullius admitteret conspectum aut alloquium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043612Z">
        <seg>It was the feast of the Blessed Virgin's purification in the temple when the three nuns, among them Mary of Oliva (more in congratulation than in reproach), laid before Rose her steadfast desire to build for herself a narrow, solitary cell in which, without her confessor's permission, she would admit no one's presence or conversation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fidelibus Peruanis commune studium fuit, in prodigiosæ virtutis memoriam, passim per ædes Rosæ effigiem habere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040233Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040233Z">
        <seg>It was the common desire of the Peruvian faithful, in memory of a prodigious virtue, to have the image of Rose everywhere in their homes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fieri tamen posset, ut hic assignetur locus dictus&lt;/e1&gt; Oruro &lt;e2&gt;vel&lt;/e2&gt; Horuro, &lt;e3&gt;de quo Joannes Laetius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040211Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040211Z">
        <seg>However, it could be that the place called Oruro or Horuro is assigned here, about which John Laetius in his book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Filia templum adit, preces fundit, eique domum redeunti vir plane ignotus occurrit, simulque involutum strophiolo argentum porrigit, &amp; jubens præsentem parentum necessitatem illo sublevari, statim abscessit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042558Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042558Z">
        <seg>A daughter goes to the temple, offers prayers, and as she returns home she encounters a complete stranger, who at once hands her a silver ingot wrapped in a band and, telling her to relieve her parents' present need with it, immediately departs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Filia, ne quos innocentes oneraret furti imputatio, lepide affata matrem, “Quid (inquit) jacturam tuam incertumque authorem dubitabunda incusas?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061116Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061116Z">
        <seg>The daughter, so as not to burden any innocent person with an accusation of theft, sweetly said to her mother, "Why do you hesitantly accuse your loss and its uncertain author?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Filiolus interim ultro se sepulchro instravit ore prono, ibidem mox hilarescere cœpit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033517Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, the little boy eagerly lay prone in the tomb, and there he soon began to brighten up.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Filius tandem fidejubebat pro Matre;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043559Z">
        <seg>The son finally stood as surety for his mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fimbrias ac limbos tam straguli, quam fasciolarum ambiunto 33 sacræ Communiones, 33 interventus Missarum, 33 horæ mentalis orationis, 33 Orationes dominicæ, angelicæque Salutationes, totidem vicibus recitatio Symboli Apostolorum, cum &lt;b0&gt;Gloria Patri&lt;/b0&gt;, &amp; antiphona &lt;b1&gt;Salve Regina&lt;/b1&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010942Z">
        <seg>Let 33 sacred Communions, 33 Masses, and 33 hours of mental prayer border the fringes and linings of both the shroud and the bandages. 33 Our Fathers and Hail Marys, the Apostles' Creed recited the same number of times, with the Glory be to the Father and the antiphon Salve Regina;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fimbrias ac limbos tam straguli, quam fasciolarum ambiunto 33 sacræ Communiones, 33 interventus Missarum, 33 horæ mentalis orationis, 33 Orationes dominicæ, angelicæque Salutationes, totidem vicibus recitatio Symboli Apostolorum, cum &lt;b3&gt;Gloria Patri&lt;/b3&gt;, &amp; antiphona &lt;b4&gt;Salve Regina&lt;/b4&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053604Z">
        <seg>33 recitations of the Fimbriæ et limbos tam straguli, quam fasciolarum, 33 Holy Communions, 33 Masses offered, 33 hours of mental prayer, 33 Our Fathers and Hail Marys, the Apostles' Creed recited the same number of times, with the Glory be to the Father and the antiphon Salve Regina;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Finitis demum omnibus, ac imagine decenter exornata, Rosa fatigatis sociabus compatiens, “Ite (inquit) respirate, lentaque deambulatione pariter vos reficite: nam lassæ estis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055228Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055228Z">
        <seg>When at last everything was finished and the image had been properly adorned, Rosa, commiserating with her weary companions, said, "Go," she said, "breathe, and refresh yourselves with a leisurely stroll, for you are tired."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Finito volumine, obstupuere omnes quotquot Pacecum noverant, ejusque quadrum characterem festine, fluide, indefesse tot chartis illitum considerabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032355Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032355Z">
        <seg>When the volume was finished, all who knew Pace were astonished, and they gazed at his portrait, the character so hastily, fluidly, and tirelessly daubed across so many pages.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fit concursus, &amp; inter plaudentium hymnos, Alphonsus in ecclesiam reducitur, spectandus ab omnibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033421Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033421Z">
        <seg>A gathering takes place, and amid the hymns of the applauding, Alphonsus is brought back into the church to be seen by all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Flagitavit æger ultimo suo solatio apportari ex S. Catharinæ Senensis monasterio (cui erat a sacris confessionibus) pictam in tela Rosæ imaginem, allatam jussit appendi intra lectuli sui conopeum, &amp; attractis cortinis, ita in præsenti effigie absentem Rosam affatus est: “Virgo sancta &amp; gloriosa, quæ tantum vales apud Altissimum, quo beate frueris, respice in me, qui, si aliud de te numquam merui, saltem genitricis tuæ sum confessarius;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040556Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040556Z">
        <seg>The sick man requested that, as his last comfort, the painted image of Rose, from the monastery of St. Catherine of Siena (to which he was a confessor), be brought; he ordered it to be hung inside the canopy of his bed, and, drawing the curtains, thus addressed the absent Rose in her present likeness: "Holy and glorious Virgin, who are so powerful with the Most High, in whom you happily rejoice, look upon me, who, if I have never deserved anything else from you, am at least the confessor of your mother;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Flebat amare Ludovici mater, conscia tantis impendiis longe imparem fore suam, filiique inopiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034209Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034209Z">
        <seg>Louis's mother wept bitterly, aware that she would be far from equal to such great expenses, and of her son's poverty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Flevit turbata mater, hoc maxime plorans, quod præter lachrymas non habebat, quo Filiæ dolores inungeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013542Z">
        <seg>The troubled mother wept, grieving above all that she had nothing but tears with which to anoint her daughter's pains.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Flos es, ac florem amas?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051239Z">
        <seg>Are you a flower, and do you love a flower?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fluctuabat misera inter saxum &amp; sacrum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032234Z">
        <seg>The poor one wavered between the rock and the sacred.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fluebat sanguis in humerum, nec Rosa exhorruit tincturam suam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034857Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034857Z">
        <seg>Blood flowed onto the shoulder, and Rosa did not recoil from her own stain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fluxerunt sub velo sanguinis rivi, ut acrimonia doloris ingratissimum alienæ laudis obtunderet auditum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041256Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041256Z">
        <seg>They flowed beneath a veil of blood, so that the acrimony of pain might blunt the ear to the most unwelcome praise of another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Forma ingentis molossi erat, villosa, tetra, picea: lucem, qua videretur, dabant solæ rubentes flammæ, quas naribus, oculis, auribus affatim protrudebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051924Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051924Z">
        <seg>The form was that of a huge molosser, shaggy, dark, and pitch-black; the only light by which it could be seen came from the red flames it was spewing from its nostrils, eyes, and ears in abundance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Formatur fere in pilulas, quo facilius deglutiri possit, &amp; in ventriculo dilui, indeque transire in venas.&lt;/e4&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045946Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045946Z">
        <seg>It is formed into almost pill-like shapes so that it can be more easily swallowed, dissolved in the stomach, and then pass into the veins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Forsan hi competitores pro variis Americæ partibus inter se conciliari possent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041427Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041427Z">
        <seg>Perhaps these competitors for the various parts of America could reconcile with one another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Forsitan idcirco providerat Deus, pater misericordiarum, quod toto fere biduo palam prostaret in tumulo inhumatum Rosæ suæ corpusculum, ut ejus spinæ tam multis fierent in salutem: nam juxta mellifluum Bernardum, “Felicius numquam pungeris, quam ubi compungeris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032516Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032516Z">
        <seg>Perhaps God, the Father of mercies, had foreseen this, so that for nearly two full days the little body of his Rose would lie openly in a tomb, unburied, so that her thorns might become a means of salvation for so many: for, as the honey-tongued Bernard says, "You will never be pricked more happily than when you are pierced."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Forte Rosa in Seraphicæ magistræ suæ bicolori habitu attentius defixa, secum de capessendo Dominicani Ordinis amictu deliberabat anceps;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040153Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040153Z">
        <seg>Chance had Rosa, more attentively fixed on her Seraphic mother in her two-colored habit, undecidedly deliberating with herself whether to take the habit of the Dominican Order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Forte de proximo conclavi hanc invita percipiebat Rosa confabulationem, sed laudum suarum nauseam non ferens, exsiliit præ dolore, inque cubiculum interius, ubi filiola quæstoris Michaëla erat, sese proripuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040605Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040605Z">
        <seg>Rosa happened to overhear this conversation from the next room, but unable to bear the nausea of her own praises, she leapt up in pain and rushed into the inner chamber where the questor's little daughter, Michaela, was.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Forte hanc petebat umbram, quæ ambiebat a Domino custodiri ut pupilla oculi, &amp; sub umbra alarum ejus protegi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051824Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051824Z">
        <seg>Perhaps he sought this shadow, which surrounded the Lord, to be kept as the pupil of the eye, and to be protected under the shadow of His wings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055749Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055749Z">
        <seg>Brother</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Francisca ancilla Angelæ de Aguirre tertianam, cum inveterato asthmate, in termino peremptorio incunctanter proscripserunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040029Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040029Z">
        <seg>Francisca, a servant of Angela de Aguirre, was summarily dismissed from the frontier on account of tertian fever and chronic asthma, under an absolute order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Francisca nupsit Hieronymo de Villalobos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012541Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012541Z">
        <seg>Francisca married Jerome de Villalobos.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Francisca post imaginis amplexum somno obruta, cum horis duabus quietissime dormivisset, sana evigilavit, nec deinceps hoc modo infestata est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040413Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040413Z">
        <seg>Francisca, after embracing the image, was overcome by sleep; having slept very peacefully for two hours, she awoke in good health, and was not troubled in this way thereafter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Francisca sat certa, se incolumitatem suam Rosæ orationibus debere, Virgini eventum retulit, ac pro benevola cura gratias egit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055315Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055315Z">
        <seg>Francisca was quite certain that she owed her safety to Rose's prayers, recounted the event to the Virgin, and gave thanks for her benevolent care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Francisco Nicto cum aliis juxta ibidem vigilanti ingenue at secreto dixit: “Proh, Pater!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030124Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030124Z">
        <seg>Franciscus Nicto, with others, while keeping watch nearby, said frankly but in secret to the priest: "Oh, Father!"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Francisco Nieto fassus est, [ac pedibus,] aliquando sibi instar dolioli tumuisse tibiam, sed auditis, quæ ad Rosæ monumentum indies crebrescebant, signis ac virtutibus, eo se perrexisse cum aliis, breviterque precatum, correpta sepulchri terra, perfricuisse obiter suram pedis immane turgidam, eodemque puncto temporis molestissimum tumorem plane desedisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035923Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035923Z">
        <seg>Francisco Nieto confessed that his shin had at one time swollen to the size of a cask, but upon hearing the signs and virtues that were daily growing more frequent at Rose's monument, he went there with others and, briefly praying, took some earth from the tomb and rubbed it on his own immense, swollen calf. and at that very moment the most troublesome swelling had completely subsided.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Franciscus Nieto, &amp; magister Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032612Z">
        <seg>Franciscus Nieto, &amp; master Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Franciscus Solanus, in vita &amp; post mortem, constanti fama sanctitatis ac prodigiorum gloria clarus&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, pro concione obscurius forte nonnulla protulerat, quæ Limensium vulgus prave intellecta detorserat in sensum prorsus alienum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041541Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041541Z">
        <seg>Francis Solanus, both during his life and after his death, renowned for his constant reputation of holiness and the glory of his miracles, had perhaps delivered certain things more obscurely in his sermon, which the common people of Lima, having misunderstood them, twisted into a meaning entirely foreign to them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Franciscus sacerdos erat: Rosæ patrocinium implorat: postridie matertera illum accersit, quem toto decem &amp; octo annorum spatio videre abnuerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043116Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043116Z">
        <seg>Franciscus was a priest: he implores the protection of the Roses: the next day his aunt summons him, whom she had refused to see for the entire span of eighteen years.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Franciscus tot simul gaudia non capiens, inque matertera matrem Rosam tacite devenerans, paruit festinus &amp; lubens, catervam miserabilem adduxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032829Z">
        <seg>Franciscus, unable to contain so many joys at once, and having silently gone to his aunt to fetch Mother Rosa, hurriedly and willingly brought the miserable crowd.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Franciscæ Urtado de Bustamante: “Utinam idonea ratio modusve occurreret adornandi fugam ad barbarorum provincias, ut illic ab idololatris pro Jesu Christo immanissime trucidemur”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060313Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060313Z">
        <seg>Francisca Urtado de Bustamante: "If only a suitable plan or method could be found for decorating the escape to the provinces of the barbarians, so that there we might be most horribly slaughtered by the idolaters for the sake of Jesus Christ."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Franciscæ quoque de Montoya ab eadem Sancta obtinuit, ut sulphuratum missilium ignium crepitaculum, quod vultum petiit, &amp; [in] supercilium impegit, non noceret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042242Z">
        <seg>Francisca also obtained from the same Saint that a sulfurous firecracker missile, which sought her face and struck her on the eyebrow, would do her no harm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Frater Petrus de Loaysa Ordinis Prædicatorum, qui cupiens evidentius experiri, quam firmiter in tantæ promissionis fide sibimet constaret Virgo, in quadam ejus confessione rem puerilem ac mininam cœpit attollere tamquam dubiam, ac solicitiori dignam examine, visurus, quid improvisus nec forte inutilis hic terror operaretur in Virgine, quam tanta (ut noverat) divinæ gratiæ securitas possidebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062002Z">
        <seg>Brother Peter of Loaysa of the Order of Preachers, desiring to experience more clearly how firmly the Virgin stood by the faith of so great a promise, in one of her confessions began to raise a childish and insignificant matter as if it were doubtful, and worthy of a more careful examination, he was to see what this sudden and not perhaps useless terror would work in the Virgin, whom such security of divine grace, as he knew, possessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Frequentes &amp; fere quotidianæ erant hujus sanctæ animæ deliciæ istæ, &amp; interdum, moram faciente Sponso, querulis vocibus lamentabatur, donec veniret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041913Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041913Z">
        <seg>These delights were frequent and almost daily for this holy soul, and sometimes, when the Bridegroom delayed, she would lament in querulous voices until he came.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Frigidus stagnabat sudor, &amp; in omnibus membris, &amp; juncturis arctabatur doloribus &amp; pœnis, quas olim ei Sponsus in visione ostenderat futuras multo graviores ceteris omnibus, quas in vita pertulerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042728Z">
        <seg>A cold sweat stood upon her, and throughout all her limbs and joints she was wracked with pains and torments which her Spouse had once shown her in a vision would be far more severe than any she had endured in her life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Frustra pavidæ obstetrices ultimum suæ artis tentarant, quo prolem aptarent ad egressum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035114Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035114Z">
        <seg>The terrified midwives had in vain attempted the last of their art to prepare the child for birth;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Frustra videbatur omne consilium, plangebant circumstantes Indæ, singultiebat maritus cum afflicta familia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035815Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035815Z">
        <seg>All counsel seemed in vain; the surrounding Indians mourned, and the husband sobbed with his afflicted family.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Frustratis omnibus adhibitis naturalibus remediis, ejus parentes humili corde voverunt, eam in sepulchro prædictæ Virginis ad obtinendam ei salutem per novenam ponere: &amp; in ultima novenarii die, dum votum persolverant, infantula supra sepulchrum statim in pedes surrexit, cunctis admirantibus, tam expedite deambulans, acsi numquam hujusmodi læsionem passa fuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043150Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043150Z">
        <seg>When all natural remedies had failed, her parents, with humble hearts, vowed to place her in the tomb of the aforementioned Virgin for a novena to obtain her health: And on the last day of the novena, while they were fulfilling their vow, the little girl immediately rose to her feet above the tomb, to the amazement of all, walking about so nimbly as if she had never suffered such an injury.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fuerat is truncus ex arbore, quam illic pacay&lt;a0&gt;e&lt;/a0&gt; vocant indigenæ, excavatus in medio, ut caput Virginis collumque exciperet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T002119Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T002119Z">
        <seg>It was a trunk from a tree, which the natives call pacaya, hollowed out in the middle to receive the head and neck of the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fuerat is truncus ex arbore, quam illic pacay&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;e&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; vocant indigenæ, excavatus in medio, ut caput Virginis collumque exciperet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042821Z">
        <seg>It was a trunk from a tree, which the natives call pacay, hollowed out in the middle to receive the head and neck of the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fuere &amp; alii;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050027Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050027Z">
        <seg>There were others as well.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fuere pro visitatione reliquiarum Rosæ de more deputati inspectores, qui, aperta arca lignea, repererunt virgineum corpusculum post quintum decimum expletum ab obitu annum, consumptis indumentis, ossibus integris &amp; adhuc sicca hinc inde carne obductis, quæ odorem rosarum exhalabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043045Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043045Z">
        <seg>Inspectors, appointed as usual to visit the relics of Rose, upon opening the wooden chest found the virgin's little body after fifteen years had passed since her death, her garments consumed, her bones intact and still covered here and there with flesh, which exhaled the fragrance of roses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fuit, qui gratis offerret ædes suas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061203Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061203Z">
        <seg>There was one who offered his house for free.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fuitque tam publice notum hoc prodigium, ut in tribunali jurejurando assertum fuerit, a tempore, quo Peruvia nostris detecta fuit, nullum umquam ibi claruisse concionatorem, qui tot ac tantis stimulis gentes illas ad virtutem impulerit, &amp; tam universalem pœnitentiæ spiritum, &amp; devotionis incendium excitarit, ut Rosa suis aspectibus, contactibus &amp; precibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043106Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043106Z">
        <seg>And this miracle was so publicly known that it was attested to under oath in court, that since the time Peru was discovered by our people, no preacher had ever shone there, who, with so many and such great incentives, had urged those peoples to virtue, and had kindled such a universal spirit of penance and a fire of devotion, as Rose had with her appearances, contacts, and prayers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fumo vivi occludunt oculos, mortua aperuit: nam ecce, illo momento, quo suffitus infantem attigit, hæc revixit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033133Z">
        <seg>The living smoke closed her eyes, the dead opened them: for behold, at the very moment the puff touched the infant, she came back to life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fundatrix, eademque cœnobii tunc præfecta, jusserat omnes perquiri angulos, excuti scrinia, lustrari singula, sicubi amissum pignus inveniretur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035641Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035641Z">
        <seg>The foundress, who was also the abbess at the time, had ordered every corner to be searched, the chests to be ransacked, and everything to be gone through, in case the lost pledge could be found.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fœtus corpore grandiusculus, cranio molli, pectusculo flaccido, nigris pedibus, jam cœperat membris extremis putrescere, ac fœtorem exhalabat intolerabilem, extincturus indubie matrem, ni Rosæ imago subvenisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040518Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040518Z">
        <seg>The fetus, large in body, with a soft skull, a flabby chest, and black feet, had already begun to rot at its extremities and exhaled an intolerable stench, which would undoubtedly have killed the mother, had not the image of Rose come to her aid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>GLORIA POSTHUMA EX DIVERSIS MONUMENTIS, QUÆ COMMUNEM OPINIONEM DE SANCTITATE HUJUS VIRGINIS LEGITIME CONFIRMARUNT.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030505Z">
        <seg>Posthumous glory from various monuments, which legitimately confirmed the common opinion of the sanctity of this virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gabriël de Zarate tunc Provincialis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034035Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034035Z">
        <seg>Gabriel de Zarate, then Provincial.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Galli, quod Joannes Nicotius, regius aliquando in Lusitania orator, ejus semen primus ad reginam, regis Galliæ matrem detulerit, illiusque facultates docuerit &lt;b2&gt;Nicotianam &amp; herbam reginæ&lt;/b2&gt; nuncuparunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060946Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060946Z">
        <seg>The French, because John Nicot, once a royal envoy to Portugal, was the first to bring its seed to the queen, mother of the King of France, and to teach its properties, called it the Nicotian herb or the queen's herb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gaudebat, inter flores &amp; odorifera plantaria pariter &amp; crucem oculis suis continuo obversari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054940Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054940Z">
        <seg>He rejoiced, continually turning his eyes among the flowers and fragrant plants alike and the cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gavisa domina, de modo ac mora curationis inquisivit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054019Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054019Z">
        <seg>The lady, pleased, inquired about the manner and duration of the treatment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gazam prodigis soli Numinis thesauro inferendam?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053402Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053402Z">
        <seg>Are you going to lavish the treasure of the one and only God on Gaza?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gemellæ erant filiolæ, sanæ, formosæ, corpulentulæ, ac in baptismo Mariæ &amp; Franciscæ de Rosa nomen sortitæ sunt in beneficii memoriam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040457Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040457Z">
        <seg>The twins were little girls, healthy, beautiful, and a bit plump, and at their baptism they were named Mary and Francisca de Rosa in memory of the benefactor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Generalem de totius vitæ suæ delictis exomologesim amarissimo doloris sensu perfecit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032554Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032554Z">
        <seg>He made a general confession of all the sins of his life with the most bitter sense of sorrow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Germano fratri, quid ageretur adhuc dubitanti, innuit se migrare, jussitque cervical subtrahi, ut, capite ad nudum lecticæ lignum acclinaro, sentiret, in cruce se mori: denique sensibus plane integris, pleno judicio, fixis in cælum oculis, nullo vel minimo pavoris aut horroris signo in facie, dum postrema hæc verba, pronuntiabat: &lt;b2&gt;Jesus, Jesus, Jesus mecum sit&lt;/b2&gt;, quietissime expiravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030232Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030232Z">
        <seg>He motioned to his brother Germanus, who was still uncertain what was happening, and ordered the cradle-board to be removed, so that, leaning his head against the bare wood of the bier, he might feel himself dying on a cross: Finally, with his senses completely intact, in full possession of his faculties, his eyes fixed on heaven, and with no sign of fear or horror on his face, he quietly expired while uttering these last words: "Jesus, Jesus, Jesus be with me."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Germanus nil ultra simplicem jocum &amp; animi oblectamentum suspicans, replicuit: “Spargam &amp; ego rosas in aëra, &amp; forte, quam tu, jaciam altius”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062526Z">
        <seg>Germanus, suspecting nothing more than a simple jest and pastime, replied, "I too will scatter roses into the air, and perhaps I will throw them higher than you."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gloria posthuma ex Pontificio canonizationis diplomate, quod Vitam Sanctæ &amp; pleraque ejusdem miracula breviter continet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041017Z">
        <seg>Posthumous glory from the Pontifical diploma of canonization, which briefly contains the Life of the Saint and most of her miracles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gloria posthuma ex honorificis ejus exsequiis, posteriore sacri corporis translatione, &amp; solicita ejusdem conservatione.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030517Z">
        <seg>Posthumous glory from his honorary funeral rites, the later translation of his sacred body, and its careful preservation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gloria posthuma ex illustribus Sanctæ apparitionibus &amp; diversis miraculis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031819Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031819Z">
        <seg>Posthumous glory from the illustrious apparitions of the saints and various miracles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Grande id fuit victrici solatium, grandis &amp; eruditione plenissima lectio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052221Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052221Z">
        <seg>It was a great consolation to the victor, a great and most instructive lesson.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Grandia sunt ac prolixa, quæ circa mansuetissimam Rosæ humilitatem notavit, edixitque Gundisalvi domus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040707Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040707Z">
        <seg>He noted the great and lengthy things concerning the most gentle humility of Rose, and he ordered them to be read in Gundisalvi's house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Grandiuscula ex umbrosis arborum ramis oratoriolum cum altariolo construxit, illucque a mensa, &amp; a lectulo surgens, ut ad orandi asylum properabat Proverbium erat: Hortum adeas si Rosulam quæris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041600Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041600Z">
        <seg>She built a small oratory with an altar in the shade of the trees' branches, and from the table and from her little bed she would hurry there as if to a sanctuary for prayer. The proverb was: "Go to the garden if you seek a rose."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gratissimis auribus hoc nuntium admisit turba, maxime ut vidit episcopum, depositis omnino pontificalibus ornamentis, rhedam conscendere, ac domum properare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031103Z">
        <seg>The crowd received this news with the greatest delight, especially when it saw the bishop, having laid aside all his pontifical ornaments, mount a chariot and hurry home.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gratis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031410Z">
        <seg>For free</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gratiæ sympathia hæc erat, quæ Rosam docebat amare secreta, quæ non noverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044151Z">
        <seg>This was the sympathetic grace that taught Rosa to love the secrets she did not know.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gravius aliquando feminam afflixit inflati cruris acutus dolor, ut per quindenam &amp; amplius nec interdiu usquam potuerit consistere, nec somnum etiam brevissimum noctu libare;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034847Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034847Z">
        <seg>A severe pain in her swollen leg sometimes afflicted the woman, so that for fifteen days and more she could not stand at all, not even by day, nor get even the briefest sleep at night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gundisalvi de la Massa, e paterno domicilio invitari ad suarum ædium gratuitum contubernium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035613Z">
        <seg>Gundisalvi de la Massa, to be invited from his father's home to a free lodging in his own house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gundisalvus, qui forte cum medico aderat, tristi manus spectaculo æger, oratorio abscessit, velut nimiæ compassioni impar.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055352Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055352Z">
        <seg>Gundisalvus, who by chance was present with the physician, distressed by the sad spectacle, withdrew from the oratory, as though he were unfit for such excessive compassion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Habebat domi inter pretiosiora cimelia, particulam pallii, quod Rosam adhuc mortalem amicierat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034618Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034618Z">
        <seg>He had at home, among his most precious treasures, a piece of the cloak that had wrapped the still-mortal Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Habebat domi villosam pelliculam, quæ quondam Rosæ apoplecticum obvolvebat brachium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034950Z">
        <seg>He had at home a shaggy little blanket that had once wrapped Rose's apoplectic arm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Habebat in paupere thesauro coronam precariam ex corallis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043519Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043519Z">
        <seg>She had in her poor treasure a precious crown of coral.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Habebat uxoris dono Basilius modicum lanæ ex pulvillo, in quo Rosa nere, aut flores acu pingere consueverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035139Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035139Z">
        <seg>Basil had, as a gift from his wife, a small quantity of wool from a cushion on which Rosa used to spin or embroider flowers with a needle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hac ambitione pia Virgo obibat mulierum valetudinaria, quotiescumque honestæ comitivæ aderat occasio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061259Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061259Z">
        <seg>With this pious ambition, the Virgin would visit the women's infirmaries whenever there was an opportunity to be in the company of honorable women.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hac fiducia cellulam reliqui, ad te huc veni, consedi expectatura hic servum quæstoris, &amp; cum parente beneficium divisura”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051753Z">
        <seg>With that confidence I left the cell, came here to you, sat down here to wait for the questor's servant, and to share the benefit with my parent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hac fornace probatur aurum, nitescit charitas, loricatur mascula virtute robustus amor, assuescens diligere non tam delicias Dei, quam Deum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050417Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050417Z">
        <seg>In this furnace gold is tested, charity shines, and love, armored in robust masculine virtue, grows accustomed to loving not so much the delights of God as God Himself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hac permissione obtenta, æque solicita erat Rosa, ut eum numerum non transiliret, quam ut impleret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041603Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041603Z">
        <seg>Having obtained this permission, Rosa was just as anxious not to exceed that number as she was to fill it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hac specie circuibat orantem Rosam latratu horrisono, rictu immani, ferociterque dentato, lingua fœdissime exerta, odore sulphureo, simulque pendulas aures, caudam, villos arrigebat, quasi impetum facturus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051927Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051927Z">
        <seg>In this manner it prowled around the praying Rose, barking with a horrid bark, a monstrous, fiercely-toothed mouth, its foul tongue lolling, with a sulfurous stench, while simultaneously pricking its hanging ears, tail, and bristles, as if about to pounce.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hac ætate velabor, profitebor religionem severam, arctam, difficilem?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062836Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062836Z">
        <seg>In this age, shall I embrace and profess a strict, narrow, and difficult religion?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc Christiani conquirendarum Indiarum auspicem ex Hispaniis secum advectam, inter prima Limanæ urbis molimina, primo ibidem veræ religionis templo (cui a sanctissimo Rosario coævum nomen est) solemni ritu collocarunt, adstructo Fratrum Prædicatorum insigni cœnobio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054227Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054227Z">
        <seg>This, brought from Spain by the Christians as a banner for the conquest of the Indies, was, among the first undertakings of the city of Lima, solemnly placed in the first temple of the true religion there (which bears the name of the Most Holy Rosary, contemporary with it), with the founding of a distinguished monastery of the Friars Preachers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc dum affectu religioso circumplectitur, obdormiscit inter preces, &amp; mane experrecta, miraculum subito clamore testata est, marito indicans, perfecte se doloribus liberatam esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040422Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040422Z">
        <seg>While embracing it with religious devotion, she fell asleep amid her prayers, and upon waking in the morning, she suddenly cried out in testimony to the miracle, telling her husband that she had been completely freed from her pains.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc febrientem parentes anxii detulere ad tabulatum, ut Rosæ nondum sepultæ virgineum cadaver attingeret: attingit, statimque, pulso febrili paroxysmo, coram omnibus convaluit, nec relata domum novis morbi accessibus tentata fuit: quin &amp; illa congenita indolis asperitas, eodem simul correcta miraculo, in summam transiit mansuetudinem &amp; suavitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033558Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033558Z">
        <seg>The anxious parents carried this feverish girl to the floor so that she might touch the virgin body of Rose, not yet buried: she touched it, and immediately, seized by a paroxysm of feverish pulse, she recovered before everyone, and when she was brought home, she was not troubled by any new attacks of the disease. Indeed, that innate harshness of disposition was also corrected by the same miracle, and she was transformed into the utmost meekness and sweetness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc igitur fiducia reverenti corripuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034953Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034953Z">
        <seg>He therefore seized this with reverent confidence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc interim ego donavi Montoyæ pauperrimæ, quæ, quod hujuscemodi veste careat, ecclesiam decenter adire, Missisque &amp; concionibus adsistere non potest.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061122Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061122Z">
        <seg>In the meantime, I have given this to the very poor Montoya, who, because she lacks such clothing, cannot attend church decently or attend Mass and sermons.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc itaque nunc in remotiorem horti angulum, nunc in solarium domus sevocatam, flexis humi geniculis precabatur, ut (quod suismet ipsa manibus tenella nondum poterat) humerulos suos vi laterum ibi sparsorum oneraret: sub ea mole, gravique obtritu, durabat parvula in oratione prolixa &amp; laboriosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041628Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041628Z">
        <seg>And so she, now plucked to a more remote corner of the garden, now to the house's solarium, would kneel on the ground and pray that (which she herself with her tender hands could not yet do) she might load her little shoulders with the force of the stones scattered there: under that weight and crushing pressure, the little one endured in a long and laborious prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc itaque, mox ab ortu, parens dicaret offerretque augustissimæ Deiparæ: siquidem hæc filiola, puritate &amp; innocentia vitæ, multum placitura erat Altissimo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012829Z">
        <seg>Therefore, as soon as she was born, her parent would dedicate and offer her to the most august Mother of God; for this little daughter, by the purity and innocence of her life, would greatly please the Most High.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc nectaream sanguinis &amp; aquæ mamillam non ori, sed cordi Virginis benigne applicuit: hic illa debilitati suæ pharmacum, hic vitalem succum invenit &amp; traxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051118Z">
        <seg>This nectarous breast of blood and water she did not apply to her mouth, but kindly to the Virgin's heart: here she found and drew the medicine for her weakness, here she drew the vital sap.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc posteriorem, decimo circiter ante obitum anno, hoc est, paulo postquam Dominicani Ordinis togam sumpserat, sibi imposuit, nec nisi cum vita deposuit: nimirum, ut cœperat attentius contemplari spineam sui Redemptoris coronam, in caput S. Catharinæ Senensis mirabiliter translatam, minime sibi quiescendum duxit, nisi eamdem pariter in suum inde verticem, æmulatione qua posset religiosiori, accerseret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042108Z">
        <seg>She imposed this latter upon herself about ten years before her death, that is, shortly after she had taken the habit of the Dominican Order, and she did not lay it aside until her life ended: For, having begun to contemplate more attentively the crown of thorns of his Redeemer, miraculously transferred to the head of St. Catherine of Siena, she judged it by no means fitting to rest until she, too, had brought that same crown to her own head with as pious an emulation as she could.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc rudi papyro obvolutam ut fronti obligavit, fontem corrosivæ defluxionis funditus exsiccavit, &amp; plene convaluit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035142Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035142Z">
        <seg>He wrapped this in rough papyrus, tied it to his forehead, completely dried up the source of the corrosive discharge, and made a full recovery.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc sacerdotes communicaturi totam flammeam, &amp; vultum ipsius cælesti &amp; vehementi splendore radiantem conspexere: quin &amp; presbytero sacram Communionem porrecturo videbatur flammescens aperiri clibanus, dum removebat pallium, ut divinam reciperet Hostiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042155Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042155Z">
        <seg>The priests who were to communicate saw the entire flame, and the face of the one himself shining with a heavenly and vehement splendor: indeed, to the priest about to administer the sacred Communion, the furnace seemed to open up blazing as he removed the pallium to receive the divine Host.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc sacræ imaginis virtutem admirans Didacus, ejusdem in se experimentum fecit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040537Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040537Z">
        <seg>Admiring the virtue of this sacred image, James tried it on himself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc sæpius in secreta domus parte oravit, exoravit, adegit Rosa, ut se dejectam humi, nunc pronam, nunc supinam calcaret pedibus, ac velut contemptibile peripsema studiose plantis detereret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040509Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040509Z">
        <seg>Rosa often prayed, implored, and compelled her in the secret part of the house to lie down on the ground, now prone, now supine, and to trample her with her feet, and as if she were a contemptible rag, to diligently wear her away with her soles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc ubi obtinuit, pari observantia in Gundisalvum ejusque uxorem respiciens, quandoquidem &amp; hos uti secundos parentes coluerat, pariter ipsorum benedictione voluit ad arduum illud iter communiri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030145Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030145Z">
        <seg>Having obtained this, he, with equal respect for Gundisalvus and his wife—since he had honored them as second parents—also wished, with their blessing, to set out on that arduous journey.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc vero in negotio, de quo agimus, Rosæ non defuisse ad assequendum in symbolicis prævisionibus genuinum ostenti sensum, patebit ex sequentibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062511Z">
        <seg>However, in this matter under discussion, it will become clear from the following that Rosa did not lack the ability to grasp the genuine meaning of the symbolic visions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc vigilandi facilitatem juvere longæ inediæ, cilicii, lectique carduëtum, frigidæ omnisque potus diutina abstinentia, profunda meditatio &amp; applicatæ mentis ad superna tenax defixio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043029Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043029Z">
        <seg>This facility for vigilance is aided by prolonged fasting, by wearing sackcloth and the hair-shirt, by abstaining for a long time from all food and drink, by deep meditation, and by the steadfast fixing of the mind on heavenly things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Has cum Jesulo Sponso delicias Virgini frequentes obtigisse, imo fortasse quotidianas argumento est, quod si ille tardaret interdum, nec solito more adesset, Rosa querulis, at submissis vocibus, quasi secum perplexe sermocinans, ita lamentabatur:“ Hora est, &amp; nondum approperat?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050935Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050935Z">
        <seg>That she had often enjoyed these delights with Jesus, her Spouse, indeed perhaps daily, is evidenced by the fact that if he were ever delayed and did not come as usual, Rosa would lament in querulous yet subdued tones, as if perplexedly speaking to herself, saying: "It is time, and he has not yet come?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Has innocenti æmulatione ostentabant silenti Rosæ, hortantes, ut &amp; suam promeret, aut formaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043224Z">
        <seg>They displayed these with innocent rivalry to the silent Rosa, urging her to produce or fashion her own.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Has pectori devota fide adhibuit juvenis, dormivit quietissime, thorace libero, ac sine ronchis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034804Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034804Z">
        <seg>The young man applied these with devoted faith to his chest, slept most peacefully, with his chest free and without snoring.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Has rugas, hoc tonitru, hunc pavorem videbatur sibi experiri mœstissima Rosa, dum laborabat sub hora sui deliquii;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045844Z">
        <seg>The most sorrowful Rosa seemed to experience these wrinkles, this thunder, this terror, as she labored under the hour of her fainting.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Has subito Antonius periclitanti conjugi apportans, ut (præmissa Rosæ devota imploratione) ventri imponeret, facile persuasit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035043Z">
        <seg>Antonius, suddenly bringing these to his wife in peril, easily persuaded her to place them on her womb, having first offered a devout prayer to Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hausit, plus solito incaluit, quievit, ac postridie discussa pleuritide, abacta febri, detersis papulis, Anna incolumis surrexit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035530Z">
        <seg>She drank, grew hotter than usual, rested, and the next day, with the pleurisy gone, the fever abated, and the papules cleared, Anna rose up unharmed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Henrico Susone&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt; id, velut compertum, tradit historia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010350Z">
        <seg>History relates this as a matter of fact, according to Henry Suso.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Henrico Susone&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; id, velut compertum, tradit historia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045834Z">
        <seg>History relates it as a matter of fact.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Herinaceum credidisses, non rosam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041848Z">
        <seg>You would have thought it was a hedgehog, not a rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Heu me, quam procul disto a perfecta, intima, robustissima tui dilectione!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053040Z">
        <seg>Alas, how far I am from a perfect, intimate, and most robust love for you!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Heu mihi!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050950Z">
        <seg>Woe is me!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Heu tantilla soporis indulgentia, quantam subinde jacturam infert!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051412Z">
        <seg>Alas, how little indulgence in sleep, and what a loss it brings!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Heu!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053148Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053148Z">
        <seg>Alas!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hi cum anno MDCXXVII mense Octobri prope urbem rusticabantur in prædio Balthasaris de la Cona, ibidem febris cum diarrhœa&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt; tenellam filiolam invasit, demum &amp; extinxit inter materna brachia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033114Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033114Z">
        <seg>While they were living in the countryside near the town in October 1627 on the estate of Balthasar de la Cona, a fever with diarrhea seized their tender little daughter and ultimately carried her off in her mother's arms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Joannes id, quod erat, suspicans, “Obsecro (ait) mi Hieronyme, dic libere, nonne cor tuum hæsitat incredulum circa sanctitatem virginis Rosæ”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033911Z">
        <seg>John, suspecting what it was, said, "I beg you, my Jerome, speak freely: does your heart not waver in unbelief concerning the virgin Rose's sanctity?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Rosa exterrita, quasi colubrum pressisset pede, resilivit, expalluit, obmutuit, probe conscia, si respondendum sit, quanta &amp; rerum copia, &amp; verborum inopia sibi foret laborandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050442Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050442Z">
        <seg>Rosa, startled as if she had trodden on a snake, recoiled, turned pale, and fell silent, well aware that, if she were to reply, she would have to contend with both the abundance of the subject matter and her own poverty of words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Rosa fratrem intuita, exspectabat, quid ille decerneret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044449Z">
        <seg>Here Rosa, looking at her brother, waited to see what he would decide.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Rosa, mendacem larvam sonoris vocibus detestata, lemuri dorsum obvertit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052209Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052209Z">
        <seg>Here Rosa, detesting the lying mask with sonorous voices, turned her back on the lemur.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Sponsus subridens, “Ne credideris (inquit) dilecta mea, solam te de feminis esse, quam duris hisce laboribus occuparim”: statimque proximi conclavis ostio latissime diducto, ostendit Rosæ lapidariam officinam peramplam ac speciosam, quam solæ implebant virgines arduo labori gnaviter intentæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044207Z">
        <seg>The Groom, smiling, said, "Do not think, my beloved, that you are the only woman I have occupied with these hard labors": and immediately, with the door of the adjoining room thrown wide open, he revealed to Rose a vast and splendid stone-cutting workshop, filled by the virgins alone, who were diligently intent on the arduous work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Virgo inops subitanei consilii tantillam impetravit dilationem, dum adiret confessarium, cui anxie protestata est, nisi præceptum revocaretur, se non posse non morem gerere matri hoc gestamen imperanti: malle se huic quantumvis exosi ornatus redimiculo suum illigare verticem, quam resistendo solvere jus, quo matris obligabatur imperio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035427Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035427Z">
        <seg>This penniless virgin obtained only a brief postponement of the sudden decree while she went to her confessor, to whom she earnestly protested that unless the order were revoked, she could not but obey her mother's command to wear this adornment; she would rather bind her head with this most odious ornament than, by resisting, forfeit the duty by which she was bound to her mother's authority.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Virgo primum ingenue fassa ac protestata est, se quidem illud mortalibus oculis minime conspecturam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062706Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062706Z">
        <seg>Here the Virgin first ingenuously confessed and protested that she would by no means behold it with mortal eyes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Virgo ultra solitum cœpit vehementius succuti amoris stimulis, ut nec voci temperare jam posset, quin erecta in pedes, fervidissime ac sonore affaretur sacram imaginem, quasi sola in oratorio fuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053703Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053703Z">
        <seg>This virgin, more than usual, began to be stirred more vehemently by the pangs of love, so that she could no longer restrain her voice; instead, rising to her feet, she addressed the sacred image with the greatest fervor and in a loud voice, as if she were alone in the oratory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic ab arboribus ad flores transfertur.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054807Z">
        <seg>Here it is transferred from trees to flowers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic accipitur pro illa senatorum vel judicum congregatione,quæ ad exsequias&lt;/e1&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e2&gt;convenerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030929Z">
        <seg>This is taken to be the assembly of senators or judges that had convened for the funeral of St. Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic ad primum conspectum attenuatæ &amp; exanguis filiæ itum in singultus &amp; lachrymas, nec ulli circumstantium ea fuit pectoris durities, quam ista lugubris spectaculi teneritudo non emolliret in plorandi societatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030139Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030139Z">
        <seg>At the first sight of her daughter, emaciated and pale, she broke into sobs and tears, and there was no one among those present so hard-hearted that the tenderness of that mournful spectacle did not soften him into joining in the weeping.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic autem pro&lt;/e8&gt; convenientia affectuum &lt;e9&gt;sumitur, sicut&lt;/e9&gt; &lt;s10&gt;ἀντιπάθεια&lt;/s10&gt; &lt;e11&gt;pro&lt;/e11&gt; repugnantia affectuum, &lt;e12&gt;de quo Castellus consuli potest&lt;/e12&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054748Z">
        <seg>Here, however, it is taken for the congruence of affections, just as antipathy is taken for the repugnance of affections, on which Castell can be consulted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic autem scriptor pro opinione sua allegat Thomam Bosium de signis veræ Ecclesiæ, &amp; Gilbertum Genebrardum in Chronologia, aliosque auctores, quos curiosus lector apud ipsum pag. 25 &amp; sequentibus citatos in fonte consulere poterit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041423Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041423Z">
        <seg>This writer, however, for his opinion, cites Thomas Bosius on the marks of the true Church, and Gilbert Genebrard in his Chronology, and other authors, whom the curious reader can consult in the original source cited on page 25 and following.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic demum illa evidenter agnoscens, ab unica sua pertinaci incredulitate hos esse paroxysmos, subito manus dedit, duritiem posuit, animoque trepide in Deum elevato supplex protestata est, se credere, se corde toto fateri, quod Rosa vere Sancta sit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033003Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033003Z">
        <seg>At last, clearly recognizing this, she suddenly abandoned her single-minded, stubborn unbelief, let go of her hardness, and with a trembling heart lifted to God, she humbly declared that she believed and with her whole heart confessed that Rosa was truly holy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic demum in se reflexa mater, postquam se Ludovicæ comiter excusarat, pensitare cœpit attentius, quid sibi velit, quod Rosæ oculos tunc potissimum obsideret importuna ista defluxio, quandocumque honestarum matronarum visitatio, aut domi excipienda, aut foris exequenda instabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043407Z">
        <seg>The mother, now at last reflecting on herself, after she had politely excused herself to Louisa, began to ponder more attentively what she herself wanted, why that troublesome discharge was besieging Rose's eyes just then, whenever a visit from respectable matrons was to be received at home or paid abroad.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic ei tenor est: “Domine mi Jesu Christe, Deus &amp; homo, vere conditor &amp; Redemptor noster, quod umquam te offenderim, equidem mente discrucior;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053058Z">
        <seg>This is his tenor: "My Lord Jesus Christ, God and man, truly our founder and Redeemer, for whatever I have ever offended you, I am truly tormented in mind;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic fassa est Maria, sibi, dissuaviata pluries Filiæ sacra imagine, obrepsisse somnum, at sub noctis gallicinio vigilem innatasse sudoribus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040853Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040853Z">
        <seg>Here Mary confesses that to herself, the sacred image of the Daughter, repeatedly disfigured, had let sleep creep over her, but at the cock's crow in the night she had woken, drenched in sweat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic igitur non mutata, sed duplicata fuit obediendi occasio: siquidem humilis Rosa paterno tecto, at non subjectione exemptam se reputans, qua promptitudine domi propriæ, eadem alienæ parebat, nec solum mandatis Gundisalvi aut uxoris, sed ad nutum quoque filiarum, domesticæque familiæ, usque ad infima domus mancipia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035619Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035619Z">
        <seg>Thus the occasion to obey was not changed but doubled: for humble Rosa, though she considered herself free from the paternal roof, did not deem herself exempt from submission; with the same promptness with which she obeyed in her own home, she obeyed in a foreign one, not only the commands of Gundisalvus or his wife, but also at the beck of their daughters and of the household staff, down to the lowest servants of the house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic igitur puerilis sui lusus &amp; crepundiorum exitus fuit, ut mirabilis, sic felicissimus: nam rarus est hic gratiæ tractus, qui Rosam ab innocentibus jocis unico subitoque passu deduxit in illam maturitatem judicii, quo simul nosset æstimare Dei celsitudinem, æternitatis pretium, &amp; virginei floris arcanam prærogativam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035034Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035034Z">
        <seg>Thus, this childish play of hers and the outcome of her toys were, as was marvelous, so exceedingly happy: for it is a rare stroke of grace that led Rosa, from innocent amusements, with a single, sudden step, into that maturity of judgment by which she might at once know how to appreciate God's majesty, the price of eternity, and the secret prerogative of the virgin's flower.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic illi a prima pueritia sensus fuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053322Z">
        <seg>This sense had been with him since early childhood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic ipsa die, qua Rosam sepulturæ fossa operuerat, ad ædem sancti Dominici anhelus venit, in ea aliquamdiu amarissime lachrymatus, ac de loco sepulturæ sciscitans demum, ut potuit, in aulam capituli prorepsit, Rosæ sepulchro pronus incubuit, per horam sic oravit, gemuit, flevit, denique sudavit, ignarus se jam curatum esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033347Z">
        <seg>On that very day, when he had covered Rosa's grave with earth, he came panting to the church of Saint Dominic, where he wept bitterly for a while. Finally, after inquiring about the burial place, he crawled into the chapter house as best he could, He lay prone on Rose's tomb, and for an hour he prayed, groaned, wept, and finally sweated, unaware that he had already been cured.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic itaque per&lt;/e1&gt; armadillos &lt;e2&gt;intelliguntur pisces, qui mihi hactenus ignoti sunt&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034438Z">
        <seg>Here, therefore, by "armadillos" are meant fish which have been unknown to me until now.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic itum in oscula, in dulces lachrymas, in teneros amplexus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032832Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032832Z">
        <seg>Here one goes into kisses, into sweet tears, into tender embraces;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic mirifico naturæ lusu, pleraque Dominicæ mortis nostræque redemptionis instrumenta, velut in exigui proscenii compendium, sese conspicue explicant: nam inter foliola, cruentis maculis venuste dispuncta, assurgit columnula flagellati Dei statumen;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041250Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041250Z">
        <seg>Here, in this marvelous play of nature, most of the instruments of the Lord's death and our redemption unfold themselves conspicuously, as if in a miniature stage-set: for among the little leaves, charmingly dotted with bloody spots, the little column of the God of the scourge rises up.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic misera Virgo secretum suum prodere debuit, ut ab ancilla consilium modumque exquireret, quo solvatur catena, priusquam experrecta mater in auxilium utrique accurrat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041727Z">
        <seg>This poor virgin had to reveal her secret in order to ask the maid for advice and a way to free herself, before her mother, upon waking, would rush to help them both.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic morbus, quod vitæ minus putetur insidiari, pro quantitate miseriæ commiserationem, pro magnitudine doloris remedium nusquam invenit aut levamen;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052627Z">
        <seg>This disease, because it is considered less life-threatening, finds no remedy or relief anywhere for the amount of misery, nor for the magnitude of the pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic nimirum spiritus erat seraphicæ suæ magistræ, quæ &amp; ipsa optaverat (si fieri posset) suopte corpusculo obturari gehennæ fauces, ne tam populosæ animarum catervæ illuc deinceps immigrarent, suæque frustrarent lytrum redemptionis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060406Z">
        <seg>This was indeed the spirit of her seraphic mistress, who herself had wished (if it could be done) to be sealed up in her own little body to block the jaws of hell, so that such populous throngs of souls would no longer migrate there and frustrate her ransom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic per usum monasticum vel recentiorem metaphoram accipitur pro reprehensione, sicut ex sensu patet.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040956Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040956Z">
        <seg>Here, through monastic usage or a more recent metaphor, it is taken as a reproach, as is clear from the sense.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic perpetuus tumultus erat offerentium vota, suspendentium anathemata, deponentium grallas &amp; scipiones: denique aliter consulendum fuit reverentiæ sacri tabernaculi prostantis in ara majori, ne huic assidue obverterentur dorsa hærentium ad Virginis sepulchrum, quod &amp; locum presbyterii incommodabat: siquidem hoc in Ordine Prædicatorum, ad cornu Epistolæ (ut vocant) construi solet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031624Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031624Z">
        <seg>There was a perpetual tumult here of votaries offering their vows, of those suspending anathemas, of those laying down their staves and scythes: in short, a different arrangement had to be made for the reverence of the sacred tabernacle, which stood on the high altar, lest the backs of those constantly turned toward the Virgin's sepulchre should be constantly turned away from it, which also inconvenienced the presbytery: for in the Order of Preachers, it is customary to build this at the Epistle corner (as they call it).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic resalutandi pariter &amp; excusandi formulas tentabat proloqui, sed frustra: relabebatur enim in eadem Dei &amp; Rosæ præconia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044622Z">
        <seg>He tried in vain to utter the customary greetings and excuses; for he kept slipping back into the same praises of God and Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic rursus inter adjectiva&lt;/e6&gt; anareticum &lt;e7&gt;&amp;&lt;/e7&gt; anachoreticum &lt;e8&gt;est analogia, qualem biographus sæpius affectat, ut jam supra monuimus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041426Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041426Z">
        <seg>Here again, among the adjectives anareticum and anachoreticum, there is an analogy such as the biographer often affects, as we have already noted above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic tandem archiepiscopus, solitudine pium fervorem acuente, in genua procubuit, manui Virginis reverenter apprehensæ pluries devota oscula fixit, eamque non solum in junctura, sed in singulis digitorum articulis, acsi viveret, tractabilem sensit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030734Z">
        <seg>At last the archbishop, his piety sharpened by solitude, knelt down, reverently took the Virgin's hand and repeatedly pressed devout kisses upon it, and he felt it tractable not only at the joint but at every finger joint, as if it were alive.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic tandem convicta mendacii, sed admirans, unde id Rosa potuerit divinare, sic coram omnibus orsa est loqui: “Dum ex Panama huc in ædes Isabellæ veni, reliquæ ibidem Æthiopissæ me per ludibrium EQUAM&lt;a0&gt;e&lt;/a0&gt; appellitabant, quod baptizata nondum essem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012916Z">
        <seg>At last convinced of the lie, but admiring how Rosa had been able to divine it, she began to speak before everyone: "While I was coming from Panama to this house of Isabella, the other Ethiopians there were mockingly calling me a mare, because I had not yet been baptized."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic, ut erat in quæstoris ædibus frequens ac pene domesticus, ibidem ex negotiorum occasione Rosam persæpe viderat, sæpius de illius sanctimonia vitæque singularitate mira audierat, ita de Virgine sublimiter, ut dignum erat, sentiebat, præsertim quod sciret, Gundisalvum Rosæ precibus meritisque pluries gravissimis infirmitatibus fuisse liberatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061435Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061435Z">
        <seg>He, as he was a frequent and almost domestic visitor to the questor's house, had there often seen Rosa on business occasions, and had heard more than once of her sanctity and the wonderful singularity of her life; so he thought of the Virgin in a sublime manner, as was fitting, especially since he knew, Gundisalvus had been freed from very serious illnesses by Rosa's prayers and merits on several occasions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hieronymi sacellum traxit, illic exporrecto in hilaritatem vultu, pavidas hortari cœpit ad subeundum secum martyrium, occasionem hanc nimis pretiosam esse ac fortunatam, videlicet non quomodocumque, sed sub ipso sanctæ Hostiæ conspectu nostiam cadere, coram &amp; pro divina illa Victima victimam cædi, pro exposito Christi Corpore animam exponere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060214Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060214Z">
        <seg>He dragged Jerome into the chapel, and there, with a face beaming with joy, he began to urge the fearful ones to undergo martyrdom with him, saying that this was an opportunity too precious and fortunate, namely, not just any kind of death, but to fall under the very gaze of the Holy Host, to be slain before and for that divine Victim, to lay down one's life for the exposed Body of Christ.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hilarior tamen fuit Rosa, quod talem nacta esset, cui tam occulte, &amp; pie viscera suæ misericordiæ impertiret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061218Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061218Z">
        <seg>Rosa, however, was all the more joyful, because she had found someone to whom she could so secretly and piously extend the bowels of her mercy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc &amp; Rosæ quotannis festum divi Bartholomæi (quo se morituram præsciebat) tam pretiosum &amp; charum fuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013059Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013059Z">
        <seg>Hence, the festival of Saint Bartholomew was every year so precious and dear to Rose, for she foresaw that she would die on that day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc Ferdinandus (id fratri nomen erat) tot morarum impatiens, ac velut superfluæ pietati Sororis admurmurans;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054859Z">
        <seg>Hence Ferdinand (for that was his brother's name), impatient of so many delays, and as it were murmuring at his sister's superfluous piety;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc Ludovica de Melgareyo femina sanctissima tanti Rosam faciebat, ut, sicubi cum ea concurrisset, nonnisi flexis humi genibus exciperet repugnantem honorari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050712Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050712Z">
        <seg>Hence, the most holy Ludovica of Melgareyo held Rosam in such high esteem that, whenever she met her, she would greet her with nothing less than a humble bow on the ground, honoring her even when she resisted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc Rosa moritura tenerius suum osculabatur scapulare, quo quasi viam lacteam sibi signari ad sedes empyreas sentiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030006Z">
        <seg>Hence, Rosa, about to die, kissed her scapular more tenderly, for she felt as if the Milky Way were being marked out for her to the heavenly seats.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc Rosa velut armillam brachio circumligarat, parvum ex minutissimis orbiculis rosariolum: cujus granula occulte trahebat ad singulas salutationes angelicas, etiam ubi alloquiis &amp; conversationi se reperiebat implicitam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045134Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045134Z">
        <seg>Thus Rosa had fastened a bracelet of the finest little beads around her arm, whose beads she secretly drew at each Angelic Salutation, even when she found herself absorbed in conversation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc Rosæ colloquia, salutationes, responsa, jugiter amoris divini mentione condiebantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053130Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053130Z">
        <seg>Henceforth, Rose's conversations, greetings, and responses were continually seasoned with mention of divine love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc ad grandiora miracula transeundum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032358Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032358Z">
        <seg>Hence, we must move on to greater miracles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc apud Limenses suos Rosa, vulgariter altera Catharina Senensis appellabatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051443Z">
        <seg>Hence, among her fellow Limans, Rosa was commonly called the other Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc apud antiquos celebrantur apes Hybleæ, sicut patet ex hoc carmine Virgiliano:&lt;/e0&gt; Hyblæis apibus florem depasta salicti.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044721Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044721Z">
        <seg>Hence the Hylæan bees are celebrated among the ancients, as is clear from this Virgilian verse: "The Hylæan bees have stripped the willow of its blossom."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc biographi allusio ad nomen&lt;/e2&gt; S. Rosæ, &lt;e3&gt;&amp; hoc epitheton&lt;/e3&gt; rosata &lt;e4&gt;saltem in idiomate Italico satis apta est&lt;/e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T000217Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035204Z">
        <seg>Hence the biographers' allusion to the name&lt;/e2&gt; of St. Rose, &lt;e3&gt;and this epithet&lt;/e3&gt; "rosata" &lt;e4&gt;is at least suitably apt in the Italian idiom.&lt;/e4&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc carmen illud Ovidianum:&lt;/e5&gt; Centum luminibus cinctum caput Argus habebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040937Z">
        <seg>Hence that Ovidian poem: Argus had a head surrounded by a hundred eyes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc colligimus, cur Baudrandus in Geographia sua insulam&lt;/e4&gt; Tabago &lt;e5&gt;etiam&lt;/e5&gt; Nicotianam &lt;e6&gt;vocaverit&lt;/e6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060955Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060955Z">
        <seg>From this we conclude why Baudrand in his Geography called the island of Tabago also Nicotiana.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc conversa ad titionem, quem manu gerebat, undequaque extinctum vidit, mirata se tanto tempore illic substitisse, dum totus paulatim extingui titio potuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045044Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045044Z">
        <seg>Then, turning to the torch she had been carrying, she saw it completely extinguished, and was amazed that she had stood there for so long while it could gradually be put out by the tinder.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc etiam fortasse nomen acceperunt nonnullæ Sanctæ, quæ in Ecclesia Catholica coluntur.&lt;/e5&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T213902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035223Z">
        <seg>Hence, perhaps, some of the Saints who are venerated in the Catholic Church also received their names.&lt;/e5&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc factum est, quod pleraque illustria ejus opera, visiones, &amp; arcanæ Spiritus deliciæ sic delituerint sub taciturnæ humilitatis abstruso inumbraculo, ut matris, sodalium, confessariorum assidua sagacitas, vix ad paucorum notitiam potuerit umquam penetrare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040813Z">
        <seg>Hence it came about that most of her illustrious works, visions, and the secret delights of the Spirit were so concealed under the obscure veil of silent humility that the constant vigilance of her mother, companions, and confessors could scarcely ever penetrate to the knowledge of a few.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc fuit, quod vocatus ad excipiendam Virginis confessionem, dicente subsacrista, in templo ipsum expectari a Rosula seu Rosella, velut irreverenter locutum increpuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050647Z">
        <seg>Hence, when he was called to hear the Virgin's confession, he rebuked the subdeacon for saying that Rosula, or Rosella, was waiting for him in the church, as if he had spoken irreverently.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc in Filiæ vultum curosius intenta, vidit, non fingi, quod apparebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043410Z">
        <seg>Hence, looking more closely at her daughter's face, she saw that what appeared to be the case was not a sham.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc in ipsius anima mirabiles illi effectus, tamque isti Sacramento connaturales ac proprii, quos nemo novit, nisi qui accipit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055848Z">
        <seg>Hence in his very soul those marvelous effects, so innate and proper to this Sacrament, which no one knows except the one who receives it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc inter acutissimos ischiadum cruciatus vox illa frequens: “Ure, preme, stringe, Domine;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030102Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030102Z">
        <seg>Hence, amid the most acute torments of sciatica, that frequent cry: "Burn, press, squeeze, O Lord;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc itum ad sinistram, quæ ut pectori avelleretur, pari anhelitu laborandum fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044703Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044703Z">
        <seg>From there, turning to the left, it was necessary to struggle with equal breath so that it might be torn from the chest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc mater anxia, sibique &amp; familiæ probrum metuens, arguebat Filiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062802Z">
        <seg>Hence the anxious mother, fearing disgrace for herself and her family, rebuked her daughter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc novit in florigero serto vera filia Hierusalem, jussa illud capiti imponere, celare acum tempora figentem, non solum intuita, sed imitata verum regem Salomonem in diademate, quo coronavit eum mater sua.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041154Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041154Z">
        <seg>Hence she recognized in the flower-wreathed garland the true daughter of Jerusalem, and, commanded to place it on her head, she concealed the sharp point that pierces the brow—not only beholding but imitating the true King Solomon in the diadem with which his mother had crowned him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc opus fuit confessariorum imperio, qui Virgini præcepere vespertinum lactucæ ac soporiferorum seminum usum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051313Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051313Z">
        <seg>Hence, it was necessary to use the authority of the confessors, who prescribed to the Virgin the evening use of lettuce and soporific seeds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc patet, Majores nostros non potuisse Acta hujus sancti præsulis illustrare, cum judicium Sedis Apostolicæ illis exspectandum fuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035213Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035213Z">
        <seg>Hence it is clear that our ancestors could not have illustrated the Acts of this holy prelate, since they had to await the judgment of the Apostolic See.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc per Bilacongæ vasta juga descenditur in vallem Xaquixaguanam (quam Garcilassus vocat Sacsahuanam) inter altissimos montes angusto spatio conclusam, in qua veteres Peruviæ reges hortos suos &amp; viridaria habuerunt &lt;e1&gt;&amp;c.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054717Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054717Z">
        <seg>From here, one descends the vast ridges of the Bilacongas into the Xaquixagua Valley (which Garcilaso calls Sacsahuanam), enclosed by the narrow space between the highest mountains, in which the ancient kings of Peru had their gardens and orchards &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc pluries in ipsa observarunt quæstoris filiolæ, quod, cum inter suendum Rosa filum &amp; acum trahebat sursum, una cum brachio videbatur &amp; animus elevari in exstasim, brevique morula in sublimi suspensa manus, redibat ad opus, nec aberrabat acus a puncto, quem reliquerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044849Z">
        <seg>Hence they often observed the quæstor's little daughter herself doing this: when, while sewing, she drew the thread and needle upward, her arm and spirit seemed to be lifted into ecstasy; and, after a brief moment, her hand suspended in midair, she returned to her work, and the needle never strayed from the point it had left.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc populus dilationis jam securus, imminente meridie, a templo diffluit ad ædes proprias.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031140Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031140Z">
        <seg>Thus the people, now secure from any further delay, at noon stream from the temple to their own homes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc saltem una opinio nostra confirmatur, &amp; altera dubitatio in eodem statu manet.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041439Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041439Z">
        <seg>Hence at least one of our opinions is confirmed, and another doubt remains in the same state.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc statim exarsit in ipsa conatus orandi, qui &amp; formulam dictavit jaculatoriam, hanc videlicet: “Jesus sit benedictus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035018Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035018Z">
        <seg>From this, an immediate attempt to pray arose within him, which dictated the following short prayer: "Jesus be blessed."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc subitus pavor totam pervasit urbem, quasi, ex publico P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041544Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041544Z">
        <seg>Hence a sudden panic seized the entire city, as if from the public square of P.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc subitus pavor totam pervasit urbem, quasi, ex publico P. Solani vaticinio, terræ hiatu proxime absorbendam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000530Z">
        <seg>Hence a sudden panic seized the entire city, as if, by the public prophecy of P. Solanus, it were about to be swallowed up by an opening in the earth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc successive tot sive scholarum, sive cœnobiorum, ac provinciarum in eum confluxerant præfecturæ: nam regiæ Universitatis Limanæ matutinus erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050141Z">
        <seg>Thus, one after another, the prefectures of so many schools, monasteries, and provinces had converged on him: for he was the morning prefect of the Royal University of Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc sæpe in ea notarunt religiosæ feminæ, quod sicubi in populosissimo templo angulum occuparat, e regione majoris aræ, attentius precatura, sedebat per horas immota, defixis in aram oculis, nec prætereuntes videbat, nec ad repentinos circa se strepitus deflectebat obtutum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044855Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044855Z">
        <seg>Hence, devout women often noted that whenever she had taken a corner in the most crowded temple, opposite the high altar, to pray more attentively, she would sit for hours motionless, her eyes fixed on the altar; she neither saw those who passed by nor turned her gaze to the sudden noises around her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc sæpissime per noctem audita est dormiens, numerato, totidemque verbis reddere precatiunculas, quibus per diem prolixius invigilarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044802Z">
        <seg>Hence, she was very often heard at night, while asleep, counting and repeating in equal number the little prayers with which she had kept vigil more at length during the day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc tanto devotionis studio suebat lintea, quæ CORPORALIA dicimus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060108Z">
        <seg>Hence, with such devotion, he would prepare the linens we call CORPORALS.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc tantopere afficiebatur initio Psalm.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061723Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061723Z">
        <seg>Hence he was so deeply moved at the beginning of the Psalms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc turba languentium, aut mutili corporis vitio miserabilium pene cum jurgio flagitabat accessum, [ut] feretrum contrectando speratam apprehenderet sanitatem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031010Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031010Z">
        <seg>Hence the crowd of the sick, or of the miserable crippled by bodily defect, almost in a clamorous dispute demanded access, hoping to seize the hoped-for health by handling the bier.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc vice quadam ab illustri femina interrogata, cur non rogaret seraphicam magistram suam, ut his ærumnis potenti illius suffragio eriperetur, heroïce respondit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052534Z">
        <seg>Hence, on one occasion, when asked by a distinguished woman why she did not ask her seraphic mistress to be delivered from these torments by her powerful intercession, she heroically replied;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc vicinam mortem non aliter ac certam paradisi janitricem læta aspexit: nam ut primum Rosæ denuntiarunt medici, naturam succubuisse morbo, nec aliud restare præter decretorium diem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061844Z">
        <seg>Hence she looked upon the neighboring death with joy, as upon the certain gatekeeper of paradise: for as soon as the doctors announced to Rosa that nature had succumbed to the disease, and that nothing remained but the decisive day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc, si deprehenderet de virtutibus se tantillum commendari, cruciabatur misera, expallescebat, diffluebat lachrymis, dehiscebat illico se mergens in abyssum humilitatis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041247Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041247Z">
        <seg>Hence, if she perceived that she was being praised even slightly for her virtues, the poor woman would be tormented, turn pale, be drenched in tears, and at once fall apart, plunging herself into the abyss of humility.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc, si quidpiam falsum etiam bona fide narrabatur aliis, pro accurato veritatis zelo, placidissime corrigebat referentem, interloquendo comiter, non sic, sed tali modo rem accidisse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053331Z">
        <seg>Hence, if anything false were told to others, even in good faith, out of a zeal for accurate truth, he would most calmly correct the person reporting it, interjecting politely that it had not happened that way, but in this manner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc, sæpe rogata, ut hoc illudve peteret, si videretur reipublicæ, aut cujusquam privatæ salutis esse, curam in se haud gravate recipiebat: deinde a sacra imagine rediens bonos successus tanta securitate pollicebatur, quasi concessæ gratiæ diploma recepisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054410Z">
        <seg>Hence, often asked to seek this or that if it seemed to be for the good of the republic or for anyone's private welfare, she would not take on the task without some reluctance; then, returning from the sacred image, she would promise good results with such assurance as if she had received a diploma of granted grace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc&lt;/e1&gt; dies caniculares &lt;e2&gt;nomen accipiunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054757Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054757Z">
        <seg>Hence the days get their name.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc&lt;/e1&gt; veredarius &lt;e2&gt;ab aliquibus pro rhedario accipitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052327Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052327Z">
        <seg>Hence a courier is taken by some for a charioteer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;ἄδυτον&lt;/s3&gt; &lt;e4&gt;dicitur quasi&lt;/e4&gt; inaccessibile, &lt;e5&gt;ab alpha privativo, &amp; verbo&lt;/e5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;δύω&lt;/s6&gt; ingredior.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032414Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032414Z">
        <seg>Hence&lt;e2&gt;ἄδυτον&lt;/e2&gt; is said, as it were, to be inaccessible, &lt;e5&gt;from the privative alpha, &amp; the verb&lt;/e5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;δύω&lt;/s6&gt; to enter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc&lt;/e2&gt; gargarismus &lt;e3&gt;a voce Græca&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;γαργαρεὼν&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;qua medici indicant eam gutturis partem, qua faucibus adjacet, &amp; quam nonnulli&lt;/e5&gt; plectrum &lt;e6&gt;sive&lt;/e6&gt; vocis refractorium &lt;e7&gt;appellant, quia ad vocis modulationem facit, ut Castellus in Lexico medico pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045406Z">
        <seg>Hence the gargle from the Greek word γαργαρεὼν, &lt;e5&gt;by which physicians indicate that part of the throat adjacent to the pharynx, and which some call the plectrum or vocal refractor, because it contributes to the modulation of the voice, as Castellus in his Medical Lexicon, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc&lt;/e2&gt; gargarismus &lt;e3&gt;a voce Græca&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;γαργαρεὼν&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;qua medici indicant eam gutturis partem, qua faucibus adjacet, &amp; quam nonnulli&lt;/e5&gt; plectrum &lt;e6&gt;sive&lt;/e6&gt; vocis refractorium &lt;e7&gt;appellant, quia ad vocis modulationem facit, ut Castellus in Lexico medico pag. 231 affirmat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010303Z">
        <seg>Hence the gargle from the Greek word γαργαρεὼν, &lt;e5&gt;by which physicians indicate that part of the throat adjacent to the pharynx, and which some&lt;/e5&gt; call the plectrum or vocal refractor, &lt;e7&gt;because it contributes to the modulation of the voice, as Castellus affirms in his Medical Lexicon, p. 231.&lt;/e7&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;ὑπογραφεὺς&lt;/s5&gt; &lt;e6&gt;est amanuensis, qui dictata excipit aut primum exemplar alicujus libri exscribit&lt;/e6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032445Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032445Z">
        <seg>Hence, a scribe is a copyist who takes dictation or copies the first edition of a book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc&lt;/e4&gt; bronchocele &lt;e5&gt;dicitur magnus &amp; rotundus gutturis tumor, qui in aspera arteria exoritur&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043128Z">
        <seg>Hence a bronchocele is called a large, round swelling of the throat that arises in the trachea.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc&lt;/e9&gt; &lt;s10&gt;τρίγωνος&lt;/s10&gt; triangularis &lt;e11&gt;&amp; similia originem habent&lt;/e11&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043134Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043134Z">
        <seg>Hence the triangle and similar figures have their origin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hincpatet, quo sensu vox illa hic usurpetur.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032426Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032426Z">
        <seg>Hence it is clear in what sense that word is used here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His Rosæ dormientis vultus immodice dispungebatur, adeo quod mater in genis ac fronte Filiæ persæpe notarit extemporanea hæc stigmata, nec tamen originem divinare poterat, jam secura de lana, quæ cervical implesset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042753Z">
        <seg>The face of the sleeping Rose was excessively smudged, so much so that her mother often noticed these impromptu stains on her daughter's cheeks and forehead, yet could not divine their origin, being already certain of the wool that had filled the pillow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His ad plenitudinem solatii adjecit, velle se (modo Deus permiserit) ab obitu conveniri a defuncti anima, ut si forte ulterioribus suffragiis eguerit, statim adjuvetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060840Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060840Z">
        <seg>He added to this full consolation that he wished, God willing, to be visited by the deceased's soul upon his death, so that if it should happen to need further suffrages, it might be helped at once.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His agnovit se teneri, in dextra Salvatoris, velut rosam electam, quam non raperet quisquam de manu ejus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042517Z">
        <seg>By these he knew himself to be held on the right hand of the Savior, like a chosen rose that no one could snatch from his hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His artibus somnum suum intra duarum horarum angustias coërcuit, &amp; quandoque etiam minus spatii importuno exactori concessit: diurnum enim, nocturnumque tempus sic distribuebat, ut ex utroque duodecim integras horas orationi seponeret: alias decem labori manuum, quo parentes sustentaret, impendebat: reliquas duas brevi quieti somnoque deputabat, adversus cujus insidias clavum parieti infixerat, cui capillitium, quod ad tegendam coronam reservarat, strictissime implicabat, eoque tormento somnolentiam fugans, ad preces recitandas vigilias accrescere&lt;a1&gt;*&lt;/a1&gt; consueverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041554Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041554Z">
        <seg>With these methods he confined his sleep to the narrow limits of two hours, and sometimes even granted the importunate exactor less time: for he distributed his day and night so that he would set aside twelve full hours from each for prayer: the remaining ten he devoted to manual labor, by which he supported his parents. He devoted the remaining two hours to brief rest and sleep. Against the snares of slumber, he had driven a nail into the wall, on which he most tightly fastened the hair he had reserved for weaving a crown, and by this torment, driving away drowsiness, he had grown accustomed to lengthen his vigils for prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His auditis, (pergebat Rosa) invasit me validissimus impetus, quem verbis effari nequeo, prosiliendi in medium fori, ut publice fortissimisque clamoribus vociferarer ad omnem hominum ætatem, sexum, statum: Audite populi, audite gentes universæ, ex ore Christi vobis denuntio, non adquiri gratiam sine prævia afflictione, ærumnis supra ærumnas concumulatis opus est, ut ad intimum divinæ naturæ participium, ad gloriam filiorum Dei, ad perfectam animæ pulchritudinem pertingatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052829Z">
        <seg>Upon hearing this, (Rosa continued) I was seized by a most powerful impulse, which I cannot express in words, to leap into the middle of the forum, and there to cry out with the loudest and most powerful shouts to all men of every age, sex, and station: Hear, O people, hear, O all you nations, I declare to you from the mouth of Christ, that grace is not acquired without prior affliction; that it is necessary to heap up tribulation upon tribulation, in order to attain to the innermost participation of the divine nature, to the glory of the sons of God, to the perfect beauty of the soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His auditis, adeo exarsit mater, irritari se putans, ut filiam a conspectu suo abegerit, altum vociferans, “Egone monialis ero?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062830Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062830Z">
        <seg>Upon hearing this, the mother, thinking herself insulted, was so enraged that she drove her daughter from her sight, crying out loudly, "Shall I become a nun?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His cum fremitu in se prolatis (obstrue nares lector) pelvim ori apposuit, totumque illum saniosum cruorem (dicam, an cruentam saniem) ausu heroico sensim epotavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061410Z">
        <seg>Having brought these forth with a roar (reader, stop up your nostrils), he placed the basin to his mouth and with heroic daring slowly drank down all that pus-filled gore (shall I call it pus or bloody pus?).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His deliciis gustum mulcebat, quibus, ne simile condimentum abesset, persæpe cineres admiscebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041212Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041212Z">
        <seg>He would season these delicacies with ashes, so that no similar condiment would be lacking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His dictis, feminas (Catharina, &amp; Francisca Montoya vocabantur) dimisit domum ridentes, quod illo anni tempore, ex languido, sterilique frutice cariophylla expectaret, imo promitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055145Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055145Z">
        <seg>Having said this, he dismissed the women (Catherine and Francisca Montoya) to their home, laughing, because at that time of year they expected, indeed promised, cloves from a languid, barren shrub.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His dictis, magna vi, at robustiori fide, utramque supposuit manum tumenti tibiæ, &amp; veniam precatus, usque ad picturæ marginem sublevavit ægerrimum pondus, postque brevissimi contractus morulam demisit lente in culcitram, &amp; (quod dudum non potuerat) placido sopore per dimidiam horam quievit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040608Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040608Z">
        <seg>Having said this, with great strength but firmer faith he placed both hands under the swollen part of your shin and, having begged permission, lifted the very heavy weight up to the edge of the painting. Then, after the briefest moment of contraction, he slowly lowered it onto the mattress, and (which he had long been unable to do) rested in peaceful slumber for half an hour.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His expeditis, advocavit ambas quæstoris filiolas, quas ob innocentiam rectamque indolem impense amaverat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030155Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030155Z">
        <seg>Having finished these tasks, he summoned the two daughters of the quaestor, whom he had dearly loved for their innocence and upright character.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His igitur ambas manus applicabat in somnum pugnatura, ab horum tenaci complexu pendulum librabat corpusculum, dum Deiparæ Virginis Officium absolveret, his cuneolis findebat impetum somni, erudiebatur ad excubias, crucifigebat hostem, nec nisi victrix obrigidos lacertorum tendines retrahebat a charis victoriæ manubriis: sic palmam obtinebat, sed palmis callosis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043051Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043051Z">
        <seg>So she applied both hands to the struggle against sleep, her little body swinging in a pendulum from their tenacious clasp, while she finished the Office of the Virgin Mother, broke the onslaught of slumber with these little jabs, roused herself for her watch, crucified the enemy, and only as a victor did she draw back the rigid tendons of her arms from the dear handles of victory: Thus she won the palm, but with calloused palms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His igitur constituta dies XXVII Augusti, velut quæ proregi minus videbatur negotiis suis impedita.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031221Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031221Z">
        <seg>Thus, the 27th of August was set, as it seemed least to interfere with her own affairs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His igitur mensibus, etiam herbarum panisque diminutione accuratius se macerabat, donec ineunte Quadragesima, omnem sibi omnino subtraheret panem, solis malorum aureorum seminibus parcissime victitans;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041315Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041315Z">
        <seg>During those months, therefore, he more strictly mortified himself by also reducing his intake of herbs and bread, until, at the beginning of Lent, he deprived himself of all bread entirely, subsisting only on the seeds of golden apples, most sparingly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His illanguebat tota affectuum dulcedine, horum repetitione inardescebat medullitus, nec fatigabatur orationis prolixitate, modo in eadem verba sæpius recurreret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030021Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030021Z">
        <seg>He was enraptured by the whole sweetness of these affections; their repetition kindled him to the marrow, and he was not wearied by the length of the discourse, so long as he could return to the same words again and again.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His interpellabat verum Deum, &amp; Sponsum animæ suæ, lætitiam cordis sui, ac benignissimum Jesum amore illo perfectissimo, quo simul universi cives empyrei eum diligunt, se prosequi, &amp; consumi toto corde protestabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041640Z">
        <seg>She thus addressed the true God and Spouse of her soul, the joy of her heart, and the most benevolent Jesus, protesting that she was consumed with that perfect love with which all the citizens of heaven simultaneously love him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His malis pressa Catharina, tertio post Rosæ depositionem die accessit ad tumulum, preces fudit cum lachrymis, pulveres scypho intritos ebibit, primoque sensit, depulso melancholiæ tabisico pondere, cor suum hilarescere &amp; confirmari: mox &amp; capitis restingui ardores, quos tunc patiebatur intensissimos;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035551Z">
        <seg>Overwhelmed by these misfortunes, Catherine went to the tomb on the third day after Rose's deposition, poured out prayers with tears, drank the powders ground in a mortar, and at first felt, once the leaden weight of melancholy was cast off, her heart brightening and strengthening; soon the burning ardors of her head, which she had then endured most intensely, were also assuaged.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His ostendit verissimum, quod in ecstasi cuidam dixerat, id est, se Rosam gestare in intimo sui divini cordis penetrali, cum sciret vicissim in ejus virgineo corculo se solum tranquillissime inhabitare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041932Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041932Z">
        <seg>By this she showed to be true what she had said in a certain ecstasy, namely, that she carries the Rose in the innermost sanctuary of her divine heart, since she knew that, in turn, she herself dwells most tranquilly in her virgin heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His peractis Deum Patrem æternum, regemque gloriæ Christum Dominum Patris sempiternum Filium, &amp; sanctum Paraclitum Spiritum, unum Deum unumque Dominum laudibus &amp; confessionibus venerati, sacroque hymno &lt;b0&gt;Te Deum&lt;/b0&gt; solemniter decantato, peculiari in honorem sanctæ Rosæ oratione precati sumus per ejusdem merita, atque ad altare sancti Petri apostoli Missam celebravimus cum ejusdem Sanctæ commemoratione, omnibus Christi fidelibus tunc præsentibus plenariam omnium peccatorum suorum indulgentiam &amp; remissionem concessimus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043419Z">
        <seg>These things being accomplished, we have venerated God the eternal Father, and Christ the Lord, the King of glory, the eternal Son of the Father, and the Holy Paraclete Spirit, one God and one Lord, with praises and confessions, and having solemnly sung the sacred hymn &lt;b0&gt;Te Deum&lt;/b0&gt;, We prayed a special prayer in honor of Saint Rose through her merits, and at the altar of Saint Peter the Apostle we celebrated Mass with the commemoration of the same Saint, granting to all the faithful of Christ then present a plenary indulgence and remission of all their sins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His pœnis eruditur anima ad sui notitiam, dum in lucis ac tenebrarum vicissitudine discit experimento sui nihili, quid a seipsa, quid a Deo habeat, quidque inter illa &amp; ista intersit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050408Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050408Z">
        <seg>By these punishments the soul is taught to know itself, as in the alternation of light and darkness it learns by experience of its own nothingness what it has from itself, what from God, and what the difference is between the two.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His quanto potuit conatu pronuntiatis, infirmum dulcis corripuit somnus hora circiter dimidia, quo discusso, petiit admoveri sibi propius sacram imaginem, quod osculo eam cuperet revereri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040636Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040636Z">
        <seg>After uttering these words with all his strength, a sweet, faint sleep seized the frail man for about half an hour; when it was broken, he asked for the sacred image to be brought closer, for he wished to honor it with a kiss.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His quidem ad extrema paulatim me deduci comperio, sed quia dolorum meorum intensioni accessura est certa in plusculos dies extensio, simul efflictim compatior labori ac molestiæ, qua domum istam, diutius, quam vellem, oneratura sum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013434Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013434Z">
        <seg>I find that these things are indeed gradually driving me to my wit's end, but since a definite extension of a few more days is about to be added to the intensity of my pains, I am also made more patient with the labor and trouble I will be imposing on that house for longer than I would wish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His sanctis artibus spes omnes nuptiarum elusit, non tamen quamcumque pugnam evasit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041309Z">
        <seg>By these holy arts she eluded all hopes of marriage, yet she did not escape every conflict.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His sarcinis plane mortiferis exonerata Blanca respiravit, &amp; quæ, suscepto pœnitentiæ sacramento, jam sese morti certissimæ præpararat, intra pauculos dies, abacta febri, lectum deseruit, integræque valetudinis beneficio diu potita est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035052Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035052Z">
        <seg>Blanca, relieved of those truly deadly burdens, breathed again, and she, having received the sacrament of penance, had already prepared herself for a most certain death. Within a few days, the fever having departed, she left her bed and for a long time enjoyed the benefit of perfect health.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His tertia supererat germana soror, Francisca nomine, quam econtra tantum accenderat desiderium subeundi tertium Ordinem sancti Dominici, ut vix aliud cogitare libuerit, aut loqui.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012532Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012532Z">
        <seg>A third sister, named Francisca, remained with them, who, on the contrary, had so kindled her desire to join the Third Order of Saint Dominic that she could scarcely think or speak of anything else.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His ultro citroque velut ex pacto mutuo constitutis, prudens Filia culinariam ancillam Marianam, suarum partium fecit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041203Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041203Z">
        <seg>With these things thus established by mutual agreement, the prudent daughter made the kitchen maid Mariana one of her own.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His vero exercitationibus tantum insumebat studium &amp; curam, ac si per totam vitam suam pro illa dumtaxat unica vice communicandum sibi fuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055734Z">
        <seg>He devoted such study and care to these exercises as if he were to communicate with her for that one and only time in his entire life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hispani TABACO nominarunt ab insula quadam ejus nominis, in qua frequentissima reperitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060942Z">
        <seg>The Spaniards named it TABACO after an island of that name, on which it is found most abundantly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hisque sancti amoris exercitiis sic animam, &amp; cor suum illi sacraverat, ut tota ipsius, veluti dilectissima sponsa esset, &amp; nihil sibi reservaret, quod Sponsus ejus omnino non possideret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041647Z">
        <seg>And by these exercises of holy love she had consecrated her soul and heart to Him, so that she was entirely His, like a most beloved bride, and reserved nothing for herself that her Bridegroom did not wholly possess.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc Rosa centies timidæ quæstoris conjugi ad solatium occantarat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012259Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012259Z">
        <seg>This, Rosa, the hundred-times timid wife of the questioner, had sung to console herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc Rosæ obtigit, cui tota conversatio in cælis erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050901Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050901Z">
        <seg>This happened to Rose, whose entire conversation was in the heavens.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc agone defuncta Rosa, quietem repetiit, ut eamdem ancilla repeteret, ac mane facto, velut nil grave passa, ad consuetos labores properavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041746Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041746Z">
        <seg>When this task was done, Rosa rested, so that the same maid could take her turn, and at dawn, as if she had suffered nothing serious, she hurried back to her usual labors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc animo excogitavit, inopem parvulum quasi in filium assumere, quem Christianæ vitæ rudimentis imbueret, &amp; piis eleemosynis ad virtutes, &amp; sacrarum litterarum plenam instructionem se evecturam sperabat eo pacto, ut is, idoneus factus &amp; sacerdotio insignitus, ad apostolicas missiones pro conversione gentium properaret, ut, quod per se præstare non poterat, per alium suppleret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042338Z">
        <seg>He devised this with that intent, to take a poor little boy as a son, to imbue him with the rudiments of the Christian life, and by pious almsgiving to advance him to virtue and a full instruction in the sacred Scriptures, hoping that, when he had become suitable and ordained to the priesthood, he would hasten to the apostolic missions for the conversion of the Gentiles, so that what she could not accomplish herself she might supply through another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc de se credebat ipsa, hoc itidem credi ab aliis peranxie cupiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040531Z">
        <seg>She believed this of herself, and she was also desperately eager for others to believe it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc esse tantummodo indicium, quam enixe cupiat Servator noster ab omnibus redamari, testata est ipsa virgo Rosa divini conscia secreti, timentibus domesticis, ne impendentis divinæ ultionis præsagium esset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042143Z">
        <seg>This was merely a sign of how earnestly our Savior desires to be loved by all, as the virgin Rose herself, conscious of the divine secret, testified, for fear that it might be a portent of impending divine vengeance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc filio præbuit, sæviente paroxysmo, temporibus circomponendum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035021Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035021Z">
        <seg>He gave this to his son, to be applied at the proper times during a raging fit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc in tenera puella canæ prudentiæ moderamen fuit, quo sic corpusculo detractum voluit, ut simul alieno in se juri atque imperio nil detraheret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041020Z">
        <seg>This was the prudent guidance of a wise dog for the tender girl, by which he wished to withdraw from her little body so that at the same time he would take nothing away from another's right or authority over her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc ipsum Rosæ insciæ pluries coram diversis accidisse, compertum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053027Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053027Z">
        <seg>It has been discovered that this very thing happened to an unsuspecting Rose on multiple occasions in front of different people.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc itaque castissimo schemate obarmavit se Rosa, ut illius præsidio a se longius repelleret omnem secularem muliebris ornatus vanitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035446Z">
        <seg>Thus, Rosa armed herself with this most chaste scheme, so that by its protection she might repel from herself all worldly vanity of female adornment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc loco honestam animi recreationem seu relaxationem significari, ex adjunctis abunde colligitur.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050845Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050845Z">
        <seg>From the accompanying circumstances, it is abundantly clear that this term signifies an honorable recreation or relaxation of the mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc nimirum suæ quondam novellæ Sponsæ inter paraphernalia&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt; &amp; antidora&lt;a1&gt;e&lt;/a1&gt; pollicitus erat cælestis Lapicida (de quo supra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011822Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011822Z">
        <seg>This, indeed, the heavenly Stonecutter had promised to his former young bride among the paraphernalia and antidora, as mentioned above in the chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc nimirum suæ quondam novellæ Sponsæ inter paraphernalia&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; &amp; antidora&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;e&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; pollicitus erat cælestis Lapicida (de quo supra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062309Z">
        <seg>This, indeed, the heavenly Lapicida had promised among the paraphernalia and antidora for his former bride (of whom above, chapter).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc nomen etiam ad brevem notitiam aliarum scientiarum transferri potest.&lt;/e2&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060930Z">
        <seg>This name can also be applied, for a brief introduction, to other sciences.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc parturienti mortale esse mussitabant circumstantes feminæ, jamque maritus, ultimum vale dicturus charissimæ conjugi, petebat aditum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040444Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040444Z">
        <seg>The women standing by whispered that this was fatal to the woman in labor, and now the husband, about to bid his dearest wife a final farewell, was seeking admission.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc pene joco, indigitabat molem emortui genu &amp; brachii, quibus graviori pondere sua paralysis insederat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025705Z">
        <seg>He almost jokingly pointed to the stiffness in his dead knee and arm, where the heavier weight of his paralysis had settled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc perfuncti officio, evolabant ad pastum: similiter, ubi ad solis occubitum repetebant hospitalem domunculam, rursum instabat Rosa, ut, priusquam se quieti darent, secum tantisper serotinas laudes communi depangerent Creatori.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043835Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043835Z">
        <seg>Having completed this duty, they flew off to feed; similarly, when at sunset they returned to the hospitable little house, Rosa would again insist that, before they gave themselves to rest, they share with her a few evening praises to the Creator.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc potu indigenæ hospites suos etiam magnates excipiunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052349Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052349Z">
        <seg>With this drink, the natives also entertain their guests of high rank.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc pro similibus citationibus, quæ postmodum occurrent, semel monuisse sufficiat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035907Z">
        <seg>This should suffice as a single warning for similar citations that will arise later.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc proregis archiater, aliusque medicus Rocca sentiebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040646Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040646Z">
        <seg>This was the view of the pro-regent's chief physician and another doctor, Rocca.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc summarie constat, Virginem per S. Catharinæ Senensis vestigia gradatim abstinendi jejunandique usum ferme ad ipsum usque inediæ culmen mirabiliter produxisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041002Z">
        <seg>It is briefly evident that the Virgin, by following in the footsteps of St. Catherine of Siena, gradually led her to the practice of abstinence and fasting, almost to the very height of starvation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc supplemento credebat Rosa utcumque satisfactum iri generoso ardori suo, quo ultra femineam mensuram ac modum avida sitiebat salutem proximorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060511Z">
        <seg>Rosa believed that this supplement would in any way satisfy her generous ardor, by which, beyond the measure and bounds of a woman, she eagerly thirsted for the well-being of her neighbors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc ubi Ludovicæ ex ore sui confessarii innotuit, cœpit vaticinii eventum anxia metuere, habitum ipsum cordicitus abhorrere: imo, siqua eminus illius suscipiendi aut prætervolaret mentio, aut illaberetur cogitatio, velut ad Medusæ&lt;a0&gt;h&lt;/a0&gt; conspectum obrigescebat pavida, repugnabat animo, cor obdurabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032851Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032851Z">
        <seg>When this became known to Ludovica from the mouth of her confessor, she began anxiously to fear the fulfillment of the prophecy, and to loathe the habit itself with all her heart: Indeed, if from a distance any mention of undertaking it arose, or a thought of it drifted in, she would stiffen in terror at the mere prospect, her spirit would rebel, and her heart would harden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc vero omnium molestissimum erat, tam procul exulare a Dilecti notitia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045524Z">
        <seg>This, however, was the most troublesome of all: to be exiled so far from the knowledge of the Beloved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc ærumnoso tormento semi-lacer, at non minus fide alacer, vespere quodam Rosæ tumbam adiit, devote oravit, terramque inde arreptam inguinibus applicuit, nec ab illo temporis momento pristini doloris ullum notare potuit vestigium: quin, resumpto prædicationis officio, ad laborem vegetior induruit, equitaturus, a terra in sellam agilissime se libravit, quatuor millia leucarum terra marique sine impedimento aut herniæ sensu robuste confecit, liber a ligaculis, ubi quotidianæ precis votivo penso se alligarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035508Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035508Z">
        <seg>Half-torn by this painful torment, but no less eager in his faith, he went one evening to Rose's tomb, prayed devoutly, and applied the earth he had taken from there to his loins, and from that moment on, he could not notice a single trace of his former pain: Rather, resuming his preaching duties, he became more vigorous in his work. About to ride, he agilely swung himself from the ground into the saddle, and robustly completed four thousand leagues by land and sea without any impediment or sensation of hernia, free from the bonds with which he had bound himself to his daily prayers as a votive offering.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoccine te docuit seraphica tua magistra?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061401Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061401Z">
        <seg>Did your seraphic teacher teach you this?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hora duodecima est, nec tamen ille venit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051000Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051000Z">
        <seg>It is twelve o'clock, and yet he has not come.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hortabatur Joannes ut iterum jaceret, &amp; ad singulos jactus elata voce “Eia (inquiebat) in nomine benedictæ Rosæ cadat feliciter”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033902Z">
        <seg>John urged her to lie down again, and with each throw he would raise his voice and say, "Come on! In the name of blessed Rose, may it fall successfully."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Horum alterum domi forte in sedili a matre depositum conspicata Rosa, libere corripuit, &amp; inopi cuidam virgini donum fecit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061110Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061110Z">
        <seg>Rosa, having by chance at home spotted one of them left on a seat by her mother, freely took it and gave it as a gift to a certain poor virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hos itaque timores quotidie ingerebant cum jurgio, his minis exagitabant undique derelictam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052516Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052516Z">
        <seg>Thus they would daily instill these fears with their scolding, tormenting her with these threats, leaving her abandoned on all sides.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huc Rosa ut intravit, arrectis horrore subito pilis, agnovit, ibidem sese a SCABIOSO ad pugnam expectari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052032Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052032Z">
        <seg>As soon as Rosa entered here, her hair suddenly standing on end with horror, she recognized that she was being awaited there by the Scabby One for a fight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huc igitur afflicta mater parvulum suum detulit, ad sepulchrum stitit, oravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033514Z">
        <seg>So the afflicted mother brought her little child here, stood by the tomb, and prayed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huc igitur artes artusque omnes intendebat Rosa, ut piorum operum singulari ac pretiosa consutura dignas Amori infantulo vestes præpararet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053539Z">
        <seg>Thus, Rosa devoted all her skill and effort to preparing for little Love garments worthy of a singular and precious stitching of pious deeds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huc ille cum socio religioso F. Hieronymo Jambrano, alioque Indo piscatum ierat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033855Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033855Z">
        <seg>He had gone there fishing with his religious companion, Father Jerome Jambrano, and another Indian.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huc jussa Rosa cum parentibus secesserat, nec multo post, noxiis illis frigoribus impar, cœpit in manibus ac pedibus dira nervorum attractione laborare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035501Z">
        <seg>Rosa, having been ordered here, withdrew with her parents, and not long after, unprepared for those noxious frosts, she began to suffer in her hands and feet from a dreadful strain of the nerves.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huc postquam se recluserat, cuidam probatæ sanctitatis matronæ in raptu visa est sub specie ac figura lucidissimæ stellæ, cujus radios &amp; quaquaversum scintillantem claritudinem opaca parietum angustia cohibere nequaquam poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043705Z">
        <seg>After she had withdrawn here, she appeared in a rapture to a matron of proven sanctity in the form and likeness of a most brilliant star, whose rays and all-around sparkling brightness the narrow walls could in no way contain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic Rosa familiariter se applicuit, totamque illam cogitationem velut ex pugillaribus accurate recitavit, ac saluberrimi documenti aphorismos miranti præscripsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012135Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012135Z">
        <seg>Rosa familiarly applied herself to this, and accurately recited the whole of that thought as if from little notebooks, and prescribed to the astonished man the aphorisms of the most salutary document.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic Rosa peculiari devotione erat addictissima, hanc effigiem (quotiescumque diducto velo prostabat in altari) fixis obtutibus, avidissimis oculis hauriebat, ac fere sorbebat intentissima contemplatione;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053650Z">
        <seg>Rosa was devoted to this particular image with the utmost devotion; she would gaze at this effigy (whenever the veil was drawn back on the altar) with fixed eyes, drinking it in with the most avid eyes, and almost absorbing it in her most intense contemplation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic Virgo succincte, quantum oportuit eam scire, respondit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054435Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054435Z">
        <seg>The virgin replied to him succinctly, as much as she needed to know.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic dextre, ac sub fide silentii extorsit, ut sibi in singula prandia solam pararet offam ex panis frustis &amp; herbarum pugillo, at sine sale &amp; condimento, simplici aqua decoctam, pauculis dumtaxat uvis passis nonnumquam inspersis, ut matri, in singulos bolos attentæ, videretur genialiter obsonare ac dulciter, ubi rarus bolum augebat acinus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041206Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041206Z">
        <seg>He skillfully extorted from her, under an oath of silence, that she prepare for herself at each meal a single morsel of bread crusts and a handful of herbs, but without salt or seasoning, boiled in plain water, with only a few raisins sometimes sprinkled on it, so that the mother, attentive to every morsel, seem to be cheerfully and sweetly providing for her, where a rare grape enhanced each mouthful.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic in extremo fere agone laboranti, Maria de Oliva, Rosæ mater, concubia nocte assidebat sola.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034317Z">
        <seg>As he lay laboring in the final throes of his struggle, Mary of Oliva, Rose's mother, sat alone by his side through the night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic pater erat Gregorius de Torres agricola, mater Joanna Michaëla, ambo victitantes opere rustico in suburbanis Limæ ad viam Melambo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033111Z">
        <seg>His father was Gregory de Torres, a farmer, and his mother was Joanna Michaela; both earned their living by farm work in the suburbs of Lima on Melambo Road.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic per certa intervalla nonnullos indiderat claviculos introrsum præacutos, eoque diademate clam sibi caput non sine vulnere obcingebat, sub vittis victima coronata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042059Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042059Z">
        <seg>He had set certain small, inwardly sharpened pins at intervals in it, and with that diadem he secretly bound his head, not without wounding it, the victim crowned beneath the bands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic per edictum maternum proscripto successerat nodosorum stipitum fascis, in extremitate funibus collectus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042824Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042824Z">
        <seg>In place of the bundle of knotted sticks, which had been banished by a mother's decree, a bundle bound at the ends with ropes had taken its place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic periculose infirmabatur ancilla domestica, Francisca nomine, quæ simul nutricis fungebatur officio, &amp; tunc quidem viduæ filiolum Josephum lactabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055219Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055219Z">
        <seg>A household servant named Francisca, who also served as a nurse, was dangerously ill; at that time she was nursing the infant son of a widow, Joseph.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic usui singulare genus suaveolentium chirothecarum emerat, ut iis more femineo Rosa saltem gestatione nocturna cogeret suas manus in adscititiam venustatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035331Z">
        <seg>For this purpose he had purchased a special kind of fragrant gloves, so that with them, in a feminine manner, Rosa might at least compel her hands, during the night's wear, into the borrowed charm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic ut innotuit quod gestum erat, extemplo mandavit Rosæ, quidquid illud esset, quo caput suum hactenus spinose discruciaverat, sibi adferret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042310Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042310Z">
        <seg>As soon as he learned what had been done, he immediately ordered Rosa to bring him whatever it was with which she had so far been spikily tormenting his head.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic, dum incautus thema concionis habendæ ruminat inter prandendum, transversus in epiglottide bolus spiritum intercluserat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035632Z">
        <seg>For him, while he was carelessly pondering the theme of a sermon to be delivered over his lunch, a bolus lodged in his epiglottis had cut off his breath.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic, leucis plus minus quingentis Lima distanti, Rosa soror epistolam scripsit, (nam divinitus didicerat, illum in Chile honesto matrimonii jugo succollasse.) Hac solicite hortabatur fratrem, quandoquidem hunc statum amplexus erat, Christiani patrisfamilias obligationi inter militares strepitus accurate satisfacere haud negligeret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012817Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012817Z">
        <seg>To him, who was more than five hundred leagues from Lima, his sister Rosa wrote a letter (for she had learned by divine revelation that he had entered into an honorable marriage in Chile). In it she earnestly urged her brother that, since he had embraced this state, he should not neglect to fulfill the obligations of a Christian head of household amid the clatter of military life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus aliquot ansulæ seu circuli post Rosæ obitum apud D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041804Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041804Z">
        <seg>Several of these little rings or circles were found after Rose's death at D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus aliquot ansulæ seu circuli post Rosæ obitum apud D. Mariam de Usategui aliquamdiu asservati, mirabilem, at omnibus peregrinam spirarunt suaveolentiam, testes nimirum pœnosi sudoris, quem de Rosæ corpusculo imbiberant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000921Z">
        <seg>Some of these little vials or circles, after Rose's death, were kept for a while at Our Lady of Usategui and gave off a marvelous yet unfamiliar fragrance to everyone, a testament to the painful sweat they had absorbed from Rose's little body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus amœni fulgoris centrum insederat arcus multicolor, splendidus, ac multiplici luminum pictura gratiose variegatus, supraque istum alius extendebatur arcus, paris cum inferiori pulchritudinis, ac majestatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052727Z">
        <seg>The center of this delightful radiance was occupied by a multicolored, splendid arch, gracefully variegated with a manifold painting of lights, and above that another arch extended, of equal beauty and majesty with the lower one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus antibari&lt;a0&gt;g&lt;/a0&gt; molestissimo pondere tenetur spiritus in suo æquilibrio, ne extollatur in donis Altissimi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010455Z">
        <seg>The spirit is held in its balance by the most troublesome weight of this antibaryg, lest it be lifted up in the gifts of the Most High.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus antibari&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;g&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; molestissimo pondere tenetur spiritus in suo æquilibrio, ne extollatur in donis Altissimi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050411Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050411Z">
        <seg>The spirit is held in its own balance by the most troublesome weight of this antibari&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;g&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus circuitum faces cereæ triginta occuparunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031503Z">
        <seg>Thirty wax torches occupied this circuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus diadematis triplici actu&lt;a0&gt;*&lt;/a0&gt; in sanctissimæ Triadis reverentiam dæmonum suggestiones illico fugare consueverat, satis instructa, tales spinas non violare, sed vallare has rosas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041544Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041544Z">
        <seg>By the triple act of this diadem, she had become accustomed to instantly banish the demons' suggestions in reverence of the most holy Trinity, sufficiently instructed not to violate such thorns but to hedge about these roses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus insigne specimen habes infra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035622Z">
        <seg>You have a prime example of this below the chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus mensis vigesimus dies beatæ Agneti de Monte Politiano&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt; virgini Dominicanæ a cælesti rore, ac diversis repente enatis floribus notissimæ sacer est, idemque Rosæ huic natalis fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203310Z">
        <seg>The twentieth day of this month is sacred to Blessed Agnes of Montepulciano&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;, the Dominican virgin, renowned for the heavenly dew and the various flowers that suddenly sprang up, and it was also the birthday of the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus mensis vigesimus dies beatæ Agneti de Monte Politiano&lt;s8&gt;&lt;a9&gt;d&lt;/a9&gt;&lt;/s8&gt; virgini Dominicanæ a cælesti rore, ac diversis repente enatis floribus notissimæ sacer est, idemque Rosæ huic natalis fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T193837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211758Z">
        <seg>The twentieth day of this month is sacred to Blessed Agnes of Montepulciano&lt;s8&gt;&lt;a9&gt;d&lt;/a9&gt;&lt;/s8&gt;, the Dominican virgin, renowned for the heavenly dew and the various flowers that suddenly sprang up, and it was also the birthday of the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujusmodi bestiolas ad Dei laudes pro suo modulo personandas sic incitabat, &amp; mirabiliter regebat, ut Rosa prope attigisse imperium statui innocentiæ reservatum, &amp; in ea solitudine velut in paradiso morari crederetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041631Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041631Z">
        <seg>He so encouraged these little creatures to sing the praises of God in their own way and guided them so wondrously that Rosa was believed to have nearly attained the realm reserved for innocence and to be dwelling in that solitude as if in paradise.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Humiliter replicuit modesta Filia: “Utique si ad mortalem opem, si ad hominum manus respicerem, faterer ultro, impossibilia esse, quæ tracto;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062331Z">
        <seg>The modest Daughter humbly replied: "Indeed, if I were to rely on mortal aid, if I were to look to human hands, I would freely admit that what I undertake is impossible."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc cibum paravit in dulcedine sua pauperi Deus, attamen recolendum, cui dixerit: “Cibus sum grandium, cresce, &amp; manducabis me”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055637Z">
        <seg>God prepared this food in His sweetness for the poor, yet it is to be remembered, to whom He said: "I am the food of the great, grow, and you will eat me."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc forte colebat studiosius, quod odore ac nomine regius, Regi seculorum immortali &amp; invisibili aptius conveniret in tributum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051211Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051211Z">
        <seg>He was perhaps cultivating this one more diligently because, by its fragrance and name, it was more fittingly suited as an offering to the immortal and invisible King of ages.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc itaque ambire Virgo, hortari, stimulare verbis ardentissimis, obsecrare serio, nil metueret, maturaret iter, subveniret pereuntibus, Deo acceptius obsequium præstari non posse, apostolici pectoris &amp; dignitatis id esse munus, cui divina adsistentia deesfe nequeat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060412Z">
        <seg>Therefore, to urge this Virgin to go forth, to exhort, to spur on with the most ardent words, to beseech earnestly that she fear nothing, hasten her journey, come to the aid of the perishing—that no more acceptable obedience could be offered to God, that it is the duty of the apostolic heart and dignity, to which divine assistance can in no way be lacking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc mater Francisca de Leon plena fide ad Rosæ tumulum stitit, scrobi, unde eruebantur salutiferi pulveres, alternos immittere fecit pedes, deinde etiam domi singulas ejus corpusculi juncturas eodem pulvere defricuit, sicque Virginis intercessioni commendatum in lectulo composuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035929Z">
        <seg>Mother Francisca de Leon, full of faith, stood at the tomb of Rose, had her feet take turns stepping into the pit from which the salutary powders were dug, and then at home she rubbed every joint of her little body with the same powder, and thus, entrusted to the Virgin's intercession, laid her in her little bed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc miser corripuit, laceravit, ac procul in spurcitiarum sentinam abjecit, rem magnam se præstitisse ratus, quod tantæ Amazoni quidquam saltem armorum furto ignobili sublegisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052007Z">
        <seg>The wretch seized him, tore him apart, and cast him far away into a sewer of filth, believing he had done a great thing by having at least stolen some weapons for such an Amazon in a base theft.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc revelationis modum ut plane defœcatum sensuque superiorem, ita ineffabilem fuisse talique objecto apprime dignum existimavit, &amp;, ut par erat, æstimavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031903Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031903Z">
        <seg>He considered this mode of revelation, as plainly perfected and of a higher order, to be ineffable and most worthy of such an object, and, as was fitting, he esteemed it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc successum habuit Rosæ prædictio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012042Z">
        <seg>Such was the success of Rosa's prediction.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc tamen Rosa &amp; hospitio frequenti æquanimiter excepit, &amp; cum invicta tolerantiæ virtute, pacifice uno communi lectulo conjacere fecit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052634Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052634Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, Rosa received him with frequent hospitality and equanimity, and with the invincible virtue of tolerance, peacefully made him lie down with her on the same bed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc versum jugiter ferebat in ore, hunc inter labores manuum submissa vocula suaviter cantillabat, hunc sedens, stans, ambulans devotissime iterabat, nec satiabatur frequenti illius repetitione, præsertim cum aliunde rescivit postea, eumdem versum beatæ Catharinæ Senensi perpetim in usu, ac deliciis fuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061730Z">
        <seg>She constantly had this verse on her lips; while working with her hands she sweetly hummed it under her breath; whether sitting, standing, or walking she devoutly repeated it, and she never grew tired of its frequent repetition, especially when she later learned that the same verse had been in the blessed Catherine of Siena's constant use and delight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc &amp; similia quamcumque aliam exanimassent, at minime Rosam, cui intus patientia pro chalybe erat, eoque amplius, quod ex hujuscemodi præduris afflictionum cautibus deprehendebat manifestius, se per ipsissimas B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052528Z">
        <seg>These and similar things would have stunned any other, but not Rosa, who had steel for patience within her, and moreover, because she perceived more clearly from such harsh trials of affliction that she was being led by the very hand of the Blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc Paulo, hæc Rosæ fornax divinitus obtigit, sed diverso igne.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045438Z">
        <seg>This oven was divinely granted to Paul, this one to Rosa, but by a different fire.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc Rosam (de qua memoranda audierat) illic infirmæ Marianæ servitio pie districtam conspicata, eidem cœpit devotius affici, &amp; captata affandi occasione, post mutuos ultro citroque urbanæ comitatis ritus, impense Virginem rogavit, sui meminisse ut velit in quotidianis ad Deum precibus, sanctisque exercitiis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062911Z">
        <seg>She saw Rosa (of whom she had heard) there, piously occupied in the service of the infirm Mariana, and began to feel a more devoted affection for her. Seizing the opportunity to speak, and after the mutual courtesies of urban courtesy had been exchanged, she earnestly begged the Virgin to remember her in her daily prayers to God and in her holy exercises.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc a promontorio Africæ (Hesperium olim, nunc Caput viride&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; appellatur) ab interiori Lybia&lt;a1&gt;c&lt;/a1&gt; per mare Atlanticum abvecta in Indias, primum quidem in isthmo Panamæ&lt;a2&gt;d&lt;/a2&gt; servierat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012848Z">
        <seg>These, carried from the promontory of Africa (formerly called Hesperides, now called Cape Verde) across the Atlantic Ocean from inland Libya, had first served at the Isthmus of Panama.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc ad portum Gallao&lt;a0&gt;l&lt;/a0&gt; marito cohabitans, ipsa die exsequiarum defunctæ Rosæ, anno MDCXVII cœperat angi sævis puerperii doloribus, quorum vi proles in utero transversim acta, dum neque retineri neque emitti potest, se una cum matre præsentissimæ mortis laqueo implicabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035107Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035107Z">
        <seg>This woman, living with her husband at the port of Gallao, on the very day of the funeral for the late Rosa, in the year 1617, had begun to suffer severe labor pains, by the force of which the child in the womb, turned transversely, while it could neither be retained nor delivered, entangled itself with its mother in the snare of imminent death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc altero oculorum (cui nebecula insederat) omnino cæca, altero obscure, vix modicum nictitando videbat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034630Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034630Z">
        <seg>She was completely blind in one eye (in which a film had settled), and in the other, though dimly, she could barely see by barely blinking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc de doctore Castillo tunc superstite, post alios juridice testatus est Petrus de Ortega Sotomayor, ex Limensi cathedratico primario, canonico magistrali, archidiacono metropolitano, &amp; sancti Officii consultore episcopus Truxilli&lt;a1&gt;b&lt;/a1&gt;, dein Arequipæ,&lt;a2&gt;c&lt;/a2&gt;, denique Cusci&lt;a3&gt;d&lt;/a3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010421Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010421Z">
        <seg>These things about Doctor Castillo, then still living, were legally attested by Peter of Ortega Sotomayor, bishop of Trujillo&lt;a1&gt;b&lt;/a1&gt;, later of Arequipa&lt;a2&gt;c&lt;/a2&gt;, a former first professor at the University of Lima, a master canon, metropolitan archdeacon, and consultant to the Holy Office. and finally of Cuzco&lt;a3&gt;d&lt;/a3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc de doctore Castillo tunc superstite, post alios juridice testatus est Petrus de Ortega Sotomayor, ex Limensi cathedratico primario, canonico magistrali, archidiacono metropolitano, &amp; sancti Officii consultore episcopus Truxilli&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;b&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, dein Arequipæ,&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;c&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt;, denique Cusci&lt;s9&gt;&lt;a10&gt;d&lt;/a10&gt;&lt;/s9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050110Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050110Z">
        <seg>These things about Doctor Castillo, then still living, were then legally attested by Peter of Ortega Sotomayor, from Lima, a primarius professor, master canon, metropolitan archdeacon, and consultant to the Holy Office, bishop of Trujillo&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;b&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, later of Arequipa,&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;c&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt;, and finally of Cuzco&lt;s9&gt;&lt;a10&gt;d&lt;/a10&gt;&lt;/s9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc est mera conjectura nostra, cui certiorem hujus vocis significationem libenter præferemus.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055541Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055541Z">
        <seg>This is merely our conjecture, to which we would gladly prefer a more certain meaning of this word.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc illustris visio, tempestive Rosam præparavit ad ultimos illos extremæ suæ paralysis&lt;a0&gt;i&lt;/a0&gt; dolores, qui præmaturam ei mortem intulerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010840Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010840Z">
        <seg>This illustrious vision timely prepared Rosa for the final pains of her extreme paralysis, which brought about her untimely death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc illustris visio, tempestive Rosam præparavit ad ultimos illos extremæ suæ paralysis&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;i&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; dolores, qui præmaturam ei mortem intulerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052857Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052857Z">
        <seg>This illustrious vision timely prepared Rosa for the final pains of her extreme paralysis, which brought about her untimely death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc in octavum mensem gravida sensit manifestis indiciis, fœtum in utero suo immobilem disiisse vivere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035036Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035036Z">
        <seg>She, eight months pregnant, sensed by clear signs that the fetus in her womb, having become motionless, was no longer alive.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc inter sacra cum Rosa colloquia, viso tot muscarum famelico assultu, nonnihil extimuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043922Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043922Z">
        <seg>These sacred conversations with Rosa, seeing the hungry assault of so many flies, were somewhat frightened.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc lusum aut jocum arbitrata, subrisit, interrogans, qua manu hos arripere velit, sat sciens in dextra sic tumere digitos, ut nullis forficum ansulis immitti queant, sicque velut per ludum, angustiores, quos reperire potuit, forfices porrexit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055405Z">
        <seg>Thinking it a game or a joke, she smiled and asked which hand he wanted to use to grab them, well aware that her fingers on her right hand were so swollen that they couldn't fit into any scissor handles, and so, as if in play, she offered the smallest pair of scissors she could find.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc narrans inardescebat, nec cohibere se poterat, quin fortius vocem attolleret in parænesim: “Eia (inquiebat) o mater, æstimato hunc cælestem parvulum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054128Z">
        <seg>As she spoke these words she grew animated and could not restrain herself from raising her voice more forcefully in her exhortation: "Come now, O mother, esteem this heavenly little one;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc obiter de Rosea imagine, nunc ad Rosam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054311Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054311Z">
        <seg>This, by the way, about the Rosea image; now to the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc omnia inter illustriores a Deo sibi collatos favores recensebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042056Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042056Z">
        <seg>He counted all these among the more illustrious favors bestowed upon him by God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc ordine aptissimo in quindecim decades secrevit, unicuique decadi versum subjecit: &lt;b1&gt;Gloria Patri &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010213Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010213Z">
        <seg>He arranged these in the most perfect order into fifteen decades, and placed the verse under each decade: Glory be to the Father, etc.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc ordine aptissimo in quindecim decades secrevit, unicuique decadi versum subjecit: &lt;b5&gt;Gloria Patri &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044948Z">
        <seg>He arranged these in the most perfect order into fifteen decades, and placed the verse under each decade: Glory be to the Father, etc.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc recole, hæc pœnali strato tuo in trutina oppone, &amp; videbitur tibi sub charitatis dictamine lectulus noster floridus”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042657Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042657Z">
        <seg>Remember these things, weigh them against your penitential cloak on the scales, and you will see our bed, under the dictate of charity, appear in bloom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc sunt subsidia, quæ Sponsus divinus parentibus ipsius sub specie cælestis Lapicidæ, huic novellæ Sponsæ inter paraphernalia, &amp; antidora se ministraturum olim pollicitus erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042607Z">
        <seg>These are the gifts that the divine Spouse once promised to provide to his parents, in the guise of a heavenly Lapicida, among the paraphernalia and antidora for this new bride.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc ut Rosæ innotuerunt, vidisses flammigerum ejus zelum tot succendi affectibus, quot rerum species in diversa sensum abripiebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053421Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053421Z">
        <seg>When these things became known to Rosa, you would have seen her zeal blazing with as many affections as the various objects of sense carried her off in different directions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc verba, quemadmodum illi fuerant in infantia prima orandi formula, ita his ipsis in suæ infantilis innocentiæ testimonium decedens, Limæ ad limen supernæ patriæ evolavit, anno ætatis suæ trigesimo secundo, mense quinto vix inchoato.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042908Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042908Z">
        <seg>These words, which had been for him in early infancy the first formula of prayer, thus, as a testimony of his own infantile innocence, carried him to the threshold of the heavenly homeland, in the thirty-second year of his age, when the fifth month had scarcely begun.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc vultu tam sereno ac gratioso, tranquillitate tam secura &amp; hilari proferebat, quasi jam in paradisi vestibulo expectaret cum lampade, dum media nocte clamor fieret: Ecce Sponsus venit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030226Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030226Z">
        <seg>She spoke with such serene and gracious countenance, with such calm and cheerful composure, as though she were already waiting at the threshold of paradise with a lamp, while at midnight a cry rang out: Behold, the Bridegroom comes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc, ubi Rosam meditationi implicatam reliquerat, clam sese proripuit ad matrem suam, in vicino conclavi laborantem: inde post horæ spatium erepsit, visura num Rosa ab oratione surrexisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051140Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051140Z">
        <seg>She, having left Rosa absorbed in meditation, slipped quietly to her mother, who was working in the adjoining room; after an hour she crept back to see if Rosa had risen from her prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hærebat in illo plexili senticeto, quod Christi verticem cruente insederat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042049Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042049Z">
        <seg>It clung to that thorny crown which had crimsonly sat upon Christ's head.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hærebat parens mirata novitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034758Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034758Z">
        <seg>The parent stood amazed at the novelty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæsit in spectaculo vere mellifluo tota domus attonita, eoque magis, quod novo prodigio mel cælitus datum integris octo mensibus toti familiæ sat fuit in usum quotidianum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062235Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062235Z">
        <seg>The entire household stood amazed at the truly wondrous spectacle, and all the more so because, by a new miracle, the honey that had been given from heaven lasted the whole family for eight full months for their daily use.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>III relatum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030306Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030306Z">
        <seg>The third has been reported.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>IV) superne advolans insedit Rosæ, notatum est, quod aliquamdiu per sinistrum Virginis latus reptando oberrabat, donec recta e regione cordis constitisset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044114Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044114Z">
        <seg>IV) Flying down from above, it settled on Rose. It was noted that for a while it wandered, crawling along the left side of the Virgin, until it came to a stop directly opposite the heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>IX) adeo quod Gundisalvi quæstoris uxor, quæ Virginis arcana præ aliis noverat, rem in familiare adagium converterit, dicere solita: “Numquid iterum empyreus Lapicida tuo monitu excussit loculos”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062312Z">
        <seg>IX) so much so that Gundisalvi the quaestor's wife, who knew the Virgin's secrets better than anyone else, turned the matter into a familiar proverb, saying: "Has the empyrean, at your bidding, once again rattled your Lapicida's little casket?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ibi altaris ornandi curam, ibi totius nitoris œconomiam sibi devote assumpserat, ibi quotidianum sibi cum Deipara ex sedulo ministerio commercium erat, ac mutua conversatio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051304Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051304Z">
        <seg>There she had devoutly taken upon herself the care of adorning the altar, the management of all its splendor; there she maintained a daily communion with the Mother of God through diligent service and mutual conversation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ibidem (longo post tempore) Æthiopissa olitrix, multis horis, eventu ancipiti, ac pene complorato laborabat in partu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035747Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035747Z">
        <seg>There, long afterward, an Ethiopian midwife labored for many hours with a precarious outcome and nearly collapsed while assisting with the birth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Icta responso genitrix, permisit Filiæ, quantum vellet latere domi, tantum ne amplius sibi oculos periculoso hoc defrictu obpiperaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043435Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043435Z">
        <seg>Stunned by the reply, the mother allowed her daughter to stay at home as long as she wished, only on the condition that she would no longer tear her eyes with this dangerous rubbing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Id itaque faciendum erit in supplemento Operis nostri ad diem 27 Aprilis, quo nunc in Martyrologio Romano annuntiatur.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T000329Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035216Z">
        <seg>This must therefore be done in the supplement to our work on April 27, the day on which he is now commemorated in the Roman Martyrology.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Id porro erat, quod Christus beatam Catharinam Senensem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052249Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052249Z">
        <seg>That, moreover, was what Christ did to the blessed Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idcirco lamella utendum fuit, eaque argentea, quo &amp; solidius adjaceret vulnerandæ cuti, &amp; infixas spinulas tenacius retineret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042155Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042155Z">
        <seg>Therefore a blade had to be used, and a silver one at that, so that it would lie more solidly against the wounded skin and hold the embedded little spines more firmly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem Philippo Ægidio tertiana duplici molestissime aggravato præstitit chartacea Rosæ imago, sub ipso frigoris paroxysmo capiti trementis imposita.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040819Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040819Z">
        <seg>The same paper image of Rosa, most painfully affixed to Philip Aegidius, who was grievously afflicted by a double tertian fever, was placed upon his head during the very paroxysm of the chill.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem Rosæ jam sexennis patuit robur sub ejusdem chirurgi forcipe, cum sibi ex intimo nare eximendus fuit phaseolus&lt;a0&gt;m&lt;/a0&gt;, tertio demum tractu erutus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203037Z">
        <seg>The same was true for Rosa, now six years old, whose strength was tested under the same surgeon's forceps when a bean had to be removed from deep within her nose&lt;a0&gt;m&lt;/a0&gt;, finally extracted on the third attempt.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem Rosæ jam sexennis patuit robur sub ejusdem chirurgi forcipe, cum sibi ex intimo nare eximendus fuit phaseolus&lt;s0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;m&lt;/a1&gt;&lt;/s0&gt;, tertio demum tractu erutus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202845Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T202845Z">
        <seg>The same was true for Rosa, now six years old, whose strength was tested under the same surgeon's forceps when a bean had to be removed from deep within her nose, finally extracted on the third attempt.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem Rosæ jam sexennis patuit robur sub ejusdem chirurgi forcipe, cum sibi ex intimo nare eximendus fuit phaseolus&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;m&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, tertio demum tractu erutus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034922Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034922Z">
        <seg>The same was true for Rosa, now six years old, whose strength was tested under the same surgeon's forceps when a bean had to be removed from deep within her nose, finally extracted on the third attempt.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem S. Catharinæ Senensi cum Lapa matre certamen, idem stratagema fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042517Z">
        <seg>The same contest between St. Catherine of Siena and her mother Lapa, and the same stratagem.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem Sabbato repetebat, compassura Virgini Matri, cujus animam in morte Filii doloris gladius pervaserat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042210Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042210Z">
        <seg>She repeated it again on Saturday, in commemoration of the Virgin Mother, whose soul had been pierced by the sword of sorrow at the death of her Son.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem est, quod mare Meridionale &amp; Austrinum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035105Z">
        <seg>The same is true of the Southern and Australasian Sea.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem experiebatur in se minister sacrificantis, dum ex more scyphum ablutionis ei porrigebat, ut hoc etiam testimonio probaretur, hoc esse Sacramentum illud, quo Deus ipse venit ignem mittere in terram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042201Z">
        <seg>The same thing was experienced in the minister offering the sacrifice, as he, according to custom, handed him the chalice of the washing, so that this too might be proven by testimony to be that Sacrament by which God Himself came to cast fire on the earth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem hic de Sancta nostra refertur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051521Z">
        <seg>The same is reported here about our Saint.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem hoc deinceps admiratus est in methodo confitendi, qua Rosa utebatur, ea sermonis tum perspicuitate, tum proprietate ac circumspectione, ut sibi videretur Lorenzana non feminam audire, sed annosum utriusque theologiæ professorem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050640Z">
        <seg>He was likewise impressed thereafter by the method of confession used by Rosa, by the clarity, propriety, and circumspection of her speech, so that it seemed to him he was not hearing a woman, but an elderly professor of both theology.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem huic Virgini accidit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052423Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052423Z">
        <seg>The same thing happened to this virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem in ædibus D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051146Z">
        <seg>The same in the houses of D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem in ædibus D. Isabellæ Mexiæ contigisse, quæ vidit, retulit filia Isabellæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010601Z">
        <seg>The same thing happened in the house of Ms. Isabella of Mexico, which her daughter, who saw it, reported.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem matri, quæstori Gundisalvo, hujus consorti, aliisque accidisse constat, ne dubitari posset de innocentis Rosæ in domesticos sibi culices admirando imperio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043915Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043915Z">
        <seg>It is likewise established that the same thing happened to the mother, to the quaestor Gundisalvus, to his wife, and to others, so that there could be no doubt about the innocent Rose's admirable authority over the household flies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem me stimulus urgebat impetuosissime ad prædicandam divinæ gratiæ venustatem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052832Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052832Z">
        <seg>The same stimulus urged me most impetuously to preach the beauty of divine grace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem momentum fuit, quo frustulum oculos attigit, mobilesque &amp; sanos reliquit, neque vel stupor illis, vel dolor umquam rediit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034602Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034602Z">
        <seg>It was the same moment the splinter struck the eyes, leaving them mobile and healthy, and neither stupor nor pain ever returned to them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem paulatim cum querula plebe censuere nobiles, ac primores;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031342Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031342Z">
        <seg>The nobles and leading men gradually came to agree with the complaining commoners.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem pavoris genius Rosam filiam ex materna crasi&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; velut hæreditarius insederat, nec tamen ideo Puella orationi dedita solitudinem ac loca obscuriora devitabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011757Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011757Z">
        <seg>The same spirit of terror had, as if by heredity, taken up residence in Rosa, the daughter, through her mother's temperament; yet the girl, devoted to prayer, did not avoid solitude and darker places.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem pavoris genius Rosam filiam ex materna crasi&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; velut hæreditarius insederat, nec tamen ideo Puella orationi dedita solitudinem ac loca obscuriora devitabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062040Z">
        <seg>The same spirit of terror had, as if by inheritance, taken up residence in Rosa, the daughter born of her mother's blood, yet the girl, devoted to prayer, did not shun solitude or darker places.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem probavere demum confessarii, urserunt viri graves, &amp; spirituum magisterio spectabiles, quorum concordi judicio ut suum Rosa humiliter submitteret, conscia &amp; annuente avia, successum tentavit per germanum fratrem, divino interim (ut supra) arbitrio se remittens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040103Z">
        <seg>The confessors themselves at last approved it; men of standing and distinguished in the spiritual life urged it on, and, with the grandmother aware of it and consenting, Rosa humbly submitted to their unanimous judgment and sought success through her brother, meanwhile entrusting herself to the divine will (as above).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem quæstoris Gundisalvi, idem confessariorum erat judicium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062759Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062759Z">
        <seg>The same was the judgment of the quæstor Gundisalvus and of the confessors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem reliquæ Gundisalvi familiæ, idem extraneis, quotquot aderant, intimæ ac secretæ jucunditatis inhæsit stimulus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030310Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030310Z">
        <seg>The same stimulus of intimate and secret delight seized the rest of Gundisalvus's family and all the outsiders who were present.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem videbatur circa virginem Rosam accurrentium sensus: nam ab ara maxima subito convertebantur ad propinquum Virginis sepulchrum, velut ad secundam aram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031621Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031621Z">
        <seg>The same seemed to be the case with the feelings of those who flocked to the Virgin Rose: for they were suddenly diverted from the high altar to the Virgin's nearby tomb, as if to a second altar.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem viderunt quotquot promiscue aderant in sacello;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043544Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043544Z">
        <seg>All who were present in the chapel saw the same thing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idque crebrius liquenti gaudio replicans, simul decenti osculo attonitam, minimeque repugnantem dissuaviabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062935Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062935Z">
        <seg>And, repeatedly echoing it with overflowing joy, he at the same time, with a fitting kiss, stunned her and, without the slightest resistance, won her over.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur Peruani regni excellentissimus prorex armatæ suæ custodiæ prætoriam cohortem eo celeriter destinavit, portisque ædium adstare jussit, ne indigesta tot agminum collisio exardesceret in tumultum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030427Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030427Z">
        <seg>Therefore the most illustrious Viceroy of the Kingdom of Peru swiftly dispatched the Praetorian cohort of his armed guard and ordered it to stand at the gates of the buildings, lest the unordered clash of so many troops ignite into a riot.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur abbatissa Isabella de la Fuente, ad cæli præsidia confugiendum rata, in unius Rosæ thaumaturgis favoribus spem fixit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035446Z">
        <seg>Therefore, Abbess Isabella de la Fuente, deeming it right to flee to the heavenly defenses, fixed her hope on the favors of a single miraculous Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur ad securiores pluribusque valvis disclusos recessus infra novitiorum adyta, comitante cum paucis archipræsule, transportandum fuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030725Z">
        <seg>Therefore, he had to be transported to a safer, more spacious recess beneath the novices' cells, accompanied by a few archpriests;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur addenda videbantur tum duriora tum stabiliora in equulei supplementum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042545Z">
        <seg>Therefore, additions seemed to be both tougher and more stable for the stirrup supplement.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur caveto, ne tuæ facundiæ dives talentum in plausibilibus acutorum conceptuum flosculis inutiliter consumas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060552Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060552Z">
        <seg>Therefore, be careful not to waste the rich talent of your eloquence on flowery, clever notions that are merely pleasing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur confessario suo eminus conspecto, festina accurrit, cor effudit, multisque lachrymis obtinuit accelerari gratiam, qua confestim adhuc illamet die in numerum Sororum Tertiariarum adlegeretur, assumpto nomine Ludovicæ de S. Maria.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032925Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032925Z">
        <seg>Thus, upon catching sight of her confessor from a distance, she hurried over, poured out her heart, and with many tears obtained the favor of being admitted that very day into the number of the Tertiaries, taking the name of Louise of St. Mary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur crebrius accitis confessariis, tenerrimam conscientiam exomologesi pluries iterata quam diligentissime expiavit, semel quidem generaliter de tota vita sua;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025913Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025913Z">
        <seg>Thus, by frequently calling for confessors, he most diligently purged his most tender conscience through the repeatedly repeated act of confession, once indeed in a general confession of his entire life;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur deputati inspectores Joannes de Texeda, &amp; Joannes de Vega doctores medici, Joannes de Valenzuela &amp; Bartholomæus de Tivico secretarii cum Aloysio de Molina chirurgo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031645Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031645Z">
        <seg>Therefore, the deputies and inspectors John of Texeda and John of Vega, doctors of medicine; John of Valenzuela and Bartholomew of Tivico, secretaries; together with Aloysius of Molina, surgeon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur hujus arreptam imaginem sinistro tempori bis terve induxit, mox in cervical recidens obdormivit, ac media nocte evigilans, omnem fugisse dolorem comperit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040735Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040735Z">
        <seg>Thus he impressed this fleeting image upon his left temple two or three times; soon, sinking back into his pillow, he fell asleep, and when he awoke at midnight, he found that all pain had fled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur ipso die, quo Rosa sepulta fuit, sub vesperam a religioso germano fratre adjutus, irrepsit in ædem capitularem, oravit ad sepulchrum Virginis, imploravit opem, subitoque obtinuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033313Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033313Z">
        <seg>Thus, on the very day that Rosa was buried, at dusk, aided by a religious brother, he crept into the chapter house, prayed at the Virgin's tomb, implored help, and suddenly received it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur matrem adiit, prætexens aliquot sibi ex tenui carbaso pretiosis collaribus opus esse, hæc se velle a Rosa confici, quam constabat eo opificio victum parentibus lucrari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060614Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060614Z">
        <seg>So she went to her mother, pretending that she needed some precious collars made from a thin piece of cloth, saying she wanted them made by Rosa, who was known to earn her parents' living by that craft.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur omnibus rite ordinatis, die qua supra, domesticus religiosorum cœtus frequentissima undique ceterorum regularium caterva stipatus, a choro recta processit ad ædem capitularem, præeunte crucis vexillo cum acolythorum quaternione, binisque thuribulis, subsequente Provinciali in sacra diploïde&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; cum sacris ministris, itidem pretiose tunicatis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031435Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031435Z">
        <seg>Thus, with everything duly arranged, on the day above mentioned, the domestic assembly of the religious, crowded on every side by the most numerous throng of the other regulars, proceeded in a straight line from the choir to the chapter house, the standard of the cross preceding with a quaternion of acolytes and two thuribles, the Provincial following in the sacred diptych with the sacred ministers, also vested in precious tunics.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur quoties domi coquendis panibus accendebatur clibanus, prensabat occasionem Rosa, nudatisque plantis calcabat immota summum fornacis orificium, ubi vis ardoris vehementior erumpebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041919Z">
        <seg>So whenever the oven was fired up at home to bake bread, Rosa would seize the opportunity, and barefoot she would step onto the still-warm top of the oven opening, where the fiercest heat burst forth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur respexit denuo ad pristina dolorum supplementa, jamque pœnoso tæniarum attractui etiam hoc addebat, ut interdum illiso pugno coronam tunderet, quo altius in caput penetraret: neque enim diligens lima sic detriverat aculeos, ut suo non fungerentur cruentandi officio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042331Z">
        <seg>Thus he once again looked back to his former remedies for pain, and to the painful pulling of the bands he now added this: that he would sometimes strike the crown with his knuckles so that it would penetrate more deeply into his head. For the diligent file had not worn down the barbs so much that they could not perform their bloody work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur usque ad hanc subtilissimam divisionem carnis &amp; spiritus, elegerat conformari Sponso crucifixo, quatenus ille nec gloriam animæ in suum adhuc mortale corpus, nec gaudia partis superioris in inferiorem derivari permisit, ne passionem impediret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041941Z">
        <seg>Thus, down to this most subtle division of flesh and spirit, he had chosen to conform himself to the crucified Spouse, in that he permitted neither the glory of the soul to flow into his still mortal body, nor the joys of the higher part to descend into the lower, lest he impede the Passion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur ut erat immobilis, ad S. Rosarii clementissimam Reginam conversa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040131Z">
        <seg>Therefore, being motionless, she turned to the most merciful Queen of the Holy Rosary;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur vade, ancilla, jussa exequere”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051707Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051707Z">
        <seg>"Therefore, go, servant girl, and carry out the command."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ignis autem volatilis in auctiore Cangii Glossario vocatur&lt;/e1&gt; impetigo, &lt;e2&gt;vel&lt;/e2&gt; lichen, &lt;e3&gt;qui est morbus ab una parte corporis ad aliam volans, ut apud medicos jam sæpe citatos distinctius legi potest, &amp; de quo clarior in textu expositio postea recurret&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034444Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034444Z">
        <seg>However, the volatile fire in the more extensive glossary of Cangius is called impetigo, or lichen, which is a disease that flies from one part of the body to another, as can be read more distinctly in the now often-cited physicians, and a clearer exposition of which will recur later in the text.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illa advertens, ratione, non imperio secum agi, suas vicissim rationes humiliter opposuit, demum in hoc utrimque conventum est, quod poscenti sua redderetur corona, sed mitigata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042322Z">
        <seg>She, perceiving that reason, not force, was being used with her, humbly set forth her own reasons in return; finally, it was agreed on both sides that she would be restored to her crown, but with its powers diminished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illa brevi labiorum motu rem Deo commendavit, mox magna fide arcam panariam aperiens, plenam azymis ac saporosis panibus reperit, testante illo rum figura peregrina, a domestico panificio non prodiisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042549Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042549Z">
        <seg>With a brief movement of her lips she commended the matter to God; soon, with great faith, she opened the bread chest and found it full of unleavened and flavorful loaves, as evidenced by its strange shape, which did not come from the household baker.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illa mysterium celans, “Hoc, (inquit) quod vides me agere”;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062520Z">
        <seg>Hiding the mystery, she said, "This, (she said) that you see me doing";</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illa sciens filiam Rosam, pro ægritudinum frequentia ac munditiei studio, pluribus ex eodem lino indigere, seorsim huic notabilem illius telæ partem (XXXVI ulnæ erant) attribuit, ut ipsamet sibi pro arbitratu ad usus proprios sudaria, pepla, fascias, gremialia, vela, mantilla, tegmina, vittas, cervicalia, mappas, omnemque necessariam candidi nitoris supellectilem formaret, ac sueret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061032Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061032Z">
        <seg>Knowing that her daughter Rosa, because of her frequent illnesses and her love of cleanliness, needed more than one of the same linen, she set aside for her a notable portion of that cloth (it was thirty-six ells), so that she herself could make handkerchiefs for her own use as she saw fit, handkerchiefs, peplums, bandages, aprons, sails, mantillas, coverings, ribbons, collars, napkins, and all other necessary articles of pure whiteness, and to sew them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illam quot osculis Rosa dissuaviarit, quot lachrymis humectarit, quot ignitis suspiriis adusserit, quam tenacibus ulnis ad livorem usque sibi interdiu noctuque adstrinxerit, quam crebra &amp; supplici adoratione coluerit, non omnino domesticos latuit, qui interdum per rimulas insciam observabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054828Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054828Z">
        <seg>How many kisses Rosa had sweetened it with, how many tears she had moistened it with, how many fiery sighs she had scorched it with, how tightly she had pressed it to herself day and night until it was bruised, how often and with what suppliant devotion she had worshiped it—this was not entirely hidden from the household servants, who at times watched her unwittingly through the keyhole.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ille Victricis animositate confusus, rabiem, qua puellæ nocere non poterat, in ejusdem libellos spirituales turpissime effudit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052001Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052001Z">
        <seg>Confused by Victrix's spirit, he poured out his rage, which could not harm the girl, in the most disgraceful manner upon her spiritual writings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ille de fructu nil dubitans, laborem difficultatemque majorem credidit, quam Soror aut prædicabat, aut credi vellet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045103Z">
        <seg>He, not doubting the fruit, believed the labor and difficulty to be greater than the Sister either preached or would have it believed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ille ex adverso contendebat, rem fore certissimam, nec aliud sibi accidere posse felicius, quam si paulo post celebratum Missæ sacrificium juberetur spiritum suo reddere Creatori;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012234Z">
        <seg>He, on the other hand, contended that the matter was most certain, and that nothing could happen to him more fortunate than if, shortly after the sacrifice of the Mass had been celebrated, he were commanded to return his spirit to its Creator.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ille, arrepto circino, justam anuli formam delineavit in charta, debitæque proportionis addidit palam figuram circuli, cui Virgo parvulum Jesum optarat insculpi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044442Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044442Z">
        <seg>He, seizing a compass, drew a perfect ring shape on the paper, and to the proper proportion added the figure of a circle, on which the Virgin had wished the little Jesus to be engraved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illic plexili ramorum, palmitum, virgultorum circumductu, modicum inclusit spatium, ac superne frondosis arcubus affabre concameravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043246Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043246Z">
        <seg>There, by weaving a lattice of branches, palm fronds, and twigs, he enclosed a small space and skillfully arched it above with leafy curves.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illic pulverem e Rosæ sepulchro, dilutum aqua bibendum porrexit solicita custos, &amp; subito, discussa phrenesi, resipuit Raphaëla, cibum petiit, liberisque faucibus alacriter manducavit, ac sine alio remedio integerrime convaluit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035502Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035502Z">
        <seg>There, the anxious guardian offered her a drink of water mixed with dust from the Rose's tomb, and suddenly, the fever broken, Raphaela recovered her senses. She asked for food, ate heartily, and recovered completely without any other remedy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illic singuli successive manu immissa explorarunt cavam modicæ scrobis profunditatem, ex qua nonnisi quatuor aut quinque pondo terræ videbantur eruta aut asportata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031701Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031701Z">
        <seg>There, one by one, they explored by hand the depth of a small hollow, from which no more than four or five pounds of earth appeared to have been dug up or carried away.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illic volebat suum hoc amoris pignus tantisper cum Sponso mortuo tumulari, ut cum eodem resurgente pariter rediret ad lucem: sic protestabatur amabili Sponso, se nec illo defuncto solutam esse a lege viri, cum quo &amp; mortis thalamum cupiebat sortiri communem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044507Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044507Z">
        <seg>There she wished to bury her pledge of love with her dead Spouse for a time, so that when he rose again she might return to the light along with him: thus she declared to her beloved Spouse that she was not freed from the law of the husband even by his death, since she longed to share with him a common marriage bed even in death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illico cessavit cruciatus, quiete obdormiit Petrus, ac mane evigilans, advertit inutili brachio rediisse vires, quæ, nullo alio adhibito remedio, sic crevere, ut intra dies paucissimos perfecte convaluerit, nullumque inter sinistri ac dexteri brachii agilitatem senserit residuum discrimen.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033719Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033719Z">
        <seg>The torment ceased at once, Peter fell asleep peacefully, and upon waking in the morning he noticed that strength had returned to his useless arm, which, with no other remedy applied, had grown so much that within a few days he had made a full recovery, and he felt no remaining difference in the agility of his left and right arms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illum stimulus carnis subjecit colaphis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045441Z">
        <seg>He subjected that fleshly stimulus to slaps.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Immarcescibilem fateri oportet Rosam, quam ejuscemodi spineta non suffocant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050733Z">
        <seg>One must declare the Rose imperishable, for thorns of that sort do not suffocate it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Impendebat utrique necessitas proficiscendi &amp; relinquendi parvulos in summa egestate.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043113Z">
        <seg>Both were compelled to depart, leaving their little ones in utter destitution.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Imperialis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043621Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043621Z">
        <seg>Imperial</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Implexas alibi dum trahit ille moras.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051006Z">
        <seg>He drags out delays elsewhere while she weaves intricate patterns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Imprimis otiosas in ecclesia fabulas (nobilitatis rusticum malum) adeo detestabatur, ut, quamquam timida, verborumque parcissima, continere se non posset, quin loquaces, ob loci reverentiam, debitæ urbanitatis admoneret, ea tamen moderatione, humilitate, prudentia, ut obsecrare potius, quam corripere videretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053309Z">
        <seg>First of all, she detested idle tales in church (the rustic scourge of nobility) so much that, though timid and very sparing of words, she could not help admonishing the talkative, out of respect for the place and for proper urbanity, yet with such moderation, humility, and prudence that she seemed more to beseech than to rebuke.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In Canta ex dira nervorum attractione triennium lecto decubuit, cruciatu immenso, gemitu nullo, nisi quod compassione acerbius, quam passione torquebatur ex illorum incommodo, quorum ministeriis carere non poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052606Z">
        <seg>In Canta, she lay in bed for three years, afflicted by a dreadful nerve-traction, enduring immense torment without a single groan—except that she was wrung out more by compassion for their plight than by her own suffering, since she could not do without their care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In Palama P. F. Joannes de Figueroa Ordinis Prædicatorum ex indigestis stomachi putredinibus lethali correptus febri, totius urbis medicis desperantibus, extrema migrantium sacramenta pie susceperat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040754Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040754Z">
        <seg>In Palama, P. John de Figueroa of the Order of Preachers, struck down by a fatal fever from putrid indigestion of the stomach, and with the city's physicians having despaired, had piously received the last sacraments of the dying.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In Rosariano beatissimæ Virginis sacello Rosa plane domestica evaserat, illic totos hærebat dies;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051257Z">
        <seg>In the chapel of the Most Blessed Virgin, the Rose had become thoroughly domestic, and there she lingered all day long.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In apoplexia vero &amp; morbo comitiali utraque, ut scribit Galenus lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033642Z">
        <seg>In apoplexy and epilepsy, however, in both, as Galen writes in his book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In cellulæ pariete oblongum fixerat clavum paulo altiorem, quique Rosæ verticem palmi fere interstitio superaret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043057Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043057Z">
        <seg>He had fixed a slightly taller nail oblong on the cell wall, which rose almost to the palm's tip.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In connubiali annulo, quem in tanti Sponsi mnemosynon cum ipsius infantis Jesu effigie elaborari mandaverat, fratri ejus, dum in eo delineando incumbit, quamvis tanti mysterii penitus ignaro, pro lemmate divino instinctu illa eadem Regis regum sponsalitia verba occurrerunt: &lt;b0&gt;Rosa cordis mei, tu mihi sponsa esto&lt;/b0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041650Z">
        <seg>While working on the wedding ring that he had commissioned to be made as a memento of the great Spouse, with an image of the infant Jesus on it, his brother—though completely ignorant of so great a mystery—by divine inspiration for its motto came upon those same betrothal words of the King of kings: "Rose of my heart, be thou my bride."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In domestico horto areolas discreverat sativis violis cura sua manuque excolendis, quas simul, ut maturuissent, fasciculatim collectas ancillæ dabat venum exponendas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035759Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035759Z">
        <seg>In the home garden, she had divided the beds for the violets she tended with her own hands, and when they were ripe she would gather them in bunches and give them to the maids to be sold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In eo sita est Panama celeberrimum emporium Americæ Meridionalis, estque urbs episcopalis sub archiepiscopo Limensi.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013037Z">
        <seg>Panama, the most famous emporium of South America, is located there, and it is a bishop's city under the Archbishop of Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In hac celebritate nemo erat, quem sub virginei cadaveris præsentia, recens gestorum memoria medullitus non compungeret: multis lachrymæ devotionis fluebant: nomen ipsum Rosæ ab omnibus aperto reverentiæ signo recipiebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043013Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043013Z">
        <seg>At this celebration there was no one who, in the presence of the virgin's corpse and with the memory of her recent deeds fresh in his mind, was not deeply moved to the marrow; many tears of devotion flowed; and the very name of Rose was received by all with an open sign of reverence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In hac felici eremo ascensiones in corde suo disponebat, &amp; supra se elevata videbatur potius extra corpus, quam in corpore degere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041612Z">
        <seg>In this happy wilderness she arranged her ascensions in her heart, and, lifted up above herself, she seemed rather outside the body than to dwell in it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In hebdomadarum spatia protrahebat inediam, quæque in puerilibus annis insignem exercuerat abstinentiam, monialis postea eam sibimet jejunandi legem indixit, quæ plane supra naturam esset: nam integras quadragesimas transigebat sine pane, dietim quinis dumtaxat aurei mali granulis in memoriam quinque vulnerum Christi sese reficiebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041129Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041129Z">
        <seg>She would stretch her fasting out for weeks, and in her childhood years had practiced remarkable abstinence. Later, as a nun, she imposed upon herself a rule of fasting that was plainly beyond nature: for she would spend entire Lents without bread, refueling herself each day with only five grains of a golden apple in memory of Christ's five wounds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In his Rosæ eximie charus erat Ludovici Granatensis codex, quo ad meditationum digerendos articulos diligentius utebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052004Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052004Z">
        <seg>Among his roses, the codex of Louis of Granada was especially dear to him, which he used more diligently for arranging the articles of his meditations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In his affectu tenerrimo recolebat singula Dei in se beneficia, mirabatur ejus erga se dignationem, adorabat munificam prodigamque tot donorum, gratiarum, charismatum liberalitatem: &amp; sane habebat quotidie nova, quæ ex pugillaribus attentissimæ gratitudinis recitaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044923Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044923Z">
        <seg>In that most tender affection she recalled each of God's benefits toward her, marveled at His condescension toward her, and adored the munificent, prodigious generosity of so many gifts, graces, and charisms; and indeed she had new ones every day, which she would recite from the little book of her most attentive gratitude.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In his biviis, plusquam humanæ artis fuit, incolumi utrimque obedientia neque a Sponsi ductu vel latum unguem deflexisse, nec tamen abivisse umquam a maternis præceptis in Sponsi sequela.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035257Z">
        <seg>At these crossroads, it was more than human skill to remain obedient on both sides, never to have deviated so much as a fingernail from the Groom's guidance, yet never to have strayed from her mother's precepts in following the Groom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In his eminebant Ludovici Granatensis erudita Opera&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; de oratione ac meditatione, quibus assidue evolvendis, revolvendisque, Rosa hebdomadam diviserat, variisque, colorum signaculis, quid sibi quoque die legendum, distinxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010232Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010232Z">
        <seg>Among these stood out the learned Works of Louis of Granada on prayer and meditation, which Rosa had divided into sections for constant development and review, and had marked with various colored notations to show what she was to read each day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In his eminebant Ludovici Granatensis erudita Opera&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; de oratione ac meditatione, quibus assidue evolvendis, revolvendisque, Rosa hebdomadam diviserat, variisque, colorum signaculis, quid sibi quoque die legendum, distinxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045112Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045112Z">
        <seg>Among these stood out the learned Works of Louis of Granada on prayer and meditation, which Rosa had divided the week for, and to which she had assigned, by various colored markers, what she was to read each day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In his nexibus clam dolorosis meditabunda, vinctum in horto Getsemani Sponsum comitabatur ad ædes Caïphæ, Annæ, &amp; Pilati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041817Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041817Z">
        <seg>Meditating on these secretly painful bonds, she accompanied the bound Spouse from the Garden of Gethsemane to the houses of Caiaphas, Annas, and Pilate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In hoc Opere pag. 143 lego, remedium ab experto medico compositum &amp; inventum&lt;/e2&gt; compositionem magistralem &lt;e3&gt;nominari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034454Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034454Z">
        <seg>In this work, on page 143, I read that a remedy composed and invented by an experienced physician is called a masterful composition.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In hujus amœnissimo centro comparebat Rosa amicta habitu S. Patris sui Dominici mirabiliter candente ac radioso, cujus incomparabilem decorem protestabatur se verbis assequi non posse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031909Z">
        <seg>In the most delightful center of this scene appeared Rosa, clad in the habit of her holy Father Dominic, which shone and radiated wonderfully, whose incomparable beauty she declared she could not express in words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In hujus textura sumptuosa Dominicarum orationum, &amp; angelicarum Salutationum numerum Virgo non jam centuriis (ut supra) sed chiliadibus diffiniebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054645Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054645Z">
        <seg>In this sumptuous texture of Dominican prayers and angelic salutations, the Virgin defined the number not by hundreds (as above) but by thousands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In hujus tugurioli recessu cum sola erat, aut a nemine se observari putabat, tenerius simul ac liberius prosa, versu, colloquiis, &amp; cantu in solemnia quæque seraphici amoris &amp; laudes erumpebat eo fervore &amp; impetu, ut audientium corda ad charitatem &amp; compunctionem ignara commoveret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041634Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041634Z">
        <seg>In the seclusion of this little hut, when she was alone or believed herself unseen by anyone, she would burst forth in a more tender and free prose, verse, conversation, and song into every solemn act of seraphic love and praise with such fervor and impetus that she moved the hearts of her listeners to charity and contrition, though they had been unaware.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In hunc usum procul ab agro Panamæ navigiis advecta gleba totum illius capituli pavimentum profunde infarsit, terra mollicula, tenuis, soluta &amp; arenosa, sed innato calore subtiliter vorax;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035331Z">
        <seg>For this purpose, a clod brought from a distance by ship, far from the fields of Panama, deeply filled the entire floor of that chamber, a soft, fine, loose, and sandy soil, yet subtly voracious by its innate heat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In ictu oculi se inveniebat illic, ubi nulla suavissimi Numinis memoria, nullus divinæ præsentiæ gustus, nullum cujuscumque solatii aderat vestigium, umbra, mentio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045456Z">
        <seg>In the blink of an eye he found himself there, where there was no memory of the most sweetest Deity, no taste of divine presence, no trace, shadow, or mention of any consolation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In ipso adolescentiæ ac pueritiæ confinio, Rosæ corculum acerbissime compunxerat devota ac tenerrima compassio ex avidissimo obtutu piæ imaginis, quæ muto præconio clamitabat, &lt;b1&gt;Ecce Homo&lt;/b1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000940Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000940Z">
        <seg>At the very threshold of her adolescence and childhood, a devout and most tender compassion had pierced Rose's little heart most acutely, from the avid gaze of a pious image that proclaimed with a silent cry, "Behold the Man."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In ipso adolescentiæ ac pueritiæ confinio, Rosæ corculum acerbissime compunxerat devota ac tenerrima compassio ex avidissimo obtutu piæ imaginis, quæ muto præconio clamitabat, &lt;b2&gt;Ecce Homo&lt;/b2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042046Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042046Z">
        <seg>At the very threshold of her adolescence and childhood, a devout and most tender compassion had pierced Rose's little heart most acutely, arising from the avid gaze of a pious image that, with a silent proclamation, cried out, "Behold the Man."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In nomine Patris, &amp; Filii, &amp; Spiritus sancti.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043410Z">
        <seg>In the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In omni conversatione unicum Rosæ oblectamentum erat ac solatium, de amore divino aut audire, aut loqui;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053155Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053155Z">
        <seg>In every conversation, Rose's only delight and comfort was to hear or speak of divine love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In omni familiari consessu, conversatione, societate, crux siqua prostabat, Rosæ sol, ipsa cruci heliotropium&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt; erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011153Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011153Z">
        <seg>In every family gathering, conversation, and society, if a cross was laid out, Rose's sun, she herself, was a heliotrope to the cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In omni familiari consessu, conversatione, societate, crux siqua prostabat, Rosæ sol, ipsa cruci heliotropium&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;a&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt; erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054835Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054835Z">
        <seg>In every family gathering, conversation, and society, if a cross was laid out, Rosæ sol, herself, was the heliotrope to the cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In persona Joannis Zelilli ab imminenti morte mirabiliter præservati: [ut post severum examen] hunc enim per quatuor menses oppresserat febris hectica tertiæ speciei, progressa in exquisitum marasmum&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt; cum ulcere pulmonis, tussi marcidum cruorem excreante, ac rosione gutturis pariter ulcerati, quibus accesserat febris putrida &amp; maligna;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043240Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043240Z">
        <seg>In the person of John Zelilli, miraculously preserved from imminent death: [after a severe trial] for he had been afflicted for four months by a third-degree remittent fever, which had progressed into an extreme marasmus with a lung ulcer, a cough spitting putrid blood, and a similarly ulcerated throat, to which a putrid and malignant fever had also been added;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In portu Callao licentiatum Joannem de Quisado continuis quadraginta diebus torrebant æstuosæ febres, neque ab humanis remediis spes ulla reparandæ salutis affulgebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040822Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040822Z">
        <seg>In the port of Callao, the licensed John de Quisado was afflicted by raging fevers for forty consecutive days, and no hope of recovery shone from human remedies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In primis haud absurda spe tumida mater, jam a teneris filiolam nobili atque honestissimo connubio destinarat: nec abludebant proposito egregia formæ venustas, acre judicium, suavitas erectæ indolis, moresque ad omnem civilitatis gratiam apprime tum facti, tum eruditi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035932Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035932Z">
        <seg>At first, the mother, inflated with not unreasonable hope, had already destined her tender little daughter for a noble and most honorable marriage; nor did the outstanding beauty of her form, her keen judgment, the sweetness of her upright disposition, and her manners—both then and thereafter most polished in every grace of civility—deter her from her purpose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In probationis anno fraus deprehensa Alphonsum Velasquez cœnobiarcham permovit, ut de patrum consilio clam statuerit, inutilem novitium honeste ad seculum remittere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012423Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012423Z">
        <seg>In the probationary year, when fraud was discovered, Abbot Alphonsus Velasquez, acting secretly on the council of the fathers, decided to honorably send the useless novice back to the secular world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In pœnarum cumulum recordabatur, velut in nebula, aliquando se novisse Deum, &amp; dilexisse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045518Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045518Z">
        <seg>He remembered, as if in a fog, that he had once known and loved God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In quo etiam promissa Sponsi providentia affulsit: carente enim illa dote necessaria, unus ex reservatis ab ipsa fundatrice gratuitæ receptioni pro dignioribus, sed egenis personis locus ei obtigit, ceteraque omnia ad amussim etiam minima, ut S. virgo prædixerat, evenerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042629Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042629Z">
        <seg>In which the promise of the Spouse's providence was also fulfilled: for, lacking that necessary dowry, one of the places reserved by the foundress herself for the free reception of more worthy yet needy persons was granted to her, and everything else, down to the smallest detail, happened exactly as the holy virgin had foretold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In statu enim vitæ deligendo diu, multumque luctandum fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041312Z">
        <seg>For in choosing a state of life, there was much to struggle with for a long time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In sublimi enim &amp; singulari mentis elevatione viderat Christum Dominum dividentem lance ex cruce facta gratias &amp; dona ad mensuram pœnarum, audivitque dicentem, sine pondere afflictionum ad gratiæ cumulos non perveniri, unicam veram scalam paradisi esse crucem, nec præter eam superesse aliam, per quam in cælum ascendatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042059Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042059Z">
        <seg>For in a sublime and singular elevation of mind he had seen Christ the Lord dividing, by a lance made from the cross, graces and gifts in proportion to one's sufferings; and he heard him say that without the weight of afflictions one cannot reach the storehouses of grace, that the cross is the one true ladder to paradise, and that there is no other by which one can ascend to heaven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In templo sancti Dominici Rosa cum matre, honestisque feminis substiterat, Deo precibus commendatura extremum agonem Mariæ de Vera, quæ in ædibus Didaci de Requena argentarii decumbens, ultimam vitæ horam exspectabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012200Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012200Z">
        <seg>In the church of Saint Dominic, Rosa, with her mother and other honorable women, had paused to commend to God by prayer the final agony of Mary of Vera, who, lying ill in the house of Diego de Requena the argentine, awaited the last hour of her life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In ultimo vitæ jamjam fugientis articulo, Rosa dolentem genitricem juxta se conspicata, neque ignara, quam immaniter hanc exanimaturus esset proximus ex Filiæ morte dolor, rogavit Sponsum, (isque ultimus precantis anhelitus fuit) matrem immodico mœrori imparem, ac facile succubituram, robusto firmaret solatio, adque fatalem hunc ictum miseram obduraret suavi potenti manu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035827Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035827Z">
        <seg>In the very last article of her life, now about to flee, Rosa, seeing her suffering mother beside her, and not unaware how terribly the impending blow of her daughter's death would overwhelm her, begged her Spouse—and this was the dying woman's last breath—to spare her mother, who was not equal to such excessive sorrow, and easily succumbing, would strengthen her with robust consolation, and, with a sweet and mighty hand, harden the poor mother for this fatal blow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inanem hunc rumorem ipsa publici timoris caligo pepererat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060201Z">
        <seg>This empty rumor had been born of the very fog of public fear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Incertum tamen, acus an ornatus exosi circuli dirius pupugerit Rosam, spinis quam sertis amiciorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035322Z">
        <seg>Uncertain, however, whether the needle or the circle's hated adornments pierced Rosa more deeply, she who was friendlier to thorns than to garlands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Increpuit ille Virginem, utpote nondum ex circumstantiis tunc assequens, quid laudis illa potius meruisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040633Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040633Z">
        <seg>He reproached the Virgin, since he had not yet, from the circumstances at the time, understood what praise she might more fittingly have deserved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde Rosam adiit collocutura de aliis, quæ sibi tunc pressius instabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012142Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012142Z">
        <seg>Then she went to Rosa to talk about other matters that were pressing on her at the time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde Rosæ sepulchrum adiit, Virgini causam supplex commendavit, obtineret sibi a Sponso salubrem electionem illius status, qui maxime ipsam aptaret divinis obsequiis, promoveret in via æternæ salutis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032910Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032910Z">
        <seg>Then he went to the tomb of Rose, humbly commended the Virgin's cause, and asked that the Spouse grant her a fitting choice of that state which would best enable her to render divine homage and advance her on the path to eternal salvation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde Virgini cum suo angelo tam domestica fiducia, ac necessitudo, ut illo soleret uti veredario&lt;a6&gt;e&lt;/a6&gt;, quotiescumque ad horam consuetam Sponsus non comparebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010644Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010644Z">
        <seg>Hence the Virgin had with her angel such a domestic confidence and familiarity that she would often use him as a messenger whenever the Bridegroom failed to appear at the usual hour.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde Virgini cum suo angelo tam domestica fiducia, ac necessitudo, ut illo soleret uti veredario&lt;s13&gt;&lt;a14&gt;e&lt;/a14&gt;&lt;/s13&gt;, quotiescumque ad horam consuetam Sponsus non comparebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051604Z">
        <seg>Hence the Virgin had with her angel such a domestic confidence and familiarity that she would use him as a messenger whenever the Bridegroom failed to appear at the usual hour.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde continuo rhedarum ordine &amp; commeatu, per mensem &amp; amplius, personæ illustriores proficiscebantur ad paternam Rosæ domum, visuræ ac veneraturæ hortensem cellulam, quam Virgo solitudinis studio inhabitarat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031208Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031208Z">
        <seg>Hence, in a continuous procession of carriages and with an escort, for a month and more, more distinguished persons journeyed to Rose's father's house, to see and venerate the garden cell which the Virgin had inhabited out of a love for solitude.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde credula &amp; ignara multitudo densatur concursu, invalescit fremitus, nec leve imminuit periculum innoxiis religiosis, dum rudis &amp; impetuosa plebs jam ad convicia &amp; minas ferociter progressa, primo affatu aut non capiebat, aut abnuebat capere facti rationem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031732Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031732Z">
        <seg>Hence the credulous and ignorant multitude gathers in a throng, the uproar swells, and the danger to the harmless religious is not in the least diminished, while the rough and impetuous mob, already fiercely resorting to insults and threats, at the first explanation either did not grasp or refused to accept the reason for the matter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde est, quod sæpius coram intimis ac familiaribus suis timida frequentabat hujusmodi voces;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040534Z">
        <seg>Hence it was that she often used such timid expressions in the presence of her intimates and family.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde igitur allatum pulverem non modo hausit, sed &amp; vertici infricuit, unaque &amp; vertiginem &amp; dolores fugavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035616Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035616Z">
        <seg>Therefore, he not only inhaled the powder that had been brought, but also rubbed it on his crown, and with one action banished both dizziness and pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde mater infusos aquæ pulveres languenti præbuit exhauriendos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035526Z">
        <seg>Then the mother gave the languishing man powdered water to drink.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde per octiduum visus est quotidie adire sepulchrum Virginis ad gratias palam &amp; ex debito persolvendas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033437Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033437Z">
        <seg>For eight days thereafter, he was seen going daily to the Virgin's tomb to publicly and dutifully give thanks.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde pergebat Rosa: “Persensi ex vicina Christi humanitate suaviter in animæ meæ fundum defluere inexplicabiles flammas &amp; gloriæ stricturas, adeo quod existimarim, a mundo corruptibili me penitus jam solutam, &amp; ad perpetuæ fruitionis beata gaudia translatam esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052745Z">
        <seg>Then Rosa continued: "I perceived from Christ's neighboring humanity sweetly flowing into the depths of my soul inexplicable flames and strains of glory, so that I believed myself already wholly released from this corruptible world and translated to the blessed joys of perpetual fruition."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde quos quantosque insultus, quæ domestica prælia, probra, convicia a parente, fratribus, agnatis sustinuerit æquanimis, at inermis Filia, nemo ad vivum propius aut comprehenderit aut æstimarit, quam quisquis ad sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis historiam respexerit, ubi Lapa mater tenacem propositi virginem inclementer objurgat, premit, exagitat, nisi quod in Rosam ultra verba, etiam verbera, calces, alapæ sævierint.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040007Z">
        <seg>Hence, no one has more closely understood or appreciated the insults, the domestic quarrels, the slanders, and the abuse that the Daughter, meek but unarmed, endured from her parent, brothers, and kinsmen, than whoever has looked into the history of Saint Catherine of Siena, where Lapa, the mother, cruelly reproves, presses, and agitates the tenacious virgin of her purpose, were it not that against Rosa, beyond words, even beatings, kicks, and slaps have raged.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde satis colligitur, quid&lt;/e7&gt; gargarismis &lt;e8&gt;istius aviculæ canentis indicetur.&lt;/e8&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045413Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045413Z">
        <seg>From this, one can gather enough about what is indicated by the gurgles of this singing little bird.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde, post emensam gravi tardoque incessu primam plateam, ventum ad quadrivium, ubi feretro deposito, successit in piæ sarcinæ bajulatum senatus curiæ secularis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030613Z">
        <seg>From there, after crossing the first street with heavy and slow step, they came to the crossroads, where the bier was set down, and the secular senate took up the pious burden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde, ubi vegeta lecto se proripuit, tertio post die feliciter infantem enixa est incolumem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040435Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040435Z">
        <seg>Then, once she had recovered, she happily gave birth to a healthy baby three days later.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Indignata parens substomachabatur filiæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061308Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061308Z">
        <seg>An indignant parent was scolding her daughter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Indusiolum sunto 50 Litaniæ, 900 Rosaria, 5 dies inediæ in reverentiam SS. Incarnationis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053551Z">
        <seg>Let there be 50 Litanys, 900 Rosaries, and 5 days of fasting in reverence of the Holy Incarnation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Infertur decumbentis conclavi picta Rosæ effigies, quam Joannes ut præviis devotis basiis capiti imposuit, pectorique fortiter adstrinxit, febrim extrusit, locumque reduci valetudini statim cedere compulit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040825Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040825Z">
        <seg>A painted image of the Rose is brought into the sick man's chamber. John, with the devout kisses he had previously offered, placed it on his head and pressed it firmly to his breast, banished the fever, and compelled the place to yield at once to restored health.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Infimæ dumtaxat pedum supererant plantæ, quas neque cilicium nec disciplinæ verbera attingebant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041913Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041913Z">
        <seg>Only the soles of their feet remained untouched, by neither cilice nor the scourge of discipline.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Infirmabatur Gaspar Flores, Virginis genitor, mœrens etiam ob quinquaginta librarum debitum, cui præ inopia solvendo non erat: tristabatur perplexa mater, importunitate creditoris augente angustias.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042555Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042555Z">
        <seg>Gaspar Flores, the Virgin's father, was growing weak, also grieving over a fifty-pound debt which, for lack of funds, he could not pay; the perplexed mother was distressed, her anxieties increased by the creditor's importunity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Infirmæ eorum valetudini semper prompte &amp; provide assistens, &amp; ministrans visa fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041216Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041216Z">
        <seg>She was seen to be ever ready and provident in attending to and ministering to their infirmities.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Infremuit parens, &amp; “Aut nugaris (inquit) aut somnias: quis enim intempesta hac hora calentem chocolatam ad te mittet?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051657Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051657Z">
        <seg>The parent frowned and said, "Either you're joking or you're dreaming: for who at this ungodly hour would send you hot chocolate?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ingemiscebat deinde ruditati suæ, quod præ inopia sterilis (ut putabat) ingenioli, ac loquendi inscitia, non poterat afflictionem suam idoneis vocabulis explicare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045720Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045720Z">
        <seg>He then groaned over his own rudeness, because, in the face of what he believed to be a barren lack of wit and an ignorance of how to speak, he could not express his distress in fitting words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ingressi domum repererunt Indam sine loquela aut motu supinam, obrigidam, sanguinea labiorum spuma horribilem, ac mortuæ quam vivæ similiorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035812Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035812Z">
        <seg>Upon entering the house, they found Inda lying supine, speechless and motionless, stiff, with a horrifying bloody foam on her lips, and more like one dead than one alive.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Initio Rosa diversos adibat confessarios, si quid forte remedii, levaminis, aut saltem consilii dare nossent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045655Z">
        <seg>At first, Rosa went to various confessors, if perhaps they might know of some remedy, relief, or at least advice.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Initio quidem illud tametsi asperum, minus tamen, quam Virgo optaverat, latum, sinuosumque videbatur, utpote quod vix duorum palmorum mensuram excedebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041832Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041832Z">
        <seg>At first, although rough, it seemed less broad and winding than the Virgin had wished, since it scarcely exceeded a span of two palms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Initio, cum S: Catherinæ de Senis gestare cœpisset habitum, non contenta vulgaribus funiculis, duas sibi catenas ferreas aptarat in flagrum, hisque ad imitationem S. Patris sui Dominici per singulas noctes tam dure ac dire sibi dorsum &amp; scapulas cruentabat, ut rivi sanguinis in parietibus, in pavimento, in subligaculis fuerint notati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041510Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041510Z">
        <seg>At first, when she had begun to wear the habit of St. Catherine of Siena, not content with ordinary cords, she had fastened two iron chains to her back as a whip, and with these, in imitation of her holy Father Dominic, she scourged her back and shoulders so harshly and cruelly each night that streams of blood were left on the walls, on the floor, and on her undergarments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Innotuit Virginis confessario gliscens malum, isque Rosæ injunxit, SS. Rosarii sacellum adiret, ibique coram Deiparæ imagine periclitantis illius congregationis necessitatem Matri misericordiæ fusis precibus commendaret, nec curam dimitteret quoadusque gratiam &amp; succursum impetrasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054419Z">
        <seg>The confessor of the Virgin perceived the evil creeping in, and he ordered Rose to go to the chapel of the Holy Rosary and there, before the image of the Mother of God, commend the need of that congregation in peril to the Mother of Mercy with fervent prayers, and not to desist until she had obtained grace and help.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Innotuit postea (revelante ancilla Mariana) Rosam omni fere diluculo, quo sacra Synaxi reficienda non erat, austero hoc fellis promulsidario in Dominicæ Passionis memoriam os totum imbuere solitam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041231Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041231Z">
        <seg>It later became known (as revealed by the handmaid Mary) that Rosa, at almost every dawn when she was not required to renew the sacred Synaxis, used to bathe her entire mouth with this austere gall-based ointment in memory of the Passion of the Lord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Innumeri culices hanc anachoreticam cellulam intrabant, nec tamen vel unus fuit, qui eam morderet, aut attingeret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041625Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041625Z">
        <seg>Countless mosquitoes entered this hermit's cell, yet not one of them bit or even touched him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inops consilii Joanna, postremo confugit ad Rosam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035954Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035954Z">
        <seg>Joanna, at a loss for counsel, finally fled to Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Instabat anxia matrona: interrogabat, ubi, quid potissimum doleret?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013325Z">
        <seg>An anxious matron stood by: she asked where and what pained her most.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Instabat filius, saltem interea quieti ac sopori se redderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032123Z">
        <seg>His son was nearby, so at least for the time being he gave himself over to rest and sleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Instabat hora funeris ad sepulturam efferendi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042412Z">
        <seg>The hour of the funeral was at hand; the body was to be carried out for burial.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Instat alter, flagitans suo id saltem dari solatio, ut iterum quærat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034016Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034016Z">
        <seg>Another one insists, demanding that at least this be given to him as a consolation so that he may ask again.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Insuper ad hujus lectuli caput occulte ampullam felle plenam appenderat, nec ante ad somnum sese componebat, quam isthoc sorbillo amaricasset fauces, in memoriam Sponsi felle ac myrrha potati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042632Z">
        <seg>Moreover, she had secretly hung a vial filled with gall at the head of this little bed, and she would not settle down to sleep until she had moistened her throat with a drop of it, in memory of the Bridegroom who had drunk gall and myrrh.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Insuper concupisco amare te, o Deus cordis ac vitæ meæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053108Z">
        <seg>Moreover, I long to love you, O God of my heart and my life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Insuper vivis floribus neutiquam contenta, pretiosiores e filis sericis fingebat versicolori artificio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060115Z">
        <seg>Moreover, not at all content with living flowers, she fashioned more precious ones from silk threads with a multicolored artistry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intellexit Rosa mysterium, nempe ex decreto cælestis Sponsi non esse, quod ipsa populosissimi illius monasterii optato receptaculo furtim sese abderet, aliud de genere sui instituti provisum esse in secreto æternitatis consilio: denique summa rei erat, quod solius Catharinæ Senensis vestigia Rosam exspectabant spinis sepiendam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040128Z">
        <seg>Rosa understood the mystery: that by the decree of the heavenly Spouse it was not that she should secretly hide herself in the most populous monastery's eagerly sought refuge, but that something else of her order's institution had been provided for in the secret counsel of eternity. In short, the essence of the matter was that only the footsteps of Catherine of Siena awaited Rosa to be enclosed among thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intellexit Virgo sapiens, quorsum delicati Sponsuli verba tam seria ac pretiosa tenderent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061912Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061912Z">
        <seg>The wise Virgin understood where the delicate Spouse's words were tending, so serious and precious as they were.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intellexit vir religiosus ac prudens, quorsum Rosa tum loquendo, tum reliqua subticendo alluderet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012008Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012008Z">
        <seg>The religious and prudent man understood to what Rosa was alluding, both by what she said and by what she left unsaid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter alios præcipui, quos bona sors huic subtilissimæ explorationi destinavit, fuere doctor Joannes de Castillo, &amp; P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050017Z">
        <seg>Among other prominent figures whom fortune destined for this most subtle exploration were Doctor John de Castillo and Father.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter alios præcipui, quos bona sors huic subtilissimæ explorationi destinavit, fuere doctor Joannes de Castillo, &amp; P. magister F. Joannes de Lorenzana, cui in Rosam totum, usque ad extrema, competebat imperium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010412Z">
        <seg>Among other prominent figures whom fortune appointed to this most subtle investigation were Doctor John de Castillo and Father Master Friar John de Lorenzana, to whom the entire Rosary, down to its very last part, was entrusted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter eas hæc illustrior fuit, qua vidit coram divino solio augustissimam cæli Reginam stantem, ac miri fulgoris coronam manu gestantem, velut novam hospitem expectaret, ad illius solii limbum solemniter coronandam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031831Z">
        <seg>Among these, the most illustrious was that in which she saw before the divine throne the most august Queen of Heaven standing, and holding in her hand a crown of wondrous splendor, as though awaiting a new guest to be solemnly crowned upon the rim of that throne.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter has mendicas nulla erat tam intolerabilis aspectu, aut fœtore horribilis, quam Rosa dedignari, vel præ nausea aversari posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042425Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042425Z">
        <seg>Among these beggars there was none so intolerable to look at, or so foul-smelling, that Rosa could scorn her or turn away in disgust.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter has similesque voces, quæstoris uxor clam ex oratorio in vicinum atrium se proripuit cum filiabus, ne forte loquenti Virgini instreperent, aut liberiores hos impetus respectu importunæ præsentiæ impedirent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053731Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053731Z">
        <seg>Amid these and similar voices, the quaestor's wife slipped quietly from the oratory into the adjoining atrium with her daughters, so that they might not interrupt the Virgin while she was speaking, or, by their more unrestrained impulses, hinder these impulses in deference to her importunate presence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter has vero mendicas ac miserabiles nulla tam pannosa aut sordida, nulla tam deformis aut ulcerosa, nulla aspectu horribilis aut intolerabilis fœtore, quam Rosa vel dedignari posset, vel præ nausea aversari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061252Z">
        <seg>Among these beggars and wretches there was none so ragged or filthy, none so deformed or ulcerous, none so hideous in appearance or so intolerably foul-smelling that Rosa could not but loathe or turn away in disgust.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter hos Franciscus Cardoso, novem mensium tenellus infans, continua febrium vicissitudine a tribus mensibus nunc rigens nunc æstuans, subito, ut sacro cadaveri admotus fuit, planctum simul &amp; febrim posuit, mirante populo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033604Z">
        <seg>Among them was Francis Cardoso, a tender nine-month-old infant, who for three months had been suffering from a constant fever, now shivering, now burning. Suddenly, as soon as he was brought to the sacred corpse, he ceased his crying and his fever, to the amazement of the people.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter hos duos mellifluos vultus, æstu reciproco, oberrabat Rosa, festinabat ab uno ad alterum, deliciabatur in alterno spectaculo, &amp; quo sæpius ad alterutram redibat, faciem inveniebat dulciorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044318Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044318Z">
        <seg>Between these two honeyed faces, in mutual ardor, fluttered Rosa, hurrying from one to the other, delighting in the alternating spectacle, and the more often she returned to either, the sweeter she found the face.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter hæc Rosa, quæ cum honestis matronis in ecclesia S. Dominici exitum horum tumultuum expectabat, soli metuebat prostanti in ara divinissimo Sacramento, cui utique sacrilegæ manus perfidæ gentis parcituræ non erant, fortasse nec pedes, quin illud ausu horrendo protererent, humique projectum rabie blasphema proculcarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060155Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060155Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, Rosa, who with the honorable matrons in the Church of St. Dominic was awaiting the outcome of these disturbances, feared alone for the Most Blessed Sacrament on the altar, which the sacrilegious hands of that perfidious people would surely not spare—perhaps not even its feet—but would trample with a horrendous audacity, and, cast upon the ground, would be kicked about with blasphemous fury.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter manuales labores intellectus aciem immobiliter in Deum figebat, ita ut nec in vigilia, nec in somno mentis ejus oculo cælestis Sponsi elaberetur præsentia, quæ ita suaviter occupabat interiores animæ illius vires, ut loquendo cum ipso Deo intus, simul etiam alia quæque necessaria expedite foris pertractaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041705Z">
        <seg>While engaged in manual labor, she fixed the keenness of her intellect immovably on God, so that neither in wakefulness nor in sleep did the presence of the heavenly Spouse slip from the eye of her mind, a presence that so sweetly occupied the inner powers of her soul that, speaking within with God Himself, she at the same time also expeditiously attended to all other necessary matters without.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter utramque pactum fuerat, quod, quæ prima ex hac luce migraret, Deo permittente, alteram sui transitus admoneret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030242Z">
        <seg>It had been agreed between them that whichever of them was the first to depart this life would, God willing, warn the other of her passing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interdum huic visa est Rosa in hortis empyreis jucunde spatiari cum Christo, qui pauculis passibus ante se Virginem præmittebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031955Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031955Z">
        <seg>Sometimes it seemed to her that Rose was pleasantly strolling in the heavenly gardens with Christ, who placed the Virgin a few paces ahead of him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interdum nimio algore excitata, roris marini ramusculis ad ellychnium accensis, obiter hoc fumo tantillum fugitivi caloris, quo se foveret, nitebatur concipere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042927Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042927Z">
        <seg>Sometimes, roused by the bitter cold, she would kindle sprigs of sea spray at her brazier, and in doing so would try to catch a little fugitive warmth from the smoke to warm herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interdum prope erat, ut inconditos clamores ederet imploratura auxilium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045602Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045602Z">
        <seg>Sometimes she was close to uttering incoherent cries to implore for help.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interdum, ut matri videretur lautius obsonare, amarissima Granadillæ folia vel alias e sylva extremæ amaritudinis herbas, injectis ad speciem paucis uvarum siccarum acinis, in pulmentum adhibebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041138Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041138Z">
        <seg>Sometimes, to make her mother think she was buying more expensive provisions, she would add the most bitter granadilla leaves—or other herbs of extreme bitterness from the woods—to the porridge, tossing in a few dried grapes for show.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interea Rosam torquebat febris non sua.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053520Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, a fever not her own was tormenting Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interea a vicina sartoris conjuge Mariana Inda (quæ cum Rosa a teneris educata fuerat) Virginis pictam in tela effigiem petiit, allatam vultui applicuit, osculis devote libavit, demum in ejus amplexu dulciter consopita, dum mane expergiscitur, se undequaque sanam mirata, refectionem petiit, lectoque exsiliit ac imaginem festivius deveneratura, arulæ stratæ decenter imposuit, accensis luminaribus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040243Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040243Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, from the tailor's neighbor's wife, Mariana Inda (who had been raised with Rosa from a young age) She asked for the Virgin's painted image on canvas, had it brought, pressed it to her face, kissed it devoutly, and at last, sweetly fallen asleep in its embrace, when she awoke in the morning, marveled to find herself in perfect health, sought refreshment, leapt from bed, and, intending to venerate the image more fittingly, placed it decently on a small altar, lights already lit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interea bis, ter, ab ecclesiæ porta reversus frater cunctantem increpuit, moræ periculum exaggeravit, surgere conanti brachium subjecit: connituntur ambo, sed incassum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040121Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040121Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, having turned back twice or three times from the church gate, the brother rebuked the hesitating man, exaggerated the danger of delay, and seized his arm as he tried to rise. They both struggled, but to no avail.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interea fletuum nimbo utcumque discusso, Rosa placide summaque reverentia parentis manum deosculata est, simul blande ac imperturbate exposuit, sibi nunc modicissimam vitæ, quam parens dederat, partem superesse, in hujus extrema linea sese amborum benedictionem supplici demissione præstolari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030142Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030142Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, having somehow wiped away her tears, Rosa calmly and with the utmost reverence kissed her parent's hand, and then softly and unperturbed explained that only the humblest portion of the life her parent had given her now remained, and that in this final phase she awaited both their blessings with humble submission.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interea memor viscerum charitatis, quibus erga ægrotos norat Virginem abundare, summis precibus egit per quæstoris conjugem, ut Rosam adduceret in tanto sibi periculo bene precaturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061441Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, mindful of the charitable deeds with which he knew the Virgin abounded toward the sick, he earnestly implored the husband of the questor to bring Rosa, who in such a peril would pray well for him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interea terror &amp; angor strenue obibant partes suas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045534Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, terror and anguish were vigorously playing their parts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interea, cum aliis sororibus tertiariis expectabat ibidem &amp; Rosa palmam suam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044238Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044238Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, she was waiting there with the other tertiary sisters and Rose for her palm;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interfuere spectaculo infantis pater, soror, aliusque juvenis, qui junctim in genua provoluti, suoque gaudio ac stupori impares, sublatis in cælum vocibus laudarunt Dominum in sua Rosa tam odorifera.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033139Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033139Z">
        <seg>The child's father, sister, and another young man were present at the spectacle; they fell to their knees together, and, overcome with their joy and wonder, they praised the Lord with raised voices for His so fragrant Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim Josepha esculentis (quæ alacriter glutiebat) refecta, sine alterius medelæ adminiculo convaluit, promissamque Novenam fideliter exsolvit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035434Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035434Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, Joseph, having been refilled with food (which she eagerly swallowed), recovered without the aid of any other medicine, and faithfully fulfilled the promised Novena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim cautæ filiolæ ingeniosa non defuere stratagemata, quibus matri diu occuleret hunc severum abstinentiæ tenorem, ne ab ipsa valentioribus eduliis vesci cogeretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041023Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041023Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, the cautious little daughter did not lack ingenious stratagems by which she could hide from her mother this strict regimen of abstinence for a long time, so that she would not be forced by her to eat more substantial food.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim cœpit eminus ille nutibus procum, mox &amp; procacem agere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052206Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052206Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, from a distance he began to woo her with gestures, soon acting boldly and impudently.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim dolebat tam prope imminere decretorium Rosæ diem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013204Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, she was distressed that the fateful day of the rose was looming so close.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim esuriei, sitis, lassitudinis immemor, tota occupabatur cum dilecto, ea quidem veneratione ac reverentia, ut toto XXIV horarum decursu, nec sedere coram illo, nec muro contiguo tantisper acclinari præsumeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060047Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060047Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, oblivious to hunger, thirst, and fatigue, she was entirely occupied with her beloved, with such veneration and reverence that throughout the entire twenty-four-hour period she would not presume to sit before him or even lean for a moment against the adjoining wall.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim ipsa velut moræ jam impatiens, nunc experiri incessum, nunc ad aram sacramque Hostiam, mox ad ecclesiæ fores prospectare, si qua venirent hostes, quibus velocior ad altare convolaret, certa ibidem occumbere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060248Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060248Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, she herself, as if already impatient of delay, would now try to walk, now approach the altar and the sacred Host, then soon look toward the church doors to see if any enemies were coming, to whom she would rush more swiftly to the altar, certain to fall there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim mittam illi medicum meum, hunc ad lectuli caput jugiter sub oculis habeat, nec de recuperanda valetudine diffidat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012355Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012355Z">
        <seg>"In the meantime, I will send him my physician; let him keep him constantly by the head of the bed, within sight, and not despair of his recovery."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim non secus atque ad publicam supplicationem longis agminibus diversisque viis ad ædes quæstoris confluxere pia variorum titulorum sodalitia, nemine aut jubente aut rogante, vel stipendium offerente.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030542Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, just as if by a public supplication, pious societies of various titles flocked in long processions and by different routes to the house of the quaestor, with no one either commanding or requesting it, or offering a stipend.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim parvula Magdalena pauxillo aquæ, cui mater pulverem sepulchri B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033142Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033142Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, little Magdalena with a little water, to which her mother had added dust from the tomb of the Blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim pergebat lingua, quæ sola mobilis erat, prosequebatur nunc Dei, nunc Rosæ encomia, dum simul persona tota cum nimia interni sui gaudii vehementia luctabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044613Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile the tongue, the only part that could move, went on, now praising God, now praising Rose, while the whole person struggled with the excessive vehemence of her inner joy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim precibus, gemitibus, suspiriis pulsabat ad cæli fores;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035815Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035815Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, he knocked at the gates of heaven with prayers, groans, and sighs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim toto corpore sudabat adhuc, &amp; quia sub plantis pro callo mollior caro succreverat, terram passibus nonn hil suspensis calcabat, idque alii infirmitatem rati, euntem subjectis brachiis juvare nitebantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033415Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile he was still sweating all over his body, and because softer flesh had grown under his soles in place of calluses, he trod the ground with somewhat unsteady steps; others, thinking him ill, tried to support him as he walked with outstretched arms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim, Rosa clausit extremum diem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032854Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032854Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, Rosa closed the last day;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intermedio itinere labitur fluvius, quem Hispani vocant &lt;b2&gt;De Condorillo&lt;/b2&gt;, octo quidem leucas a la Plata, septemdecim autem a Misque.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034416Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034416Z">
        <seg>Halfway along the journey flows a river which the Spaniards call De Condorillo, eight leagues from La Plata, seventeen from Miské.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogat, cur unice hunc præ ceteris versiculis amaret, respondit, totum solatium seraphicæ suæ matris eo contineri, &amp; singulas illius syllabas arcano quodam familiaris in Deo confidentiæ succo turgere, nec suo palato aliud his propriis verbis sapidius posse occurrere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042459Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042459Z">
        <seg>When asked why he loved this verse above all others, he replied that the entire consolation of his seraphic mother was contained in it, that each of its syllables swelled with a certain secret, familiar sap of confidence in God, and that nothing could taste sweeter to his palate than these very words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogata aliquando a viro religioso (teste vigesimo primo) quantum commodi suis parentibus ex tenui isto flosculorum mercimonio accederet, respondit, illud quidem permodicum esse, ni Sponsus cælestis aliunde miris, at arcanis, modis suppleret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035805Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035805Z">
        <seg>When once asked by a religious man (the twenty-first witness) how much benefit her parents received from this meager trade in flowers, she replied that it was indeed very small, unless the heavenly Spouse supplied it from elsewhere by wondrous, yet mysterious, means.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogata de modo &amp; qualitate visionis, imaginaria sit an intellectualis, diuturna an momentanea, facie ad faciem, an oblique per transparitiones?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050541Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050541Z">
        <seg>When asked about the manner and quality of the vision—whether imaginary or intellectual, lasting or momentary, face to face or indirectly through transparencies?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogata de successivis in hoc studio profectibus, an pari semper applicatione animi, facilitate, collectione, ac imperturbatæ mentis serenitate oraverit, respondit, usque ad duodecimum circiter ætatis annum, se varia inter orandum temperie, difficultatum subinde vicissitudines percepisse, at breves, &amp; modicas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050250Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050250Z">
        <seg>When asked about her successive progress in this study, whether she had always prayed with equal application of mind, facility, concentration, and an untroubled serenity of mind, she replied that until about the age of twelve, she had perceived various fluctuations in her prayerful temper, though brief and moderate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogata, cur ex tot millibus unice hunc versiculum amasset præ ceteris, respondebat: Seraphicæ matris suæ totum illo solatium contineri, singulas hujus syllabas turgere quodam arcano succo familiaris in Deum confidentiæ, nec suo palato aliud his tam propriis verbis sapidius posse occurrere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061739Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061739Z">
        <seg>When asked why, out of so many thousands, she loved this single verse above all others, she replied that the entire consolation of her seraphic mother was contained in it, that each of its syllables swelled with a certain secret sap of familiar confidence in God, and that nothing could taste sweeter to her palate than these words so uniquely her own.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogata, quid ageret, quid sibi vellet hac novitate?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060220Z">
        <seg>When asked what she was doing and what she wanted with this novelty?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogata, quid solatii captaret ex creaturis, si contingeret animum contemplandi labore fessum nonnihil remittere, aut per eutrapeliam&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt; tantisper relaxare?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010449Z">
        <seg>When asked what comfort she would draw from the creatures, should it happen that her mind, weary from the labor of contemplation, needed to be somewhat eased, or to be relaxed for a while through good humor?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogata, quid solatii captaret ex creaturis, si contingeret animum contemplandi labore fessum nonnihil remittere, aut per eutrapeliam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; tantisper relaxare?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050343Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050343Z">
        <seg>When asked what solace she would draw from creatures, should it happen that her mind, weary from the labor of contemplation, needed to rest a little, or be relaxed for a while through good humor?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogata, velitne advocari medicum?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013335Z">
        <seg>When asked, would she like a doctor to be called?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intravit denique Rosa jacentis cubiculum, salutavit ægrum, sed ea compassionis ac misericordiæ dulci affluentia, ut nec circumstantibus sanis ambiguum fuerit, loqui in modestissima Virgine Deum totius consolationis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061500Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061500Z">
        <seg>At last Rosa entered the sick woman's chamber, greeted the patient, but with such a sweet abundance of compassion and mercy that even to the healthy bystanders it was plain that in that most modest Virgin spoke the God of all consolation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intravit medicus Franciscus Ximenes, primoque aspectu indissimulanter parvulum habuit pro deplorato.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040630Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040630Z">
        <seg>Doctor Francis Ximenes entered, and at first glance he could not hide his astonishment at how small the child was for being so desperately ill.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intravit uterque, ac brevi oratione præmissa, ad altare propior accessit, sudantem adhuc vultum diu, attenteque contemplatus, demum petito gossipio leviter humorem abstersit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053840Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053840Z">
        <seg>Both of them entered, and after a brief speech, approached the altar; having long and attentively contemplated the still-sweating face, he finally wiped away the moisture with the requested rag.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intumuerat manus ad æqualitatem doliolorum, quibus mella selectissima ex Hispaniis Limam deferuntur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055343Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055343Z">
        <seg>The hand had swollen to the size of the casks, by which the choicest honey is brought from Spain to Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intus humile altariolum muro applicuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043249Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043249Z">
        <seg>Inside, he affixed a humble little altar to the wall.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Invaluit sensim apud optimum patrem instantis sui termini concepta opinio, ut a collegio ad novitiatum, seu tironum domicilium migrans, non sociis tantum, sed ipsius quoque arboribus, muris, tectis valedixerit, velut domum priorem numquam revisurus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012241Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012241Z">
        <seg>A notion gradually gained ground among the most devoted father of his household that, when moving from the college to the novitiate, or the house of novices, he should bid farewell not only to his companions but also to the very trees, walls, and roofs, as if he would never revisit his former home.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Invenit, ac præsentaneam quidem: nam ubi ectypon amplexa est, capitique applicuit, repente liberata est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040711Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040711Z">
        <seg>She found it, and indeed immediately: for when she embraced the ectypon and pressed it to her head, she was suddenly freed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Invicto animo vix imitabile suæ sanctæ magistræ exemplum&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; secuta in ædibus Isabellæ de Mexia nauseam stomachi heroïco actu superavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042437Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042437Z">
        <seg>With an unconquerable spirit, scarcely imitating the example of her holy mistress, she overcame a stomachache in the house of Isabella of Mexico with a heroic act.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Invisente eam Priore Dominicani cœnobii Limæ, cui per quinquennium alias suæ conscientiæ latebras aperuerat, humilibus precibus ab eo obtinuit perlegi&lt;a0&gt;*&lt;/a0&gt; formulam exactam, qua jam moriens veniam a Deo exoraret pro omnibus, qui eam quocumque modo in suæ vitæ decursu læsissent, cujus verba voce sequebatur Virgo Crucifixum stringens, nec satiari poterat illis tenerrimis vocibus: “Pater ignosce illis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042821Z">
        <seg>When the Prior of the Dominican monastery in Lima, to whom she had for five years otherwise revealed the secrets of her conscience, visited her, she obtained from him by humble prayers that he should read it through&lt;a0&gt;*&lt;/a0&gt; the exact formula by which, already dying, she would beg God's pardon for all who had in any way offended her in the course of her life, the words of which the Virgin, clasping the Crucifix, followed with her voice, and could not be satiated with those most tender words: "Father, forgive them."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Invisit ægrum P. M.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012315Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012315Z">
        <seg>He visited the sick P. M.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inæstimabiles divini amoris thesauri, &amp; connubialia tanti Sponsi maxima in Rosam munera tam largiter depluerunt, ut ad ea imposterum occultanda humilioribus adhuc exercitiis continuo vacando, &amp; de se abjectissime sentiendo, ac in omnibus sui contemptum sibi comparandum esse cognoverit, &amp; reipsa opere compleverit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041516Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041516Z">
        <seg>The inestimable treasures of divine love and the greatest nuptial gifts of so great a Spouse were poured out upon the Rose so lavishly that, in order to hide them thereafter, she continually devoted herself to ever humbler exercises, and by thinking the most contemptible thoughts of herself, she came to know that she must acquire self-contempt in all things, and in fact she accomplished it by her deeds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipsa diligenter collectis undequaque monilibus, velis, torquibus, floccis, lemniscis, omnique pretiosa supellectile (cujus nulla umquam pars amissa fuit, quin tota rediret ad dominos) operi strenue incumbebat, assumptis fidissimis adjutricibus in laboris ac meriti participium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055033Z">
        <seg>She herself, having diligently collected from every quarter jewels, veils, torques, flocks, love-knots, and all other precious furnishings (of which no part was ever lost without the whole being returned to its owners), eagerly applied herself to the task, taking on the most faithful helpers as partners in the labor and its reward.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipsa rem sic interpretata est, non ut omnino sibi catena parceret, sed tantum non cæderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041653Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041653Z">
        <seg>She interpreted the matter thus: not that the chain would spare her altogether, but only that it would not kill her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipsa, hoc urbanissime recusato, potius deposcebat familiarem sibi adferri dapem, panem &amp; aquam: his allatis, velut pollucibiliter deliciatura, panis medullam intingebat frigidæ, eaque sumpta, post modicum surgebat e lecto, quasi refecta, satisque opipare recreata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041104Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041104Z">
        <seg>She herself, most urbanely declining this, would rather request that familiar fare be brought to her—bread and water. When these were brought, as if to indulge herself deliciously, she would dip the heart of the bread into the cold water, and having taken it, after a little she would rise from her bed, as though refreshed and amply restored.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipse confessarius, qui Rosæ arcanum hoc pœnitentiæ genus permiserat (nam citra illius nutum Virgo morigera nihil aggrediebatur vel minimum) obiter dumtaxat aculeati gestaminis conscius, neque coronam viderat, neque vel tenui suspicione attigerat peregrinas inventiones, quibus hanc Rosa in se consueverat atrocius irritare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042217Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042217Z">
        <seg>The confessor himself, who had permitted Rose this secret form of penance (for without his nod the obedient Virgin would undertake nothing, not even the slightest thing), merely aware in passing of the thorny garment, had neither seen the crown nor even in the faintest suspicion touched the foreign contrivances by which this Rose had trained herself to provoke it more atrociously within herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipse vero solius Rosæ beneficio tam se illæsum professus est, ut nec phlebotomiam, nec quidquam fomentorum sibi adhiberi permiserit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034311Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034311Z">
        <seg>He himself, by the sole benefit of the rose, declared himself so uninjured that he would allow neither phlebotomy nor any application of fomentations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipsemet doctor Joannes de Castillo (quem Rosa ob notam viri sanctimoniam tanti faciebat) conabatur Virginem ab hac persuasione divertere, quod opinaretur difficultates plane insuperabiles fore.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062756Z">
        <seg>Doctor John de Castillo himself (whom Rosa held in such high esteem for his renowned piety) was trying to dissuade the Virgin from this conviction, believing the difficulties to be utterly insurmountable.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipsemet ultro in postrema anni MDCXXXI depositione coram iisdem judicibus persancte asseveravit, integro sex mensium spatio interdiu noctuque se divinitus admissum ad tantæ beatitatis spectaculum, ac toties, quoties mentem destinatis horis elevabat in adyta&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; contemplationis, ostensam sibi fuisse Rosam inter agmina beatorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031931Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031931Z">
        <seg>He himself, of his own accord, in the final deposition of the year 1631 before the same judges, most solemnly affirmed that, for a full space of six months, day and night, he had been divinely admitted to the spectacle of such great beatitude, and that as often as he raised his mind to the innermost recesses of contemplation at the appointed hours, the Rose was shown to him among the ranks of the blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipso dein festivissimo Pentecostes die (qui Hispanis&lt;a1&gt;g&lt;/a1&gt; æque ut Italis&lt;a2&gt;h&lt;/a2&gt; &lt;b3&gt;Rosarum pascha&lt;/b3&gt; dicitur) baptizata est Rosa, primoque rore cælestis gratiæ permaduit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211732Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203314Z">
        <seg>Then on the most festive day of Pentecost itself (which to the Spaniards&lt;a1&gt;g&lt;/a1&gt; is called just as to the Italians&lt;a2&gt;h&lt;/a2&gt; the &lt;b3&gt;Feast of the Roses&lt;/b3&gt;), Rose was baptized, and was made entirely wet by the first dew of heavenly grace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipso dein festivissimo Pentecostes die (qui Hispanis&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;g&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; æque ut Italis&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;h&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt; &lt;b9&gt;Rosarum pascha&lt;/b9&gt; dicitur) baptizata est Rosa, primoque rore cælestis gratiæ permaduit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T200406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034637Z">
        <seg>Then on the most festive day of Pentecost itself (which to the Spaniards&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;g&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; is called just as to the Italians&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;h&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt; the &lt;b9&gt;Feast of the Roses&lt;/b9&gt;), Rose was baptized, and was made entirely wet by the first dew of heavenly grace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Irritum cadebat, quidquid sublevandæ molestiæ ingesserat medicina.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040354Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040354Z">
        <seg>Whatever medicine had been administered to relieve the discomfort proved ineffective.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Irruerunt dolores partus, at proles sine motu exanimis, frigida, ponderosa obstabat naturæ conatibus, matremque ultimo vitæ discrimini pariter implicabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040503Z">
        <seg>The pains of labor broke upon her, but the lifeless, cold, and heavy child, without movement, blocked the efforts of nature and likewise involved the mother in the final peril of life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Is erat P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061959Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061959Z">
        <seg>He was P.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Is erat P. Frater Petrus de Loaysa Ordinis Prædicatorum, qui cupiens evidentius experiri, quam firmiter in tantæ promissionis fide sibimet constaret Virgo, in quadam ejus confessione rem puerilem ac mininam cœpit attollere tamquam dubiam, ac solicitiori dignam examine, visurus, quid improvisus nec forte inutilis hic terror operaretur in Virgine, quam tanta (ut noverat) divinæ gratiæ securitas possidebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011748Z">
        <seg>He was Father Brother Peter de Loaysa of the Order of Preachers, who, desiring to experience more clearly how firmly the Virgin stood in the faith of so great a promise, in one of her confessions began to raise a childish and insignificant matter as if it were doubtful, and worthy of a more careful examination, to see what this sudden and not perhaps useless terror would work in the Virgin, whom such security of divine grace, as he knew, possessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Is erat P. M.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031518Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031518Z">
        <seg>He was P. M.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Is erat R.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042303Z">
        <seg>He was R.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Is ex omnibus unicam sibi in donum poposcit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061903Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061903Z">
        <seg>He asked for only one gift from all of them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Is miratus Virginis eximiam pulchritudinem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060607Z">
        <seg>He marveled at the Virgin's exceptional beauty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Is nil moratus, veluti si arcanis Rosæ nuptiis arbiter interfuisset, hæc eademmet verba circumscripsit annulo: “Rosa cordis mei, tu mihi sponsa esto”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044452Z">
        <seg>He, without delay, as if he had been the arbiter at Rose's secret wedding, inscribed these very words in a ring: "Rose of my heart, be my bride."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Is vero de statu rei, ardentissimoque omnium studio admonitus, confestim eadem die XXVII Februarii MDCXIX decretum expediri jussit hujus tenoris: “Coram illustrissimo Dom.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031354Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031354Z">
        <seg>He, however, informed of the state of the matter and urged on by the most ardent zeal of all, immediately on that same day, February 27, 1619, ordered the decree to be issued in the following terms: "Before the Most Illustrious Lord."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Isabellam de Peralta pluribus annis ex acri capitis stillicidio cruciabat immanis gingivarum dolor;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035936Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035936Z">
        <seg>For many years, Isabella de Peralta was tormented by a terrible pain in her gums, caused by an acute head cold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Isabellæ Mexiæ contigisse, quæ vidit, retulit filia Isabellæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051149Z">
        <seg>The daughter of Isabella reported what she saw, that it had happened to Isabella of Mexico.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Isabellæ Mexiæ ignavus hostis Rosam a fronte, &amp; cominus aggredi formidans, a tergo eminus rude saxum in eam libravit, connisu tam impetuoso ac valido, ut euntem solo tenus prostraverit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051952Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051952Z">
        <seg>A cowardly enemy of Isabella of Mexico, fearing to attack Rosa in the front and at close quarters, from behind and at a distance hurled a rough stone at her with such violent and powerful force that it struck her to the ground.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Isabellæ de Mexia quædam ex domesticis famulabus, putri humore, febriumque æstu prætumida;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061333Z">
        <seg>A certain one of Isabella of Mexico's maids, swollen with a putrid humor and feverish heat;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Istud est, quod Virgo tam secure, ac fidenter, tam crebro &amp; palam, tam libenter ac libere dulci suæ patriæ vaticinando promittebat, ea quidem asseverationis firmitate atque constantia, quasi illud jam constructum ante oculos habuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062440Z">
        <seg>This is what the Virgin so securely and confidently, so often and openly, so gladly and freely promised to her sweet homeland by prophesying, with such firmness and constancy of assertion, as if she already had it built before her eyes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita accidit, &amp; quidem hoc ordine: Dominica Palmarum, annuo ritu peracta benedictione, distribuebant ramos sacristæ per chorum &amp; ecclesiam, dum adornatur solemnis processio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044235Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044235Z">
        <seg>Thus it happened, and in this order: on Palm Sunday, after the annual blessing had been performed, the sacristans distributed the branches throughout the choir and the church while the solemn procession was being prepared.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita accidit: nam anno MDCXV, nempe biennio ante Rosæ obitum, binæ sorores Maria &amp; Joanna una cum avia in sanctissimæ Trinitatis monasterio velatæ sunt, ac prior, Mariæ de la O, altera Joannæ a Jesu, ultima Ludovicæ a Cruce nomen assumpsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012538Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012538Z">
        <seg>Thus it happened: for in 1615, that is, two years before Rose's death, two sisters, Mary and Joanna, together with their grandmother, were veiled in the Monastery of the Most Holy Trinity, and the first took the name Mary of the O, the second Joanna of Jesus, and the last took the name Louise of the Cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita accidit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061934Z">
        <seg>So it happened.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita brevissimum somnum Rosa libabat magis, quam capiebat, præsertim hyberno tempore, quo plerumque ex nocturnis frigoribus sic tremebat, rigebat, stupebat, ut surgens pedibus consistere vix posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042923Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042923Z">
        <seg>Thus Rosa took her briefest sleep more in measure than in depth, especially in winter, when she was so chilled, stiffened, and stunned by the night's frosts that upon rising she could scarcely stand on her feet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita cunctorum suffragio uni Rosæ delatum hoc religiosæ pietatis officium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055030Z">
        <seg>Thus, by the unanimous vote of all, this office of religious piety was entrusted to one Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita cupiebat cum S. martyre Ignatio mundus Christi panis inveniri, pro illa charitate, qua Christus Panis angelicus fit panis hominum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060319Z">
        <seg>He so desired that the world might find the Bread of Christ with St. Ignatius the martyr, for that charity by which Christ, the Angelic Bread, becomes the bread of men.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita dum mater egressura vocabat Filiam in comitatum, sufficiebat ostendisse fluidos, noxioque pruritu tumentes oculos, tamquam perferendæ apertæ luci tunc minime pares.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043339Z">
        <seg>Thus, as the mother was about to leave and called her daughter to accompany her, it was enough to show eyes fluid and swollen with a noxious itch, as if they were then in no way fit for the open light to bear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita evenit: nam paulo post mirabiliter coalueruut divisi animi, evanuit pomum discordiæ, dissiliit sathanæ cuneus, qui fissuras diduxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054456Z">
        <seg>Thus it happened: for shortly thereafter the divided minds were wonderfully reconciled, the apple of discord vanished, and Satan's wedge, which had opened the fissures, splintered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita evenit: nam post biennium circiter nata est Ferdinando prima hæc soboles, secumque ab utero matris attulit rosæ typum in vultu tam belle &amp; accurate effigiatum, ut nec penicillo venustius depingi potuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012833Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012833Z">
        <seg>Thus it happened: for about two years later Ferdinand had his first child, and she brought with her from her mother's womb a rose-like feature on her face so beautifully and accurately formed that it could not have been painted more charmingly with a brush.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita fuit: nam matre adhuc disceptante cum filia, pulsatur ad fores.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051713Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051713Z">
        <seg>It was so: for while the mother was still arguing with her daughter, there was a knock at the door.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita hic evenit: nam Gundisalvus imprimis Angelinum Medorium Romanum, qui imaginem pinxerat, in ædes suas advocavit, ut sudoris originem, si forte a natura colorum esset, investigaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053808Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053808Z">
        <seg>This is how it happened: for Gundisalvus first summoned Angelinus Medorius the Roman, who had painted the image, into his house, so that he might investigate the origin of the sweat, if perhaps it was due to the nature of the colors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita nimirum nec indulgebatur quidquam afflicto corpori, &amp; demebatur somno, quod orationi accederet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042505Z">
        <seg>Thus, of course, nothing was permitted to the afflicted body, and sleep was taken away so that it might be devoted to prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita plane accidit: nam paulo post a maternis ædibus translata in dominæ Mariæ Usategui contubernium, pietate matronæ linea supellectile adeo non eguit, ut etiam abundarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061100Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061100Z">
        <seg>And so it happened: for shortly thereafter, having been moved from her mother's house into the household of Lady Maria Usategui, she, by virtue of the matron's piety, lacked no furnishings, indeed had more than enough.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itane oscitasti somnolenta, dum has ineptias tam deformes tua imperita segnities abortiret?” Cui illa respondit mitissime, pariter quidem &amp; sibi, quamquam indoctæ, visum fuisse talem pingendi modum oppido peregrinum ac devium, nec tamen ausam aliter, quam mater præscripserat, acum filumque ducere, aut proprio arbitratu conari in adversa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035545Z">
        <seg>"Did you doze off, sleepyhead, while your unskilled laziness was producing such hideous nonsense?" To this she replied very meekly, and indeed to herself, that although untrained, she had found this way of painting utterly foreign and misguided to the town, yet she had not dared to guide the needle and thread any other way than her mother had prescribed, nor to attempt anything on her own in the opposite manner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque Limanus archiepiscopus D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053901Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053901Z">
        <seg>And so the Archbishop of Lima, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque Limanus archiepiscopus D. Bartholomæus Lobo Guerrero&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;, capiendis legitimis informationibus judicem constituit doctorem Joannem de la Rocca, parochum &amp; archidiaconum metropolitanum, qui coram notario Jacobo Blanca presbytero, auditis omnium (qui spectaculo interfuerant) unisonis testimoniis, rei gestæ seriem ex ordine totam excepit, ac tabellioni rite in protocollum referendam commisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011013Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011013Z">
        <seg>Therefore, the Archbishop of Lima, D. Bartholomew Lobo Guerrero&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;, for taking the legal depositions, appointed Doctor John de la Rocca, parish priest and metropolitan archdeacon, who, in the presence of the notary James Blanca, priest, and having heard the unanimous testimonies of all who had been present at the spectacle, took down the entire sequence of events in order and committed it to the clerk to be duly entered in the protocol.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque Rosa, mutato consilio, in infimam domus partem descendit, profundioris silentii quæsitura latibulum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052026Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052026Z">
        <seg>So Rosa, having changed her mind, descended to the lowest part of the house, seeking a hiding place of deeper silence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque Rosæ in cibum placuerunt hujus benedicti floris non grana, quia dulcia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041256Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041256Z">
        <seg>And so the roses were pleasing to eat, not the grains of this blessed flower, because they were sweet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque Rosæ lectulum ærumnis trium lustrorum gravem cupidæ matri dedunt in direptionem ac prædam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042852Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042852Z">
        <seg>And so they hand over Rose's little bed, heavy with the hardships of three lustrums, to the greedy mother for plunder and spoil.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque Virginem per tenebras aggressus est tenebrio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051921Z">
        <seg>And so the dark one attacked the Virgin in the darkness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque Virgo, ut matri satisfaceret, post diurni laboris pensum, aliquam noctis partem conficiendis altarium ornamentis seponebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060124Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060124Z">
        <seg>Thus the Virgin, in order to please her mother, would set aside part of the night after her daily labor to make altar ornaments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque a P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044938Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044938Z">
        <seg>And so from P.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque a P. magistro F. Joanne de Lorenzana&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; grandius supplementum obtinuit, donec summa numeri ad centum quinquaginta pertingeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010210Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010210Z">
        <seg>Thus, from Father Master Francis John of Lorenzana, a larger supplement was obtained, until the total number reached one hundred and fifty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque ad famam defunctæ Rosæ cum reliqua turba penetravit in ecclesiam divi&lt;a0&gt;*&lt;/a0&gt; Dominici, commendavit se sanctæ Virgini, exporrexit illico sanatum brachium, illudque in sublime elatum magnis vocibus ostentavit multitudini circumfusæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043209Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043209Z">
        <seg>And so, to the fame of the late Rose, he entered the church of Saint Dominic with the rest of the crowd, commended himself to the Holy Virgin, immediately stretched out his healed arm, and, raising it high, displayed it to the surrounding multitude with loud cries.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque coram sacra imagine (quæ adstabat mensulæ imposita) procubuit, breviterque oravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055359Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055359Z">
        <seg>And so he knelt before the sacred image (which stood upon a small table) and prayed briefly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque dispositis omnibus, dum inchoandum erat Officium sepulturæ, quo Rosæ corpus e templo efferretur ad fossam in aula capitulari præparatam, subito per omnes latissimæ ecclesiæ angulos tanta insonuit promiscuæ multitudinis vociferatio, tam inconditus ac flebilis deprecantium ululatus, ut metu confusionis ac tumultus supersedendum, funebrique processione abstinendum fuerit, admonito populo, usque in posterum diem charissimi illius pignoris sepulturam differri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030709Z">
        <seg>And so, with everything arranged, as the Office of Burial was about to begin, by which Rose's body was to be carried from the temple to the grave prepared in the chapter house, suddenly throughout the farthest corners of the vast church there resounded such a clamor from the mingled multitude, such a disjointed and mournful wailing of those who pleaded, that for fear of confusion and tumult it was deemed best to desist, and the funeral procession was stayed, the people being informed that the burial of that most dear pledge would be postponed until the following day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque examini datum initium anno MDCXXX, die XVII Maii;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031639Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031639Z">
        <seg>And so the examination was begun on May 17, 1630.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque hanc lumbis suis triplicato circumductu fortissime adstrinxit, extremis commissuræ ansulis seram immisit, claviculam abdidit, ubi recuperare nec ipsa posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041656Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041656Z">
        <seg>And so she fastened it most securely around her waist with a triple wrap, inserted the key into the little loop at the seam's end, and hid the key where even she herself could not retrieve it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque hoc scrupulo saucia, &amp; animo inquieta, sacellum Dei Genitricis in æde FF.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034727Z">
        <seg>Thus, wounded by this scruples and troubled in mind, she entered the chapel of the Mother of God in the house of the Fathers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque hoc scrupulo saucia, &amp; animo inquieta, sacellum Dei Genitricis in æde FF. Prædicatorum SS. Rosario dicatum adiit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203315Z">
        <seg>[4] [so that she would first be called Rosa,] However, after several years had passed, the Virgin herself, with a fearful conscience, brought another suit concerning the name Rosa, namely to provide an occasion and a place for a heavenly oracle by which the name Rosa was to be finally confirmed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque humilis Rosa voce tremula flebilique supplex obsecravit domesticos, secum rogarent Deum, ut ex ea dumtaxat parte mitigaret vim morborum, qua menti, liberoque usui rationis nocere poterant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025714Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025714Z">
        <seg>So humble Rosa, with a trembling and pitiful voice, implored her household to pray with her to God to temper the power of diseases, so that they might harm only that part of her which could affect her mind and the free use of her reason.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque in Gundisalvi conjugem obtutu miserabili, cernuo, lachrymoso, frequenter, languide, dolorose respiciens, orabat supplex, tantillum aquæ ad refrigerium sibi porrigi, fel (cui pridem assueverat) cum aceto jam in deliciis reputatura, si impetrare potuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025745Z">
        <seg>And so, looking at his wife Gundisalva with a miserable, downcast, tearful gaze—often, languidly, and painfully—he prayed imploringly for a little water to refresh himself, considering bile (to which he had long been accustomed) along with vinegar a real delicacy, if he could have obtained it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque invitam aliquoties adegit imperio, certa, edicto sine replica obedituram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043323Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043323Z">
        <seg>And so he compelled her, unwilling as she was, several times by his authority, certain that she would obey the order without reply.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque jussus fuit, in causa prædicta bis mille folia intra certum temporis spatium scribendo absolvere, eo quidem pacto, ne illo, cui semper assueverat, charactere uteretur fluido, laxo, facilique, sed alio morosiori, quadro, &amp; adstricto, quem illic NOTHUM appellat amanuensium vulgus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032309Z">
        <seg>He was therefore ordered, in the aforementioned case, to complete two thousand pages in writing within a fixed period of time, on the condition that he not use the fluid, loose, and easy handwriting to which he had always been accustomed, but another, more fussy, square, and cramped style, which the common scribes there call NOTHUM.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque ludum abrupit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034950Z">
        <seg>And so he interrupted the game.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque omni alio catoscopo&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; desperato, Virginis confessarium authoritate, qua valebat, induxit, ut ipse per anfractuosæ quæstionis obliquas ambages, si quæ posset, vel ab invita eliceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203526Z">
        <seg>And so, having despaired of every other catoptroscope, he, by the authority of the Virgin's confessor, which he wielded, compelled her to herself, through the winding, oblique detours of the question, extract whatever she could, even from an unwilling source.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque omni alio catoscopo&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; desperato, Virginis confessarium authoritate, qua valebat, induxit, ut ipse per anfractuosæ quæstionis obliquas ambages, si quæ posset, vel ab invita eliceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040819Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040819Z">
        <seg>And so, having despaired of every other catoptroscope, he, by the authority of the Virgin's confessor, which he wielded, compelled her to herself, through the winding, oblique detours of the question, extract whatever she could, even from an unwilling source.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque primam sibi tunc coronam (de secunda agetur paulo inferius) ex ductili stanno complicuit, quæ seu funium, seu virgularum mutuo implexu contortarum, spiram effigiabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042056Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042056Z">
        <seg>And so he then fashioned his first crown (the second will be discussed a little later) from malleable tin, which, whether of cords or of rods twisted together, formed a spiral.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque præfecta Lucia de SS. Trinitate ad pendulam illic Rosæ jam defunctæ imaginem conversa, sub ingenti cogitationum tumultu erupit in hæc verba: “Audito, Rosa benedicta, tibi ego præcipio sub illa obedientia, quæ mihi in hoc cœnobio debetur ab omnibus, faxis inveniatur argentum, quod perdidimus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035650Z">
        <seg>And so, Prefect Lucia of the Holy Trinity, turning to the portrait of the late Rosa hanging there, broke out amid a great tumult of thoughts into these words: "Hear me, blessed Rosa, I command you, by the obedience owed to me in this convent by all, to have the silver we lost found."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque simplex ac timida negavit tandem, quidpiam sibi innotuisse de prædestinationis subtilissima notione.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061829Z">
        <seg>And so the simple and timid woman finally denied that she knew anything about the most subtle notion of predestination.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque socias obiter secum in D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060211Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060211Z">
        <seg>And so, incidentally, he takes his associates with him to D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque socias obiter secum in D. Hieronymi sacellum traxit, illic exporrecto in hilaritatem vultu, pavidas hortari cœpit ad subeundum secum martyrium, occasionem hanc nimis pretiosam esse ac fortunatam, videlicet non quomodocumque, sed sub ipso sanctæ Hostiæ conspectu nostiam cadere, coram &amp; pro divina illa Victima victimam cædi, pro exposito Christi Corpore animam exponere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011422Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011422Z">
        <seg>And so she drew her companions with her into the chapel of St. Jerome, where, with a face beaming with joy, she began to urge the fearful ones to undergo martyrdom with her, saying that this was an opportunity too precious and fortunate, that is to say, not just any kind of martyrdom, but to fall under the very gaze of the Holy Host, to be slain before and for that divine Victim, to lay down one's life for the exposed Body of Christ.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque tam rarum pudicitiæ donum, tam humilis ac pura virginitas, a primo ætatulæ suæ quinquennio jam Christo sacrata per votum, flos simul &amp; dos erat futuro Sponsi Nazareni connubio: Rosa quantum amabat lilium inter spinas, tam abjecte sentiebat de seipsa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044101Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044101Z">
        <seg>Thus such a rare gift of chastity, such humble and pure virginity, consecrated to Christ by a vow from the first five years of her life, was both a flower and a dowry for the future marriage of the Nazarene Spouse: as the rose loved the lily among thorns, so she felt herself to be despicable.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque tenerrimis blanditiis genitricem obsedit, donec permissionem eliceret, qua decolorem rudemque saccum ex obvia vilique materia assumeret pro amictu, quod vestimenti genus nec immodestum est, nec inusitatum feminis, quæ a pompis, &amp; nuptiis alienæ, sub simplicissimo cultu solitariam nituntur sectari devotionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035443Z">
        <seg>And so he besieged his mother with the most tender flatteries until he had elicited permission to take a plain, unadorned sack of coarse, undyed cloth for a cloak—a type of garment that is neither immodest nor unusual for women who, estranged from pomp and nuptials, strive under the simplest attire to pursue a solitary devotion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque tunc, velut inscia mysterii, calente vino caput filiæ leniter abluit, quantum læsis partibus sat fore ad medelam aut credebat, aut credere simulabat, aliud interim adversus ipsam coronam r emedium excogitatura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042254Z">
        <seg>And so then, as if unaware of the mystery, she gently washed her daughter's head with hot wine, believing—or pretending to believe—that it would be enough to heal the wounded parts, while she devised another remedy against the crown itself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque ubi tantilla ab incursibus libertas fuit, repente adornatur funebris processio, silent campanæ, at cantu submisso psallunt Fratres;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031146Z">
        <seg>And so, whenever there was a brief respite from the raids, a funeral procession would suddenly be prepared, the bells would fall silent, and the Brothers would chant in a subdued voice.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque ut alias usa ingenio, selectis ex Ordine Prædicatorum quatuor præstantissimis theologis, rem permisit examini, pollicita id amplecti, quod ipsorum potiora suffragia diffinirent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040304Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040304Z">
        <seg>And so, as she had done on other occasions, she entrusted the matter to the examination of four of the most eminent theologians selected from the Order of Preachers, promising to embrace whatever their majority vote might decide.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque vernant hic omnia: siquidem &amp; progenitores Virgini fuere Gaspar de Floribus, Maria ab Oliva;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211802Z">
        <seg>Therefore, everything here is in springtime: for indeed the Virgin's ancestors were Gaspar de Floribus, Maria ab Oliva;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque ægra de creberrimis Rosæ edocta miraculis, pulverem Lima sibi allatum in lympha cum fide bibit, &amp; repente febrim tunc frigore per medullas sævientem, funditus extinxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035858Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035858Z">
        <seg>Thus, the sick woman, informed of Rose's most frequent miracles, drank with faith the powder brought to her from Lima in water, and instantly extinguished the fever that was raging through her very marrow with a chill.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque ægrum extremi periculi admonuit P. magister Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034032Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034032Z">
        <seg>And so Master Father admonished the sick man of the extreme danger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque, &amp; ipsa novellam sponsam affata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044411Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044411Z">
        <seg>And so, she herself addressed the new bride;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque, peracta sacra exomologesi, Franciscum de Coloma presbyterum obnixe rogavit Catharina, tantisper sibi afferret, quam domi suæ habebat, Rosæ effigiem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040649Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040649Z">
        <seg>Therefore, after the sacred confession was completed, Catherine earnestly begged Father Francis de Coloma to bring her, for a short time, the image of the Rose that he had in his home.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque, ubi coronæ seu bracteæ extrema coibant, decussatim tænias innexuit, quarum attractu non solum cohibebat cuspidatum hunc circulum, ne aut loco exerraret, aut sponte laxaretur, verum etiam quoties lubebat dolorem intendere, tæniarum capita strictius adducendo, ipsi tormento suo vim faciebat, præcipue vero sextis feriis placebat Virgini hoc pœnarum epimetron, in memoriam Sponsi, sentibus coronati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042142Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042142Z">
        <seg>Therefore, where the ends of the crowns or plates met, he fastened bands crosswise, by the tension of which he not only held this pointed circle in place, so that it would neither slip from its position, or loosen of its own accord, but also, whenever she wished, she would intensify the pain by drawing the ends of the bands tighter, thus applying force with her own torture; and on the sixth day, in particular, the Virgin delighted in this epimetron of punishments, in memory of the Spouse, crowned with thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque, vidua suggerente, cernua sursum respexit in Rosam, promittens una cum filio Novenam peragere ad sepulchrum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034212Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034212Z">
        <seg>And so, at the widow's suggestion, she looked up at Rosa, promising to make the Novena to the tomb together with her son.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Item alio anno, totam ejusdem quinquagenæ intercapedinem decurrit prorsus sine potu, ne guttulam [quidem] aquæ sorbens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041336Z">
        <seg>Also, in another year, he ran the entire length of that same fifty-mile stretch completely without drinking, not even sipping a drop of water.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Item, suis demeritis illum apud inferos deberi locum, qui omnium sit infimus &amp; summe infamis: vilissimum se terræ pondus, abominabile generis humani carcinoma, purulentam sentinam ac fæcem esse, indignam, cui luceant cæli, cui aspiret aër, cui tellus dorsum substernat: suo contagio inquinari elementa, suis piaculis gravari solum, amarescere oceanum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040540Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040540Z">
        <seg>Likewise, by his own demerits, he deserves in the underworld the place that is the lowest of all and most infamous: the vilest burden of the earth, an abominable cancer of the human race, a purulent sewer and dregs, unworthy of the light of heaven, of the breath of air, or of the earth's sustaining back: that by his contagion the elements are polluted, the soil burdened by his crimes, and the ocean made bitter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Iteratur bis, ter, frustrata perquisitio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055132Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055132Z">
        <seg>The search is repeated twice, three times, in vain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Iterum obedit Rosa, sed ultra litteram;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042812Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042812Z">
        <seg>Rosa obeys again, but beyond the letter;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itum deinde in preces &amp; lachrymas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040512Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040512Z">
        <seg>Then it proceeded to prayers and tears;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Iverunt, &amp;, ut Virgo dixerat, invenerunt tria caryophylla de illomet, quem pridie Rosa indicarat, cauliculo, trepidæ carpserunt, apportarunt, suæque diffidentiæ veniam precatæ, cum Rosa in gratiarum actionem Deo se prostraverunt, simul admirabundæ ac lætæ, quod illa die sacra imago cum ornatu tam peregrino in publicum erat proditura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055210Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055210Z">
        <seg>They went, and, as the Virgin had said, they found three cloves of that very thing which Rosa had indicated the day before, plucked them from the trembling stalk, brought them, and, begging pardon for their own distrust, They prostrated themselves with Rose in thanksgiving to God, at once amazed and joyful that on that sacred day the image, with such a foreign adornment, was to be brought out in public.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ivit illa, viditque imaginis vultum insolito splendore fulgidius radiare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055107Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055107Z">
        <seg>She went, and saw the face of the image shining more brightly with unusual splendor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ivit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034534Z">
        <seg>He went;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jaciebant pariter sua retia Hieronymus &amp; Indus, atque hic prædam aliquam traxit, ille vacuo reti adfremuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033859Z">
        <seg>Jerome and Indus were casting their nets together, and Indus hauled in some catch, while Jerome, with his net empty, grumbled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jacuit ab illo vespere usque in claram posteræ diei lucem exanime corpusculum sine calore, sine motu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033124Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033124Z">
        <seg>The little body lay lifeless from that evening until the bright light of the next day, without warmth or movement.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jam ante (nimirum XI Decembris anno MDCXVII) id ipsum judicibus indicarat P. Antonius de la Vega Loaysa Societatis Jesu, quondam sancti Officii commissarius, diversorumque collegiorum successive Rector, qui &amp; singulariter ponderandum esse monuit, hoc genus beneficii inter validiora &amp; excellentiora solidæ sanctitatis argumenta ex communi sanctorum Doctorum sententia merito computari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032606Z">
        <seg>As early as December 11, 1617, Father Anthony de la Vega Loaysa of the Society of Jesus, formerly commissioner of the Holy Office and successive rector of various colleges, had already informed the judges of this very fact, and he also warned that this kind of favor should be regarded as one of the strongest and most excellent arguments for solid sanctity, rightly counted among the common judgments of the holy Doctors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jam decreta erat executionis dies, &amp; novitiorum magistro Petro de Loaysa injuncta dimissio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012427Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012427Z">
        <seg>The day of execution had already been decreed, and the novice master, Peter of Loaysa, was ordered to dismiss him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jam desierat matrem ad ejuscemodi officia foras comitari, sed ne ipsa domi visitaretur, effugere cupiebat, nec poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043447Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043447Z">
        <seg>She had already stopped accompanying her mother to such duties outside, but she wanted to escape being visited at home herself, and she could not.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jam foras prodierat intestinum (evidens imminentis lethi indicium) &amp; adhuc detorta infantis obliquitas suæmet obstabat genesi, propiusque exitium, quam exitum reperiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035111Z">
        <seg>The intestine had already protruded (an obvious sign of impending death), and the infant's twisted deformity still impeded its own birth, finding more destruction than deliverance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jam in SS. Incarnationis monasterio, (quod sub regula S. Patris Augustini militat) a sanctimonialibus Rosam exoptantibus, admissionem locumque internuntia fratris industria impetrarat, nilque deerat, quam ut relicta paterna domo, clam sine pompa ac strepitu ad cœnobium recta pergeret, valvis patulis admittenda, candidisque ibidem liliis interserenda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040106Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040106Z">
        <seg>Now in the Holy Scriptures At the Monastery of the Incarnation (which follows the Rule of Saint Augustine), the nuns, eager for a rose, had by the brothers' diligence secured her admission and a place; and nothing remained but for her to leave her father's house and secretly, without pomp or noise, make her way to the convent, to be admitted through its open gates and planted among the white lilies there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jam infirma cibi somnique incapax ad ultimum vitæ marginem festinabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040813Z">
        <seg>Already frail, unable to eat or sleep, she was hastening to the very brink of life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jam pene convaluerat, &amp; denuo invitam ad carnis ferculum compulerunt: denuo item rediere prædicta symptomata, nec remedium nisi ab usitato panis &amp; aquæ pastu superfuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041055Z">
        <seg>She had almost recovered, and once again they forced her, unwillingly, to eat a meat dish. The aforementioned symptoms returned again, and there was no remedy except the usual diet of bread and water.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jam perplexi hærebant medici, &amp; pharmaca adhibere destiterant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012334Z">
        <seg>The doctors were already perplexed and had stopped administering medicines.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jam vultu pallida distabescebat lethalibus deliquiis, omnis arteriarum pulsus interquieverat, spes vitæ exciderat, quando afflicta mater promptam e scriniolo chartam, Rosæ effigie ruditer lineatam, attulit moribundæ filiæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040509Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040509Z">
        <seg>Already pale-faced, she was sinking into deadly fainting spells; every pulse in her arteries had ceased, all hope of life was gone, when her anguished mother brought from her little case a hastily drawn paper bearing Rosa's likeness and handed it to her dying daughter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jesule;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053244Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053244Z">
        <seg>Jesus, little one</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jesuli somnum, &amp; Mariæ vigilias ignem suis medullis injicere, ut nec parvuli mansuetissimum soporem audeat interrumpere, nec tamen vigilem Matrem possit sine canoro succensi cordis alloquio præterire;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054515Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054515Z">
        <seg>To kindle in Jesus sleep, and in Mary watchings a fire in their very marrow, so that neither the little one dares to interrupt the most gentle slumber, nor yet can the watchful Mother pass by without a song-filled, ardent heart's discourse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jesus mecum sit, Amen”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035021Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035021Z">
        <seg>Jesus be with me, Amen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joachimus Brulius Augustinianus in Peruana sui Ordinis Historia lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041833Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041833Z">
        <seg>Joachim Bruliau the Augustinian, in the History of his Order in Peru, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joanna, tam inops consilii, quam periculo &amp; calamitati impar, non inveniebat rationem, qua discrimini occurreret citra pejus discrimen: nam ut eo, ubi habitabat ultra amnem, quotidie accerseret ex urbe medicum, obstabat viæ longinquitas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061153Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061153Z">
        <seg>Joanna, as lacking in counsel as she was unprepared for danger and calamity, could find no way to meet the crisis without bringing on an even worse one: for to summon a doctor from the city each day to the place where she lived across the river, the long distance was an obstacle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joanne Miguel Ord.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051428Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051428Z">
        <seg>Joanne Miguel, Order</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joanne de Lorenzana&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; grandius supplementum obtinuit, donec summa numeri ad centum quinquaginta pertingeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044945Z">
        <seg>Joanne de Lorenzana received a larger supplement, until the total number reached one hundred and fifty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannem de Palomares tertiana cum asthmate morti destinabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040032Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040032Z">
        <seg>John of Palomares was doomed to die of tertian fever with asthma.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050801Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050801Z">
        <seg>John the Latter, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes Miguel Ordinis Prædicatorum, [Quemdam F. Dominicanum periculo liberat,] causa œconomiæ ac negotiorum, obequitabat vallem de Xauxa&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033827Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033827Z">
        <seg>John Miguel of the Order of Preachers, [He rescues a certain Dominican friar from danger,] was riding through the valley of Xauxa&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;on account of the economy and business affairs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes Perez: at primorum duorum examen tot ac tanta complectitur, ut ex reliquis nil addi possit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050225Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050225Z">
        <seg>John Perez: for the examination of the first two encompasses so much and so many things that nothing can be added from the rest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes Villalobos Societatis Jesu jurata fide testatus est, suo se experimento spiritum propheticum in ea deprehendisse, aperiente illi Virgine arcanum negotium, quod aliter ac cælitus innotuisse non poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042632Z">
        <seg>John Villalobos, under oath, testified that by his own experience he had discerned a prophetic spirit in her, the Virgin having revealed to him a secret matter that he could not have known otherwise than by a heavenly revelation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Aguero præsentatus, &amp; P. Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035638Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035638Z">
        <seg>John of Aguero was presented, and Father Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Laet lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035052Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035052Z">
        <seg>John of Laet, book</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Lorenzana Ordinis prædicatorum (testante eodem illustrissimo præsule) eumdem cum D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050123Z">
        <seg>John of Lorenzana of the Order of Preachers (as attested by the same most illustrious prelate) the same with D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Lorenzana confessarius Virginis, &lt;s2&gt;[ferreo flagello aliisque modis,]&lt;/s2&gt; cum hunc disciplinarum rigorem imbecillitati corporis paulo æquius censuisset attemperandum, Rosa maximis ab eo precibus extorsit, quo saltem liceret sibi intra certum dierum spatium verbera quinquies mille sibi infligere, qui pie creditur esse numerus ictuum, quos in sua crudeli flagellatione Christus excepit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041600Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041600Z">
        <seg>John of Lorenzana, the Virgin's confessor, &lt;s2&gt;[with an iron scourge and other methods,]&lt;/s2&gt; When John of Lorenzana, the Virgin's confessor, &lt;s2&gt;[with an iron scourge and other means,]&lt;/s2&gt; judged that this severity of discipline should be tempered somewhat more justly to her bodily frailty, Rose extorted from him by the most earnest prayers that she might at least be permitted, within a certain space of days, to inflict upon herself five thousand lashes, which is piously believed to be the number of blows that Christ received in his own cruel scourging.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Lorenzana simillimo ostento feliciter perculsus occasionem inde obtinuit penitius Rosam cognoscendi, quam illactenus numquam de facie noverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055811Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055811Z">
        <seg>John of Lorenzana, happily struck by a very similar vision, thereby had the opportunity to get to know Rosa more deeply, whom until then he had never known face to face.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Lorenzana, Gundisalvus de la Massa quæstor regius, &amp; quæstoris uxor Maria de Usatigui.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043608Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043608Z">
        <seg>John of Lorenzana, Gundisalvus de la Massa, royal treasurer, and the treasurer's wife, Maria de Usatigui.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Lorenzana, ambo Virginis olim confessarii, de quibus supra.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032615Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032615Z">
        <seg>John of Lorenzana, formerly confessor of both Virgins, about whom mentioned above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Lorenzana, cui in Rosam totum, usque ad extrema, competebat imperium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050023Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050023Z">
        <seg>John of Lorenzana, to whom the entire Rose, down to its very extremities, was subject.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Villalobos&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, Rector Limensis collegii Societatis Jesu, Vir sanctimonia ac religione celeberrimus, &amp; animarum per tutiora compendia perfectionis deducendarum auriga peritissimus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042307Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042307Z">
        <seg>John of Villalobos, Rector of the Society of Jesus's college in Lima, a man celebrated for his holiness and piety, and a most skilled guide in leading souls to perfection by safer paths.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joanni Miguel Ordinis sancti Dominici Limam revertenti seriatim &amp; exacte narravit ea, quæ illi in aliena regione acciderant, quæque humanitus non potuerant in Rosæ notitiam pervenisse: quin &amp; abditissimas suæmet conscientiæ latebras Virgini patuisse evidenter agnovit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042645Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042645Z">
        <seg>As John Miguel of the Order of Saint Dominic was returning to Lima, he recounted in sequence and in detail to him the things that had happened to him in a foreign land, which for human reasons had not been able to come to Rose's knowledge; indeed, he clearly recognized that the most hidden recesses of his own conscience had been laid open to the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jocus erat, ac puerilis ridendi materies;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034944Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034944Z">
        <seg>It was a joke, and a childish cause for laughter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joseph de Castro confestim ut bibit, noxias cruditates omnes evomuit, &amp; valuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040013Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040013Z">
        <seg>Joseph de Castro immediately vomited all the harmful crudities as soon as he drank, and recovered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jubentibus confessariis obtemperans Virgo, conabatur ex his nonnullos indicare;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055851Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055851Z">
        <seg>Obeying the orders of the confessors, the Virgin tried to point out some of them from among these.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Juravit tamen primus medicorum, peculiarem simul interhalasse suaveolentiam, quæ præsentium non olfactum dumtaxat mirifice recrearit, sed &amp; animos improviso solatio perfuderit, atque arcano devotionis sensu omnes demulserit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031655Z">
        <seg>Yet the foremost of the physicians swore that he had infused a peculiar fragrance which, while not merely wonderfully refreshing the senses of those present, had also unexpectedly flooded their spirits with solace and, by a mysterious sense of devotion, had moved them all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jurejurando testata est genitrix (in Processu Remissoriali) sibi fuisse hunc partum, ut felicissimum, ita facillimum, minimeque obnoxium illis angoribus, quibuscum solebat in parturitione reliquarum prolium dirissime conflictari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211817Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211817Z">
        <seg>The mother testified under oath (in the Remissorial Process) that this birth had been for her, as the most joyful, so the easiest, and least subject to those agonies with which she was wont to struggle most terribly in the childbirth of her other children.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jussa semel a confessario, alias a matre, enarrare suos dolores, licet omnium fere ægritudinum vim, &amp; nomina per experientiam cognovisset, hunc tamen non nisi per similitudines candentis ferri tempora adigentis, &amp; pugionis igniti a sinistro latere meditullium corporis transverberantis, in quo Crucifixi erat expressio, &amp; candentis galeæ caput includentis, explicare non potuit, simulque singulos nervos, juncturas, ossa, ac medullas contundi, resolvi, &amp; aruisse dixit, ac denique non habere nomen, nec nosse tormentum, cui eos compararet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042731Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042731Z">
        <seg>Ordered by her confessor once, by her mother at other times, to recount her pains, though she had endured the force of nearly all illnesses, and though he had learned their names by experience, he could not explain this one except by likening it to the times of a white-hot iron pressing in, and a flaming dagger piercing the body's midriff from the left side, in which the image of the Crucified One was imprinted, and a white-hot helmet enclosing the head, and at the same time each nerve, joint, and bone, and said that the nerves, joints, bones, and marrow were crushed, dislocated, and burned, and that in the end it had no name, nor did he know of any torture to compare it to.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Juvenis coram Virgine sedere jussus, comiter ex urbano ritu sciscitari cœpit, qui valeret, venisse se pro demandando opere conficiendorum aliquot collarium: petiit in singula quot ulnæ melioris telæ forent necessariæ, quibus impendiis, quo loco aptius emenda materies, intra quod tempus absolvi possit opus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060620Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060620Z">
        <seg>The young man, having been ordered to sit before the Virgin, politely began, in the urban fashion, to inquire what was required, saying he had come on a commission to have several collars made: he asked how many ells of the finest cloth would be needed for each, at what cost, where the material could most conveniently be purchased, and within what time the work could be completed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laborabat hic Rosa, ut verba reperiret idonea, sed tandem per solos terminos remotionis, eminentiæ, &amp; causalitatis, utcumque se explicuit, insinuans lumen esse absque figura, mensura, fine: unde patuit ex ea parte, visionem pure intellectualem esse, incomprehensum, &amp; omnia comprehendens, subtile, stabile, mundissimum, summe multiplex summeque unum, summe distans &amp; summe propinquum, intimum &amp; ambiens, nobile, excelsum, &amp; nulli prorsus creaturæ vel tenuiter comparabile, quodque magis in vitalium quarumdam effluxionum mirandis &amp; immediatis effectibus, quam in suamet substantia deprehendi possit ab animo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050600Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050600Z">
        <seg>Rosa here labored to find suitable words, but at last, by the terms of remoteness, eminence, and causality alone, however she expressed herself, she insinuated that the light is without figure, measure, or end: from which it became clear on that score that the vision is purely intellectual, incomprehensible, and all-comprehending, subtle, stable, most pure, supremely manifold and supremely one, most distant and most near, intimate and surrounding, noble, exalted, and in no way at all comparable to any creature, even faintly, which can be apprehended by the mind more in the marvelous and immediate effects of certain vital effluxes than in its own substance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laborabat sub gaudiorum mole, nec se capiebat Rosa, experta verissime dici: “Sit licet ignis amor, non leve pondus habet”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044421Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044421Z">
        <seg>Rosa was laboring under a burden of joy, and could not contain herself, for she truly knew it to be said: "Though love is a fire, it bears no light weight."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Labore manuum eorum inopiam levem reddebat, prudens, &amp; providens in removendo quidquid eis ingratum, aut inquietum esse posset, in pacandis contentionibus, &amp; sedandis animis mirifice discreta.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041213Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041213Z">
        <seg>Through their labor, they alleviated their need; prudent and provident in removing whatever might be disagreeable or unsettling to them, and wonderfully discreet in pacifying disputes and calming minds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lachrymas, quia unice ad Dei ærarium pertinere didicerat, non patiebatur alio a quoquam inutiliter profundi, cupiens has soli divinæ majestati in tributum ab omnibus reservari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053356Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053356Z">
        <seg>Tears, because he had learned that they belonged exclusively to God's treasury, he would not allow anyone to shed them in vain, desiring that they be reserved by all as a tribute to the divine majesty alone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Langueo, deficiunt oculi, præcordia frigent,</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051003Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051003Z">
        <seg>I languish, my eyes fail, my vitals grow cold,</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Latine hæc satis efferri non possunt, quæ in Hispano Rosæ idiomate, propriis numeris venustius illigabantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045348Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045348Z">
        <seg>In Latin, these cannot be rendered adequately, since in the Spanish dialect of Rosæ they were more charmingly bound by their own numbers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Latinis rei tam sero visæ proprium adhuc deesse nomen crediderim, nisi quod supplente periphrasi &lt;b1&gt;florem Passionis&lt;/b1&gt; vocitet fidelis Europa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203332Z">
        <seg>I believe that the Latins still lack a proper name for a thing so late discovered, except that faithful Europe, by way of a circumlocution, calls it the Flower of Passion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Latinis rei tam sero visæ proprium adhuc deesse nomen crediderim, nisi quod supplente periphrasi &lt;b5&gt;florem Passionis&lt;/b5&gt; vocitet fidelis Europa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041247Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041247Z">
        <seg>I believe that the Latins still lack a proper name for a thing so late discovered, except that faithful Europe, by way of a circumlocution, calls it the Flower of Passion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Latuisset perpetuo nobile hoc stratagema, ni postea manu aliena sat operose extrahenda fuisset acus, quæ sub florido blandimento profunde sævierat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035319Z">
        <seg>This noble stratagem would have remained hidden forever, had it not later been so laboriously extracted by a foreign hand—the needle that had deeply gnawed beneath a flowery blandishment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Latuit ejus matrem generosum facinus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061417Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061417Z">
        <seg>His mother's noble deed was hidden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laudabat assidue priscorum eremicolarum felicissima tempora, suspirabat ad omnem Nitriæ ac Thebaïdis&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; mentionem, &amp;, ut verbulo absolvam, migrarant in Rosam ignita S. Catharinæ Senensis desideria, quibus olim antra deserti haud perfunctorie depoposcerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010018Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010018Z">
        <seg>He constantly praised the most blessed times of the ancient hermits, sighed at every mention of Nitria and Thebaid, and, to sum up in a word, his desires had migrated to the burning Rose of St. Catherine of Siena, for whom he had once earnestly longed for the caves of the desert.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laudabat assidue priscorum eremicolarum felicissima tempora, suspirabat ad omnem Nitriæ ac Thebaïdis&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; mentionem, &amp;, ut verbulo absolvam, migrarant in Rosam ignita S. Catharinæ Senensis desideria, quibus olim antra deserti haud perfunctorie depoposcerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043953Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043953Z">
        <seg>He constantly praised the most blessed times of the ancient hermits, sighed at every mention of Nitria and Thebaid, and, to sum up in a word, his desires had migrated to the burning Rose of St. Catherine of Siena, for whom he had once earnestly longed for the caves of the desert.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laudavit simplicem obedientis Rosæ candorem doctor, præcepitque, ne trepidaret, nihil hactenus erratum esse, tantum ad reliqua procederet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050532Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050532Z">
        <seg>The doctor praised the simple purity of the obedient Rose and instructed her not to be alarmed, saying that nothing had been done wrong so far and that she should proceed with the rest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laudetur SS. Trinitas, cui in excelso gloriæ fastigio, inter præcipuos paradisi cives assistit Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044548Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044548Z">
        <seg>Praised be the Holy Trinity, before whom Rose stands among the chief citizens of paradise on the highest summit of glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laurentii, &amp;, brevi oratione peracta, accinxit se operi, mirantibus adstantibus, quæstore ipso vidente, manum paulo ante acerbissime læsam, tunc validam &amp; agilem, medico ipso testante evidentiam miraculi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042254Z">
        <seg>Lawrence, and, after a brief speech, set himself to the task, to the amazement of those present, with the quailer himself watching, his hand, which had been grievously wounded only a short time before, now strong and agile, the physician himself bearing witness to the miracle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laus tibi Christe”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054639Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054639Z">
        <seg>Praise to you, Christ.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laxa fibras philomela</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045230Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045230Z">
        <seg>Loose the threads, Philomela</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Legebat quandoque interdiu libellos spirituales, &amp; seligebat studiose capitula præsenti affectui temporique magis accommodata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050904Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050904Z">
        <seg>He would sometimes read spiritual pamphlets during the day, and would diligently select chapters more suited to his present mood and the occasion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Leonardo Hansen Ordinis Prædicatorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211734Z">
        <seg>Leonardo Hansen of the Order of Preachers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Leonardo Hansen.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211746Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211746Z">
        <seg>Leonardo Hansen</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Leonardo Hansen&lt;/b0&gt;, EX IMPRESSIS.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030511Z">
        <seg>Leonardo Hansen, from the printed works.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lepores erant, quibus Virgo liberius adblandiebatur dignationi sui Dilecti, qui Rosam pauperem, utpote matre Oliva &amp; patre de Floribus natam, minime fastidierat, ex quo ab ea se tam tenere amari permiserat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053251Z">
        <seg>They were charms by which the Virgin more freely courted the favor of her Beloved, who, having been born of a poor mother, Olive, and a father, de Floribus, had in no way disdained the Rose, since he had allowed himself to be so tenderly loved by her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lethalia decumbentis signa dissimulari jam non poterant, erantque Virgini acceptissima, quod his, quasi fidejussoribus, liberior sibi concedebatur campus, submota corporis cura, tractandi de sola anima.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025910Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025910Z">
        <seg>The dying man's lethal symptoms could no longer be concealed, and this was most welcome to the Virgin, for it granted her, as it were with sureties, a freer field in which to treat of the soul alone, the care of the body having been set aside.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lexikon der Heiligen, Seligen und Verehrten der katholischen, orthodoxen und protestantischen Kirchen</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211702Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211702Z">
        <seg>Dictionary of the Saints, Blessed, and Venerated of the Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant Churches</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Libeat interim mirari lusum æternæ Sapientiæ, quæ ad fructum uberiorem electas animas sic mortificat &amp; vivificat, deducit ad inferos, &amp; reducit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045906Z">
        <seg>Let us meanwhile marvel at the play of eternal Wisdom, which, to bring forth a more abundant fruit, thus mortifies and vivifies the chosen souls, leads them to the underworld, and brings them back.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lima, alio nomine dicta Civitas Regum, debuit &amp; suam habere stellam, ad arcta Salvatoris cunabula tacite invitantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043708Z">
        <seg>Lima, otherwise called the City of Kings, ought to have had its own star, silently inviting to the Savior's narrow cradle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Limam itaque veniens, ut melioribus chirurgis se subderet, in S. Andreæ nosocomio per sex menses frustra sub variis unctionibus &amp; emplastris laboravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035849Z">
        <seg>Thus, upon arriving in Lima to place himself under the care of better surgeons, he labored in vain for six months at the Hospital of St. Andrew under various ointments and plasters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Limam redux, amare questus est de Virgine, tandem &amp; coram Virgine, quod in medio viæ ipsum destituisset orationum suarum præsidio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051849Z">
        <seg>Upon his return to Lima, he bitterly complained about the Virgin, and at last even before her, that she had abandoned him in the middle of the road without the protection of his prayers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Limanus ager ut psittacos, ita papiliones gignit, &lt;s2&gt;[per duplex miraculum]&lt;/s2&gt; colorum varietate ac elegantia plane mirabiles, ut fere de utriusque gloria hi cum illis certare videantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040149Z">
        <seg>The Limian plain produces butterflies as well as parrots, &lt;s2&gt;[by a double miracle]&lt;/s2&gt; truly marvelous in the variety and elegance of their colors, so that they almost seem to rival one another in glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Limanus ager ut psittacos, ita papiliones gignit, [per duplex miraculum] colorum varietate ac elegantia plane mirabiles, ut fere de utriusque gloria hi cum illis certare videantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T212007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203504Z">
        <seg>The Limian plain produces butterflies as well as parrots, [by a double miracle] truly marvelous in the variety and elegance of their colors, so that they almost seem to rival one another in glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Limense emporium fama impleverat, quamdam mulierem Deo solemniter dicatam, fracto religionis vinculo, e Cantabria profugisse in Indias, ac sub virili habitu in civitate Guamanga&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt; diu errabundam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010926Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010926Z">
        <seg>A rumor had spread throughout the market of Lima that a certain woman, solemnly dedicated to God, having broken the bonds of her religion, had fled from Cantabria to the Indies, and, under the guise of a man, had long wandered about the city of Guamanga.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Limense emporium fama impleverat, quamdam mulierem Deo solemniter dicatam, fracto religionis vinculo, e Cantabria profugisse in Indias, ac sub virili habitu in civitate Guamanga&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; diu errabundam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053415Z">
        <seg>A rumor had spread throughout the market of Lima that a certain woman, solemnly consecrated to God, having broken the bonds of her religion, had fled from Cantabria to the Indies, and, under the guise of a man, had long wandered about the city of Guamanga.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Limenses &amp; alii in universa Peruana regione sacramentalis pœnitentiæ ministri, communicatis indiciis, generatim stupuere de tanta mutatione morum in populo a die, qua Rosa in Sponsi sui paradiso recepta fuit: nam certatim feminæ, abjecto vestium luxu, simplicioris modestiæ habitum ubique sumpserunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043100Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043100Z">
        <seg>The Limeños and others throughout the entire Peruvian region, the ministers of the sacrament of penance, upon exchanging reports, were generally astonished at such a change in the people's morals since the day Rosa was received into her Spouse's paradise; for the women, in a contest, cast aside the luxury of their clothing and everywhere adopted a simpler and more modest attire.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Limensis SS. Rosarii conventus, ut religiosorum abundat numero, (plures trecentis plerumque numerantur) ita sepeliendis tot Fratrum successive decedentium cadaveribus nimis arctum foret unicæ capitularis domus spatium, nisi de industria talis aliunde terra illuc fuisset illata, quæ citissime funera sepulta absumat, ut subsecuturis locum concedant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035328Z">
        <seg>Limensis SS. The convent of the Rosary, though it abounds in religious (usually numbering more than three hundred), would find the space of its single chapter house far too cramped for burying the successive bodies of so many brothers, unless earth had been brought in from elsewhere to hasten the burial of the dead and make room for those who would follow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Limæ ab annis centum &amp; amplius floret sub invocatione B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055014Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055014Z">
        <seg>Lima has flourished for more than a hundred years under the invocation of the Blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Litarat &amp; ipse in Rosarii sacello, &amp; post Sacrificium, promiscuo populo ibidem communicaturo Sacramentum distribuebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055814Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055814Z">
        <seg>He himself would pray in the chapel of the Rosary, and after the Sacrifice, would distribute the Sacrament to the common people there to receive Communion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Locum etiam integro anno antea eidem matronæ prædixerat in eodem cubiculo, in quo mortalitatis debitum persolvit, rogans, ne alteri feminæ præter ipsam, &amp; genitricem suam corpusculum vestiendum, &amp; feretro imponendum committeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042716Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042716Z">
        <seg>He had also foretold the place to the same matron a full year earlier in the very same room in which he paid the debt of mortality, requesting that she entrust the task of dressing his little body and placing it in a coffin to no other woman besides herself and his mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Locum tibi designavi, illic erit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062647Z">
        <seg>I have marked the place for you; he will be there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Longa dolorum acerbitas pene mentem feminæ everterat, quando suggerente marito, sese efflictim multisque cum lachrymis cœpit charæ suæ Rosæ intercessionibus commendare;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034150Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034150Z">
        <seg>The long acerbity of her sorrows had nearly overturned the woman's mind, when, at her husband's suggestion, she began to commend herself with heartfelt entreaties and many tears to the intercession of her dear Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Longe alias, quam de collaribus, cogitationes versat cor tuum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060635Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060635Z">
        <seg>Your heart turns its thoughts far away from collars.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Longe mirabilius fuit, quod notarunt domestici, Rosam plus virium semper hausisse a jejuniis &amp; longa inedia, quam a cibo corporali: verum (ut infra narrabimus) etiam hoc ipsi cum magistra sua S. Catharina Senensi commune fuit, quod os famelicum vulnerato Christi lateri applicuerit: siquidem extra hunc favorem &amp; favum tam Discipula quam magistra non norant aliud, quo saturari possent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041404Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041404Z">
        <seg>Far more marvelous was what her household noticed, That Rosa always drew more strength from fasting and prolonged hunger than from bodily food: yet (as we shall relate below) she shared with her teacher, St. Catherine of Siena, the fact that she applied her hungry mouth to the wounded side of Christ: for apart from this favor and honey, neither disciple nor teacher knew of anything else by which they could be satisfied.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Longe suavius erat Rosæ, filiolam vocari a tali Matre, quam Rosam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051403Z">
        <seg>It was far sweeter for Rosa to be called "little daughter" by such a mother than "Rose."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lubet hic simplicius ex ipsis Rosæ pugillaribus recitare formulam, qua sibimet Virgo hoc opus præfiniebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053542Z">
        <seg>It seems fitting here to quote more simply from Rose's own pamphlets the formula by which the Virgin defined this work for herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lucia Guerra de la Daga, loco situque commodissimo stetit in Lima celebre hoc cœnobium, &amp; introducto candidi velaminis novitio gynæceo, cœpere Divina celebrari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062418Z">
        <seg>Lucia Guerra de la Daga stood in a most convenient place and location in Lima, and with the introduction of a novice of the white veil to the convent, divine services began to be celebrated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lucia Guerra de la Daga.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062907Z">
        <seg>Lucy, daughter of the Sword</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lucia hoc solatio dives, ac domum reversa, quiescere non potuit, dum Rosam, plane jam suam, denuo conveniret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062920Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062920Z">
        <seg>Rich with this comfort, Lucia, upon returning home, could not rest until she met Rosa, who was now truly her own.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludens cum eo, cujus guttur suavissimum est, obtinuit vincens curari a doloribus gutturis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041916Z">
        <seg>Playing with the one whose throat is the sweetest, the victor obtained relief from throat pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovica Barba audierat a confessario, Rosam vaticinatam fuisse, se assecuturam habitum religiosum sancti Dominici, quem ipsa horrescebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043122Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043122Z">
        <seg>Ludovica Barba had heard from her confessor that Rosa had prophesied she would take the religious habit of Saint Dominic, which she herself detested.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovica de Mendoza encomia sanctitatis hujus Virginis nec credere, nec concipere esse vera poterat, aut volebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043131Z">
        <seg>Ludovica de Mendoza could neither believe nor even conceive that the praises of this Virgin's sanctity were true, nor did she want to.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovica ejusque filia Isabella Alexia substitit ante fores.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043358Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043358Z">
        <seg>Ludovica and her daughter Isabella Alexia paused before the doors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovicus de Bilbao Virginis confessarius, &lt;s2&gt;[asseveranter suo confessario]&lt;/s2&gt; tametsi reliquis ejus dictis plurimum deferret, huic tamen ejusdem vaticinio scrupulosius addubitabat, eo quod ad humana respiciens, ne quidem e longinquo videre poterat vel minimam verisimilitudinis stricturam, quæ tanti moliminis ullum aliquando successum promitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062616Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062616Z">
        <seg>Louis of Bilbao, confessor of the Virgin, &lt;s2&gt;[firmly to his confessor]&lt;/s2&gt; although he gave the greatest deference to his other sayings, he nevertheless hesitated more scrupulously over this same prophecy of his, because, looking to human things, he could not even from a distance see the slightest semblance of probability that could promise any success for so great an undertaking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovicus de Bilbao Virginis confessarius, [asseveranter suo confessario] tametsi reliquis ejus dictis plurimum deferret, huic tamen ejusdem vaticinio scrupulosius addubitabat, eo quod ad humana respiciens, ne quidem e longinquo videre poterat vel minimam verisimilitudinis stricturam, quæ tanti moliminis ullum aliquando successum promitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011856Z">
        <seg>Louis of Bilbao, the Virgin's confessor, [firmly to his own confessor] although he gave the utmost deference to her other sayings, nevertheless more scrupulously doubted this same prophecy of hers, because, looking to human things, he could not even from a distance see the slightest semblance of probability that would promise any success whatsoever for so great an undertaking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovicus de Bilbao post Missam in eodem sacello, dum Virgini sacram Synaxim porrigit, notavit sæpius vultum ipsius cælesti splendore perfusum, ita quod præ fulgoris vehementia sibi tremuerint oculi, velut impares illi sidereæ claritati, quam gratiæ venustas conspicue accenderat, &amp; Panis angelorum in angelicam (uti aiebat) pulchritudinem mirifice transformarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055805Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055805Z">
        <seg>Louis of Bilbao, after Mass in the same chapel, while offering the sacred Communion to the Virgin, often noticed her face bathed in a heavenly splendor, so that her eyes trembled before the intensity of the glow, as if unworthy of that starry clarity which the beauty of grace had conspicuously kindled. and had wonderfully transformed the Bread of Angels into an angelic (as he said) beauty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovicus de Bilbao, &amp; F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050222Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050222Z">
        <seg>Louis of Bilbao, &amp; F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovicus de Bilbao, cœpitque voce lachrymosa affari Virginem iis formulis, quibus ad postremum agonem solicitius animari solent moribundi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013124Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013124Z">
        <seg>Louis of Bilbao began to address the Virgin in a tearful voice with the words by which the dying are more earnestly prepared for their final struggle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovicus de Bilbao, notavit eadem in Rosa, dum ejus confessiones excipiebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050659Z">
        <seg>Louis of Bilbao noted the same in Rosa, while he was taking her confession.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovicus de Bilbao, primarius Limanæ universitatis cathedraticus, &amp; in S. Officio censor, qui, ut pluribus annis Rosæ confessiones exceperat, ita plus ceteris illius spiritum, exercitia, arcana lumina, Sponsique favores habebat exploratos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031521Z">
        <seg>Louis of Bilbao, a primarius and full professor at the University of Lima and a censor in the Holy Office, who, having for many years heard the confessions of Rosa, thus had more thoroughly than anyone else explored her spirit, exercises, secret illuminations, and the favors of her Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Læta hoc successu, tibiam pariter eodem pulvere defricuit, statimque detumescere cœpit inflatio, coire chasmata&lt;a1&gt;k&lt;/a1&gt;, relictis dumtaxat in beneficii memoriam nigricantibus signis, quæ ut ab extima cute diluerentur, aquam huic usui distillatam chirurgus Joannes de Lezana suggessit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035901Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035901Z">
        <seg>Delighted by this success, she rubbed the tibia in the same powder, and immediately the swelling began to subside, the fissures to close, only the blackish marks remaining as a memorial of the benefit. To wash these away from the outer skin, the surgeon John de Lezana recommended distilled water for this purpose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Magis in dies laxabatur lacerum peritonæum&lt;a1&gt;e&lt;/a1&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033533Z">
        <seg>The torn peritoneum was becoming more lax day by day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Magister Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055802Z">
        <seg>Master Father</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Magistri F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040205Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040205Z">
        <seg>Master F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Magistrum F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040627Z">
        <seg>Master F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Magna profecto Rosæ dignitas, matris ac magistræ Senensis fuisse, non modo filiam ac discipulam, sed &amp; collactaneam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051127Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051127Z">
        <seg>It was truly a great honor for Rosa to have been a mother and teacher of the Sena, not only her daughter and pupil, but also her foster-sister.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Malebat Rosa totum hoc tempus orationi dare;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012947Z">
        <seg>Rosa would rather devote all this time to prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maluisset auro gemmisque rigentem cycladem subinde offerre sacræ imagini florum loco, sed obstabat inopia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054556Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054556Z">
        <seg>He would have preferred to offer the sacred image a gilded and jeweled cycad from time to time in place of flowers, but poverty stood in the way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maluisset, pro conformiori similitudine, gestare coronam ex spinis agrestibus, eaque de re serio tractaverat cum confessario;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042146Z">
        <seg>He would have preferred, for a closer resemblance, to wear a crown of wild thorns, and had seriously discussed the matter with his confessor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maluit deinde ab ipso Virginis ore modum repentinæ curationis ediscere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055436Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055436Z">
        <seg>He then preferred to learn the method of the sudden cure from the Virgin's own mouth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maluit itaque per confessarios tentare successum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042710Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042710Z">
        <seg>He therefore preferred to try to succeed through the confessors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mane &amp; vespere singulorum dierum altari aureo in Sancto imponebatur, ejusque deferendi munus ad sacerdotes hebdomadario officio fungentes spectabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031751Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031751Z">
        <seg>In the morning and evening of each day it was placed on the golden altar in the sanctuary, and the duty of carrying it fell to the priests serving the weekly office.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mane Hieronymum Bernarda admonuit, puellam ab omni lepra mundatam esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040339Z">
        <seg>In the morning, Jerome informed Bernarda that the girl had been cleansed of all leprosy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mane a contubernali suo repertus est jugulo catarrhis liberrimo, tumor omnis desederat, respirandi patula redierat facultas, statimque Basilio sua restituta est lana, de qua illud inter alia dignum est notatu, quod cujusnam aut villi aut velleris ea sit, discerni haud possit: nativo colore nec bombycem refert, nec gossipium, sed sparsis auri fulgoribus filatim interlucet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035155Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035155Z">
        <seg>In the morning he was found by his roommate with a throat completely free of catarrh; all swelling had subsided, his ability to breathe had returned, and at once Basil's wool was restored to him. A noteworthy thing about it, among other things, is that it cannot be determined whether it is from a fleece or a woolly sheep: in its native color it resembles neither silk nor cotton, but shines through in threads with scattered flashes of gold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mane facto, acciti confessarii infirmæ adstiterunt, mirantes vivum illud ac taciturnum omnium dolorum simulacrum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013356Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013356Z">
        <seg>At daybreak, the summoned confessors of the sick woman stood by, marveling at that living, silent likeness of all sorrows.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mane facto, ancilla circa horam quintam ante meridiem inventa, reprehensa, reducta est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033945Z">
        <seg>Early in the morning, the maid, found at around five o'clock before noon, was reprimanded and sent back.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mane facto, crudeles chirothecas matri placidissime restituens, totam rei seriem candore simplici exposuit, manus fibratim ambustas ostendit, ac, ne deinceps ad hujusmodi revocaretur supplicia, obnixe rogavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035402Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035402Z">
        <seg>When morning came, he most placably returned the cruel gloves to his mother, laid out the whole affair in simple candor, showed his hands, which were burned in places, and earnestly begged that he might not be subjected to such punishments again.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mane facto, dum reducta fascia oculos Margaritæ inspiciunt, primo notant evanuisse nubeculam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034643Z">
        <seg>In the morning, as the bandage is removed and they look into Margaret's eyes, they first notice that the cloud has vanished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mane redeuntes medici, ut desperata matre, saltem nascituræ proli quoquo modo, si possent, consulerent, ancillam reperiunt sanam, valentem, cibosque sibi afferri postulantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040432Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040432Z">
        <seg>When the doctors returned in the morning, they found the maidservant healthy and strong, and she was demanding that food be brought to her, as if she were a desperate mother trying in every way to care for her unborn child.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mane, parvulus exsilivit sanus, ambulavit, expediteque percurrit domum, acsi numquam pedibus languisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035933Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035933Z">
        <seg>In the morning, the little boy leaped up healthy, walked, and ran swiftly home, as if he had never been lame in his feet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mane, ut se doloribus liberum sensit, inspecto ac palpato latere, omnem pariter fugisse tumorem lætus deprehendit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035539Z">
        <seg>In the morning, feeling himself free from pain, he inspected and palpated his side and was pleased to find that the swelling had likewise disappeared.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Manebit is tecum Limæ, nec emittetur foras ad molestissimæ functionis periculum”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012805Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012805Z">
        <seg>He will remain with you in Lima, and will not be sent outside to the danger of the most troublesome function.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Manent autem per catalepsim palpebræ apertæ, quæ per carum clauduntur, servaturque secundum naturam spiratio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033648Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033648Z">
        <seg>They remain, however, in catalepsy with the eyelid open, which is closed by the brow, and breathing is preserved according to nature.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Manicatum hoc erat, atque a collo &amp; humeris infra genua propendebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041839Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041839Z">
        <seg>This was a tunic, and it hung from the neck and shoulders down below the knees.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mansuetudo, comitas, modestia Virginis trahebat notas feminas, ut specie honoris dum accedebant ad matrem, postularent videre &amp; Filiam, quam suavis odor virtutis, honestas indolis, admiratio sanctitatis, quam plurimis etiam illustribus commendarat invitam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043451Z">
        <seg>The Virgin's meekness, courtesy, and modesty drew distinguished women, so that, under the guise of honor as they approached the mother, they would ask to see the Daughter as well—whom the sweet fragrance of virtue, the nobility of her character, and the admiration of her sanctity had, even against her will, recommended to many distinguished persons.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Marcebat stupore lætissimo quæstor, ac tacite in uxorem respiciens, pendebat attonitus ab ore narrantis, ut Virgo se in preces, ipso abeunte, prostraverat, ut brevissimo intervallo sana in pedes se erexerat, strenue laborem, forficibus postulatis, fuerat auspicata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055433Z">
        <seg>The quaestor was overcome with the happiest astonishment, and, silently glancing at his wife, hung spellbound on the narrator's lips, as the Virgin had prostrated herself in prayer when he departed, and in the briefest interval had risen to her feet in perfect health and had vigorously undertaken the task, scissors in hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria Sanchez, alias de Jesu, novennis puella, quæ cum sexennis esset, fortuito in terram prolapsa, ita nervis &amp; ossibus offensa fuit, ut contractis cruribus, eorumque sensu deperdito, omni prorsus standi &amp; ambulandi potentia deperdita, continuo immobilis affixa lectulo per triennium, crescente ætate, magis viribus destituebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043147Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043147Z">
        <seg>Maria Sanchez, also known as de Jesu, a nine-year-old girl, who, when she was six, accidentally fell to the ground and was so injured in her nerves and bones that, with her legs contracted and their sensation lost, she completely lost the ability to stand or walk, and was immediately confined to a bed for three years, growing older, was growing weaker with advancing age.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria Usategui propriis manibus, &lt;s2&gt;[statim Sancta sanatur,]&lt;/s2&gt; Rosæ domum reduci brachium nudavit, gossipium circumduxit, alligaturam reposuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054013Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054013Z">
        <seg>Maria Usategui, with her own hands, immediately healed the Saint, and, having stripped her arm to the house of Rosæ, she wound a bandage around it and put the splint back in place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria Usatigui, quod ejus in ædibus dum triennlum vixit Rosa, adeo sese omnium reputarit infimam, ut non soli patrifamilias &amp; conjugi, sed tenellis quoque filiabus, imo servis, ancillis, mancipiis domesticis subjecta ad nutum, singulorum æque placita, velut imperia, exceperit, favorem arbitrata, si quid sibi ceu precario in domum receptæ heriliter mandabatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040701Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040701Z">
        <seg>Maria Usatigui, because Rosa had lived in her house for three years, considered herself so inferior to everyone that she submitted to the wills of not only the head of the household and her husband, but also to her tender daughters, indeed, subject to the will of servants, maids, and household slaves, she received everyone's commands as if they were orders, believing she had favor whenever anything was commanded of her as a mistress, as if she had been taken into the house on sufferance;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria Velasquez, uxor centurionis Didadaci Ruiz de Campos, febrim continuam, unaque sanguinis fluxum mortiferum evasit Ursula de terra Felutpa, ancilla Marianæ de Sea, febrim cum diarrhœa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040026Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040026Z">
        <seg>Maria Velasquez, wife of the centurion Didacus Ruiz de Campos, suffered a continuous fever and a deadly hemorrhage; Ursula, a servant of Mariana de Sea from the land of Felutpa, suffered a fever with diarrhea.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Mesta, pictoris Medori Angelini conjux, cum solo marito, nemine conscio, tractare de utriusque profectione in Hispanias, deque necessaria pecuniarum quantitate ad vitam illic honeste, &amp; commode sustentandam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012138Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012138Z">
        <seg>Maria de Mesta, wife of the painter Medor Angelini, to confer with her husband alone, with no one else aware, about both of their journey to Spain, and about the necessary amount of money to support a life there honorably and comfortably.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Mesta, pictoris Medori Angelini conjux, impatientiæ ac præcipitis iracundiæ pugnaci morbo jam pene sibimet ipsi facta erat intolerabilis: quavis re minima turbabatur ac turbabat, manu prompta, minax clamoribus, &amp; sonora rixandi intemperie totarum ædium implacabilis tempestas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060719Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060719Z">
        <seg>Maria de Mesta, wife of the painter Medor Angelini, had by her impatient and headlong temper become almost unbearable to everyone: she was disturbed and disturbing over the slightest thing, ready with her hand, menacing with her shouts, and with her loud quarrelsomeness an implacable storm throughout the entire house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Oliva Rosæ mater, singulari complexionis indole, tenebras &amp; spectra metuebat, nec facile aut noctu prodibat ad secretiora domus, aut ad vesperam sine comite sese proprio fidebat horto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062037Z">
        <seg>Maria de Oliva, mother of Rosæ, of a singular temperament, feared darkness and spectres, and would not easily venture at night into the more secluded parts of the house, nor did she trust herself alone in the garden after dusk.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Oliva, seu ignara, seu immemor penuriæ, bis ter in caveam misit, qui de vase omnibus noto mel adferrent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062222Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062222Z">
        <seg>Maria de Oliva, whether ignorant or forgetful of the scarcity, sent three times into the cage those who were to bring honey from the jar known to everyone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Quinnones, cui avunculus erat ille ipse famigeratissimus Dei servus, Limensium archiepiscopus tunc superstes, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040019Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040019Z">
        <seg>Maria de Quinnones, whose uncle was that very infamous servant of God, the then surviving Archbishop of Lima, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de S. Joseph excalceata monialis, improviso in terram lapsu oculorum nervos utrimque graviter offenderat, ut neque dextrorsum, neque lævorsum oculos potuerit convertere, simulque in iisdem continuus sæviebat dolor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034556Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034556Z">
        <seg>Maria, a barefoot nun of St. Joseph, had, by an unexpected fall to the ground, severely injured the nerves of both eyes, so that she could not turn them to the right or to the left, and a constant, severe pain persisted in them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Sala ultro per famulum misit ferici subtegminis aptam materiam, quanta conficiendæ novæ pallæ sat erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061138Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061138Z">
        <seg>Maria de Sala sent, through a servant, a suitable piece of coarse under-cloth, enough to make a new pall.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Usategui porrectus fuerat, modicæ chartæ involutum stringebat intra pugnum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044526Z">
        <seg>Maria de Usategui lay stretched out, clutching in his fist a small roll of paper.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Usategui, consorte regii quæstoris D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035610Z">
        <seg>Maria de Usategui, wife of the royal treasurer, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Usategui.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050234Z">
        <seg>Mary of Usategui</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Vera dysenteria, febri maligna, dolore stomachi, &amp; lethali vomitu per octo dies laborabat in extremis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043221Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043221Z">
        <seg>Maria de Vera was at death's door for eight days, suffering from dysentery, a malignant fever, stomach pain, and a lethal vomiting.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariam Sanchez novennem fortuitus in terram lapsus ita afflixerat, ut exinde attractis tibiis, omnique erepto pedum usu, nec stare, nec sedere usquam potuerit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034122Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034122Z">
        <seg>A nine-year-old girl, Maria Sanchez, was so afflicted by a chance fall to the ground that, with her shins drawn in and all use of her feet taken away, she could neither stand nor sit anywhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariam de Mesta impatienti, ac præcipitis iracundiæ pugnaci morbo jam sibimetipsi ex diuturna consuetudine factam intolerabilem, patientiæ ac mansuetudinis aphorismis sic instruxit, ut ab illa die emendatior ac mitior visa fuerit: eoque brevi in patientiæ gymnasio profecit, ut jam a Deo ultro plus adversitatis postularet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042357Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042357Z">
        <seg>He instructed Mary of Mesta, impatient and given to sudden outbursts of anger—a malady that her long habit had made intolerable to herself—with aphorisms on patience and meekness so that from that day she was seen to be more improved and gentler; and in that brief gymnasium of patience she progressed so much that she now even asked God to send her more adversity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariam de Usategui aliquamdiu asservati, mirabilem, at omnibus peregrinam spirarunt suaveolentiam, testes nimirum pœnosi sudoris, quem de Rosæ corpusculo imbiberant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041807Z">
        <seg>They preserved Mary of Usategui for some time; marvelous though she was, they exhaled a sweet fragrance to all, a testament, indeed, to the painful sweat they had absorbed from the little body of the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariam de Usategui, ne toto illo temporis interstitio evocaretur, quidquid demum occurreret, si vel ipsa Rosæ mater veniret Filiæ locutura: causam rogata, candide respondit, se toto illo temporis spatio immobilem de pavimento surgere in pedes non posse, ne quidem ad oratorii januam aperiendam, si quis forte pulsaret ad limen.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044917Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044917Z">
        <seg>As for Maria de Usategui, she said that during that entire interval she could not be summoned—whatever might happen, even if Rosa's own mother came to speak to her daughter. When asked why, she frankly replied that she could not rise from the floor and stand on her feet during that whole time, not even to open the door of the oratory if someone happened to knock.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariana quædam serviebat in Rosæ paternis ædibus, Inda genere, agrestis conditione.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040506Z">
        <seg>A certain Mariana was serving in Rose's father's house; she was of Indian descent and of humble origin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariana, ancilla domestica, horum arcanorum sola aliquamdiu conscia, sola Virgini a secretis erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041625Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041625Z">
        <seg>Mariana, the household maid, was the only one aware of these secrets for some time; she alone was the Virgin's confidante.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Marianam de Requena opprimebat intolerabilis præcordiorum dolor, cui perpetuus erat fomes ab inordinata quadam mœstitia, sanguinisque melancholici ponderosa gravedine, quam sequebatur &amp; suspiriorum violenta frequentia, &amp; respirandi pœnosa difficultas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040351Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040351Z">
        <seg>Mariana de Requena was afflicted by an intolerable pain in her chest, perpetually fanned by a certain disordered sadness and the heavy burden of melancholic blood, which was followed by a violent and frequent sighing and a painful difficulty in breathing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maritus absque alio alexipharmaco&lt;a0&gt;k&lt;/a0&gt; venenum eluctatus, postridie lectum deseruit, ac promissi memor, ad Virginis sepulchrum properavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034345Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034345Z">
        <seg>The husband, having escaped the poison with nothing but an antidote, rose from his bed the next day and, mindful of his promise, hurried to the Virgin's tomb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariæ Magdalenæ sacro pervigilio, visa est formidabilis illa hæreticorum classis, acie disposita, in ipsum Limensis emporii portum (Callao indigenis dicitur) recta procurrere, velut in urbis excidium militares suas copias effusura in terram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060149Z">
        <seg>During the sacred vigil of Mary Magdalene, that formidable host of heretics, drawn up in battle array, was seen rushing straight for the very harbor of the port of Lima (called Callao by the natives), as if about to land its military forces on the ground for the sacking of the city.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariæ de Messa, uxori Medori Angelini pictoris, consilium, quod occultissime inierant, de profectione in Hispanias commemoravit: summam pecuniariam, quam pro arbitratu constituerant, dixit ambobus sat esse, mirante muliere &amp; attestante, plus a Virgine dici non potuisse, si in amborum colloquio tertia sterisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042642Z">
        <seg>He revealed to Mary of the Mass, the wife of Medor Angelini the painter, the plan they had entered into most secretly to set out for Spain: he said that the sum of money they had arranged at their discretion was enough for both of them, to the woman's astonishment and with her attesting that nothing more could have been said by the Virgin, had a third been present in their conversation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariæ de Usategui suggereret, me illo, quem vidisti, succursu indigere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051744Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051744Z">
        <seg>It would suggest to Mary of Usategui that I need the help you saw.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Martinez, &amp; tertia noctis hora imminebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053836Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053836Z">
        <seg>Martinez, and the third hour of the night was approaching.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater (quæ horti clavem jugiter tenebat secum) ut notavit in vultu Filiæ signa instantis deliquii, sine mora promptis a marsupio duobus denariis, jussit ancillæ, adiret subito vicinioris propolæ apothecam, emeret saccharum, carbonem, &amp; libum Indicum, quod CHOCOLATE&lt;a0&gt;g&lt;/a0&gt; vocant indigenæ, jam &amp; Europæis tam usu quam nomine sat notum: quippe in aqua calida resolutum cum triente sacchari, stomacho simul &amp; cordi potenter succurrit&lt;a1&gt;h&lt;/a1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010654Z">
        <seg>The mother (who always kept the key to the garden with her) seeing the signs of an impending fainting fit on her daughter's face, without delay took two denarii from her purse and ordered a maid to go at once to the nearest apothecary's and buy sugar, charcoal, and an Indian cake, which the natives call CHOCOLATE, already well known to Europeans both in use and by name: for when dissolved in hot water with a third part sugar, it powerfully helps both the stomach and the heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater (quæ horti clavem jugiter tenebat secum) ut notavit in vultu Filiæ signa instantis deliquii, sine mora promptis a marsupio duobus denariis, jussit ancillæ, adiret subito vicinioris propolæ apothecam, emeret saccharum, carbonem, &amp; libum Indicum, quod CHOCOLATE&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;g&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; vocant indigenæ, jam &amp; Europæis tam usu quam nomine sat notum: quippe in aqua calida resolutum cum triente sacchari, stomacho simul &amp; cordi potenter succurrit&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;h&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051642Z">
        <seg>The mother (who always kept the key to the garden with her), seeing the signs of an impending fainting fit on her daughter's face, without delay took two denarii from her purse and ordered the maid to go at once to the nearest apothecary's and buy sugar, charcoal, and an Indian cake, which the natives call CHOCOLATE, already well known to Europeans both in use and by name: for when dissolved in hot water with a third part sugar, it powerfully helps both the stomach and the heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater Virginis Maria de Oliva coram judicibus recensuit, post exsequias Filiæ complures ad se devotas accessisse personas, etiam religiosas antea ignotas, liberali subsidio suam egestatem sublevantes, &amp; profitentes, se tributa pendere virgini Rosæ, cujus intercessionibus vitæ suæ optimam mutationem debere sciebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043051Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043051Z">
        <seg>Mother Virgin Mary of Oliva recounted before the judges that, after her daughter's funeral, several devoted persons—even previously unknown religious sisters—had come to her, relieving her poverty with generous aid and professing that they paid tribute to the Virgin Rose, whose intercessions they knew had brought about the best change in their lives.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater abhorrebat inutile mancipium, utpote variis languoribus, ac præsertim crebræ dysenteriæ obnoxium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035359Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035359Z">
        <seg>The mother loathed the useless slave, since he was subject to various ailments, and especially to frequent dysentery.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater advertens Filiæ decumbenti pristinos rediisse dolores, &amp; quidem cum auctario, suspicata periculum, extimuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051040Z">
        <seg>The mother, noticing that her daughter who was lying down had her former pains return, and indeed with an increase, suspected danger and was terrified.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater cum filiarum altera Laurentia Cortes, humanis remediis frustra tentatis, respexere demum ad cælestia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035517Z">
        <seg>The mother and her daughter, Laurencia Cortes, having tried in vain human remedies, finally turned to heavenly ones.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater de novitii constantia mœste anxieque subdubitans, quod delicatum puerum tantis metuebat austeritatibus vix parem fore, denuo Rosam adiit, solicita, pusillanimis, inquieta, præsertim ex quo resciverat filium inter Quadragesimales rigores nonnihil infirmari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012644Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012644Z">
        <seg>The mother, grieving and anxiously doubtful of the novice's steadfastness—since she feared the delicate boy would scarcely be equal to such austerities—went again to Rosa, anxious, timid, and restless, especially after she had learned that her son was somewhat failing under the rigors of Lent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater filiam quæsitura venit, sed comite marito, quia (ut dictum est) ad nocturnos terrores timidissima erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062046Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062046Z">
        <seg>The mother came to look for her daughter, but with her husband as companion, because (as has been said) she was extremely fearful of nighttime terrors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater obvio, sed imperito suasa consilio, modicos pulveres inspersit effectu plane improspero: nam ii, utpote olentes Hermeticam&lt;a0&gt;l&lt;/a0&gt; pyxidem, affectas partes caustica sua acrimonia rodebant ita immaniter, ut certis intervallis toto corpusculo tremuisse puellam tacite notarint domestici.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000831Z">
        <seg>The mother, on the advice of an inexperienced person, dusted her lightly with a powder, but the result was a complete failure: for it, smelling of the Hermetic box, with its caustic acrimony gnawed so horribly at the affected parts that the household servants quietly noted the girl trembling all over at regular intervals.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater obvio, sed imperito suasa consilio, modicos pulveres inspersit effectu plane improspero: nam ii, utpote olentes Hermeticam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;l&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; pyxidem, affectas partes caustica sua acrimonia rodebant ita immaniter, ut certis intervallis toto corpusculo tremuisse puellam tacite notarint domestici.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034903Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034903Z">
        <seg>The mother, on the advice of an inexperienced person, dusted on some mild powders with completely unfortunate results: for they, smelling of the Hermetic box, gnawed at the affected parts with their caustic acrimony so terribly that the household servants quietly noted the girl trembling throughout her whole little body at regular intervals.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater vim morbi præsentissimo (ut putabat) remedio depulsura, quorumdam animalium illic frequentium hispidas pelliculas arcte circumposuit patienti Filiæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035505Z">
        <seg>The mother, thinking to drive off the force of the disease with the most immediate remedy she could find, tightly wrapped the patient daughter with the rough skins of certain animals that were common there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater, quæ in illam usque horam ad ejusmodi voces Apenninis cautibus durior fuerat, statim ut cera liquefacta cessit, nec vel momentum temporis aut deliberationi, aut dilationi in re tam ardua deposcens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043615Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043615Z">
        <seg>The mother, who until that moment had been more impervious to such appeals than the stony slopes of the Apennines, as soon as she melted like wax gave way, demanding neither a moment's time for deliberation or delay in so arduous a matter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater, ubi frustra totis ædibus suum requisivit pallium, suspicionibus indulsit, pene usque ad temerarium judicium, quod vicinorum aliquis clam irrepsisset domui, pallamque neglectim a sede pendulam sublegisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061113Z">
        <seg>The mother, having searched in vain throughout the entire house for her cloak, indulged in suspicions, almost to the point of rash judgment, that one of the neighbors had crept into the house secretly and had picked up the cloak, which had been carelessly left hanging from the chair.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater, ut cum avia patulas fauces diligentius inspexit, cancrenam mortificatam reperit, plagæ magnitudinem demirata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035430Z">
        <seg>When the mother, together with her grandmother, more closely examined the little girl's throat, she found a necrotic ulcer, amazed at the wound's size.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Matri quid acciderit, jam superius in fine cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030303Z">
        <seg>What happened to the mother is already mentioned above at the end of the chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Matrona facile animadvertit inter imaginem &amp; Rosam singulare quidpiam geri;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054531Z">
        <seg>The matron easily noticed that something peculiar was happening between the image and Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Matrona, quamvis commiseratione efflictim saucia, negabat, id sibi per medicos licere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025748Z">
        <seg>The matron, though grievously wounded by compassion, refused to allow the doctors to do that for her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Matutino crepusculo Rosa de more ad cellulam suam anachoreticam reditura, simul ut horti januam pandebat, eminus conspectas arbores, virgulta, herbulas omnes invitabat, ut secum benedicerent Creatori: “Benedicite universa germinantia in terra Domino.” Et ecce, moveri subito in euphoniam &amp; concentum ramusculos, collidi frondibus frondes, plaudere omni ex parte streperos foliorum susurros, motitari humiliorum plantularum capita;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045146Z">
        <seg>At early dawn, Rosa, as was her custom, was returning to her hermit's cell. As she opened the garden gate, she invited the trees, the shrubs, and all the herbs she saw in the distance to bless the Creator with her: "Bless the Lord, all you things that germinate on the earth." And behold, the twigs suddenly stirred into harmony and concert, leaves collided with leaves, the rustling of leaves resounded from every side, the humble little plants stirred their heads;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Me sine vita vivere, me præstolando mori.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051617Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051617Z">
        <seg>To live without you is to die of longing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Medici Lugdunenses apud Menagium in Dictionario etymologico ad vocem&lt;/e0&gt; tabac &lt;e1&gt;de hac herba sic scribunt:&lt;/e1&gt; Quemadmodum hortis omnibus magno est ornamento, ita facultatibus insignibus celeberrima est herba, quam PETUM ab Indis vocari refert Thevetus, Nicolaus Monardus PICIELT;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060936Z">
        <seg>The physicians of Lyon, in the Dictionary of Etymology under the word&lt;/e0&gt; "tabac," &lt;e1&gt;write thus of this herb:&lt;/e1&gt; "As it is a great ornament to all gardens, so it is celebrated for its remarkable properties; the herb which Thevetus reports to be called PETUM by the Indians, and Nicholas Monardus PICIELT."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Medici inflammationes faucium cum tumore&lt;/e0&gt; tonsillas &lt;e1&gt;appellant, quas Græci nominant&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;παρίσθμια&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;e3&gt;per similitudinem, de qua Lexicon medicum Castelli pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043113Z">
        <seg>Physicians call inflammations of the throat with swelling of the tonsils "quae Graeci nominant parysthmia," by reason of a similarity, concerning which see Castell's Medical Lexicon, p.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Medici inflammationes faucium cum tumore&lt;/e0&gt; tonsillas &lt;e1&gt;appellant, quas Græci nominant&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;παρίσθμια&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;e3&gt;per similitudinem, de qua Lexicon medicum Castelli pag. 373 consuli potest&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003526Z">
        <seg>Physicians call inflammations of the throat with swelling of the tonsils "tonsillitis," which the Greeks call paristimiae, on account of a similarity—see Castelli's Medical Lexicon, p. 373.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Meditabatur illic sensu acerrimo dira gehennæ incendia, quæ sibi deberi plus quam serio autumabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041922Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041922Z">
        <seg>He was there brooding with the keenest sense of the dreadful fires of hell, which he believed were due to him more than seriously.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Meliceride dissecta, inibi contentus humor non aliter, quam mel e favis diffluit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055606Z">
        <seg>When the honeycomb is cut open, the honey contained within flows out just like honey from the combs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Melos demus vocibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045304Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045304Z">
        <seg>Let us give melody to voices.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Memento, ne mihi, torridis faucibus aridisque visceribus tuam materna viscera imploraturæ, lympham deneges, qua indigebo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013155Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013155Z">
        <seg>Remember not to deny me the dew I shall need, lest you, with scorched throat and parched bowels, implore your motherly bowels for it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Meminerat ancilla, ejusmodi seras valida contusione nonnumquam aperiri: dum igitur abit quæsitura saxum, anxia Rosa, matrem aut vocari aut sponte mox affore timens, ad orationem confugit, quæ instar clavis, &amp; cælum, &amp; seram penetravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041733Z">
        <seg>The maid remembered that such strong locks could sometimes be opened by a powerful blow; so, while she went to fetch a stone, anxious Rosa, fearing that her mother would either be summoned or would soon appear of her own accord, fled to prayer, which, like a key, pierced both heaven and the lock.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Memini (inquit) de nonnullis præcipuis ac sanctissimis Dei amicis (quorum nomina publicis Ecclesiæ tabulis adscripta sunt) eadem legisse, quæ de te commemoras;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050420Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050420Z">
        <seg>I remember (he says) having read the same things about some of the chief and most holy friends of God (whose names are inscribed on the public tables of the Church) that you mention about you.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Memor deinde, eamdem Gundisalvi uxorem assedisse examini, de quo supra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054348Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054348Z">
        <seg>Remember, then, that the same Gundisalvi's wife was subjected to the ordeal, about which see above, chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Memor itaque opis, quam alias Rosa præstiterat, rursus ad eam supplici devotione respexit, Novenam ad ejus sepulchrum Limæ peragere, &amp; utrumque miraculum publico evulgare testimonio, pollicita.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035058Z">
        <seg>Remembering, then, the favor that Rosa had previously granted, she once again turned to her with humble devotion, promising to perform a novena at her tomb in Lima and to publicly proclaim both miracles by testimony.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Memor ulterius, non decere sub Capite spinoso membrum delicatum inveniri, compuncta obtutu piæ imaginis muto præconio dicentis: &lt;b0&gt;Ecce homo&lt;/b0&gt;, primam coronam capiti suo ex stanno peracutis claviculis infixit, &amp; per aliquot annos non sine vulneribus cinxit, cui in majoribus annis successit secunda, nonaginta &amp; novem aculeis armata, quam indies diversimode semper magis pœnalem arte familiaribus occultam reddebat, eamque nonnisi cum vita dimisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041538Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041538Z">
        <seg>Further mindful that it is not fitting for a delicate member to be found under a spiky head, it, pierced by the gaze of the pious image, silently proclaims: &lt;b0&gt;Behold the man&lt;/b0&gt;, he fixed the first crown to his head with exceedingly sharp little nails, and for several years wore it not without wounds. In his later years a second one succeeded it, armed with ninety-nine spikes, which he made ever more painful in various ways each day, keeping it hidden from his family by his skill, and he did not lay it aside until he left this life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mendacium est, quidquid ad Deum non ducit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060651Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060651Z">
        <seg>A lie is whatever does not lead to God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mendicorum tantummodo Limensium ejulatibus &amp; lacrymis publicis fuit celebratum hoc funus, conquerente egenorum turba, veram pauperum nutricem &amp; matrem fato acerbissimo immature sibi ereptam fuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042920Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042920Z">
        <seg>This funeral was celebrated only with the public wails and tears of the Limesian beggars, the crowd of the needy lamenting that the true nurse and mother of the poor had been cruelly snatched from them by the most bitter fate, far too soon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Meos mihi hospites trucidas”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043900Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043900Z">
        <seg>"Have my guests been slaughtered for me?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Metaphorice propter similitudinem hic sumitur pro voragine spirituali,quo peccatores absorbentur, ut satis liquet.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042654Z">
        <seg>It is used here metaphorically, because of its resemblance to the spiritual vortex into which sinners are absorbed, as is quite clear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Metrico sui idiomatis rhythmo&lt;a7&gt;f&lt;/a7&gt; tunc familiarius affabatur angelum, hoc fere sensu:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010648Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010648Z">
        <seg>In the metrical rhythm of his own idiom, he then addressed the angel more familiarly, in this sense:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Metrico sui idiomatis rhythmo&lt;s15&gt;&lt;a16&gt;f&lt;/a16&gt;&lt;/s15&gt; tunc familiarius affabatur angelum, hoc fere sensu:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051607Z">
        <seg>In the metrical rhythm of his own idiom, he then addressed the angel more familiarly, in this sense:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Meum ego Salvatorem:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045245Z">
        <seg>My Savior, I:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mi Pater, neque si vastos cælorum orbes quis huic telluri complicuerit, in eam me sententiam flectet, qua credere possum, Dei permissu me noxam mortalem commisisse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062021Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062021Z">
        <seg>My Father, not even if someone were to fold the vast orbs of the heavens onto this earth could he bend me to the belief that, with God's permission, I have committed a mortal sin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mi vere Deus, &amp; Sponse animæ meæ, tota lætitia cordis mei: ego, ego amare te desidero, benignissime Jesu, amore illo perfectissimo, amore efficacissimo, sincerissimo, ineffabili, intensissimo, incomparabili, incomprehensibili, irrefragabili, invictissimo, quo simul universi cives empyrei te diligunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053105Z">
        <seg>My true God and Spouse of my soul, the entire joy of my heart: I, I desire to love you, most benign Jesus, with that perfect, most efficacious, most sincere, ineffable, most intense, incomparable, incomprehensible, irrefutable, unconquerable love with which all the citizens of heaven love you at once.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mihi quidem hic abunde retributum est pro eo, quod laboravi”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031949Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031949Z">
        <seg>I have indeed been amply rewarded here for my labor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mira hæc Sanctorum est ambitio, qua malunt Christi spineam, quam auream coronam in hujus vitæ exilio circumferre.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042037Z">
        <seg>This is the remarkable ambition of the saints, by which they prefer to carry Christ's thorny crown rather than a golden one in the exile of this life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mira res!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041736Z">
        <seg>What a wonder!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mira res, tantum valuisse unius Rosæ salutiferos aculeos ad tantorum compunctionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032653Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032653Z">
        <seg>How remarkable that the salutary thorns of a single rose were enough to bring about the repentance of so many.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mira res: quæstor subito intromissus ad arcanum proregis colloquium, ab eodem variis sermonibus detentus per horam &amp; dimidiam, illo momento temporis, quo jam adamantini decreti insinuationem pavidus exspectabat, comiter dimissus est, nec prorex, toto seriæ confabulationis spatio, odiosam illam commissionem verbulo attigit, tametsi quatuor integris mensibus studuisset hanc, per supra memoratos, quæstori invito efficacissime persuadere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012808Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012808Z">
        <seg>A strange thing: the treasurer, suddenly brought in for a secret conference with the deputy-king, was detained by him in various conversations for an hour and a half, at the very moment when he was already anxiously awaiting the intimation of an adamantine decree, was politely dismissed, and the deputy-king, during the entire course of the conversation, he did not so much as mention that odious commission, even though he had for four whole months been striving, through the aforementioned, to most effectively persuade the unwilling quaestor to it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirabantur attonitæ moniales, sub sacræ imaginis valido præsidio id unicam potuisse decennem puellam, quod vix quatuor robusti bajuli credebantur sine sudore præstituri;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040931Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040931Z">
        <seg>The nuns were amazed and astonished that the ten-year-old girl, under the strong protection of the sacred image, could do what four strong porters were scarcely believed capable of performing without breaking a sweat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirabantur domestici, unde, aut quo modo nuntium defunctæ &amp; calentis adhuc Rosæ tota urbe diffundi potuerit hora tam intempestiva.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030335Z">
        <seg>The household wondered where or how the news of the late, still-warm Rose could have spread throughout the entire city at such an ungodly hour.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirabantur, Virginem modestissimam in publico revoluto circa humeros pallio, veste curta, projectis crepidis, liberrimis brachiis, alto præcinctu, solisque rosarii globulis precariis ferociter obarmatam, spe martyrii ultro lacessere fatalem horam, ac morti pugnacissime minitari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060245Z">
        <seg>They marveled that the most modest virgin in public, with her pallium thrown over her shoulders, her dress cut short, her sandals flung aside, her arms bare, her waist high-cinched, and armed ferociously only with the precious beads of the rosary, would of her own accord provoke the fatal hour of martyrdom and most fiercely threaten death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirabantur, sub Ægyptia hac plaga, Rosam integros dies immotam consistere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043810Z">
        <seg>They were amazed that, under this Egyptian plague, Rosa stood motionless for entire days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirabatur Virgo tam celeriter absumi potuisse annonam, quam pridie posuerant, utique in biduum suffecturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062210Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062210Z">
        <seg>The virgin was amazed that the provisions they had laid out the day before could have been consumed so quickly, provisions that would surely have lasted for two days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirabatur confessarius, hæc tanta cum securitate ac fiducia proferri a Virgine, quam sciebat nec temere loqui solitam, nec in re tam abdita quidquam asseveraturam, nisi diem sui transitus divina revelatione compertum habuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013143Z">
        <seg>The confessor was amazed that the Virgin should utter these things with such assurance and confidence, for he knew she was neither accustomed to speaking rashly nor to assert anything in so profound a matter, unless she had been informed of the day of her passing by divine revelation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirabatur curiosa mater, quid filiæ tam peculiare cum sancto Bartholomæo verteretur?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013108Z">
        <seg>The curious mother wondered what was so special about her daughter's relationship with Saint Bartholomew.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirabili consensu, &amp; occulto instinctu maxima hominum multitudo, clarescente jam die, implevit quæstoris palatium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042936Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042936Z">
        <seg>By a marvelous consensus and a hidden instinct, the great multitude of people, with the day already breaking, filled the questor's palace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Miraculum istud inibi tot habuit testes, quot Rosa tunc in suo feretro spectatores.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033251Z">
        <seg>That miracle there had as many witnesses as Rosa then had spectators at her bier.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirantur ambæ, in roseis illis brevium pagellarum foliolis tantum latuisse ignitabulum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032721Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032721Z">
        <seg>Both are amazed that the spark had been contained in those rose-colored little leaves of the brief pages.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirata est mater præsentaneum &amp; velox Angeli obsequium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051756Z">
        <seg>The mother was amazed at the prompt and swift obedience of Angel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirata est mulier, fassa plus a Virgine dici non potuisse, si ipsa amborum secreto consilio tertia adsedisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012151Z">
        <seg>The woman was amazed, admitting that nothing more could have been said by the Virgin, had she herself sat as a third party to their secret counsel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirata est universa civitas, tantopere se totam sponte commoveri ad cohonestandum funus pauperrimæ Virginis, obscurorum parentum humilis filiæ, quæque, dum vixit, adeo sese ab omnium oculis, notitia, plausu occultarat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030431Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030431Z">
        <seg>The entire city was amazed that it was moved so wholeheartedly to honor the funeral of the poorest Virgin, the humble daughter of obscure parents, who, while she lived, had so hidden herself from everyone's eyes, knowledge, and praise.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirata simplicitatem parens, dum pelliculas dimovet, videt harum mordaci ardore pustulatim Virginis carnem vesicatam, accensam, &amp; turgidis undique tuberculis ulceratam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035517Z">
        <seg>The mother, amazed at the simplicity, while removing the films, sees the Virgin's flesh, eaten away, inflamed, and ulcerated on all sides by pustules burning with a biting heat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirati sunt, quicumque Rosæ hortulum norant, ejus areolis numquam deesse flores, quos altari dedicaret: sive enim hoc germen alibi sustulisset consueta variantis anni temperies, sive ardentior Sirius&lt;a0&gt;k&lt;/a0&gt; ubique torrida siccitate hoc terræ solatium corruperat, semper in modico Rosæ viridario intacti vernabant pulluli&lt;a1&gt;l&lt;/a1&gt;, quos in proximum Sabbathum cælesti Antophoræ&lt;a2&gt;m&lt;/a2&gt; consecraret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011125Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011125Z">
        <seg>Those who knew Rosa's garden were amazed that its flowerbeds were never without flowers which she would dedicate to the altar: for whether the changing seasons of the year had carried this seed elsewhere, or whether the more scorching Sirius had with its torrid heat corrupted this solace of the earth everywhere, always in Rose's modest garden remained untouched the buds of spring, which she would consecrate to the heavenly Antiphora on the coming Sabbath.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirati sunt, quicumque Rosæ hortulum norant, ejus areolis numquam deesse flores, quos altari dedicaret: sive enim hoc germen alibi sustulisset consueta variantis anni temperies, sive ardentior Sirius&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;k&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; ubique torrida siccitate hoc terræ solatium corruperat, semper in modico Rosæ viridario intacti vernabant pulluli&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;l&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, quos in proximum Sabbathum cælesti Antophoræ&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;m&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt; consecraret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054553Z">
        <seg>Those who knew Rose's garden were amazed that its flowerbeds were never without flowers for the altar: for whether the changing seasons of the year had carried this plant elsewhere, or the more scorching Sirius had with its burning heat ruined this comfort of the earth everywhere, always in Rose's modest garden, untouched, the shoots of spring would sprout, which she would consecrate for the next Sabbath to the heavenly Antiphora.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirum dictu!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040137Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040137Z">
        <seg>How strange!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirum, quam alte Virginis animum hæc verba penetrarint, quantum ei roboris constantiæque affuderint, ne ab horrido illo patientiæ exercitio resiliret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042700Z">
        <seg>It is remarkable how deeply these words penetrated the Virgin's soul, and how much strength and constancy they gave her, so that she would not falter from that dreadful exercise of patience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirum, quot quantasque uno hoc telo victorias contra stygios dolos reportarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052237Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052237Z">
        <seg>It is amazing how many and what great victories he has won with this single weapon against the Stygian deceptions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Misit deinde patri, quam unice habebat in deliciis, brevem parvuli Jesu iconem decenter vestitam, suum (uti aiebat) medicum, quem deinde a convalescente repetiit, nuntians, absque hoc sibi nimium se solitariam videri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012359Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012359Z">
        <seg>She then sent her father—whom she dearly loved—a small, neatly dressed icon of the infant Jesus, her "physician," as she called him, which she later retrieved from the convalescent, saying that without it she found herself far too lonely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Misit igitur, qui Gundisalvi consensum elicerent, regiæ (quam vocant) audientiæ senatorem, &amp; fisci procuratorem, quibus honoris &amp; auctoritatis gratia proprium suum adjunxit confessarium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012733Z">
        <seg>He therefore sent to win Gundisalvus's consent the senator of the royal (as they call it) audience and the fiscal procurator, to whom he added his own confessor for the sake of honor and authority.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Misit igitur, qui Rosæ nuntiarent, improvisa ægritudine se obrui, nec vires sibi futuras ad prædicandum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053452Z">
        <seg>He therefore sent messengers to tell Rose that he was overcome by a sudden illness and would not have the strength to preach.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Missas quidem auscultabat omnes, quotquot in æde FF.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060012Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060012Z">
        <seg>He indeed attended all the Masses, however many there were in the Franciscan friary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Missas quidem auscultabat omnes, quotquot in æde FF. Prædicatorum celebrabantur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011357Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011357Z">
        <seg>He indeed attended all the Masses celebrated in the church of the Preachers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Molestissimo cruciatu sic permansit foris per octennium, donec Beatæ hujus (cui impense Candida se commendarat) imagine papyracea ventri applicita, sensit in continenti ægra, matricem in alvum rediisse: unde confestim sana &amp; robusta lecto exsiliit, ac per domum visa est præ gaudio saltitare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043249Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043249Z">
        <seg>She endured this most excruciating torment outdoors for eight years, until a paper image of this blessed one (to whom Candida had entrusted herself) was applied to her belly, and the bedridden woman felt the womb return to her womb. Immediately she leapt from her bed, healthy and strong, and was seen dancing about the house with joy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Molestum hominibus animal si quod aliud: nam unica sua proboscide, &amp; tubicinem agit &amp; militem, cum non minus feriat tubo, quam tuba ac bombo sopitos infestet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043748Z">
        <seg>A troublesome animal to people, if any other: for with its single proboscis, it both plays the pipe and serves as a soldier, since it strikes with its tube no less than it torments the drowsy with its pipe and drum.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Monasterii præfecta, ut Rosæ matrem amabat tenerrime, sic febrientis mœsta periculo, dum nocte quadam stipata monialibus infirmam visitat, post dulces affatus, sensim de apportanda Virginis benefica imagine quæstionem interjecit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040841Z">
        <seg>The abbess, who loved Rose's mother most tenderly, was likewise deeply distressed at the sick woman's peril; and one night, accompanied by a number of nuns to visit the infirm, after sweet words she gently broached the question of bringing the Virgin's beneficent image.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Monialis a gaudio pulcherrimæ visionis evigilans, iterum (concepta jam ingenti spe) Virgini rem devote commendavit, rursumque dormivit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033942Z">
        <seg>The nun, awakened from the joy of the most beautiful vision, once again devoutly entrusted the matter to the Virgin and fell asleep again.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Monitu sui confessarii dictos pulveres denti, cum maxime furebat dolor, admovit, &amp; illico dimovit omne tormentum, liberisque gingivarum&lt;a0&gt;*&lt;/a0&gt; solida quæque esculenta manducavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035942Z">
        <seg>At the advice of his confessor, he applied the said powders to his tooth when the pain was at its worst, and it immediately removed all the torment; and with his gums now free, he chewed solid food.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Monuere, qui de Rosæ virtutibus post obitum solemniter testificati sunt, duplex jejuniorum ei fuisse genus, unum commune, quo semel in die pertenuem panis &amp; aquæ libabat refectiunculam, at non nisi sub noctem: alterum vero appellabat suum, quo per totum diem noctemque penitus gustabat nihil.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041305Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041305Z">
        <seg>Those who solemnly testified to Rose's virtues after her death reported that she observed two kinds of fasts: one common, in which she once a day took a very small refection of bread and water, but only at night; the other, which she called her own, in which she tasted nothing at all, neither by day nor by night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mortificationis etiam &amp; pœnitentiæ exercitia, &amp; in his non suo, sed confessariorum arbitratu modum constituisse, suarumque virium diffidentiam, fidei ardorem, spei securitatem, divini amoris incendia sic dilucide explicavit, ut sinceram ac tutam esse viam, qua hæc electa Dei Virgo incedebat, censores disparatis colloquiis, scrutiniis &amp; censuris, unanimi spiritu, voto &amp; sententia concluserint.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041814Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041814Z">
        <seg>He also explained the exercises of mortification and penance, and in these, not in his own, but established the measure of them by the confessors' judgment, and explained so clearly his own lack of confidence in his own strength, the ardor of his faith, the security of his hope, and the fires of divine love, that the censors, with one mind, concluded by vote and decree that the path on which this chosen Virgin of God walked was sincere and safe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox &amp; sepulchri mirificos pulveres ut cum jusculo ebibit, totius indigesti marcoris corruptam massam simul excrevit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040803Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040803Z">
        <seg>Soon, as he drank the marvelous powders of the sepulchre along with the juice, he simultaneously expelled the entire undigested, corrupt mass of the worm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox Virgo in oratorium se proripuit, ac inde post duas circiter horas egressa brachium undequaque sanum &amp; flexile ostentavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054016Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054016Z">
        <seg>Soon the virgin rushed into the oratory, and when she emerged about two hours later, she displayed her arm completely sound and supple.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox aliquantulum cælos intuens, postque brevem morulam in se reflexa, “Nil (inquit) ambigito, Rodericus tuus post menses admodum paucos religiosam induet togam, sed non illam, quam tu unice volebas, Societatis Jesu”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012600Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012600Z">
        <seg>Soon, looking up at the heavens for a moment, and after a brief pause in thought, she said, "Doubt not, your Roderick will don the religious habit in just a few months, but not that of the Society of Jesus which you so dearly desired."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox altis ac prætumidis passibus totum conclave perambulans, ad Rosæ nutum pluries toto distento pectusculo alacriter cantavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061547Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061547Z">
        <seg>Soon, pacing the entire chamber with lofty and proud strides, he sang eagerly several times at Rose's nod, his whole chest distended.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox cogitabundus ait intra se: “Quæcumque est hæc Virgo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055823Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055823Z">
        <seg>Soon, pondering, he said to himself: "Whatever this Virgin may be;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox consurgens, hilariori voce poposcit forfices ab uxore Gundisalvi, quæ illic cum aliis ad opus substiterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055402Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055402Z">
        <seg>Rising soon after, he asked in a more cheerful voice for scissors from Gundisalvi's wife, who was standing there with others at work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox conticescente Rosa, ex abrupto carmen suum resumebat avicula, duplicabat intercisionum minutias, tremulis arteriolis volubiles confundebat accentus, deprimebat, elevabat, torquebat facillimas lubricæ voculæ argutias, &amp; repente, velut ad pausæ signum, interquiescebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045317Z">
        <seg>As soon as Rosa fell silent, the little bird would abruptly resume its song, double the minutiae of its interruptions, confuse the accents with trembling, whirling arterioles, depress, elevate, and twist the most delicate witticisms of its slippery little voice, and suddenly, as if on a pause sign, it would fall silent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox correptam imaginem reverenter pectori jacentis imposuit, cumque tribus reliquis ibidem tristi silentio eventum exspectavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033217Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033217Z">
        <seg>He soon reverently laid the recovered image upon the chest of the one lying there, and with the other three he awaited the outcome in sorrowful silence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox eo confluxit torrens ingentis populi, &amp; pavori gaudioque medius palam solvitur in lachrymas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030641Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030641Z">
        <seg>Soon a torrent of immense people converged there, and he, caught between fear and joy, openly broke down in tears.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox hilares susurri certatim implebant angulos, organique pneumatici modulos gestiebat æmulari volatilis harmonia, donec, jubente Rosa, simul omnes conticescerent velut sub una lege nocturni silentii.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043838Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043838Z">
        <seg>Soon cheerful whispers filled the corners in eager competition, and the organ's pneumatic pipes boasted a flying harmony, until, at Rosa's command, they all fell silent at once, as if under a single law of nocturnal silence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox illa, ut potuit, reverenter picturam deosculans, dum ei successive cervicem, tempora, vultum admovet, somnum (quo tribus fere dierum hebdomadibus caruerat) cumque somno dolorum inducias, denique perfectam valetudinem attraxit a sacra imagine.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040655Z">
        <seg>Soon she, as best she could, reverently kissed the painting, successively pressing her neck, her temples, and her face against it, drew from the sacred image sleep (of which she had been deprived for nearly three weeks), along with respite from her pains, and finally perfect health.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox ingenti fiducia panariam arcam aperuit, plenamque reperit panibus, ultra domus illius morem candidis, azymis, ac saporosis, testante eorumdem figura illic inusitata ac peregrina, hos nequaquam a domestico panificio prodiisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062216Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062216Z">
        <seg>He soon opened the bread chest with great confidence and found it full of loaves—unusually white, unleavened, and flavorful for that household—as evidenced by their shape, which was entirely unfamiliar and exotic there, proving they could in no way have come from the household baker.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox intra penum occlusa animadvertit, porriginosum hostem suum in prægrandi sporta, quam labri fundus occultabat, licentius tumultuari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052048Z">
        <seg>Soon, shut inside the pantry, she noticed her slimy enemy stirring more licentiously in a huge basket hidden beneath the lip of the floor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox nivei candoris Attalicam&lt;a1&gt;d&lt;/a1&gt; emit, novamque iconi vestem aptavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011227Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011227Z">
        <seg>He soon bought an Attalic one of snowy whiteness and fitted a new garment to the icon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox nivei candoris Attalicam&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;d&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; emit, novamque iconi vestem aptavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055101Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055101Z">
        <seg>He soon painted Attalus white as snow and dressed the icon in a new garment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox percepto, quid acciderat, animatus consedit in lectulo, nec solum incolumem, sed &amp; sanum se vegetumque sensit, nec febris deinceps rediit: attamen urgentibus, quæ aderant, feminis, medicinam ante præparatam, tametsi jam superfluam, sumpsit, quievit, ac post biduum lecto doloris valedicens, Rosæ sepulchrum adiit, totumque ibi diem in gratiarum actione transegit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033226Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033226Z">
        <seg>As soon as he realized what had happened, he sat up in bed, feeling himself not only unhurt but also healthy and vigorous, and the fever never returned. However, at the urging of the women who were there, he took the medicine that had been prepared beforehand, though it was now superfluous, rested, and after two days, bidding farewell to his bed of pain, He went to Rose's tomb and spent the entire day there in thanksgiving.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox puparum lusu coæqualibus relicto, abdebat se in secretum angulum, quasi arcanis cogitationibus quietius vacatura: illic interdum deprehensa a fratre, ac interrogata, quidni mallet, puerulis festive nugantibus colludere, quam in pulverulento, ac pulicibus referto angulo desidere?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043234Z">
        <seg>Soon, having left her playmates to their dolls, she withdrew to a secret corner, as if to devote herself more quietly to her secret thoughts. There, sometimes discovered by her brother and asked what she would rather do, she would reply that she preferred to play with the boys in their festive nonsense than to sit in that dusty, flea-infested corner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox rapta, intellectuali visione conspicata est Rosam divina luce coruscam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032008Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032008Z">
        <seg>Soon, in a flash of intellectual vision, she saw Rose shining with divine light.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox rediit genis detritis vividus color, reddita faciei corpulentior plenitudo, fronti nitor, luminibus vigor, ut propemodum jurari potuisset, Rosam, quid sit jejunium, penitus ignorare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040741Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040741Z">
        <seg>Soon a vivid color returned to her cheeks, the fuller roundness of her face was restored, a glow to her brow, vigor to her eyes, so that one could almost swear that Rosa was utterly ignorant of what fasting is.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox tota urbe trepidatum est, jussique cum civibus ecclesiastici arma corripere: siquidem certum erat, Calvinianum hunc hostem non solis incolarum fortunis &amp; jugulis, sed &amp; sacrorum profanationi, templorum spoliis, religionis exterminio sævissime inhiare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060152Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060152Z">
        <seg>Soon the whole city was in an uproar, and the clergy, together with the citizens, were ordered to seize arms; for it was certain that this Calvinist foe was most savagely intent not only on the fortunes and throats of the inhabitants, but also on the desecration of sacred things, the plundering of churches, and the extermination of religion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox ubi in mea cellula cœpi deficere, misi angelum, qui D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051741Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051741Z">
        <seg>Soon, when I began to fail in my cell, I sent an angel, who D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox ubi in mea cellula cœpi deficere, misi angelum, qui D. Mariæ de Usategui suggereret, me illo, quem vidisti, succursu indigere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010703Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010703Z">
        <seg>Soon, when I began to fail in my cell, I sent an angel to inform Mrs. Mary of Usategui that I needed the help you had seen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Multa de similibus amantis Rosæ vaporariis, de anteroticis&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; ad sponsum aspiriis, &amp; eclogis dicenda superessent, forte non indigna relatu;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010914Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010914Z">
        <seg>Many things would remain to be said about the similar vapors of a loving Rose, about the pre-nuptial aspirations to the bridegroom, and about the eclogues; perhaps not unworthy of relation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Multa de similibus amantis Rosæ vaporariis, de anteroticis&lt;s0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;c&lt;/a1&gt;&lt;/s0&gt; ad sponsum aspiriis, &amp; eclogis dicenda superessent, forte non indigna relatu;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053254Z">
        <seg>Many things would remain to be said about the similar vaporings of the lover Rose, about the pre-nuptial aspirations to the bridegroom, and about the eclogues; perhaps not unworthy of relation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Muniebant utrimque portantium latera excellentissimi proregis armati stipatores, tum, ut per densam multitudinem vi &amp; clamore laxarent vias, tum vero ut a devota rapacitate populi reliquias poscentis protegerent virgineum cadaver: nam ut singuli velut in tumultu Rosam acclamabant &lt;b0&gt;Sanctam&lt;/b0&gt;, ita violentiores ac valentiores passim inhiabant sandapilæ, avidi paratique inde decerpere, quidquid possent, ni hastatorum bipennes quasi sepem objecissent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030620Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030620Z">
        <seg>On both sides, the most excellent lieutenants of the deputy-king, armed escorts, flanked the sides of the bearers, both to clear the way through the dense crowd with force and shouting, and also to protect the virgin's body from the devout rapacity of the people clamoring for relics: For just as individuals, in the midst of the tumult, cried out "Holy" to Rosa, so the more violent and stronger everywhere gnawed at the sandalwood, eager and ready to pluck from it whatever they could, had not the double-edged spears of the spearmen formed a kind of hedge.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mutua harum Virginum conversatio tam amica &amp; crebra fuit, ut sicut Moysi, post longam cum Deo in monte confabulationem, remansere in vultu luminum vestigia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051434Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051434Z">
        <seg>The mutual conversation of these Virgins was so friendly and frequent, that, just as with Moses, after a long conversation with God on the mountain, traces of light remained on his face.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mysterii intelligentia hic fratri defuit, non item Rosæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062536Z">
        <seg>The brother here lacked an understanding of the mystery, but Rose did not.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mysticam theologiam non nudis speculationibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050051Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050051Z">
        <seg>Mystical theology not by bare speculation;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nam quotiescumque Rosa primo diluculo reserabat cellulæ portam, &lt;s2&gt;[eam non pungerent,]&lt;/s2&gt; &amp; laxabat fenestellæ valvulas, culicibus (quotquot intus ad parietes densi pernoctarant) præcipiebat: “Eia amici, ad laudes omnipotentis Dei”;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043829Z">
        <seg>For whenever Rosa opened the cell door at first light and loosened the little window shutters, she would call out to the mosquitoes (all those that had spent the night clustered against the walls): "Come, friends, to the praises of Almighty God."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nam quotiescumque Rosa primo diluculo reserabat cellulæ portam, [eam non pungerent,] &amp; laxabat fenestellæ valvulas, culicibus (quotquot intus ad parietes densi pernoctarant) præcipiebat: “Eia amici, ad laudes omnipotentis Dei”;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010009Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010009Z">
        <seg>For whenever Rosa opened the cell door at first light, [they would not sting her,] and loosened the window shutters, she would call to the mosquitoes (whichever had spent the night clustered against the walls): "Come, friends, to the praises of Almighty God."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nam repente frigido sudore madidus, dum successive digitos, manum, brachium motu lenissimo pertentat, omnia sana, vegeta, flexilia reperit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033316Z">
        <seg>For suddenly, drenched in cold sweat, he, while successively testing his fingers, hand, and arm with the gentlest motion, finds everything sound, vigorous, and supple.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nam reversa domum, &amp; vixdum evoluta palliolo, recta properabat ad secretum cubiculi, ibi in seram usque noctem se recludens, meditabatur suscepti beneficii magnitudinem, neque in ullius prodibat conspectum aut conversationem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055944Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055944Z">
        <seg>For when she returned home, and had scarcely taken off her cloak, she hurried straight to her private chamber, where, shutting herself in until late at night, she pondered the greatness of the favor she had received, and she appeared to no one nor conversed with anyone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Narravit Virgini confessarius dolorem proreginæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054956Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054956Z">
        <seg>The Virgin's confessor told the queen regent of her pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nautæ æquatorem vulgo appellant&lt;/e1&gt; mediam lineam, &lt;e2&gt;vel antonomastice&lt;/e2&gt; lineam, &lt;e3&gt;quo sensu hic accipi videtur.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041355Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041355Z">
        <seg>Nauta commonly call the equator the middle line, or, by antonomasia, the line, in which sense it seems to be taken here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ne matris præceptum floccifieret, hos floccos condensasti in solidæ petræ duritiem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042840Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042840Z">
        <seg>So that you would not flout your mother's command, you condensed these flakes into the hardness of solid rock.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ne tamen oblatam remedii gratiam videretur aspernari, continuo ipsamet confessarium adiit in æde S. Dominici: candore, quo solebat, rem exposuit, nutum expectavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054001Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054001Z">
        <seg>However, so as not to appear to spurn the offered remedy, she immediately went herself to the confessor in the church of St. Dominic: with the candor she was accustomed to, she laid the matter before him and awaited his nod.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ne tamen sic pendula paucis capillis totam corpusculi molem committeret, utcumque summis pedum digitis insistens, tangebat terram, sed ægre satis, ac per alternas successiones nutante lubrico fulcimento.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043103Z">
        <seg>Yet so that she would not entrust the entire weight of her little body to a few strands of hair, she stood on the tips of her toes, touching the ground—but barely—and, with each successive sway, her slippery support wobbled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec (quod fere illi regioni præter morem est) ullum Virgini discrimen erat originis aut nationis, quin ex æquo &amp; indifferenter egentibus Hispanis, &amp; Indis, Æthiopibus, candidis, mixtisque (quas Mulatas&lt;a1&gt;d&lt;/a1&gt; vocant) prompta charitate succurreret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011633Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011633Z">
        <seg>Nor (which is almost unusual for that region) was there any distinction in Virginia of origin or nation, but she would equally and impartially come to the aid of needy Spaniards, Indians, Ethiopians, whites, and mixed-race people (whom they call mulattos) with ready charity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec (quod fere illi regioni præter morem est) ullum Virgini discrimen erat originis aut nationis, quin ex æquo &amp; indifferenter egentibus Hispanis, &amp; Indis, Æthiopibus, candidis, mixtisque (quas Mulatas&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;d&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; vocant) prompta charitate succurreret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061246Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061246Z">
        <seg>Nor (which is almost unusual for that region) was there any distinction in Virginia of origin or nation, but she would equally and impartially come to the aid of needy Spaniards, Indians, Ethiopians, whites, and mixed-race people (whom they call mulattos) with ready charity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec Rosa his contenta, ipsos quoque divini verbi præcones frequentissime compellabat, suos auditores ad orationis exercitamenta inflammarent, usum piarum meditationum populariter inveherent, disciplinæ hujus utilitatem, suavitatem, præstantiam, omni argumentorum vi, totoque facundiæ suæ connisu, privatim ac publice commendarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045121Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045121Z">
        <seg>Nor was Rosa content with this; she also most frequently urged the preachers of the divine word to inflame their hearers with exercises in prayer, to popularize the practice of pious meditations, and to recommend, both privately and publicly, the usefulness, sweetness, and excellence of this discipline with all the force of their arguments and the full exertion of their eloquence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec Virgo rosa mystica virgunculam Rosam distulit consolari: siquidem in alto, sed inexplicabili intimæ illustrationis radio, pavidam affata, docuit, mire hoc suum nomen probari divino, quem gestabat, Puerulo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034736Z">
        <seg>Nor did the Virgin, the mystical Rose, delay to console the little maiden Rose; for from on high, yet by an inexplicable ray of inner illumination, she spoke to the fearful one and taught her that, remarkably, this very name of hers was being proven divine by the little Boy she carried.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec adeo evanuit hic sudor, quin sub barba, usque ad oram marginis, vestigia reliquerit, multis post annis &amp; lustris palam conspicua.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053855Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053855Z">
        <seg>Nor has this sweat so vanished but that it has left traces under the beard, right to the very edge, plainly visible many years and lustres later.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec adeo has alligaturas, quamquam strictissimas, pellis obduxerat, quin videre eas potuerit Mariana, quando medebatur laceris Rosæ scapulis: nam sæpe postulabatur hoc genus officii, eo quod jugiter in Rosæ dorso patebant scissuræ, quibus, ex intervallo, jussa Mariana inspergebat ignotos pulveres, ne saniem conciperent, chartaque bibula obducebat, repetitis postridie tunsionibus renovandas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041820Z">
        <seg>Nor had the skin, though it covered these ligatures so tightly, closed up so completely that Mariana could not see them when she was treating Rose's torn shoulders: for this kind of service was often required, because the cracks on Rose's back were constantly open, into which, at intervals, Mariana would sprinkle unknown powders by order, so that they would not fester, and cover them with blotting paper, to be renewed by repeated rubbings the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec alio fuste ad abigendum hunc Cerberum&lt;a0&gt;k&lt;/a0&gt; opus fuit: nam continuo elumbis, victus, fractus resiliit, fugit, evanuit, &amp; Rosa prorsus illæsam, indemnemque se admirata, corde tranquillo ad orationem rediit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010719Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010719Z">
        <seg>Nor was there any need for another club to drive off this Cerberus; for at once, his loins struck, defeated and broken, he recoiled, fled, and vanished, and Rosa, admiring herself completely unhurt and unharmed, returned to her prayer with a tranquil heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec alio fuste ad abigendum hunc Cerberum&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;k&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; opus fuit: nam continuo elumbis, victus, fractus resiliit, fugit, evanuit, &amp; Rosa prorsus illæsam, indemnemque se admirata, corde tranquillo ad orationem rediit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051942Z">
        <seg>Nor was there any need for another club to drive off this Cerberus: for at once, his loins struck, defeated and broken, he recoiled, fled, and vanished; and Rosa, admiring herself completely unhurt and unharmed, returned to her prayer with a tranquil heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec defuerunt, qui in morientis pallido vultu notarint ipsissimam Christi in cruce exspirantis effigiem, quod alio loco, &amp; tempore, in S. Catharina Senensi, itidem lecto decubua, fuerat observatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043010Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043010Z">
        <seg>Nor were there lacking those who recognized in the dying man's pale face the very image of Christ expiring on the cross, which had been similarly observed in St. Catherine of Siena when she was also bedridden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec diu capillata occasio defuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040729Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040729Z">
        <seg>A hair-raising opportunity did not long fail.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec dubitavit Virgo pacisci cum Deo sub tali onere: malebat enim alienis febribus torreri per omnes medullas, quam quod ex concionis defectu gloria Dei in mutila festivitate detrimentum pateretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053514Z">
        <seg>Nor did the Virgin hesitate to make a pact with God under such a burden: for she preferred to be scorched to the very marrow by others' fevers, rather than have the glory of God suffer detriment in a mutilated celebration because of a lack of participants.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec fefellit eventus: nam post Virginis obitum aurifaber, cui injunctum erat aliquot bracteolæ spinas evellere, (forsitan ut devotis personis eas flagitantibus in domum usumve cederent) adhibitis forcipibus, adeo singulas invenit firmas, ut extrahi nulla potuerit, quamquam ad ferri tractum huc illuc inflecterentur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042158Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042158Z">
        <seg>And the outcome did not disappoint: for after the Virgin's death the goldsmith, who had been instructed to remove several of the little plates from the spikes (perhaps so that they might be given to devout persons who requested them for home or devotional use), using forceps found each one so firmly set that none could be pulled out, although they bent here and there under the pressure of the iron.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec fefellit eventus: nam sub ejusmodi luteo, nullaque tinctura infecto corporis sui tegumento Rosa tutissime latuit, usque ad vigesimum ætatis annum, quo tandem ad Dominicanæ togæ candorem sublevata, facile perrexit reliquas eludere pomposi luxus insidias.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035449Z">
        <seg>And the outcome did not disappoint: for under such a clay-like coating, unstained by any dye, Rosa lay hidden in the covering of her body until she reached the age of twenty, when at last, lifted up to the whiteness of the Dominican habit, she easily went on to evade the remaining snares of pompous luxury.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec fefellit opinio: vix enim per mensem Limæ sic decubuerat, quando Rosæ tunc recens sepultæ odor sanctimoniæ, &amp; publica miraculorum fama quotidie grandior ad ægram hanc penetravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034900Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034900Z">
        <seg>Nor did the opinion fail: for she had scarcely lain thus sick in Lima for a month when the scent of sanctity from Rose, then newly buried, and the public fame of her miracles, growing daily greater, penetrated to this sick woman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec fefellit pia fiducia, ut in appendice Mantissa summatim recensebitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031307Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031307Z">
        <seg>Nor did the pious trust fail, as will be briefly reviewed in the appendix, Mantissa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec longe abfuit præsentanea occasio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060137Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060137Z">
        <seg>A present opportunity was not far off.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec malo inveniebatur remedium, nec a malo requies.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035005Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035005Z">
        <seg>Neither was there a remedy for the evil, nor any rest from the evil.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec minor ipsi cura fuit citissime abeundi, ut plausum effugeret, quem merito timebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061513Z">
        <seg>He was no less anxious to depart as quickly as possible, to avoid the applause he justly feared.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec minori emphasi reliquos per ordinem domesticos affata est, quemque decenter admonens officii, &amp; Christianæ pietatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030201Z">
        <seg>She addressed the other household servants in turn with no less emphasis, admonishing each one decently of their duty and of Christian piety.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec minus adstupuit obstetrix, ut vidit hanc infantem secundarum involucris prodire gravem, &amp; quasi penulatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034630Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034630Z">
        <seg>Nor was the midwife any less astonished when she saw this infant emerging from the umbilical wrappings heavy and as if encased in a little coat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec minus aperte, quotquot sudoris spectaculo coram fruiti erant, fatebantur, se sub illius intuitu novos, acutos, insolitos sensisse divini amoris stimulos, idque erat, quod Virgo tam ardenter &amp; anxie flagitarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053933Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053933Z">
        <seg>Nor did those who had enjoyed the spectacle of sweat before him any less openly confess that, under his gaze, they had felt new, sharp, unusual stirrings of divine love—and this was what the Virgin had so ardently and anxiously longed for.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec minus ejusdem ardoris indices ac favillæ erant, ignita illa suspiria, quibus, veluti flabellis, cor illud succensum &amp; æstuans, simul difflabat calores suos, simul eosdem validius succendebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053030Z">
        <seg>Nor were the sparks of that same ardor any less numerous: those fiery sighs by which, like fans, that kindled and burning heart both blew away its heat and at the same time fanned it more fiercely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec mirum si medicinæ cælestes a centro ad superficiem soleant operari, ac prius animam, quam corpus attingere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055458Z">
        <seg>It is no wonder that heavenly medicines are accustomed to work from the center to the surface, and to touch the soul before the body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec mirum, duabus his vicibus Rosam solito fulsisse gloriosiorem: nam ubi in priori de patientia agebatur, ostendenda fuit eodem, quo seraphica Mater sua, patientiæ speciali privilegio decorata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032033Z">
        <seg>It is no wonder, then, that on these two occasions Rose shone more gloriously than usual: for when in the first instance she was speaking of patience, she had to show herself adorned with the same special privilege of patience as her seraphic Mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec mitiores in eam fuere domestici, postquam nimirum innotuit ejus vitæ singularitas, cælestium visorum frequentia &amp; commercium, ac præcipue illa humanis viribus evidenter superior abstinentia, qua facile arguebatur, tam modico ac fere nullo alimento, juvenculam naturaliter in vita sustentari non posse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052510Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052510Z">
        <seg>Nor were her household any gentler toward her, once it became known how extraordinary her life was—the frequency and intercourse of heavenly visions, and especially that abstinence, clearly beyond human strength, by which it was easily shown that a young woman could not naturally be sustained on so meager, almost nonexistent, a diet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec mitiores in eam fuere domestici, quando ejus virtutes &amp; cælestia dona innotuerunt, vano timore correpti, ne cum magno familiæ dedecore, ut de imposturis suspecta ad sacræ Inquisitonis tribunal raperetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042031Z">
        <seg>Nor were her own household any more lenient toward her when her virtues and heavenly gifts became known, seized by a vain fear that, to the great disgrace of the family, she would be dragged before the tribunal of the Holy Inquisition as suspected of imposture.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec mitiori successu obediebat medicis, si quando aut languentem, aut lentius convalescentem tentabant esu carnium ad vires pristinas revocare: tantum enim aberat isthoc genere alimenti juvari infirmam, ut etiam a bolo, velut a toxico, recidiva magis debilitaretur, morboque redintegrato, non ægra, sed ægritudo vires acciperet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041042Z">
        <seg>Nor did he obey the physicians with any better success when, at times, they tried to restore a languishing or slowly recovering patient to his former strength by having him eat meat: for it was so far from helping a weak person with this kind of nourishment that even a single dose, like a poison, would further weaken the patient, and with the disease renewed, it was not the patient but the illness that gained strength.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec mora, neglectis grallis surrexit puer, stetit, ambulavit, neque præ lætitia seipsum capiens subsiliit, tripudiavit, denique turbam densissimam rogavit, tantisper in partem secederet, sibique spatium currendi daret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033456Z">
        <seg>Without delay, the boy, neglecting the gulls, rose up, stood, walked, and, unable to contain himself for joy, leaped, capered, and finally asked the densest part of the crowd to step aside for a moment and give him room to run.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec mora, postridie adornavit thalamum, comportavit in hortum tabulas, intraque pauculos dies perfectum stetit solitariæ devotionis breve, arctum, humile sacrarium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043637Z">
        <seg>Without delay, the next day he adorned the bridal chamber, carried boards into the garden, and within a few days a perfect, brief, narrow, humble shrine of solitary devotion stood complete.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec mora: exaudivit Ludovicam Rosa, Rosam Deus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032913Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032913Z">
        <seg>Without delay: he heard Ludovica Rosa, the Rose of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec mora;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052110Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052110Z">
        <seg>Without delay</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec multum rogari se passus Georgius, coram eis (&amp; plerisque notissimus pridem erat, tum ipse, tum brachii sui stupor) fideliter totam rei seriem exposuit: simul rogati notarius &amp; testes, priusquam aliquid in tabulas referretur, miraculum penitissime explorarunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033325Z">
        <seg>George, not one to be asked much, before them (and he, and the stupor of his arm, were already well known to most) faithfully laid out the entire sequence of events: at the same time, the notary and the witnesses, before anything was entered into the records, investigated the miracle most thoroughly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec pavori cedens, mandavit transeunti illac ancillulæ, candelam sibi adferri;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052035Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052035Z">
        <seg>Not yielding to fear, she commanded the little maid passing by to bring her a candle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec perfunctorie hæc dicebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053220Z">
        <seg>Nor did he say these things perfunctorily.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec prætereundum, quod in hac pietate erga parentes cursum vitæ suæ feliciter clausit: matrem enim prævidens pro sui obitus adventu summo mœrore afficiendam, Sponsum suum enixe precata, tantam divinarum consolationum sensibilem affluentiam ei obtinuit, ut postmodum jurejurando deposuerit, se in tam dilectæ Filiæ præmatura morte expertam fuisse cor suum nimis angustum, &amp; incapax ad cælestia illa gaudia capessenda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041225Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041225Z">
        <seg>Nor should it be passed over that in this piety toward his parents he happily concluded the course of his life: for foreseeing that his mother would be overcome with the deepest grief at the approach of his death, Having earnestly implored her betrothed, she obtained for her such an abundant outpouring of sensible divine consolations that she afterward testified under oath that, upon experiencing the untimely death of her most beloved daughter, her heart had become too constricted and incapable of partaking in those heavenly joys.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec publici præcones, si vicatim emissi fora &amp; compita stentoreis implessent clamoribus, primo mane tam densum collegissent populum, quantus ultro ex omni platea ad Gundisalvi ædes confluxit, nulla humana diligentia excitus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030338Z">
        <seg>Nor could the public heralds, even if sent out in relays and filling the forums and crossroads with stentorian cries, have by early morning gathered a crowd as dense as the one that of its own accord streamed from every street to Gundisalvi's house, roused by no human diligence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec reliquum anni tempus vacuum est a perpetuo concursu, præsertim quoties aut terræmotus, aut epidemiæ&lt;a3&gt;f&lt;/a3&gt; contagium, aliave, seu publica seu privata necessitas admonet cives præsentanei sui asyli.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011057Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011057Z">
        <seg>Nor is the rest of the year free from a constant influx, especially whenever earthquakes, the contagion of epidemics, or other such events—whether a public or private necessity—compel citizens to seek refuge in their own asylum.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec reliquum anni tempus vacuum est a perpetuo concursu, præsertim quoties aut terræmotus, aut epidemiæ&lt;s8&gt;&lt;a9&gt;f&lt;/a9&gt;&lt;/s8&gt; contagium, aliave, seu publica seu privata necessitas admonet cives præsentanei sui asyli.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054307Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054307Z">
        <seg>Nor is the rest of the year's time free from a constant influx, especially whenever earthquakes, epidemics, or other contagion, or some other public or private necessity, compels citizens to seek refuge in their present asylum.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec silendum, quod in dextra nascentis palpebra conspicue apparuit Rosa ad vivum effigiata, diuque, velut beneficii indelebile sigillum, remansit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035130Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035130Z">
        <seg>Nor should it be left unsaid that on the right side of the newborn's eyelid a rose, lifelike in its depiction, appeared and long remained, as if an indelible seal of a favor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec sine fructu abiit solicita intercessio: nam dum gyrantem processionem hinc inde pro magnifico applausu, vario fragore excipiunt sulphurata missilium ignium crepitacula, unum istorum recta Franciscæ oculum petiit, inque supercilium impegit, nullo tamen Franciscæ damno;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055309Z">
        <seg>Nor did the anxious intercession go without result: for while the circling procession was being greeted here and there with magnificent applause, the sulphurous crackling of fiery missiles met it with varied din; one of these struck Francis squarely in the eye and struck her eyebrow, yet without any harm to her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec sinebat Virginem tantæ commiserationis affectus quiescere: unde rationem excogitavit, qua (nisi morte præventa fuisset) sperabat in apostolicæ missionis negotio supplere per alium, quod per semetipsam præstare non poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060502Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060502Z">
        <seg>Nor did she allow the Virgin to rest in such a compassionate affection; hence she devised a plan by which, had she not been prevented by death, she hoped to supply by another in the work of the apostolic mission what she could not accomplish by herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec solis suis occiduis Indis condolebat, sed quidquid nationum vastissimi Chinarum tractus, aut Orientis imperia continent, Rosæ quotidiana plangendi materies erant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060400Z">
        <seg>Nor did she lament only the western suns of the Indians, but whatever nations the vastest tract of China or the empires of the East contain were daily matter for Rose's lament.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec spei eventus defuit: nam e Lima usque Potosium&lt;a0&gt;i&lt;/a0&gt;, per varios casus, per mille rerum discrimina, iter habuit tutissimum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010713Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010713Z">
        <seg>Nor did the outcome disappoint: for from Lima to Potosí, through various incidents and a thousand perils, he made the journey in the safest possible way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec spei eventus defuit: nam e Lima usque Potosium&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;i&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, per varios casus, per mille rerum discrimina, iter habuit tutissimum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051843Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051843Z">
        <seg>Nor did the outcome disappoint: for from Lima to Potosí, through various incidents and a thousand perils, he had the safest journey.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen (quantumvis certa) videri volebat, quod erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050011Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050011Z">
        <seg>Yet he did not want to appear to be what he was, however certain it was.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen diu latuit matrem solertia, fartum corripuit, lignea inde retrimenta projici, lanam substitui, lanam ingeri, diserte &amp; cum clamore præcepit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042741Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042741Z">
        <seg>But the mother's shrewdness did not remain hidden for long; she snatched the stuffing, threw away the wooden bits, had wool substituted, had wool stuffed in, and gave the orders clearly and loudly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen hic jugis, &amp; acerrimus dolor ab ea, vel minimum potuit exprimere gemitum aut molestiæ indicium, nisi quod a matre interrogata de dolore, cum totum negare non posset, modicum ac toleratu facilem esse respondit: sicque in iis tormentis cum sua matre pernoctavit silens, immota, quietissima.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034906Z">
        <seg>Yet this constant, most intense pain from her could not draw even the slightest groan or sign of distress, except that when her mother asked about the pain, since she could not deny it entirely, she replied that it was slight and easily borne. And so she spent the night with her mother in those torments, silent, motionless, and very still.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen in tot culicum legionibus vel unicus fuit, qui Rosam in sua cellula umquam attingeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043754Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043754Z">
        <seg>Yet not even among so many legions of mosquitoes was there a single one that ever touched Rosa in her cell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen omnino id latere permisit Altissimus, dum pluries in vultu Rosæ visibiliter scintillare fecit interioris flammæ indices, evibrationes atque stricturas, tunc, cum anima sumptione divinæ Hostiæ succendebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055743Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055743Z">
        <seg>Yet the Most High did not allow it to remain entirely hidden, for He repeatedly made visible in Rose's face the signs of the inner flame—its vibrations and constrictions—whenever her soul was kindled by the reception of the divine Host.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen plorabat Rosula, sed, proprii pollicis inutili suctu, famem tacita fraudabat magis, quam solabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052438Z">
        <seg>Yet Rosula did not weep, but by the idle sucking of her thumb she more deceived her hunger in silence than she soothed it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen sermone abesse voluit, quia febrilem æstum facile superabat exæstuans divinæ gloriæ zelus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053523Z">
        <seg>Yet he did not wish to be absent from the conversation, for he easily overcame the feverish heat, inflamed by zeal for the glory of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen sub tam vigili ferreaque custodia caveri potuit, ne, ubi ad valvas ecclesiæ ventum est, cadaveri partes vestium, corona &amp; palma deesse notarentur, ibidemque pro ablatis alia fuerint reponenda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030626Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030626Z">
        <seg>Yet even under such vigilant and iron-clad guard it could not be prevented that, when they came to the church doors, it would be noticed that the corpse was missing parts of its clothing, the crown, and the palm, and that others would have to be put in their place there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tanta militum ac religiosorum cautela, vigilia, custodia sat fuit, ne pluries Rosæ defunctæ vela, capilli, vestes frustillatim a solertioribus diripentur, captandarum reliquiarum prætextu;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031016Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031016Z">
        <seg>Nor was the utmost vigilance and guard of soldiers and religious men enough to prevent the veil, hair, and garments of the late Rose from being repeatedly torn to pieces by the more cunning, under the pretext of obtaining relics.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec ullum voraci malo inveniebatur remedium, donec ad Rosæ pharmacopœiam anxia pulsaret devotio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035136Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035136Z">
        <seg>Nor was there any remedy for the voracious evil, until devotion anxiously knocked at Rose's apothecary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec verebatur ultro improvida mater, publice coram suis &amp; alienis confundere Filiam, proclamando uti hypocritam, fallacem, simulatricem, omnisque sinceræ virtutis inanem &amp; vacuam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052519Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052519Z">
        <seg>Nor did the thoughtless mother hesitate to publicly shame her daughter before friends and strangers, proclaiming her a hypocrite, a deceiver, a pretender, and utterly devoid of any genuine virtue.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec vero musculi tantum, sed &amp; interna subjectaque illis viscera eo pariter nomine appellantur, quod sub notharum costarum cartilaginibus condita sint.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025851Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025851Z">
        <seg>Not only the muscles, but also the internal organs subject to them are called by the same name, because they are enclosed beneath the cartilages of the ribs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec ægre assensit ægra, quin adductam lecto exciperet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040844Z">
        <seg>The sick woman did not object, but rather welcomed her to the bed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Necdum verba finierat, cum ferox animal terrifico mugitu in alias procul personas impetum fecit, Rosa cum suo comitatu incolumi manente, quæ adhuc post periculum paventes socias monuit, sperandum firmius in adjutorium Altissimi, &amp; tunc maxime cum periculi vicinitas omne effugium interclusit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042533Z">
        <seg>She had not yet finished speaking when the ferocious beast, with a terrifying roar, charged at the other people far off, Rosa and her party remaining unharmed. She then urged her still trembling companions to place their hope more firmly in the help of the Most High, especially since the closeness of the danger had cut off every means of escape.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Negabat illa, se pupam vel habere, vel etiam contrectare velle;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043227Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043227Z">
        <seg>She denied that she wanted to have or even touch a doll.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Negasses Rosam esse, quæ adeo carebat spinis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040927Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040927Z">
        <seg>You would have denied that the rose was, which was so devoid of thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Negavit Rosa laborem, vim, connixum adesse a parte sua, ultro ad tractum quasi magneticum potentias sequi, &amp; suo centro obverti cum suavitate, cujus vel unica stilla infiniti Oceani amaritudinem corrigeret, ac dulcoraret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050259Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050259Z">
        <seg>Rosa denied that labor, force, or effort was on her part, but that she willingly followed the powers as if by a magnetic attraction, and turned toward her center with a sweetness whose single drop would correct and sweeten the bitterness of an infinite ocean.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Negavit Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051651Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051651Z">
        <seg>Rosa denied it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Negavit inops Virgo talium librorum sibi adesse copiam aut usum, in his solam experientiam &amp; praxim sibi fuisse pro codice;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050315Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050315Z">
        <seg>The poor Virgin denied having a supply or use of such books, saying that for her, experience and practice alone had served as her code.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Negavit se posse Christophorus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032126Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032126Z">
        <seg>Christopher denied that he could.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neglexit ille, quod scala sibi deesset, qua ad sublimem in ara imaginem pertingeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043528Z">
        <seg>He neglected that he lacked a ladder by which he could reach the lofty image on the altar.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nemo aderat, cui tantæ humilitatis verba lachrymas non excuterent: nam, ut tenerrime Rosam suam diligerent, sat fuerat singulis ipsam novisse, ejusque sanctitatem, innocentiam, animi demissionem, candorem, &amp; obsequendi affectum tantis experimentis comprobasse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030037Z">
        <seg>There was no one present whose eyes would not be filled with tears by such words of humility; for, as they loved their Rose most tenderly, it had been enough for each to have known her personally and to have confirmed her holiness, innocence, humility of spirit, purity, and devotion to obedience by so many trials.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nempe corpusculum ex inedia vacuum sustentabat pinguedo spiritus, cui cælestium meditatio sagina erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041343Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041343Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the spirit's fatness sustained the body, which was emptied by fasting, and the meditation of heavenly things was its nourishment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nempe de obedientia Rosæ jam prædefunctæ remanserat tam celebris apud omnes conceptus, tam alta præsumptio, ut crederetur, vel post mortem obeditura superiori, cui numquam in vita subfuerat, videbaturque sat esse ad imperium, quod sua illic hospitabatur imago.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035700Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the reputation of Rose, now deceased, for obedience had become so celebrated among all, the presumption so lofty, that it was believed she would even in death obey a superior to whom she had never been subject in life, and it seemed enough to command the very image that had once dwelt there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nempe hoc unum adhuc defuerat tantæ suæ calamitatis fastigio, quod insuper cogebatur, velut male sana, ad nauseam catapotia&lt;a1&gt;d&lt;/a1&gt;, pharmaca, phlebotomias&lt;a2&gt;e&lt;/a2&gt; admittere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010341Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010341Z">
        <seg>Indeed, this was the one thing still lacking at the height of her great calamity: that she was furthermore compelled, like a sickly patient, to endure, to the point of nausea, purges, medicines, and bloodlettings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nempe hoc unum adhuc defuerat tantæ suæ calamitatis fastigio, quod insuper cogebatur, velut male sana, ad nauseam catapotia&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;d&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, pharmaca, phlebotomias&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;e&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt; admittere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045757Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045757Z">
        <seg>Indeed, this one thing still remained to crown the height of her great calamity, that she was furthermore compelled, like a sick woman, to endure, to the point of nausea, purges, medicines, and bloodlettings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nempe hyblæam&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; Rosam non tangit scarabæus&lt;a1&gt;b&lt;/a1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010034Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010034Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the scarab does not touch the Hyblean rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nempe hyblæam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; Rosam non tangit scarabæus&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;b&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044058Z">
        <seg>Surely the scarab does not touch the Hylæan Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nempe melius docuerat unctio, quam Oliva, &amp; idem, qui sanctæ Catharinæ Senensi artem legendi scribendique superne defuderat, dignatus est &amp; Rosam pari infusionis compendio divinitus erudire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013003Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013003Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the anointing had taught better than the olive, and the same one who had supernaturally bestowed upon Saint Catherine of Siena the art of reading and writing was pleased to divinely instruct Rose with the same infusion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nempe sic Rosam exaudierat, qui novit totum hominem sanum facere in Sabbatho.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060815Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060815Z">
        <seg>Indeed, this was how he had heard Rosam, who knows how to make a whole person well on the Sabbath.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque hic stetit Virginis beneficium: simul cum vitio inveteratum asthma pectoris, hæmorrhagiam&lt;a0&gt;g&lt;/a0&gt;, flatuumque tormina depulit, quibuscum miser ille per annos plus minus quatuordecim fuerat colluctatus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011543Z">
        <seg>Nor did the Virgin's benefit stop there: she at once cast out an old, chronic asthma, hemorrhage, and colic, with which that poor man had been struggling for more than fourteen years.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque hic stetit Virginis beneficium: simul cum vitio inveteratum asthma pectoris, hæmorrhagiam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;g&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, flatuumque tormina depulit, quibuscum miser ille per annos plus minus quatuordecim fuerat colluctatus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060809Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060809Z">
        <seg>Nor did the Virgin's benefit stop there: at the same time, it drove out a chronic asthma of the chest, hemorrhage, and colic, with which that poor man had been struggling for more than fourteen years.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque his contenta, præter frequentes in hebdomada exomologeses, insuper quotidianam sibi coram S. Patre suo Dominico indixerat confessionem, qua, velut in Capitulo correctionis, eidem culpas suas minutim exponebat, veniamque &amp; remedium ab ejus suffragio humiliter postulabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040655Z">
        <seg>Not content with these, besides the frequent weekly confessions, she had also imposed upon herself a daily confession before her holy Father Dominic, in which, as if in a chapter of correction, she minutely laid open her faults to him and humbly asked for pardon and a remedy from his counsel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque his facile accessus poterat denegari, quantumcumque se Rosa absconderet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043454Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043454Z">
        <seg>Nor could access to these be easily denied, however much Rosa hid herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque hoc satis: ipsemet parvus Jesulus, digiti statura vix procerior, sæpe apparebat amanti Rosæ in eadem pagellæ planitie: stabat pusillus ac nudus Amor, mox inambulabat passu levissimo, ac interim obtutu serenulo, tenero, suavi adblandiebatur Virgini, ingerebat se, utpote &lt;b1&gt;Verbum&lt;/b1&gt; attenta Rosæ lectione dignissimum, in quo omnes thesauri scientiæ &amp; sapientiæ Dei.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010533Z">
        <seg>Nor was this enough: the little Jesus himself, scarcely taller than a finger, often appeared to the beloved Rose on the same plane of the page: a little naked Love stood, then would walk with the lightest step, and in the meantime, with a serene, tender, and sweet gaze, he would caress the Virgin, pressing himself upon her, for he was the Word, most worthy of Rose's attentive reading, in whom are all the treasures of the knowledge and wisdom of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque hoc satis: ipsemet parvus Jesulus, digiti statura vix procerior, sæpe apparebat amanti Rosæ in eadem pagellæ planitie: stabat pusillus ac nudus Amor, mox inambulabat passu levissimo, ac interim obtutu serenulo, tenero, suavi adblandiebatur Virgini, ingerebat se, utpote &lt;b2&gt;Verbum&lt;/b2&gt; attenta Rosæ lectione dignissimum, in quo omnes thesauri scientiæ &amp; sapientiæ Dei.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050910Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050910Z">
        <seg>Nor was this enough: the little Jesus himself, scarcely taller than a finger, often appeared to the beloved Rose on the same flat page: Standing there was little naked Love, who would soon be pacing in the lightest of steps, and meanwhile with a serene, tender, sweet gaze he would caress the Virgin, pressing himself upon her, for he was the Word, most worthy of Rose's attentive reading, in whom are all the treasures of the knowledge and wisdom of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque hujus incomparabilis viri admiranda sanctitas, pastoralis zelus, numerosa miracula ignorantur Romæ in Sacro Rituum Senatu, ubi de illius apotheosi pridem ac strenue agitur&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203459Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203459Z">
        <seg>Nor are the admirable sanctity, pastoral zeal, and numerous miracles of this incomparable man unknown in Rome at the Sacred Congregation of Rites, where his apotheosis has long been vigorously debated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque hujus incomparabilis viri admiranda sanctitas, pastoralis zelus, numerosa miracula ignorantur Romæ in Sacro Rituum Senatu, ubi de illius apotheosi pridem ac strenue agitur&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040025Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040025Z">
        <seg>Nor are the admirable sanctity, pastoral zeal, and numerous miracles of this incomparable man unknown in Rome at the Sacred Congregation of Rites, where his apotheosis has long been vigorously debated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque id satis: ipsa simul arbusta flectebant ramos in terram, velut osculatura solum, in Conditoris sui reverentiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045152Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045152Z">
        <seg>Nor was that enough: the shrubs themselves bent their branches to the ground, as if to kiss the soil in reverence to their Creator.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque id solum, sed &amp; postridie Æthiopissæ ancillæ ulnis implicitus infans, nutu gestuque indicavit, velle se in terram deponi: vix plantulis humum attigerat, cum cœpit ambulare solus per atrium, neque ab illo die indiguit ope, aut ulnis bajulæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033607Z">
        <seg>Not only that, but the next day the infant, cradled in the arms of an Ethiopian slave girl, signaled by gesture and motion that he wished to be set down on the ground. Hardly had he touched the floor with his tiny feet when he began to walk alone across the atrium, and from that day on he needed no help or the arms of a bearer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque jam de argento, sed de fama cruciabatur solicitudo: nam periculum erat, nequa innocens temere in furti suspicionem veniret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035647Z">
        <seg>He was now troubled not by money but by his reputation: for there was a danger that some innocent person might rashly be suspected of theft.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque spem matrisfamilias fefellit, cui nimirum paulo post a pulcherrimo hoc gallo pulcherrimi fuere pulli.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061559Z">
        <seg>Nor did it disappoint the housewife, for shortly thereafter this most handsome rooster produced the most beautiful chicks.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihil ei jucundius contingere poterat, quam si a matre permitteretur cujusque conditionis egenas, aut morbis afflictas mulieres paternis inferre ædibus, ut illic suis manibus assidue ministraret languentibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042418Z">
        <seg>Nothing could have been more pleasing to him than if his mother would permit him to bring needy women of any condition, or those afflicted by illness, into his father's house, so that he could there constantly minister to the sick with his own hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus &amp; hoc in Ludovico impleri debuit: siquidem Provincialis paulo ante festum ægritudine correptus, prædicare non potuit, ignarusque vaticinii, sermonem habendum Ludovico demandavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012414Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012414Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, this too had to be fulfilled in Ludovicus: for the Provincial, having been struck by illness shortly before the feast, could not preach, and, unaware of the prophecy, entrusted the sermon to Ludovicus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus &amp; tunc sævissime urebat Sponsi morosa absentia, affligebat durissimi exilii cruda relegatio, contristabat amarissime viduitatis longum divortium, &amp; quod reliquam superabat molestiam, tunc quoque conabatur &amp; nosse, &amp; amare, sed neutrum poterat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045624Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045624Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, even then he was most fiercely consumed by the bridegroom's morose absence, afflicted by the harsh exile of a cruel banishment, most bitterly saddened by the long divorce of widowhood, and what surpassed all the rest of his trouble was that even then he strove to know and to love, but could do neither.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus Rosa, quod futurum erat, spe bona percipiens, aiebat: “Potens est Dominus concedere, quod optamus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055138Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055138Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, Rosa, perceiving with good hope what was to come, said, "The Lord is able to grant what we desire."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus Rosa, ut agnovit sic placitum esse ante Deum, virili planeque heroïco spiritu amplexa tormentum, “Non mea (inquit) voluntas fiat, sed tua.” Sicque constanter ad secuturæ diei similem equuleum sese præparabat, numquam secura, in quam horam is pavidam expectaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045648Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045648Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, Rosa, recognizing that it was God's will, embraced the torment with a truly manly and heroic spirit, saying, "Not my will be done, but Yours." And so she steadfastly prepared herself for a similar rack the next day, never certain at what hour it would await her in fear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus Rosæ tantus inerat castigandi corporis ardor ac studium, ut ei per confessarios modus fuerit imponendus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041507Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041507Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, Rose was so zealous and eager to punish her body that a limit had to be set for her by her confessors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus Virgo &amp; librum suum recuperavit, &amp; pudefecit reduncum depeculatorem, quem non alio, quam improbæ felis, aut SCABIOSI nomine dignabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052010Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052010Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, Virgo recovered her book and shamed the backstabbing thief, whom she dignified with no other name than that of a wicked cat or a SCABIOTIC.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus hinc spe, inde hera animante, resumpsit spiritum, ac residuis diebus accessum, quamquam difficillimum, continuavit modo, quo potuit, nullo interim convalescentiæ signo vel eminus comparente;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033747Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033747Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, encouraged by hope and by his wife, he recovered his spirits and, for the remaining days, continued his treatment, though it was exceedingly difficult, in whatever way he could, with no sign of recovery appearing, not even in the slightest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus hæc trepidabat, quoties Missæ sui confessarii intererat, metuens ne ille sui desiderii compos illamet hora, peracto Sacrificio, abiret e vivis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012253Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012253Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, she was anxious whenever she attended her confessor's Mass, fearing that he, having satisfied his desire, would depart this life as soon as the Sacrifice was over.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus remotiora hæc erant, quam ut tanti thalami spem certam invergerent, &amp; tamen (ut postea claruit) eminus ista felici sponsalitio præludebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044132Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044132Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, these things were too remote to give any certain hope of such a marriage, and yet (as later became clear) they were, from afar, a prelude to that happy betrothal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus ubi semel iterumve, sub gravi onere conscientiæ, tum a confessariis, tum a doctore Joanne de Castillo (ut infra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040841Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, whenever, under the heavy burden of conscience, he did so—whether at the hands of confessors or of Doctor John de Castillo (as below in the chapter).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus, premente necessitate, ceu conscia periculi hoc sapuit, ut, omni alio medicamine omisso, in sola Rosæ imagine medelam quæreret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040707Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040707Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, driven by necessity, as if conscious of the danger, she knew that, having abandoned all other remedies, she must seek healing in the image of the Rose alone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nil profuerant adhibita pro illius ætatis captu remedia, nil matris solicitudo &amp; lachrymæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033118Z">
        <seg>The remedies applied for his age had been of no avail, nor the mother's solicitude and tears.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum apophoreta&lt;a1&gt;e&lt;/a1&gt; hæc erant, quæ Rosa promiserat, dum moriens ad solemne convivium se dixerat evocari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032749Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032749Z">
        <seg>Surely these were the apophoreta that Rosa had promised, while, dying, she had said she was being summoned to the solemn feast.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum conceperat animo, adoptare sibi aliunde pupillum inopem, &amp; undique desolatum, quem a primis unguiculis ipsa materno studio ad virtutem &amp; litteras educaret ex piis familiarium sibi matronarum eleëmosynis: huic puero mox a teneris proposuerat firmiter ac sensim implantare avida euangelicæ missionis desideria, cum parvulo gradatim adultura, ac intetim providere, ut in pietate sacrisque disciplinis accuratissime formaretur, donec grandior factus ac sacerdotio rite initiatus jam censeri posset idoneus operarius ad laborandum in conversione gentilium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060505Z">
        <seg>She had indeed conceived in her mind to adopt from elsewhere an impoverished and utterly destitute ward, whom she would raise from his very infancy with a mother's care to virtue and learning, from the pious alms of the matrons of her family: For this boy, she had soon, from his tender years, firmly and gradually proposed to implant eager desires for the evangelical mission, as he would grow little by little, and to provide in advance that he be formed most carefully in piety and sacred disciplines, until, having become older and duly initiated into the priesthood, he could be considered a suitable worker to labor in the conversion of the Gentiles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum hic ille calix erat, quem Sponsus biberat, quem Sponsæ miscuerat, ac tamdiu ante præmonstrarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013403Z">
        <seg>Surely this was the cup that the Bridegroom had drunk, that he had mixed for the Bride, and had foretold so long before.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum obedivit matri;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035312Z">
        <seg>He certainly obeyed his mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum propria hæc&lt;a2&gt;*&lt;/a2&gt; est superno amori hydrops, ut solum id sitiat, quo magis abundat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010855Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010855Z">
        <seg>Indeed, this is a proper thirst for the love above, so that it thirsts only for that which it already has in abundance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum propria hæc&lt;s4&gt;&lt;a5&gt;*&lt;/a5&gt;&lt;/s4&gt; est superno amori hydrops, ut solum id sitiat, quo magis abundat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053052Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053052Z">
        <seg>Indeed, this is the very thirst of the higher love, that it thirsts only for that by which it abounds the more.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum sic nascuntur Rosæ, congenitis integumentis undique velut amictu paludatæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034633Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034633Z">
        <seg>Indeed, this is how roses are born, their innate coverings cloaking them on all sides like a mantle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum simillimo ritu olim S. Catharina Senensis excepta fuerat in cælis, cum reliquit terras, ut sic in magistram ac Discipulam a spinea usque ad auream coronam nulla superesset difformitas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042434Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042434Z">
        <seg>Indeed, in a very similar manner, St. Catherine of Siena was once received into heaven when she left the earth, so that as both teacher and disciple there was no flaw from the thorn to the golden crown.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum tanta ei inerat cura veritatis, quia Deus est veritas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053353Z">
        <seg>Indeed, he had such great concern for the truth, because God is truth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum tantas pridie vires adhalaverat Rosa puerulo tam diuturnis febribus cocto, &amp; medullitus exhausto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033610Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the day before, Rosa had summoned such strength for the little boy, who had been scorched by such prolonged fevers and utterly drained.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum tanto viatico illam hinc abeuntem ditarat profusa misericordis Rosæ liberalitas, cui nil tam pretiosum aut charum esse potuit, quod animarum saluti non impenderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060859Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the generous charity of Rose had enriched her with such a provision for her departure from here, for to her nothing could be more precious or dear than what she would not expend for the salvation of souls.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum ut Rosa infans ab his verbis auspicata fuerat primam orandi formulam, ita his ipsis immortua, quasi tesseram suæ innocentiæ ac infantilis simplicatis ad limen supernæ patriæ invariatam reportavit, anni sui trigesimi secundi quinto mense vix inchoato.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030235Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030235Z">
        <seg>Indeed, just as little Rosa had, at the sound of those words, begun her first prayer, so she, having died uttering them, carried back to the threshold of the heavenly homeland, unchanged, the token of her innocence and childlike simplicity, in the fifth month of the thirty-second year of her life, scarcely begun.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum vidit tunc Aloysia Rosam eodem, quo pridie, nuptiali ac festivissimo virginum agmine decore circumdatam, nec sola jam palma, sed &amp; immensæ gloriæ corona insignitam, cui seorsim applaudebant dispositæ angelorum phalanges, &amp; beata plurimorum Sanctorum caterva jubilo publico suavissime gratulabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031850Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031850Z">
        <seg>Surely Aloysia then saw Rosa, in the same bridal and most festive procession of virgins as the day before, beautifully surrounded, and now adorned not only with the palm but also with a crown of immense glory, to which the ranks of angels stationed there separately applauded, and the blessed multitude of many saints most sweetly congratulated her with public jubilation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum, sic exolvit promissa fidejussor Deus, qui non minus parentis cor, quam coralium Filiæ gestabat in manu sua.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043618Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043618Z">
        <seg>Surely, thus the God who stood as surety fulfilled His promises, holding in His hand not only a parent's heart but also His Daughter's heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nobis sufficit ex his epithetis distinctius morbum illum nosse, cujus causæ &amp; remedia apud ipsum legi possunt.&lt;/e2&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045937Z">
        <seg>It is sufficient for us, from these epithets, to know that disease more distinctly, the causes and remedies of which can be read in the original.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nocte concubia Rosam in strato acutissimus coxendicum dolor invaserat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041708Z">
        <seg>During the night, Rosam was seized by the sharpest pain in her hip while lying in bed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nocte quadam Rosæ dormiendum fuit in uno conclavi cum socia virgine;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053015Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053015Z">
        <seg>One night, Rose had to sleep in a room with a virgin companion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nocte quadam, acrius sæviente ossis cruciatu, papyraceam Rosæ imaginem, Romæ Superiorum permissu excusam, ac inde multiplicatis exemplaribus Peruviæ importatam, sibi ocyus adferri petiit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040419Z">
        <seg>One night, the torment of his bone growing more acute, he had the papyrus image of Rosa, printed in Rome with the permission of the Superiors and thence imported into Peru in multiple copies, quickly brought to him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nolint errare;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052823Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052823Z">
        <seg>They do not want to be wrong.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nomen Græcum provenit a particula&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;δὺς&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;quæ difficultatem vel depravationem importat, &amp; nomine substantivo&lt;/e5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;πέψις&lt;/s6&gt;, &lt;e7&gt;quod&lt;/e7&gt; concoctionem &lt;e8&gt;significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040125Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040125Z">
        <seg>The Greek name comes from the particle δὺς, which implies difficulty or corruption, and the noun πέψις, which signifies digestion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nomen Græcum&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;ἔκστασις&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;e3&gt;oritur ex verbo&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;ἐξίσασται&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;quod est&lt;/e5&gt; obstupefieri, &lt;e6&gt;vel&lt;/e6&gt; extra se rapi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030440Z">
        <seg>The Greek name ekstasis comes from the verb existai, which means to be stupefied, or to be carried away.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nomen habet a parte læsa: os enim, quod femoris caput excipit &lt;s0&gt;ἰσχίον&lt;/s0&gt;, id est, coxa appellatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041414Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041414Z">
        <seg>It takes its name from the injured part: for the mouth, which receives the head of the femur, is called the ischion, that is, the hip.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nominis ratio est, quod &lt;s3&gt;ὑπὸ&lt;/s3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;τὸν&lt;/s4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;χόνδρον&lt;/s5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;ἐστὶ&lt;/s6&gt;, hoc est, cartilagini subjacet, postque eam sit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025848Z">
        <seg>The reason for the name is that it is under the cartilage, that is, it lies beneath it and behind it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non defuit tamen aliena pietas, quæ miserrimum Rosæ recens tumulatæ commendaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032541Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032541Z">
        <seg>Yet the piety of others did not fail, which would commend the most wretched Rose, newly interred.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non defuit, qui nugas, aut sane dæmonum præstigias, &amp; vana terriculamenta suspicaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045710Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045710Z">
        <seg>There was no shortage of those who suspected nonsense, or indeed the prestidigitations of demons, and vain terrors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non negavit Rosa, id verum esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051853Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051853Z">
        <seg>Rosa did not deny that it was true.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non potuit, uti in miraculosis corporum, ita in his animarum curationibus annotandis procedi usque ad singularia personarum nomina, imo nec decuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032634Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032634Z">
        <seg>It was not possible, as in the miraculous cures of the body, to proceed in these treatments of the soul to the individual names of the persons; indeed, it was not fitting.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non probabatur matri sumptuosa Filiæ devotio, ægreque ferebat tantum temporis subduci aliis Rosæ laboribus, qui parentes, &amp; inopem familiam sustentabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060121Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060121Z">
        <seg>The mother did not approve of her daughter's devotion to luxury, and she resented so much time being taken away from Rose's other labors, which supported her parents and her needy family.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non puduit fateri ista coram matre, quæ quam vera diceret, nimis conscia erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050247Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050247Z">
        <seg>He was not ashamed to confess those things in front of his mother, who was all too aware of how true they were.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non quidem ad hoc tibi religiosus iste candicat amictus, ut eum alienis spurcitiis devenustes, neque te Rosam appellavimus, ut sic oleres: quando te murinis ac suaveolentibus chirothecis uti jubebam, repugnasti parvula: at nunc adulta, si musco fragrare non vis, muco saltem ne fœteas alieno”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061314Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061314Z">
        <seg>"I'm not at all asking you to don that pious, snow-white robe so you can soil it with other people's filth, nor did I call you Rosa so you'd smell like a cabbage. When I told you to use mousemeat and fragrant gloves, you little one, you resisted; but now that you're grown, if you don't want to smell of musk, at least don't stink of someone else's sweat."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non recusavit Castillus edicere, quæ sentiebat: “Quando (inquit) in illa obtenebratione videbaris tibi exitum finemque sperare posse, scito animarum in carcere purgatorii lugentium, dilationes pœnosissimas te delibasse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050401Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050401Z">
        <seg>Castille did not hesitate to declare what he felt: "When," he said, "in that darkness you thought you could hope for an outcome and an end, know that you have tasted the most grievous delays of the souls wailing in the prison of purgatory."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non satiantur sic amantes orare jugiter: “Flammescat igne charitas, accendat ardor proximos”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053936Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053936Z">
        <seg>Those who love this way never grow weary of praying continually: "May charity blaze with fire, may ardor kindle those around."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non sic reliqua illius cœmeterii saburra, tritu facillima, tractabilis, fluida.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035347Z">
        <seg>Not so the rest of that cemetery's sand, which is very easily ground, tractable, and fluid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nondum Rosa (fixis interim in cælum oculis) verba finierat, cum ferox ac torosum animal, terrifico mugitu instar turbinis prætervectum, procul in alias personas impetum fecit, velut si Rosam ejusve imbellem comitatum ne vidisset quidem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062126Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062126Z">
        <seg>Rosa had not yet finished speaking (her eyes fixed on the sky) when a fierce, muscular beast, rushing past with a terrifying roar like a whirlwind, charged off at a distance at other people, as if it had not even seen Rosa or her unarmed companion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nondum Rosa loqui desierat, cum Gundisalvi uxori in memoriam recurrerunt ignita alloquia, quibus Virgo imaginem affata fuerat ante sudorem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053926Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053926Z">
        <seg>Rosa had not yet ceased speaking, when the fiery words with which the Virgin had addressed the image before the sweat came back to Gundisalvi's wife.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nondum Rosa quadrimula erat, cum mordax suppuratio in ejus auricula succrevit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034848Z">
        <seg>Rosa was not yet four months old when a painful abscess developed in her ear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nondum quartumdecimum annum egressa fuerat, cum notari cœpit inter alia pœnitentiæ opera, quod noctu perambulabat hortum nudipes, imposita sibi oblonga cruce, quam dum lividis gestabat humeris, semetipsam crebris genuflexionibus terræ allidebat, amarissimam Calvariæ viam æquis tum passibus, tum suspiriis dimetiens;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041643Z">
        <seg>She had not yet turned fourteen when she began to be noted, among other penitential works, for walking barefoot at night through the garden, a long cross laid upon her, which, as she carried it on her livid shoulders, she brought herself to the ground with frequent genuflections, measuring the most bitter Way of Calvary with equal steps and sighs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nondum tunc Lucia trigesimum ætatis annum excesserat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011943Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011943Z">
        <seg>Lucia had not yet passed her thirtieth year.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nondum ultimus novenarii dies illuxerat, &amp; Ludovicus jam perfecte sanatus exibat domo, Virginisque sepulchrum invisebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034218Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034218Z">
        <seg>The last day of the novena had not yet dawned, and Louis, already fully recovered, was leaving the house to visit the Virgin's tomb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nonnulla attigimus supra;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054320Z">
        <seg>We have touched on some things above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nonnumquam de confessariorum, ipsiusque Gundisalvi permissu, maternam adibat domum, ut pristinæ cellulæ suavi eremo per aliquot dies frueretur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043947Z">
        <seg>Sometimes, with the permission of her confessors and of Gundisalvus himself, she would go to her mother's house to enjoy the sweet solitude of her former cell for a few days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nonum ætatis mensem attigerat, cum lactanti matri aruerunt ubera, lac defecit, &amp; ne aliunde nutrix conduceretur, obstabat res angusta domi: en primos infanti tribulos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052429Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052429Z">
        <seg>He had reached the ninth month of his life when his nursing mother's breasts failed, her milk ran out, and, since there was no room at home to hire a wet nurse, the infant's first hardships began.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nos quoque videntes eam ubique omnium populorum solemni plausu devotissime honoratam, hujusmodi patronatum pro omnibus provinciis, regnis, insulis &amp; regionibus terræ firmæ totius Americæ, atque Philippinarum, &amp; Indiarum fecimus extensum, formatisque auctoritate apostolica processibus de his, quæ supervenerant, eorumque validitate approbata, &amp; percrebrescentibus venerationis &amp; devotionis populorum piis affectibus, &amp; novis ostensis &amp; miraculis accedentibus&lt;a3&gt;*&lt;/a3&gt;, ex quibus post maturam discussionem quatuor admissa fuere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043230Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043230Z">
        <seg>We also, seeing that she is everywhere most devoutly honored with solemn applause by all peoples, have established her as patroness of all the provinces, kingdoms, islands, and mainland regions of the entire Americas, and of the Philippines, and of the Indies, we have extended this patronage to all the provinces, kingdoms, islands, and regions of the whole continent of America, and of the Philippines, and, having formed processes by apostolic authority concerning those matters which had arisen and their validity approved, and the pious affections of the people in their veneration and devotion growing more and more, and with new signs and miracles being added&lt;a3&gt;*&lt;/a3&gt;, of which, after mature deliberation, four were admitted;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nosti, quid fellis ibidem propter tui amorem exhauserim: scis, quæ illic non testeæ, sed ferreæ cuspides mihi manus pedesque terebrarint, donec ipsam mihi extunderent dilectam animam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042654Z">
        <seg>You know what torments I endured there for the sake of your love; you know what iron spikes, not wooden, pierced my hands and feet until they had torn my beloved soul from me.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Notarunt hunc Rosæ spiritum domestici, &amp; quotiescumque roganda erat, ut fomenta suo corpusculo admitteret, aut requiem captaret, aliave se ratione sineret indulgentius haberi, subjiciebant ita mandasse confessarium, statimque Rosa sine replica plane infantiliter obediebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035718Z">
        <seg>They noted this spirit of domestic obedience in Rosa, and whenever she was asked to apply fomentations to her little body, or to take rest, or to allow herself in any other way to be treated more indulgently, they would say that her confessor had so commanded, and at once Rosa, without a word, obeyed in a thoroughly childlike manner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Notarunt precantis vultum circumstantes ejusdem tertii habitus Sorores, videruntque Rosam a sacræ imaginis obtutu supplici diu pendulam, initio quidem exalbescere pallore niveo, post intervallum decora suffusione rubescere, denique tota facie lucescere, ac scintillatim radiare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040354Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040354Z">
        <seg>The Sisters of the Third Order, who were standing by, noticed the supplicant's face and saw Rosa hanging for a long time before the sacred image with a devout gaze; at first she turned pale with a snowy pallor, then after a while she flushed with a lovely blush, and finally her whole face shone and sparkled with rays.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Notavi adstantes animas eodem gratiæ thesauro tam distentas, &amp; ad summum usque refertas, ut ore &amp; oculis exundaret arctata plenitudo”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052813Z">
        <seg>I noticed the souls of those present so distended by the same treasure of grace, and so filled to the brim, that their mouths and eyes overflowed with its restrained fullness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Notent, quicumque religiosis communitatibus divisionis machinas invergunt, quam abominabilem Deo, quam gratam cacodæmoni, quam perniciosam proximis operam præstent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054500Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054500Z">
        <seg>Let those who sow discord among religious communities know how abominable their work is to God, how pleasing to the devil, and how harmful to their neighbors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Noti ignotique pone transibant coram geniculata Virgine, ac fere incurrebant in oculos, nec tamen ipsa quemquam tunc norat, nec vel leviter movebat palpebras, intensissima iis, quæ gerebantur in altari, quorum intuitum forsitan alieni objecti intercursus nec obiter eclipsabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060028Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060028Z">
        <seg>Known and unknown alike passed by her, kneeling before the Virgin, and almost ran into her gaze; yet she herself recognized no one at that moment, nor did she so much as bat her eyelids, so intent was she on what was taking place at the altar, whose sight perhaps no intervening object even briefly eclipsed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Noverat Joannes de Tineo Almanza, quæstorem Gundisalvum Rosæ precibus &amp; meritis pluries a gravissimis infirmitatibus liberatum: ille ex cruciatibus stomachi periculose ægrotans obtinuit, ut Virgo ista post auditum sacrum decenter associata eum inviseret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042440Z">
        <seg>John of Tineo was aware that Gundisalvus Rosa, the treasurer, had been freed several times from the gravest illnesses by the prayers and merits of this Virgin; he, suffering dangerously from stomach torments, obtained that she, properly adorned after the sacred service, would visit him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Noverat æstimare animas ex infinito pretio, quo redemptæ sunt: hinc, quoties ad interiora meridionalis Americæ montana flectebat oculos, cruciabatur medullitus, illachrymans perditioni tot barbarorum, qui, post illa nivosa altissimarum costarum&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt; juga, formicatim innumeras, sed inaccessas convalles populabant, avita adhuc idolomania obcæcati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011444Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011444Z">
        <seg>He knew to value souls by the infinite price for which they were redeemed: Hence, whenever he turned his eyes to the interior mountainous regions of South America, he was deeply tormented, weeping over the perdition of so many barbarians who, beyond those snowy summits of the highest ridges, swarmed in countless but inaccessible valleys, still blinded by ancestral idolatry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Noverat æstimare animas ex infinito pretio, quo redemptæ sunt: hinc, quoties ad interiora meridionalis Americæ montana flectebat oculos, cruciabatur medullitus, illachrymans perditioni tot barbarorum, qui, post illa nivosa altissimarum costarum&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;a&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt; juga, formicatim innumeras, sed inaccessas convalles populabant, avita adhuc idolomania obcæcati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060350Z">
        <seg>He knew to value souls by the infinite price for which they were redeemed: Hence, whenever he turned his eyes to the interior mountainous regions of South America, he was deeply tormented, weeping over the perdition of so many barbarians who, beyond those snowy summits of the highest ridges, swarmed like ants through countless but inaccessible valleys, still blinded by ancestral idolatry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Novercali corporis odio, nec vigil insidere usquam pulvino voluit, sed vel stans peragebat labores suos quousque poterat, vel cum necessario sedendum erat, duro palo utebatur pro sellula.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042930Z">
        <seg>Hating the very body he loathed, he would not sit on a cushion anywhere, but either carried out his labors standing as long as he could, or, when he had to sit, used a hard staff instead of a stool.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Novissimum denique in Angela Gabaja, quæ duplici febri tertiana continua &amp; maligna, cum ingenti capitis ac stomachi dolore, flatuumque copia per dies XXVII dire excruciata, demum a medico palam conclamata properabat ad mortem, perque sex horarum spatium laborans in agone, hortatu matris invocavit opem B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043308Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043308Z">
        <seg>Finally, the most recent case was that of Angela Gabaja, who, afflicted with a double, continuous, and malignant tertian fever, with intense headaches and stomach pain, and tormented for twenty-seven days by a copious emission of flatulence, was at last openly declared by the physician to be dying. And, after six hours of labor in her agony, at her mother's urging she invoked the aid of the Blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nox erat, &amp; sancti Bartholomæi pervigilium&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030210Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030210Z">
        <seg>It was night, and the vigil of Saint Bartholomew;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nulla ægritudo tam putrida ac fœda, nullum ulcus tam tetrum aut saniosum, nulla scabies tam crustosa aut fluida, cui Rosa medelam, famulatum, curam offerre, cui virgineas admovere manus, dimovere spurcitiem, applicare, mundare, mutare emplastra&lt;a2&gt;e&lt;/a2&gt; defugeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011636Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011636Z">
        <seg>No sickness so foul and putrid, no sore so dark or festering, no scabies so crusted or oozing, to which Rosa would refuse to offer her remedy, her service, her care; to which she would not apply her virgin hands, to remove the filth, to apply, to cleanse, to change the poultice.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nulla ægritudo tam putrida ac fœda, nullum ulcus tam tetrum aut saniosum, nulla scabies tam crustosa aut fluida, cui Rosa medelam, famulatum, curam offerre, cui virgineas admovere manus, dimovere spurcitiem, applicare, mundare, mutare emplastra&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;e&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt; defugeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061256Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061256Z">
        <seg>No sickness so putrid and foul, no sore so dark or festering, no scab so crusted or oozing, to which Rosa would offer a remedy, her service, her care; to which she would press her virgin hands, remove the filth, apply, cleanse, and change the poultice.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nulli ergo omnino hominum liceat hanc paginam nostri decreti, definitionis, adscriptionis, mandati, statui, concessionis, relaxationis, elargitionis &amp; voluntatis infringere, vel ei ausu temerario contraïre.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043429Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043429Z">
        <seg>Therefore, let no one whatsoever be permitted to infringe upon this page of our decree, definition, inscription, mandate, statute, concession, relaxation, grant, and will, or to act rashly against it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nullus apparebat negotii tam ardui procurator, nemo fundator, nemo fautor aut suasor, nullibi auspex vel patronus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062746Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062746Z">
        <seg>There appeared to be no manager for such a difficult undertaking, no founder, no supporter or persuader, nowhere an augur or patron.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nullæ proderant unctiones, nulli tenacissimum phlegma findebant gargarismi, quin nocte quadam, propemodum intercluso anhelitu, suffocaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035148Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035148Z">
        <seg>No ointments helped, no gargles broke up the most tenacious phlegm, until one night, with his breath almost cut off, he was suffocated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Numquam sane officiosissima Rosæ pietas aut solicitius, aut lautius refecit matrem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035836Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035836Z">
        <seg>Never, indeed, did Rose's most dutiful piety care for her mother more solicitously or more sumptuously.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nunc (sed brevius) ad alterum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050116Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050116Z">
        <seg>Now (but more briefly) to the other.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nunc ad curationum historiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035353Z">
        <seg>Now to the history of treatments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nunc ergo per omnia nostræ amicitiæ juta te obtestor, ne, quando hic exspiravero, corpusculum meum aliis feminis committas vestiendum, feretroque aptandum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013214Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013214Z">
        <seg>Now then, I beseech you by all our friendship not to entrust my little body to other women for dressing and preparing for the coffin when I have breathed my last.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nunc est episcopalis sub archiepiscopo Limensi, a cujus metropoli 120 leucis Hispanicis in ortum distat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050758Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050758Z">
        <seg>It is now a diocese under the Archbishop of Lima, from whose metropolis it lies 120 Spanish leagues to the east.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nunc vero etiam quædam resurrectionis Dominicæ reviviscebat imago;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031422Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031422Z">
        <seg>Now, however, a certain image of the Lord's resurrection was also reviving.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nuntiatum fuit Rosæ, vacua esse penuaria, neque vel crustam reliquam esse ab ultima pistura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062207Z">
        <seg>It was reported to Rosa that the larders were empty, and that not even a crust remained from the last baking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nusquam is sibi occurrebat pauperior, quam ubi in Bethlehem nascebatur parvulus, nudus, cum inopi Matre extorris diversorio, algens in stabulo, panniculis obvolutus in vili præsepio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053536Z">
        <seg>Nowhere was he poorer than when he was born in Bethlehem as a little child, naked, with his destitute mother driven out of the inn, freezing in a stable, wrapped in swaddling cloths in a humble manger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>O gratum surgendi tintinnabulum!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051344Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051344Z">
        <seg>Oh, welcome alarm clock!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>O odorifera Rosa, in æternum ante Dominum germinans, &amp; florens, spargens ubique vivificum suavitatis odorem, cujus apud nos memoria in benedictione est, apud superos præsentia in honore, da canentibus te, tanta plenitudinis participatione non fraudari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040946Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040946Z">
        <seg>O fragrant Rose, forever sprouting before the Lord and blooming, spreading everywhere the life-giving scent of sweetness, whose memory is a blessing among us and whose presence is honored in heaven, grant that those who sing of you may not be deprived of such a participation in fullness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>O quis det, ut cuncti agnoscant, quanti quantoque amore dignus sis, ut capiant, non pœnarum servili formidine, non mera spe mercenaria præmiorum, sed propter te amandum te esse?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053712Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053712Z">
        <seg>Oh, who would grant that all might recognize how worthy you are of love, and embrace you not for fear of servile punishments, nor for the mere hope of mercenary rewards, but for the sake of loving you—because you are you?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>O quis donet, ut eum perfecte diligamus!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053145Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053145Z">
        <seg>Oh, who will grant that we may love him perfectly!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obedivit Rosa, sed non ultra litteram;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042744Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042744Z">
        <seg>Rosa obeyed, but not beyond the letter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obedivi”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035526Z">
        <seg>I obeyed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obiter hic attigerim, quod in Rosa, dum vixit, plurimi observarunt, mirifice oblectatam fuisse aspectu sereni cæli, adeo ut puella omnis astrologiæ penitus ignara, magnam interdum noctis partem immota soli fiderum spectaculo dulciter inhæreret: nimirum nova Regum stella amabat sphæram suam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043711Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043711Z">
        <seg>By the way, I should mention here that, as many who knew Rosa during her life observed, she was wonderfully delighted by the sight of a clear sky, so much so that the girl, utterly ignorant of all astrology, would sometimes spend a great part of the night motionless, sweetly absorbed in the spectacle of the fixed stars: for the new Star of the Magi loved her sphere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Oblata ei cum anulo fuit corona spinis horrida, vixque his manus applicuerat, cum repente æstuare cœpit inusitato quodam divini amoris incendio, colliquescere in intimo fundo spiritus, dulcescere gustu admirabili supernarum deliciarum, tamquam ex illismet spinis paradisi uvas, ex tribulis ficus collegisset in animæ palatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042353Z">
        <seg>A crown of horrid thorns was offered to him along with the ring, and he had scarcely touched it with his hands when suddenly he began to blaze with an unusual fire of divine love, to melt in the inmost depths of his spirit, to grow sweet with the wondrous taste of heavenly delights, as if he had gathered grapes from the very thorns of paradise and figs from thistles for the palate of his soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Oblato chirographo, in quo sepulchrum apud suos religiosos postulabat, cum omni animi submissione, nihil minus, quam de sui corporis cura solicita, subscripsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042831Z">
        <seg>Having been presented with the document in which he requested a burial among his religious order, he signed it with the utmost humility, nothing less than a solicitous care for his own body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obmutuit, stupuitque repentino gaudio Rosa, ut vidit, non deliberatione, sed impetu, raptim incidisse fratrem in symbolum, quod unica ac propria totius negotii erat periocha;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044455Z">
        <seg>Rose was dumbfounded and stunned by a sudden joy when she saw that her brother had, not by deliberation but by impulse, rashly plunged into the symbol, which was the very crux of the entire affair.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obscurum ænigma perfacile capiebant, quotquot prævii conflictus consciæ fuerant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040403Z">
        <seg>They grasped the obscure enigma with great ease, as many as had been aware of the previous conflicts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obsecundavit ille, collegitque pluscula;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044932Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044932Z">
        <seg>He complied, and gathered a few things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Observabat insciam e vicino angulo mater, audiitque filiam arguto fluidorum rhythmorum jubilo blandientem sancto Patri Dominico, cui impense commendabat Mariam de Oliva matrem suam, crebro inculcans ac repetens;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013257Z">
        <seg>Her mother, observing her from a nearby corner, heard her daughter sweetly caressing the Holy Father Dominic with the joyful flow of her eloquent rhythms, earnestly commending her mother, Mary of Oliva, to him, repeatedly urging and repeating it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Observabatur ab uxore &amp; consobrina, qui dum noctu circa infirmum excubabant, repente notarunt ambo, Antonium jacere sine motu, sine respiratione, ulloque vel minimo signo vitali: invocabant ægrum ex nomine proprio, vellicabant, pulsabant, sed nonnisi responsa mortis extundebant a corpore jam gelido.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033152Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033152Z">
        <seg>He was being watched over by his wife and his cousin, who, while on watch by the sick man at night, suddenly both noticed that Antony was lying motionless, without breath, and without the slightest sign of life: they called him by his own name, pinched him, struck him, but elicited nothing but the responses of death from the already cold body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Observarunt domestici, numquam in fasciis parvulam voce querula, aut stridulis vagitibus ædium turbasse silentium, numquam plorasse, nisi quod semel, cum mater nobilem matronam visitans foras extulisset Rosam, compesci a planctu non potuit, quoadusque ex aliena in propriam domum referretur, quasi jam tunc nosset publicum fugere, &amp; aversari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034820Z">
        <seg>The household observed that the little one never cried in her swaddling clothes, or with shrill cries had ever disturbed the silence of the house, had never cried, except that once, when her mother had taken Rosa outside while visiting a noble matron, she could not be restrained from crying until she was brought back from the other house to her own, as if even then she knew to shun and avoid public company.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Observat hæc tacite Maria de Usategui, prudentique solertia demum a Virgine per ambages elicuit, quid rerum illic lateret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054122Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054122Z">
        <seg>Maria of Usategui observed these things in silence and, with prudent skill, finally elicited from the Virgin, by indirection, what was hidden there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Observatum fuit, Virginem cum pane unico, illoque sat exiguo, &amp; aquæ vasculo, integram dierum quinquagenam transegisse absque omni alio cibo aut potu, nimirum a Paschate Resurrectionis usque ad Pentecosten.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041333Z">
        <seg>It was observed that the Virgin, with a single loaf of bread, and that a very small one, and a small vessel of water, spent the entire fifty days without any other food or drink, namely from the Feast of the Resurrection until Pentecost.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Observatum id crebro, inque diversissimis materiis, quarum vix ulla effugiebat hanc spiritualem &amp; sanctam æquivocandi solertiam, ne quid Rosæ ori excideret, quod non esset oratio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045013Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045013Z">
        <seg>This was often observed, and in the most diverse matters, so that scarcely any of them escaped this spiritual and holy skill of equivocation, so that nothing might escape the Rose's mouth that was not a prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstabant demum alia, providente Numine, quod florido S. Patris Dominici viridario hanc Rosam incomparabilem per tot ambages reservabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040050Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040050Z">
        <seg>At last, other obstacles arose, by the providence of God, who was reserving this incomparable Rose for the flourishing garden of Saint Dominic through so many twists and turns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstabat simul ætate morbisque fatiscens avia, cui decumbenti unica a fomentis erat, assiduoque ministerio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040047Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040047Z">
        <seg>Also standing in the way was the grandmother, who, worn out by age and illness, had as her only support a bed and constant care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstabat, quod Rosæ pro pudore virgineo durum &amp; insolitum erat, viri, quamquam familiarissimi &amp; infirmi, subire conspectum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061448Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061448Z">
        <seg>What prevented Rose, out of virgin modesty, from submitting to the sight of the man, though he was the most familiar and infirm, was that it was harsh and unusual.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstitit tamen, causata domus inopiam, quam Rosa manuum labore sustentabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040044Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040044Z">
        <seg>However, she objected, citing the household's poverty, which Rosa supported by her labor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstupuere omnes;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034653Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034653Z">
        <seg>Everyone was stunned.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstupuit parens;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051722Z">
        <seg>The parent was stunned.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstupuit præ aliis Joannes Perez de Zumeta (id erat chirurgo nomen) haud capiens, unde tantillæ Virgunculæ tanta in iis tormentis obduruisset constantia: nam toto illo acerbissimæ curationis tempore nec gemitum ediderat, nec umquam expalluerat ad styli, sanguinis, novorumque vulnerum aspectum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034845Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034845Z">
        <seg>John Perez de Zumeta, the surgeon, was more astonished than the others, unable to understand how so slight a maiden could have endured such steadfastness in those torments; for throughout that entire period of the most bitter treatment she had neither uttered a groan nor ever paled at the sight of the stylet, the blood, or the fresh wounds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstupuit socia, præsertim quod videret, id Rosæ peregrinum, aut novum non esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045202Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045202Z">
        <seg>The companion was astonished, especially because she saw that what Rosæ was seeing was neither strange nor new.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obtemperavit Virgo, quia parentes jubebant, imo ut Gundisalvo &amp; uxori in omnibus morigeram se exhiberet, imperabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035616Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035616Z">
        <seg>The virgin complied, because her parents were ordering her—indeed, commanding her to be obedient in all things to Gundisalvo and his wife.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obtinuit, ac susque deque per aulam, ceu levis caprea, cucurrit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033459Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033459Z">
        <seg>He obtained it and ran back and forth through the hall like a nimble goat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Occasionem dederat popularis quædam in Limana civitate turbatio, levibus (ut assolet) orta principiis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041535Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041535Z">
        <seg>A popular disturbance had broken out in the city of Lima, arising from trivial (as is usual) beginnings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Occultissimum hoc erat tormenti genus, quodque vel diu vel forte semper latuisset universos: nam Rosa, vel ipsam operum suorum secretariam Marianam, hujus insciam voluit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041659Z">
        <seg>This was the most secret kind of torture, which, whether for a long time or perhaps always, had remained hidden from everyone: for Rosa, or even her own secretary Mariana, wished her to be unaware of it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Odescalcus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043553Z">
        <seg>Odescalcus</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Omne atrium, ambulacra, angulos confusa turba impleverat, ut Gundisalvo necesse fuerit, posticum suarum ædium aperire, quo per aliam viam sese evolverent, qui venturis locum cedebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030421Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030421Z">
        <seg>The entire courtyard, the corridors, and the corners were filled with a confused crowd, so that Gundisalvus was forced to open the back of his house, through which those who were arriving could make their way out by another route.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Omnibus frustra adhibitis medicamentis, in illo deploratæ salutis statu cor erexit ad superna, devote se commendavit dictæ Virgini, &amp; ejus vestium allatis particulis tetigit partes læsas, atque in instanti perfectam recuperavit salutem, omnibusque admirantibus, propriis pedibus adiit immediate Prædicatorum ecclesiam, ibique publicas gratias dictæ Virgini reddidit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043218Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043218Z">
        <seg>Having tried all remedies in vain, in that deplorable state of health she lifted her heart to heaven, devoutly commended herself to the said Virgin, and touched her wounded parts with particles of her vestments, and instantly recovered perfect health, to the amazement of all who were present, on his own feet he went immediately to the church of the Preachers, where he publicly gave thanks to the said Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Omnibus medelam, famulatum &amp; curam afferebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042428Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042428Z">
        <seg>He brought healing, service, and care to all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Omnium hactenus patebat oculis invariata defunctæ Rosæ species, qualis a primo exhalati spiritus momento fuerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031109Z">
        <seg>The unchanged appearance of the deceased Rose had been visible to all eyes until then, just as it had been from the very moment her spirit first departed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Opinor hic voce&lt;/e9&gt; hieratica &lt;e10&gt;artem sacram contemplationis indicari&lt;/e10&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050820Z">
        <seg>I think that here the term "hieratic" indicates the sacred art of contemplation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Opportune Maria de Mesta quasdam Rosæ reliquias (a quibus ipsa pluries salutem hauserat) infirmo suo domestico obtulit, quando arctius laborabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034801Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034801Z">
        <seg>At the right moment, Mary of Mesta offered certain relics of Rose (from which she herself had often drawn healing) to her ailing household servant, when he was suffering more severely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Optabat numquam fieri noctem, ne sibi lectulus adeundus esset cum tali manuum obvolucro.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035340Z">
        <seg>She wished that night would never come, so that she wouldn't have to go to bed with such a bandage on her hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Optabat visceratim discerpi, ac retis instar obpandi latissimæ viæ, qua tot miserabiles animæ ruebant ad infernum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060403Z">
        <seg>He longed to be torn to pieces and spread out like a net across the broadest road, down which so many wretched souls were rushing to hell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Orabat Virgo, &amp; in mentis excessu vidit circa se undique solum conspersum rosis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061854Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061854Z">
        <seg>The Virgin was praying, and in a trance of mind she saw all around her the ground strewn with roses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Oritur hoc nomen a verbo Græco&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;σπάω&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;quod est&lt;/e3&gt;, convello.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040143Z">
        <seg>This name comes from the Greek word σπάω, which means to pull out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Os homini intus omnino fuligineum, camini speciem referebat, olidæ nares mucebant cremore favillaceo, atta nebula vexatum cerebrum stillabat in pectus, &amp; obstructis pulmonibus raucum asthma vocalem obsidebat arteriam, nec tamen cessabat miser a noxio suctu accensæ fistulæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060747Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060747Z">
        <seg>The man's mouth inside was utterly sooty, like a chimney; his foul nostrils were caked with a sooty cream; a fog-vexed brain dripped onto his chest; and with his lungs blocked, a hoarse asthma besieged his windpipe, yet the wretch did not cease from the noxious draught of the kindled pipe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ostien.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043451Z">
        <seg>Ostia</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Otthobonus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043612Z">
        <seg>Ottobonus</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Oviedus in Hispaniola insula PETEBECENUC.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060939Z">
        <seg>Oviedo on the island of Hispaniola, PETEBECENUC.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211727Z">
        <seg>Father</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>P. F. Didacus de Palomino, Ordinis Prædicatorum, duplicem tertianam depulit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040022Z">
        <seg>Father Francis Didacus de Palomino, of the Order of Preachers, overcame a double tertian fever.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>P. F. Leonardo Hansen Ordinis Prædicatorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000825Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000825Z">
        <seg>Father Francis Leonard Hansen of the Order of Preachers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>P. Joannem de Villalobos, qui tunc Societatis Jesu novitiatui Limensi Rector præerat, ad æes D. Isabellæ de Mexia divertere, ubi in Rosæ colloquium incidens, postquam de certitudine futuri monasterii beatæ Catharinæ Senensis varius utrimque alternarat sermo, didicit a Virgine, se illic pridie locutam esse cum Lucia de la Daga annis quidem juvene, at maturitate indolis gravi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012002Z">
        <seg>P. John de Villalobos, who at that time was the Rector of the Society of Jesus' novitiate in Lima, to the house of D. to turn aside to the home of D. Isabella de Mexia, where, in a conversation with Rose, after various arguments had been exchanged on both sides about the certainty of the future monastery of St. Catherine of Siena, she learned from the Virgin that she had spoken there the day before with Lucia de la Daga, who was indeed young in years, but mature in character;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>P. Joannes de Villalobos&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;, Rector Limensis collegii Societatis Jesu, Vir sanctimonia ac religione celeberrimus, &amp; animarum per tutiora compendia perfectionis deducendarum auriga peritissimus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001017Z">
        <seg>Father John de Villalobos, Rector of the Society of Jesus's college in Lima, a man celebrated for his holiness and piety, and a most skilled guide for leading souls to perfection by safer means.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>P. Philippo de Tapia Callaoënsis collegii Rectori simillimum cum Virgine accidisse, palam asseveravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012129Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012129Z">
        <seg>He publicly asserted that something very similar to what happened to the Virgin had happened to Father Philip de Tapia, rector of the Callao College.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>P. Villalobos Societatis Jesu jurata fide testatus est, suo se experimento spiritum propheticum deprehendisse in Virgine.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012114Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012114Z">
        <seg>P. Villalobos of the Society of Jesus testified under oath that he had discerned the prophetic spirit in the Virgin through his own experience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Palpitabant cæteris anhela præcordia etiam post periculum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062130Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062130Z">
        <seg>The other's panting hearts still fluttered even after the danger;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pande guttur cantillando:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045258Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045258Z">
        <seg>Open your throat by singing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pande rostrum,</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045255Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045255Z">
        <seg>Open your beak,</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pangat hymnum vox anhela,</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045236Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045236Z">
        <seg>Let a breathless voice sing a hymn,</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Panniculi constanto 9 stationibus ad SS. Eucharistiam, novem trientibus psalterii rosariani, &amp; 9 diebus jejunis, pro numero mensium, quos in utero purissimæ Matris exegit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053554Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053554Z">
        <seg>The devotion consists of 9 stations for the Holy Eucharist, nine decades of the rosary, and 9 days of fasting, corresponding to the number of months the Most Pure Mother carried Him in her womb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Parce manui ad ista jam inutili;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055414Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055414Z">
        <seg>Spare the hand that is now useless for these things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Parebat Hieronymus, sed non pisces;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033905Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033905Z">
        <seg>Jerome appeared, but not the fish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Parebat mœsta Filia, sed maximo inassueti stomachi supplicio: nam quidquid carnium reluctanti ingesserat, paulo post, captato secretiori angulo, totum superne reddendum erat, non sine miserabili cruciatu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041036Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041036Z">
        <seg>The daughter appeared sorrowful, but suffering the greatest punishment of an unsettled stomach: for whatever meat she had forced down with reluctance, she would shortly afterward, having found a more secluded corner, have to bring it all back up, not without miserable torment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pari commiseratione vernaculas, peregrinas, serviles, ingenuas, notas, ignotas, agrestes, &amp; indigenas prosequebatur, solo evidentioris indigentiæ respectu habito.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061249Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061249Z">
        <seg>He treated vernacular, foreign, servile, free, familiar, unfamiliar, rustic, and indigenous people with equal compassion, regarding only the more obvious need.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pari obedientia suo confessario adquievit, quando nimiam ferreæ catenæ atrocitatem in aliud, ex filis contortum, at nodosum asperumque flagellum præcepit commutari, ut sui Ordinis communiori usui humilius sese conformaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041612Z">
        <seg>With equal obedience he acquiesced to his confessor, when he ordered the excessive cruelty of the iron chain to be exchanged for another, twisted from strands into a knotted and rough scourge, so that he might conform himself more humbly to the common practice of his Order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pari solicitudine pios libros, quotquot de oratione peritius tractare noverat, &amp; legebat ipsa, &amp; legendos aliis facunda efficacitate consulebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045109Z">
        <seg>With equal diligence she read the devout books she knew how to handle most skillfully on the subject of prayer, and she also advised others to read them with eloquent effectiveness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pari sumptu connitebatur agonizantibus impetrare subsidia pro illo tanti momenti supremo momento, a quo pendet æternitas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041522Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041522Z">
        <seg>At great expense, he strove to secure for the agonizing the means for that supreme moment of such momentous importance, from which eternity depends.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Parte altera niveus candidarum virginum chorus mediam complexus Rosam tunc recens defunctam, hilari &amp; canoro agmine deducebat ad Deiparam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031834Z">
        <seg>On the other side, a snow-white choir of virgins, having enclosed the newly deceased Rose in their midst, escorted her to the Virgin Mary with a cheerful and melodious procession.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Parthenici rosarii recitationem cum innexa decadatim mysteriorum consideratione, prout sanctus Dominicus instituerat&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt;, singulariter diligebat, eo quod in ea coalescant mentalis &amp; vocalis simul orationis affectus, petitiones, laudes, gratiarum actiones: &amp; plurimis hæc Rosæ incitamenta profuisse, reipsa compertum fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041730Z">
        <seg>He particularly loved the recitation of the Virgin's rosary with the attached decade-by-decade meditation on the mysteries, as Saint Dominic had instituted, because in it the affections of mental and vocal prayer, petitions, praises, and thanksgivings come together; and it was found in practice that these incentives of the Rosary had benefited many.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paruit Rosa, sed eo ingenio, quo nihilominus obedientiæ ac pœnitentiæ merita in illomet lecto materno dexterrime conjungeret: vix enim obrepserat matri sopor, cum Virgo culcitræ partem, cui incubuerat, sensim removebat, donec substratæ tabulæ corpusculum exciperent, capiti vero lignum, aut prope absconditus later cervicali subducto succederet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042514Z">
        <seg>Rosa obeyed, but with that ingenuity by which she nonetheless most skillfully combined the merits of obedience and penance on that very motherly bed: For scarcely had slumber overtaken her mother when the Virgin slowly withdrew the part of the mattress on which she had been lying, until the planks beneath received her little body, and the wood of the headboard, or one hidden nearby, took the place of the removed side rail.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paruit Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035533Z">
        <seg>Rosa obeyed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paruit Virgo, ad obsequia charitatis mire proclivis, sacellum adiit, preces fudit, sed mœsta præter consuetudinem, animoque saucia domum rediit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054422Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054422Z">
        <seg>The Virgin, wonderfully inclined to acts of charity, went to the chapel, offered prayers, but returned home sadder than usual and wounded in spirit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paruit humilis Rosa, cui metus &amp; pudor totum verecundo rubore vultum accenderat, veniamque præfata, si quod paradoxum rudi, &amp; incautæ excideret, sic orsa est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050504Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050504Z">
        <seg>The humble Rosa, whose face fear and shame had all aflame with bashful blush, and having first asked pardon for any rude or careless paradox that might slip from her, thus began:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paruit illa, catenam denuo carni adnatam a lumbis revulsit, pannoque diligenter una cum sera obvolutam sacristæ B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041755Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041755Z">
        <seg>She complied, tore the chain, which had once again grown into the flesh, from her loins, and carefully wrapped it, together with the key, in a cloth for the sacrist of B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paruit iste, caputque, ubi acutius dispungebatur, cinxit, &amp; unico hoc ceromate&lt;a1&gt;h&lt;/a1&gt; febrim simul cum cephalæa abegit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035024Z">
        <seg>He complied, and bound the head where it was most acutely throbbing, and with this single application of ointment he banished the fever along with the headache.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paruit scriptor;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032312Z">
        <seg>The writer obeyed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paruit, &amp; illo temporis puncto, omissis aliis remediis, ultro secundæ prodierunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034237Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034237Z">
        <seg>It obeyed, and at that very moment, other remedies being set aside, the seconds of their own accord came forward.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paruit, ac sine alio quocumque humano remedio cœpit ex illo momento convalescere, intraque pauculos dies ad consueta sui famulatus officia rediit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034112Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034112Z">
        <seg>He obeyed, and without any other human remedy began to recover from that moment, and within a few days returned to his usual duties as a servant.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Parum ei videbatur, quod domi, Filiam se oblita, ancillæ domesticæ vilissima quæque ministeria occupabat, nisi &amp; infra ancillam sese demitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040503Z">
        <seg>It seemed to her that it was not enough that at home, having forgotten her daughter, she occupied herself with the vilest household tasks, unless she also humbled herself below a slave.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pater F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060821Z">
        <seg>Father F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pater F. Petrus de Loaysa Dominicanus, [&amp; alium morti proximum,] probe conscius zeli &amp; charitatis, qua flagrabat misericors Rosa in opem periclitantium proximorum, indicarat Virgini, decumbere in cœnobio, cumque mortis angoribus trepide luctari N.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011550Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011550Z">
        <seg>Father Peter de Loaysa, a Dominican, [and another near death,] being well aware of the zeal and charity with which the merciful Rose burned for the aid of her fellow travelers in peril, had informed the Virgin that N. was lying in the convent, anxiously struggling with the pangs of death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Patuit Joanna, &amp; insperatum beneficium verecunda hilaritate acceptavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061215Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061215Z">
        <seg>Joanna opened up and accepted the unexpected favor with modest cheerfulness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Patuit hoc vice quadam, cum Rosa, fortuito in terram lapsu caput leviter ad vicinum pluteum impegit, ubi confestim ob coronam vertici validius impactam, copiosus sanguis defluxit in humerum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042335Z">
        <seg>It happened once when Rosa, by chance falling to the ground, lightly bumped her head on a nearby shelf, where, because the crown had been struck more forcefully, a copious flow of blood ran down onto her shoulder.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paucas hic e plurimis compendio substringemus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040459Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040459Z">
        <seg>Here we will briefly summarize a few of the many.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paucis accipe hujus rei notabile argumentum, quod in Rosæ funere observatum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042406Z">
        <seg>Here is a notable argument for this matter, observed at Rose's funeral.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paucis ante diebus (nempe feria secunda Paschatis) Rosa, lapsu fortuito terræ allisa, brachium graviter offenderat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053942Z">
        <seg>A few days before (namely, the second day of Easter), Rosa, having accidentally fallen to the ground, had severely injured her arm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paucis ante obitum horis ab ejuscemodi prægustu in se rediens, ac suis gaudiis impar, P. Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030121Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030121Z">
        <seg>A few hours before his death, recovering from such a foretaste and unrestrained in his joy, P. Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paulo aliter id experta est soror Francisca de Montoya, itidem S. Patris Dominici alumna Tertiaria.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043919Z">
        <seg>Sister Francisca de Montoya experienced it somewhat differently; she too was a tertiary alumna of Saint Dominic the Father.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paulo post effudit etiam secundas, comite fœtore, qui adstantes pene exanimabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035049Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035049Z">
        <seg>Shortly after, he also let out a second fart, accompanied by a foul odor that nearly suffocated those present.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paulo post, instantissime postulantibus primatibus civitatis, sacrum illud corpus in locum celebriorem transferendum, decreto archiepiscopi Limensis extractum, inventum fuit corruptioni non subjacuisse, &amp; æque ex eo cælestem illum odorem prodire, quemadmodum, quando primo repositum fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043010Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043010Z">
        <seg>Shortly thereafter, at the urgent request of the city's primates that the sacred body be transferred to a more venerable place, by decree of the Archbishop of Lima it was removed and found not to have succumbed to corruption, and to emit the same heavenly fragrance as when it was first laid to rest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paulo remotius constiterat hastatum proregis satellitium, ut confusam molem irruentis populi distineret, si posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030700Z">
        <seg>A little farther back, the pro-regent's spear-bearing retinue had taken up position to hold back the confused mass of the rushing crowd, if they could.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pavida mater, binos religiosos illac forte transeuntes rogavit, num quid pulveris e Rosæ sepulchro secum ferrent?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035731Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035731Z">
        <seg>A fearful mother asked two religious men who happened to be passing by if they had any powder from the Tomb of the Rose with them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Peccatrix sum, equidem fateor, &amp; ut fatear, hic provolvor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062015Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062015Z">
        <seg>I am a sinner, I confess, and to confess, I cast myself down here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pellibus ab ipsa genitrice pro reparanda valetudine circum ligatis summe cruciata, silens &amp; patiens sustinuit, vitam potius, quam obedientiam perdere parata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041200Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041200Z">
        <seg>Severely tortured by the flogging her own mother inflicted to restore her health, she endured it in silence and patience, ready rather to lose her life than her obedience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Penetrarunt medullas Rosæ hæc verba, &amp;, velut ictu improviso, exanimis in se recidit, brevique lucta confusorum affectuum, simul &amp; dehiscebat tremula in abyssum sui nihili, &amp; natabat sublimis tumore lætitiæ, nec tamen succumbens tantæ dignationis miraculo animus inveniebat, quid responderet, ni succurrissent verba humilitatis purissimæ: “Ecce ancilla Domini.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044346Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044346Z">
        <seg>These words penetrated to the marrow of Rose, &amp;, as if struck by a sudden blow, she fell lifeless within herself, and in a brief struggle of confused emotions, she was at once torn apart by a trembling plunge into the abyss of her own nothingness, and she swam, exalted by a swelling of joy; yet her spirit, not yielding to the miracle of such exaltation, could find no answer, save for the words of the purest humility: "Behold the handmaid of the Lord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Per quadrantem horæ sic jacuit Perez, neque, ut erat manibus pedibusque ex arthritide nodosus ac debilis, quidquam juvare seipsum potuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034258Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034258Z">
        <seg>For a quarter of an hour Perez lay like that, and, his hands and feet knotted and weakened by arthritis, he could do nothing to help himself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Per viam, hospitii causa, ad præfectum loci Guando diverterant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035759Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035759Z">
        <seg>They had turned aside to the local prefect, Guando, for lodging on their way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Peracto ibidem reliquo precum, antiphonarum, psalmorumque ritu, Provincialis cum ceteris religiosorum Ordinum prælatis (sacerdotali singulos amiciente stola) tumbam e tumulo demissam suscepit in humeros, sicque adstante archiepiscopo, &amp; circumstante diversorum tribunalium &amp; civilis curiæ magistratu, illata fuit propriæ cryptæ ad dexteram summi altaris affabre in hunc usum elaboratæ, quæ tota intus oblita auro, reddebat fulgores, quos arcuato sinu recipiebat a luminibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031546Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031546Z">
        <seg>After the remaining rite of prayers, antiphons, and psalms was completed there, the Provincial, together with the other prelates of the religious orders (each wearing a priestly stole), took the bier, lowered from the tomb, upon his shoulders, and thus, with the archbishop present and the magistrates of the various tribunals and the civil court standing around, It was brought to its own crypt, skillfully prepared for this purpose on the right side of the high altar, which, entirely gilded on the inside, reflected the splendors it received from the lights in its arched recess.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Percelluit matrem arcanus horror, &amp; tunc quidem diffimulavit, forte subdubitans, non admodum seria esse, quæ metrice, ac per cantilenam efferuntur, non quia vera, sed quia obvia: donec tertius dies (qui mensis Augusti primus erat) aperuit, non poëtice, sed prophetice cantatos fuisse migraturi oloris threnos, ni mavelis dicere jubilos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013307Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013307Z">
        <seg>A secret horror seized the mother, and at that time she concealed it, perhaps doubting that what is expressed in verse and in a ditty was very serious—not because it was true, but because it was commonplace: until the third day (which was the first of August) revealed that the swans' migratory laments, or rather, if you will, their jubilos, had been sung not poetically, but prophetically.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Perculsi superno spectaculo idololatræ, pacem armorum experimento prætulerunt, &amp; obstinatione una cum ferro excussa, certatim jugo fidei cum gaudio succollarunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054242Z">
        <seg>Struck by the divine spectacle of the idolatry, they preferred the peace of arms by experience, and having cast off obstinacy along with iron, they joyfully submitted to the yoke of faith.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Perditus erat, ni Rosam denuo &amp; multo ardentius invocasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033843Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033843Z">
        <seg>He was lost, had he not invoked Rosa again and much more ardently.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Perfecit ille, quod mandaveram;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051747Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051747Z">
        <seg>He did what I had commanded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pergebat alacris Virgo, nec aliud replicuit, nisi quod idem ille, qui sibi manus dederat, quas vestiendæ sacræ imagini impenderet, tunc dextram sanarat, qua eidem devestiendæ famularetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055420Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055420Z">
        <seg>The Virgin went on eagerly, and said nothing else, except that the same man who had helped her dress the sacred image then healed the right hand with which he would serve in its undressing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pergebat avicula modulari, variabat lepidæ vocis argutias, duplicabat gargarismos&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010226Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010226Z">
        <seg>The little bird continued to sing, varying the witty tricks of its charming voice, doubling its gurgles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pergebat avicula modulari, variabat lepidæ vocis argutias, duplicabat gargarismos&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045028Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045028Z">
        <seg>The little bird continued to sing, varying the witty turns of its charming voice, doubling its gurgling trills.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pernecessarium in regionibus illis condimentum mel domi omnino defuerat: descendit ipsa in nomine Domini, secretissimo instinctu jussa optime sperare de Dei protectione, cupam invenit usque ad summitatem recenti melle plenam, tota domo stupente, eoque magis, quod novo prodigio mel cælitus datum integris octo mensibus toti familiæ sat fuit in usum quotidianum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042552Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042552Z">
        <seg>An absolutely essential household condiment, honey, had completely run out in those regions: she herself went down in the name of the Lord, secretly inspired by a most secret instinct to hope for God's protection, and found the hive filled to the brim with fresh honey, the whole household amazed—and all the more so because, by a new miracle, the honey, given from heaven, was enough for the entire family's daily use for a full eight months.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Perpaucis Dei amicis id curæ fuisse legitur, de unico Gregorio Lopes Mexicano&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; id Rosa legerat, statimque sibi imitandum sumpsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000924Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000924Z">
        <seg>It is recorded that very few of God's friends cared about it; Rosa had read of the one Mexican, Gregorio Lopes, and immediately resolved to imitate him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Perpaucis Dei amicis id curæ fuisse legitur, de unico Gregorio Lopes Mexicano&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; id Rosa legerat, statimque sibi imitandum sumpsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041938Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041938Z">
        <seg>It is recorded that very few of God's friends cared about it; Rosa had read it in the single work of the Mexican Gregory Lopes, and immediately resolved to imitate it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Perrexit ille securus promissi, quod non ignoraret, quid Rosa valeret apud Deum, &amp; angelos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051840Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051840Z">
        <seg>He went on, secure in the promise, for he was not unaware of how much Rosa was worth to God and the angels.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Perrexit tamen solicitus Pater, at famulum sine loquela, colore, spiritu, supinum &amp; immobilem reperit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033806Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033806Z">
        <seg>The anxious Father went on, however, and found the servant speechless, colorless, and breathless, lying on his back and motionless.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pertæsa mater, quod crederet frustra ab ignavo progeniem exspectari, in mensa coram marito &amp; filiis constituit, illo vespere pullum cetera inutilem jugulare, ac postridie comedendum apponere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061534Z">
        <seg>Fed up, the mother, believing that a cowardly offspring would be of no use, decided at the table before her husband and sons to slaughter the useless chick that evening and serve it for dinner the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pervenere ambo ad horti januam, quæ ad umbræ tactum mox sponte patuit sine clavi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051818Z">
        <seg>They both reached the garden gate, which, at the touch of the shade, soon opened of its own accord without a key.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Petierat a Rosa precum suffragium pro felici exitu occulti negotii, sed ardui, quodque, ut sileretur a cæteris, primus silebat ipse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012117Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012117Z">
        <seg>He had asked Rosa to pray for the successful outcome of a secret but arduous affair, and, so that the others would remain silent, he himself was the first to keep quiet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Petiit doctor, quandoquidem in eum gradum nonnisi per tribulos &amp; spineta ascenditur, num saltem aliunde persecutiones &amp; ærumnas passa fuerit?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050349Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050349Z">
        <seg>The doctor asked, since that rank can only be reached through tribulations and thorns, whether he had at least suffered persecutions and hardships elsewhere?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Petri de Vergas cognata Joanna de Mendoza cum filiola hoc remedio febres abegerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040016Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040016Z">
        <seg>Joanna de Mendoza, the sister-in-law of Peter de Vergas, and her little daughter cured their fevers with this remedy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Petrus de Loaysa Dominicanus, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; alium morti proximum,]&lt;/s2&gt; probe conscius zeli &amp; charitatis, qua flagrabat misericors Rosa in opem periclitantium proximorum, indicarat Virgini, decumbere in cœnobio, cumque mortis angoribus trepide luctari N.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060825Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060825Z">
        <seg>Peter de Loaysa, a Dominican, [and another near death,] well aware of the zeal and charity with which the merciful Rose burned for the aid of her fellow travelers in peril, had informed the Virgin that she was lying in the convent, and that N. was anxiously struggling with the pangs of death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Petrus de Loaysa, additque duo notatu digna;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032622Z">
        <seg>Peter de Loaysa adds two things worthy of note.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Peñalosa cum socio Francisco Lopez coadjutore;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053830Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053830Z">
        <seg>Peñalosa with his associate Francisco Lopez, assistant;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Philippa de Vargas ex intolerabili æstu febrium, simul toto sincipite diris velut aculeis incessanter dispungebatur: tandem ex Rosæ reliquiis particulam vertici applicuit, &amp; obdormivit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034540Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034540Z">
        <seg>Philippa de Vargas, tormented by an unbearable fever, was at the same time being incessantly pricked throughout her skull by dreadful, thorn-like pangs; at last she applied a particle of Rose's relic to her crown and fell asleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Philosophiæ ac medicinæ studiis ad summum apicem eruditus, insuper in omnia metaphysices adyta summo ingenii acumine penetrarat: celebris ob id in scholis, in exedris, in doctissimorum virorum congressibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050042Z">
        <seg>He had been trained to the highest degree in the study of philosophy and medicine, and had moreover penetrated with the keenest intellect into all the inner recesses of metaphysics; celebrated for this in schools, in lecture halls, and in the gatherings of the most learned men.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Placebat Virgini bicolor tot vernantium plantularum venustas &amp; gratia, maxime ubi deintus responsum accepit, omnes &amp; singulas uni Dilecto candido ac rubicundo servari in coronam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062548Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062548Z">
        <seg>The Virgin delighted in the twofold beauty and charm of so many budding plants, especially when she received the reply that all of them, one by one, were being reserved for the crown of the one Beloved, fair and ruddy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Placet ei, quod ambæ in elaborando ipsius scapulari jucunde occupamur”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055117Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055117Z">
        <seg>He is pleased that we are both happily occupied with making his scapular.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Placuit hic Rosæ ardor Salvatori, tantamque propagandi sui amoris sollicitudinem ingenti miraculo obsignavit Deus, quod capite sequenti seorsim narrandum sepono.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053619Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053619Z">
        <seg>Salvator was pleased by Rose's ardor here, and God sealed that great zeal for spreading her love with an immense miracle, which I set aside to relate separately in the next chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Placuit multis novum amœnitatis artificium, quod, irrigante gratia, sub hortulanæ devotione feliciter adoleverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054943Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054943Z">
        <seg>Many were pleased with the new art of beauty, which, watered by grace, had happily grown under the gardener's devotion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Placuit utrimque tam lauta conditio, præsertim quod Rosæ parentes ut facultatibus tenues, ita graves erant multitudine prolium: nam undecim numerabantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035957Z">
        <seg>Both sides were pleased with the generous terms, especially since Rosa's parents, though modest in means, were heavy-handed in the number of their children—for they numbered eleven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Planta similis est hederæ, eodem modo repens &amp; scandens quocumque loco ponatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041448Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041448Z">
        <seg>The plant is like ivy, creeping and climbing in the same way wherever it is placed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Plerumque dicitur de præmiis cælitum, de quibus Josephus Angles apud Cangium in Glossario ad vocem&lt;/e0&gt; aureola &lt;e1&gt;scribit&lt;/e1&gt;, quod in corporibus virginum, martyrum atque doctorum erunt insignia quædam, quibus hujusmodi aureolas in anima habere præ se ferent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032405Z">
        <seg>It is commonly said of the rewards of the heavenly, concerning which Joseph Angles, in the Glossary under the word aureola, writes that on the bodies of virgins, martyrs, and doctors there will be certain insignia, by which they will display that they have such aureolas in their souls;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Plerumque etiam post illas tenebras apparuisse ei humanitatem Christi Domini, necnon &amp; sanctissimam virginem Deiparam, intellectuales etiam visiones, quibus in se præsentiam Dei experiebatur, per terminos remotionis, eminentiæ, &amp; causalitatis, aliasque circumstantias explicavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041808Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041808Z">
        <seg>He also explained that, for the most part, even after those dark periods the humanity of Christ the Lord, as well as the most holy Virgin Theotokos, appeared to him, along with intellectual visions in which he experienced the presence of God within himself, through the categories of removal, eminence, and causality, and other circumstances.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pleuritidem auxit ischiadum acerbus dolor, cholica&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt;, hypochondriarum&lt;a1&gt;g&lt;/a1&gt; dira convulsio, accensa in pede dextro nodosa arthritis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025615Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025615Z">
        <seg>Pleuritis was aggravated by a severe pain in the sciatic nerve, colic, a dreadful convulsion of the hypochondria, and an inflamed, nodose arthritis in the right foot.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Plura addidit ex mysticæ theologiæ aphorismis, de simplificatione cordis, de cynosura purificatæ intentionis, de nuditate affectuum, de indifferentia resignata, de arcanis introductionibus, de abysso luminis, de sapore omnis discursus intuitu cælestium, de fonte vitæ, de aliis, quæ Rosæ talium capaci &amp; placuere supra modum, &amp; profuere deinceps, ut suis confessariis, ex hoc vocabulario, se nosset clarius, atque significantius explicaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050330Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050330Z">
        <seg>He added more from the aphorisms of mystical theology: on the simplification of the heart, on the guiding star of purified intention, on the nakedness of the affections, on resigned indifference, on the secret introductions, on the abyss of light, on the savor of all discourse through the intuition of heavenly things, on the fountain of life, and on other matters, which the Rose, capable of such things, found exceedingly pleasing. and thereafter, to her confessors, she would know herself more clearly and explain herself more meaningfully from this vocabulary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Plura parabat dicere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044356Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044356Z">
        <seg>He was preparing to say more.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pluribus horis duravit obscurissima lucta, ingenti probro adversarii, qui victoriam desperans, vel unicum saltem gemitum, aut metus indicium extorquere tentabat, nec poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052128Z">
        <seg>The darkest struggle lasted for many hours, to the great shame of the adversary, who, despairing of victory, tried to wring out at least a single groan or a sign of fear, but could not.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pluries deinde Virginem, non tam examinaturus, quam collaturus, fidenter convenit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050622Z">
        <seg>He then repeatedly approached the Virgin, not so much to examine her as to converse with her, with confidence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pluries in eadem S. imagine (ut infra narrabitur) demulserat hujuscemodi suavis annictus orantem Rosam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044322Z">
        <seg>On several occasions in the same holy image (as will be related below), this sweet gesture had moved the praying Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Plurima alia hujusmodi, ac etiam mirabiliora commemorantur hujus Sponsæ Christi vaticinia, quibus omnibus correspondebat eventus, comprobans in ejus anima plenitudinem gratiæ, non tantum sanctificantis, sed etiam gratis datæ Spiritum sanctum inhabitare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042648Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042648Z">
        <seg>Many other such, and even more marvelous, prophecies of this Spouse of Christ are recorded, all of which were fulfilled by events, confirming that in her soul there dwelt the fullness of grace, not only sanctifying, but also the Holy Spirit given freely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pollicere hodie, mater, quod in illo mortifero extremæ sitis incendio me non destitues, quin poscenti aquam præbeas”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013158Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013158Z">
        <seg>"Promise me today, mother, that in that deadly, extreme fire you will not abandon me, but will provide water to anyone who asks."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ponderosa hæc asperitas ut suapte grossitie fere implicabilis, ita duritie implicabilis, cuivis robustiori pœnitentiæ alumno sufficere potuisset ad summam austeritatem, nec tamen Rosæ sat fuit: nam infixis undique brevissimis acubus, hirsutam interulam diligentius in se armavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041845Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041845Z">
        <seg>This ponderous harshness, almost unwieldy by its own coarseness and unyielding in its rigidity, could have sufficed for the most robust penitent to attain the utmost austerity; yet it was not enough for Rosa, for she armed herself more diligently with a shaggy undergarment, fixing on every side the shortest spikes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pone, suppone, ut volueris, obstitura vetantium imperia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062632Z">
        <seg>Suppose, for whatever reason you wish, that the commands of those who forbid are obstructed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Poro affirmabat hanc orandi formulam terrori esse dæmonibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044954Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044954Z">
        <seg>Poro affirmed that this formula of prayer was a terror to demons.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro Elisabeth successu læta, exinde per complures annos palam visa est brachio suo uti ad quævis officia, quasi numquam languisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033248Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033248Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, Elizabeth, delighted by her success, was thereafter seen for several years openly using her arm for any task, as if she had never been weak.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro adjectivum generis masculini, quod ab auctore vel typotheta huic malo perperam addebatur, in femininum commutavi.&lt;/e10&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052919Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, I changed the masculine adjective, which had been mistakenly added to this word by the author or the typographer, to the feminine form.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro celeberrimo tot illustrium heroïnarum contubernio digne apteque respondet augusta nobilissimæ fabricæ amplitudo, fontes, horti, viteta, porticus, dormitoria, peristylia, officinæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062430Z">
        <seg>Moreover, the august and noblest fabric responds fittingly and appropriately to the company of so many celebrated and illustrious heroines with its spaciousness, its fountains, gardens, vineyards, porticoes, dormitories, peristyles, and workshops.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro de immensitate gaudii ex arcta unione cum Sponso amicissimo, de fruitionis altitudine, de spirituali sapore veræ dulcedinis, ubi gustatur in suo proprio fonte, de fructuum præstantia, copia, sagina, deque similibus, pigebat tentare quemcumque sterilis elocutionis modum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055904Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055904Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, regarding the immensity of joy from the close union with the most beloved Spouse, the height of fruition, the spiritual savor of true sweetness where it is tasted at its own fountain, the excellence, abundance, and richness of the fruits, and the like, it was deemed unworthy to attempt any barren mode of expression.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro dum brachia &amp; lumbos vincula nervique arctabant, manipulatim urticæ ac minutuli sentes morsu creberrimo vexabant pectus, axillas, latera, ne qua pars vacaret a cruciatu, dum forte cilicium adhuc deerat præ inopia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041823Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041823Z">
        <seg>Moreover, as ropes and thongs bound their arms and loins, they tormented the chest, armpits, and sides with handfuls of nettles and tiny thorns in a most frequent bite, so that no part was spared from the torture, while, by chance, the cilicium was still lacking on account of the scarcity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro eadem nocte Raphaëla per soporem vidit Rosam candido amictu, facieque præfulgida gloriosam, dicentem sibi: “Crastino mane Christina recuperabitur”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033939Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, that same night, Raphael saw in a dream Rosa, dressed in a white robe and with a face shining gloriously, who said to him, "Tomorrow morning Christina will be recovered."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro gavisa est Rosa cum a Patre Lorenzana suo confessario, quod numquam sciverat, tunc primum didicit, hunc perpetuum in Ordine morem esse, quod moribundis religiosis illo modo sacrum scapulare insternatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030003Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030003Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, Rosa was delighted when she learned from her confessor, Father Lorenzana, for the first time something she had never known: that it was a perpetual custom in the Order to lay the sacred scapular in that manner on dying religious.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro in ipso ad tremenda illa mysteria accessu quis fuerit Virgini attentissimæ devotionis ardor, quæ vultus gestuumque religiosa compositio, quod bullientis spiritus incendium, aliter describi non potest, quam si ipsammet quis Catharinam Senensem, Eucharistico bolo excipiendo procubuam, huc transcribendo delineet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055737Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055737Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, at the very approach to those awesome mysteries, the ardor of the Virgin's most devoted fervor, the reverent composure of her face and gestures, the blazing fire of her spirit—none of this can be described otherwise than by transcribing here the very image of Catherine of Siena herself, prostrate as she received the Eucharistic Host.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro interiorem arcum impleverat humanitas Domini mei Jesu Christi radiosa, clara, prælucida, tanto gloriæ amictu circumdata, quantum in ea numquam alias fueram conspicata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052733Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, the inner arc was filled by the radiant, clear, and pre-lucid humanity of my Lord Jesus Christ, surrounded by so great a mantle of glory as I had never before beheld in it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro ne capilli, qui post rescissionem breviores succreverant, profundiori obstarent aculeorum impressioni, ad vivam usque cutem novacula crinem omnem submoverat, aliquid tamen inculti cincinni relictum in fronte, quo corona interdiu obtegeretur, ut matrem lateret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042121Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042121Z">
        <seg>Moreover, so that the hair, which had grown back shorter after the cutting, would not hinder a deeper impression of the spikes, she had shaved every strand right down to the living skin with a razor, leaving only a few unkempt curls on her forehead to cover her crown by day and hide it from her mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro odorem exhalabant, non qui cadaverum esse solet, sed quo fragant siccatæ ad solem Rosæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031652Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031652Z">
        <seg>Moreover, they exhaled a fragrance, not the kind usually associated with corpses, but that of roses dried in the sun.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro probabilius existimamus, hæc &amp; similia potius imaginationi sanctæ Virginis repræsentata esse, quam re ipsa contigisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051505Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, we consider it more probable that these and similar things were rather represented to the imagination of the holy Virgin than actually happened.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro qualibet sexta feria hoc addebat in cumulum, ut usque ad auricularum cartilagines imumque occiput coronam demitteret, quo tempora (ubi caro mollior, sensusque delicatior) prædensa stimulorum obsidio &amp; molestius incingeret, &amp; profundius sauciaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042207Z">
        <seg>Moreover, every Friday he would add to the heap so that he would lower the crown down to the cartilages of the ears and the lower part of the occiput, by which the temples (where the flesh is softer and the sensation more delicate) were besieged by the pointed stimuli and more painfully girdled, and wounded more deeply.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro quantus fuerit Lorenzana in ea, quam jure scientiam Sanctorum dixeris, mystica theosophia, quantus in sapore &amp; experimento rerum cælestium, quam exercitatus in altissimæ contemplationis disciplina, quam oculatus ac lynceus in prudenti spirituum discretione, hoc unum (si reliqua deessent) judico sufficere, quod divina providentia singulariter ipsi spiritum Rosæ commiserit gubernandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050209Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050209Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, how great Lorenzana was in it, however you may rightly call the knowledge of the Saints a mystical theosophy, how great he was in the savor and experience of heavenly things, how practiced in the discipline of the highest contemplation, how keen-eyed and lynx-like in the prudent discernment of spirits—this one thing alone, if all the rest were lacking, I judge sufficient: that divine providence singularly entrusted to him the spirit of the Rose to govern.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro quatuor postremis vitæ suæ annis in majori (quam vocamus) hebdomada, quamdiu sacrum Christi corpus adorabatur clausum de more in monumento, nec noctu inde recedebat affixa tumulo Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060040Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, during the last four years of her life, in the greater week (as we call it), while the sacred body of Christ was being adored, enclosed as was the custom in the monument, and the Rose was affixed to the tomb, nor did she depart from it at night;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro reliquam ipsius erga parentes observantiam, amorem, pietatem nulla satis capit historia: quam solicita jugiter sategerit, ut genitorum suorum inopiæ provideret ex labore suarum manuum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035731Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035731Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, history cannot adequately capture the rest of his respect, love, and piety toward his parents: how diligently he always worked to provide for his parents' needs through the labor of his own hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro singulas distantium lignorum intercapedines præacutis tegularum fragmentis, coctilium patinarum frustulis, testarumque fractarum triquetris sic implevit, ut singulorum pars mucronata obverteretur corpori, ac ea quidem confertissima densitate, qua nutare aut cedere nulla posset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042610Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, he filled each of the gaps between the distant timbers with sharp fragments of tiles, shards of clay pots, and broken triangular pottery, so that the pointed end of each piece was turned inward toward the body, and with such a dense packing that none could waver or give way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro specimen obedientiæ hic assignatum apud nos infra num.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035911Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, the specimen of obedience assigned here is found below in our number.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro ut primum in candido S. Patris sui Dominici habitu se circumspexit, solatiis ex alto copiosis tota permaduit, nec sufficiebat explicare animi sui contentissimam voluptatem, hoc unum dolens, quod tantæ felicitati diu obstiterat corde pavido &amp; obstinato.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032929Z">
        <seg>Moreover, as soon as she found herself in the white habit of her Father Saint Dominic, she was completely filled with abundant consolations from on high, and could scarcely express the soul's most contented delight, grieving only that she had long stood in the way of such great happiness with a fearful and obstinate heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro visionem neque cassam aut inanem fuisse, nec pure imaginariam, probavit effectus: nam Rosa mox convaluit, redierunt corpusculo vires, agilitas, robur, quo postridie templum adiit, Panem angelorum manducavit, satura, vegeta, sana domum repetiit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051121Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051121Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, the effect proved that the vision had been neither empty nor vain, nor purely imaginary: for Rosa soon recovered, her strength, agility, and vigor returned, and the next day she went to the temple, ate the Bread of Angels, and returned home full, refreshed, and healthy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro, ne his minutioribus immoremur, tandem hoc Magistro, suæ mortis diem, locum, horam edocta, nemini dulcius, quam sibi suoque funeri felix vates præcinuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013006Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, so that we may not linger over these minor details, this Master, at last informed of the day, place, and hour of his own death, foretold nothing more sweetly than his own happy fate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro, quam plene cum illo magistræ suæ virtutes induerit (quamquam id sequentibus capitibus reponamus) ad coronidem hujus capitis, unicum id argumentum esto, quod semel tacite spectanti, mirantique confessario visa fuerit Rosa, specie, vultu, lineamentis sic transformata, ut viva similitudine ipsissimam S. Catharinæ Senensis effigiem referret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040413Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040413Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, how fully she had put on the virtues of her Master (though we shall reserve that for the following chapters), let this be the crowning point of this chapter: that once, when the confessor was silently observing and marveling, Rosa appeared to him, in form, countenance, and features, so transformed that in her living likeness she bore the very image of St. Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro, quæ statuæ ornamenta conquireret, tractaret, circumponeret, una hac Rosa (dum vixit) nulla reperiebatur aptior, dignior, peritior;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055024Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, for the task of procuring, handling, and arranging the ornaments for the statues, no one was found more suitable, more worthy, or more skilled than this one Rose (during her lifetime).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro, ut visionis sincera assereretur veritas, in muliere comperta fuerunt a peritis vera divinæ revelationis critiria, profundior humilitas, agnitio sui nihili, alta mentis tranquillitas, gaudium spirituale novis divini amoris incendiis inflammatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032039Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032039Z">
        <seg>Moreover, to establish the sincerity of the vision, experts found in the woman the true criteria of divine revelation: a deeper humility, the recognition of her own nothingness, a lofty tranquility of mind, and a spiritual joy inflamed by new fires of divine love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post Virginis obitum ancillula in Limensi S. Catharinæ monasterio argenteum cochlear per incuriam perdiderat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035638Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035638Z">
        <seg>After the Virgin's death, a little handmaid at the Lima monastery of St. Catherine had carelessly lost a silver spoon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post assumptam tertii Ordinis candidam vestem, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; alteram]&lt;/s2&gt; nocte quadam in somnis ostensa fuit Rosæ pulcherrima ac decentissima Viri species: pene suggerebat animus cælitem quemdam esse, imo ipsummet Speciosum forma præ filiis hominum, nisi quod habitus latomum fingebat, seu marmorum cædendorum nobilem artificem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044139Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044139Z">
        <seg>After assuming the white habit of the Third Order, &lt;s2&gt;[and another]&lt;/s2&gt; one night in a dream the most beautiful and most graceful form of a man was shown to Rose: the mind almost suggested that he was some heavenly being, indeed the very one Fairer in form than the sons of men, except that his attire suggested a noble craftsman of marble, or of hewing stone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post assumptam tertii Ordinis candidam vestem, [&amp; alteram] nocte quadam in somnis ostensa fuit Rosæ pulcherrima ac decentissima Viri species: pene suggerebat animus cælitem quemdam esse, imo ipsummet Speciosum forma præ filiis hominum, nisi quod habitus latomum fingebat, seu marmorum cædendorum nobilem artificem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010043Z">
        <seg>After donning the white garment of the Third Order, another night in a dream the most beautiful and most graceful form of a man was shown to Rosa: it almost suggested a heavenly being, indeed the very one Fairer in form than the sons of men, were it not that his attire suggested a noble craftsman of marble or of cutting stone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post biduum, domi Melchiora de los Reyes caput filiolæ detegens, nullum in eo reperit impetiginis obduratæ vestigium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040320Z">
        <seg>Two days later, Melchiora de los Reyes, at home, uncovered her little daughter's head and found no trace of hardened impetigo on it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post cassas preces, &amp; obstinatas repulsas, Virgo de more confugit ad cælitum auxilia, Deum in primis Deique Matrem assidue invocans, ut maternum sibi consensum potenti afflatu elicerent: nec dubitavit exaudiri;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043512Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043512Z">
        <seg>After fruitless prayers and stubborn refusals, the Virgin, as was her custom, fled to the help of the heavenly powers, constantly invoking God first and the Mother of God, so that they might by their powerful intercession win for her the maternal consent: and she did not doubt that she would be heard.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post duas horas ad infirmæ habitaculum reversus cum sociis, maritum cum tota familia reperit in jubilo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035824Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035824Z">
        <seg>After returning with his companions to the sick woman's dwelling two hours later, he found the husband and his entire family in great joy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post hunc devotissime succollarunt primores Ordinum religiosorum, partiendo vices per vicos &amp; terminos compitorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030616Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030616Z">
        <seg>After this, the foremost members of the religious orders most devoutly took up the work, dividing the duties among the villages and the boundaries of the crossroads.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post hæc, ille Speciosus forma præ filiis hominum bilancem, &amp; nescio quæ pondera disposuit in propatulo: mox populosæ angelorum phalanges festivo ornatu, ac nitore illustres accessere, decora cum reverentia sese inclinantes Domino majestatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052748Z">
        <seg>After these things, he, fair in form above the sons of men, set up a balance and, I know not what, weights on the open platform; soon the populous phalanxes of angels, adorned with festive attire and shining with splendor, drew near, bowing with graceful reverence to the Lord of majesty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post intervallum temporis illudmet debitum rursus exigitur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033955Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033955Z">
        <seg>After a period of time, that very debt is again demanded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post menses quatuor aut quinque Joanna integre convaluit, suumque foris habitaculum sana repetiit, hoc unum dolens, quod hospitæ palam referre grates minime liceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061224Z">
        <seg>After four or five months, Joanna had fully recovered and returned to her lodgings abroad in good health, grieving only that she could not publicly express her gratitude to her hostess.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post quæ, crescente devota populi frequentia ob eximia curationum beneficia, quæ in dies crebrescebant, solemniores exsequiæ celebratæ fuerunt, quibus prorex, &amp; major multitudo interfuit festivis applausibus &amp; publicæ lætitiæ signis, tam incolis, quam advenis novæ cælorum Civi acclamantibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043007Z">
        <seg>After this, as the people's devout attendance grew because of the exceptional benefits of the cures, which became more frequent by the day, more solemn funeral rites were celebrated, to which the viceroy and a greater multitude attended with festive applause and signs of public joy, as both the inhabitants and the newcomers acclaimed the new Citizen of Heaven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Posterior ei continuus erat, &amp; quantum nostræ mortalitatis via permittit, nulli interruptioni obnoxius, nisi dum ad horam patiebatur visiones, de quibus capite sequenti.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044827Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044827Z">
        <seg>His posterior was continuous, and, as far as the course of our mortality allows, subject to no interruption, except when he endured visions at the appointed hour, of which in the next chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Postrema hæc verba ænigma erant, quod tunc quidem sacristanus non satis assequebatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043534Z">
        <seg>These last words were an enigma, which the sacristan did not quite understand at the time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Postridie Gundisalvi uxor non destitit, donec, enarrante Rosa, universam rei seriem prolicuisset, ubi inter alia &amp; hoc notatum fuit, quod hujuscemodi conflictuum duri agones constantissimæ Virgini neque peregrini erant, aut minus frequentes;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052147Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052147Z">
        <seg>The next day, Gundisalvi's wife did not stop until, with Rosa narrating, she had elicited the entire sequence of events, where, among other things, it was noted that such conflicts were neither unfamiliar nor infrequent for the most steadfast Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Postridie fasciam solvens, totam Aloysiæ faciem ardore ac tumore undequaque, non secus ac manuum volas, reperit liberam, nullo vel minimo vestigio hesterni mali residuo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034739Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034739Z">
        <seg>The next day, when she removed the bandage, she found Aloysia's entire face, as well as the palms of her hands, completely free of any burning or swelling, with not the slightest trace of yesterday's ailment remaining.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Postridie lecto exsiliens, dum Missæ sacrificio interventura ad odeum properat, præfectæ occurrit miranti ac sciscitanti, cur, &amp; quo iret, præcipue autem, qui valeret?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040850Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040850Z">
        <seg>The next day, springing out of bed and hurrying to the odeum to attend the Mass, she ran into the prefect, who was surprised and asked why and where she was going, and above all, how she was.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Postridie reversa ad pedes sacræ imaginis diutius procubuit, ferventius oravit, lachrymas addidit, prolixæque moræ æquanimi patientia favorem expectavit a solo Deiparæ vultu pendula, nec surrectura de loco, donec exauditam se intelligeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054425Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054425Z">
        <seg>The next day she returned and prostrated herself longer at the feet of the sacred image, prayed more fervently, added tears, and with patient equanimity awaited favor, her hopes hanging solely on the face of the Mother of God, and she would not rise from her place until she understood herself to have been heard.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Postridie summo mane Franciscum matertera accersit, quem toto viginti octo annorum spatio videre noluerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032814Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032814Z">
        <seg>The next day, at the crack of dawn, she summoned Francis, whom she had refused to see for a full twenty-eight years.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Postridie summo mane respirans Catharina, tot simul malis se ereptam experta &amp; fassa est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040054Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040054Z">
        <seg>The next day, breathing a sigh of relief, Catherine realized and confessed that she had been rescued from so many evils at once.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Potosium trecentis leucis a Lima dissitum, illactenus Rosam neque de nomine noverat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031252Z">
        <seg>Potosí, three hundred leagues from Lima, had until then known neither Rosa nor her name.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Premebat bajulantium vestigia archiepiscopus, inde reliqui ex ordine proceres, virique illustres.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031447Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031447Z">
        <seg>The archbishop pressed on, followed by the vestments-bearers, then the remaining nobles and distinguished men.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Presbyter Georgius de Aranda Valdivia ante clericatum in bellis Chilensibus contra infideles complura sinistro brachio exceperat vulnera, quæ deinde male curata paulatim saniem, convulsiones, tumores attraxerant, ut jam præ inflatione brachium neque extendi potuerit, nec judicari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033307Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033307Z">
        <seg>Before entering the priesthood, Father George de Aranda Valdivia had received several wounds in his left arm during the Chilean wars against the infidels, which, poorly treated, had gradually brought on pus, convulsions, and tumors, so that by then, due to the swelling, he could neither extend nor straighten his arm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Presbyter Mauritius Rodriquez dicti monasterii confessarius, importuna vexabatur capitis vertigine, ac cephalæa, nullumque e pharmacopœia omisit remediorum genus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035610Z">
        <seg>The priest Maurice Rodriguez, the confessor of the aforementioned monastery, was vexed by an importunate dizziness and headache, and he omitted no kind of remedy from the pharmacopoeia.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pressus his malis, nec cibum sumere, nec somnum capere infelix potuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033725Z">
        <seg>Overwhelmed by these misfortunes, the unfortunate man could neither eat nor sleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pridie sacræ Communionis asperius emacerabat corpusculum flagellis &amp; rigido jejunio, ut famem animæ comitaretur esuries corporis: venturo Sponso adornabat secretum mentis thalamum affectibus reverentiæ, humilitatis, ac desiderii;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055724Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055724Z">
        <seg>The day before Holy Communion, she more harshly scourged her little body with whips and a strict fast, so that the hunger of the soul would be accompanied by the thirst of the body: she adorned the secret chamber of her mind for the coming Bridegroom with affections of reverence, humility, and desire.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prima ad hoc festum accita comparuit lateris sinistri paralysis, qua totius corporis læva pars a summo deorsum enervata, in lethalem stuporem distabuit, ut inde inciperet Rosa per partes emori.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025556Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025556Z">
        <seg>The first thing to appear at this festival was a paralysis of the left side, by which the entire left half of the body was enervated from top to bottom and fell into a lethal stupor, so that from there Rosa began to die piecemeal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Primam futurarum monialium præfectam (priorissam dicimus) ex vultu agnovit, spiritu suo imbuit, osculo pacis quodammodo initiavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062458Z">
        <seg>He recognized the first future abbess (whom we call the prioress) by her face, imbued her with his spirit, and in a manner initiated her with the kiss of peace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Primo interrogata, a quanto tempore cælestes stimulos, &amp; tranquillum orationis spiritum concepisset, respondit simplicissime, temporis quidem se non meminisse, eo quod a prima pueritia se repererit fundendis precatiunculis apprime deditam, ac tota mente ad serios meditandi conatus erectam, ut nihil occurrere sibi dulcius potuerit, quam Deo loqui, Deum cogitare, inhiare supernis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050244Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050244Z">
        <seg>When first asked how long she had conceived heavenly aspirations and a tranquil spirit of prayer, she answered most simply that she did not remember the time, for she had found herself from her earliest childhood most devoted to pouring out little prayers and with her whole mind raised to serious acts of meditation, so that nothing could have seemed sweeter to her than to speak with God. To think of God, to long for heavenly things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Primo tunicam interulam conficiunto 600 Salutationes angelicæ, totidemque vicibus recitanda antiphona &lt;b0&gt;Salve Regina&lt;/b0&gt;, cum 15 diebus jejuniorum, in reverentiam purissimi gaudii ex nuntio angelico, quod in suis castissimis visceribus humana carne induendum erat æternum Patris Verbum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011131Z">
        <seg>First, let a tunic be made of interweaving 600 Angelic Salutations, and let the antiphon "Hail Mary" be recited as many times, together with 15 days of fasting, in reverence for the purest joy of the angelic announcement that the eternal Word of the Father was to be clothed in human flesh in her most chaste womb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Primo tunicam interulam conficiunto 600 Salutationes angelicæ, totidemque vicibus recitanda antiphona &lt;b3&gt;Salve Regina&lt;/b3&gt;, cum 15 diebus jejuniorum, in reverentiam purissimi gaudii ex nuntio angelico, quod in suis castissimis visceribus humana carne induendum erat æternum Patris Verbum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054608Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054608Z">
        <seg>First, let a tunic be made of interweaving 600 Hail Marys, and let the antiphon "Hail Queen" be recited as many times, together with 15 days of fasting, in reverence for the purest joy of the angelic announcement that the eternal Word of the Father was to be clothed in human flesh in her most chaste womb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Primum his merito sibi deposcit locum resuscitatio mortuæ filiolæ semestris Magdalenæ de Torres.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033108Z">
        <seg>First, the resuscitation of the six-month-old daughter, Magdalena de Torres, rightfully claims its place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Primum æterna sua beatitudo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061745Z">
        <seg>First, his eternal beatitude;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Primum, tametsi prope quotidianum esset, præsertim ultimis vitæ annis, quos in Gundisalvi quæstoris ædibus consummavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041308Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041308Z">
        <seg>First, although it was almost a daily occurrence, especially in the last years of his life, which he spent in the house of the treasurer Gundisalvus;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Primævum illi nomen ab avia Elizabeth fuit, quod postea divinitus non solum a matre propter cæleste prodigium rosæ in facie ejus illico apparentis, sed etiam a Thuribio&lt;a2&gt;f&lt;/a2&gt; archipræsule Limæ dignissimo (non absque cælesti, ut creditur, afflatu) in sacri Chrismatis unctione in Rosæ nomen commutatum, &amp; a Deipara Virgine agnomine &lt;b3&gt;a sancta Maria&lt;/b3&gt; auctum fuit, ut hoc etiam testimonio abundantius comprobaretur, eximia sanctorum merita a Deo nomina fortiri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041104Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041104Z">
        <seg>Her original name was Elizabeth from her grandmother, which was later divinely changed to Rose, not only by her mother because of the heavenly miracle of a rose appearing instantly on her face, but also by the most worthy Archbishop of Lima, Thuribius&lt;a2&gt;f&lt;/a2&gt; (not without a heavenly, as is believed, inspiration) at the anointing with the sacred Chrism, and was augmented by the Virgin Mother of God with the surname &lt;b3&gt;a Sancta Maria&lt;/b3&gt;, so that this too might be more abundantly confirmed by the testimony that the merits of the saints are mighty before God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Priori, (ut supra attigimus) cujusvis diei naturalis horas non minus duodecim seposuerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044824Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044824Z">
        <seg>He had set aside no fewer than twelve hours of any natural day (as we noted above).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pro arctiori recessu non sine magna lucta, &amp; prævio mirabili ostento Deiparæ &amp; pueri Jesu in sanctissimi Rosarii sacello, tandem obtinuit a matre in extremo horti angulo cellulam quinque pedum longitudinis, &amp; quatuor latitudinis, ubi non solum manuum labori, sed potissimum collectioni spiritus, &amp; contemplandi studio vacaret, tantum ibi solum inesse spatii gaudens, quantum sibi &amp; Sponso cælesti sufficeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041609Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041609Z">
        <seg>For a more secluded retreat, not without great struggle, and a prior miraculous apparition of the Virgin Mother and the child Jesus in the chapel of the most holy Rosary, he finally obtained from his mother a cell in the far corner of the garden, five feet long and four feet wide, where he could devote himself not only to manual labor, but especially to the collection of his spirit and the study of contemplation, rejoicing that there was just enough space there for herself and her heavenly Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pro his vere miseris quotidie cruenta sibi infligebat verbera, gemebat, suspirabat coram offenso Numine, nullique parcebat lachrymarum sumptui, ut talibus sinceram scelerum pœnitentiam vitæque seriam emendationem a Deo exoraret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060518Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060518Z">
        <seg>For these truly wretched ones, he inflicted bloody scourges on himself daily, groaned and sighed before the offended Deity, sparing no tears, so that by such deeds he might implore from God a sincere repentance for his sins and a serious amendment of his life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pro periclitantibus Christianis, qui per culpam lethalem a statu divinæ amicitiæ infeliciter exciderant, nulli lachrymarum sumptui parcebat, &amp; quotidie cruenta sibi vulnera infligebat, ut his vere miseris vitæ seria emendatio a Deo concederetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042344Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042344Z">
        <seg>For the Christian souls in peril, who by a fatal fault had wretchedly fallen from the state of divine friendship, he spared no expense of tears and daily inflicted bloody wounds on himself, so that these truly miserable souls might be granted by God a serious amendment of their lives.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Probarunt factum quicumque mysterium agnoverant, certi non ab humano studio, sed necessitatis fortunato potius quam fortuito consilio hoc singulare virtutis testimonium Virgini obtigisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042424Z">
        <seg>All who had recognized the mystery attested to the fact that this singular testimony of virtue had been granted to the Virgin not by human design, but rather by the necessity of fortune—more by chance than by deliberate plan.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Probe memini eorum, quæ mihi, ut peterem, fuere commendata”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032014Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032014Z">
        <seg>I clearly remember those things that were recommended to me to ask for.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Processerunt &amp; religiosorum mendicantium Ordines sub distinctis labaris&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt;: mox &amp; metropolitanum collegium ultro adfuit præter expectationem &amp; morem, cum alias numquam (nisi dum effertur proprius archiepiscopus) soleat humandis corporibus intervenire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030545Z">
        <seg>The mendicant religious orders processed under distinct banners; soon after, the metropolitan college came of its own accord, contrary to expectation and custom, since otherwise it never intervenes in the burial of bodies (unless its own archbishop is being interred).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Processit Rosa, dixitque voce humili ac intercisa, plerumque post illas horrendas tenebras conspicue sibi ostendi humanitatem Christi, nunc virili, nunc infantili aut puerili facie blandam, formosam, affabilem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050535Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050535Z">
        <seg>Rosa stepped forward and said in a low, broken voice that, for the most part, after those horrible darknesses, the humanity of Christ was clearly revealed to her, now with a manly face, now with an infantile or boyish one, gentle, beautiful, and affable.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Processit graviter, qui numquam aut festivior aut spectabilior ambularat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033424Z">
        <seg>He proceeded gravely, who had never before walked more festively or more stately.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Procubuit ante imaginem sanctæ Catharinæ in die B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042251Z">
        <seg>He prostrated himself before the image of Saint Catherine on the day of the blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Procubuit ille in genua, conatus confiteri;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035647Z">
        <seg>He fell to his knees, trying to confess.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prodigiosum erat videre, tenerrimam Virginem ardentibus juncturis, tumentibus nodis, inflatis articulis, lancinantibus ex consensu musculis &amp; ligaminibus, inter extremos dolorum laniatus decumbere quietam, serenam, placidam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052637Z">
        <seg>It was a prodigious sight to see the most tender Virgin, with burning joints, swollen knots, and distended limbs, her muscles and ligaments tearing apart in unison, lying amid the extremes of pain, yet calm, serene, and placid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prodigium erat, siqua persona cuicumque vitio obnoxia Rosam accederet, &amp; melior ab ea non recederet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042353Z">
        <seg>It was a miracle if anyone, given over to any vice, approached Rosa and did not come away better.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prodigium hoc triplex abbatissæ salutem, toti monasterio stuporem &amp; gaudium, Truxilli civibus cum gratulandi affectu novam devotionem instillavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035917Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035917Z">
        <seg>This triple prodigy brought health to the abbess, astonishment and joy to the entire monastery, and instilled a new devotion in the citizens of Truxilli with heartfelt congratulations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Profecta classe, tres supradicti non junctim, sed singuli ad Gundisalvum rediere, ac pluries quidem: premebat enim negotii gravitas &amp; regis obsequium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012740Z">
        <seg>After the fleet set sail, the three aforementioned men returned to Gundisalvus, not together but separately, and on several occasions: for the gravity of the matter and the king's orders pressed them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Profecto &amp; hic quæri posset: Quis dedit gallo intelligentiam, qua obediret sanctæ Puellæ jubenti jugulum dare cantui, ne daret cultro?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061606Z">
        <seg>Indeed, one might ask here: Who gave the rooster the intelligence to obey the holy maiden's command to give his throat to song, rather than to a knife?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Profuit sane permultis hoc exemplum, Rosæque ardens hortatio, ut frequentandis Dei laudibus familiarius adsuescerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045137Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045137Z">
        <seg>This example certainly benefited many, and Rose's ardent exhortation that they become more familiar with frequenting the praises of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde Rosa ferreum hunc scapularum vomerem sanctiusne posuerit, an sumpserit, in medio relinquo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041615Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041615Z">
        <seg>Therefore, I leave it undecided whether Rosa placed this iron plow of scapulae more sacredly or took it up.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde cum de felici Virginis transitu agetur infra, opportunus de his redibit sermo: neque enim unico capitulo tota Rosæ patientia potuit explicari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052900Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052900Z">
        <seg>Therefore, when the happy passage of the Virgin is treated of below, the discussion of these matters will be resumed; for the whole patience of the Rose could not be explained in a single chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde in se denuo retracta, cœpit &amp; ipsa laudare Deum, eo fervore, qui sensim Rosam abripuit in extasim, nec ab ea usque ad vesperam sese explicuit, cum mane, tribus circiter ante meridiem horis, aviculæ melodiam cœpisset auscultare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045050Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045050Z">
        <seg>Thus, drawing herself inward once more, she too began to praise God with such fervor that it gradually carried Rosa into ecstasy, and she did not come out of it until evening, having begun to listen to the little bird's melody in the morning, about three hours before noon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde nil mirum quod ([ut] postea innotuit) Rosam cetera intrepidam sæpius horror pavorque medullitus pervaserit ad unius sui tam immitis lectuli cogitatum: tremebat, æstuabat, sudabat misera, priusquam vel spondam attingeret, memor, quales ipsam expectarent ibidem plumæ, quot minaces cristæ arrigerentur illic in suam lanienam, quo sibi cruciatu distabescendum, quoties in alterum latus revolvendum esset lassum corpusculum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042638Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042638Z">
        <seg>Therefore, it is no wonder that (as later became known) horror and dread often pervaded the very marrow of the otherwise intrepid Rosa at the mere thought of her own so cruel little bed: the poor woman trembled, was on fire, and sweated before she even touched the pillow, remembering what feathers awaited her there, how many menacing quills would be pricked up there for her slaughter, what torments she would have to endure, how many times she would have to turn her weary little body to the other side;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde non diffideret vaticinio tantæ Virginis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012027Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012027Z">
        <seg>Therefore, he would not distrust the prophecy of such a Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde rationibus adortus est Virginem, ut omnino severissimum crudelis instrumenti usum dissuaderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042319Z">
        <seg>Therefore, he appealed to the Virgin on the grounds that she should entirely discourage the use of the most severe and cruel instrument.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde se sui pudere, quod tantis cæli beneficiis nondum, uti par erat, responderit: atque ex hoc capite, se creaturarum omnium, quotquot per orbem vivunt, merito ingratissimam esse reputandam, ex quo nimirum viderat, eo se modo tractari a Domino, quo ipse nonnisi amicissimos suos tractare solet, nec tamen se pro dignitate redamare tam amabilem, &amp; amantem Deum: &amp; forsan hic ei sensus erat, quando cum doloribus una augeri amorem flagitabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052714Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052714Z">
        <seg>Therefore she was ashamed of herself, that she had not yet responded to so many heavenly benefits as she ought; and from this, she rightly considered herself the most ungrateful of all creatures living throughout the world, since she had seen that the Lord treated her in the same way that he is accustomed to treat only his dearest friends, and yet she did not love so loving and amiable a God in a manner worthy of him; and perhaps this was her feeling when she prayed that, together with her pains, her love might be increased.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde videns, frustra se repugnare, nec fidem suis dictis haberi, tacuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045751Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045751Z">
        <seg>Therefore, seeing that he was fighting in vain and was not believed, he fell silent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prolixum foret singula membratim prosequi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050631Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050631Z">
        <seg>It would be too lengthy to go through each item in detail.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Promiserat Isabella de Catanno novitiarum magistra, se proxima quaque die curaturam, ut intromissis quatuor robustis mancipiis super dispositos lateres attolleretur moles, &amp; laxaretur tam Mariæ, quam cellulæ importuna angustia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040909Z">
        <seg>Isabella of Catanno, the novice mistress, had promised that on the very next day she would see to it that, with four strong slaves brought in, the heap would be lifted onto the prepared timbers and the oppressive narrowness of both Mary's cell and the cell itself would be eased.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Promisit Rosa, ut erat ad subveniendum proximis mire liberalis &amp; prompta, hominemque proficiscentem Deo, &amp; custodi angelo impense commendavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051837Z">
        <seg>Rosa promised, as she was wonderfully liberal and ready to help her neighbors, and she earnestly commended the man setting out to God and his guardian angel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prompte respondit Rosa, nil eam cum corporeis figuris habere commune, at longe minus cum colore, aut quavis creata pulchritudine;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052844Z">
        <seg>Rosa promptly replied that she had nothing in common with corporeal forms, but far less with color, or any created beauty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prosperrimum id propagandæ illic fidei nomen &amp; exordium fuit: nam simul in hoc templo, quod unicum tunc extabat, sub tutelari præsidio divæ Virginis, ad titulum S. Rosarii, constituta fuit parochia: hic primus instructis catechumenis patuit fons lustralis, quo infideles ad veri Numinis cultum adducti, cœpere per sacri baptismi januam intromitti ad caulam ovium Christi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054230Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054230Z">
        <seg>That was the most auspicious name and beginning for spreading the faith there: for at that time, in this one and only temple, under the protective patronage of the Blessed Virgin, dedicated to the title of St. of the Rosary, a parish was established: here the font was opened for the first time to the instructed catechumens, by which the infidels, brought to the worship of the true God, began to be admitted through the door of holy baptism into the fold of Christ's sheep;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prostabat in altari oratorii imago Jesu parvuli, venustis coloribus adumbrata: in hanc Virgo sæpissime jaciebat ardentes oculos;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054109Z">
        <seg>On the altar of the oratory stood an image of the infant Jesus, painted in charming colors; to this the Virgin would often turn her ardent gaze.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prosultu, cursuque flexuoso dextrorsum, lævorsum properabat minax animal, cauda rigide arrecta, spumante ore, boatu horrisono, naribus ira fumantibus, arenas calcibus jactabat in aëra, &amp; sicubi in truncum, pannum, aut studiose objectum pileolum offenderat, atroci &amp; crebra impactorum cornium vi infodiebat solo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062142Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062142Z">
        <seg>With a forward rush and a winding course to right and left, the menacing beast hurried on, its tail rigidly erect, mouth frothing, with a horrid bellow, nostrils smoking with rage, flinging sand into the air with its hooves, and whenever it struck a log, a rag, or a cap eagerly thrown in its path, it drove them into the ground with the cruel and repeated force of its horns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Protestabatur innocens Virgo, incassum hujusmodi sumptus, labores, tempus disperdi, malum suum nequaquam in corpore, sed intus in spiritu sævire ac dominari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045745Z">
        <seg>The innocent Virgin protested that such expenses, labors, and time were wasted in vain, that her evil did not rage and dominate in her body at all, but within, in her spirit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Protestabatur medicus, dissuadebant, rogabant amici, demum prælati regulares severissimis inhibitionibus addidere mulctas, præcepta, dirarum ac censurarum fulmina;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060750Z">
        <seg>The doctor protested, they dissuaded him, friends begged him; finally, the regular prelates added to the severest prohibitions fines, precepts, and the thunderbolts of anathemas and censures.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Provenit a vehementi refrigeratione cum quadam siccitate;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033635Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033635Z">
        <seg>It comes from a severe cold spell with some dryness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Provenit a verbo Græco&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;μαλάττω&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;quod&lt;/e3&gt; mollio &lt;e4&gt;significat: nam malagmate molliuntur apostemata &amp; matura fiunt&lt;/e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040153Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040153Z">
        <seg>It comes from the Greek word malatto, which means to soften: for with malagmate the apostemata are softened and become ripe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Provincialis commiserat) induta est veste bicolori, quam in Senensi magistra dilexerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040211Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040211Z">
        <seg>The provincial had taken pity on her; she was dressed in a two-colored garment that she had loved in her Sena teacher.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Provincialis, assumpta casula, cum ministris ad altare facturus accessit: phonasci &amp; cantores ex dispositis odeis symphoniæ modulos inchoarunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031456Z">
        <seg>The provincial, having put on his cassock, approached the altar with the ministers to celebrate Mass: the trumpeters and singers from the arranged choirs began the symphonic modulations;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Provocabat in se omnia mundi elementa ad vindicias injuriarum, quas eorum Creatori a se illatas querebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041241Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041241Z">
        <seg>He challenged all the elements of the world to avenge the wrongs he claimed they had done to their Creator.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proxima huic cura fuit, se palam ac devote S. Patris sui Dominici sobolem profiteri, unde candidum sui Ordinis intersigne, quod scapulare dicimus, foris super stragulas coram se continuo &amp; patule extensum videre ac videri voluit, tamquam proprium religiosæ militiæ vexillum, sub quo meruerat usque ad extremum spiritum: estque hæc vestis illa, qua Virgo Mater nascentem Fratrum Prædicatorum Ordinem, ceu pannis infantiæ, suismet manibus involverat&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030000Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030000Z">
        <seg>Her next concern was to profess openly and devoutly the offspring of her Holy Father Dominic, and so she willed to see and be seen extending before her, outside upon the blankets, the white insignia of her Order, which we call the scapular, as if it were the proper standard of her religious militia, under which she had merited until her last breath: and this is that very vestment with which the Virgin Mother had, as with swaddling clothes, wrapped the nascent Order of the Preachers in her own hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Præcipuam vero &amp; cognominem hujus insulæ urbem laudatus Baudrandus assignat his verbis:&lt;/e3&gt; Urbs Americæ Septemtrionalis episcopalis sub archiepiscopo sancti Dominici, munita cum arce duplici &amp; portu capaci in parte Septemtrionali insulæ cognominis in parte boreali.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T232141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035130Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the celebrated Baudrand assigns as the principal and eponymous city of this island the following words:&lt;/e3&gt; "A North American episcopal city under the Archbishop of Saint Dominic, fortified with a double fortress and a capacious harbor on the northern part of the eponymous island in the northern region."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Præcipue in majori Dominicæ Passionis hebdomada, a cruce fidelium adorationi palam exposita divelli non poterat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054841Z">
        <seg>Especially during the greater week of the Passion of the Lord, it could not be removed from the public adoration of the faithful;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Præcipue tamen sextis feriis, velut ex acetosa spongia cum Christo in cruce pransura, ex eodem felle &amp; panis crustula sibi componebat electuarium, mordaci aceto &amp; copiosis lachrymis stillatim superfusis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041234Z">
        <seg>Especially on Fridays, however, she would prepare for herself an electuary from the same gall and crusts of bread, as if to dine with Christ on the cross on a sponge soaked in vinegar, sprinkling it drop by drop with biting vinegar and copious tears.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Præcurrebat ultro filia, quasi numquam pedibus languisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034137Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034137Z">
        <seg>The daughter ran ahead eagerly, as if her feet had never grown weak.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prædicatores sacello invisunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054301Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054301Z">
        <seg>Preachers visit the chapel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prædicatorum SS. Rosario dicatum adiit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034730Z">
        <seg>He attended the convent of the Preachers of the Holy Rosary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prædicatorum celebrabantur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060016Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060016Z">
        <seg>The preachers were celebrated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prædicatorum religioso laïco) transmitti demum impetrarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051431Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051431Z">
        <seg>He had finally obtained the transmission of the religious preacher to the layman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prædixit, præsignatam virgulam, nulla fœcunditatis nota tunc turgidam, in honorem sanctissimæ Trinitatis tres flores garyophyllorum producturam, quibus sacram imaginem condecoraret eo tempore, quo tali florum genere regio illa careret, quod frustra diu quæsitum fuerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042229Z">
        <seg>He foretold that the predestined virgin, then swollen with no sign of fertility, would in honor of the most holy Trinity produce three carnations, with which she would adorn the sacred image at a time when that kingdom was devoid of such flowers, which had been sought in vain for a long time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Præparata mater advocat Rosam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043401Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043401Z">
        <seg>The prepared mother calls Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Præsentatus P. F. Nicolaus de Aguero in sua encyclica ad totam Ordinis Prædicatorum provinciam Peruanam (cui tunc uti Vicarius generalis præsidebat) scripta die primo Septembris MDCXVII, dum admirabilem Rosæ vitam, obitum, prodigia strictim suis denuntiat, inter alia refert, ad solum virginei corpusculi in feretro contactum quam plurimos repentino compunctionis stimulo ita fuisse perculsos, ut statim ibidem coram universa multitudine cœperint elatis vocibus detestari sua scelera, deplorare vitam anteactam, resolvi in gemitus ac singultus, humiliari coram Altissimo in lacrymosam criminum confessionem, audiente, spectante, mirante innumera circumstantium turba.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032510Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032510Z">
        <seg>In his encyclical letter to the entire Peruvian Province of the Order of Preachers, written on September 1, 1617, Father Francis Nicholas de Aguero—then presiding as Vicar General—strictly recounts the life, death, and miracles of the admirable Rose, among other things, that upon the mere contact of the virgin's little body in the coffin, the greatest number were so struck by a sudden spur of compunction that they immediately, right there before the entire multitude, began to denounce their sins in loud voices, to deplore their past life, to break into groans and sobs, and to humble themselves before the Most High in a tearful confession of their crimes, as the countless crowd of onlookers heard, saw, and marveled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Præsertim mane, dum instabat surgendi hora, incipiebant soporifera modicum operari, somnumque lentissimum vi attrahebant in cerebrum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051325Z">
        <seg>Especially in the morning, as the time to rise drew near, the soporifics would begin to take effect and would force the slowest sleep upon the brain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prævaluit tamen apud constantissimam puellam idea Seraphicæ magistræ suæ (ita virginem Senensem appellare consueverat) quam a teneris elegerat statu, habitu, moribus imitandam, prout diserte in Processu testis sexagesimus octavus asseverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040010Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040010Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, the idea of her Seraphic mistress (as she had grown accustomed to calling the Virgin of Siena) prevailed in the mind of the most steadfast maiden—one she had chosen from her earliest years to imitate in bearing, dress, and manners, as the sixty-eighth witness clearly affirmed in the Process.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Psal.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051546Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051546Z">
        <seg>Psalm</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Publicas urbis, aut regionis calamitates suis imputabat demeritis, ægritudines creberrimas, &amp; acutissimas acceptabat, &amp; amabat, seu vindices, ac justa supplicia suæ in Deum perpetuæ ingratitudinis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041238Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041238Z">
        <seg>He attributed the public calamities of the city or region to his own demerits, accepted the most frequent and most acute illnesses, and loved them as if they were the avengers and just punishments of his perpetual ingratitude toward God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pudebat Rosam adversario succumbere tam ignavo, pugnabat etiam cadendo, illidebat cervicem parieti, tundebat sibi latera, si quo modo extunderet irrepentem lethargum: vincebat tandem, at non sine agone;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043044Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043044Z">
        <seg>Rosam was ashamed to succumb to such a feeble adversary; she fought even as she fell, striking her neck against the wall, beating her sides to drive out the creeping lethargy. She was victorious in the end, but not without a struggle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Puduit generosam Virginem præsentis ibi luminis uti solatio, aut qualicumque præsidio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052051Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052051Z">
        <seg>It was shameful for the noble Virgin to use the light present there as a comfort, or as any kind of protection.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Puella, unico haustu facillime trajecto, statim liberrimo hiatu os diduxit: jamque bene sibi esse affirmavit, ac cibum petiit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035427Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035427Z">
        <seg>The girl, having taken a single, very easy gulp, immediately opened her mouth in a wide yawn; she then assured them she was fine and asked for food.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pulchrius tamen eadem tot ægrorum beneficio ubique emicuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040308Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040308Z">
        <seg>Yet more beautifully did the same thing shine everywhere for the benefit of so many sick people.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Putabatur hæc prima omnium consensura, sive ob tanti archiepiscopi reverentiam, sive quod onusta prolibus, Filiam sine dote posset decentissime accommodare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040041Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040041Z">
        <seg>This was thought to be the first thing everyone would agree to, either out of reverence for such a great archbishop, or because, being burdened with children, she could most fittingly provide a dowryless daughter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Qua de re infra loco proprio cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061941Z">
        <seg>On this matter, see the proper place below, chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Qua in re, ut humanum effugeret plausum, nec de singularitate notari posset, singulis his diebus studiosissime variabat horam, nunc maturius nunc serius ad sacram Communionem accedendo: cælestis enim thesaurus (monente Gregorio) absconditur, ut servetur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055712Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055712Z">
        <seg>In this matter, to avoid human applause and not be noticed for his singularity, he most diligently varied the hour on each of these days, approaching the sacred Communion now earlier, now later: for the heavenly treasure (as Gregory advises) is hidden, so that it may be preserved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Qua vero animi demissione tunc ad circumstantes conversa, petiverit condonari sibi, quidquid forsitan in eam domum aut familiam, seu pravo exemplo, seu tetricis ac inofficiosis moribus deliquisset, longiorem exigeret periodum, nisi ad reliqua foret properandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040720Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040720Z">
        <seg>With what humility she then turned to those present, asking that whatever she might have wronged in that household or family—whether by a bad example or by gloomy and ungracious conduct—be forgiven her, provided it did not require a longer period than was necessary to attend to the remaining matters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quadam vice inter suspiria, exciderunt Virgini (at perfunctorie, &amp; submisse) hæc verba: “Nosti utique, mater mea dulcissima, quod si XV aut XVI pataconum&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; mihi facultas esset, vestirem te, pro genii mei dictamine, novo candidiori amictu”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011224Z">
        <seg>At one point, between sighs, the Virgin let these words slip (though perfunctorily and in a low voice): "You know full well, my sweetest mother, that if I had the means of fifteen or sixteen patacons, I would clothe you, by the dictate of my genius, in a new, whiter garment."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quadam vice inter suspiria, exciderunt Virgini (at perfunctorie, &amp; submisse) hæc verba: “Nosti utique, mater mea dulcissima, quod si XV aut XVI pataconum&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; mihi facultas esset, vestirem te, pro genii mei dictamine, novo candidiori amictu”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055042Z">
        <seg>At one point, between sighs, these words escaped the Virgin (but perfunctorily and in a low voice): "You know full well, my sweetest mother, that if I had the means of fifteen or sixteen patacons, I would, according to the dictates of my genius, clothe you in a new, whiter garment."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quadriennis erat, cum tenellos humeros (quibus flagellandis tunc adhuc deerant rudi puellæ funiculi) incepit vexare nunc crudorum laterum ponderibus, nunc gravis trunci onere, velut ex tunc conata bajulare crucem post Christum, sub infamis ligni molestia laborantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041622Z">
        <seg>He was four years old when he began to torment his tender shoulders—shoulders that then still lacked the rough girls' whips—now with the weight of raw sides, now with the burden of a heavy trunk, as if from that moment he had set out to carry the cross after Christ, laboring under the infamous wood's vexation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quam bene mellifluus Bernardus in suo exclamat jubilo: “Qui te gustant esuriunt”!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055656Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055656Z">
        <seg>How sweetly Bernard exclaims in his joyful exultation: "Those who taste you hunger!"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quam doluit Rosa, pro illa vice maternum sibi vultum ereptum fuisse, dum cunctabatur!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051415Z">
        <seg>How Rosa regretted that, on that occasion, the maternal face had been snatched from her while she hesitated!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quam putas luctabatur hic cum obedientia vani ornatus pudicus horror!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035306Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035306Z">
        <seg>How do you suppose he struggled here with the obedience of vain, modest adornment—horror!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quamquam permodici hujus capillamenti in sincipite residui alius quoque usus fuerit, ad nova dolorum incrementa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042124Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042124Z">
        <seg>Although the very small remainder of this hair on the crown had been used for something else, it was also used for new intensifications of pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quamvis hæc regio fere urbibus careat, tamen episcopali dignitate insignitur sub archiepiscopo de Plata, &amp; ejus episcopus in oppido Assumptionis commoratur.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032433Z">
        <seg>Although this region is almost devoid of cities, it is nevertheless distinguished by episcopal dignity under the Archbishop of Plata, and its bishop resides in the town of Assumption.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quando in isto Rosarii sacello Rosa coram Virgineo hoc simulacro Dominicani Ordinis habitum suscepit, adfuit publicæ functioni plorans mater, viditque filiam, blando favore Deiparæ elevari in cælum, unde perfusa gaudio facile compescuit lachrymas, cœpitque tacito applausu felici puellæ gratulari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054326Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054326Z">
        <seg>When in this chapel of the Rosary Rosa, before the Virgin, received the habit of this Order of the Dominicans, her mother was present at the public ceremony weeping, and saw her daughter, by the gentle favor of the Mother of God, being lifted up to heaven; where, filled with joy, she easily restrained her tears and began to congratulate the happy maiden with silent applause.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quando obedientiæ vi ad carnis esum cogebatur, duris symptomatibus torqueri visa est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041141Z">
        <seg>When by force of obedience she was compelled to eat meat, she was seen to be tormented by severe symptoms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quantulum enim (cogitabat) perfectionis gradum spatio vitæ tam exiguæ assequi potuit fragilis puella, annum ætatis trigesimum primum vix superegressa?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032935Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032935Z">
        <seg>For how much perfection (she thought) could a frail girl, barely having passed the age of thirty-one, achieve in so short a span of life?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quantulum est, quod huic donavit animalculo naturæ Auctor, &amp; en totis præcordiis, totis viribus, ei rependit laudis tributum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045038Z">
        <seg>How little the Author of nature has given to this little creature, and yet with its whole heart and all its strength, it repays a tribute of praise to Him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quantum hæ scruporum sub stragulo extantium dissitæ cotes jacentem Rosam dispunxerint, facilis conjectura est, sane in ipsis solidis asseribus conspicue apparuerunt impastinata lapillorum vestigia, ipsæ tabulæ scrobiculatim hinc inde extritæ protestabantur satis, quam molliter his incubuerit macilenta Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042539Z">
        <seg>It is easy to guess how many of the pebbles from the scree beneath the blanket pierced the lying Rose; indeed, on the solid planks themselves the traces of the stones were clearly visible, and the boards themselves, worn away here and there in little hollows, testified amply how gently the gaunt Rose had lain upon them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quapropter Castellus in Lexico medico pag. 398&lt;/e0&gt; cacochymiam &lt;e1&gt;in humano corpore, quasi&lt;/e1&gt; succorum vitium &lt;e2&gt;dici affirmat&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034401Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034401Z">
        <seg>Therefore, Castellus in the medical lexicon, page 398, affirms that cacochymia in the human body is, as it were, a vitiation of the humors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quare Majores nostri die 24 Februarii hanc virginem inter prætermissos collocarunt, donec quis legitimum sanctitatis ejus titulum probaverit.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031803Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031803Z">
        <seg>Why our ancestors on February 24 placed this virgin among the omitted, until someone proves her legitimate title to sanctity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quare suspicor, hic&lt;/e0&gt; dentilum &lt;e1&gt;pro denticulo scriptum vel usurpatum esse, quia flores e scapis acuminati prodeunt instar parvorum dentium seu denticulorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055538Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055538Z">
        <seg>Therefore I suspect that here a denticle has been written or used in place of a small tooth, because the flowers emerge from the stems in a pointed fashion like little teeth or tiny teeth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quarto fibulas apponunto 600 Salutationes (ut supra) in reverentiam intimi gaudii, dum benedictum Filiolum suum offerebat in templo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054621Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054621Z">
        <seg>Fourth, let us present 600 greetings (as above) in reverence of the innermost joy, while she offered her blessed little Son in the temple.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quatriduum effluxerat, quando applicitæ curæ recordata mater, silentem Filiam interrogavit, qui valeret, quidve egisset de pellium involucris?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035511Z">
        <seg>Four days had passed when, remembering the care she had given, the mother asked her silent daughter how she was and what she had done with the bundles of skins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quatuor continuis horis &amp; ultra duravit pium ostentum, cunctis per gyrum admirantibus, quod nullo colorum aut venustatis detrimento, vultus ille sacerrimus, post sudores pulchrior, vividiorque solito appareret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053852Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053852Z">
        <seg>The pious spectacle lasted for four consecutive hours and beyond, with everyone in the crowd marveling that, without any loss of color or beauty, that most sacred face appeared more beautiful and more vivid than usual after the sweat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quatuor horis duravit pium ostentum, cunctis adstantibus admiratione &amp; sancto horrore stupentibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042136Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042136Z">
        <seg>The pious spectacle lasted for four hours, with all those present stunned with admiration and holy awe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Questa est Virgini jacturam suam, quodque domum rediens, aut arcam vestiariam, cujus clavis subducta erat, vi effringere, aut in diem posterum aliunde necessariam vestem mutuo petere cogebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012712Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012712Z">
        <seg>This is a virgin's loss: that upon returning home, she was forced either to break into her chest of clothes, whose key had been taken away, or to borrow the necessary garments from elsewhere for the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Qui Rosam viderant adeo cælestium avidam, adeo spiritualibus exercitiis, solitudini, macerationi corporis addictam, &amp; seculi pertæsam, suadebant identidem alicujus monasterii ingressum, quo liberius vacaret Deo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040100Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040100Z">
        <seg>Those who had seen Rosa so eager for heavenly things, so devoted to spiritual exercises, solitude, and the mortification of the body, and so weary of the world, repeatedly urged her to enter a monastery, where she could more freely devote herself to God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Qui conscientiæ Virginis præerant, tribus circiter ante illius obitum annis consideraverant, ipsam viribus nimium attenuari, corpusculum tot flagris, jejuniis, morbis exhaustum, arduo tam asperi lecti supplicio non amplius par fore, &amp;, si fomenta illi persuaderi nequeant, saltem tormenta debili aut subtrahenda esse, aut moderanda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042849Z">
        <seg>Those who were in charge of the Virgin's conscience had, about three years before her death, observed that she was being unduly weakened by her own exertions: her little body, exhausted by so many scourges, fasts, and illnesses, was no longer fit for the ordeal of such a harsh bed, and that, if she could not be persuaded to accept the remedies, the torments should at least be eased or removed, or their severity reduced.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Qui remotius substiterant, defixi hærebant in niveo &amp; gratioso Rosæ vultu, quem florida corona, velique candor venustissime commendabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030409Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030409Z">
        <seg>Those who stood farther off were fixed, gazing at the snow-white and graceful face of the Rose, which a flowering crown and the whiteness of her veil most charmingly commended.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Qui servum familiarius noverant, accessere propius gratulabundi, nec dedignati, osculis fatigare manum beneficio Rosæ jam patulam &amp; redivivam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043212Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043212Z">
        <seg>Those who knew the servant more intimately came closer, congratulating him and not deigning to tire his hand with kisses, now that it was open and restored by Rose's kindness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Qui virgam Aaronis florere jussit, providit &amp; nobis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055206Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055206Z">
        <seg>He who commanded the rod of Aaron to blossom, also provided for us.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quia amore langueo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051635Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051635Z">
        <seg>Because I am sick with love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quibus sublatis, Lucia amplissimis suis facultatibus cœnobium fundavit, dotavit, ac denique se ipsam dedit, cum sacro velo nomen sororis Luciæ de sanctissima Trinitate assumens, ac prima novelli sui asceterii præfecturam gessit, religionis spiritum mirifice propagavit, demumque sanctimoniæ fama celebris obdormivit in Domino.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042623Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042623Z">
        <seg>Having laid these aside, Lucia, with her abundant resources, founded and endowed a convent, and finally gave herself to it, taking the sacred veil and the name of Sister Lucia of the Most Holy Trinity, and, as the first superior of her new ascetic community, she wonderfully spread the spirit of religion, and at last, celebrated for her sanctity, she fell asleep in the Lord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid ageret sub his tormentis Virgo tam divulsa in se, quam avulsa a Dilecto?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045549Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045549Z">
        <seg>What would the Virgin do under these torments, so torn within herself as to be wrenched away from her Beloved?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid cogitabunt, quid dicent obvii, hærere te in plateis, ut transversas ubique stipulas, statuo&lt;a0&gt;*&lt;/a0&gt; labore divarices?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011159Z">
        <seg>What will they think, what will they say, the passersby, that you stand rooted in the streets, like a crossroads of sticks everywhere, I decree, your thighs gaping from labor?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid cogitabunt, quid dicent obvii, hærere te in plateis, ut transversas ubique stipulas, statuo&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;*&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; labore divarices?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054906Z">
        <seg>What will they think, what will they say, the passersby, that you stand in the streets, spreading your thighs everywhere, straining your groin with your labors?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid cunctamini?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055203Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055203Z">
        <seg>What are you waiting for?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid egisti?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035542Z">
        <seg>What did you do?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid enim tenuium parentum inops filia partiri aliis indigentibus potuit, vix sibi, alendisque genitoribus sufficiens?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061017Z">
        <seg>For what could the poor daughter of poor parents share with other needy people, when she herself had barely enough to support herself and her parents?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid hic remedii inter saxum &amp; sacrum?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051108Z">
        <seg>What remedy is here between rock and sacred?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid hæsitatis?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055200Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055200Z">
        <seg>What are you hesitating about?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid multa?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051022Z">
        <seg>What's the matter?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quidam ex Virginis confessariis dono tam inæstimabili tacitus adstupuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061956Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061956Z">
        <seg>One of the Virgin's confessors stood speechless, dumbfounded by such an inestimable gift.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quidam vir religiosus diris ejus torminibus compatiens hortabatur pati fortiter, terminum jam in prospectu esse, hoc æstu animam feliciter purgari a cruditate, ut Sponsi palatum æternum oblectet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030111Z">
        <seg>A certain devout man, commiserating with his dreadful torments, urged him to endure bravely, saying that the end was now in sight and that by this heat the soul would be happily purged of its crudeness, so that it might delight the eternal palate of the Bridegroom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quidquid hortabatur proximos, quidquid inculcabat, unum orationis studium erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045056Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045056Z">
        <seg>Whatever he urged on his neighbors, whatever he impressed upon them, his sole aim in speaking was one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quidquid sit de certa horum nominum significatione, potuisset scriptor Actorum supersedere plerisque his synonymis, quæ non satis apte angelis tribui videntur.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052333Z">
        <seg>Whatever the precise meaning of these names may be, the author of the Acts could have omitted most of these synonyms, which do not seem to be sufficiently appropriate to be attributed to angels.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quidquid sit hac de re, teste Gorræo in Definitionibus medicis pag. 157&lt;/e2&gt;, &lt;s3&gt;ἐρυσίπελας&lt;/s3&gt; est tumor præter naturam in cute, a sanguine &amp; flava bile calidioribus, vel a sanguine fervente tenuique proveniens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035223Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035223Z">
        <seg>Whatever this matter may be, according to Gorræus in his Medical Definitions, page 157, erysipelas is an unnatural swelling of the skin, arising from blood and yellow bile, which are warmer, or from boiling, thin blood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quidquid sit, sensus hoc loco satis indicat, pro quo vexillo vox ea hic usurpetur.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030744Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030744Z">
        <seg>Whatever it may be, the sense in this place sufficiently indicates for which standard that word is here used.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quidquid sit, ut herba illa apud varias nationes ex nomine suo cognoscatur, Hispani eam&lt;/e3&gt; hiniesta &lt;e4&gt;vel&lt;/e4&gt; retama, &lt;e5&gt;Galli&lt;/e5&gt; genest, &lt;e6&gt;Itali&lt;/e6&gt; ginestra, &lt;e7&gt;Germani&lt;/e7&gt; pfriemen &lt;e8&gt;&amp; Belgæ&lt;/e8&gt; brem &lt;e9&gt;appellant&lt;/e9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033629Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033629Z">
        <seg>Whatever the case, so that this plant may be known by its name among various nations, the Spaniards call it hiniesta or retama, The French call it genest, the Italians ginestra, the Germans pfriemen, and the Belgians brem.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quiescebat in cunis trimestris parvula, quando cum ancillula &amp; filiabus accita mater conspexit filiolæ vultum undique opertum repanda, sed paulo post disparente rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034649Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034649Z">
        <seg>The little three-month-old lay resting in her cradle when, summoned with the handmaid and the daughters, her mother came and saw her daughter's face covered all over with a rash, though the rose soon vanished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quin &amp; pluries sub ejuscemodi visione Rosa suaviter affata est spectatorem suum, multa ac sublimia illi edisserens de felicitatis suæ excelso fastigio, quæ humanæ linguæ idiomate efferri non possunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031924Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031924Z">
        <seg>Quin, and on several occasions under such a vision, Rosa gently addressed her spectator, unfolding to him many sublime things about the lofty pinnacle of her happiness, which cannot be expressed in the idiom of human language.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quin &amp; quatuor ante obitum mensibus quæstoris Gundisalvi uxori atrocissimos suæ extremæ infirmitatis dolores futuros narravit, &amp; immanissimam ei atque ardentissimam sitim instare prænuntiavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042713Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042713Z">
        <seg>Five months before his death, the treasurer Gundisalvus told his wife of the most atrocious pains of his final illness and foretold that an immense and burning thirst would assail her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quin &amp; usque ad animam transiit energia saluberrimæ correctionis: nam mens illa ad sobrietatem mirabiliter revocata, cœpit tractare attentius æternæ salutis negotium, emundare conscientiam, reformare ignavos mores, veternum excutere, gratulantibus cunctis, qui umquam antea in tabe illius vitii putrescentem defleverant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060812Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060812Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the energy of the most salutary correction penetrated to the very soul: for that mind, wonderfully restored to sobriety, began to attend more diligently to the business of eternal salvation, to purify its conscience, to reform its cowardly habits, to shake off its old sluggishness, to the joy of all who had ever before lamented its decay in the corruption of that vice.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quin potius cum primoribus Societatis jam pertractata, conclusa, parata erant omnia, solæ consensuri P. Provincialis expectabantur litteræ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012637Z">
        <seg>Rather, since everything had already been discussed, concluded, and prepared with the Society's superiors, only the letter of consent from the Father Provincial was awaited.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quin potius omnis undequaque tum ratio, tum regiminis potestas videbatur inexorabiliter obstitura, præsertim cum pro conditione temporis abunde sat monasteriorum esset in civitate novella, cujus tunc ætas primam annorum centuriam nondum expleverat, plurimumque adhuc distabat ab ea, qua hodie tumet, amplitudinis vastitate.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062619Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062619Z">
        <seg>Rather, every consideration and the authority of the government everywhere seemed inexorably opposed, especially since, given the times, there were already more than enough monasteries in the new city, which then had not yet completed its first century and was still a long way from the vast extent it now boasts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quin tu potius apud hanc seraphicam matrem, qua polles &amp; gratia &amp; fiducia, vitam ac valetudinem ancillæ meæ exora”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055234Z">
        <seg>Rather, you, who are endowed with both grace and confidence by this seraphic mother, pray for the life and health of my handmaid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quin tu potius memineris pretiosum hunc liquorem deberi soli Deo in ablutionem nostrorum peccatorum”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053405Z">
        <seg>Rather, you should remember that this precious liquid is owed to God alone for the washing away of our sins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quin usque adeo menti Virginis insederat sanctæ crucis religio, ut, ubicumque a fortuita trabium commissura, in fenestrarum clathris, in decussatis sepium bacillis, in ostiorum cardinibus, in vectibus, in ipsis demum festucis ac paleis, aut alio quovis rejectaneo figura crucis occurrisset, hanc sine attenta ac tacita veneratione nusquam præteriret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054847Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054847Z">
        <seg>For the devotion to the holy cross had so taken possession of the Virgin's mind, that wherever she encountered the figure of a cross, whether from the chance meeting of beams, in the bars of windows, in the crossed rods of hedges, on door hinges, on beams, or even in the very straws and poles, or wherever she encountered the figure of a cross on any other scrap or refuse, she would nowhere pass it by without a careful and silent veneration.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quin vulgaris tota urbe fremebat querimonia, sacrum Virginis corpusculum loco nimis abdito situm esse, ubi ob clausuræ leges, nec omnibus horis, nec ab omni sexu visitari poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031339Z">
        <seg>Indeed, a common complaint was heard throughout the city that the sacred little body of the Virgin was kept in a place too hidden, where, because of the laws of enclosure, it could not be visited at all hours or by people of every sex.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quindecenni puellæ Catharinæ Indæ febrilis æstus omnem ademerat sensum, loquelam, &amp; spem vitæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040047Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040047Z">
        <seg>A feverish heat had deprived the fifteen-year-old girl Catherine of India of all sense, speech, and hope of life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quingentorum coronatorum censum annuum Guamangensi sanctæ Claræ monasterio rite persolverant, ac de more apocham solutionis testem ab œconomo receperant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033951Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033951Z">
        <seg>They had duly paid the annual tribute of five hundred crowns to the Monastery of Saint Clare in Guamanga, and, as was the custom, had received from the steward a receipt as proof of payment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quinimo de illorum consensu, bis quot hebdomadis puella reficiebatur divino hoc pabulo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055647Z">
        <seg>Rather, with their consent, the girl was nourished twice a week with this divine food.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quinimo erga locupletissimum hunc Sponsum ea erat Rosæ fiducia, ut ausu liberali Virgo pauperrima frequenter &amp; palam in se receperit providere de toto sumptu ad excitandum a fundamentis novum sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis monasterium, dummodo in diebus suis appelleret, quod solum tunc deerat, regiæ permissionis diploma.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062315Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062315Z">
        <seg>Indeed, Rose's confidence in this exceedingly wealthy sponsor was such that, with a generous spirit, the very poor virgin frequently and openly accepted from him the provision of the entire cost for erecting from the foundations a new monastery of Saint Catherine of Siena, provided that in her lifetime she obtained the only thing then lacking—a royal permission diploma.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quinto collari impenduntor 600 Salutationes, &lt;b3&gt;Salve&lt;/b3&gt;, jejunia (ut supra) in reverentiam felicissimi gaudii, dum amissum triduo Filium invenit in templo inter doctores disputantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011140Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011140Z">
        <seg>On the fifth step they offer 600 greetings, "Hail," and fasts (as above) in reverence of the most blessed joy, while they find their Son, who had been missing for three days, in the temple among the doctors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quinto collari impenduntor 600 Salutationes, &lt;b6&gt;Salve&lt;/b6&gt;, jejunia (ut supra) in reverentiam felicissimi gaudii, dum amissum triduo Filium invenit in templo inter doctores disputantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054624Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054624Z">
        <seg>On the fifth step they offer 600 greetings, "Hail," and fasts (as above) in reverence of the most blessed joy, when she found her Son, who had been missing for three days, in the temple among the doctors disputing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quis credet videri denique potuisse dignum hujuscemodi pratum, in quo Rosa quiesceret?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042620Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042620Z">
        <seg>Who would believe that it was finally possible to see a meadow worthy of this kind, in which Rosa could rest?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quis enim perciperet laxari in hunc clamorem poros, nisi cum sudant?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053923Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053923Z">
        <seg>For who would perceive their pores opening to this clamor unless they were sweating?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quis porro netrici inter hæc solatia fuerit sensus lætitiæ, æstiment, quibus vacat: plus miror, tunc Rosæ oculos superfuisse, quibus filum, acum, telam videret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050929Z">
        <seg>Let those who have the leisure judge what sense of joy the midwife might have had amid these consolations: I am more amazed that Rosa's eyes were still intact, able to see the thread, the needle, the web.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quis pudor!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053046Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053046Z">
        <seg>What a shame!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quis tot tamque seria exspectasset e ludo ac luto?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035012Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035012Z">
        <seg>Who would have expected so many serious things from play and mud?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quo animo id acceperit Filia pœnitentiæ, quam vitæ studiosior, non facile dixerim;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042855Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042855Z">
        <seg>I could hardly say what spirit the Daughter of Penance, more eager for life, received it in.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quo evulgato, plures alii advenientes prodigium non solum eminus, sed etiam cominus sic se habere conspexerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042958Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042958Z">
        <seg>When this became public, many others who came saw the prodigy not only from a distance but also up close.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod Rosa bibendi vices in triduum aut quatriduum differre sit solita, &lt;s2&gt;[Ceterum minimos matris nutus observans,]&lt;/s2&gt; ex eo patuit, quod legem sibimet indixerat, dum parentibus cohabitabat, numquam bibere, nisi quoties veniam rogata mater expresse annuisset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035725Z">
        <seg>That Rosa was accustomed to postpone her drinking for three or four days, [nevertheless observing her mother's slightest wishes,] was evident from the fact that she had imposed a rule upon herself, while living with her parents, to never drink unless her mother, when asked for permission, had expressly consented.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod Rosa bibendi vices in triduum aut quatriduum differre sit solita, [Ceterum minimos matris nutus observans,] ex eo patuit, quod legem sibimet indixerat, dum parentibus cohabitabat, numquam bibere, nisi quoties veniam rogata mater expresse annuisset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000915Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000915Z">
        <seg>That Rosa was accustomed to postpone her drinking for three or four days at a time [nevertheless observing her mother's slightest wishes], was evident from the fact that she had imposed a law upon herself, while living with her parents, to never drink unless her mother, when asked for permission, had expressly consented.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod autem idonea careret dote, replicabat quæstor, se curam hanc totam sibi deposcere, acturum se omnia, ut a religiosissimo monasterio haud gravate admitteretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040255Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040255Z">
        <seg>But when he lacked the necessary endowment, the treasurer would reply that he was entrusting this entire matter to him and would do everything to secure his admission to the most devout monastery without difficulty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod hoc miræ immunitatis privilegium secessus ille solitarius e cælo possederit, bene est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043937Z">
        <seg>That this marvelous privilege of immunity was possessed by that solitary retreat from heaven, is well;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod si Mariana conabatur parcere, aut, non nisi obiter, imperata exsequi, acrius instabat supplex Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040515Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040515Z">
        <seg>But if Mariana tried to be sparing, or, only in passing, to carry out the orders, Rosa, as a supplicant, pressed her more sharply.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod si aliquando æger ventriculus buccellam exosam nulla vi potuisset eluctari, paroxysmus&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; erat in foribus, ac nauseantem ardentissima febris invadebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203528Z">
        <seg>If ever the sick stomach could not by any force rid itself of the loathsome morsel, a paroxysm was at the door, and the most burning fever would seize the one who was nauseous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod si aliquando æger ventriculus buccellam exosam nulla vi potuisset eluctari, paroxysmus&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; erat in foribus, ac nauseantem ardentissima febris invadebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041039Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041039Z">
        <seg>If ever the sick stomach could not by any force rid itself of the loathsome morsel, a paroxysm was at the door, and the most burning fever would seize the one who was nauseous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod si hac ratione crucis figuræ prospectum cupis, utique ingentem tibi sumpsisti operam, imo ridiculam, cui a prætereuntium sannis ac sarcasmis totum sit præmium”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054909Z">
        <seg>If, by this method, you desire a view of the figure of the cross, you have certainly undertaken a tremendous—nay, a ridiculous—labor, the sole reward of which is the jeers and sarcasm of passersby.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod si in aliena manu tanta potuit nuptialis Rosæ anulus, ipsæmet nuptiæ quid non potuerint in Sponsæ anima?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044715Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044715Z">
        <seg>If in another's hand the bridal ring of Rose could do so much, what could not the marriage itself do in the soul of the Bride?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod si nunc morerer, bis mihi moriendum foret: nam, qui ultimo lethum mihi allaturus est dies, procul distat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013136Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013136Z">
        <seg>For if I were to die now, I would have to die twice: for the day that will bring me my final death is still far off.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod si ob gratiam nuperrimi miraculi pictura illa hinc auferenda est, totum simul oratorium cum reliquis ejusdem sanctis iconibus auferant necesse est, quia pleræque miris in nos beneficiis certant, scatentque arcanis prodigiis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054103Z">
        <seg>"If, because of the favor of the most recent miracle, that painting must be removed from here, then the entire oratory, together with the other holy icons, must also be taken away, for they all compete in bestowing wondrous benefits upon us and abound with secret prodigies."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod si tantum in aliena, quantum in ipsa Sponsæ anima divinæ illæ nuptiæ valuerunt!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041656Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041656Z">
        <seg>For just as much as those divine nuptials availed in the soul of the Bride herself, so too did they avail in the soul of another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod ubi advertit Rosa, “Ne (inquit) metuas, soror;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043925Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043925Z">
        <seg>When Rosa turned and saw it, she said, "Do not be afraid, sister;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod ubi certius rescivit, ante omnia sategit explorare divinæ voluntatis beneplacitum, suæque vocationis mysterium supremo Arbitro cernua commendavit, non recusans pro asylo suæ virginitatis, &amp; coævo sibi strictioris vitæ desiderio arctissimam oblati cœnobii clausuram, arduumque vivendi genus amplecti;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040032Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040032Z">
        <seg>When she learned this for certain, she first and foremost sought to discern the divine will's good pleasure, and she commended the mystery of her vocation to the supreme Arbiter, not refusing, for the sake of the asylum of her virginity and her desire for a stricter life, to embrace the most strict enclosure of the offered convent and the arduous way of living.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod vero conjugum sit relinquere patrem &amp; matrem, ut sint duo in carne una, monuit Rosam suam Neonymphus, alendorum parentum curam posthac ipsa deponeret, aliunde se illis provisurum, ne quo egeant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044157Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044157Z">
        <seg>And Neonymphe, admonishing his wife Rosa that it is proper for a married couple to leave father and mother and become two in one flesh, told her to henceforth lay aside the care of supporting her parents, saying that he would otherwise provide for them so that they would lack for nothing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod vultu adeo sereno &amp; læto protulit, ut appareret vere Virgo prudens &amp; sapiens, in vestibulo paradisi exspectans cum lampade, ut dilectus ejus Sponsus veniret, cujus in intimo cordis vocantis vocem sentiens, candelam benedictam nutu petens, crucis signaculo frontem, os &amp; pectus communivit, mandavitque subtrahi cervical, ut capite ad nudum lignum inclinato sentiret in cruce se mori.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042859Z">
        <seg>Which she did with such a serene and joyful countenance, that she appeared truly a prudent and wise Virgin, waiting in the vestibule of paradise with her lamp, for her beloved Bridegroom to come, whose voice, feeling in the inmost depths of her heart, requesting the blessed candle with a nod, she marked her forehead, mouth, and breast with the sign of the cross, and commanded the head-rest to be removed, so that, inclining her head to the bare wood, she might feel herself to die on the cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quodam vespere cubitum ituram domesticæ feminæ cæliti Rosæ commendarunt, &amp; particulam virginei indumenti fascia inter oculos puellæ alligarunt, sicque fasciatam composuere in lectulo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034640Z">
        <seg>One evening, they entrusted the household maid to the heavenly Rose as she was about to go to bed, and tied a piece of the virgin's garment as a band between the girl's eyes, thus arranging the bandaged girl on the little bed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quodque admirere magis, a prima pueritia hoc insigni munere dotata fuit, quo sibi &amp; aliis mirifice profuit, ut nulla umquam Virgini occurrerit tam fallax adversarii tentatio, nulla tam fraudulenta seductoris vafricies, quam non clare, statimque primo in limine deprehenderit, arguerit, exarmarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052234Z">
        <seg>What is even more admirable is that from early childhood she was endowed with this remarkable gift, by which she benefited herself and others so wonderfully that she never encountered a Virgin who faced a more deceitful temptation from an adversary, nor a more fraudulent cunning from a seducer, than she did not at once and clearly perceive, expose, and disarm at the very threshold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quodque mirabilius est, ab eo tempore, quoad Rosa supervixit, ejus hortulo nulla anni parte recentia caryophylla defuerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055213Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055213Z">
        <seg>What is even more remarkable is that, for as long as Rosa lived, her garden never lacked fresh carnations at any time of the year.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quodque sat mirabile videbatur, inter hos impolitos saxorum cumulos &amp; pulverulentas operas, vestis erat singulis virginibus, non plebeia ex more opificii, sed pretiosa, fulgida, solemnis, quæ nuptiale convivium, aut publicam theatri pompam saperet magis, quam sudoriferam officinam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044216Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044216Z">
        <seg>It seemed all the more marvelous that, amid these rough heaps of stones and dusty works, each virgin wore a garment not of plebeian, artisan-made cloth, but a precious, gleaming, and solemn one, more fitting for a wedding feast or a public theatrical pageant than for a sweat-drenched workshop.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quomodo autem&lt;/e5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;κεφαλαία&lt;/s6&gt; &lt;e7&gt;&amp;&lt;/e7&gt; &lt;s8&gt;κεφαλαλγία&lt;/s8&gt; &lt;e9&gt;inter se differant, apud Gorræum, Castellum, aliosque medicos obvios invenitur&lt;/e9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035204Z">
        <seg>However, how&lt;e5&gt;κεφαλαία&lt;/e5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;and&lt;/s6&gt; &lt;e7&gt;κεφαλαλγία&lt;/e7&gt; &lt;e9&gt;differ from one another is found in Gorræus, Castellus, and other physicians&lt;/e9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quondam post lachrymarum prolixa diluvia capitis exinanitione languebat non sine discrimine, nec tamen solitis moderabatur vigiliis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035706Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035706Z">
        <seg>Once, after prolonged floods of tears, he languished from a headache not without danger, nor was he soothed by his usual vigils.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoquo se verteret, prævidebat aut virium defectu, aut sumptione cujuscumque fomenti impeditum iri, ne postridie sacra Communione frui liceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051105Z">
        <seg>Whichever way he turned, he foresaw that either a lack of strength or the taking of any nourishment would prevent him from partaking of Holy Communion the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quot, quantaque mysteria, ex unica hac visione didicerit Rosa, particulatim apparebit in sequentibus: hic ad solius cum Lapicida depacti sponsalitii eventum festinat historia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044229Z">
        <seg>How many and what mysteries Rosa learned from this single vision will appear in detail in what follows; here the story hastens to the outcome of the betrothal arranged solely with the Mason.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoties Rosa sibi aut aliis grande aliquod ac peculiare e cælo subsidium postulare constituisset, supplex ad aram sanctissimi Rosarii procumbebat in preces, illic vultum charæ imaginis speculabatur attentissima, &amp; non sine ingenti fiducia post teneros affatus, ab illius faciei pendebat oraculo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054335Z">
        <seg>Whenever Rosa resolved to ask for some great and special help from heaven for herself or for others, she would fall prostrate at the altar of the Most Holy Rosary in prayer, there she would gaze most attentively at the face of the beloved image, and not without great confidence, after tender words, would hang upon its face for an oracle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoties a matre præsensit, se invitandam ad visitationis obsequia, clam Indico pipere, quod mordacissimum est, cilia palpebrasque sibi oblinebat, quo quidem affrictu mox ardebant oculi, rubebant, stillabant, velut si acris e capite defluxio eos suffudisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043336Z">
        <seg>Whenever she foresaw that her mother would invite her to a visitation, she would secretly smear her eyelashes and eyelids with Indian pepper, which is the most pungent, so that by this very rubbing her eyes would soon burn, redden, and stream, as if a bitter discharge from her head had suffused them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoties ad interiora meridionalis Americæ montana oculos vertebat, cruciabatur me dullitus, illachrymans perditioni tot barbarorum, qui inaccessas illas post nivosa juga valles populabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042322Z">
        <seg>Whenever he turned his eyes to the interior mountain regions of southern America, he was tormented by grief, weeping over the fate of so many barbarians who populated those inaccessible valleys beyond the snowy ridges.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoties aut nimiam in exangui vultu maciem, &lt;s2&gt;[sumendus vilis &amp; modicus cibus,]&lt;/s2&gt; aut vires infra mediocritatem dejectas notabat, unam hanc rigidam castigatissimæ parsimoniæ diætam, instar atrocis delicti, vocabat in crimen, vociferabatur impatiens, ac pene illismet, quibus olim in S. Catharinam Senensem Lapa mater, tonabat verbis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041151Z">
        <seg>Whether he noted an excessive emaciation in a pale face, &lt;s2&gt;[one should take cheap &amp; moderate food,]&lt;/s2&gt; or noted the strength depressed below the mediocre level, he would call this one rigid diet of the most severe frugality, as if it were a heinous crime, to account, crying out impatiently, and almost at those very people who once thundered with words at Mother Lapa of St. Catherine of Siena;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoties aut nimiam in exangui vultu maciem, [sumendus vilis &amp; modicus cibus,] aut vires infra mediocritatem dejectas notabat, unam hanc rigidam castigatissimæ parsimoniæ diætam, instar atrocis delicti, vocabat in crimen, vociferabatur impatiens, ac pene illismet, quibus olim in S. Catharinam Senensem Lapa mater, tonabat verbis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T212034Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203530Z">
        <seg>Whether he noted an excessive emaciation in a pale face, [one should take cheap &amp; moderate food,] or noted the strength depressed below the mediocre level, he would call this one rigid diet of the most severe frugality, as if it were a heinous crime, to account, crying out impatiently, and almost at those very people who once thundered with words at Mother Lapa of St. Catherine of Siena;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoties improsperum quidpiam aut domi aut foris accideret, non dubitabat suis id imputare delictis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040543Z">
        <seg>Whenever something unfortunate happened, whether at home or abroad, he did not hesitate to blame it on his own crimes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoties sibi per diem ad nendi, suendi, texendive labores redeundum erat, numquam e scrinio, semper e manu parentis acum, fila, colum, ceteraque opificio necessaria demisse postulabat, credens id deberi filiali matris reverentiæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035551Z">
        <seg>Whenever she had to return during the day to the tasks of spinning, sewing, or weaving, she never took anything from the chest but always, with her hand outstretched, asked her parent for the needle, the threads, the shuttle, and all the other necessities of the craft, believing that such things were owed to a daughter's reverence for her mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quotiescumque in colloquio, seu proprio seu alieno ore nominabatur sanctissimum altaris Sacramentum, Rosa, capitis totiusque corporis profunda inclinatione, reverentiam testabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060053Z">
        <seg>Whenever, in conversation, whether spoken by her own lips or by someone else's, the most holy Sacrament of the altar was mentioned, Rosa would show her reverence with a deep bow of her head and entire body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quotiescumque ob singularitatem vitæ a fratribus aut matre proscindebatur injuriis, credebat se iis, imo &amp; decuplo gravioribus tam dignam esse, ut etiam ultro, quæ sibi imputabantur ad culpam, soleret in majus attollere, suumque reatum amplificare, ne præter, aut ultra meritum videretur affligi ac despici, quæ tantopere ambiebat ubique succumbere &amp; conculcari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040521Z">
        <seg>Whenever she was assailed with insults by her brothers or mother on account of the singularity of her life, she believed herself to be so worthy of them, nay, and of ten times more grievous ones, that she would even of her own accord magnify the faults imputed to her, and increase her own guilt, lest besides, or be seen as suffering and being despised beyond her deserts, which she so eagerly sought to do everywhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quotiescumque sacramentaliter confessura procubuerat ad confessarii pedes, munita crucis signaculo, erumpebat in hæc verba: “Dominus tecum, mi Pater.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053139Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053139Z">
        <seg>Whenever she had fallen down to the confessor's feet to make her sacramental confession, fortified by the sign of the cross, she would burst out with these words: "The Lord be with you, my Father."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quousque patieris hominum peccatis te irritari, atque irreverenter haberi?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053709Z">
        <seg>How long will you put up with being angered by people's sins and treated with disrespect?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæ sic vulnerabatur ex laudum molestia, qua putas animi jucunditate exceperit contemptum, probra, convicia?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040617Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040617Z">
        <seg>How was she wounded by the very praise she believed would be met with delight by her spirit—contempt, slanders, insults?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quædam spiritu ac religione apprime celebris persona flagrabat annoso desiderio, penitius divinæ gratiæ magnalia explorandi, quæ in Rosæ prato celari jam ex toto non poterant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040816Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040816Z">
        <seg>A certain person, exceedingly celebrated for her spirit and piety, burned with a long-standing desire to explore more deeply the wondrous works of divine grace, which could no longer be hidden in the Rose's meadow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæsitum de modo, &amp; compertum, id superne datum fuisse Virgini, quod spiritu (non secus acsi corporaliter præsens adfuisset) intererat quotidie Missis singulis, quotquot in vicino sancti Spiritus xenodochio legebantur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043733Z">
        <seg>It was inquired into how this was, and it was discovered that it had been granted to the Virgin from above, that by spirit (as if she had been physically present) she was present daily at each Mass, however many were said in the neighboring hospice of the Holy Spirit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæsitum diu in Rosæ hortulo, sicubi forte in caryophyllorum areola, vel noduli apparerent, propediem erupturi in florem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055126Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055126Z">
        <seg>Seeking for a long time in Rose's garden, whether by chance in the bed of carnations, or buds would appear, about to burst into a flower;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæsitura utique, egone aliam mihi exoptem semitam, præter illam, quam ipsamet gloriosa calcavit?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052541Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052541Z">
        <seg>I will surely seek another path for myself, apart from that one which the glorious one herself trod?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæsivit doctor, num forte de mystica theologia libros lectitaret, qui aut methodum artemque hujus introversionis docuissent, aut saltem illius naturam, signa, proprietates, effectus dissertando exponerent?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050312Z">
        <seg>The doctor asked whether he had perhaps been reading books on mystical theology that either taught the method and art of this introversion or at least expounded its nature, signs, properties, and effects through dissertations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæsivit obiter, velut re desperata, &amp; ecce in illomet loculamento, ubi ambo toties quæsierant, supra reliquas scripturas in propatulo reperitur apocha pulcherrimæ inscriptioni peregrinæ manus decenter involuta, non secus ac si illo momento clam ad scrinium fuisset relata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034020Z">
        <seg>He searched casually, as if in desperation, and behold, in that very compartment where they both had searched so often, on top of the other writings in plain view lay a receipt, its most beautiful foreign inscription neatly rolled up, as if it had just at that moment been secretly returned to the desk.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæsivit sæpe horum verborum emphasim: sed nullus umquam profundiorem ei attulit explicationem ea, quam ipsa, intime Deo docente, in animo conceperat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042456Z">
        <seg>She often sought the emphasis of these words; but no one ever provided her with a deeper explanation than the one she herself had conceived in her mind, God teaching her inwardly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstor Gundisalvus domi suæ bis terve hoc medicamine, non anaretico&lt;a1&gt;c&lt;/a1&gt;, sed plane anachoretico, vidit Rosam convalescere, protestans neutiquam se capere, unde huic jejunio vis nutriendi inesset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203529Z">
        <seg>Questor Gundisalvus, at his home, saw Rosa recover from this medicine—not an anaretic one, but a thoroughly ascetic one—and he protested that he could in no way understand how this fast could have the power to nourish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstor Gundisalvus domi suæ bis terve hoc medicamine, non anaretico&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;c&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, sed plane anachoretico, vidit Rosam convalescere, protestans neutiquam se capere, unde huic jejunio vis nutriendi inesset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041107Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041107Z">
        <seg>Questor Gundisalvus, at his home, saw Rosa recover from this medicine—not an anaretic one, but a thoroughly ascetic one—and he protested that he could in no way understand how this fast could have the power to nourish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstor Gundisalvus una cum uxore &amp; liberis aliquando domi suæ clam auscultabat Rosam loco solitario divinos amores piis clamoribus fervidius celebrantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053213Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053213Z">
        <seg>Questor Gundisalvus, together with his wife and children, would sometimes secretly listen at home to Rosa, in a secluded spot, more fervently celebrating divine loves with pious cries.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstor legationis dignitate perculsus, simulque cum seriam proregis mentem, tum negotii difficultatem probe intelligens, spatium quasi deliberaturus petiit, maxime quod tunc ob classem propediem inde Hispanias petituram, expediendis infinitis litteris, suique officii ratiociniis distringebatur, ne domo vel ad unum diem abesse posset: revera autem hæc prætexebat, ut per longioris moræ inducias effugeret ipsum periculosæ plenum opus aleæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012737Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012737Z">
        <seg>The quaestor, struck by the dignity of the mission and at the same time perceiving the seriousness of the viceregal intent and the difficulty of the undertaking, asked for a delay, chiefly because he was then occupied with the fleet about to set sail for Spain, burdened with the dispatch of countless letters and the accounts of his office, so that he could not be away from home for even a single day: In truth, however, he was using these as a pretext to escape the very dangerous undertaking itself through the delay of a longer stay.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstori Gundisalvo, tum communione officii, tum pari virtutis &amp; amicitiæ studio necessarius intimeque familiaris erat Joannes de Tineo Almianza, regiæ (quam vocant) cameræ chartophylax&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt;, &amp; Quitensis&lt;a1&gt;g&lt;/a1&gt; tribunalis supremus circitor&lt;a2&gt;h&lt;/a2&gt;, vir pietate ac religione spectabilis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011658Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011658Z">
        <seg>To the Quaestor Gundisalvus, both by the bond of office and by a like devotion to virtue and friendship, was intimately attached and closely related John of Tineo Almianza, chartophylax of the royal (as they call it) chamber and supreme circuit judge of Quito, a man distinguished for his piety and religion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstori Gundisalvo, tum communione officii, tum pari virtutis &amp; amicitiæ studio necessarius intimeque familiaris erat Joannes de Tineo Almianza, regiæ (quam vocant) cameræ chartophylax&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, &amp; Quitensis&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;g&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; tribunalis supremus circitor&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;h&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt;, vir pietate ac religione spectabilis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061432Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061432Z">
        <seg>To the Quaestor Gundisalvus, bound by both the communion of office and a like zeal for virtue and friendship, was intimately familiar John of Tineo Almienza, chartophylax of the royal (as they call it) chamber, and supreme judge of the Quito tribunal, a man distinguished for his piety and religion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstoris conjux aulam, in qua exposuerant Virginis corpus, undique luminoso illapsu cælestis gloriæ totam micare conspexit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042927Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042927Z">
        <seg>The Questor's wife saw the hall in which they had laid the Virgin's body all aglow with a radiant outpouring of heavenly glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstoris conjux, ut semel infirmam vidit fatiscere, totoque latere imbecillem ac stupidam vix trahendo spiritui sufficere, graviter hortata est, labenti corpusculo tunc saltem ab iis laboribus tantillum vacationis indulgeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035743Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035743Z">
        <seg>The Questor's wife, once she saw her frail form beginning to collapse and her entire side, weak and numb, scarcely able to draw breath, earnestly urged her to grant her failing little body at least a brief respite from those labors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstoris uxor utraque vice frontem Virginis domum redeuntis tacite observarat, ac diversitatem mirata, quid novæ rei gereretur, interrogavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054432Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054432Z">
        <seg>The questor's wife had silently observed the Virgin's brow on both occasions as she returned home, and, marveling at the difference, asked what new matter was afoot.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstoris uxor, quasi de Virginis ore verbum rapiens, confestim gossipium attulit, fascias solvi, floccos imponi jubet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053951Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053951Z">
        <seg>The Questor's wife, as if snatching a word from the Virgin's mouth, immediately spread the gossip, ordering the bandages to be loosened and the plaitings to be put on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Radix est in verbo Græco&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;προξενέω&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;quod significat&lt;/e5&gt; conciliatorem contractuum ago.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052305Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052305Z">
        <seg>The root is in the Greek word προξενέω, which means to act as a conciliator of contracts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Raphaëla de Esquivel monialis, tunc janitricis munere fungebatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033933Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033933Z">
        <seg>Raphaela de Esquivel, a nun, was then serving as the doorkeeper.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rari erant, quos sub concione, tum sacri cadaveris præsentia, tum recens ac viva gestorum memoria non medullitus compungeret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031534Z">
        <seg>They were few whom, in the presence of the sacred corpse and the recent and vivid memory of its deeds, the public assembly did not pierce to the marrow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Recitatis de more orationibus, dataque aspersione &amp; suffitu, sex sacerdotes linteati, stolisque ac manipulis decori, arcam cedrinam sublevarunt in humeros;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031441Z">
        <seg>After the customary prayers were recited, and with the sprinkling and suffumigation completed, six priests, robed and adorned with stoles and maniples, lifted the cedar ark onto their shoulders.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Reclusæ Virginis arctissimæ solitudini magnum id, prodigiosumque fuisse solatium, nemo ibit inficias.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043739Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043739Z">
        <seg>No one will deny that it was a great and marvelous consolation for the cloistered Virgin's most strict solitude.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Recta domum festinavit Euphemia, illic Franciscam suam, depulso periculo, convalescentem invenit, adeo ut postridie, jubente medico, rursus infanti nutritias, ut ante, præbuerit mamillas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055259Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055259Z">
        <seg>Euphemia hurried straight home, where she found her Francisca, the danger having passed, recovering so well that the next day, at the doctor's orders, she once again provided the infant with nourishment, as before, by nursing her at the breast.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Recusabat interdum austera parens, quod existimaret, Filiam raro sanam ac jugiter valetudinariam, priusquam in aliorum curam se intromitteret, debere charitatis ordinem a semetipsa auspicari, suæque magis, quam alienæ valetudini prospicere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061237Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061237Z">
        <seg>Her austere mother would sometimes object, believing that a daughter who was seldom well and constantly ailing, before she undertook the care of others, ought to begin the work of charity with herself and look after her own health more than that of others.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Redditus matri filiolus, occluso reticulo visceribusque repositis, inventus est sine hernia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033549Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033549Z">
        <seg>The infant was returned to his mother, with the mesh closed and the intestines replaced, and was found to have no hernia.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Redeundum hic ad virginem Rosæ cadaver, quod respirare credebatur, quando spirare desierat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030251Z">
        <seg>We must return here to the corpse of the Virgin Rose, which was believed to be breathing when she had ceased to breathe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Redibat ad luctam conatuum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045543Z">
        <seg>He returned to the struggle of efforts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Redibat inde protinus ad eam, de qua supra, virilem Servatoris nostri effigiem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054134Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054134Z">
        <seg>A manly effigy of our Savior, of whom we have spoken above, returned thence directly to her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rediit a choro Maria, vidensque machinam apposite elevatam, &amp; arcas sub ea commodissime dispositas, obstupuit, capere non valens, cui in toto monasterio ad talia brevissimo tempore peragenda sufficere vires &amp; animus potuerint.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040918Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040918Z">
        <seg>Mary returned from choir and, seeing the machine raised into position and the chests arranged beneath it most conveniently, was astonished, unable to believe that in the entire monastery anyone could have had the strength and spirit to accomplish such a thing in so short a time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rediit domum Helena plane altera, nempe lumbricis ad unum vacua, pedibus sana, febrium ignara, sanguine, vigore, corpulentia mirabiliter instaurata, nulliusque deinceps ex his symptomatis conscia, valetudinem possedit integram ac diuturnam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033756Z">
        <seg>Helen returned home a completely different person: her bowels entirely free of worms, her feet sound, unaware of any fever, her blood, vigor, and flesh marvelously restored, and from then on conscious of none of those symptoms, she enjoyed perfect and lasting health.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Religiosum cum mortis angoribus trepide ac periculose luctantem, urentibus scrupulis vexatum, sic juvit promissione suarum precum, &amp; exhortatione, ac sponsione participationis suarum satisfactionum, ut confestim pavor lætæ spei locum cesserit, qui postea salutaribus sacramentis munitus ingenti salutis fiducia placidum &amp; serenum spiritum reddidit Creatori, de cujus anima postea Rosa nuntiavit, cum ea feliciter ac benigne agi, jamque illam regioni lucis adscriptam frui perenni Sanctorum felicitatis refrigerio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042400Z">
        <seg>He helped a religious man, who was struggling with the pangs of death in a fearful and dangerous manner, tormented by burning scruples, with the promise of his prayers and an exhortation, and by the pledge of the participation of his satisfactions, so that immediately the fear gave way to joyful hope, who, later, fortified with the salutary sacraments, returned a peaceful and serene spirit to the Creator, of whose soul Rose later announced that she was being treated felicitously and kindly, and was already enrolled in the region of light, enjoying the perpetual refreshment of the Saints' felicity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Religiosus quidam per ardua, &amp; prolixa itinera prælatum ecclesiasticum associaturus, necessitatem suam Virgini aperuit, precum suffragia pro avertendis periculis flagitavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051834Z">
        <seg>A certain religious man, about to accompany a church prelate on arduous and lengthy journeys, revealed his need to the Virgin and begged for prayers to avert dangers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Reliquum, quod sibi in illa sacra Communione acciderat, Virgo humilis verecunde subticuit, matrique sat fuit, Rosæ nomen, quod infanti prima imposuerat, cælo placuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034810Z">
        <seg>The humble Virgin modestly kept silent about the rest that had happened to her in that sacred Communion, and it was enough for her mother to know that the name Rose, which she had first given the infant, had pleased heaven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rem adfero inauditam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045143Z">
        <seg>I bring an unheard-of thing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rem tamen indigne tulit avia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034705Z">
        <seg>However, the grandmother took the matter ill.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Remediis diu incassum tentatis, demum in ædem sancti Dominici adducitur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040314Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040314Z">
        <seg>After long and fruitless attempts at remedies, he is finally brought to the house of Saint Dominic;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Renuit ille, protestans se jam sibi abunde sufficere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033418Z">
        <seg>He refused, protesting that he was already more than enough for himself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Renuntiarunt singuli, nonnisi vacuam sese ollam reperisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062226Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062226Z">
        <seg>They each reported that they had found the pot empty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Repentino angore correpta in manibus, &amp; pedibus summe dolens, sensit, spiritum suum caligine &amp; pavore concuti.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043134Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043134Z">
        <seg>Struck suddenly by a sharp pang in her hands and feet, she felt her spirit shaken by darkness and dread.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Repentino prodigio omnes invasit arcanus lætitiæ sensus, ut mater ipsa aptans cadaver (sicut ei vivens Filia obtinuerat) &amp; tota domus hilariter non exsequiis defunctæ, sed nuptiali gaudio interesse viderentur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042914Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042914Z">
        <seg>By a sudden miracle, a mysterious sense of joy seized everyone, so that even the mother herself, fitting the corpse (just as her living daughter had done), and the entire household seemed, not to be attending the deceased's funeral, but to be sharing in a wedding joy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Replicuit Catharina: “Tu vero, charissima, non meministi, nos heri tecum inutili quæsitione floris totum aliquoties hortum permeasse, nec usquam vel nodulum in stylis, aut dentilum&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt; reperisse in scapis, qui huic integro mensi pubem ullius flosculi polliceatur”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011236Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011236Z">
        <seg>Catherine replied, "You, dearest, surely don't remember that yesterday we went through the entire garden with you several times in that pointless search for a flower, nor did we find anywhere so much as a bud on the stems or a tiny denticle on the scapes that would promise the down of any little bloom for this whole month."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Replicuit Catharina: “Tu vero, charissima, non meministi, nos heri tecum inutili quæsitione floris totum aliquoties hortum permeasse, nec usquam vel nodulum in stylis, aut dentilum&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; reperisse in scapis, qui huic integro mensi pubem ullius flosculi polliceatur”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055154Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055154Z">
        <seg>Catherine replied, "But you, dearest, do not remember that yesterday we went through the entire garden with you several times in that useless search for a flower, nor did we find anywhere so much as a bud on the stems or a tiny tooth on the stalks that would promise the bloom of a single little flower for this whole month."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Replicuit mater: Putasne in tota hac domo vel micam chocolatæ reperiri?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051648Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051648Z">
        <seg>The mother replied, "Do you think there's a single chocolate crumb to be found in this whole house?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Replicuit mater: “At ego tuis impendi usibus præceperam, solicita, ne quid desit, quando infirmaberis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061051Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061051Z">
        <seg>The mother replied, "But I had instructed you to spend on your own needs, making sure that nothing would be lacking when you fall ill."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Replicuit morigera Filia: “ Et nonne diserte vetueras mater, ne illas absque tuo nutu deponerem?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035523Z">
        <seg>The obedient daughter replied, "And did you not, mother, wisely forbid me to put them down without your permission?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Replicuit pater, se vero non per insomnia, sed expeditis, liberrimisque sensibus &amp; vidisse &amp; audisse Rosam hæc sibi serio præcipientem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032120Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032120Z">
        <seg>The father replied that he, for his part, had not seen and heard Rosa earnestly instructing him in these matters in a dream, but with his senses fully alert and unrestrained.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Reposuit operosa Virgo, se parentum suorum necessitatibus absque scrupulo deesse non posse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035747Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035747Z">
        <seg>The industrious virgin replied that she could not, without scruple, fail to attend to her parents' needs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Repræsentabatur coloribus in tela Genitrix Virgo, dormientem parvulum Jesum complexa sinu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054509Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054509Z">
        <seg>The Virgin Mother was depicted in colors on canvas, embracing the sleeping little Jesus in her arms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Res cum risu excepta ab omnibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062712Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062712Z">
        <seg>The matter was met with laughter by all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Res demum ita patuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012941Z">
        <seg>In the end, the matter became clear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Res erat sacra &amp; religiosa, quam in privatum usum adhibere capitali pœna vetitum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031748Z">
        <seg>It was a sacred and religious matter, the private use of which was forbidden under capital punishment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Res ita contigit: Lusitabat cum ea natu major germanus frater, isque (casu an studio, incertum) luto imbuit rutilam pulcherrimamque innocentis puellæ cæsariem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034941Z">
        <seg>This is how it happened: her older brother was playing with her, and he (whether by accident or on purpose, it is uncertain) smeared the innocent girl's beautiful, golden hair with mud.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Res ita evenit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034721Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034721Z">
        <seg>This is how it happened.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Res ita innotuit: mox a felici Rosæ transitu, persona quædam eximiæ sanctitatis ac nominis in visione imaginaria conspexit inter angelorum myriades gloriosam sanctarum Virginum catervam, recta gradientem ad thronum SS. Trinitatis: in medio agminis fulgebat Rosa palmam manu præferens, niveoque candicans amictu, sed nondum ut reliquæ coronata: siquidem ad divini illius throni crepidinem stabat virgo Deipara, coruscam in dextera coronam tenens, qua Rosam suis ipsa manibus inauguratura, solemnem hanc Virginum processionem exspectabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042431Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042431Z">
        <seg>The matter became known in this way: soon after the blessed Rose's passing, a certain person of exceptional holiness and renown saw in an imaginary vision among the myriads of angels a glorious host of holy Virgins, walking straight toward the throne of the Saints. of the Trinity: in the midst of the procession shone Rose, holding a palm in her hand, resplendent in a snow-white robe, but not yet crowned like the others: for at the foot of that divine throne stood the virgin Mother of God, holding a radiant crown in her right hand, with which she was to crown Rose with her own hands, awaiting this solemn procession of virgins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Res ita se habuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040859Z">
        <seg>This is how things were.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rescivit hoc Rosa, iterumque dixit Euphemiæ: “Certo certius religiosum videbis filium tuum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012625Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012625Z">
        <seg>Rosa learned of this, and again said to Euphemia: "You will certainly see your son a religious man."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Resonabat undequaque ecclesia perpetuis vocibus Rosam &lt;b1&gt;Sanctam&lt;/b1&gt; proclamantium, ita ut neque canentem religiosorum chorum foris audiret populus, neque ad chorum pertingeret vox sacerdotis aut ministrorum altaris: pulsu ac tintinnabulis signum dabatur choro, cum respondendum erat sacerdoti;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031029Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031029Z">
        <seg>The church resounded from every side with the ceaseless voices proclaiming "Holy Rose," so that neither the people outside could hear the religious choir singing, nor could the priest's or the altar ministers' voice reach the choir: a signal was given to the choir by the pulse and bells when a response was to be made to the priest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respexit humilitatem Ancillulæ suæ divina pietas, illique integrum expediti judicii &amp; linguæ vigorem peculiari prodigio inter assidua exhausti cerebri deliquia immunem servavit usque ad extremum halitum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025717Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025717Z">
        <seg>Divine piety looked upon the humility of its little handmaid, and preserved for her the full vigor of her judgment and speech, by a special miracle, immune from the constant lapses of a brain worn out by exhaustion, until her very last breath.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondebat planta cultricis industriæ, &amp; quo prodibat lætior, magis lætificabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051214Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051214Z">
        <seg>The plant responded to the industrious gardener's care, and the more it grew, the more it delighted him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit Euphemia: “Deambulare me jubes, cum non ignores, quæ me domi meæ exspectet afflictio?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055231Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055231Z">
        <seg>Euphemia replied, "You order me to walk, when you know full well what affliction awaits me at home?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit Virgo, nescire se, quo illa nomine apud talium rerum peritos appelletur, modum tamen locutionis hunc fuisse, quod ab ore Christi promanabat clarus &amp; purissimus sapientiæ conceptus, qui animæ fundum penetrans, significabat intus, quæcumque vellet intelligi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052854Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052854Z">
        <seg>The Virgin replied that she did not know by what name she would be called among those skilled in such matters, but that this was the manner of speech: that from the mouth of Christ proceeded a clear and most pure conception of wisdom, which, penetrating to the depths of the soul, signified within whatever it wished to be understood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit Virgo: “At hoc ipsum est, quod Sponsum rogo, ne cesset me exquisitis ardoribus excoquere, donec ei maturescam in fructum, qui recta hinc mensæ ipsius inferri mereatur”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030114Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030114Z">
        <seg>The Virgin replied, "But this is precisely what I ask of the Spouse: that he never cease to refine me with his most exquisite ardors, until I ripen for him into a fruit worthy to be brought straight from here to his own table."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit blande commiserans Virgo: “Quin tu potius adora divinam providentiam, quæ tibi hæc tempestive constare voluit, ut sensim maritum ad justam æquanimitatem disponas, ne frustra obstrigillet spiritui, ubi vult, spiraturo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012612Z">
        <seg>The Virgin replied gently and compassionately, "Rather, worship the divine providence that willed these things to be made clear to you in due time, so that you may gradually bring your husband to a just equanimity, lest he vainly constrain the spirit where it longs to breathe."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit filia: “Jam aptata est, ut aptari melius non possit, quinimo nunc primum illa decore canduit, ex quo in eleësynam conversa est”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061048Z">
        <seg>The daughter replied, "It is already fitted as well as it can be; indeed, it has only now first shone with beauty since it was turned into a charitable gift."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit illa, se occurrisse supplicaturam pro novitio jamjam exeundo, simulque rogavit decretum expulsionis rescindi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012442Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012442Z">
        <seg>She replied that she had come to intercede for the novice who was about to leave, and at the same time asked that the decree of expulsion be rescinded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit quidam ex vicinioribus: “Si ita est, surge in pedes, ambula, &amp; obtentæ gratiæ fidem facito”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033452Z">
        <seg>One of those standing nearby replied, "If that's the case, stand up, walk, and bear witness to the grace you have received."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit subridens filia: “Ne mireris, dulcissima mater: nam sæpe hujusmodi officiis me dignatur angelus meus tutelaris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051738Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051738Z">
        <seg>The daughter replied with a smile, "Do not be surprised, dearest mother: for my guardian angel often honors me with such duties."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit successus vaticinio: nam Philippæ ardens religiosi status desiderium cunctis adstupentibus migravit in Franciscam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012520Z">
        <seg>Success confirmed the prophecy: for Philippa's ardent desire for a religious state, to the astonishment of all, passed over to Francis.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit ægra, se quidem nihilo melius ac ante valere, pelles autem ita, ut mater adstrinxerat, immotas adhuc sibi adhærere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035514Z">
        <seg>She replied in her illness that she was no better than before, and that the bandages, just as her mother had bound them, were still stuck to her without moving.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit, ibidem se prius ante pendulam Rosæ imaginem procubuisse in genua, postulasse auxilium &amp; robur, demum se lecto, qui cum catasta movendus erat, reverenter imposuisse imaginem, mox facili conatu in partem retracta machina, se quatuor illis locis ordinasse lateres, &amp; denique in Rosæ nomine, dorso fidenter subjecto, ligneam iis molem imposuisse, quam dum attollebat, putasse non gravem machinam, sed levem paleam se portare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040928Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040928Z">
        <seg>He replied that he had first knelt before the hanging image of Rosa, had asked for help and strength, and finally had reverently placed the image on the bed, which was to be moved with a cart, and that soon, with little effort, the machine was retracted to one side, he had arranged bricks in those four places, and finally, in the name of Rose, had confidently placed a wooden beam across them, which, as he lifted it, he thought he was carrying not a heavy machine, but light straw.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit, nulla re creata recreari se posse, in hoc solo &amp; unico omne suum oblectamentum consistere, quod tanta cum certitudine sentiebat in anima sua præsentem Deum, quem si vel ad momentum e conspectu perderet, id sibi acerbius omni jactura, dirius omni gehenna videri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050346Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050346Z">
        <seg>He replied that he could be refreshed by nothing created; that his sole and only delight consisted in feeling the God who is so certain in his soul; and that if he were to lose Him from his sight, even for a moment, it would seem to him more bitter than any loss, more dreadful than any hell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit, propria illa differentium visionum nomina sibi quidem adhuc ignota esse, at gloriosam Christi humanitatem exhiberi sibi in transitu quodam vicino &amp; claro, sicut cum fulgidum aliquod sidus moroso tractu procurrit in cælo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050544Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050544Z">
        <seg>He replied that the proper names of those different visions were indeed still unknown to him, but that the glorious humanity of Christ was displayed to him in a certain near and clear passage, just as when some bright star, with a sluggish course, runs across the sky.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit, vix se meminisse talium certaminum, ab infantia se magno Dei favore sensisse faciles, &amp; summe connaturales propensiones in virtutem, absque ullo tumultu passionum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050337Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050337Z">
        <seg>He replied that he could barely remember such struggles, that from infancy he had felt himself to be the object of God's great favor, and that his inclinations toward virtue were supremely natural, without any turmoil of the passions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Retinuit tamen, quamquam varie tentata, ut alium assumeret: neque enim cessabat humana prudentia, divini ignara consilii, diversa suadere, offerre, ingerere, quasi meliora ac digniora.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040242Z">
        <seg>Yet it held back, though sorely tempted to take another: for human prudence, ignorant of the divine counsel, did not cease to urge, offer, and thrust forward various things as if they were better and more worthy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Risere, quotquot aderant, revocata subito mortis sententia, plaudebat cantu repetito pullus cum plaudentibus, spatiabatur velut alte præcinctus, colloque arrecto stridulus identidem renovabat domesticorum acclamantium cachinnos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061550Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061550Z">
        <seg>All who were present laughed; with the death sentence suddenly revoked, the chick, its song repeated, clapped along with the others, strutted as if proudly belted high, and with its neck held erect, the shrill little one again and again renewed the household's shrieks of delight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Robur ex S. Communione deprehendit in filia mater: nam quotiescumque Rosa sacram Synaxim receptura templum adibat cum parente, ex pridianis jejuniis, flagris, ac vigiliis tam fessa, attenuata ac debilis erat, ut sæpe per viam [in] angiportis hincinde fuerit utrique subsistendum, quoad lassa Virgo recuperaret anhelitum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055935Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055935Z">
        <seg>The mother perceived the strength drawn from Holy Communion in her daughter: for whenever Rosa went to the church to receive the sacred Communion with her parent, she was so weary, emaciated, and weak from the previous days' fasts, scourges, and vigils that they were often forced to pause in the lanes along the way so that the exhausted Virgin could catch her breath.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Robustus hic fuit aries ad pulsanda Rosæ præcordia, maxime quod ipsa quoque mater, cui res angusta domi, contenta jam erat, pluriumque servorum Dei conspirabant arbitria.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040258Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040258Z">
        <seg>He was a mighty ram for smiting Rose's heart, especially since the mother herself, who was already content in her straitened circumstances at home, was also pleased, and the designs of many servants of God were conspiring.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rodericus mox ut lineam subuculam cum lanea commutavit, convaluit, exacto probationis anno solemnia vota emisit, diuque in seraphica religione pius &amp; exemplaris sacerdos supervixit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012653Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012653Z">
        <seg>As soon as Rodericus exchanged his undergarment for a woolen one, he recovered; after completing the probationary year, he took his solemn vows and lived on for a long time as a devout and exemplary priest in the Seraphic Order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Roga;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051626Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051626Z">
        <seg>Ask</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rogabat interdum Latinam horum verborum emphasim doctius ac profundius sibi enucleari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061733Z">
        <seg>He would sometimes ask that the emphasis of these words be explained to him more learnedly and profoundly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rogata, velletne medicum, respondit: Cælestem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042725Z">
        <seg>When asked if she wanted a doctor, she replied, "Heavenly."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rogaverunt Agnetem adstantes, denuo ipsis præsentibus tentaret vires suas, num elevandæ machinæ sufficerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040937Z">
        <seg>The onlookers asked Agnes to try her strength once more in their presence to see if she could lift the machine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rogavit, &amp; obtinuit per aliquot dies solitariam quietem &amp; silentium, ut intimius suos dolores intingeret extremis cruciatibus crucifixi Amoris, cui se membratim confixam in cruce sentiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042738Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042738Z">
        <seg>She asked for, and obtained, for several days a solitary peace and silence, so that she might more intimately partake of the extreme torments of the crucified Love, to which she felt herself nailed limb by limb on the cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa ad confessarii vocem repente sibi reddita, respondit, jam Hostiam sacrosanctam per guttur ad intima descendisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025935Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025935Z">
        <seg>Rosa, suddenly brought back to herself by the confessor's voice, replied that the most sacred Host had already descended through her throat into her inmost being.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa angelum suum non modo custodem, sed &amp; congerronem&lt;a2&gt;a&lt;/a2&gt; habuit amicissimum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010638Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010638Z">
        <seg>Rosa had her angel not only as a guardian, but also as a most friendly companion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa angelum suum non modo custodem, sed &amp; congerronem&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;a&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; habuit amicissimum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051558Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051558Z">
        <seg>Rosa had her angel not only as a guardian, but also as a most friendly companion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa apud sanctam Catharinam precante, ista, depulso periculo, ita convaluit, ut postridie permissa fuerit a medico nutritias infanti, quas prohibita fuerat, præbere mamillas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042239Z">
        <seg>While Rosa was praying at Saint Catherine's, once the danger had passed she recovered so well that the next day the doctor permitted her to give the infant the nourishments she had been forbidden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa candidissimi Agni sponsa!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044557Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044557Z">
        <seg>Rose, bride of the most pure Lamb!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa deprehensam se videns, simpliciter respondit, illo se uti ad offulam suam inspergendam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041225Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041225Z">
        <seg>Seeing that she had been discovered, Rosa simply replied that she was using it to sprinkle her doll.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa dilecta!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044551Z">
        <seg>Beloved Rose!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa graviter modesteque arridens subjecit: “Scito, mi Pater, hac nocte cum ineunte S. Bartholomæi festo&lt;a1&gt;e&lt;/a1&gt; me abituram ad festa sempiterna: jam cælitus invitata sum ad splendidum illud ac solemne convivium, jam præfixa est hora, &amp; non vis, quod vadam, dum patent fores”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030223Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030223Z">
        <seg>Rosa, smiling gravely and modestly, said: "Know, my Father, that tonight, with the coming of the Feast of St. Bartholomew, I shall depart to the eternal feasts: I have already been invited from heaven to that splendid and solemn banquet, the hour is already set, and you do not wish me to go while the doors are open."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa in præsentem imaginem defixis oculis avide, quæ dicebantur, auscultabat: sed cum matrona sensim ad alia diverteret, insolita verborum emphasi interpellavit Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054524Z">
        <seg>Rosa listened eagerly to what was being said, her eyes fixed on the present image; but when the matron slowly turned to other matters, Rosa interrupted her with an unusual emphasis of words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa involucrum explicante, ipsa librarum quinquagena, cujus pater debitor erat, apparuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042601Z">
        <seg>As Rosa unfolded the wrapper, the fifty-pound book, for which her father was a debtor, appeared.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa miranti similis, “Quid agis (inquit) charissima soror?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043857Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043857Z">
        <seg>Like a rose in wonder, she said, "What are you doing, dearest sister?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa nec læsa, nec territa, agilior surrexit, quam ceciderat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051955Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051955Z">
        <seg>Rose, neither hurt nor frightened, rose more agile than she had fallen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa nomine florebat &amp; opere: nam ut altaribus nulla anni parte deessent flores, diligentissime procurabat, discretis hunc in usum areolis, quarum uni basilicum (plures ozimum&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt; dicunt) implantarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010607Z">
        <seg>She excelled both in name and in deed: for she made sure that flowers would never be lacking on the altars at any time of the year, carefully providing them by means of small beds, one of which she had planted with basil (which many call ozimum).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa nomine florebat &amp; opere: nam ut altaribus nulla anni parte deessent flores, diligentissime procurabat, discretis hunc in usum areolis, quarum uni basilicum (plures ozimum&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; dicunt) implantarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051208Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051208Z">
        <seg>She excelled both in name and in deed: for she made sure that flowers would never be lacking on the altars at any time of the year, carefully providing them by means of small flower beds, one of which she had planted with basil (which many call ozimum).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa nondum ultra pueriles annos creverat, jamque hunc manducabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055641Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055641Z">
        <seg>Rosa had not yet grown beyond her childhood years, and was already devouring him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa nostra idipsum experiri meruit &amp; in suo: nam quotiescumque hostis tartareus appropinquabat Virgini, suggesturus fœda ac sordida, aliis ad eum procul abigendum catapultis opus non fuit, quam quod vigil Rosa ter digito pulsaret suam coronam, in SS. Triadis reverentiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042341Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042341Z">
        <seg>Our Rose herself deserved to experience it: for whenever the Tartar foe drew near to the Virgin, about to utter foul and filthy words, no other catapult was needed to drive him away than the Rose's vigil, who thrice touched her crown with her finger in reverence to the Holy Trinity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa palmam deferebat, ut ceteræ, at nondum coronam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031841Z">
        <seg>Rosa was yielding the palm, like the others, but not yet the crown.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa patiendi assuetudine ac desiderio fortis, hæc quasi de alieno brachio dicta tranquille excipiebat: nihilominus die quadam familiariter cum Gundisalvi uxore de prodigio sudantis imaginis conferens, repente se sensit exciri ad fiduciam recuperandæ sanitatis, modo gossipium, quod prodigioso imaginis sudore maduerat, contusis brachii sui nervis ac musculis applicaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053948Z">
        <seg>Rosa, strong in habit of suffering and in desire, calmly received these words, as if spoken of another's arm. Nevertheless, one day, while familiarly discussing with Gundisalvi's wife the miracle of the sweating image, she suddenly felt herself drawn to the hope of regaining her health, provided that the gossamer which had been drenched by the image's miraculous sweat were applied to the crushed nerves and muscles of her arm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa periculi admonita, quo tum sancti Viri celebre nomen, tum marcentis Limæ nutabat tranquillitas, mœsta indoluit, noctemque illam horrisonis flagris in sua carne depurpuravit, ut proprii sanguinis hostimento sopiret communem populi sui turbationem, quæ cordatis periculosior quocumque terræ motu videbatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041553Z">
        <seg>Rose, warned of the danger that threatened both the renowned name of the holy Man and the tranquility of a withering Lima, mourned sorrowfully and imprinted that night's horrid scourges upon her own flesh, so that by the sacrifice of her own blood she might soothe the common turmoil of her people, which to the thoughtful seemed more perilous than any earthquake.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa pro compassionis affectu diligentius pelvim, qua coagulus sanguis asportabatur, intuita, sensit ab imo fundo sibi evert i stomachum, alterari in faucibus salivam, totisque se visceribus in vomitum propendere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061349Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061349Z">
        <seg>Rosa, moved by compassion, gazed more intently at the basin in which the blood clot was being carried away, and felt her stomach turn from deep within, her saliva alter in her throat, and her entire being incline toward vomiting.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa præelecta!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044554Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044554Z">
        <seg>Rose is chosen!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa tametsi vocationis suæ tenax &amp; certa, noluit tamen aut cerebrosa videri, aut palam tot peritissimorum benevola judicia immodesto supercilio aspernari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040301Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040301Z">
        <seg>Rosa, though tenacious and certain of her calling, nevertheless refused to appear conceited or to openly scorn the favorable judgments of so many experts with an immodest air.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa tot jam edocta malis, attamen urgenti conabatur obtemperare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041113Z">
        <seg>Rose, already so well-versed in evil, nevertheless tried to comply with the urgent request.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa tristi nuntio exterrita, post brevem deliberationem respondit: “De Dei honore &amp; obsequio hic agitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053501Z">
        <seg>Rose, startled by the sad news, after a brief deliberation replied: "It is a matter of honor and obedience to God."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa tunc in oratione defixa venientibus innuit, hortum adirent, atque in nomine sanctissimæ Trinitatis tria inde caryophylla confestim sibi adferrent, sacræ iconi coaptanda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055151Z">
        <seg>Rosa then, with her eyes fixed in prayer, beckoned to those who were coming to approach the garden and, in the name of the Most Holy Trinity, to bring her three carnations from there at once to be attached to the sacred icon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa virgo, ex tertio Ordine S. Dominici, Limæ in Peruvia, Americæ provincia (S.)</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211740Z">
        <seg>Rose, virgin, of the Third Order of St. Dominic, of Lima in Peru, Province of the Americas (S.)</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, conspecto hoc suo vespertino choraule, sese &amp; ipsa parabat ad laudes Deo modulandas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045224Z">
        <seg>Rose, upon seeing this evening choir, herself prepared to sing praises to God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, cui nec per somnium ulla umquam placuerat nuptiarum cogitatio, sentiebat intus, uno isto conjugio nihil felicius in tota sibi vita posse obtingere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044148Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044148Z">
        <seg>Rosa, to whom the very thought of marriage had never once been pleasing, even in her dreams, felt deep down that nothing more fortunate could come into her entire life than that one marriage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, disploso aliorum metu, cœpit verecundari ipsa de brevitate truncatarum vestium, &amp; ut decenter rediret domum, necesse fuit in prædicto sacello noctem expectare cum dolore erepti martyrii, quamquam gratularetur patriæ liberatæ, ac præcipue inviolatæ templorum religioni.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060301Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060301Z">
        <seg>Rosa, her fear of others dispelled, began to feel self-conscious about the brevity of her truncated garments, and in order to return home decently, she had to wait out the night in the aforementioned chapel with the pain of her martyrdom being snatched away, although she rejoiced in her liberated homeland and especially in the inviolable sanctity of the temples.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, dum exesæ cartilaginis pars vitiata præscinditur, ea vultus animique firmitate vulnus excepit, qua nec intueri adstantes poterant sulcantem novaculam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034854Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034854Z">
        <seg>Rosa, while the damaged part of her cartilage was being cut away, bore the wound with such strength of face and spirit that even those standing by could not look at the razor cleaving it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, quod putaret in hoc genere se pauca, &amp; admodum vulgaria præstare, obiter de suis jejuniis, ciliciis, disciplinis, &amp; reliquis austeritatibus nonnulla edixit, absente matre, quodque in his non suo, sed confessariorum arbitratu modum ac mensuram constituisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050615Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050615Z">
        <seg>Rosa, thinking that in this regard she was doing little and very ordinary things, on the side issued some edicts about her fasts, hairshirts, disciplines, and other austerities in her mother's absence, and that in these she had established the measure and limit not by her own judgment, but by that of her confessors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, ut erat accelerandis mœstorum solatiis promptissima, statim quidquid suæ apud Deum orationes valerent, Euphemiæ addixit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012557Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012557Z">
        <seg>Rosa, ever most ready to hasten the consolations of the sorrowful, immediately devoted whatever her prayers to God could avail to Euphemia.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, ut prodigium, quod viderat, non suo, sed matronæ merito tribueret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054537Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054537Z">
        <seg>Rose, attributing the prodigy she had seen not to her own merit, but to that of the matron.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, vel admirata munus tam copiosum &amp; affluens, vel ut humilius parenti gratias ageret, “Mihine (inquit) omnem hanc lini portionem donas mater?” Respondit illa: “Tibi, filia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061035Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061035Z">
        <seg>Rosa, either admiring the generous gift or, more humbly, to thank her mother, said, "Are you giving me this entire portion of linen, mother?" She replied, "To you, my daughter."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, velut si Luciæ cor penitius inspexisset, eamdem familiarius, quam pridie complexa, nil de marito, nil de filiola filiisve tunc respondit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062926Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062926Z">
        <seg>Rosa, as if she had looked more deeply into Lucia's heart, answered her more familiarly than the day before, saying nothing about her husband, nor about her little daughter or sons.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosam Catharinæ discipulam idem certamen exercuit, eadem victoria cohonestavit: nam &amp; ipsa somnum suum intra duarum horarum angustias coërcuit, &amp; aliquando etiam minus spatii indulsit importuno exactori.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043023Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043023Z">
        <seg>He put the same test on his pupil Rosa Catharina and honored her with the same victory: for she too confined her sleep within the narrow limits of two hours, and at times even granted the importunate exactor less time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosam ceu vanam prophetidem cum dedecore propediem vulgi fabulam fore;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062811Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062811Z">
        <seg>That Rosam, like a vain prophetess, will soon be a disgrace and a public spectacle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosam eo loco, quem tunc deputati judices excipiendis testium de ea responsis ac depositionibus destinarant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032209Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032209Z">
        <seg>I brought Rosa to the place which the judges then appointed for receiving the witnesses' answers and depositions concerning her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosam ingenti solatio perfudit hæc visio, quod oblique per eam intelligeret, suo præcipue labori ac merito hoc donari a Deo, quod Lima tali aliquando sacrarum virginum asceterio, velut monili pretioso, erat condecoranda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062607Z">
        <seg>This vision filled Rosa with immense consolation, for she understood by it, especially in regard to her own labor and merit, that God was granting that Lima, at some future time, would be adorned with such an astery of holy virgins as a precious jewel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosam suam alii ultimo contrectare;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031044Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031044Z">
        <seg>To let another have one's rose at the last.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosis ad corda subigenda, mulcenda, concilianda vim philtri inesse obscure innuit Dioscorides&lt;a0&gt;g&lt;/a0&gt;, clarius ostendit Rosa nostra Limana.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032842Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032842Z">
        <seg>While Dioscorides obscurely hints that roses have the power of a philter to subdue, soothe, and conciliate hearts, our Lima Rose makes it clearer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosæ commendans;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033837Z">
        <seg>Commending the roses;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosæ insperserat, avide epoto, sana consedit, ubi supina jacuerat, nec præteritæ infirmitatis ullum in ea superfuit vestigium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033146Z">
        <seg>Having sprinkled herself with rosewater and eagerly drunk it, she sat up in good health where she had been lying on her back, and no trace of her former illness remained in her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosæ permagno stetere hæc prælia: nam, sive genibus humi fixis inchoabat preces, videbatur inesse plumbum gravatis palpebris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043035Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043035Z">
        <seg>These contests were a great trial for the roses: for whenever she began her prayers with her knees fixed to the ground, it seemed as if lead had been placed in her heavy eyelids.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosæ videbantur in hac imagine omnia spirare, ac vivere, sæpiusque fatebatur, hanc apud se peculiariter magno in pretio esse, quod inde suavissimis interdum solatiis fuerat delibuta;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054512Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054512Z">
        <seg>In this image, the roses seemed to breathe and live, and she often confessed that she held this one in particularly high esteem, because from it she had at times been treated to the sweetest consolations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosæ, oleoque ex lampade coram istius altari pendula desumpto, in stomachi regione, collo, pectore peruncta, statim &amp; in continenti pristinæ salutilcum omnimoda fractarum virium reparatione fuit restituta&lt;a0&gt;g&lt;/a0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043311Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043311Z">
        <seg>Anointed with oil from a lamp hanging before that altar, on her stomach, neck, and chest, she was immediately and on the spot restored to her former health with a complete recovery of her broken strength.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rudimenta hæc erant severioris militiæ, quam ætas maturior, ut promittebat animo, sic corpori minabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041640Z">
        <seg>These were the rudiments of a more severe discipline, which threatened the body as much as the more mature age threatened the mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rudis Juvencula hæsit ad PRÆDESTINATIONIS scholasticum vocabulum, &lt;s2&gt;[sicut doctori Castillo indicavit,]&lt;/s2&gt; cujus propriam vim, ac etymon haud satis assequebatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061823Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061823Z">
        <seg>The uneducated young woman was stuck on the scholastic term PREDESTINATION, [as she indicated to Doctor Castillo,] whose true meaning and etymon she did not fully grasp.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rudis Juvencula hæsit ad PRÆDESTINATIONIS scholasticum vocabulum, [sicut doctori Castillo indicavit,] cujus propriam vim, ac etymon haud satis assequebatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011729Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011729Z">
        <seg>The unlettered maiden was puzzled by the scholastic term PREDESTINATION, [as she indicated to Doctor Castillo,] whose true meaning and etymon she did not fully grasp.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rudis ista rhetorica est in auribus seculi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053120Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053120Z">
        <seg>That rhetoric is crude in the ears of the world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rupem dixissos adnatam solo, aut ingentem plumbi massam, nulla trochlearum vi attollendam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040124Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040124Z">
        <seg>A rock you would call firmly fixed to the ground, or a huge mass of lead, to be lifted by no power of pulleys.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rursus igitur chirurgi manu, ac post lene emplastrum, denique &amp; ferro opus fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034851Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034851Z">
        <seg>Thus, once again, by the surgeon's hand, and after a gentle plaster, it was finally necessary to use iron.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rursus interea succrevit curiosa multitudo, rursus etiam circa tumulum conglomerabantur impetuosæ turmæ, quas ut vidit archiepiscopus, gestu, signis (vox enim audiri præ sonoro strepitu non poterat) Patribus innuit, ut corpus Virginis in sacristiam deportarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030719Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030719Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile a curious crowd had again gathered, and once more impetuous throngs were pressing around the tomb. When the archbishop saw them, he motioned to the Fathers with gestures and signals—for his voice could not be heard above the loud din—to carry the Virgin's body into the sacristy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>S. Alexii presb.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043559Z">
        <seg>St. Alexius the Priest</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>S. Catharinam Senensem, cui modo ipsa famulata erat, famulæ suæ discrimini minime defuturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055256Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055256Z">
        <seg>Saint Catherine of Siena, to whom she herself had just been a servant, would by no means fail her servant in her hour of need.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>S. Chrysogoni Cardin.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043618Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043618Z">
        <seg>St. Chrysogonus, Cardinal</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>S. Honuphrii presbyter Cardin.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043550Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043550Z">
        <seg>Cardinal, Priest of St. Honorius</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>S. Marcelli presbyter Cardin.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043640Z">
        <seg>Cardinal S. Marcellus, priest</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>S. Petri ad Vincula presb.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043540Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043540Z">
        <seg>Saint Peter in Chains, Priest</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>S. Praxedis presbyt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043531Z">
        <seg>Saint Praxedes, presbyter</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>S. Stephani in Monte Cælio presb.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043627Z">
        <seg>Of St. Stephen, priest in the Celian Hill.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>SS. Quatuor Coronator.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043652Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043652Z">
        <seg>The Four Crowned Martyrs</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Saccum villis crassissimis gravem sibimet in formam indusii aptaverat, &amp; ne posset deprehendi pilosa ruditas, extremis manicis carbasina integumenta obduxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041857Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041857Z">
        <seg>He had fashioned a sack of the thickest wool into a tunic, and so that its hairy coarseness could not be detected, he had covered the cuffs of the sleeves with carbasin cloth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sacerdoti tamen sanctissimam Hostiam porrigenti, &amp; de more roganti apte &amp; expedite respondit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042809Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042809Z">
        <seg>However, when the priest presented the most holy Host and, as was the custom, asked, he responded appropriately and promptly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sacra Communione refecta, jejuna usque ad vesperam, subinde usque in sequentem diem, quandoque per octavam integram ea sola contenta ab omni alio cibo abstinuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042208Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042208Z">
        <seg>Having been refilled with Holy Communion, she fasted until evening, sometimes until the following day, and on occasion for a full week, content with that alone and abstaining from all other food.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sacrista ex pondere suspicatus aureos inesse torques, aut pretiosa monilia, digressus a Virgine explicuit involucrum, ac dissimilium auro thesaurum reperit, catenam recenti imbutam sanguine, quam frustillatim adhærescens caro &amp; pellicula variis locis conspicue satis interpunxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041801Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041801Z">
        <seg>The sacristan, suspicious from the weight that it contained gold torcs or precious jewels, stepped away from the Virgin, unfolded the wrapping, and discovered a treasure of miscellaneous gold—a chain freshly stained with blood, the flesh and membrane clinging to it in places, quite clearly interspersed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sacrista prodigii admonitus venit, vidit, adstupuit, certus, nec se nec suorum ullum attigisse coronam a die, qua collo divæ Virginis circumducta fuerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043550Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043550Z">
        <seg>The sacristan, warned of the miracle, came, saw, and was amazed, certain that neither he nor any of his own had touched the crown since the day it had been placed around the neck of the holy Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sacrum corpus deferebant insueto honore ecclesiæ cathedralis canonici, succedentibus in hoc munere certatim religionum superioribus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042948Z">
        <seg>They were carrying the sacred body with unusual honor to the cathedral church, the canons taking turns in this duty, with the superiors of the various religious orders competing to succeed one another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sacrum sui Ordinis scapulare, ut proprium suæ religiosæ militiæ vexillum, quo Deipara virgo nascentem Prædicatorem Ordinem insigniverat, expansum super stragulas videre voluit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042818Z">
        <seg>He wished to see the sacred scapular of his Order, as the proper standard of his religious militia, which the Virgin Mother had marked on the newborn Order of Preachers, spread out over the swaddling clothes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Saltem optabat ex illo, cui incumbebat, humaniori stratu, humi deponi, ac nudo in pavimento mori.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043001Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043001Z">
        <seg>At least he wished to be laid on the more humane layer of the couch he was leaning against, and to die naked on the floor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sancti Marci presb.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043608Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043608Z">
        <seg>Saint Mark the Priest</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sanctimoniales postmodum S. Doctoris ecclesiæ Augustini illam jam domo ad hoc egressam præstolabantur sacro hoc amictu [induendam;] &amp; nec frustra, ni ante aram beatissimæ Virginis sanctissimi Rosarii immobilem vis divina tamdiu detinuisset, quoad cœptum itineris propositum retractaret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041321Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041321Z">
        <seg>The nuns of St. Augustine, the Doctor of the Church, were later awaiting her, already gone from home for this purpose, to be clothed in this sacred vestment; and not in vain, had she not been held motionless by divine power before the altar of the Most Blessed Virgin of the Most Holy Rosary for so long, until she retracted the journey she had undertaken.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sanctus Thomas 2.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050829Z">
        <seg>Saint Thomas 2.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sane Rosam verecundæ taciturnitatis amantissimam solus animarum zelus disertam fecerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060738Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060738Z">
        <seg>Indeed, it was only the zeal of souls that had made the shy and taciturn Rosa eloquent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sane apud omnes (quotquot tunc Limæ vivebant) multorum opinione sanctimoniæ &amp; perfectionis fama celebres, ea sensim invaluerat constantissima opinio, Rosam spiritu Dei agere &amp; agi, dono sapientiæ abundare, scientia cælitus infusa gubernari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050708Z">
        <seg>Indeed, among all who then lived in Lima—celebrated by many for their sanctity and perfection—a most steadfast opinion had gradually prevailed: that Rosa was acted upon and carried along by the Spirit of God, abounded in the gift of wisdom, and was guided by knowledge poured down from heaven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sane devoto populo nil hac translatione gratius potuisse accidere, testatus est confluxus, qui exinde ut liberior, sic quotidie numerosior fuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031553Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the influx of devout people, which from that time on was both more free and daily more numerous, testified that nothing could have been more pleasing to them than this translation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sane per ipsos decem &amp; sex annos in hujus agonis acerbissimo tormento invicta perseveravit, &amp; (quod mireris amplius) conscia demum atque invita, sed tamen connivente matre.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042703Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042703Z">
        <seg>Indeed, for sixteen years she remained unconquered in the most bitter torment of this ordeal, and (what will surprise you even more) only at the end did she become aware of it and unwilling, though her mother looked the other way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sane quam corpori suo Rosa amica non fuerit, supremum hoc paradigma esto, quod Virgo cautissima nec delicias spiritus usque ad corporis solatium exundare sinebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041935Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041935Z">
        <seg>Indeed, if Rosa was not a friend to her own body, let this be the ultimate example: that the most cautious virgin did not allow the delights of the spirit to overflow into the solace of the body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sane videbatur eum unctio docuisse de omnibus, ut neque errate posset in Rosæ nomine, cui exinde pariter &amp; avia adquievit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034714Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034714Z">
        <seg>Indeed, it seemed that the anointing had taught him everything, so that he could not err in the name of Rose, to whom from then on his grandmother also consented.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sane vim narrationis transcendunt, quæ de Rosæ jejuniis jejune satis innotuerunt: nam (ut diximus) latent plurima.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040958Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040958Z">
        <seg>Indeed, they surpass the force of a narrative that has become rather dryly familiar through the bare accounts of Rose's fasts; for (as we have said) many things lie hidden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Santacrucius.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043630Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043630Z">
        <seg>Saint Cross</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sat est summatim &amp; obiter nomina perstringere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040010Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040010Z">
        <seg>It is enough to briefly and incidentally list the names.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sat esto, quod Rosæ ea orationis vis fuerit, qua volucres cæli, qua terræ germina sibi attraxit in alternam adorandi societatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045351Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045351Z">
        <seg>Let it suffice that for Rose the power of that prayer was such that she drew to herself the birds of the air and the earth's seedlings into a reciprocal fellowship of worship.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Satis est, imo plusquam satis: nam tibi &amp; lanam in lanienam scivisti convertere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042834Z">
        <seg>It is enough, indeed more than enough: for you have known how to turn wool into a slaughterhouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Saxa enim, &amp; cruces, quibus parvula, usus flagellorum ignara, a Mariana ancilla, suarum mortificationum fere sola conscia, ad orationem, vel ad hortum pergens onerabatur, in ferreas catenas commutavit, quas aptavit in flagra, quibus ad sancti Dominici exemplum&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;singulis noctibus usque ad rivulorum sanguinis copiosam effusionem, vel pro ærumnis sanctæ Ecclesiæ, vel pro periclitantis ærumnis sanctæ Ecclesiæ, vel pro periclitantis regni, aut urbis Limensis necessitatibus, vel pro compensandis peccatorum injuriis, vel pro expiandis defunctorum animabus, vel ad impetranda divina subsidia in extremo agone constitutis, cruentam se Deo victimam ad ejus justam iram avertendam offerebat, horrescentibus quandoque domesticis ad tam diros catenarum ictus, quarum usu interdicto, earum una sic triplicato ductu lumbos latenter diu præcinxit, ut numquam nisi maximorum cruciatuum ischiadis vi apparere potuerit, quæ postea nonnisi interveniente miraculo soluta fuit, cujus annulos post virginis obitum miram &amp; peregrinam suaveolentiam spirare compertum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041526Z">
        <seg>For she exchanged the stones and crosses, with which the little girl, ignorant of the use of the whip, was burdened by the handmaid Mary—almost the sole witness of her own mortifications—whenever she went to prayer or to the garden, for iron chains, which she fitted into scourges, with which, following the example of Saint Dominic, she scourged herself each night until the blood flowed copiously, either for the sufferings of the holy Church, or for the sufferings of the holy Church in peril, or for the peril of the kingdom, or for the needs of the city of Lima, or for making amends for the injuries of sinners, or for the expiation of the souls of the departed, or for obtaining divine assistance for those in their last agony, she offered herself to God as a bloody victim to avert His just wrath, sometimes horrifying her household with the dire blows of the chains, whose use was forbidden, she secretly girded her loins for a long time with one of them, twisted three times, so that she could never appear except under the force of the greatest sciatica pains, which was later loosed only by a miracle; whose rings, after the virgin's death, were found to give off a marvelous and strange fragrance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Scatebant his undique parietes, personabat ostiolum, implebatur fenestra perpetuo commeantium refluxu;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043757Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043757Z">
        <seg>The walls were dripping with them from every side, the doorway echoed, the window was filled by the constant backwash of passersby.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sciebant ambæ, quidquid Rosa a B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052547Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052547Z">
        <seg>Both of them knew whatever Rosa had from B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Scio, hæc retrimenta sine scrupulo aut culpa promiscue calcari ab aliis, horum incuriam nec damnare ausim, nec arguere præsumo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054918Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054918Z">
        <seg>I know that these shortcomings are trampled by others without scruple or guilt; I neither dare condemn their carelessness nor presume to reproach them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Scito, illum in ordine suo perseveraturum, &amp; suppone, te illum jam videre professum”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012650Z">
        <seg>Know that he will persevere in his order, and suppose that you already see him professed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Scripsit (jubente Didaco Alvarezio) Castillus iste nobilem tractatum de iis, quæ sibi inter contemplationum radios ac lumina occurrerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050104Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050104Z">
        <seg>At the behest of Diego Alvarez, this Castille wrote a noble treatise on those things that occurred to him amid the rays and lights of contemplation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Scripsit ab aurora usque ad Salutationis angelicæ pulsum vespertinum, nulla digitorum lassitudine, nullo nervorum cruciatu, brachio levissimo ac supra morem expedito.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032346Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032346Z">
        <seg>He wrote from dawn until the evening ringing of the Angelus, with no fatigue in his fingers, no torment of his nerves, his arm exceedingly light and unusually agile.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sebastiana de Vega aliquando cum marito doctore Cypriano de Medina, regii tribunalis advocato, peregre Succacaram profectura, dum stapedi insistens, sellam nititur conscendere, mula recedente, varicavit, ac osse circa inguinem luxato, intolerabiles dolores passa est, ut neque in lecto mutare latus, neque robustiori cuiquam operi manum adhibere potuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040416Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040416Z">
        <seg>Sebastiana de Vega, sometime before setting out with her husband, Doctor Cyprian de Medina, a lawyer for the royal tribunal, on a journey to Succaram, while standing on the stirrup and trying to mount her saddle as her mule backed away, dislocated a bone in her groin and suffered such unbearable pain that she could neither turn over in bed nor apply herself to any strenuous work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Secretam cogitationem, quam nulli umquam aperuerat, neque aperitura erat filia quæstoris, accurate ei Rosa recitavit, &amp; saluberrima documenta præscripsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042638Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042638Z">
        <seg>Rosa accurately recounted to him the secret thought that the quæstor's daughter had never revealed to anyone, nor ever would, and prescribed the most salutary instructions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Secundo pannum hujus amictus contexunto 600 Salutationes angelicæ, totidem vicibus &lt;b1&gt;Salve Regina&lt;/b1&gt;, 15 Rosaria, &amp; 15 dies jejunii, in reverentiam lætissimi gaudii, dum visitabat cognatam suam Elizabeth.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011134Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011134Z">
        <seg>Secondly, let 600 Angelic Salutations, as many Hail Marys, 15 Rosaries, and 15 days of fasting be woven into the cloth of this vestment, in reverence of the most joyful gladness, when she visited her kinswoman Elizabeth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Secundo pannum hujus amictus contexunto 600 Salutationes angelicæ, totidem vicibus &lt;b4&gt;Salve Regina&lt;/b4&gt;, 15 Rosaria, &amp; 15 dies jejunii, in reverentiam lætissimi gaudii, dum visitabat cognatam suam Elizabeth.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054611Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054611Z">
        <seg>Secondly, let 600 Angelic Salutations, as many Hail Marys, 15 Rosaries, and 15 days of fasting be woven into the cloth of this vestment, in reverence of the most joyful gladness, when she visited her kinswoman Elizabeth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Secuta fuerat labentes mollior terra, &amp; pene utrumque operiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034252Z">
        <seg>A softer earth had followed the sloping ground and almost covered both.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed Dominica Palmarum, quam Galli vulgo&lt;/e1&gt; Paque-fleurie &lt;e2&gt;vocant, ab Hispanis&lt;/e2&gt; Pascua florida &lt;e3&gt;vel&lt;/e3&gt; Domingo de Ramos &lt;e4&gt;nominatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T233133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035155Z">
        <seg>But Palm Sunday, which the French commonly call&lt;/e1&gt; Paque-fleurie, &lt;e2&gt;is called by the Spaniards&lt;/e2&gt; Pascua florida &lt;e3&gt;or&lt;/e3&gt; Domingo de Ramos&lt;e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed Virgo diffidentiam mulieris leniter perstringens, “Tam modicam (ait) fidem habes in auxilio Reginæ sanctissimi Rosarii, cui novitium Rodericum commendavi?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012647Z">
        <seg>But the Virgin, gently rebuking the woman's lack of confidence, said, "Do you have so little faith in the help of the Queen of the Most Holy Rosary, to whom I entrusted the novice Roderick?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed Virgo memor propositi, ac suæ pulchritudinis obtundendæ, quam acuendæ, solicitior, hæc omnia defixis humi oculis excipiebat invicto silentio, &amp; quotidianam hanc cramben usu ac dissimulatione surda concoquere assuescebat, quoadusque ultima materni præcepti violentia deerat, cui refragari aut lentius parere æquale nefas putabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035418Z">
        <seg>But the Virgin, mindful of her purpose, and more anxious to blunt her own beauty than to sharpen it, she received all this with fixed eyes on the ground in unconquerable silence, and grew accustomed to digest this daily drudgery with habitual use and deaf dissimulation, until the final violence of her mother's command was at hand, against which to rebel or to obey more slowly she deemed equally forbidden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed Virgo pietate saucia, conabatur has inutiles afflictæ matris lachrymas sistere, rogans cohiberi fletum, quasi Sponsi verbis diceret: “Nolite flere super me”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013545Z">
        <seg>But the Virgin, wounded by piety, tried to stop these useless tears of her afflicted mother, begging that the weeping be restrained, as if speaking in the words of the Spouse: "Do not weep for me."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed ad alia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054503Z">
        <seg>But to other matters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed ad emollitiones cordium obstinatorum, Rosa mediatrice factas, revertamur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032755Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032755Z">
        <seg>But let us return to the softening of obstinate hearts, accomplished through the mediation of the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed ad graviora.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061609Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061609Z">
        <seg>But to graver matters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed animosa Virgo, ut comperit anxium Gundisalvum hac formidine cruciari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054053Z">
        <seg>But the spirited Virgin, upon learning that the anxious Gundisalvus was being tormented by this fear;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed anno 1625 illam præcesserat alia ejusdem Pontificis circa eamdem materiam, quæ apud citatum Cherubinum tomo 4 Bullarii pag. 299 legi potest.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031813Z">
        <seg>But in 1625 another one by the same Pontiff on the same subject had preceded it, which can be read in the cited Cherubinus, volume 4 of the Bullarium, page 299.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed confestim Sponsus gloriosus Virgini se stitit conspicuum, dixitque: “Audi Rosa, ni tecum ego fuissem, tunc victoriam reportasset”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052218Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052218Z">
        <seg>But at once the glorious Spouse appeared to the Virgin, and said, "Hear, Rose, if I had not been with you, then victory would have been won."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed cordata Filia, ut metum eximeret, blande modesteque detexit secretum, nempe merum hunc Sponsi ludum esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051043Z">
        <seg>But the prudent daughter, to dispel the fear, gently and modestly revealed the secret, namely that this was merely a game of the suitor's.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed correxit pertinacem nocturna in somnis visio: putabat horrisono terræ motu totam ab imis fundamentis everti Limam, interque hos pavores, vel invita se Rosæ commendabat præsidio, ac tanta quidem contentione vocis, ut de solo vociferandi conatu fuerit expergefacta.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040720Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040720Z">
        <seg>But a persistent nightmare corrected her: she believed that a horrifying earthquake had overturned all of Lima from its very foundations, and amid these terrors, even unwillingly she entrusted herself to Rosa's protection, and with such strain in her voice that she awoke merely from the effort of crying out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed diversissimum erat, quod reipsa gerebatur: nam herbæ, quas sibi Virgo in pastum singulari cura selegerat, amarissimæ erant, &amp; agresti saporis malignitate torquebant palatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041209Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041209Z">
        <seg>But what was actually happening was quite different: for the herbs the Virgin had selected with special care for her own nourishment were exceedingly bitter, and their rustic, malignant flavor tormented the palate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed divinæ buccellæ refecta Pabulo, cum a templo redibat domum, præibat matrem, &amp; hortabatur sequi, velut si in fortitudine Cibi illius usque ad montem Horeb&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; fuisset ambulandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011348Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011348Z">
        <seg>But having been refilled with the divine morsel, Paul, as he was returning home from the temple, outpaced his mother and urged her to follow, as if, by the strength of that Food, they were to walk all the way to Mount Horeb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed divinæ buccellæ refecta Pabulo, cum a templo redibat domum, præibat matrem, &amp; hortabatur sequi, velut si in fortitudine Cibi illius usque ad montem Horeb&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; fuisset ambulandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055938Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055938Z">
        <seg>But having been refilled with the divine morsel, Paul, as he was returning home from the temple, outpaced his mother and urged her to follow, as if, by the strength of that Food, they were to walk all the way to Mount Horeb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed en arcanum cælestis providentiæ obstaculum a matre fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040038Z">
        <seg>But behold, the mother was an obstacle to the secret of divine providence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed evoluta septimana, ac superveniente totidem recentium panum demenso, inveniebatur, Rosam ex octo panibus permodicis, unum dumtaxat cum dimidio per totam illam hebdomadam comedisse, restitutis nimirum sex reliquis cum dimidio, qui in penu superfuerant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041327Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041327Z">
        <seg>But at the end of the week, with an equal number of fresh loaves having arrived, it was found that Rosa had eaten only one and a half of the eight very small loaves over the entire week, and had returned the remaining six and a half that had been left in the pantry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed exemit solicitudinem Petro secura Rosa, dixitque cum defuncti anima feliciter ac bene agi, jam illam regioni lucis adscriptam frui perenni refrigerio, ut ei nequaquam opus fuerit redire ad alterutrum, pro stipe suffragiorum emendicanda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060856Z">
        <seg>But the secure Rose dispelled Peter's anxiety and said that the deceased's soul was doing well and happily, already enrolled in the region of light and enjoying perpetual refreshment, so that it would by no means need to return to either place to beg for the alms of suffrages.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed hanc notitiam ab Hispanis istorum locorum peritioribus exspectabimus.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040218Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040218Z">
        <seg>But we will await this information from the Spaniards more experienced in these parts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed hinc nondum scimus, utrum die 24 Augusti an sequente obierit ob rationem, quam superius in Annotatis allegavinums.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043029Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043029Z">
        <seg>But from this we still do not know whether he died on August 24 or the following day, for the reason we cited above in the annotations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed hoc loco maxime mihi placent sequentia auctoris verba:&lt;/e0&gt; Hanc nectaream sanguinis &amp; aquæ mammillam non ori, sed cordi Virginis benigne applicuit &lt;e1&gt;Christus, de quo sermo est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051524Z">
        <seg>But here I especially like the author's following words: "This nectarous nipple of blood and water Christ, of whom we speak, kindly applied not to the mouth but to the heart of the Virgin."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed ignoramus, an truncus hujus plantæ tam magnus sit, ut ad hunc usum adhiberi possit.&lt;/e4&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043159Z">
        <seg>But we do not know whether the trunk of this plant is large enough to be used for this purpose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed in vacuo animæ exinanitæ, respondebat nemo, nec echo quidem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045540Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045540Z">
        <seg>But in the void of the emptied soul, no one answered, not even an echo.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed inde ad quemlibet non est extendenda consequentia, cum Latius capite mox citato sic moneat:&lt;/e0&gt; Immodicus usus potionis e cacao confectæ plurimarum infirmitatum &amp; morborum causa est, quia generat obstructiones, corrumpit colorem, &amp; inducit cacochymiam &amp; similes pravos affectus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052401Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052401Z">
        <seg>But from that one cannot draw a conclusion for everyone, since Latius, in the chapter soon to be cited, warns as follows: "Excessive use of a potion made from cacao is the cause of many infirmities and diseases, because it produces obstructions, corrupts the complexion, and induces cacochymia and similar perverse affections."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed magis dolebat Rosa, quod illo die, qui sibi anniversarius erat assumpti Dominicani habitus, prohiberetur seraphicæ matris suæ festivum habitum solito religionis ministerio contrectare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055355Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055355Z">
        <seg>But Rosa was even more distressed that on the day which was the anniversary of the Dominican's assumption she was forbidden to handle the festive habit of her seraphic mother in the usual religious service.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed mater, ipso noctis illius conticinio memor, aliquid penes se esse reliquiarum, quæ Rosam attigerant, inopiæ suæ scriniola excussit, particulam vestis cum ramusculo ginestæ&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; (cui Virgo olim indormierat) invenit: cum his properavit ad algidum cadaver, cœpitque longis &amp; anxiis precibus Rosam invocare in auxilium: denique fide jam robustior, ac velut Rosæ vivificos e reliquiis odores prolicitura, patellæ carbones, his ramusculum &amp; alterius particulæ aliquot filamenta imposuit: mox defunctam filiolam suffumigavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033130Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033130Z">
        <seg>But the mother, remembering that very stillness of the night, rummaged through the little box of her poverty for some relics that had touched Rose, and found a scrap of clothing with a sprig of broom (on which the Virgin had once slept): With these she hurried to the cold corpse and began to invoke Rose for help with long and anxious prayers. Finally, her faith now stronger, and as if about to draw forth from the relics the life-giving odors of Rose, she placed the twig and a few threads of the other fragment on the embers in the brazier and soon suffused the dead daughter with their smoke.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed materfamilias paginam itidem chartaceam, cui Rosæ imago inerat, produxit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040528Z">
        <seg>But the matron likewise produced a paper on which the image of Rosa was inscribed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed mirum dictu!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035341Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035341Z">
        <seg>But strange to say!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed mirum est, hæc Rosæ nequaquam fuisse mira, utpote cui a beata Catharina Senensi ceu matre, pene quotidiana erant ex mutuis commerciis solatia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055501Z">
        <seg>But it is strange that these things were by no means remarkable to Rose, for she had almost daily consolations from the blessed Catherine of Siena, as from a mother, through their mutual communications.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed neque tunc Rosæ defuit par dolori tam moroso constantia, quantam ab illa imo ab ulla ætate sperari posse chirurgus diffidebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034919Z">
        <seg>But even then, Rose did not lack the steadfastness to endure such a stubborn pain, a quality the surgeon doubted could be expected from her or indeed from anyone at her age.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed neutiquam hoc responso contenta mater, ac forte persuasa, ex taciturnitate patientis Filiæ retardari opem medicinæ, voluit exponi sibi minutim singula;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013511Z">
        <seg>But by no means content with this answer, the mother, perhaps convinced that the patient's silence was delaying medical help, insisted on having every detail explained to her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed non est nostrum litesillas componere vel dirimere.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041430Z">
        <seg>But it is not our place to settle or decide petty disputes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed nos annuam illius memoriam nullis antiquis Martyrologiis hactenus inscriptam legimus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031800Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031800Z">
        <seg>But we have found his memorial in no ancient Martyrologies, though it is celebrated annually.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed parens, matronis, quam Filiæ complacendi studiosior, jubebat hanc omnino carnibus vesci, ne inurbana singularitate adstrictioris parsimoniæ, videretur mensæ honorem profanare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041033Z">
        <seg>But the mother, a matron more eager to please her daughter, ordered her to eat nothing but meat, so that she would not appear to be profaning the honor of the table with an uncouth singularity of parsimony.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed paulo post aliud successit ex religiosæ personæ liberali dono, quod Rosam undequaque contentam reddidit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041835Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041835Z">
        <seg>But shortly afterward, another thing happened thanks to the generous gift of a religious person, which made Rosa content in every way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed pergamus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040620Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040620Z">
        <seg>But let's go on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed plura sentiebat in se Rosa, quam verba ista exprimerent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045816Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045816Z">
        <seg>But Rosa felt more within herself than those words could express.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed postea inspexi librum, qui inscribitur&lt;/e1&gt; Medicina pharmaceutica, &lt;e2&gt;&amp; Bruxellis anno 1681 Flandrice impressus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034451Z">
        <seg>But later I examined the book entitled Pharmaceutical Medicine, which was printed in Flemish in Brussels in 1681.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed quandoquidem hoc animal est terrestre &amp; ad magnitudinem porcelli accedit, illudhoc loco nequaquam indicatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034435Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034435Z">
        <seg>But since this animal is terrestrial and approaches the size of a piglet, it is by no means indicated here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed quia hæc Dominica erat, quæ (nisi pro præsenti defuncto) lugubribus Officiis interpolari facile non debet, hortatu archiepisciopi eligitur dies vicinior minus impedita.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031224Z">
        <seg>But since this was a Sunday, which (except for the present deceased) ought not easily to be interspersed with mourning offices, at the archbishop's urging a nearer, less occupied day was chosen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed quinam hi effectus?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050603Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050603Z">
        <seg>But what are these effects?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed respondente illo commodius hanc differri posse in crastinum, tempus non defore, sat tempestive se postero mane adfuturum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030220Z">
        <seg>But when he replied that it could more conveniently be postponed until tomorrow, that time would not be lacking, and that he would arrive the next morning in good time;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed revertendum ad valetudinaria.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034023Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034023Z">
        <seg>But we must return to the hospitals.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed rudimenta hæc erant ac tempestiva præludia animi corpusculum proprium ceu alienum tractaturi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034928Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034928Z">
        <seg>But these were the rudiments and timely preludes of a mind about to treat its own little body as if it were another's.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed simul timorem exemit superna irradiatio, Rosam erudiens, tempus fore, quo selectum illud germen floridæ propaginis colligatur in unum S. Catharinæ Senensis hortum conclusum, septoque proprio segregetur a profani seculi commercio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062557Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062557Z">
        <seg>But at the same time, a heavenly radiance dispelled the fear, instructing Rosa that the time would come when that chosen germ of the flourishing stock would be gathered into one enclosed garden of St. Catherine of Siena, and separated by its own fence from the commerce of the profane world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed statim ut Rosei sepulchri terram offensæ parti imposuit, placide consopitus, totam noctem quieti indulsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035536Z">
        <seg>But as soon as he laid the earth of Rose's tomb upon the offended party, he quietly fell asleep and spent the entire night in rest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed tandem Numinis providentia rem diutius occultari non sivit, effecitque, ut tantæ virtutis exemplum, in plurium opportunam notitiam promanaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042220Z">
        <seg>But at last, by the providence of the Deity, the matter could no longer be hidden, and it brought about that such an example of virtue would come to the attention of many.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed tandem deprehendi, nomen&lt;/e0&gt; dinomilus &lt;e1&gt;compositum esse ex adverbio Græco&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;δὴν&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;e3&gt;id est&lt;/e3&gt; diu, &lt;e4&gt;&amp; verbo&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;ὁμιλέω&lt;/s5&gt;, &lt;e6&gt;hoc est&lt;/e6&gt;, colloquor &lt;e7&gt;aut&lt;/e7&gt; conversor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052317Z">
        <seg>But I finally realized that the name&lt;/e0&gt; dinomilus is composed of the Greek adverb&lt;/e1&gt; δὴν&lt;/s2&gt; that is&lt;/e3&gt; long, &amp; the verb&lt;/e4&gt; ὁμιλέω&lt;/s5&gt;, that is&lt;/e6&gt;, I converse or&lt;/e7&gt; conversational.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed vicit invictus Rosæ animus: prius enim Cantæ, ubi cum suis morabatur, per quadriennium semel tantum a matre jussa domo exivit: post exemplo suæ magistræ rigidiore inedia vultus venustatem imminuere, veste humili &amp; despecta corporis agilitatem celare, hominumque prospectum, quoad poterat, declinare curavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041306Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041306Z">
        <seg>But the unconquerable spirit of Rosa prevailed: for at Cante, where she was staying with her own people, she left home at her mother's command only once in four years; then, following her teacher's example, she strove to lessen the beauty of her face by harsher fasting, to hide the agility of her body in humble and scorned clothing, and to avoid the gaze of men as much as she could.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed, deposito anulo, cessarunt gaudia, cessarunt vincula, surrexit liber e sella, qui paulo ante tenebatur in captivitate lætitiæ nuptialis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044706Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044706Z">
        <seg>But once the ring was removed, the festivities ceased, the bonds were broken, and the freed man rose from his seat, who had just a little while before been held captive in the joyful bonds of marriage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sedebat labori manuum cum puellis intenta Rosa, cui mater innuit, audiret Vincentium, deque pretio ex more, &amp; æquo coram se convenirent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060617Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060617Z">
        <seg>Rosa sat intent on the girls' handiwork, when her mother motioned for her to listen to Vincent and to meet him face to face, as was the custom, to discuss the price.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Seipsam redarguebat supinæ ignaviæ, vociferabatur, se indignam communi aëre, mirabatur, se vivam terræ hiatu nondum absorberi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041231Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041231Z">
        <seg>She reproached herself for her supine cowardice, cried out that she was unworthy of the common air, and wondered that she had not yet been swallowed by an earth-gulf while alive.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Semel cujusdam occulta seu arcta necessitas ad virginem relata fuit, sed cum aliud, unde subveniret, ad manum non esset, octo dierum alimoniam (contenta interim solo pane &amp; aqua) sibi detraxit pro egentis subsidio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061026Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061026Z">
        <seg>Once, when a certain person's hidden or urgent need was reported to the virgin, but since no other help was at hand, she withdrew eight days' worth of food (living in the meantime on only bread and water) to provide relief for the needy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Semel cum a matre invitaretur ad secum obeunda foris urbanarum salutationum officia, Virgo juxta domesticum furnum transiens, saxum a clibani orificio studiose sibi attraxit in pedem, eoque vulnere &amp; contusione obtinuit, ut non ficte claudicans residere domi sineretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043326Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043326Z">
        <seg>Once, when her mother was urging her to accompany her on the customary rounds of urban greetings, the virgin, passing by the domestic furnace, eagerly drew a stone from the furnace's opening onto her foot, and from that wound and contusion she was allowed to remain at home, limping not feignedly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Semel curiosa parens in horti sepimento vasculum reperit felle vervecino plenum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041219Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041219Z">
        <seg>Once a curious mother found a pot full of goat's bile in the garden fence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Semel ergo sic abdicato exclusoque mundo muliebri, haud visum Rosæ difficile, in ceteris quamquam arduis parenti obedire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035452Z">
        <seg>Once, then, having thus abdicated and been excluded from the feminine world, it did not seem difficult to Rosa to obey her parents in all other matters, however arduous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Semel extra consuetum tempus quid modicum sumere coacta, cum illud stomacho retinere non posset, reddidit, ac velut intemperantiæ rea, seipsam decem dierum rigida inedia damnavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041349Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041349Z">
        <seg>Once, forced to take a small amount outside her usual mealtime, she could not keep it down, so she returned it and, as if guilty of intemperance, condemned herself to a strict ten-day fast.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Semel hanc in imaginaria visione sic allocuta est virgo: “Laborandum est, o mater: nam permagna res est cæleste præmium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031946Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031946Z">
        <seg>Once, in an imaginary vision, the virgin spoke thus: "We must work, O mother, for the heavenly reward is a very great thing."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Semianimis in suo illo extremi doloris extremo lectulo dolebat, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; in ea usque ad mortem perseverare cupiens,]&lt;/s2&gt; altero illo se carere lectulo extreme dolorifico.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042955Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042955Z">
        <seg>Semianimus lay suffering on his deathbed of extreme pain, wishing to persevere in it until death, and deprived of that other, most painful bed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Semianimis in suo illo extremi doloris extremo lectulo dolebat, [&amp; in ea usque ad mortem perseverare cupiens,] altero illo se carere lectulo extreme dolorifico.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003510Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003510Z">
        <seg>Semianimus lay on his own deathbed of extreme pain, [and in it, desiring to persevere until death,] deprived of that other deathbed of extreme pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sensit infirmus illomet puncto temporis, quo nuntium acceperat, dolores suos mitigari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061457Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061457Z">
        <seg>The sick man felt his pains eased at the very moment he had received the message.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sentiebat &amp; Rosa ex mirifico cælestium sapore ac pergustu suum dulcescere spiritum, inebriari mentem, saginari animum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041944Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041944Z">
        <seg>She felt that from the marvelous, heavenly flavor and savor, her spirit was sweetened, her mind intoxicated, her soul enriched.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sentiebat ægra incerti mali certa incrementa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051056Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051056Z">
        <seg>She felt the certain growths of an uncertain evil.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Seorsim id postea anno MDCXXX vidua judicibus apostolicis aperuit, addens;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032200Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032200Z">
        <seg>Separately, she later revealed this to the apostolic judges in 1630, adding;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Seraphini Puglisi ex Ordine beatæ Mariæ de Monte Carmelo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043258Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043258Z">
        <seg>Seraphim Puglisi of the Order of the Blessed Mary of Mount Carmel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Serpebat incertus per urbem rumufculus, sacram imaginem de privato oratorio auferendam iri, velut publico ac celebriori loco dignissimam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054038Z">
        <seg>A rumor was spreading uncertainly through the city that the sacred image was to be removed from the private oratory, as though it were most worthy of a public and more celebrated place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Servus erat quæstoris, intromitti petiit, adferre se a quæstoris consorte, quod Rosæ traderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051716Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051716Z">
        <seg>He was the quaestor's servant; he asked to be admitted, saying he had come from the quaestor's associate to deliver something to Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sex menses integros sic dimidiata languit, donec in templo sancti Dominici exaltato in sandapila Rosæ corpusculo, illuc deportata fuit: ubi, ut defunctæ Virginis faciem bis terve attigit, repente inflatio detumuit, &amp; parvula suis parentibus reddita, post biduum per ædes sana cucurrit, nulla interim alia medicina adhibita.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034144Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034144Z">
        <seg>For six full months she languished thus half-alive, until her body was carried to the high altar of the Church of Saint Dominic, where, as soon as she touched the face of the deceased Virgin two or three times, the swelling suddenly subsided, and the little girl, returned to her parents, ran about the house healthy after two days, with no other medicine applied in the meantime.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sexenni virgini Josephæ de Zarate musculos gutturales atrox undique inflammarat synanche, ut nec cibum glutire, nec os sine tormento potuerit aperire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035412Z">
        <seg>A severe case of synanchia had inflamed the throat muscles of six-year-old virgin Joseph of Zarate so severely that she could neither swallow food nor open her mouth without agony.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sexennis, feria quarta, sexta, sabbatho (ni mater aut medicus ad aliud cogerent) omni prorsus abstinebat obsonio, nil præter panem &amp; aquam degustans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041014Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041014Z">
        <seg>On Wednesdays, Fridays, and Saturdays (unless his mother or a doctor compelled him otherwise), he abstained entirely from all food, tasting nothing but bread and water.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sexto ramusculum Virgineis suis manibus gestandum componunto, 33 orationes Dominicæ, totidem Salutationes angelicæ, cum versu, &lt;b4&gt;Gloria Patri, Salve Regina&lt;/b4&gt;, totidemque rosariis laudum divinarum, &amp; rursus totidem rosariis de laudibus Virginis, in reverentiam 33 annorum, quos Dominus meus Jesus vixit in terris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011143Z">
        <seg>On the sixth, let them prepare a small branch to be carried in their Virgin's hands, 33 Our Fathers, as many Hail Marys, with the verse, &lt;b4&gt;Glory be to the Father, Hail Mary&lt;/b4&gt;, and as many rosaries of divine praises, and again as many rosaries of the Virgin's praises, in reverence of the 33 years that my Lord Jesus lived on earth."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sexto ramusculum Virgineis suis manibus gestandum componunto, 33 orationes Dominicæ, totidem Salutationes angelicæ, cum versu, &lt;b7&gt;Gloria Patri, Salve Regina&lt;/b7&gt;, totidemque rosariis laudum divinarum, &amp; rursus totidem rosariis de laudibus Virginis, in reverentiam 33 annorum, quos Dominus meus Jesus vixit in terris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054627Z">
        <seg>On the sixth, let them prepare a small sprig to be carried in their Virgin's hands, 33 Our Fathers, as many Hail Marys, with the verse, &lt;b7&gt;Glory be to the Father, Hail Mary&lt;/b7&gt;, and as many rosaries of divine praises, and again as many rosaries of the Virgin's praises, in reverence of the 33 years that my Lord Jesus lived on earth."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sfortia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043544Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043544Z">
        <seg>Fortune</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Si cum familiaribus, matronis, virginibus sermocinandum, prima Rosæ vox erat: “Amemus Deum nostrum, amemus”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053133Z">
        <seg>When speaking with relatives, matrons, and virgins, Rose's first words were: "Let us love our God, let us love."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Si de quacumque persona absente proferendum encomium, rem totam Rosa hoc absolvebat compendio: “Deum fortiter ac serio amat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053136Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053136Z">
        <seg>If an encomium was to be offered for any absent person, Rosa would sum up the whole matter with this concise summary: "She loves God with great strength and seriousness."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Si lubet, consule Castellum in Lexico medico, ubi pag. 314 &amp; sequente hunc morbum variasque ejus species pluribus describit.&lt;/e5&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043336Z">
        <seg>If you wish, consult Castell in the medical lexicon, where on page 314 and following he describes this disease and its various species in greater detail.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Si quis autem hoc attentare præsumpserit, indignationem omnipotentis Dei, &amp; beatorum Petri &amp; Pauli apostolorum ejus se noverit incursurum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043432Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043432Z">
        <seg>If anyone, however, presumes to attempt this, let him know that he will incur the indignation of Almighty God and of His blessed apostles Peter and Paul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Si rogabatur, qui valeret: nonnisi bene sibi esse respondebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052643Z">
        <seg>If he was asked how he was, he would answer that he was fine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic &amp; alacri omnium sensuum officio Oleum infirmorum moribunda accepit, quo potius ad triumphum, quam ad agonem sensit se animari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042812Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042812Z">
        <seg>Thus, with the eager cooperation of all her senses, she received the oil of the sick and dying, by which she felt herself animated more for triumph than for agony.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic &amp; alia vice taurus alter recta via in quadrigam, qua Rosa vehebatur cum matre &amp; aliis, cursum libravit: ceteris fugam tentantibus, sola Rosa, oculis in cælum jactis, prædixit, taurum usque ad rhedam non procursurum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042536Z">
        <seg>Thus, on another occasion, a bull once again charged straight at the chariot in which Rosa was riding with her mother and others, throwing it off course; while the others tried to flee, only Rosa, with eyes cast heavenward, predicted that the bull would not rush as far as the carriage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic Gundisalvi uxor, quandoque pomeridiana hora quarta invenit Rosam eodem geniculatam angulo, quem in puncto meridiei, ad meditationis exercitium occuparat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044907Z">
        <seg>Thus Gundisalvi's wife, at around the fourth hour of the afternoon, found Rosa in the same kneeling corner she had occupied at noon for the exercise of meditation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic Rosa, quod dura sibi quæreret, durissima sufferre debuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042806Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042806Z">
        <seg>Thus Rosa, who sought hard things for herself, had to endure the hardest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic Rosa, tota foris, &amp; tota intus, dum exterius satagebat circa frequens ministerium, simul in secretissimo cordis thalamo deliciabatur cum Sponso.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044846Z">
        <seg>Thus Rose, wholly outward and wholly inward, while outwardly she busied herself with frequent ministry, was at the same time delighted with the Spouse in the secretest chamber of her heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic affecta, domum rediit, per viam sic Deum alloquens: “Domine, si ex tuo æterno placito, tuique nominis gloria est, quod ego a seculari isthoc ad religiosum aliquando statum transeam, perge operari in me, quod mirabiliter cœpisti;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062948Z">
        <seg>Thus affected, she returned home, speaking to God along the way in this way: "Lord, if it is according to your eternal will, and for the glory of your name, that I should at some time pass from this secular to a religious state, go on working in me what you have so wonderfully begun;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic afflicta, per octiduum lecto decubuit, quotidianis tot morborum incrementis haud leviter adtremiscens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040838Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040838Z">
        <seg>Thus afflicted, she lay in bed for eight days, trembling greatly at the daily increase of her ailments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic agminatim &amp; sensim dilapsa plebeia frequentia, proceribus aperiri cœpit liber aditus, spatiumque accedendi propius ad venerandum corpus Famulæ Dei, [quod adhuc spirare videbatur,] manusque innocentissimas deosculandas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030716Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030716Z">
        <seg>Thus, as the plebeian crowd filed away in orderly procession and gradually dispersed, the nobles were granted free access and a closer approach to venerate the body of the Handmaid of God, [which still seemed to breathe,] and to kiss her most innocent hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic compertum in examine, de quo infra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044811Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044811Z">
        <seg>Thus it was established in the examination, about which see below, chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic demum a planta pedis usque verticem capitis nil ei residuum fuit, quo voluntarios cruciatus exciperet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041925Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041925Z">
        <seg>Thus, from the sole of his foot to the crown of his head, there was nothing left in him to endure voluntary torments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic denique assueta jejuniis erat, ut ejus creberrimis infirmitatibus nullum aliud prodesset medicamentum, quam panis &amp; aqua: sic enim divina gratia curat, &amp; sanat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041148Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041148Z">
        <seg>She had become so accustomed to fasting that for her very frequent infirmities no other medicine was of any use, only bread and water: for thus divine grace heals and makes whole.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic ergo incurabilitatis suæ culpam imputabat totam sibi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045723Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045723Z">
        <seg>Thus, he blamed himself entirely for the fault of his incurability.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic etiam extraneis, quotquot aderant, intime &amp; secrete inhæsit jucunditatis stimulus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042917Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042917Z">
        <seg>Thus, even the outsiders, whoever they were, were intimately and secretly seized by a stimulus of pleasure.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic etiam jugis planctus materies ei erant quæcumque aliæ infidelium nationes.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042325Z">
        <seg>Thus, the constant complaints of the Jews were also a matter against any other nations of unbelievers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic excrescentia cancrosa in naribus appellatur&lt;/e3&gt; polypus &lt;e4&gt;a similitudine animalis, quod hoc nomen habet, ut Sennertus tomo 2 Operum medicorum pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035238Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035238Z">
        <seg>Thus a cancerous growth in the nose is called a polyp because of its resemblance to the animal of the same name, as Sennertus states in volume II of his Medical Works, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic excrescentia cancrosa in naribus appellatur&lt;/e3&gt; polypus &lt;e4&gt;a similitudine animalis, quod hoc nomen habet, ut Sennertus tomo 2 Operum medicorum pag. 347 ex Galeno tradit&lt;/e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T030031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T165636Z">
        <seg>Thus a cancerous growth in the nose is called a&lt;/e3&gt; polyp &lt;e4&gt;because of its resemblance to the animal of the same name, as Sennertus states in volume II of his Medical Works, page 347 &lt;/e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic floridus erat hujus Sponsæ lectulus, &amp; Sponso divino adeo gratus, ut eam quandoque propter hæc pene exanimem visibiliter apparens consolatus fuerit, proponendo ei duriorem &amp; angustiorem suum lectulum Calvariæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041551Z">
        <seg>Thus the bed of this Bride was so flourishing, and so pleasing to the divine Spouse, that He would sometimes, on account of this, visibly console her, who appeared almost lifeless, by setting before her His own harsher and more narrow bed of Calvary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic horæ dimidium tacitis circa lectulum votis exactum est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033220Z">
        <seg>Thus, half an hour was spent in silent prayers by the bedside.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic hærentem &amp; ultra condictum morosius cunctantem interpellavit frater, eundi tempus esse, longiori orationi commodius in monasterio spatium fore.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040115Z">
        <seg>Thus he interrupted the brother who was lingering and, beyond the appointed time, dawdling morosely, saying that it was time to go and that there would be more convenient space in the monastery for a longer prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic implebatur, quod Christus Dominus futurum esse revelaverat, mortem scilicet Rosæ &amp; sepulturam ejus fore gloriosam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042930Z">
        <seg>Thus was fulfilled what Christ the Lord had revealed would happen: namely, the death of Rose and her glorious burial.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic in ecclesiis, ubi aut quadraginta horarum supplicationi, aut alias SS. Eucharistiæ Sacramentum prostabat adorandum, Rosa (quem summo mane insederat) loco velut impetrita, durabat in vesperam, sine refectione, sine motu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044910Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044910Z">
        <seg>Thus in the churches, where either the Forty Hours' devotion or otherwise the Sacrament of the Holy Eucharist was exposed for adoration, Rosa (who had been seated there from early morning) would remain in her place as if petrified until evening, without refreshment or movement.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic in quæstoris domestico oratorio, cum sese occludebat Rosa a mane feriæ quintæ usque ad Sabbatum (nonnumquam &amp; usque in Dominicam) primo rogabat D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044914Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044914Z">
        <seg>Thus, in the domestic oratory of the treasurer, when Rosa shut herself in from the morning of the fifth day of the fast until Saturday (sometimes even until Sunday), she first prayed to the Lord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic in quæstoris domestico oratorio, cum sese occludebat Rosa a mane feriæ quintæ usque ad Sabbatum (nonnumquam &amp; usque in Dominicam) primo rogabat D. Mariam de Usategui, ne toto illo temporis interstitio evocaretur, quidquid demum occurreret, si vel ipsa Rosæ mater veniret Filiæ locutura: causam rogata, candide respondit, se toto illo temporis spatio immobilem de pavimento surgere in pedes non posse, ne quidem ad oratorii januam aperiendam, si quis forte pulsaret ad limen.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010204Z">
        <seg>Thus, in the domestic oratory of the steward, when Rosa shut herself in from the morning of the Friday holiday until Saturday (sometimes even until Sunday), she first asked Our Lady of Usategui not to be summoned during that entire interval, whatever might happen, even if Rosa's own mother came to speak with her daughter: When asked the reason, she frankly replied that she could not rise from the floor during that entire period, not even to open the door of the oratory if someone should happen to knock.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic misera per biduum suis colluctabatur angoribus, lenteque se mori sentiens, animam pœnitentiæ sacramento expiarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040506Z">
        <seg>Thus the poor woman wrestled with her anxieties for two days, and, slowly sensing her death, had purified her soul by the sacrament of penance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic nempe Rosa in sentibus configurabat se lilio inter spinas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041826Z">
        <seg>Thus indeed, Rosa, in the thickets, fashioned herself into a lily among thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic nondum didicisti omni hac putredine te viliorem esse ac putridiorem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061404Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061404Z">
        <seg>Thus you have not yet learned that with all this rot you are both more worthless and more rotten.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic passim regio Peruana (etiam ubi Rosa, dum vixit, penitus ignorabatur) mortuam celebravit universali jubilo, acclamatione, votis, quod sibi cuncti populariter ab ea solatium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031301Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031301Z">
        <seg>Thus throughout the Peruvian kingdom (even where Rosa, during her life, was utterly unknown) the people celebrated her death with universal joy, acclamation, and vows, because she had popularly comforted them all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic per totam, quæ festo SS. Corporis Christi appendet, solemnem octavam, integros illos dies transigebat coram patulo extantis Sacramenti tabernaculo, mirantibus præcipue confessariis, unde tam extenuato corpusculo vires suppeterent ad genuflexiones tam prolixas, continuas, arduas, cum dierum inedia sustinendas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060034Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060034Z">
        <seg>Thus, throughout the entire solemn octave of the Feast of Corpus Christi, she spent those full days before the open tabernacle of the exposed Sacrament, to the amazement of the confessors, who wondered whence such a frail little body could draw the strength to endure such prolonged, continuous, and arduous genuflections, all the while fasting.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic placide experrecta videbat cominus, &amp; (uti fassa est) facie ad faciem illud gloriæ sidus gratiosum, illam frontem decora majestate plenissimam, illum gloriæ &amp; amœnitatis paradisum, agnoscebat vocem, venerabatur præsentiam, tacite secum disquirens: “Unde hoc mihi, ut Mater Domini mei veniat ad me”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051341Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051341Z">
        <seg>Thus, gently awakened, she saw it up close and, as she confessed, face to face—that gracious star of glory, that brow most full of majestic beauty, that paradise of glory and delight—she recognized the voice, she revered the presence, silently questioning herself: "Where did this come from for me, that the Mother of my Lord should come to me?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic pro Sponsæ honore zelabat ille Sponsus, cui venti &amp; mare obediunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033009Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033009Z">
        <seg>Thus the Groom, to whom the winds and the sea obey, zealous for the honor of the Bride, was zealous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic putrescenti nulla spes vitæ supererat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035716Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035716Z">
        <seg>Thus, the decaying one had no hope of life left.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic quæ trepidabat in nomine, firmabatur cognomine, secura &amp; læta, quod utrumque cælitus datum possideret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034745Z">
        <seg>Thus she, who trembled at her given name, was steadied by her surname, secure and joyful that she possessed both as gifts from heaven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic repente sopitis doloribus, ipsa etiam placito sopore totam noctem quietissime præter morem exegit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034915Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034915Z">
        <seg>Thus, with the pains suddenly stilled, she herself, in a pleasant slumber, spent the entire night most quietly, contrary to her custom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic tandem in tuto locatis Rosæ exuviis, omnique precum &amp; hymnorum ritu ex rubrica Ordinis peracto, itum ad refectionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031159Z">
        <seg>Thus, with the relics of Rose safely deposited, and all the rites of prayers and hymns from the Order's rubric performed, they proceeded to the refectory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic tandem utrimque ex pacto conventum est, quod Rosa huic confessario medietatem cederet omnium fructuum, quos interea ex toto bonorum operum exercitio se concumulaturam sperarat: ac vicissim ille dimidium lucri transcriberet Virgini ex conversione animarum, quas sua prædicatione in viam salutis erat attracturus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060434Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060434Z">
        <seg>Thus, by mutual agreement, it was finally settled that Rosa would cede to this confessor half of all the fruits she had hoped to accumulate in the meantime through the full exercise of good works; and, in return, he would transfer to the Virgin half of the gain from the conversion of souls he was to lead onto the path of salvation through his preaching.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic videbatur sibi rediisse ad ligneum illud pulvinar, quod pridem sibi mater subtraxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042818Z">
        <seg>It seemed to him that he had returned to that wooden pillow which his mother had long ago taken away from him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic ægre demum inter sonoros templi strepitus quasi canoro quodam silentio ac rituum nutu consummata fuere divina mysteria.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031035Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031035Z">
        <seg>Thus, with difficulty at last, amid the temple's sonorous clatter, the divine mysteries were completed in a kind of melodious silence and by the gesture of the rites.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic, alternante feminarum bile, patiens Virguncula quoquo se verteret, incidebat in pœnam, &amp; ob hoc ipsum, quod non peccarat, luebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052457Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052457Z">
        <seg>Thus, as the women's wrath alternated, the suffering Virgin, wherever she turned, fell into punishment and, for that very reason, because she had done no wrong, suffered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Siccine jejunant Beatæ?” Displicuit matri ineptissimæ censuræ temeraria dicacitas: at mire placuit humili Rosæ, sic sua latere jejunia, etiam usque ad tam ridiculam saginæ ac ingluviei calumniam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040803Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040803Z">
        <seg>"Do the Blessed fast like this?" The mother was displeased by the rash, inept criticism; but humble Rosa was strangely pleased that her fasts should thus remain hidden, even to the point of such a ridiculous charge of gluttony and gorging.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sicque his demum visionibus comiter impositus est finis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031940Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031940Z">
        <seg>And so, these visions were finally brought to a gentle end.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sicque providus ac discretus Pater, arrepta lima hinc inde prominentiores coronæ aculeos hebetavit, obtudit, defricuit, si non satis, attamen nec parum hoc temperamento se putans assecutum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042325Z">
        <seg>Thus the prudent and discreet Father, seizing a file, smoothed down the more prominent thorns of the crown, dulling, blunting, and scraping them; if not enough, he nonetheless considered himself to have achieved no small measure with this treatment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sicubi audisset eminus campanis signum dari ad ejus adorationem, vel sacris ejusdem solemniis tintinnabula præsonare, subitanea intimæ lætitiæ signa cohibere non poterat, quin cor &amp; caro sua exultarent in Deum vivum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060056Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060056Z">
        <seg>Whenever he heard the signal of bells from afar given for his adoration, or the little bells ringing at his solemn rites, he could not restrain the sudden signs of inner joy, so that his heart and flesh rejoiced in the living God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sicubi in templis, sacellis, ædibus frequens vivificæ crucis simulachrum incurrisset in oculos, singula mœsto obtutu &amp; calente gemitu salutabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054831Z">
        <seg>Whenever in temples, chapels, or houses he happened to come upon the frequent image of the life-giving cross, he greeted it with a sorrowful gaze and a warm sigh.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sicubi in vicinia aut nota domo rescivisset, quodcumque vile mancipium ex ægritudine decumbere, ac negligentius haberi, sibi confestim illius deposcebat curam, multisque precibus exorabat matrem, annueret, languentem in suas ædes deportari ad curam solicitiorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061234Z">
        <seg>Whenever she learned in the neighborhood or from a familiar household that any humble servant was down with illness and being cared for more negligently, she would at once demand to look after him, and with many entreaties she would persuade her mother to allow the ailing one to be brought to her own home for more attentive care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sicut erat &amp;c&lt;/b1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010217Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010217Z">
        <seg>As it was &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sicut erat &amp;c&lt;/b5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044951Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044951Z">
        <seg>As it was &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Signatum ab eodem D. episcopo coram me doctore Ferdinando Vecerril.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031407Z">
        <seg>Signed by the same Bishop D. in my presence, Doctor Ferdinand Vecerril.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Simile, at multo operosius vestimentum divæ Virginis consuere cœpit prima die anni MDCXVI.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054642Z">
        <seg>Likewise, but much more laboriously, she began to sew the garment of the Virgin Queen on the first day of the year 1616.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Similia de nomine hujus herbæ in Trivultiano linguæ Gallicæ Dictionario legi possunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060952Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060952Z">
        <seg>Similar things about the name of this herb can be read in Trivulzio's Dictionary of the French Language.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Similiter hæc sunt, &amp; plane germana iis, quæ B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052224Z">
        <seg>Similarly, these are, and are plainly genuine, like those of B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Similiter iisdem fere diebus D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061135Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061135Z">
        <seg>Similarly, at about the same days of D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Similiter iisdem fere diebus D. Maria de Sala ultro per famulum misit ferici subtegminis aptam materiam, quanta conficiendæ novæ pallæ sat erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011614Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011614Z">
        <seg>Likewise, around the same days, D. Maria de Sala sent, through a servant, a suitable material for the feric undergarment, enough to make a new pall.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Simillima hæc sunt iis, quæ de S. Catharina prodit vitæ suæ historia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060003Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060003Z">
        <seg>These are very similar to those which appear in the life story of St. Catherine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Simillimam ab his pulveribus experta est opem Isabella de Peralta in Limensi SS. Incarnationis monasterio professa monialis, quam, ab annis viginti præter assiduos præcordiorum dolores, apoplexia (maxime brumali tempore) quotidie exagitabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035558Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035558Z">
        <seg>A very similar relief from these powders was experienced by Isabella de Peralta, a professed nun at the Monastery of the Incarnation in Lima, who, for twenty years besides her constant heart pains, had been daily tormented by apoplexy (especially in winter).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Simillimo beneficio &amp; ipsa prænominata vidua ex utriusque pedis dira inflatione &amp; cruciatu repente convaluit, mox ut Rosam auxiliatricem flebiliter invocavit, promisso simili dierum seu stationum novenario, quem &amp; postridie, votivis onusta donis, est auspicata sanisque pedibus alacriter peregit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034221Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034221Z">
        <seg>By a similar benefit, the aforementioned widow herself was suddenly cured of the severe swelling and torment in both feet, as soon as she had pitifully invoked the helper Rosa, having promised a similar novenary of days or stations, which she also began the next day, laden with votive offerings, and cheerfully completed on healthy feet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Simillimum est, quod refert D. Maria de Usategui Gundisalvi quæstoris uxor, hoc addens, gloriosam Virginem in ea functione visam alacriter ipsa graphiaria instrumenta notariis disponere, chartamque digerere, protestando gaudium, quo ex divinorum in se beneficiorum juridica in terris comprobatione mulcebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032216Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032216Z">
        <seg>It is very similar to what D. Maria de Usategui Gundisalvi, the quæstor's wife, reports, adding that she saw the glorious Virgin in that role eagerly preparing the scrivener's instruments for the notaries and arranging the chart, proclaiming the joy with which she was delighted by the earthly juridical confirmation of the divine benefits bestowed upon her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Simillimum quid cum eadem Antonius a Vega, ejusdem Societatis vir integerrimus, sibi evenisse narravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042635Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042635Z">
        <seg>Something very similar happened to Antonius a Vega, a most upright man of the same Society.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Simulque e sinu protulit recisam ex lineo Rosæ velo particulam, qua sinistram extemplo obvolutam adstrinxit fasciis, ea fide, securitate &amp; constantia, ut inde mox in cellulam redierit, reliquum operis perfecturus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034829Z">
        <seg>At the same time, she drew from her bosom a piece cut from Rose's linen veil, with which she at once bandaged her left hand, binding it with bandages so faithfully, securely, and steadfastly that she soon returned to her cell to finish the rest of the work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Singularis fuit, quo Speciosus forma præ filiis hominum habitu marmorum cædendorum peritissimi artificis, festivus, &amp; amans in somnis apparuit, ut virginem illic sponsam quæsiturus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041455Z">
        <seg>It was unique that the Handsome one, in appearance like the sons of men, appeared in dreams in the guise of a most skilled marble-carver, cheerful and loving, to seek there a virgin bride.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Singularum creaturarum ordines ad amorem dulcissimi Creatoris gradatim compellabat, tum ignitis vocibus, tum pulsu citharæ, quem non didicerat, ut omni ritu, &amp; gestu, actuque suo se Dilecto ostenderet amantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042108Z">
        <seg>He gradually compelled the orders of each creature to love the most sweetest Creator, both with fiery voices and with the strumming of a harp he had not learned, so that with every rite, gesture, and act he might show himself loving to the Beloved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Singulis virginibus singulæ erant in manibus palmæ, in verticibus coronæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031838Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031838Z">
        <seg>Each virgin held in her hand a palm frond, at the tip of her crown.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sinistram cordi apposuerat, contra immodicam palpitationem exundantis lætitiæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044607Z">
        <seg>He had placed his left hand over his heart, against the excessive fluttering of overflowing joy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sinite in Roderico operari divinam potius voluntatem, quam vestram;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012616Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012616Z">
        <seg>Let the divine will, rather than your own, work in Rodrigo.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Siquidem tumor ipsi cancri corpus, venæ tumidæ crassæque ejusdem pedes oculis repræsentant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061621Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061621Z">
        <seg>For the swelling itself of the cancer, its swollen and thick veins represent the body and its feet to the eye.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Siquidem, ne transiliret, novam a confessario permissionem toties exorabat, quoties aut aliqua proximorum necessitas, aut causa publica videbatur peculiarem ab ea succursum exigere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041606Z">
        <seg>Indeed, so that he would not overstep, he would ask his confessor for a new permission as often as either some need of his relatives or a public cause seemed to demand special assistance from him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sitis immanissima præ ceteris, ut prædixerat, intime eam cruciabat, nec aquæ potus sitienti dabatur, obsistente medicorum præscripto;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042756Z">
        <seg>A thirst far greater than the others, as he had predicted, tormented him inwardly, and no water was given to the thirsty man, the doctors' prescription standing in the way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sive enim invocata sub Rosæ nomine accurrisset domesticis, aut matri, frendens avia in parvulam virgis sæviebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052451Z">
        <seg>For whether, when called by the name of Rose, she had rushed to the household, or to her mother, the grumbling grandmother would be raging in the little one with her switches.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sola Francisca paulo tenacius adhærescebat mundo, totisque præcordiis abhorrebat a sæculi divortio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012504Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012504Z">
        <seg>Francisca alone clung to the world a little more tenaciously, and with all her heart abhorred the divorce from the world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sola Rosa conscia miraculi, signique tacita interpres, exultabat arcano jubilo, sat intelligens, quo ista spectarent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043553Z">
        <seg>Rosa alone, aware of the miracle and the silent interpreter of the sign, exulted in a secret joy, fully understanding what it all meant.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sola Rosa impavido gradu fixa perstitit, matremque, ne fuga periculum jam nimis propinquum irritaret, hortata est, una promittens, immanem belluam innoxie transituram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062123Z">
        <seg>Rosa alone stood firm with unshaken step, and urged her mother not to provoke the already too-near danger by fleeing, promising together that the immense beast would pass by harmlessly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sola Rosa, tantum abfuit, quod sibi metueret, fugamve aut latibulum circumspiceret, ut potius triumphanti similis ingentis lætitiæ signa cohibere nequiverit, rata nimirum adesse felicissimam, totque suspiriis optatam horam, qua posset pro honore prostantis ibidem sanctissimi Sacramenti vitam animamque profundere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060208Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060208Z">
        <seg>Rosa alone was so far from being afraid for herself or looking for a way to flee or hide that, on the contrary, she, like one triumphant, could not restrain the signs of immense joy, convinced, of course, that the most blessed and long-sought hour had come, when she could pour out her life and soul for the honor of the Most Holy Sacrament exposed there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sola esuriens languebat America, nec Domini volentis salutem omnium arcani mysterii conscia, nec ministri vocantis voces audire valens, donec venit plenitudo temporis, &amp; per ministros fideles sedentium in tenebris, &amp; umbra mortis auribus vox Patris intonuit&lt;a1&gt;*&lt;/a1&gt;, illatumque est Euangelium ultra Indum, &amp; Gangem&lt;a2&gt;a&lt;/a2&gt;, &amp; æquatorem&lt;a3&gt;b&lt;/a3&gt;, &amp; per omnes compitorum angulos famelici quæsiti gentiles, ut in parato convivio pinguium ac medullatorum cum Deo Abraham, Isaac, &amp; Jacob in regia sua recumberent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041039Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041039Z">
        <seg>America languished, famished and alone, conscious of the secret mystery of the Lord willing the salvation of all, yet unable to hear the voice of the calling minister, until the fullness of time came, and through faithful ministers to those sitting in darkness and in the shadow of death, the voice of the Father thundered&lt;a1&gt;*&lt;/a1&gt;, and the Gospel was carried beyond the Indus, &amp; the Ganges&lt;a2&gt;a&lt;/a2&gt;, &amp; the equator&lt;a3&gt;b&lt;/a3&gt;, &amp; through all the corners of the crossroads the hungry Gentiles were sought, that in the prepared feast of fat things and marrow with God of Abraham, Isaac, &amp; Jacob they might recline at his table.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sola iterum Rosa, oculis in cælum jactis, &amp; illico reductis, exemit trepidis metum, stitit fugam velut minime necessariam affirmans, taurum usque ad rhedam neutiquam procursurum, sed via, qua veniebat, retrocessurum, Dixerat: &amp; respondit eventus, stupentibus universis non magis tauri velocem absque nocumento reditum, quam Virginis animum quovis metu superiorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062154Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062154Z">
        <seg>Rosa, alone again, with eyes cast up to heaven and immediately brought back down, dispelled the trembling fear, halting the flight as if it were by no means necessary, affirming that the bull would by no means rush toward the chariot, but would turn back the way it had come, She had spoken: and the outcome responded, to the astonishment of all, that the bull's swift return without harm was no more wondrous than the Virgin's spirit, superior to any fear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sola lingua, singulari Dei beneficio, intacta ad pietatis usum viguit usque ad extremum halitum, reliqua ex eo latere membra sic obtorpuere, ut numquam ad officium redierint, quidquid demum unguentorum difrictionum, fomentorum, magis ad cumulandam molestiam, quam ad conciliandum levamen, impenderetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025559Z">
        <seg>Only the tongue, by a singular gift of God, remained intact for the use of piety until his last breath; the other members on that side had grown so numb that they never returned to function, no matter how many ointments, rubbings, or fomentations were applied—more to increase his discomfort than to bring relief.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sola temporis &amp; anni, quo construendum erat, circumstantia, ob altiores divinæ sapientiæ fines, Virginem diu latuit, seroque didicit, se illud corporeis oculis non visuram;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062443Z">
        <seg>The Virgin was hidden from the time and year in which it was to be built for a long time, and learned too late that she would not see it with her physical eyes;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Solani vaticinio, terræ hiatu proxime absorbendam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041547Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041547Z">
        <seg>By the prophecy of Solanus, to be soon swallowed by an earth-sinkhole;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Solus deerat consensus Virginis, quam voti sui memorem omnis mentio nuptiarum exanimabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040000Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040000Z">
        <seg>Only the Virgin's consent was lacking, whom any mention of marriage, mindful of her vow, terrified.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Solus non expavit saucius Joannes præsentis remedii conscius;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034822Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034822Z">
        <seg>John, wounded but not startled, was aware of the present remedy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Solæ virgineæ manus tot osculis fidelium millies obtritæ, (forsan &amp; avidorum ferro dentibusve undique pertentatæ) nonnihil de primo candore remiserant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031428Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031428Z">
        <seg>The virgin hands alone, worn down by the kisses of the faithful a thousand times (perhaps even pierced on all sides by the eager with iron or teeth), had somewhat lost their original whiteness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Spada.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043643Z">
        <seg>Sword</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Spatiabantur ambo pariter consertis manibus, gressu gestuque tenere amantium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051155Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051155Z">
        <seg>They strolled together, hand in hand, with the gentle gait and bearing of lovers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Spectabat universus populus, &amp; dum hinc una vox omnium erumpit in publicas Dei laudes, dum inde mixti consurgunt gratulantium clamores, nonnulli, qui servum familiarius noverant, manum ejus festive arreptam deosculati sunt coram turba, piaque curiositate lustrabant vetusta signa, ubi attractio manum Æthiopis cum brachio in plicas tenacissime quondam curvaverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033300Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033300Z">
        <seg>The entire people were watching, and while from this place one voice of all burst forth in public praise of God, while from the other side the mingled cries of those offering congratulations rose, some who had known the servant more intimately festively seized his hand and kissed it before the crowd, and with pious curiosity they examined the old scars where the attraction had once most tenaciously bent the Ethiopian's hand with his arm into folds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sperabat, transitoriam fore debilitatem, sed durabat ultra consuetam periodum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051053Z">
        <seg>He hoped it would be a temporary weakness, but it persisted beyond the usual period.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Spiritum denique suum in manus Patris commendans fixis in cælum oculis, ut cum Sponso intraret pronuntians &lt;b1&gt;Jesus Jesus Jesus mecum sit&lt;/b1&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042902Z">
        <seg>Finally, commending his spirit into the hands of the Father, with his eyes fixed on heaven, as he entered with the Bridegroom, he pronounced, "Jesus, Jesus, Jesus be with me."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sponsum rogabat, ne cessaret eam exquisitis ardoribus excoquere, donec ei maturesceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042834Z">
        <seg>She begged her suitor not to cease to refine her with the most exquisite ardors until she ripened for him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Spopondit intrepide agonizans Virgo, ac paulo postquam expirasset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032733Z">
        <seg>The Virgin, agonizing intrepidly, promised, and a little after she had expired;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Statim evanuit metus, abscessit pavor, &amp; Rosa ab oratione domum regressa, instrumentum suum reperit, non ubi reliquerat, sed ubi illud a Matre gratiæ recondi flagitarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040912Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040912Z">
        <seg>Fear vanished at once, terror fled, and Rosa, returning home from the sermon, found her instrument not where she had left it, but where the Mother of Grace had insisted it be kept.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Statua est ex ignoto ligno, ad humanæ staturæ proceritatem, affabre sculpta in formosam effigiem magnæ Deiparæ, parvulum Jesum gestantis in ulna, manuque altera mundo Rosarium suum porrigentis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054224Z">
        <seg>The statue is crafted from an unknown wood to the height of a human figure, skillfully carved into a beautiful effigy of the Blessed Virgin Mary, holding the infant Jesus in her arms and with her other hand offering her Rosary to the world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Stetimus utrimque pactis, nec solum communi hoc tecto fruimur sine hostilitate;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043819Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043819Z">
        <seg>We have stood by our agreements on both sides, and we enjoy this common shelter without hostility.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Stetit promissis Rosa, &amp; illamet nocte in forma corusci luminis Aloysiam suaviter excitans, sese ad lucis regionem tunc perrexisse indicavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030245Z">
        <seg>Rosa kept her promises, and that very night, in the form of a shining light, she gently awakened Aloysia, indicating that she had then gone to the realm of light.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Stomachi, pericardii&lt;a4&gt;g&lt;/a4&gt;, intestinorum dolores, sine lege, sine numero, frequentes convulsiones invehebant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010812Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010812Z">
        <seg>Stomach, pericardial, and intestinal pains, without order or number, brought on frequent convulsions;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Stomachi, pericardii&lt;s11&gt;&lt;a12&gt;g&lt;/a12&gt;&lt;/s11&gt;, intestinorum dolores, sine lege, sine numero, frequentes convulsiones invehebant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052615Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052615Z">
        <seg>Stomach, pericardial, and intestinal pains, without order or number, brought on frequent convulsions;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Stragulis, 5 dies esuriales, 5 stationes, totidemque integra rosaria impenduntor pro honore sui Natalis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053558Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053558Z">
        <seg>They spend five days of fasting, five stations, and as many entire rosaries in honor of His Nativity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Strati sui duritiem eam esse voluit, quæ somnum magis abigeret, quam conciliaret, ita ut dormituræ idem esset lectulus &amp; equuleus, cervical aut impolitus truncus, aut lapides in hunc usum absconditi, quod cubile præacutis postea tegularum fragmentis, testarumque fractarum triquetris sic implevit, ut singularum pars mucronata obverteretur corpori: nec ante se ad somnum componebat, quam fellis haustu fauces amaricasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041548Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041548Z">
        <seg>He wanted its hardness to be such that it would drive sleep away rather than bring it on, so that for the sleeping woman the bed would be the same as a horseblock, a pillow or an unhewn log, or stones hidden for this purpose, for she filled the bed with pre-sharpened fragments of tiles and broken, three-cornered pots so that the pointed end of each piece was turned toward the body. Nor would she lie down to sleep until she had made her throat bitter with a draught of gall.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Stupebat ista, nondum assequens, unde idipsum, quod reliquæ juvenculæ ultra modum exambire solent, uni Rosæ tam exosum esset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043320Z">
        <seg>She was astonished, not yet understanding why that very thing, which the other young women are accustomed to praise excessively, was so distasteful to Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Stupuere quotquot in navi erant, præsertim ut viderunt paulo post comparentem in superiori tabulato sanum, incolumem, ac sine humano remedio integræ valetudini restitutum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033812Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033812Z">
        <seg>All who were on the ship were astonished, especially when they saw him appear shortly afterward on the upper deck, healthy, unharmed, and restored to perfect health without any human remedy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suadenti feminæ, precibus impetrare a sua seraphica magistra eripi ab his ærumnis, constanter renuit: pudebat enim Crucifixi Sponsam vel per momentum extra cruces reperiri, a quibus numquam vacavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042037Z">
        <seg>When the woman urged her, imploring that she be delivered from these torments by her seraphic mistress, she steadfastly refused: for she was ashamed for the Spouse of the Crucified to be found even for a moment outside the crosses, from which she was never absent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suavius, ac forte mirabilius est, quod de hujus coronæ virtute post obitum Virginis compertum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042347Z">
        <seg>More delightful, and perhaps more marvelous, is what was discovered about the virtue of this crown after the Virgin's death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sub dextro poplite dolorificus ipsi obduruerat humor, quo multum impediebatur gradiendi facultas: igitur illammet Rosæ imaginem dolori leniter imposuit, &amp; ad primum attactum e vestigio se liberum sensit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040540Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040540Z">
        <seg>Under his right knee a painful swelling had hardened, greatly impeding his ability to walk; so he gently pressed that very image of Rose onto the sore spot, and at the first touch he felt himself instantly free.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sub eo tædio obdormiscens, vidit felicem Rosam ad se intrantem gradu placido ac festivo, vultu propitio ac læto, qui terrere nequaquam posset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032321Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032321Z">
        <seg>Dozing off from boredom, he saw the happy Rose entering toward him with a calm and festive step, her face benevolent and joyful, which could in no way be frightening.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sub hac perplexitate calamitosa infertur Romanum Rosæ ectypon, sed in tela, sistiturque ex adverso periclitantis infirmæ, ut commode ab ea videretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040450Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040450Z">
        <seg>Amid this calamitous perplexity, the Roman Rosæ's ectypon is brought in, but on a cloth, and is placed opposite the stricken invalid so that she can see it conveniently.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sub hoc fine vitæ crebriora fuere raptuum dulcissima intervalla, in quibus excelsa &amp; jucunda de suavitate Dei, ac læta de regione æternitatis libamina prægustasse innotuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042840Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042840Z">
        <seg>Toward the end of his life, there were more frequent raptures, the sweetest intervals, in which he was known to have tasted sublime and delightful foretastes of the sweetness of God and of the land of eternity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sub hoc pondere æstuabat, rigebat, lassabatur extenuata viribus Rosa, incessu non nisi difficillimo gradum promovebat, &amp; sive consederat, sive genua humi fixerat, ægre se attollebat in pedes;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041900Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041900Z">
        <seg>Under this weight, she was burning, stiffening, and weakening from her exhausted strength; she could advance her foot with the greatest difficulty, and whether she had sat down or fixed her knees on the ground, she could scarcely raise herself to her feet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sub hoc tormento integros quandoque cum noctibus dies sine cibo transigebat: verum sedato nonnihil cruciatu, mox instabat domesticorum pietas, ut a carnibus jusculum admitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041101Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041101Z">
        <seg>Under this torture he would sometimes spend entire days, even with the nights, without food; but once the torment was somewhat eased, his family's piety soon compelled him to take a little broth with his meat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sub vitæ finem crebriora Virgini fuere dulcissimorum raptuum intervalla, quibus paratæ cælestis gloriæ sapores jam summis labiis prælibabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030117Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030117Z">
        <seg>Toward the end of her life, the intervals between the Virgin's most sweet raptures grew more frequent, in which she already tasted on her highest lips the savor of heavenly glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subest autem dominio Hispanorum, &amp; dicitur etiam Fanum sancti Joannis de Portu divite.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035136Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035136Z">
        <seg>It is, however, under the dominion of the Spaniards, and is also called the Church of Saint John of the Rich Port.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subinde cum laterum dolore urgebatur ad sanguinis vomitum, familiari idiomatis sui rhythmo alloquebatur Sponsum: “Domine, tantum ne in ira corripias me, nec arguas in furore, dum vis, quod mea crimina detergam hoc cruore”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025649Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025649Z">
        <seg>Whenever he was seized by side pain and driven to vomit blood, he addressed the Spouse in the familiar rhythm of his own idiom: "Lord, only do not punish me in your anger, nor chide me in your fury, while you will, that I may wash away my crimes with this blood."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subinde tamen, ne misericordem immisericorditer contristaret, ei indulsit pro domestica sua tenuitate operari, quæ posset, non quanta vellet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061240Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061240Z">
        <seg>However, from time to time, so as not to grieve the merciful one mercilessly, he granted her permission to work according to her domestic frailty, as much as she could, not as much as she wished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subintrabat continuo æmula vox Rosæ, instaurabat jubilum, magnificabat ineffabiles perfectiones altissimi Numinis, attollebatur urgente spiritu, languescebat facundis suspiriis, demum prærupto suo silentio, antiphonascum suum revocabat ad cantum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045320Z">
        <seg>Rose's rival voice would continually enter, renewing the jubilation and magnifying the ineffable perfections of the Most High. It was lifted up by the urgent spirit, languished with eloquent sighs, and finally, with its abrupt silence, would bring its antiphon back to song.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subito ex Potosi responsum, quo suavius desiderari non potuerat, adfuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040001Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040001Z">
        <seg>Suddenly, a reply arrived from Potosi, which could not have been more welcome.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subjecit Rosa, neutiquam id suo reclusorio imputandum, sed quia mater (quacum sola jusserant confessarii primis annis ecclesiam adire) domesticis impediebatur curis, ne omni die ad Missam exiret, sibi tamen divina bonitate prospectum esse, ut in cellula residens, non unicam, sed complusculas quotidie Missas videret, audiretque.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043729Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043729Z">
        <seg>Rosa added that this was by no means to be attributed to her own convent, but because her mother (with whom, in her early years, the confessor had ordered her to attend church alone) was hindered by domestic cares from going to Mass every day, divine goodness had nonetheless provided for her so that, while remaining in her cell, she might see and hear not one but several Masses each day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subjecit Rosa: Non vane conjectasti;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051902Z">
        <seg>Rosa said: You did not guess in vain;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sublime augurium &amp; observatu dignum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054329Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054329Z">
        <seg>A sublime omen &amp; worthy of observation;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suboluit tempestive attentæ matri, quo Filia tenderet: nam pene ab infantia utrumque, &amp; durius cubandi, &amp; austeritatem dissimulandi studium notarat in puella;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042508Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042508Z">
        <seg>The attentive mother promptly sensed where her daughter was headed; for she had noticed in the girl, almost from infancy, both a penchant for sleeping on hard beds and a habit of hiding her austerity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subrisit Virgo &amp; “Nonne (ait) potestatem mihi diserte fecisti utendi, ut vellem?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061054Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061054Z">
        <seg>The virgin smiled and said, "Haven't you clearly given me the power to use it as I please?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subrisit Virgo, blandeque subintulit: “Christi bonus odor sumus, dum ministramus infirmis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061317Z">
        <seg>The Virgin smiled, and sweetly added: "We are the good odor of Christ, while we minister to the sick;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subrisit Virgo, matrique &amp; aliis respondit: “Quando huc intravi, cum culicibus amicitiam, pactumque inii, ne umquam me turbarent aut affligerent, vicissim in nullo me ipsis nocituram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043816Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043816Z">
        <seg>The maiden smiled and replied to her mother and the others: "When I entered here, I made friends with the mosquitoes and struck a bargain with them that they would never disturb or harm me, and in return I would never harm them."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subsecuta deinde opulentissimi Sponsi nuptialia antidora: nam (ut in examine, de quo infra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044430Z">
        <seg>Then followed the most opulent bridegroom's nuptial gifts: for (as in the examination, of which below in the chapter…</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Substitit Rosa, non dedignata volucrem auscultare tantisper, utpote Dei laudes (ut sibi imaginabatur) modulo suo cantillantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045025Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045025Z">
        <seg>Rosa paused, not deigning to listen to the bird for a while, since it was, as she imagined, singing praises of God in its own way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Substomachans ad hæc patrum unus, replicuit: “Nescis, Rosa, quam male hoc intercessionis beneficium colloces;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012445Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012445Z">
        <seg>One of the fathers, scoffing at this, replied: "You don't know, Rosa, how badly you're misapplying this benefit of intercession."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subvenit ergo cordatæ Virginis pietati simul ac nodo prudens confessarius, ac matrem e vestigio conveniens graviter dehortatus est, cessaret tandem pretiosæ vanitatis inutili molestia tentare perplexam Filiam, quam ab infantia divinus Spiritus præparaverat secretissimis instinctibus ad ratam altioris sanctimoniæ sublimitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035430Z">
        <seg>A prudent confessor, perceiving the Virgin's pious devotion and the knot of her perplexity, at once came forward and earnestly dissuaded her mother, urging her to cease at last from needlessly testing the perplexed Daughter with the vain and costly burden of useless cares—the Daughter whom from infancy the divine Spirit had prepared by most secret instincts for the fitting sublimity of a higher sanctity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subvenit illa, imo &amp; venit: siquidem exinde, ubi imminuisset surgendi hora, en in puncto aderat conspicua Dei Mater, &amp; voce melliflua compellabat sopitam Rosam: “Surge ad orationem Filia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051335Z">
        <seg>She came to her aid, nay, she came: for from that moment, when the time to rise had grown short, behold, in an instant the Mother of God appeared in sight, and with a honeyed voice she called to the slumbering Rose: "Arise to prayer, Daughter;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Succendebatur vividior in facie color, &amp; exultabat spiritus ejus in Deo salutari suo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060059Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060059Z">
        <seg>His face grew more vivid, and his spirit exulted in God, his Savior.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Successere, aut potius accescere paralysi varia minoris notæ symptomata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025609Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025609Z">
        <seg>Various minor symptoms of paralysis followed, or rather developed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Successum videbunt hi oculi tui;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062343Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062343Z">
        <seg>These eyes of yours will see success.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Succrevit interim flamma charitatis, nec celari ulterius potuit arcanum incendium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032715Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032715Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, the flame of charity grew, and the secret fire could no longer be hidden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Succurre huic necessitati, nec distuleris solatium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055241Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055241Z">
        <seg>Come to this need, and do not delay comfort.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sudor ille brachio ipsius Rosæ ex confessarii imperio applicitus, quod antea solo collisum sic graviter læsum erat, ut chirurgorum judicio ex ea parte Virgo manca mansura esset, sanum &amp; flexibile reddidit, stupente chirurgo, qui pridie censuerat omnem curam esse vanam, cum brachium omnino intractabile appareret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042146Z">
        <seg>That sweat, applied to Rose's own arm by the confessor's command—an arm that had previously been so severely injured when struck against the ground that, in the surgeons' judgment, she would have remained maimed on that side—made it sound and supple, to the astonishment of the surgeon, who the day before had deemed all treatment futile, since the arm had appeared utterly unmanageable.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Summa contentæ vociferationis hæc erat: Invitabat per ordinem singulos creaturarum ordines ad amorem dulcissimi Creatoris, elementa, cælos, angelos, plantas, animalia compellabat, identidem replicando: “Amemus Deum, Deum amemus, amor Deus, Deus amor”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053216Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053216Z">
        <seg>The gist of the outcry was this: it invited, in turn, each order of creatures to the love of the sweetest Creator, urging the elements, the heavens, the angels, the plants, the animals, repeatedly repeating: "Let us love God, let us love God, love is God, God is love."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suo ordine dispiciamus singula.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041005Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041005Z">
        <seg>Let us consider each item in turn.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Supererat Luciæ nobilissima parentela, quæ viduam, generis, ætatis, opumque intuitu ad secundas nuptias ita urgebat, ut pene adigeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012036Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012036Z">
        <seg>Lucy was left with the most distinguished of relatives, who, considering her rank, age, and wealth, urged the widow so insistently toward a second marriage that they nearly forced her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Supererat publicæ authoritatis legale examen &amp; disquisitio, ut evidentius innotesceret miraculi certitudo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053858Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053858Z">
        <seg>A legal examination and inquiry by public authority remained, so that the certainty of the miracle might be more clearly recognized.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Supererat, ut de lemmate, quod anuli convexo inscriberetur per gyrum, convenirent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044446Z">
        <seg>All that remained was for them to agree on the motto to be inscribed on the ring's convex surface around its band.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Superest, quod reliquam ejus pueritiam cursim delibemus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034931Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034931Z">
        <seg>All that remains is to briefly touch on the rest of his childhood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Superfuit trimus filiolus, nomine Franciscus de Contreras, quem idem morbus simili parabat exitio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034704Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034704Z">
        <seg>A three-year-old son, named Francis de Contreras, survived, whom the same disease was preparing for a similar fate;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Supervenit Franciscus Gutierez, allataque Rosæ imagine, præivit ægro Ludovico verba, quibus se Virgini efflictim commendaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040633Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040633Z">
        <seg>Francis Gutierrez arrived, and bringing Rose's image, he went ahead of the sick Louis with words by which he might commend himself earnestly to the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Supervenit fortuito Joannes de Tineo Almansa familiaris, qui Virginem obiter salutans ejus se precibus commendabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013245Z">
        <seg>John of Tineo Almansa, a familiar, happened to arrive by chance; he greeted the Virgin in passing and commended himself to her prayers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Supervenit germanus Virginis frater, ac sororem innocenti ludo se oblectare, nec serium quidquam subesse autumans, accessit familiariter, quidque ageret, interrogavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062517Z">
        <seg>The Virgin's brother arrived, and, thinking his sister was amusing herself with an innocent game and that there was nothing serious about it, he approached familiarly and asked what she was doing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Supervenit is, cui cura erat Virginis sepulchrum latere coctili obtegere: jubet Alphonsum loco abscedere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033350Z">
        <seg>He who was in charge of covering the Virgin's tomb with a brick covering arrived; he orders Alphonsus to leave the place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Supervenit triste nuntium, infirmam exspirasse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012203Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012203Z">
        <seg>Sad news arrived: the sick woman has died.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Supervenit, providente Numine, post dies undecim Gundisalvi quæstoris mancipium Franciscus, qui Speranzæ ex Africa Panamam, inde Limam jugiter comes fuerat: is interrogatus respondit simpliciter, se semper credidisse, quod utique intra hoc sexennium Speranza Limæ baptizata sit: nam quod in Panama Baptismus ei non obtigerit, habere se compertissimum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012913Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012913Z">
        <seg>After eleven days, Francis, the slave of the quaestor Gundisalvus, arrived, by the providence of God. He, who had been Speranza's constant companion from Africa to Panama and thence to Lima, when questioned replied simply that he had always believed—and indeed was certain—that within the past six years Speranza had been baptized in Lima; for he knew for a fact that she had not received baptism in Panama.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Surgo Domina, nunc surgo: conseditque erecta in lectulo, sed prægravante somni pondere, oculisque caligantibus, recidit misera in cervical.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051353Z">
        <seg>I rise, my lady, I rise now: and she sat up in her bed, but weighed down by the burden of sleep and her eyes growing dim, the poor woman fell back onto her pillow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Susceptis Ecclesiæ sacramentis, se commendavit prædictæ Rosæ intercessioni, cujus amplexa est imaginem, &amp; statim sana visa est cum totali virium recuperatione, atque e lecto exsiliens illico altare ornavit, ibique apposuit imaginem, &amp; ob recuperatam sanitatem gratias egit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043224Z">
        <seg>After receiving the Church's sacraments, she commended herself to the intercession of the aforementioned Rose, whose image she embraced, and was immediately seen to be healed with a full recovery of her strength. Leaping from her bed, she at once adorned the altar, placed the image there, and gave thanks for her restored health.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suscepto divino Sacramento, pallida &amp; exanguis nullo vel minimo labiorum motu sic fixa hæsit, ut diu inter adstantes dubitatum fuerit, num sacram Hostiam in stomachum transmisisset: denique P. magister de Lorenzana metuens, ne forte Virgo tam aridis, morboque adstrictis faucibus, glutire non posset, quæsivit solicite, quo statu res esset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025932Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025932Z">
        <seg>After receiving the divine Sacrament, she lay pale and bloodless, her lips motionless, so that for a long time those present doubted whether she had passed the sacred Host into her stomach. Finally, Father Master de Lorenzana, fearing that the Virgin might not be able to swallow with her throat so dry and seized by illness, anxiously asked what condition she was in.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suspexit in cælum Rosa, &amp; “O (inquit) suavissime Jesu, quam fidelis amicus es”!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055058Z">
        <seg>Rosa looked up at the sky and said, "O sweetest Jesus, what a faithful friend you are!"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suspicata est postmodum, rosis ceteris, quas per terram sparsas viderat, significari pientissimas virgines per Limam sparsas, quas tandem aliquando colligi oportebat in novellum sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis monasterium, ut illic religiosæ professionis nexu votivo colligatæ, Sponsum virginum casto famulatu coronent, vicissim ab illo gloria, &amp; honore coronandæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061931Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061931Z">
        <seg>She later suspected, that the other roses, which she had seen scattered across the ground, signified the most pious virgins scattered throughout Lima, who at long last ought to be gathered into the new monastery of Saint Catherine of Siena, so that there, bound by the vow of religious profession, they might crown the Spouse of virgins with chaste service, and in turn be crowned by him with glory and honor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suspicor, hic ab Hansenio forsan designari nummum argenteum, jussu Philippi regis Hispaniæ cusum, quem biographus alibi&lt;/e0&gt; pataconem &lt;e1&gt;vocavit, ut supra in Annotatis explicui&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040134Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040134Z">
        <seg>I suspect that here Hansen may be referring to the silver coin struck by order of Philip, King of Spain, which the biographer elsewhere called a pataco, as I explained above in the annotations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suspicor, hic ob similitudinem officii virum illum vocari&lt;/e1&gt; circitorem, &lt;e2&gt;quia observare debebat, an ministri tribunalis Quitensis munere suo fungerentur&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061708Z">
        <seg>I suspect that this man is called a circuit judge because of the similarity of his office, since he was supposed to observe whether the ministers of the Quito tribunal were performing their duties.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sæpe Joanna per litteras monebat genitorem, urgebat, instabat lachrymans;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035948Z">
        <seg>Often Joanna would admonish her father in letters, urging and pressing him while weeping.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sæpe acutissima eam angina, asthma, permolesta ischias, pleuritis, stomachi, pericardii, &amp; intestinorum dolores sine numero, &amp; sine lege frequentes convulsiones invadebant, medicis ut plurimum stupentibus, quomodo in tam arido corpuscula vires esset ad perferenda, quæ prosternendo cuilibet robustissimo satis fuissent, Rosa optime penetrante, non tam intemperie, quam a benevola Sponsi manu hæc sibi agonalia festa provenire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042047Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042047Z">
        <seg>She was often assailed by the most acute angina, asthma, extremely painful sciatica, pleurisy, pains in the stomach, pericardium, and intestines, and by countless, unceasing convulsions, the doctors being for the most part amazed how a body so dry could have the strength to endure what would have been enough to prostrate even the strongest man. She perceived that these agonizing torments did not arise from disorder, but from the benevolent hand of her Spouse, who, by penetrating her most perfectly, was bringing her these festive torments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sæpe obstupuit attenta mater, parvulam suam nulla talium cupediarum aut suavitate, aut varia pulchritudine capi: nam fructus sibi datos mox aliis donabat, numquam vel primis labellis admovebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041011Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041011Z">
        <seg>The attentive mother was often astonished that her little daughter was not captivated by any such delicacies, sweetness, or varied beauty; for she would at once give away the fruits offered to her, never even bringing them to her lips.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sæpe quæstoris uxor notavit, Rosam a sacello reducem aliquid novi solatii ipso premendi conatu prodere: plerumque enim bulliebat lætitiæ fervor, ut familiarem &amp; pie curiosam observatricem latere indicia, aut fallere ex toto non possent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054339Z">
        <seg>The questioner's wife often noted that Rosa, returning from the chapel, would betray some new comfort in her very effort to suppress it: for she was usually bubbling with such fervent joy that she could neither hide the signs from her familiar and piously curious observer, nor deceive her altogether.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sæpe, ac præcipue sextis feriis palatum, &amp; viscera cruciabat felle vervecino, quod in hunc usum potabile servabat, ut Christi sui in cruce sitibundi gustaret spongiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041135Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041135Z">
        <seg>Often, and especially on Fridays, he would torture his palate and bowels with goat's bile, which he kept drinkable for this purpose, so that he might taste the sponge that his Christ thirsted for on the cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sæpius conversa ad Sponsum: “Domine (inquit) auge, auge dolorem, dummodo augeas &amp; amorem”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052659Z">
        <seg>Often turning to the Spouse: "Lord," she said, "increase, increase the pain, so long as you increase love as well."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sæpius coram adstantibus, voce quantum potuit elata &amp; clara orthodoxæ fidei professionem replicuit, non sine jubilo, ac pene addito cygnæo cantu, protestans, se, uti hactenus vixerat, ita jam mori filiam sanctæ Romanæ &amp; universalis Ecclesiæ: se credidisse semper &amp; credere, quidquid credendum proponit ac docet magistra fidelium apostolica Sedes: numquam se (cum sui compos sit) ab hujus fidei solidissima firmitate recessuram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025956Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025956Z">
        <seg>Often in the presence of those standing by, she repeated the profession of the orthodox faith in a voice as loud and clear as she could, not without joy, and almost adding a swan's song, protesting that she, just as she had lived until then, would now die a daughter of the holy Roman and universal Church: that she had always believed and believed, whatever the apostolic See, the teacher of faith, proposes and teaches: that she would never, so long as she remained in her right mind, depart from the most solid firmness of this faith.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tabulis arcte commissis ligna omnino septem instravit justis intervallis, quæ, ne laxarentur, bubulis loris apte distrinxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042607Z">
        <seg>He laid seven planks entirely on the tightly joined boards at even intervals, which he properly secured with ox-hide thongs so they wouldn't loosen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tacenti aviculæ succedebat Rosa, ac voce, qua præstabat, tinnula suavissimaque prosequebatur divinas laudes, ingenio ad miraculum fœcundo, ac prompto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045313Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045313Z">
        <seg>Following the silent little bird was Rosa, and with the voice in which she excelled, she continued the divine praises with a most sweet and tinkling sound, her talent miraculously fruitful and prompt.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tacuere ambo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032327Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032327Z">
        <seg>Both were silent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tacuit Rosa, nec urgenti aliud umquam replicuit, quam stetisse intra mediocritatem suos dolores.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034913Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034913Z">
        <seg>Rosa fell silent, and when pressed she never replied to anything except that her sufferings had remained within moderation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tacuit Rosa, pulvinar suscepit, infarsit, sed non lana, uti credebat mater: vim segmentorum collegit, quæ de fabri lignarii dolabra excidunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042734Z">
        <seg>Rosa fell silent, took up a pillow, stuffed it, but not with wool, as her mother believed: she gathered the force of the shavings that are cut from the carpenter's adze.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tacuit homo, demum annuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044647Z">
        <seg>The man was silent, then finally nodded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Talis erat, quæ ibidem virgineum Rosæ corpusculum excepit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035337Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035337Z">
        <seg>Such was she who there received the virgin body of Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tam caro constitit Rosæ optati secessus tarda licentia, quam illo pretio perpaucæ sibi emptam (pleræque ademptam) vellent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043438Z">
        <seg>Rose's long-awaited leave of absence proved as costly as very few would have been willing to buy (and most would have been unwilling to grant) at that price.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tam cito oblita es, nuperrime Ferdinandi Perez mancipium, simillimo abusu allii &amp; piperis, omnino visum usumque oculorum perdidisse”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043429Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043429Z">
        <seg>Have you so soon forgotten that only recently the slave of Ferdinand Perez, by a very similar abuse of garlic and pepper, lost the sight and use of his eyes altogether?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tam profunde animo Juvenculæ insedit robusta hac cogitatio, tam funditus evacuavit congenitam formidandi proclivitatem, ut ex eo momento seipsam mirata sit Rosa, quod noctu, interdiu, domi, foris, ad nullius periculi, monstri, belluæ pavebat occursum, unico præsentis Sponsi præsidio animosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062111Z">
        <seg>This robust thought had taken such deep root in the maiden's mind, and so completely dispelled her innate propensity for fear, that from that moment on, Rosa marveled at herself, for she feared no encounter, by night or day, at home or abroad, with any danger, monster, or beast, emboldened by the sole protection of her present Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tamen ex sensu conjicio, per eam hic viles vestes indicari.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061637Z">
        <seg>However, from the context I infer that by it cheap clothes are meant here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tamen videtur hoc nomen oriri ex voce Græca&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;κηρὸς&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;qua Latini&lt;/e4&gt; ceram &lt;e5&gt;nominant&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035248Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035248Z">
        <seg>However, this name appears to derive from the Greek word κηρὸς, which the Latins call cera.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem Rosa nictu fortuito in se reflexa, vidit eodem, quo reliquæ ibi virgines, splendidissimo amictu se pariter circumdatam inclaruisse, &amp; quæ illactenus suo dumtaxat habitu Dominicano simpliciter canduerat, repente se deprehendit in cyclade gemmis &amp; auro distincta, ut ad easdem illic operas se quoque nosset auctoratam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044225Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044225Z">
        <seg>At last, Rosa, by a chance glance cast upon herself, saw that she too, like the other virgins there, had been made glorious by the same most splendid robe. And she, who until then had shone only in her simple Dominican habit, suddenly found herself arrayed in a girdle adorned with gems and gold, so that she recognized herself as having been commissioned to join in those same labors there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem Rosæ, &amp; rosarioli sui memor, loca dolorum offricuit, quievit, posteroque diluculo expergiscens, undequaque se sanum invenit, tibiæ vero molestus tumor ita desederat, ut jurasses hanc numquam tumuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033728Z">
        <seg>At last, Rose, remembering her rosary, offered up the stations of the Cross, rested, and when she awoke at dawn the next morning, she found herself well all over; indeed, the troublesome swelling on her shin had gone down so completely that you would have sworn it had never been there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem adfuit medicus, obiterque examinato sanguine, ac notatis in eo periculosi morbi criteriis, fœtentem massam de more projici imperavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061345Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061345Z">
        <seg>At last the physician arrived, and after examining the blood and noting in it the criteria of a dangerous disease, he ordered the foul-smelling mass to be thrown away as usual.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem cælestis religiosæ vitæ inspiratio eam invasit, Rosæ sepulchrum adiit, ei causam supplex commendat, repente illius co di ignitum sacri habitus Dominicani desiderium immittitur, in numerum Sororum tertiariarum adscribitur, divinis solatiis repletur, hoc unum dolens, quod tamdiu corde obstinato tantam felicitatem respuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043128Z">
        <seg>At last a heavenly inspiration for the religious life seized her. She went to the tomb of Rose, humbly commended her cause to her, and suddenly a burning desire for the sacred Dominican habit was implanted in her. She was enrolled among the Third Order Sisters, filled with divine consolations, grieving only that she had so long rejected such happiness with a stubborn heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem ex Rosæ reliquiis frustulum arripuit, &amp;, “Experiar, (inquit) num revera ea sit sanctitas Rosæ, quæ passim deprædicatur”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034559Z">
        <seg>Finally, he snatched a fragment from the relics of Rose and said, "I will test whether the sanctity of Rose, which is so widely preached, is truly hers."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem in conspectu multitudinis, quæ aderat, lente se erexit, vociferans: “Sanus sum, videor mihi posse incedere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033443Z">
        <seg>Finally, in the sight of the crowd that was present, he slowly rose, shouting: "I am well, I seem to be able to walk;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem in puncto horæ sextæ pomeridianæ, quasi perfuncta officio, avolabat, postridie ad idem reditura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045326Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045326Z">
        <seg>Finally, at exactly six o'clock in the afternoon, as if her duty were done, she would fly away, to return to the same place the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem ingenioso consilio suggessit Virgini, quod legitimum obtinendæ apud FF. Prædicatores sepulturæ desiderium potius mendicando, quam testando notificaret per scripturam, in hunc usum a Gundisalvo jam præparatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030049Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030049Z">
        <seg>Finally, he suggested to the Virgin an ingenious plan: that she should notify the Friars Preachers of her legitimate desire for burial by mendicating rather than by testament, using the document already prepared by Gundisalvus for this purpose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem interrogavit doctor, quo genere locutionis, intellectuali an vocali Christus verba superius recitata protulerit?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052851Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052851Z">
        <seg>Finally, the doctor asked by what kind of utterance—intellectual or vocal—Christ had spoken the words quoted above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem media nox dispescuit longum certamen;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052138Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052138Z">
        <seg>At last, the middle of the night broke the long contest;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem opportunitate seu facta seu arrepta, tectis interrogationibus eminus circumivit Virginem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040825Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040825Z">
        <seg>Finally, seizing the opportunity, he skirted around the Virgin with veiled questions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem particulam, ex Rosæ indumento sibi residuam, alacri fide manuque arripuit, hac subito nervos attractionis vinculo exsolvit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034937Z">
        <seg>At last he seized the particle, the last remnant of Rose's garment, with eager faith and hand, and with it suddenly broke the bonds of attraction's nerve.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem præmature utroque parente orbata, remansit sub tutela pupillari, donec Franciscus Lasso de la Vega, Chilensis regni gubernator &amp; præses, ob Rosæ tunc defunctæ piam &amp; usque in Chile celebrem memoriam, in se officium curamque suscepit ultro, ut orphanam suo sumptu e Chile Limam transmitteret, ubi aviæ Mariæ de Oliva (jam tunc moniali) in S. Catharinæ Senensis monasterio sanctissime convixit, ac singulari virtutum exemplo mirabiliter coruscavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012842Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012842Z">
        <seg>Finally, prematurely deprived of both parents, she remained under a guardian's care until Francisco Lasso de la Vega, governor and president of the kingdom of Chile, on account of the pious and throughout Chile celebrated memory of the then-deceased Rosa, of his own accord assumed the duty and care of sending the orphan from Chile to Lima at his own expense, where she most devoutly lived with her grandmother, Mary de Oliva (already a nun at the time), in the monastery of St. Catherine of Siena, and shone wonderfully by her singular example of virtue.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem repentina hilaritate perfusa, in pedes se erexit, &amp; gratiis Virgini matri devotissime persolutis læta domum properavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054428Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054428Z">
        <seg>Finally, overcome with sudden joy, she rose to her feet and, having most devoutly given thanks to the Virgin Mother, she hurried home happily.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem severius attracto supercilio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050451Z">
        <seg>Finally, with a more severe frown;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem supremo obedientiæ præcepto adegit filiam, ediceret, unde sciverit, tali hora e domo quæstoris sibi adferri chocolatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051735Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051735Z">
        <seg>Finally, by the supreme command of obedience, he ordered his daughter to find out, from wherever she could, at what hour chocolate was to be brought to him from the treasurer's house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem ut in postremum hoc mere laneum cervical incidit, ulterioris inops consilii, sic Filiam affata est: “Sane paruisti, Rosa mea, paruisti: siquidem hic modo nil nisi lanam reperio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042831Z">
        <seg>Finally, when this last purely woolen shawl came to hand, with no further counsel at hand, she said to her daughter, "You have indeed prepared, my Rose, you have indeed prepared; for here I find nothing but wool."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem, ipso B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060146Z">
        <seg>Finally, by B. himself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem, ut e sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis monasterio chartaceam Rosæ obtinuit imaginem, tota nocte dolenti lateri obpansam tenuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040429Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040429Z">
        <seg>Finally, having obtained a paper image of the Rose from the monastery of St. Catherine of Siena, she held it pressed against her aching side all night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tanta Virgini zeloti cura fuit, ne quid vel honori vel tributo cælestis Sponsi decederet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053409Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053409Z">
        <seg>The Virgin was so zealous that nothing might detract from the honor or tribute of the heavenly Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tanti favoris occasio hæc fuit: Rosam a multo tempore continuis integrisque noctibus adeo destituerat necessarius somnus, ut valetudini suæ non parum fuerit timendum, si medelam respueret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051310Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051310Z">
        <seg>This was the opportunity for such a favor: for a long time, necessary sleep had so deprived Rosa of uninterrupted, full nights that her health would have been seriously endangered had she refused treatment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tanti nimirum erat, obscuri &amp; vix noti militis pauperem filiam efferri ad sepulturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030604Z">
        <seg>It was, indeed, of such great importance that the poor daughter of an obscure and barely known soldier was carried out to burial.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tanti nimirum faciebat etiam leviores parendi occasiones.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035604Z">
        <seg>He certainly valued even the slightest opportunities to obey.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tanto constitit victrici Rosæ candidum seraphicæ Catharinæ habitum &amp; obtinere, &amp; retinere constantissime.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040410Z">
        <seg>It cost so much for the victor Rosa to obtain and most steadfastly retain the white seraphic habit of Catherine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tantum effecit, quod unus ex confessariis indulsit Virgini, ut per totam illius &amp; proximi anni (qui Rosæ postremus fuit) Quadragesimam, ex pristini rigoris forma denuo sibi lectulum instrueret e lignis, testis, tegulis constipatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042939Z">
        <seg>All he achieved was that one of the confessors granted the Virgin permission to spend the entire Lent of that year and the next (which was Rose's last) in a bed he had rebuilt from the old form of austerity, packed with wood, stones, and tiles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tantum in hoc Virgini bene erat, cum subinde libero ore Jesu nomen tenerrime pronuntiabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013353Z">
        <seg>Only in this virgin was it well, when she would from time to time tenderly pronounce the name of Jesus with her own lips.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tantum in vilissimas bestiolas imperium statui innocentiæ reservatum fuerat, quem quidem Rosa tam prope attigit, ut in solitudine cellæ versaretur tamquam in paradiso.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043841Z">
        <seg>Only over the vilest little creatures was the power of innocence reserved, which Rosa approached so closely that she wandered in the solitude of her cell as if in paradise.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tantum vel in bruti animalis salutem valuit unica vox miserentis Rosæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061602Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061602Z">
        <seg>The single, merciful voice of Rosa was enough to save even a brute animal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tantæ molis erat pacifice seraphicæ suæ magistræ habitu, &amp; instituto potiri, quam postea sic ad amussim expressit, ut non solum ab exteris altera Senensis Catharina non nisi magno humillimæ Virginis angore animi diceretur, sed &amp; ipsi confessario in lineamentis, gestu, &amp; vultu, sic in illam mirabili divini amoris metamorphosi commutata appareret, ut jam non similis, sed eadem prorsus esse videretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041343Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041343Z">
        <seg>It was such a monumental task to peacefully attain the habit and rule of her seraphic mistress, as she later expressed so precisely, that not only was she called by outsiders another Catherine of Siena, but only with the greatest anguish of the most humble Virgin's soul, but even to her confessor, in her features, gestures, and countenance, she appeared so transformed into that marvelous metamorphosis of divine love, that she seemed no longer like her, but the very same.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tardavit is ultra biduum, atque interea sanguis ab ipsa scaturigine jam notatus de putredine, horridius obputruit, seroso virore, flavis nigrisque maculis ac ramosa sanie undequaque pinguis &amp; &amp; decolor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061342Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061342Z">
        <seg>It delayed for more than two days, and in the meantime the blood from the very source, already noted for its putrefaction, had rotted even more horribly, becoming a foul, serous pus streaked with yellow and black, and everywhere thick and discolored with branching matter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Te vivo ac vidente, stabit in urbe hac illud, quod sæpe dixi, B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062625Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062625Z">
        <seg>While you are alive and present, that which I have often said will stand in this city, B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Telam, a genitoribus ejus pro suis ægritudinum necessitatibus concessam, illico inter duas virgines virtute &amp; genere nobiles, extreme tamen egenas, divisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042406Z">
        <seg>Telam, a gift granted by his parents for his own needs, immediately divided it among two virgins, noble in virtue and birth, yet extremely poor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tempestiva hæc fuere in tenella indole præludia, ut quid maturior esset molitura, ex his præambulis conjiceretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043308Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043308Z">
        <seg>These were timely preludes to her tender disposition, so that from these forerunners one could infer what she would undertake later.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tentabat Rosa gradatim abjectis mollioribus, ad exemplum S. Catharinæ Senensis, eo pertingere, ut, inscia matre, demum informe saxum capiti dormitura supponeret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042722Z">
        <seg>Rosa was trying, by gradually discarding softer things, following the example of St. Catherine of Siena, to get to the point where, without her mother's knowledge, she would finally place an unformed stone under her head to sleep on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tentabat deinde, saltem per vestigia illum quærere in creaturis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045527Z">
        <seg>He then tried, at least by the traces, to seek him in the creatures.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tentandum supererat ultimum subducendo ventri remedium, sed violentum &amp; acre, cui perferendo Didacus se parem fore diffidens, Rosam denuo ac instantissime implorat: &amp; ecce, omisso illo remedio, detumuit venter, abscessit duorum mensium possessione radicata febris, &amp; cum stupore omnium Didacus ad vota sua implenda lætus exsiliit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034041Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034041Z">
        <seg>The final remedy for the swelling of his belly remained to be tried, but it was a violent and bitter one. Doubting his ability to endure it, Diego implored Rosa again and most earnestly. And behold, that remedy having been omitted, his belly subsided, the two-month-old, deep-rooted fever departed, and to everyone's astonishment, Diego leapt up joyfully to fulfill his vows.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tentarant pluries familiares matronæ, adductis palpebris oculos defunctæ Virginis obtegere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030412Z">
        <seg>They had several times tried to cover the eyes of the deceased Virgin with her eyelids, drawing them down.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tentarunt ambæ, quid possent, alternabat vices lucta &amp; consultatio: sed neutri cedebat tenacis seræ firma contumacia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041730Z">
        <seg>Both tried their utmost; alternately they wrestled and conferred, but neither could overcome the stubborn, late-clinging obstinacy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tentavit, sed, acsi montem tergore excepisset, conatu irrito ridiculoque mox lassata destitit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040940Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040940Z">
        <seg>She tried, but, as if she had taken up a mountain on her back, soon grew weary of the futile and ridiculous attempt and gave up.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Territæ mulieres dolenter respexere in Rosam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012206Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012206Z">
        <seg>The terrified women looked back at Rosa with sorrow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Terruissent hæc quamcumque aliam, aut saltem eripuissent loquendi fiduciam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062722Z">
        <seg>These would have terrified any other, or at least robbed them of the confidence to speak.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tertio oras ac fimbrias huic vestitui circumdato 600 Salutationibus angelicis, toties &lt;b2&gt;Salve&lt;/b2&gt; &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011137Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011137Z">
        <seg>Thirdly, the edges and fringes of this garment are surrounded by 600 angelic salutations, each one saying "Hail," etc.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tertio oras ac fimbrias huic vestitui circumdato 600 Salutationibus angelicis, toties &lt;b5&gt;Salve&lt;/b5&gt; &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054614Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054614Z">
        <seg>Thirdly, the borders and fringes of this garment are surrounded by 600 Angelic Greetings, each one saying "Hail," etc.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Teste Castello, dicitur etiam&lt;/e5&gt; lingula, ligula, &lt;e6&gt;id est&lt;/e6&gt;, laryngis lingua: &lt;e7&gt;est enim corpus quoddam simile linguæ alicujus fistulæ, ubi arteria &amp; larynx inter se connectuntur, &amp; velut operculum omni tempore, quo animal respirat, apertum stans, quo transglutit, laryngi accumbens &amp;c&lt;/e7&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043122Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043122Z">
        <seg>According to Castalio, it is also called the lingula, ligula, that is, the tongue of the larynx: for it is a certain body resembling the tongue of some kind of pipe, where the artery and the larynx are connected with each other, and like a lid, always standing open whenever the animal breathes, through which it swallows, lying against the larynx, etc.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Theologorum scrutiniis in hac causa anceps sententia stetit, donec ipsa cælitus roborata constantissime status religiosi delectum non humanæ, sed divinæ inspirationis opus esse demonstravit, sibique vivendum usque ad extremum spiritum sub sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis magisterio, &amp; tandem futurum tempus, quo ibi Limæ exsurgeret hujus nominis, &amp; instituti monasterium, quod prophetico spiritu pronuntiatum fuisse, eventus ipse postea comprobavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041334Z">
        <seg>The scrutiny of theologians in this matter remained inconclusive, until she herself, confirmed from heaven, most steadfastly demonstrated that the choice of the religious state is the work not of human, but of divine inspiration, and that she was to live until her last breath under the guidance of St. Catherine of Siena, and that a time would finally come when a monastery of this name and institution would arise there in Lima, a prediction which subsequent events themselves later confirmed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Thomas Marcos;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035641Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035641Z">
        <seg>Thomas Marcos</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tibi, o Mater, alterum superest pallium novum adhuc fere intactum, quod priusquam vel modicum veterascat, divina bonitas non uno, sed pluribus te palliis ditabit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061125Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061125Z">
        <seg>"To you, O Mother, another new cloak remains, still almost intact; and before it has even slightly worn, divine goodness will bless you not with one, but with several cloaks."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tibi, tibi famulentur universi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053719Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053719Z">
        <seg>Let all things serve you.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Timuerunt germani fratres, ne forte propediem soror, magno familiæ suæ dedecore, ad sanctæ Inquisitionis&lt;a1&gt;b&lt;/a1&gt; tribunal raperetur, velut suspecta de imposturis ac fraudibus, aut sacrilegi mendacii fictæque sanctimoniæ palam vel rea, vel convincenda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010753Z">
        <seg>The brothers feared that their sister might soon be dragged before the tribunal of the Holy Inquisition, to the great disgrace of their family, as a suspect in impostures and frauds, or openly guilty of sacrilegious falsehood and feigned sanctity, or to be convicted of it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Timuerunt germani fratres, ne forte propediem soror, magno familiæ suæ dedecore, ad sanctæ Inquisitionis&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;b&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; tribunal raperetur, velut suspecta de imposturis ac fraudibus, aut sacrilegi mendacii fictæque sanctimoniæ palam vel rea, vel convincenda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052513Z">
        <seg>The brothers feared that their sister might soon be dragged before the tribunal of the Holy Inquisition, to the great disgrace of their family, as a suspect in impostures and frauds, or openly guilty of sacrilegious falsehood and feigned piety, or to be convicted of them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tormenta hæc erant, quæ sacram illi vestem reddere exosam poterant, ac primo verecunde occultandam, denique &amp; seponendam suggerebant, usque adeo, ut suum familiare asylum (sacellum dico SS. Rosarii, in quo Rosa habitum assumpserat) cursim adierit, quasi denuo illic firmitate immobili radicanda, solitoque magnæ Deiparæ præsidio tentationem molestissimam depulsura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040345Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040345Z">
        <seg>These were torments that could make her sacred habit hateful to her, and at first suggested it should be shyly hidden, then finally set aside, to the point that her familiar refuge (I mean the chapel of the Holy Rosary, in which Rosa had taken her habit) she ran to it, as if to be there once again rooted in an immovable firmness and, by the usual protection of the great Mother of God, to drive off the most troublesome temptation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Torquebat dæmonem illa hortensis cellula, tot cæli deliciis plena;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051914Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051914Z">
        <seg>That garden cell tormented the demon, so full of heavenly delights.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Torquebat hæc fama quæstorem, consortem, filias;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054041Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054041Z">
        <seg>This rumor tormented the quaestor, his partner, and his daughters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tota deinceps cura erat salvandi exanime illud corpusculum, ne ante sepulturam importunis vellicationibus discerperetur: jam enim notatum erat, unum ei deesse digitum, ferro, an morsu occulte detruncatum, non liquet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031022Z">
        <seg>All subsequent care was devoted to preserving that lifeless little body, lest it be torn by importunate tugging before burial: for it had already been noted that one finger was missing, whether secretly severed by iron or by a bite is not clear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tota serena, comis, benigna, de se nesciebat altum sapere, sed tantum subjici omni humanæ creaturæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040924Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040924Z">
        <seg>Entirely serene, courteous, and kind, she was unaware of her own high-mindedness, but only of her submission to every human being.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Toto septennio omnifaria tentata fuere medicamina;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033734Z">
        <seg>Throughout the seven years, all kinds of medicines were tried.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Totum evenit, prout Virgo prædixerat: nam anno MDCXXII accedens ad eam functionem Ludovicus, inter solemnia, sub Missa recordatus est vaticinii, peractoque Sacrificio, palam adstantibus enarravit, quam asseveranter, clare, diserte ante complures annos Rosa superstes, quod illa die locoque gesserat, prænuntiarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062657Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062657Z">
        <seg>Everything happened just as the Virgin had foretold: for in the year 1622, as Louis was about to assume that office, during the solemn Mass he remembered the prophecy, and after the Sacrifice was completed, he publicly explained to those present how confidently, clearly, and eloquently the surviving Rose had, many years before, predicted what she had done that day and in that place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Totum evenit, uti Virgo prædixerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012455Z">
        <seg>Everything happened just as the Virgin had predicted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Totum triennium frustra datum remediis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034128Z">
        <seg>Three whole years were wasted on remedies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Trahebat cælestis Sponsus electam sibi Virginem per arcanas sanctimoniæ ac perfectionis semitas: ex adverso trahebat rudis &amp; cruda mater Filiam morigeram ad pompas nugasque seculi, ad vanos corporis ornatus, ad mundi commercia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035254Z">
        <seg>The heavenly Bridegroom was drawing the Virgin he had chosen for himself along the secret paths of holiness and perfection; on the other hand, the rough and crude mother was drawing her obedient daughter to the pomps and vanities of the world, to vain adornments of the body, to the affairs of the world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tranquillitatem cujusdam communitatis, in qua invaluerat discordia, non sine lachrymis, &amp; aliorum admiratione biduo orans obtinuit, plurimisque aliis ibi dignata fuit favoribus, alloquiis, &amp; visionibus, quæ in examine obtemperans aperuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042223Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042223Z">
        <seg>She obtained, by praying for two days, the peace of a certain community, in which discord had prevailed, not without tears and to the admiration of others, and was there honored with many other favors, revelations, and visions, which she revealed by submitting to an examination.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Transegerat Virgo totam illam Quadragesimam solo pane &amp; aqua, sed præsertim illis hebdomadæ sanctæ postremis diebus vix micatim aliquid cibi admiserat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040748Z">
        <seg>The Virgin had spent the entire Lenten season on nothing but bread and water, but especially in those last days of Holy Week she had barely allowed herself a morsel of food.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Transeundum erat per amplissimum urbis forum, ubi tunc metu dilapsa clamosi populi multitudo ferocienti tauro spatium fecerat, confusaque vociferatione, &amp; lapidum jactu furiosam bestiam pro voluptate spectaculi sævius irritabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062139Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062139Z">
        <seg>One had to pass through the city's largest forum, where, then, a fear-stricken, clamorous crowd had made way for a raging bull, and with confused shouting and the hurling of stones, they more fiercely provoked the furious beast for the sake of the spectacle's enjoyment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Transeundum per singula.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061755Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061755Z">
        <seg>Go through each one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Transivit, certus, ejuscemodi coruscum decorem a mortalitatis nostræ regione non esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055820Z">
        <seg>He passed on, certain that such a brilliant splendor did not belong to the realm of our mortality.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tres circiter abiere menses, quando Rodericus inflammari se sensit impetuoso ac subito religiosæ vitæ desiderio, jamque ultro parentes, quandoquidem ita ipsis collibitum erat, incunctanter serioque agerent cum Patribus de sua in inclytam eorum Societatem admissione.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012622Z">
        <seg>About three months had passed when Rodericus felt himself seized by a sudden and impetuous desire for the religious life, and he himself, since it had been so agreed, without delay and in earnest spoke with the Fathers about his admission into their illustrious Society.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tria potissima erant, de quorum assecutione Virginem dubitare non sinebat cælitus concessa fiduciæ certitudo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061742Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061742Z">
        <seg>There were three things of paramount importance, and the Virgin did not allow herself to doubt their attainment, for a heavenly certainty of her trust had been granted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tribus horis laboratum frustra, rebusque jam desperatis, Isabella se mori sentiens, ad lectum reportari petiit, decentius illic moritura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034230Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034230Z">
        <seg>After three hours of fruitless labor, and with all hope now lost, Isabella, sensing she was about to die, asked to be carried back to her bed, where she would die more fittingly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Triduo ante felicem obitum supplex voce flebili divinum Viaticum, &amp; extremam petiit Unctionem: delato Eucharistico epulo instar coruscantis auroræ gratiose rubuit, &amp; gaudio suo impar in altissimam ecstasim rapta fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042806Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042806Z">
        <seg>Three days before her happy passing, she humbly and with a mournful voice petitioned for the divine Viaticum and the last Anointing. When the Eucharistic food was brought, she blushed graciously like a shining dawn, and, rapt in joy beyond measure, was carried away into the highest ecstasy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Triennio ante discessum gravi morbo decubuit, ut jam extremo funeri matura &amp; deficiens cum a confessario, ac domesticis collachrymaretur, quem intrepida jussit intempestivum abigere mœrorem, &amp; certum esse, tunc se non morituram;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042707Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042707Z">
        <seg>Three years before her departure, she was laid low by a serious illness, so that, already near her end and failing, she was weeping with her confessor and household when she ordered the intrepid one to drive away the untimely sorrow, and to be certain that she would not die then.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Triennis puerulus Alphonsus Cortes adeo languebat pedibus, ut eos, manibus reptans, per solum traheret ceu pondus inutile.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035926Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035926Z">
        <seg>The three-year-old boy Alphonsus Cortes was so weak in his legs that he dragged them along the ground with his hands, as if they were a useless burden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tristabatur perplexa mater;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062244Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062244Z">
        <seg>The perplexed mother was grieving.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tu ergo Sponsum tuum exora, ut mearum immemor culparum, me valetudini, qua ipsi queam famulari, restituat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040605Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040605Z">
        <seg>Therefore, pray to your Spouse that, forgetting my faults, He may restore me to health, so that I may be able to serve Him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tu in horto occlusa, utique non habuisti, quem illuc mitteres, neque ex hac domo ullus abiit, qui tuam necessitatem quæstori indicaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051704Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051704Z">
        <seg>You, shut up in the garden, of course had no one you could send there, nor did anyone from this house go who could inform the tax collector of your need.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tuguriolum erat quinque pedes longum, quatuor pedes latum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043640Z">
        <seg>The little hut was five feet long, four feet wide;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tum mater velut substomachans, “Eia (inquit) non flores, sed monstra florum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035539Z">
        <seg>Then the mother, as if scolding, said, "Come on, not flowers, but monsters of flowers."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tum vero attonita, “Ut quid (inquit) tot dies noctesque exegisti sub his cruciatibus, nec abjecisti pelles, quæ sic te excarnificabant”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035520Z">
        <seg>Then, truly astonished, she said, "Why did you endure so many days and nights under these torments, and not cast off the skins that were so dismembering you?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tum vero patentibus oculis vidit Rosam (quam ex visis hinc inde imaginibus noverat) in genua procubuam orare pro urbis suæ incolumitate.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040723Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040723Z">
        <seg>Then, with eyes wide open, he saw Rosa (whom he had known from the various images he had seen) kneeling and praying for the safety of her city.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tunc memineris, hæc modo fuisse tibi prædicta a vili muliercula”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062653Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062653Z">
        <seg>Then you will remember that these things were just now foretold to you by a lowly little woman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tunc noctes transigebat insomnes, assidebat lectulo, stratum componebat, agebat omnia, præteribat nihil, quod solatium adferre posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035811Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035811Z">
        <seg>Then he would spend sleepless nights, sit on his bed, make the bed, do everything—omitting nothing that could bring comfort.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tunc patuit inter has derelictiones repente in ipso pristinæ unionis meridie, veluti in Sponsi brachiis, se deprehendisse restitutam, sensisse quoddam donum inexplicabile, solidum &amp; fundamentale, quo videbatur sibi reddita impeccabilis, ut ille, qui dicebat: “Quis nos separabit a charitate Christi”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041805Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041805Z">
        <seg>Then it became clear, amid these abandonments, that she had suddenly found herself restored in the very midday of her former union, as if in the arms of the Bridegroom; she had perceived an inexplicable, solid, and foundational gift by which she seemed to have been made impeccable, like him who said, "Who will separate us from the love of Christ?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tunc vero statuerat Virgo, hoc unicum ab alumno exigere diuturnæ alimoniæ maternæque solicitudinis præmium, iret, properaret ad gentes Christianæ legis ignaras, trophæum salutis in medio barbararum nationum erigeret, &amp; quotquot posset animas a restibus diaboli strenue explicaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060508Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060508Z">
        <seg>Then indeed the Virgin had resolved to demand this one thing from her protégé as the reward for her long-standing support and maternal care: that he go, that he hurry to the peoples ignorant of the Christian law, that he raise the trophy of salvation in the midst of barbarian nations, and that he mightily free as many souls as he could from the snares of the devil.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Turibius Alphonsus Mogroveio, de quo supra.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040022Z">
        <seg>Turibius Alphonsus Mogrovejo, of whom mention has been made above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tuum lauda Creatorem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045242Z">
        <seg>Praise your Creator.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tympanum erat, cujus levi tactu perculsus dæmon fuga turpissima declinabat certamen.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042344Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042344Z">
        <seg>It was a drum, whose light touch struck the demon, who, in the most shameful flight, shunned the contest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ubi Rosa in tanti connubii fœdus consensit, arduos ei cædendorum marmorum labores imposuit, &amp; brevem terminum ad perficiendum opus, dum peregre rediret, indixit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041458Z">
        <seg>When Rosa consented to such a marriage contract, he imposed upon her the arduous task of chiseling marble and set a short deadline for completing the work before his return from abroad.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ubi anachoretica Virginis cella constiterat, soli humiditas &amp; arbuscularum frequentia infinitam culicum multitudinem aut gignebat, aut trahebat in amicum suo generi umbraculum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043745Z">
        <seg>Where the Virgin's hermitage had been established, the damp soil and the abundance of saplings either bred an endless multitude of gnats or drew them into the shade of her dwelling.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ubi leges Rosam in ipso mortis articulo morigeram Gundisalvo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035632Z">
        <seg>Where you will see Rosa, at the very point of death, obedient to Gundisalvo.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ubi sub templi foribus tumba deaurata promiscuæ multitudini in conspectum venit, festivus undique clamor ad astra increpuit, confusæque vociferationes plaudentium, lachrymantium, implorantium, omnem reliqui cantus ecclesiastici auditum surripuerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031450Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031450Z">
        <seg>When the gilded tomb appeared before the temple gates in full view of the assembled multitude, a festive cry from every quarter rose to the heavens, and the mingled shouts of those applauding, weeping, and imploring drowned out all other sound of ecclesiastical song.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ubi vero Seraphicam ejusdem vitæ historiam domi per capitula legi audivit, attentissima, tamquam soli legeretur sibi, medullitus se sensit inflammari ad sequelam, non quietura, donec &amp; foris in habitu, &amp; intus in fimbriis aureis, tantæ heroïnæ ac magistræ discipula conformaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040236Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040236Z">
        <seg>But when she heard the Seraphic history of that same life read at home by chapters, she listened with the utmost attention, as if it were being read to her alone, and felt herself kindled to the marrow to follow in its footsteps, not to rest until she conformed herself, both outwardly in her dress and inwardly in her golden fringes, to the example of so great a heroine and teacher.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ubi vero quæstoris eam domus contubernalem excepit, nil novum fuit, si complures continuos dies absque ullo omnino alimento transmittebat: plerumque etiam feria quinta solebat ibidem se domestico oratorio includere, nec inde se movere usque ad Sabbatum: ita quod infra hoc triduum plane insomnis, non modo carebat omni cibo potuque corporali, verum etiam (quod quæstorem Gundisalvum maxime rapiebat in admirationem) toto illo tempore remanebat uni affixa angulo, ut neque ad momentum pro quacumque necessitate ab illo discederet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041340Z">
        <seg>When, however, the quæstor's house received her as a housemate, it was nothing new if she passed several consecutive days without any food at all: she also usually used to shut herself in her private oratory on Thursdays, and would not leave until Saturday: So that during this three-day period, completely sleepless, she not only lacked all bodily food and drink, but also (which most amazed the quæstor Gundisalvus) remained for the entire time fixed to one corner, so that she did not leave it for a moment for any need.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ubique conclamatum ad arma, &amp; interim clerus uterque (ut fit publicis periculis) divina imploraturus præsidia, Limæ per omnes ecclesias sanctissimum Corporis dominici Sacramentum exposuerat palam, ab imbelli multitudine adorandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060143Z">
        <seg>Now everywhere the call to arms rang out, and in the meantime both clergy, as is done in public dangers, to implore divine aid, had publicly exposed the most holy Sacrament of the Body of the Lord in all the churches of Lima, to be adored by the unarmed multitude.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ultimam utriusque parentis benedictionem summe exoptavit, eamque venerabunda recepit: sic &amp; a quæstore Gundisalvo, ejusque uxore, quos veluti parentes coluerat, benedici voluit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042843Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042843Z">
        <seg>He earnestly sought the final blessing of both parents and received it reverently; in the same way, he wished to be blessed by the quæstor Gundisalvus and his wife, whom he had honored as parents.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ultimæ infirmitatis meæ dolores, qui me conficient, atrocissimi erunt, sed omnium erit immanissimum tormentum sitis, cui ut tunc subvenias, ex nunc obnixe &amp; anxie te precor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013152Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013152Z">
        <seg>The pains of my final illness, which will consume me, will be the most atrocious, but the greatest torment of all will be thirst, for which I earnestly and anxiously beg you to relieve me then.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Una hic commemorandi forent, laude non multum impari, P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050212Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050212Z">
        <seg>One here might be mentioned, not much less worthy of praise, P.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Una hic commemorandi forent, laude non multum impari, P. Didacus Martinez Societatis Jesu, Patres magistri F. Alphonsus Velasquez, F. Ludovicus de Bilbao, &amp; F. Joannes Perez: at primorum duorum examen tot ac tanta complectitur, ut ex reliquis nil addi possit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010427Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010427Z">
        <seg>One here deserves to be mentioned, with no great disparagement of the others: Father Diego Martinez of the Society of Jesus, and the Fathers-masters Francis Alphonsus Velasquez, Francis Louis de Bilbao, and Francis John Perez. But the examination of the first two encompasses so much and so many things that nothing can be added from the rest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Una præsentium, uti viderat exspirantis Rosæ grabatum numerosa angelorum corona circumdari, ita notavit, jam circa defunctæ feretrum eosdem alternante concentu festivissime tripudiare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030316Z">
        <seg>One of those present, having seen the dying Rose's bier surrounded by a numerous crown of angels, likewise observed that they were now dancing most festively around the deceased's bier in an alternating chorus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde Gorræus pag. 484 operis proxime laudati illam corporis partem sic describit:&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;Ὑποχόνδριον&lt;/s2&gt; appellatur tota illa corporis humani regio, quæ a mucronata cartilagine utrimque ad ilia usque protenditur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025845Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025845Z">
        <seg>Hence Gorræus, on page 484 of the work just cited, describes that part of the body as follows: "Hypocondrium" is the name given to that entire region of the human body which extends from the costal cartilage on both sides down to the ilia.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde Rosæ inter plebeios natæ &amp; educatæ, manuum suarum labore sustentatæ, in sæculari domo domestice moratæ ac mortuæ, tantarum virtutum schola patuit, aut schola tam sublimium contemplationum in promptu fuit?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032938Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032938Z">
        <seg>Where, then, for the Roses—born and raised among the common people, supported by the labor of their own hands, living and dying in a secular home—was there a school of such virtues, or a school of such sublime contemplations, readily at hand?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde Virgini cruciabiliter arescenti unum id superfuit, cum moriente Sponso clamare: &lt;b0&gt;Sitio.&lt;/b0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025758Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025758Z">
        <seg>Thus, the Virgin, wasting away painfully, had only this left to her, as she cried out with her dying Spouse: "I thirst."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde ad faciliora effatu prudenter digrediens, inquisivit de exercitiis mortificationis ac pœnitentiæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050612Z">
        <seg>Therefore, prudently turning to easier matters, he inquired about exercises in mortification and penance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde arrepta coram incredulis tabella &amp; cera, futuri monasterii extemporaneum formavit archetypon, ea tum peritia, tum securitate, ut spectantes rapuerit in stuporem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062728Z">
        <seg>So, seizing a tablet and wax before the unbelievers, he improvised an extemporaneous archetype of the future monastery, with such skill and confidence that he left the onlookers in astonishment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde conscientia, sive illa facultas animæ, quæ vitiis adversatur, &amp; perpetrata peccata statim arguit, a Græcis dicitur&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;συντήρησις&lt;/s5&gt;, &lt;e6&gt;id est, quasi reverens observatio, quam nos communiter remorsum conscientiæ vocamus&lt;/e6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033018Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033018Z">
        <seg>Hence conscience, or that faculty of the soul which opposes vices and immediately reproves committed sins, is called by the Greeks synteresis, that is, as it were a reverent observance, which we commonly call the reproach of conscience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde dicit, sanctam Virgunculam&lt;/e6&gt; chirothecas, &lt;e7&gt;quas jussu matris induerat&lt;/e7&gt;, pyrothecas &lt;e8&gt;sensisse, id est, illas ex thecis seu tegumentis manuum thecas ignis factas esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T044109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035843Z">
        <seg>Hence he says that the holy Virgin's&lt;/e6&gt; gloves, &lt;e7&gt;which she had put on at her mother's command&lt;/e7&gt;, had become pyrotechnic &lt;e8&gt;gloves, that is, that they had been made into fire-boxes from the cases or coverings of the hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde facile obtinuit, ut tum in Paschate, tum in solemnitate Corporis Christi quotidie per totas octavas sibi licuerit vesci hoc delicato Pane angelorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055709Z">
        <seg>Thus he easily obtained permission to partake daily, throughout the entire octave, of this delicate Bread of Angels, both at Easter and on the Solemnity of Corpus Christi.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde hæc biographi comparatio satis manifeste ad illam fabulam alludere cognoscitur.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033059Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033059Z">
        <seg>Hence this biographical comparison is clearly known to allude to that story.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde in hac, aliisque suis infirmitatibus solebat dicere: “O quam salubris, quam jucunda &amp; felix sors foret, crebriori ægritudine divexari, dummodo proximorum abesset incommodum!” Sæpe Virginis guttur &amp; fauces, ex inferiorum musculorum inflammatione, acutissima invadebat angina&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;, cui interdum periculosior succrescebat cynanche&lt;a1&gt;d&lt;/a1&gt;: sæpius totis pulmonibus &amp; bronchis decertandum fuit cum asthmate&lt;a2&gt;e&lt;/a2&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010805Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010805Z">
        <seg>Hence, in this and her other infirmities, she was accustomed to say: "Oh, what a healthy, pleasant, and happy fate it would be to be beset by more frequent illnesses, so long as it were free from the inconvenience of those closest to her!" Often the Virgin's throat and windpipe, from inflammation of the lower muscles, was attacked by the most acute angina, to which at times a more dangerous cynanche would sometimes grow: more often the whole lungs and bronchi had to contend with asthma;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde in hac, aliisque suis infirmitatibus solebat dicere: “O quam salubris, quam jucunda &amp; felix sors foret, crebriori ægritudine divexari, dummodo proximorum abesset incommodum!” Sæpe Virginis guttur &amp; fauces, ex inferiorum musculorum inflammatione, acutissima invadebat angina&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, cui interdum periculosior succrescebat cynanche&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;d&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;: sæpius totis pulmonibus &amp; bronchis decertandum fuit cum asthmate&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;e&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052609Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052609Z">
        <seg>Hence, in this and her other infirmities, she was accustomed to say: "Oh, what a healthy, pleasant, and happy fate it would be to be beset by more frequent illness, so long as it were free from the inconvenience of those around her!" Often the Virgin's throat and windpipe, from inflammation of the lower muscles, was invaded by the most acute angina&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, to which at times a more dangerous cynanche&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;d&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;would sometimes grow: more often the whole lungs and bronchi had to contend with asthma&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;e&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde iterum anima ejus in malis tabescebat, iterum diffugiebat omne evadendi solatium, iterum exanimem abyssi operiebant, &amp; Rosa tota jam spinea, imo spina, rigebat in ima voragine tribulationum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045614Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045614Z">
        <seg>Whereupon her soul withered again in evil, again fled from every comfort of escape, again the abysses covered her lifeless, and Rosa, now all thorny, nay, a thorn herself, lay rigid in the deepest vortex of tribulations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde miserata parens, ne affectis luminibus liberior foris noceret aër, indulgebat filiæ optatissimam solitudinis ac latibuli facultatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043342Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043342Z">
        <seg>Therefore, out of pity, the mother, so that the air outside might not harm her daughter's affected eyes, granted her the most eagerly desired freedom of solitude and concealment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde non levis conjectura fuit, ut S. Raymundi&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;, ita &amp; Rosæ veteri sepulchro hoc datum esse divinitus, quod in locum sublati pulveris, alius continua scaturigine perpetim subnascatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031707Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031707Z">
        <seg>Hence it was not a slight conjecture that, just as with St. Raymond, so too this was divinely granted to the old tomb of the Rose, that in the place of the dust that was removed, another springs up continuously and perpetually.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde per&lt;/e1&gt; Attalicam &lt;e2&gt;hic vestis pretiosa vel magnifica intelligitur&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055529Z">
        <seg>Hence, by Attalic here, a precious or magnificent garment is understood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde pluries de cantantis ore auditi sunt versus isti, numeris Hispanicis illigati, quos utcumque latine reddiderim:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053238Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053238Z">
        <seg>Hence, these verses, bound to Spanish numbers, have often been heard from the singer's mouth, which I have rendered into Latin however I could:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde quadam occasione videns plorantem matrem, quasi divinæ quæsturæ correpta zelo, “Hem (inquit) o mater, quid agis?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053359Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053359Z">
        <seg>So, on a certain occasion, seeing a weeping mother, as if seized by zeal for a divine quest, she said, "Hey there, mother, what are you doing?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde quatuor ante obitum mensibus, valens ac sana Gundisalvi uxorem sic allocuta est: “Scito, mater, me quarto abhinc mense viam universæ carnis certo ingressuram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013149Z">
        <seg>Four months before her death, while still strong and healthy, Gundisalvus's wife spoke to her thus: "Know, mother, that in the fourth month from now I shall surely enter the common path of all flesh."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde quæ tunc verecunde invita aperuit, perpauca ac pene vulgaria crediderim, si conferantur iis, quæ Rosa sub divino secreti beneficio ac humilitatis sigillo retinuit obsignata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040850Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040850Z">
        <seg>Therefore, what she then modestly and reluctantly revealed, I would consider very few and almost commonplace, if compared to those things that Rosa kept sealed under the divine benefit of secrecy and the seal of humility.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde satis clarum est, hic eam vocem pro scintillis usurpari.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045912Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045912Z">
        <seg>Hence it is quite clear that this word is used here for sparks.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde sic diurnum nocturnumque tempus distribuit, ut ex utroque duodecim horas universim orationi seponeret, alias decem impenderet labori manuum, quo parentes sustentabat, reliquas corporis necessitatibus &amp; brevi quieti deputaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043026Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043026Z">
        <seg>Thus he divided his time between day and night so that he would set aside twelve hours in all for prayer from both, devote another ten to manual labor, by which he supported his parents, and allocate the rest to the body's necessities and brief rest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde synodus Mexicana apud eminentissimum Cardinalem de Aguirre tomo 4 Conciliorum pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040428Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040428Z">
        <seg>Hence the Mexican synod, at the eminent Cardinal de Aguirre, in the fourth volume of the Councils, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde synodus Mexicana apud eminentissimum Cardinalem de Aguirre tomo 4 Conciliorum pag. 352 § 16&lt;/e1&gt;, Præcepit, ut nulla prædictarum mulierum, quas BEATAS vocant, habitum alicujus religionis approbatæ deferat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000521Z">
        <seg>Hence the Mexican synod, under the Most Eminent Cardinal de Aguirre, in volume 4 of the Councils, page 352, section 16, decreed that none of the aforementioned women, whom they call BEATAS, should wear the habit of any approved religious order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Undique ex Asia, Africa, &amp; Europa sanctorum virorum, atque mulierum cujuscumque ætatis, conditionis, &amp; gradus agmina collecta fuere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041036Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041036Z">
        <seg>From all over Asia, Africa, and Europe, processions of holy men and women of every age, condition, and rank were gathered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unica demum Mariana ancilla secreti conscia fuit, quam validis obtestationibus adegit Rosa, ne proderet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042555Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042555Z">
        <seg>Finally, only Mariana's handmaid was privy to the secret, whom Rosa had compelled by strong entreaties not to reveal it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unicum hujus paradigma dabo, unde conjiciatur de reliquis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035455Z">
        <seg>I will give a single example of this paradigm, from which the rest can be inferred.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unicum istud singularissimæ pietatis erga matrem, &amp; extremæ commiserationis specimen sileri hic minime debet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035824Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035824Z">
        <seg>That single act of the most singular piety toward his mother, and the most extreme example of compassion, should by no means be omitted here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unicum paradigma dabo, &amp; finiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055324Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055324Z">
        <seg>I will give a single paradigm, and I will finish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unicum supererat, in quo plurimum hæsitabat, nimirum haud videns, quomodo sibi obvenire jam posset concionandi munus in propinqua sanctissimi Rosarii solemnitate, quandoquidem pro illo anno hæ vices non suæ erant, sed sui Provincialis M.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012408Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012408Z">
        <seg>Only one thing remained, and he was most perplexed about it, namely, not seeing how he could possibly be assigned the duty of preaching at the upcoming solemnity of the Most Holy Rosary, since for that year those duties did not belong to him but to his Provincial, M.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unius ex suis confessariis missioni deputati animum ad maturandum opus inflammare studuit, &amp; cum eo convenit, ut medietas omnium fructuum, quos interea ipsa ex toto bonorum operum exercitio se cumulaturam sperabat, ei adscriberetur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042332Z">
        <seg>She sought to inflame the spirit of one of her confessors, who had been appointed to a mission, to hasten his work, and she agreed with him that half of all the fruits which, in the meantime, she herself hoped to gather from the full practice of good works would be credited to him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Universim decem &amp; novem personæ ingenuæ, præter domestica promiscui sexus mancipia, Rosæ morientis lectulum mœsto silentio ambierant, &amp; ubi Virgo spiritum reddidit, singulæ occulta quadam spiritus suavitate a planctu in plausum ultro se adigi compererunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030313Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030313Z">
        <seg>In all, nineteen noble persons, besides the household slaves of both sexes, had surrounded the dying Rose's little bed in mournful silence, and when the Virgin yielded her spirit, each one found herself, by a certain hidden sweetness of spirit, drawn of her own accord from weeping to applause.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Uno horum suggerente, affertur imago Rosæ ex Prioris cubiculo, quam ut æger aspexit, ut impacto osculo devote salutavit, verticique reverenter imposuit, acturum sensit corroborari nervos, redire vires.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040800Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040800Z">
        <seg>At one of them suggesting it, an image of the Rose is brought from the Prior's chamber, which, as the sick man beheld it, he greeted devoutly with a fervent kiss and reverently placed upon his brow, feeling his nerves strengthened and his strength restored.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unum hoc superioribus addidisse sufficiat, quod, ubi Rosam in via illuminativa probare cœpit, adstupuit responsis puellæ simplicis atque illiteratæ, cum de arcano SS. Trinitatis mysterio, de hypostatica Verbi unione, de sacramento Altaris, de gloria Beatorum, de libro vitæ ac prædestinationis, de natura gratiæ, aliisque fidei ac theosophiæ secretis interrogata, promebat axiomata tam profunda ac solida, conceptus tam acutos ac sublimes, sententias tam perspicuas, breves, fœcundas ac ponderosas, verba tam propria, apposita, clara, succincta, ut alius examinator non dubitarit fateri ingenue, numquam se in tam luminosum &amp; perspicax ingenium incidisse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050634Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050634Z">
        <seg>Let it suffice to add this one thing to the foregoing: that when he began to test Rosa on the illuminative way, he was astonished by the replies of the simple and uneducated girl, when speaking of the mystery of the Holy Sacrament. When she was questioned about the mystery of the Trinity, the hypostatic union of the Word, the sacrament of the Altar, the glory of the Blessed, the book of life and predestination, the nature of grace, and other secrets of faith and theology, she produced axioms so profound and solid, concepts so acute and sublime, sentences so clear, brief, fecund and weighty, words so proper, apt, clear, and concise, that another examiner did not hesitate to frankly confess that he had never encountered such a luminous and perspicacious mind;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unus ex confessariis Virginis obiter habitaculi angustiam perstrinxit, cui Rosa lepide replicuit, tantum inesse spatii, quantum sibi Sponsoque cælesti abunde sufficeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043646Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043646Z">
        <seg>One of the Virgin's confessors casually remarked on the narrowness of her dwelling, to which Rose wittily replied that there was just enough space for herself and her heavenly Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unus respondebat, aut deliria esse, aut insomnia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045704Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045704Z">
        <seg>One answered that it was either delirium or insomnia.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Urbs est nova ab Hispanis, quibus paret, excitata in valle seu tractu &lt;b3&gt;Chuquisaca&lt;/b3&gt; dicto (unde urbs ipsa sæpe sic vocatur, teste Laetio) estque valde culta, &amp; dives, celebrisque fodinis argenteis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040205Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040205Z">
        <seg>The city, founded by the Spaniards to whom it is subject, was established in the valley or tract called Chuquisaca (from which the city itself is often so called, as Laetius attests), and it is very cultivated, wealthy, and famous for its silver mines.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Urgebat Rosa, meminisset datæ fidei, qua quarto abhinc mense promiserat valenti ac sanæ, quantum aquæ in ultima ægritudine flagitaret, se prompte daturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025751Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025751Z">
        <seg>Rosa urged him to remember the promise she had made four months earlier to a strong and healthy man, that she would promptly give him as much water as he demanded in his final illness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Urgebat interim doctor, instabat bis, ter;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050445Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050445Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile the doctor urged him on, pressing him twice, three times.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ursinus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043525Z">
        <seg>Bear</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Usa sum, nec mihi deinceps, ubi decubuero ex morbo, quidquam defuturum puta: Dominus abunde providebit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061057Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061057Z">
        <seg>I have used it, and from now on, when I fall sick, do not think that I will lack anything: the Lord will provide abundantly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Usque ad bruta animalia clemens Rosæ suavitas, &amp; tenera ostendebat &amp; juvabat commiseratio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042449Z">
        <seg>Rosa's gentle sweetness extended even to the brute animals, and her tender compassion both showed itself and offered help.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Usque ad gradum perpetis cum Deo unionis Virgo ascenderat, simulque cœpit in dies singulos atrocissima pati mentalium obtenebrationum intervalla, quæ non obiter, sed solidis plerumque horis eo deprimebant miseram, ut sæpe nescierit in gehenna esset, an in purgatorii diro carcere, vel quibus in ergastulis retrusa caligaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045453Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045453Z">
        <seg>She had ascended to the degree of perpetual union with God, and at the same time she began to suffer each day the most atrocious intervals of mental obscuration, which, not merely occasionally but for solid hours, so oppressed the poor soul that she often did not know whether she was in hell, in the dreadful prison of purgatory, or in what dungeons she was shut up and blinded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Usque adeo non aberravit religiosa matrona, quæ Rosam, stellis amicam, in stellæ figura agnovit, cui tunc pro firmamento exiguum cellæ latibulum erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043720Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043720Z">
        <seg>The devout matron did not stray so far as to fail to recognize Rose, a friend of the stars, in the shape of a star, whose only shelter then was a tiny cell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Usque adeo verum est, quod is, qui adhæret Domino, unus spiritus est, &amp; ei quoque obediunt omnia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041737Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041737Z">
        <seg>It is so true that he who clings to the Lord is one spirit with him, and that all things also obey him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Usque in digitos proserpserat molestissimus tumor, ut afflictissimus sacerdos in Missa nec divinam Hostiam commode satis tractare, nec fine alterius brachii suffulcimento alterum potuerit elevare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033310Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033310Z">
        <seg>A most troublesome swelling had crept up to his fingertips, so that the afflicted priest, at Mass, could neither properly handle the sacred Host nor raise his other arm without the support of his forearm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Usque in illam horam vultus imaginis is erat, qui Rosæ in suo mortuali feretro pallentis fuerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040301Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040301Z">
        <seg>Until that hour, the face in the image was the same one that had been pale in Rosa's coffin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut allata fuit, repente Virgo perplexum quid secum mussitans, demum aperte dixit matri: “Sentio, Speranzam non esse baptizatam”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012857Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012857Z">
        <seg>As it was brought in, the Virgin, suddenly perplexed and muttering to herself, finally said aloud to her mother, "I feel that Speranza is not baptized."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut audivit, epulum Eucharisticum sibi adferri, vivido colore suffusa, coruscantis auroræ instar gratiose rubuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025925Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025925Z">
        <seg>When she heard that the Eucharistic feast was being brought to her, she, suffused with a living color, blushed graciously like a shining dawn.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut autem impleret, observabat attentissime, quot diebus, subinde ex infirmitate decumbens, ab usu quotidianæ disciplinæ fuisset impedita, subductoque diligenti calculo, ubi convaluisset, ita in proximam dierum periodum dividebat omissorum ictuum portiones, ut compensatione facta, permissum numerum quinque millium exacte adæquaret, nec segnius numerum, præsenti dierum periodo tunc respondentem, una absolveret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041609Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041609Z">
        <seg>However, to make up for it, she paid the closest attention to how many days, from time to time being laid up by illness, she had been prevented from her daily discipline, and after carefully calculating, once she had recovered, she would divide the portions of the missed strokes into the next period of days in such a way that, with the compensation made, the permitted number of five thousand would be exactly met, and no less diligently would she complete the number corresponding to the current period of days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut ergo audivit, Rosæ virgineum corpusculum, in æde FF. Prædicatorum feretro impositum, parari ad sepulturam, plena fide, ac duabus stipata filiabus, eo properavit, ut potuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033232Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033232Z">
        <seg>So, when he heard that the virgin Rosa's little body, placed on a bier in the church of the Friars Preachers, was being prepared for burial, he, full of faith and accompanied by two of his daughters, hurried there as best he could.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut ergo ita esse reipsa comprobarunt, desiere cibi mentionem importune ingerere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055954Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055954Z">
        <seg>So, since they had confirmed that this was indeed the case, they stopped bringing up food so importunately.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut illuxit, monita a filio conjux, omnia dixit, egitque, ne maritus visionem relaturus domo abscederet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032132Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032132Z">
        <seg>As soon as it dawned, urged by her son, she said and did everything to keep her husband from leaving home to relate the vision.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut intelligas tam suas Rosæ patienti non cessisse spinas, quam his non cessit illa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042201Z">
        <seg>So that you may understand that the rose's thorns yielded no more to its own patient than they did to her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut obitus Virginis mortem Christi exspirantis in cruce per complura symbola expresserat, ita &amp; sepulchrum ejus fore gloriosum, diu ante fuerat revelatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031419Z">
        <seg>Just as the Virgin's death had expressed the death of Christ expiring on the cross through various symbols, so too had her glorious tomb been revealed long before.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut paulatim cessavit hæc apparitionum gratiosa frequentia, non cessavit tamen ex lege urbanitatis Rosa per aliquot exinde dies cælesti ephebo suas vices committere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031934Z">
        <seg>As this gracious frequency of apparitions gradually ceased, Rosa nevertheless, by the law of urbanity, did not stop entrusting her turns to the heavenly youth for several days thereafter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut tamen te, o Rex, me cuncta stringunt jura;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045332Z">
        <seg>Yet, O King, all rights bind you to me.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut vero ad horti januam, volando magis, quam currendo pervenerat, in propinqua porticu substitit, correptaque ferrea catena, nudas sibi scapulas flagris acerbissimis cruentavit: inter fluentes tum sanguinis, tum lachrymarum rivos, Sponso suo querula, quod ipsam ad tempus destituerat, dum in ejuscemodi angustias incideret: numquam enim sibi, præsente ipso, tam periculosam larvatæ impuritatis imaginem fuisse occursuram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052215Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052215Z">
        <seg>When she had reached the garden gate more by flying than by running, she paused in a nearby portico, and seizing an iron chain, she lacerated her bare shoulders with the most bitter whips: amid the flowing streams of both blood and tears, she complained to her Spouse that He had forsaken her for a time while she was plunged into such straits: for she had never imagined that, in his very presence, she would encounter such a dangerous image of impure disguise.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Uterque præ angustia clamavit in cælum, utrique responsum cælitus: “Sufficit tibi gratia mea: nam virtus in infirmitate perficitur”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045447Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045447Z">
        <seg>Both, in their anguish, cried out to heaven, and both received a heavenly answer: "My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Utique plenam hæc fidem faciunt illi religiosissimæ cujusdam mulieris extaticæ visioni, cui in raptu protestatus est Christus, se Rosam gestare in intimo sui divini cordis penetrali;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051248Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051248Z">
        <seg>They certainly give full credence to the ecstatic vision of a most devout woman, to whom Christ, in a rapture, declared that He bore the Rose in the innermost sanctuary of His divine heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Utitur eo nomine Latino Plautus in significatione tam vili, ut angelis non conveniat.&lt;/e4&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052259Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052259Z">
        <seg>Plautus uses that Latin name in such a base sense that it is unworthy of angels.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Utque Christi Domini fideles intelligant, quam sit eximium hoc pietatis exemplum, quod in novo orbe in præsentibus calamitatibus, &amp; periculis toti illuxit Ecclesiæ, illustriora ejus charismata, virtutes, &amp; gesta ex multis magnalibus, quæ contulit illi Dominus, his nostris litteris proponenda duximus, ut ejus sanctitas, apostolicæ hujus Cathedræ testimonio comprobata, in universo mundo perpetuo magis illucescat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041058Z">
        <seg>And that the faithful of Christ the Lord may understand how exceptional this example of piety is, which in the new world has shone forth for the entire Church amid present calamities and dangers, its more illustrious charisms, virtues, and deeds from the many great gifts which the Lord bestowed upon her, we have deemed it fitting to set forth in these our letters, so that her sanctity, confirmed by the testimony of this apostolic See, may shine forth more brightly throughout the entire world for all time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Utque miraculo fidem faceret, lacertum tum quaquaversus jactavit expedite, vivide &amp; celeriter, spectante ac plaudente confertissimo populo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033242Z">
        <seg>And to prove the miracle, he then threw the lizard in every direction with agility, vividly and swiftly, while the densely packed crowd watched and applauded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Utriusque angorem ut rescivit Filia, clave, qua solebat, cordialis in Deum confidentiæ tentavit opulentum æterni provisoris gazophylacium: vixque in templo preces fuderat, cum inde domum redeunti adstitit vir plane ignotus, at vultu modeste decorus, ac voce comis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062251Z">
        <seg>When the daughter learned of both their distress, she tried with the key she was accustomed to use to open the opulent treasury of the eternal Provider's confidence in God. And scarcely had she poured out prayers in the temple when a man, entirely unknown to her, appeared as she was returning home, his countenance modestly handsome and his voice courteous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Utrumque contextum Rosa prædixerat, utrumque Mesta postridie impletum vidit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012727Z">
        <seg>Rosa had predicted both contexts, and Mesta saw both fulfilled the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Utut sit, Rosæ non tam diluculum fuit quam meridies, dum sic illustrata præferbuit &amp; viam elegit quam debuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035040Z">
        <seg>Be that as it may, for Rose it was not so much dawn as midday, while thus enlightened she blossomed and chose the path she ought to have taken.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Uxor horrore ac mœrore gravis cucurrit ad heram, &amp; cum ejulatu brevissime indicavit maritum suum efflasse animam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033155Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033155Z">
        <seg>The wife, overcome with horror and grief, ran to her mistress and, with a brief sob, indicated that her husband had breathed his last.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Uxor metu dispalluit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012749Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012749Z">
        <seg>The wife paled with fear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>V utcumque tractavimus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044040Z">
        <seg>We have treated V in any case;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>VIII) tempora clam obsederat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040614Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040614Z">
        <seg>VIII) had secretly besieged the times.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>VITA</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211721Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211721Z">
        <seg>Life</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vado nunc cum tripudio ad perenniter contuendam pulcherrimam illam faciem, quam toto peregrinationis meæ exilio quæsivi, toto desideriorum æstu concupivi”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030130Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030130Z">
        <seg>I now go with a dance to gaze forever upon that most beautiful face, which I have sought throughout my entire exile of wandering, and longed for in the whole heat of my desires.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vagiebat continuo parvulus tanto dolori impar, ac sæpe toto triduo nauseabundus ubera nutricis omnino respuebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033539Z">
        <seg>The little one wailed continuously, unable to bear so much pain, and often for three whole days, feeling nauseous, he utterly refused the nurse's breast.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Valetudo redierat integra, neque in posterum sæva arthritis Francisco, ut antea, scipionis usum imperavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040614Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040614Z">
        <seg>His health had returned completely, and from then on the severe arthritis no longer forced Francis, as before, to use a cane.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vale”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055055Z">
        <seg>Goodbye.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Varias hujus morbi species vide pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052944Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052944Z">
        <seg>See various species of this disease, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Varias hujus morbi species vide pag. 378 apud Gorræum proxime citatum, si lubet.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010849Z">
        <seg>See the various species of this disease on page 378 of the recently cited Gorræus, if you wish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Varie diuque sudatum est in remediis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034726Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034726Z">
        <seg>Much effort has been expended on the remedies for a long time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Variæ sunt hujus aut similis morbi species, nimirum&lt;/e8&gt; apepsia &lt;e9&gt;&amp;&lt;/e9&gt; bradypepsia, &lt;e10&gt;de quibus medicos jam sæpe citatos consule&lt;/e10&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040128Z">
        <seg>There are various forms of this or a similar disorder, namely apepsia and bradypepsia; consult the physicians who have already been frequently cited on these subjects.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vas erat argenteum calenti chocolata plenum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051719Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051719Z">
        <seg>The vessel was silver and filled with hot chocolate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Velociter inquirito:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051623Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051623Z">
        <seg>Quickly investigate:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Veneratio ejus erga venerabile mysterium fuit adeo eximia, ut semel se accinxerit, videntibus aliis, ad subeundum martyrium, quod imminere rumor sparserat: defuitque tunc hostiæ martyrium, non hostia martyrio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042211Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042211Z">
        <seg>His veneration for the venerable mystery was so extraordinary that, once he had girded himself in the sight of others to undergo the martyrdom which rumor had spread was imminent, the martyrdom lacked a victim, not the victim a martyrdom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Venere ambo, mirati, quid Virgo hora tam insolita ac præmatura sibi vellet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012439Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012439Z">
        <seg>Both came, amazed, wondering what the Virgin could want at such an unusual and premature hour.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Venerunt ambo, ignari quid rerum ageretur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053756Z">
        <seg>They both came, unaware of what was going on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Veniet dies, qua universi intelligent, quam sit eximie alta, quam magna, &amp; spectabilis hæc Rosa”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050653Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050653Z">
        <seg>A day will come when all will understand how exceedingly high, how great, and how magnificent this Rose is.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Venit P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053827Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053827Z">
        <seg>The father came.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Venit P. Peñalosa cum socio Francisco Lopez coadjutore;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011007Z">
        <seg>Father Peñalosa came with his associate, Francisco Lopez, coadjutor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Venit cum prætextu commendandi maritum &amp; filiolam: nam Virginis apud Deum suffragio tunc forsitan magis indigebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062923Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062923Z">
        <seg>She came under the pretext of recommending her husband and little daughter; for they perhaps then stood in greater need of the Virgin's intercession with God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verbis supra ætatem sensatis respondit: “Sinito hic me delitescere solam cum Deo: nam illic inter pupas, an pariter adsit Deus, quis novit”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043237Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043237Z">
        <seg>She replied to the words, wise beyond her years: "Let me here withdraw alone with God; for among dolls, who knows whether God is present there as well?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verecunde renuit modesta Virgo: instabant molestæ feminæ, quibus complacitura mater jussit edicto parere filiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035303Z">
        <seg>The modest virgin demurred shyly; the troublesome women pressed her, and the mother, wishing to please them, ordered her daughter to obey the decree.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Veredos etiam adhibebant veteres principum tabellarii, qui mutatis per stationes equis, iter velocissime conficiebant, atque inde&lt;/e2&gt; veredarii &lt;e3&gt;dicti sunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052330Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052330Z">
        <seg>The ancients also used relays of horses for their princes' couriers, who, changing horses at the stations, completed the journey very swiftly, and from this they were called riders.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum &amp; hoc Virgini tandem novæ vexationis auctarium fuit: nam ut aliquoties in vultu Filiæ notarat parens, eam statis horis interdiu expallescere, angi, palpitare, ac mortali sudore propemodum congelari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045732Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045732Z">
        <seg>But this, too, was a fresh torment for the Virgin: for, as the parent had often noticed on his daughter's face, at certain hours of the day she would grow pale, be seized with anguish, tremble, and be almost frozen with a deathly sweat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum &amp; hoc patri spirituali (forsitan suggerente &amp; exaggerante matre) visum Filiæ vires excedere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060127Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060127Z">
        <seg>But this, too, seemed to the spiritual father (perhaps suggested and exaggerated by the mother) to exceed the daughter's strength.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum &amp; hunc mater abegerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042827Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042827Z">
        <seg>But this one, too, had been driven away by his mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum &amp; machinæ pondus obstabat, ne femineis viribus attolleretur in hypostylia, &amp; lata subjectæ crepidinis sponda, ne saltem loco, quem semel insederat, moveretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040906Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the weight of the machine prevented it from being lifted by feminine strength into the hypostyle, and the broad ledge of the underlying platform kept it from being moved even from the spot where it had once been set.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum (quæ Sponsi mei summa est benignitas) intra me sentio tam infallibiles suæ erga me non interruptæ hactenus dignationis syngraphas, ut facilius mihi persuaserit quivis, me saxum esse aut paleam, quam quod dulce Numen a me aversum sit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062018Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062018Z">
        <seg>But I feel within me such infallible tokens of His uninterrupted favor toward me that it would be easier for anyone to persuade me that I am a rock or chaff than that the sweet Deity has turned away from me.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum Didacus, occulte supplices ad Rosam preces, ad Deum vota conceperat, jamque altius spe salutis, quam aquis tumebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034038Z">
        <seg>But Diego, secretly offering supplications to the Rose, had conceived vows to God, and now his hope of salvation swelled higher than the waters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum Joannes vultu Rosæ observato, notavit in eodem majestatem angelicam, tesseram pacis, &amp; vivam spem incolumitatis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061503Z">
        <seg>But John, observing Rose's face, perceived in it an angelic majesty, a token of peace, and a living hope of safety.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum a spiritualibus ad corporalia beneficia tandem hic descendendum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033012Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033012Z">
        <seg>But from spiritual to corporal benefits, one must ultimately descend here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ad alia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051130Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051130Z">
        <seg>But to other matters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ad rem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042043Z">
        <seg>But to the point.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum angelico ad hoc opus foret tum calamo tum penicillo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055740Z">
        <seg>Indeed, for this work, an angelic pen and brush would be required.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum biduo vel triduo ante, horrenda arthritis&lt;a0&gt;g&lt;/a0&gt; dexteram Virginis manum per omnes articulos importunissimo cruciatu invaserat, ut non modo operi vel tenuiter attingendo prorsus inutilem reddiderit, verum etiam præ immanitate monstruosæ inflationis, medicum terruerit, gravioris mali atheromata&lt;a1&gt;h&lt;/a1&gt; suspicantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011249Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011249Z">
        <seg>But two or three days before, a horrible arthritis had invaded the Virgin's right hand through all its joints with the most excruciating pain, so that it had rendered her utterly useless for any work or even the slightest touch, and had also terrified the doctor with the monstrous enormity of the swelling, suspecting a more serious atherosclerotic disease.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum biduo vel triduo ante, horrenda arthritis&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;g&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; dexteram Virginis manum per omnes articulos importunissimo cruciatu invaserat, ut non modo operi vel tenuiter attingendo prorsus inutilem reddiderit, verum etiam præ immanitate monstruosæ inflationis, medicum terruerit, gravioris mali atheromata&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;h&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; suspicantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055340Z">
        <seg>But two or three days before, a horrible arthritis had invaded the Virgin's right hand through all its joints with the most excruciating torment, so that it had rendered her not only completely useless for any work or even the slightest touch, but had also terrified the doctor with the monstrous enormity of the swelling, suspecting a more serious atherosclerotic condition.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum de his in Mantissa&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030657Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030657Z">
        <seg>But of these, in the Mantissa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum de his opportunius in descriptione examinis capite sequente.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045902Z">
        <seg>But more about these at a more opportune time in the description of the examination in the following chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum de hoc in sequenti capitulo, ubi ad Rosæ vigilias transibit succincta narratio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042127Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042127Z">
        <seg>But of this in the next chapter, where a concise account will pass to Rose's vigils.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum de hoc prodigio commodior redibit tractatio infra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042356Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042356Z">
        <seg>A more convenient treatment of this prodigy will be given below in chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum de luminosis Rosæ post obitum apparitionibus sermo copiosior Mantissæ reservetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030248Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030248Z">
        <seg>But let more ample discussion of the luminous apparitions of the Rose after her death be reserved for the Mantis.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum divinus Collusor (quasi jacturam non ferens) rediit, ludum instauravit, jactuque feliciori victor evasit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051028Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051028Z">
        <seg>But the divine Collutor (as if not bearing the loss) returned, restored the game, and with a more fortunate throw emerged victorious.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum dubitanti &amp; prope exanimi subvenit Christus, visibili blandaque specie se Virgini objiciens, ipsamque placidissime his verbis compellans: “Memento Filia, longe duriorem, angustiorem, horribiliorem fuisse lectulum Calvariæ, in quo mortis somnum pro te cepi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042651Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042651Z">
        <seg>But Christ came to the aid of the doubting and nearly fainting Virgin, appearing to her in a visible and gentle form, and most peacefully addressed her with these words: "Remember, Daughter, that the bed of Calvary was far harsher, more constricting, more dreadful, on which I took the sleep of death for you."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ex quo in hujus mentionem incidimus, seorsim capite sequenti narranda sunt, quæ circa fundandum illud monasterium Virgini cælitus &amp; promissa &amp; ostensa fuerunt: hic breviter, quod præsenti capiti sufficit, superestque in coronidem, subnecto &amp; finio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062319Z">
        <seg>But since we have come to mention this, the things that were divinely promised and shown to the Virgin concerning the founding of that monastery must be told separately in the next chapter. Here, briefly, what suffices for the present chapter remains, and I append and conclude it in the coronis.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum hac adhibita, mox rediit cum doloribus inflatio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035905Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035905Z">
        <seg>But when this was applied, the swelling soon returned with pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum hac solerti fuga trepidam matrem nec fefellit nec effugit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042239Z">
        <seg>But with this clever escape, the anxious mother was neither deceived nor eluded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum hanc mater, ut animadvertit, sustulit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042728Z">
        <seg>But this mother, as she noticed, lifted it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum his facile Rosa, totius arcani probe conscia, occurrit, voce tranquilla &amp; placida jubens, ne timerent: nihil terrificum, nil sinistrum, horridumve portendi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053911Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, readily meeting them, fully aware of the entire secret, commands them with a calm and placid voice not to be afraid: nothing terrifying, nothing sinister, or horrible is foretold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum humilis Rosa velut culpam in opere sanctissimo agnosceret, veniam deprecata, spopondit emendationem, læta ibi se objurgari, ubi maxime plausum, vanamque gloriam metuerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040636Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040636Z">
        <seg>But the humble Rose, as if acknowledging a fault in the most sacred work, begged for forgiveness, promised to amend, and was glad to be rebuked there where she had most feared applause and vain glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum hæc deinde non vacavit proregi, sicque pluries inter hunc &amp; archipræsulem variata die, tandem ambo in quartam proximi Septembris ex abrupto conveniunt, quod utrique postea tanto fuit jucundius, ubi intellexere, hanc diem, ex Romani Calendarii censu, alterius S. Rosæ Viterbiensis&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; anniversario honori esse dedicatam, neque in illam se humano consilio, sed divino impulsu concorditer incidisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031227Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031227Z">
        <seg>But she was not then free to proceed, and so, after several postponements between this date and that of the archbishop, they both at last, quite unexpectedly, met on the fourth of the following September, which was all the more pleasant for both of them afterward, when they learned that this day, according to the Roman Calendar, was the feast of another St. of the Rose of Viterbo&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;—a day dedicated to the anniversary honor of the other—and that they had not fallen upon it by human counsel, but by divine impulse in unison.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum hæc minime admirans, respondit paucis: “Numquid vidisti, soror, quam sereno indicio dignatur approbare seraphica nostra mater, quod agimus?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055113Z">
        <seg>But paying no heed to this, she replied briefly, "Have you seen anything, sister, that our seraphic mother deigns to approve by a clear sign—what we are doing?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum id commodius proximo capitulo servatum esto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013009Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013009Z">
        <seg>But let that be more conveniently reserved for the next chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum illic populosissima glirium orchestra, imo hippodromus erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052017Z">
        <seg>But there was a most populous orchestra of gnomes, nay, a hippodrome.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum incredula mater, dum pium hic Filiæ commentum suspicatur, curiosius utramque inspexit manum, quas ut revera pustulatas, ustulatas, torridas comperit, ut contrectavit, demum adtremuit, desiitque in posterum Filiam obedientissimam ad eadem incendia durius invitare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035405Z">
        <seg>But the unbelieving mother, while suspecting her daughter's pious pretext, more curiously inspected both hands and, finding them truly blistered, scorched, and parched, as soon as she touched them she finally trembled and thereafter ceased to urge her most obedient daughter more harshly toward the same fires.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ingemiscebat Virgo identidem suspirans ac palam deplorans infirmam non baptizatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012910Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012910Z">
        <seg>But the Virgin groaned, repeatedly sighing and openly lamenting the weak unbaptized one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum institit Rosa efficaciori imperio: “Itote, sorores, trinum, quem dixi, florem maturrimum, decussatum, repandum ocyus adferte.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055157Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055157Z">
        <seg>But Rosa insisted with more effective authority: "Go, sisters, and quickly bring me the triple flower I mentioned—the ripest, cross-pollinated one."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ipsa Rosæ prædictionis memor, &amp; consilii tenax restitit, vicit, triumphavit, rebusque ex voto, imo supra votum feliciter compositis, novo sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis monasterio auspicando, struendo, dotando amplissimas suas facultates, denique &amp; se ipsam dedit, &amp; cum sacro velo nomen sororis Luciæ de sanctissima Trinitate assumens, prima novelli sui asceterii præfecturam gessit, ac religionis spiritum, quem ab osculo Rosæ pridem conceperat, hortatu, cura, exemplo mirifice in subditas moniales propagavit, ac demum sanctimoniæ fama celebris, ibidem extremum clausit diem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012039Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012039Z">
        <seg>But she herself, mindful of Rose's prophecy and steadfast in her resolve, resisted, conquered, and triumphed, and with things happily brought to a successful conclusion according to her vow, indeed beyond her vow, devoting her vast resources to founding, building, and endowing a new monastery of St. Catherine of Siena, she finally gave herself to it, and, taking the sacred veil and the name of Sister Lucia of the Most Holy Trinity, she served as the first prioress of her new convent, and by exhortation, care, and example, wonderfully spread the spirit of religion to the nuns under her charge, and finally, celebrated for her sanctity, she ended her life there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ipsa præcipue indigitabat hortum animæ, cui a Deo precabatur florida augmenta virtutum: sicque ingeniose uni parœmiæ duplicem sensum includens, altero satisfaciebat alloquenti, altero Deum alloquebatur ipsa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045010Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045010Z">
        <seg>But she herself especially pointed to the garden of her soul, for which she prayed to God for the flourishing growth of virtues; and thus, ingeniously enclosing a double meaning in a single proverb, she satisfied the speaker with one sense and addressed God with the other.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum is conflictus erat, non afflictio, aut fuga roboris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045828Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045828Z">
        <seg>But it was a conflict, not an affliction, nor a flight of strength.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ne sic quidem effugere sat potuit, quin complures tum celebri virtutis opinione, tum pudici vultus majestate in sui admirationem, mox &amp; honesti thalami ambitum invita accenderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035951Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035951Z">
        <seg>Yet even so she could not escape, but would kindle admiration for herself in the minds of many, both by her then celebrated reputation for virtue and by the majesty of her modest countenance, and would soon, against her will, set aflame the circle of her honorable nuptials.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum nec pauperum multitudine, nec debilium, cæcorum, aut claudorum copia, nec his, qui prope, nec his, qui longe erant, contentus, ad regale epulum, in quo non pauca, sed omnia parata erant, &amp; ad cælestem domum implendam, in ulteriores gentes destinatum nuntium progredi jussit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041033Z">
        <seg>Not content with the multitude of the poor, nor with the abundance of the weak, the blind, or the lame, nor with those who were near, nor with those who were far away, he commanded the message destined to fill the royal feast, in which not a few, but all things were prepared, and the heavenly home, to proceed to the distant peoples.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum negabat mater, se permissuram Filiæ, quod tali sepulchro vivani se abderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043509Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043509Z">
        <seg>The mother denied that she would allow her daughter to hide herself alive in such a tomb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ostioli angustia videbatur scabello negare exitum: unde laicus mucrone, quem attulerat, assulatim cœpit obstantia ligna incidere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034813Z">
        <seg>But the narrowness of the doorway seemed to deny the stool an exit; so the layman, with the knife he had brought, began to cut away the obstructing wood in a series of short strokes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum plus Rosa præstitit, quam rogabatur: nam subito ægra in se rediit, exomologesim rite peregit, ac brevi integram valetudinem recuperavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034100Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034100Z">
        <seg>But Rosa did more than was asked of her: for the sick woman suddenly came to herself, made her confession properly, and soon regained her full health.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum post Rosæ obitum, soluta promissæ taciturnitatis vinculo, beneficium liberrime omnibus, prædicavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061227Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061227Z">
        <seg>But after Rose's death, freed from the bond of her promised silence, she freely proclaimed the benefit to all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum post fratris decessum non levis cura momordit Petrum, quid esset, quod juxta condictum defuncti anima non comparebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060853Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060853Z">
        <seg>But after his brother's death, Peter was deeply troubled by the question of why, according to the deceased's own stipulation, his soul did not appear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum pro illa vice mater, vel quod crederet, se filiam ab horto jam reduxisse, vel quod aliis distracta minus applicuisset animum, oblita fuerat hortum ad horam consuetam exituræ Virgini aperire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051806Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051806Z">
        <seg>But on that occasion the mother, whether because she believed she had already brought her daughter back from the garden, or because she was distracted by other matters and had paid less attention, had forgotten to open the garden at the usual time for the Virgin to go out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum præstantius fuit alterum, quod intus Rosæ animam dulcorabat eodem illo momento, quo foris convalescebat manus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055452Z">
        <seg>But the other was more wonderful, that within, Rose's soul was being sweetened at the very moment that the hand was being healed without.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum prævenit decennis ancillulæ pia diligentia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040912Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040912Z">
        <seg>But the pious diligence of the ten-year-old maid prevented it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum quia isthoc solatio non nisi interdiu licebat frui, paulo grandior facta, impetravit a matre aliud intra ædes cubiculum, in quo seorsim a cæteris prolibus solitaria pernoctaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043305Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043305Z">
        <seg>But since she was only allowed to enjoy this comfort by day, when she had grown a little older she persuaded her mother to provide another room inside the house, in which she could sleep alone at night, apart from the other children.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum quoties nox appetebat, remotis pulvillis succedebat truncus undique impolitus, ac lapilli in hunc usum interdiu sub lecto absconditi, disponebantur sub stragulo, ut ea inæqualitate offenderetur corpus, cui prædura tabularum planities in deliciis fore timebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042536Z">
        <seg>But whenever night fell, the pillows were removed and in their place a roughly hewn log was set, with small stones hidden under the bed for this purpose during the day; these were arranged beneath the blanket so that the body would be jolted by the uneven surface, for it was feared that the hard flatness of the planks would prove too luxurious.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum si Rosæ perarduum fuit, vigesimo demum ætatis anno, per tot obstacula eluctari ad sacrum habitum, vix minori lucta ipsi constitit, eumdem usque ad mortem retinere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040239Z">
        <seg>But if it was exceedingly arduous for Rose, at the age of twenty, to struggle through so many obstacles to the sacred habit, it was scarcely any less a struggle for her to keep it until her death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum sic per horæ quadrantem periclitanti opportune incidit Rosæ memoria, &amp; dum virus per viscera cordi adserpebat, extremo conatu se colligens, erupit clamorose in has voces: “Sancta Rosa succurre, &amp; Novenam polliceor”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034339Z">
        <seg>But thus, at the critical moment, the memory of Rose came to her in time, and as the poison crept through her heart's vitals, she summoned her last strength and burst out in a loud cry: "Saint Rose, help me, and I promise a novena."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum singula quæque suæ devotionis exercitia minutim prosequi non vacat, præsertim cum ex innumeris innotuerint pauciora.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054649Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054649Z">
        <seg>However, it is not possible to pursue each and every one of his devotional exercises in detail, especially since only a few have become known among the countless.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum sub eodem radio progressa ulterius Rosa limpide agnovit, Dei auxilium sibi fore &amp; necessarium, &amp; jugiter implorandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035015Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035015Z">
        <seg>But under the same radio, as she progressed further, Rosa clearly recognized that God's help would be for her, necessary, and to be implored continually.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum sub ipsa extrema inunctione a presbytero &amp; monialibus admonita fuit, virgini Rosæ angorem fide ac affectu, quo posset maximo, commendaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034109Z">
        <seg>However, at the very moment of her last anointing by the priest and the nuns, she was admonished to commend her anguish to the Virgin Rose with the greatest faith and affection she could muster.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum subridens, morique certa Virgo replicuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025730Z">
        <seg>Smiling truly, the Virgin replied with a certain grace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum tunc mysterii ob ætatem ignarus, taciteque mirari contentus præteribat, sed post annos XV ab obitu Virginis, jam sacerdos judicioque maturior, prodigium, uti acciderat, sub fide juramenti enarravit, adjiciens, ex ejusmodi sacris ardoribus sibi juvenculo rude cor nihilominus concaluisse arcana veneratione præsentis Sacramenti, quod suscipi tanta religione &amp; æstu viderat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055839Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055839Z">
        <seg>But then, ignorant of the mystery because of his age, and contentedly passed by in silent wonder, but fifteen years after the Virgin's death, now a priest and more mature in judgment, he recounted the prodigy, as it had occurred, under oath, adding that from such sacred ardors his young and uncouth heart had nevertheless been stirred by the secret reverence for the present Sacrament, which he had seen received with such great religion and fervor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ubi æs campanum solito maturius funebri Missæ &amp; solemni exsequiarum Officio signum dedit, subito reliqua templi capacissimi amplitudo supervenientibus angusta fuit: neque enim (uti pridie) soli incolæ, sed &amp; remotiores accolæ a sex &amp; amplius leucis accurrerant, nemine invitante.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030954Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030954Z">
        <seg>But when the bell struck its usual signal for the funeral Mass and the solemn Office of the Exequies, the vast remainder of the church suddenly proved too small for the crowd that poured in: for not only the townspeople, as on the previous day, but also outlying villagers from six or more leagues away had come, unbidden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ut comperit, his paulatim indiciis omnem austeritatem suorum jejuniorum a curiosis deprehendi, æstimari, extolli, plus sibi a vanæ gloriæ laudumque tinea, quam ab eximiæ formæ præstantia timendum rata, confugit ad solitum orationis præsidium, anxiisque precibus impetravit a Deo talem sibi aptari vultum, qui saltem abstinentiæ suæ rigores, ac tot jejuniorum extima signa mortalium oculis non venditaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040738Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040738Z">
        <seg>But when he discovered that, by these gradual clues, the austerity of his fasts was being detected, judged, and extolled by the curious—deeming he had more to fear from the moth of vain glory and praise than from the splendor of an exceptional form—he resorted to his customary refuge of prayer, and with anxious prayers he implored God to grant him a face that would at least not betray to mortal eyes the rigor of his abstinence and the many outward signs of his fasts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ut neque hoc se impetraturam a domesticis intellexit, adstantem germanum fratrem ultimis affata verbis rogavit, cervicalia ut submoveret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043004Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043004Z">
        <seg>But when she realized that she would not be able to get this from her household servants, she addressed her brother, who was standing by, with her last words and asked him to remove her necklace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ut sexus iste ad severam publici juramenti religionem facile trepidat, ac, velut inter fulmina censurarum, suæ conscientiæ metuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032228Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032228Z">
        <seg>For this sex, as it were, easily trembles at the stern religion of a public oath, and, as if under the thunderbolts of censure, fears its own conscience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum, an hæc visio a superiori diversa sit, an eadem, haud facile discreverim.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032219Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032219Z">
        <seg>However, whether this vision is different from the previous one or the same, I cannot easily tell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum, ne Mariæ obstaret egestas, providerat omniscia cura Numinis inspirans fundatrici, ut in ipso fundationis programmate reservarentur loca nonnulla gratuitæ receptionis digniorum, sed inopum personarum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062849Z">
        <seg>Indeed, so that poverty would not stand in the way of Mary, the founder, inspired by the care of Providence, had provided in the very program of the foundation that certain places for the free reception of worthy but needy persons be reserved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum, operante magistro P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054946Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054946Z">
        <seg>But, with Master P. at work</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum, operante magistro P. F. Alphonso Vasquez Rosæ confessario, ex tribus unica dumtaxat Rosæ hortulo mansit residua, siquidem is alteram poposcit sibi, alteram proregina dono ejusdem obtinuit, summaque cura penes se nutrire studuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011212Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011212Z">
        <seg>However, with the master P. F. Alphonso Vasquez, confessor to Rose, at work, only one of the three remained in Rose's garden, for he had asked for one for himself, obtained another for the Queen Regent as a gift from her, and strove with the utmost care to nurture it;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum, quo plus detergebatur, plus novi sudoris erumpebat: rursus ergo Peñalosa gossipium, qua siccum erat, applicuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053843Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053843Z">
        <seg>But the more it was wiped away, the more fresh sweat broke out; so Peñalosa again applied the gossypium, which was dry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum, ubi prudens examinator per idoneas periphrases&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; dilucidius, quid sciscitaretur, explicuit, Rosa diffiteri amplius nec debuit, nec potuit, superna illustratione admodum tempestive se didicisse, quod ab æterno electa erat ad cælestem gloriam, idque miro Dei beneficio se tanta certitudine tenere, ut limpidiorem hujus revelationem nequeat desiderare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011732Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011732Z">
        <seg>But when the prudent examiner, by means of suitable periphrases, more clearly explained what he was asking, Rosa could no longer deny, nor indeed could she, that by a heavenly illumination she had learned with great timeliness that she had been chosen from eternity for celestial glory, and that she held this with such certainty by a marvelous gift of God that she could not desire a clearer revelation of it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum, ubi prudens examinator per idoneas periphrases&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; dilucidius, quid sciscitaretur, explicuit, Rosa diffiteri amplius nec debuit, nec potuit, superna illustratione admodum tempestive se didicisse, quod ab æterno electa erat ad cælestem gloriam, idque miro Dei beneficio se tanta certitudine tenere, ut limpidiorem hujus revelationem nequeat desiderare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061832Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061832Z">
        <seg>But when the prudent examiner, by means of suitable circumlocutions, more clearly explained what he was asking, Rosa could neither deny nor refuse any longer, having by a heavenly illumination learned with great promptness that she had been chosen from all eternity for heavenly glory, and that she held this with such certainty by a marvelous gift of God that she could not desire a clearer revelation of it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum, ut ad Rosæ opopanaceam&lt;a2&gt;i&lt;/a2&gt; confugit, ejusque calceamenti ligulam prævia oratione sibi applicuit, e vestigio morbus abscessit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035030Z">
        <seg>But when she resorted to rose opopanacea and applied its heel-clasp to herself with a preliminary prayer, the disease immediately withdrew.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum, ut breve est a floribus gaudium, Rosa mane quodam invenit basilicum suum non modo siccum ac marcidum, sed radicitus evulsum ac mortuum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051217Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051217Z">
        <seg>But, as the joy of flowers is short-lived, one morning Rosa found her basil not only dry and withered, but uprooted and dead.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verumtamen &amp; hanc supplevit ingeniosæ devotionis industria: nam, ut quotannis Reginam sanctissimi Rosarii bis spirituali veste amiciret, materiam, artem, formam excogitavit longe pretiosiorem ad modum, quem supra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054559Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, she also supplied this with the industry of her ingenious devotion: for, in order to clothe the Queen of the Most Holy Rosary twice each year in a spiritual vestment, she devised a material, workmanship, and form far more precious, in a manner described above in the chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verumtamen Patres ad primum saltem privatæ illius epistolæ conspectum subito parendum rati, ex norma dictæ Constititionis, quidquid intempestivum venerabilis Rosæ cultum sapere forsan videbatur, eamdem nocte aboleverunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031717Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031717Z">
        <seg>However, the Fathers, deeming it necessary to prepare at least a private draft of that letter at once, in accordance with the norm of the said Constitution, abolished that very night whatever might have seemed untimely in the venerable cult of Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verumtamen infirmitas ista non est ad mortem, convalescet infirmus, &amp; in proximo sanctissimi Rosarii triumphali festo coram universo populo diserte e suggestu perorabit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012352Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012352Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, this illness is not unto death; the sick man will recover, and at the next triumphal feast of the Most Holy Rosary he will eloquently speak from the pulpit before the entire congregation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verumtamen siluit, haud ignara, Filiam, si spinetum suum prodere aut abjicere cogeretur, facile alia &amp; quidem longe truculentiora mox adinventuram, quibus verticem pergeret divexare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042251Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, she remained silent, well aware that if her daughter were forced to betray or cast aside her own thorn, she would soon invent other—and indeed far more cruel—means to continue tormenting her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verumtamen subdebat, plurimum se præsidii ac spei in Rosæ orationibus, jejuniis, ceterorumque bonorum operum fido suffragio collocasse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060425Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060425Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, he added that he had placed the greatest reliance and hope in the faithful intercession of the prayers, fasts, and other good works of Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verumtamen, ut in his alieno potius, quam suo fideret ingenio, pium doctumque e Societate Jesu theologum adiit, divinorum epithetorum sinceram e sacris Paginis nomenclaturam sibi in epitomen colligi flagitavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044929Z">
        <seg>However, since in these matters he trusted rather to another's than to his own ingenuity, he consulted a pious and learned theologian of the Society of Jesus, demanding that a sincere compendium of the divine epithets from the sacred Scriptures be compiled for him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vicariatus est (corregiemento) imprimis utilis &amp; locuples.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035303Z">
        <seg>The vicarage is (with correction) first and foremost useful and rich.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vicissim Rosa flammante vultu (quem serium istud Deiparæ colloquium vehementer accenderat) precum subsidia ab illo flagitavit, perplexe aliqua interserens, quæ Joanni sat erant, ut intelligeret, tunc Rosæ præcisum vicini obitus sui diem fuisse revelatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013248Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013248Z">
        <seg>In turn, Rosa, her face aflame (for that earnest conversation with the Virgin had greatly stirred her), implored him for the aid of her prayers, weaving in some perplexed words that were enough for John to understand that the precise day of her neighbor's death had then been revealed to her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vicissim a parente Filia supplex impetravit, saltem ea sibi permitti ac parari fercula, quæ non modo ingerere, sed &amp; digerere posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041200Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041200Z">
        <seg>In turn, the daughter implored her parent to grant her at least those dishes that she could not only eat but also digest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vicissim confessarius, illo temporis puncto memor verborum, quæ pridem a Rosa audierat, Luciam edocuit, illud ipsum esse, quod Virgo matronæ promiserat, quando eidem prædicabat, magno cuidam operi ipsam a Deo reservari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012024Z">
        <seg>In turn, the confessor, at that moment recalling the words he had long ago heard from Rosa, informed Lucia that this was precisely what the Virgin had promised the matron when she had told her that she herself was being reserved by God for some great work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vicissim marito reposuit Mesta: “Et crastinus dies alteram nobis restituet profugam”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012724Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012724Z">
        <seg>Mesta replied to her husband, "And tomorrow will restore another fugitive to us."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vicissim millies replicuit Virgo, &amp; matronam jussit securam esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012250Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012250Z">
        <seg>The virgin repeated it a thousand times in turn and told the matron to be safe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vicit ergo constantia Virginis, quæ exinde fidentior, Gundisalvum aliosque suasores modesto ac gravi responso interpellavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040314Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040314Z">
        <seg>Thus the virgin's steadfastness prevailed, and she, now more confident, interrupted Gundisalvus and the other suitors with a modest and serious reply.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vicit nihilominus Rosæ ingenium: nam serto clam ab ima parte suffixit oblongam acum, sicque corollam latenter aculeatam suo capiti non tam imposuit, quam impressit, valideque impegit, ut sibi non nisi tormentum esset, quod ornamentum aliis videbatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035309Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, Rose's ingenuity prevailed: for she secretly fastened a long, pointed spike to the underside, and thus she did not so much place the thorn-studded wreath upon her head as press and drive it in, so that it was nothing but torture to her, though to others it seemed an ornament.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Victoriæ præmium fuit Rosæ patientia, adauctis repente doloribus, qui totam ei noctem fecere insomnem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051031Z">
        <seg>The reward for Victoria was Rose's patience, as her pains suddenly increased, keeping her awake all night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbantur Gaspari narrari insomnia, donec Filia explicato linteolo, quinquaginta libras, uti acceperat, prompte adnumeravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062303Z">
        <seg>Gaspari's nightmares seemed to be coming true until the daughter, having unfolded her handkerchief, promptly counted out the fifty pounds she had received.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbantur analecta esse nuptialis convivii, quo Rosa cum Sponso fruebatur in cælis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044542Z">
        <seg>They seemed to be excerpts from the wedding feast with which Rosa was enjoying herself in heaven with her Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbantur fartæ Mulcibero: nec videbantur solum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035347Z">
        <seg>They seemed full of Mulciber; nor did they seem alone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbantur intimi Virginis dolores advocasse in suum adjutorium reliquam naturalium morborum cohortem, ne aut ullum pœnarum genus tantæ patientiæ deesset, aut ulterius ambigeretur, infirmitatem Rosæ lethalem esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025439Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025439Z">
        <seg>It seemed that the Virgin's innermost pains had summoned to her aid the rest of the host of natural ailments, so that neither any kind of punishment would be lacking to such great patience, nor would there be any further doubt that Rose's infirmity was lethal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbatur anulo restituto simul exuisse manicas &amp; pedicas, quamquam fateretur, longe melius sibi fuisse in illa deliciarum compede, quam in miserantis amici præpropera solutione.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044709Z">
        <seg>He seemed to have shed his sleeves and stockings at once when the ring was restored, though he admitted it had been far better for him in that delightful shackle than in his compassionate friend's hasty release.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbatur ex pondere &amp; permodico involucri tumore parum metalli inesse sudariolo: at ubi Rosa id domi explicuit, ipsam argentearum librarum quinquagenam, quam pater debebat, reperit, ægrique lectulum cum nummis adiens, identidem repetebat, plurimum divinæ bonitati fidendum esse, hanc modo succurrisse medio peropportuno, quantum sufficiat ad amoliendam duri creditoris molestiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062300Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062300Z">
        <seg>It seemed from the weight and the very slight bulge of the wrapper that there was little metal in the handkerchief: But when Rosa unwrapped it at home, she found the very fifty silver pounds her father owed, and going to the sick man's bed with the money, she repeated again and again that one must trust greatly in divine goodness, which had just come to the rescue by the most opportune means, enough to remove the hard creditor's trouble.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbatur fortitudo lapidum fortitudo ejus, &amp; caro ejus ænea, id quod invictissimus Job sibi deesse querebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060037Z">
        <seg>The strength of his stones seemed to be his strength, and his flesh was like bronze, which the most invincible Job complained was lacking in him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbatur huic angello Rosula universas suas inclusisse delicias, adeo totos in eo hærebat dies solitaria: illuc velut ad orandi, meditandique secessum a prandio, a lectulo, a cœna properabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043255Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043255Z">
        <seg>It seemed that this little angel, Rosula, had packed all her delights into him, so completely did the solitary girl dwell in him all day long: she would hurry there from lunch, from her bed, from supper, as if to a retreat for prayer and meditation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbatur ipsamet Virgo conclave perambulare, cum cura omnia circumspicere, suis candidis manibus ordinate singula disponere, polire, adornare, simulque indicabat ista se agere, quod illic justa honoris ac veritatis tributa a mortalibus eo die erat receptura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032213Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032213Z">
        <seg>It seemed as though the Virgin herself was pacing the chamber, carefully surveying everything, arranging each object in order with her white hands, polishing and adorning them, and at the same time signaled that she was doing this because on that day she was to receive from mortals the just tributes of honor and truth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbatur tam simili Filiæ deberi iste sinus seraphicæ suæ Matris ad quietem, si tamen quies dicenda erat, quam tanti quotidie strepitus, &amp; clamores devotæ plebis circumsonabant, licet minori impedimento rituum ecclesiasticorum, quibus solis aræ maximæ spatium esse liberrimum oportebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031633Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031633Z">
        <seg>It seemed that this bosom of her seraphic Mother owed the Daughter such repose, if repose could be called such, as the daily din and clamors of the devout crowd resounded around, though they were a lesser hindrance to the ecclesiastical rites, for which alone the greatest altars ought to have the freest space.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbatur, sub his innocentissimis symphoniæ conatibus, Rosa cum puello suo Amore repuerascere, &amp; oblita omnium dolorum, indulgebat simplici teneritudini intimorum affectuum: bulliebat corde, &amp; existimans, nullos e vicino sibi imminere arbitros, tantilla evaporatione ferventem spiritum solabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053232Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053232Z">
        <seg>It seemed that, beneath these most innocent attempts at harmony, Rosa, with her boy Love, was being reborn, and, forgetting all her sorrows, she indulged in the simple tenderness of her innermost affections: her heart bubbled with joy, and, believing no judges to be looming near her, she soothed her fervent spirit with that slight evaporation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videtur aliquid hujus artigisse Augustinus, quando invenit se longe a Deo in regione dissimilitudinis: aliquid item in se percepisse Rex citharœdus ps.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045809Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045809Z">
        <seg>It seems that Augustine sensed something of this when he found himself far from God in a region of dissimilarity; and likewise that the king-harpist perceived something in himself (Ps.).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videtur autem hæc antithesis vel oppositio in eo constistere, quod anachoretæ cibo non&lt;/e8&gt; selecto, &lt;e9&gt;sed primum obvio utantur&lt;/e9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041429Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041429Z">
        <seg>However, this antithesis or opposition appears to consist in the fact that the anchorites use not selected food, but rather whatever is readily available.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videtur hic eadem mortificatio vel corruptio imminens indicari.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035313Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035313Z">
        <seg>It seems that the same imminent destruction or corruption is indicated here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videtur itaque hoc loco vox&lt;/e8&gt; crasis &lt;e9&gt;pro mixtionedesolationum accipi&lt;/e9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045921Z">
        <seg>It therefore appears that the word "crasis" is used here to mean a mixture of desolations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videtur tamen annua S. Bartholomæi festivitas ibi die 24 Augusti celebrari propter testimonium Clementis IX Pontificis, quod mox proferemus.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030452Z">
        <seg>However, it appears that the annual feast of St. Bartholomew is celebrated there on August 24 by virtue of the testimony of Pope Clement IX, which we shall soon present.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vidisses certatim feminas, abjecto vestium luxu, simplicioris modestiæ habitum domi forisque ostentare;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032640Z">
        <seg>You would have seen women, casting aside the luxury of their clothing, eagerly displaying a simpler and more modest attire at home and abroad.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vidisses hic denuo Lapam matrem filiæ Catharinæ probra, convicia, criminationes acervantem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042759Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042759Z">
        <seg>You would have seen here again Lapa, the mother of Catherine's daughter, heaping up insults, abuse, and accusations;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vidisses immotam sub his cruciatibus Rosam, acsi non suus esset pollex, cui toties ferrum insæviebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034842Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034842Z">
        <seg>You would have seen Rose unmoved under these torments, as if the thumb it was so often lashing with iron were not her own.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vidisses, mox omnes arrectis auribus diriguisse immobiles;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031525Z">
        <seg>You would have seen, soon after, everyone stand perfectly still with their ears pricked up.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vidit librum, &amp; obstupuit Myrensis archiepiscopus, talium rerum scientissimus, illumque transcribi fecit, Romano Pontifici coram offerendum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050107Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050107Z">
        <seg>The Archbishop of Myrenae, who was most learned in such matters, saw the book and was astonished, and had it transcribed to be presented to the Roman Pontiff in person.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vidit, illamque sinistræ nihil absimilem reperit, sanam, valentem, agilem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055430Z">
        <seg>He saw her and found her to be nothing like the left one: healthy, strong, and agile.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vidua supradicta, paucis a Virginis obitu diebus, privatim orabat in ecclesia, ac Rosæ, quam pluries in choro Beatorum conspexerat, Limanam civitatem, &amp; regnum Peruanum supplex commendabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032005Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032005Z">
        <seg>The aforementioned widow, a few days after the Virgin's death, was praying privately in the church and was earnestly commending the city of Lima and the kingdom of Peru to the Rose, whom she had seen several times in the choir of the Blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Viduæ Ludovicæ Faxardo morbus caducus binas proles, masculam annorum septemdecim, femellam mensium decem extinxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034701Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034701Z">
        <seg>Epilepsy had killed two of the children of the widow Ludovica Faxardo: a seventeen-year-old boy and a ten-month-old girl.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Viget in hoc casto pietatis sacrario diurnus &amp; nocturnus Dei cultus, devota festorum pompa, canonicus Officiorum ordo, jugis virginei chori psalmodia, sacræ supellectilis ac ecclesiæ accuratus nitor, apparatuum magnificentia copiosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062424Z">
        <seg>In this chaste sanctuary of piety, the daily and nightly worship of God flourishes, the devout pomp of feasts, the canonical order of the Offices, the unceasing psalmody of the virgin choir, the meticulous splendor of the sacred furnishings and church, and the abundant magnificence of the apparatus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vir apostolicus, ac Seraphici Ordinis jubar eximium, venerabilis Pater F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041538Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041538Z">
        <seg>Apostolic man, and an outstanding luminary of the Seraphic Order, the venerable Father F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vir apostolicus, ac Seraphici Ordinis jubar eximium, venerabilis Pater F. Franciscus Solanus, in vita &amp; post mortem, constanti fama sanctitatis ac prodigiorum gloria clarus&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt;, pro concione obscurius forte nonnulla protulerat, quæ Limensium vulgus prave intellecta detorserat in sensum prorsus alienum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000529Z">
        <seg>The apostolic man, and an outstanding luminary of the Seraphic Order, the venerable Father F. Francis Solanus, in life and after death, renowned for his constant reputation of holiness and the glory of his miracles, had perhaps delivered some things more obscurely in his sermons, which the common people of Lima, having misunderstood them, twisted into a meaning entirely foreign to them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vir ille sanctitatis fama conspicuus (de quo supra in fine capitis 8) dum Rosæ defunctæ coronam spineam devotionis ergo contrectarat&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;, diutius hæsit in anulo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010121Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010121Z">
        <seg>That man, conspicuous for his reputation of holiness (of whom, above, at the end of chapter 8), while he was handling the crown of thorns of the deceased Rose out of devotion, it stuck fast in the ring for a long time;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vir ille sanctitatis fama conspicuus (de quo supra in fine capitis 8) dum Rosæ defunctæ coronam spineam devotionis ergo contrectarat&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, diutius hæsit in anulo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044520Z">
        <seg>That man, conspicuous for his reputation of holiness (of whom above, at the end of chapter 8), while he had handled the crown of thorns of the deceased Rose for the sake of devotion, lingered longer on the ring;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vir iste religiosus maligna febri lethaliter exustus, ac decimo septimo suæ infirmitatis die omnino derelictus a medicis constanter asseverantibus, hora quinta aut sexta noctis proxime insequentis exspiraturum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043302Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043302Z">
        <seg>This religious man, having been fatally consumed by a malignant fever and on the seventeenth day of his illness completely abandoned by doctors who constantly affirmed that he would expire at about the fifth or sixth hour of the following night;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virginem per humeros corripuit, strinxit, contorsit, quasi in minutissimas partes membratim laceraturus obluctantem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052116Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052116Z">
        <seg>He seized the virgin by the shoulders, squeezed her, and twisted her, as if he were going to tear her limb from limb into the smallest pieces while she struggled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virginem tunc quidem sibi fuisse conspicuam eo vestimentorum schemate, quo vivens dignosci ac tegi consueverat, facie tamen multo lætiori, &amp; exporrecta, quasi quæ pro obsequio gratiam spiraret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032203Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032203Z">
        <seg>The virgin then appeared to him in that same attire in which she was accustomed to be recognized and wrapped while alive, yet her face was much more cheerful and open, as if she were breathing forth grace in return for the homage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virgini Luciæ de Montoya hiabat profunda in umbilico plaga, magnitudine argentei Philippei&lt;a1&gt;e&lt;/a1&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035619Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035619Z">
        <seg>The virgin Lucia de Montoya had a deep wound in her navel, the size of a silver Philippa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virgini tamen hæc rudissima gausapina non tam cilicium, quam abolla videbatur, eaque per complures annos est usa, donec ob frequentem sanguinis vomitum, alieno coacta fuit imperio, noxiam hanc exuere cataphractam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041851Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041851Z">
        <seg>To the virgin, however, this most rudimentary hairshirt seemed less a hairshirt than a scourge, and she wore it for several years, until, because of frequent vomiting of blood, she was forced by another's command to cast off this harmful armor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virginis hujus virtutes &amp; mortificationes matri aliud intendenti mera delicta videbantur, quibus castigandis nonsolum durissima verba, sed persæpe etiam sæva verbera adhibebat, præsertim cum rutilam cæsariem deprehensa fuit sibi præscidisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042028Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042028Z">
        <seg>The virgin's virtues and mortifications, which her mother regarded as mere faults, seemed to her to deserve punishment, and she inflicted not only the harshest words but often also severe beatings, especially when she discovered that her daughter had cut off her red hair.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virginis modestia, sobrietas, devotio, seculi fuga, silentium, vigiliæ, impatienti matri mera delicta erant, quibus castigandis non clamores, scommata, convitia, non demum pugni sufficiebant, &amp; calces: nodosus ex malo cydonia&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; baculus persæpe ad manum fuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010750Z">
        <seg>The virgin's modesty, sobriety, devotion, flight from the world, silence, and vigils were, to an impatient mother, mere offenses, which she punished with shouts, scoffs, insults, and finally fists and kicks; a gnarled quince-wood rod was often at hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virginis modestia, sobrietas, devotio, seculi fuga, silentium, vigiliæ, impatienti matri mera delicta erant, quibus castigandis non clamores, scommata, convitia, non demum pugni sufficiebant, &amp; calces: nodosus ex malo cydonia&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; baculus persæpe ad manum fuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052503Z">
        <seg>The virgin's modesty, sobriety, devotion, flight from the world, silence, and vigils were mere offenses to her impatient mother, for chastising which shouts, scoffs, insults, and finally fists and kicks were not enough; a gnarled cudgel of bad quince was often at hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virginis sanctissimo Rosario præstantis imaginem elegerunt, &amp; quotannis feria secunda post Dominicam, &lt;b3&gt;Quasi modo geniti&lt;/b3&gt;&lt;s4&gt;&lt;a5&gt;d&lt;/a5&gt;&lt;/s4&gt;, instituta publica supplicatione (cui tum ipsi, tum singulorum Ordinum Regulares, &amp; universa tribunalium dicasteria&lt;s6&gt;&lt;a7&gt;e&lt;/a7&gt;&lt;/s6&gt; interveniunt) eamdem in suo apud FF.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054258Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054258Z">
        <seg>They chose an image of the most holy Rosary of the Virgin, and every year on the second feria after Dominica, &lt;b3&gt;As newly born&lt;/b3&gt;&lt;s4&gt;&lt;a5&gt;d&lt;/a5&gt;&lt;/s4&gt;, by a public decree of supplication (to which both they themselves, the Regulars of each Order, and all the dicasteries of the tribunals&lt;s6&gt;&lt;a7&gt;e&lt;/a7&gt;&lt;/s6&gt; attend), they instituted the same in their own church at the Franciscan Fathers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virgo sat perite sentiebat, non tam a noxa vel intemperie humorum, quam a benevola Sponsi manu hæc sibi agonalia festa provenire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052621Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052621Z">
        <seg>The virgin rightly perceived that these agonizing festivals did not arise so much from harm or the temperaments of the humors, but from the Spouse's benevolent hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virgo sermone obiter deflexo etiam ad id, quod latere putabatur, ita respondit, acsi de eo pariter fuisset interrogata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012145Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012145Z">
        <seg>The maiden, her speech casually diverted to that which was thought to be hidden, responded as if she had been asked about it as well.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Viros religiosos, &amp; præsertim sui Ordinis Fratres, quotquot aptos noverat, ad gentilium conversionem hortabatur, &amp; per Jesu Christi viscera obsecrabat, ut huic se operi accingerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042328Z">
        <seg>He urged religious men, and especially the brothers of his own order whom he knew to be suitable, to convert the Gentiles, and he implored them, by the bowels of Jesus Christ, to devote themselves to this work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Visa est ad hujus doloris aliquam similitudinem aut proportionem assurgere ille, quem a mordacissimis scrupulis patitur mens delicata, cum cælum sibi vectibus clausum, Deum hostiliter aversum, infernum rictu immani patulum suspicatur anima, sibimet ad modicum derelicta.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045837Z">
        <seg>It seems that some likeness or proportion of this pain arises in the delicate mind that suffers from the most biting scruples, when the soul, left to itself for a little while, suspects heaven barred against it by its beams, God turned away in hostility, hell gaping open with a monstrous yawn.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Visum est etiam a plurimis simulacrum Deiparæ sanctissimi rosarii luce &amp; splendoribus coruscare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042955Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042955Z">
        <seg>It also seemed to many that the statue of the Most Holy Rosary of the Virgin Mother was shining with light and splendor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vita ipsius merum erat pietatis speculum, ita quod putaretur virtutes singulas in gradu heroico possidere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050048Z">
        <seg>His life itself was a pure mirror of piety, so that he was thought to possess each virtue in a heroic degree.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vividus faciei color, labella rubentia modiceque velut in suavem risum diducta, oculi integri, lucidaque amœnitate subfulgidi diu ambiguam reddidere præsentibus Rosæ mortem, donec adhibito speculo nullum in ea superesse halitum innotuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030254Z">
        <seg>The vivid color of her face, her lips slightly parted as if in a gentle smile, her eyes unblemished and faintly aglow with a bright, pleasant light, long kept those present in doubt about Rosa's death, until, with a mirror applied, they discovered that no breath remained in her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix Angela, conceptis mente supplicibus precatiunculis, imaginem devote respexerat, cum subito novis pressa doloribus, partu celerrimo residuam sobolem cum secundis effudit in lectum, quo jacebat, deficiente spatio temporis, quo obstetricem nutu accerseret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040453Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040453Z">
        <seg>Scarcely had Angela, with her mind full of supplicating prayers, devoutly looked back at the image, when suddenly, seized by new pains, she, in a very rapid delivery, gave birth to the remaining offspring along with the afterbirth onto the bed where she lay, with no time to summon the midwife.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix Puella verbum protulerat, cum sub oculis omnium pullus repente assurrexit in pedes, alisque fortiter excussis, canore &amp; hilariter cucurivit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061544Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061544Z">
        <seg>Scarcely had the girl spoken a word when, before everyone's eyes, the chick suddenly sprang to its feet, flapped its wings vigorously, and chirped cheerfully.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix bolum glutiverat, cum præsaga periculi, comiter mensam reliquit, in vicinum conclavi oratorium se proripuit, valvas adduxit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041116Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041116Z">
        <seg>She had scarcely swallowed the morsel when, with a premonition of danger, she politely left the table, rushed into the adjoining oratory, and drew the bolts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix fari cœperat, cum propter nominis controversiam virgis cædebatur ab avia, si Rosa, a matre, si Elisabeth vocata respondisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042025Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042025Z">
        <seg>He had barely begun to speak when, because of the dispute over his name, he was beaten with rods by his grandmother if he answered "Rosa," and by his mother if he answered "Elizabeth."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix horulam illic exegerat, cum rursus ad exspectantes providens, ultro dissolvit fasciam, ostendit manum undequaque sanam, incolumem, nec jam alterius chirurgi ope indigentem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034832Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034832Z">
        <seg>He had scarcely spent an hour there when, once more turning to the waiting ones, he willingly unfastened the bandage, showed his hand whole and sound, and no longer in need of another surgeon's help.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix maritus pestem hauserat, cum, intercepto anhelitu, tympani instar extumuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034333Z">
        <seg>Scarcely had the husband inhaled the pestilence when, with his breath caught, he swelled up like a drum.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix mulieris uterum reliquiæ attigerant, cum intra breve spatium, quod recitandæ semel angelicæ Salutationi sufficeret, enixa est infantem mortuum, ac putrido virore totum sublividum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035046Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035046Z">
        <seg>Scarcely had the remains touched the woman's womb, when within the short space of time it takes to recite the Angelic Salutation once, she gave birth to a dead infant, all of it bluish-black with a putrid greenishness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix pluribus horis, hac sese angustia Virgo explicuit, ac per aliquot dies nec passum progredi, nec plantis consistere potuit, donec restituta sibi consuetæ abstinentiæ potestate, sensim &amp; ipsa restitueretur sibi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041051Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041051Z">
        <seg>In barely a few hours, this anguish had worn the Virgin out, and for several days she could neither walk a step nor stand on her feet, until, having regained the power of her accustomed abstinence, she too was gradually restored to herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix quinquennis erat, cum a Spiritus sancti unctione interius edocta, virginitatis votum emisit, &amp; ex fratris admonitu vanitatis sæculi conscia facta, ipsamet sibi comas ad vivam usque cutem, inscia matre, præscidit, ne quid funium superesset, quibus adversus devotam puritatem, aut traheret, aut traheretur ad nuptias;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041117Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041117Z">
        <seg>She was barely five years old when, inwardly instructed by the anointing of the Holy Spirit, she made a vow of virginity. And, warned by her brothers of the vanity of the world, she herself, unknown to her mother, cut her hair down to the living skin, so that no locks might remain with which she could be drawn away from devoted purity. or be drawn to marriage;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix quintumdecimum a nativitate diem infans attigerat, cum deprehensus est hernia&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt; miserabiliter laborare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033530Z">
        <seg>The infant was barely fifteen days old when it was discovered that he was suffering miserably from a hernia.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix suis credidere oculis, qui puerum ante noverant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033502Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033502Z">
        <seg>Those who had known the boy before could hardly believe their eyes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix tamen potuit: nam prementium tanta vis fuit, ut lessi mortualis cantoribus in altaris crepidinem eluctandum fuerit, ad lugubre officium utcumque solito cantu explendum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030703Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030703Z">
        <seg>Yet he could scarcely manage it: for the press was so great that the wailing singer had to struggle up onto the altar's edge in order to carry out the mournful rite with the customary chant.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix verba finierat, cum superveniens sacrista F. Joannes Fernandez ait, a Rosa in templo coram sanctissimo Sacramento geniculata illuc se missum, ut Bartholomæum certum faceret, ex illa ægritudine quantumcumque jam complorata, neutiquam sibi mortem imminere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012321Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012321Z">
        <seg>He had scarcely finished speaking when the sacristan, Father John Fernandez, arrived and said that he had been sent by Rosa, who was kneeling in the church before the Blessed Sacrament, to assure Bartholomew that, despite his illness and all his lamentations, he was by no means near death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vixit, convaluit, sed alio Rosæ miraculo, de quo in Mantissa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012216Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012216Z">
        <seg>He lived and recovered, but by another miracle of Rose, about which in the Appendix.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Viæ pro dignitate urbis spatiosæ &amp; amplæ (a quæstoris domicilio usque ad sacram ædem Prædicatorum mille circiter passuum iter erat) nec tamen sat capiebant numerosam plebem, tempestive per omnia compita agglomeratam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030533Z">
        <seg>The streets, spacious and wide for a city of such dignity (the route from the quaestor's residence to the sacred edifice of the Preachers was about a thousand paces), nevertheless could not adequately hold the numerous crowd, which had gathered in time at every intersection;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vocatur a Græcis&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;δευτέριον&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;vel&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;χορίον&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;quemadmodum Gorræus in Definitionibus medicis pag. 513 testatur, ac fusius explicat&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034509Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034509Z">
        <seg>It is called by the Greeks δευτέριον, or χορίον, as Gorræus testifies in his Medical Definitions, page 513, and explains more fully.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vociferabatur cor afflictum, Deus, Deus meus, ut quid me dereliquisti?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045537Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045537Z">
        <seg>The afflicted heart cried out, "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vola ephebe cælice;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051611Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051611Z">
        <seg>Fly, heavenly youth;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vox Græca&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;περικάρδιον&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;e3&gt;oritur ex præpositione&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;περὶ&lt;/s4&gt; circum &lt;e5&gt;&amp; substantivo&lt;/e5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;καρδία&lt;/s6&gt;, &lt;e7&gt;quod&lt;/e7&gt; cor &lt;e8&gt;significat&lt;/e8&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052953Z">
        <seg>The Greek wordpericardion&lt;/e1&gt; comes from the preposition peri&lt;/s4&gt; "around" and the noun kardia&lt;/s6&gt;, which means "heart."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vult igitur indicare biographus&lt;/e0&gt;, S. Rosam &lt;e1&gt;huic Francisco in negotio tam perplexo fuisse auxiliatricem, sicut Ariadna Theseo fuerat in labyrintho, quem ope fili illius evasit&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033046Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033046Z">
        <seg>The biographer therefore wishes to indicate that St. Rose was an assistant to this Francis in so perplexing a matter, just as Ariadne had been to Theseus in the labyrinth, from which he escaped by means of her thread.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>X referetur) de consensu parentis adinvenit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040143Z">
        <seg>X is referred to) concerning the parent's consent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XI inter dotalia pacta solicitudinem alendorum parentum a Rosa in se transtulerat) ipsa de consensu matris manum suam aperuit inopi, &amp; palmas suas extendit ad pauperem, cum his dividendo eleëmosynas, quas insperato receperat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061023Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061023Z">
        <seg>Having taken upon herself the care of her parents' support as one of her dowry stipulations, she, with her mother's consent, opened her hand to the needy and stretched out her palms to the poor, sharing with them the alms she had unexpectedly received.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XI, cum de pronubo Rosæ anulo agendum erit: hic sufficiat innuisse, quam serio ac festive cælum ipsum huic coronæ applauserit, quando in honorem tam rigidæ compunctionis, unctionem distillavit tam suavem ac pretiosam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042400Z">
        <seg>XI, when it comes to the betrothal ring of the Rose: it suffices here to have hinted at how seriously and festively heaven itself applauded this crown, when in honor of such rigid compunction it distilled an anointing so sweet and precious.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XII) spiritus examini subjecta fuit, grandia ac miranda detexit, quia debuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040844Z">
        <seg>XII) The spirit was subjected to an examination, discovered great and wonderful things, because it had to;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XIII) quotidie in suo ad divinam unionem reditu nova illustratione comperiebat, sese in Sponsi favore confirmatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061950Z">
        <seg>XIII) Every day, in her return to divine union, she discovered a new illustration, finding herself confirmed in the favor of the Bridegroom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XIII.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035123Z">
        <seg>Thirteen</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XIV, atque ex eo plura &amp; potiora rescivisse de arcanis suis cum hac imagine commerciis, visionibus, alloquiis, paulo fidentius deinceps huic matronæ fassa est magnam eorum partem, quæ examinatori suo pridem aperuerat eo candore, quo debebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054351Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054351Z">
        <seg>XIV, and having learned from this more and greater things about her secrets through this image, commerce, visions, and conversations, she thereafter confessed to this matron with a little more confidence a large part of them, which she had long since revealed to her examiner with the candor that was due.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XIV, fateri compulsa fuit) de intimis gratiæ thesauris rara quædam seraphici amoris incendia descenderunt in Rosam, secumque traxere longo ordine divitias inæstimabiles variorum charismatum, quibus animam Sponsæ ditabat, illustrabat, ornabat, profusa tam nobilis Sponsi magnificentia: pignora erant, &amp; monilia, datæque fidei pretiosi arrhabones, humanis verbis inexplicabiles: unde &amp; Virgo de his a suo examinatore pressius interrogata, nec negare ausa, simplicissime per solam vocabulorum inopiam se excusavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044433Z">
        <seg>XIV, compelled to confess) rare fires of seraphic love descended upon Rose from the innermost treasures of grace, and with them drew in long train the inestimable riches of various charisms, by which the soul of the Bride was enriched, illuminated, and adorned, through the lavish magnificence of so noble a Spouse: they were pledges and jewels, and precious arrabones of the given faith, inexpressible in human words: hence, when the Virgin was pressed by her examiner on these matters, and dared not deny, she excused herself most simply by the mere poverty of words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XIV.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044814Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044814Z">
        <seg>Fourteen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XVII) pius Salvator tempestive coram fulgido innumerabilium angelorum exercitu revelavit Virgini totam seriem immanium dolorum, [&amp; cruciatus eam præcessuros antea noverat,] quibus ultimo lancinanda ac præparanda erat ad beatam immortalitatem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013226Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013226Z">
        <seg>XVII) The devout Savior timely revealed to the Virgin the entire series of immense pains, [and had previously known that these torments were to precede her,] by which she was ultimately to be pierced and prepared for blessed immortality.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XVII.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025950Z">
        <seg>Seventeen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XVIII) ut Rosæ adhuc mortali familiaris &amp; chara fuerat, ita ab immortali meruit frequenti apparitione familiarius honorari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031828Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031828Z">
        <seg>XVIII) Just as Rose had been familiar and dear to a mortal, so she merited to be honored more familiarly by the immortal through frequent appearances.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XXII) brevissime illam hortatus est, frueretur nunc Sole suo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025941Z">
        <seg>XXII) He briefly urged her to enjoy her sun now.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XXVI.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061944Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061944Z">
        <seg>Twenty-six.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XXXIII narratum est) verum nunc binos sibi datos esse, quorum unum sinistro genu, alterum lævo brachio exceperat portandum ac nutriendum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025702Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025702Z">
        <seg>As was related in chapter thirty-three, he now had two given to him, one of which he had taken up on his left knee and the other on his left arm to carry and nurture.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[&amp; alterum, cui venenum propinatum fuerat.] Limæ crudelis femina (cujus nomen in Processu ubique subticetur) maritum suum extreme oderat, &amp;, ut ignarum e medio tolleret, clam ei toxicum in potu commiscuit, sed prævalidum, quod, antequam possent remedia circumspici, brevi mora hominem extingueret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034330Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034330Z">
        <seg>[and the other, to whom poison had been administered.] A cruel woman of Lima (whose name is omitted throughout the proceedings) hated her husband to the extreme, and, in order to remove the unwitting from her way, secretly mixed a very potent poison into his drink, one that, before any remedy could be sought, would quickly kill the man.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[&amp; crysipelate liberatur ipsa Sanctæ mater,] Hæc pauculis diebus postquam S. Catharinæ Senensis monasterium velanda intrarat, tota pene facie in erysipelatem deformiter exarsit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040831Z">
        <seg>[&amp; the mother herself is delivered from erysipelas,] A few days after she had entered the monastery of St. Catherine of Siena to take her veil, she was hideously disfigured by erysipelas on almost her entire face;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[&amp; incredulis confessariis,] dum initio, quod sibi persuaserant, nitebantur &amp; Virgini persuadere, videlicet, ipsam itinere non recto, aut saltem non tuto incedere, vertigine cerebri, aliisque noxiis intemperiis laborare, frivolas illusiones esse, quas ipsa supernas illustrationes credebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010759Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010759Z">
        <seg>[And to the unbelieving confessors,] at first they strove to persuade the Virgin of what they had convinced themselves, namely, that she was not walking on a straight path, or at least not safely, that she was suffering from dizziness of the brain and other harmful disorders, and that the frivolous illusions she believed to be heavenly illuminations were nothing of the sort.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[&amp; plures utriusque sexus] Negabant medici, vetulam tot annis onustam, ac viribus effœtam, per methodica remedia morbo liberari posse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034048Z">
        <seg>[and many others of both sexes] The doctors denied that an old woman, burdened by so many years and worn out by age, could be cured of her illness by methodical remedies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[100] Antonio de Umbela operario annosa paralysis brachium manumque attraxerat, [corporisque incommodis laborantes,] ut nullis umquam remediis potuerint explicari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034116Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034116Z">
        <seg>A long-standing paralysis had afflicted Antonio de Umbela the worker's arm and hand, and, suffering from bodily infirmities, they were never able to be relieved by any remedies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[100] [&amp; matrem hæc observantem] Cæterum lapilli sparsim ac mobiliter dispositi facile cedebant moli, quoties in aliud latus vertendum erat corpusculum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001045Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001045Z">
        <seg>[100] [and the mother observing these things] Moreover, the little stones, scattered and arranged so as to be movable, gave way easily to the force whenever the little body had to be turned to the other side.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[101] [invocata sancta Rosa,] Maria Farsan triennis puella, dum anno MDCXVII indigestis cruditatibus periculose oppilata curatur a matre infusione argenti vivi, (quod alia femina suggesserat) inflatis mox tibiis, gressum perdidit, adeo quod ab umbilico deorsum velut emortua, solis manibus reptaret, tractis per terram cruribus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034141Z">
        <seg>[101] [Saint Rose invoked,] Maria Farsan, a three-year-old girl, while being treated in 1617 for a dangerous blockage from undigested crudities with an infusion of mercury (which another woman had suggested), soon lost the use of her legs, which swelled, and lost her ability to walk, so that from the navel down she was as good as dead, crawling on her hands with her legs dragged along the ground.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[101] [pie falleret;] Subiit ergo nova ac postrema lectuli dirissime asperandi cogitatio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T002019Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001058Z">
        <seg>[101] [She would deceive piously;] Then a new and final thought occurred to him for making the little bed most dreadfully harsh.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[102] Aliquando fassa est Rosa, in lectuli sui aditu hoc felleum libamen sibi non adeo cum sumeret, [quam lecti duritiem] quam si citius evigilabat, fuisse molestissimum: tunc enim sentiebat palatum usque ad tonsillas&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; &amp; epiglottidem&lt;a1&gt;b&lt;/a1&gt; aruisse penitus, linguam præ siccitate vix non immobilem, gutturis bronchos&lt;a2&gt;c&lt;/a2&gt; undique accensos, ita quod siccatis faucibus supplicium esset ipsa respiratio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T002103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T002103Z">
        <seg>Rosa once confessed that, on her bed's doorstep, this bitter draught was no less troublesome to her when she took it than the hardness of the bed itself, if she had woken up sooner. For then she felt her palate had completely dried up to her tonsils and epiglottis, her tongue was almost motionless from dryness, and the windpipes of her throat were inflamed on all sides, so that breathing itself was torture for her parched throat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[102] [prompte ac mirabiliter] Agnes de Figueroa (Rosæ, dum vivebat, amicissima) immani cruciatu lancinabatur in coxendicibus, &amp; ignara quid mali esset, jam eo devenerat, ut toto triduo vesicam exonerare non potuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034147Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034147Z">
        <seg>[102] [swiftly and miraculously] Agnes of Figueroa (Rosæ's dearest friend while she lived) was being torn apart by excruciating pain in her loins, and, unaware of what was wrong, had already reached the point where she could not void her bladder for three whole days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[103] Contigit subinde, Rosam velut justo pavori pene imparem, [Christus ei apparens approbavit.] diutius hærere in ancipiti, lectumne tot pœnarum fertilem inscenderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T002106Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T002106Z">
        <seg>It happened that Rosam, as if scarcely equal to the just terror, hesitated longer on the horns of a dilemma, whether she should mount the bed so fertile in so many torments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[103] [auxilium ejus experiuntur.] Ludovicus Rodriguez Mexicanus, opificio sartor, in ædibus viduæ Joannæ de Vargas dolorifica nervorum attractione laborabat, ita ut nec incedere, nec pedem humi figere per duos menses umquam potuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034202Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034202Z">
        <seg>[103] [They experience its help.] A Mexican tailor named Luis Rodriguez, working in the house of the widow Joanna de Vargas, suffered from a painful nerve traction, so that for two months he could never walk or set foot on the ground.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[104] [Periculo eripit mulierem parturientem,] Isabellæ Morales parienti (jamque partum effuderat) secundæ&lt;a0&gt;i&lt;/a0&gt; remanserant in utero.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034224Z">
        <seg>[104] [He rescues a woman in labor from danger,] for the afterbirth had remained in the uterus of Isabella Morales, who was giving birth (and had already delivered).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[104] [Unde pulvinar, quod capiti subjicere jussa erat,] Dicendum nunc de cervicali.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T002110Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T002110Z">
        <seg>[104] [Hence the pillow that was ordered to be placed under the head,] We must now speak of the cervical.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[105] Christophorus Perez aulæ criminalis secretarius cum uxore &amp; domesticis ad prædium suum in valle Lurigancho secesserat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034243Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034243Z">
        <seg>Christopher Perez, secretary of the criminal court, had retired to his estate in the Lurigancho Valley with his wife and household servants.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[105] [nova industria identidem durius reddidit,] Exinde mater in virtute sanctæ obedientiæ præcepit Filiæ, rursum vacuaret suum cervical, lanamque reponeret, at lanam solam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T002116Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T002116Z">
        <seg>[105] [new industry made it repeatedly harder,] From then on the mother, in the virtue of holy obedience, commanded her daughter to empty her pillowcase again and to put back only the wool.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[106] Interim supra fossam ejulabant domestici, plangebat uxor, [cum equo delapsum,] denique omnes Rosam in subsidium advocarunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034301Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034301Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, above the ditch the household was wailing, his wife was mourning [that he had fallen from his horse], and at last everyone called for Rosa to come to his aid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[106] [ita ut mater totum ejus lectulum dejecerit,] Demum advenit matri tempus optatissimum, quo sibi de Patrum confessariorum permissu licuit pœnosum Rosæ lectulum manibus avidissimis dissipare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T002122Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T002122Z">
        <seg>[106] [so that the mother threw down her entire little bed,] At last the mother's most eagerly awaited moment arrived, when, with the permission of the confessing Fathers, she was allowed to tear apart with her most avaricious hands the penitent little bed of Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[107] Antonii de Terrabia grandævi mancipii ut lapsus miserabilior, [quemdam jam morti proximum,] ita mirabilior fuit sublevatio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034314Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034314Z">
        <seg>The rescue of the old slave Anthony of Terrabia was all the more miraculous because his fall had been all the more miserable—he was already close to death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[107] Ergo aliter post hæc Rosæ jacendum, &amp; dormiendum fuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T002125Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T002125Z">
        <seg>Therefore, after these things, Rose had to lie down and sleep differently.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[108] Claudat hoc capitulum Rosa, veneni cælica excantatrix.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034326Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034326Z">
        <seg>Let this chapter be closed by Rosa, the heavenly exorciser of poison.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[108] Interim prioris sui lectuli memor &amp; cupida, assidue tundebat confessariorum aures, [At illa solitam cubandi duritiem repetens,] querebatur, se vitam suam otiose perdere, dum a pristinis pœnitentiæ exercitiis in vita cogebatur feriari, omni se patientiæ merito vacuam ex tanta Patrum indulgentia remanere, S. Patri suo Dominico indies occantari titulum &lt;b0&gt;Rosa patientiæ&lt;/b0&gt;, se vero, adempta sibi patientia, nec Rosam esse, nec tanti Patris Filiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003504Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003504Z">
        <seg>[108] Meanwhile, remembering and longing for her former bed, she constantly pounded the ears of the confessors, [But she, recalling the usual hardness of her bed,] complained that she was idly wasting her life, since she was forced to take a break from the former exercises of penance, and that she remained, deprived of all merit of patience, from such great indulgence of the Fathers, She would daily sing to her holy Father Dominic the title &lt;b0&gt;Rose of Patience&lt;/b0&gt;, and that she herself, patience being taken from her, was neither a Rose nor a Daughter of so great a Father;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[109] Sed nec Rosam morti jam vicinam deseruerat hoc pene coævum durissime cubandi desiderium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003507Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003507Z">
        <seg>But neither had he abandoned Rosa, now near death, because of this almost contemporary, most severe desire to sleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[10] [&amp; tantum quinquennis,] Rosæ quinquenni &amp; adhuc inter pueritiæ infantiæque confinia constitutæ, notitia ac timor Dei sese insinuavit modo prorsus admirabili;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203451Z">
        <seg>[10] [Only five years old,] to Rose, who was five and still standing on the border between childhood and infancy, the knowledge and fear of God insinuated itself in a truly marvelous way;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[10] [usque ad sequentem diem differenda fuerit.] Verum nec ibi sat tutum fuit, sequente ac premente turba.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030722Z">
        <seg>[10] [if it was to be postponed until the next day.] But it was not safe there either, with the crowd following and pressing on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[110] Luciam a sanctissima Trinitate, sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis monasterio præfectam, [sive dolor capitis,] a dextro tempore&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; gravis torquebat cranii dolor, cum intemperie cerebri &amp; guttosa defluxione;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034547Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034547Z">
        <seg>[110] Lucia, appointed by the most holy Trinity to the monastery of St. Catherine of Siena, was severely afflicted by a headache, with cerebral intemperance and a gouty discharge from the right temple.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[110] [longas vigilias protrahebat,] Porro quisque ex tanta usualis strati duritie haud difficulter intelliget, quantum Rosa vigiliis dederit, cui ipsemet lectus soporem adimebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003514Z">
        <seg>[110] [She would stay up late,] Moreover, anyone accustomed to the usual hardness of a bed will not find it difficult to understand how much time Rosa devoted to her vigils, since the bed itself robbed her of sleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[111] Virgo Lucia de Montoya ab ineunte pueritia fœdo ac fluido marcore oculorum, [lippitudo,] non sine excæcationis periculo, lippiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034605Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034605Z">
        <seg>[111] The Virgin Lucia de Montoya, from early childhood, suffered from a foul and flowing discharge from her eyes, a condition called lippitude, not without the risk of being blinded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[111] [&amp; dæmoni somnolentiam immittenti] Nec tamen suas artes partesve omisit versutus hostis, quin Virginem, tunc maxime, quando ad nocturnam orationem surrexerat, molestissime niteretur revocare ad somnum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003520Z">
        <seg>[111] [and to the demon who instills drowsiness] Yet the cunning foe did not abandon his arts or his schemes, but most persistently strove to draw the Virgin back to sleep, especially when she had risen for her night prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[112] [&amp; aliæ malæ oculorum affectiones,] Apud Isabellam Mendoza tenebrosius cæcutiebat triennis parvula, Margarita nomine.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034627Z">
        <seg>[112] [&amp; other bad eye conditions,] A little three-year-old girl named Margaret was going blind more severely in the care of Isabella Mendoza.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[112] [acriter resistebat.] Invenit &amp; alium pugnandi modum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003523Z">
        <seg>[112] [He resisted fiercely.] He also found another way to fight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[113] [dentium cruciatus, aliique morbi] Joannæ de Velasco mordax e capite salsugo gingivas impleverat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034652Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034652Z">
        <seg>[113] [Toothache, and other ailments] A biting worm from the head had filled Joanna of Velasco's gums with pus;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[114] [a tenerit annis amabat solitudinem,] Cum annis crescebat latendi studium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003535Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003535Z">
        <seg>[114] [From tender years he loved solitude,] As he grew older, his desire to hide grew;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[114] [ad attactum reliquiarum S. Rosæ] Septennio circiter post felicem Rosæ transitum evoluto, Aloysiam de Escobar S. Claræ monialem tormento instabili discruciabat ignis volaticus, nunc tempora, mox genas, inde oculos aliasque partes non sine periculo oberrans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034723Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034723Z">
        <seg>[114] [at the touch of the relics of St. Rose] About seven years after Rose's blessed passing, a volatile fire tormented Aloysia of Escobar, a nun of St. Clare, now roaming the seasons, then the cheeks, then the eyes and other parts, not without danger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[115] Eamdem prius opem adversus erysipelam&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt; senserat Maria de Jesu excalceata Carmelitana;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034745Z">
        <seg>The same Mary of Jesus, a Discalced Carmelite, had previously experienced the same help against erysipelas.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[115] [ad quam obtinendam] Paulatim eo statura &amp; ætas Rosam provexerat, ut idonea esset honorario comitatu stipare matrem foras egressuram, maxime cum invisendæ erant digniores matronæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003538Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003538Z">
        <seg>Gradually, by her stature and age, Rosa had come to be fit to accompany her mother on her outings, especially when more distinguished matrons were to be visited.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[116] Frater Joannes Garzia, laïcus Ordinis Prædicatorum, [Vulnus manui inflictum,] quarto post Rosæ obitum die, Superiorum jussu hortensem Virginis cellulam intrarat, asportaturus inde modicum scabellum, cui Rosa, dum vixit, insidere solitaria consueverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034810Z">
        <seg>Brother John Garzia, a layman of the Order of Preachers, [Wound inflicted on the hand,] on the fourth day after Rose's death, by order of his superiors, had entered the Virgin's little garden cell to remove from there a small stool on which Rose, during her life, had been accustomed to sit in solitude.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[116] Porro &amp; aliud Virgo excogitavit stratagema, ut solitudinis amans, [sæpius utebatur astuto stratagemate,] redimeret vexam prodeundi in publicum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003545Z">
        <seg>[116] Moreover, the Virgin devised another stratagem so that, as a lover of solitude, she might more often use a cunning stratagem to redeem herself from the vexation of going out in public.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[117] Condicto mutuo domina Ludovica Vargas Carvajal cum Maria de Oliva statuerant, [quod tandem detexit mater,] assumptis utrimque filialibus, vehi ad celebrem divæ Virginis stationem, cui a Monte Serrato&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; nomen est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003548Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003548Z">
        <seg>[117] By a mutual agreement, Lady Ludovica Vargas Carvajal and Maria de Oliva had arranged, [which the mother eventually discovered,] each taking her own daughters, to travel to the famous shrine of the Virgin, which is named Monte Serrato&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[117] Pictoris Angelini Medori conjugem Mariam de Mesta viginti circiter diebus exhauserat copiosi sanguinis fluxus admodum periculosus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034835Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034835Z">
        <seg>The wife of the Angelino painter Medor, Maria de Mesta, had been worn out for about twenty days by a very dangerous, copious flow of blood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[118] Quis enarret scommata, clamores, dicteria, quæ tunc in Filiam detonuit mater?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003554Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003554Z">
        <seg>Who can recount the scoffs, the shouts, the taunts that the mother then hurled at her daughter?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[118] [&amp; diuturna membrorum contractio] In convallibus Cinchæ&lt;a0&gt;e&lt;/a0&gt; Magdalena Chimasso Inda, ex regulorum Chincensium (quos Caciquos&lt;a1&gt;f&lt;/a1&gt; vocant) nobili prosapia, viro Hispano Francisco Morales matrimonio copulata, triennium &amp; amplius ab umbilico deorsum adeo languebat attractione corporis, ut nonnisi lanato velleri potuerit insidere, nec aliter, quam cum eo huc illuc raptanda fuerit, &amp; ad necessaria corporis officia manibus domesticorum deportanda: numquam sine his mutare latus, numquam se sponte erigere visa est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034853Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034853Z">
        <seg>[118] [&amp; long-standing contraction of the limbs] In the valleys of Chincha&lt;a0&gt;e&lt;/a0&gt; Magdalena Chimasso Inda, from a noble lineage of the Chincha rulers (whom they call Caciquos&lt;a1&gt;f&lt;/a1&gt;), married to a Spanish man, Francisco Morales, for three years and more she had been so weak from the navel down due to her body's contraction that she could only sit on a woolen blanket, and even then only when she was carried back and forth on it and carried by her household servants to the necessities of nature. She was never seen to change her side without them, nor to raise herself of her own accord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[119] [Cum cuperet in angusto tugurio domi sola degere,] Usque adeo Rosæ placebat desertum, displicebat publicum, ut non solum privata palatia, communiaque urbis spectacula mordicus devitarit, verum &amp; generales celebrium processionum circuitus, ac populosas supplicationes curiose spectare defugerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003600Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003600Z">
        <seg>[119] [Since she wished to live alone at home in a cramped hovel,] So much did Rosæ prefer solitude and dislike public life that she not only scrupulously avoided private palaces and the city's public spectacles, but also shunned the general circuits of celebratory processions and the crowded supplications.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[119] [eodem remedio statim curantur.] Hæc una cum sepulchri modico pulvere, Magdalena fide ac devotione, qua decuit, sibi arctissime circumligavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034912Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034912Z">
        <seg>[119] [They are immediately cured by the same remedy.] These, together with a little dust from the sepulchre, were most closely bound to her by Magdalen's faith and devotion, as was fitting.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[11] Hinc subito in se reducta cœpit odisse suosmet capillos, [accepta singulari divinitatis cognitione,] firmare animum adversus omnes offensas Dei, detestari occasiones, quæcumque inferno militarent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203317Z">
        <seg>[11] [Only five years old,] to Rose, who was five and still standing on the border between childhood and infancy, the knowledge and fear of God insinuated itself in a truly marvelous way;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[120] [Etiam alii ægri,] Mariam de Morales viduam Marci Antonii non sola nervorum attractio, sed insuper varii aliorum membrorum cruciatus integro quinquennio acerbissime divexabant: nec hilum profuere remedia, quæ plurima tanto temporis ærisque impendio conquisierat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034934Z">
        <seg>[120] [Other patients, too,] Mary de Morales, widow of Mark Antony, was afflicted not only by a nerve spasm but also by various torments in other limbs for a full five years, and she was most bitterly tormented: nor did the remedies she had procured—the very many of them—at such cost of time and money help her in the least.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[120] [matre id recusante,] Igitur, aspirante Numine, sublimiorem speculata est viam, qua sese ab iis impedimentis viriliter explicaret: parentes verecunde supplex oravit, permitti sibi in extremo horti angulo locum solitarium pro architectanda cellula, quam cupiebat unicæ dumtaxat staturæ suæ capacem, cum exigua fenestella, quæ lumen exciperet, ostioli clavem sola (si vellet) mater teneret: ibi se labori manuum, orandi meditandique studio, &amp; collectioni spiritus absque ullo minimi temporis dispendio utilissime vacaturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003603Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003603Z">
        <seg>[120] [with her mother refusing,] Therefore, with the Divine favor inclining her, she discerned a nobler path by which she might bravely free herself from those impediments: she humbly besought her parents, asking that she be permitted in the farthest corner of the garden a solitary spot for a cell, which she desired to be of a size suitable only to her single stature, with a small window to admit light, the key to the little door to be held by her mother alone (if she wished): there she would most usefully devote herself to manual labor, to the study of prayer and meditation, and to the collection of her spirit without the slightest waste of time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[121] [confugit ad intercessionem Deiparæ,] Postridie ad sacellum Virgo rediit Missam auditura, cumque notasset suam imagini coronam deesse, magnis precibus majorem (quem vocant) sacristanum exoravit, ut tandem ipse coronam imaginis collo circumdaret, sua interesse plurimum, ut id fiat, velle se pretioso hoc vinculo suaviter captivare Matrem Virginem, ut ita Filiolus vadem seu fidejussorem se interponat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003606Z">
        <seg>[121] [She fled to the intercession of the Mother of God,] The next day the Virgin returned to the chapel to hear Mass, and when she noticed that her image was missing a crown, she earnestly implored the senior sacristan to finally place the crown around the image's neck, insisting that it was of the utmost importance. She wanted to sweetly captivate the Mother and Virgin with this precious bond, so that her Son would thus interpose himself as a surety.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[122] Provinciæ de Guailas&lt;a0&gt;k&lt;/a0&gt; in regno Peruano gubernator præerat D. Antonius de Contreras, [&amp; mulieres] eoque titulo vicum Jungai cum uxore Blanca de Zuniga incolebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035033Z">
        <seg>The governor of the province of Guailas in the Kingdom of Peru was Don Antonio de Contreras, and by that title he and his wife, Blanca de Zuniga, inhabited the village of Jungai.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[122] Rosa ex hoc mirabili ostento jam sui voti secura, [&amp; per amicos ad matrem missos fit voti compos,] ac pene compos, gravem ac honorificam adornavit legationem ad matrem: hanc obierunt magister &amp; cathedraticus P. F. Joannes de Lorenzana, Gundisalvus de la Massa quæstor regius, &amp; quæstoris uxor Maria de Usatigui.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003610Z">
        <seg>[122] Rosa, now secure in her vow by this marvelous sign [and, through friends sent to her mother, fulfills her vow], and almost fully recovered, appointed a solemn and honorable embassy to her mother: it was headed by the master and chancellor, Father Francis John de Lorenzana, Gundisalvus de la Massa, royal treasurer, and the treasurer's wife, Maria de Usatigui.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[123] Eadem alia vice, cum partu infeliciori fœtum quadrimestrem abortivisset, [in partu] retentis in utero secundis, per duos menses &amp; quinque dies tantam vim corrupti sanguinis protrusit alvo, ut prope actum de sua vita existimarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035055Z">
        <seg>On another occasion, when she had miscarried a four-month-old fetus after an unfortunate labor, with the afterbirth retained in the womb, she expelled such a quantity of corrupted blood from her abdomen over the course of two months and five days that she nearly despaired of her life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[123] Porro obtenta hac permissione, quam putas festivo dulcique gaudio diem illum transegit Rosa?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T005938Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T005938Z">
        <seg>[123] And having obtained this permission, how do you suppose Rosa spent that day in festive, sweet joy?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[124] Nempe, ut dicebam, thalamus erat, quem Virgo filia Sion adornarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T005950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T005950Z">
        <seg>Indeed, as I was saying, it was the bridal chamber that the Virgin, daughter of Zion, had adorned.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[124] [periclitantes] Felicius Rosæ obstetricantem sibi in partu experta est opem Bernarda de la Sierra, uxor Joannis de Quixano Zevallos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035104Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035104Z">
        <seg>[124] [in peril] Bernarda de la Sierra, wife of Juan de Quiñones Zevallos, was more fortunate in having Rosa as her midwife during childbirth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[125] [similem sacrarum reliquiarum vim senserunt.] Mercator Basilius de Vargas Hispalensis, dum negotiorum causa ab emporio Limensi Po tosium tendit, per viam gravatur ex sincipite jugibus stillicidiis, quorum acrimonia sensim naribus cancrenam&lt;a0&gt;m&lt;/a0&gt; minabatur, &amp; jam quinque mensium spatio mordax humor eo usque invaluerat, ut os nasi perfregerit, cœperitque cartilaginem non sine cruciatu depascere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035133Z">
        <seg>[125] [They felt a similar power in the sacred relics.] The merchant Basil de Vargas of Seville, while traveling on business from the port of Lima to Potosí, was troubled on the way by constant trickles of discharge from his forehead, the acrimony of which was slowly threatening to produce a cancer in his nostrils, and by the fifth month the corrosive humor had grown so strong that it had broken through the nasal cavity, and began to consume the cartilage not without great agony.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[125] [ut nempe Missis procul celebratis interesset,] Qui Virginis in templa &amp; res sacras ardentem affectum noverant, mirabantur ipsam jugiter suo reclusoriolo sic adhærescere, ut ferialibus diebus vix aliquando ecclesiam adiret, proinde a nonnemine interrogata fuit, quomodo cor ejus pientissimum sufferre posset, quod non saltem ad interveniendum Missæ sacrificio, tantisper omni mane tuguriolum suum relinqueret mox repetendum?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T005956Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T005956Z">
        <seg>[125] [so that she might attend Masses celebrated at a distance,] Those who knew of her ardent devotion to the Virgin in temples and sacred things were amazed that she so constantly clung to her little reclusory, that on weekdays she scarcely ever went to church; accordingly, she was asked by some one how her most devout heart could bear it, that she did not at least go to assist at the Sacrifice of the Mass, leave her little cell every morning only to return so soon?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[126] [&amp; culices,] Aliud Rosæ jam solitariæ privilegium a culicibus fuit, imo obsequium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010000Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010000Z">
        <seg>[126] [&amp; mosquitoes,] Another privilege of the now solitary Rose was the mosquitoes, indeed their deference.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[127] Accidit, quod vel mater, vel ex permissione confessarii nonnullæ religiosiores personæ, [quamvis aliis adventantibus infesti,] Rosam in angusto suo habitaculo, de divinis collocuturæ, inviserent: has vero, mox ubi ad januam aut fenestellam consedissent, hostiliter invadebat culicum exercitus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010003Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010003Z">
        <seg>It happened that either the mother herself or, with the confessor's permission, some more devout persons [though hostile to other visitors] would visit Rosa in her narrow dwelling to converse about divine matters; but these, as soon as they sat down at the door or the little window, were hostilely attacked by an army of gnats.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[127] [e sepulcro S. Rosæ erutus,] Porro in hoc Rosæ cenotaphio, ad perenne humani generis beneficium, vivam salutiferi pulveris latere scaturiginem id argumento fuit, quod anno MDCXXXII post exportatos inde totoque regno distributos terrarum cophinos, (&amp; quidem ab ea dumtaxat parte foveæ, ubi caput Virginis collocatum erat) vix tria vel quatuor pondo, cavo scrobiculo eruta videbantur: &amp; cum P. F. Bernardus Marques pauculis ante diebus totum immisisset brachium foramini, paulo post succrescente intus pulvere, vix manum immergere totam potuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035350Z">
        <seg>[127] [dug up from the tomb of St. Rose,] Furthermore, in this cenotaph of Rose, the fact that a living spring of the salutary powder lay hidden for the perpetual benefit of mankind was shown by this: that in the year 1632, after the earthen jars had been exported from there and distributed throughout the entire kingdom, (and indeed from that part of the pit only where the head of the Virgin had been placed) scarcely three or four pounds, dug up from a small hollow, were found: and when Father Bernard Marques had a few days before inserted his whole arm into the opening, a little later, with the dust having grown inside, he could scarcely get his whole hand in.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[128] Plane sic erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010006Z">
        <seg>That was indeed the case.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[128] [profuit plurimis ægris,] Decennem Æthiopissam quinquaginta argenteis Rosa emerat pro parentum domesticis servitiis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035356Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035356Z">
        <seg>[128] [She was of great benefit to many sick people,] Rosa had bought the ten-year-old Ethiopian girl for fifty pieces of silver for the domestic services of her parents.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[129] In Limensi sanctæ Claræ monasterio Soror Grimaneca de Valverde duplicis tertianæ ardoribus, [ut probatur sanatione subita] &amp; copioso sanguinis profluvio post insomnem quindecim dierum &amp; noctium inquietudinem sibimet erepta, miserrime delirabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035437Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035437Z">
        <seg>[129] In the monastery of Saint Clare in Lima, Sister Grimaneca of Valverde, having delivered herself up to the torments of a double tertian fever, [as is proven by her sudden recovery] and a copious hemorrhage after fifteen days and nights of sleepless unrest, was miserably delirious;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[129] Soror Catharina de S. Maria tertii Ordinis S. P. Dominici, [sicut experientia compertum est,] ac D. Eleonoræ de Castro annosa contubernalis, Rosam in sua eremo visitabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010012Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010012Z">
        <seg>[129] Sister Catherine of St. Mary of the Third Order of St. Dominic, [as experience has shown,] and a long-time companion of Lady Eleanor of Castile, visited Rose in her hermitage;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[12] Certatim omnis ætas &amp; sexus urgebat aditum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031004Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031004Z">
        <seg>[12] All ages and both sexes rushed the entrance in a frenzy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[12] Demum sub hac tam præmatura ac sublimi Dei notitia quinquennis Rosa sensit, [perpetuam virginitatem] apertius trahi se ac præparari ad S. Catharinæ Senensis excelsa tirocinia: siquidem hujus exemplo confestim &amp; ipsa virginitatis votum emisit inter hæc primula tenellæ ætatis crepuscula, quinto nimirum ætatis anno;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202758Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T202313Z">
        <seg>[12] Finally, under this so early and sublime knowledge of God, five-year-old Rosa felt, [perpetual virginity] was more clearly drawn and prepared for the exalted disciplines of St. Catherine of Siena: for by her example she too immediately made a vow of virginity in the very dawn of her tender years, namely in the fifth year of her age;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[130] Ibidem soror Raphaëla de Esquivel æstu bilioso torrida, [tum feminarum,] &amp; insuper arctata faucium angustiis, dum nimiis phlebotomiis, &amp; evacuationibus exhauritur, una judicii mentisque sanitatem perdiderat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035455Z">
        <seg>[130] There, Sister Raphael de Esquivel, scorched by a bilious fever, [and of the women,] and further strangled by the constriction of her throat, while being drained by excessive bloodlettings and evacuations, had lost both her reason and her sanity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[130] [dum illa usque ad mortem in simili vitæ genere perseveravit.] Ultimo vitæ suæ triennio, in Gundisalvi quæstoris domo, paris fere solitudinis desertum sibi Virgo diffinierat, totosque cum noctibus dies, vel in domestico oratorio latebat abdita, vel in ultimo ædium cubiculo arctis sese cancellis jugiter cohibebat, omnia præstruens, omnia interponens, ut inutiles visitationes devitaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010015Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010015Z">
        <seg>[130] [while she persevered to the end of her life in a similar way of life.] In the last three years of her life, in the house of the treasurer Gundisalvus, the Virgin had appointed for herself a desert of almost equal solitude, and for the entire day and night, she would either hide herself in the domestic oratory, or keep herself constantly confined within the innermost chamber of the house behind barred doors, arranging everything and interposing every obstacle to avoid useless visits.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[131] Pater F. Ferdinandus de Esquivel Ordinis Prædicatorum Limensis cœnobii ad S. Mariæ Magdalenæ Subprior, [tum virorum,] ab utroque peritonæi latere diram passus rupturam, hernia (quam enterocelam&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; vocant) acerbissime vexabatur, ut nec prædicare, nec iter agere potuerit, quantiscumque subligaculis ac fasciis conaretur dependula in scrotum viscera coarctare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035505Z">
        <seg>[131] Father F. Ferdinand de Esquivel, Subprior of the Dominican Order's convent of St. Mary Magdalene in Lima, [then of men,] suffered a dreadful rupture on both sides of his peritoneum, a hernia (which they call an enterocele) that afflicted him so severely that he could neither preach nor travel, no matter how many supports and bandages he tried to constrain the viscera bulging into his scrotum.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[132] Humilitatem Rosæ jam cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010028Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010028Z">
        <seg>[132] Already the humility of the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[132] [qui diversis malis affecti] Virginem Annam Cortes, ex continua duorum mensium febri simul in costarum membrana pleuritis, totoque sparsæ corpore liventes papulæ (quas dicunt epinyctidas&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;) lethaliter dispungebant: omnem cibi potusque orexin&lt;a1&gt;c&lt;/a1&gt; gravis nausea peremerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035511Z">
        <seg>[132] [who, afflicted with various ailments,] the Virgin Anna Cortes, from a continuous two-month fever accompanied by pleurisy of the rib membrane, and papules (which they call epinyctides) that were fatally puncturing her entire body, had lost all appetite for food and drink to severe nausea;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[133] Primum (ex iis, quæ innotuerunt) hoc fuit: [ut post obscura quædam præludia,] quando bicolor ille papilio (de quo sup.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010037Z">
        <seg>[133] The first (of those that became known) was this: [that after certain obscure preludes,] when that two-colored butterfly (of which above)</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[133] [vel doloribus oppressi,] Catharinam de sancta Maria, Ordinis S. Dominici monialem Tertiariam, diversa gravium morborum genera pene membratim a quadriennio occuparant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035542Z">
        <seg>[133] [or oppressed by pains,] Catherine of St. Mary, a tertiary nun of the Order of St. Dominic, had been afflicted for nearly four years with various severe illnesses that had nearly torn her limb from limb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[134] Paulo apertius alterum, quod sequitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010040Z">
        <seg>[134] Let us be a little more explicit about the other, which follows.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[134] [eo reverenter accepto,] Ibidem Beatrix de Montoya sanctimonialis, stomachi dyspepsiam&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt; annis viginti passa, demum convulsionum atrocitate ad extrema properabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035604Z">
        <seg>[134] [Having received him reverently,] There, Beatrix de Montoya, a nun who had suffered from dyspepsia for twenty years, was at last, through the severity of her convulsions, hastening to her end.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[135] In Cusco P. Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035626Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035626Z">
        <seg>In Cusco, Father Brother</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[135] [mirabilem] Visus deinde ex itinere rediisse Sponsus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010046Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010046Z">
        <seg>Then the bridegroom was seen to have returned from his journey, marvelous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[136] Catharinæ de Arriaga anno MDCXVIII die XXI Martii in plurium matronarum præsentia, [id ulterius confirmatur] rivi sanguinis fluxu prodigioso scaturiebant e naribus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035654Z">
        <seg>[136] On March 21, 1618, in the presence of several matrons, Catherine of Arriaga's nostrils gushed a prodigious flow of blood; [this is further confirmed]</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[136] [mysticamque visionem,] Mirabatur Virgo virgines operi tam insueto implicitas, at significabatur virtus occupata in arduis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010049Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010049Z">
        <seg>[136] [and the mystical vision,] The Virgin marveled at the virgins engaged in such an unusual task, but it signified virtue occupied with arduous deeds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[137] Anno MDCXXXI in SS. Incarnationis monasterio ancillabatur puella Æthiopissa annorum plus minus viginti quatuor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035706Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035706Z">
        <seg>In the year 1631, a twenty-four-year-old Ethiopian girl was serving as a handmaid in the Monastery of the Holy Incarnation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[137] [quæ in sacello Rosariano] Jamque post ejusmodi præambula supererat ultimum, quo demum hic Sponsus manifeste ac palam se insinuaret vigili Rosæ, &amp;, exuto latomi schemate, Virginem ad connubialem thalamum apertius invitaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010053Z">
        <seg>[137] [which is in the Rosary chapel] And now, after such preambles, the final one remained, by which this Spouse would at last manifestly and openly make himself known to the watcher of the Rose, and, having laid aside the guise of a mason, would more openly invite the Virgin to the nuptial chamber.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[138] Hac igitur novitate confusa Virgo, ut assolent teneriores conscientiæ, [coram imagine Deiparæ] extimuit, ne aliquo forsan obliquo animi motu indignam se reddidisset illius ramiferæ processionis consortio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010056Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010056Z">
        <seg>[138] Confused by this novelty, the Virgin—as is the way with the more tender of conscience—dreaded, before the image of the Mother of God, lest by some perhaps oblique movement of the mind she might have rendered herself unworthy of the company of that branch-bearing procession.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[138] Limæ puella innupta carnis voluptatem pudori male prætulerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035725Z">
        <seg>[138] A virgin girl in Lima had wrongly preferred the pleasure of the flesh to modesty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[139] Ad hæc verba, mox vidit cæli Reginam, vultu exporrecto &amp; hilari, [explicata est,] converti in parvulum (quem ulnis gestabat) Filium, ac inde velut a fausto oraculo suavius respicere in Rosam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010059Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010059Z">
        <seg>At these words, she soon saw the Queen of Heaven, with a beaming and cheerful countenance, transformed into the little Son she was carrying in her arms, and from him, as from a favorable oracle, she cast a more gracious look upon Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[139] [vel diuturno morbo languentes,] Anno MDCXXXI FF. Antonius Montoya &amp; Joannes de Estrada sacris Ordinibus initiandi mittebantur Guamangam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035756Z">
        <seg>[139] [or languishing from a long-standing illness,] In the year 1631, Fathers Anthony Montoya and John de Estrada were sent to Guamanga to be ordained to the sacred orders.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[13] Inter hos motus collectis in chorum cœnobitis, proceditur ad solemne Missæ sacrificium, [ita ut Missa funebris audiri non posset.] adsistente in pontificalibus ornamentis illustrissimo D. Petro de Valentia Guatimalæ&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; episcopo, qui advenerat, intromissus per sacristiæ posticum (nam aditus per oppletum multitudine templum haud patebat) Officium sepulturæ post Missam propriis manibus peracturus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031026Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031026Z">
        <seg>[13] Among these movements, the monks gathered in the choir, and the solemn Mass sacrifice proceeded, [so that the funeral Mass could not be heard.] with the Most Illustrious Lord Peter of Valencia of Guatemala, bishop, present in his pontifical vestments, who had arrived, admitted through the sacristy's rear entrance (for the entrance through the temple, filled with a crowd, was not open) to perform the burial office after Mass with his own hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[13] Unum hic prætereundum non est, quod, qui deinde Virginis conscientiam moderati sunt, [Deo vovit.] prudenter conjecere, superna hæc gratiæ allectamenta ipsi obtigisse sub illo primo usus rationis diluculo, quo juxta veriorem theologiam quilibet in Deum finem ultimum sese tenetur convertere pro suæ capacitatis modulo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203453Z">
        <seg>[13] One thing here must not be overlooked, that those who have since moderated the Virgin's conscience, [Vowed to God.] prudently conjectured that these heavenly allurements of grace had been granted to her at that first dawn of the use of reason, when, according to the more accurate theology, every person is bound to turn himself to God as his ultimate end, in proportion to his capacity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[140] Quid multa?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010102Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010102Z">
        <seg>What more is there to say?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[140] [in curandis vulneribus,] Aliam, in Panamensi empotio, inflammata exulceratorum viscerum putredo cum sanguinis excretione prope ad extrema deduxerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035837Z">
        <seg>[140] [in treating wounds,] Another, in a Panamense jar, inflamed by the putrefaction of ulcerated viscera with an excretion of blood, had brought her near her end;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[141] Abbatissa monasterii sanctæ Claræ in civitate Truxillo, [tibiis,] ab annis circiter viginti miserrime totquebatur ex inflatione tibiæ, in qua sparsim plus quam quadraginta ulcerum hiabant ora, ciceris magnitudine, nec malo tam veteri idoneus reperiebatur Chiron&lt;a0&gt;i&lt;/a0&gt;, præsertim ubi cruris affectum reliqui sanguinis secuta est intemperies cum molestissima febrilis rigoris atque ardoris vicissitudine.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035855Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035855Z">
        <seg>The abbess of the monastery of Saint Clare in the city of Trujillo, [of her legs,] had been miserably afflicted for about twenty years by an inflammation of the tibia, in which more than forty ulcers, each the size of a chickpea, gaped here and there, nor was Chiron&lt;a0&gt;i&lt;/a0&gt;deemed suitable for such a chronic malady, especially since the affected leg, with the remaining blood, was beset by a disorder with the most troublesome alternation of feverish chills and burning heat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[141] Sed quæ olim in Canæ Galileæ nuptiis interlocuta virgo Mater, [eique magnos gratiæ thesauros dederit.] hic minime tacuit, eo vel maxime, quod sola ipsa hujus sponsalitii paranympha aderat, sola Rosam promoverat, Filiumque pronuba exorarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010108Z">
        <seg>[141] But the Virgin Mother, who at the wedding in Cana of Galilee once spoke up [and had received for herself great treasures of grace], here by no means remained silent, especially since she herself alone was present as the bridesmaid of this betrothal, had alone presented the Rose, and had alone implored the Son as sponsor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[142] Miles quidam, officio ac dignitate vexillifer, suo confessori P. Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035920Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035920Z">
        <seg>[142] A certain soldier, by office and rank a standard-bearer, to his confessor, Father Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[142] [Rosa in memoriam anti beneficii] Porro ut jugiter tanti beneficii mnemosynon versaretur ante oculos, domum reversa, cœpit extemplo cogitare de conflando connubiali anulo, quem cordis digito, in nuptiarum suarum gratam memoriam, gestaret perpetim ac palam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010111Z">
        <seg>[142] [A Rose in Memory of a Benefit] Furthermore, so that a constant memorial of so great a benefit might be ever before her eyes, when she returned home, she at once began to think of having a wedding ring cast, which she would wear on her ring finger, as a pleasant memory of her nuptials, forever and openly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[143] In monasterio SS. Incarnationis novitia Joanna de Ulloa, [aliisque afflictionibus] probationis anno prope expleto, dolebat anxia, se ad solemnem professionem pertingere non posse, eo quod durus pater (qui procul degebat in Potosi) cunctabatur circa filiæ dotem sese resolvere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035945Z">
        <seg>At the Monastery of the Holy Incarnation, novice Joanna de Ulloa, with her year of probation nearly completed, was distressed and anxious that she could not reach solemn profession, because her strict father (who lived far off in Potosí) was delaying in providing her dowry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[143] [jussit fieri annulum connubialem] Annulum deinde ex hoc jam chariorem, ejusdem majoris hebdomadæ feria quinta in ecclesiam detulit, magnisque precibus a patre sacrista impetravit eidem locum in arcula, quæ illo triduo SS. Eucharistiæ repositorium est, &amp; sepulti Redemptoris monumentum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010114Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010114Z">
        <seg>[143] [He ordered a wedding ring to be made.] He then brought the ring, now more precious for its origin, on the Thursday of the same week to the church, and by great entreaty obtained from the sacristan a place for it in the little reliquary that serves as the repository for the Blessed Eucharist during those three days, and as a monument to the buried Redeemer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[144] Post Virginis obitum una cum reliqua ipsius permodica supellectile anulus remansit penes uxorem Gundisalvi quæstoris, [cujus attactus post obitum Virginis] istudque memorabile accidit anno MDCXVIII, mense Februario, in dicti quæstoris ædibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010117Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010117Z">
        <seg>After the Virgin's death, a ring remained with her very modest personal effects, in the possession of the wife of Gundisalvus the treasurer, [whose touch after the Virgin's death] and this remarkable event occurred in the year 1618, in the month of February, in the house of the said treasurer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[144] [&amp; corporis incommodis,] Porro febricitantes, quos eorumdem pulverum haustus a vitæ periculo interdum extremo revocavit, hic sigillatim distinctis paragraphis recensere prolixum foret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040007Z">
        <seg>[144] [and bodily ailments,] Furthermore, it would be too lengthy to enumerate here, in separate paragraphs, the feverish patients whom the inhalation of those same powders has at times rescued from the very brink of death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[145] Paulo post, hic servus Dei, velut internis stimulis erupit in voces: [quemdam Dei servum ita affecit,] “Benedictus Deus, qui tam mirabilis est in Rosa sua.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010127Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010127Z">
        <seg>Shortly afterward, this servant of God, as if spurred on by inner promptings, broke out in words: "Blessed be God, who is so marvelous in His Rose."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[145] [eamdem hujus pulveris medicinam experti sunt.] Denique ut multos globatim consarcinando compendium &amp; finem faciam, sacramento coram judicibus firmavit Joannes Lobo presbyter, se diversissimis locis, Chuquisaque&lt;a0&gt;l&lt;/a0&gt;, Potosii, Oruræ&lt;a1&gt;m&lt;/a1&gt;, alibi, flagitantibus promiscue omnis ætatis, sexus, conditionis infirmis præbuisse hujus vivificæ terræ potum, unicoque isthoc catapotio palam in omnium conspectu tot fuisse curata languorum genera, tot quotidie febrientes integram valetudinem reportasse domum, ut nimia horum multitudo omnem superarit annotandorum nominum diligentiam, cassumque reddiderit catalogi laborem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040057Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040057Z">
        <seg>[145] [They also tried the same medicine of this powder.] Finally, to make a long story short, Father John Lobo swore an oath before the judges that he had, in various places—Chuquisaca, Potosí, Oruro, and elsewhere—at the request of people of all ages, of every age, sex, and condition, he provided this life-giving earth as a drink, and with this single remedy, openly in full view of all, so many kinds of ailments were cured, so many who were feverish daily returned home in perfect health, that their sheer multitude surpassed all diligence in recording names and rendered the catalog's labor in vain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[146] Quæstoris uxor, devoto spectaculo jam diu oblectata, [ut præspirituali gaudio se non caperet,] maritum advocat, quasi ad perfunctoriæ salutationis debitum officium: nam hospes, qui consederat, illis in ædibus notissimus &amp; charus erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010130Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010130Z">
        <seg>The quæstor's wife, long entertained by the devoted spectacle, [unable to contain herself with pre-spiritual joy,] calls her husband, as if to the perfunctory duty of a greeting: for the guest, who had taken his seat, was a very well-known and beloved figure in that household.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[147] Mirum dictu!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010133Z">
        <seg>[147] Strange to say!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[147] [(harum quarumdum color miro modo mutatur)] Simillimum in portu Callao eadem die circa aliam Rosæ imaginem accidit: hanc virgo Petronilla de Quisano, in ædibus centurionis Alvari de Lugares, circumpositis lemniscis ac floribus exornarat, utpote illa die comparituram in principis ecclesiæ pulpito, dum supradictæ litteræ compulsoriales collectæ multitudini prælegerentur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040258Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040258Z">
        <seg>[147] [(of some of these, the color changes in a marvelous way)] A very similar event occurred in the port of Callao on the same day around another image of the Rose: this one had been adorned by the virgin Petronilla de Quisano, in the house of the centurion Alvar de Lugares, with ribbons and flowers, since on that day it was to be placed in the pulpit of the prince's church, while the aforementioned compulsory letters were being read aloud to the assembled multitude.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[148] [duæ puellæ,] Anno MDCXXXI mense Decembri, novenni puellæ Mariæ de los Reyes anno prope integro saniosa ac fœda porrigo&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; totum undequaque verticem occrustarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040311Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040311Z">
        <seg>In December of the year 1631, for nearly a whole year, a nine-year-old girl named María de los Reyes had her entire scalp covered all over with a sickly and foul porridge.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[149] Michaëlæ de la Massa cum dirum sub brachio succrevisset apostema, [aliæque nonnullæ feminæ] chirurgi cantharides&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; intriverunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040345Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040345Z">
        <seg>When Michael de la Massa had developed a dreadful abscess under her arm, the surgeons inserted cantharides.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[149] Porro duplici assueverat orandi modo: prior, isque præcipuus erat, quando ex occupato simul tum corpore, [duplici orationum generi,] tum animo, quietissime se totam collegerat ad loquendum cum Deo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010149Z">
        <seg>[149] Moreover, she had grown accustomed to two modes of prayer: the first, and chief, was when, her body and mind both occupied, she would most quietly gather herself together to speak with God;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[14] At rursus novis ac validioribus ingemuit clamoribus stipata turba, [Tum signis decepta plebs facile credidit,] rursus densato cuneo undique irruebatur in suggestum tumuli;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031041Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031041Z">
        <seg>[14] But again, the crowd, packed together, groaned with fresh and louder cries; [Then, deceived by the signals, the mob readily believed,] and again, in a denser wedge, it rushed in from all sides onto the platform of the mound.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[150] Rarum hoc &amp; singulariter admirandum in Virgine donum fuit omnipotentis dexteræ, [quarum unum erat assiduum] quod sive dormiret Rosa, sive vigilaret, numquam &amp; nusquam mentis suæ oculo elabebatur cælestis Sponsi præsentia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010152Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010152Z">
        <seg>[150] This was a rare and singularly admirable gift of the omnipotent right hand in the Virgin, [one aspect of which was its constancy] that whether the Rose slept or was awake, the presence of the heavenly Spouse never and nowhere slipped from the eye of her mind;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[150] Servilis conditionis femina Francisca, eodem correpta morbo, &amp; insuper febrium onusta auctario, [morbis eripiuntur,] rugiebat noctibus, impar tormento: dumque ipso conticinio noctis gravius torquebatur, surgens hera, detractam parieti imaginem languenti attulit, stomacho superponi jussit, ac Rosæ opem imploravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040410Z">
        <seg>[150] A female slave named Francisca, seized by the same disease and further burdened by a fever, was writhing in agony at night, unable to bear the torment. And as she was writhing most severely in the dead of night, her mistress rose, brought the image that had been taken down from the wall to the sick woman, ordered it to be placed over her stomach, and implored Rosa for help.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[151] Nempe in S. Catharina Senensi simillimum gratiæ paradigma præcesserat, [quo sic cum Deo conjungebatur,] quando streperis culinæ officiis damnata, construxit in intimo animæ recessu felicissimum illud imperturbabilis quietis conclave, quo nulla exteriorum tumultuum importunitas penetrabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010155Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010155Z">
        <seg>[151] Indeed, in St. Catherine of Siena a most similar paradigm of grace had already appeared, [by which she was thus united with God,] when, condemned to the noisy duties of the kitchen, she built in the innermost recess of her soul that most blessed chamber of unperturbed peace, into which no importunity of external tumults could penetrate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[151] [&amp; tres puerperæ] Ancilla Elizabeth Biafora, prægnans ac vicina partui, dispungebatur immani costarum pleuritide, ardebat febribus, cruorem &amp; saniem vomebat ore, &amp; demum a medicis derelicta, ac munita sacramentis, properabat ad mortem cum extremo fœtus maturi periculo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040425Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040425Z">
        <seg>[151] [&amp; three postpartum women] The maid Elizabeth Biafora, pregnant and near her delivery, was being consumed by a severe pleurisy of the ribs, was burning with fever, was vomiting blood and pus from her mouth, and finally, abandoned by the doctors and having received the sacraments, was hastening toward death with the extreme danger of a mature fetus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[152] Aliud æque mirabile huic dono accessit in cumulum, [ut exteriora objecta eam non distraherent,] quod inter orandum non facile movebatur objectis, quæ ad ipsam minus pertinebant, tamquam si ad hæc sola obtorpuissent Rosæ externi sensus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010158Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010158Z">
        <seg>Another equally marvelous gift was added to this endowment, so that external objects would not distract her: when she prayed, she was not easily moved by things that had little to do with her, as if Rose's external senses had grown numb to everything except these.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[152] [in periculoso partu] Anno MDCXXXII Angela de Albildo, conjux Francisci de las Cuentas, gravida portabat utero gemellas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040438Z">
        <seg>[152] [in a dangerous childbirth] In the year 1632, Angela de Albildo, the wife of Francis de las Cuentas, was carrying twins in her womb;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[153] Hinc &amp; illa Virginei sui corpusculi rupea immobilitas, [eique diu velut immobilis inhæreret.] dum in templo, in sacello Rosarii, in domestico oratorio fundendis precibus vacabat: nam quo loco, situque, ad orandum initio se composuerat, eodem post horas, post diem integrum, post solidum cum die binoctium, reperiebatur omnino invariata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010201Z">
        <seg>[153] Hence the rock-like immobility of her own little body, [and she would long seem to cling to it as if motionless.] while she was occupied with prayer in the temple, in the chapel of the Rosary, in the domestic oratory: for whatever place and posture she had assumed at the beginning of her prayer, she was found in exactly the same position after hours, after a full day, after a solid day and night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[153] [prodigiose adjuvantur.] Anna Maria, filia viduæ Mariæ Morales, onustum fœtu mortuo gestabat uterum, ac prius sepultum, quam natum anxia circumferebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040500Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040500Z">
        <seg>[153] [They are prodigiously aided.] Anna Maria, daughter of the widow Maria Morales, carried a womb burdened with a stillborn fetus, and anxiously carried it about until it was buried rather than born.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[154] [Divina attributa contemplans,] Tres dietim horas selegerat, quas sigillatim mane, meridie, vespere solis gratiis Deo reddendis insumebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010207Z">
        <seg>Contemplating the divine attributes, he had set aside three hours each day, which he devoted separately to giving thanks to God in the morning, at noon, and in the evening.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[154] [Per similes imagines apoplexia,] Puellam Æthiopissam, Mariam nomine, in ædibus Didaci de Requena impetuosissimus apoplexiæ defluxus totam a summo deorsum pervaserat, nervis stupentibus, brachia &amp; crura immaniter palpitabant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040521Z">
        <seg>[154] [By similar images of apoplexy,] A most violent stroke of apoplexy had pervaded the entire body of an Ethiopian girl, Mary by name, in the house of Diego de Requena, from head to foot; her nerves were stunned, and her arms and legs throbbed violently.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[155] In autumno anni MDCXXX Franciscus Gutierez Magan, [arthritis cum dysenteria,] Hispalensis presbyter, gravi coxendicum dolore pressus, dum calidis emplastris nimium fidit, irritatum humorem descendere in dextrum pedem coëgit, ubi arthriticum, quo laborabat, cruciatum vehementissime succendit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040550Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040550Z">
        <seg>In the autumn of 1630, Francis Gutierrez Magan, a priest from Seville suffering from arthritis with dysentery, was afflicted with severe hip pain. While placing too much trust in hot poultices, he forced the irritated humors to descend into his right foot, where the arthritis from which he suffered intensified his torment to the utmost.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[155] [&amp; cum Deo vel de Deo semper loquens,] Insuper notatum fuit, adeo Virginem orationi adhæsisse, ut familiares sermones, quibus erga alios utebatur, simul vim orationis plerumque continerent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010220Z">
        <seg>[155] [&amp; always speaking with or about God,] Furthermore, it was noted that the Virgin was so devoted to prayer that her familiar conversations with others usually also contained the power of prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[156] Accidit, quod die quadam ab ecclesia domum rediens, [cantu aviculæ in exstasim rapitur,] ut sensit male sibi esse, pulmentum sibi ex aqua tritoque pane obiter parare constituerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010223Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010223Z">
        <seg>[156] It happened that one day, returning home from church, [rapt in ecstasy by a little bird's song,] he felt unwell and had decided to prepare for himself a porridge of water and crushed bread.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[156] Idem Franciscus patrinus erat Ludovici Cortes filioli Marianæ de Sea.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040618Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040618Z">
        <seg>The same Francis was the godfather of Mariana de Sea's little son, Louis Cortes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[157] Paradoxum foret, si Rosa quod aliis tanto conatu suadebat, [&amp; alios tum per se,] sibimet ipsi non æque serio persuasisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010229Z">
        <seg>It would be paradoxical if Rosa, who urged others on with such great effort, had not persuaded herself just as earnestly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[157] [dolores capitis,] Catharina de Vera continuis viginti diebus atrocissimos cerebri temporumque sustinebat aculeos, ut nec cibum, nec somnum valens admittere, brevi putaretur mortis faucibus immigratura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040642Z">
        <seg>[157] [Headaches,] Catherine of Vera endured for twenty consecutive days the most atrocious pains in her brain and temples, so that, unable to take either food or sleep, she was soon thought to be about to pass into the jaws of death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[158] Nec minore cura Patres confessarios, quotquot adire poterat, [tum per suos confessarios] monebat, rogabat, obsecrabat, ut suos pœnitentes omni suadela &amp; arte inducerent, ad mentalis orationis subeunda gymnasmata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010235Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010235Z">
        <seg>[158] Nor with less care did he admonish, beg, and implore the confessors, as many as he could reach [then through his own confessors], to induce their penitents by every counsel and art to undertake the exercises of mental prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[158] [aliique morbi sugantur.] Anna de Herrera, quantum olim incredulitate obstinata pugnarat adversus Rosæ miracula, tantumdem postea, explorato in semetipsa beneficio, eorumdem tuba &amp; buccinatrix fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040714Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040714Z">
        <seg>[158] [and other diseases are cured.] Anna de Herrera, as much as she had once stubbornly fought against the miracles of the Rose out of incredulity, was afterward, having experienced the benefit in herself, its trumpet and herald.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[159] Peculiariter afficiebat Rosam parthenici Rosarii devota recitario;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010238Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010238Z">
        <seg>[159] It particularly moved Rosa, a devotee of the Virgin Mary's Rosary, to recite it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[159] [Denique plurimi febricitantes] De febribus id compertum est, ad sacræ imaginis tactum, imo conspectum citius abscessisse, quam fluat cera a facie ignis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040739Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040739Z">
        <seg>[159] [Finally, the great majority of those who were feverish] It has been established concerning fevers that, at the touch of the sacred image—nay, at its mere sight—they abated more quickly than wax melts from the face of a flame.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[15] Accidit, quod coram honestis matronis Rosa, [corpus ornare] præsente genitrice, importunius rogaretur suo capiti circumdare textum e floribus sertum, quod illic forte odoris gratia in medio positum cunctis arridebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203318Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203318Z">
        <seg>[15] It happened that in the presence of honorable matrons, Rosa, with her mother present, was more importunately asked to place the wreath of flowers on her head, which by chance, for the sake of its fragrance, had been set in the middle and pleased everyone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[15] [sepulturam cadaveris iterum dilatam esse,] Favebat confirmandæ populari credulitati publicum argumentum ex constanti virginei cadaveris tum pulchritudine, tum suaveolentia miraculosa, quæ duo veluti fidejubebant, nullum fore seu deformitatis seu corruptelæ aut graveolentiæ periculum, si vel in plusculos dies sepultura differretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031106Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031106Z">
        <seg>[15] [that the burial of the body had been delayed again,] A public argument was advanced to confirm the popular credulity: the constant virgin's body, both by its beauty and its miraculous fragrance, seemed to vouch that there would be no danger of deformity, corruption, or foul odor, even if the burial were postponed for a few more days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[160] Alphonso de Yta, monasterii S. Catharinæ Senensis Limæ administratori, [ad pristinam valetudinem revocantur,] uxor erat Maria de Aspitia, quam duplici tertiana bullientem medici, extracto quinquies sanguine, aliisque prævalidis evacuationibus pene exanimarant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040810Z">
        <seg>To Alphonso de Yta, administrator of the Monastery of St. Catherine of Siena in Lima, [they are restored to their former health,] his wife was Maria de Aspitia, whom the doctors, seeing her suffer from a double tertian fever, had nearly exanimated by drawing her blood five times and by other very powerful evacuations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[160] [quin imo plantas arboresque,] Quin imo, hic orandi affectus in Rosa tantæ energiæ fuit, vel ipsas insensibiles plantas, stupida ligna, mutosque stipites, ad laudem, reverentiam, adorationem Deo exhibendam mirabiliter inclinarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010245Z">
        <seg>[160] [For indeed he planted and set up trees,] Indeed, this fervor for prayer in Rosa was of such great energy that it miraculously inclined even the most insensitive plants, the dullest wood, and the mute trunks to offer praise, reverence, and adoration to God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[161] Claudat historiam ipsa, quæ Rosam genuit, Maria de Oliva.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040828Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040828Z">
        <seg>Let Mary of Oliva, who gave birth to Rosa, close the history herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[161] [non sine prodigioso miraculo,] Accidit, quod mane quodam probatæ fidei persona se Rosæ hortum adeunti comitem adjunxerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010248Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010248Z">
        <seg>[161] [not without a prodigious miracle,] It happened that one morning a person of proven faith had joined Rose as a companion on her way to the garden;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[162] Aliud.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040900Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040900Z">
        <seg>[162] Another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[162] [excitat ad laudes divinas,] Aliud suppar, &amp; finio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010251Z">
        <seg>[162] [stirs up to divine praises,] One more thing, and I'm done.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[163] Confestim avicula tractu vocis lenissimo inchoabat argutulam cantilenam, [quas diu cum avicula] ac sensim vocalior assurgebat in epiphonema;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010254Z">
        <seg>[163] Immediately the little bird would begin a witty little ditty with the softest drawl, [which she had long had with the bird] and gradually, growing more vocal, would rise into an epiphany.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[163] [etiam fieri alia mirabilia,] Agnes (id ancillulæ nomen erat) libere se solam peregisse totum professa est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040921Z">
        <seg>[163] [and other miracles were also performed,] Agnes (for that was the little handmaid's name) freely confessed that she had done it all by herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[164] Ita in Dei laudibus alternabat Rosa &amp; avicula: [alternatim decantat.] idque non perfunctorie, sed integro labentis horæ spatio, tanta lege ac ordine, ut canente avicula, ne quidem mutiret Rosa, &amp; vicissim Rosa modulante, avicula attentissima nec pipiret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010257Z">
        <seg>Thus Rose and the little bird took turns in praising God, each singing in turn, and not perfunctorily, but for the entire span of the passing hour, with such discipline and order that when the bird was singing, Rose would not even move, and when Rose was singing, the bird, most attentive, would not chirp.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[164] [quibus auctor epilogum subnectit.] Fas sit &amp; mihi, hic desistere, dum tamen apis mellificæ (Bernardi) verbis mellifluis Rosam alloquar, Olivæ filiam, favorum matrem: “O stirps Olivæ fructifera, in domo Dei ungens &amp; lucens, fovens beneficiis, coruscans miraculis, fac nos ejus, qua frueris, lucis, suavitatisque participes.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040943Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040943Z">
        <seg>[164] [to which the author adds an epilogue.] Let it be lawful for me, too, to pause here, while I address Rose, daughter of the Olive, mother of favors, with the honeyed words of the honey-making bee (Bernard): "O fruitful stock of the Olive, anointing and shining in the house of God, fostering benefits, radiant with miracles, make us partakers of the light and sweetness of which you enjoy."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[166] Tandem ultra totius antiqui orbis extremos fines longe lateque usque ad ultimas Atlantici oceani metas, [tandem addit incolas Americæ,] &amp; usque ad extimas occidentalium Indiarum oras, quæ maris (quod Pacificum dicitur) sepiunt immensa littora, iterum atque iterum fideli servo (per quem Prædicatorum Ordo, maximo hujus S. R.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041042Z">
        <seg>[166] Finally, beyond the farthest limits of the entire ancient world, far and wide to the ultimate shores of the Atlantic Ocean, [finally adds the inhabitants of America,] and to the outermost shores of the western Indies, which the sea (called the Pacific) borders with immense coasts, again and again to the faithful servant (through whom the Order of Preachers, the greatest of this S. R.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[166] [sic Sancta internæ tribulationis igne probatur,] Jacebat sub tenebrarum immani pondere gemens Rosa, nec ad objecta supernaturalia, imo neque ad naturalia sese valebat attollere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010316Z">
        <seg>Thus the Holy One is tested by the fire of inner tribulation. The Rose lay groaning under the immense weight of darkness, unable to lift herself to supernatural things, nor even to natural ones.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[167] [ac tantis terroribus angebatur,] Verum præcipua dolorum pars erat, quod hi faciem præferebant, quasi æternum duraturi, nec finem ullum promittebat vasta miseria, nusquam patebat hujus labyrinthi exitus, videbatur quasi muro adamantino obstructum effugium: unde perplexa Rosa, non reperiebat, quo ab infernali pœna damni hanc suam infelicitatem secerneret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010322Z">
        <seg>[167] [and was tormented by such terrors,] But the chief part of her sorrows was that these seemed destined to last forever, and the vast misery promised no end; nowhere was there an exit from this labyrinth, and the escape appeared as if blocked by an adamantine wall. Hence perplexed Rosa could find no way to separate this her own misfortune from the infernal punishment of loss.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[167] [inter quos floruit S. Rosa,] Æquum igitur, &amp; summopere rationi consentaneum judicamus, ut, quam Dominus noster Jesus Christus, cujus vices in terris gerimus, singulariter in dilectissimam a se sponsam assumptam, monilibus &amp; coronis suis ornatam demonstrare dignatus est, nos quoque pro muneris nostri in universa Ecclesia debito, cui licet immerito præsidemus, ut venerandam a Deo præelectam, Sanctamque colendam apostolica auctoritate decernamus, ut a solis ortu usque ad occasum omnis lingua, &amp; omnes populi confiteantur, quod magnus Dominus in omnibus operibus, &amp; laudabilis valde in mirabilibus suis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041052Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041052Z">
        <seg>[167] [among whom St. Rose flourished,] We therefore judge it just and entirely in keeping with reason, that the one whom our Lord Jesus Christ, whose place we hold on earth, was pleased to show forth in a singular manner, taken up by Himself as His most beloved bride, adorned with His jewels and crowns, we also, for the duty of our office throughout the universal Church, which we preside over, though unworthy, by apostolic authority decree that the venerable one, chosen by God, is to be venerated as holy, so that from the rising of the sun to the setting of the same, every tongue and all peoples may confess that great is the Lord in all things, and very praiseworthy in His wonders;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[168] Limæ, quæ est Meridionalis Americæ in regno Peruano metropolis, [quæ anno christi 1586 Limæ nata ] anno a reparata salute MDLXXXVI duodecimo Kalendas Maii, parentibus honestis Gaspare de Floribus, &amp; Maria ab Oliva orta est hæc mystica Rosa in vico sancti Dominici juxta S. Spiritus ædem, &amp; in ipsa solemni Pentecostes die, quam &lt;b0&gt;Rosarum Pascha&lt;/b0&gt; dicimus,&lt;a1&gt;e&lt;/a1&gt;, sacro Baptismatis fonte purificata, ut pene videantur omnia fuisse infantis hujus futuræ sanctitatis indicia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041101Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041101Z">
        <seg>[168] In Lima, which is the metropolis of the Kingdom of Peru in South America, [who was born in Lima in the year of Christ 1586] in the year from the reparation of the salutation MDLXXXVI, on the twelfth day before the Kalends of May, this mystical Rose was born to honorable parents, Gaspar de Flores, and Mary of the Olive, in the street of Saint Dominic near S. The Spirit built a church, and on the very solemn day of Pentecost, which we call the &lt;b0&gt;Feast of Roses&lt;/b0&gt;, she was purified in the sacred font of Baptism, so that it seems almost as if all these were signs of this infant's future sanctity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[168] Per ipsos quindecim continuos annos quotidie, minimum semel, [ut alioquin ad patiendum promptissima] in hanc desolationis caliginem demergebatur pavens ac tremens Rosa, nec minus hora integra, quandoque diutius, palpitabat sub hoc agone;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010325Z">
        <seg>For fifteen consecutive years, every day at least once—though otherwise most ready to suffer—Rose was plunged into this darkness of desolation, trembling and afraid, and for a full hour, sometimes longer, she panted under this ordeal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[169] Denique tantus fuit istarum figurarum horror, [hunc amaritudinis calicem a se transferri peteret.] tanta horroris acerbitas, quod Rosa, tametsi ad quidvis sufferendum a teneris impavida, unicum hunc amaritudinis calicem a se transferri enixe petierit, tamquam suis viribus omnino superiorem, ac simpliciter intolerabilem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010328Z">
        <seg>[169] For the horror of these figures was so great, [that she would beg for this cup of bitterness to be removed from her.] so intense was the acerbity of the horror, that Rosa, though from her tenderest years impassive to anything, earnestly begged that this single cup of bitterness might be removed from her, as though it were altogether beyond her strength and simply intolerable.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[169] Infans sæpe visa est humi jacens fixis ocellis jucunde cælum intueri, [teneram suam ætatem] quasi jam peregrina in terris, in civem cælestis Hierusalem eloquenti silentio, quo ex tunc prædita erat, adscribi postularet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041107Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041107Z">
        <seg>The infant was often seen lying on the ground, gazing sweetly at the sky with fixed eyes, as if already a stranger on earth, demanding to be enrolled as a citizen of the heavenly Jerusalem by the eloquent silence with which she had been endowed from that time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[16] Aliud.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000837Z">
        <seg>[16] Another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[16] [&amp; hac spe domum discedit.] Porro mirifica cælestis odoris, qui a sacro corpusculo spirabat, fragrantia, ne quidem cessavit in sepulchro;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031131Z">
        <seg>[16] [And with this hope he departs home.] Moreover, the marvelous fragrance of the heavenly scent that emanated from the sacred little body did not even cease in the tomb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[170] Quapropter jejunium adhuc parvula sic servavit, ut omni se fructuum esu interdiceret, quos sibi donatos mox aliis largiebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041126Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041126Z">
        <seg>Therefore, the little girl still kept the fast by forbidding herself to eat any of the fruits that had been given to her, which she would soon give away to others.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[170] [Talis erat hæc mentis afflictio,] Tam abstrusa, monstrifica, ignota erat harum desolationum figura &amp; natura, ut vix demum reperti fuerint theologi perspicaces &amp; gnari, qui de illarum crasi&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; quidpiam certi præsumerent diffinire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010331Z">
        <seg>[170] [Such was this affliction of the mind,] So abstruse, monstrous, and unknown was the form and nature of these desolations, that scarcely have any perceptive and learned theologians been found who would presume to define anything certain about their crasi&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[171] [prompta obedientia,] Ut loqueretur victorias, mirabilis fuit in Rosa obedientia, tum in sequendis divini Sponsi tractibus, quibus eam per arcanas sanctitatis semitas in unguentorum suorum currentem odorem trahebat, tum in duræ &amp; rudis matris præceptis adimplendis, quibus eam ex adverso ad sæculi nugas, &amp; vanos corporis ornatus impellebat: in tanta enim oppositorum mandatorum diversitate, arte excelluit plusquam humana non aberrando, neque ad dexteram, neque ad sinistram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041151Z">
        <seg>[171] [prompt obedience,] In speaking of her victories, she was marvelous in obedience to the Rose, both in following the divine Spouse's ways, by which He drew her through the secret paths of holiness by the flowing scent of His ointments, and in fulfilling the precepts of her harsh and uncouth mother, which urged her from the opposite direction toward the vanities of the world and the vain adornments of the body: For in such a diversity of opposing commands, she excelled with a skill beyond human, neither straying to the right nor to the left.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[171] [ut quidam putarent, esse morbum corporis,] Unum supererat exile solatium, quod mater, sicut auxiliari non poterat, ita hos Filiæ terribiles agones penitus ignorabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010334Z">
        <seg>[171] [as some might think, it was a bodily illness,] One meager consolation remained: that the mother, just as she could not help, was also completely unaware of her daughter's terrible struggles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[172] [quem ipsa explicare non posset,] Nitebatur subinde Rosa, ubi de hac suæ desolationis fornace fidentius loquendum erat, adinvenire similitudines, quibus utcumque rem adumbraret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010344Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010344Z">
        <seg>[172] [which she herself could not explain,] Rosa would then strive, whenever it was necessary to speak more confidently of this furnace of her desolation, to find similitudes by which she might in some way shadow it forth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[172] [singulari veneratione erga parentes,] Sciens etiam Deo, parentibus, &amp; magistris numquam reddi æquale, eximia prosequuta est genitores reverentia, pietate, &amp; charitate.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041210Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041210Z">
        <seg>[172] [With singular reverence toward her parents,] Knowing that she could never be equal to God, her parents, and her teachers, she treated her parents with exceptional reverence, piety, and charity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[173] Fuit, qui putarit Virginem deduci a Deo per scrupeas vias S. Antonii eremitæ, [&amp; alii illam, sicut S. Antonium eremitam,] quem inferni monstra formis terrificis agitarunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010347Z">
        <seg>[173] There was one who thought the Virgin was led by God through the scarped ways of St. Anthony the Hermit, [and others, like St. Anthony the Hermit,] whom the monsters of hell agitated with terrifying forms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[173] [sincero sui ipsius contemptu,] Humilis Virgo sibi videbatur nil agere, aut pati in Sponsi famulatu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041228Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041228Z">
        <seg>[173] [With sincere self-contempt,] the Humble Virgin seemed to herself to be doing nothing, or suffering anything, in her service to the Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[174] Bis Virgo compulsa fuit edicere alteram hujus scenæ partem, [visibiliter a dæmonibus terreri suspicarentur.] videlicet, qualis sibi soleret post hujusmodi nubila illucescere Phœbus: fieri enim non posse, quin Sponsus ille dulcissimus, post tam diram ac tenebricosam eclipsim, insigniter recreet, ac reficiat animam, tot doloribus humiliatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010356Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010356Z">
        <seg>[174] The Virgin was twice compelled to utter the other part of this scene, [that they might be visibly terrified by demons.] namely, what sort of Phoebus was wont to shine upon her after such clouds: for it could not be otherwise, but that that most sweet Spouse, after so dire and gloomy an eclipse, would notably refresh and restore the soul, so humbled by so many sorrows.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[174] Ducebatur inter hæc virtutum exercitia a Sponso tam ad exteriorem corporis, [aliisque virtutibus illustravit,] quam ad intimam spiritus solitudinem, ut ibi loqueretur ad cor ejus, obsistentibus ex adverso importunis genitricis conatibus, quibus eam ad connubia terræ parare satagebat, ad quæ non solum verbis durioribus, sed etiam verberibus impellebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041302Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041302Z">
        <seg>[174] She was led amid these exercises of virtue by the Bridegroom, both to the outward discipline of the body and to the inner solitude of the spirit, that there she might speak to his heart, while the importunate efforts of her mother strove on the contrary to prepare her for the marriages of the earth, to which she was urged not only by harsher words, but also by beatings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[175] Tandem obtinuit vigesimo ætatis anno, ut, consentiente matre, [ac deinde tertium Ordinem S. Dominici,] cooparetur palam in filiam S. Dominici, assumpto S. Catharinæ Senensis habitu, quo candent Sorores tertii Ordinis vulgo de Pœnitentia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041315Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041315Z">
        <seg>Finally, at the age of twenty, with her mother's consent, she was publicly received into the daughter of St. Dominic, taking the habit of St. Catherine of Siena, by which the Sisters of the Third Order are commonly known as the Penitents.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[176] [duo ex præcipuis] Doctor Joannes de Castillo, professione medicus, statu quidem secularis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010415Z">
        <seg>[176] [two of the principal ones] Doctor John de Castillo, a physician by profession, and indeed a secular;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[176] [prius superatis quibusdam tum externis] Verum nec adhuc integra pax;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041327Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041327Z">
        <seg>[176] [after having first overcome certain external foes] But peace is not yet complete.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[177] Pater magister F. Joannes de Lorenzana Ordinis prædicatorum (testante eodem illustrissimo præsule) eumdem cum D. Castillo, [actiones S. Rosæ examinarunt,] aut saltem non inferiorem obtinebat sanctimoniæ, publicæque opinionis gradum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010424Z">
        <seg>Father Master Fr. John of Lorenzana of the Order of Preachers (as attested by the same most illustrious prelate) held him to be on a par with Don Castillo, [examined the actions of St. Rose,] or at least not inferior in the degree of sanctity and public opinion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[177] [tum internis impedimentis, amplexa est.] His omnibus superatis, eo gravior, quo intimior adhuc difficultas emersit: importune enim se ingerebat in humillima sui cognitione molesta cogitatio, quod ipsa peccatis sordida illo niveo, &amp; sacro amictu nimis esset indigna;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041337Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041337Z">
        <seg>[177] [Then, overcome by internal impediments, she embraced them.] Having overcome all these, a still more serious difficulty arose, the more serious because it was more intimate: for an importunate and troublesome thought kept intruding upon her humblest self-knowledge, that she herself, defiled by sins, was altogether unworthy of that snowy and sacred vestment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[178] Modo ad rem pressius.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010431Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010431Z">
        <seg>[178] Now, to the matter more closely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[179] His igitur, &amp; eo virginali candore nitens, [Christo de sponsata] ut numquam nec venialis impuritatis macula eam aspersam fuisse, omnes ejus a confessionibus jurati testes probaverint;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041507Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041507Z">
        <seg>Therefore, with this and that virginal purity shining forth, [to Christ concerning the betrothed] that she had never been stained even by a venial spot of impurity, all her witnesses sworn by the confessors have attested.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[179] [interrogavit eam de modo contemplationis,] Instabat doctor, an non suæ imaginativæ vim aliquam adhiberet, dum reliquæ internæ potentiæ sic inhærebant sapori Dei, an, &amp; quo, quantove connixu, labore, fulcro, in hac suspensione dulcissima se sustineret?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010440Z">
        <seg>[179] [He questioned her about the manner of contemplation,] The doctor pressed her, whether she was not applying some force of her own imagination while her other interior faculties thus clung to the savor of God, and whether, and by what, and with what effort, labor, or support, she sustained herself in this most sweet suspension?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[17] [Sed interea clam tumulatum fuerat,] Verum non diu stetit unda populi, quin post meridiem, velut ex induciis ad templum copiosior afflueret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031202Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031202Z">
        <seg>[17] [But in the meantime a secret burial had taken place,] Yet the crowd did not remain long; by the afternoon, as if by agreement, it poured more numerous toward the temple.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[17] [filia subtiliter excogitavit modum,] Verumtamen induciæ fuerunt istæ, non sincera pax: nam mater ad alias artes brevi conversa, nunc tentare Filiam, ut puellari usu se comeret, ut collo monilia, brachiis armillas, vultu cerussam pro more regionis admitteret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000841Z">
        <seg>[17] [The daughter had subtly devised a plan.] Nevertheless, these were only a truce, not a sincere peace: for the mother, soon resorting to other arts, now tried to persuade her daughter to adopt a girlish style, to wear necklaces around her neck, bracelets on her arms, and ceruse on her face according to the custom of the region.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[180] [crudeliter corpus suum] His donis aucta, &amp; charismatibus cumulata ferventius Virago ista accinxit adhuc arctius fortitudine lumbos suos, &amp; roboravit brachium suum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041519Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041519Z">
        <seg>[180] [cruelly her own body] Enriched with these gifts, and endowed with charisms, the fierce maiden girded herself more fervently, tightening still more tightly her loins with strength, and strengthened her arm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[180] [de oratione unionis,] Tunc Castillus, ut erat in hac schola versatissimus, aggressus est propriis notionibus explicare Virgini secretam hujus excelsæ illustrationis hieraticam&lt;a0&gt;e&lt;/a0&gt;, nempe asceticæ disciplinæ magistris hoc genus contemplandi ORATIONEM UNIONIS dici: hic intellectum formari specie, non adquisita, sed superne infusa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010443Z">
        <seg>[180] [On the Prayer of Union,] Then Castille, being most versed in this school, undertook to explain to the Virgin the secret of this exalted hieratic illumination, namely that among the masters of ascetic discipline this mode of contemplation is called the Prayer of Union: here the intellect is formed by a kind of vision, not acquired, but poured down from above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[181] Perrexit doctor, &amp; studiose in viam purgativam resiliens quæsivit, [&amp; gradibus, quibus ad hanc ascenderat.] quam diu, quibus industriis, quanto sudore pugnandum sibi fuerit in pravas animi inclinationes, in vitiorum armata latibula, in morbos indomitarum effræniumque passionum?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010446Z">
        <seg>The doctor went on, and, eagerly reverting to the purgative path, he inquired [and by the steps by which he had ascended to it] how long, by what labors, with what sweat he had been compelled to fight against the perverse inclinations of the mind, against the armed ambushes of vices, against the diseases of untamed and unbridled passions?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[181] [ac singula hujus membra cruciabat,] Nequa vero pars innocentis corpusculi vacaret a supplicio, pœnalibus vinculis brachia, &amp; lacertos torquebat, urticarumque manipulis, ac minutulis sentibus pectus, axillas, ac latera arctabat, ut suo lilio inter spinas constituto omnino configuraretur hæc Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041529Z">
        <seg>[181] [and tormented each of its members,] So that no part of the innocent little body should be free from punishment, he twisted the arms and sinews with penal bonds, and pressed the breast, armpits, and sides with handfuls of nettles and tiny thorns, so that this Rose, placed like a lily among thorns, might be completely conformed to it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[182] Verum ab iis opportuna digressione incidit in horrendas illas visiones, [Inde dilapsus ad memoratos animi angores,] figuras, pavores, de quibus capite superiori actum est, magnisque precibus exoravit doctorem, ut sibi illius desolationis ac supplicii naturam, originem, critica, propriamque interpretationem docte elucidaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010452Z">
        <seg>[182] But from them he opportunely digressed into those horrifying visions, [Then, having lapsed back to the aforementioned torments of the mind,] figures, and terrors, which were dealt with in the preceding chapter, and with great entreaties he implored the doctor to eruditely elucidate for him the nature, origin, critique, and proper interpretation of that desolation and punishment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[182] [&amp; orationis] Neque enim videbatur ei seraphicæ magistræ plene induisse habitum, nisi &amp; spineam illius assumpsisset coronam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041541Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041541Z">
        <seg>For it did not seem to her that she had fully assumed the habit of the seraphic mistress unless she had also taken up her crown of thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[183] [jussit illam revelare divinas illuminationes,] His cursim explicatis, vicissim rogavit Virginem doctor, post illas inferni tenebras &amp; horrores qualiter sibi fuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010459Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010459Z">
        <seg>[183] [He commanded her to reveal the divine illuminations,] Having briefly explained these, the doctor in turn asked the Virgin how she had fared after those infernal darkness and horrors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[183] Æmulata sanctorum anachoretarum secessus, qui in eremi solitudine Christum Dominum receperunt, [ac solitudinis amans,] adhuc infans solitarios domi angulos quærebat, puellarum nugas fugiens, ut sola cum Deo suo delitesceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041557Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041557Z">
        <seg>[183] Emulating the retreats of the holy anchorites, who in the solitude of the desert received Christ the Lord, [and a lover of solitude,] she, still an infant, sought lonely corners at home, shunning the frivolities of girls, so that she might hide alone with her God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[184] Sub specie stellæ, probatæ perfectionis matronæ in visu apparuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041616Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041616Z">
        <seg>In the likeness of a star, the proven perfection of the matron appeared in a vision.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[184] [ac cælestes visiones,] Jussa ulterius dicere, novo pudore hæsit, rubuit, titubavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010502Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010502Z">
        <seg>[184] [and heavenly visions,] When ordered to say more, she was seized with a new modesty, blushed, and faltered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[185] Unde subintulit Castillus, imaginarias esse visiones;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010505Z">
        <seg>[185] Hence Castille concluded that the visions were imaginary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[185] [spiritualia dona Christo nascenti offerebat.] Adventante hujus sui dilecti in Bethleem natali, cum eum parvulum &amp; nudum cum inope matre contemplaretur, piorum operum singulari ac pretiosa consutura dignas Amore infantulo vestes pluries præparabat: indusioli enim, panniculorum, temporis, quo delituerat, stragulæ, fasciæ, fimbriarum, &amp; limborum, ac crepundiorum loco substituerat rosaria, stationes ad sanctissimam Eucharistiam, jejunia, coronas Domini, orationes Dominicas, Salutationes angelicas, symboli apostolici recitationes, &amp; plurium aliarum precum, flagellationes, lachrymas, interventus Missarum, &amp; sanctissimæ Communionis sumptiones, quæ omnia frequentissime repetebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041644Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041644Z">
        <seg>[185] [offered spiritual gifts to Christ being born.] When her beloved was to be born in Bethlehem, as she contemplated him, a little child and naked with a poor mother, she repeatedly prepared worthy garments for the infant Love, with a singular and precious stitching of pious works: for undergarments, swaddling cloths, a cloth for the time when he lay wrapped, bandages, fringes, and borders, and in place of cradle-beds, she had substituted rosaries, stations to the most holy Eucharist, fasts, crowns for the Lord, Our Father prayers, Hail Marys, recitations of the Apostles' Creed, and many other prayers, scourges, tears, Masses offered, and Communions of the most holy Eucharist, all of which she repeated most frequently.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[186] Videbat sibi doctor satisfactum in eo, quod maxime scrutabatur, [&amp; has omnes a Deo provenisse judicat,] haud ignarus, in materia unionis quo plura dicuntur, minus dici.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010511Z">
        <seg>[186] The doctor saw that he had been satisfied in what he was most earnestly investigating, [and judges that all of these came from God,] not unaware that, in the matter of union, the more that is said, the less is said.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[186] [Cum in contemplatione] Habitavit igitur hæc amantissima Domini confidenter, &amp; quasi in thalamo tota die morabatur in eo: edocta enim in ipsa infantia interius ab ipso ferventer orare, etiam dormiens deprehensa fuit ore diurnas proferre preces, &amp; cor ejus in oratione vigilare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041659Z">
        <seg>[186] [In contemplation] She therefore, the most loving of the Lord, lived confidently and, as it were, dwelt in his chamber all day long. For she had been taught in her very infancy by him to pray fervently within; even when sleeping she was found to utter daily prayers with her mouth, and her heart was watchful in prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[187] Idem Patri magistro de Lorenzana sensus fuit, [cui judicio consenserunt Joannes de Lorenzana] postquam crebriori &amp; per partes interciso examine spiritum Virginis diligentissime exploravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010514Z">
        <seg>[187] He held the same view of Father Lorenzana's master, [whose judgment John of Lorenzana agreed with], after he had most diligently probed the Virgin's spirit through a more frequent and piecemeal examination.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[187] [per plures horas defixa hæreret,] Per horas immota ante aras, nec prætereuntes vidit, nec ad repentinos strepitus animum deflexit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041709Z">
        <seg>[187] [She stood rooted to the spot for many hours,] For hours she remained motionless before the altars; she neither saw those who passed by nor turned her attention to sudden noises.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[188] Prope obitum per Quadragesimam cum avicula mire canora, [ac prodigiosa quædam patraret,] quæ sole occiduo ante suum cubiculum advolabat, alternavit Rosa cantica &amp; laudes Dei quotidie per horam integram tanto ordine, ut canente avicula, Virgo sileret, &amp;, Virgine modulante, avicula attentissima&lt;a0&gt;*&lt;/a0&gt; nec pipiret, quin &amp; ipsas insensibiles plantas exemplo prorsus singulari ad laudes &amp; ad orationem Dei, eo versiculo prolato, &lt;b1&gt;Benedicite universa germinantia in terra Domino&lt;/b1&gt;, invitavit, &amp; visibiliter sic inclinavit, ut arborum summitates terram attingerent, ac veluti solemni venerationis ritu Creatorem suum adorarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041733Z">
        <seg>[188] Near her death during Lent, with a wonderfully melodious little bird, [and performing certain prodigies,] which would fly to her cell at sunset, Rosa the Canticle and the praises of God were alternated daily for a full hour with such order that, when the bird sang, the Virgin would be silent, and, when the Virgin sang, the bird would be most attentive&lt;a0&gt;*&lt;/a0&gt; nor would it chirp, but even invited the very insensitive plants by a wholly unique example to the praises and prayer of God, by uttering that verse, &lt;b1&gt;Bless the Lord, all you that germinate upon the earth&lt;/b1&gt;, and visibly inclined them so that the tops of the trees touched the ground, as if in a solemn rite of veneration, adoring their Creator.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[188] [&amp; Ludovicus de Bilbao Dominicani,] Lorenzanæ suppar magister, &amp; primarius cathedraticus F. Ludovicus de Bilbao, notavit eadem in Rosa, dum ejus confessiones excipiebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010517Z">
        <seg>[188] [&amp; Louis de Bilbao, a Dominican,] the master of the studium at Loreto, and the first chair, Father Louis de Bilbao, noted the same in the Rose while he was taking its confessions;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[189] Ut probaretur, an spiritus ejus ex Deo esset, [spiritus ejus seu methodus vivendi] subtili virorum, tum doctrina, tum pietate &amp; spirituum discretione excellentium, examini pluries subjecta fuit, ex quo claruit, ejus lumina &amp; dona divina fuisse, &amp; ab ipso rationis diluculo nihil ei dulcius occurrisse, quam de Deo cogitare, &amp; supernis ejus beneplacitis inhiare;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041740Z">
        <seg>[189] To prove whether her spirit was from God, [her spirit or method of living] was subjected to a subtle examination by men, it was repeatedly subjected to examination by men, both learned and pious, and distinguished by spiritual discernment, from which it became clear that its lights and gifts were divine, and that from the very dawn of reason nothing had been sweeter to it than to think of God and to long for His heavenly good pleasure;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[189] [qui sanctitatem Virginis semper magni fecerunt.] Præcipue vero Castillus &amp; Lorenzana, de Rosæ viis perfectissimis aliquoties mutua collatione instituta, concordes mirabantur hæc duo singularissima: primum, quod brevissimo compendio, &amp; quasi transsultu, gradum illuminativum, mox &amp; unitivum possederat, absque eo, quod gradum purgativum vix obiter attigisset, quia nimirum ab infantia benedictionibus dulcedinis præventam, ii semper comitati erant purissimi affectus, in quibus nil terrenum sorduerat operosius depurgandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010520Z">
        <seg>[189] [who always held the Virgin's sanctity in high esteem.] Especially Castillon and Lorenzana, having on several occasions, through mutual consultation, established the most perfect ways of the Rose, marveled in unison at these two most singular things: first, that by the briefest compendium, and as it were by a single leap, she had possessed the illuminative, and soon the unitive, degree, without having scarcely touched the purgative degree in passing, because, indeed, from infancy having been preempted by the blessings of sweetness, she had always been accompanied by the purest affections, in which nothing earthly had ever soiled itself as to require more diligent purging.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[18] Augebatur quotidianis incrementis devota populi frequentia circa sepulchrum ob eximia, [&amp; exsequiæ tranquillius repetitæ sunt] quæ palam inde crebrescebant, beneficia curationum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031215Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031215Z">
        <seg>[18] The devotion of the people around the sepulchre was increased by daily increments on account of the extraordinary, [&amp; the funeral rites were more tranquilly repeated] which openly multiplied from there, benefits of healing;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[18] [quo matri obediret,] Visum hoc matri opportunum compendium, quidquid vellet, extorquendi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000844Z">
        <seg>[18] [so that he would obey his mother,] She saw this as a convenient way to extort from him whatever she wanted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[190] Tum ex obedientia aperuit, quod certamen forte dedit illi Deus, [per viros peritos examinatur,] ut vinceret, &amp; sciret, quod potentior omnium est sapientia: horrendas enim visiones, pavores, &amp; spiritus derelictiones, quas per quindecim annos summa constantia pertulit, enarravit, quæ veluti pœnales animarum in purgatorio lugentium dilationes, &amp; quidem ipsius inferni typus videbantur, cum nulla ei spes evadendi affulgebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041801Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041801Z">
        <seg>[190] Then, out of obedience, he revealed the contest that God had perhaps given him, [he is examined by learned men,] so that he might win and know that wisdom is mightier than all: For he recounted the horrifying visions, terrors, and spirit desertions that he had endured with the utmost constancy for fifteen years, which seemed like the penal postponements of souls in purgatory, and indeed a type of hell itself, since no hope of escape shone for him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[191] Spiritu Dei agere, &amp; agi Rosam, ut Dei filiam, [&amp; ab omnibus approbatur.] fuit constans opinio omnium, qui tunc Limæ perfectionis &amp; sanctimoniæ fama celebres erant, eamque dono sapientiæ abundare, &amp; scientia cælitus infusa gubernari judicarunt: difficillimas enim de arcano sanctissimæ Trinitatis mysterio, hypostatica Verbi divini unione, sanctissimo Altaris sacramento, profundo prædestinationis abysso, natura gratiæ, ceterasque theologicas quæstiones sacrarum Litterarum professoribus proponentibus, tam sublimibus conceptibus, profundis sententiis, &amp; verbis adeo propriis explicavit, ut laudarent omnes cum Servatore nostro Patrem omnipotentem, Dominum cæli &amp; terræ, quod tanta, sapientibus ac prudentibus abscondita, humillimis parvulis &amp; indoctis revelaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041817Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041817Z">
        <seg>[191] It was the constant opinion of all who at that time were celebrated in Lima for perfection and holiness that Rosa, as a daughter of God, acted and was acted upon by the Spirit of God, and that they judged her to abound in the gift of wisdom and to be governed by knowledge poured out from heaven: for she explained the most difficult questions concerning the secret of the most holy Trinity, the hypostatic union of the divine Word, the most holy sacrament of the Altar, the profound abyss of predestination, the nature of grace, and to other theological questions proposed by professors of the sacred Scriptures, she explained them with such sublime concepts, profound sentences, and words so proper, that all praised the Almighty Father, the Lord of heaven and earth, with our Savior, because she revealed to the humblest little ones and the uneducated so many things hidden from the wise and prudent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[191] [manibus operanti,] Familiarius est, quod sequitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010536Z">
        <seg>[191] [working with the hands,] What follows is more familiar.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[192] [&amp; sæpius ægrotanti] Accidit, Rosam infirmari ex dolore gutturis, &amp; en totus ille Desiderabilis, cujus guttur suavissimum prædicatur Cant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010542Z">
        <seg>[192] [&amp; often ailing] It happened that Rosa fell ill from a sore throat, and behold that entire Desirable One, whose throat is proclaimed the sweetest in Cant.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[193] Illi jam languenti, &amp; viribus deficienti, omnique humano auxilio destitutæ occurrit idem ipse, [Christus,] qui est adjutor in opportunitatibus &amp; tribulatione, &amp; pretioso vulnerati lateris myrothecio eam, benigne applicatam ad hauriendam aquam salutis de fontibus Salvatoris, illico ægram sanavit&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041922Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041922Z">
        <seg>[193] To her, already languishing, failing in strength, and destitute of all human aid, the same [Christ] came—He who is a helper in times of need and tribulation—and with the precious ointment from the wounded side of the Myrrhbearer, kindly applied to draw the water of salvation from the fountains of the Savior, He immediately healed the sick woman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[193] [Jesus sub varia forma] Alia vice, cum diutius ac fere ad mediam usque noctem in solitaria horti cellula substitisset Rosa, invasit fatigatam subitaneus languor ac virium defectio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010548Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010548Z">
        <seg>[193] [Jesus in various forms] On another occasion, when Rosa had been standing for a long time—almost until midnight—in the solitary cell of the garden, a sudden languor and exhaustion seized her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[194] Adstitit etiam sæpe Regina cæli a dexteris ejus, [Deipara,] ita ut ab undecimo ætatis suæ anno usque ad extremum non defuerit ei hoc pretiosum familiaritatis contubernium: in Rosariano enim sacello ex sedulo ministerio mutua cum illa erat ei conversatio, eoque honore hanc suam Famulam dignata est, ut cubiculariæ ipsi exhibuerit ministeria excitando eam illis vocibus: “Surge ad orationem, Filia, surge, nam instat hora”: illique experrectæ se præbebat videndam facie ad faciem, majestate decoram, &amp; gloriæ cælestis paradisum, exclamanti cum altera Elisabeth: “Unde hoc mihi, ut veniat mater Domini mei ad me”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041935Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041935Z">
        <seg>[194] The Queen of Heaven also often stood at her right hand, [The Mother of God] so that from the age of eleven until her final breath she never lacked this precious companionship of familiarity: for in the little chapel of the Rosary, through diligent ministry, she had mutual conversation with her, and with this honor she dignified her handmaid, that she might perform for her the duties of a chambermaid by awakening her with those words: "Arise to pray, Daughter, arise, for the hour is at hand": and upon waking, she would present herself to be seen face to face, adorned with majesty and a paradise of heavenly glory, causing the other Elizabeth to exclaim: "How is it that the mother of my Lord comes to me?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[194] In domo cujusdam illustris feminæ, Rosa de more post sancta colloquia veniam petiit secedendi tantisper ad orationem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010555Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010555Z">
        <seg>In the house of a certain illustrious lady, Rosa, as was her custom after holy conversations, asked permission to withdraw for a while to pray.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[195] Zelus, ut intensi amoris præco simul &amp; explorator est, [apparuisse dicitur.] ita vel ipsummet Sponsum cælestem curis sollicitat, rivalem non patitur, ne flosculum quidem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010604Z">
        <seg>Zeal, as the herald and scout of intense love, is said to have appeared, and so it even troubles the heavenly Bridegroom himself with its cares, allowing no rival, not even a tiny blossom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[195] [angeli] Misit etiam Dominus angelum suum, qui præcederet Rosam, &amp; visibiliter custodiret, ac introduceret eam in locum, quem illi paraverat: fuit enim tam domestica fiducia hujus Virginis cum eo, ut habuerit illum non solum ut amicissimum, sed etiam ut pararium, aut veredarium, quotiescumque hora consueta Sponsus non apparebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041938Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041938Z">
        <seg>[195] [Angels] The Lord also sent his angel, who would precede Rosa, visibly guard her, and lead her into the place he had prepared for her: for this Virgin had such a domestic confidence with him that she had him not only as a most dear friend, but also as a servant or messenger, whenever at the usual hour the Bridegroom did not appear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[196] [&amp; S. Catharina Senensis apparuerunt.] Sæpe etiam, ex quo fuit ei a Christo Domino data in magistram sancta Catharina Senensis, hæc eam visibiliter conveniebat in terris, præsertim cum ejus regulam scrutaretur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041950Z">
        <seg>[196] [&amp; St. Catherine of Siena appeared.] Often also, since the Lord Christ had given her St. Catherine of Siena as a teacher, she would visibly meet her on earth, especially when she was examining her rule;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[196] [Etiam cum Deipara,] Porro quam affabili, serena, frequenti apparitione Rosam suam dignata fuerit augustissima cælorum Imperatrix, sat inde conjicere fas erit, quod ab undecimo suæ ætatis anno ad extremum usque non defuerit umquam Virgini hoc pretiosum assiduæ familiaritatis solatium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010610Z">
        <seg>[196] [Even with the Mother of God,] Furthermore, from the very fact that the most august Empress of heaven was so gracious, serene, and frequent in her appearances to her Rose, it is permissible to infer that from the age of eleven until her final breath she never failed to grant the Virgin this precious comfort of constant familiarity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[197] [Etiamsi Deus illam] Probavit Pater cælestis inter has spiritualis vitæ delicias hanc suam Electam, ut aurum in fornace, igne tentationum, afflictionum, persecutionum, &amp; plurium ac gravium infirmitatum: luctabatur enim quam frequentissime cum dæmonibus, multisque illorum molestiis vexabatur, ex quibus sub tetra specie molossi unus eam orantem latratu horrisono terrere, &amp; rabido morsu per solum illam raptans tentavit discerpere, donec clamavit ad Sponsum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041957Z">
        <seg>[197] [Even though God tested her] The heavenly Father proved this His Electa among these delights of the spiritual life, like gold in a furnace, by the fire of temptations, afflictions, persecutions, and many grave infirmities: for she often wrestled with demons and was vexed by many of their torments, among which, under the hideous form of a molossus, one of them tried to terrify her while she was praying with a horrid bark, and to tear her apart by dragging her along the ground with a rabid bite, until she cried out to her Spouse;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[197] [quæ illam e somno] Conabatur obedire infirma, sed prævalente serotinarum vigiliarum assuetudine, non obediebat somnus, seu veniendum, seu abeundum esset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010613Z">
        <seg>[197] [which roused her from sleep] The frail one tried to obey, but, overcome by her habit of late-night vigils, sleep would not obey, whether she came or went.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[198] [a dæmonibus vexari,] Sed invictus Virginis animus insaniam hujus furentis hostis pluribus horis frustra certantis repressit &amp; irrisit, donec victoriæ desperabundus evasit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042009Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042009Z">
        <seg>[198] [to be vexed by demons,] But the invincible spirit of the Virgin repressed and mocked the madness of this raging foe, who had contended in vain for many hours, until, despairing of victory, he withdrew.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[198] [ad orationem excitabat,] Semel evenit, quod veniente Domina, profundius dormiebat Rosa: primus hic somnus erat, ac nonnisi paulo ante cœperat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010616Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010616Z">
        <seg>[198] [She was rousing her for the oration,] Once, when the Lady arrived, Rosa was sleeping more deeply: it was her first sleep, and she had only just begun it a little while before.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[199] Ex quo semel a Christo (ut compertum postea) Catharina Senensis cælitus Rosæ missa, [&amp; S. Catharina Senensi amice conversabatur.] ac data fuit magistra, frequenter hæc charam Discipulam visibiliter convenit in terris, præsertim dum occupabatur in perlegenda seraphicæ magistræ regula, quam sibi e Cusco (procurante Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010620Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010620Z">
        <seg>[199] From the time that Catherine of Siena was once sent by Christ from heaven to Rosa (as was later learned), and was made her teacher, she frequently met this dear Disciple visibly on earth, especially while she was occupied with reading the rule of the seraphic teacher, which had been sent to her from Cusco (by the procurator Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[199] In ærumnis ab infantia fuit: nono enim mense ætatis suæ lacte destituta est, [variisque adversitatibus] cujus defectus nonnisi cibo valde inepto compensari potuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042022Z">
        <seg>She had been in hardship since infancy: for in the ninth month of her life she was deprived of milk, and the various adversities that followed could only be compensated by a very unsuitable diet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[19] Adfuere igitur ambo die præfixa cum universo tum ecclesiastico tum sæculari magistratu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031230Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031230Z">
        <seg>[19] Both were therefore present on the appointed day, together with the entire ecclesiastical and secular magistracy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[19] Excogitavit tandem ingeniosa Virgo rationem idoneam, [ac Deum non offenderet.] qua deinceps &amp; matris substaret imperio, &amp; nihilominus reliquam de corporis sui cultu vexam redimeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000847Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000847Z">
        <seg>[19] The ingenious Virgin at last devised a suitable plan [so as not to offend God], by which she would henceforth both submit to her mother's authority and nevertheless free the rest of her person from vexation in its adornment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[200] In Canta ex dira nervorum attractione per triennium lecto decubuit cruciatu intenso, [&amp; morbis affligi permitteret,] gemitu nullo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042043Z">
        <seg>In Canta, she lay in bed for three years, suffering intense torment from a dreadful nerve traction, [and would allow herself to be afflicted by diseases,] without a single groan.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[200] [Sancta tam familiariter utebatur obsequio angelorum,] &lt;b0&gt;Sanctos&lt;/b0&gt; angelos esse ipsissimum cor Dei, sensit Bernardus serm.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010632Z">
        <seg>[200] [The Holy One made such familiar use of the service of the angels,] &lt;b0&gt;the Holy&lt;/b0&gt; angels are the very heart of God, Bernard, sermon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[201] Languens &amp; æstuans divini amoris ignibus, in nocte orans visa fuit ab alia virgine magnis luminibus coruscans, [tamen suscitabat in se] veluti igniculis per os oculosque evibratis, protestantibus, quantum incendium intus ferveret, quod alias pluries, ea inscia, coram diversis contigisse innotuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042102Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042102Z">
        <seg>Languishing and burning with the fires of divine love, she was seen in prayer at night by another virgin, who was radiant with great lights; yet she herself kindled in her, as if sparks were shot from her mouth and eyes, proclaiming how great a fire was burning within, which she noted had on other occasions, without her knowledge, appeared before various people.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[201] [ut animi deliquium passa,] Nocte quadam occlusis horti januis, Rosa de more vigilabat in angusta sui eremi cellula, sensitque se viribus repente destitui, adeo ut gravem timeret syncopen: unde in domum sese recipere ad matrem constituit, collato prius consilio cum angelo suo tutelari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010651Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010651Z">
        <seg>[201] [As if she had suffered a fainting spell,] One night, with the garden gates locked, Rosa, as was her custom, was awake in the narrow cell of her hermitage, and she felt herself suddenly deprived of her strength, so much so that she feared a serious fainting spell. Therefore, she decided to return to her mother's house, after first consulting her guardian angel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[202] [aliisque, ignem divini amoris,] Obsignavit hanc Dei amoris sollicitudinem Dilectæ suæ ipse Christus Dominus hoc insigni miraculo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042127Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042127Z">
        <seg>[202] [and others, the fire of divine love,] Christ the Lord himself sealed this concern for God's love for His Beloved with this remarkable miracle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[202] [per eos potum sibi] Filia, ne sui causa is sumptus fieret, obnixe rogavit matrem, affirmans, sibi non defuturam chocolatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010657Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010657Z">
        <seg>[202] [through them to get herself a drink] The daughter earnestly begged her mother not to incur the expense for her own sake, assuring her that she would not run out of chocolate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[203] Hic Mater confusa multiplici prodigio, sciscitari, [opportunum acceperit,] quid Gundisalvi uxorem moverit, ut tali profundæ noctis hora vernam ædibus emitteret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010700Z">
        <seg>Here the mother, bewildered by the manifold prodigy, inquired, [deeming it opportune,] what had moved Gundisalvus's wife to send a springtime gift to the household at such a late hour of the night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[203] [quo præcipue ardebat,] Nondum pueriles annos excesserat, quando eam confessarii, spiritu jam grandem agnoscentes, manducandi frequenter Panem illum angelorum, qui præbet delicias regibus, facultatem dedere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042149Z">
        <seg>[203] [in which he was especially ardent,] He had not yet passed his boyhood when his confessors, recognizing him as already great in spirit, granted him the frequent permission to eat that Bread of angels, which provides delights for kings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[204] Alia vice in eadem horti cellula ultra mediam noctem exspectabat, [januam clausam aperiri jusserit,] dum in ædes vocaretur pro more ad nocturnam quietem: solebat enim circa horam, quæ medium noctis antecedit, descendere mater ad aperiendum horti ostium, ut filia inde abducta, ad suum cubiculum sese reciperet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010706Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010706Z">
        <seg>Another time she waited in that same garden cell beyond midnight, having ordered the closed door to be opened, while she was being summoned into the house as was the custom for nighttime rest: for her mother used to come down about the hour before midnight to open the garden gate, so that her daughter, once led out, could return to her own chamber.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[204] [cum sacram Eucharistiam perciperet,] Jussa aperuit ex virginea Dei carne diffundi in anima sua mirabilem cælestis Agni mansuetudinem, vegetari spiritum suum inusitato robore, instaurari ac refici vires per quamdam veluti novam transformationem sui spiritus in hoc divinum alimentum: interea clarere in corde suo placidam altioris sphæræ serenitatem, pacem, temperiem, gaudii immensitatem ex arcta unione cum Sponso divino, fruitionis altitudinem, &amp; spiritualem saporem veræ dulcedinis, quæ nulla prorsus comparatione explicabilia erant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042204Z">
        <seg>[204] [as she received the Holy Eucharist,] She opened herself to receive from the virgin flesh of God into her soul the wondrous meekness of the heavenly Lamb, to have her spirit quickened with unusual vigor, her strength restored and renewed through a kind of new transformation of her spirit into this divine nourishment: Meanwhile, she perceived in her heart a placid serenity of a higher sphere, peace, temperance, an immensity of joy from the close union with the divine Spouse, the depth of fruition, and a spiritual taste of true sweetness, which were in no way explicable by any comparison.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[205] Occasio angelicæ custodiæ exigit, sequentem historiolam hic subnecti, [&amp; cuidam iter agenti custodiam angelicam procuraverit.] qua forte patebit, non proprium dumtaxat, verum &amp; alienos tutelares angelos sibi ad nutum habuisse obsequiosos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010709Z">
        <seg>[205] The occasion of angelic guardianship requires that the following little story be appended here, [and how he procured an angelic guard for a certain traveler.] by which it will perhaps be made clear that he had not only his own, but also the guardian angels of others, obedient to his will.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[205] Summo etiam cultu prosequebatur sanctæ Crucis signum, [aut funderet preces,] ubicumque illud reperisset, ita ut ex plantis horti impressionem illius formaret, quarum una proreginæ dono data paulo post exaruit, Rosæ reddita post quatuor dies revixit, quod totum virtuti ejusdem sanctæ Crucis Virgo tribuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042214Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042214Z">
        <seg>[205] She also treated the sign of the holy Cross with the highest veneration, [or offered prayers,] wherever she found it, so that she would form its impression from the garden plants, one of which, given as a gift to the queen-regent, withered shortly afterward; but when it was returned to Rose after four days, it revived, which the Virgin attributed entirely to the virtue of that same holy Cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[206] Electa, ut aptior &amp; dignior ad exornandum simulacrum sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis, [quarum efficaciam Deus] quam ut matrem ex intimis præcordiis colebat, a lachrymis, osculis &amp; alloquiis in eo obsequio non cessabat, &amp; miro modo tantæ pecuniæ litterulas accepit, quantum ipsa pro veste ejus paulo ante optarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042226Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042226Z">
        <seg>[206] Chosen as the most fitting and worthy to adorn the statue of Saint Catherine of Siena—whom God had honored more than He had honored her as a mother from His inmost heart—she never ceased in that devotion from tears, kisses, and prayers, and in a marvelous way received in coin the very amount of money she had herself asked for a little earlier for her vestment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[206] [Invidi demones variis modis, ac sub diversis formis] Quantum vero Angelicam hanc puellam amabant, ambiebant, mulcebant sancti angeli, tantumdem ei clam, palam, vi ac fraude nocere conabantur furiæ infernales.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010716Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010716Z">
        <seg>[206] [The envious demons, by various means and under different forms] As much as the holy angels loved, surrounded, and caressed this angelic girl, so much the infernal furies sought to harm her, secretly and openly, by force and by fraud.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[207] Adjuverat eam in hoc munere Euphemia de Pareja, [diversis miraculis ostendit.] cujus famula Francisca sui infantis nutritia graviter ægrotabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042236Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042236Z">
        <seg>[207] Euphemia de Pareja had assisted her in this task, [she showed it by various miracles.] whose servant Francisca, the nurse of her own infant, was gravely ill.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[207] [hanc Virginem impugnarunt;] Semel a domestico Gundisalvi quæstoris oratorio, ad interius cubile se Virgo recipiebat: per viam adrepens ex insidiis cacodæmon, quanta vi potuit, improvisum ei colaphum impegit, majori tamen sonitu, quam nocumento: subrisit enim intrepida Virago, insuper alteram maxillam dare parata, si hostem conspicuum palam habuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010722Z">
        <seg>[207] [they attacked this Virgin;] Once, as the Virgin was retiring from the oratory of Gundisalvus the quaestor to her inner chamber, a demon from the underworld, lying in ambush along the way, struck her with a sudden slap as hard as he could, doing more harm with the sound than with the blow. But the intrepid maiden smiled, ready even to offer her other cheek, had she had a visible enemy before her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[208] Alia vice, postquam in eodem oratorio preces absolverat, [sed illis semper impavida restitit,] incumbente vespere, secretius in altissima ædium parte cubiculum adiit, solitaria meditatione dulcedinem ruminatura, quam in oratorio gustarat inter preces.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010728Z">
        <seg>Another time, after she had finished her prayers in the same oratory, she went, as evening fell, to a more secluded chamber in the highest part of the building, to savor in solitary meditation the sweetness she had tasted there during her prayers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[209] Abierat ancilla, &amp; Rosa vix januam clauserat, cum repente audivit, [ita ut eam numquam terrere,] simul a foris inducto pessulo, sese a cacodæmone velut captivam obserari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010731Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010731Z">
        <seg>The maid had gone away, and Rosa had barely closed the door when she suddenly heard—so that it would never terrify her—and at the same time, with the bolt drawn from the outside, that she was being locked up like a captive by a demon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[209] [resipiscentia malorum Christianorum] Divinitus edocta de pravo desiderio, quo Vincentius nomine juvenis, nobili genere oriundus, ad eam ficte accesserat, tanta cum charitate corripuit, ut fateretur, se agnoscere Christum Dominum in ea loquentem, &amp; totus mutatus ab illo, qui paulo ante fuerat, fretus &amp; adjutus precibus ejusdem Virginis, juvenilibus desideriis &amp; culpis vale faciens, sanctam vivendi rationem instituit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042350Z">
        <seg>[209] [Repentance of a Wicked Christian] Divinely instructed about the wicked desire with which a young man named Vincent, of noble birth, had approached her under false pretenses, she rebuked him with such charity that he confessed he recognized Christ the Lord speaking in her, and was completely changed from the man he had been a little while before, Confident and aided by the prayers of the same Virgin, he bid farewell to his youthful desires and faults, and established a holy way of life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[20] Canta&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; in regno Peruano locus est metalli fodinis percelebris, [In aliis autem rebus] at putris soli exhalationibus, &amp; algore tetro admodum insalubris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000850Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000850Z">
        <seg>Cañab in the Peruvian kingdom is a place renowned for its metal mines, but in other respects it is extremely unhealthy because of the exhalations from its putrid soil and its bitter cold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[20] Quando hæc agebantur Limæ, celeberrima Rosæ fama, [qua occasione sanctitas hujus virginis ubique innoruit.] rapido cursu totius pene regni oppida, vicos, municipia permeavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031246Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031246Z">
        <seg>[20] While these things were happening in Lima, the most celebrated fame of Rose—by which the holiness of this virgin became known everywhere—spread with rapid speed through the towns, villages, and municipalities of almost the entire kingdom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[210] Interea quæstoris uxor, familiam de Rosa interrogans, [multo minus vincere,] ab ancillula didicit, quo ipsam loco reliquerat cum candela, quodque diserte vetuerat evocari ad cœnam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010734Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, the questor's wife, while asking the household about Rosa, learned from a little maid where she had left her with a candle, and that she had expressly forbidden being called to dinner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[210] Ordinavit etiam Dominus in ea charitatem in corporalibus misericordiæ operibus, [ærummisque pauperum adeo erat solicita,] quibus proximos juvare studuit: nam inops hæc Virgo sibimet necessaria subtrahens dispersit, deditque pauperibus: ut enim occultæ cujusdam necessitati subveniret, octo dierum alimoniam solo pane &amp; aqua contenta sibi pro egentis subsidio detraxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042403Z">
        <seg>[210] The Lord also ordained in her a charity in the corporal works of mercy, [and she was so solicitous for the necessities of the poor,] by which she strove to help her neighbors: For this poor Virgin deprived herself of what was necessary and distributed it to the poor: for, in order to relieve a certain hidden need, she withdrew from herself a supply of food for eight days, living on nothing but bread and water as aid for the needy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[211] Indignanti matri propter graveolentiam mucidi putoris, [ut omnibus succurrere conaretur.] a Rosa in vestibus ex cura infirmæ contractam respondit: “Christi bonus odor sumus, dum ministramus infirmis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042431Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042431Z">
        <seg>[211] To the mother, indignant at the foul stench of moldy decay, [so that she might try to help everyone], she replied from among the clothes she had taken from the sick woman's care: "We are the sweet-smelling fragrance of Christ as we minister to the sick."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[211] [vel ad impudicitiam excitare potuerint,] Aliquando in hortum oratura processerat, cum de vicinarum arborum opaco vidit obvium sibi antagonistam, non jam terrificum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010737Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010737Z">
        <seg>[211] [or might have been able to arouse to impudicity,] She had once stepped out into the garden to speak, when from the shady grove of the neighboring trees she saw an adversary coming toward her, no longer terrifying.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[212] [Præterea tantam conceperat fiduciam] Tota hujus sanctæ Virginis spes in Deo erat, qui prævenerat eam in benedictionibus dulcedinis: hinc frequentissime decantabat Davidicum illud, sanctis Patribus &amp; eremicolis familiare: “Deus in adjutorium meum intende, Domine ad adjuvandum me festina”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042453Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042453Z">
        <seg>[212] [Moreover, she had conceived such confidence] The entire hope of this holy Virgin was in God, who had anticipated her with blessings of sweetness: hence she most frequently sang that Davidic psalm, familiar to the holy Fathers and hermits: "O God, come to my aid; O Lord, make haste to help me."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[212] [cum inspirationes diabolicas a divinis statim distingueret.] Eminebat in Rosa (confessariis attestantibus) mirabile donum internoscendi sinceras visiones ab illusionibus sathanæ, &amp; distinguendi sanctas inspirationes a versutis suggestionibus antiqui serpentis, quod Apostolus discretionem spirituum vocat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010741Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010741Z">
        <seg>[212] [since she immediately distinguished diabolical inspirations from divine ones.] In Rosa there shone forth (as her confessors attested) a marvelous gift for discerning true visions from Satan's illusions and for distinguishing holy inspirations from the cunning suggestions of the ancient serpent, which the Apostle calls the discernment of spirits.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[213] [de divina providentia,] Angebatur quondam innocens hæc anima, recogitans pavida formidine prædestinationis suæ arcanum omni animæ tremendum, quam Dominus ad adjuvandum festinus sic est affatus: “Filia ego neminem condemno, nisi volentem condemnari: ex nunc tranquillo animo esto”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042505Z">
        <seg>[213] [On Divine Providence,] This soul was once tormented, pondering with fearful dread the mystery of its predestination, which is awesome to every soul, when the Lord, eager to help, thus spoke to it: "Daughter, I condemn no one, except the one who is willing to be condemned; from now on, be of tranquil mind."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[214] [ab austeris domesticis;] Quam suavis erat indoles Filiolæ, tam aspera conditio matris, ut pene videretur sola patientiæ biga par illud dissimillimum conjunxisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010747Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010747Z">
        <seg>[214] [from the austere household;] The disposition of the little daughter was as sweet as her mother's was harsh, so that it seemed as if only the chariot of patience had joined that most dissimilar pair.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[214] [ut eam sæpius in manifestis vitæ periculis] Eadem vi confidentiæ in Deum a prima ætate Rosa adversus quascumque mortalis vitæ difficultates &amp; incommoda stetit impavida: cujus fidem, constantiam, &amp; nutus visi sunt immanes tauri, in extremam aliunde rabiem exstimulati, revereri: nam adstupentibus, quotquot spectabant, ad invictæ Virginis conspectum eminus, &amp; repente innocentibus parcere didicerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042527Z">
        <seg>[214] [so that those who watched, at the mere sight of the invincible Virgin from afar, suddenly learned to spare the innocent.] With the same confident strength in God, from her earliest years Rose stood fearless against whatever difficulties and hardships of mortal life: whose faith, constancy, and counsels the savage bulls, otherwise goaded into the utmost rage, seemed to revere. for those who watched, amazed at the sight of the invincible Virgin from a distance, suddenly learned to spare the innocent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[215] Accessit in persecutionis cumulum incredula quorumdam confessariorum seu oscitantia, seu ruditas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010756Z">
        <seg>[215] Added to the sum of the persecution was the unbelief of certain confessors, whether through negligence or ignorance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[215] Minor non erat ejus fiducia in sperandis a divina providentia necessariis vitæ subsidiis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042542Z">
        <seg>His confidence in relying on divine providence for the necessary means of life was not small.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[216] Et sane, aut numquam, aut raro deerat grandi ejus patientiæ grandis materia, [quibus successerunt morbi] siquidem vacantem a cruce externa, gravior occupabat interna;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010802Z">
        <seg>And indeed, either never or rarely was there a lack of ample material for his great patience [which was succeeded by illnesses], for when he was free from external affliction, a more severe one occupied him internally.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[216] Testimonio etiam Domini nostri Jesu Christi, [&amp; prædixerit multa futura,] quod est spiritus prophetiæ, fuit illustris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042610Z">
        <seg>[216] He was also illustrious by the testimony of our Lord Jesus Christ, [and had foretold many things to come,] who is the spirit of prophecy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[217] Febres tum ephimeras&lt;a0&gt;h&lt;/a0&gt;, tum reliquas contumaciores, [omnis ferme generis,] ipsa assuetudo pene in jocum verterat, non item articulares illas manuum, pedumque fidiculas, quibus notissima (attamen pavenda) chiragræ, podagræ, arthritidis nomina insederunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010815Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010815Z">
        <seg>[217] She had turned fevers—both the fleeting ones and the more stubborn ones, of almost every kind—into a mere matter of habit, almost a joke, but not so the joint pains of the hands and feet, to which the well-known (yet dreadful) names of gout, podagra, and arthritis have clung.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[217] Ipsa Maria Oliva Rosæ genitrix, quæ antea hoc ei vaticinanti Filiæ acerbe contradixerat, anno Domini MDCXXIX post mariti obitum in novello monasterio sexagenaria devote filiabus S. Patris Dominici aggregata fuit, [quorum veritatem eventus probavit.] &amp;, assumpto cum habitu nomine Mariæ de S. Maria, emissa postmodum professione, optimo fine quievit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042626Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042626Z">
        <seg>[217] Mary herself, the mother of Olive Rose, who had previously bitterly opposed this prophecy to her daughter, in the year of the Lord 1629, after her husband's death, at the age of sixty, was devoutly received into the new monastery, joining the daughters of St. Father Dominic. [The truth of which events proved.] And, having taken the name Mary of St. Mary with her habit, she made her profession and rested in the Lord with a good death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[218] [quos vocabat cælestes favores,] De his aliquando familiarius devotæ cuidam personæ collocuta, universam hanc morborum seriem inter pretiosos ac illustriores sibi a Deo collatos favores recensuit, subjecitque, nisi hos ipsamet experta fuisset, numquam persuadere sibi potuisse, unicum miserabile corpus tantorum ac tam multiplicium dolorum capax fore;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010818Z">
        <seg>[218] [which she called heavenly favors,] Speaking of these more familiarly at one time to a certain devout person, she recounted this entire series of illnesses among the precious and more illustrious favors bestowed upon her by God, and added that unless she herself had experienced them, she could never have been persuaded that a single miserable body could be capable of so many and such varied pains.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[219] Triduo ante suum extremum decubitum cellam hortensem adiit, [incidit in morbum ignotum] &amp; cygni more exorsa modulos decantando commendabat Deo, &amp; sancto Dominico genitricem suam, quam desolatam relinquebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042719Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042719Z">
        <seg>Three days before her final illness she went to the garden cell, contracted an unknown disease, and, in the manner of a swan, commended her mother, whom she was leaving desolate, to God and to Saint Dominic by singing little songs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[219] [quosque ex mira quadam visione præsciebat,] Huc plane referenda videtur celebris illa visio, quam inter diviniores Rosæ excessus tanti fecere graves mysticæ theologiæ professores.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010821Z">
        <seg>[219] [which he had foreseen from a certain marvelous vision,] That famous vision, which the most learned professors of mystical theology have held in such high esteem among the diviner excesses of the Rose, seems plainly to belong here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[21] Aliquando mater exploratura tam accuratæ obedientiæ pronitatem, [mira simplicitate &amp; reverentia] mandavit Filiæ, quod sericos flores, quos tunc forte pingebat acu, præpostero filorum ductu formaret contra omnem artis ideam, rectumque ordinem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000853Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000853Z">
        <seg>[21] At one point, the mother, to test her daughter's readiness for such exacting obedience, [with remarkable simplicity and reverence] commanded her to shape the silk flowers she was then painting with a needle by running the threads in reverse, against all the rules of the art and the proper order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[220] Aperuit insuper confessario, frustra medicos in exploranda hujus infirmitatis specie laborare, [&amp; doloribus acerbissimum,] quam nec pulsus, nec aliud extrinsecum signum indicaret, ac proinde non alia, quam immedicabilis Jobi patientia sibi opus esse, ut quoquomodo singulos Crucifixi sponsi sui dolores membratim experiretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042735Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042735Z">
        <seg>He also revealed to his confessor that the doctors were in vain striving to diagnose this malady, which showed no pulse nor any other external sign, and that therefore he needed nothing other than the incurable patience of Job, so that he might in some way experience the individual pains of his Spouse, the Crucified One, one by one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[220] Interrupit hic Virginem doctor, sciscitando, cujus coloris fuerint illi arcus?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010824Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010824Z">
        <seg>The doctor here interrupted the Virgin by asking what color those arches had been.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[221] Angeli, correpta bilance ac ponderibus, cœperunt onerare trutinam, [&amp; uberioris gratiæ noverat præcursores,] afflictiones afflictionibus exaggerantes in cumulum, velut earum molestiam ad amussim exploraturi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010830Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010830Z">
        <seg>[221] The angels, having seized the balance and weights, began to load the scales, [and knew the forerunners of a more abundant grace,] piling affliction upon affliction into a heap, as if to probe their annoyance to the very limit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[221] Propius accedente acceptabili tempore, quo Rosa ista e terris in Sponsi paradisum, depositis mortalitatis exuviis, [cujus intervallo se piissime] transferenda erat, pluries advocatis confessariis, tenerrimam conscientiam, &amp; semel generaliter de tota vita exomologesi purgavit, semper cum pœnitudinis singultibus &amp; lacrymis, ita ut compuncti audientes mirarentur in sævissimis cruciatibus non flentem, levissimas imperfectiones tam alte deflere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042803Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042803Z">
        <seg>[221] As the acceptable time drew nearer, when Rose was to depart from this earth to her Spouse's paradise, having laid aside the vestments of mortality, [during which interval she most piously prepared herself] was to be transferred, she repeatedly summoned her confessors, purged her most tender conscience, and once in general of her entire life made her confession, always with sobs and tears of penitence, so that the moved listeners marveled that in her most severe torments she did not weep, yet so deeply lamented the slightest imperfections.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[221] Uxor Basilii de Bargas, Francisca del Pulgar, [qui vario morborum genere laborabant,] a partu repente mariscarum&lt;a0&gt;g&lt;/a0&gt; ulcerosis tuberculis gravissime torquebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035002Z">
        <seg>The wife of Basil of Bargas, Francisca del Pulgar, [who were suffering from various kinds of diseases,] was suddenly, after childbirth, afflicted most severely by ulcerous tubercles of the sea urchins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[222] His peractis, sonoram decentissime extulit vocem Salvator: [sicut ipsa paucis ante obitum diebus] “Noverint (inquit) universi, quod tribulationem subsequitur gratia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010833Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010833Z">
        <seg>[222] When these things were accomplished, Salvator most fittingly raised a sonorous voice: [as she herself did a few days before her death] "Let all know," she said, "that grace follows tribulation;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[222] [ad mortem præparavit,] Sæpius etiam coram adstantibus voce clara orthodoxæ fidei professionem emisit, protestans, se, ut vixerat, ita mori sanctæ Romanæ &amp; universalis Ecclesiæ filiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042815Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042815Z">
        <seg>[222] [prepared for death,] She often also, in the presence of those who were standing by, with a clear voice made a profession of the orthodox faith, protesting that she would die, as she had lived, a daughter of the holy Roman and universal Church.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[223] [&amp; post monita quædam adstantibus data] A domesticis quæstoris Gundisalvi voce humili &amp; flebili oravit condonari, si qua in re ulli umquam nocuerat, lachrymantibus omnibus, qui ejus innocentiam &amp; puritatem tot experimentis comprobaverant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042827Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042827Z">
        <seg>[223] [and after certain admonitions given to those present] He implored his householders, Gundisalvus's stewards, in a low and mournful voice to forgive him if he had ever wronged anyone in any way, while all wept who had proven his innocence and purity by so many trials.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[223] [doctori Castillo explicuit.] Interpellavit doctor, cupiens explorare magis, sub quali specie aut figura Virgini apparuisset gratia?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010837Z">
        <seg>[223] [He explained it to Doctor Castillo.] The doctor interrupted, wishing to probe further as to under what form or figure the Virgin had appeared to him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[224] [placide Creatori reddidit animam.] A Joanne de Lorenzana postremo ejus confessario paranti se in nocte pervigilii S. Bartholomæi ad matutinarum officium accedere, sciens sibi non superesse amplius, nisi quatuor horas, supremam benedictionem ex corde efflagitavit, monens ea ipsa nocte, ineunte festivitate&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; se ad festa sempiterna abituram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042856Z">
        <seg>[224] [He peacefully returned his soul to the Creator.] From John of Lorenzana, her last confessor, she earnestly implored a final blessing, preparing herself to attend the morning office on the vigil of St. Bartholomew, knowing she had no more than four hours left. She warned that on that very night, at the beginning of the festival, she would depart for the eternal feast.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[225] Virgineum Rosæ cadaver cum spirare desiisset, adhuc ex vivido vultus colore, [Tanta populi multitudo concurrit] &amp; rubentibus labiis respirans credebatur, donec innotuit haud amplius inesse halitum ex applicito ori ejus speculo, cujus sicut fuit hic ultimus usus, sic etiam fuit primus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042911Z">
        <seg>When the virgin Rose's body had ceased to breathe, she was still believed to be breathing from the vivid color of her face and her red lips, until it was discovered that no breath remained from the mirror applied to her mouth, which was as much its last use as it was its first.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[225] [ut hic foras erumperet] Hoc affectu calescens Rosa, pro impetrando divino amore, ex ipsa divini amoris ignita pharetra nonnullas jaculatorias collegit, in formam precatiunculæ, quam lubet hic verbatim transcribere, quia cunctis ea placuit, multis usu profuit, amandique tum signis, tum stimulis scatet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010858Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010858Z">
        <seg>[225] [so that he might break forth here] Warmed by this affection, Rosa, in order to obtain divine love, gathered from the very fiery quiver of divine love several dart-like phrases into the form of a little prayer, which I am pleased to transcribe here verbatim, because it pleased everyone, benefited many in its use, and is teeming with both signs and stimuli for the beloved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[226] Visa fuit angelorum numerosa caterva, &amp; ante obitum circa grabatum decumbentis, [ad incorruptum Sanctæ cadaver,] &amp; post circa feretrum defunctæ, alternante concentu festive tripudiare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042924Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042924Z">
        <seg>A numerous company of angels was seen, before her death dancing festively in turn around the bed of the dying [to the incorrupt body of the Saint], and afterward around the bier of the deceased.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[226] [in omni publica conversatione;] Noverat ingeniosa Virgo ceteras in se virtutes abscondere: sola charitas, quia flamma est, premi ac celari nequaquam poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010902Z">
        <seg>[226] [in all public conversation;] The ingenuous virgin knew that she could conceal all other virtues within herself; only charity, because it is a flame, could in no way be restrained or hidden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[227] Ad exsequias, vulgata jam fama, innumerabilis utriusque conditionis hominum multitudo convenit, [celebratisque exsequiis, ad ejus tumulum,] &amp; pia variorum titulorum sodalitia sponte, &amp; religiosorum mendicantium Ordines processere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042942Z">
        <seg>[227] For the funeral, a rumor having already spread, an innumerable multitude of people from all walks of life gathered, [and after the funeral rites were celebrated, to his tomb,] both pious brotherhoods of various titles of their own accord and mendicant religious Orders processed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[227] [dum autem se solam esse existimabat,] At vero cum sola erat in recessu tugurioli, aut cum putabat a nemine se observari, tenerius simul atque liberius prosa, versu, sermone, cantu ructabat solemnissima quæque seraphici amoris axiomata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010908Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010908Z">
        <seg>[227] [while she believed herself to be alone,] But when she was truly alone in the recess of her little hut, or when she thought no one was watching her, she would more tenderly and freely, in prose, verse, speech, and song, utter the most solemn axioms of seraphic love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[228] Subinde non inveniens modum, quo sibi inter hos fervores aut temperaret aut satisfaceret, [liberius affectui suo indulgebat.] citharam (quam pulsare numquam didicerat) arripiebat de proximo pariete forte pendulam, tactuque chordarum moderabatur impetum vocis, suaviter cantillando saucii querimonias amoris, ut omni ritu gestuque sese Dilecto suo ostentaret amantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010911Z">
        <seg>[228] Soon, finding no way to either temper or satisfy himself amid these passions, he gave himself more freely to his feelings. She would seize the harp (which she had never learned to play) hanging by chance on a nearby wall, and with the touch of its strings she would moderate the impulse of her voice, sweetly singing the wounded laments of love, so that with every gesture and rite she might display herself as a lover to her Beloved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[228] [at locus sepulturæ postea mutari debuerit.] Resonabat undequaque ecclesia valde ampla continuis vocibus Rosam BEATAM &amp; SANCTAM acclamantibus, donec sepulturæ pretiosum depositum mandatum fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043004Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043004Z">
        <seg>[228] [but the burial place ought later to have been changed.] The very spacious church resounded on every side with continuous voices acclaiming Rose as BLESSED and HOLY, until the precious deposit was committed to burial.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[229] Vehementissimæ dilectionis comes &amp; testis est zelus: [Etiam vehementer ideo dolebat,] nec hujus in se ardores Virgo celare poterat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010917Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010917Z">
        <seg>Zeal is the companion and witness of the most vehement love: [She also grieved all the more because of it,] nor could the Virgin conceal its ardors within herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[22] [jussu archiepiscopi Limani] Sed exspectandum erat Limensis archiepiscopi officiosum arbitrium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031351Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031351Z">
        <seg>But the official decision of the Archbishop of Lima had to be awaited.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[22] [obtemperabat matri] Triennio circiter ante felicem Rosæ obitum contigit, eam ab illustri matrona D. Maria de Usategui, consorte regii quæstoris D. Gundisalvi de la Massa, e paterno domicilio invitari ad suarum ædium gratuitum contubernium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000856Z">
        <seg>[22] [She obeyed her mother] About three years before the blessed death of Rose, it happened that she was invited by the illustrious matron D. Maria de Usategui, wife of the royal treasurer D. Gundisalvi de la Massa, from her father's home to a free lodging in her own house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[230] Maximis signis ostendere voluit omnipotens Deus, quam sublimem gloriæ gradum in cælis obtineat Rosa, [multi tepidi ad fervorem,] quorum illa celeberrima, &amp; crebra fuere, quæ cæli terræque creationem excedunt, conversiones scilicet peccatorum, quæ, a Deo precibus hujus Virginis obtentæ, insignes fuerunt: ad solum enim virginei corpusculi contactum in feretro quam plurimi repentinis compunctionis stimulis ita fuere perculsi, ut statim elatis vocibus detestati fuerint sua scelera, prorumpentes in lachrymosam criminum confessionem, audiente, spectante &amp; mirante maxima circumstantium turba.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043048Z">
        <seg>[230] The Almighty God wished to show by the greatest signs what a sublime degree of glory in heaven Rose possesses, [many tepid to fervor,] whose most celebrated and frequent conversions, which surpass the creation of heaven and earth, were the conversions of sinners, which, obtained from God by the prayers of this Virgin, were remarkable: For at the mere touch of the virgin's little body in the coffin, so many were struck by sudden stings of compunction that they immediately, with raised voices, detested their own crimes, bursting into a tearful confession of their sins, as the great crowd of onlookers heard, saw, and marveled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[230] [Deum peccatis offendi,] Mendacia, quæ puerorum assiduis jocis &amp; loquacitati abesse vix umquam solent, tam dire execrabatur, ut proprium suum apophthegma esset: “Nec pro cælo, nec pro terra mentiendum, quia Deus veritas est”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010920Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010920Z">
        <seg>[230] [That God is offended by sins,] He detested lies, which are seldom absent from the constant jesting and talk of boys, so terribly that it became his own apophthegm: "One should not lie for heaven's sake, nor for the sake of the earth, because God is truth."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[231] Bartholomæus Martinez quondam hujus Virginis confessarius ex certa scientia aperuit in judicio, [&amp; peccatores ad pœnitentiam,] quemdam profligatæ vitæ hominem, longissima annorum serie sacrilege frequentantem sacramenta, Rosæ jam tumulatæ precibus commendatum fuisse, &amp; mox illum sensisse divinæ gratiæ motionem, qua cor ejus liquescens generalem totius vitæ exomologesim amarissime perfecit, &amp; adeo conscientiam ejus timor Domini pervasit, ut postmodum quoscumque defectus, tamquam pergrandes excessus, eluere festinaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043054Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043054Z">
        <seg>[231] Bartholomew Martinez, formerly the confessor of this Virgin, declared on oath in court, [and sinners to penance,] a certain man of a dissolute life, who for a very long series of years had been sacrilegiously frequenting the sacraments, that he had been commended to the prayers of the already buried Rose, and soon he felt a motion of divine grace, by which his heart, melting, he most bitterly made a general confession of his entire life, and so the fear of the Lord pervaded his conscience, that thereafter he hastened to wash away whatever failings, as if they were the gravest excesses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[231] [&amp; maxime lætabatur ob conversionem cujusdam peccatricis,] Ejusdem amicissimi zeli genuina soboles erat immensum illud gaudium, quo vernabat Rosa, quotiescumque Dei honorem &amp; obsequium insigni aliquo successu videbat promoveri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010923Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010923Z">
        <seg>[231] [&amp; was especially delighted by the conversion of a certain sinner,] A genuine offspring of that most friendly zeal was the immense joy with which Rosa rejoiced whenever she saw the honor and worship of God promoted by some notable success.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[232] Francisco &amp; Alexandro de Coloma germanis fratribus matertera erat Maria de Xuata mulier opulenta, [aliique ad miram animi mutationem excitati sunt.] sed animo implacabiliter in cognatos quamvis pauperrimos adverso &amp; implicato&lt;a0&gt;*&lt;/a0&gt;, quorum primus alebat suis impensis sex consobrinas &amp; duos consobrinos, omnes in parva ætate constitutos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043109Z">
        <seg>[232] Francisca and Alexander de Coloma, the siblings, had as their maternal aunt Maria de Xuata, a wealthy woman, [and others were stirred to a remarkable change of heart.] But she was implacably hostile in spirit toward her kinsmen, however poor they might be, the first of whom supported at his own expense six cousins—all of them still in their early years.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[232] Nil erat, quod satiaret Rosam, amores suos Sponso testificari cupientem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010930Z">
        <seg>Nothing could satisfy Rosa, who longed to testify to her love for her Bridegroom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[233] Inquietissima vis amoris nihil non comminiscebatur, quo serviret unice dilecto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010936Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010936Z">
        <seg>The most restless force of love devised nothing that might not serve its one and only beloved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[233] [Præterea plurimi] Præter prædicta spiritualia beneficia, multa alia &amp; insignia miracula in curandis corporibus patrata narrantur, quibus sanctitas &amp; merita hujus Virginis satis superque comprobata sunt: ex processibus enim authoritate apostolica confectis, &amp; formiter a sacra congregatione Rituum discussis, referente dilecto filio nostro Decio Cardinali Azzolino&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; hujus causæ ponente, non solum constat de fama sanctitatis, &amp; virtutibus theologalibus, &amp; cardinalibus cum annexis in gradu heroïco, sed præterea ex concludentibus probationibus tamquam rite &amp; recte admissa fuere sequentia miracula.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043144Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043144Z">
        <seg>[233] [Moreover, numerous] Besides the aforementioned spiritual benefits, many other and significant miracles performed in the healing of bodies are related, by which the holiness and merits of this Virgin have been amply proven: for from the proceedings conducted by apostolic authority, and formally discussed by the Sacred Congregation of Rites, with the case being presented by our beloved son Decio Cardinal Azzolini, it has been established not only that there is a reputation for sanctity, and for the theological and cardinal virtues with their annexes in a heroic degree, but furthermore, by conclusive proofs duly and rightly admitted, the following miracles were approved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[234] [diversis morbis asslicti,] Isabellæ Duran apoplexia in paralysim terminata brachium exsiccarat, omnemque illius usum a quinque circiter mensibus cruciabiliter ademerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043153Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043153Z">
        <seg>[234] [afflicted with various diseases,] an apoplexy that ended in paralysis had withered the arm of Isabella Duran, painfully depriving her of all use of it for about five months;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[235] Summa vaporantis alloquii hæc erat: “Domine, [ferventer orans coram picta Christi imagine,] o quando te omnes diligent, prout diligi mereris?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010948Z">
        <seg>The gist of the fervent prayer was this: "Lord, [praying fervently before the painted image of Christ,] oh, when will everyone love you as much as you deserve to be loved?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[235] [implorato ejus auxilio,] Magdalena Chimisso nobilis Inda (utpote de stirpe regulorum Chincensium) ab umbilico deorsum corporis attractione, ariditate crurum, &amp; pedum misere per plures languebat annos, &amp; non nisi in aliorum manibus huc &amp; illuc poterat deferri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043215Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043215Z">
        <seg>[235] [Having implored his help,] Magdalena Chimisso, a noble Indian woman (being of the lineage of the Chincian rulers), had for many years been miserably afflicted from the navel down with bodily atrophy, dryness of the legs, and feet, and could only be carried back and forth in the arms of others.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[236] His igitur &amp; plurimis aliis signis coruscante per orbem Rosæ sanctitate, [mirabiliter sanitatem recuperarunt,] ejus exigentibus meritis, felicis recordationis Clemens Papa nonus&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; prædecessor noster indulsit, ut hæc Dei Serva ubique terrarum &lt;b1&gt;Beatæ&lt;/b1&gt; nomine nuncuparetur, &amp;, celebrata solemni ritu ejus beatificatione, eam civitatis Limæ &amp; totius regni Peruani principaliorem patronam authoritate apostolica decrevit &amp; declaravit, ejusque festum ab omnibus illic degentibus de præcepto servari mandavit, &amp; ejus nomen apponi in Martyrologio Romano&lt;a2&gt;c&lt;/a2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043227Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043227Z">
        <seg>[236] Therefore, with the sanctity of Rose shining forth throughout the world by these and many other signs, [they were miraculously restored to health,] by her meritorious deeds, our predecessor of blessed memory, Pope Clement the Ninth, granted that this Servant of God be everywhere called &lt;b1&gt;Blessed&lt;/b1&gt;, and, after celebrating her beatification with solemn rite, he decreed and declared her the principal patroness of the city of Lima and of the entire kingdom of Peru by apostolic authority, and commanded that her feast be observed by all who live there under penalty, and that her name be inserted in the Roman Martyrology&lt;a2&gt;c&lt;/a2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[236] Una tamen filiarum, non abnuente matre, irrepsit, [precibus ex ea elicit sudorem,] quasi candelas emunctorio in altari purgatura: statimque velut subitaneo pavore Rosam interpellans;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010951Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010951Z">
        <seg>[236] Yet one of the daughters, with her mother's consent, crept in, [her prayers drew sweat from her,] as if she were going to purify the altar with candles: and immediately, as if seized by a sudden terror, she interrupted Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[237] Obest miraculorum fidei præceps credulitas, prodest circumspecta dubitatio, [quem multi testes oculati] ut severitate examinis, ceu polituræ defrictu simul exploretur soliditas, &amp; clarescat veritas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010958Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010958Z">
        <seg>[237] Rash credulity is detrimental to the faith of miracles; circumspect doubt is beneficial, [as many eyewitnesses testify], so that by the severity of the examination, like the abrasion of a polish, the solidity may be explored and the truth made clear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[237] Primum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043237Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043237Z">
        <seg>First.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[238] Alia itidem bina miracula ex processu Panormitano;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043252Z">
        <seg>[238] Two other miracles likewise from the Palermo process;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[238] His minime contentus quæstor fidum hominem Andream Lopes ablegat, jubetque e vicino Societatis Jesu collegio accersiri nominatim Patres Didacum Martinez, &amp; Didacum de Peñalosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011001Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011001Z">
        <seg>[238] Not at all satisfied with this, the quaestor sends his trusted man, Andrew Lopes, and orders that the Fathers Diego Martinez and Diego de Peñalosa be summoned by name from the nearby Jesuit college.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[239] [Hoc prodigio territam familiam metu liberat,] Interea Gundisalvum, uxorem, familiam gravissimus invasit timor, ne forte ob aliquod suum, aut domesticorum occultum scelus, hoc in suis ædibus portentum accidisset, aut impendentem aliquam divinæ ultionis cladem miseris comminaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011016Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011016Z">
        <seg>[239] [This prodigy frees the terrified family from fear,] Meanwhile, Gundisalvus, his wife, and their household were seized by the gravest fear, lest perhaps on account of some hidden crime of their own or of their household this portent had occurred in their own home, or was threatening the miserable with some impending calamity of divine vengeance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[23] [aliisque superioribus] Sed longe mirabilior obedientia ultra mortem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000905Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000905Z">
        <seg>[23] [and other preceding ones] But obedience beyond death is far more marvelous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[23] [anno 1619 adhuc incorruptum] Ergo translationis actui designatur ipsum sancti patriarchæ Joseph pervigilium, dies nimirum XVIII Martii;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031413Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031413Z">
        <seg>[23] [still uncorrupted in the year 1619] Therefore, the very vigil of the holy patriarch Joseph is designated for the act of translation, namely the day of March 18th.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[240] Propterea annuimus, ut hodierna die publice in sacrosancta basilica principis Apostolorum, [cur Rosam Limanam Sanctis adscripserit,] Missæ sacro peracto, ejus canonizatio haberetur, eademque die in prædicta sacrosancta Vaticana basilica, in qua solemni ritu cum ejusdem sanctæ Romanæ Ecclesiæ Cardinalibus, patriarchis, archiepiscopis &amp; episcopis, ac dilectis filiis Romanæ Curiæ prælatis, officialibus &amp; familiaribus nostris, clero sæculari &amp; regulari, ac maxima populi frequentia mane convenimus, repetitis ter pro canonizationis decreto nobis per dilectum filium Ludovicum tituli sanctæ Sabinæ Cardinalem Portocarrero supplicationibus, post sacros hymnos, litanias, aliasque preces Spiritus sancti gratia rite implorata, ad honorem sanctæ &amp; individuæ Trinitatis, &amp; fidei catholicæ exaltationem, auctoritate omnipotentis Dei, Patris, Filii &amp; Spiritus Sancti, beatorum Apostolorum ac nostra, de venerabilium fratrum nostrorum sanctæ Romanæ Ecclesiæ Cardinalium, patriarcharum, archiepiscoporum &amp; episcoporum in Romana Curia existentium consilio, ac unanimi consensu, beatam Rosam de sancta Maria virginem Limanam, de cujus vitæ sanctitate, fidei sinceritate, &amp; miraculorum excellentia plene constat, sanctam esse diffinivimus, ac sanctarum Virginum catalogo adscribendam decrevimus, prout præsentium tenore decernimus, diffinimus &amp; adscribimus, statuentes, ab Ecclesia universali quolibet anno die trigesima Mensis Augusti&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; memoriam ejus inter sanctas virgines recoli debere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043407Z">
        <seg>[240] Therefore, we have granted that on this day, publicly in the most sacred basilica of the Prince of the Apostles, [why he included the Rose of Lima among the Saints,] After the sacred Mass was concluded, her canonization was to be held, and on the same day in the aforementioned most holy Vatican Basilica, in which, with solemn rite, together with the Cardinals of the same Holy Roman Church, patriarchs, archbishops, and bishops, and our beloved sons, the prelates, officials, and household of the Roman Curia, the secular and regular clergy, and the greatest crowd of the people having assembled in the morning, the decree of canonization having been thrice repeated to us by the supplications of our beloved son Louis, Cardinal Portocarrero of the title of Saint Sabina, after sacred hymns, litanies, and other prayers duly implored for the grace of the Holy Spirit, to the honor of the holy and indivisible Trinity, and for the exaltation of the Catholic faith, by the authority of Almighty God, the Father, of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, of the blessed Apostles and of Ourselves, with the counsel of our venerable brothers, the Cardinals of the Holy Roman Church, the patriarchs, archbishops, and bishops in the Roman Curia, and with the unanimous consent, we declare and define the blessed Rose of Lima, virgin, whose holiness of life, sincerity of faith, and whose excellence of miracles is fully attested, we have defined to be a saint, and have decreed that she be enrolled in the catalog of holy Virgins, as we by the tenor of these presents decree, define, and enroll, establishing that her memory be celebrated by the universal Church among the holy virgins on the thirtieth day of the month of August each year.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[240] [suumque brachium læsum eo sudore liniri jubens,] Neque hic stetit miraculum, quin partu novi miraculi &amp; suam certitudinem assereret, &amp; Rosæ brachium fidemque liberaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011019Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011019Z">
        <seg>[240] [and ordering his wounded arm to be anointed with that sweat,] Nor did the miracle stop there, but with the birth of a new miracle it both confirmed its own truth and delivered Rose's arm and faith.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[241] Eadem authoritate omnibus &amp; singulis vere pœnitentibus &amp; confessis, [concessisque indulgentiis,] qui annis singulis in die festo ipsius sanctæ Rosæ ad sepulcrum, in quo ejus corpus requiescit, visitandum accesserint, septem annos &amp; totidem quadragenas de injunctis eis, aut alias quomodolibet debitis pœnitentiis misericorditer in Domino relaxavimus in forma Ecclesiæ consueta.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043416Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043416Z">
        <seg>[241] By the same authority, to all and each who are truly penitent and have confessed, [and granted indulgences,] to all and each of the truly penitent and confessed, who in each year on the feast day of the same Saint Rose shall have approached to visit the sepulchre in which her body rests, we have mercifully in the Lord, in the usual form of the Church, remitted seven years and as many forty days of the penances enjoined upon them, or otherwise in any way due.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[241] Meridiei punctus erat, quando D. Maria Usategui propriis manibus, [statim Sancta sanatur,] Rosæ domum reduci brachium nudavit, gossipium circumduxit, alligaturam reposuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011022Z">
        <seg>It was noon when D. Maria Usategui, with her own hands, stripped her arm to bring Rose home [she is healed immediately], wrapped it in gauze, and reapplied the bandage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[242] Cum omni igitur humilitate pro tam insigni beneficio benedicimus &amp; glorificamus Deum Patrem omnipotentem, [Deo pro illo virtutis exemplo gratias agit,] cui benedictio, &amp; honor, &amp; gloria, &amp; potestas in secula seculorum: assiduis precibus ab eo postulantes, ut per intercessionem Electæ suæ a peccatis nostris faciem avertens, misereatur nostri, &amp; respiciat nos, quibus ostendit misericordiam suam in medio templi sui, dum novum nobis propugnaculum totius Americæ contra antiquas diaboli insidias &amp; infidelitatis errores dedit, &amp; omnibus apud divinam suam Majestatem protectricem &amp; advocatam concessit ad sanctæ Ecclesiæ tranquillitatem, fidei catholicæ incrementum, &amp; infidelium lumen &amp; conversionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043423Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043423Z">
        <seg>[242] With all humility, therefore, we bless and glorify God the almighty Father for such an outstanding benefit, [Gives thanks to God for that example of virtue,] to whom be blessing, and honor, and glory, and power, for ever and ever: earnestly praying to him, that through the intercession of his Electa, turning away his face from our sins, he may have mercy on us, and look upon us, to whom he has shown his mercy in the midst of his temple, while he has given us a new bulwark for all of America against the ancient snares of the devil and the errors of infidelity, and has granted to all a protective and advocate with his divine Majesty for the tranquility of the holy Church, the increase of the Catholic faith, and the light and conversion of infidels.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[242] Exinde gravior tempestas formidinis Gundisalvo incubuit, [&amp; hospiti timenti, ne ideo imago transferatur,] sed novo Rosæ solatio mox dissipanda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011029Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011029Z">
        <seg>[242] From then on a more severe storm of dread descended upon Gundisalvo [and the fearful guest, lest on that account the image be transferred], but was soon to be dispelled by a new consolation of Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[243] [&amp; ubique promulgari jubet suum decretum,] Ceterum, quia difficile foret, præsentes nostras litteras ad singula loca, ubi opus esset, deferri: volumus, ut earum exemplis etiam impressis, manu publici notarii subscriptis, &amp; sigillo alicujus personæ in dignitate ecclesiastica constitutæ munitis, eamdem ubique fidem haberi, quæ ipsis præsentibus haberetur, si essent exhibitæ, vel ostensæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043426Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043426Z">
        <seg>[243] [and he orders his decree to be promulgated everywhere,] However, since it would be difficult to have our present letters carried to every place where they might be needed, we decree that printed copies of them, subscribed by the hand of a public notary and sealed with the seal of any person in ecclesiastical office, shall have the same faith everywhere as the originals would have if they were exhibited or shown.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[243] [affirmate prædicit, illam in domo ejus mansuram.] Ita esse pridem conjecerant ex affectu, devotione, reverentia, quam peculiariter modo huic, modo illi ibidem imagini, Rosa impendebat, velut inde favoris divini copiosior eam aura afflasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011032Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011032Z">
        <seg>[243] [He confidently predicts that she will remain in his house.] They had long since inferred from the affection, devotion, and reverence that Rosa showed in a particularly special way to this image and that one there that a more abundant breeze of divine favor had blown upon her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[244] [cui ipse ac plures S. R.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043438Z">
        <seg>[244] [to whom he himself and several of the S. R.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[245] Anno MDLIII juxta Caxaguanam&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; in Cusco ac finitimis provinciis, [quæ quondam in aëre apparens, Indos terruerat,] gentilium Indorum plusquam ducenta millia sub signis erant, ut Catholicum exercitum, sexcentis non amplius capitibus numerosum, proculcarent potius, quam profligarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011044Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011044Z">
        <seg>[245] In the year 1553, near Caxaguana&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; in Cusco and the neighboring provinces, [which, once appearing in the air, had terrified the Indians,] more than two hundred thousand pagan Indians were under arms, so that they would trample the Catholic army, numbering no more than six hundred men, rather than defeat it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[246] Demum anno MDCXLIII sexto Idus Maii, rex Catholicus regnum suum Peruanum cælesti Virginis matris præsidio firmius contra impendentia pericula communiri exoptans, [&amp; quæ regni Peruani protectrix electa] mandavit, hanc ab universis incolis, publico ritu, eligi Protectricem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011051Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011051Z">
        <seg>Finally, on May 9, 1643, the Catholic King, desiring to fortify his Peruvian kingdom more securely against impending dangers under the heavenly protection of the Virgin Mother [and having chosen her as Protector of the Peruvian kingdom], commanded that she be publicly chosen by all the inhabitants as Protector.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[247] [multis beneficiis S. Rosam cumulavit,] A teneris hic Rosæ (quamquam nil minus quam ferreæ) magnes fuit ac siderites&lt;a0&gt;g&lt;/a0&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011100Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011100Z">
        <seg>[247] [He showered St. Rose with many blessings,] From her earliest years, she was a magnet and siderite for Rose (though nothing less than iron).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[248] [ut ipsa ingenue fatebatur.] Inter alia de modo, quo Deiparam in hac imagine sibi loquentem percipiebat, interrogata, simplicissime respondit, mirabile hoc genus allocutionis sine idiomate, sonitu, motuque labiorum pure sympatheticum&lt;a0&gt;h&lt;/a0&gt; esse, ac solum iconis vultum varia serenissimæ frontis eradiatione, velut quibusdam notis ac nutibus, tam apposite, dextre, &amp; perspicue exprimere sensa &amp; affectus pro re nata, ut nulla exquisitissimorum verborum facundia melius præstare id possit: eamdem vultui divini Filioli vim inesse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011103Z">
        <seg>[248] [as she herself ingenuously confessed.] When, among other things, she was asked how she perceived the Mother of God speaking to her in this image, she answered most simply that this marvelous kind of address was without idiom, sound, or movement of the lips, purely sympathetic. and that only the icon's face, by various radiations of its most serene brow, as it were by certain signs and nods, so aptly, skillfully, and clearly expressed the feelings and affections appropriate to the occasion, that the most exquisite eloquence of words could not do so better: that the same power resides in the face of the divine Son;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[249] Accidit permissu Dei, quod invidus sathan cuidam regulari communitati Limæ inseverat discordiarum zizania.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011106Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011106Z">
        <seg>[249] It happened by God's permission that the envious Satan had sown the tares of discord among a certain religious community in Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[24] Hæc notata fuere ab iis, qui ipso translationis die summo mane effossum Rosæ cadaver e veteri tumba lignea transposuere in novam, [solenni pompa transfertur,] itidem cedrinam, at intus forisque deauratam, &amp; duplici sera præmunitam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031432Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031432Z">
        <seg>These were the notes taken by those who, on the very morning of the translation, removed Rose's corpse from its old wooden tomb and transferred it to a new one, solemnly carried in a procession, likewise cedar, but gilded inside and out, and secured with a double lock.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[24] [ac confessariis suis.] Ex his quilibet facillime æstimarit, quam accurate suis confessariis obtemperarit Rosa ad huc mortalis: nam ex quo vestem S. Patris Dominici assumpserat, ab ore suorum patrum spiritualium pendebat tota, &amp; quidquid hi vel obiter innuissent, id Virgini non mandatum, sed cæleste oraculum videbatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000909Z">
        <seg>[24] [and to her confessors.] From this anyone could easily see how precisely Rosa had obeyed her confessors up to this point: for ever since she had taken the habit of St. Dominic, she hung entirely on the words of her spiritual fathers, and whatever they even hinted at seemed not a command to the Virgin but a heavenly oracle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[250] Verum paulo post a confessario solicitius examinata, [in Religioso cœtu exortam, precibus dissipavit] nec ausa quidquam reticere, totam ingenue seriem exposuit, ut pridie inter simplices preces utrumque tum Deiparæ, tum Filioli vultum invenerat non modo chalybis instar prædurum, asperum, inexorabilem, sed &amp; severe minacem, austerum, ac manifesta indignatione caperatum: ut frustra conata fuerat Filium emollire per Matrem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011112Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011112Z">
        <seg>[250] But a little later, more carefully examined by her confessor, [having arisen in the religious assembly, she dissipated it with prayers] and not daring to conceal anything, she ingenuously laid out the whole series of events, that the day before, during simple prayers, she had found the face of both the Mother of God and her little Son not only as hard as steel, asper, inexorable, but also severely threatening, austere, and possessed by manifest indignation: so that she had tried in vain to soften the Son through the Mother;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[251] [(ab alia picta &amp; domestica Deiparæ effigie] Non abs re fuerit paucis subnectere, quæ Rosæ cum alia Deiparentis imagine, non sculpta sed picta, in Gundisalvi oratorio contigerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011115Z">
        <seg>[251] [(from another painted and domestic image of the Virgin Mary) It would not be out of place to briefly relate what happened to Rosa with another image of the Virgin Mary, not carved but painted, in Gundisalvi's oratory.]</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[252] [etiam varios faveres obtinuit)] Accidit, quod in oratorio Maria de Usategui, coram Rosa &amp; duobus familiaribus instituta de beneficiis beatissimæ Virginis spirituali collatione, incideret in magnalia, quæ ad imaginem Deiparæ in Atocha&lt;a0&gt;i&lt;/a0&gt;, vico Matritensibus vicino, manus superna dignatur operari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011119Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011119Z">
        <seg>[252] [he also received various favors] It happened that in the oratory of Maria de Usategui, during a spiritual conference on the benefits of the most blessed Virgin held before Rosa and two family members, he came upon the great miracles that the divine hand deigns to work at the image of the Mother of God at Atocha, a village near Madrid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[253] [cumque ad illam ornandam flores deessent,] Sed redeundum tantisper ad sacellum Rosarii, cui Rosa toto anno per omne Sabbatum flores, quos ipsa coluerat, fasciculatim aut mittebat aut deferebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011122Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011122Z">
        <seg>[253] [And since there were no flowers to adorn it,] But for the time being she had to return to the chapel of the Rosary, to which Rosa, every Sabbath throughout the year, would send or bring in bunches the flowers she herself had cultivated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[254] Unius idea reperta fuit in Virginis pugillaribus, hoc tenore: “Memoria pro vestimento, quod ego Rosa de S. Maria, [ei spirituale vestimentum contexuit.] indignum mancipium Reginæ angelorum, ordior contexere Virgini Dei Matri, auxiliante Domino.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011128Z">
        <seg>One idea was found in the Virgin's pamphlets, to the following effect: "In memory of the vestment that I, Rosa of St. Mary, unworthy handmaid of the Queen of Angels, have woven for the Virgin Mother of God, with the help of the Lord."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[256] Subinde Rosam ad sacras ædes processuram comitabatur honestatis causa germanus frater, [ubicumque positum erat,] doluitque toties sibi cum ea subsistendum esse, quoties Soror ibidem ad stipulas de terra sublevandas sese inclinabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011156Z">
        <seg>[256] He would accompany Rosa to the sacred buildings as a mark of honor, [wherever she was stationed,] and he grieved that he had to stand with her so often whenever the Sister bent down there to lift the stakes from the ground.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[257] At graviter modesteque replicuit Virgo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011202Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011202Z">
        <seg>But the Virgin replied gravely and modestly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[258] [quarum unam, quæ in horto proreginæ exaruerat,] Rosa in suo hortulo plantaverat libanotim&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;, (rosmarinus vulgo dicitur) ejusque tres arbusculas distinctis cespitibus ita in crucis formam coëgerat, ut singulæ glebæ seorsum accuratam montis Calvariæ figuram undequaque exprimerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011208Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011208Z">
        <seg>[258] [one of which, which had withered in the queen's garden,] She had planted a rosemary in her little garden (commonly called rosemary) and had arranged its three small shrubs in separate mounds in the shape of a cross, so that each mound, taken by itself, would accurately reproduce the form of Calvary from every angle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[259] [mirabiliter pristino virori restituit.] Vix in quartum diem exsucca plantula penes Rosam fuit, cum speciosior, quam ante fuerat, undique reviruit, novis ramusculorum comata pullulis, novo radicularum ac medullarum succo rediviva, plexilique virgularum obedientia in pristinam crucis figuram reformata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011215Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011215Z">
        <seg>[259] [miraculously restored to its former vigor.] Barely by the fourth day had the shriveled little plant beside Rosa dried out, when, more beautiful than before, it burst forth anew from every side, with new, bushy shoots of twigs, revived by the fresh sap of its roots and pith, and the obedience of its boughs reforming it into its original cross-shape.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[25] Adfuit paulo post archiepiscopus cum suis, comitante S. Officii judice ordinario D. Feliciano de Vega canonico metropolitano, [comitantibus illustribus viris,] &amp; archipræsulis vicario generali.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031438Z">
        <seg>Shortly thereafter the archbishop arrived with his entourage, accompanied by the ordinary judge of the Holy Office, Don Feliciano de Vega, metropolitan canon, by illustrious men, and by the archbishop's vicar general.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[25] Sed redeamus ad matrem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000912Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000912Z">
        <seg>But let us return to the mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[260] [Ad imaginem S. Catharinæ Senensis ornandam] Digrediendum nunc ad hujus seraphicæ magistræ imaginem, crucis ac stigmatum characteribus insignitam, quacum Rosæ plurimum fuit commercii, nec sine prodigiis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011218Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011218Z">
        <seg>[260] [To adorn the image of St. Catherine of Siena] We must now turn to the image of this seraphic teacher, marked with the characters of the cross and stigmata, with whom the Rosary had the closest connection, and not without miracles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[261] [prodigiose pecunia subministratur,] Sed dum circuibat charam iconem, dum vestiebat, dum plicas distinguebat, temperare sibi a lachrymis, ab osculis, ab alloquiis præ ardore non poterat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011221Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011221Z">
        <seg>[261] [A prodigious sum of money is provided,] But while she was circling the precious icon, while she was dressing it, while she was arranging its folds, she could not, for her ardor, restrain herself from tears, from kisses, from addresses;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[262] Alio tempore optabat Rosa, caryophylli&lt;a0&gt;e&lt;/a0&gt; copiam ad manum esse, [&amp; flores, quos illa tempestas anni non ferebat,] qua sacram imaginem condecoraret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011230Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011230Z">
        <seg>At another time, Rosa wished to have an abundance of carnations on hand, [and flowers which that season of the year did not produce,] with which to adorn the sacred image.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[263] Postridie summo mane (siquidem is processioni decretus erat dies) properarunt ad Rosam, [in horto sanctæ Virginis inveniuntur.] ut quod in ornanda imagine reliquum erat, cum ea perficerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011233Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011233Z">
        <seg>The next morning at daybreak (for that day had been appointed for the procession) they hurried to Rose, [who are found in the garden of the holy Virgin,] so that they might complete with her whatever remained to be done in adorning the image.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[264] [Deinde B Catharina cuidam ornatus adjutrici] Alia vice Rosa ad vestiendam sanctæ Catharinæ iconem cum aliis adjutricibus vocarat Mariam Euphemiam de Parejas viduam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011240Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011240Z">
        <seg>[264] [Then B. Catherine to a certain assistant in the adornment] On another occasion, Rosa had summoned Mary Euphemia de Parejas, a widow, along with other helpers to dress the icon of Saint Catherine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[265] [duplex beneficium contulit,] Aliquando eadem, quæ supra, Francisca de Montoya Rosam in exornanda S. Catharinæ Senensis icone per totam noctem adjuverat, completoque opere, dum nonnihil quietura discedebat, ut post aliquot horas festivæ processioni interveniret, Rosa digressam B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011243Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011243Z">
        <seg>[265] [She conferred a twofold benefit,] At one time the same Francisca de Montoya had helped Rosa all night in decorating the icon of St. Catherine of Siena, and when the work was finished, as she was departing to rest a little so that she could take part in the festive procession a few hours later, Rosa, having strayed, saw the Blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[266] [ac sanctam Rosam arthritide,] Beata Catharina in reliquas imaginis suæ vestiarias &amp; excultrices tam munifica, utique in suam Rosam illiberalis non fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011246Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011246Z">
        <seg>[266] [and Saint Rose, who suffered from arthritis,] Blessed Catherine was so munificent toward the vestments and carvers of the rest of her image that, of course, she was not stingy toward her own Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[267] Dies erat S. Laurentii martyris, quando sub vesperam medicus, [quæ manum ejus invaserat,] inspecta manu, purulentam meliceridem&lt;a0&gt;i&lt;/a0&gt;, aut aliud quodcumque genus apostematis subesse ratus, lenta cataplasmata&lt;a1&gt;k&lt;/a1&gt; decrevit applicanda, jussitque in diem posterum distrahendo sanguini, venam in lævo brachio aperiri scalpello.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011252Z">
        <seg>[267] It was the day of St. Lawrence the martyr, when toward evening the physician, [which had invaded his hand,] having inspected the hand, a purulent melicerid&lt;a0&gt;i&lt;/a0&gt;, or whatever other kind of abscess was thought to be underneath, he decreed that slow poultices&lt;a1&gt;k&lt;/a1&gt; be applied, and ordered that the next day, by drawing blood, a vein in the left arm be opened with a scalpel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[268] Post horulam supervenit quæstor, vidensque Rosam utraque manu expeditissime operantem, [confestim liberavit,] adstupuit, ac leniter interpellans, “Itane (inquit) dextra tua valet, ut ad ista sufficiat?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011255Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011255Z">
        <seg>[268] After an hour the magistrate arrived, and seeing Rosa working with both hands as swiftly as possible, he immediately released her, was astonished, and gently interrupting her, said, "Is your right hand so strong that it can handle these tasks?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[269] [sicut ipsa Virgo candide fassa est.] Accersitus postridie medicus examinavit manum, deinde Virginem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011258Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011258Z">
        <seg>[269] [as the Virgin herself candidly confessed.] The next day, the doctor was summoned, examined the hand, and then the Virgin;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[26] Post decantatum Euangelium, in editiori suggestu comparuit facundus prædicator, [&amp; post varias ceremonias] dicturus ad populum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031515Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031515Z">
        <seg>After the Gospel had been chanted, a eloquent preacher appeared on the raised pulpit, [and after various ceremonies] about to speak to the people.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[26] Quæstor Gundisalvus (de quo supra, &amp; frequentius infra) tacitus mirabatur exhaustam viribus Rosam, [indefesso manuum labore,] ac diurnis operibus extreme lassam, nihilominus in mediam usque noctem labori manuum insistere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203455Z">
        <seg>[26] Quaestor Gundisalvus (of whom mention is made above, and more frequently below) marveled in silence that Rosa, though drained of strength, [with tireless labor of her hands,] and utterly weary from her daily tasks, nevertheless persisted in manual work until the middle of the night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[271] Neque id satis erat: nam siqua interveniebat aut festæ diei celebritas aut solemnis statio, [ex consilio confessariorum suorum,] etiam quater, imo quinquies intra septimanam divino hoc Edulio avidissime fruebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011317Z">
        <seg>[271] Nor was that enough: for if any festival or solemn station intervened, [by the advice of his confessors,] he would even four, nay five times within a week, most eagerly partake of this divine Edule.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[272] [idque semper faciebat cum tanto fervore,] Hic vero notatu dignum est, quod Virgo in tanta communicandi frequentia, simulque tanta immaculatæ suæ conscientiæ serenitate, nihilominus, quotiescumque ad divinum epulum accedendum erat, toties sacramentalem confessionem præmittebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011320Z">
        <seg>[272] [and she always did this with such fervor,] It is truly worthy of note, however, that the Virgin, in such frequent communion and at the same time with such serenity of her immaculate conscience, nevertheless, whenever she was to approach the divine banquet, she would always precede it with sacramental confession;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[273] [ut sacerdotes ei sacram Hostiam porrigentes,] Quadam feria secunda Pentecostes in sacello Rosarii sacris operante P. Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011323Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011323Z">
        <seg>[273] [as the priests were presenting him with the sacred Host,] On a certain Tuesday after Pentecost, in the chapel of the Rosary, while the Mass was being celebrated by Father Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[274] [faciem ipsius coruscantem viderent,] Magister Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011326Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011326Z">
        <seg>[274] [they would see his face shining,] Master Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[275] P. Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011329Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011329Z">
        <seg>Father Friar</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[276] Voluit Deus per extima hæc &amp; sensibilia, utcumque manifestari foris, [Ipsa Virgo explicare non poterat] quanta devotionis pyra flagrabat intus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011336Z">
        <seg>God willed that through these outward and sensible things, however manifest they were on the outside, [the Virgin herself could not explain] how great a pyre of devotion was burning within.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[277] Ex ore Virginis hæc audierat confessarius P. M.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011339Z">
        <seg>[277] The confessor, P. M., had heard these words from the Virgin's own mouth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[278] [quo sic confortabatur ac satiebatur,] Ne isti solares effectus omnino laterent abditi, providit Christus, dum ex ipsismet foras redundare fecit usque in Rosæ corpusculum duo prodigia, robur &amp; saturitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011345Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011345Z">
        <seg>[278] [by which she was thus comforted and satisfied,] So that these solar effects might not at all remain hidden and secret, Christ provided, while he caused two prodigies—strength and fullness—to overflow from them themselves even into Rose's little body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[279] [ut diu alimento corporali non indigeret.] Rogata cibum sumere, maxime quod pridiano exhausta jejunio videretur refectione indigere, meminissetque diem Domincum a lege jejunii exsortem esse, respondebat, præ ingenti satietate a Mensa Domini, alium se cibum admittere nondum posse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011351Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011351Z">
        <seg>[279] [so that she would not need bodily food for a long time.] When asked to take food, especially since she seemed, after the previous day's fast, to need refreshment and would remember that Sunday was exempt from the law of fasting, she would answer that, because of her great satisfaction from the Lord's Table, she could not yet admit any other food.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[27] Mirabilius est, quod Ludovica Urtado de Bustamante, [&amp; solerti industria] vidua Bartholomæi Alphonsi de Lombrera vexilliferi, asseverat in Processu, Rosam tot sanctis exercitiis interdiu noctuque districtam, tot affatim morbis impeditam, tot supernis raptibus crebro absorptam, unico tamen die tot laborum pensa solitam absolvere, quot alia quævis etiam celerrima, peritissima, vix integro quatriduo absolvisset: &amp; ne putemus ob tantam festinæ Rosæ velocitatem, (ut fit) hiulca, manca, imperfecta prodiisse opera;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000918Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000918Z">
        <seg>[27] More marvelous is the fact that Ludovica Urtado de Bustamante, [by her diligent industry] widow of Bartholomew Alphonsus de Lombrera, standard-bearer, affirms in the Process, that Rosa was so occupied with holy exercises day and night, so impeded by a multitude of illnesses, so often absorbed in heavenly raptures, yet accustomed to complete in a single day so many tasks that any other, even the swiftest and most skilled, would have scarcely finished in four days: and lest we suppose that, because of Rose's great haste, her works were (as is often the case) produced hollow, incomplete, and imperfect;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[27] [loco magis conspicuo collocatur.] Finita panegyri, &amp; solemni Sacrificio ad aram consummato, archiepiscopus pontificalem assumens habitum, cum pedo &amp; infula processit ad tumulum, sacris ministris, &amp; quatuor ex metropolitano collegio dignioribus medius.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031543Z">
        <seg>[27] [It is placed in a more conspicuous location.] When the panegyric was finished and the solemn sacrifice at the altar completed, the archbishop, donning his pontifical vestments, proceeded to the tomb with the crozier and pectoral cross, accompanied by the sacred ministers and four of the most dignified members of the metropolitan college.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[280] [Summa cum reverentia] Ceterum quanta religione solita fuerit Rosa venerari hoc Sacramentum, sicubi aut immolabatur in Missæ sacrificio, aut prostabat adorandum in altari, utcumque supra attigimus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011354Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011354Z">
        <seg>[280] [With the utmost reverence] Moreover, how religiously Rose was accustomed to venerate this Sacrament, whether it was being offered in the sacrifice of the Mass or exposed for adoration on the altar, as we have already touched upon above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[281] Idem Virgini erat immotæ attentionis tenor, quando in hierotheca expositum venerabile Sacramentum adorabatur a populo, [&amp; immotæ mentis attentione] ut fit in publicis XL horarum supplicationibus: illic, a mane usque ad vesperam continua &amp; prorsus immota geniculatione hærebat Rosa oblita prandii, neque vel guttulam acceptabat in refrigerium, contenta jubilo atque deliciis, quas percipiebat ex corporali Sponsi præsentia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011401Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011401Z">
        <seg>[281] The same was the Virgin's unmoving tenor of attention, when the venerable Sacrament was exposed in the oratory and adored by the people, [and with unmoving mental attention] as is done in public Forty Hours' devotions: There, from morning until evening, she remained on her knees in a continuous and utterly motionless posture, Rose, having forgotten her lunch, nor did she accept even a drop for refreshment, content with the joy and delights she received from the bodily presence of her Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[282] Neque segnior erat ad cultum hujus adorandi mysterii, [adorabat hoc venerabile Sacramentum] etiam quando extra conspectum erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011404Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011404Z">
        <seg>Nor was he any less diligent in the observance of this adored mystery, [adoring this venerable Sacrament] even when it was out of sight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[283] Nullus ei manuum labor jucundior erat, quam quo altare, [&amp; ad illius ornatum strenue laborabat;] tabernaculum, aut in majori hebdomada S. Sepulchrum veniebat exornandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011407Z">
        <seg>No task was more pleasing to him than decorating the altar, the tabernacle, or, during Holy Week, the Holy Sepulchre.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[284] [Imo, cum classis Calvinistarum] Denique erga SS. Eucharistiæ mysterium is Rosæ affectus erat, quo maluit pro illo sanguinem vitamque expendere, quam flores.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011413Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011413Z">
        <seg>[284] [Rather, with the Calvinist faction] Finally, toward the mystery of the Holy Eucharist, Rose was so disposed that she preferred to shed her blood and her life for it rather than flowers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[285] [ad littora Peruana appulisset,] Tristius interea nuntium supervenit usque ad Rosam, nempe inimicos fidei, ex alto navium in lembos, inde in arenam prosultare manipulatim, &amp; ad portas civitatis globatim procedere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011419Z">
        <seg>[285] [having landed on the Peruvian shores,] Meanwhile a more distressing report reached Rosa: namely, that the enemies of the faith, from the ships, were jumping down into boats, then onto the sand in squads, and advancing toward the city gates in a body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[286] His facie rutilante prolatis, exemit vagina forfices, [pro defensione ac veritate sacræ Eucharistiæ] sibique vestem, qua longior in terram defluebat, alacriter præcidit, manicisque in cubitum usque replicatis, quod reliquum erat tunicæ, intra cingulum, usque at talos attraxit, cothurnos subere sublevatos exuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011425Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011425Z">
        <seg>[286] With his face flushed by these words, he drew scissors from his sheath [in defense of and for the truth of the sacred Eucharist], and eagerly cut off the part of his garment that hung longer on the ground, and, rolling up his sleeves to the elbow, drew what remained of his tunic inside his belt, down to his ankles, He took off the cork-soled shoes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[287] Hæc ita pronuntiabat, ut ex oculorum scintillis, [sanguinem fundere parabat,] ex impavidi vultus constantia, ex vivaci tono heroicæ vocis, ceteroque gestu animosæ Viraginis facile conjiceretur, Rosam pro Eucharistico altaris Agno leænam induisse: nec circumstantes feminæ suismet credebant oculis, videntes Amazonem, quæ hactenus non nisi viva mansuetudinis idea fuerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011429Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011429Z">
        <seg>[287] She spoke these words in such a way that from the sparks in her eyes, [she was preparing to shed blood,] from the steadfastness of her fearless countenance, from the lively tone of her heroic voice, and from the entire bearing of the spirited Maiden, it was easy to infer that Rosa had donned the lioness's guise for the Eucharistic Lamb of the altar: nor did the women standing by believe their own eyes, seeing an Amazon who until now had been nothing but the living embodiment of meekness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[288] Defuit ergo non martyrio animus, sed animo martyrium, [&amp; sæpe pro eadem martyrium subire cupiebat.] toties &amp; ante, &amp; postea votis ardentissimis exoptatum: nam crebro deflebat sortem suam, quod pro conditione sexus infidelium regiones adire non poterat, pro Christo millies mortem quæsitura: dolebat, se non illis temporibus locisque natam, ubi tyrannica persecutio Christianorum sanguine orchestras, fora, amphitheatra purpurabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011435Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011435Z">
        <seg>[288] Thus it was not a lack of martyrdom that failed him, but a lack of spirit; [and he often longed to undergo martyrdom for the same cause.] so often before and after, most ardently desired in her prayers: for she often lamented her lot, that on account of her sex she could not go into the regions of the infidels, where she would have sought death a thousand times for Christ's sake; she grieved that she was not born in those times and places when tyrannical persecution stained the orchestras, the forums, and the amphitheaters with the blood of Christians;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[28] Neque hic stetit anxius amor ac solicitudo filiæ, [egestatem parentum suorum sublevare conabatur,] conantis omni ingenio parentum egestatem sublevare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203456Z">
        <seg>[28] Nor did the anxious love and concern for his daughter stop here; he was trying with all his ingenuity to relieve his parents' poverty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[290] [unum ex confessariis suis hortabatur,] Accidit, unum ex Rosæ confessariis tractare de sua missione ad annuntiandum vicinis barbaris Euangelium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011450Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011450Z">
        <seg>[290] [He was encouraging one of his confessors,] It happened that he was talking with one of Rose's confessors about his mission to announce the Gospel to the neighboring barbarians.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[291] Hic Virgo, tametsi de suis piis operibus humillime sentiret, [ut impavide missionem apud Indos institueret,] nihilominus, ut iturienti quantacumque poterat calcaria figeret, concepta ingenti fiducia promisit, universo exercitiorum suorum merito se illi in sancta expeditione succursuram, dummodo is vicissim pro rata ipsam participem vellet totius spiritualis lucri, quod ex infidelium conversione erat reportaturus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011453Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011453Z">
        <seg>[291] This Virgin, although she felt most humbly about her own pious works, [so that she might fearlessly establish the mission among the Indians,] nevertheless, to spur the departing one on as much as she could, she promised with great confidence that, by the merit of all her exercises, she would assist him in the holy expedition, provided that he, in turn, would make her a participant, in proportion to her contribution, in all the spiritual gain he was to receive from the conversion of the infidels.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[292] Eodem fervore spiritus nitebatur pia Virgo religiosos viros (quotquot aptos noverat) ad gentilium conversionem succedere: [&amp; alios viros aptos excitabat ad idem opus,] præsertim sui Ordinis Fratres rogabat, monebat, obtestabatur, huc huc suos in litteris profectus intentione tempestiva destinarent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011457Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011457Z">
        <seg>With the same fervor of spirit, the pious Virgin strove to have religious men (whichever she knew to be suitable) succeed in the conversion of the Gentiles; she also stirred up other suitable men for the same work, especially she begged, admonished, and implored the Brothers of her Order to send their men, well-versed in letters, to this work with timely dispatch.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[293] Asseverabat gravi sensu &amp; impetu, se nisi feminam natura finxisset, [illud per se factura, nisi sexus obstitisset.] hoc primum in votis fuisse habituram, ut decursa studiorum litterariorum encyclopedia, se totam missioni euangelicæ devoveret, cupida adire gentes ferocissimas, belluinas, anthropophagas&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;, dummodo suis Indis, adhuc gentilismo implicitis, opem ac remedium sua catechesi, sudore, sanguine impertiret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011500Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011500Z">
        <seg>[293] She asserted with great earnestness and force that, had nature not made her a woman, she would have accomplished that on her own, had her sex not been an obstacle. that this would have been her first desire, that, having completed the encyclopedia of literary studies, she would devote herself entirely to the evangelical mission, eager to go to the fiercest, most savage, man-eating peoples, provided she could bring help and remedy to her own Indians, still entangled in paganism, through her catechesis, sweat, and blood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[294] [Etiam malos Christianos monebat,] Nec tepidior ei zelus &amp; cura fuit erga periclitantes Christianos, si quos noverat aut per culpam mortalem a statu divinæ amicitiæ infeliciter excidisse, aut capitali aliquo vitio ad certum interitum illaqueari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011503Z">
        <seg>[294] [He also admonished wicked Christians,] Nor was his zeal and concern any less fervent toward Christian souls in peril, whether he knew any who had, by mortal sin, wretchedly fallen from the state of divine friendship, or who were entangled by some capital vice and thus drawn into certain ruin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[295] [aut moneri curabat, ut resipiscerent,] Hæc plerumque misericors Virgo tam luculento offerebat pectoris incendio, tantoque inflammati cordis affectu, ut non raro audientes compunxerit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011506Z">
        <seg>[295] [or she would see to it that they were warned to repent,] This, the merciful Virgin, would most often offer with such a brilliant burning in her breast, and with such a passionate affection of her heart, that she would not seldom move those who heard her to compunction.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[296] Evenit, dum adhuc matri cohabitabat Rosa, juvenem genere, quam moribus nobiliorem Vincentium Montesium Venegas in eadem platea haud procul a Rosæ ædibus sortiri domicilium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011512Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011512Z">
        <seg>It happened, while Rosa was still living with her mother, that a young man of noble birth, even more distinguished by his manners, Vincent Montes Venegas, took up residence on the same street not far from Rosa's house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[297] At Virgo divinitus edocta secretum, quod corde premebat Vincentius, [divinitus edocta redarguit,] efflictim misereri cœpit fœtentis animæ, in qua stagnabant lubricæ juventutis impura desideria;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011518Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011518Z">
        <seg>But the Virgin, divinely instructed, rebuked the secret that weighed on Vincent's heart and began to pity the stinking soul, in which the impure desires of a slippery youth were stagnating.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[298] Hac improvisa Virginis concione ictus Vincentius, [&amp; ad pœnitentiam adducit.] dejecit oculos, seseque introspiciens, aliquantisper obmutuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011522Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011522Z">
        <seg>Struck by the Virgin's sudden sermon, Vincent lowered his eyes and, looking into himself, fell silent for a while.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[299] Prodigium erat, siqua persona cuicumque vitio obnoxia Rosam accessisset, [Præterea mulierem iracundam corrigit,] nec ab ea melior discessisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011525Z">
        <seg>It was a miracle if anyone, no matter how flawed, approached Rosa and did not come away better.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[29] Interea Limam pervenere litteræ apostolicæ, quibus auctorati judices commissarii novum ibidem erexere tribunal ex formula a sacra Rituum Congregatione præscripta, [&amp; legitime constituti judices] ad effectum examinandi testes de vita, &amp; gestis ancillæ Dei, sororis Rosæ a sancta Maria.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031636Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031636Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, letters apostolic arrived in Lima, by which the judges commissioners were authorized to establish a new tribunal there according to the formula prescribed by the Sacred Congregation of Rites, and the judges legitimately constituted, for the purpose of examining witnesses concerning the life and deeds of the handmaid of God, Sister Rose of Saint Mary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[29] [eos ægrotantes obsequio juvabat,] Si quando patrem aut matrem dejecisset corporis ægritudo, nil Filiæ antiquius, nil sanctius, nil solemnius erat, quam, sepositis omnibus, decumbenti servire, medicinas conquirere, suis manibus parare, inferre, bolatimque porrigere esculenta, refrigeria adaptare, applicare lenitiva.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203456Z">
        <seg>[29] [she helped the sick with care,]If at any time her father or mother was laid low by illness, nothing was more important, more sacred, or more solemn for the daughter than, setting all else aside, to serve the invalid, to seek out medicines, to prepare them with her own hands, to administer them, to bring food in small portions, to provide cooling remedies, and to apply soothing lotions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[2] Illustrissimus Limæ archiepiscopus prodierat, funebri pompæ honorem suæ præsentiæ impertiturus, [præsul Limanus cum capitulo, Ordines religiosi,] sed usque ad Gundisalvi ædes per densissimas spectatorum myriades eluctari diffidens, permeatu frustra tentato, maluit ad D. Dominici templum deflectere, ibique virgineum funus ad valvas expectare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030539Z">
        <seg>[2] The Most Illustrious Archbishop of Lima had come forth, to lend the funeral procession the honor of his presence, [the Lima prelate with his chapter, the religious Orders,] but, not trusting himself to force a way through the densest multitudes of spectators to Gundisalvo's house, and after a fruitless attempt to pass, he preferred to turn aside to the church of St. Dominic, and there await the virgin's funeral at the doors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[2] [&amp; in Baptismo accipit nomen Elizabethæ] Nascenti Rosæ paterna domus obtigit in vico S. Dominici, ac pone quidem juxta Ædem S. Spiritus&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt;, ut vel ipse locus videatur futuræ tum sanctimoniæ tum instituti signasse præsagium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203313Z">
        <seg>[2] [&amp; at Baptism she receives the name Elizabeth] When Rose was born, the paternal house on St. Dominic Street fell to her lot, and indeed right next to the Church of the Holy Spirit&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt;, so that even the place itself seems to have foretold both her future sanctity and her institute.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[2] [&amp; in Baptismo accipit nomen Elizabethæ] Nascenti Rosæ paterna domus obtigit in vico S. Dominici, ac pone quidem juxta Ædem S. Spiritus&lt;s0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;f&lt;/a1&gt;&lt;/s0&gt;, ut vel ipse locus videatur futuræ tum sanctimoniæ tum instituti signasse præsagium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T202332Z">
        <seg>[2] [&amp; at Baptism she receives the name Elizabeth] When Rose was born, the paternal house on St. Dominic Street fell to her lot, and indeed right next to the Church of the Holy Spiritf, so that even the place itself seems to have foretold both her future sanctity and her institute.&lt;s0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;&lt;/a1&gt;&lt;/s0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[300] [Religiosi cujusdam vilem passionem] Virum quemdam professionem Religiosum illa non minus sordida, quam fatua ac monstruosa fumantis tabaci&lt;a0&gt;e&lt;/a0&gt; ingluvies usque ad præsentissimam corporis animæque perniciem deduxerat, ita ut de utriusque remedio conclamatum fere putaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011531Z">
        <seg>[300] [A religious man's vile passion] A certain man whose religious profession had been led by that foul, as well as foolish and monstrous, gluttony for smoking tobacco to the very brink of the destruction of both body and soul, so that a remedy for both was almost universally cried out for.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[301] [ac inveteratam consuetudinem,] Facile annuit Virgo, miserta periclitantis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011534Z">
        <seg>[301] [and an age-old custom,] The Virgin readily agreed, pitying the one in peril;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[302] [morbosque inde ortos oratione sua tollit,] Rosa in unico sanavit hanc insaniam, curatura plurimos, ubi rite fuerit implorata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011540Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011540Z">
        <seg>[302] [and with her speech she removes the diseases that arise from it,] Rosa healed this madness in a single instance, and will cure many more, if she is properly implored.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[303] Unum addidero &amp; finiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011546Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011546Z">
        <seg>I will add one thing and finish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[304] Intime hæc sauciabant cor Virginis, prompto compassionis affectui tam flexile ac morigerum: [qui ob imminens Dei judicium nimis anxiabatur,] unde acturum injunxit Petro, infirmum adiret, suoque ex nomine juberet fidentius sperare de immensa optimi &amp; benignissimi Numinis misericordia: se quidem precaturam pro felici ipsius transitu, at simul ex nunc offerre se de suorum piorum exercitiorum qualicumque censu, quidquid moribundo ad supplendam meritorum vacuitatem opus sit, imo totum is sibi vendicet, &amp;, si quod bonum opus a Rosa umquam per totam illius vitam hactenus prodierat, integre a se in ægrum dono liberrimo translatum velle, hoc ipse, velut suum, Judici exhibeat, desinatque interim abjecte &amp; humiliter sentire de magnifica liberalitate Altissimi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011553Z">
        <seg>[304] These things wounded the heart of the Virgin most intimately, so prompt to compassion and so pliable and obedient: [who, on account of the imminent judgment of God, was exceedingly anxious,] wherefore he enjoined Peter to visit the sick man and, in his name, to bid him hope more confidently in the immense mercy of the most good and most benign Deity: that she would indeed pray for his happy passing, but at the same time offer herself from that moment, whatever merit she might have acquired through her pious exercises, for whatever the dying man needed to make up for any deficiency in his merits; indeed, she would claim it all for herself, and, if any good work had ever proceeded from Rosa throughout her life up to now, she wishes to transfer it entire from herself as a most free gift to the sick man, so that he may present it, as if it were his own, to the Judge, and in the meantime cease from thinking meanly and humbly of the magnificent liberality of the Most High.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[305] Fideliter universa ad infirmum retulit Petrus, [de salute æterna securum reddi jubet.] &amp; confestim immodicus ille pavor gaudio lætioris spei locum cessit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011556Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011556Z">
        <seg>Peter faithfully reported everything to the sick man, [commanding him to be made secure of eternal salvation.] and immediately that excessive fear gave way to the joy of a more joyful hope;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[307] Alia vice Gaspar Flores Virginis pater sat grande volumen candidioris telæ (donatum, [&amp; telam sibi datam inter inopes distribuit,] an emptum nescio) domum attulit, ut inde conjux Maria de Oliva sibi, gregique tot filiorum de omnibus, quæ ad munditiem spectant, copiosius provideret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011602Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011602Z">
        <seg>On another occasion Gaspar Flores, the Virgin's father, brought home a large roll of whiter cloth (whether donated, or the cloth given to him among the poor, I do not know), so that his wife Maria de Oliva could more generously provide for herself and her flock of children in all things pertaining to cleanliness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[308] Mater, ut vidit filiam nil minus, quam de conficiendis sibi linteis cogitare, [matrique id ægre ferenti] suspicata quod erat, causam quæsivit, cur traditam sibi telam candidam, subtilem, bonam tardaret suis aptare usibus?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011605Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011605Z">
        <seg>[308] The mother, seeing that her daughter was thinking of nothing less than making herself linens, and the mother, displeased at this, suspected what was happening and asked why she was delaying to adapt the white, fine, good cloth entrusted to her for her own use.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[309] Possidebat Rosæ mater in suo mundo muliebri duas pallas, [(hujus etiam pallam pauperi virgini dederat)] seu oblonga &amp; a vertice in terram usque demissa pallia, femineum ac decens tegumentum, quotiescumque proceditur in publicum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011608Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011608Z">
        <seg>Rosa's mother possessed in her own feminine world two pallia, one of which she had also given to a poor virgin—that is, long, head-to-toe cloaks, a feminine and becoming covering whenever one appears in public.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[30] Hi coram P. magistro F. Gabriële de Zarate tunc Pronvinciali, [fragrans Sanctæ corpus examinant,] aperta arca lignea, repererunt virgineum corpusculum, expleto fere post obitum anno quintodecimo, consumptis undequaque indumentis, at ossibus integris, &amp; adhuc sicca hinc inde carne obductis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031649Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031649Z">
        <seg>[30] Before Father Master F. Gabriel de Zarate, then Provincial, [while examining the fragrant body of the Saint,] they opened the wooden chest and found the virgin's little body, almost fifteen years after her death, with all her garments consumed, but her bones intact and still covered here and there with flesh that was dry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[30] [&amp; afflictos consolabatur.] Factum est, ac abundantius quidem, quam moribundæ Filiæ solicita pietas flagitarat: simul enim ut Virgo spiritum reddidit, matrem superne tanta solatiorum diluvia inundarunt, ut sub juramento fassa sit, diu cor suum capiendæ huic dulcedini angustum laborasse sub quadam tempestate gaudiorum, ac velut pectoris intersepta perrupturum, anhelis subsultasse tripudiis, ita quod in eodem Rosæ morientis conclavi quærendus sibi fuerit secretior angulus, in quo liberius sub tantæ lætitiæ suavissima divulsione palpitans respiraret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203456Z">
        <seg>[30] [&amp; consolated the afflicted.]It happened, and more abundantly indeed, than the solicitous piety of the dying Daughter had demanded: for as soon as the Virgin yielded up her spirit, such a flood of consolations from on high inundated the mother, that she confessed under oath, her heart had long laboured to contain this sweetness, under a certain tempest of joys, and as if her chest were about to burst, she leapt about with gasping, triumphant leaps, so that in the very same chamber of the dying Rose she had to seek a more secluded corner, in which, under the most exquisite tearing-asunder of such great joy, she could breathe more freely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[310] [remunerationem cum fœnore promittit.] Evenit, quod Virgo prædixerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011611Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011611Z">
        <seg>[310] [He promises repayment with interest.] What the Virgin had foretold came to pass;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[311] [Egenam ægramque nobilis prosapiæ virginem] Extra mœnia in extremis Limanæ urbis pomœriis habitabat Joanna de Bobadilla &amp; Azevedo, virgo illustris parentelæ, sed orphana, ac virtute, quam censu ditior: nam amissis parentibus, in arcto res erant, miseramque sat premebat occulta frugalis œconomiæ angustia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011618Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011618Z">
        <seg>[311] [A poor and ailing virgin of noble lineage] Outside the walls, in the outermost suburbs of the city of Lima, lived Joanna de Bobadilla y Azevedo, a virgin of illustrious parentage but an orphan, and richer in virtue than in wealth: for having lost her parents, her circumstances were straitened, and she was already sorely pressed by the hidden hardship of frugal economy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[312] [in domo materna curari jubet,] Joannam secreto adiit, metum anxietatemque ponere jussit, vacare in maternis ædibus locandum pretio conclave idoneum, &amp; usui peropportunum, eo concederet, ac de menstruo locationis sumptu pacisceretur cum matre, sibi deinde curam procurandæ solutionis totam relinqueret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011624Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011624Z">
        <seg>[312] [He orders her to be cared for in her mother's house,] he secretly approached Joanna, told her to cast aside her fear and anxiety, to secure a suitable and very convenient room to rent in her mother's house at a reasonable price, to have her mother agree on the monthly rent, and to leave the entire responsibility for arranging the payment to him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[313] [&amp; quibuslibet infirmis,] Rosæ, quoad matri convixit, nil jucundius accidere poterat, quam si permitteretur, cujuscumque sortis aut conditionis pauperes personas morbis afflictas, paternis inferre ædibus, ut illic suismet manibus assidue ministraret languentibus: nec solius tecti, cubilis, strati, sed &amp; medicamentorum, ciborumque solatio egentes affatim reficere conabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011627Z">
        <seg>[313] [&amp; to any sick person,] For Rose, as long as she lived with her mother, nothing could have been more delightful than if she had been permitted to bring into her father's house poor people of any sort or condition afflicted by illness, so that she could there constantly minister to the sick with her own hands: and she strove to abundantly refresh those in need, not only with shelter, a bed, and bedding, but also with the comfort of medicines and food.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[314] Mox illa primo obvium mendicabulum invitabat ad lares proprios, [etiamsi sordido morbo laborarent,] huic abstergebat, ungebat, alligabat purulenta ulcera, hujus mundabat vestes, reficiebat laceros pluderones&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;, repurgabat subnatas plicatim sordes, lavabat pedes &amp; impetiginosum verticem, nullique ministerio deerat, quo illius fami, siti, pressuræ levamen adferret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011630Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011630Z">
        <seg>[314] Soon she would invite the first beggar she met into her own home, [even if they were suffering from a filthy disease,] she would wipe this one, anoint him, bind up his pus-filled sores, she would clean this one's clothes, mend his tattered undergarments, scrape away the filth that had crept into the folds of his skin, washed his feet and his impetigined head, and was lacking in no service by which she might bring relief to his hunger, thirst, and pressure.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[315] Accidit, die quadam ab infirmæ mulieris cura Virginem tantisper ad maternas ædes divertere: [officiosissime inservit,] sensit curiosa mater in veste filiæ insolitam mucidi putoris graveolentiam: inde conjecto in illius togam attentiori oculo, notavit, hinc inde respersim aliquid purulentarum sordium fortuito adhæsisse pannis ex aliena sanie, quam Virgo in detersione ulcerosæ mulieris inscia contraxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011639Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011639Z">
        <seg>[315] It happened that one day, while tending to an infirm woman, the Virgin was for a short time diverted to her mother's house: [serving with the utmost care,] the curious mother detected in her daughter's garment an unusual, musty odor: then, examining the tunic more closely, she noticed that here and there some purulent filth had accidentally adhered to the cloth from another's pus, which the Virgin had unknowingly contracted while washing the ulcerous woman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[316] [&amp; semel de nausea sua] Aliud in hoc genere vix imitabile præstitit Rosa, quod silendum non est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011643Z">
        <seg>[316] [&amp; once about her nausea] Rosa performed another feat of this kind, scarcely imitable, which should not be kept silent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[317] Facile consensit homo, tali se putore exonerari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011649Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011649Z">
        <seg>The man readily agreed, thinking that such a stench would rid him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[318] [Ægrotantem hospitis sui amicum invisens,] Hinc sane credibiliora fiunt, quæ sequuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011655Z">
        <seg>[318] [Visiting the sick friend of his host,] Hence, indeed, what follows becomes more credible.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[319] Itaque his tuta præsidiis, cum honestissima matrona &amp; pedissequis in viam se conjicit, [eum statim morbo liberat;] prius tamen ad ædem sancti Dominici Missæ sacrificio interesse voluit, ac interim præmisit, qui nuntiaret ægro, se, uti petierat, a templo mox ei cum matrona adfuturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011701Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011701Z">
        <seg>[319] And so, with these safe precautions, she set out on the road with the most honorable matron and her attendants, [she immediately cured him of his illness;] but first she wished to attend the sacrifice of the Mass at the church of Saint Dominic, and in the meantime she sent ahead someone to announce to the sick man that she, as he had requested, would come to him from the temple shortly with the matron.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[31] Hinc iidem inspectores aulam capituli adiere, lustraturi primum Virginis sepulchrum, [primumque ejus sepulcrum visitant.] e quo fideles certatim hauriebant pulverem ad varia morborum genera depellenda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031658Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031658Z">
        <seg>[31] From there the same inspectors went to the chapter house to visit first the Virgin's sepulchre, from which the faithful eagerly gathered dust to ward off various kinds of diseases.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[320] Plura hujuscemodi curationum miracula in alium tantisper locum tempusque differo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011704Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011704Z">
        <seg>[320] I defer the many miracles of this kind of cure to another place and time for the time being.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[321] [ut lepido exemplo demonstratur.] Hic adstans juvencula Rosa miserta est bestiæ, &amp; innocenti simplicitate conversa pueriliter ad avem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011711Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011711Z">
        <seg>[321] [as is demonstrated by a charming example.] The young maiden Rosa, standing here, pitied the beast, and with innocent simplicity turned childishly to the bird;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[323] Quærebat Sponsi pietas occasionem, qua charam Virginem privilegio singulari securam redderet æternæ felicitatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011720Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011720Z">
        <seg>The Spouse's piety sought an opportunity by which to make the beloved Virgin secure in a singular privilege of eternal happiness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[324] Doctor Joannes Castillus aliquando inter alia sciscitabatur ex Virgine, an prædestinationis suæ certam notitiam cælitus revelatam accepisset?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011726Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011726Z">
        <seg>Doctor John of Castile once asked the Virgin, among other things, whether she had received a certain knowledge of her predestination revealed from heaven?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[325] [ac in articulo mortis ostendit,] Manifestius hæc patuere in ultimo Rosæ moribundæ decubitu: siquidem felicissimi sui transitus ad beatam requiem adeo certa erat, ut etiam præscierit, ab omni purgatorii mora immunem se fore.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011735Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011735Z">
        <seg>[325] [and at the point of death she showed,] These things were more manifestly evident in the dying Rose's final bed: for the most blessed passage of her soul to blessed rest was so certain that she even foresaw she would be free from all purgatorial delay.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[326] [aliamque mysticam visionem] Memorabilius est, quod Rosam, dum hortensem suam cellulam incolebat, salutis æternæ securam reddidit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011739Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011739Z">
        <seg>[326] [and another mystical vision] It is more memorable that he made Rosa secure in eternal salvation while she was dwelling in her little garden cell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[327] [certior reddita est de sua prædestinatione] Porro præ magnitudine gaudii, quod se videbat rosam acceptabilem tuto vernare in pugillo Parvuli, nesciebat, nec anxie quærebat, quid de reliquis ipsa rosis ageret, nisi quod brevi consilio subitaneum inde sertum confecit, ac cælesti Infanti reverenter imposuit, qui Virgini suavissime arridens, data benedictione, disparuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011742Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011742Z">
        <seg>[327] [She was made certain of her predestination] Moreover, from the greatness of her joy, seeing that a lovely rose was safely springing to life in the Little One's palm, she neither knew nor anxiously cared what to do with the other roses, except that with a brief, spontaneous plan she made a wreath from them and reverently placed it upon the heavenly Infant, who, smiling most sweetly at the Virgin, gave his blessing and vanished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[328] Pari imperturbabilis certitudinis fruebatur solatio felix Rosa in securitate gratiæ, [&amp; confimato gratiæ statu,] divinæque amicitiæ: nam præterquam quod post quindecennales illas horrendarum obtenebrationum eclipses (de quibus supra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011745Z">
        <seg>[328] She enjoyed the same unshaken certainty and was happily comforted by Rosa in the security of grace, [and with her state of grace confirmed,] and of divine friendship: for besides that after those fifteen years of the most dreadful eclipses of obscuration (of which above chapter</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[329] Mirabatur cordata Rosa inusitatum rigidi censoris amphibolon&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;, [licet confessarius eam terrere conaretur.] permisitque illum ad satietatem loqui.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011751Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011751Z">
        <seg>[329] The level-headed Rosa was amazed at the unusual amphiboly of the stern censor, [though the confessor tried to frighten her.] and she allowed him to speak to his heart's content.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[32] Accidit demum anno MDCXL, quod Ordinis Prædicatorum in Romana curia Procurator generalis, [Ob decretum Urbani VIII publicus cultus impeditur,] litteris privatis Limam scriptis admonuerit Patres, habendam esse rationem exactissimam apostolicæ Constitutionis Urbani VIII anno MDCXXXIV, die V Julii emanatæ, quæ incipit: &lt;b0&gt;Cælestis Hierusalem cives&lt;/b0&gt; &amp;c&lt;a1&gt;d&lt;/a1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031710Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031710Z">
        <seg>[32] It finally happened in the year 1640 that the General Procurator of the Order of Preachers in the Roman Curia, [because of a decree of Urban VIII public worship is impeded,] in a private letter written to Lima, admonished the Fathers that the most exact account must be taken of the apostolic Constitution of Urban VIII, emanated on July 5, 1634, which begins: &lt;b0&gt;Cælestis Hierusalem cives&lt;/b0&gt; &amp;c&lt;a1&gt;d&lt;/a1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[32] Ex adverso credebat innocens Rosa, tempestivo (de quo supra) capillorum excidio, [cui mater illam destinabat,] ut quondam S. Catharina de Senis, amputasse spem matris, ac nuptiarum tendiculas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203458Z">
        <seg>On the contrary, the innocent Rosa believed that by the timely (as mentioned above) cutting of her hair, [which her mother had destined for her,] she had, like St. Catherine of Siena of old, cut off her mother's hope and the snares of marriage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[330] Eadem vis confidentiæ in Deum a prima ætate Rosam adversus qualibet mortalis vitæ difficultates, [Tanta erat ejus in Deo fiducia,] pavores, incommoda prævalide obarmarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011754Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011754Z">
        <seg>[330] That same strength of confidence in God, from her earliest years, had powerfully armed Rosa against any difficulties, fears, and hardships of mortal life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[331] Rosa, ut vidit eminus, utrumque parentem ad se venire, [quam ex similitudine quadam confirmaverat] processit obvia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011800Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011800Z">
        <seg>Rosa, seeing from a distance both her parents coming toward her—a fact she had confirmed by a certain resemblance—stepped out to meet them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[332] [ut bis in manifesto vitæ periculo] Nec defuit occasio, qua manifestaretur illa heroïca virginei pectoris soliditas inter discrimina ardua ac repentina.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011804Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011804Z">
        <seg>[332] [as if twice in manifest peril of life] Nor was there lacking an occasion to demonstrate that heroic steadfastness of the virgin's breast amid arduous and sudden dangers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[333] [nequaquam trepidaret,] Alia vice Limæ cum matre ac honestissimis matronis in quadriga revertebatur a festiva celebris ecclesiæ statione.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011807Z">
        <seg>[333] [She would by no means be flustered,] On another occasion, she was returning in a chariot from the festive station of a celebrated church in Lima, accompanied by her mother and the most honorable matrons.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[334] Cui tanta inerat divinæ protectionis fiducia in vitæ periculis, [&amp; in domestica penuria panem,] utique in necessariis vitæ sperandis subsidiis minor esse non potuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011810Z">
        <seg>[334] Who had such confidence in divine protection amid life's dangers, [and in having bread at home,] that he could not be wanting in the necessary means of life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[335] Alio tempore in eadem domo (pernecessarium illis regionibus condimentum) mel defuit, [magnam mellis copiam,] ejusque cupa fundo tenus exhausta arescebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011813Z">
        <seg>[335] At another time in the same house (a most necessary provision in those regions) there was a shortage of honey, [a great quantity of honey,] and its barrel was drying out, having been emptied to the bottom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[336] Gasparem Flores patrem Virginis premebat molesta ægritudo corporis, [&amp; necessariam pecuniam mirabiliter obtineret,] simul marcebat afflictus mœrore animus ob quinquaginta librarum debitum, cui præ inopia solvendo non erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011816Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011816Z">
        <seg>Gaspar Flores, the Virgin's father, was afflicted by a troublesome bodily illness, [and would miraculously obtain the necessary money,] while at the same time his spirit withered under the sorrow of a fifty-pound debt, which, because of his poverty, he had no means to pay.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[337] Ejuscemodi peregrinis ac inexspectatis subsidiis pluries egena domus recreata fuit, [ac inops Virgo speraret,] ne sanctæ Filiæ constans ac robusta in Deum confidentia fraudaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011819Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011819Z">
        <seg>[337] The house was repeatedly refreshed by such foreign and unexpected aid, [and the poor Virgin hoped,] lest the holy Daughter's steadfast and robust confidence in God be defrauded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[338] [a se novum monasterium fundari posse.] Domi Rosa cum devotis virginibus mira ac seria fiducia sermocinabatur de fundatione prætacti monasterii;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011825Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011825Z">
        <seg>[338] [that she could found a new monastery.] At home, Rosa spoke with the devout virgins with remarkable and earnest confidence about the founding of the aforementioned monastery;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[33] In his matrona quædam, genere &amp; opibus clara, [acriter objurgatur,] uni filio suo Rosam conjugem, sibi nurum, vehementer optabat, utique felici commercio plus bonorum adeptura, quam datura cum hærede filio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203458Z">
        <seg>[33] In these circumstances a certain matron, distinguished by her birth and wealth, was earnestly urging her one and only son to take Rosam as his wife, for she believed that by this happy alliance she would acquire more goods than she would give up with her son the heir.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[33] [&amp; incolis Limanis ægre persuasum fuit,] Repentina isthæc mutatio postridie devoto populo visa est intolerabilis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031720Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031720Z">
        <seg>[33] [And it was with difficulty that the inhabitants of Lima were persuaded,] This sudden change seemed intolerable to the devoted people the next day;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[340] Nec ab extimo decore quidquam dissidet anterior exactæ observantiæ pulchritudo, [quod post obitum S. Rosæ construi cœpit,] orationum, mortificationum, exercitiorum assiduitas, perfectionis studium, uniformis morum puritas &amp; sanctimonia, disciplinæ rigidus tenor, perque omnes angulos viva &amp; prorsus angelica virtutum omnium exemplaria.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011835Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011835Z">
        <seg>[340] Nothing in the external adornment is at odds with the inner beauty of exact observance: [which was begun after the death of St. Rose,] the assiduity of prayers, mortifications, and exercises, the pursuit of perfection, the uniform purity and sanctity of morals, the strict discipline, and throughout every corner living and truly angelic examples of all virtues.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[341] Denique illud est, quod integro fere ante fundationem decennio toties Rosæ, [&amp; cujus ædificationem ipsa Sancta,] nunc per symbola &amp; figuras, nunc manifeste in propria forma ac typo fuit divinitus præmonstratum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011838Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011838Z">
        <seg>Finally, this is what, for almost a full decade before the foundation, was so often divinely foreshown to Rose—[and whose construction the Holy Virgin herself,] now by symbols and figures, now manifestly in its own form and type.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[342] [per crebras visiones] Dudum expertus propheta monuit, intelligentia opus esse in visionibus, Daniel.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011844Z">
        <seg>[342] [Through frequent visions] The prophet, having long since experienced them, warned that insight is needed in visions, Daniel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[343] [&amp; varias revelationes] Atticuit Virgo, sed loqui cœpere prodigia: nam ecce, quas frater sursum projecerat, levissimo delapsu mox revolvebantur in terram;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011847Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011847Z">
        <seg>[343] [&amp; various revelations] The Virgin was startled, but prodigies began to speak: for behold, those which her brother had thrown upward, by the slightest fall, were soon rolling back to the ground;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[344] [cælitus edocta] Alia vice oranti Rosæ divinitus ostensum fuit miræ amœnitatis pratum, undique liliorum candore ac rosarum purpura (tametsi sparsim &amp; sine ordine) variegatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011850Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011850Z">
        <seg>[344] [Divinely instructed] On another occasion, while praying, Rose was divinely shown a meadow of marvelous beauty, variegated on all sides by the whiteness of lilies and the purple of roses (though scattered and without order).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[345] P. Magister Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011853Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011853Z">
        <seg>Father Master Brother</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[346] Aliquando coram domesticis inter familiaria colloquia, [aliisque multis prædixit,] dum B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011900Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011900Z">
        <seg>[346] At one time, in the presence of his household during familiar conversations, [and to many others he had foretold,] while B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[347] [&amp; matri suæ incredulæ prænuniavit;] Indignabatur præ ceteris Rosæ mater, nec æquo ferebat animo tam crebram, &amp; seriam domi forisque mentionem fieri novelli cœnobii, quod Filia tam asserte &amp; imperterrite notis atque ignotis præfigurabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011903Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011903Z">
        <seg>[347] [&amp; forewarned his unbelieving mother;] Above all others, Rosa's mother was indignant, and could not bear with a calm mind the frequent and serious mention, both at home and abroad, of the new convent, which her Daughter so assertively and fearlessly presented to friends and strangers alike.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[348] [eam ibi monialibus adscribendam esse;] At Rosa tot contradictionibus alacrior, voce humili, sed læta replicuit: “Eia, mater dulcissima, hic demum cessent verborum pugnæ, tu magno tuo emolumento experieris, quam durum sit contra stimulum calcitrare: in sanctæ Catharinæ Limensi monasterio, quod prædico, tu ipsa inter primas candidum sanctimonialium indues habitum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011909Z">
        <seg>[348] [that she was to be enrolled there among the nuns;] But Rosa, all the more eager for so many contradictions, replied in a humble but joyful voice: "Come, most sweet mother, let the battles of words cease here at last; you, for your great benefit, will experience how hard it is to kick against the pricks: in the monastery of Saint Catherine of Lima, which I foretell, you yourself will be among the first to don the pure habit of the nuns;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[349] [sicut anno Christi 1629 contigit.] Initium anni MDCXXIX non vidit Calendas Græcas&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;, vidit tamen Mariam de Oliva sanctimonialem in novello sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis monasterio, ubi post mariti obitum sexagenaria religiosis S. Patris Dominici filiabus devotissime aggregata, cum sacra Ordinis veste nomen Sororis Mariæ de S. Maria assumpsit, exactoque probationis anno, solemnia vota emisit, ac plena dierum, sancto fine quievit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011915Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011915Z">
        <seg>[349] [as happened in the year of Christ 1629.] The beginning of the year 1629 did not see the Greek Kalends&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;, she did, however, see the nun Mary of the Olive in the new monastery of St. Catherine of Siena, where, after her husband's death, the sixty-year-old had most devoutly joined the religious daughters of St. Dominic, and, with the sacred habit of the Order, took the name of Sister Mary of St. Mary. After completing her year of probation, she made her solemn vows and, having lived to a ripe old age, rested in holy death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[34] [eique in novo S. Claræ cœnobio] Videbantur in hoc unum conspirasse omnia, ne Rosa Tertiariarum assequeretur habitum, quem tot votis ambiebat, tot suspiriis, tanto lachrymarum sumptu licitabatur: nam ecce alia oblata est religiosi instituti via, quæ quidem Rosæ displicere neutiquam potuisset, nisi unice ad S. Catharinæ Senensis alta vestigia fuisset cælitus destinata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203459Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203459Z">
        <seg>[34] [and in the new convent of St. Clare] It seemed that everything conspired to prevent Rose from obtaining the habit of the Tertiaries, which she so earnestly desired, and for which she was entreating with so many vows, so many sighs, and at the cost of so many tears: for behold, another path to the religious life was offered, which indeed could in no way have displeased Rosa, had it not been divinely destined solely for the high footsteps of St. Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[34] [ut judicum Sedis apostolicæ exspectarent.] Tarde demum, nec sine labore persuasum est importunis, sic consulendum fuisse illustrioribus, qui ab apostolico throno sperantur Rosæ honoribus, nullum in Romanorum decretorum obedientia detrimentum esse, inobedientiam sanctæ Virgini placere non posse, hujus pretiosas exuvias loco tutissimo sub primi sepulchri custodia Limensibus adservari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031735Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031735Z">
        <seg>[34] [that they should await the judgment of the Apostolic See.] At last, and not without effort, it was persuaded to the importunate that it was fitting to advise the more illustrious, who are expected to receive the honors of the Rose from the apostolic throne, that there is no detriment to the obedience of the Roman decrees, that disobedience cannot please the holy Virgin, and that her precious relics are preserved for the Limeños in the most secure place under the guard of the first sepulchre.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[350] Superest de ipsamet cœnobii fundatrice, ac prima ejusdem præfecta memorabile ac luculentum Rosæ vaticinium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011918Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011918Z">
        <seg>[350] There remains of the very founder of the convent, and its first prefect, the memorable and lucid prophecy of Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[351] Rosa jam intus moneri se sentiens, affectu reciproco matronam vinxit, [occasione quadam S. Rosam invisisset,] offerendo modicum id, quod posset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011928Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011928Z">
        <seg>[351] Rosa, already sensing she was being warned from within, bound the matron with a reciprocal affection, [on the pretext that she had visited St. Rose,] offering what little she could.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[352] Necdum sat capiebat ipsamet, unde, aut cur ita ageret, [sensit, se excitari ad fundandum monasterium,] donec præter morem oculis a terra in vultum Luciæ attentissime conjectis, cœpit exultare spiritu, suffundi colore inusitatæ lætitiæ, palpitare calidis præcordiis, ac demum hilaritate modestissima Luciam deosculans, “Euge, (inquit) mater, ingenti suo operi te Deus reservavit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011931Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011931Z">
        <seg>[352] She herself did not yet fully understand where or why she was acting as she did, [she felt herself stirred to found a monastery,] until, contrary to her habit, she fixed her eyes intently from the ground upon Lucia's face and began to exult in spirit, to flush with an unusual joy, to palpitate with warm affections, and at last, with the most modest cheerfulness, kissed Lucia, "Well done, (she said) mother, God has reserved you for his great work."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[353] [etsi maritas &amp; numerosa proles adhuc viverent.] Exinde in mulieris animum velut connexo agmine immigrarunt simul religiosi status efficax desiderium, simul spes vivacissima, simul indubia certitudo pertingendi ad hanc metam, evadendi retinacula, perrumpendi innumeros obices, quos sola divina manus poterat dimovere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011940Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011940Z">
        <seg>[353] [even though her husband and numerous children were still alive.] From that moment, a kind of connected procession immigrated into the woman's mind: the effective desire for a religious state, the most vivid hope, and the indubitable certainty of reaching this goal, of evading the snares, of breaking through countless obstacles that only a divine hand could remove.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[354] [Dein confessario suo, quem Rosa obscure præmonuerat,] Accidit interea, R.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011959Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011959Z">
        <seg>[354] [Then, regarding his confessor, whom Rosa had obscurely forewarned,] Meanwhile, R.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[355] [nobilis hæc matrona propositum suum indicat,] Hic matrona confessario detexit, se magni ac perardui moliminis opus animo versare, fundationem novi monasterii sub sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis tutela ac nomine;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012014Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012014Z">
        <seg>[355] [This noble matron reveals her plan to her confessor,] Here the matron disclosed to her confessor that she was contemplating a great and arduous undertaking: the foundation of a new monastery under the patronage and in the name of St. Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[356] [&amp; post mortem mariti ac liberorum exsecutioni mandat.] Ita fuit: nam Luciæ, maritum paulo post &amp; proles omnes ex ordine Deus ab hac luce evocavit, obstacula novæ fundationis undequaque innumera &amp; potentissima sustulit, duros repugnantium animos ad favorem pii operis flexit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012033Z">
        <seg>[356] [and after the death of her husband and children, she carried out the plan.] So it was: for God, shortly thereafter, called Lucia's husband and all her offspring from this light in due order, removed countless and most powerful obstacles to the new foundation from every quarter, and bent the hard hearts of the dissenters to the favor of the pious work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[358] Filia quæstoris Gundisalvi Michaëla de la Massa secretam quamdam cogitationem versabat animo, [arcanas cordis cogitationes] quam nulli mortalium communicarat, neque communicare decreverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012132Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012132Z">
        <seg>[358] The daughter of the quaestor Gundisalvus, Michaela de la Massa, was entertaining a certain secret thought in her mind, [the secret thoughts of her heart], which she had not shared with any mortal, nor had she resolved to share.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[359] Longe mirabilius videbatur F. Joanni Miguel Ordinis S. Dominici religioso, cum e longinqua peregrinatione Limam reversus, atque in D. Hieronymi sacello Virgini seorsim colloquens, ab ea seriatim, [aliasque res arcanas indicat,] &amp; exacte sibi narrari audivit, quæ fratri procul in aliena regione acciderant, nec humanitus inde potuerant in Rosæ notitiam pervenire;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012154Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012154Z">
        <seg>[359] It seemed far more miraculous to Fr. John Miguel, a religious of the Order of St. Dominic, when, having returned to Lima from a long pilgrimage, and in D. While speaking privately with the Virgin in the chapel of St. Jerome, he heard from her in order, [and she revealed other secret matters,] and with exactness, the things that had happened to the brother far away in a foreign land, which by human means could not have come to the knowledge of the Rose;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[35] [locus offertur;] His, inquam, auspicibus formabatur recens monasterium, &amp; ultro invitabatur velut ad dignissimum tali flore plantarium Rosa, jam inter primas ejusdem fundatrices nominatim electa, censa, approbata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203501Z">
        <seg>[35] [place offered;] With these auspices, I say, the new monastery was being founded, and Rosa—already chosen by name, consecrated, and approved as one of its foremost foundresses—was invited of her own accord as if to the most worthy planting ground for such a flower.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[360] [&amp; cuidam putanti, se anno 1615 moriturum;] Cuidam religioso Societatis Jesu, viro plane apostolico summisque virtutibus obornato insederat (nescitur unde) firma, tenax, &amp; immobilis persuasio, illo se anno (qui erat MDCXV) certissime ex hac vita migraturum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012219Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012219Z">
        <seg>[360] [&amp; to one who thought he would die in the year 1615;] A certain religious of the Society of Jesus, a man truly apostolic and adorned with the highest virtues, had (it is not known from where) a firm, tenacious, and immovable conviction that he would most certainly depart this life in that year (which was MDCXLV).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[361] [longiorem vitam promitti jubet;] Cruciabant hæc piam Gundisalvi conjugem, quæ tantum suæ conscientiæ moderatorem dolebat præmature amittere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012244Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012244Z">
        <seg>[361] [He commands a longer life to be promised;] These things tormented the pious wife of Gundisalvus, who grieved only to lose so prematurely the guide of her conscience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[362] Pater Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012309Z">
        <seg>Father Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[363] Magister F. Ludovicus de Bilbao, itidem per annos quatuordecim Virginis confessarius, [qui periculoso morbo laborabant,] sopitis, quas extinctas crediderat, febribus, timere sibi desierat: cum velut ex insidiis morbus rediit multo quam ante violentior, ac recidivum ad extrema reduxit pericula.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012331Z">
        <seg>Master F. Louis of Bilbao, likewise the Virgin's confessor for fourteen years, [who had been suffering from a dangerous illness,] once the fevers he had believed to be extinguished were laid to rest, had ceased to fear for himself: when the disease returned as if from ambush, far more violent than before, and the relapse brought him to the brink of death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[364] Virgo, tametsi spiritualis sui patris angoribus condolebat, [pristinam sanitatem spondet,] nihil tamen periculi commemoratione exterrita, paucis, at vultu alacri, respondit: “Ad serium illud ultimæ respirationis momentum omni annisu, cura, solicitudine se parare numquam intempestivum, semper laudabile est ac salutare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012349Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012349Z">
        <seg>[364] The virgin, though she grieved with her father in spirit over his anxieties, [she promises his former health,] nevertheless, not frightened by any mention of danger, replied briefly, but with a cheerful countenance: "To prepare oneself by every effort for that serious moment of the last breath, with care and concern, is never untimely, but always praiseworthy and salutary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[365] [&amp; novitium a comitiali morbo liberandum asserit.] Limæ in sancti Dominici cœnobio Joannes de Soto religionis habitum impetrarat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012417Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012417Z">
        <seg>[365] [&amp; he claims to have cured a novice of epilepsy.] In Lima, at the convent of Saint Dominic, John de Soto had received the religious habit;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[366] [Quibusdam virginibus assignat] Tres germanæ sorores, Philippa, Catharina, &amp; Francisca de Montoya Rosæ conversatione &amp; exemplo erudiebantur ad pietatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012458Z">
        <seg>[366] [Assigned to certain virgins] Three sisters, Philippa, Catherine, and Francisca de Montoya, were instructed in piety through the example and conversation of Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[367] [statum vitæ, quem postea eligerent,] Aliud nobile par virginum, Maria &amp; Joanna Urtado de Bustamante, quamquam intima ac familiari Rosæ amicitia gauderent, nil minus quam de amplectendo Religionis statu cogitabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012526Z">
        <seg>[367] [the state of life they would later choose,] Another noble pair of virgins, Mary and Joanna Urtado de Bustamante, although they enjoyed the closest and most intimate friendship with Rosa, thought of nothing less than embracing the religious state.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[368] Joanni de la Raya, &amp; Mariæ Euphemiæ de Parejas unicus adolescebat filius Rodericus, [&amp; præmonuit adolescentis cujusdam matrem,] quem ipsi a teneris destinarant sacræ militiæ Societatis Jesu tempore suo adscribendum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012544Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012544Z">
        <seg>John de la Raya and Mary Euphemia de Parejas had one son, Roderigo, who was growing up, [and he warned the mother of a certain youth,] whom they had destined from his earliest years to be enrolled in the sacred militia of the Society of Jesus in due course.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[369] Sauciarunt postrema hæc verba Euphemiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012603Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012603Z">
        <seg>These last words wounded Euphemia.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[36] Aloysia gaudio ac stupori impar, dum reliquo inhiat coronationis spectaculo, [Aloysiæ de Serrano,] disparuit visio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031844Z">
        <seg>[36] Aloysia, overwhelmed with joy and astonishment, while she gazes at the rest of the coronation spectacle, [Aloysia de Serrano's] vision vanished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[36] [sed ex voluntate divina,] Hoc ut magis innotesceret, binis e cælo prodigiis opus fuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203503Z">
        <seg>[36] [but by divine will,] For this to become more widely known, two prodigies from heaven were required;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[370] Nil minus tunc aut cogitabat Rodericus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012631Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012631Z">
        <seg>Rodrigo was thinking of nothing else at that time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[371] [&amp; concubinæ prænuntiat legitimas nuptias,] Nobilis Hispani Leonardi de Roya mancipium Maria Perez cum hero clam vivebat in statu damnabili.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012656Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012656Z">
        <seg>[371] [And the concubine announces legitimate marriage,] Maria Perez, the slave of the noble Spaniard Leonard de Roya, was living secretly with a hero in a deplorable state.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[372] [alterique mulieri reditum fugitivorum mancipiorum.] Mariæ de Mesta, Medori Angelini pictoris conjugi, duæ simul ancillæ Æthiopissæ domo aufugerant, earumque altera, Antonia nomine, etiam claves heræ tunc pernecessarias asportarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012709Z">
        <seg>[372] [And the return of the fugitive slaves to the woman.] Two Ethiopian handmaids had simultaneously run away from the house of Maria de Mesta, the wife of the painter Medeo Angelini; and one of them, named Antonia, had even carried off her mistress's keys, which were then extremely necessary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[373] [Prædicit, hospitem suum in difficili negotio,] Decreverat Peruani regni excellentissimus prorex, Gundisalvum de la Massa, quæstorem implicare procul a Lima functioni honorificæ, at perarduæ, quæque virum exigebat summæ tum experientiæ, tum fidei ac integritatis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012730Z">
        <seg>[373] [He predicts that his guest will be involved in a difficult undertaking,] The most illustrious Viceroy of the Kingdom of Peru, Gundisalvus de la Massa, had resolved to send the treasurer far from Lima on an honorable but arduous mission, one that demanded a man of the highest experience, faith, and integrity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[374] Tandem XV Aprilis, quasi peremptorie monitus, [cui prorex illum implicare statuerat,] postridie hora præfixa sese a prorege ad colloquium in palatio expectari, facile agnovit, jam nullum superesse exosæ profectionis effugium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012743Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012743Z">
        <seg>Finally, on April 15, having been warned as if peremptorily by the man the viceroy had decided to involve, that he was to appear at the palace for a conference with the viceroy at the appointed hour the next day, he readily recognized that no escape from the odious expedition remained.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[375] Dubitanti quætoris conjugi, idem bis, ter constantissime inculcavit, [certo certius adhibendum non esse,] adjiciens: “Nec si videris utrumque mariti pedem immissum stapedi, aut plateæ jam adequitantem, credideris profecturum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012801Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012801Z">
        <seg>To the quætor's wife, who was hesitating, he most steadfastly insisted on the same thing twice or three times, [that it was not to be applied with certainty,] adding: "Nor if you see both of a husband's feet planted in the stirrup, or him already riding down the street, would you believe he was going to set out."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[376] Ferdinandus Flores de Herrera germanus Rosæ frater, [&amp; fratri suo, cujus longe distantis matrimonium prænoverat,] patris exemplo militiam secutus, usque in Chilæ regnum penetrarat, ubi &amp; vexilliferi honore in sua centuria functus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012814Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012814Z">
        <seg>Ferdinand Flores de Herrera, Rosa's brother, following the military life of his father [and of his brother, whose marriage he had foreseen from afar], had penetrated as far as the kingdom of Chile, where he also served as standard-bearer in his own company.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[377] Adolevit cum hoc signo parvula, mirantibus, quotquot Rosæ litteras pridem viderant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012836Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012836Z">
        <seg>She grew up with this sign, to the amazement of all who had long seen Rosa's letters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[378] [Divinitus accepit notitiam de Æthiopissa non baptizata,] Isabellæ de Mexia nobili Matronæ ancilla erat Æthiopissa, Speranza nomine, quod ultro sibi (acsi Christiana esset) imposuerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012845Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012845Z">
        <seg>[378] [She received divine knowledge about the unbaptized Ethiopian woman,] Isabella of Mexico, a noble matron, had as a servant an Ethiopian woman named Speranza, a name she had given herself (as if she were a Christian).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[379] [quæ se Christianam mendaciter finxerat,] Perfacile ipsi fuit, tum dominæ suæ, tum Mariæ de Oliva cæterisque imponere, adeo ut Rosam velut importune molestam arguerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012907Z">
        <seg>[379] [who had falsely pretended to be a Christian,] It was very easy for her to deceive both her mistress and Mary of Oliva and the others, so much so that they accused Rosa as if she were an importunately troublesome person.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[37] Ceterum vix ad orationem humi genua fixerat, [quæ declarata fuisse dicitur] cum sensit pavimento sese immobiliter acclavatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203503Z">
        <seg>[37] However, she had scarcely knelt on the ground for the prayer, [which is said to have been declared], when she felt herself nailed immovably to the floor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[37] Quæstori Gundisalvo de la Massa (qui coram apostolicis judicibus id verbo scriptoque testatum reliquit) secreto fassus est vir, [alteri anonymo,] illis diebus mysticæ theologiæ peritia &amp; contemplationis sublimitate clarus, quod intra tres hebdomadas ab obitu Rosæ proximas, minimum viginti &amp; duabus vicibus conspicua sibi fuerit gloria hujus Virginis, intuitu nunc imaginario, nunc intellectuali;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031856Z">
        <seg>[37] A man, [another anonymous one,] secretly confessed to Qestor Gundisalvo de la Massa (who before the apostolic judges testified this both in word and in writing), a man renowned in those days for his expertise in mystical theology and the sublimity of his contemplation, that within the three weeks immediately following Rose's death, the glory of this Virgin had appeared to him at least twenty-two times, by an insight now imaginative, now intellectual;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[380] [&amp; artem legendi scribendique cælitus infusam habuit.] Nemo audebat interrogare lætam hoc successu Rosam, unde arcanum Speranzæ statum didicisset: nam hunc ei non potuisse humanitus Limæ innotescere, apud omnes in confesso erat: ex altera parte, Virgini cælitus infundi abstrusas notitias, pridem constabat matri, quæ inter alia, legendi scribendique scientiam, non acquisitione, sed divina infusione irrorasse minorenni Filiæ, grato experimento compererat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012938Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012938Z">
        <seg>[380] [&amp; she had a heavenly gift for reading and writing.] No one dared to ask the joyful Rosa, by this success, from where she had learned the secret of Speranza's status: for it was a matter of public knowledge that this could not have been revealed to her by human means in Lima: On the other hand, it had long been established to the mother that abstruse knowledge was divinely infused into the Virgin, who, among other things, had imparted to her minor daughter the skill of reading and writing, not by acquisition, but by divine infusion, as she had learned by a grateful experiment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[382] [&amp; adstantibus brevi moritura videretur,] Adhuc mortalitati Virginis supererat circiter triennium, quando ex gravi morbo decubua, videbatur omnibus jam vitæ marginem extremum attigisse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013115Z">
        <seg>[382] [&amp; to those present she seemed about to die,] There was still about three years left before the Virgin's death, when she fell ill from a serious disease; she seemed to everyone to have already reached the very brink of life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[383] [ipsa affirmavit, tunc obitum sibi nondum imminere:] Verum ut vidit circumstantes, velut re perdita liberius singultire, ipsumque confessarium lachrymas tenere non posse, cohibere nec ipsa potuit roseum commiserationis affectum: utque consolaretur ceteros, ita allocuta est patrem: “Depone metum, abige intempestivum mœrorem, ac certus esto, me nequaquam ex hac, licet tuo omniumque judicio complorata, ægritudine morituram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013130Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013130Z">
        <seg>[383] [she herself affirmed that her death was not yet imminent at that time:] But when she saw those around her sobbing more freely as if all was lost, and her own confessor unable to hold back his tears, she too could not restrain the tender feeling of compassion: and in order to console the others, she addressed her father as follows: "Lay aside your fear, banish this untimely sorrow, and be certain that I shall by no means die from this illness, though it is lamented by your judgment and that of all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[384] [nam mortis suæ tempus,] Annum denique suæ ætatis trigesimum primum Rosa attigit, quem minime se finituram dudum prænoverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013146Z">
        <seg>[384] [for the time of her death,] At last, Rosa reached the thirty-first year of her age, which she had long ago foreseen she would not complete.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[385] Porro &amp; locum (sed integro anno ante) eidem matronæ prædixerat: [locum in domo hospitis,] nam quodam vespere inter spiritualia colloquia subito præter morem interpellans Rosa, vultu amœnissimo modestisque lætitiæ signis diducto, in hæc verba erupit: “Scito, mater, me non alibi, quam hic in domo tua, &amp; hoc loco naturæ debitum soluturam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013207Z">
        <seg>[385] Moreover, she had also foretold the place (though a whole year earlier) to the same matron: [a place in the host's house,] for one evening, in the midst of spiritual conversations, suddenly interrupting in an unusual manner, Rosa, her face beaming with delightful and modest signs of joy, burst out with these words: "Know, mother, that I shall not die anywhere else, but here in your house, and in this very spot;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[386] In celebri arcuum visione (de qua supra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013223Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013223Z">
        <seg>[386] In the famous vision of the arches (about which above chapter</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[387] Rosa ne tantæ amaritudinis calicem expavesceret, in sacello Rosarii, [amicisque quibusdam indicaverat.] velut in horto Gethsemani, sese tum voluntati Patris æterni, tum adjutorio Virginis matris ardenter commendavit, quando, annuente Deipara, ibidem apertius didicit, prope adesse diem, quo totum hoc mortiferi promulsidatii&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; absynthium sibi strenue sit epotandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013242Z">
        <seg>[387] So that Rosa would not be startled by the chalice of such great bitterness, in the chapel of the Rosary, [and to certain friends she had indicated.] like in the garden of Gethsemane, she ardently commended herself both to the will of the eternal Father and to the help of the Virgin Mother, when, with the consent of the Mother of God, she learned more clearly there that the day was near on which she would have to drink down this entire deadly absinthe of the Passion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[388] Denique, triduo ante fatalem postremæ ægritudinis syncopen&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; Rosa ultimo adiit parentum ædes, forte clam valedictura suæ ibidem hortensi cellulæ tot secum cælestium deliciarum consciæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013251Z">
        <seg>Finally, three days before the fatal syncope of her last illness, Rosa made her final visit to her parents' home, perhaps to bid a silent farewell to her garden cell there, so conscious of so many heavenly delights.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[389] [anno 1617 Calendis Augusti] Calendis Augusti, Rosa sub vesperam sana ac vegeta suo sese cubiculo incluserat, exspectatura illic promissos ultimi sui accubitus dolores, quorum anteambulationes, ac strategos&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; sentire jam cœperat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013310Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013310Z">
        <seg>[389] [On August 1, 1617] On August 1, toward evening, Rosa, healthy and vigorous, had shut herself in her room, to await there the promised pains of her final labor, the forerunners of which she had already begun to feel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[38] Alterum prodigium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203503Z">
        <seg>[38] Another prodigy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[38] Doctor Joannes del Castillo medicus, anno post felicem Virginis transitum quintodecimo juravit coram apostolico secundi examinis tribunali, [doctori Joanni Castillo] sibi per visionem imaginariam apparuisse Rosam in medio cujusdam fulgidissimi luminis, quod ipsamet divinæ Majestatis claritudinem ac speciem referebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031906Z">
        <seg>[38] Doctor John del Castillo, a physician, in the fifteenth year after the Virgin's happy passing, swore before the apostolic tribunal of the second examination that the Rose had appeared to him by an imaginary vision in the midst of a most brilliant light, which itself reflected the clarity and likeness of the Divine Majesty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[390] [obruitur ignoto lethalique morbo,] E terra interim sublata in lectulum, nec movere se poterat, nec quiescere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013341Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013341Z">
        <seg>[390] [stricken by an unknown and deadly disease,] Meanwhile, lifted from the ground and placed on a bed, he could neither move nor rest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[391] [quem cum explicare non posset,] Confessariorum unus rogavit Virginem, saltem medico exponeret, quid sentiret: cunctantem adegit imperio, credens, ab humili modestia, non a tormentorum excessu provenire silentium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013406Z">
        <seg>[391] [Since he could not explain it,] one of the confessors asked the Virgin to at least reveal to the physician what she was feeling. He compelled her with an order when she hesitated, believing that her silence stemmed from humble modesty rather than from the excess of her torments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[392] Auxere hi sermones vertiginem medicorum: fluctuabant in mutuo aspectu confusi, [&amp; præsciret, inutilem fore medicorum industriam,] perplexi, attoniti: nam hinc notissima Rosæ virtus &amp; sanctitas non sinebat, quemquam de dictorum suorum veritate ambigere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013443Z">
        <seg>[392] These discourses increased the doctors' bewilderment: they wavered, confused in each other's gaze, [&amp; foresaw that the doctors' industry would be useless,] perplexed, astonished: for from this, the most well-known virtue and holiness of Rose did not allow anyone to doubt the truth of their words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[393] Verum interrupit qualemcumque istam crucis quietem superveniens Rosæ mater, [tamen jußu matris] quæ intromissa, ingenti suo terrore infirmiorem reperit Filiam, quam sibi fuerat nuntiatum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013502Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013502Z">
        <seg>But Rosa's mother, arriving and interrupting whatever peace of the cross, [yet by her mother's command], who, upon being admitted, found her daughter to be weaker than she had been told;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[394] Rosa, memor nullibi, quam in cruce ampliorem obedientiæ patere locum, ab ipsa mutuavit loquendi vires &amp; spiritum, perque eadem similitudinum compendia (siquidem aptius non poterat) explicuit matri, [dolores suos per similitudinem exposuit.] se velut oblongo ignito jaculo a vertice usque ad calcaneum transverberari miris ardoribus, quibus totum sinistrum latus videbatur elevari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013517Z">
        <seg>[394] Rosa, mindful that nowhere was a broader field for obedience open than on the cross, drew from it the power and spirit of her speech, and by the same concise similes (since she could find no more fitting ones) explained to her mother [her sufferings by way of a simile.] that she felt herself pierced as it were by a long, fiery javelin from the crown of her head to her heel, by wondrous torments which seemed to lift her entire left side;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[395] [Deinde 17 die Augusti in eam irruerunt] Illuxit interea sextus Augusti die, Christi Transfigurationi sacer;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013549Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013549Z">
        <seg>[395] [Then on August 17 they broke into it] Meanwhile, the sixth day of August, sacred to the Transfiguration of Christ, dawned;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[396] [plures diversi generis morbi,] Verum XVII Augusti, inflammata utrimque costarum membrana notabilem adjunxit pleuritidem, huic se paulatim adsociavit raucum angusti pectoris asthma, ut ardores, qui intus velut in clibano assabant viscerum reliquias, non sine novo cruciatu levamen admitterent rari suspirii.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025612Z">
        <seg>[396] [Various different kinds of diseases,] But on August 17, with the pleural membrane on both sides inflamed, a marked pleurisy set in; to this a hoarse, constrictive asthma gradually joined, so that the burning pains, which inside like a furnace consumed the remnants of the viscera, would admit relief only through the rare sigh, not without fresh torment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[397] Acervatos tot dolorum ac morborum cumulos æstimabat, [quos inter dulcia ad Deum suspiria] acceptabat resignato animo serena, &amp; constans Rosa, velut manu Christi admensos sibi ex illa mirabili statera, quam in visione arcuum attentissime observarat, secura, tantis afflictonibus successuros totidem cumulos gratiarum, nempe æternum illus gloriæ pondus, de quo Apostolus 2 Corin.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025627Z">
        <seg>[397] She regarded the heaped-up piles of so many sorrows and illnesses, [which she accepted amid sweet sighs for God] with a resigned and serene spirit, and steadfast Rose, as though measured out to herself by the hand of Christ from that marvelous balance which she had watched most attentively in the vision of the arches, secure, that to such afflictions would succeed an equal heap of graces, namely the eternal weight of glory, of which the Apostle speaks in 2 Corinthians.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[398] Suspirabat interdum, at sine gemitu: “Domine in adjutorium meum intende: [constantissime toleravit,] nam sine illo nil possum”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025646Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025646Z">
        <seg>He sighed at times, but without a groan: "Lord, attend to my help; for I can do nothing without it."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[399] Paulatim subverebatur (nec sine causa) Virgo sapiens, [&amp; in quibus semper mentis suæ compos,] ne tot simul morborum continua &amp; atrox violentia sibi rationis usum eriperet, totamque cerebri temperiem dissiparet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025708Z">
        <seg>Gradually the wise virgin was weakening (and not without cause), [and in whom her mind was always composed,] lest the continuous and atrocious violence of so many diseases at once should rob her of the use of reason and dissipate the entire temper of her brain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[39] Nec fefellit eventus jam securam: nam paulo post, [statuit imitari S. Catharinam Senensem,] obstaculis undique submotis, facta est voti compos, inque Rosariano Deiparæ sacello, ubi quondam immobilis hæserat, solemni ceremonia, de manibus P. Magistri F. Alphonsi Velasquez, Ordinis Prædicatorum sui tunc confessarii (cui id P. Provincialis commiserat) induta est veste bicolori, quam in Senensi magistra dilexerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000518Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000518Z">
        <seg>[39] Nor did the outcome disappoint her now that she was secure: for a little later, [she resolved to imitate St. Catherine of Siena,] with all obstacles removed, she fulfilled her vow and, in the Rosary chapel of the Blessed Virgin, where she had once been held motionless, by solemn ceremony, from the hands of Father Master F. From the hands of Father Master F. Alphonsus Velasquez, her then confessor of the Order of Preachers (to whom the Provincial Father had entrusted the task), she was clothed in the two-colored habit which she had loved in her Sienese mistress.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[39] [sæpius apparuit,] Quidam ex Virginis confessariis, ab ore ejusdem doctoris accepit, plusquam quinquagies gloriosam beatæ Rosæ apparitionem sibi cælitus illuxisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031927Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031927Z">
        <seg>[39] [appeared more often,] A certain one of the Virgin's confessors received from the mouth of the same doctor that the glorious apparition of Blessed Rose had shone upon him from heaven more than fifty times.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[3] Accessit demum amplissima senatorum curia ad obsequium, quod vix alteri, præterquam suis proregibus vita functis præstare consuevit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030548Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030548Z">
        <seg>Finally, the most august senate accorded its homage, which it had scarcely ever extended to any other, save to its own late pro-consuls.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[3] [quod mater ejus ob prodigium postea mutavit,] Porro ut Rosæ nomen, divinitus soboli destinatum, post trium mensium ambages demum erumperet, prodigium exspectabatur, quod tale fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211840Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203314Z">
        <seg>[3] [which his mother later changed because of a prodigy,] Furthermore, so that the name Rose, divinely destined for the child, might at last emerge after three months of twists and turns, a prodigy was awaited, which was as follows.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[400] Præ ceteris solicita mater laborabat persuadere Filiæ, hæc redituræ valetudinis signa esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025727Z">
        <seg>More than anyone else, the anxious mother was working to persuade her daughter that these were signs of her health returning.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[402] Post hæc, triduo ante felicem transitum, complicatis in supplicandi ritum manibus, [extremis ecclesiæ Sacramentis munitur,] voce flebili divinum Viaticum, extremamque Unctionem petiit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025922Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025922Z">
        <seg>After these things, three days before his blessed passing, with hands folded in the rite of supplication, he was strengthened with the last Sacraments of the Church, and with a mournful voice he asked for the divine Viaticum and the last Anointing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[403] Quam immobilis riguit, dum sacrum Viaticum sumebat in ecstasi, [fidem Catholicam profitetur,] tam alacri omnium sensuum officio extremam Unctionem moribunda excepit, vere oleum lætitiæ, quo sese potius ad triumphum, quam ad agonis conflictum sensit animari, utpote salutis adeo jam certa, quod insuper nosset absque purgatorii ignis contactu, se recto itinere ad Sponsi nuptias profecturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025953Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025953Z">
        <seg>[403] How immovably rigid she lay, while in ecstasy she received the sacred Viaticum, [professes the Catholic faith,] she received the last anointing of the dying with such alacrity of all her senses, a true oil of joy, by which she felt herself animated rather for triumph than for the conflict of the struggle, being already so certain of her salvation that she also knew, without the contact of purgatorial fire, she was to proceed on the straight path to the nuptials of the Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[404] [adversariis aut persecutoribus suis ignoscit,] Inviserat Rosam morti vicinam P. F. Bartholomæus Martinez prior Limensis cœnobii ad S. Mariæ Magdalenæ, vir admodum religiosus &amp; Dominicanæ ibidem observantiæ vigil zelotes, cui per quinquennium Virgo conscientiæ suæ latebras solita fuerat aperire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030009Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030009Z">
        <seg>[404] [forgives his adversaries or persecutors,] He had visited Rosa, who was near death, Father Bartholomew Martinez, prior of the Lima convent of St. Mary Magdalene, a very devout man and a zealous guardian of the Dominican observance there, to whom for five years the Virgin had been accustomed to reveal the secrets of her conscience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[405] [ab omnibus vicissim veniam erratorum petit,] Hinc in se reflexa, domesticos Gundisalvi quæstoris acciri demisse petiit, &amp; quamquam ex his neminem verbulo, aut minimo umquam nutu offendisset, a singulis &amp; omnibus voce lachrymosa ac supplici oravit sibi condonari, si qua in re ulli nocuerat sive incomposito morum exemplo, sive tarditate obsequiorum, aut tetrica singularitatis molestia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030028Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030028Z">
        <seg>[405] [he asks forgiveness for his mistakes from everyone in turn,] Then, turning inward, she humbly requested that the household of the treasurer Gundisalvus be summoned, and although she had never offended any of them with a word or the slightest gesture, she implored each and every one with a tearful and supplicant voice to forgive her for any harm she might have caused, whether by her disorderly conduct, her slowness in attending to their needs, or the unpleasantness of her singular disposition.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[406] Sciebat quæstor, Virgini (prout sub Dominicani Ordinis habitu, [&amp; sepulturæ locum apud PP. Prædicatores flagitat.] atque obedientia vixerat) in votis esse, non alibi, quam ad Fratrum suorum pedes sepeliri;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030040Z">
        <seg>The quaestor knew that the Virgin, having lived in the habit of the Dominican Order and in obedience (and having requested a burial place with the Fathers Preachers), wished to be buried at the feet of her brothers, and nowhere else.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[407] Notarunt plerique in Virgine, iisdem gradibus, [Deinde in crebras ecstases rapta est,] quibus debile corpusculum fatiscebat in mortem, attolli spiritum in vigorem &amp; securam hilaritatem, adeo ut pene horaria lætitiæ incrementa nec voce nec vultu satis potuerit dissimulare, velut protestans cum Apostolo: “Cum infirmor, tunc fortior sum”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030108Z">
        <seg>[407] Most have noted in the Virgin, by the same degrees, [Then she was rapt into frequent ecstasies,] by which her frail little body was failing unto death, that her spirit was lifted up into vigor and secure gladness, so that she could scarcely conceal by voice or countenance the hourly increases of her joy, as though protesting with the Apostle: "When I am weak, then I am strong."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[408] Aderat morienti Filiæ afflicta mater, aberat pater, [accersitisque parentibus,] quem lecto decubuum propria infirmitas continebat domi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030133Z">
        <seg>The afflicted mother was present with her dying daughter; the father was absent, for his own infirmity kept him at home in bed; [and the parents having been summoned,]</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[409] [post pia monita placide moritur.] Tantus in Rosa concionandi spiritus ac vigor, P. magistro de Lorenzana confessario persuaserat, ipsam ea saltem nocte haud morituram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030207Z">
        <seg>[409] [After pious admonitions, she dies peacefully.] Such was the spirit and vigor of preaching in Rosa that she had persuaded Father Lorenzana, her confessor and master, that she herself would not die at least that night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[40] Nimirum cum infante creverat hoc desiderium: siquidem a quinto suæ ætatis anno S. Catharinæ Senensi jam sese addixerat, [cujus exemplo,] quod ex ore ipsiusmet Rosæ affirmant se percepisse testes secundus &amp; vigesimus secundus in citato sæpius Processu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T212012Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203505Z">
        <seg>[40] Indeed, this desire had grown with her from infancy: for from the age of five she had already consecrated herself to St. Catherine of Siena, [whose example,] as the second and twenty-second witnesses in the aforementioned Process repeatedly affirm, they learned from Rose's own mouth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[40] [&amp; aliis visa est] Porro eamdem Rosam videre in Sponsi sui paradiso vernantibus rosis floride circumseptam, aureoque diademate redimitam, pluribus a Deo concessum fuit, præsertim viduæ cuidam, fama virtutum tunc celeberrimæ, cui se Rosa sæpius in cælesti lumine stitit conspicuam, non sine officioso angelicarum myriadum comitatu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031943Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031943Z">
        <seg>[40] [and was seen by others] Moreover, it was granted to several others to see the same Rose in her Spouse's paradise, surrounded by flourishing roses and adorned with a golden diadem, especially to a certain widow, then celebrated for the renown of her virtues, to whom Rose often appeared in a heavenly light, not without the dutiful escort of myriads of angels.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[410] Eodem festivæ noctis diluculo Aloysia de Serrano procul in ædibus parentum matutino somno implicita rescivit, [Mox ab obitu apparet cuidam muliere splendida,] deposuisse tunc Rosam cum S. Bartholomæo mortalitatis exuvias.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030238Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030238Z">
        <seg>At the same early hour of the festive night, Aloysia of Serrano, far away in her parents' house, still wrapped in morning sleep, learned that she had then laid down the mortal remains of Rosa with St. Bartholomew.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[411] Dum vestitur tractabile cadaver, dum aptatur in feretro, [&amp; domesticos potius miro gaudio, quam dolore, afficit,] dum floribus inspersum effertur in anterius conclave, spectante matre, ingerebatur universis luctuosa plangendi licentia, nec tamen ulli præsentium libuit planctibus indulgere: siquidem repentino prodigio sic omnes pervasit arcanus quidam lætitiæ sensus, ut tota domus insperata metamorphosi devotis turgere solatiis, ac nuptiali propemodum gaudio innatare videretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030300Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030300Z">
        <seg>[411] While the tractable corpse was being dressed, while it was being laid in the coffin, [and it moved the household with a strange joy rather than with grief,] while it was being carried out, sprinkled with flowers, into the front room, with the mother looking on, a mournful license to wail was granted to all, yet not one of those present chose to give way to lamentations: for by a sudden prodigy a certain secret sense of joy so pervaded them all, that the entire house seemed to swell with unexpected consolations and to float in a kind of nuptial joy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[412] Illuxit demum clarior dies, &amp; ecce, quæstoris palatium subito hominum concursu plenum fuit, [statimque tanta populi multitudo] nemine vocante.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030331Z">
        <seg>[412] At last a brighter day dawned, and behold, the quaestor's palace was suddenly filled with a crowd of people, [and immediately such a multitude of the populace] without anyone calling them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[413] [ad cadaver ejus ultro confluit,] Interea circumfusi promiscue nobiles, ignobiles, advenæ, cives, Indi, Hispani certatim rosaria sancto corpusculo applicuerunt, flosculos religiosæ jacentis vesti instratos carptim undique diripuere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030359Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030359Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, the crowd that had gathered—nobles and commoners, newcomers and citizens, Indians and Spaniards alike—competed to press rosaries against the holy little body, and they tore from every direction the tiny flowers that had been woven into the religious woman's lying-in-state dress.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[414] [ut domus armatis militibus custodienda fuerit.] Crevit accurrentium multitudo &amp; cum multitudine importunitas, ut nec ostia domus sat laxa paterent confertim irruentibus, nec abituri exitum in tanta pressura invenirent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030418Z">
        <seg>[414] [so that the house had to be guarded by armed soldiers.] The crowd of onlookers swelled, and with it grew the crush, so that neither the house's doors, though wide open, could admit those rushing in, nor could those wishing to leave find an exit in such a jam.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[41] [post superatas varias difficultates,] Quæstor Gundisalvus, vir optimus, quique apud Virginem sibi concreditam valebat autoritate, imo &amp; domestico imperio, seu sponte, seu aliunde inductus, suggerebat reformatam sacri Carmeli regulam, quam Rosæ genio credebat aptius convenire;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203506Z">
        <seg>[After overcoming various difficulties,] Quaestor Gundisalvus, a most excellent man, who had taken refuge with the Virgin,[41] [After overcoming various difficulties,] Questor Gundisalvus, a most excellent man, who, with the Virgin entrusted to his care, had authority—nay, even domestic sway—whether of his own accord or induced by others, proposed a reformed rule for the sacred Carmel, which he believed more aptly suited to the genius of the Rose;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[41] [regnum Peruanum] Consideratione haud perfunctoria dignum puto, quod in his visionibus de Rosæ erga patriam affectu ac solicitudine compertum lego.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032002Z">
        <seg>[41] [The Peruvian kingdom] I consider it worthy of no superficial consideration that in these visions I find evidence of Rosa's affection and concern for her homeland.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[42] Concordat huic visioni altera, quæ ex depositione jurata sororis Catharinæ de S. Maria refertur in Processu;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032017Z">
        <seg>[42] Another vision, which is reported in the Process from the sworn deposition of Sister Catherine of St. Mary, is consistent with this vision;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[42] [&amp; maturam deliberationem,] Voto respondit eventus: nam operante Numine, supradictorum theologorum quaternio paribus utrimque votis pependit semper immobilis, stupuitque, neutri partium occurrere pondus, quo sese a tam constanti æquilibrio tandem explicarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203507Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203507Z">
        <seg>[42] [And mature deliberation,] The outcome answered the vow: for, with the Deity at work, the quartet of the aforementioned theologians hung in equal balance on both sides, ever motionless, and marveled that neither side could find the weight by which they might at last break away from such a steadfast equilibrium.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[43] Eliso igitur hoc ariete, superfuit alius;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203507Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203507Z">
        <seg>[43] Therefore, having rammed this one, another remained.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[43] Prodigiosius est, ac plurium signorum benefico concursu fœtum, quod referam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032042Z">
        <seg>[43] What I am about to relate is even more prodigious, and the result of the beneficial concurrence of many signs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[44] Evenit, uti speraverat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203511Z">
        <seg>It happened, as he had hoped;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[44] His dictis, gloriosa Virgo disparuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032107Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032107Z">
        <seg>[44] Having said this, the glorious Virgin disappeared.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[45] Verum Christophorus tam propositi sui tenax, quam de nocturni spectaculi veritate indubius, hilari fiducia cœnobium adiit, [de longiori vita certiorem reddi jubet.] infirmum salutavit, imperata exposuit ea frontis constantia animique securitate, ut brevi vel sola dicentis alacritas omnem ambigentibus excusserit credendi formidinem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032141Z">
        <seg>But Christopher, as tenacious of his purpose as he was certain of the truth of the nocturnal vision, approached the monastery with cheerful confidence, [he ordered that he be informed of a longer life.] greeted the infirm, and laid out his commands with such steadfastness of brow and security of spirit that, in a few words or even a single one, the speaker's alacrity alone dispelled all doubt in the minds of the hesitant.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[46] [Etiam visa est S. Rosa nonnullis personis;] Doctor Balthasar de Padilla metropolitanæ Limensis canonicus &amp; pœnitentiarus, de mandato sui archiepiscopi domum viduæ cujusdam adierat cum notario, sub juramenti fide excepturus, quæ de Rosæ vita ac prodigiis afferrentur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032154Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032154Z">
        <seg>[46] [St. Rose was also seen by certain persons;] Doctor Balthasar de Padilla, a canon and penitentiary of the metropolitan church of Lima, by order of his archbishop had gone to the house of a certain widow with a notary, to receive under oath whatever was brought concerning Rose's life and miracles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[46] [morbos peccatis suis imputabat,] Assueverat morborum suorum (quos in ægro exhaustoque corpusculo sustinuit crebros &amp; sævissimos) celare magnitudinem, ne quis subveniret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203513Z">
        <seg>[46] He attributed his illnesses to his own sins. He had grown accustomed to hiding the severity of his ailments (which he endured in his sick, emaciated little body, frequent and most severe) so that no one would come to his aid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[47] Verum si eo procederent, ut refellendis hujuscemodi vituperiis encomia Rosæ laudesque opponerent, [a laudibus suis abhorrebat,] expavescebat misera, ac velut fulminis ictu perculsa tremebat, pallore, silentio, gemitu mœstissime protestans se torqueri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211936Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203514Z">
        <seg>[47] But if they went so far as to oppose the praises and lauds of Rosa with refutations of such criticisms,[she abhorred her own praises,] the poor wretch was terrified, and as if struck by lightning she trembled, protesting most mournfully with pallor, silence, and groans that she was being tormented.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[47] [quæ coram judicibus apostolicis] Alteri personæ, spiritualibus exercitiis gnaviter addictæ, obtigit alibi conspicari visione imaginaria B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032206Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032206Z">
        <seg>[47] [which before the apostolic judges] Another person, zealous in spiritual exercises, happened to be seen elsewhere in a visionary apparition of the Blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[48] Quoddam perarduum heroicæ virtutis opus ad S. Catharinæ Senensis imitationem Rosa tentaverat, [innocens reprehensa veniam deprecabatur,] perfecerat: quod cum ipsius valetudini summe noxium &amp; exitiale crederet domina Isabella Mexia, horrore pio stimulata, P. Magistrum F. Alphonsum Velasquez, confessarium Virginis, admonuit, ut acerba reprehensione factum, velut excessum temerarium improbaret, ac Rosam a similibus ultra vires tentandis absterreret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000523Z">
        <seg>[48] Rosa had attempted a certain exceedingly arduous work of heroic virtue, in imitation of St. Catherine of Siena, [an innocent woman reproved, she begged for pardon,] and had accomplished it: which, believing it to be exceedingly harmful and fatal to her health, Lady Isabella Mexia, moved by pious horror, She admonished Father Master Friar Alphonsus Velasquez, the Virgin's confessor, to disapprove of the act with a harsh reprimand, as a rash excess, and to deter Rosa from attempting similar things beyond her strength.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[48] [testimonii reddendi gratia comparebant,] Hic vero subticendum non est, quod anno MDCXXXI Mariæ de Bustamante in Limensi sanctissimæ Trinitatis monasterio professæ moniali accidit, ipsa jurato id referente.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032222Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032222Z">
        <seg>[48] [They appeared for the purpose of bearing witness,] It must not, however, be omitted that in the year 1631 this happened to a professed nun of the monastery of the Most Holy Trinity in Lima, Maria de Bustamante, as she herself testified under oath.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[49] Inter has angustias dum nocte quadam in sua cellula solitaria obdormierat, [causam Beatificationis suæ promovere,] sensit alieno se motu, &amp; imperio, at absque terriculamento blandissime a somno excitari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032250Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032250Z">
        <seg>[49] While in the midst of these anxieties, one night, as he had fallen asleep in his solitary cell, [to advance the cause of his Beatification,] he felt himself moved and commanded by another, and, without any frightening alarm, was most gently awakened from his sleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[49] Quandocumque ad confessariorum pedes in suimet accusationem procumbebat, [coram confessariis minimos defectus suos exaggerabat,] ita mox obruebatur lachrymis, interpolabatur singultibus, dehiscebat profundis ex imo corde suspiriis, ut credidisses non nisi enormium flagitiorum ream esse, quæ sic plangebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203520Z">
        <seg>Whenever he fell at the feet of the confessors to accuse himself, [he exaggerated his slightest failings before them,] she was so soon overwhelmed with tears, broken by sobs, and rent by deep sighs from the bottom of her heart, that you would have believed she was guilty of nothing less than enormous crimes, for which she wept so bitterly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[4] [&amp; sacrum corpus per vices deferunt] Prodiit denique sub vesperum e Gundisalvi laxatis foribus mortuale pretiosi pignoris feretrum, cui spontaneos humeros primum supposuit capitulum metropolitanum, eo honoris obsequio, quod solis archiepiscopis vita functis præstare solet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030610Z">
        <seg>Finally, toward evening, the bier of the precious pledge emerged from the open gates of Gundisalvi, its shoulders first voluntarily offered by the metropolitan chapter, in that honorific observance which is customarily shown only to archbishops when they have passed away.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[4] [ita ut primum appellaretur Rosa,] Verumtamen lapsis aliquot annis, de Rosæ nomine aliam indixit litem ipsamet Virginis pavida conscientia, nimirum ut ita præberetur occasio locusque cælesti oraculo, quo Rosæ nomen ultimo erat confirmandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203449Z">
        <seg>[4] [so that she would first be called Rosa,] However, after several years had passed, the Virgin herself, with a fearful conscience, brought another suit concerning the name Rosa, namely to provide an occasion and a place for a heavenly oracle by which the name Rosa was to be finally confirmed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[50] Plura hujus generis superessent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032302Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032302Z">
        <seg>[50] More of this kind would remain;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[50] [&amp; se mancipiis inferiorem reputabat.] Testabatur Gundisalvi quæstoris uxor D. Maria Usatigui, quod ejus in ædibus dum triennlum vixit Rosa, adeo sese omnium reputarit infimam, ut non soli patrifamilias &amp; conjugi, sed tenellis quoque filiabus, imo servis, ancillis, mancipiis domesticis subjecta ad nutum, singulorum æque placita, velut imperia, exceperit, favorem arbitrata, si quid sibi ceu precario in domum receptæ heriliter mandabatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203521Z">
        <seg>Maria Usatigui, because Rosa had lived in her house for three years, considered herself so inferior to everyone that she submitted to the wills of not only the head of the household and her husband, but also to her tender daughters, indeed, subject to the will of servants, maids, and household slaves, she received everyone's commands as if they were orders, believing she had favor whenever anything was commanded of her as a mistress, as if she had been taken into the house on sufferance;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[51] Igitur die quadam, cum a primo diluculo usque ad vesperam scribendo laborasset, [vires ad celeriter scribendum] enormiter fessus, totoque brachio dolens, cubitum se recepit, dubius, ne forte postridie ulteriori scriptioni inutilis surgeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032318Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032318Z">
        <seg>[51] So one day, having labored at writing from early dawn until evening, his strength for writing quickly exhausted and his whole arm aching, he went to bed, uncertain whether he might not be too weak to write the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[51] [Præter dotes corporis,] Diu nescivit Rosa, quidquam vel minimum in se reliquum esse, quod laudi pateret, aut pulchrum æstimari posset, donec femina quædam obiter manus Virginis conspicata (ut est muliebre ingenium ad similia lynceum, &amp; insigniter oculatum) commendavit in iis candorem, decentiam, proportionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203524Z">
        <seg>[51][Besides her bodily charms,] For a long time Rosa was unaware that she had anything left in herself worthy of praise or that could be considered beautiful, until a certain woman, having casually caught sight of the Virgin's hands (for women's nature is keen and remarkably observant of such things), commended their whiteness, grace, and proportion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[52] [austeritatem jejuniorum,] Pridem studuerat humilis Virgo omni arte nativam speciosissimi vultus sui gratiam abolere, ne posset mortalibus oculis placere, idque non solum jugi inedia, dirisque macerationibus, sed &amp; aqua gelida creberrime toti corpori superfusa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211949Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203524Z">
        <seg>[52] [the austerity of fasts,] The humble Virgin had long sought by every means to erase the native beauty of her most lovely face, so that it might not please mortal eyes, and this not only by constant fasting and dire austerities, but also by frequently pouring cold water over her entire body;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[52] [miro modo subministravit.] Inter has cogitationum vicissitudines sese vestiens, sensit dextrum lacertum solito agiliorem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032340Z">
        <seg>[52] [He supplied it in a marvelous way.] As he dressed himself amid these fluctuations of thought, he felt his right arm more agile than usual;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[53] Inde lepidum obvenit quibusdam Argis&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; per otium curiosis, [ita ut post diuturnum jejunium laute epulata crederetur,] qui in feria sexta Parasceves, forte in templi vestibulo confabulantes, observaverant Rosam cum matre domum ab ecclesia redeuntem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203525Z">
        <seg>[53] Hence a charming incident befell certain Argives who, out of idle curiosity, were chatting in the temple vestibule and saw Rosa returning home from church with her mother.&lt;/s4&gt; who, while chatting in the temple vestibule on the Friday of Holy Week, had seen Rosa returning home from church with her mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[54] Nonnulli (ut fit) liberioris vitæ, solo curiositatis spiritu adducti, [magni peccatores resipuerunt] ut defunctam pulcherrimam Virginem propius cum aliis contuerentur in sandapila, ad ictum primi intuitus exarserunt in subitum intimumque dolorem peccatorum, rigarunt ora lacrymis, protestantes, alio quam venerant spiritu se jam recedere, posthac emendationi vitæ ac emendationi morum se operam daturos efficacem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032513Z">
        <seg>[54] Some (as is the way) of a more dissolute life, moved only by a spirit of curiosity, [great sinners repented] so that they might gaze more closely with others at the dead, most beautiful Virgin in the coffin, At the first glance they were seized with a sudden and deep sorrow for their sins, their faces were bathed in tears, protesting that they were departing with a spirit quite different from the one with which they had come, and that from then on they would devote themselves effectively to reforming their lives and their morals.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[54] Par ei solicitudo &amp; cura fuit abscondendi reliquos suorum charismatum thesauros, [aliasque virtutes] ne humanis laudibus paterent in prædam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203526Z">
        <seg>[54] He had the same concern and care for concealing the remaining treasures of his charisms, [and other virtues], so that they would not fall prey to human praise.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[55] Diu recusavit, diu cunctatus est confessarius, haud ignarus, [solicite abscondebat,] quam sibi id arduum futurum esset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203527Z">
        <seg>[55] He refused for a long time; the confessor hesitated for a long time, not unaware that he was hiding it [carefully], how arduous it would be for him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[55] Mater Virginis Maria de Oliva anno MDCXVIII XV Februarii coram deputatis judicibus inter alia recensuit, [(his adde tepidos religiosos ad fervorem excitatos)] post exsequias Filiæ successivis intervallis ad se venisse complusculas etiam ex regularium Ordinum familiis devotas personas, antea sibi penitus ignotas, quæ, ut suam notoriam egestatem liberali aliquo subsidio sublevaturæ accesserant, ita singulæ profitebantur, hac ratione se mera gratitudinis tributa pendere virgini Rosæ, cujus intercessionibus, vitæ suæ optimam se mutationem debere sciebant, lætæ, nunc alias se esse ab eo quod fuerant, eoque felicitatis, nonnisi post imploratum Rosæ in cælesti curia interventum, se pertigisse asseverabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032519Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032519Z">
        <seg>[55] The Mother of the Virgin Mary of Oliva, on February 15, 1618, before the deputied judges, among other things recounted, [(add to these lukewarm religious stirred to fervor)] that after the funeral of her Daughter, at successive intervals, several devout persons had come to her, even from the families of the Regular Orders, before completely unknown to her, who, having come seeking to relieve their notorious poverty with some liberal aid, each in turn professed that they were paying these tributes of pure gratitude to the Virgin Rose, whose intercessions, knew they owed the best change in their lives to the virgin Rose, and were joyful, now being different from what they had been, and asserted that they had attained this happiness only after imploring Rose's intercession in the heavenly court.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[56] Admiratione præ multis dignum est, quod de quodam insigni peccatore iisdem judicibus, eodemque mense &amp; anno, ex certa sua (uti protestatur) scientia aperuit P. F. Bartholomæus Martinez, Rosæ quondam confessarius, &amp; Limæ tunc cœnobio Fratrum Prædicatorum ad sanctæ Mariæ Magdalenæ præfectus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032526Z">
        <seg>[56] It is worthy of admiration above many things that Father Francis Bartholomew Martinez, formerly confessor of the Rose and then prior of the Dominican convent of Saint Mary Magdalene in Lima, revealed concerning a certain notorious sinner, from his own certain knowledge (as he affirms), to the same judges, in the same month and year.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[56] [&amp; Deiparam adjutricem habuit hujus humilitatis,] Favit mirabiliter huic virtuti Deipara, humilium idea;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203527Z">
        <seg>[56] [&amp; He had the Virgin Mary as an assistant in this humility,] The Virgin Mary wonderfully favored this virtue, the model of the humble;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[57] [pertinacis cujusdam peccatoris,] Mox ille quasi ab annoso lethargo evigilans, cœpit sentire intus sibilum illius auræ tenuis ac potentissimæ, qua Deus afflat corda obstinatorum, ut instar ceræ liquescant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032544Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032544Z">
        <seg>[57] [of a certain stubborn sinner,] Soon he, as if awakening from a long-standing lethargy, began to feel within the hiss of that subtle and most powerful breeze, by which God breathes upon the hearts of the obstinate, so that they may melt like wax.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[57] [quam cum blanda affabilitate conjungebat.] Tantam ejus humilitatem indivulse comitabatur suavissima mansuetudo, placida affabilitas, mite supercilium: mirati sunt quotquot eam noverant, numquam ab illius ore excidisse verbum duriusculum, obstreperum, criticum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203527Z">
        <seg>[57] [which he combined with gentle affability.][57] Such great humility was inseparably accompanied by the sweetest meekness, placid affability, and a gentle brow: all who knew her were amazed that not a single harsh, boisterous, or critical word ever escaped her lips;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[58] [&amp; ut coram judicibus apostolicis] Idem P. Martinez in eodem examine sancte asseruit, sibi non tantum proprio in excipiendis confessionibus experimento, sed &amp; aliunde ex concordi plurimorum confessariorum testimoniis constare liquido, post imploratum Rosæ in cælis auxilium, innumeros tum Limæ tum in universo regno, ex infima scelerum abysso emersisse in portum veræ pœnitentiæ, sinceraque confessione &amp; lacrymis animam expiasse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032603Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032603Z">
        <seg>[58] [&amp; before the apostolic judges] The same P. Martinez in the same examination solemnly asserted that it was clear to him, not only from his own experience in hearing confessions, but also from the unanimous testimony of many confessors, that after imploring the help of the Rosary in heaven, countless numbers both in Lima and throughout the entire kingdom, from the lowest abyss of sins, they had emerged into the harbor of true penitence, and had purified their souls with sincere confession and tears.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[59] Nondum infantiæ pannos &amp; annos pupula excesserat, [a teneris annis ita assueverat abstinentiæ,] cum sibi (quod illi ætatulæ peregrinum &amp; grave est) omni fructuum esu interdixit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203528Z">
        <seg>[59] The pupil had not yet passed the swaddling clothes and years of infancy, [having been accustomed to abstinence from a tender age,] when she forbade herself, which is unusual and burdensome for that age, to eat any fruit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[59] [generatim testati sunt diversi confessarii,] Consonat his per omnia P. Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032619Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032619Z">
        <seg>[59] [Various confessors have testified in general,] Father Fr. agrees with these in every respect.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[5] Certe si cupidissimæ huic turbæ, quod libuit, licuisset, [ad templum PP. Prædicatorum,] periculum erat, ne sacri corpusculi pars modica aut nulla ad templum perveniret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030623Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030623Z">
        <seg>[5] Certainly, if this most eager crowd had been allowed to do as it pleased, there was a danger that only a small part, or none at all, of the sacred body would reach the church.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[5] Habent quidem hoc (Ambrosio teste) Sanctorum merita, [cui deinde cognomen a sancta Maria additum est.] ut a Deo nomen accipiant, sed qui ultra nomen etiam cognomen e cælo accipiant, raros puto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203450Z">
        <seg>[5] The merits of the Saints do indeed have this (as Ambrose testifies), [to whom the surname from Saint Mary was then added.] but I think those who receive a surname from heaven in addition to their name are rare.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[60] Interdum nobiles matronæ, devotionis ergo, [ut carnes aut jusculum digerere non posset,] matrem cum Filia in suas ædes invitabant, epuloque sobrie instructo, rogitabant utramque secum una accumbere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203528Z">
        <seg>Sometimes noble matrons, out of devotion, would invite the mother and daughter into their homes, and after a sober meal was set out, they would ask both of them to recline with them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[60] Sat esto, Limenses, aliosque in universa Peruana regione sacramentalis pœnitentiæ administros, [quos illam vitæ emendationem experientia docuit.] communicatis generatim indiciis, adstupuisse tantæ morum mutationi in populo, a die, qua Rosa in Sponsi sui paradiso cœpit agere suorum civium advocatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032637Z">
        <seg>[60] Let it suffice, Lima and other ministers of sacramental penance throughout the entire region of Peru—whom experience has taught that reform of life—that, having received general indications, they were astonished at such a great change of morals in the people from the day on which Rosa began to act as advocate for her fellow citizens in her Spouse's paradise.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[61] Accidit, (referente apud judices conjuge quæstoris Gundisalvi) duas matronas considere obiter ad confabulandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032656Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032656Z">
        <seg>It happened, as related before the judges by the wife of Quaestor Gundisalvi, that two matrons happened to sit down to chat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[61] Semel ex morbo sat ancipiti emerserat, utque citius instauraretur, [&amp; post ea ex mandato degustata] ex præscripto medici, pauxillum carnis paventi ejus stomacho obtruserant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203529Z">
        <seg>[61] Having once emerged from a rather serious illness, and so that he might recover more quickly, [and after that, by order, having tasted] by the doctor's prescription, they had forced a little meat down his stomach, which was still tender.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[62] Pater Joannes de Villalobos e Societate Jesu, [nostro Joanni de Villalobos,] Rector Limani tirocinii seu domus novitiorum, vir in ascetica palæstra exercitatissimus, de semetipso hæc recensuit eidem judicum tribunali;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032727Z">
        <seg>Father John de Villalobos of the Society of Jesus, [our John de Villalobos,] Rector of the novitiate house of Lima, a man most highly trained in the ascetic palestra, recounted these things about himself before the same tribunal of judges.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[62] [identidem in morbum recideret,] Laborabat frequenti ischiade&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;, ac plerumque ea dolorum vehementia, quæ stomachi fundum everteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203529Z">
        <seg>[62] [He would repeatedly relapse into illness,] He suffered from frequent sciatica, and usually from such violent pains that they would upset the very depths of his stomach.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[63] At quæstor Gundisalvus sacramento affirmat, sibi fassos esse complures tum sæculares tum Regulares, [aliisque promissa dona impetrat,] quibus rogata Virgo idem in agone promiserat, statim ab ejus felici transitu se ex superno sancti Spiritus myrothecio&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt; sensisse arcanas cordis sui unctiones, supernorum luminum stricturas insolitas, novi nominis calculos candidos, quos (ut monemur Apocal.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032740Z">
        <seg>[63] But the quaestor Gundisalvus affirms under oath that several, both secular and Regular, confessed to him, [and obtains promised gifts for others,] to whom the Virgin, when asked, had promised the same in their agony, immediately after her blessed passing they felt from the heavenly myrrh-bearing of the Holy Spirit&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt; the secret anointings of their hearts, unusual restraints of heavenly lights, the white computations of a new name, which (as we are warned in the Apocalypse</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[63] [sicut frequens experientia docuit.] Ibidem alia vice observatis in Virgine manifestæ debilitatis nimiis indiciis, voluit idem paterfamilias, quod ex ala coctæ aviculæ quid modicum sumeret, quo labentem stomachum nonnihil erigeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203529Z">
        <seg>[63] As frequent experience has shown. There, on another occasion, observing in the virgin clear signs of manifest weakness, the same paterfamilias wished to take a little from the roasted bird's wing to somewhat lift her failing stomach.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[64] [&amp; in favorem cujusdam Francisci,] Francisco &amp; Alexandro de Coloma germanis fratribus matertera erat Maria de Xuara mulier prædives &amp; opulenta, sed animo in consobrinos implacabiliter averso &amp; obstinato.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032758Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032758Z">
        <seg>[64] [and in favor of a certain Francis,] Maria de Xuara, a very wealthy and opulent woman, was a maternal aunt of Francisco and Alexander de Coloma, the two brothers, but her spirit was implacably hostile and obstinate toward her cousins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[64] [Unde arbitrio ejus relinqui debuit] Impulerunt cruda hæc experimenta commiserantem quæstorem, ut Virginis confessarios, medicos, parentes, sigillatim ac pene adjurato obsecraverit, ne deinceps imperato carnium esu, Rosam in vitæ discrimen adductam vellent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203530Z">
        <seg>[64][Wherefore it ought to have been left to his discretion] These cruel experiments moved the compassionate inquisitor to beseech the Virgin's confessors, physicians, and parents, one by one and almost under oath, not to wish to bring Rose to the brink of death by ordering her to eat meat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[65] Tardius matri persuasum fuit, ut Filiæ tam severam tenuissimi victus rationem formamque æquanimiter indulgeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203530Z">
        <seg>It was later persuaded to the mother that she should equably indulge her daughter in such a strict regimen and form of the most meager diet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[65] [qui patrocinium ejus imploraverat,] Hic Franciscus suis orphanis acerbe condolens, &amp; quo se verteret ignorans, Rosam unicam suæ perplexitatis Ariadnam&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt; imploravit, invenit: ipsa videlicet proxima ante discessum nocte, Virgini (cujus effigiem coram habebat) totam miserabilium ærumnam anxie commendans obsecrabat, suo apud omnipotentem cordium domitorem interventu emolliret saxeam illius aversi cordis duritiem, sanguinis ac humanitatis sensum obstipæ materteræ inderet, innocentibus ac pauperiei lubricitate periclitaturis succurreret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032811Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032811Z">
        <seg>[65] [who had implored her patronage,] Here Francis, bitterly commiserating his orphans and not knowing where to turn, implored the Rose, the sole Ariadne of his perplexity, and found her: she herself, namely, on the night before her departure, Anxiously commending to the Virgin (whose image he had before him) all his miserable afflictions, he implored that by her intercession with the Almighty disposer of hearts she would soften the stony hardness of his avaricious heart, restore the sense of blood and humanity to his benumbed uncle, and come to the aid of the innocent and those endangered by the slipperiness of poverty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[66] Audierat in vicina sylva pronasci herbam insigniter præ ceteris amaram: [quem herbis amarissimis] hanc sibi clam adferri, ex ea deinceps insipidum pulmentarium sibi jussit apparari, &amp; ne tam exquisitæ provisionis annona deficeret, herbam eamdem suo hortulo implantavit, suis manibus solicite coluit, persuadens matri, valere in usum medicinæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203328Z">
        <seg>[66] He had heard that in a nearby forest a notably bitter herb grew: [which, among the most bitter herbs] was secretly brought to him; from then on he had a tasteless porridge made for himself from it, and so that this most exquisite provision would not run out, he planted the same herb in his own little garden, carefully tended it with his own hands, persuading his mother that it was useful for medicinal purposes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[66] [testamentum obstinatæ materteræ mutat.] Insuper Francisco injunxit, una publicum notarium advocaret, aliud se coram eo testamentum, rescisso priori, condituram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032826Z">
        <seg>[66] [Changes her obstinate aunt's will.] Furthermore, she instructed Francis to summon a public notary, saying she would draw up another will before him, rescinding the previous one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[67] Fruticat in illo occiduarum Indiarum fertili tractu planta mirabilis, [occulte condiebat.] GRANADILLA&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt; vernaculis dicitur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203331Z">
        <seg>[67] In that fertile tract of the western Indies grows a marvelous plant, [secretly prepared.] It is called GRANADILLA by the natives;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[67] [Mulierem ab instituto Dominicano abhorrentem,] Ludovica Barba nil minus versabat animo, quam candidum S. Patris Dominici assumere habitum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032845Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032845Z">
        <seg>[A woman averse to the Dominican institute,] Ludovica Barba had nothing less on her mind than to assume the white habit of St. Dominic.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[68] At fefellit mulierem ipsa resistendi fiducia, ac pertinaciæ certitudo: [ad tertium S. Dominici Ordinem adducit,] ecce enim subito a morte Virginis elanguit illud contumaciæ robur, evanuit annosus horror, patuit ultro cordis janua cælesti inspirationi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032904Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032904Z">
        <seg>[68] But the woman was undone by her own confidence in her resistance and her certainty of obstinacy; [she is brought to the Third Order of St. Dominic,] for behold, suddenly, at the Virgin's death, that stronghold of contumacy withered away, the age-old horror vanished, and the door of her heart opened of its own accord to heavenly inspiration.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[68] [Jejunia ejus erant adeo frequentia] Transeundum nunc ad jejunia, tametsi dubitari haud immerito possit, ab ipsa esurie, an a talibus cibis durius esse tormentum potuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203332Z">
        <seg>[68] His fasts were so frequent. We must now turn to the fasts, although one might not unreasonably doubt whether the hunger itself or such food could have been a more severe torment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[69] Ludovica de Mendoza, conjux Alphonsi Gonzalez de sancto Martino, [&amp; aliam de sanctitate sua dubitantem] paulo post sanctæ Virginis obitum, videns eamdem populariter tot encomiis celebrari, incredula, seu pro feminea resistendi dulcedine seu pii affectus contumaci algore, fidem sanctitatis ejus concipere aut nequibat, aut nolebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032932Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032932Z">
        <seg>[69] Ludovica de Mendoza, wife of Alfonso Gonzalez de santo Martino, [and another who doubted her sanctity], a little after the Virgin's death, seeing her celebrated by the people with so many encomiums, incredulous—whether from the feminine delight in resisting or from the stubborn chill of pious affection—could neither conceive nor would she believe in her sanctity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[69] [diuturnaque,] Residuo anni tempore panis mensuram tam sibi curtam indixerat, ut quod unicæ diei sat esse credebatur, integro vix octiduo totum absumeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203333Z">
        <seg>[69] For the rest of the year he had prescribed such a scant measure of bread that what was believed to be enough for a single day would scarcely last him eight days in all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[6] Illatam sacello majori excepit tumulus gradatim a pavimento elevatus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030632Z">
        <seg>[6] A mound, gradually raised from the floor, received the offering in the main chapel;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[6] Sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis infantia ac pueritia tam dulcis omnibus ac blanda fuisse legitur, [Puella adhuc tenera præbet indicium] ut non Catharinam, sed Euphrosynam&lt;a0&gt;k&lt;/a0&gt; plerique cœperint appellare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T214253Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203315Z">
        <seg>[6] The infancy and childhood of Saint Catherine of Siena are said to have been so sweet and gentle to everyone [The still tender girl provides a clue.] that most people began to call her not Catherine, but Euphrosyne&lt;a0&gt;k&lt;/a0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[6] Sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis infantia ac pueritia tam dulcis omnibus ac blanda fuisse legitur, [Puella adhuc tenera præbet indicium] ut non Catharinam, sed Euphrosynam&lt;s0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;k&lt;/a1&gt;&lt;/s0&gt; plerique cœperint appellare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202724Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T202149Z">
        <seg>[6] The infancy and childhood of Saint Catherine of Siena are said to have been so sweet and gentle to everyone, [A still-tender girl provides a sign] so that most people began to call her not Catherine, but Euphrosyne&lt;s0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;k&lt;/a1&gt;&lt;/s0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[70] Repentino angore correpta est, ut manibus pedibusque se putarit demutilatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032950Z">
        <seg>She was seized by a sudden anguish, so that she believed herself to have been maimed in hands and feet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[70] [&amp; cibus tam parcus,] Nescio, jejunium an plane inediam appellavero, quod sequitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203333Z">
        <seg>[70] [And the food so meager,] I don't know whether I should call what follows a fast or outright starvation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[71] Aliquando communicanti solæ species sacramentales tantum roboris contulerunt, [ut merito inedia vocari posset.] ut in fortitudine cibi illius toto octiduo nullum aliud nutrimentum admiserit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203334Z">
        <seg>[71] At one time, the mere sacramental species, which she received, imparted such strength that, rightly, it could be called a fast, for in the fortitude of that food she accepted no other nourishment for the entire eight days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[72] Jam ad effodiendum tantillo cadaveri sepulchrum paratus adstabat sarculus, [cujus mater opem S. Rosæ imploraverat,] comportabantur flosculi, nectendi in modicam corollam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033127Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033127Z">
        <seg>A small spade stood ready to dig a grave for the little corpse, [whose mother had implored the help of St. Rose,] and little flowers were being gathered to weave into a modest wreath.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[73] Anno MDCXXXI Antonius Bran mancipium domicellæ Joannæ Barrettæ decumbebat ex febribus &amp; angore pectoris, [&amp; mancipium, quod defunctum credebatur,] jamque per tres menses &amp; amplius morbi contumacia variorum medicamentorum vim omnem eluserat, ac parum spei residuum erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033149Z">
        <seg>In the year 1631, Anthony Bran, a servant of the young lady Joanna Barrett, was lying sick with fevers and chest pains, [and the servant, who was believed to be dead,] and for more than three months the stubbornness of the disease had eluded all the power of various medicines, and little hope remained.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[73] [pro publica regni Peruani] Siqua aut regno Peruano, aut urbi Limensi, patriæ suæ, flagella divinitus imminebant, suis conabatur flagellis divertere: nec animarum in purgatoriis flammis bullientium immemor, duplicabat ictus catenarum in nova cruoris profluvia, quæ rogi partem extinguerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203336Z">
        <seg>[73][for the public good of the Kingdom of Peru] If any divine scourges threatened either the Kingdom of Peru or the city of Lima, his native land, he strove to divert them by his own: and not oblivious of the souls seething in the flames of purgatory, he doubled the strokes of the chains in new torrents of blood, which would extinguish part of the pyre.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[74] Inde conversa ad caput lectuli, vidit appensam venerabilis Rosæ chartaceam imaginem ex iis, quæ Romæ Superiorum permissu excusæ, &amp; in Indias delatæ fuerant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033211Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033211Z">
        <seg>Then, turning to the head of the little bed, she saw hanging there the venerable Rose's paper image, one of those that had been printed in Rome with the Superiors' permission and brought to the Indies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[74] [vel urbis Limensis necessitate] Assueverant domestici (quamquam inscia Virgine) ex ædium parte secretissima, per singulas noctes audire hos disciplinarum ictus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203336Z">
        <seg>[74] [or by the necessity of the city of Lima] The household servants (though the Virgin was unaware) had grown accustomed, from the most secret part of the house, to hearing these blows of discipline every night;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[75] Magister F. Joannes de Lorenzana confessarius Virginis, [ferreo flagello aliisque modis,] cum hunc disciplinarum rigorem imbecillitati corporis paulo æquius censuisset attemperandum, Rosa maximis ab eo precibus extorsit, quo saltem liceret sibi intra certum dierum spatium verbera quinquies mille sibi infligere, qui pie creditur esse numerus ictuum, quos in sua crudeli flagellatione Christus excepit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T212048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203338Z">
        <seg>John of Lorenzana, the Virgin's confessor, [with an iron scourge and other methods,] When John of Lorenzana, the Virgin's confessor, [with an iron scourge and other means,] judged that this severity of discipline should be tempered somewhat more justly to her bodily frailty, Rose extorted from him by the most earnest prayers that she might at least be permitted, within a certain space of days, to inflict upon herself five thousand lashes, which is piously believed to be the number of blows that Christ received in his own cruel scourging.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[75] [Elisabeth Duran vidua,] Elisabeth Duran, vidua Jacobi Carlos, a multo tempore siccum &amp; inutile brachium gestabat fasciis obvolutum, cujus non solo inerti pondere, sed &amp; jugi dolore vexabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033229Z">
        <seg>[75] [Elisabeth Duran, widow,] Elisabeth Duran, widow of James Carlos, had for a long time been carrying a dry and useless arm, bound up in bandages, by whose not only inert weight but also constant pain she was afflicted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[76] Porro, quam avide in pœnitentiæ exercitia propenderit Rosæ genius, [quibus ab ipsa pueritia assueverat,] jam inde ferme ab infantia seriis præludiis abunde ostenderat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203339Z">
        <seg>[76] Moreover, how eagerly Rosa's genius inclined toward the exercises of penance, [to which she had been accustomed from her very childhood,] she had already abundantly shown by serious preludes, almost from infancy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[76] [servus quidam Æthiops,] Licentiati Didaci Ayalas servus Æthiops (nomen expressum nusquam reperi) dextri brachii manusque usum ex plica &amp; nervorum attractione dudum perdiderat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033254Z">
        <seg>[76] [A certain Ethiopian slave,] The Ethiopian slave of Licentiate Diego Ayala (his name is nowhere recorded) had long since lost the use of his right arm and hand from a sprain and nerve strain;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[77] Aliis vicibus, sub eodem silentio, rogabat sibi gravari scapulam informi trabe, [tenerum corpus affligebat,] collectisque exiguis viribus, conabatur obnixa sufferre durissimam sarcinam, dum preculas suas bene longas absolveret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203339Z">
        <seg>At other times, under the same silence, she would ask for an inform bar to be placed on her shoulder, afflicting her tender body, and, gathering her small remaining strength, she would try to bear the most difficult burden with all her might, while she unwound her very long braids.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[77] [Georgius de Aranda presbyter] Verum a dextro ad brachium sinistrum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033303Z">
        <seg>[77] [George de Aranda, priest] But from the right to the left arm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[78] Mox religione ac lætitia tremulus, in ecclesiam (ubi tunc forte P. F. Christophorus de Azevedo Prior Panamensis cum plurimis sæcularibus substirerat) se propripuit, [amissum brachii sui usum recuperant.] ibique coram altari SS. Rosarii elatis vocibus in gratiarum actionem procubuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033319Z">
        <seg>[78] Soon, trembling with piety and joy, he made his way into the church (where, by chance, Father Francis Christopher de Azevedo, Prior of Panama, was standing with many laymen), and there, before the altar of the Holy Rosary, he fell to the ground in thanksgiving with raised voices.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[78] [&amp; ferrea catena se flagellare prohibita,] Inhibitum ei fuerat (uti supra attigimus) ne amplius ferream catenam in usum flagelli adhiberet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203340Z">
        <seg>[78] [and forbidden to flog himself with an iron chain,] He had been forbidden (as we noted above) to use the iron chain as a whip any longer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[79] Alphonsus Diaz mendicus toti Limæ tunc notissimus, [Alphonsus Diaz mendicus,] humili nixus fulcro, manumque &amp; pedem ex attractione inutiles per solum trahens, non gradiebatur, sed serpebat humi, stipem per domos flebiliter quærens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033344Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033344Z">
        <seg>Alphonso Diaz, a beggar then well known throughout all of Lima, leaning on a humble staff and dragging his hand and foot, rendered useless by paralysis, across the ground, did not walk but crawled on the earth, pitifully begging for alms from door to door.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[79] [ea corpusculum tam arcte cinxit, ut carnem penetraret, ] Crescebat interim sub indissolubili vinculo atrox ischiadis cruciatus, qui demum invitæ Rosæ, post longos gemitus, etiam miserandos ejulatus extorsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203340Z">
        <seg>[79] [It had so tightly girdled the little body that it pierced the flesh,] Meanwhile, under the unbreakable bond, the dreadful torment of sciatica grew, which at last, after long groans, wrung even pitiful sobs from a reluctant Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[7] Sed redeundum ad Rosam in suo jam tumulo spectabiliorem, [ut exsequiæ vix celebrari potuerint,] &amp; reciproca accensi vultus suavitate renidentem: huc enim totus populi refluxus incumbebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030651Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030651Z">
        <seg>[7] But we must return to Rosa, more magnificent now in her tomb, [so that the funeral could scarcely be held,] and reciprocally resplendent with the sweet radiance of her features: for to this spot the entire crowd was pouring back.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[7] Trimula erat, quando in tenerrima indole promicare cœperunt supra ætatem robusta virtutum specimina.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211913Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203315Z">
        <seg>[7] The infancy and childhood of Saint Catherine of Siena are said to have been so sweet and gentle to everyone, [A still-tender girl provides a sign] so that most people began to call her not Catherine, but Euphrosynek.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[80] Hic primum sensit Alphonsus, se curatum esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033400Z">
        <seg>Here Alphonsus first realized that he had been cared for.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[80] [&amp; ideo illam confessarius etiam auferri jusserit.] Vix laceratæ cutis vulnera coïerant, quando Rosa ad suam catenam rediit, pristinoque ferri tormento se rursus incinxit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203341Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203341Z">
        <seg>[80] [And so the confessor even ordered her to be taken away.] The wounds of her torn flesh had barely begun to heal when Rosa returned to her chain and again fastened herself to the iron torment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[81] Neque pulpæ brachiorum ab ejusmodi vinculis immunes erant: [Deinde brachia vinculis constringebat,] his enim Rosa, ad cruciatum, arcta quædam ligamina circumdederat, quæ perpetim torquebant complicatos musculos;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203346Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203346Z">
        <seg>[81] Nor were the paps of the arms immune from such bonds: [Then he would bind the arms with cords,] for Rosa, for the purpose of torture, had fastened certain tight ligatures around them, which perpetually twisted the coiled muscles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[81] [&amp; duo Æthiopes] Dum Rosæ cadaver prostabat in tumulo, adrepsit puer Æthiops duodenni major, ac de attractorum pedum vitio, quam de nomine per urbem notior;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033428Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033428Z">
        <seg>[81] [and two Ethiopians] While Rose's corpse lay in the tomb, an Ethiopian boy, older than twelve, crept up, and was more famous throughout the city for the deformity of his drawn-in feet than for his name.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[82] Verum ubi pro ingenti munere ditatam se vidit brevi cilicio, [hispidum cilicium gestabat,] quasi purpuram, byssum, trabeamve nacta, sibi suæque tremulæ carni gratulabatur de novo pœnitentiæ baltheo, rigoris paludamento.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T212042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203350Z">
        <seg>[82] But when she saw herself enriched with a huge gift—a short tunic of coarse cloth, as though she had received purple, linen, or a robe—she congratulated herself and her trembling flesh on the new belt of penance, the cloak of austerity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[82] [mirabiliter contractione pedum liberantur.] Hujus exemplo alius puer, itidem Æthiops, simillimaque pedum invalitudine calamito sus (nisi quod grallis utcumque se librabat a terra, non tamen a parietum subsidio) post humatum Rosæ funus accessit juxta fossam, in angulum capitularis aulæ se projecit, oravit, &amp; ultra duarum horarum spatium ibidem supplex accubuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033440Z">
        <seg>[82] [They are miraculously freed by the contraction of their feet.] At his example, another boy, also an Ethiopian, and suffering from a very similar weakness of the feet (except that he supported himself on crutches, not on the aid of walls), after Rosa's funeral was buried, came to the grave, threw himself into a corner of the capitular hall, He prayed, and for more than two hours he lay there supplicating.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[83] Aliam tamen ipsa adinvenit castigandi corpusculi rationem, [&amp; singula corporis sui membra] quam priori clam substitueret, minori forsan valetudinis damno, at non leviori molestia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203532Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203532Z">
        <seg>[83] She herself, however, devised another method of punishing her little body, [and each of her body's members], which she would secretly substitute for the previous one, perhaps with less harm to her health, but not with less inconvenience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[83] [Plures pueri] Joannæ de Castillo viduæ filiolus erat bimus Franciscus Fernandez de Sigura, cataleptis&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;, palpitationibus ab anno integro frequenter ac periculose divexatus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033508Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033508Z">
        <seg>[83] Among the many boys, the little son of Joanna de Castillo, the widow, was two-year-old Francis Fernandez de Sigura, who was frequently and dangerously afflicted with cataleptic seizures and palpitations for a whole year.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[84] [diversis morbis oppressi] Rufina Brava filiolum genuerat Petrum Tamayo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033527Z">
        <seg>[84] [Oppressed by various diseases] Rufina Brava had given birth to a little son, Peter Tamayo.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[84] [peculiari tormento cruciabat.] His blandimentis Rosa demulcebat tenue illud corpusculum, cui alias a crebris infirmitatibus sat pœnarum erat, ut infra suo loco videbitur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203543Z">
        <seg>[84] [tortured with a peculiar torment.] With these caresses Rosa soothed that frail little body, which otherwise had already suffered enough from its frequent infirmities, as will be seen below in its proper place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[85] Petro de Vega graviter a quatuor mensibus filiola quinquennis decumbebat ex febribus, [per intercessionem ejusdem Virginis] neque mitius devenustabat parvulam asperrima quædam morosæ indolis conditio, cum annis in vitium periculose adultura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033555Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033555Z">
        <seg>Peter de Vega's five-year-old daughter had been gravely ill for four months from fevers, [through the intercession of the same Virgin], and the little one's condition was made no less distressing by a very severe, morose disposition, which was dangerously likely to worsen with age.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[86] Annos complusculos duravit, &amp; induruit pia Virgo sub hoc oneroso capitis spineto;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000943Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000943Z">
        <seg>It lasted for several years, and the pious Virgin endured under this burdensome crown of thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[86] Videbatur his prodigiis Rosa clamare de tumulo: [sanitati restituuntur.] “Sinite parvulos venire ad me”, Veniebant utique, ac certatim porrigebantur de manu in manum, ut ad salutiferum Rosæ contactum infirma pertingeret populorum innocentia, cum salute ad suos reportanda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033601Z">
        <seg>[86] It seemed that by these miracles Rose was crying out from the tomb: [they are restored to health.] "Let the little ones come to me." They did indeed come, and were passed from hand to hand in turn, so that the infirm might reach the salutary touch of Rose, the innocent of the people, to carry back health to their own.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[87] Novo igitur ardore, ac patiendi desiderio, lamellam argenteam sibi flexit in circulum, [cujus forma describitur,] huic ab intus ex eodem metallo tres infixit aculeorum ordines, ita ut quælibet series triginta &amp; tres contineret aculeos, videlicet pro numero annorum Christi, quos mortalis pro mortalibus explevit in terra.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000949Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000949Z">
        <seg>[87] With a new zeal, then, and a desire to suffer, he bent a silver lamella into a circle [whose shape is described], and into this he fixed from the inside, of the same metal, three rows of spikes, so that each row contained thirty-three spikes, namely, for the number of years of Christ, which a mortal fulfilled on earth for mortals.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[88] [&amp; qua Virgo studiose utebatur,] Dentatum hoc sertum quam dire momorderit, quam cruciabiliter omni ex parte pupugerit velatum sacræ puellæ caput, cui torquendo unica sat fuisset acicula, ex eo fas est conjicere, quod spinæ non simul omnes, sed aliæ post alias, binæ, ternæ, pluresve, successione molestissima, nudatam invadebant cutem, proque occasione ac varietate motuum, singulæ obliquabantur in singulis vulnerum angustiis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000955Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000955Z">
        <seg>[88] [&amp; which the Virgin diligently used,] How terribly this crowned headgear gnawed, how painfully it pricked the veiled head of the sacred maiden from every side—for twisting it, a single needle would have been enough—can be inferred from the fact that the spikes did not all come at once, but one after another, in pairs, threes, or even more, in the most troublesome succession, invaded the exposed skin, and, according to the occasion and variety of the movements, each one was twisted in the individual narrowness of the wounds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[88] [Helenæ Æthiopissæ,] Helenam Æthiopissam, Joannis Merinii mancipium, continua &amp; copiosa subnascentium lumbricorum (quos vocant de Guinea&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt;) scaturigo septenni fluxu ita viribus, egesto cruore, exesis visceribus, exhauserat, ut actum de illius vita omnino putaretur, maxime quatriduo ante Rosæ obitum, quando tot morbis ardentissima febris, &amp; (mortale signum) inflatio talorum ac tibiarum accesserant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033731Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033731Z">
        <seg>[88] [To Helen the Ethiopian,] Helen the Ethiopian, a slave of John Merinni, was so exhausted by a seven-year discharge of a continuous and copious supply of worms that were hatching (which they call de Guinea), having expelled blood, with the bowels eaten away, had so exhausted her, that her life was thought to be entirely at an end, especially four days before Rose's death, when to so many diseases the most ardent fever, and (a mortal sign) an inflammation of the ankles and shins had been added.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[89] [quæ septem annis ægrotaverat,] At quarto mox die se perficiendo imparem protestata est: nam diuturno lassata morbo, exanguis cruentis &amp; crebris egestionibus, perusta febri, destituta anhelitu, pedibus tumidis, adire se posse reliquis diebus Rosæ sepulchrum omnino diffiderat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033744Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033744Z">
        <seg>[89] [who had been sick for seven years,] But on the fourth day she declared herself unfit to carry it out: for, worn out by a long illness, pale from bloody and frequent hemorrhages, scorched by fever, gasping for breath, and with swollen feet, she had completely despaired of being able to visit Rose's tomb during the remaining days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[89] [ut se quotidie] Verum hæc Rosæ ludicra videbantur, &amp; artis digna succursu, quo sævirent inclementius.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000958Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000958Z">
        <seg>But these things seemed like child's play to Rosa, and worthy of the aid of art, by which they might rage more fiercely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[8] Assistebant ritui funebrium Limanus archiepiscopus, regius senatus, [&amp; sepultura virginei cadaveris,] quem audientiam&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; vocant, collegia metropolitanum &amp; curiale, regularium prælati, &amp; equestris ordinis nobilitas conspicue numerosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030706Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030706Z">
        <seg>[8] Presiding over the funeral rites were Archbishop Limanus, the royal senate, [and the burial of the virgin's corpse,] whom they call the audience, the metropolitan and curial colleges, the prelates of the regulars, and a conspicuously numerous nobility of the equestrian order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[8] [dum horridum chirurgi ferrum] Quadriennium vivendo expleverat cum summam capitis sui cutem species quædam impetiginis cœpit obcrustare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203316Z">
        <seg>[of future holiness and patience,] And first of all, she was patient and her spirit among her pains was fearless, strong, and calm, so that it was clear she was a rose that neither the thorns frighten, wear down, nor bring down.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[90] Navigabat cum P. F. Dominico de Leon Ordinis Prædicatorum famulus, [&amp; cuidam servo moribundo] &amp; comes itineris Joannes, annorum quatuordecim adolescens, oppressus quartana febri, quam ipsa deinde maritima jactatio periculosius accenderat, ut die quadam paroxysmi violentia animam puero extudisse crederetur: nam subito clamore Dominicus ex superiori navis tabulato deorsum vocatus, ut moribundo suo famulo adsisteret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033759Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033759Z">
        <seg>[90] He was sailing with Father F. Dominic de Leon of the Order of Preachers, a servant [and a certain dying servant], and John, a fourteen-year-old youth, a companion on the journey, who was stricken with a quartan fever, which the sea's tossing had then made more dangerous, so that on one day it was believed the violence of a paroxysm had nearly cost the boy his life: for suddenly, at a cry, Dominic was called down from the upper deck of the ship to attend his dying servant;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[90] [magis magisque torqueret;] Nec tamen contenta hoc quotidiano cruciatu, modum reperit, quo nova indies vulnera aperiret: nam coronam mane circumdatura capiti, numquam eodem figebat loco, quo pridie steterat, sed mutato paulisper situ, in aliam aliamque partem transponebat redimiculum, ut alterna variatione immitius pungeret, atque in intacto dolorem renovaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001002Z">
        <seg>[90] [torturing more and more;] Yet not content with this daily torment, she found a way to open fresh wounds each day: for when she put the crown on her head in the morning, she never fastened it in the same spot where it had stood the day before, but by shifting its position slightly she moved the headband to one side and then the other, so that by alternating its placement it would pierce more deeply and renew the pain in the uninjured spot.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[91] Beatrix Gaves continuo quadriennio vexabatur molestissimi humoris jugi stillicidio, [alterique mulieri sanitatem restituit.] idque remediis pejus irritatum, spem curationis sustulerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033815Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033815Z">
        <seg>Beatrice of Gaves was afflicted for four consecutive years by a most troublesome, unceasing drip of humor, and she restored the other woman's health. But when this was made worse by remedies, it had destroyed all hope of cure.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[91] Diu familiares, domesticos, ipsamque matrem latuit hoc tam cruenti pectinis vepretum: [donec hoc tormentum, quod domesticos diu latuit,] quis enim sub vitta ac velo cautissimæ Virginis, aut notasset, aut quæsivisset tam ferale supplicium?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001005Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001005Z">
        <seg>[91] This most bloody briar-patch long eluded her familiars, her household, and even her own mother: [until this torment, which had long eluded her household,] for who, under the band and veil of the most cautious Virgin, would have either noticed or sought out such a fateful punishment?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[92] Anno post Rosæ felicem transitum, Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033824Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033824Z">
        <seg>[92] A year after Rose's happy passing, Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[92] Hic Rosa magis se proditam quam percussam dolens, [&amp; tandem casu detectum est,] dupliciter erubuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001008Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001008Z">
        <seg>[92] Here Rosa, feeling more betrayed than struck, blushed doubly; [and at last it was discovered by chance,]</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[93] Eidem aliud, sed lepidius accidit in amne, qui locum Misque&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt; præterlabitur, [eidem piscanti favet,] &amp; piscibus (maxime autem bocis seu candidioribus mænis&lt;a1&gt;e&lt;/a1&gt;) abundat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033852Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033852Z">
        <seg>[93] Another, but more charming, incident befell the same man on the river that flows past the place called Misque&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt; [and favors him while he fishes], and it abounds with fish (especially boci, or the whiter menae&lt;a1&gt;e&lt;/a1&gt;).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[93] Nec mora: delata fuit rei series ad eum, qui certius coronæ usum Virgini prohiberet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001011Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001011Z">
        <seg>Without delay, the series of events was brought to the one who would more surely forbid the Virgin's use of the crown.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[94] [Hæc aculeata corona] At Rosa pluris faciens coronam, quam regnum, gaudebat hanc se recuperasse salvo spinarum numero, ac saltem non nisi modice ea castigatione exarmatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001020Z">
        <seg>[94] [This thorny crown] But Rose, valuing the crown more than the kingdom, rejoiced to have recovered it with the full number of its thorns intact, and at least only slightly weakened by that chastisement.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[94] [monasterio fugitivum mancipium,] Liceat hinc brevissime divertere ad piscationem hominum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033923Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033923Z">
        <seg>[94] [a fugitive slave from the monastery,] Let us here briefly turn aside to the fishing of men.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[95] [aliisque apocham perditam restituta erat.] Non minori, quamquam dissimili, Rosæ beneficio rem amissam germani fratres Alexander &amp; Franciscus de Coloma recuperarunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033948Z">
        <seg>[95] [and the lost promissory note had been restored to others.] By no less a favor from Rosa, though of a different kind, the brothers Alexander and Francis de Coloma recovered the lost property of their brother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[95] [tangentem spirituali gaudio replevit] Quidam religiosissimus Dei servus, paulo post elatæ Rosæ exequias, domum Gundisalvi quæstoris adierat, eo dumtaxat animo, ut instrumenta, quibus Rosa virgineam carnem vivens domuerat, devote contrectaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001023Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001023Z">
        <seg>[95] [He filled the one who touched it with spiritual joy] A certain most devout servant of God, shortly after the funeral of the exalted Rose, had gone to the house of Gundisalvus the treasurer, with the sole intention of devoutly handling the instruments by which Rose, while still living, had mortified her virgin flesh.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[96] Pater Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034026Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034026Z">
        <seg>Father Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[96] [post obitum sanctæ Virginis,] Non pigeat, hic denuo reflectere oculos in spiniferum S. Catharinæ Senensis prototypon, quod Rosa nostra spinis coronata sic imitari visa est, ut plene idipsum non tam expresserit, quam in se transtulerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001026Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001026Z">
        <seg>[96] [After the death of the holy Virgin,] Let us not be reluctant to turn our eyes once more here to the thorny prototypal figure of St. Catherine of Siena, for our Rose, crowned with thorns, was seen to imitate it in such a way that she did not so much fully express it as she did transfer it into herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[97] Huc demum &amp; illud videtur pertinere, quod, [&amp; ipsa propter illam in cælo coronari visa est.] dum mortualis pompa sic Rosam coronabat in terris, ostento non absimili Beatorum curia eamdem coronavit in cælis, ut utrimque deprehendatur, successisse in ipsammet S. Catharinæ Senensis coronam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001030Z">
        <seg>[97] This, too, seems to pertain to what has been said: that while the funeral procession thus crowned Rose on earth, the court of the Blessed, by a vision not unlike a miracle, crowned her in heaven; so that it is evident from both that she succeeded to the very crown of St. Catherine of Siena herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[97] Isidora de Montalvo ætate gravis, insuper octo mensibus gravabatur febri continua &amp; admodum violenta.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034044Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034044Z">
        <seg>Isidora de Montalvo, advanced in years, had been burdened for eight months with a continuous and very violent fever.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[98] Josepham de Torres torrebat toto corpore volaticus ignis&lt;a0&gt;g&lt;/a0&gt; ex putrido sanguine febribus inflammato: [febribus æstuantes,] surgebant undique lucentes bullæ, triumque hebdomadarum spatio infirmam eo adduxerant, ut coram domesticis sine loquela, sine arteriarum pulsu jacens immobilis, putaretur efflasse animam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034054Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034054Z">
        <seg>[98] Joseph de Torres was consumed throughout his body by a burning fire from putrid blood inflamed by fevers: [Feverishly burning,] bright blisters rose up from every side, and within three weeks they had brought her to such a state of weakness that, lying motionless before her household without speech or pulse, she was thought to have breathed her last.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[99] Mariam Indam, puellam duodennem e Chilensi regno oriundam, [dut alio morborum genere] ac Limæ in monasterio Incarnationis famulantem, rigores, tormenta stomachi, demum &amp; spasmi, gutturis angustiæ, convulsiones, eo redegerant, ut nec cibo potuve refici, nec minimum quid potuerit deglutire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034103Z">
        <seg>Maria Inda, a twelve-year-old girl from the kingdom of Chile, [stricken with another kind of illness] and serving in the Monastery of the Incarnation in Lima, had been reduced by rigors, stomach torments, and finally spasms, a constriction of the throat, and convulsions, to the point that she could neither be restored by food or drink nor swallow the smallest thing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[99] [quibus ipsa durissime cubaret,] Læta successu Rosa, pergebat securior non tantum nudare sibi tabulas, quibus indormisceret, sed &amp; rudius saxum circumspicere, quod cervici supponeret: verum post longas ambages, tandem interrupit Filiæ solertiam expergefacta mater, ac velut deprehenso immani facinore puellam increpuit, adjecto callidæ obstinationis convicio: Si sic ei constitutum erat matrem fallere, iret porro, sibique deinceps suopte arbitrio lectum, quem vellet, contabularet, dum tamen capiti saltem humanius cervical concederet, ipsisque tabulis unicum simplicis straguli induceret operimentum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001042Z">
        <seg>[99] [on which she herself would sleep most harshly,] Pleased with her success, Rosa went on more securely, not only laying bare for herself the planks on which she would sleep, but also roughly hewing a stone to place under her neck: But after long detours, the mother, awakened at last, finally interrupted her daughter's cunning and, as if she had discovered some monstrous crime, rebuked the girl, adding a taunt about her clever stubbornness: If it was her intention to deceive her mother in this way, she might go on and from then on board up whatever bed she pleased of her own accord, so long as she at least provided her neck with a more humane pillow and covered the boards themselves with a single simple blanket.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[9] Sub hujus promissionis ac certæ spei solatio persuasum multis, ut domum secederent, postridie ad tumbæ spectaculum commodius redituri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030713Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030713Z">
        <seg>[9] Under the comfort of this promise and certain hope, many were persuaded to retire to their homes, to return the next day for a more convenient view of the tomb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[9] [aliosque cruciatus invicto animo sustinet,] Sed anxia parens, postridie revulso leniter cataplasmate, inhorruit, videns filiolæ verticem hinc bullis turgidum, inde fossulis profunde ulceratum, &amp; “O (inquit) misera, siccine totam hanc transegisti noctem sub hujusmodi cruciatus voraci ardore”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203303Z">
        <seg>[9] But the anxious mother, the next day gently removing the poultice, was horrified to see her little daughter's head, from one side swollen with blisters, from the other deeply ulcerated with pits, &amp; "O (she said) miserable one, did you spend the whole of this night in such a voracious ardor of this kind of torment?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Capiti suo imposuit coronam] &lt;b0&gt;Decebat&lt;/b0&gt; charam B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000937Z">
        <seg>[He placed a crown on her head] It was fitting for the beloved B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Cephalæa,] &lt;b0&gt;Asanato&lt;/b0&gt; morbo capitis, capitulum istud ordiamur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034525Z">
        <seg>[Cephalæa,] &lt;b0&gt;Asanato&lt;/b0&gt; being afflicted with a headache, let us begin this chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Christophorum Perez in altam fossam] hinc die quodam ad vicinos colles virore amœnissimos iter faciens, tellure ex pluviis madente &amp; lubrica, cum equo in fossam sedecim palmis altam prolapsus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034246Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034246Z">
        <seg>[Christopher Perez into a deep ditch] From here, on a certain day, while traveling to the neighboring hills, which are most lovely with greenery, the ground being wet and slippery from the rains, he and his horse slipped into a ditch sixteen palms deep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Christus illam sibi desponsaverit,] “Rosa cordis mei!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010105Z">
        <seg>[Christ may have betrothed her to himself,] "Rose of my heart!"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Clemens X Pontifex indicat,] &lt;b0&gt;Cum&lt;/b0&gt; igitur jam nihil deesset eorum, quæ sacrosanctæ functioni canonizationis hujus dilectæ Christi Domini sponsæ Rosæ necessaria erant, ex sanctorum Patrum auctoritate, sacrorum canonum decretis, &amp; sanctæ Romanæ Ecclesiæ antiqua consuetudine, ac novorum decretorum præscripto, rogantibus charissimis in Christo filiis nostris regina Marianna regente&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt;, ac etiam Carolo II&lt;a2&gt;b&lt;/a2&gt; itidem Hispaniarum rege, toto Ordine Fratrum Prædicatorum sancti Dominici, regno Peruano &amp; omnibus provinciis Americæ, accedentibus etiam precibus venerabilium fratrum nostrorum archiepiscoporum, &amp; episcoporum regnorum Hispaniæ &amp; Indiarum, justum &amp; debitum esse censuimus, ut prædictam Beatam, quam ipse Dominus de die in diem magis clarificat e cælis, nos quoque venerationis officio laudemus &amp; glorificemus in terris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043404Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043404Z">
        <seg>[Pope Clement X indicates that,] &lt;b0&gt;Since&lt;/b0&gt; therefore nothing was now lacking of those things which were necessary for the most sacred function of the canonization of this beloved Spouse of Christ, Rose, by the authority of the holy Fathers, the decrees of the sacred canons, and the ancient custom of the holy Roman Church, and by the prescription of the new decrees, at the request of our most beloved sons in Christ, the regent Queen Mariana&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt;, and also of Charles II&lt;a2&gt;b&lt;/a2&gt; likewise King of Spain, the entire Order of Preachers of Saint Dominic, the Kingdom of Peru and all the provinces of America, and with the prayers of our venerable brothers the archbishops, and the bishops of the kingdoms of Spain and the Indies, we judged it just and fitting that the aforesaid Blessed, whom the Lord Himself daily more and more glorifies from heaven, should also be praised and glorified by us on earth with the office of veneration.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Clemens X aliis gentibus, quibus Euangelium annuntiatum est,] &lt;b0&gt;Clemens&lt;/b0&gt; Episcopus servus servorum Dei.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041024Z">
        <seg>[To the other peoples to whom the Gospel has been proclaimed,] &lt;b0&gt;Clement&lt;/b0&gt; Bishop, servant of the servants of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Cum Virgo in gravem morbum incidisset,] &lt;b0&gt;Duriusne&lt;/b0&gt; premat certitudo mortis, an horæ ac diei incertitudo, problema sit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013053Z">
        <seg>Whether the certainty of death, or the uncertainty of the hour and day, is the more severe problem;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Cum corpus S. Rosæ loco nimis abdito quiesceret;] &lt;b0&gt;Ultimis&lt;/b0&gt; exsequiis tam magnifice splendideque peractis, credebatur, populum sensim a confluxu &amp; importunitate destiturum, ut pleraque solent tractu temporis elanguescere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031332Z">
        <seg>[When the body of St. Rose rested in a place too hidden;] &lt;b0&gt;After&lt;/b0&gt; the funeral rites were performed so magnificently and splendidly, it was believed that the people would gradually cease from the crowds and importunity, as is the way with most things to languish with the passage of time;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Cum futura memorati cœnobii fundatrix] Illustri matronæ Isabellæ de Mexia domi famula erat Mariana nomine, cui graviter ægrotanti Rosa (utpote illis in ædibus pene domestica) more suo ex charitate ministrabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011921Z">
        <seg>Mariana, a maid in the household of the illustrious matron Isabella of Mexico, was named Rosa; when she fell gravely ill, Rosa (who, as was almost a member of the household, was in her own way a domestic) ministered to her out of charity in her customary manner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Describitur monasterium Limense,] &lt;b0&gt;Illustrioribus&lt;/b0&gt; Limensis emporii ornamentis demum accessit nobile, spatiosum, elegans beatæ Catharinæ Senensis monasterium, numeroso sacrarum virginum collegio dicatum sub regulari S. Patris Dominici instituto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011828Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011828Z">
        <seg>[The monastery of Lima is described,] &lt;b0&gt;Among the more illustrious ornaments&lt;/b0&gt; of the port of Lima, the noble, spacious, and elegant monastery of the blessed Catherine of Siena was finally added, dedicated to a numerous college of holy virgins under the regular institute of St. Dominic.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Dum S. Rosa renuit inire matrimonium,] &lt;b0&gt;Variæ&lt;/b0&gt; ac sine numero sunt viæ, anfractus, itinera, quibus æterna Sapientia selectas animas in sublime perfectionis cacumen facit eluctari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203457Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203457Z">
        <seg>[While St. Rose refused to enter into marriage,] Various and countless are the ways, the winding paths, the journeys by which the eternal Wisdom leads chosen souls to struggle up to the lofty summit of perfection.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Dum mater S. Rosam invitam] &lt;b0&gt;In&lt;/b0&gt; concursu duorum præceptorum, quo simul honorare patrem &amp; matrem, simul Deo magis quam hominibus obedire justum jussumque est, Rosæ frequenter ingenio &amp; solertia opus fuit, ut neutrum offenderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T033146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203453Z">
        <seg>[While Mother was inviting St. Rose] &lt;b0&gt;In&lt;/b0&gt; the dilemma between honoring one's father and mother and obeying God rather than men, it was often necessary for Rose, by her wit and prudence, to avoid offending either.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Erat Limæ inclyta statua Deiparæ,] &lt;b0&gt;Supradictis&lt;/b0&gt; pene gemella sunt, quæ hic narrabimus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011038Z">
        <seg>[There was an illustrious statue of the Virgin Mary in Lima,] the following are almost twin tales, which we will relate here;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Exemplo S. Catharinæ Senensis] &lt;b0&gt;Fundamento&lt;/b0&gt; tam profundo, lato, ac solido dignum fuit ac tutum, prægrandia, excelsa, ponderosa superstitui fastigia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203528Z">
        <seg>[By the example of St. Catherine of Siena] A foundation so deep, broad, and solid was worthy and safe for supporting the immense, lofty, and ponderous superstructure.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Fama mortis ejus per urbem sparsa,] &lt;b0&gt;Toti&lt;/b0&gt; jam Limæ, buccinante fama, innotuerat, Rosæ cadaver sub vesperam quæstoris domo efferendum esse ad sepulchrum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030527Z">
        <seg>[As the rumor of his death spread through the city,] &lt;b0&gt;Toti&lt;/b0&gt; was already known throughout Lima, the rumor having blown up, that Rosa's corpse was to be carried from the magistrate's house at dusk to the sepulchre.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Huic Virgini libros legenti,] &lt;b0&gt;Animo&lt;/b0&gt;, cui amarescunt terrena, dulcescunt cælestia, seseque ultro insinuant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010530Z">
        <seg>To this virgin who reads books, the earthly grows bitter, the heavenly grows sweet, and they insinuate themselves of their own accord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Hæc Virgo de conversione gentilium Indorum,] &lt;b0&gt;Zelata&lt;/b0&gt; est etiam pro domo Dei salutem animarum: ipsa enim charitate, qua verum Christi Domini corpus, mysticum quoque diligebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042319Z">
        <seg>[This Virgin, regarding the conversion of the Indian Gentiles,] was also zealous for the house of God and the salvation of souls: for she loved with the same charity the true body of Christ the Lord and the mystical one as well.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Hæc Virgo imitata S. Catharinam Senensem] &lt;b0&gt;Ex&lt;/b0&gt; Historia vitæ B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010024Z">
        <seg>[This Virgin imitated St. Catherine of Siena] &lt;b0&gt;From&lt;/b0&gt; the History of the Life of the Blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Ingeniosa Virgovarios excogitavit modos,] &lt;b0&gt;Tanta&lt;/b0&gt; ei corporis macerandi cura fuit, ut nec pauculas nocturnæ quietis horas tormento vacuas esse permiserit: hinc strati sui voluit eam esse duritiem, quæ somnum abigeret magis, quam vocaret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001039Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001039Z">
        <seg>[He devised ingenious contrivances for the virgarians,] He was so intent on emaciating his body that he did not even allow the few hours of nightly rest to be free from torment: hence he had his bed made so hard that it would drive away sleep rather than invite it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Iterum locus tumuli mutatur ob turbas plebis,] &lt;b0&gt;De&lt;/b0&gt; altari ad altare tantisper se transire protestabatur S. Hieronymus, quando a suo tractatu de altari thymiamatis&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt; transibat ad scribendam epistolam octavam virgini Demetriadi&lt;a2&gt;b&lt;/a2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031617Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031617Z">
        <seg>[Again the place of the tomb is changed because of the crowds of the people,] St. Jerome protested that he was merely moving from altar to altar for a short time, when he was passing from his treatise on the altar of incense to write the eighth letter to the virgin Demetriada.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Joannes de Villalobos noster nonnihil mitigavit.] nempe ad unum ex Patribus spiritualibus, quorum ductui Rosa obtemperabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001014Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001014Z">
        <seg>[Our John of Villalobos somewhat mitigated it.] namely, to one of the spiritual Fathers, whose guidance Rosa obeyed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Patrocinio hujus Sanctæ jam defunctæ] &lt;b0&gt;Interea&lt;/b0&gt; Limam pervenere litteræ hujus sanctæ Sedis apostolicæ, ut ex formula a congregatione Rituum præscripta examinarentur testes de vita &amp; gestis hujus Ancillæ Dei;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043038Z">
        <seg>[Under the patronage of this now-deceased Saint] &lt;b0&gt;Meanwhile&lt;/b0&gt; letters from this holy Apostolic See arrived in Lima, so that witnesses concerning the life and deeds of this Handmaid of God might be examined according to the formula prescribed by the Congregation of Rites;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Per attactas S. Rosæ imagines] &lt;b0&gt;Perfidis&lt;/b0&gt; nostri seculi iconoclastis hoc caput capitilavium&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt; esto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040230Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040230Z">
        <seg>[Through the attached images of St. Rose] Let this be a crown of thorns for the perfidious iconoclasts of our age.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Per intercessionem S. Rosæ mortuæ] &lt;b0&gt;Quale&lt;/b0&gt; quantumque divinæ omnipotentiæ molimen &amp; opus sit conversio peccatoris, &amp; quam alte cæli terræque creationem excedat, item quando ipsa miraculosa censenda sit, legat, qui voluerit, apud S. Thomam 1.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032451Z">
        <seg>[Through the intercession of St. Rose of Lima] How and how great a work of divine omnipotence the conversion of a sinner is, and how far it surpasses the creation of heaven and earth, and also when it itself is to be considered miraculous, let anyone who wishes read in St. Thomas, Part I.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Postero die rursus in templo] &lt;b0&gt;Postridie,&lt;/b0&gt; adlivente matutino crepusculo, jussi custodes, venerabile pignus e novitiorum domicilio in majus ecclesiæ sacellum, ubi pridie constiterat, retulerunt, inque ejusdem tumuli reclinatorium sublimarunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030942Z">
        <seg>[The next day, again in the temple] The following day, at the approach of the morning twilight, I ordered the guards to bring the venerable relic from the novices' quarters to the larger chapel of the church, where it had stood the day before, and to raise it on the reclinatory of the same tomb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Postquam matrem suam S. Dominico commendaverat,] Illic se Virgo solitariam ac sine arbitris putans, cygnæos exorsa est modulos, suæque morti jam propinquæ dulciter præcantillavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013254Z">
        <seg>[After she had entrusted her mother to St. Dominic,] There, considering herself a solitary virgin without a chaperone, she began the swan-like modulations and sweetly sang them for her own death, now near at hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Prodigiosus pulvis,] &lt;b0&gt;Altius&lt;/b0&gt; nonnihil hic repetenda sunt aliqua.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035325Z">
        <seg>[Prodigious powder,] &lt;b0&gt;Higher&lt;/b0&gt; some things must be repeated here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Præscia mortis imminentis] &lt;b0&gt;Præscia&lt;/b0&gt; diei, quo ad Sponsi thalamum properare ex hac lachrymarum valle debebat, (quod matri celare nequivit) quotannis festum D. Bartholomæi apostoli, quod erat terminus suæ mortali peregrinationi constitutus&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt;, singulari devotionis studio colebat, &amp; in ejus pervigilio, suo non contenta jejunio, aliquos innocentes pueros, ut secum jejunarent adduxit, in eo etiam post ejus obitum perseverantes, eo quod sic a Rosa instituti fuissent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042703Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042703Z">
        <seg>[A premonition of impending death] &lt;b0&gt;A premonition&lt;/b0&gt; of the day on which she was to hasten to the Bridegroom's chamber from this valley of tears (which she could not hide from her mother), she annually celebrated the feast of St. She celebrated the feast of St. Bartholomew the Apostle, which was the appointed term of her mortal pilgrimage&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt;, with singular devotion, and on its vigil, not content with her own fast, she brought some innocent boys to fast with her, continuing in this even after her death, because they had been so instructed by Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Præter alios spiritualis vitæ magistros] &lt;b0&gt;Arcanum&lt;/b0&gt; gratiæ lumen, quod ab infantia Rosam deduxit per vias rectas, simul reflexu admirabili certitudinem indiderat attractus vere divini, minimeque fallacis, ita quod de rectitudine semitæ, per quam ibat, ambigere non posset, quamvis hanc parum tritam, verisque &amp; obscuris vestigiis vix notabilem comperisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010409Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010409Z">
        <seg>[Besides other masters of the spiritual life] the &lt;b0&gt;Arcanum&lt;/b0&gt; of grace, a light that had guided Rose along the right paths since her infancy, had at the same time, by a marvelous turn, instilled in her the certainty of a truly divine and never-deceiving attraction, so that she could not doubt the straightness of the path she was on, even though it was little-traveled, and obscure traces, scarcely noticeable.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Puella semestris mortua,] &lt;b0&gt;Tametsi&lt;/b0&gt; P. Antonius de la Vega Loaysa Societatis Jesu in suis facundissimis Responsis diserte ac sæpius monuerit deputatos judices, totam Rosæ vitam a primis incunabulis usque ad extremum halitum continui perpetuique miraculi loco censendam atque habendam esse, attamen visum ipsis progredi ad specialiora, quæque micuere post ejus vitam in beneficio alieno.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033105Z">
        <seg>[A six-month-old girl died,] &lt;b0&gt;Although&lt;/b0&gt; P. Although Father Anthony de la Vega Loaysa of the Society of Jesus, in his most eloquent Responsa, had eloquently and repeatedly warned the appointed judges that the entire life of Rose should be regarded and held as a continuous and perpetual miracle from her earliest cradle to her last breath, they nevertheless saw fit to proceed to more specific matters, each of which shone forth after her life in the benefit of others.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Rosa in oratorio hospitii sui] &lt;b0&gt;In&lt;/b0&gt; domestico Gundisalvi quæstoris oratorio inter alias sacras imagines, tum venustate, tum reverentia celebris eminebat illa, quæ virilem Christi vultum scitissimis coloribus ac lineamentis exprimebat in tela.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010945Z">
        <seg>[Rose in the oratory of her lodging] In the oratory of Gundisalvus the treasurer, among other sacred images celebrated for both their beauty and their reverence, stood out that one which depicted the manly face of Christ on canvas with the most skillful colors and lines.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Rosa solicita de salute barbarorum,] &lt;b0&gt;Quæ&lt;/b0&gt; tam mascule ardebat circa mysterium, quo verum Christi Corpus velatur, non poterat mystica ejusdem membra negligere, sicubi videbat hæc ancipiti perdendæ salutis discrimini patere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011441Z">
        <seg>[Rose, concerned for the salvation of the barbarians,] &lt;b0&gt;Who&lt;/b0&gt; burned so zealously for the mystery by which the true Body of Christ is veiled, could not neglect its mystical members wherever she saw them exposed to the twofold danger of perdition.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta Petro de Vega,] &lt;b0&gt;Anno&lt;/b0&gt; ante Rosæ obitum Petrus de Vega lapsu præcipiti ac gravi laxarat juncturam ossis in scapula, simulque exinde usum brachii ex ea parte amiserat, nec nisi ad dolorem &amp; onus lacertum sibi superesse jugiter querebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033713Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033713Z">
        <seg>[To Saint Peter of Vega,] &lt;b0&gt;In the year&lt;/b0&gt; before Rose's death, Peter of Vega had dislocated a bone in his shoulder in a severe and precipitous fall, and from that time had lost the use of his arm on that side, constantly complaining that nothing was left of his muscle but pain and burden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta Rosa vacabat] &lt;b0&gt;Si&lt;/b0&gt;, qui adhæret Domino, unus spiritus est cum eo, 1 Cor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010145Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010145Z">
        <seg>[Saint Rose was vacant] &lt;b0&gt;If&lt;/b0&gt;, who adheres to the Lord, is one spirit with him, 1 Cor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta divinam iram placatura] &lt;b0&gt;Mirum&lt;/b0&gt;, in arido totque jejuniis extenuato corpusculo superfuisse locum, qui flagella exciperet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203335Z">
        <seg>[The holy one, having placated the divine wrath] It is marvelous that in a body so dry and emaciated by so many fasts there was still room to receive the scourges.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta esurit, ut egenos pascat,] &lt;b0&gt;Mirabitur&lt;/b0&gt;, quisquis legerit, Rosæ pauperrimæ funus publicis mendicorum Limensium ejulatibus, threnis, lachrymis fuisse celebratum, vociferante egenorum turba, veram pauperum nutricem ac matrem fato acerbissimo, &amp; præmaturo sibi ereptam fuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011559Z">
        <seg>[She fasts, so that she may feed the poor,] &lt;b0&gt;She will marvel&lt;/b0&gt;, whoever reads, that the funeral of the most wretched Rosa was celebrated with the public wails, dirges, and tears of the beggars of Lima, the crowd of needy crying out that a true nurse and mother of the poor had been snatched from them by the most bitter and untimely fate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta feminam ad amorem Dei accendit,] Harum altera correpto, qui forte patulus adjacebat, libello, vidit succincte in eo vitam, gesta, obitumque virginis Rosæ contineri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032659Z">
        <seg>[The Holy Spirit kindled the woman to the love of God.] The other of them, picking up a little book that happened to lie nearby, saw that it briefly contained the life, deeds, and death of the virgin Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta firmiter Deo confidens,] &lt;b0&gt;Aprima&lt;/b0&gt; pueritia Rosam suam prævenerat Christus in benedictionibus dulcedinis, edocens confidere in adjutorio Altissimi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011717Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011717Z">
        <seg>[Saint, firmly trusting in God,] in her earliest childhood Christ had anticipated his Rose with blessings of sweetness, teaching her to trust in the help of the Most High.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta pluribus personis] &lt;b0&gt;Idem&lt;/b0&gt; (de quo paulo ante) R.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012110Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012110Z">
        <seg>[Holy to many persons] &lt;b0&gt;The same&lt;/b0&gt; (about which a little earlier) R.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta post admirabilem visionem] &lt;b0&gt;Oraculum&lt;/b0&gt; illud divinum: &lt;b1&gt;Sponsabo te mihi in fide&lt;/b1&gt;&lt;a2&gt;a&lt;/a2&gt; etiam cum hac dilecta, &amp; electa Dei eximio privilegio impletum fuisse dignoscitur, ad quod eam per mirabiles favores evehere dignatus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041451Z">
        <seg>[The Saint, after the admirable vision] that divine Oracle: "I will betroth you to myself in faithfulness" is also recognized to have been fulfilled in this beloved, chosen by God with an exceptional privilege, to which He was pleased to elevate her through marvelous graces.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta post obitum gloriosa] &lt;b0&gt;Aloysia&lt;/b0&gt; de Serrano (cujus memini supra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031825Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031825Z">
        <seg>[Glorious after death] &lt;b0&gt;Aloysia&lt;/b0&gt; of Serrano (whom I remember above in chapter</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta puellares ludos exosa] &lt;b0&gt;Ut&lt;/b0&gt; olim S. Catharinam Senensem adhuc pusillam, solitudinis amor usque ad speluncæ quæsitionem abripuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003532Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003532Z">
        <seg>[Hating girlish games] &lt;b0&gt;Thus&lt;/b0&gt; the love of solitude once carried St. Catherine of Siena, still a little girl, to the point of seeking out a cave.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta supplex coram hac imagine discordiam,] Excrescebat infelix lolium, quodque initio nonnisi opinionum prælium fuerat, ipsa repugnandi dulcedine paulatim degenerabat in amarulentiam voluntatum, non sine scissura pacis &amp; offensa Numinis, cui extra fines pacis habitare non lubet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011109Z">
        <seg>[Holy suppliant before this image of discord,] An unhappy tares grew, and what at first had been nothing more than a battle of opinions was gradually, by the very sweetness of opposing, degenerating into the bitterness of wills, not without a breach of peace and the offense of the Deity, who is unwilling to dwell outside the bounds of peace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta sæpe de peccatis confessa,] &lt;b0&gt;Adfuit&lt;/b0&gt; demum acceptabile tempus, quo Rosa, non tam Sirii, quam sitis ardoribus exsucca, dolorum spinas cum vita exereret, in Sponsi paradiso amœnius renascitura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025906Z">
        <seg>[Often having confessed her sins,] &lt;b0&gt;the time was at last acceptable&lt;/b0&gt; for Rose, withered not so much by Syria's sun as by thirst's ardors, to cast off the thorns of her sorrows along with her life, to be reborn more lovely in her Spouse's paradise.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta sæpius communicabat Corpori Domini] &lt;b0&gt;Si&lt;/b0&gt; talia acciderunt Rosæ, virginem Senensem vestienti ex aliena substantia, longe mirabiliora accepit ab illo mirabili Sacramento, in quo Christum vestiunt aliena accidentia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011314Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011314Z">
        <seg>[She often received the Body of the Lord] &lt;b0&gt;If&lt;/b0&gt; such things happened to Rose, the virgin of Siena, who clothed Christ with things from another's substance, she received far more marvelous things from that wondrous Sacrament, in which they clothe Christ with things from another's substance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta tanto divini amoris igne flagrabat,] &lt;b0&gt;Quanta&lt;/b0&gt; in Rosæ pectore flagraret divini amoris Ætna&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt;, placuit Deo interdum visibilibus signis palam facere, dum ex vultu orantis jussit splendores &amp; flammulas scintillare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010852Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010852Z">
        <seg>[The Saint burned with such a fire of divine love,] &lt;b0&gt;How great&lt;/b0&gt; an Aetna of divine love burned in the heart of the Rose, God was pleased at times to make manifest by visible signs, while from the face of the one praying He commanded splendors and little flames to sparkle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta virgo crucis signum,] &lt;b0&gt;Solitariæ&lt;/b0&gt; in horto cellulæ (de qua supra) prima &amp; fere tota supellex crux erat Virgine procerior altiorque, ut plenius tum Calvariæ supplicium meditanti ingereret, tum piis novæ Magdalenæ complexibus commodius stringi posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011150Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011150Z">
        <seg>[Holy virgin, sign of the cross,] &lt;b0&gt;Solitariæ&lt;/b0&gt; in the garden of the cell (of which above) the first and almost entire piece of furniture was a cross taller and more upright than the Virgin, so that it might more fully present to her the Passion of Calvary, and at the same time be more conveniently embraced by the pious embraces of the new Magdalene.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sæpe familiariter huic Virgini] &amp; ubicumque sanctissimum nomen Jesu legendo occurrisset, tantisper immorabatur: inde enim amoris ignes in corde sentiebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041907Z">
        <seg>[Often familiarly to this Virgin] and wherever she came upon the most holy name of Jesus in reading, she would linger for a while: for from it she felt the fires of love in her heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Velut aurum in fornace,] &lt;b0&gt;Quod&lt;/b0&gt; auro fornax, id justis electis tribulatio: præcipue, ubi obviandum periculo, ne magnitudo revelationum extollat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010313Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010313Z">
        <seg>Like gold in the furnace, the trial that refines gold is the tribulation of the elect righteous—especially when the danger must be faced lest the greatness of the revelations exalt them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Virgo ex humilitate] &lt;b0&gt;In&lt;/b0&gt; suo nihilo cominus ac penitissime agnoscendo, quam profunde jugiterque Rosa perstiterit occupata, omnis humillimæ suæ vitæ clamat actio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203513Z">
        <seg>In her acknowledgment of her own nothingness, how profoundly and unceasingly the Rose remained occupied, the very action of her humblest life proclaims.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Virguncuta pasta est persecutiones] &lt;b0&gt;Rosis&lt;/b0&gt; spinæ non tarde accedunt, sed connascuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010744Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010744Z">
        <seg>[A slender rod is the rod of persecution] &lt;b0&gt;Roses&lt;/b0&gt; and thorns do not come together late, but are born together.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[ac senatus ad pompam funebrem conveniunt;] Ceterorum, qui eadem spontanea devotione confluxerant, illustrium ex omni equestri ordine virorum ratio aut numerus in conferta multitudine iniri non potuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030552Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030552Z">
        <seg>[and the senate gather for the funeral procession;] The others, who had flocked together with the same spontaneous devotion, the number or list of the illustrious men from every order of the equestrian class in the dense multitude could not be ascertained.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[ac urbem Limanam Deo commendare,] nimirum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032020Z">
        <seg>[and to commend the city of Lima to God,] of course;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[ad studium orationis hortatur;] at sub eo ritu ac norma, qua illam primitus, ex instructione Deiparæ, tradiderat mundo S. Pater Dominicus&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt;, hoc est, cum innexa decadatim consideratione mysteriorum nostræ redemptionis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010242Z">
        <seg>[He encourages the study of prayer;] but in the same rite and rule by which St. Dominic had originally handed it down to the world, by instruction of the Mother of God, that is, with the inseparable, decade-by-decade consideration of the mysteries of our redemption.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[aliisque necessitatibus experta fuerit,] Defecerat olim in paternis ædibus numerosæ familiæ panis, nec erat, unde emerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042546Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042546Z">
        <seg>[having experienced other necessities,] The bread of the numerous family in the paternal house had once failed, and there was nowhere to buy it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[apparensque cuidam Christophoro de Ortega,] Decumbebat in Limano SS. Rosarii cœnobio, jamque extremis agonibus se præparabat P. magister F. Augustinus de Vega Ordinis Prædicatorum tunc Provincialis Peruanus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032045Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032045Z">
        <seg>[and to a certain Christopher de Ortega,] Father Master Friar Augustine de Vega, of the Order of Preachers, then the Peruvian Provincial, was lying in the convent of the Holy Rosary in Lima, and was already preparing for his final struggles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[cujus iram molli responso lenivit.] “Quorsum (inquit) hæ fraudes?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003557Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003557Z">
        <seg>[whose anger he soothed with a gentle reply.] "What are these deceptions for?" he said.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[cælitus accepit duplex privilegium;] Nacta hanc eremum felix Rosa, hoc sategit, ne quis illic temporis punctus sibi efflueret sine fructu;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T005953Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T005953Z">
        <seg>[She received a twofold privilege from heaven;] Having acquired this desert, the fortunate Rose saw to it that not a single moment of time there should slip by without fruit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[die quarta Septembris,] Rursum tota sedibus emota civitas sancti Dominici ecclesiam implevit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031233Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031233Z">
        <seg>[On September 4th,] Once again, the entire city, uprooted from its seats, filled the church of Saint Dominic.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[eamque minus incommode dormire coëgerit.] vix tamen blandius aut commodius: nam tabulas suas asseresque repetiit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003501Z">
        <seg>[and forced her to sleep less uncomfortably.] Yet scarcely more gently or comfortably: for he repeated his tablets and planks;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[ex authenticis instrumentis refertur.] videlicet unum in sanatione mirabili Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043255Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043255Z">
        <seg>[Reported from authentic documents.] namely, one in the miraculous healing of Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[eximiam victoriam reportat.] At Virgo sibimet indignata, quod horruisset, cum pelvi clam secessit in angulum foras, ibique semetipsam dure objurgans, “Itane (inquit) strenue ac serio studuisti dilectioni proximi, ut nauseæ tibi sit miserabilium infirmitas?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011652Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011652Z">
        <seg>[He reports an outstanding victory.] But the Virgin, indignant with herself because she had recoiled, secretly withdrew with the basin to a corner outside, and there, harshly reproaching herself, said, "Have you then so strenuously and earnestly devoted yourself to the love of your neighbor that the infirmity of the miserable makes you nauseous?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[familiariter ac mirabiliter] Matrona honoris gratia secedenti comitem adjunxit septennem puellam, e mancipio domi suæ natam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010558Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010558Z">
        <seg>[familiarly and wonderfully] The matron, as a mark of honor, appointed as companion a seven-year-old girl, born into her household.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[febris cum pleuritide,] Hunc mense Augusto anni MDCXXXI quinquennem puerum excoquebant febres, exanimabat pleuritis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040621Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040621Z">
        <seg>[fever with pleurisy,] This boy, five years old in August of 1631, was being consumed by fevers and was being exanimated by pleurisy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[filiam ex eo nascituram per litteras commendat.] Didacus Gonzales Montero, Christophorus de Aranda Valdivia, aliique centuriones ac Chilensis militiæ præfecti certatim adibant Ferdinandi ædes, devoto hoc spectaculo coram fruituri: nam &amp; puella (juxta Rosæ vaticinium) mire a teneris propendebat in religionis ac pietatis exercitia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012839Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012839Z">
        <seg>[He commends by letter the daughter who was to be born of him.] Diego Gonzales Montero, Christopher de Aranda Valdivia, and other centurions and commanders of the Chilean militia eagerly flocked to Ferdinand's house to enjoy this spectacle in person: for the girl (according to Rosa's prophecy) was remarkably inclined from her earliest years toward the exercises of religion and piety.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[futuræ sanctitatis ac patientiæ,] Et primum quidem tolerantiæ fuit animique inter dolores impavidi, fortis, tranquilli, ut liqueret Rosam esse, quam spinæ nec terrent, nec terunt, aut dejiciunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203316Z">
        <seg>[7] She was three years old when, in her most tender disposition, she began to display above her age robust examples of virtue.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[humilitate &amp; pudicitia sic Deo placuit,] puritas hic tractanda foret ante cælestes nuptias;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010031Z">
        <seg>[By humility and chastity, God was thus pleased,] purity must be dealt with here before the heavenly nuptials.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[ibique piis exercitiis inhærens,] Videbatur in se quadrare id, quod canebatur de justo Sene: &lt;b0&gt;Responsum accepit Simeon&lt;/b0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T005941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T005941Z">
        <seg>[and there, engaged in pious exercises,] It seemed to him that what was being sung about the righteous old man was coming true in him: "Simeon received his answer."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[imo charitas ejus ad bruta animalia se extendebat,] hic, ubi de sola Rosæ misericordia agitur, unum adjicere fas esto, sed lepidum: nam usque ad bruta animantia clemens Rosæ suavitas &amp; tenera extendebatur commiseratio: siquidem teste Salomone Prov.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011707Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011707Z">
        <seg>[Indeed, her charity extended even to brute animals,] here, where we speak only of Rose's mercy, let us be permitted to add one charming detail: for Rose's clemency and tender compassion extended even to the brute creatures; for, as Solomon testifies in Proverbs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[morbo punit, ac mox pœnitentem sanat.] Neque id satis: graviorem sensit in suo spiritu procellam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032953Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032953Z">
        <seg>[He punishes with sickness, and soon he heals the penitent.] Nor was that enough: he felt a storm more severe in his own spirit;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[nonaginta novem aculeis armatam,] verum id nonnisi puerile fuit tirocinium robustioris tormenti, quod ex secunda corona, aculeis nonaginta &amp; novem armata, imminebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000946Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000946Z">
        <seg>[armed with ninety-nine spikes,] but that was nothing more than a childish trial of a more powerful torment, which loomed from the second crown, armed with ninety-nine spikes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[patrocinio hujus Sanctæ reviviscunt.] Hinc ingenti concepta fiducia, cœpit invocare Rosam sonoris vocibus, ac libere flagitare resuscitationem sui mancipii.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033214Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033214Z">
        <seg>[Under the patronage of this Saint they are revived.] Hence, filled with great confidence, he began to invoke Rosa in a loud voice and freely to demand the resuscitation of his servant.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[per cælestem revelationem,] quapropter semel eam permisit nonnihil concuti pavida formidine circa prædestinationis suæ arcanum, omni animæ tremendum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011723Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011723Z">
        <seg>[by heavenly revelation,] for which reason He once permitted it to be somewhat shaken by a fearful dread concerning the mystery of its predestination, a dread terrible to the whole soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[periculosus sanguinis fluxus,] Memor tandem, se ex Rosæ candidis indumentis reliquias possidere, has reverenter deosculans, &amp; Virginis intercessioni efflictim se commendans, periculum evasit, pristinis viribus actutum restituta.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034838Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034838Z">
        <seg>[a dangerous hemorrhage,] Finally remembering that she possessed relics from the white vestments of the Rose, she reverently kissed them and, earnestly commending herself to the Virgin's intercession, she escaped the danger, her former strength being at once restored.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[piisque affectibus indulgens,] Civitati enim regum&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;, qualis dicitur Lima, suum debebatur sydus, quod ad Christum Dominum regem regum dux esset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041619Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041619Z">
        <seg>[and indulging pious affections,] For the city of kings, as Lima is called, its own star was due, which would lead to Christ the Lord, King of kings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[pluribus exemplis eorum,] Hujus fauces angina accenderat, carnem totam operuerant liventes papulæ, insuper toto corpore vultuque inflata, tympano quam feminæ similior extumuerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035709Z">
        <seg>[with many examples of them,] This one's throat was inflamed with angina, her entire body was covered with purplish papules, and moreover, she was swollen all over her body and face, having puffed up like a drumhead rather than a woman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[prævisum sitis tormentum pati coacta est.] nil minus, quam soporem fuisse, quod viderant, nec tales esse cruciatus suos, qui aut unici momenti starent induciis, aut cum somno possint vel tantillam inire societatem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025733Z">
        <seg>She was forced to endure the torment she had foreseen. What they had seen was nothing more than a doze, and her torments were not such as to stand on their own for a single moment during a respite, or to admit even the slightest companionship with sleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[qui periculo mortis expositi,] Instante partu, vix uni matri suæ delictum fassa, dum caret obstetricibus, ut celaret crimen, discrimen vitæ subiit, nec parere valens, nec retinere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035728Z">
        <seg>[who, exposed to the danger of death,] When labor was imminent, she scarcely confessed her crime to her mother, and, lacking midwives, in order to conceal the offense, she faced a life-threatening predicament, being neither able to give birth nor to retain the pregnancy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[quibus recreata fuerat,] petiitque ulterius, qua igitur forma apprehenderet Divinitatis præsentiam?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010508Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010508Z">
        <seg>[by which she had been restored,] she sought further, and so, by what form could she apprehend the presence of the Divine?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[sibi postea tolerandos esse,] Respondit: multiplicis ac peregrini, adeo quod nulli prorsus colori in mundo isto visibili possit vel tenuissime aptari comparatio, nisi quod arcuum venustas omnium possibilium colorum varietatem ac gratiam in se collegisse videbatur cum infinita quadam excellentia, &amp; dignitate.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010827Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010827Z">
        <seg>[that they would have to endure them later,] He replied: manifold and exotic, to the point that no comparison to any color in this visible world could be made, not even the faintest, except that the beauty of the rainbow seemed to have gathered in itself the variety and grace of all possible colors with a certain infinite excellence and dignity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[sicut probatur singulari conversione] Quidam profligatæ conscientiæ vir assidue quidem cum aliis vivificæ pœnitentiæ &amp; Eucharistiæ sacramenta frequentabat, sed nonnisi in animæ suæ mortem &amp; exitium: numquam enim toto sacrilegæ vitæ suæ decursu rite integreve confessus fuerat, ac longissima annorum serie in hoc luto computruerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032529Z">
        <seg>[as is proven by a singular conversion] A certain man of depraved conscience did indeed frequently partake with others of the sacraments of life-giving penance and the Eucharist, but only to his own soul's death and destruction: for he had never, in the entire course of his sacrilegious life, made a proper and complete confession, and for a very long stretch of years he had wallowed in this mire.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[spirituales vestes Christo nascituro præparabat.] Compererat, Dictante historia, sanctam Catharinam Senensem aliquando latenti sub forma pauperis Christo dedisse vestem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010939Z">
        <seg>[She was preparing spiritual garments for Christ about to be born.] She had learned, as history relates, that the holy Catherine of Siena had once given Christ, who was then hidden under the guise of a poor man, a garment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[subito fugantur.] huic facies tota hoc inflammata morbo tumuerat, simul reliquum corpusculum febres exurebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034748Z">
        <seg>[They are suddenly driven away.] His entire face was swollen by this inflamed disease, and at the same time the rest of his little body was being consumed by fevers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[toti urbi Limanæ notissimus,] nihilominus ob longam standi desuetudinem proximo parieti acclinis, breviterque miratus seipsum erectum ac sine fulcro, simul in clamores, simul in lachrymas erupit, jubilans atque vociferans;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033403Z">
        <seg>[Well-known throughout the city of Lima,] nevertheless, from long disuse of standing, leaning against the nearest wall, and briefly marveling at himself, upright and unsupported, he broke out at once into shouts and tears, rejoicing and crying out;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[ubi tanta populi turba concurrit,] at illustriori ostento ibidem Virgo Deipara videbatur excepisse Rosam suam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030635Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030635Z">
        <seg>[where such a great crowd of people had gathered,] but at a more illustrious sign, the Virgin Mother of God was seen there to have received her Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Apud Oseam prophetam cap.&lt;/e0&gt; 2 ℣.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041820Z">
        <seg>At the prophet Hosea, chapter 2, verse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Etiam&lt;/e0&gt; § 1 &lt;e1&gt;Commentarii prævii retulimus elogium hujus Sanctæ, quod idem eminentissimus Azzolinus coram Alexandro VII Pontifice recitavit&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043314Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043314Z">
        <seg>Also, in the preliminary notes we have recorded the eulogy of this Saint, which the same most eminent Azzolinus recited before Pope Alexander VII.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;περίφρασις&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;idem est, quod apud Latinos&lt;/e2&gt; circumlocutio, &lt;e3&gt;quæ huic examinatori adhibenda fuit, cum sancta Virgo terminos scholasticos non intelligeret&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062350Z">
        <seg>a Greek&lt;/e0&gt; periphrasis&lt;/s1&gt;, the same as the Latin circumlocutio&lt;/e2&gt;, which had to be used with this examiner, since the holy Virgin did not understand scholastic terms&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hi curiosi&lt;/e0&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e1&gt;observatores per tropum vocantur&lt;/e1&gt; Argi, &lt;e2&gt;alludendo ad Argum, filiæ Inachi custodem, quem poëtæ plurimis oculis præditum fuisse fabulantur, &amp; qui propterea a Græcis&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;πανόπτης&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;id est&lt;/e4&gt;, omnia videns, &lt;e5&gt;cognominatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040934Z">
        <seg>These curious observers of St. Rose are tropically called Argus, alluding to Argus, the keeper of Inachus's daughter, whom poets fabled to have been endowed with many eyes, and who was therefore called by the Greeks panoptes, that is&lt;/e4&gt;, all-seeing, is nicknamed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hi similesque lusus amatorii forte non placebunt iis, quorum judietum seu cenfuram dehujusmodi ludis in Commentario prævio num.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051453Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051453Z">
        <seg>These and similar amateur games may not please those whose judgment or censure of such games is expressed in the preliminary commentary, no.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hic alluditur ad duas voces Græcas&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;χεὶρ&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;&amp;&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;πῦρ&lt;/s3&gt; &lt;e4&gt;quarum prima&lt;/e4&gt; manum, &lt;e5&gt;altera&lt;/e5&gt; ignem &lt;e6&gt;significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T043948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035839Z">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Here an allusion is made to two Greek words&lt;/e0&gt;, &lt;s1&gt;χεὶρ&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;and&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;πῦρ&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;the first of which means&lt;/e4&gt; hand, &lt;e5&gt;the second&lt;/e5&gt; fire&lt;e6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hic venerabilis &amp; Apostolicus vir post Hansenianam hujus Vitæ editionem anno 1675 in numerum Beatorum relatus est, quem deinde Benedictus XIII anno Christi 1726 solenniter catalogo Sanctorum adscripsit, ut in Actis ejus apud nos ad diem 24 Julii illustratis, sive tomo&lt;/e0&gt; V &lt;e1&gt;istius mensis a pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041953Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041953Z">
        <seg>This venerable and Apostolic man was included among the Blessed after the Hansenian edition of this Life in 1675, who was then solemnly enrolled in the catalog of the Saints by Benedict XIII in the year 1726, as is shown in his Acts with us for the day of July 24, or in volume V of this month from page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hic venerabilis &amp; Apostolicus vir post Hansenianam hujus Vitæ editionem anno 1675 in numerum Beatorum relatus est, quem deinde Benedictus XIII anno Christi 1726 solenniter catalogo Sanctorum adscripsit, ut in Actis ejus apud nos ad diem 24 Julii illustratis, sive tomo&lt;/e0&gt; V &lt;e1&gt;istius mensis a pag. 847 licet videre&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000927Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000927Z">
        <seg>This venerable and Apostolic man was included among the Blessed after the Hansenian edition of this Life in 1675, who was then solemnly enrolled in the catalog of the Saints by Benedict XIII in the year 1726, as may be seen in his Acts illustrated by us for the day of July 24, or in the fifth volume of this month from page 847.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hoc loco biographus alludit ad medicam vel chirurgicam piæ duræque matris appellationem, de qua Castellus in Lexico medico apud nos pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042440Z">
        <seg>Here the biographer alludes to the appellation of the pious and stern mother as a physician or surgeon, which Castellus discusses in his Medical Lexicon, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hoc loco biographus alludit ad medicam vel chirurgicam piæ duræque matris appellationem, de qua Castellus in Lexico medico apud nos pag. 322 sic disserit:&lt;/e0&gt; Membrana dura, inviolata, crassa, cuticularis (idem enim significant) est, quæ primum calvariæ ossibus exemptis, aut saltem apertis, cerebrum in orbem contegens apparet (quæ vulgo DURA MATER appellatur) cui altera tenuis mollisque subnectitur, quæ PIA MATER nominatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001033Z">
        <seg>Here the biographer alludes to the medical or surgical appellation of a pious and strong-willed mother, about which Castellus in his Medical Lexicon, at our page. 322 thus discusses:&lt;/e0&gt; It is the dura, an inviolate, thick, cuticular membrane (for they signify the same thing), which first appears, the bones of the skull having been removed or at least opened, covering the brain in a sphere (which is commonly called the DURA MATER), to which another, thin and soft, is attached, which is called the PIA MATER.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hoc loco dicitur sancta Virgo in ecstasim&lt;/e0&gt; diriguisse, &lt;e1&gt;eo quod ecstasis sit abstractio mentis a corpore, in quam homines abrepti velut obrigescunt &amp; inflexibiles redduntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030437Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030437Z">
        <seg>At this point it is said that the holy Virgin in ecstasy shuddered, because ecstasy is an abstraction of the mind from the body, into which people, when seized, are as if frozen and rendered inflexible.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Honorius Philoponus Benedictinus eamdem Christianæ fidei originem Ordini suo adscribit, ut patet ex epilogo Operis sui de Navigatione ad Indiam occidentalem, quod anno 1620 edidit, &amp; in quo pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054652Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054652Z">
        <seg>Honorius Philoponus Benedictine ascribes the same origin of the Christian faith to his Order, as is evident from the epilogue of his work On Navigation to the West Indies, which he published in 1620, and in which page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Honorius Philoponus Benedictinus eamdem Christianæ fidei originem Ordini suo adscribit, ut patet ex epilogo Operis sui de Navigatione ad Indiam occidentalem, quod anno 1620 edidit, &amp; in quo pag. 96 habet sequentia:&lt;/e0&gt; Intentum meum hoc loco tantum est, ut demonstrem monachos sancti Benedicti primos, &amp; primates archiepiscopos &amp; vicarios sanctissimi domini Papæ fuisse in totam Americam (de quibus in hunc usque diem soli &amp; ambitiose nimis aliqui gloriantur) &amp; ibidem episcopos, præpositos, decanos, parœcianos novi orbis consecrasse, ordinasse, &amp; instituisse ecclesias;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011147Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011147Z">
        <seg>Honorius Philoponus Benedictine ascribes the same origin of the Christian faith to his Order, as is evident from the epilogue of his work On Navigation to the West Indies, which he published in 1620, and in which page. 96 has the following:&lt;/e0&gt; My intent here is only to demonstrate that the monks of Saint Benedict were the first, and the primates, archbishops, and vicars of the most holy Lord Pope for all of America (of whom to this day some boast, and boast too ambitiously) and that there they consecrated, ordained, and established parishes, churches, parishes of the New World consecrated, ordained, and established churches;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Igitur&lt;/e0&gt; S. Rosa &lt;e1&gt;præscivit, se festo S. Bartholomæi morituram esse, cum illud quotannis tamquam ultimum vitæ suæ terminum singulari pietate celebraret&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043023Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043023Z">
        <seg>Thus St. Rose foresaw that she would die on the feast of St. Bartholomew, since she celebrated that day each year with singular devotion as if it were the final term of her life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;In Bibliotheca Societatis nostræ pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050736Z">
        <seg>In the Library of our Society, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;In Bibliotheca Societatis nostræ pag. 355 &amp; sequentibus virtutes &amp; lucubrationes hujus pii viri in prolixo ipsius elogio recensentur.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010523Z">
        <seg>In the Library of our Society, on page 355 and following, the virtues and writings of this pious man are recounted in his lengthy eulogy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;In Vita S. Catharinæ Senensis apud nos tomo 3 Aprilis pag. 900 similis favor huic Virgini collatus narratur.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042300Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042300Z">
        <seg>In the Life of St. Catherine of Siena, in our volume 3 of April, page 900, a similar favor granted to this Virgin is narrated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Laudes &amp; munia hujus præclari viri apud Echardum in Bibliotheca Prædicatorum tomo 2 pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045354Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045354Z">
        <seg>Praises and offices of this distinguished man are in Echard, in the Library of the Preachers, volume 2, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Laudes &amp; munia hujus præclari viri apud Echardum in Bibliotheca Prædicatorum tomo 2 pag. 417 referuntur, &amp; alia occasione hic infra num.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010300Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010300Z">
        <seg>The praises and offices of this distinguished man are referred to in Echard's Bibliotheca Praedicatorum, vol. 2, p. 417, and on another occasion below here, no.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Papebrochius noster ad diem 30 Aprilis Acta S. Catharinæ Senensis edidit, in quibus varia cum gestis hodiernæ Sanctæ concordant, ut tomo 3 istius mensis a pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040416Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040416Z">
        <seg>Our Papebrochius, on April 30, published the Acts of St. Catherine of Siena, in which various things correspond with the deeds of the present-day Saint, as in the third volume of that month, from page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Papebrochius noster ad diem 30 Aprilis Acta S. Catharinæ Senensis edidit, in quibus varia cum gestis hodiernæ Sanctæ concordant, ut tomo 3 istius mensis a pag. 851 legi potest.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203512Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203512Z">
        <seg>Our Papebrochius, on April 30, published the Acts of St. Catherine of Siena, in which various things correspond with the deeds of the present-day Saint, as in the third volume of that month, from page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Rosa est flos sacer Veneri, quæ dum Adonidem ab apro læsum nudis pedibus opem latura accurrit, spinæ aculeo vulnerata est in pede, &amp; suo cruore tinxit rosam, ex quo sanguine rosæ, quæ prius albæ fuerant, deinde rubrum colorem induerunt, ut fabulosa poëtarum historia tradit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043956Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043956Z">
        <seg>The rose is a sacred flower to Venus, who, while rushing to aid Adonis wounded by a boar, ran on bare feet and was pricked in the foot by a thorn, staining the rose with her own blood, from which the roses, which had previously been white, then took on a red color, as the fabled history of the poets relates.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Sancta nostra hic metaphorice&lt;/e0&gt; heliotropium &lt;e1&gt;vocatur a flore, quem Græci&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;ἡλιοτρόπιον&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;e3&gt;appellant, eo quod ad solem se convertat, ex&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;ἥλιος&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;id est&lt;/e5&gt;, sol, &lt;e6&gt;&amp;&lt;/e6&gt; &lt;s7&gt;τρέπω&lt;/s7&gt; verto &lt;e8&gt;vel&lt;/e8&gt; converto, &lt;e9&gt;sicut passim notum est&lt;/e9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055504Z">
        <seg>our Holy One is here metaphorically&lt;/e0&gt; called a heliotrope, from the flower which the Greeks&lt;/e1&gt; ἡλιοτρόπιον&lt;/s2&gt; call, because it turns toward the sun, from&lt;/e3&gt; ἥλιος&lt;/s4&gt;, that is, the sun, &amp; τρέπω turn or convert, as is well known.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Scriptor Vitæ hoc loco alludit ad historiam Eliæ, cui persecutionem Jezabelis fugienti cibus ab angelo allatus est, &amp; qui in fortitudine cibi illius usque ad Horeb montem Arabiæ pervenit, ut lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060322Z">
        <seg>The author of the Life here alludes to the story of Elijah, to whom, fleeing Jezebel's persecution, food was brought by an angel, and who, by the strength of that food, reached Mount Horeb in Arabia, as in the book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Aureola &lt;e0&gt;est vox ecclesiastica, quæ præmium quoddam merito redditum significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032402Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032402Z">
        <seg>Aureola is a church term that signifies a reward justly bestowed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Callao &lt;e0&gt;est famosus Americæ portus, qui duabus leucis ab urbe Limana distat, &amp; quem Latius mox citatus lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054153Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054153Z">
        <seg>Callao is a famous port of America, which lies two leagues from the city of Lima, and which Latius, soon to be cited, mentions in his book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Cancer &lt;e0&gt;est morbus ab atra bile effervescente proficiscens, tum alias corporis partes, tum frequenter mammas mulierum infestans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061612Z">
        <seg>Cancer is a disease arising from black bile that bubbles up, then invades other parts of the body and frequently attacks women's breasts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Capitilavium &lt;e0&gt;olim dicebatur Dominica Palmarum propter causas, quas Macrius in Hierolexico pag. 120 allegat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040952Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040952Z">
        <seg>It was formerly called the Sunday of Palms because of the reasons which Macarius cites in the Hierolexicon, page 120.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Cephalæa &lt;e0&gt;vel&lt;/e0&gt; cephalalgia &lt;e1&gt;est&lt;/e1&gt; dolor capitis, &lt;e2&gt;ut ipsum nomen indicat: provenit enim ex radice Græca&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;κεφαλὴ&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;quæ&lt;/e4&gt; caput &lt;e5&gt;significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035201Z">
        <seg>Cephalalgia is a headache, as the name itself indicates: for it comes from the Greek root kephale, which means head.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Charybdis &lt;e0&gt;est proprie gurges vorticosus in freto Siculo oppositus Scyllæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042651Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042651Z">
        <seg>Charybdis is properly the whirlpool in the Sicilian Strait opposite Scylla.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Congerro &lt;e0&gt;vocatur is, cum quo familiariter versamur &amp; facete confabulamur, qui a Græcis&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;συνόμιλος&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;vel&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;συνομιλήτης&lt;/s3&gt; &lt;e4&gt;appellatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052256Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052256Z">
        <seg>He is called a companion with whom we associate familiarly and converse wittily, who is called by the Greeks synomilos or synomilites.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Costæ &lt;e0&gt;sunt in humano corpore ossa illa, quæ spinæ cohærentiam, intestinaque suo complexu coërcent, ut notum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060902Z">
        <seg>The ribs are those bones in the human body which, as is well known, connect to the spine and enclose the intestines.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Diarrhœa, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;διάῤῥοια&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;Latine&lt;/e2&gt; profluvium, &lt;e3&gt;est copiosior alvi fluor sine phlegmone &amp;ulceratione intestinorum, sicut Castellus &amp; alii medici fusius explicant&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033620Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033620Z">
        <seg>Diarrhea, from Greek &lt;s1&gt;διάῤῥοια&lt;/s1&gt;, Latin profluvium, is a copious flow of the bowels without phlegmon or ulceration of the intestines, as Castellus and other physicians explain more fully.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Diploïs &lt;e0&gt;est vestis aut pallium duplex, ut patet ex ipsa etymologia Græca: quod enim Latini duplex aut duplicatum dicunt, Græci&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;διπλοῦν&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;appellant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031559Z">
        <seg>Diplois is a double garment or cloak, as is clear from its Greek etymology: for what the Latins call duplex or duplicatum, the Greeks call diploun.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Enterocela &lt;e0&gt;vel potius&lt;/e0&gt; enterocele, &lt;e1&gt;Græcis dicta&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;ἐντεροκήλη&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;teste Gorræo&lt;/e3&gt;, est tumor scroti, delapso in id intestino.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040103Z">
        <seg>Enterocele, or rather enterocele, called in Greek ἐντεροκήλη, is, according to Gorræus, a swelling of the scrotum caused by an intestine prolapsing into it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Guatimala &lt;e0&gt;urbs Americæ Septemtrionalis in Nova Hispania, quæ nomen suum provinciæ Guatimalæ dedit, estque episcopalis sub archiepiscopo Mexicano, a qua metropoli in ortum hibernum trecentis leucis distat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031311Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031311Z">
        <seg>Guatemala, a city in North America in New Spain, which gave its name to the province of Guatemala, is a bishopric under the Archbishop of Mexico, and is located three hundred leagues east of that metropolis.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Guinea &lt;e0&gt;est ampla Africæ regio, ex qua magnus mancipiorum numerus in Americam transvehitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034348Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034348Z">
        <seg>Guinea is a large region of Africa, from which a great number of slaves are transported to America.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Hybla &lt;e0&gt;est mons Siciliæ thymo abundans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044718Z">
        <seg>Hybla is a mountain of Sicily abundant in thyme.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Indus &lt;e0&gt;&amp;&lt;/e0&gt; Ganges &lt;e1&gt;sunt celeberrima flumina Indiæ Orientalis, ut passim notum est, &amp; distinctius apud geographos obvios licet videre.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041346Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041346Z">
        <seg>The Indus and the Ganges are the most celebrated rivers of Eastern India, as is well known, and can be seen more distinctly on maps available to geographers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Labarum &lt;e0&gt;proprie olim vocabatur vexillum militare, auro intextum gemmisque intermicantibus splendidissimum, quod imperatoribus præferebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030737Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030737Z">
        <seg>The labarum was originally called a military standard, most splendidly woven with gold and glittering with gems, which was carried before the emperors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Lima &lt;e0&gt;est celeberrima novi orbis civitas, quæ ab Hispanis vernacule&lt;/e0&gt; ciudad de los reyes, &lt;e1&gt;id est&lt;/e1&gt; civitas regum, &lt;e2&gt;vocatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203135Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035049Z">
        <seg>Lima &lt;e0&gt;is the most famous city in the New World, which the Spaniards call in their native tongue&lt;/e0&gt; "Ciudad de los Reyes," &lt;e1&gt;that is&lt;/e1&gt;, "City of the Kings."&lt;e2&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Lima autem 120 in meridiem, &amp; 75 a Cusco, de qua posteriori civitate nunc aliquid notandum est&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050752Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050752Z">
        <seg>From Lima, however, it is 120 miles to the south and 75 from Cusco, about which latter city something must now be noted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Malus Cydonia, &lt;e0&gt;quam alii&lt;/e0&gt; cotoneam &lt;e1&gt;cognominant, est arbor passim nota, de qua Plinius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052903Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052903Z">
        <seg>The Cydonian apple, which others call the cotoneaster, is a tree commonly known, about which Pliny writes in his book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Nigro fluvio in duas partes inæquales secta erat, &amp; sub ipsa comprehendebantur Nigritarum regio, Garamantes, Getuli &amp; Libya deserta &lt;e2&gt;&amp;c&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013031Z">
        <seg>It was divided by the Niger River into two unequal parts, and within it were included the region of the Nigritas, the Garamantes, the Getuli, and desert Libya, etc.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Paroxysmus &lt;e0&gt;aliter vocatur&lt;/e0&gt; accessio, &lt;e1&gt;vel&lt;/e1&gt; invasio, &lt;e2&gt;teste Castello in Lexico medico, estque&lt;/e2&gt; deterius tempus totius circuitus, quod est a primo insultu morbi usque ad tempus consistendi, sive per tertium, sive per quartum, sive quotidie fiat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041408Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041408Z">
        <seg>A paroxysm, also called an accession or an invasion, according to Castelo in the Medical Lexicon, is the worst part of the entire cycle, which is from the first onset of the disease until the time of its cessation, whether it occurs on the third, the fourth, or every day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Promulsidaria &lt;e0&gt;apud Latinos dicuntur vasa vel scutellæ, quibus promulsis continetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025801Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025801Z">
        <seg>At the Romans, promulsidaria are called vessels or small bowls in which the promulsis is contained.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Sancta Crux de la Sierra &lt;e0&gt;est montana Americæ provincia, in regno Peruano sita, versus ortum &amp; Paraguaiam extensis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013012Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013012Z">
        <seg>Santa Cruz de la Sierra is a mountainous province of America, located in the Kingdom of Peru, extending eastward toward Paraguay.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Stricturæ &lt;e0&gt;proprie dicuntur scintillæ, quæ de ferro candenti micant, quando ignita ferri massa malleo tunditur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045909Z">
        <seg>Strictly speaking, sparks are called those that flash from glowing iron when a red-hot mass of iron is struck with a hammer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Synteresis &lt;e0&gt;derivatur a verbo&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;συντηρέω&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;quod&lt;/e2&gt; observo &lt;e3&gt;&amp;&lt;/e3&gt; revereor &lt;e4&gt;significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033015Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033015Z">
        <seg>Synteresis is derived from the verb *syntereo*, which I observe and revere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Thymiama &lt;e0&gt;est suffimem optimi odoris, quod Calmetus in Dictionario biblico sic describit:&lt;/e0&gt; Thymiama unguenti suaveolentis genus erat ex stacte, onyche, sive ungue odorato &amp; galgano atque thure, omnibus æquali pondere constans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031745Z">
        <seg>Thymiama is a most fragrant incense, which Calmet describes in his Biblical Dictionary as follows: Thymiama was a kind of fragrant ointment made from stacte, onyx, or scented nail, and galangal, all in equal weight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Tonsillæ &lt;e0&gt;sunt extremæ faucium partes circa radicem linguæ, gulam utrimque attingentes.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043109Z">
        <seg>The tonsils are the extreme parts of the throat around the root of the tongue, touching the gullet on both sides.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Utopia &lt;e0&gt;est confictum regionis nomen, quo scriptores quidam utuntur, dum rem nullibi factam vel futuram significare volunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012049Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012049Z">
        <seg>Utopia is a fictitious name for a region, which certain writers use when they want to signify a place that is nowhere or will be nowhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a cælesti Sponso hæc mihi securissima fiducia est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052103Z">
        <seg>From the heavenly Spouse I have this most certain confidence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a duodecimo anno expeditissime introrsum ejus animam, a Domino cum suis spiritualibus potentiis attractam, divinæ pulchritudini inhæsisse, ut nulla re creata a charitate Christi umquam separari potuerit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041743Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041743Z">
        <seg>from the age of twelve, its soul, drawn by the Lord with His spiritual powers, most swiftly clung to the divine beauty, so that it could never be separated from the charity of Christ by any created thing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a naso usque ad dextram auriculam ebulliit inflammatus tumor, inde per caput &amp; maxillas oberrabat inquieti ulceris velox incendium, cui febris, dyspepsia stomachi, aliaque cognata symptomata facile accesserunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040835Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040835Z">
        <seg>A swollen, inflamed tumor burst forth from the nose to the right ear; from there, a swift inflammation of an unsettled ulcer spread across the head and jaws, to which fever, stomach dyspepsia, and other related symptoms readily joined.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a prima Dei notitia repletam fuisse timore Dei, &amp; horrore peccati;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041749Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041749Z">
        <seg>that from the very first knowledge of God, it was filled with the fear of God, and the horror of sin;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a prima Dei notitia repletam se fuisse timore &amp; horrore peccati, &amp; si quis motus indeliberatus adversus rationem nitebatur assurgere, repente ex apprehensione divinæ præsentiæ citra conflictum evanuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050340Z">
        <seg>that from the very first awareness of God she had been filled with fear and horror of sin, and if any involuntary movement against reason sought to arise, it would suddenly vanish, beyond any conflict, at the apprehension of the divine presence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Ætna &lt;e0&gt;mons ignivomus Siciliæ, cujus nomen hic metaphorice ac hyperbolice flagranti&lt;/e0&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e1&gt;cordi tribuitur&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053622Z">
        <seg>from Mount Etna, the fiery mountain of Sicily, whose name is here metaphorically and hyperbolically attributed to the burning heart of St. Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ab infantia ex magno Dei munere faciles ad virtutes propensiones sine passionum tumultu sensisse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041746Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041746Z">
        <seg>to have felt, from infancy, a great gift of God: a ready inclination toward virtue, without the turmoil of passions;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ab omnibus, repetitum in suggestu Rosæ nomen, patulo, sed taciturno reverentiæ gestu excipiebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031540Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031540Z">
        <seg>The name of Rosa, repeated by all, was met on the platform with a broad but silent gesture of reverence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ab&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;οὐ&lt;/s1&gt;, non, &lt;e2&gt;&amp;&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;τόπος&lt;/s3&gt;, locus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012052Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012052Z">
        <seg>ab&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ou&lt;/s1&gt;, not, &lt;e2&gt;&amp;&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;topos&lt;/s3&gt;, place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>abhorrere omnes, tamquam rem humanitus impossibilem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062750Z">
        <seg>to abhor all, as if it were a humanly impossible thing;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ac certa eram, illum officio non defuturum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051750Z">
        <seg>And I was certain he would not fail in his duty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ac medicinæ se subjecit, quam sciebat nil profuturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045754Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045754Z">
        <seg>and submitted himself to a medicine which he knew would be of no benefit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ac primum quidem matrimonii sui fructum filiolam fore, a Deo peculiariter signatam in facie, quam videlicet purpureus conspicuæ Rosæ character esset ornaturus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012826Z">
        <seg>and first of all, that the fruit of her marriage would be a little daughter, specially marked by God on her face, which, of course, the purple character of the conspicuous Rose was to adorn.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ac repente illius cordi impluit ignitum at peregrinum desiderium sacri habitus Dominicani.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032916Z">
        <seg>and suddenly a fiery yet foreign desire for the sacred Dominican habit poured into his heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ac sæpe coram intimis gravissime questa est, jugi se dolore affici, quod sexus ac seculi conditio sibi non permitteret, in asperrimos montes procul secedere, ibique cavam rupem inquirere, ubi remota ab omni humano commercio, vitam degeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043950Z">
        <seg>and she has often complained most grievously in the presence of her intimates that she is afflicted by a constant pain, because her sex and her station in life would not permit her to retreat far away into the most rugged mountains, and there seek out a hollow rock, where, removed from all human contact, she might live.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ac vultui decumbentis, præmissa oratione, molliter illigavit, sicque per eam noctem quiescere jussit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034736Z">
        <seg>And, after a preliminary prayer, he gently bound it to the face of the one lying down, and thus ordered him to rest for the rest of the night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>accendebat piarum meditationum lumina præcipue ex Ludovici Granatensis libello, qui de Oratione inscribitur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055727Z">
        <seg>He would kindle the lights of pious meditations, especially from the little book of Louis of Granada, which is titled On Prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>acceptavit æger munificam Rosæ largitatem, haud ignarus, quantum opulentiæ in dono tot meritorum lateret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060846Z">
        <seg>The patient accepted Rose's generous largess, not unaware of how much wealth lay hidden in a gift of so many merits.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>accessere item copioso numero felices animæ, similique adorationis ritu Salvatorem reveritæ, seorsim constiterunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052752Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052752Z">
        <seg>A great number of happy souls also approached, and, with a similar rite of adoration, reverently honored the Savior, standing apart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>acclament populi, magis ipsa obsurdescebat: ergo quæ ita cor suum, ne crederet, obloricarat, validiori pulsanda fuit ariete, ut se dederet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032947Z">
        <seg>The more the people cheered, the more she herself grew deaf; therefore, the one who had so fortified her heart against believing had to be struck with a stronger ram, so that she would surrender.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>acerbius omni jactura &amp; gehenna ipsa videri ei, si vel ad momentum e conspectu eum perderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041758Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041758Z">
        <seg>It would seem to him more bitter than any loss and hell itself to lose him from sight, even for a moment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ad SS. Trinitatis honorem soli tres hodie culices te pungent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043928Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043928Z">
        <seg>In honor of the Holy Trinity, only three mosquitoes will bite you today.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ad hujus aspectum cor Virginis pulsu validiori, &amp; concitato videbatur in horologium transmutari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054838Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054838Z">
        <seg>At the sight of him, the Virgin's heart seemed to be transformed into a clock, beating more strongly and agitated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ad istud (quod puerile, nimisque femineum aliis videbatur) crucis honorandæ studium se stimulis tam excelsis ac seriis excitari, ut repugnandi modus aut ratio numquam sibi ad manum sit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054931Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054931Z">
        <seg>that his zeal for honoring the cross (which to others seemed childish and overly feminine) was stirred by such lofty and serious incentives that he never had a way or reason to resist;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ad quem, velut ad commune urbis oraculum, ardua quæque negotia certatim adferebantur ex consiliis antistitum, ex regio audientiæ (ut vocant) senatu, ex consistoriis, judiciis, dicasteriis, ex interno extimoque foro;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050203Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050203Z">
        <seg>to whom, as to the city's common oracle, the most arduous affairs were eagerly brought from the councils of the high priests, from the royal senate (as they call it), from the consistories, courts, and dicasteries, from the internal and external forum;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ad quid mihi cor istud, si nondum id pro tuo amore totum diffluit in cineres”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053049Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053049Z">
        <seg>What good is that heart to me, if it has not yet completely burned to ashes for your love?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adde his in obnixum universam hanc nostram Americam, imo totum potius terrarum orbem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062635Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062635Z">
        <seg>Add to these, in a manner of speaking, all of our America, or rather the whole world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>addebat ejusdem sumptus &amp; operis ramusculos prasinos&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;, porraceos, herbidos, omnique viroris amœnitate variegatos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011410Z">
        <seg>He added to it sprigs of the same cost and workmanship: greenish, leek-like, herbaceous, and variegated with every pleasing hue of foliage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>addebat ejusdem sumptus &amp; operis ramusculos prasinos&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, porraceos, herbidos, omnique viroris amœnitate variegatos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060118Z">
        <seg>He added to it sprigs of the same cost and workmanship, greenish, leek-like, herbaceous, and variegated with every pleasing hue of foliage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>addebat, torqueri ægrum urentibus scrupulis, affligi non certitudine vicinæ mortis, sed incertitudine vitæ melioris, eo quod meritis se vacuum agnosceret, contremiscere pavidum, ac sudare ad continuam severi Judicis, quem aditurus erat, memoriam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060831Z">
        <seg>He added that the sick man was tormented by burning scruples, afflicted not by the certainty of imminent death, but by the uncertainty of a better life, because he recognized himself to be devoid of merit; he trembled in fear and sweated from the constant memory of the stern Judge he was about to approach.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeo hæc sacra imago trahebat, adeo firmabat virgunculam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054317Z">
        <seg>So powerfully did this sacred image draw her, so strongly did it fortify the maiden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeo post varias, at numquam sat æquas comparationes, effari aliud non poterat quam: “Dolores inferni circumdederunt me, præoccupaverunt me laquei mortis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045847Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045847Z">
        <seg>After various, but never sufficiently fair comparisons, he could say nothing other than: "The pains of hell have surrounded me; the snares of death have overtaken me."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeo quod de sanctioris propositi executione cum suis tam secura tractare solebar, acsi, dispositis omnibus, de proximo velum sibi imponendum exspectaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011956Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011956Z">
        <seg>So secure was she in her dealings with her own people about the execution of her holier purpose, as if, having arranged everything, she were awaiting the veil to be placed upon her at the appointed time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeo quod famosus ille nostri seculi mystagogus Didacus Alvarez de Paz&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; Societatis Jesu, in Peruano tunc regno Provincialis, dum suas de oratione, meditatione, &amp; contemplatione præclarissimas lucubrationes apparabat, hunc virum in plurimis sibi duxerit consulendum, ipsumque tomo 3 de contemplatione lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010418Z">
        <seg>so much so that the famous mystagogue of our century, Diego Alvarez de Paz, of the Society of Jesus, then Provincial of the Peruvian province, while preparing his most excellent treatises on prayer, meditation, and contemplation, considered this man the foremost authority to consult in many matters, and even cites him in Book 3 on Contemplation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeo quod famosus ille nostri seculi mystagogus Didacus Alvarez de Paz&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; Societatis Jesu, in Peruano tunc regno Provincialis, dum suas de oratione, meditatione, &amp; contemplatione præclarissimas lucubrationes apparabat, hunc virum in plurimis sibi duxerit consulendum, ipsumque tomo 3 de contemplatione lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050058Z">
        <seg>so much so that the famous mystagogue of our century, Diego Alvarez de Paz of the Society of Jesus, then Provincial of the Peruvian province, while preparing his most excellent treatises on prayer, meditation, and contemplation, considered this man the foremost to consult in many matters, and even cites him in the third volume on contemplation, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeo quod toto illo quadriennio, quo cum parentibus in Canta morabatur, nec pedem ultra domicilii limen extulerit, nec vicinum deliciarum hortum videre dignata fuerit, nisi quod semel dumtaxat ibidem, ad invisendas Indicarum operarum molas, jussa fuit matronarum filias comitari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035945Z">
        <seg>so that during the entire four years she lived with her parents in Kent, she neither set foot beyond the threshold of her home nor deigned to see her neighbor's pleasure garden, except that on a single occasion she was ordered to accompany the matrons' daughters there to visit the mills of the Indian works.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeo ut ante sepulturam de integro sexies fuerit revestienda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031019Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031019Z">
        <seg>so that before burial it had to be completely rewrapped six times.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeo ut citra extremam vim &amp; cruciatum, nec panis bolum glutire, nec aquæ guttulam sorbillare se posse adverteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055951Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055951Z">
        <seg>He realized that, short of the utmost violence and torture, he could neither swallow a loaf of bread nor sip a drop of water.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeo ut tandem de alia translatione fuerit cogitandum, ne vicini summi altaris ministeria interturbarentur quotidie immodesto concursu populorum, quos partim veneratio, partim miraculorum &amp; gratiarum frequentia attrahebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031556Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031556Z">
        <seg>to the point that it was finally necessary to consider another translation, lest the services at the high altar be disturbed daily by the undignified throng of people, drawn partly by devotion, partly by the frequent occurrence of miracles and graces.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeoque crebra &amp; amica fuit inter has virgines conversatio, ut ex vultu beatæ Catharinæ in faciem Rosæ transirent lumina, ita ut &amp; vivens &amp; defuncta per oris lineamenta hæc eam omnino repræsentaret&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;, præter innumeras delicias, favores, officia &amp; virtutes, quibus ab ea ornata &amp; aucta fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041954Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041954Z">
        <seg>And so frequent and friendly was the conversation between these virgins that the eyes of the blessed Catherine would pass from her own face to Rose's, so that both in life and in death she would completely resemble her by the very features of her mouth, besides the countless graces, favors, services, and virtues with which she had been adorned and enriched by her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adjunge per hypothesim totius orci vires, artes, potentiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062638Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062638Z">
        <seg>Add, by hypothesis, the forces, arts, and power of the entire underworld;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>admonitus confessarius mandavit, a media saltem nocte horas omnino quatuor somno tribueret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035709Z">
        <seg>The confessor, having been admonished, commanded him to devote at least four hours to sleep, beginning at midnight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adverterent, magnam hanc esse pharmacopœam, omni adversus peccata antidoto instructissimam, orationis usu purgari spiritum, religari animarum vulnera, hac aperiri cellam Sponsi unguentariam, omnisque ceromatis apothecam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045118Z">
        <seg>if they would turn their attention, this is a great pharmacopoeia, most fully equipped with every antidote against sins, by which the spirit is purified through the use of prayer, the wounds of the soul are bound up, the Spouse's perfumed chamber is opened, and the entire apothecary of ointments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>advocatoque in consilium unico germano fratre, paucis desiderium suum explicuit, mysterium celavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044439Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044439Z">
        <seg>And with his brother, the only one of his kind, as his advocate, he briefly explained his desire and concealed the mystery.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>affabatur, venerabatur, dissuaviabat sacram imaginem, acsi præsentem e cælis haberet magistram suam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055039Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055039Z">
        <seg>She spoke to, venerated, and sweetly caressed the sacred image, as if she had her teacher present from the heavens.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>affirmat præ aliis testis sexagesimus octavus, illa singula tam consummatæ fuisse perfectionis, elegantiæ, curæ, ut plerumque metas humanæ artis &amp; industriæ viderentur excedere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035753Z">
        <seg>The sixty-eighth witness, above the others, affirms that those particulars were so consummate in perfection, elegance, and care that they generally seemed to exceed the limits of human art and industry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>age in hoc vile corpusculum, quæ tibi Deus permiserit, certe in animam nil poteris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052100Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052100Z">
        <seg>Act in this vile body, whatever God has permitted you; you can certainly do nothing to the soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>agnoscite periculum, cui vos implicuistis, misereamini vestrarummet animarum, oves perditæ, quas bonus Pastor per spinas &amp; tribulos, per sudores sanguineos, per vulnera &amp; crucem requirit, ut salvet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060533Z">
        <seg>Recognize the danger you have involved yourselves in, have mercy on your own souls, O lost sheep, whom the good Shepherd seeks to save through thorns and thistles, through bloody sweat, through wounds and the cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>aiebat terrores illos, horridasque imagines talem sibi inferre dolorem, qui millies &amp; millies sufficeret cor, vitam, animam eripere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045819Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045819Z">
        <seg>He said that those terrors and horrid images inflicted upon him a pain sufficient a thousand times over to snatch away his heart, his life, his soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>alii extrema ejus manibus, si possent, aut vestibus dare oscula;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031047Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031047Z">
        <seg>others would, if they could, give kisses to his extremities, either his hands or his clothes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>alii saltem postremo eam intuitu eminus salutare importunissime contendebant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031050Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031050Z">
        <seg>others at least, in the end, most importunately insisted on saluting her from a distance;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>aliis, de inferorum pœna cogitandum esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052652Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052652Z">
        <seg>that others should think about the punishment of the underworld.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>alter, quando inter labores manuum, externasque occupationes, mentem immobiliter figebat in Deum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044821Z">
        <seg>the other, when, amid manual labors and external occupations, he fixed his mind immovably on God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>alteram fugitivam, scito cras ad te reducendam”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012718Z">
        <seg>the other fugitive, know that she will be brought back to you tomorrow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>alterum perpetua &amp; numquam interpolanda Dei amicitia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061748Z">
        <seg>the other, the perpetual and never-to-be-interpolated friendship of God;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>an non ego Flos campi melior tibi sum, omni basilico, totoque floreto paradisi?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051232Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051232Z">
        <seg>Am I not better for you, Flower of the Field, than all basil and the entire flower of paradise?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>angebar, sudabam, anhelabam, videbaturque anima corporeo carcere teneri jam non posse, quin, eo relicto, sola agilior erumperet, clamatura per mundum: O si nossent mortales, quid sit gratia, quam formosa, nobilis, pretiosa, quantas in se complectatur divitias, quas opes, jubilos, delicias, utique ambirent omnes affligi &amp; pati, ultro postularent cuncti molestias, persecutiones, ægritudines, tormenta pro solo gratiæ opulentissimo lucro assequendo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052835Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052835Z">
        <seg>I was in anguish, I was sweating, I was gasping for breath, and it seemed the soul could no longer be held in its bodily prison, but, leaving it behind, would burst forth alone and more agile, crying out through the world: Oh, if mortals knew what grace is, how beautiful, noble, and precious it is, and how many riches, what treasures, what joys, and what delights it embraces, surely everyone would desire to be afflicted and to suffer, and all would willingly ask for hardships, persecutions, illnesses, and torments for the sake of obtaining the single, most opulent gain of grace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>animam interimit, quidquid carni petulanti lenocinatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060654Z">
        <seg>It destroys the soul, whatever flatters the licentious flesh.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>apage hos inanium argutiarum calamistros, facessant theatricæ ostentationis cothurni &amp; phaleræ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060555Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060555Z">
        <seg>Away with these calamus-wielding pedants of empty cleverness; let the theatrical display of cothurnus and phalera make way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>apertius ad mysterium ventum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041510Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041510Z">
        <seg>The mystery was approached more openly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ardores Spiritus usque ad animæ liquefactionem efficaces, blandos, subtiles.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044539Z">
        <seg>The ardors of the Spirit, effective up to the melting of the soul, are gentle, subtle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>arduum illud negotium, quod subvereris, in alium transferetur, tu plane immunis eris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012758Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012758Z">
        <seg>That arduous task you dread will be transferred to another, and you will be completely safe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>art.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T071539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T071539Z">
        <seg>Article 72</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>aspirat mox gratiarum placidus Auster, &amp; fluunt aromata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050516Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050516Z">
        <seg>The gentle South Wind soon breathes its grace, and spices flow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>asserebat, hac se medullitus instaurari ac refici, nonnullosque in anima &amp; corpore effectus experiri simillimos iis, quos in sumptione SS. Eucharistiæ percipiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054137Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054137Z">
        <seg>He asserted that he was thereby thoroughly renewed and restored, and experienced certain effects in soul and body very similar to those he perceived in the reception of the Holy Eucharist.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>assultu, dente, ungue sæviebat, qua poterat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051933Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051933Z">
        <seg>He raged with a rush, with his teeth and nails, as best he could.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Isabellæ (Deo mirabiliter disponente) innotuit, quæ &amp; pannum, quo Virgo labia deterserat, fortuito demum inventum, studiosius in facti memoriam asservavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061420Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061420Z">
        <seg>But to Isabella (as God miraculously arranged), it became known, and she, having at last by chance found the cloth with which the Virgin had wiped her lips, more devoutly preserved it as a memorial of the event.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Ludovica dubitabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032944Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032944Z">
        <seg>But Ludovica hesitated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Rosa confestim servum expediens, heræ suæ renuntiare jussit, opportune beneficium venisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051725Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, immediately freeing the slave, ordered him to tell his mistress that the favor had come at the right time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Rosa leniter subridens, “Nequaquam, (ait) mi pater, hic annus te nobis eripiet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012228Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012228Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, smiling gently, said, "By no means, my father, will this year take you from us."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Rosa subito pudore in se reducta, objurgavit seipsam: “Quid (inquiens) rudis ista bestiola meum suumque Creatorem laudat, pastus sui oblita;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045031Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, suddenly abashed, rebuked herself: "What," she said, "is this rude little creature praising my Creator and its own, forgetting its own pasture?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Rosa, nec mutato leviter colore, monuit, sperandum firmius in adjutorio Altissimi, &amp; maxime tunc, quando periculi vicinitas omne effugium interclusit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062133Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, her color not having changed in the slightest, warned that one should place a firmer hope in the help of the Most High, especially then, when the proximity of danger had cut off all escape.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Rosa, quæ interim opus suum lapidarium ex toto nondum absolverat, pudore suffusa, multis excusare se conabatur, quod necessitatibus parentum impedita, quod rudis adhuc illius artis, quæ tam inusitata esset femineis manibus, lino dumtaxat ac lanæ assuetis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044204Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, who had not yet completely finished her stonecutting work, blushing with shame, tried in many ways to excuse herself, saying she had been hindered by her parents' needs and was still unskilled in an art so unusual for a woman's hands, accustomed only to linen and wool.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Rosam is non in Thabor, sed in Calvariæ monte invenit, fortiusque defixit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013552Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013552Z">
        <seg>But he found Rosam not on Tabor, but on the mount of Calvary, and fastened her more strongly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Rosæ præ ipsa pupilla oculi charior erat sua anachoresis, longoque tempore ipsi valuit hæc solertia, donec tandem suspecta frequentia rem prodidit genitrici, quod ita accidit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043348Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043348Z">
        <seg>But to Rosa, her own seclusion was dearer than the pupil of her eye, and this cunning served her well for a long time, until at last the frequent comings and goings betrayed the matter to her mother, which is how it happened.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Sabbatum erat, ac mane proximo ad sacram Synaxim accessura, cavebat jejunio, si forte medietas noctis jam præterierat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051102Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051102Z">
        <seg>But it was the Sabbath, and she was to approach the sacred Synaxis the next morning; she was fasting, in case the midnight hour had already passed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at alacris mater, velut signo ad assultum dato, sævissimam illam catastam non aliter ac hostilem arcem invasit, truncos, ligna, tigna dejecit, luxavit asseres, &amp; non sine præcipiti manuum offendiculo testacea fragmenta sublegit, sparsit, tandemque in vicinum urbi fluvium, ne ad usum redirent, abjecit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042859Z">
        <seg>But the eager mother, as if a signal had been given for an assault, invaded that most savage pile as if it were a hostile fortress, threw down logs, beams, and timbers, loosened the planks, and not without a great risk of stumbling, picked up the broken pottery fragments, scattered them, and finally threw them into a nearby river, so that they could not be used again.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at biliosa complexio, &amp; assuetudo callum vitio induxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060725Z">
        <seg>but a choleric temperament, and habit had bred a callousness to wrongdoing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at ceteris altius icta spectaculo mater subito complexu tenerisque osculis dissuaviavit parvulam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034656Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034656Z">
        <seg>But when the others were struck higher, the mother, suddenly distracted by the spectacle, soothed the little one with a embrace and tender kisses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at coronidem imponat Didacus Hyacinthus Pacecus Hispalensis&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt;, [&amp; amanuensi in eadem causa laboranti] qui Limæ ex quotidiano hypographicæ&lt;a1&gt;g&lt;/a1&gt; artis lucello victitans, a Didaco Morales, in causa venerabilis Rosæ notario, exemplandis transcribendisque Processuum seu Actorum voluminibus adhibebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032305Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032305Z">
        <seg>but let the coronet be placed upon Diego Hyacinth Pacecus of Seville, [and the amanuensis working on the same case], who in Lima, living on the daily earnings of the art of hypography, was employed by Diego Morales, notary in the case of the venerable Rosa, to copy and transcribe the volumes of the Processes or Acts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at eamdem rescidit apportata Rosæ effigies, mox ut sub ægræ conspectum venerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040751Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040751Z">
        <seg>But the same was torn apart by the portrait of Rose that had been brought, as soon as it came into the sick woman's view.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at ego convitii impatiens, replicui audacter, æque ut illas Christianam me esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012919Z">
        <seg>But I, impatient of the insult, replied boldly, just as they had, that I was a Christian.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at exercitio, conversatione, spiritu, plane religiosus, Limæ illis diebus inter selectos Dei servos eximius habebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050039Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050039Z">
        <seg>At prayer, in conversation, and in spirit, he was a thoroughly devout man, and in those days at Lima he was regarded as an outstanding member of God's chosen servants.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at gloriosissimum martyrii genus est lente, imo articulatim mori.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025624Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025624Z">
        <seg>But the most glorious kind of martyrdom is to die slowly, indeed, piece by piece.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at honesta &amp; timida virgo se ignotam ignotis credere noluit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061206Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061206Z">
        <seg>But the honorable and timid virgin would not trust strangers with her identity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at huic ubi Lucia modicum terræ e sepulchro Rosæ inspersit, tridui spatio plaga coaluit, relicta sola in beneficii memoriam cicatrice.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035623Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035623Z">
        <seg>But on that spot where Lucia sprinkled a little earth from Rose's tomb, the wound healed within three days, leaving only a scar as a reminder of the favor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at hæ ignorabant arcanum symbolum cordis, virgineo cordi inaratum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044123Z">
        <seg>but these were ignorant of the secret symbol of the heart, inscribed on the virgin's heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at hæc prodit turgentibus rursum oculis, palpebrisque subtristi rubore inflammatis, ut facile constaret, visum ea defluxione accensum, &amp; nimio dolore nictitantem, auræ solisque pericula neutiquam laturum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043404Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043404Z">
        <seg>But this is revealed by his eyes swelling again and his eyelids inflamed with a saddened flush, so that it was clear his vision, inflamed by that discharge and blinking from excessive pain, could in no way endure the dangers of the breeze or the sun.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at ii, si pridie impetum plebis sustinere vix potuerunt, hac die ne vix quidem potuere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031001Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031001Z">
        <seg>But they, if on the previous day they had barely been able to withstand the onslaught of the mob, on this day could not even barely manage it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at illi nec satis assequebantur, quid Virgo dicere conaretur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045658Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045658Z">
        <seg>but they did not fully understand what the Virgin was trying to say.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at inter reliquas visiones unam excelluisse pure intellectualem, qua puritatis suæ virgineus candor, cum sua inæstimabili dote &amp; floridissima aureola&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; ostendebatur absque omni figura corporea, perque simplicem irradiationem tranquillæ notitiæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031859Z">
        <seg>but that among the other visions one had excelled as purely intellectual, in which the virgin brightness of its purity, with its inestimable grace and most flourishing aureola, was displayed without any corporeal form, and through the simple radiation of tranquil knowledge.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at interim delicatæ conscientiæ deerat secura tranquillitas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032247Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032247Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, the delicate conscience lacked secure tranquility.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at inventum nihil.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055129Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055129Z">
        <seg>but nothing was found.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at ipsa, quo illæ tendebant, celerrime prægressa, occupavit indaginem, hisque mitissimis verbis abrupit molestiam: “Scito (inquit) mi Pater, me usque a teneris ardenter orasse Deum, ne sinat ulli mortalium innotescere, si quid altissima sua misericordia cœperit forsan intra animulæ meæ penetralia dignanter operari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040828Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040828Z">
        <seg>and she herself, having most swiftly anticipated their purpose, seized the inquiry, and with these most gentle words put an end to the disturbance: "Know, my Father," she said, "that from my earliest years I have ardently prayed to God not to allow any mortal to learn of it, should He perhaps begin to work within the innermost recesses of my little soul by His highest mercy."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at is dissuasit, quod vereretur facile sub putri aculeo, gravem in Rosæ capite purulentiam posse succrescere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042149Z">
        <seg>But he dissuaded her, for he feared that a serious, pus-filled infection could easily develop on Rose's head under a rotten thorn.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at magis, ut vidit filiam non mirari, utpote his usu ac frequentia dudum assuetam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051800Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051800Z">
        <seg>but rather, seeing that his daughter was not surprised, since she had long been accustomed to such things by frequent use.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at majori semper damno, fructuque prorsus nullo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034729Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034729Z">
        <seg>but to ever greater harm, and with absolutely no benefit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at mater fastidio impar, Filiam, quasi manifeste insomniis rerum impossibilium delirantem, objurgavit coram omnibus: “Desine (inquit) ineptire fatua, memento non esse plebeiæ sortis, aut patrimonii vulgaris negotium, cœnobia fundare;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062325Z">
        <seg>But the mother, with unfeigned disgust, rebuked her daughter before everyone, as if she were clearly delirious with impossible dreams: "Stop your foolish nonsense," she said, "remember you are not of common birth, nor is it a matter of ordinary patrimony to found monasteries."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at metuebat vir prudens, ne forte post Rosæ obitum de condendo tanti funeris thesauro lites inter parochiam &amp; sancti Dominici conventum exurgerent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030043Z">
        <seg>but the prudent man feared that perhaps after Rose's death, disputes would arise between the parish and the convent of Saint Dominic over the founding of a treasury for such a funeral;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at mysterium ignorabant, quod supponerent, coronam per ipsummet sacristam e collo Deiparæ in digitos Filioli fuisse translatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043547Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043547Z">
        <seg>But they were unaware of the mystery they were implying: that the crown had been transferred by the sacristan himself from the neck of the Mother of God to the fingers of the little Son.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at mœsta &amp; verecunda;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044257Z">
        <seg>but sad and shy</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at ne labente memoria quidquam præteriret, hoc ordine singula notarat in pagella: “Jesus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053545Z">
        <seg>So that nothing might be overlooked through a failing memory, he had noted each item in the following order on a small slip of paper: "Jesus."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at necdum suspicatus, quid rei esset, prosequendæ scriptioni, uti pridie, se denuo totum applicuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032343Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032343Z">
        <seg>Not yet suspecting what the matter was, he once again applied himself entirely to the writing he was to continue, just as he had the day before.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at nesciebam, tantum pœnarum in corpus humanum cadere, ac per singulos artus distribui posse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013412Z">
        <seg>But I did not know that so many punishments could fall upon the human body and be distributed among its individual limbs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at nihil elicuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050448Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050448Z">
        <seg>but nothing came of it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at non ita placuit Virgunculæ, quam non contemptus, sed sordes offendebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034947Z">
        <seg>But this did not please the maiden, who was offended not by contempt, but by the filth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at nondum se scire, num forsitan illi divinitus sit reservata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040326Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040326Z">
        <seg>but he does not yet know whether perhaps it is divinely reserved for him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at numquam eo, quo tunc dulcedinis excessu, ea familiaritatis aura ac gratia, illo reconditi favoris singularissimo fulgetro;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044325Z">
        <seg>but never with that excess of sweetness, that aura and grace of familiarity, that most singular flash of hidden favor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at nunc aliter tibi visum est: benedicta sit abundantia tam copiosa misericordiæ tuæ”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025655Z">
        <seg>But now it has seemed different to you: blessed be the abundance of your mercy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at omnium importunior, quia subtilior: nam per ipsos humilitatis cuniculos inferne adactus, quatiebat Rosam unius commotione scrupuli, qua patuit via cogitationi pavidissimæ, &lt;s2&gt;[tertium Ordinem S. Dominici]&lt;/s2&gt; nimirum se sordidam peccatricem illo niveo Senensis Catharinæ velamento nimis indignam esse, plus eo vestis candore promitti foris, quam lateret intus, uni sibi deesse omnia, quæ extimo nitori respondeant: proinde habitum, qui Seraphicæ magistræ fuerat protestativum virtutis ac religionis symbolum, in se non nisi larvam esse, publicumque usurpatæ sanctimoniæ mendacium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040335Z">
        <seg>but the most importunate of all, because the most subtle: for through the very narrow passages of humility, hellishly driven, it shook Rose with a single tremor of scruples, which opened the way to the most fearful thought, &lt;s2&gt;[the Third Order of St. Dominic]&lt;/s2&gt; namely, that she was a filthy sinner, far too unworthy of that snowy veil of Catherine of Siena; that more was promised by the garment's whiteness outwardly than was hidden within; that she lacked everything that would correspond to its external splendor: Therefore, the habit, which for the Seraphic mistress had been a protestative symbol of virtue and religion, was in herself nothing but a mask and a public lie of assumed sanctity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at omnium importunior, quia subtilior: nam per ipsos humilitatis cuniculos inferne adactus, quatiebat Rosam unius commotione scrupuli, qua patuit via cogitationi pavidissimæ, [tertium Ordinem S. Dominici] nimirum se sordidam peccatricem illo niveo Senensis Catharinæ velamento nimis indignam esse, plus eo vestis candore promitti foris, quam lateret intus, uni sibi deesse omnia, quæ extimo nitori respondeant: proinde habitum, qui Seraphicæ magistræ fuerat protestativum virtutis ac religionis symbolum, in se non nisi larvam esse, publicumque usurpatæ sanctimoniæ mendacium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T212027Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203508Z">
        <seg>but the most importunate of all, because the most subtle: for through the very narrow passages of humility, hellishly driven, it shook Rose with a single tremor of scruples, which opened the way to the most fearful thought, [the Third Order of St. Dominic] namely, that she was a filthy sinner, far too unworthy of that snowy veil of Catherine of Siena; that more was promised by the garment's whiteness outwardly than was hidden within; that she lacked everything that would correspond to its external splendor: Therefore, the habit, which for the Seraphic mistress had been a protestative symbol of virtue and religion, was in herself nothing but a mask and a public lie of assumed sanctity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at primo die, quo laborem orsus est, præ difficultate insueti characteris, desperavit se umquam tot foliis exarandis parem fore: siquidem (ut diserte ipsemet coram judicibus apostolicis sub jurata fide deposuit) adeo sibi inter scribendum lassabantur digiti, stupebat brachium, divaricabantur nervi, ut sæpius exclamarit, vereri se, ne ex tanta fatigati pulsus attractione citius ipse deficeret, usumque manus perderet, quam injunctum opus absolveret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032315Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032315Z">
        <seg>but on the first day he began the work, he despaired of ever being able to write out so many leaves, overwhelmed by the difficulty of the unfamiliar script: for indeed (as he himself eloquently deposed before the apostolic judges under oath) his fingers grew so weary while writing, his arm grew numb, and his nerves were strained, that he often exclaimed he feared that from such great fatigue the pulse of his hand would fail sooner, and he would lose the use of his hand, than he would complete the assigned task.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at prælibanda sunt ex multis paucula de singulari prærogativa celeberrimæ hujus imaginis, quæ inde ab introductæ Christianæ fidei primis initiis prima toti Peruano regno per publica beneficia innotuit, &amp; privatim Rosæ nostræ tam eximie chara fuit atque propitia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054221Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054221Z">
        <seg>But a few things must be selected from the many concerning the singular prerogative of this most celebrated image, which, from the very beginnings of the Christian faith, first became known throughout the entire kingdom of Peru by its public benefits, and was privately so exceedingly dear and gracious to our Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at puellæ animum altius hæc verba, quam pro dicentis impetu, suæve ætatis modulo penetrarunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034959Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034959Z">
        <seg>But the words penetrated the girl's mind more deeply than was fitting for the speaker's impetuosity or her own age.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at quando nulla tibi affulgebat spes evadendi, &amp; quidam æternitatis fumus pervadebat horroris caliginem, ipsissimus inferni typus erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050405Z">
        <seg>But when no hope of escape shone for you, and a certain smoke of eternity permeated the darkness of horror, it was the very type of hell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at reprimebat cassos impetus, memor, neminem posse calamitati tam inexplicabili succurrere, sibique deesse verba idonea, quibus eam proximis vel tenuiter adumbraret, sed neque in orbe toto reperiri doctorem adeo disertum, profundum, &amp; eruditum, qui hujus pœnæ gravitatem aut speculatione assequi, aut animo complecti posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045605Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045605Z">
        <seg>but he restrained his futile impulses, mindful that no one could come to the aid of such an inexplicable calamity, and that he himself lacked the fitting words to even faintly sketch it for those close to him; nor could a doctor be found anywhere in the whole world so eloquent, profound, and learned that he could either grasp the gravity of this punishment through speculation or encompass it with his mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at schola divini amoris hic suam agnoscit, reperitque phrasim.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053123Z">
        <seg>But in the school of divine love, one recognizes oneself here and finds one's own expression.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at scito, illa tibi accidisse incommoda, quia non ille eras, qui esse solebas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051905Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051905Z">
        <seg>But know that those misfortunes happened to you because you were no longer the person you used to be.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at scito, juvenem, pro quo rogas, emittendæ professioni prorsus inidoneum esse”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012448Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012448Z">
        <seg>But know that the young man for whom you ask is completely unfit for the profession to which he is to be sent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at scito, mater, me altius collocasse fiduciam meam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062334Z">
        <seg>But know, mother, that I have placed my trust higher.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at semel ultra solitum cohorruerunt, dum Rosa, nullo jam læsarum aut illæsarum partium discrimine, vehementius ac diutius in se totam desæviebat plagarum strepitu late sonoro.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041532Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041532Z">
        <seg>But once, they shuddered beyond the usual, while Rosa, with no distinction now between her wounded and uninjured parts, raged more fiercely and for longer against her entire self with the loud clatter of her blows.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at sentiebat, se nunc eumdem nec nosse, nec diligere, utpote quem velut peregrinum, absentem, alienum, extraneum, vixque de fama notum, eminus, raptim, tenuiter adnictabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045521Z">
        <seg>But he felt that he no longer knew or loved the same man, whom he now barely glanced at from a distance, hastily and faintly, as if he were a stranger, absent, alien, a foreigner, and barely known by reputation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at simul currebat in odorem Sponsi spinis coronati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035316Z">
        <seg>but at the same time she ran toward the scent of the Spouse crowned with thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at simul discessit suavissima Excitatrix: nam ut Rosa diduxit graves palpebras, ut respexit, agnovit Deiparam, sed a tergo: jam enim suspensis vestigiis abire cœperat, velut perfuncta amico officio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051409Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051409Z">
        <seg>But at once the most delightful Exciter departed: for as soon as Rosa lifted her heavy eyelids and looked back, she recognized the Mother of God, though only from behind, for she had already begun to go away with suspended footsteps, as if having fulfilled a friendly duty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at simul ei tripudiabat animus in victoria divinæ gratiæ: compatiebatur lapsæ peccatrici, simulque effuse lætabatur, in ea miram Domini bonitatem ac misericordiam publice ab omnibus celebrari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053427Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053427Z">
        <seg>but at the same time his soul rejoiced in the victory of divine grace: he pitied the fallen sinner, and at the same time he overflowed with joy that in her the Lord's wondrous goodness and mercy were publicly celebrated by all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at stupebant magis, postquam observarunt, in ejus vultu ac manibus nec minimum hujus cruentæ molestiæ signum apparere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043813Z">
        <seg>But they were even more astonished when they noticed that not the slightest sign of this bloody ordeal appeared on his face or in his hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at sub fragili carne vigebat infractus animus, &amp; corculum in Deo fixum ridebat, despuebat, laxabat furentem cyclopis insaniam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052125Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052125Z">
        <seg>But beneath fragile flesh, an unbroken spirit thrived, and the little heart fixed in God laughed, scorned, and loosed the raging madness of the cyclops.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at sub hoc affectuum duello animam suam liquefieri ex arcano Dilecti alloquio, quasi dicat: “Ego dormio, &amp; cor meum vigilat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054518Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054518Z">
        <seg>but under this duel of affections, her soul is melted by the Beloved's mysterious speech, as if to say: "I sleep, &amp; my heart is awake."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at subito ad circumstantes reductis oculis, “Non plangamus, (inquit) nam amica nostra mortua non est, vivit Maria de Vera: tantum oremus Deum, ut convalescat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012213Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012213Z">
        <seg>But suddenly, turning her eyes to those around her, she said, "Let us not weep, for our friend is not dead; Mary of Verona is alive. Let us only pray to God that she may recover."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at subjecit, hanc brevi adfore ex domo quæstoris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051654Z">
        <seg>but he added that this would soon come from the house of the quaestor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at subrisit Virgo, monuitque, hujuscemodi cruces inter seculares aulæ pompas, &amp; assidua mundi commercia virere non posse: vetuit tamen emarcidum arbusculæ cadaver projici, jussitque ad se remitti propediem resuscitandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054959Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054959Z">
        <seg>But the Virgin smiled and warned that such crosses could not flourish amid the secular pomps of the court and the constant commerce of the world. Yet she forbade the withered little tree to be cast away and ordered it to be brought back to her at once to be revived.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at succedanei, quique ex occasione dumtaxat pœnitentiæ Sacramentalis, interdum studiosius intimas ejus animæ latebras pervestigarunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050030Z">
        <seg>but successors, and each one, only on occasion of the Sacrament of Penance, have at times more diligently searched the innermost recesses of his soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at successive his singulis eorumdem pulverum haustus repentinam salutem reddidit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040041Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040041Z">
        <seg>But by successively inhaling each of these same powders, he was suddenly restored to health.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at superabat tum pia commiseratio, quæ negare justa solatia ægris nesciebat, tum humilis obedientia, quam Rosa Mariæ de Usategui, velut secundæ matri, deberi agnoscebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061451Z">
        <seg>But above all shone a pious compassion, which could not deny the sick just consolations, and a humble obedience, which she acknowledged she owed to Rosa Maria de Usategui, as to a second mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at sustine: nam in semetipsam tota spinosa fuit, quod ex varia corporis sui castigatione, proxima quatuor capitula fuse docebunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040930Z">
        <seg>But bear with me: for she was all thorns in herself, which the next four chapters will teach at length, through the varied discipline of her own body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at tandem didicit, morbo suo unicum Rosæ sepulchrum pharmacopœiam esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035613Z">
        <seg>But at last he learned that, by his illness, Rose's only tomb was the apothecary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at taurus velocior, jam prope aderat, nec ultra quindecim passus distabat ab homine.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033840Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033840Z">
        <seg>But the faster bull was already nearby, and was no more than fifteen paces away from the man.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at tum vel maxime vexabant lacertos, quoties aut aliquid de terra levandum, aut brachia sursum extendenda erant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041814Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041814Z">
        <seg>But then their muscles ached the most whenever they had to lift something from the ground or stretch their arms upward.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at tunc ei importune occurrebat animæ suæ immortalitas, quam nulla pœnarum vis interimere, nullus infernus extinguere sufficeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045559Z">
        <seg>But then the immortality of his soul importunately occurred to him, which no power of punishments could kill, nor any hell could extinguish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at tunc nondum ei licebat sine matre adire ecclesiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055653Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055653Z">
        <seg>But at that time, he was not yet allowed to go to church without his mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at ubi ventum est ad partum, die Dominica XVI Maii duabus horis ante meridiem, altera prolium erupit sola, remanentibus intra puerperam secundis una cum fœtu residuo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040441Z">
        <seg>But when it came time to give birth, on Sunday, May 16, at two hours before noon, one of the membranes ruptured on its own, leaving the second one and the remaining fetal tissue inside the mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at una certus, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; juvenem, qui libidinoso animo ad se venerat,]&lt;/s2&gt; nuptias sperari non posse, quærebat occasionem, qua saltem oculos liberius pasceret Rosæ dulcissima venustate.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060611Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060611Z">
        <seg>But certain of one thing, [and of the young man who had come to him with a lustful mind,] that a marriage could not be hoped for, he sought an opportunity by which he might at least more freely feast his eyes on Rosa's most sweet beauty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at una certus, [&amp; juvenem, qui libidinoso animo ad se venerat,] nuptias sperari non posse, quærebat occasionem, qua saltem oculos liberius pasceret Rosæ dulcissima venustate.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011515Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011515Z">
        <seg>But certain of one thing, [and seeing that the young man who had come to him with a lustful mind could not hope for marriage,] he sought an opportunity by which he might at least more freely feast his eyes on Rosa's most sweet beauty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at videbantur fugisse terreni flores, velut indigni, qui Rosam coronarent jam cælestem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042415Z">
        <seg>But the earthly flowers seemed to have fled, as if unworthy to crown the now heavenly Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at videbatur omnis obcalluisse synteresis&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032535Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032535Z">
        <seg>but it seemed that everyone had become calloused by synteresis.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at æque certum est, ipsum Societati Jesu adscriptum non iri: rudior seraphici Francisci tunica illum expectat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012628Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012628Z">
        <seg>But it is equally certain that he will not be assigned to the Society of Jesus: a rougher tunic of Seraphic Francis awaits him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>atque indies serpebat malum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035418Z">
        <seg>and the evil spread day by day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>atque utinam sic operer, uti debeo!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062012Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062012Z">
        <seg>And may I work like this, as I ought!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>attamen longe majus fuit, a via, quam elegerat, numquam postea ullius noxæ gravioris admissione per totam vitam exerrasse, quod ut iidem sui confessarii concorditer asseruerunt, ita patulum fiet ex iis, quæ sequuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035043Z">
        <seg>However, it was by far a greater thing that, from the path he had chosen, he never afterward deviated in the admission of any more serious fault throughout his entire life, which, as his own confessors unanimously affirmed, will become clear from what follows.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>attamen quotquot illic adstabant reliqui, certissime illius intuitu, præsentia, solatio fruituros.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062709Z">
        <seg>However, the others who were there would certainly enjoy his gaze, presence, and comfort.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>attamen rogatu Virginis, quæ ultimam utriusque parentis benedictionem expectabat, ad Gundisalvi ædes portari se fecit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030136Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030136Z">
        <seg>However, at the request of the Virgin, who was awaiting the final blessing of both her parents, she had herself carried to Gundisalvi's house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>attamen subjecit, inter hos favores arctissimæ unionis, se sibi videri immobiliter radicatam in Deo, securam in ejus amicitia, confirmatam in gratia, sentire donum quoddam inexplicabile, fundamentale, solidum, quo reputabat se impeccabilem, ut ille qui fidenter aiebat: “Quis nos separabit a charitate Christi?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050526Z">
        <seg>However, she added that amid these favors of the most perfect union she seemed to herself to be immovably rooted in God, secure in His friendship, confirmed in grace, and to feel a certain inexplicable, fundamental, and solid gift by which she considered herself impeccable, like the one who confidently said, "Who will separate us from the love of Christ?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>attamen te superstite, te vivo &amp; oculato teste, monasterium, quod dixi, exstruetur, habitabitur, florebit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062644Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062644Z">
        <seg>However, with you alive and as an eyewitness, the monastery that I mentioned will be built, inhabited, and flourish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>attamen ultra interrogata, ne quid responderet, cautelam, &amp; parsimoniam verbis suis appendit in trutina.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040847Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040847Z">
        <seg>However, when questioned further, he was cautious and sparing in his words, weighing his replies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>attolluntur in sublime voces laudantium Deum, accurrunt religiosi, adducunt puerum in ecclesiam, totoque personant templo canoræ gratiarum actiones.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033505Z">
        <seg>Voices of those praising God are lifted up to the heights, the devout rush in, they bring the boy into the church, and the entire temple resounds with melodious acts of thanksgiving.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>audiitque blande respondentem sibi: “Plane sic fiet, o soror, &amp; quidquid consentaneum fuerit gloriæ hujus dulcissimi Numinis, quo fruor, me petente, summa illa bonitas concedet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032011Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032011Z">
        <seg>and she heard him answer her kindly: "It will surely be so, O sister, and whatever is fitting for the glory of this most sweet Deity, whom I enjoy, that supreme goodness will grant at my request."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>audita fama defunctæ Virginis, ad ecclesiam sancti Dominici cum aliis contendit, Rosæ precibus se commendavit, statimque, in signum obtentæ sanitatis, brachium alte sublatum &amp; exporrectum toto templo circumtulit, rejectis vetustis pannorum involucris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033257Z">
        <seg>Upon hearing of the Virgin's death, she rushed with others to the church of Saint Dominic, commended herself to the prayers of Rose, and immediately, as a sign of her restored health, she carried her arm, held high and outstretched, around the entire church, casting off the old bandages.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>audivisses in regularium peristyliis &amp; claustris nocturno tempore per omnes angulos cruentis flagrorum ictibus pœnitentiam, velut tympanistriam, acediæ ac tepori bellum indicere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032643Z">
        <seg>you would have heard in the cloisters and cells of the regulars, at night, in every corner, penitence, like a tympanistress, declaring war on acedia and tepidity with bloody blows of the flagellum.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>aures &amp; tempora velut ingesto candenti orbiculo dispunctim torreri;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013530Z">
        <seg>ears &amp; temples as if being dottedly scorched by a glowing orb thrust in;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>aut optabat cum marito Euphemia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012634Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012634Z">
        <seg>or she wished, with her husband, Euphemia.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Decretum Beatificationis &amp; alia diplomata, quibus Clemens Pontifex hanc virginem numero Beatarum adscripsit, ejusque cultum extendit, in Commentario prævio exhibuimus.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043317Z">
        <seg>We have presented the Decree of Beatification and other diplomas, by which Pope Clement enrolled this virgin among the number of the Blessed and extended her cult, in the preliminary commentary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Examen apotheoseos istius diu durasse, colligi potest ex nota superiori ad caput primum littera&lt;/e0&gt; i, &lt;e1&gt;in qua diximus, quo anno venerabilisantistes ille catalogo Beatorum &amp; Sanctorum inscriptus fuerit.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040422Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040422Z">
        <seg>It can be gathered from the above note to chapter one, letter i, that the examination of this apotheosis lasted a long time, in which we said in what year that venerable antipope was entered in the catalog of the Blessed and Saints.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;πράσινος&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;idem est, quod Latine&lt;/e2&gt; viridis &lt;e3&gt;a colore porri, quod Græce&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;πράσον&lt;/s4&gt; &lt;e5&gt;appellatur.&lt;/e5&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060332Z">
        <seg>Greek πράσινος is the same as the Latin viridis, from the color of leek, which is called πράσον.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἐπιγλωττὶς&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;(quasi Latine dicas&lt;/e2&gt; superlingua) &lt;e3&gt;est membrana cartilaginosa rotunditatis oblongæ, in interiori gutturis parte collocata, &amp; exoritur a radice linguæ, quæ Græce&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;γλῶττα&lt;/s4&gt; &lt;e5&gt;appellatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043119Z">
        <seg>b Greek&lt;/e0&gt; ἐπιγλωττὶς&lt;/s1&gt; (as if you were to say in Latin&lt;/e2&gt; super-tongue) It is a cartilaginous membrane of an oblong shape, located in the inner part of the throat, and it arises from the root of the tongue, which in Greek is called glōtta.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Hansenius hic &amp; alibi&lt;/e0&gt; mantissam &lt;e1&gt;appellat appendicem miraculorum, quam Vitæ&lt;/e1&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e2&gt;subjunxit, ut in fine Commentarii prævii monuimus&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030747Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030747Z">
        <seg>Here and elsewhere, Hansenius calls the appendix "the supplement of miracles, which he appended to the Life of St. Rose," as we noted at the end of the preliminary commentary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Hic allusio est ad signa cælestia mensis Augusti, in quibus tunc sol naturalis erat, &amp; ad Solem Eucharisticum, tempusque, quo&lt;/e0&gt; S. Rosa &lt;e1&gt;hunc percepit, &amp; ex hac vita migravit, ut satis manifestum est&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030443Z">
        <seg>Here is an allusion to the heavenly signs of the month of August, in which the natural sun was then present, and to the Eucharistic Sun, and to the time when St. Rose perceived this and departed from this life, as is quite evident.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Hic in metropolitanam Limensis urbis cathedram S. Thuribio successit, &amp; sacras&lt;/e0&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e1&gt;reliquias solenniter ad honorificentiorem locum transferri permisit, ut infra videbimus&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054159Z">
        <seg>Here he succeeded St. Turibius as Metropolitan of the See of Lima, and permitted the sacred relics of St. Rose to be solemnly transferred to a more honorable place, as we shall see below.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Hinc confirmatur, post mediam noctem, ineunte ipsa S. Bartholomæi festivitate, Sanctam ad superos migrasse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043026Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043026Z">
        <seg>Hence it is confirmed that, after midnight, at the very beginning of the feast of St. Bartholomew, the Saint departed for the heavens.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Hujus viri elogium apud Joannem Nadasinostrum in Anno dierum memorabilium Societatis Jesu ad diem 29 Martii pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042446Z">
        <seg>An eulogy of this man is found in John Nadasynski's *Annus Dierum Memorabilium Societatis Iesu*, on March 29, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Hujus viri elogium apud Joannem Nadasinostrum in Anno dierum memorabilium Societatis Jesu ad diem 29 Martii pag. 174 legi potest.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001036Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001036Z">
        <seg>An eulogy of this man can be read in John Nadasynski's *Annus Dierum Memorabilium Societatis Iesu* on March 29, page 174.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Hæc fuit illustris virgo Romana, quam SS. Hieronymus &amp; Augustinus impense laudant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031754Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031754Z">
        <seg>This was an illustrious Roman virgin, whom Saints Jerome and Augustine praise highly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Hæc verba sponsæ Canticorum&lt;/e0&gt; 2 ℣.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042304Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042304Z">
        <seg>These are the words of the bride of the Song of Songs, verse 2.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;In Actis S. Dominici ad diem quartam Augusti retulimus, hunc Sanctum consuevissesingulis noctibus ita corpus suum castigare.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041826Z">
        <seg>In the Acts of St. Dominic, on August 4, we recorded that this Saint was accustomed to chastise his body every night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Mare pacificum novam Hispaniam &amp; Peruam alluit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035102Z">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;The Pacific Ocean washes New Spain and Peru.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Medici papulas istas appellant&lt;/e0&gt; epinyctidas, &lt;e1&gt;Græce&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;ἐπινύκτιδας&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;quasi Latine dicas&lt;/e3&gt; nocturnas, &lt;e4&gt;ab&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;ἐπὶ&lt;/s5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;νυκτὸς&lt;/s6&gt;, &lt;e7&gt;id est&lt;/e7&gt;, sub noctem, &lt;e8&gt;quia tempore nocturno plerumque proveniunt vel maxime cruciant, ut Gorræus testatur&lt;/e8&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040112Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040112Z">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;The physicians call these papules&lt;/e0&gt; epinyctidas, &lt;e1&gt;in Greek&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;ἐπινύκτιδας&lt;/s2&gt;, as if you were to say in Latin nocturnal, from &lt;s5&gt;ἐπὶ&lt;/s5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;nyktos&lt;/s6&gt;, &lt;e7&gt;that is to say&lt;/e7&gt;, at nightfall, &lt;e8&gt;because they most often arise at night or torment most severely, as Gorræus testifies&lt;/e8&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Nomen Græcum&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;κατάσκοποσ&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;Latine significat&lt;/e2&gt; speculatorem, &lt;e3&gt;vel&lt;/e3&gt; exploratorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040940Z">
        <seg>The Greek name κατάσκοποσ&lt;/e0&gt; in Latin means speculator, or explorer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Non invenio in Peruvia locum&lt;/e0&gt; Canta &lt;e1&gt;appellatum, nisi forte sit&lt;/e1&gt; Guanta &lt;e2&gt;pagus Indorum, de quo Joannes Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T045930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035849Z">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;I do not find in Peru a place called&lt;/e0&gt; Canta, &lt;e1&gt;unless perhaps it is the Indian village&lt;/e1&gt; Guanta, &lt;e2&gt;about which John Latius writes in his book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Non satis assequor, cur Hansenius rosascognominet&lt;/e0&gt; Hyblæas, &lt;e1&gt;nisi forsan hoc epitheto meliores aut magis odoriferas designare voluerit: nam scarabæi vel cantharides odore cujuslibet rosæ exstinguuntur, ut apud Ulyssem Aldrovandum in Opere de Insectis lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044725Z">
        <seg>I do not fully understand why Hansen calls the Hyblæan roses by that name, unless perhaps he meant to designate with this epithet those that are more beautiful or more fragrant: for scarabs or cantharides are driven away by the scent of any rose, as Ulysses Aldrovandus notes in his Work on Insects, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Non tantum in Hispania est celebris istius nominis imago Deiparæ, sed etiam in urbe Limana, sicuti apud Franciscum de Echave superius citatum in Opere Hispanico de S. Turibio pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044002Z">
        <seg>Not only in Spain is there a famous image of the Virgin of that name, but also in the city of Lima, as stated by Francisco de Echave in the aforementioned Spanish work on St. Turibius, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Non tantum in Hispania est celebris istius nominis imago Deiparæ, sed etiam in urbe Limana, sicuti apud Franciscum de Echave superius citatum in Opere Hispanico de S. Turibio pag. 227 legitur.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010021Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010021Z">
        <seg>Not only in Spain is there a famous image of the Virgin of that name, but also in the city of Lima, as is read in the above-cited Francisco de Echave in his Spanish work on St. Turibius, page 227.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Per&lt;/e0&gt; vocabula emphatica &lt;e1&gt;intelliguntur verba valde significantia vel efficaciter expressiva, quemadmodum colligimus ex figura rhetorica, quæ Græce dicitur&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;ἔμφασις&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;&amp; est expressio cum energia, quando verbis subest tacita significantia, quæ pronuntiatione vel gestibus indicatur&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053626Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053626Z">
        <seg>Through emphatic words, we understand words that are very significant or effectively expressive, as we gather from the rhetorical figure, which in Greek is called ἔμφασις. &amp; is expression with energy, when there is an underlying tacit significance to the words, which is indicated by pronunciation or gestures.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Sanctus Augustinus lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033021Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033021Z">
        <seg>Saint Augustine, Book</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Sanctus Franciscus Solanus concionem de spirituali animæ ruina instituerat, quam populus Limanus de materiali &amp; subita urbis suæ ruina perperam interpretabatur, ut in memoratis Actis ejus cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042000Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042000Z">
        <seg>Saint Francis Solanus had delivered a sermon on the spiritual ruin of the soul, which the people of Lima mistakenly interpreted as referring to the material and sudden ruin of their city, as related in the aforementioned Acts of his life, chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Simile facinus heroïcum S. Catharinæ Senensis in Actis ejus apud nos tomo 3 Aprilis pag. 892 &amp; sequente legitur.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042657Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042657Z">
        <seg>A similar heroic deed of St. Catherine of Siena is read in her Acts in our volume for April 3, page 892 and following.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Souegius in Gallicis&lt;/e0&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e1&gt;Actis tomo 2 Augusti pag. 651 tantum dicit, Christum nocte sub forma lusoris ei apparuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010623Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010623Z">
        <seg>In the Gallican *Acta* of St. Rose (Vol. II, August, p. 651), it is said only that Christ appeared to her at night in the form of a player.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Suspicor, eumdem locum indicari a Joanne Latio, dum lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054707Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054707Z">
        <seg>I suspect that the same passage is cited by John Latius, while in the book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Verbum Græcum&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;γαργαρίζω&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;proprie significat liquorem ore haustum sæpe ad gurgulionem seu guttur revocare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045403Z">
        <seg>The Greek word gargarizo properly means to draw a liquid into the mouth and often to draw it back into the throat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;s0&gt;Κρᾶσις&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;e1&gt;apud medicos Græcos idem est quod&lt;/e1&gt; temperamentum, &lt;e2&gt;vel&lt;/e2&gt; temperies, &lt;e3&gt;id est, proportio quædam primarum qualitatum existentium in elementis, ex quibus corpus mixtum &amp; conflatum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045915Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045915Z">
        <seg>Mixture among the Greek physicians is the same as temperament, or temperance, that is, a certain proportion of the primary qualities existing in the elements, from which the body is mixed and composed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Ad Calendas Græcas &lt;e0&gt;ex proverbio usurpatur pro&lt;/e0&gt; numquam, &lt;e1&gt;quia Græci non habent Calendas more Latinorum&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012055Z">
        <seg>b Ad Calendas Græcas &lt;e0&gt;is used by proverb for&lt;/e0&gt; never, &lt;e1&gt;because the Greeks do not have Calends in the Latin manner&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Adytum &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἄδυτον&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;est locus secretior templi, ad quem nulli est aditus, nisi sacerdoti.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032411Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032411Z">
        <seg>Sanctuary (Gr. ἄδυτον) is the most secret part of the temple, to which no one has access except the priest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Amphibolon, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἀμφίβολον&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;hic significat sermonem ambiguum, ab adverbio&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;ἀμφὶ&lt;/s3&gt; utrimque, &lt;e4&gt;&amp; verbo&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;βάλλω&lt;/s5&gt; jacio &lt;e6&gt;vel&lt;/e6&gt; ferio, &lt;e7&gt;quasi&lt;/e7&gt; utrimque feriens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062353Z">
        <seg>b Amphibolon, Greek&lt;/e0&gt; amphibolon&lt;/s1&gt;, here it means an ambiguous speech, from the adverb ἀμφὶ on both sides, and the verb βάλλω to throw or strike, as if striking on both sides.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Cacochymia &lt;e0&gt;est vitiosus succus, aut abundantia vitiosi alicujus humoris in toto corpore.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034358Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034358Z">
        <seg>Cacochymia is a vicious juice, or an abundance of a vicious humor throughout the entire body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Caput viride, &lt;e0&gt;quod ab Hispanis&lt;/e0&gt; el Cabo verde &lt;e1&gt;nominatur, est promontorium Africæ in Nigritia regione, quod Dionysius Ferdinandus Lusitanus anno 1446 detexit, &amp; jam passim notum est&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013022Z">
        <seg>The Green Cape, which is called by the Spaniards Cabo Verde, is a promontory of Africa in the region of Nigritia, which Dionysius Ferdinandus the Portuguese discovered in 1446, and it is now widely known.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Carcinoma &lt;e0&gt;est species cancri, de cujus periculo &amp; remediis obvii medici consuli possunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061624Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061624Z">
        <seg>Carcinoma is a type of cancer, about whose risks and treatments one can consult a doctor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Chile &lt;e0&gt;vel&lt;/e0&gt; Cile &lt;e1&gt;est ampla Americæ regio, per quadringentas leucas Gallicas a Septemtrione in Meridiem extensa, quæ terminatur a Septemtrione Peruvia, ab Occidente mari Pacifico, a Meridie Magellanica regione, &amp; ab Oriente Tucumania.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060911Z">
        <seg>Chile or Cile is a vast region of America, extending four hundred French leagues from north to south, which is bordered on the north by Peru, on the west by the Pacific Ocean, on the south by the Magellanic region, and on the east by Tucumania.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Ginesta &lt;e0&gt;vel potius&lt;/e0&gt; genista &lt;e1&gt;est herba humilis &amp; sponte nascens, sic dicta, quod in modum genu flexilis sit, ideoque vitibus ligandis idonea, vel quod genibus dolentibus medeatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033623Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033623Z">
        <seg>b Ginesta &lt;e0&gt;or rather&lt;/e0&gt; genista &lt;e1&gt;is a low-growing, self-seeding herb, so called because it is flexible at the joint like a knee, and therefore suitable for tying vines, or because it is used to treat aching knees.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Inquisitio &lt;e0&gt;est tribunal in America, Lusitania, Hispania &amp; aliis quibusdam regionibus usitatum, verosimiliter ab&lt;/e0&gt; inquirendo &lt;e1&gt;dictum, quia administratores hujus sacri officii in hæreticos, hypocritas, aliosque sacrilegos inquirunt&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052922Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052922Z">
        <seg>The Inquisition is a tribunal used in America, Portugal, Spain, and certain other regions, probably so called from inquiring, because the administrators of this sacred office inquire into heretics, hypocrites, and other sacrileges.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Ischias est arthritis coxendicis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041411Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041411Z">
        <seg>b Sciatica is arthritis of the hip.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Libanotis, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;λιβανοτὶς&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;est frutex spirans odorem thuris, a&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;λιβανωτὸς&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;quo apud Græcos&lt;/e4&gt; thus &lt;e5&gt;significatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055507Z">
        <seg>Libanotis, Greek&lt;/e0&gt; libanotis&lt;/s1&gt;, is a shrub that gives off the scent of incense, from&lt;/e2&gt; libanotis&lt;/s3&gt;, by which incense is signified among the Greeks.&lt;/e5&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Pararius &lt;e0&gt;est ille, qui stipulationibus aut contractibus faciendis intervenit, &amp; quem poëta Martialis&lt;/e0&gt; proxenetam &lt;e1&gt;vocat a nomine Græco&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;προξενήτης&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;id est, licitator, mediator, conciliator, sive interpres duorum inter se stipulantium, aut spondentium, aliumve contractum ineuntium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052302Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052302Z">
        <seg>b A proxenetas is one who intervenes in making stipulations or contracts, and whom the poet Martial calls by the Greek name προξενήτης, that is, a negotiator, mediator, conciliator, or interpreter for two who are stipulating with each other, or pledging, or entering into another contract.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Peristylium &lt;e0&gt;est locus columnis cinctus, Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;περιστύλιον&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;appellatus, a præpositione&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;περὶ&lt;/s3&gt; circum, &lt;e4&gt;&amp; nomine&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;στύλος&lt;/s5&gt; columna, &lt;e6&gt;quasi diceres&lt;/e6&gt; circumcolumnium, &lt;e7&gt;id est, locum circumquaquecolumnis clausum, qualis in monasteriis passim solet esse&lt;/e7&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031320Z">
        <seg>b A peristyle is a place surrounded by columns, called in Greek &lt;s1&gt;peristyleon&lt;/s1&gt; from the prefix &lt;e2&gt;peri&lt;/e2&gt; meaning "around," and the name &lt;s5&gt;stylos&lt;/s5&gt; for column, &lt;e6&gt;as if you were to say&lt;/e6&gt; columniatum, &lt;e7&gt;that is, a place enclosed on all sides by columns, such as is commonly found in monasteries&lt;/e7&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Porrigo, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;πιτυρίασις&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;quasi&lt;/e2&gt; furfuratio, &lt;e3&gt;est, quando quædam squamulæ tenues instar furfuris e capite decidunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040959Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040959Z">
        <seg>Dandruff, from the Greek *pityriasis*, literally "flaking," is when small, thin scales like dandels fall from the head.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Syncope, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;συγκοπὴ&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;apud medicos est præceps omnium virium lapsus, de quo Gorræus &amp; Castellus fusius agunt&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025810Z">
        <seg>Syncope, from the Greek συγκοπὴ, is, among physicians, a sudden loss of all strength, which Gorræus and Castellus discuss at greater length.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Systema &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;σύστημα&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;præter alias significationes hic videtur indicare compagem operis ex certis partibus coagmentatam, &amp; derivatur a verbo Græco&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;συνίστημι&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;quod est&lt;/e4&gt; constituo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031605Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031605Z">
        <seg>System, from the Greek *systēma*, besides other meanings, here seems to indicate the structure of a work composed of certain parts, and is derived from the Greek verb *synistēmi*, which means "to constitute."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Tempora &lt;e0&gt;sunt partes capitis a lateribus sincipitis inter oculos, &amp; aures, &amp; verticem, seu ossa cranii utrimque ad aures sita.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035207Z">
        <seg>b. The temporal parts are the sides of the head between the temples, the ears, and the crown, and the bones of the skull situated on both sides of the ears.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Truxillum &lt;e0&gt;est urbs Americæ in parte septemtrionali Peruviæ, episcopalis sub archiepiscopo Limensi, quæ ab aliis ejusdem nominis oppidis distinguitur, ut in Annotatis ad Vitam S. Francisci Solani tomo 5 Julii pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050743Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050743Z">
        <seg>Trujillo is a city of America in the northern part of Peru, a bishopric under the Archbishop of Lima, which is distinguished from other towns of the same name, as in the annotations to the Life of St. Francis Solano, volume 5 of July, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Truxillum &lt;e0&gt;est urbs Americæ in parte septemtrionali Peruviæ, episcopalis sub archiepiscopo Limensi, quæ ab aliis ejusdem nominis oppidis distinguitur, ut in Annotatis ad Vitam S. Francisci Solani tomo 5 Julii pag. 870 olim monuimus&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010527Z">
        <seg>Trujillo is a city in northern Peru, an episcopal see under the Archbishop of Lima, which is distinguished from other towns of the same name, as we once noted in the Annotations to the Life of St. Francis Solano, volume 5 of July, page 870.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Æquator &lt;e0&gt;est circulus quinque parallelorum maximus, qui globum in duas partes æquales dividit, æqualem utrimque versus polos mundi distantiam relinquens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041349Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041349Z">
        <seg>The Equator is the largest circle of parallels, which divides the globe into two equal parts, leaving an equal distance on both sides toward the world's poles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>baptismalem gratiam nulla umquam lethali noxa interpunctam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030353Z">
        <seg>baptismal grace has never been interrupted by any lethal harm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>baptizatur Speranza, moriturque postridie.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012935Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012935Z">
        <seg>Speranza is baptized, and dies the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>benedicta hora, quæ te nascentem excepit!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030344Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030344Z">
        <seg>Blessed be the hour that received you at your birth!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>benedicta tu a Domino, felicissima Dominici filia, quæ nunc frueris vultu beatifico tui Creatoris!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030347Z">
        <seg>Blessed are you by the Lord, most blessed daughter of Dominic, who now enjoys the beatific vision of your Creator!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>benedictus sit Deus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054633Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054633Z">
        <seg>Blessed be God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>bibitura vero, non frigidam sed calentem sorbebat lympham, metuens in aqua subgelida tantillum invenire deliciarum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041355Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041355Z">
        <seg>But she drank not a cold, but a warm draught, fearing to find any pleasure in the ice-cold water.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>bina insuper pulvinaria in speciem imposuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042530Z">
        <seg>He also placed two cushions on the seat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>bono proinde animo esset, valetudinem citius, quam sperari poterat, redituram, eo quod prius ipsum oporteat divinæ gloriæ in quodam singulari ministerio strenue famulari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012324Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012324Z">
        <seg>He would therefore be of good cheer, his health returning more quickly than could be hoped, because he must first zealously serve the divine glory in a certain unique ministry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>brachium viri Gundisalvus obstipum reperit ac inflexibile, &lt;s2&gt;[ac immobilis hæreret.]&lt;/s2&gt; dexteram algido sudore madidam, ut in mortuis solet, digiros circa anulum ita contortos, implexos, nodoseque incurvos, ut vi ac mora opus fuerit, qua sensim explicarentur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044700Z">
        <seg>He found the man Gundisalvus's arm stiff and inflexible, [and frozen in place.] His right hand was drenched in cold sweat, as is common in the dead, its fingers twisted, entwined, and knotted around a ring so that it took force and time to straighten them out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>brachium viri Gundisalvus obstipum reperit ac inflexibile, [ac immobilis hæreret.] dexteram algido sudore madidam, ut in mortuis solet, digiros circa anulum ita contortos, implexos, nodoseque incurvos, ut vi ac mora opus fuerit, qua sensim explicarentur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010136Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010136Z">
        <seg>He found the man Gundisalvus's arm stiff and inflexible, [and frozen in place.] His right hand was drenched in a cold sweat, as is common in the dead, its fingers twisted, entwined, and knotted around a ring so tightly that it took both force and time to straighten them out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>brevi effectum manibus contrectandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054453Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054453Z">
        <seg>A brief, hands-on effect.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Ab Hispanis passim&lt;/e0&gt; beata &lt;e1&gt;vocatur femina, quæ sub modesto habitu vivit, &amp; mundo renuntiasse censetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040425Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040425Z">
        <seg>She is called blessed by the Spaniards everywhere, who lives in modest attire and is considered to have renounced the world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Acta hujus sancti Raymundi in Opere nostro ad diem 7 Januarii illustrata sunt, ubi tomo primo istius mensis pag. 414 etiam fit mentio mirifici pulveris, qui ex sepulcro ipsius erumpit.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031806Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031806Z">
        <seg>The Acts of this Saint Raymond are illustrated in our Work on January 7, where in the first volume of that month on page 414 mention is also made of the miraculous powder that bursts forth from his tomb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Apud Philostratum Epistola 27 rosæ vocantur&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἔρωτος&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;αἱ&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;λαμπάδες&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;id est&lt;/e4&gt;, amoris faces.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033031Z">
        <seg>In Philostratus's Letter 27, roses are called &lt;s1&gt;ἔρωτος&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;αἱ&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;λαμπάδες&lt;/s3&gt;, that is, torches of love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Biographus hac phrasi sat clare asserit, ineunte anno 1629 prophetiam&lt;/e0&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e1&gt;impletam vel eventu probatam fuisse&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012058Z">
        <seg>The biographer clearly states with this phrase that at the beginning of the year 1629, the prophecy of St. Rose was fulfilled or proven by events.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Collector Vitæ hoc loco alludit ad sequens Ovidianum carmen:&lt;/e0&gt; Aprilem memorant ab aperto tempore dictum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203246Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035111Z">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;The Collector of Life alludes here to the following Ovidian poem:&lt;/e0&gt; "They call April 'the open season.'"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Cum nusquam hoc nomen in masculino genere invenerim, propter oscitantiam typothetæ dubito, an non potius&lt;/e0&gt; pulpas, &lt;e1&gt;quam&lt;/e1&gt; pulpos &lt;e2&gt;hic sit legendum: nam&lt;/e2&gt; pulpa &lt;e3&gt;est pars carnosa in animali, quam medici&lt;/e3&gt; musculum, &lt;e4&gt;Græci&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;μῦς&lt;/s5&gt; &lt;e6&gt;ob similitudinem, quam cum mure excoriato habet, ut Gorræus in opere jam sæpius citato pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054202Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054202Z">
        <seg>c Since I have found this name nowhere in the masculine gender, I doubt, because of the typographer's oversight, whether it is not rather pulpas, than pulpos, that should be read here: for pulp &lt;e3&gt;is the fleshy part in an animal, which the doctors&lt;/e3&gt; call muscle, &lt;e4&gt;the Greeks&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;μῦς&lt;/s5&gt; &lt;e6&gt;on account of the resemblance it bears to a skinned mouse, as Gorræus in the work already cited several times, p.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Cum nusquam hoc nomen in masculino genere invenerim, propter oscitantiam typothetæ dubito, an non potius&lt;/e0&gt; pulpas, &lt;e1&gt;quam&lt;/e1&gt; pulpos &lt;e2&gt;hic sit legendum: nam&lt;/e2&gt; pulpa &lt;e3&gt;est pars carnosa in animali, quam medici&lt;/e3&gt; musculum, &lt;e4&gt;Græci&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;μῦς&lt;/s5&gt; &lt;e6&gt;ob similitudinem, quam cum mure excoriato habet, ut Gorræus in opere jam sæpius citato pag. 307 tradit&lt;/e6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011035Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011035Z">
        <seg>c Since I have found this name nowhere in the masculine gender, I doubt, because of the typographer's oversight, whether it is not rather pulpas, than pulpos, that should be read here: for pulp &lt;e3&gt;is the fleshy part in an animal, which the doctors&lt;/e3&gt; call muscle, &lt;e4&gt;the Greeks&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;μῦς&lt;/s5&gt; &lt;e6&gt;on account of the resemblance it has to a skinned mouse, as Gorræus relates in the work already cited several times, page 307&lt;/e6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Daniel Sennertus tom.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045924Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045924Z">
        <seg>Daniel Sennert, vol.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;De hac sancta virgine Viterbiensi ad diem quartam Septembris cum Martyrologio Romano nobis agendum erit.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031323Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031323Z">
        <seg>We will have to deal with this holy virgin of Viterbo on September 4th according to the Roman Martyrology.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;De veste, quam Deipara Ordini Prædicatorum dedit, in Commentario prævio ad vitam S. Dominici die 4 Augusti&lt;/e0&gt; § 28, &lt;e1&gt;sive tomo primo istius mensis pag. 460 &amp; sequentibus sat fuse egimus.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030446Z">
        <seg>On the vest which the Virgin Mary gave to the Order of Preachers, we have treated at sufficient length in the preliminary commentary on the life of St. Dominic on August 4, § 28, or in the first volume of that month, pages 460 and following.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Eruditus lector observet, caput illud, quod hic citatur&lt;/e0&gt; quintum, &lt;e1&gt;apud nos esse&lt;/e1&gt; quartum, &lt;e2&gt;&amp; sic deinceps editionem nostram uno Capite Hansenianam præcedere, quia duo priora Vitæ Capita nimis brevia in unum conflavimus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035904Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035904Z">
        <seg>The learned reader should note that the chapter cited here as the fifth is the fourth in our edition, and so on, our edition preceding the Hansenian by one chapter, because we have combined the two preceding chapters of the Vita, which were too brief, into one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Ex&lt;/e0&gt; crasi, &lt;e1&gt;id est, temperie vel indole materna, sicut hanc vocem Græcam superius explicui&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062356Z">
        <seg>c Ex&lt;/e0&gt; crasi, that is, by maternal temperament or disposition, as I explained this Greek word above&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Hic eximius favor apud nos tomo 3 Aprilis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051514Z">
        <seg>Here is an exceptional favor among us, on April 3, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Hic eximius favor apud nos tomo 3 Aprilis pag. 894 in Actis S. Catharinæ Senensis narratur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010629Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010629Z">
        <seg>Here this extraordinary favor among us is recounted on April 3, page 894, in the Acts of St. Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Hispanice&lt;/e0&gt; audiencia &lt;e1&gt;vocatur tribunal vel locus, ubi justitia administratur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030750Z">
        <seg>In Spanish, audiencia is called a tribunal or a place where justice is administered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Hoc loco designatur sanctus Gregorius Magnus &amp; primus istius nominis Pontifex Romanus,qui explicans Euangelia lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041359Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041359Z">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;At this point, Saint Gregory the Great is designated, the first Pope of that name, who explained the Gospels, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Iste Gregorius Lopez cognominatur&lt;/e0&gt; Mexicanus &lt;e1&gt;habitatione, ni fallor, cum Nicolaus Antonius in recentiore Bibliotheca Hispanica tomo 1, pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042006Z">
        <seg>This Gregory Lopez is nicknamed the Mexican, a resident, if I am not mistaken, as Nicholas Antonio states in the more recent Library of Hispanic Literature, volume 1, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Iste Gregorius Lopez cognominatur&lt;/e0&gt; Mexicanus &lt;e1&gt;habitatione, ni fallor, cum Nicolaus Antonius in recentiore Bibliotheca Hispanica tomo 1, pag. 417 de illo scribat sequentia:&lt;/e1&gt; Gregorius Lopez, cujus patria in obscuro est (alii Matritensem existimant, natumque anno MDXXXII, alii Lusitanum, ut Georgius Cardosus in Hagiologio suo XIII Martii pag. 164, qui ortum ait in oppido Linhares Beiræ provinciæ) cum ex Hispania in Septemtrionalem Americæ plagam, Mexici nempe regnum transfretasset, insigni apud eas gentes vixit morum innocentiæ ac sanctitatis fama &lt;e2&gt;&amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000930Z">
        <seg>This Gregory Lopez is nicknamed Mexican, a resident, if I am not mistaken, with Nicholas Antonio in the more recent Library of Spain, volume 1, page. 417 writes the following about him:&lt;/e1&gt; Gregory Lopez, whose nationality is obscure (some consider him a native of Madrid, born in 1532, others a Portuguese, as George Cardoso does in his *Hagiologio* on March 13, p. 164, who says that he was born in the town of Linhares in the province of Beira) when he crossed from Spain to the northern part of the Americas, namely the kingdom of Mexico, he lived among those peoples with a remarkable reputation for innocence and sanctity of character &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Istud medicamen dicitur&lt;/e0&gt; non anareticum, &lt;e1&gt;id est&lt;/e1&gt;, non selectum, &lt;e2&gt;quod epitheton ex verbo Græco&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;ἀναιρέω&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;id est&lt;/e4&gt;, eligo &lt;e5&gt;vel&lt;/e5&gt; seligo &lt;e6&gt;componitur, ni fallor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041423Z">
        <seg>c This medicine is called&lt;/e0&gt; not anaretic, that is&lt;/e1&gt;, not selective, which epithet is from the Greek verb&lt;/e2&gt; ἀναιρέω&lt;/s3&gt;, that is&lt;/e4&gt;, to choose or&lt;/e5&gt; to select is formed, if I am not mistaken.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Laudatus Echardus tomo 2 Bibliothecæ Prædicatorum pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045416Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045416Z">
        <seg>Praised be Echard, in volume 2 of the Library of Preachers, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Laudatus Echardus tomo 2 Bibliothecæ Prædicatorum pag. 285 &amp; sequentibus gesta &amp; scripta piissimi Ludovici Granatensis enumerat.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010306Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010306Z">
        <seg>Praised Echard, in volume 2 of the Library of Preachers, pages 285 and following, enumerates the deeds and writings of the most pious Louis of Granada.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Lima ab Hispanis vulgo&lt;/e0&gt; Ciudad de los reyes, &lt;e1&gt;id est&lt;/e1&gt;, Civitas regum &lt;e2&gt;nominatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041829Z">
        <seg>Lima is commonly called by the Spanish Ciudad de los Reyes, that is, City of the Kings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Mense Januario anni 1669 annuntiatio hujus beatæ Virginis Martyrologio Romano inserta est, ut ex num.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043320Z">
        <seg>In January of the year 1669 the entry of this blessed Virgin was inserted into the Roman Martyrology, as appears from no.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Quanto gaudio spirituali vir iste delibutus fuerit, dum acutam&lt;/e0&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e1&gt;coronam contrectaret, apud nos superius num.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044740Z">
        <seg>How spiritual joy this man must have been filled with while handling the sharp crown of St. Rose, as we noted above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Sunt celebres Ægypti solitudines, quas olim sancti anachoretæ incoluerunt.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044008Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044008Z">
        <seg>There are the famous deserts of Egypt, which were once inhabited by holy anchorites.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Veram &amp; authenticam&lt;/e0&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e1&gt;Limanæ effigiem hactenus frustra quæsivi&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042310Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042310Z">
        <seg>I have so far searched in vain for the true and authentic effigy of St. Rose of Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Vocem&lt;/e0&gt; pluderones &lt;e1&gt;apud scriptores Latinos &amp; Græcos frustra quæsivi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061634Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061634Z">
        <seg>I searched in vain for the word pluderones among Latin and Greek writers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Vox&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἄγαλμα&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;pure Græca est, quam necdum apud Latinos usitatam legi, &amp; quæ&lt;/e2&gt; simulacrum &lt;e3&gt;vel&lt;/e3&gt; ornamentum &lt;e4&gt;significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054720Z">
        <seg>Vox ἄγαλμα&lt;/s1&gt; is purely Greek, which I have not yet found in use among the Latins, and which&lt;/e2&gt; signifies a statue or&lt;/e3&gt; an ornament .</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Angina &lt;e0&gt;sic dicta, quod angat guttur viamque spiritui præcludat, est genus morbi acutissimi, quo fauces anguntur, ita ut ægre respiretur&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052925Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052925Z">
        <seg>Angina, so called because it constricts the throat and blocks the passage of breath, is a very acute form of disease in which the throat is constricted, so that one can barely breathe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Anteroticis, &lt;e0&gt;id est, quasi&lt;/e0&gt; redamatoriis, &lt;e1&gt;a verbo composito&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;ἀντεράω&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;e3&gt;vel&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;ἀντερῶ&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;quod significat&lt;/e5&gt; redamo &lt;e6&gt;vel&lt;/e6&gt; vicissim amo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053629Z">
        <seg>Anterotics, that is, quasi-redamatories, from the compound verb ἀντεράω&lt;/s2&gt; or&lt;/e3&gt; ἀντερῶ&lt;/s4&gt;, which means to love back or to love in return.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Anthropophagæ &lt;e0&gt;vocantur illæ nationes, quæ carnibus humanis vescuntur, ex origine Græca&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἄνθρωπος&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;id est&lt;/e2&gt;, homo, &lt;e3&gt;ac verbo&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;φάγω&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;quod est&lt;/e5&gt; comedo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060921Z">
        <seg>c Cannibals are called those nations that feed on human flesh, from the Greek origin ἄνθρωπος, that is, man, and the verb φάγω, which is to eat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Arequipa &lt;e0&gt;est urbs meridionalis Americæ in regno Peruviæ episcopalis sub metropoli Limana, quæ distat a mari 12 leucis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050749Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050749Z">
        <seg>Arequipa is a southern city of America in the kingdom of Peru, a bishopric under the metropolitan see of Lima, which is 12 leagues from the sea.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Batavi &lt;e0&gt;sunt veteris Germaniæ populi, qui nunc vulgo vocantur&lt;/e0&gt; Hollandi, &lt;e1&gt;&amp; quos biographus hoc loco designat&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060335Z">
        <seg>The Batavi are a people of ancient Germany, who are now commonly called the Dutch, and whom the biographer designates here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Bronchus &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;βρόγχος&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;est corpus quoddam cartilagineum Galeno secundum asperam arteriam, quod sic nominatur, quia maxima ex parte constat cartilaginibus, quas Græci&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;βρόγχια&lt;/s3&gt; &lt;e4&gt;appellant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043125Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043125Z">
        <seg>Bronchus (Greek βρόγχος) is, according to Galen, a certain cartilaginous body on the trachea, so named because it consists for the most part of cartilages, which the Greeks call bronchia.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Cantharides &lt;e0&gt;sunt animalia parva &amp; venenosa,ex quibus tamen præstantissima medicamenta conficiuntur, ut passim notum est, &amp; apud medicos accuratius legi potest&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041005Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041005Z">
        <seg>Cantharides are small, poisonous animals, from which, however, the most excellent medicines are made, as is commonly known, and can be read more thoroughly by physicians.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Catalepsis &lt;e0&gt;apud rhetores est figura, apud medicos est morbus, quam Gorræus in Definitionibus medicis pag. 213 sic describit:&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;Κατάληψις&lt;/s1&gt; affectus est cerebri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033632Z">
        <seg>c Catalepsy: among rhetoricians it is a figure, among physicians a disease, which Gorræus in his Medical Definitions, p. 213, describes as follows: "Catalepsy is an affection of the brain."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Collyria, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;κολλύρια&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;apud Latinos vocantur illa medicamenta, quæ oculis male affectis admoventur, ut Castellus in Lexico medico pag. 97 &amp; sequente tradit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035213Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035213Z">
        <seg>Collyria, &lt;e0&gt;Greek&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;kollýria&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;are among the Latins those medicaments which are applied to the eyes when they are afflicted, as Castellus relates in his Medical Lexicon, page 97 and following.&lt;/e2&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Garyophyllus &lt;e0&gt;idem est, quod&lt;/e0&gt; caryophyllus, &lt;e1&gt;de quo supra in Annotatis egimus&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032417Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032417Z">
        <seg>c Caryophyllus &lt;e0&gt;is the same as&lt;/e0&gt; caryophyllus, &lt;e1&gt;which we have discussed above in the Annotations&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Lybia &lt;e0&gt;vel&lt;/e0&gt; Libya, &lt;e1&gt;cum latissime sumitur, totam Africam significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013025Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013025Z">
        <seg>c Libya &lt;e0&gt;or&lt;/e0&gt; Libya, &lt;e1&gt;when taken in the broadest sense, signifies all of Africa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Orexis, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ὄρεξις&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;Latine dicitur&lt;/e2&gt; appetitus &lt;e3&gt;vel appetentia cibi &amp; potus, ab&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;ὀρέγομαὶ&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;quod est&lt;/e5&gt; appeto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040115Z">
        <seg>Orexis, &lt;e0&gt;Greek&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ὄρεξις&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;is called in Latin&lt;/e2&gt; appetite &lt;e3&gt;or the desire for food and drink, from&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;ὀρέγομαὶ&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;which means&lt;/e5&gt; I long for.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Pataco &lt;e0&gt;est species monetæ, Hispanis vulgo dicta&lt;/e0&gt; patagon &lt;e1&gt;vel&lt;/e1&gt; real de a ocho, &lt;e2&gt;quia æquivalet octo solidis argenteis, quos ipsi&lt;/e2&gt; reales de plata &lt;e3&gt;appellant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055513Z">
        <seg>Pataco is a type of coin, commonly called by the Spaniards patagon or real de a ocho, because it is equivalent to eight silver solids, which they themselves call reales de plata.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Peripetasmata &lt;e0&gt;sunt aulea sive tapetia, quæ ad parietes extenduntur ornatus gratia, &amp; hinc etiam Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;περιπετάσματα&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;vocantur, a verbo&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;περιπετάζειν&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;quod&lt;/e4&gt; extendere &lt;e5&gt;significat&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031608Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031608Z">
        <seg>Peripetasmata are curtains or tapestries, which are hung on the walls for decoration, &amp; hence they are also called by the Greeks &lt;s1&gt;peripetasmata&lt;/s1&gt;, from the verb &lt;s3&gt;peripetazein&lt;/s3&gt;, which means to stretch.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Strategus &lt;e0&gt;est proprie dux exercitus a&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;στρατὸς&lt;/s1&gt; exercitus, &lt;e2&gt;&amp; verbo&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;ἄγω&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;hoc est&lt;/e4&gt;, duco.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025813Z">
        <seg>A strategist is properly the leader of an army from the strategos of the army, by the verb ago, that is, I lead.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Symmysta &lt;e0&gt;proprie in lingua Latina est&lt;/e0&gt; consacerdos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052308Z">
        <seg>Symmysta properly in Latin is&lt;/e0&gt; a fellow priest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Xauxa &lt;e0&gt;est celeberrima totius Peruviæ vallis, quæ patet quatuordecim leucis in longitudinem, quatuor aut quinque in latitudinem, olimincolis frequens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034404Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034404Z">
        <seg>Caxa is the most famous valley in all of Peru, stretching fourteen leagues in length and four or five in width, once densely populated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>caligabat trepida, recogitans judiciorum Dei abyssum multam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061807Z">
        <seg>She trembled in fear, reflecting on the great abyss of God's judgments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>caligo, pavor, tempestas mentem obsederant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032957Z">
        <seg>A fog, fear, and turmoil had besieged the mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cancellos omnes, subsellia, pegmata, scamna confertim implevit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030948Z">
        <seg>He filled all the stalls, benches, platforms, and seats densely packed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044111Z">
        <seg>chapter</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cathedraticus theologiæ, quam primus invexit regno;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050144Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050144Z">
        <seg>a professor of theology, whom he was the first to introduce to the kingdom;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>causabatur se citra confessarii permissionem nil præsumpturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053957Z">
        <seg>She claimed that she would do nothing without the confessor's permission.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>causam, cur levi ex tactu sanguis eruperit, sciscitatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042245Z">
        <seg>He asks the reason why blood has burst forth from a slight touch.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cedo igitur pio &amp; ignito hortatui tuo, sequar invitantem ad meliora Deum, tu illum mihi propitium fac reddas, ut, quod hodie in me cœpit, perficiat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060710Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060710Z">
        <seg>I yield, therefore, to your pious and fiery exhortation; I will follow the God who invites me to better things. May you make Him propitious to me, so that He may complete what has today begun in me.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>certamina adversus dæmones, constantia in adversis, &amp; ardens Dei amor&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041901Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041901Z">
        <seg>battles against demons, steadfastness in adversity, and a burning love for God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>certe nec levissima dissimulabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053306Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053306Z">
        <seg>She certainly did not conceal even the slightest thing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>certus sum, quia neque mors, &amp;c.” Verumtamen hic protestabatur Virgo, numquam se ulli mortalium hoc dicere ausam, &amp; ne nunc quidem id dicturam fuisse, nisi coëgisset rigor præsentis examinis, &amp; si forte errasset in phrasi locutionis, quam necessitas omnia efferendi expresserat, censuram correctionis demisse flagitabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050529Z">
        <seg>"I am certain, for neither death, etc." Nevertheless, here the Virgin protested that she had never dared to say this to any mortal, nor would she have said it even now, had not the rigor of the present examination compelled her; and if she had perhaps erred in the phrasing of her words, which necessity had forced her to utter, she humbly begged for the censure of correction.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cervicali infarsit lanam, at non solam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042747Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042747Z">
        <seg>He stuffed the wool into the neck, but not by itself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cessantique foris persecutioni, agminatim corporis ægritudines succedebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052603Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052603Z">
        <seg>And as they ceased from persecution abroad, one sickness after another fell upon the body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cetera complures, qui visuri essent, digito monstravit personas, quæ illic Deo famulaturæ essent, (ac inter has matrem propriam) designavit, numerum diffinivit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062446Z">
        <seg>He then pointed out with his finger several others who were to be there to serve God (among them his own mother), and he set the number.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cetera, sive erigenda tantisper in lectulo, sive in alterum latus vertenda, manibus egebat alienis, plus condolens aliorum incommodo, quam dolens ex proprio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025606Z">
        <seg>The rest, whether to be propped up for a while in bed or turned onto the other side, required the hands of another, feeling more sympathy for the inconvenience of others than pain from his own.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>charitas delicata non est, nec proximum fastidit, memor, nos omnes ex eodem esse luto ac cœno;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061320Z">
        <seg>Charity is not delicate, nor does it despise its neighbor, remembering that we are all made of the same clay and mire.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>charæ prolis animulam mors delibavit in ulnis afflictæ genitricis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033121Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033121Z">
        <seg>A morsel of death nibbled the soul of the beloved child in the arms of its afflicted mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>chirurgi, tumidis ac liventibus indiciis territi, judicarunt, aut Virginem deinceps mancam ea parte remansuram, aut saltem curationis laborem admodum prolixum incertumque fore, periculum longe minus fuisse intercessurum, si ossa in partes diffracta crepuissent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053945Z">
        <seg>The surgeons, alarmed by the swollen and bruised indications, judged that either the Virgin would remain lame in that part, or at least the labor of the cure would be exceedingly long and uncertain, and that the danger would have been far less had the bones cracked, breaking into pieces.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>circumfusæ multitudini ultro se involvit, si quo modo ad tumulum Virginis penetraret: pervenit, filiabus aliisque viam aperientibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033235Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033235Z">
        <seg>She thrust herself into the surrounding crowd, trying by any means to reach the tomb of the Virgin; she arrived, her daughters and others clearing the way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>circumspiciebat singularum artificum marmora jam elaborata, absoluta, seposita, quorum nulli ad perfectionem exactissimam quidquam deerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044222Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044222Z">
        <seg>He surveyed the already finished, completed, and set-aside marbles of the individual artisans, in none of which anything was lacking to the most exacting perfection.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>circumstabat matrem soboles numerosa, quatuor nempe filii &amp; una filiola;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011949Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011949Z">
        <seg>The mother was surrounded by numerous offspring, namely four sons and one daughter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>citius enim novi conditorii fabrica cum suo ornatu absolvi non poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031416Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031416Z">
        <seg>For the new bakery could not be finished with its decoration any sooner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>collyriis&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; nil proficientibus, credebatur paulatim omni videndi facultate caritura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034633Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034633Z">
        <seg>With the ointments proving ineffective, it was believed that she would gradually lose all her sight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>coloris, odoris, tactus, aliarumque circumstantiarum experimenta neutiquam fallere, naturæ artisque industrias tali prodigio longe abesse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053818Z">
        <seg>that experiments of color, smell, touch, and other circumstances can in no way be deceived, and that the industries of nature and art are far from such a prodigy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>comminiscebatur patrinos numquam natos, locum, annum, ritum, omnia præter veritatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012904Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012904Z">
        <seg>He remembered godfathers never born, the place, the year, the rite, everything except the truth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>comperisses confessariorum ubique sedilia planctu &amp; gemitibus obsideri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032647Z">
        <seg>You would have found the confessionals everywhere besieged by groans and sighs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>conata tamen dissimulare quod erat, caute in secretius cubiculum se subduxit, coronam festina seposuit, cruorem quasi ludibunda abstersit, impositisque panniculis, qui sauciæ plagæ stillicidium imbiberent, caput solitis velamentis confestim operuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042235Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042235Z">
        <seg>However, attempting to conceal what she was, she cautiously withdrew to a more secluded chamber, hastily set aside her crown, wiped away the blood as if in jest, and, placing cloths to soak up the trickle from her wound, she immediately covered her head with her usual veils.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>concipitur firma in Rosæ opem fiducia, funditur brevis precatio, imponuntur ventri periclitantis allatæ particulæ, &amp; subito nullis obstetricum suppetiis dirigitur sponte in utero proles ad enixum, nascitur facilitate prosperrima, vixit, valuit, adolevit, pueroque in baptismate nomen fuit Petrus de Quixano.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035127Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035127Z">
        <seg>A firm reliance on the help of the Virgin Mary was conceived, a brief prayer was offered, the particles brought for the perilous womb were applied, and suddenly, without any obstetric assistance, the child in the womb turned itself for delivery. It was born with the greatest ease, lived, thrived, and grew up, and at its baptism the boy was named Peter de Quixano.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>concordi omnium peritorum sententiæ obstinate refragandum non esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062808Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062808Z">
        <seg>it should not be stubbornly resisted, according to the unanimous opinion of all experts;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>concurrebant ardens contemplandi studium &amp; activa gubernandi dexteritas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050132Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050132Z">
        <seg>A burning zeal for contemplation and an active skill in governing came together.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>conferebat anteriora postremis, &amp; mire invicem consonare deprehendit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053929Z">
        <seg>He compared the former with the latter and found them to harmonize wonderfully with one another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>confestim illi concentu lenissimo erumpebant in bombos, sparsique in gyros varie miscebant arguta murmura, phalange tam ordinata, ac in se flexibus aptissimis retorta, ut credidisses aut chorum aut choreas esse, quibus ratio dux præsideret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043832Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043832Z">
        <seg>Immediately they burst forth in the softest harmony into booms, and scattered in circles they wove various witty murmurs, the phalanx so orderly and twisted into itself with the most graceful curves that you would have thought it was either a chorus or a dance led by reason.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>confestim omnis dolor evanuit, infirmum somnus, qui omnino diffugerat, placide corripit, mox altum obdormiscens (Virgine interim cum comitibus domum reversa) integre sanus evigilavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061506Z">
        <seg>Immediately all pain vanished, and a gentle sleep, which had completely fled, peacefully seized the invalid; soon he fell into a deep slumber (while the Virgin, having returned home with her companions, had awakened). He awoke fully recovered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>confuso venatu turbabant omnia, nec sperari quietiora poterant, cum fœdis ac lucifugis bestiis nox ipsa locique faveret conditio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052023Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052023Z">
        <seg>The confused hunt disrupted everything, and nothing quieter could be hoped for, since the foul, light-fearing beasts were favored by night itself and the nature of the place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>conquisitum undique apud Rosæ parentes, apud quæstorem Gundisalvum, si quid suorum utensilium, pannorum, aut pauperrimæ supellectilis obtineri posset, grata memoria asservandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031211Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031211Z">
        <seg>to gather from all sources at the parents of Rosa, and from the treasurer Gundisalvus, any of her personal belongings, garments, or the poorest of furnishings, to be kept as a cherished memory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>consobrinos calamitosos jubet adducere, &amp; advocato notario, rescisso priori inofficioso testamento, gratificato Francisco, instituit parvulos consobrinos ex asse hæredes, &amp; Rosæ debitæ grates persolvuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043119Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043119Z">
        <seg>He orders the bringing in of his unfortunate cousins, and, in the presence of an attorney and a notary, having revoked the previous ineffectual will and made a gift to Francis, he names the little cousins as heirs of the estate, and the debts owed to Rosa are duly settled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>contendunt ambo, sed prior ille integris viribus Alphonso robustior, hominem circumplectitur, &amp; repugnantem abstrahit, sensimque, at molliter in pedes erigit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033356Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033356Z">
        <seg>They both struggle, but the former, in his prime and stronger than Alfonso, embraces the man, pulls him away despite his resistance, and slowly but gently lifts him to his feet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cor in varias deliberationes repente dilatatum erigebatur totum ad cælestia, quasi pulsu citatiori vociferans: “Domine quid me vis facere”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062945Z">
        <seg>His heart, suddenly expanded in various deliberations, was lifted entirely to heavenly things, as if, with a quicker pulse, crying out: "Lord, what do you want me to do?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cor turbatum quodam velut terræmotu succutiebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033000Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033000Z">
        <seg>The heart was shaken, as if by an earthquake.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>corpus ejusdem undique circumdederant rosæ candidæ atque purpureæ sine numero;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031915Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031915Z">
        <seg>Countless white and purple roses had surrounded his body on all sides.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>corrasos de Virginis sepulchro pulveres ingenti fide produxit, confessori innuit, ut eos in puram lympham excussos, infirmæ bibendos porrigat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035449Z">
        <seg>He produced the dust from the Virgin's sepulcher with great faith, signaled to the confessor to dissolve it in pure water, and to give it to the sick woman to drink.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>costasque obsiderent ligna, ne laxaretur phalanx.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042617Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042617Z">
        <seg>and they would wedge the sides with logs, so that the phalanx would not be loosened.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>crediditque discussum omnino vitæ periculum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035737Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035737Z">
        <seg>and he believed that the discussion was an absolute danger to his life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>crudele ac Deo palam ingratum fore, quidquid ulterius tentaretur ad oppugnandam Rosæ abstinentiam, a qua tam perspicue illius vita pendebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041129Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041129Z">
        <seg>It would be cruel and manifestly ungrateful to God to attempt anything further to assail the fast of Rose, from which his life so clearly depended.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cui Rosa ornamentum, ex tractabili ficulneæ medulla, complusculos cælestes genios ordine aptissimo circumposuit, &amp; crucis basi accubuam sanctæ Mariæ Magdalenæ icunculam perite adjecit, sicque renatam, ornatam, pampinatam remisit confessarius proreginæ, miranti, libanotim suam sub Rosæ manibus tam celeriter, tam amœne, tam plene revixisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055005Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055005Z">
        <seg>to whom Rosa, an ornament crafted from the pliable pith of a fig tree, had most fittingly arranged a number of heavenly genii in a circle, and had skillfully added a small image of Saint Mary Magdalene reclining at the base of the cross, thus the queen's confessor returned her, reborn, adorned, and flourishing, to the astonished regent, her own Lebanon had so quickly, so charmingly, so fully come back to life under Rose's hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cui Rosa: “Vix (inquit) in oratorio ad preces coram sacra imagine me composueram, quando attractu prævalido sensi, redire ad sua loca distortos nervos, detumescere pulpos&lt;a0&gt;*&lt;/a0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;c&lt;/a1&gt;, distringi musculos, nec tamen prodire statim volui, donec cælesti medico grates ex debito persolvissem: in his nonnihil me distinui: tolle jam gossipium, tolle fascias, quia in tuto sunt omnia”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011026Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011026Z">
        <seg>to whom Rosa said: "I had barely composed myself in the oratory for prayer before the sacred image, when by a powerful attraction I felt my nerves, which had been twisted, return to their places, my fleshly parts* swell, my muscles contract, yet I did not wish to come out at once, until I had duly offered thanks to the heavenly physician: in these I distinguished myself somewhat: now take away the gossamer, take away the bandages, for everything is safe."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cui Rosa: “Vix (inquit) in oratorio ad preces coram sacra imagine me composueram, quando attractu prævalido sensi, redire ad sua loca distortos nervos, detumescere pulpos&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;*&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;c&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, distringi musculos, nec tamen prodire statim volui, donec cælesti medico grates ex debito persolvissem: in his nonnihil me distinui: tolle jam gossipium, tolle fascias, quia in tuto sunt omnia”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054022Z">
        <seg>to whom Rosa said: "I had scarcely (she said) composed myself in the oratory for prayer before the sacred image, when by a powerful attraction I felt the twisted nerves return to their places, the flesh swell&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;**2* I was disfigured, my muscles strained, and yet I did not immediately want to come out, until I had duly offered thanks to the heavenly physician: in this I somewhat delayed myself. Now remove the gossamer, take off the bandages, for everything is safe."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cui beneficio accessit, quod exinde numquam in posterum arthritis manum illam invasit: quod mirum non fuit ipsi Rosæ, quæ ab ipsa, ceu a matre, pene quotidiana ex mutuis consortiis solatia hauriebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042257Z">
        <seg>To this blessing was added the fact that from that time on arthritis never again afflicted that hand; which was no surprise to Rosa herself, who from it, as from a mother, drew almost daily comfort from their mutual companionship.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cui bono tam ineptæ præstigiæ, si libuit matris oculos deludere, at ludere cum propriis oculis quale nefas, quantum periculum?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043426Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043426Z">
        <seg>What good is such a foolish trick, if it pleased you to deceive your mother's eyes? But to play with your own eyes—what a crime, what a danger?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cui mox frater: “Hem (inquit) soror mea, si te deturpati capillitii tui injuria tam serio affligit, scito, cultos puellarum cincinnos inferni rudentes esse, quibus illaqueantur juvenum incautæ animæ, ut in gehennam æternam detrahantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034953Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034953Z">
        <seg>To whom his brother soon said, "Behold, my sister, if the offense of your disfigured hair afflicts you so seriously, know that the styled ringlets of maidens are the snares of hell, by which the unwary souls of young men are entangled and dragged down into eternal gehenna."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cui nomen, &lt;b0&gt;Gratia consummata.&lt;/b0&gt; Hinc illa inter acerbissimos cruciatus placida ac suavis animæ quies: hinc illud fiduciæ ac spei infractum robur, quod multos, ac præcipue spirituales suos patres abripuit in stuporem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025634Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025634Z">
        <seg>whose name is, &lt;b0&gt;Grace perfected.&lt;/b0&gt; Hence that placid and sweet rest of the soul amid the most bitter torments; hence that unshaken strength of confidence and hope, which snatched many, and especially its spiritual fathers, away into astonishment;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cujus sudoris vestigia per aliquot lustra conspicua mansere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042139Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042139Z">
        <seg>whose traces of sweat remained visible for several centuries.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cum Mariana ancilla e vestigio Filiam secuta, de vulnere interrogat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042242Z">
        <seg>When Mariana, the maid, followed the Daughter from the track, she asked about the wound.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cum ecce Antonius, qui integro duarum horarum spatio algidus, pallidus, immobilis jacuerat, alto cum suspirio (quale admodum lassorum esse solet) oculos aperuit, circumspexit, ac miratus heram illo noctis incommodo tempore sibi adstantem, interrogavit, quid rei ageretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033223Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033223Z">
        <seg>Then, behold, Antony, who for a full two hours had lain frozen, pale, and motionless, opened his eyes with a deep sigh (such as the very weary are wont to make), looked around, and, surprised to see his master standing there at that inconvenient hour of the night, asked what was going on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cum ecce repentina totius corporis lassitudo, mox tremor &amp; deliquium prostravit miseram, subitumque pectori ac pulmonibus asthma incubuit, pressit, ac respirandi facultatem interclusit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041048Z">
        <seg>when suddenly a total bodily weakness, soon followed by a tremor and fainting, struck the poor woman down, and a sudden asthma descended upon her chest and lungs, pressing down and cutting off her ability to breathe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cum ex iis papilionibus unus, solo nigro alboque colore pulcherrime striatus, leni, sed minime fortuito, allapsu Virginem festive circumvolitavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040156Z">
        <seg>when one of those butterflies, most beautifully striped in black and white, fluttered playfully around the Virgin with a gentle, yet by no means accidental, flight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cum inferne sat prohiberent subjecti asseres, nequa excideret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042613Z">
        <seg>since the subjects below were firmly holding the beams, lest anything fall out;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cum saxo rediens Mariana audit sonoro crepitu seram sponte dissilientem, laxatur sensim catena, deducitur, abstrahitur tota;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041739Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041739Z">
        <seg>Returning with the rock, Mariana hears the gate with a loud creak spring open of its own accord; the chain slowly slackens, she is drawn out, carried off whole.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cum sciret, vicissim in ejus virgineo corculo se solum tranquillissime habitare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051251Z">
        <seg>Knowing that, in turn, he alone dwelt most peacefully in her maidenly heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cum totus præter naturam esset, ac aforis immissus, via multiplici undequaque a peripheria ad centrum tenderet, mutuisque occursibus alii alios paroxysmi summa affectionum varietate intersecarent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013452Z">
        <seg>since the whole was unnatural and filled with orifices, with a multiplicity of paths leading from the periphery to the center, and the mutual encounters of the others intersected one another with the greatest variety of paroxysms of feeling;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cumque Laurentio novis ardebat ignibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040227Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040227Z">
        <seg>and burned with new fires for Laurentio;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cumque accedentes angeli pararent libram attollere, rursum intercessit Christus, velut omnipotenti suo brachio dignam functionem accuratius peracturus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052804Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052804Z">
        <seg>and as the angels drew near to lift the balance, Christ interceded again, as though to more accurately perform the worthy function with his own omnipotent arm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cumque de more sub seram noctem pallidula inde prodiisset, quæstori &amp; conjugi subtrepide fassa est, minimum abfuisse, quin exiguum id, quod ex avicula gustaverat, morte lueret, præ dira pectoris obstructione, quæ miseram prope suffocarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041119Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041119Z">
        <seg>And when, as was her custom, she came out a little pale late in the evening, she told her quæstor and husband in a trembling voice that she had come very near dying; for from the small amount she had tasted of the bird, she had suffered a dreadful obstruction of the chest that had nearly suffocated her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cumque nec liquidorum infusione, nec valida succussione dorsi, quidquam dimoveretur bolus, semianimem refectorio, extractum hortati sunt ad edenda pœnitentiæ signa, ut sacramentalem absolutionem reciperet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035644Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035644Z">
        <seg>And since neither the infusion of liquids nor a vigorous percussion of the back could dislodge the bolus, they urged the half-conscious man to take the viaticum, so that he might receive sacramental absolution.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cumque nihil aut olidum aut oleaceum posset deprehendere, lustratis diligenter omnibus, edixit, supernaturale sibi omnino videri, quod in imagine gerebatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053815Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053815Z">
        <seg>And since he could detect nothing either foul or oily, having carefully examined everything, he declared that what was taking place in the image seemed entirely supernatural to him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cumque post sesquihoram interrogaretur a matre, num jacendo lassus erigi vellet, ac abduci, petiit adhuc paulisper ibi relinqui.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033521Z">
        <seg>And when, after an hour and a half, his mother asked him if, being tired of lying down, he wanted to get up and be taken away, he asked to be left there for just a little while longer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cumque tam serio ista &amp; sentiret &amp; diceret, ferre non poterat, quod ejus innocentiæ gnari talia credere recusarent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040546Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040546Z">
        <seg>And though he felt and said these things so earnestly, he could not bear that those who knew his innocence would refuse to believe such things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cunctisque unum fuit stuporis argumentum, illic se videre congregatos, quo nullus convocarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030555Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030555Z">
        <seg>There was one cause of astonishment for everyone: to see themselves gathered there, where no one had summoned them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cupiebat sane dissolvi, &amp; esse cum Christo: unde, captata occasione, dum semel quæstoris conjugi loquebatur, adstante Rosa, &amp; utriusque orationibus humiliter &amp; enixe commendabat suum ab hac vita transitum, velut ante proximi anni Calendas indubie futurum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012222Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012222Z">
        <seg>He truly desired to be dissolved and be with Christ; so, seizing the opportunity, while speaking once to his wife, the quaestor's wife, with Rosa present, he humbly and earnestly commended his passing from this life, as if it were to occur without a doubt before the Kalends of the coming year.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cupiebat voluntas amare, sed congelata obtorpuerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045512Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045512Z">
        <seg>The will to love desired, but it had grown numb and frozen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cæcutiebat mens confusa, oberrabat, palpabat, impingebat orbata lumine;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045627Z">
        <seg>The mind, blinded, was confused, wandering, groping, and bumping about, deprived of light.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Constitutio Urbani VIII hic indicata legitur in Bullario Romano Laërtii Cherubini tomo 5 pag. 283 &amp; sequentibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031810Z">
        <seg>The Constitution of Urban VIII, indicated here, is found in the Bullarium Romanum of Laërtii Cherubini, volume 5, page 283 and following.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Cum festum S. Bartholomæi in quibusdam locis die 24, in aliis die 25 Augusti celebretur, nondum scio, an Limæ istud ejusdem Apostoli pervigilium in diem 23, an 24 Augusti inciderit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030449Z">
        <seg>Since the feast of St. Bartholomew is celebrated in some places on August 24, and in others on August 25, I do not yet know whether in Lima the vigil of that same Apostle fell on August 23 or 24.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Designatur Dominica in Albis, cujus Missa inchoatur his verbis:&lt;/e0&gt; Quasi modo geniti infantes &lt;e1&gt;&amp;c.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054726Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054726Z">
        <seg>Designated as Low Sunday, whose Mass begins with these words: As newborn infants &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Honorius Philoponus ex Ordine S. Benedicti, sicut superius ad caput decimum nonum Vitæ annotavi, multis argumentis contra alios Ordines religiosos contendit, quemdam Benedictinum cognomento Buil, natione Catalanum &amp; antea abbatem Montis Serrati, primo cum duodecim Ordinis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041417Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041417Z">
        <seg>Honorius of Philoponus of the Order of St. Benedict, as I noted above at chapter nineteen of the Vita, contended with many arguments against other religious Orders, a certain Benedictine surnamed Buil, a Catalan by nation and formerly abbot of Montserrat, first with twelve of the Order;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;In Commentario prævio ad Vitam S. Dominici tomo 1 Augusti pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045422Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045422Z">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;In the Preliminary Commentary to the Life of St. Dominic, Volume 1, August, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;In Commentario prævio ad Vitam S. Dominici tomo 1 Augusti pag. 423 &amp; sequentibusfuse disputavimus, quis fuerit primus Rosarii institutor.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010310Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010310Z">
        <seg>In the preliminary commentary to the Life of St. Dominic, Volume 1, August, page 423 and following, we have discussed at length who was the first institutor of the Rosary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Joannes Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034410Z">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;John Latius, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Majores nostri ad diem 20 Aprilis Acta hujus sanctæ Agnetis illustrarunt, ubi tomo secundo istius mensis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035114Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035114Z">
        <seg>Our ancestors illustrated the Acts of this holy Agnes on April 20, in the second volume of that month, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Majores nostri ad diem 20 Aprilis Acta hujus sanctæ Agnetis illustrarunt, ubi tomo secundo istius mensis pag. 796 miraculum de floribus in loco orationis ejus cælitus inventis refertur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000834Z">
        <seg>Our ancestors, on April 20, illustrated the Acts of this holy Agnes, where in the second volume of that month, page 796, a miracle concerning flowers found from heaven at the site of her prayer is recounted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Omnia pretiosa in supellectile vel vestitu dicta sunt&lt;/e0&gt; Attalica, &lt;e1&gt;ab Attalo Pergami rege, qui pecunia &amp; splendida supellectile abundabat, ut Plinius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055520Z">
        <seg>All things precious in furniture or clothing are called Attalic, from Attalus, king of Pergamon, who abounded in money and splendid furniture, as Pliny says.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Plerumque vocatur&lt;/e0&gt; erysipelas &lt;e1&gt;in genitivo&lt;/e1&gt; erysipelatis, &lt;e2&gt;estque neutrius generis, etsi biographushoc loco vocem illam aliter inflectat, &amp; alibi tamen communem hujus vocis inflexionem sequatur, ut postea videbimus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035220Z">
        <seg>It is most often called erysipelas, in the genitive erysipelatis, and is of neuter gender, although the biographer in this passage inflects that word differently, yet elsewhere follows the common inflection of this word, as we shall see later.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Quamvis hic Pontifex exordium Rosarii Sancto Dominico tribuat, tamen aliqui pii doctique scriptores Catholici putant, hanc institutionem antiquiorem vel recentiorem esse, sicut in Commentario prævio ad Acta S. Dominici&lt;/e0&gt; § 19 &lt;e1&gt;&amp; duobus sequentibus retulimus: nam Pontifices Romani pure historicas hujusmodi opiniones asserunt aut negant eadem fide, qua illas ab aliis acceperunt.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041845Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041845Z">
        <seg>Although this Pope attributes the origin of the Rosary to Saint Dominic, some pious and learned Catholic writers think that this institution is either older or more recent, as in the Preface to the Acts of St. Dominic" § 19 &amp; two following, we have related: for the Roman Pontiffs assert or deny such purely historical opinions with the same faith with which they received them from others.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Significationem hujus appellationis in variis lexicis frustra quæsivi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052314Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052314Z">
        <seg>I searched in vain for the meaning of this appellation in various dictionaries.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Aphorismus, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἀφορισμὸς&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;est sententia, quæ omnes rei proprietates brevissimis verbis complectitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060924Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060924Z">
        <seg>An aphorism, from the Greek ἀφορισμὸς, is a statement that embraces all the properties of a thing in the briefest terms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Archithalassus, &lt;e0&gt;id est&lt;/e0&gt;, princeps maris, &lt;e1&gt;quod nomen ex&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;ἀρκὴ&lt;/s2&gt; principatus, &lt;e3&gt;&amp;&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;θάλασσα&lt;/s4&gt; mare &lt;e5&gt;compositum est, &amp; quo hic præfectus illius classis indicatur&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060338Z">
        <seg>d Archithalassus, that is&lt;/e0&gt;, prince of the sea, a name composed of&lt;/e1&gt; ἀρκὴ&lt;/s2&gt; (principality) and&lt;/e3&gt; θάλασσα&lt;/s4&gt; (sea), and by which the prefect of that fleet is designated&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Cardialgia &lt;e0&gt;generatim est&lt;/e0&gt; cordis dolor, &lt;e1&gt;de quo Gorræus ita scribit:&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;Καρδιαλγία&lt;/s2&gt; est dolor &amp; morsus oris ventriculi ab humoris acrimonia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041008Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041008Z">
        <seg>Cardialgia is generally heart pain, about which Gorræus writes as follows: "Cardiálgia is the pain and biting at the opening of the ventricle from the acrimony of the humor."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Catapotia &lt;e0&gt;proprie sunt&lt;/e0&gt; pilulæ &lt;e1&gt;a nomine Græco&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;κατάποσις&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;id est&lt;/e3&gt;, deglutitio: &lt;e4&gt;teste enim Gorræo in Definitionibus medicis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045940Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045940Z">
        <seg>Catapotia are properly pills from the Greek name kataposis, that is, deglutition: as Gorræus testifies in his Medical Definitions, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Catapotia &lt;e0&gt;proprie sunt&lt;/e0&gt; pilulæ &lt;e1&gt;a nomine Græco&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;κατάποσις&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;id est&lt;/e3&gt;, deglutitio: &lt;e4&gt;teste enim Gorræo in Definitionibus medicis pag. 215, est medicamentum, quod non diluitur, sed ita, ut est, devoratur aut deglutitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010403Z">
        <seg>Catapotia are properly pills from the Greek name κατάποσις, that is, deglutition: For, as Gorræus states in his Medical Definitions, page 215, it is a medicine that is not dissolved, but is swallowed or devoured as it is.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Critirion, &lt;e0&gt;vel&lt;/e0&gt; criterion, &lt;e1&gt;Græce&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;κριτήριον&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;aliquando significat tribunal judicum vel controversiam a judicibus dirimendam, a verbo&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;κρίνω&lt;/s4&gt; &lt;e5&gt;quod est&lt;/e5&gt; judico.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025820Z">
        <seg>Critirion, or criterion, from the Greek kriterion, sometimes means a tribunal of judges or a controversy to be settled by judges, from the verb krino, which means to judge.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Cusco &lt;e0&gt;erat urbs primaria Americæ ante adventum Hispanorum, &amp; sedes regum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050755Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050755Z">
        <seg>Cusco was the primary city of the Americas before the arrival of the Spaniards, and the seat of the kings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Cynanche, (&lt;e0&gt;scribitur a Græcis&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;κυνάγχη&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;&amp; interdum&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;συνάγχη&lt;/s3&gt;) &lt;e4&gt;differt ab angina in quibusdam circumstantiis, quas curiosus hujusmodi rerum lector apud Gorræum in Definitionibus medicis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052928Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052928Z">
        <seg>d Cynanche, (&lt;e0&gt;written by the Greeks&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;κυνάγχη&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;&amp; sometimes&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;συνάγχη&lt;/s3&gt;) &lt;e4&gt;differs from angina in certain circumstances, which the curious reader of such matters may find in Gorræus in his Medical Definitions, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Cynanche, (&lt;e0&gt;scribitur a Græcis&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;κυνάγχη&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;&amp; interdum&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;συνάγχη&lt;/s3&gt;) &lt;e4&gt;differt ab angina in quibusdam circumstantiis, quas curiosus hujusmodi rerum lector apud Gorræum in Definitionibus medicis pag. 253 &amp; sequente reperiet&lt;/e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010843Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010843Z">
        <seg>d Cynanche, (&lt;e0&gt;written by the Greeks&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;κυνάγχη&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;&amp; sometimes&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;συνάγχη&lt;/s3&gt;) &lt;e4&gt;differs from angina in certain circumstances, which the curious reader of such matters will find in Gorræus, in his Medical Definitions, page 253 and following&lt;/e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Dyspepsia, &lt;e0&gt;quam Galli&lt;/e0&gt; indigestion &lt;e1&gt;vernaculedicunt, a nobis Latine describi posset&lt;/e1&gt; depravata alimenti concoctio, &lt;e2&gt;quam Celsus&lt;/e2&gt; cruditatem &lt;e3&gt;appellat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040122Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040122Z">
        <seg>Dyspepsia, which the French call indigestion, could be described in Latin by us as a depraved concoction of food, which Celsus calls crudity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Enthusiasmus &lt;e0&gt;proprie est divinus quidam vigor, qualem habent dicta aut scripta eorum, qui divino numine sunt afflati, ab&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἐνθουσιάζω&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;quod significat&lt;/e2&gt; numine afflor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032420Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032420Z">
        <seg>Enthusiasm is properly a certain divine vigor, such as is found in the sayings or writings of those who are inspired by divine spirit, from &lt;s1&gt;ἐνθουσιάζω&lt;/s1&gt;, which means to be inspired by spirit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Guamanga &lt;e0&gt;est episcopalis civitas Americæ meridionalis inter Limam ad occasum &amp; Cuscum ad ortum sita, &amp; nonaginta leucis utrimque distans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053632Z">
        <seg>Guamanga is a episcopal city of South America, situated between Lima to the west and Cusco to the east, and ninety leagues distant from each.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Hernia, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;κήλη&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;quam vulgus&lt;/e2&gt; rupturam &lt;e3&gt;vocat, est morbus scroti intumescentis, aut proprie sic appellatur, cum corpus aliquod in scrotum deciderit, erupto peritonæo, cujus ultimæ vocis explicationem jam dabimus&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033654Z">
        <seg>A hernia, from the Greek *kēle*, which the common people call a rupture, is a swelling of the scrotum, or more properly so called, when a part of the body has fallen into the scrotum through a rupture of the peritoneum, the explanation of which last word we shall now give.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Isthmus Panamæ &lt;e0&gt;est tractus parvus Americæ octodecim leucarum, quo America Septemtrionalis conjungitur Meridionali in provincia Terræ firmæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013034Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013034Z">
        <seg>The Isthmus of Panama is a small eighteen-league stretch of America, by which North America is connected to South America in the province of Tierra Firme.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Mulati &lt;e0&gt;ab Hispanis vocantur ii, qui ex parentibus Africanis &amp; Indis mixtim nati sunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061640Z">
        <seg>Mulattoes are those who are born of mixed African and Indian parents.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Myrothecium &lt;e0&gt;est&lt;/e0&gt; unguentorum repositorium, &lt;e1&gt;quo nomine Græco etiam metaphorice usus est Cicero, dum in epistola ad Atticum scribit, se in commentario consulatus sui totum Isocratis myrothecium consumpsisse&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033037Z">
        <seg>Myrothecium is a repository of ointments, a name which Cicero also used metaphorically in his letter to Atticus, when he wrote that he had consumed the entire myrothecium of Isocrates in his commentary on his consulship.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Ozimum &lt;e0&gt;est planta jam passim per Europamnota, de qua Calepinus in Dictionario octolingui sic scribit:&lt;/e0&gt; Ozimum item regia herba sive basilica, aut basilicum a Liguribus vocata, fragrantissimi &amp; vehementissimi odoris, unde nomen accepit, nempe a verbo &lt;s1&gt;ὄζω&lt;/s1&gt;, quod est redoleo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051530Z">
        <seg>Ozimum is a plant now common throughout Europe, about which Calepinus in his Octilingual Dictionary writes as follows: "Ozimum, also called the royal herb or basilica, or basilicum as the Ligurians call it, has a most fragrant and vehement odor, from which it received its name, namely from the verb ὄζω, which means 'to smell.'"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Paraphernalia &lt;e0&gt;vel&lt;/e0&gt; parapherna &lt;e1&gt;in jure dicuntur ea, quæ sibi propria habet uxor, a&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;παρὰ&lt;/s2&gt; præter, &lt;e3&gt;&amp;&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;φέρνη&lt;/s4&gt; dos, &lt;e5&gt;quasi diceres&lt;/e5&gt; præter-dotalia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062359Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062359Z">
        <seg>Paraphernalia, or parapherna, are in law those things which a wife holds as her own, from *pará* (beside) and *phérne* (dowry), as if one were to say, "things beside the dowry."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Polygonus, &lt;e0&gt;id est&lt;/e0&gt;, multos angulos habens &lt;e1&gt;ab adjectivo Græco&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;πολύγωνος&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;quod componitur ex adverbio&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;πολὺ&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;id est&lt;/e5&gt;, multum, &lt;e6&gt;&amp; voce&lt;/e6&gt; &lt;s7&gt;γωνία&lt;/s7&gt;, &lt;e8&gt;quæ&lt;/e8&gt; angulum &lt;e9&gt;significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043131Z">
        <seg>from Polygonus, that is, having many angles from the Greek adjective polygonous, which is composed of the adverb πολὺ, that is, much, and the word γωνία, which means angle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Pæan &lt;e0&gt;est carmen, quod in signum victoriæ plerumque canitur, ut colligimus ex hoc hemistichio Virgiliano&lt;/e0&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040438Z">
        <seg>A paean is a song, which is usually sung as a sign of victory, as we gather from this hemistich of Virgil.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Suessa &lt;e0&gt;est urbs episcopalis Italiæ sub archiepiscopo Capuano sita in regno Neapolitano, ubi hic processus institutus est&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043326Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043326Z">
        <seg>Sessa is a bishopric city of Italy, situated in the Kingdom of Naples under the Archbishop of Capua, where this process was instituted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Æsculapius &lt;e0&gt;apud veteres erat deus sanitatis, aut medicinæ, de quo poëtæ multa fabulantur, quæ non sunt hujus loci&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041433Z">
        <seg>d. Asclepius was, among the ancients, a god of health or medicine, about whom the poets tell many fables, which are not relevant here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>de Lorenzana, qui adstans eidem jam moribundæ, dum pro extremo viatico sacram Hostiam in ecstasi sensim deglutiebat, hæc pauca subjecit: &lt;s2&gt;[omnes in se effectus hujus cælestis Cibi,]&lt;/s2&gt; “Filia, nunc fruere Sponso tuo, ac sola cum solo deliciare, quia vere dulcissimus est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055916Z">
        <seg>de Lorenzana, who was present with the same one, now dying, while she was slowly swallowing the sacred Host as her last viaticum in ecstasy, added these few words: "Daughter, now enjoy your Spouse, and delight alone with the Alone, for He is truly most sweet;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>de Lorenzana, qui adstans eidem jam moribundæ, dum pro extremo viatico sacram Hostiam in ecstasi sensim deglutiebat, hæc pauca subjecit: [omnes in se effectus hujus cælestis Cibi,] “Filia, nunc fruere Sponso tuo, ac sola cum solo deliciare, quia vere dulcissimus est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011342Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011342Z">
        <seg>de Lorenzana, who was present with the same woman as she was dying, while she was slowly swallowing the sacred Host as her last viaticum, said these few words: [all the effects of this heavenly Food in her,] "Daughter, now enjoy your Spouse, and delight alone with the Alone, for He is truly most sweet;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>de Rocca duodennis erat filiola, Maria nomine;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040745Z">
        <seg>Rocca had a twelve-year-old daughter named Maria.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>de ea nunc plura se nolle dicere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012005Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012005Z">
        <seg>He says he now doesn't want to say more about it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>de pretiosi illius pignoris in locum celebriorem translatione jam serio tandem esse cogitandum, Rosam communem Limensium thesaurum, piorum solatium, afflictorum remedium publico deberi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031348Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031348Z">
        <seg>that the transfer of that precious pledge to a more celebrated place must now be seriously and finally considered, and that the common Rose of the Limeños—treasure, solace of the devout, remedy for the afflicted—ought to be dedicated to the public.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>debilis enim, languens, &amp; sæpe ægrotans, ac orationibus, &amp; aliis exercitiis detenta, aliarum artificum opera &amp; celeritate &amp; perfectione quam longe superabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041222Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041222Z">
        <seg>For she was weak, languishing, and often ill, and detained by prayers and other exercises, she far surpassed the works of other artists in both speed and perfection.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>deficientibus enim ad suæ fidei martyrium tyrannis, &amp; tortoribus, se ipsa fortior, utriusque in se explevit ministerium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041523Z">
        <seg>For when tyrants and torturers failed to force her to renounce her faith, she, stronger on her own, carried out the duties of both upon herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dehinc spe plenus Alexandrum rogat, nova ultimaque diligentia scriniolum oculis undequaque pervadat: respondet iste, operam perdi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034013Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034013Z">
        <seg>Then, full of hope, he asks Alexander to examine the little box with the utmost care, scanning it from every angle with his eyes. Alexander replies that it is a waste of time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>deinceps (ut ego) immunis eris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043931Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043931Z">
        <seg>From then on, you will be immune (like me).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>deinde &amp; temporis spatium, quo dormiret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051316Z">
        <seg>and then a space of time in which to sleep;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>deinde continuæ febris caustica&lt;a2&gt;h&lt;/a2&gt; intemperies reliquorum morborum confudit distinctionem, tamque potenti, varia, &amp; concumulata uredine opus fuit, ut unica hæc Rosa fatali demum marcori succumberet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025618Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025618Z">
        <seg>Then the continuous caustic fever&lt;a2&gt;h&lt;/a2&gt; confounded the distinction of the other diseases, and such a potent, varied, and cumulative rust was required that only this single fatal Rose would finally succumb to the blight;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>deinde frequentius, at brevius, semper tamen cum sonoro pœnitudinis intimæ singultu &amp; lachrymis se accusans;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025916Z">
        <seg>then more frequently, but more briefly, always, however, with a loud sob of deep remorse and tears accusing himself;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>deinde in altero oculo miratæ sunt claritatem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034646Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034646Z">
        <seg>Then they gazed in wonder at the brightness of the other eye.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>deinde provinciæ S. Joannis Baptistæ Vicarius generalis, tandem provincialis &amp; visitator;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050154Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050154Z">
        <seg>then Vicar General of the province of St. John the Baptist, and finally provincial and visitor;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>deinde virgini Rosæ se commendans, totus convaluit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032148Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032148Z">
        <seg>Then, commending himself to the virgin Rose, he recovered completely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>demum &amp; ecclesiarum prælati, præfecti regularium, senatores, aliique celsioris notæ privatis suffragiis in eam convenere sententiam: constantibus tam accolarum, quam incolarum votis condescendendum aliquando fore;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031345Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031345Z">
        <seg>Finally, the prelates of the churches, the superiors of the regulars, the senators, and other men of higher rank, by private ballot, came to this conclusion: that it would be necessary at some point to yield to the unanimous wishes of both the neighbors and the inhabitants.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>demum eadem hora totum illud multiplicium morborum collegium cessit Roseo pulveri, feminamque reliquit valentem &amp; perfecte sanam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035555Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035555Z">
        <seg>Finally, at that very hour, the whole host of those multiple diseases yielded to the powder of Rosea, leaving the woman strong and perfectly healthy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>demum objurgare, percellere, cædere innocentem, quod rudis, incompta, &amp; incomposita supino ac nimio mundi muliebris neglectu abiret a seculi genio, quod infra coæqualium sortem vilissima singularitate descenderet, quod spem opulenti splendidique matrimonii, quo ipsam natura formæque præstantia vocabat, ultro hypocrisi abjectissima corrumperet, sordideque præfocaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035415Z">
        <seg>finally to rebuke, to terrify, to slaughter the innocent, because she was crude, uncouth, and unkempt, departing from the spirit of the age with a wanton and excessive neglect of feminine adornment; because she descended to the vilest singularity, below the lot of her peers; because she would of her own accord corrupt the hope of a wealthy and splendid marriage, to which nature and the excellence of her form were calling her, with the most abject hypocrisy, and sordidly stifled it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique ad episcopalem mitram assumptus in Paraguay&lt;a0&gt;e&lt;/a0&gt;, satur vitæ, in Domino obdormivit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032151Z">
        <seg>Finally, having been raised to the episcopal mitre in Paraguay, he, full of days, fell asleep in the Lord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique compererunt, hinc cæcitatem, inde visus debilitatem prorsus ablatam: parvula enim acie utrimque firma &amp; irretorta sudum exinde aërem lucemque intuebatur cum voluptate.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034649Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034649Z">
        <seg>Finally they discovered that blindness on one side and a total loss of sight on the other had been completely removed: for with a small, firm, and unclouded field of vision on both sides he now looked upon the air and light with pleasure.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique creditur ex eo pulvillo esse, in quo nenti Virgini pusillus Jesulus aliquando familiariter solebat assidere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035158Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035158Z">
        <seg>Finally, it is believed to be from the pillow on which the little Jesus once familiarly used to sit as a child with the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique excusante Rosa, &amp; nequidquam deprecante, suspicacior vittam solvi, caput nudari imperat: quod ut vidit in orbem undique immaniter dispunctum, facile conjecit, unde hæc signa tam cruento inessent zodiaco.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042248Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042248Z">
        <seg>Finally, with Rosa excusing herself and pleading in vain, the more suspicious one ordered her to loosen her veil and bare her head. When he saw it, gaping in a monstrous circle on every side, he easily guessed whence these marks so bloody were in the zodiac.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique finge animo, quidquid obstaculorum potes concipere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062641Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062641Z">
        <seg>Finally, imagine whatever obstacles you can conceive of.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique fossa obturatur, &amp; cocti lateres, tenaci calce revincti, insternuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031156Z">
        <seg>Finally, the trench is sealed, and the baked bricks, bound with strong mortar, are laid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique in tanti sudoris mercedem ac solatium sat fore, si vel unicum apud infideles infantem per Baptismi januam ad superos transmitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060415Z">
        <seg>Finally, it would be enough reward and consolation for so much sweat, if he were to send even a single infant among the infidels to the upper world through the gate of Baptism.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique in utriusque figuræ labiis, genis, oculis quoddam velut arcanæ affabilitatis horologium inesse, signorumque congruam diversitatem nulla linguarum peritia explicabilem, humana tamen locutione longe certiorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054403Z">
        <seg>Finally, on the lips, cheeks, and eyes of both figures there seemed to be a kind of clock of mysterious affability, and a corresponding diversity of signs, inexplicable by any knowledge of languages, yet far more certain than human speech.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique miro Dei beneficio rediisse ad cor, jamque in dicta civitate, resumpto sacro schemate, sub religiosa clausura inter sanctimoniales degere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053418Z">
        <seg>Finally, by a marvelous benefit of God, having returned to her heart, she now lives in the aforementioned city, having resumed her sacred habit and dwelling in religious enclosure among the nuns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique omne aliud dissertationis argumentum, quantumvis diversissimum, comi dexteritate huc inflectere, ut amor &amp; amabilitas Dei in thema reliquorum discursuum succederet, quo obtento, mirabile erat audire Virginem, alias taciturnam, verbis aptissimis, at vehementer ignitis, ratiocinantem de debito charitatis, de affabili bonitate Dei dignantis amari, de pulchritudine majestatis tam dignæ amari: tantum hic Rosæ non deerant vocabula emphatica&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010905Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010905Z">
        <seg>finally, she would skillfully bend every other argument of the dissertation, however diverse, to this point, so that the love and loveliness of God might become the theme of the remaining discourses. Once this was achieved, it was marvelous to hear the Virgin, otherwise taciturn, reasoning with the most fitting yet fervently ignited words about the debt of charity, about the affable goodness of God worthy of being loved, of the beauty of a majesty so worthy of being loved: it was only that emphatic words did not fail Rose here;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique omne aliud dissertationis argumentum, quantumvis diversissimum, comi dexteritate huc inflectere, ut amor &amp; amabilitas Dei in thema reliquorum discursuum succederet, quo obtento, mirabile erat audire Virginem, alias taciturnam, verbis aptissimis, at vehementer ignitis, ratiocinantem de debito charitatis, de affabili bonitate Dei dignantis amari, de pulchritudine majestatis tam dignæ amari: tantum hic Rosæ non deerant vocabula emphatica&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053204Z">
        <seg>finally, to bend every other argument of the dissertation, however diverse, by graceful dexterity to this point, so that the love and loveliness of God might become the theme of the remaining discourses; which once achieved, it was marvelous to hear the Virgin, otherwise taciturn, reasoning with the most fitting, yet vehemently fiery, words about the debt of charity, about the affable goodness of God worthy to be loved, of the beauty of a majesty so worthy of being loved: it was only that here the Rose did not lack emphatic words&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique ordinato agmine tunc immigrarunt alta Dei æstimatio, filialis timor Numinis, cura salutis, abominatio peccati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035009Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035009Z">
        <seg>Finally, with an orderly procession, they then immigrated: a high estimation of God, filial fear of the Deity, concern for salvation, and the abhorrence of sin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique rationi, non contumaciæ in his parendum esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062814Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062814Z">
        <seg>Finally, that these are to be obeyed for the sake of reason, not out of stubbornness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique sciebat, inde se neutiquam surrecturam absque omnium membrorum gelido stupore, ac velut ex ossifragio, coxis, humeris, cruribus, brachiis miserrime divaricatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042642Z">
        <seg>for she knew that she would by no means rise from it without a frozen stupor in all her limbs, and as if from a fracture of the hips, shoulders, legs, and arms, miserably torn apart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique sinerent eam, quæ nil boni ageret, saltem mala pati, corpusque affligere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042936Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042936Z">
        <seg>Finally, they would allow her, who did no good, to at least suffer evil and afflict her body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique subverendum esse, ne nimia formido graviori periculo implicet desolatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060834Z">
        <seg>Finally, it must be overturned, lest excessive fear involve the desolate one in a more serious danger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique toti domui una hæc speciosa bestiola in deliciis erat, gaudebantque omnes hanc a matrefamilias nutriri &amp; asservari in spem sobolis, quando crevisset: nam sperabatur illi simillimos fore pullos, quos procrearet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061528Z">
        <seg>Finally, this one charming little creature was the delight of the entire household, and everyone was pleased that the matriarch was nurturing and keeping it in hopes of offspring when it grew up; for it was hoped that the chicks it would hatch would resemble it very closely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique unico post partum mense filiolum Hieronymum idem morbus stitit in vitæ limine;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040038Z">
        <seg>Finally, one month after his birth, the same disease struck little Jerome on the threshold of life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique videbatur adhuc non mortua, sed sopita.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031128Z">
        <seg>Finally, she still seemed not dead, but asleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denuo hæc detumuit, &amp; undequaque ulcera siccando conclusit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035914Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035914Z">
        <seg>Again, it subsided and sealed up the sores from every side by drying them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>deplorans jacturam pretiosi temporis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043457Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043457Z">
        <seg>Lamenting the loss of precious time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>deportantur in ædem capitularem pretiosa virginis Rosæ lipsana;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031150Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031150Z">
        <seg>The relics of the precious Virgin Rose are carried into the chapter house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>detecta erat venerabilis illa Salvatoris effigies, cui ad utrumque latus binæ ardebant candelæ in altari, ac tertia procul inde lucebat seorsim in mensula.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053700Z">
        <seg>The venerable image of the Savior had been uncovered, to either side of which two candles burned on the altar, and a third shone separately on a small table some distance away.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dic nostro Creatori:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051614Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051614Z">
        <seg>say to our Creator:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>digitos stringebat, diducebat, motitabat celerrime;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033338Z">
        <seg>He was stringing, separating, and moving his fingers very quickly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>discordes arteriarum temporumque pulsus certabant de palma vehementiæ, &amp; incertis intervallis nunc tumor, nunc tremor exangue corpusculum oberrabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013350Z">
        <seg>The discordant pulses of the arteries and the times vied for the palm of intensity, and at uncertain intervals now a swelling, now a tremor would overtake the pale little body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>displicebat religiosa vestis, pannosa simplicitas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012507Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012507Z">
        <seg>He disliked the religious garb, its shabby simplicity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dissimulavit tamen, ne pretiosæ lectionis ignitam dulcedinem intempestive abrumperet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032711Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032711Z">
        <seg>He nevertheless concealed it, so as not to inappropriately interrupt the fiery sweetness of the precious lesson.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dixisses arcam potius aut cistam grandiusculam, nisi modica ad latus fenestella esse domunculam clamitasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043643Z">
        <seg>You would have called it a chest rather than a box, or a rather large chest, if a small window on the side hadn't proclaimed it to be a little house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>doctore &amp; prædecessore nostro Gregorio&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; testante, significatur) excurrendum fuit, donec ad Peruviam perveniret, in quam una cum iis, qui regionem illam felici introitu ac successu, Deo &amp; sanctæ Romanæ Ecclesiæ subjecerunt, primus ille penetravit Ordo&lt;a1&gt;d&lt;/a1&gt;, qui singulari hujus sanctæ Sedis apostolicæ privilegio Prædicatorum titulo jam diu insignitus fuit, &amp; ante alios Peruvianæ gentilitati sanctum Jesu Christi Euangelium annuntiavit, primusque ibi salutiferæ prædicationis verbum suo sanguine obsignando universam illam gentem ad cœnam Agni providi efficaciter invitavit, cujus voce, labore, &amp; cruore aurifera illa terra exculta ad exhilarandam universæ, tam militantis, quam triumphantis Ecclesiæ cœnam inter ceteros Christianæ pietatis illius regionis flores Deo, angelis, &amp; hominibus suaveolentem protulit Rosam, totius euangelicæ perfectionis exemplar, &amp; primum ex orbe novo in Sanctorum albo reponendum ornamentum: cum enim ad hoc nata, &amp; data fuisset, eam omnium impartitor bonorum Spiritus sapientiæ, &amp; intellectus implevit, adeoque suæ charitatis igne inflammavit, ut non tantum odore recrearet, sed etiam fulgore luceret in ea domus Dei parte, quæ in tenebris latebat, ut quasi stella matutina in medio nebulæ, quasi luna plena in diebus nostris, &amp; quasi sol refulgens in perpetuas æternitates splendesceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041049Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041049Z">
        <seg>as is signified by the testimony of our doctor and predecessor Gregory&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;), he had to make a detour until he reached Peru, into which, together with those who had a happy entry and success in that region, submitting themselves to God and the holy Roman Church, first penetrated the Order&lt;a1&gt;d&lt;/a1&gt;, which had long been distinguished by the singular privilege of this holy apostolic See with the title of Preachers, and before others announced the holy Gospel of Jesus Christ to the Peruvian nation, and first there, by sealing the word of salutary preaching with his blood, he effectively invited the entire nation to the providential supper of the Lamb, whose voice, labor, and blood cultivated that golden land to adorn the supper of the whole Church, both militant and triumphant, among the other flowers of Christian piety of that region for God, He brought forth for angels and men a fragrant Rose, the exemplar of all evangelical perfection, and the first ornament to be placed on the roll of the Saints from the new world: for since she had been born and given for this purpose, the Spirit of wisdom and understanding, the dispenser of all good things, filled her, and so inflamed her with the fire of His charity, that she not only refreshed with fragrance, but also shone with splendor in that part of God's house which lay hidden in darkness, so that she might shine forth like a morning star in the midst of a cloud, like the full moon in our days, and like the sun resplendent for all eternity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dolebat se ipsis adhuc fere per biduum oneri fore, rogabatque, ne prope terminum amittere velint longanimitatis suæ fructum, ac patientiæ meritum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030031Z">
        <seg>He lamented that he would still be a burden to them for nearly two days, and he begged them not to lose the fruit of their long-suffering and the merit of their patience so close to the end.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dolebat superbus, illic se non timeri ab imbelli juvencula, imo ea solitudine ultro ad monomachiam se provocari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051917Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051917Z">
        <seg>He was angry and proud that she, a timid young woman, did not fear him, but rather, in that solitude, was voluntarily challenging him to single combat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dolere se ob notabilem defectum, qui festivitatem ex parte obscuraturus erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053455Z">
        <seg>to lament a notable defect that was to have partially obscured the festival;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>doluit, suum nomen in tenera nepte sic obliterari, ac sere despici;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034708Z">
        <seg>He grieved that his name was thus erased from his tender granddaughter and almost despised.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>domum suam ad Dei timorem ac pietatis observantiam studiose formaret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012820Z">
        <seg>to diligently form his household in the fear of God and the observance of piety;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>donec ferocius materno compulsa imperio obtemperavit, munusque invisum admisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035337Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035337Z">
        <seg>Until, more fiercely compelled by her mother's command, she obeyed and accepted the hated task.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>donec fortuito casu manum cervicali incuriose imponens contrectavit hostem in latebra, juncos deprehendit, extraxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042756Z">
        <seg>Until, by chance, carelessly placing his hand on the neck, he seized the enemy in ambush, discovered the rushes, and pulled them out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>donec impavida, sed temeritatis pertæsa Virgo, exclamavit ad Sponsum, “Ne tradas bestiis animas confitentes tibi”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051939Z">
        <seg>Until the fearless, but disgusted with rashness, Virgin exclaimed to the Spouse, "Do not hand over the souls that confess to you to the beasts."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>donec tandem (incertum quibus indiciis) rescivit confessarius, qui continuo præcepit Virgini, catenam, ut erat, sine mora sibi transmitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041752Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041752Z">
        <seg>Until at last the confessor, by some uncertain means, learned of it, and immediately ordered the virgin to send him the chain, as it was, without delay.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dotem &amp; quidquid filia postulaverat, concessit emollitus pater, longaque dilatione feliciter abrepta, monialis, quod ad solemnia vota emittenda pertigerat, modico pulveri debere agnovit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040004Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040004Z">
        <seg>The father, softened, granted the dowry and everything his daughter had asked for, and, the long delay happily overcome, the nun acknowledged that she owed her vow of solemn profession to a little dust.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>duabus ac interdum tribus horis uno defixa loco cælumque respiciens, his vocibus immorabatur, eo calore &amp; impetu, ut audientium corda ad amorem &amp; compunctionem ignara commoverit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053223Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053223Z">
        <seg>For two and sometimes three hours, she would remain fixed in one spot, gazing at the sky, and linger over these words with such warmth and impetus that she moved the hearts of her listeners to a love and contrition they had never known.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dubius, num viveret, pluries utramque jacentis auriculam validis clamoribus tentavit: at frustra se vitalia signa exspectare videns, ad Rosæ confugit præsidium, vitam puero supplex petiit, Novenam promisit: &amp; ecce, illo momento respiravit Joannes, resedit, cibum sumpsit, attonitis, qui spectaculo intervenere, viris religiosis, P. Agnello de Oliva Societatis Jesu, P. F. Laurentio de Texeda Ordinis S. Francisci, &amp; secularibus utriusque sexus non paucis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033809Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033809Z">
        <seg>Unsure whether he was alive, he repeatedly tried to rouse him with loud cries, pulling on both of his floppy ears. But seeing that he was in vain awaiting signs of life, he fled to Rosa's protection and, on his knees, pleaded for the boy's life, He promised a novena: and behold, at that very moment John breathed, sat up, and took food, to the astonishment of the religious men who had intervened in the spectacle—Father Agnello de Oliva of the Society of Jesus, Father Francis Laurentio de Texeda of the Order of St. Francis, and many laymen of both sexes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dum in opertum navigii sinum descendit, obvium habuit virum conjugatum Franciscum Flores, qui Joannem jam exspirasse asseverabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033802Z">
        <seg>As he descended into the ship's covered hold, he encountered a married man, Francis Flores, who asserted that John had already expired.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dum nebat, texebat, dum sericos acu pingebat flores, dum aliis colloquebatur, dum comedebat, legebat, ambulabat, in templo, in horto, domi, foris, in platea, in cellula, ubique obversabatur interno obtutui cominus (ac velut in speculo) amabilis illa Majestas, cui serviunt angeli, cujus pulchritudinem sol &amp; luna mirantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044833Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044833Z">
        <seg>while she was spinning, weaving, painting flowers on silk with a needle, talking with others, eating, reading, walking, in the temple, in the garden, at home, outside, in the street, in a cell, everywhere she turned, that lovely Majesty, whom angels serve and whose beauty the sun and moon admire, was close to her inward gaze (as if in a mirror).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dumque agebat omnia, &lt;s2&gt;[febrique concionatoris in se translata,]&lt;/s2&gt; nihil se egisse querebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053443Z">
        <seg>And while he was doing everything, he complained that he had done nothing, having taken on the duties of a public speaker.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dumque agebat omnia, [febrique concionatoris in se translata,] nihil se egisse querebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010933Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010933Z">
        <seg>And while he was doing everything, [with the fever of a public speaker upon him,] he complained that he had done nothing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dumque sub feretri tabulato orat (siquidem nec palmum a terra se erigere, nec ad sacri corpusculi attactum in gradus potuerat eluctari) repente prodiit alacer, erectus in pedes, quos ante nonnisi reptans miserrime per terram traxerat, dum nequidem grallis uti, sed dimidio corpore dumtaxat solum verrere licebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033431Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033431Z">
        <seg>And so he was praying on the floor beneath the bier (for he could neither raise himself a palm's breadth from the ground, nor struggle up the steps to touch the sacred little body), when suddenly he appeared, brisk and upright on his feet, which he had previously dragged most wretchedly along the ground, crawling, since he was not even permitted to use his hands, but was allowed only to drag half his body along the ground.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dumque verbis humillimis enarrat successum, visa est (ut quondam Stephanus) vultu prorsus angelico aliquamdiu radiare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051047Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051047Z">
        <seg>And as she recounted the success in the humblest of words, she seemed (like Stephen of old) to glow for a while with a wholly angelic countenance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>duo scilicet ex processu Suessano&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;, &amp; duo alia ex processu Panormitano&lt;a5&gt;e&lt;/a5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043234Z">
        <seg>two, namely, from the Suessan process&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;, and two others from the Panormitan process&lt;a5&gt;e&lt;/a5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>duplex item aquila binæque columnæ cum epigraphe PLUS ULTRA, adjectis præterea duabus litteris I &amp; K, quibus Joanna mater &amp; Carolus imperator significaretur, omnia autem junctim indicarent, quod sicut reges Magi fidei nascentisque Ecclesiæ primitiæ fuerunt, ita Joanna Carolusque primordia darent ecclesiæ Peruanæ, patrocinium illius defensionemque suscipientes.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041839Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041839Z">
        <seg>a double eagle and two columns with the inscription PLUS ULTRA, with the two letters I &amp; K added, by which Joanna the mother and Charles the emperor would be signified, and all together would indicate that just as the Magi were the first fruits of faith and of the nascent Church, so Joanna and Charles would give the beginnings to the Peruvian church, taking up its patronage and defense.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Antiqui&lt;/e0&gt; veredos &lt;e1&gt;appellabant equos velocissimi cursus, quibus in venatione utebantur, aut ipsas rhedas, quas equi tales vehebant, ut alii malunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052324Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052324Z">
        <seg>The ancients called veredos the very fastest racehorses, which they used for hunting, or the chariots themselves that such horses drew, as others prefer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Apostolicus Societatis nostræ sacerdos, qui aliquot annis in America missionarium egit, &amp; in ea adhuc vivit, mihi asseveranter narravit, inter mancipia jam ad Christianam fidem conversa moris esse, ut populares suos nondum baptizatos cognomine equorum vel asinorum appellent, &amp; cum iis conversari nolint.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013040Z">
        <seg>An apostolic priest of our Society, who for several years has served as a missionary in America and still lives there, told me for certain that among the slaves already converted to the Christian faith it is customary to call their fellow countrymen who have not yet been baptized by nicknames like "horses" or "asses," and they refuse to associate with them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;De bocis &amp; mænis, quæ sunt pisciculi marini ex genere halecum, Ulysses Aldrovandus in Opere suo piscario fuse disserit, ut lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034422Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034422Z">
        <seg>About the bocas and mænas, which are small marine fish of the genus Halec, Ulisse Aldrovandi discusses at length in his piscatorial work, as in book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Ex his aperte colligimus&lt;/e0&gt;, S. Rosam &lt;e1&gt;ineunte ipso festo S. Bartholomæi obiisse, ac proinde sequitur, istud festum Limæ die 24 Augustitunc celebratum fuisse: nam Clemens IX Pontifex in decreto Beatificationis, quod § 2 Commentarii prævii retulimus, ibidem num.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030455Z">
        <seg>From these we openly conclude that St. Rose died on the very eve of the Feast of St. Bartholomew, and therefore it follows that this feast was then celebrated in Lima on August 24; for Pope Clement IX, in the decree of Beatification which we cited in § 2 of the Preliminary Commentary, states the same thing there, no.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Gorræus in Definitionibus medicis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T072813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T072813Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, Gorræus in the medical definitions, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Gorræus in Definitionibus medicis pag. 369 hanc corporis humani partem describit his verbis:&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;Περιτόναιον&lt;/s1&gt;, peritonæum est membrana seu tunica (utramque enim sortitur appellationem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033657Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033657Z">
        <seg>Gorræus, in the Medical Definitions, page 369, describes this part of the human body with these words: "Peritoneum is a membrane or tunic (for it is called both)."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Græca vox composita&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἀντιπερίστασις&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;simpliciter de verbo translata, Latine&lt;/e2&gt; circumobsistentia &lt;e3&gt;dici posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025826Z">
        <seg>The Greek compound word, literally translated from the verb, could be called 'surroundings' in Latin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Hæc herba Indica, jam per Europam passim nota &amp; usitata, primo nomen accepit ab Americana ejusdem nominis insula, in qua potissimumcrescebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060933Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060933Z">
        <seg>This Indian herb, now widely known and used throughout Europe, was first named after the American island of the same name, on which it grew most abundantly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;In Annotatis ad caput primum Vitæ monuimus, Pentecosten ab Italis&lt;/e0&gt; Rosarum pascha &lt;e1&gt;dici;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041433Z">
        <seg>In the annotations to the first chapter of the Vita, we noted that the Italians call Pentecost the Feast of the Roses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Joannes de Laet lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T074440Z">
        <seg>g John of Laet, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Suspicor, esse eamdem speciem arboris, quam Joannes Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043137Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043137Z">
        <seg>I suspect it is the same species of tree that John Latius mentions in his book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Teste Baudrando&lt;/e0&gt;, Portus dives &lt;e1&gt;est insula ampla Americæ Septemtrionalis, quæ ab Hispanis&lt;/e1&gt; la isla de Puerto rico, &lt;e2&gt;&amp; ab incolis&lt;/e2&gt; Boriquen &lt;e3&gt;appellatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T231511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035127Z">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;According to Baudrand&lt;/e0&gt;, the rich port &lt;e1&gt;is a large island of North America, which is called by the Spaniards&lt;/e1&gt; la isla de Puerto Rico, &lt;e2&gt;and by the inhabitants&lt;/e2&gt; Boriquen&lt;e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Antidora, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;ἀντίδωρα&lt;/s1&gt;,&lt;e2&gt;sunt remunerationes, quæ pro dono alicui facto redduntur, ab&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;ἀντί&lt;/s3&gt; contra, &lt;e4&gt;&amp;&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;δῶρον&lt;/s5&gt; donum, &lt;e6&gt;quasi&lt;/e6&gt; contra-donum, &lt;e7&gt;seu donum dono relatum&lt;/e7&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062402Z">
        <seg>Antidora, Greek&lt;/e0&gt;ἀντίδωρα&lt;/s1&gt;,are returns, which are given in return for a gift made to someone, from&lt;/e2&gt; ἀντί&lt;/s3&gt; against, &amp;&lt;/e4&gt; δῶρον&lt;/s5&gt; gift, as if&lt;/e6&gt; a counter-gift, or a gift in return for a gift&lt;/e7&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Apophoreta &lt;e0&gt;hic accipiuntur pro donis: nam veteres munuscula, quæ convivis a cœna domum deferenda dabantur, apophoreta appellabant a verbo Græco&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἀποφέρειν&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;quod&lt;/e2&gt; auferre &lt;e3&gt;significat&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033040Z">
        <seg>Here "apophoreta" is taken to mean gifts: for the ancients called the small presents given to guests to take home from a banquet "apophoreta," from the Greek verb ἀποφέρειν, which means "to carry away."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Asthma, &lt;e0&gt;Græcis&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἄσθμα&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;est obstructio pulmonis, frequentem &amp; difficilem sine febre respirationem inducens, qualem hi habent, qui velociter cucurrerunt, ut Gorræus in Opere mox citato pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052934Z">
        <seg>Asthma, from the Greeks ἄσθμα, is an obstruction of the lungs, causing frequent and difficult breathing without fever, such as that experienced by those who have run quickly, as Gorræus states in the work cited below, p.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Asthma, &lt;e0&gt;Græcis&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἄσθμα&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;est obstructio pulmonis, frequentem &amp; difficilem sine febre respirationem inducens, qualem hi habent, qui velociter cucurrerunt, ut Gorræus in Opere mox citato pag. 58 docet&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010846Z">
        <seg>Asthma, from the Greeks ἄσθμα, is an obstruction of the lungs, causing frequent and difficult breathing without fever, such as that experienced by those who have run quickly, as Gorræus teaches in the work cited above, page 58.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Caryophyllum &lt;e0&gt;est flos odoriferus, quasi&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;καρύου&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;φύλλον&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;id est&lt;/e3&gt;, nucis folium &lt;e4&gt;appellatum, qui a Gallis&lt;/e4&gt; giroflee, &lt;e5&gt;&amp; ab Hispanis&lt;/e5&gt; sanamunda &lt;e6&gt;nominatur&lt;/e6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055532Z">
        <seg>from the Caryophyllum is a fragrant flower, as if&lt;/e0&gt; καρύου&lt;/s1&gt; leaf&lt;/s2&gt;, that is, the leaf of the nut, called giroflee by the French, and sanamunda by the Spaniards.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Dicasterium &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;δικαστήριον&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;pro foro judiciali proprie sumitur a verbo Græco&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;δικάζω&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;id est&lt;/e4&gt;, judico.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054729Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054729Z">
        <seg>A dicastery (from the Greek δικαστήριον) is, in the proper sense, a judicial forum, taken from the Greek verb δικάζω, meaning "to judge."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Emplastrum, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἔμπλαστρος&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;teste Gorræo, est&lt;/e2&gt; medicamentum in massam coactum, quod præmollitum vulneribus imponitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061646Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061646Z">
        <seg>An emplaster, from the Greek ἔμπλαστρος, according to Gorræus, is a medicine pressed into a mass, which, when softened, is applied to wounds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Gaspacho, &lt;e0&gt;vel potius&lt;/e0&gt; gazpacho &lt;e1&gt;ab Hispanis vocatur jusculum sive pulmentarium ex oleo, aceto, sale, pane tosto, aliisque rebus confectum, ut Franciscus Sobrinus in Dictionario suo Hispano-Gallico ad hanc vocem Gallice exponit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041436Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041436Z">
        <seg>From Gaspacho, or rather gazpacho, which the Spaniards call a broth or porridge made from oil, vinegar, salt, toasted bread, and other ingredients, as Francisco Sobrino explains in his Spanish-French Dictionary under this word.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Hieratica &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἱερατικὴ&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;teste Plinio lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050808Z">
        <seg>from Hieratic Greek&lt;/e0&gt; hieratike&lt;/s1&gt;, as attested by Pliny, book.&lt;/e2&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Panormus &lt;e0&gt;est notissima &amp; præcipua Siciliæ civitas, ubi duo postrema miracula examen subierunt&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043330Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043330Z">
        <seg>And Palermo is the most famous and principal city of Sicily, where the last two miracles were put to the test.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Paraguay &lt;e0&gt;sive Paraguaia proprie dicta provincia est inter Brasiliam ad ortum &amp; Paraguaium fluvium ad occasum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032430Z">
        <seg>Paraguay, or properly speaking the province of Paraguaia, is situated between Brazil to the east and the Paraguay River to the west.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Philippei &lt;e0&gt;olim vocabantur a Philippo Macedone quidam aurei nummi, de quibus apud veteres frequens occurrit mentio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040131Z">
        <seg>They were formerly called by Philip of Macedon certain gold coins, which are frequently mentioned by the ancients.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Phlebotomia &lt;e0&gt;est venæ sectio, vel sanguinis detractio, incisa vena.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045949Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045949Z">
        <seg>Phlebotomy is the incision of a vein, or the drawing of blood from an incised vein.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>eamdem transacto festo devestiendam in domo Gundisalvi quæstoris exspectabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055334Z">
        <seg>The same, after the festival was over, awaited its dismantling in the house of Gundisalvus the treasurer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ego neque mortem, nec morte diriora supplicia hæc recusabo”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013440Z">
        <seg>I will not refuse either death or punishments more dire than death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ego vero de pastu cogito, nec solicitor, quid retribuam Domino pro omnibus, quæ retribuit mihi”!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045041Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045041Z">
        <seg>As for me, I am thinking about my food, and I am not concerned about what I shall repay the Lord for all that He has repaid me!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ego vitam æternam do eis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061925Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061925Z">
        <seg>I give them eternal life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ego, quæ tam fidelem, solicitum, fortem comitem, non ad latus, sed in corde circumfero, timebo in posterum a timore nocturno?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062101Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062101Z">
        <seg>Will I, who carry such a faithful, caring, and brave companion not at my side but in my heart, ever again be afraid of the night?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ejusdem instituti Religiosum, a medicis pridie derelictum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060828Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060828Z">
        <seg>A Religious of the same institute, abandoned by the doctors the day before;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ejusque Mater sanctissima prægrandi sua pietate suppleat defectus meos, &amp; ausui det veniam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054636Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054636Z">
        <seg>and may His most holy Mother, by her preeminent piety, make up for my shortcomings and grant me forgiveness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>empyrei favores delicati sunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051418Z">
        <seg>The favors of the heavens are delicate;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>en XVI patacones, quos penes me reperi, his utere ad seraphicæ imaginis ornatum, si opus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055052Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055052Z">
        <seg>Use the sixteen coins I found on me to decorate the seraphic image, if necessary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>en toto cordis affectu meipsam tibi offero una cum prolibus &amp; universa mea substantia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011934Z">
        <seg>with all the affection of my heart, I offer myself to you, together with my children and all my substance;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>en, quam splendide profitetur vultu, se cupediis ab aliquo dapsiliter hodie refectam!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040800Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040800Z">
        <seg>Behold, how splendidly she shows on her face that she has been sumptuously feasted on cakes by someone today!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>eo anhelabat, hanc unice deperibat siderea indoles, nec facile a cælestium flammularum nocturno obtutu avelli poterat mens cælo nata: fatebatur, cæli siderumque diutina contemplatione se instaurari ac refici;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043714Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043714Z">
        <seg>Her soul yearned for it, her starry nature longed for it alone, and the mind, born of the heavens, could not easily be torn away from the nightly gaze of celestial flames: she confessed that she was renewed and restored by the long contemplation of the heavens and the stars.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>eodem momento sensit Maria se scrupulo suo liberatam, evanuit inquietudo &amp; timoris anxietas, rediit cordi suo tranquillissima malacia, ita ut noctis residuum potuerit sub placido lætæ securitatis silentio transigere, certa, tantam tamque repentinam animi quietem aliunde, quam de sursum infundi sibi non potuisse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032256Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032256Z">
        <seg>At that very moment Mary felt herself freed from her scruples; anxiety and the dread of fear vanished, and the most tranquil serenity returned to her heart, so that she was able to spend the rest of the night in the placid silence of joyful security, certain that such a sudden peace of mind could not have been poured into her from above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>eoque privilegio gavisa est Rosæ mater.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062852Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062852Z">
        <seg>And Rosæ's mother enjoyed that privilege.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>episcopus Portuen.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043506Z">
        <seg>Bishop of Portuen</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>est oppidum satis cultum sub domino Hispanorum&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013018Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013018Z">
        <seg>It is a fairly cultured town under Spanish rule.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>esto adhuc resiliens valido ictu alterius feminæ vestes læserit &amp; succenderit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042245Z">
        <seg>Let her still be resilient; with a strong blow, another woman's clothes have been torn and set on fire.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>esto etiam religiosas personas accedentes hostiliter invaderent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041628Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041628Z">
        <seg>Even religious persons who approached would be hostilely attacked.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ex adverso his oppositas impressiones, timorem, divisi cordis confusionem, sui complacentiam, &amp; inquietudinem redolere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042018Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042018Z">
        <seg>On the contrary, to oppose these, one smells impressions, fear, the confusion of a divided heart, self-complacency, and restlessness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ex altera parte, quas Rosa in sublime sparserat, hærebant in aëre, ibidem in conspicuam sanctæ crucis figuram aptissime coibant, eidemque cruci a succedentibus rosis decorus per circuitum formabatur limbus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062533Z">
        <seg>On the other hand, the roses that had been scattered aloft hung in the air, fittingly coming together there into a conspicuous figure of the holy cross, and a decorative border was formed around that same cross by the roses that followed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ex creta insula advecta.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052913Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052913Z">
        <seg>Brought from the island of Crete.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ex horrendis, omnemque modum ac numerum supergressis flagitiis nullum subticuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032557Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032557Z">
        <seg>He did not omit any of the most horrific and outrageous crimes, having surpassed all measure and number.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ex quo feroces incolæ, excusso regis &amp; religionis jugo, redierant ad pristinæ gentilitatis vomitum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060357Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060357Z">
        <seg>since the fierce inhabitants, having shaken off the yoke of the king and of religion, had returned to the vomit of their former paganism.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>exaggerabat difficultatem operis, famem, sitim, ferarum pericula, æstum, lassitudines, exilia, demum &amp; venena, quibus gens illa truculentissima minimeque hospitalis in exteros fidei præcones sævire solebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060422Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060422Z">
        <seg>He exaggerated the difficulty of the work, the hunger, the thirst, the dangers from wild beasts, the heat, the fatigue, the exiles, and finally the poisons, with which that most cruel and inhospitable people used to rage against the preachers of the faith.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>exaruerat cordis fundus, distabuerat vigor, nulloque vitali motu, nullius affectus sapore divinis afficiebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045633Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045633Z">
        <seg>The depths of his heart had grown parched, his vigor was gone, and with no vital motion, he was touched by divine affections, tasting them with no other emotion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>exclamavit mulier, haud assueta sic in Deo liquefieri &amp; bullire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032718Z">
        <seg>The woman exclaimed, not accustomed to thus melting and bubbling in God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>excogitavit ergo &amp; amans Rosa, methodum ac rationem, qua Christum indumento, spiritualibus laciniis operose contexto, devotissime amiciret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053533Z">
        <seg>So then the loving Rose devised a method and a way by which she might most devoutly clothe Christ with a garment, laboriously woven from spiritual threads.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>eximiam corporis habitudinem sacco pannoso deformare, publicos conspectus fugere, &amp;, ne hominum placeret obtutibus, domi latere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035942Z">
        <seg>to deform her exceptional figure with a shapeless sack, to shun public view, and, so as not to please people's gaze, to hide at home;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>exinde Limam deducta, jam a sexennio ancillabatur Isabellæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012851Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012851Z">
        <seg>She was then taken to Lima, where she had been a servant to Isabella for six years.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>expavit infirma, satisque intelligens, miraculi complementum non a chirurgo, sed a Rosa fuisse exspectandum, denuo eosdem pulveres recidivæ inspersit tibiæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035911Z">
        <seg>Having grown weak and sufficiently understanding that the miracle's completion was to be expected not from the surgeon, but from Rosa, she once again sprinkled the same powder for a relapse onto the tibia.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>expergefactus, sensit laxata undique pulmonum vincula, nec ullam deinceps gravioris anhelitus in se comperit molestiam aut pressuram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034807Z">
        <seg>Upon waking, he felt the bonds of his lungs loosed on all sides, and from then on he found no further discomfort or pressure from heavy breathing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>experire, si quid vales;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052057Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052057Z">
        <seg>Try it, if you're any good.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>extimuit, ne forsan inde aliena fraude sublecta disparuerit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034004Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034004Z">
        <seg>he feared that perhaps she had disappeared, having been taken by some other's fraud.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>extra hunc paradisum nusquam ei bene esse poterat, adeo ut domi abiret in proverbium: “Hortum adeas, si Rosulam quæris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043302Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043302Z">
        <seg>Nowhere outside this paradise could he be happy, so much so that at home the proverb went: "Go to the garden if you seek a rose."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Ab Hispanis&lt;/e0&gt; armadillo &lt;e1&gt;vocatur animal Americanum, quod osseis squamis veluti laminis quibusdam tectum est, &amp; cujus figuram Joannes Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034429Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034429Z">
        <seg>From the Spanish it is called armadillo, an American animal covered with bony scales like little plates, &amp; whose figure John Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Bollandus noster ad diem 25 Januarii, sive tomo 2 istius mensis a pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045955Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045955Z">
        <seg>Our Bollandus to the 25th of January, or volume 2 of that month, from page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Bollandus noster ad diem 25 Januarii, sive tomo 2 istius mensis a pag. 652 Acta illius venerabilisHenrici Susonis illustravit.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010406Z">
        <seg>Our Bollandus, on January 25, that is, in the second volume of that month on page 652, illustrated the Acts of that venerable Henry of Suession.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Dum Hansenius hic asserit, sanctam Virginem&lt;/e0&gt; metrico sui idiomatis rhythmo &lt;e1&gt;angelum allocutam esse, satis indicat, hanc allocutionem versibus Hispanicis compositam fuisse: nam&lt;/e1&gt; S. Rosa &lt;e2&gt;idiomate Hispanico utebatur, utpote ex Hispanis oriunda, quamvis Limæ nata&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052336Z">
        <seg>While Hansen here asserts that the Holy Virgin spoke to the angel in the metrical rhythm of her own idiom, he sufficiently indicates that this speech was composed in Spanish verses: for St. Rose used the Spanish language, being of Spanish origin, although born in Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Hanc vocem apud scriptores Latinos non reperi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055535Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055535Z">
        <seg>I did not find this word among Latin writers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Hic alluditur ad fabulas poëticas, in quibus fingitur Ariadna, filia Minoïs Cretensium regis, Thesea, quem diligebat, e labyrintho per filum extricasse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033043Z">
        <seg>This alludes to poetic tales in which Ariadne, daughter of the Cretan king Minos, is said to have led Theseus, whom she loved, out of the labyrinth with a thread.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Hic venerabilis antistes Limanus postea numero Sanctorum adscriptus est, ut in Annotatis ad proxime citatum Vitæ caput exposuimus.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041442Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041442Z">
        <seg>This venerable bishop of Lima was later numbered among the Saints, as we explained in the notes to the immediately preceding chapter of the Vita.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;In supradicta civitatis Limensis ichnographia vicus S. Dominici &amp; nosocomium sancti Spiritus, quod pro nautis ægrotantibus exstructum est, non longe ab invicem dissita assignantur.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T233006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035148Z">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;In the aforementioned plan of the city of Lima, the village of St. Dominic and the Hospital of the Holy Spirit, which was built for sick sailors, are assigned not far from each other.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Joannes de Laet jam aliquoties citatus lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041442Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041442Z">
        <seg>John de Laet, already cited several times, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Majores nostri ad diem 7 Januarii, sive tomo 1 istius mensis a pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043202Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043202Z">
        <seg>Our ancestors up to January 7, or volume 1 of that month, from page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Majores nostri ad diem 7 Januarii, sive tomo 1 istius mensis a pag. 404 Acta hujus Sancti illustrarunt.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003529Z">
        <seg>Our ancestors illustrated the Acts of this Saint up to January 7, or volume 1 of that month, page 404.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Nomen Græcum&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;εὐτραπελία&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;idem fere significat apud Græcos, quod apud Latinos&lt;/e2&gt; urbanitas,lepor, festivitas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050823Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050823Z">
        <seg>A Greek word, *eutrapelia*, means almost the same to the Greeks as *urbanitas, lepore,* and *festivitas* to the Latins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Tubus ex creta confectus est instrumentum, quo fumus tabaci bibitur, sicut Europæi æque acIndi jam norunt.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060958Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060958Z">
        <seg>A clay pipe is an instrument for drinking tobacco smoke, as Europeans as well as Indians already know.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Tum in negotiis ecclesiasticis, tum in secularibus&lt;/e0&gt; chartophylax &lt;e1&gt;est custos chartarum seu præfectus scriniorum, qui privilegia &amp; similia asservat&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061655Z">
        <seg>In both ecclesiastical and secular affairs, the chartophylax is the keeper of the charters or prefect of the archives, who preserves privileges and the like.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f Caciqua &lt;e0&gt;verosimiliter est uxor alicujus reguli aut ducis Indi, sicut ex nomine&lt;/e0&gt; cazique &lt;e1&gt;superius explicato colligi potest&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040137Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040137Z">
        <seg>Caciqua is most likely the wife of some Indian ruler or chief, as can be gathered from the name cazique explained above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f Cacique &lt;e0&gt;vel&lt;/e0&gt; cazique &lt;e1&gt;est nomen dignitatis, quod Hispani generatim omnibus regulis aut præfectis Indorum tribuunt, etiamsi illud proprie in Mexico &amp; insulis usitatum fuerit: nam veteres Indi gubernatores aut reguli Peruviæ olim&lt;/e1&gt; curacas &lt;e2&gt;appellabantur, ut Dictionarium Trivultianum ad voces&lt;/e2&gt; cacique &lt;e3&gt;ac&lt;/e3&gt; curaca &lt;e4&gt;solerter observavit&lt;/e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035232Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035232Z">
        <seg>Cacique or cazique is a name of dignity, which the Spaniards generally attribute to all the rulers or prefects of the Indians, even though it was properly used in Mexico and the islands: for the ancient rulers or chiefs of Peru were formerly called curacas, as the Trivultian Dictionary has carefully observed under the words cacique and curaca.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f Cholica &lt;e0&gt;vel&lt;/e0&gt; cholera, &lt;e1&gt;a Græco nomine&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;χολὴ&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;id est&lt;/e3&gt;, bilis &lt;e4&gt;sic dicta, est morbus peracutus, ut Gorræus in Definitionibus medicis pag. 509 eum vocat, ubi plura de diversis ejus speciebus &amp; causis legi possunt&lt;/e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025838Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025838Z">
        <seg>Cholika or cholera, from the Greek name cholē, that is, bile so called, is a very acute disease, as Gorræus in his Medical Definitions, page. 509, where more can be read about its various species and causes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f Epidemia &lt;e0&gt;est morbus populariter vagans vel inter populum sparsus, ab&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἐπὶ&lt;/s1&gt; in, &lt;e2&gt;&amp;&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;δῆμος&lt;/s3&gt; populus, &lt;e4&gt;quasi&lt;/e4&gt; in populo versans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054735Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054735Z">
        <seg>An epidemic is a disease that spreads widely or is scattered among the people, from epi, "upon," and demos, "people," as if moving among the people.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f Hispalis &lt;e0&gt;est notissima Hispaniæ Bæticæ civitas, ex qua forte vir ille erat oriundus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032436Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032436Z">
        <seg>Hispalis is the most famous city of Baetica in Spain, from which that man was, by chance, a native.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f Marasmus, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;μαρασμὸς&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;a verbo&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;μαραίνομαι&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;quod&lt;/e4&gt; tabesco &lt;e5&gt;significat, apud medicos est tabis species, quæ contingit ex febri ardente adeo propagata, ut humorem totius corporis absumat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043333Z">
        <seg>Marasmus, &lt;e0&gt;Greek&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;marasmós&lt;/s1&gt;, from the verb &lt;s3&gt;marainōmai&lt;/s3&gt;, which means to waste away, is, in medicine, a type of wasting away that occurs from a fever so intense and widespread that it dries up the moisture of the entire body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f Pleuritis, &lt;e0&gt;quam Plinius&lt;/e0&gt; lateris compunctionem &lt;e1&gt;vocat, est inflammatio membranæ costas succingentis, quam Græci&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;πλευρὰν&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;e3&gt;appellant, cum febri acuta, dolore pungente, respirationis difficultate &amp; tussi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052941Z">
        <seg>Pleuritis, which Pliny calls a compunction of the side, is an inflammation of the membrane surrounding the ribs, which the Greeks call pleura, with acute fever, a stabbing pain, difficulty breathing, and a cough.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>facilius poëtarum fabulosas metamorphoses, quam talem obfirmati sui cordis mutationem reipsa eventuram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032900Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032900Z">
        <seg>It is easier for poets to create fabulous metamorphoses than for such a change in one's own heart to actually occur.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>famulare huic Domino, cole, venerare, ama hunc Regem gloriæ, qui sine intermissione ex pusilla icone, tam grandia in nos dona congerit, paratus majora &amp; plura effundere, modo velimus capere”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054131Z">
        <seg>Serve this Lord, cultivate, venerate, love this King of glory, who without ceasing from a small icon pours upon us such great gifts, ready to pour out greater and more, if only we are willing to receive them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>fassa est, modico illo tempore, quo cum S. Catharina Senensi de curanda sua dextera supplex egerat, simul in intimos cordis sui recessus largum insolitæ suavitatis rorem depluisse, qui etiam inde exciderit in corpus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055455Z">
        <seg>She confessed that during that brief time when she had humbly petitioned St. Catherine of Siena to heal her right hand, a generous dew of unusual sweetness had simultaneously descended into the innermost recesses of her heart, which had even from there trickled into her body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>fatebatur ipsa disertissime, sibi insciæ Rosam saluti fuisse, huic se vitam debere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035830Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035830Z">
        <seg>She herself declared most eloquently that, without knowing it, she had been a blessing to Rosa, and that she owed her life to her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>fecit tamen quod Rosa petiit, scalaque applicita, coronam haud sine labore circumdedit collo imaginis, ac scalam removit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043537Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043537Z">
        <seg>He nevertheless did what Rosa asked, and, placing the ladder, he, not without some effort, placed the crown around the image's neck and removed the ladder.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>felicem se quilibet arbitrabatur, sicubi loco editiori potuisset stationem occupare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030951Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030951Z">
        <seg>Everyone considered himself fortunate if he could find a place to stand on higher ground.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>femina ab illo die sedatior ac mitior, visa est induisse tractabile pacis ingenium, tolerabat corde quietissimo domesticos tribulos ac molestias, &amp;, si repentino aliquo flamine succendebatur bilis, hanc sola recordatione mansuetissimæ Virginis, mira facilitate sopiebat, brevique eo pervenit in patientiæ studio, ut jam ultro a Deo plus ac plus adversitatum postularet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060735Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060735Z">
        <seg>From that day on, the woman was more tranquil and gentle, seeming to have put on a tractable disposition of peace. She bore the domestic thorns and troubles of her household with a very quiet heart, and if any sudden gust of anger was kindled, she would quench it by the mere remembrance of the most meek Virgin with remarkable ease. In a short time she reached such a degree of patience that she would now of her own accord ask God for more and more adversity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>feriatam enim ab exteriori gravior occupabat interior, &amp; cessanti foris persecutioni ægritudinum agmina succedebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042040Z">
        <seg>For the holiday, the interior was occupied by a more serious affliction than the exterior, and as the external persecution ceased, hordes of illnesses took its place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>festinet, cælos inclinet,</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051629Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051629Z">
        <seg>Let him hurry, let him incline the heavens,</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>filiæque pulvinar obtrusit lana vacuum, sed cum jussu, ut illud farciret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042731Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042731Z">
        <seg>and stuffed the daughter's pillow with wool, but at her command, to stuff it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>frequenter ab illa benedictionem supernæ dulcedinis emanare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054050Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054050Z">
        <seg>Frequently, a blessing of heavenly sweetness emanates from her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>frigidus sudor stagnabat in pallida fronte;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013344Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013344Z">
        <seg>Cold sweat stood on the pale forehead;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>fuga seculi &amp; usus negotiorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050138Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050138Z">
        <seg>Flight from the world &amp; the practice of business.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>fugite, declinate lubrica infelicissimæ æternitatis præcipitia, qui unico eoque incertissimo fugacis vitæ momento distatis a gehenna;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060530Z">
        <seg>Flee, you who stand on the slippery precipices of the most wretched eternity, separated from hell by a single, most uncertain moment of fleeting life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>futuræ ibidem fabricæ plantam&lt;a0&gt;*&lt;/a0&gt; ac systema in tabella delineavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011841Z">
        <seg>He drew the plant and system of the future factory there on a tablet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>futuræ ibidem fabricæ plantam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;*&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; ac systema in tabella delineavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062452Z">
        <seg>He drew the plant and system of the future factory there on a tablet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g &lt;e0&gt;Castellus in Lexico medico pag. 315 de&lt;/e0&gt; mariscis &lt;e1&gt;ita disserit:&lt;/e1&gt; Mariscæ venulæ sedis sunt, quæ aliquando foris extuberant, ore hiantes &amp; stato tempore sanguinem melancholicum excernentes, alias hæmorrhoïdes dicuntur, quibus plus justo fluentibus, aqua inter cutem subsequitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035235Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035235Z">
        <seg>Castellus, in the medical lexicon on page 315, discusses marshes as follows: Marshes are veins of the seat, which sometimes protrude outward, their mouths gaping, and at a fixed time excrete melancholic blood; otherwise they are called hemorrhoids, and when they flow more than is proper, water follows between the skin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g &lt;e0&gt;Hic vir parvo&lt;/e0&gt; hypographicæ, &lt;e1&gt;id est&lt;/e1&gt;, transcriptoriæ &lt;e2&gt;artis lucro vivebat: nam&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;ὑπογράφω&lt;/s3&gt; &lt;e4&gt;significat transcribo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032442Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032442Z">
        <seg>This man made a living from a small hypographic art, that is, the art of transcription; for ὑπογράφω means "I transcribe."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g &lt;e0&gt;Hoc nomen ad inflexionem Latinam detortum est, &amp; componitur ex præpositione Græca&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἀντὶ&lt;/s1&gt; contra, &lt;e2&gt;&amp; voce&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;βάρος&lt;/s3&gt; pondus, &lt;e4&gt;quod Galli vernacule dicunt&lt;/e4&gt; contre-poids, &lt;e5&gt;quasi&lt;/e5&gt; contra-pondus, &lt;e6&gt;quod Latini proprie&lt;/e6&gt; libramen &lt;e7&gt;vel&lt;/e7&gt; libramentum &lt;e8&gt;appelant&lt;/e8&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050848Z">
        <seg>This name was borrowed for the Latin inflection, and is composed of the Greek prefix ἀντὶ, meaning "against," and the word βάρος, meaning "weight," which the French call contre-poids. as if&lt;/e5&gt; counter-weight, which the Latins properly&lt;/e6&gt; call libramen or libramentum.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g &lt;e0&gt;Instrumentum, quo hæc ultima quatour miracula approbata sunt, in Commentario prævio num.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043339Z">
        <seg>Instrument by which these last four miracles were approved, in the Preliminary Commentary, no.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g &lt;e0&gt;Joannes de Laet lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052339Z">
        <seg>g John of Laet, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g &lt;e0&gt;Oportet, Dioscoridem Anazarbæum id obscure prorsus innuisse: nam pervolvi capita 130 &amp; 131 libri primi, ubi de effectibus rosarum agitur, &amp; nullam philtri mentionem reperi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033050Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033050Z">
        <seg>It seems that Dioscorides of Anazarbus must have hinted at this very obscurely; for I have read through chapters 130 and 131 of the first book, where the effects of roses are discussed, and I found no mention of any philter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g &lt;e0&gt;Pentecosten ab Hispanis vernacule&lt;/e0&gt; Pascua del Espiritu santo &lt;e1&gt;communiter appellari audivi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T233105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035151Z">
        <seg>g &lt;e0&gt;I have heard that Pentecost is commonly called&lt;/e0&gt; Pascua del Espiritu Santo &lt;e1&gt;by the Spaniards.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g Arthritis &lt;e0&gt;a Latinis vocatur&lt;/e0&gt; morbus articularis, &lt;e1&gt;cujus species peculiares sunt ischias, podagra, chiragra, &amp; alii morbi hujusmodi, ut apud Gorræum in Definitionibus medicis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055545Z">
        <seg>Arthritis is called a Latin disease of the joints, whose particular species are sciatica, gout, chiragra, and other diseases of this kind, as in Gorræus in his Medical Definitions, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g Arthritis &lt;e0&gt;a Latinis vocatur&lt;/e0&gt; morbus articularis, &lt;e1&gt;cujus species peculiares sunt ischias, podagra, chiragra, &amp; alii morbi hujusmodi, ut apud Gorræum in Definitionibus medicis pag. 50 fusius explicatur&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011301Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011301Z">
        <seg>Arthritis is called a Latin disease of the joints, whose particular species are sciatica, gout, chiragra, and other such diseases, as is more fully explained by Gorræus in his *Medical Definitions*, page 50.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g Hypochondrion, &lt;e0&gt;quasi Latine dicas&lt;/e0&gt; subcartilagineum, &lt;e1&gt;quia sub cartilagine positum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025842Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025842Z">
        <seg>Hypochondrium, as if you were to say in Latin, subcartilagineum, because it is situated under the cartilage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g Hæmorrhagia &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;αἱμοῤῥαγία&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;est copiosus immoderatusque sanguinis effluxus, ab&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;αἷμα&lt;/s3&gt; sanguis, &lt;e4&gt;&amp;&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;ῥέω&lt;/s5&gt; fluo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061001Z">
        <seg>Hemorrhage from Greek&lt;/e0&gt; αἱμοῤῥαγία&lt;/s1&gt; is an abundant and excessive flow of blood, from&lt;/e2&gt; αἷμα&lt;/s3&gt; blood, &amp;&lt;/e4&gt; ῥέω&lt;/s5&gt; to flow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g Ignis volaticus &lt;e0&gt;&amp;&lt;/e0&gt; volatilis &lt;e1&gt;idem est, ni fallor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034441Z">
        <seg>A flying fire and a volatile one are the same thing, if I'm not mistaken.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g Magnes &lt;e0&gt;a Græcis dicitur&lt;/e0&gt; siderites, &lt;e1&gt;estque lapis occulta naturæ vi ferrum ad se trahens, a voce Græca&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;σίδηρον&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;qua ferrum significatur&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054742Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054742Z">
        <seg>A magnet is called a siderite by the Greeks, and is a stone that draws iron to itself by a hidden natural force, from the Greek word σίδηρον, which signifies iron.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g Pericardion &lt;e0&gt;est tunica crassa, dura, a cordis basi nata &amp; ipsum undique ambiens, quampropterea vulgus&lt;/e0&gt; capsulam cordis &lt;e1&gt;appellat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052950Z">
        <seg>The pericardium is a thick, tough sac arising from the base of the heart and surrounding it on all sides; for this reason, the common people call it the heart's capsule.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g Quito &lt;e0&gt;est provincia &amp; urbs Americæ, in qua est conventus juridicus &amp; episcopalis cathedra, quemadmodum Joannes Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061659Z">
        <seg>Quito is a province and city of America, in which there is a legal assembly and a episcopal see, as John Latius states in his book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g Spasmus, &lt;e0&gt;Latine&lt;/e0&gt; convulsio, &lt;e1&gt;est contractio nervorum, rigor, aut distentio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040140Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040140Z">
        <seg>Spasm, Latin: a convulsion, is a contraction of the nerves, rigidity, or distension.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>gaudebat, se teneri in dextera Salvatoris velut rosam electam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061916Z">
        <seg>He rejoiced to be held in the Savior's right hand like a chosen rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>geniculata in Rosariano divæ Virginis sacello.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044241Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044241Z">
        <seg>Kneel in the chapel of the Virgin Mary's Rosary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>gravius dolebat, si replicarent, ex mera humilitate similia dici;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040549Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040549Z">
        <seg>He would have been more distressed if they had replied that such things were said out of mere humility.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>guttur cum tonsillis, instar pumicum emarcuisse præ ariditate ac siti, (&amp; quidni in cruce audiatur &lt;b1&gt;Sitio&lt;/b1&gt;?) cranium, velut incudem, ictibus creberrimis ac lacertosis affatim contundi, nec ullum ex omnibus suppliciis vel ad momentum interquiescere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013536Z">
        <seg>the throat, with the tonsils, had withered like pumice from drought and thirst, (and why should it not be heard on the cross, "I thirst"?) the skull, like an anvil, was thoroughly pounded by the most frequent and powerful blows, and not one of all the tortures paused for even a moment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Adjectivum&lt;/e0&gt; sympathetica &lt;e1&gt;provenit a nomine appellativo&lt;/e1&gt; sympathia, &lt;e2&gt;quod a præpositione&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;συν&lt;/s3&gt; cum, &lt;e4&gt;&amp;&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;πάθεια&lt;/s5&gt; passio &lt;e6&gt;componitur, &amp; verbum de verbo&lt;/e6&gt; compassionem &lt;e7&gt;vel&lt;/e7&gt; condolentiam &lt;e8&gt;significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054745Z">
        <seg>The adjective sympathetic&lt;/e0&gt; comes from the noun&lt;/e1&gt; sympathy&lt;/e1&gt;, which is composed of the prefix συν&lt;/s3&gt; with, &amp;&lt;/e4&gt; πάθεια&lt;/s5&gt; passion, and literally means&lt;/e6&gt; compassion or sympathy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Laudatus Gorræus pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055551Z">
        <seg>Praised be Gorræus, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Laudatus Gorræus pag. 8 Operis proxime citati de hac specie tumoris ita scribit:&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;Ἀθέρωμα&lt;/s1&gt; est tumor concolor, doloris expers, in quo aliquid pulticulæ, quæ &lt;s2&gt;ἀθήρα&lt;/s2&gt; vocatur, simile: tunica quadam membranosa concluditur, cum quo simul aliquando lapidosa quædam duraque corpuscula, &amp; alia instar sulphuris ramenta reperiuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011304Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011304Z">
        <seg>The praised Gorræus, on page 8 of the work most recently cited, writes thus about this type of tumor: "An &lt;s1&gt;atheroma&lt;/s1&gt; is a tumor of a uniform color, free from pain, in which there is something like a pulp, called &lt;s2&gt;athera&lt;/s2&gt;:" is enclosed by a certain membranous tunica, with which at times are found certain stony and hard particles, and other debris resembling sulfur.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Non dubito, quin hic potus sanctæ Virgini nostræ, ex jejuniis ac pœnitentiis attenuatæ, profuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052358Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052358Z">
        <seg>I have no doubt that this drink of our holy Virgin, weakened by fasts and penances, has been beneficial.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Per&lt;/e0&gt; febres ephimeras &lt;e1&gt;vel potius&lt;/e1&gt; ephemeras &lt;e2&gt;intelliguntur&lt;/e2&gt; diurnæ &lt;e3&gt;vel potius&lt;/e3&gt; ephemeras &lt;e4&gt;intelliguntur&lt;/e4&gt; diurnæ &lt;e5&gt;vel quotidianæ, a radice&lt;/e5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;ἐπὶ&lt;/s6&gt; per &lt;e7&gt;&amp;&lt;/e7&gt; &lt;s8&gt;ἡμέρα&lt;/s8&gt; dies, &lt;e9&gt;quasi per diem durans&lt;/e9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052956Z">
        <seg>By intermittent fevers, diurnal, or rather ephemeral, are understood diurnal, or rather ephemeral, are understood diurnal, or rather ephemeral, are understood diurnal, or daily, from the root ἐπὶ&lt;/e5&gt; through &amp;&lt;/e7&gt; ἡμέρα&lt;/s8&gt; day, as if lasting through the day&lt;/e9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Poëtæ fabulantur, crines Medusæ in angues conversos fuisse, qui eam vim haberent, ut omnes illud caput aspicientes obrigescerent, ac lapidescerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033056Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033056Z">
        <seg>Poets fabulize that Medusa's hair was turned into snakes, which had the power to petrify anyone who looked upon her head.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Primum hæsitavi nesciens, quid illa&lt;/e0&gt; unctione magistrali &lt;e1&gt;significaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034447Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034447Z">
        <seg>At first I hesitated, not knowing what that masterful unction signified.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Veneroni in Lexico suo Italico testatur, Pentecosten ab Italis vernacule&lt;/e0&gt; Pasqua rosata &lt;e1&gt;vel&lt;/e1&gt; Pasqua di Maggio &lt;e2&gt;vocari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T000148Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035201Z">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Veneroni, in his Italian Lexicon, testifies that Pentecost is vernacularly called by Italians&lt;/e0&gt; Pasqua rosata &lt;e1&gt;or&lt;/e1&gt; Pasqua di Maggio&lt;e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h Caustica, &lt;e0&gt;id est urens vel inflammata intemperies, ex adjectivo&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;καυστικὸς&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;id est&lt;/e2&gt;, vim urendi habens, &lt;e3&gt;a verbo&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;καίω&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;quod est&lt;/e5&gt;, uro, accendo &lt;e6&gt;vel&lt;/e6&gt; cremo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025857Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025857Z">
        <seg>h Caustic, &lt;e0&gt;that is, burning or inflamed heat, from the adjective&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;kaustikos&lt;/s1&gt;, that is, having the power to burn, from the verb καίω, which is to burn, to set on fire, or to consume.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h Ceroma, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;κήρωμα&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;est unguentum ex oleo &amp; cera mixtum, quo veteres athletæ se ungere solebant, ut robustiores essent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035241Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035241Z">
        <seg>Ceroma, &lt;e0&gt;Greek&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;κήρωμα&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;is an ointment made from oil and wax, which the ancient athletes used to anoint themselves with to become stronger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h Circitor &lt;e0&gt;vel&lt;/e0&gt; circuitor &lt;e1&gt;olim a præfecto Romæ constitui solebat, ut urbem circuiret vel custodibus ejus invigilaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061705Z">
        <seg>A watchman or patroller was formerly appointed by the prefect of Rome to patrol the city or to keep watch over its guards.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h Malagma &lt;e0&gt;est proprie fomentum vel emplastrum&lt;/e0&gt;, &lt;e1&gt;quod ad rem duram emolliendam adhibetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040150Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040150Z">
        <seg>A malagm is properly a fomentation or plaster, which is applied to soften a hard matter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hac faciem infirmæ operuit, simulque Rosæ imploravit auxilium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040531Z">
        <seg>She covered her weak face with this, and at the same time implored Rose for help.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hac una meditatione acutissimos quosque hujus temporis dolores obtundi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052655Z">
        <seg>With this single meditation, to dull the sharpest pains of this time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hac ustulatione sibi ossa incinerari, (nimirum crucis holocaustum erat) medullas ac tendines frustillatim candenti forcipe evelli, passim per fibras oberrare acutissimum gelu, ipsaque tot flammularum antiperistasi&lt;a0&gt;e&lt;/a0&gt; sævius irritari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013524Z">
        <seg>by this roasting, his bones were being incinerated (for it was, in effect, a holocaust of the cross), his marrow and tendons torn away bit by bit with a white-hot forceps, the keenest frost wandering through his fibers, and the very antiperistasis of so many flames made all the more savage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hanc Lucia capiti reverenter admovit, eadem oculos defricuit, &amp; illo temporis puncto, simul cum lippitudine omnem capitis dolorem abstersit, inque miraculi fidem, ipso adhuc suæ curationis die acum arripuit, &amp; ante vesperam subtilioris telæ quatuor quadra consuendo absolvit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034621Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034621Z">
        <seg>Lucia reverently applied this to the head, rubbed the eyes with it, and at that very moment, along with the squint, she wiped away all the headache. As proof of the miracle, she, still on the very day of her cure, seized a needle and by evening had finished sewing four squares of a finer cloth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hanc ardentibus ocellis jugiter adurebat, omnique gestu, motu, flexu, protestabatur amorem&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010539Z">
        <seg>He constantly burned her with his ardent eyes, and with every gesture, movement, and turn, he proclaimed his love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hanc ardentibus ocellis jugiter adurebat, omnique gestu, motu, flexu, protestabatur amorem&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050926Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050926Z">
        <seg>He constantly burned her with his ardent eyes, and with every gesture, movement, and turn, he proclaimed his love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hanc brevi (se moriente) mansuram solam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013300Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013300Z">
        <seg>this alone will remain brief (as it dies).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hanc carbones desolatorii coxerunt in internæ derelictionis pœnali sartagine.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045444Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045444Z">
        <seg>They cooked these desolating coals in the punitive frying pan of internal abandonment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hanc deperibat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054115Z">
        <seg>was perishing for this;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hanc prompsit, ut in sacello SS. Rosarii imaginem divæ Virginis speciosus hic torques ornaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043522Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043522Z">
        <seg>He presented this so that in the chapel of the Holy Rosary, this splendid torque might adorn the image of the Blessed Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>has autem Rosa jacere humi, &amp; commeantium pedibus neglectim proculcari non ferebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054856Z">
        <seg>But Rosa could not bear to see them lying on the ground and being carelessly trampled by the feet of passersby.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>haudprocul abesse horam, quæ domum Gundisalvi a fœtidæ Rosæ longo fastidio inutilique pondere liberaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030034Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030034Z">
        <seg>The hour was not far off that would free Gundisalvus's home from the foul Rose's long tedium and useless burden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hausit eosdem pulveres, &amp; in ictu oculi sese integerrime sanatam vidit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035843Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035843Z">
        <seg>He inhaled the same dust, and in the blink of an eye saw himself perfectly healed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hi fuere undecim;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044049Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044049Z">
        <seg>These were eleven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hi vero etiam post Rosæ obitum perrexerunt ejusdem Apostoli recurrentes vigilias spontaneo celebrare jejunio, nec aliam norant piæ consuetudinis rationem dare, quam quod sic a Rosa fuerant instituti.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013105Z">
        <seg>Indeed, even after Rose's death they continued to hold the same Apostle's vigils by voluntarily fasting, and they knew of no other reason for this pious custom than that they had been so instructed by Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic Rosæ matrem ea donavit: sic intra pauculos dies Maria de Oliva pro unico suo pallio, quod Rosa egenæ Montoyæ dederat, tria recepit nova ac meliora, didicitque animosius fidere tum vaticinio Filiæ, tum providi Numinis largitati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061144Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061144Z">
        <seg>Here Rosa gave her mother this gift: thus, within a few days, Maria de Oliva received three new and better cloaks in exchange for her only cloak, which Rosa had given to the needy woman of Montoya, and she learned to trust more boldly both the prophecy of the Daughter and the providence of the generous Deity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic Virginem obiter salutans, simul involutum strophiolo argentum porrexit, jubens illo præsentem necessitatem parentum sublevari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062254Z">
        <seg>Greeting the Virgin in passing, he at the same time offered a silver coin wrapped in a small cloth, ordering that it be used to relieve the present need of the parents.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic aptissime per varia pietatis exercitia dispensabat horas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043658Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043658Z">
        <seg>He most fittingly spent his hours on various exercises of piety.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic calculos precarios, [ingens populi tumultus ortus est,] ille cerea agnorum Dei amuleta, alius sudarium, numisma, coronulas, &amp; quidquid charum ad manus erat, contingendo sacro corpori eminus cominus suppliciter prætendebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031007Z">
        <seg>This one precious stones, [a great tumult of the people arose,] that one amulets of the lambs of God, another a handkerchief, a coin, garlands, and whatever was dear at hand, by touching the sacred body, humbly stretched out from a distance and up close.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic ille inter amica, ut fit, colloquia, delapsus ad varia, cœpit narrare perfunctorie, quæ Rosæ (tunc iisdem ædibus cohabitantis) mirabilem vitæ sanctimoniam, castigationem corporis, operumque &amp; morum præstantiam commendabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040602Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040602Z">
        <seg>Here he, among his friends, as is customary, straying into various topics, began to recount perfunctorily those things which extolled to Rosa (then living in the same house) the marvelous sanctity of life, the discipline of the body, and the excellence of her deeds and manners.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic liberrimo contemplationis usu ascensiones in corde sua disponebat, propemodum ignara, in corpore an extra corpus degeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043701Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043701Z">
        <seg>She arranged her ascensions in her heart with the freest exercise of contemplation, almost unaware whether she was in the body or outside it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic manum, ille pedes raptim deosculari studebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030403Z">
        <seg>He was eager to hastily kiss this one's hand, that one's feet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic partem affectivam increati Amoris decidua flamma igniri &amp; candescere, atque in palato voluntatis felicem beatæ fruitionis prægustum inchoari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050327Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050327Z">
        <seg>Here the affective part of uncreated Love is kindled and made bright by a fleeting flame, and on the palate of the will a happy foretaste of blessed fruition is begun.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic solum abundabat fluida ejus facundia, hic ei inardescebat spiritus, acuebatur vox, fulgurabant oculi, succendebantur præcordia, nemoque præsentium dubitare poterat, ex abundantia cordis os loqui.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053207Z">
        <seg>Here his fertile eloquence flowed abundantly; his spirit burned within him, his voice sharpened, his eyes flashed, his heart was kindled, and no one present could doubt that his mouth spoke from the abundance of his heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic totos hærebat dies addita majori noctis parte;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043655Z">
        <seg>He would stay here all day, adding the greater part of the night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hinc demum ad alias, quæ paulatim fundabantur, sacras ædes tota Sacramentorum administratio promanavit, ac velut ex uno augustissimæ cælorum Reginæ parthenico roseto, ver nascentis fidei in omnem circuitum floride se diffudit&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011041Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011041Z">
        <seg>From this, at last, the entire administration of the Sacraments flowed forth to the other sacred edifices that were gradually being founded, and, as if from a single, most august, parthenic rose-garden of the Queen of Heaven, the spring of the nascent faith spread itself in a flourishing bloom in every direction.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hinc demum ad alias, quæ paulatim fundabantur, sacras ædes tota Sacramentorum administratio promanavit, ac velut ex uno augustissimæ cælorum Reginæ parthenico roseto, ver nascentis fidei in omnem circuitum floride se diffudit&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;a&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054233Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054233Z">
        <seg>From this, at last, the entire administration of the Sacraments flowed forth to the other sacred edifices that were gradually being founded, and, as if from a single, most august, virgin rose-garden of the Queen of Heaven, the spring of the nascent faith spread itself in a flourishing bloom in every direction.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hinc illa festiva &amp; blanda cum Dilecto soliloquia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025637Z">
        <seg>Hence those festive and gentle soliloquies with the Beloved;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hinc importunitate creditoris, inde necessitatibus domus, &amp; cumprimis ægri mariti, sensim impar.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062247Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062247Z">
        <seg>Thus, from the creditor's importunity on one hand, and the needs of the household on the other, and especially of the sick husband, she was gradually becoming inadequate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hinc radiare continuo in animæ suæ fundo præsentiam serenæ, amabilis, propitiæ Divinitatis, hanc certo se illic deprehendere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050305Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050305Z">
        <seg>from this, to continually radiate into the depths of his soul the presence of a serene, loving, and propitious Divinity, and to perceive it there for certain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hinc tantopere urebatur, sicubi privatim, aut publice quidquam committeretur, quod Dei honorem vel in minimo violaret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053303Z">
        <seg>He was so zealous that he would not tolerate anything being done, either privately or publicly, that would in the slightest violate the honor of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hinc urgebat animi inquietudo ad nonnulla ex dictis seu moderanda seu retractanda;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032237Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032237Z">
        <seg>Hence, a restlessness of spirit urged him to either moderate or retract some of the things he had said.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hinc, tentato pulsu, deprehendisse febrium absentiam, denique per faciem ducta manu, nullum adusti tumoris palpasse vestigium, jamque se &amp; integerrime valere, &amp; vivaciter esurire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040856Z">
        <seg>Thus, having felt his pulse, he discovered that the fever was gone; finally, running his hand over his face, he detected no trace of a blister, and now found himself in perfect health and feeling a keen hunger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his denique totum collucere cubiculum, imminere palam incendium: at iis procul abjectis, mox ardor, flamma, cruciatus evanuit, &amp; Rosa miræ suavitatis refrigerio cælitus perfusa dulcissime conquievit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035359Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035359Z">
        <seg>At last the entire room seemed to glow, and an open fire loomed: but when these were cast far away, the heat, the flame, and the torment vanished, and Rosa, bathed from heaven in a wondrously sweet coolness, rested most sweetly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his energia mirabili inculcabat timorem Dei, virtutis studium, amorem parentum, quibus satagerent tranquillam &amp; lætam conciliare senectutem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030158Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030158Z">
        <seg>He instilled in them, with marvelous energy, the fear of God, a love of virtue, and a love for their parents, by which they would secure for them a tranquil and happy old age.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his faciem &amp; manus obsidere festinabat importuna societas: repellebatur unus, succedebant quaterni, &amp; plerumque ex insidiis etiam incautorum sanguinem hauriebant, relicto pruritus ac tuberculi vestigio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043807Z">
        <seg>An importunate horde rushed to besiege his face and hands: one was repelled, four would take his place, and they would often draw blood from the unwary from ambush, leaving behind a trace of itching and pustules.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his in partem retractis, gaudebat moribunda, quod se lecticæ tabulis sentiret occipite humerisque acclinem, mirificeque contenta, quod crucifixo Sponso qualitercumque commoreretur in duro ligno, felicem spiritum paulo post in manus Creatoris placidissime exhalavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043007Z">
        <seg>When these were set aside, the dying woman rejoiced that she felt herself leaning on the planks of the bier against her head and shoulders, and was wonderfully content that she would dwell with her crucified Spouse in the hard wood. A little later, she peacefully breathed out her happy spirit into the hands of the Creator.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his inermem jactando, trudendo, premendo miserrime concutiebat, ut jam singula Virgini ossa viderentur commolita, luxatæ scapulæ, nervi distracti;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052122Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052122Z">
        <seg>He was miserably shaking her, throwing her, thrusting her, and pressing her, so that each of the Virgin's bones seemed crushed, her shoulder blades dislocated, her nerves torn;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his intererat usque ad meridiem tanta quiete ac silentio, quanto nec lapis in suo defigitur centro.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060019Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060019Z">
        <seg>It remained like this until noon, with such peace and silence that not even a stone held fast in its center.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his pro acu malleus &amp; scalprum, pro lana durissimi lapides &amp; saxa erant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044210Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044210Z">
        <seg>In place of a needle, there was a hammer and a chisel; instead of wool, there were the hardest stones and rocks.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his pulvinum implevit, impleto indormivit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042738Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042738Z">
        <seg>He filled this pillow, and fell asleep on it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his regerebat, utique sibi, quæ sola se nosset, fidem habendam esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040552Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040552Z">
        <seg>He was guided by this: that he should trust himself, who alone knew himself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his sine culcitra paleave simplex ac detritum peripetasma instravit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042911Z">
        <seg>He laid a simple and worn-out mat, without a cushion, for them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his tundendis, æquandis, &amp; ad normam affabre conquadrandis instabant totæ, secabant, excavabant, lævigabant, defricabant, &amp;, ut ferramenta citius durior lapis admitteret, hunc affatim emolliebant crebro suarum lachrymarum stillicidio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044213Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044213Z">
        <seg>They were occupied with pounding, leveling, and skillfully squaring these to a standard; they cut, hollowed, smoothed, and scraped, and, so that the tool would take a harder stone more quickly, they thoroughly softened it with the frequent trickle of their tears.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his velut fiduciariis intimorum conceptuum indicibus excitari in anima sua quamdam attentionem luminosam, qua sine discursu penetret limpideque percipiat singula;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054400Z">
        <seg>to be stirred in his soul by these, as though by intimate fiduciaries of his innermost thoughts, a certain luminous attention, by which, without discursive reasoning, he may clearly perceive each thing;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hispidum, ex crassioribus equorum setis prædense contextum, vix non loricæ gravitatem æquabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041842Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041842Z">
        <seg>The coarse, plaited from the coarser horsehair, scarcely equaled the weight of a cuirass.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hoc Puellam jam grandiusculam frequentius delumbare solebat, præsertim quando comperta fuit, exemplo S. Catharinæ Senensis, rutilam sibimet cæsariem præscidisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052506Z">
        <seg>He used to frequently tickle the now-older girl, especially when it was discovered that, following the example of St. Catherine of Siena, she had cut a red lock of hair for herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hoc age, obsecro, hoc unicum stude &amp; conare, ut quam plurimas flagitiosas animas vorticibus eximas, ut ab ima vitiorum charybdi perituros evoces, &amp; ad salutaris pœnitentiæ securum litus educas”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060601Z">
        <seg>Do this, I beg you, strive for this one thing: to rescue as many wretched souls as possible from the whirlpools, to snatch from the very Charybdis of vices those who are about to perish, and to bring them to the safe shore of salutary repentance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hoc ipse demum eventus plene comprobavit: nam &amp; pater post Rosam annis novem supervixit, &amp; in conversione perditorum hominum, fructus, quos illa prædixerat, admirabiles reportavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012302Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012302Z">
        <seg>This very outcome fully proved it: for the father also survived Rosa by nine years, and in the conversion of lost people, he reported the fruits that she had foretold, which were marvelous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hoc sanctæ patientiæ mercimonium est, &amp; utile fœnus: nemo quereretur de cruce, si hanc tantorum thesaurorum agnoscerent legitimam stateram”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052838Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052838Z">
        <seg>This is the merchandise of holy patience, and a useful interest: no one would complain of the cross, if they recognized this as the legitimate scale for such great treasures.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hoc studio singulari colebat devotius, nec suo contenta jejunio, aliquot innocentes puerulos induxit, ut secum in hujus festi pervigilio jejunarent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013102Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013102Z">
        <seg>He observed this unique devotion more devoutly, and not content with his own fast, he persuaded several innocent little boys to fast with him on the vigil of this feast.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hoc, si ullum aliud, dicendæ veritatis tempus esse, nunc vel maxime ab ea se filialis benevolentiæ sancta obsequia exspectare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012346Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012346Z">
        <seg>This, if ever, is the time to speak the truth, and now above all she expects from him the sacred observances of filial affection.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>horam, qua mane surgeret, præscripserunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051319Z">
        <seg>They prescribed the hour at which he should rise in the morning.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>horum examina &amp; agmina immigrabant Rosæ tugurio, præsertim, quando vel per diem siccantes radii solis, aut sub vesperum serenæ noctis algores tenuissimis corpusculis imminebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043751Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043751Z">
        <seg>These swarms and troops would migrate to Rose's hut, especially when either the sun's drying rays by day, or the chills of a clear evening under a serene night, threatened their tiny bodies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hos Rosa digitis induit, opus arripuit, cœpitque sedulitate liberrima gemmas, torques, monilia suo, quo adhærebant, ordine disnectere, fila ligaminum apte dissuere, nodos incidere, ornatum per partes concinne deponere, imaginem nudare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055408Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055408Z">
        <seg>Rosa put on these gloves, seized the task, and with the utmost diligence began to unloose the gems, torcs, and bracelets from their setting, deftly to undo the threads of the fastenings, to cut the knots, to lay out the adornment in its proper parts, and to bare the image.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hos tam acutos, sævos, multiplices fore, ut cæteri, quos illactenus pertulerat, istis collati, vix nomen doloris mereantur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013229Z">
        <seg>these would be so acute, fierce, and manifold that the others he had endured until then, when compared to them, would scarcely deserve the name of pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huc se impelli divinis stimulis, quibus omnino parendum sit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012017Z">
        <seg>to be driven by divine impulses, to which one must entirely obey;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huc se vires, opes, cogitationes omnes conversuram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012021Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012021Z">
        <seg>She will turn all her strength, resources, and thoughts to this.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huc toto ferebatur amoris pondere, adeo ut horis integris vix (aut forte ne vix quidem) nictu unico cilia dimisisse, multo minus faciem vel ad momentum ab ara deflexisse notata sit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060025Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060025Z">
        <seg>She was carried here, almost overwhelmed by the weight of her love, so that for entire hours she barely—or perhaps not even barely—let her eyelids flutter once, much less is it recorded that she ever turned her face away from the altar, even for a moment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic Rosa nocte concubia se clare visibilem objicit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032058Z">
        <seg>This Rose, who lies with him at night, clearly reveals herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic adversus somni insidias modicum id capillitii, quod obtegendæ coronæ in fronte residuum superfuerat, implectebat strictissime, eoque tormento accersebat vigiliam, recitabat preculas, somnolentiam strangulabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043100Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043100Z">
        <seg>Against the snares of sleep, he would most tightly bind that little tuft of hair which had remained on his forehead to cover his crown, and with that torment he would summon wakefulness, recite prayers, and strangle drowsiness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic arridebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050919Z">
        <seg>He smiled at him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic assidebat immobilis, hanc dilapso frequentiori populo cominus tenerrimis basiis obterebat, in hanc eminus acclavabat oculos, nec alio vel ad momentum respicere poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054844Z">
        <seg>He sat motionless beside her; when the crowd thinned, he showered her with the most tender kisses; he fixed his eyes on her from a distance and could not look away, not even for a moment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic febres &amp; papulæ denuntiabant præmaturam mortis sententiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040748Z">
        <seg>Fevers and papules announced a premature death sentence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic festum diem agebat Rosa, non sic lætatura, si totius Americæ sceptrum sibi, imperiumque sorte vel dono obtigisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053430Z">
        <seg>Rosa was celebrating this festive day, not that she would have been any happier if she had been granted the scepter and empire of all America by lot or as a gift.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic inferne spinea corona circumjacet, superne tres clavi infixi prominent, foliorum ambitum quasi filatim pendula flagella interstingunt, medium sinum acervatim implent granula eximii saporis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041253Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041253Z">
        <seg>Below, a crown of thorns surrounds it; above, three nails are fixed and protrude. The surrounding leaves are interspersed with thread-like, pendulous tendrils. The center of the sinus is filled in heaps with granules of exquisite flavor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic intranti una ex adstantibus feminis bona fide dixit: “Opportune venis, o Pater;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053340Z">
        <seg>As he entered, one of the women present said to him in all sincerity: "You have come at the right time, O Father;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic mittebat crebra ex imo pectore suspiria;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054112Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054112Z">
        <seg>He sent her frequent sighs from the bottom of his heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic protendebat breves ulnas velut amplexum offerens;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050922Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050922Z">
        <seg>He stretched out his forearms toward him, as if offering a embrace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic rei seriem, infirma annuente, exposuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012932Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012932Z">
        <seg>He laid out a series of reasons for this matter, with the weaker ones being allowed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic solemne est, quotannis ter in publici circuitus festivam processionem sublimi gestatorio efferre conspicuam sanctæ suæ tutelaris statuam, coronis, floribus, multoque gemmarum fulgore ac pretio decenter adornatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055020Z">
        <seg>It is the custom here to carry on a festive procession three times a year around the public circuit the conspicuous statue of their patron saint, suitably adorned with crowns, flowers, and much glitter and preciousness of gems.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hujus liberalitati diffidere mihi jam non licet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062340Z">
        <seg>I can no longer doubt this generosity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hujus ministerium &amp; curam, utpote in familiari domo permissu matris, sibi Rosa assumpserat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061336Z">
        <seg>Rosa had taken upon herself the care and service of this one, as she was in the family home with her mother's permission.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hujus pharmacopœiæ se commendat, medicinam corroborativam petit, impetrat, nec aliunde, quam ex illo pretioso myrothecio vulnerati lateris, ubi quondam S. Catharina Senensis avide suxerat&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010552Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010552Z">
        <seg>He commends himself to this apothecary, asks for a strengthening medicine, and obtains it, not from anywhere else but from that precious myrrh-box of the wounded side, from which St. Catherine of Siena once eagerly sucked.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hujus pharmacopœiæ se commendat, medicinam corroborativam petit, impetrat, nec aliunde, quam ex illo pretioso myrothecio vulnerati lateris, ubi quondam S. Catharina Senensis avide suxerat&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051115Z">
        <seg>He commends himself to this apothecary, asks for a strengthening medicine, and obtains it, and from no other source than that precious myrrh-bearing ointment of the wounded side, from which St. Catherine of Siena once eagerly sucked.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hujus providam curam mihi assumo: tu de reliquis, ut magis volueris, disponito”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061909Z">
        <seg>I will take charge of this with careful foresight; you may arrange the rest as you see fit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hunc Hispani, sed magis Hispanæ, ita depereunt, ut postquam semel illi assueverunt, se sine ullo vivere posse negent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052352Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052352Z">
        <seg>The Spaniards, but especially the Spanish women, are so infatuated with him that once they have grown accustomed to him, they deny that they can live without him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hunc enim ut digito indidit, confestim morbus evanuit, nec in reliquis partubus (nam sæpius deinceps peperit) ulli mariscarum mordacitati subjacuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035011Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035011Z">
        <seg>For as soon as she touched it with her finger, the disease vanished at once, and in her subsequent births (for she bore young several times afterward) she was not subject to any of the sea-urchin's stinginess.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hunc generosum, præclarum, sublimem studiis suis finem scopumque præfigerent, ut residuis Americæ plagis, quidquid idololatriæ usquam supererat, profligent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060440Z">
        <seg>that they would set this generous, excellent, and sublime endeavor a goal and an end, so that with the remaining plagues of America, they would drive out whatever was left of idolatry anywhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hunc sibi dolorem videri prorsus intolerabilem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054143Z">
        <seg>this pain seemed to him to be utterly unbearable.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hæc Limæ nata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T214204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211808Z">
        <seg>she, born in Lima;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hæc Rosam absque alio magistro edocuerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052252Z">
        <seg>She had taught Rosa without another teacher.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hæc circa gallicinium evigilans, vidit per tenebras frequentia micare lumina in cubiculo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053018Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053018Z">
        <seg>Waking around the cock's crow, she saw in the darkness a multitude of eyes sparkling in the room.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hæc enim lectulo suo clam, ad fundendas preces, in pavimentum sese demiserat, &amp; ubi maxime in obscuro se latere credebat, prodebatur lucidis igniculis, per os oculosque inter orandum evibratis, testante prodigio, quantum incendium fervebat intus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053024Z">
        <seg>For she had secretly let herself down from her bed onto the floor to pour out her prayers, and where she thought she was hiding most securely in the darkness, she was betrayed by bright sparks, shaken from her mouth and eyes as she prayed, a prodigy that testified to how fierce a fire raged within.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hæc habet:&lt;/e0&gt; Sed quis per hunc servum, qui a patrefamilias ad invitandum mittitur, nisi prædicatorum ordo designatur?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041405Z">
        <seg>It reads: "But who is designated by this servant, who is sent by the head of the household to invite, if not the order of preachers?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hæc ignoto percusa gaudio, vertit oculos in parvulum, ipsumque similiter arridentem sibi conspexit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044315Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044315Z">
        <seg>Struck with joy by this unknown thing, she turned her eyes to the little boy and saw him smiling at her in the same way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hæc in cælum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012210Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012210Z">
        <seg>this to heaven;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i &lt;e0&gt;Ad hanc celeberrimam Deiparæ imaginem fit magnus populi concursus, ut ipse vidi, quando illam prope Matritum veneratus sum.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054751Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054751Z">
        <seg>A great crowd of people gathers at this most celebrated image of the Virgin Mary, as I myself saw when I venerated it near Madrid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i &lt;e0&gt;Castellus pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055600Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055600Z">
        <seg>Castle page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i &lt;e0&gt;Castellus pag. 520 Lexici medici de meliceride habet sequentia:&lt;/e0&gt; Meliceris, &lt;s1&gt;μελικηρὶς&lt;/s1&gt;, humorem melli similem membrana conclusum habens, species est apostematis, ut &amp; &lt;s2&gt;ἀθέρωμα&lt;/s2&gt; &amp; &lt;s3&gt;στεάτωμα&lt;/s3&gt;, quorum hoc ab adipe, illud a pulticula intra membranam conclusa nomen habet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011308Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011308Z">
        <seg>i &lt;e0&gt;Castellus, p. 520 of the Medical Lexicon, has the following regarding meliceris:&lt;/e0&gt; Meliceris, &lt;s1&gt;μελικηρὶς&lt;/s1&gt;, having a membrane enclosing a honey-like humor, is a species of apostema, like &amp; &lt;s2&gt;athērōma&lt;/s2&gt; &amp; &lt;s3&gt;steatōma&lt;/s3&gt;, of which this one takes its name from fat, that one from a porridge-like substance enclosed within a membrane.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i &lt;e0&gt;Hic venerabilis archiepiscopus Limanus die 23 Martii anno 1606 obiit, quem Innocentius XI Pontifex anno 1679 primum Beatis, ac deinde Benedictus XIII Sanctis adscripsit, ut apud illustrissimum Fontaninum in Codice Canonizationum pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035207Z">
        <seg>This venerable Archbishop of Lima died on March 23, 1606, whom Pope Innocent XI in 1679 first enrolled among the Blessed, and then Pope Benedict XIII among the Saints, as is found in the Most Illustrious Fontanini's Codex Canonizationum, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i &lt;e0&gt;Hic venerabilis archiepiscopus Limanus die 23 Martii anno 1606 obiit, quem Innocentius XI Pontifex anno 1679 primum Beatis, ac deinde Benedictus XIII Sanctis adscripsit, ut apud illustrissimum Fontaninum in Codice Canonizationum pag. 578 &amp; sequentibus videre est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T000308Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T165635Z">
        <seg>i &lt;e0&gt;This venerable Archbishop of Lima died on March 23, 1606, whom Pope Innocent XI in 1679 first enrolled among the Blessed, and then Pope Benedict XIII among the Saints, as is found in the Most Illustrious Fontanini's Codex Canonizationum, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i &lt;e0&gt;Nomine&lt;/e0&gt; secundarum &lt;e1&gt;appellatur illa membrana, qua fœtus in utero matris obvolvitur, &amp; quæ ordinarie nascentem infantem sequitur, ut passim notum est, &amp; in Opere nostro jam sæpius recurrit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034506Z">
        <seg>By the name of membranes the second one is called that membrane by which the fetus in the mother's womb is enveloped, and which ordinarily follows the newborn infant, as is well known and as has already been mentioned several times in our work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i Chiron &lt;e0&gt;est nomen proprium expertissimi medici, de quo veteres poëtæ plurima fabulantur&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040156Z">
        <seg>Chiron is the proper name of the most expert physician, about whom the ancient poets tell many fables.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i Opopanax &lt;e0&gt;est succus herbæ, quæ&lt;/e0&gt; panacea &lt;e1&gt;vocatur, &amp; componitur ex&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;πᾶν&lt;/s2&gt; omne, &lt;e3&gt;&amp; verbo&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;ἀκέομαι&lt;/s4&gt; sano.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035251Z">
        <seg>Opopanax is the juice of the herb called panacea, and it is composed of πᾶν (all) and ἀκέομαι (to be healed).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i Paralysis (&lt;e0&gt;a verbo Græco&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;παραλύω&lt;/s1&gt; resolvo &lt;e2&gt;vel&lt;/e2&gt; debilito) &lt;e3&gt;est nervorum resolutio, cum nervi &amp; corporis partes omnino sensus &amp; motus expertes sunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052959Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052959Z">
        <seg>Paralysis (from the Greek word paralýō, to loosen or weaken) is the relaxation of the nerves, when the nerves and parts of the body are completely devoid of sensation and movement.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i Potosium &lt;e0&gt;est urbs Americæ in regno Peruano ad radices montis Potosii, &amp; argenti-fodinis inclyta, cujus situm, sterile solum, divitias ac fodinas proxime citatus Latius lib.&lt;/e0&gt; XI &lt;e1&gt;Indiæ occidentalis cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052404Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052404Z">
        <seg>Potosi is a city in the Kingdom of Peru in America, at the foot of the mountain of the same name, and renowned for its silver mines; its location, barren soil, wealth, and mines are described in the aforementioned book, Book XI, of the Western Indies, chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ibi domesticis se subducebat tumultibus, jocis, fabulis, uni sibi cæloque vacans;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043258Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043258Z">
        <seg>there he withdrew from the household's turmoil, games, and stories, devoted to himself and to the heavens.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ibi mox liberrimo ejulatu, ac lacrymis suam amarissime sortem incusans, verberabat innoxium pectus, despuebat laudationis suæ cramben, caligabat pudore sui;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040608Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040608Z">
        <seg>There, with a most free outcry, and with tears bitterly blaming his own lot, he beat his innocent breast, despised the dregs of his own praise, and was blinded by his own shame.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ibique vicinæ arboris frontem occupabat, quasi dato signo eruptura in cantum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045220Z">
        <seg>And there, the neighbor's tree stood at the front, as if about to burst into song at a given signal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>id unum se ardenter optare, imo &amp; sperare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012238Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012238Z">
        <seg>To ardently desire, indeed to hope for, that one thing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ideo frequenti hujus gratissimæ compellationis auditu velle se sui tum nominis tum officii sæpiuscule admoneri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034807Z">
        <seg>Therefore, by frequently hearing this most pleasant address, he wishes to be reminded from time to time of both his name and his office.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ideoque se intima animi sui sensa, uti vellet, apposite efferre non posse, ruditati suæ ne nomen quidem, si quod proprium extaret, hujus intuitivæ orationis innotuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050318Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050318Z">
        <seg>Therefore, he could not aptly express the innermost feelings of his soul; his ignorance was not even aware of a name, if any existed, for this intuitive mode of speech.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>idque non a libris acceptum, sed Spiritu sancto interius docente assecutam fuisse, mirifice innotuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041811Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041811Z">
        <seg>It was wonderfully evident that she had not received this from books, but had attained it through the Holy Spirit teaching her inwardly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>idque perspicuum satis indicium erat, Deo sic placitum esse, in asperrimi lecti tolerantiam electam Rosam dilecto suo Sponso concrucifigi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042716Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042716Z">
        <seg>And this was a clear enough sign that it was God's will for Rosa, chosen for her tolerance of the harshest bed, to be crucified with her beloved Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>igitur ex nunc tranquillo animo esto”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061813Z">
        <seg>Therefore, be of a calm mind from now on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>iisdem auxiliis ægre in tumuli gradibus eluctata, brachium vultui Rosæ bis terve applicuit, mox eodem temporis puncto exclamavit: “Euge, brachium meum sanatum est, &amp; revixit brachium meum”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033239Z">
        <seg>Struggling with the same supports up the steps of the tomb, she pressed her arm to Rose's face two or three times, and then at that very moment exclaimed: "Hurrah, my arm is healed, and my arm has come to life again."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illa confestim rapta in mentis excessum, perspicue intellexit, quod animo dudum agitarat, nempe divinum id esse responsum de tertio habitu Prædicatorii Ordinis tandem assumendo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040159Z">
        <seg>She was immediately seized by ecstasy and clearly understood what she had long pondered in her mind: that it was a divine response concerning the third habit of the Order of Preachers, which was finally to be assumed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illa maritum, hæc sancti Dominici habitum una cum Catharina sorore obtinuit: capillos, pompam, seculum, fervore ac lætitia inenarrabili ejuravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012523Z">
        <seg>She obtained a husband; the other, together with her sister Catherine, received the habit of Saint Dominic: she renounced her hair, her pomp, her worldly life, with ineffable fervor and joy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illaque fortius instante, promisit, se in singulas hebdomadas peculiariter certum diem selecturam, quo ipsam Deo commendaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062917Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062917Z">
        <seg>And with greater insistence, she promised that she would select a specific day each week on which she would commend herself to God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ille ex insula Hispanorum oriundus, cui a Portu divite&lt;a5&gt;e&lt;/a5&gt; nomen est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203312Z">
        <seg>he, a native of the island of the Spaniards, who is named from the Port of Divite&lt;a5&gt;e&lt;/a5&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ille ex insula Hispanorum oriundus, cui a Portu divite&lt;s10&gt;&lt;a11&gt;e&lt;/a11&gt;&lt;/s10&gt; nomen est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T193911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211805Z">
        <seg>he, a native of the island of the Spaniards, who is named from the Port of Divite&lt;s10&gt;&lt;a11&gt;e&lt;/a11&gt;&lt;/s10&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illi, ut in monasterio ad sacrum habitum susciperetur, opportunus adjutor fui;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040559Z">
        <seg>I was a helpful assistant to him in being received into the monastery to take holy orders.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illic Antonii consobrinus ad caput lectuli plangebat mortuum, &amp; intrantibus feminis mœsta voce occurrit: “Nolite (inquiens) vos incommodare: jam enim Antonius noster spiritum reddidit Creatori”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033201Z">
        <seg>There, at the head of the little bed, Antony's cousin was weeping over the dead man, and as the women entered, he met them in a mournful voice: "Do not trouble yourselves," he said, "for our Antony has already given up his spirit to the Creator."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illic Ordinis sui habitum, illic Jesulum Sponsum nacta, divelli a loco non poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051301Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051301Z">
        <seg>Having there received the habit of her Order, and there espoused to Jesus, she could not be moved from the place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illic coram lignea Rosæ icone procumbens, lineam calanticam suo detractam capiti admovit suppliciter virgineo simulachro, ac rursum capiti imposuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040317Z">
        <seg>There, kneeling before the wooden icon of the Rose, she pressed the line she had drawn on her head against the virgin's image in supplication, and then placed it on her head again.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illic decretum, hic secretum affligit mortales, nisi quod solis Dei amicis mori sit lucrum, &amp; insuper nonnullis horum, eximio favore Numinis, secretum diei &amp; horæ reveletur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013056Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013056Z">
        <seg>There it decrees, here it conceals from mortals, except that for the friends of God alone to die is a gain, and moreover to some of them, by the extraordinary favor of the Deity, the secret of the day and hour is revealed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illic in præparatam e cedro arcam reclinata, ferreis undique clavis occluduntur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031153Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031153Z">
        <seg>There, reclined in a coffin made of cedar, they are sealed with iron nails on all sides.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illic lacrymose procubua misericordiæ Matri angorem suum exposuit, remedium poposcit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034733Z">
        <seg>There, in tears, he prostrated himself before the Mother of Mercy, laid bare his anguish, and asked for a remedy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illic pro Christi corpore corpus meum vulneribus objiciam, nec cedam, donec impiorum bipennibus undique perfossa cadam, quinimo precabor hostes, ne uno ictu repente me conficiant, sed lenta crudelitate articulatim corpusculum hoc totum deartuent, inque modicissima frustula partes singulas comminuant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060233Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060233Z">
        <seg>There I will lay my body, a substitute for Christ's, open to wounds, and I will not yield, until I fall, pierced on all sides by the two-edged weapons of the wicked. Nay, I will even pray my enemies not to finish me off with a single blow, but to dismember this little body of mine limb by limb with slow cruelty, and to crush each part into the smallest fragments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illic se Virgini toto cordis affectu commendavit, loculum tetigit, domum sana rediit, nec umquam deinceps ea defluxione se sensit infestari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033821Z">
        <seg>There she entrusted herself to the Virgin with all her heart, touched the reliquary, returned home in good health, and never again felt troubled by that discharge.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illic videbatur morosius &amp; amorosius occupari: nam instar apis favo construendo intentæ, vertebatur in breves gyros, quasi penicillo quidpiam delineans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044117Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044117Z">
        <seg>There she seemed to be occupied more slowly and lovingly: for, like a bee intent on building a honeycomb, she turned in short circles, as if sketching something with a brush.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illius solemnem professionem nigri veli benedictione auctoravi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040602Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040602Z">
        <seg>I authorized his solemn profession of the black veil with a blessing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illo se mirifice posthac delectari, mulceri, colliquescere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034755Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034755Z">
        <seg>to be wonderfully delighted, pleased, and enraptured by it from then on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illuc tu velaberis, ibi solemniter profiteberis, ibi Religiosa, vitæ reliquum consummabis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062827Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062827Z">
        <seg>"There you will be veiled, there you will solemnly profess, there, as a Religious, you will consummate the rest of your life."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illum ipsum, in quo omnes thesauri, fidejussorem habeo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062337Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062337Z">
        <seg>I have him as a surety for all the treasures.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>immaculatam totius vitæ innocentiam, virginei candoris infantilem puritatem intulisti cælo: sequere, sequere nunc Agnum, quocumque ierit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030356Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030356Z">
        <seg>You brought to heaven the immaculate innocence of your entire life, the childlike purity of virginial whiteness: follow, follow now the Lamb, wherever he may go.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>immergitur anima divinæ bonitatis immenso pelago, &amp; metamorphosi ineffabili e se desciscens, transformatur in Dilectum, fitque unum cum illo”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050519Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050519Z">
        <seg>The soul is immersed in the immense ocean of divine goodness, and by an ineffable metamorphosis, detaching from itself, is transformed into the Beloved, and becomes one with him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>immorabatur tantisper lineolis, ubicumque prædulce Jesu nomen occurrisset: nam e singulis horum characterum apicibus quasi igniculos &amp; amorosos aculeos in corde sentiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050907Z">
        <seg>He lingered for a while over the little lines wherever the sweetest name of Jesus appeared; for from the tips of each of those characters he felt as if tiny sparks and amorous thorns were piercing his heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>imo &amp; alia quæcumque vegetioris succi esculenta se admissuram negabat, ita visum esse Numini, cui ipsæmet naturæ leges famulantur ad nutum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041135Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041135Z">
        <seg>Indeed, whatever other food of more vigorous sap she would not admit, so it seemed to the Deity, to whose will the very laws of nature themselves obey.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>imo communem &amp; publicum illic tam jumentorum, quam hominum quaquaversus commeantium, frequentari transitum: proinde serio metuebat, flosculos tam decoros, tam eximias terræ gemmulas, prætereuntium vestigiis improvide conculcari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062554Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062554Z">
        <seg>Rather, the common and public passage there, frequented by both beasts and men traveling in every direction, was used by all; and so he was seriously afraid that the so-beautiful little flowers, the earth's most exquisite gems, would be thoughtlessly trampled by the feet of passersby.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>imo exinde oculos semper tam limpidos clarosque habuit, ut videretur numquam antea lippivisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034624Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034624Z">
        <seg>In fact, from then on he had eyes so clear and bright that it seemed he had never been cross-eyed before.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>imo indies obbrutescebat magis, &amp; prolapsa usque in profundum impietas contemnebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032538Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032538Z">
        <seg>Rather, it grew more brutish with each passing day, and its impiety, having sunk to the depths, held nothing sacred.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>imo pararium&lt;a3&gt;b&lt;/a3&gt;, symmistam&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;, dinomilum&lt;a5&gt;d&lt;/a5&gt;, ut etiam hinc adverteret, quam dulciter Christus apposuerat erga eam cor suum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010641Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010641Z">
        <seg>rather, the paramour&lt;a3&gt;b&lt;/a3&gt;, the symposite&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;, the dyad&lt;a5&gt;d&lt;/a5&gt;, so that even from this she might notice how sweetly Christ had set His heart upon her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>imo pararium&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;b&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt;, symmistam&lt;s9&gt;&lt;a10&gt;c&lt;/a10&gt;&lt;/s9&gt;, dinomilum&lt;s11&gt;&lt;a12&gt;d&lt;/a12&gt;&lt;/s11&gt;, ut etiam hinc adverteret, quam dulciter Christus apposuerat erga eam cor suum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051601Z">
        <seg>rather, the paramour, the companion, the champion, so that even from this she might notice how sweetly Christ had set his heart upon her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>imo si quid videbatur recta incurrere in pupillas, non attrahebat nictu cilia, non claudebat palpebras, non declinabat vultum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044858Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044858Z">
        <seg>in fact, if anything seemed to be heading straight for her pupils, she wouldn't bat her eyelashes, close her eyelids, or turn her face away;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>impavida, nihilumque turbata respondit, oppido se lætari tam grato nuntio, solum mirari se, cur metuerint citius exspectanti indicare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061848Z">
        <seg>Fearless and unperturbed, she replied that she was delighted with the town by such a welcome message, and only wondered why they had feared to inform the expectant sooner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>imple trutinam, superaccumula doloribus dolores, at memento, simul augere meam patientiam”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025643Z">
        <seg>Fill the scale, heap pain upon pain, but remember to increase my patience at the same time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>impletur sacellum questibus, &amp; quæ vulgi, sub pietatis obtentu, esse solet suspicandi judicandique temeritas, spargitur, Rosæ veneranda lipsana aut furto insciis Patribus sublecta, aut his consciis clam in Hispaniam destinata esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031729Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031729Z">
        <seg>The chapel is filled with complaints, and what is usually the rashness of the common people in suspecting and judging under the guise of piety is spread abroad, that the venerable relics of Rose were either stolen from the Fathers without their knowledge, or, with their knowledge, secretly destined for Spain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>importune, quod repentinæ ægritudinis id genus esset, curque dissimularet, rogabat: &amp; quo minus Filia, quid pateretur, eloqui poterat, eo vehementius miseram urgebat mater, instabat, adjurabat, quasi internum tormentum alio tormento investigatura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045735Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045735Z">
        <seg>She importunately asked what kind of sudden illness it was and why she was hiding it; and the less her daughter could tell what she was suffering, the more vehemently the mother urged the poor girl, pressing her, insisting, and swearing, as if she would probe one inner torment with another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in brevi hac veste expeditior conscendam superiorem aræ crepidinem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060229Z">
        <seg>In this short garment, I will more easily climb to the upper ledge of the altar.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in cujus vultu majestatem angelicam intuitus, spem indubiam incolumitatis concepit, statimque omnis dolor evanuit: somnus, qui omnino diffugerat, blande corripuit infirmum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042443Z">
        <seg>Seeing an angelic majesty in his face, he conceived an unwavering hope of safety, and at once all pain vanished: sleep, which had entirely fled, gently seized the infirm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in ea curanda omnis ars medica desperarat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040329Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040329Z">
        <seg>All medical art had despaired of curing it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in ea divinam misericordiam secretius operari sua magnalia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054047Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054047Z">
        <seg>in it, to work its divine mercy more secretly, its great wonders;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in eo quam longinquus Virgini Deus, tam procul ipsa sibi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045502Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045502Z">
        <seg>The farther she is from the Virgin God, the farther she is from herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in fructum, qui ejus mensæ inferri mereretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042837Z">
        <seg>into fruit that deserved to be brought to his table.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in hoc quam plurimas præstantis sanctimoniæ rosas certatim commigraturas, ut in cruciformem arctioris vitæ regulam coaptatæ, e sublimi perfectionis gradu terram despiciant, ipsisque hic mundus crucifixus sit, &amp; ipsæ mundo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062542Z">
        <seg>that in this place the most numerous roses of outstanding holiness may strive to settle, so that, fitted into the cruciform rule of a stricter life, they may look down upon the earth from the lofty height of perfection, and that this world may be crucified to them, and they to the world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in lemma, quod Christus ipse dictarat, &amp; ore proprio gratiose expresserat: sciebat, illud humanitus non potuisse aut fratri suo, aut ulli mortalium innotescere, altioris id instinctus fuisse dictamen;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044458Z">
        <seg>in the lemma, which Christ himself had dictated and graciously expressed with his own mouth, he knew that it could not have been known to his brother, or to any mortal, by human means, but that it was the dictate of a higher instinct.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in maxillis, in naribus, in palpebris nil flaccidum, decolor, triste, aut emaciatum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031122Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031122Z">
        <seg>in the jaws, in the nostrils, in the eyelids, nothing flabby, discolored, sad, or emaciated;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in omnibus enim, &amp; per omnia a confessariis, parentum, &amp; majorum nutibus pendens, mundi, &amp; sui victrix etiam in minimis omnes obtemperantium palmas obtinuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041203Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041203Z">
        <seg>For in all things, and through all things, depending on the guidance of confessors, parents, and elders, she, virtuous and a conqueror of herself even in the smallest matters, obtained the palm of obedience from all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in posteriori, quæ patriam concernebat, debuit utique ardentissimus erga concives amor post mortem luculentius rutilare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032036Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032036Z">
        <seg>In the latter, which concerned his country, his most ardent love for his fellow citizens was, of course, to shine more brightly after his death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in sancta veritate protestor tibi, o Rosa, tuum erit hanc curam suscipere, ut præsto sit cochlear, dum a vesperis redeo” Rediit, quæsivit, circumspexit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035654Z">
        <seg>"I swear to you in holy truth, O Rosa, it will be your duty to see that a spoon is ready by the time I return from the evening service." She returned, searched, and looked around.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in æde S. Dominici siccarum rosarum foliola per ligneam Rosæ iconem sparsa collegerat: his domum allatis, clam singula leprosæ parvulæ operuit ulcera, sicque diligenter fasciatam in lectulo composuit ad soporem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040336Z">
        <seg>In the church of St. Dominic, she had gathered the little petals of dried roses scattered over a wooden image of the Rose; bringing these home, she secretly covered each of the little leper's sores with them and, having carefully bandaged her, laid her on a bed to rest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inculcare supremam obligationem diligendi Numen, quod tantopere nos dilexit &amp; diligit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053158Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053158Z">
        <seg>to inculcate the supreme obligation to love God, who so greatly loved us and loves us;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inde aberant indicia, quæ lethalis morbi suspicionem confirmarent, cum nec febrile quidquam pulsaret in venis, nec insolita tot simul alterationum vicissitudo ullum foras emitteret critirion&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt; alicujus morbi, quem schola Æsculapiinosset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013446Z">
        <seg>There were no signs to confirm the suspicion of a lethal disease, since there was no trace of fever in the veins, nor did the unusual succession of so many simultaneous changes present any outward criterion of a disease that the school of Aesculapius would have recognized.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inde acerbissimus dentium dolor miseram nec somnum capere, nec ullo patiebatur loco consistere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034655Z">
        <seg>Hence, the most excruciating toothache kept the poor woman from sleeping and could not bear to remain in any one place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inde arrepto calamo, cum ultimam patri scripsisset epistolam, Rosei monumenti pulveres magna fide inspersit paginæ, ut, si paternum cor exarata illic argumenta non emollirent, præstaret officium injectus pulvis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035958Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035958Z">
        <seg>Then, having seized a pen, since she had written her last letter to her father, she faithfully sprinkled the dust of Rose's monument onto the page, so that if the arguments she had inscribed there did not soften her father's heart, the dust she had added would do the work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inde dictabat animus nulla opus esse retractatione;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032244Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032244Z">
        <seg>Hence, my mind dictated that no retraction was necessary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inde divinus Infans, una petita &amp; gratanter accepta, subintulit: “Hæc Rosa tu es, hujus providam curam mihi assumo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042511Z">
        <seg>Then the divine Infant, having been asked once and graciously accepted, added: "You are this Rose; I undertake its prudent care."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inde febris destituta fomite ultro extincta est, quando ægrum extinctura credebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040807Z">
        <seg>Thus, the fever, deprived of its fuel, was extinguished of its own accord, when it was believed to be about to kill the patient.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inde infantes per obstantium capita sublimes mittebantur ad salutiferum contactum sacri cadaveris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031013Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031013Z">
        <seg>From there, infants were carried aloft over the heads of the crowd to the salutary contact with the sacred corpse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inde notabiliter in cor suum descendere calores &amp; æstus perjucundos, quos explicare non possit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050302Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050302Z">
        <seg>from which, remarkably, descend into his heart most delightful heats and heats, which he cannot explain;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inde pauxillum terræ, quam de Virginis sepulchro in hunc usum avia Josephæ attulerat, in aqua dissolvit, ægræque, adhibito cochleari infudit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035424Z">
        <seg>Then she dissolved a little of the earth that Joseph's grandmother had brought from the Virgin's tomb for this purpose in the water, and fed it to the sick woman with a spoon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inde per reliqua membra eamdem circumferens, quo successivo ordine loca dolorum obibat contingendo, languor &amp; cruciatus illico fugiebant, donec ad extremum se undique totam mirata est sanam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034940Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034940Z">
        <seg>Then, carrying it through the rest of her limbs in the same way, she touched the various places of her pains in the order in which they came, and the weakness and torment immediately fled, until at last she found herself, in every part, completely and miraculously healed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inde querula suspiriorum dulcedine accusabat violentiam gaudiorum, quæ felicis Rosæ anulus ingerebat: “O (inquit) amores!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044625Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044625Z">
        <seg>Then the querulous one, with the sweetness of sighs, accused the violence of the joys that the happy Rose's ring was bringing: "O, loves!"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ingressus cubiculum, vidit Caciquam hilariter insidentem lectulo, &amp; avide manducantem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035827Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035827Z">
        <seg>Entering the room, he saw the Cacique sitting cheerfully on the little bed and eating ravenously.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inquit, S. virgo Rosa nunc a me recessit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032113Z">
        <seg>He said, "Saint Virgin Rose has now departed from me."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inquit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051229Z">
        <seg>he said</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>insignem illam heroïnam Catharinam Senensem æmulata, quam sibi in ipso rationis diluculo in sanctitatis exemplar, &amp; magisterium delegerat, quam subinde magistram appellare non destitit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041120Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041120Z">
        <seg>She emulated that distinguished heroine, Catherine of Siena, whom she had appointed for herself at the very dawn of reason as an exemplar of holiness and a teacher, and whom she never ceased to call her teacher.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inspexere brachium omni tumore liberum, quod presbyter sursum, deorsum, dextrorsum, lævorsum, &amp; in omne latus pari, qua dextrum, agilitate rotabat sine dolore;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033332Z">
        <seg>they inspected the arm, free of all swelling, which the priest rotated up, down, to the right, to the left, and in every direction with equal, painless agility.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>instabant sequacium agmina, vim portis illatura, nisi religiosorum cuneus locique reverentia intercessissent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030728Z">
        <seg>Hordes of followers were pressing forward, about to force their way through the gates, had not the wedge of the religious and the reverence for the place intervened.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>instabat, utique decentius in celebri Excalceatarum virginum parthenone, quam foris inter ultimas Tertiarias militaturam Deo, vacaturam spiritui, inhæsuram contemplationi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040252Z">
        <seg>It was imminent, and of course more fitting in the famous parthenon of the Barefoot Virgins than out in the open, where she was to become a Tertiary for God, devote herself to the spirit, and immerse herself in contemplation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>insuper a summo mane præcedentis feriæ quintæ (cui nomen a Cœna Domini) continuo usque ad istius feriæ sextæ meridiem immota perstiterat in eodem S. Dominici templo, vigilias agens coram sacrosancta Hostia, tunc de more in S. sepulchri arculam solemniter illata, nec bolum aut aquæ guttulam toto illo triginta circiter horarum intervallo sumpserat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040751Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040751Z">
        <seg>Moreover, from early morning on the preceding Thursday (named from the Lord's Supper), it had remained motionless in the same St. until noon on this Friday. Dominic, in the temple of the same S. Dominic, had kept vigil before the most sacred Host, then, according to custom, solemnly placed in the little shrine of the Holy Sepulchre, and had taken neither a morsel nor a drop of water during that entire interval of some thirty hours.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>insuper constrictis mandibulis aditum cibo intercluserat, ferro ad hiatum os diducendum fuit, ut ultimum remedium, pulvis e Rosæ tumulo sumptus, infunderetur nescienti, quid fieret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040050Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040050Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, with his jaws clenched, he had blocked the passage for food; it was necessary to force his mouth open with iron as a last resort, to pour in powder taken from the mound of Rosæ, without him knowing what was being done.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>insuper programma exhibuit sua manu eleganter conscriptum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013000Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013000Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, he presented a program elegantly written by his own hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>insuper vetuisse, ne Virginis funeri circumponantur panni atro colore lugubriter pullati, sed candidi, nivei, solemnes, quique non exequias indicent, sed triumphum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030325Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, he had decreed that at the Virgin's funeral, cloths of a mournful black color should not be draped, but rather white, snowy, and solemn ones, which would not indicate a funeral, but a triumph.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inter tot suffitus, halitus, &amp; lumina;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031115Z">
        <seg>amid so many sighs, breaths, and glances;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>interea clarescere in corde suo placidam altioris sphæræ serenitatem, pacem, temperiem nulla comparatione explicabilem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055901Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055901Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, a placid serenity of a higher sphere, a peace and temper, inexpressible by any comparison, was growing clear in his heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>interim Aguerus pauxillum hujus salutiferæ terræ anhelo in aquæ vasculo obtulit, quo sumpto, statim fatalem bolum exspuit, ac mortem, cujus frigido sudore jam permaduerat, excreando evasit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035651Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035651Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, Aguerus offered a little of this salutary earth in a small vessel of water; having taken it, he immediately spat out the fatal lump and, by expelling death, from whose cold sweat he was already drenched, was saved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>interjecit se blandiuscule Rosa, patris iram propitiatura;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042226Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042226Z">
        <seg>Rosa interjected herself rather sweetly, to appease her father's anger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>intimas ossium medullas ardore intolerabili depascendas, nec ullum ex tot simul atrocibus morbis, solitum naturæ ordinem, periodum, leges, aut intervalla admissurum, eo quod præternaturalis futura sit tot mortalium tormentorum exlex congeries.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013239Z">
        <seg>devouring the very marrow of the bones with an intolerable ardor, and would admit no regular order, period, laws, or intervals of nature, since the lawless gathering of so many mortal torments would be supernatural.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>invalescit supplicum clamor, ut non defuerint, qui suspicarentur imaginem portentoso insuper sudore commaduisse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030645Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030645Z">
        <seg>The cry of the supplicants grew stronger, so that there were those who suspected the image had been made to sweat with a prodigious, additional sweat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inæstimabilis sibi videbatur illorum felicitas, quibus datum fuerat ultima gutta sanguinis obsignare fidei characterem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060307Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060307Z">
        <seg>Inestimable seemed to him the happiness of those who had been given the final drop of blood to seal the character of faith.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ipsa legumina, flores, frutices, corymbos agitatione varia quodammodo animari, in placidam hymnidici murmuris harmoniam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045149Z">
        <seg>The very legumes, flowers, shrubs, and clusters, through varied agitation, are somehow enlivened into a placid harmony of hymeneal murmuring.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ipsa ædium tecta leniter accubua sub imbelli ac populosa omnis ætatis turba procul nigricabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030601Z">
        <seg>The roofs of the buildings themselves lay softly nestled beneath the unarmed, populous crowd of all ages, which blackened in the distance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ipse auriga circumspectabat trepidus, qua parte tutius in terram descenderet, seseque in pedes conjiceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062151Z">
        <seg>The charioteer himself was anxiously looking around, seeking a safer spot to descend to the ground and set himself on his feet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ipsi denique Francisco (nam ordine presbyter erat) capellaniæ titulo centum quadraginta patacones (quos vocant) in annum reditum assignantur, certis ædibus transcriptis in hypothecam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032839Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032839Z">
        <seg>Finally, to Francis himself (for he was a priest by order) a yearly income of one hundred and forty patacones (as they are called) is assigned under the title of chaplaincy, with certain houses mortgaged.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>is enim secretarium Joannem de Tineo, tunc e portu Callaoensi&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; Limam reversum, negotiorum causa convenerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010954Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010954Z">
        <seg>For he had summoned the secretary, John de Tineo, who had then returned to Lima from the port of Callao, on business.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>is enim secretarium Joannem de Tineo, tunc e portu Callaoensi&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; Limam reversum, negotiorum causa convenerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053753Z">
        <seg>For he had summoned the secretary, John de Tineo, who had then returned to Lima from the port of Callao, on business.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>is vero dum Filiam sibi obstantem impetuose dimovet, fortuito manus errore, impegit leviter in caput Virginis, ea parte, qua tecta corona sæviebat: mox improvisum ictum tres sanguinis rivi secuti, frontem innocentis Filiæ decursim purpurarunt, manifesto satis indicio, qualis herinaceus sub candido ejus tegmine clam nidularetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042229Z">
        <seg>But as he forcefully pushed aside his daughter who stood in his way, he accidentally, by a slip of the hand, struck the Virgin's head lightly, on the part where the hidden crown was raging. Immediately, three streams of blood followed the unexpected blow, running down the innocent Daughter's forehead and staining it purple, a clear enough sign of what kind of porcupine was secretly nesting under its white covering.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>istique morbi, qui prosternendo cuivis robustissimo sat erant singuli, plerumque bini, terni, quaterni simul, ex diversis oppositisque causis, in languidulam irruebant, mirantibus interdum medicis, ubinam in exsucco, arido, exhaustoque corpusculo tam diversi ardores incendium fomitemque reperirent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052618Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052618Z">
        <seg>And these diseases, which were each strong enough to prostrate anyone very robust, would often strike the invalid in pairs, threes, or fours at once, from various and opposing causes, to the astonishment of the physicians, who wondered where in that dry, parched, and exhausted little body such diverse fires could find fuel and kindling.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ita quod auditi gemitus non paucos compunxerint, mirantes Virginem in summis corpusculi tormentis adeo gemendi parcam, nunc in levissimis animi morbis enarrandis tam amare altumque ingemiscere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025919Z">
        <seg>so that the groans they heard had moved many, marveling that the Virgin, in the greatest torments of her body, was so sparing of groans, yet now, in describing the slightest troubles of her spirit, she sighs so bitterly and deeply.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ita quod sentiebat jam in animæ suæ fundo, magnum aliquod secum agi, nec suum procul abesse Lapicidam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044328Z">
        <seg>so that he already felt deep in his soul that something great was being accomplished within him, and that his Lapicida was not far from him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ita tibi secura spondeo”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012231Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012231Z">
        <seg>So I safely promise you.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ita viderentur ex vultu B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051437Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051437Z">
        <seg>they would thus appear from B.'s face.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>itaque cum illo se fidenter in viam conjecit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051815Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051815Z">
        <seg>And so, he confidently set out on the road with him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>itaque domum quantocyus repeteret, totam horum calamitosorum catervam in conspectum suum adduceret, velle se omnes amplecti ex ordine, domique suæ honeste alere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032823Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032823Z">
        <seg>Therefore, as soon as he returned home, he would bring the entire band of these wretched people before him, wanting to embrace them all in turn and to feed them honorably in his house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>itaque moleste &amp; anxie urgebat ægram, diceret, qua parte gravius doleret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013505Z">
        <seg>And so he would press the sick woman painfully and anxiously, asking her which part hurt more severely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>item 33 trientes rosarii Mariani, 33 dies jejuniorum, &amp; tria millia verberum, in venerationem suæ ætatis, quam vixit in terris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053607Z">
        <seg>Also, 33 Hail Marys, 33 days of fasting, and three thousand lashes, in veneration of the age she lived on earth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>item Conventus Limensis Prior;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050151Z">
        <seg>also the Prior of the Convent of Lima;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>item binæ candidi scapularis &amp; veli particulæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034909Z">
        <seg>Also two pairs of the scapular and veil particles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>item possessiones, latifundia, servitia, proventus annui &amp; instructissima totius abundantiæ œconomia, sic quod inclytum hoc monasterium intra primam annorum quadragenam tot cæli benedictionibus perpetim irrigatum, exemplo, fama, splendore in eum profecerit gradum, quo possit grandioribus præcipuisque totius Europæ monasteriis æquiparari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062433Z">
        <seg>Likewise, with possessions, latifundia, services, annual revenues, and the most fully developed economy of abundance, so that this illustrious monastery, within the first forty years, perpetually irrigated by so many heavenly blessings, by example, fame, and splendor has advanced to such a degree that it can be equated with the grandest and most principal monasteries of all Europe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>iterum laborabatur sub avido tot rosariorum, crucum, numismatum affrictu, denuo Virginis habitus tentabatur forficibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031054Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031054Z">
        <seg>Again, labor was being performed under the eager rubbing of so many rosaries, crosses, and coins, and once more the Virgin's habit was being tested with scissors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>itum ad ecclesiam, conchiliatis peristromatis, holosericis aulæis, velis byssinis a summo deorsum obpalliatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031444Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031444Z">
        <seg>the church was thus adorned, draped with tapestries, silk curtains, and linen veils hung from top to bottom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jam Bernarda inter summos cruciatus, tam sibi, quam soboli illum diem ominabatur extremum, quando improvisus Joannes de la Massa Bernardæ maritum domi conveniens dixit, se Lima pauculas ex Rosæ vestibus adferre particulas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035117Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035117Z">
        <seg>Already, amid the greatest torments, Bernarda, for both herself and her offspring, foresaw that day as the last, when John de la Massa, unexpectedly arriving at Bernarda's husband's home, said he had brought from Lima a few scraps of Rosa's clothing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jam a quatriduo loquela exciderat, sensus aberat, ac opinione medicorum vitæ ultima imminebat hora.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040525Z">
        <seg>For four days now he had been unable to speak, his mind was absent, and, in the doctors' opinion, the final hour of his life was imminent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jam enim Rosa vocari te jusserat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053344Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053344Z">
        <seg>For Rosa had already ordered you to be called.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jam venis arteriisque omnibus bulliebat Virgini sanguis effundi avidus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060251Z">
        <seg>Already the blood, eager to be spilled, was surging through all the veins and arteries of the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jam æger (cui natura vocem dederat sonoram, &amp; liquidam) præ angustia obstructi pectoris, utrimque&lt;a0&gt;*&lt;/a0&gt; languore, vix, quæ intelligerentur, proferre poterat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012337Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012337Z">
        <seg>The patient, already endowed by nature with a sonorous and clear voice, was so obstructed by a constricted chest and enfeebled on both sides that he could barely utter words that could be understood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jamque amissa loquela &amp; visu luctans cum ultimo agone, mox ut opem beatæ Rosæ toto corde imploravit, eamdem habuit manifeste conspicuam, subitoque valens, incolumis, vegetus cum stupore omnium evasit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043305Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043305Z">
        <seg>And now, having lost the power of speech and struggling with her dying breath, as soon as she earnestly implored the help of Blessed Rose, she saw her clearly, and at once, strong, unhurt, and vigorous, she emerged to the astonishment of all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jamque morituri lectulo circumfusi religiosi signum ultimi agonis vigiles exspectabant, ut secundum Ordinis ritum Deo &amp; cælitibus abeuntem animam commendarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040757Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040757Z">
        <seg>And the dying man, surrounded on his little bed by the religious, awaited the sign of the final agony, so that, according to the Order's rite, the watchers might commend his departing soul to God and the heavenly hosts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jamque pupula intelligens meliorem esse patientem viro forti, suavis, lepida, &amp; serena semper apparuit, nec umquam vagitibus, aut voce querula ædium audita est turbare silentium, aversata solum in publicum efferri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041110Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041110Z">
        <seg>And the intelligent little one always appeared a better patient to the strong man, sweet, charming, and serene, and was never heard to disturb the silence of the house with cries or a querulous voice, shunning only to be carried out in public.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jamque stillæ ad ipsum usque marginem ligneum, qui picturam includebat, defluxerant, ac novæ identidem guttæ a fronte, maxillis, oculis succrescebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053802Z">
        <seg>And already the drops had trickled down to the very edge of the wooden frame that enclosed the painting, and new drops were constantly forming from the forehead, cheeks, and eyes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jamque ultimis sese munierat sacramentis, certa mori, nisi, trajecto in stomachum terræ illius haustu, simul omnem inveterati doloris equuleum miraculo perfregisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035607Z">
        <seg>and had already received the last sacraments, certain to die, unless, by swallowing a mouthful of that earth, he at the same time miraculously broke every twinge of his long-standing pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jurato asseruit, religiosæ cuidam personæ triduo ante felicem Rosæ transitum a Christo fuisse revelatum, Virginis mortem fore admirabilem &amp; sepulturam gloriosam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030322Z">
        <seg>He swore that it had been revealed by Christ to a certain religious person three days before the blessed passing of Rose, that the Virgin's death would be admirable and her burial glorious.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jusserunt Georgium in pedes se erigere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033328Z">
        <seg>They ordered George to stand up.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>juveniliter agis, &amp; doleo vices tuas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060645Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060645Z">
        <seg>You act like a youth, and I pity your lot.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>k &lt;e0&gt;Biographus hoc loco alludit ad vocem Græcam&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;εὐφροσύνα&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;vel Dorice&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;αὐφροσύνα&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;quæ&lt;/e4&gt; lætitiam &lt;e5&gt;significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T000434Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035220Z">
        <seg>k &lt;e0&gt;The biographer here alludes to the Greek word&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;εὐφροσύνα&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;or, in Doric&lt;/e2&gt;, &lt;s3&gt;αὐφροσύνα&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;which means&lt;/e4&gt; joy&lt;e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>k &lt;e0&gt;Jam sæpe citatus Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035257Z">
        <seg>k &lt;e0&gt;Already often cited, Latius, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>k &lt;e0&gt;Teste eodem Gorræo pag, 214&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;κατάπλασμα&lt;/s1&gt; est medicamentum molle &amp; consistentia mediocre, quod repellendo, concoquendo, digerendo, resolvendo, laxando, vacuando, detergendo, calefaciendo, siccando, dolorique sedando adhibetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055619Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055619Z">
        <seg>According to the same Gorræo (p. 214), a cataplasm is a soft, moderately consistent medicine that is applied to repel, digest, digest and dissolve, loosen, evacuate, cleanse, warm, dry, and soothe pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>k Alexipharmacum, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἀλεξιφάρμακον&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;est remedium, quod datur contra venenum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034512Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034512Z">
        <seg>An antidote, from the Greek *alexipharmakon*, is a remedy given against poison.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>k Cerberus &lt;e0&gt;triceps canis &amp; custos inferni, quemadmodum poëtæ fabulantur, hic accipitur pro ipso dæmone, ut ex sensu patet&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052410Z">
        <seg>Cerberus, the three-headed dog and guardian of the underworld, as the poets fabulize, is here taken for the very demon, as the sense makes clear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>k Chasma, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;χάσμα&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;idem est, quod Latine&lt;/e2&gt; hiatus, &lt;e3&gt;aut&lt;/e3&gt; fissura, &lt;e4&gt;provenitque a verbo&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;χασμάω&lt;/s5&gt; &lt;e6&gt;hoc est&lt;/e6&gt;, hio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040159Z">
        <seg>chasma, &lt;e0&gt;Greek&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;chásma&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;which is the same as the Latin&lt;/e2&gt; hiatus, &lt;e3&gt;or&lt;/e3&gt; fissure, &lt;e4&gt;and comes from the verb&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;chasmáō&lt;/s5&gt; &lt;e6&gt;that is&lt;/e6&gt;, to yawn.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>k Sirius &lt;e0&gt;est astrum seu signum zodiaci, quod a Latinis&lt;/e0&gt; canicula &lt;e1&gt;appellatur, &amp; magnum calorem efficit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054754Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054754Z">
        <seg>The star Sirius, which the Latins call the Dog Star, is a star or sign of the zodiac that produces great heat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>l &lt;e0&gt;Cum Mercurius a Græcis vocetur&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;Ἑρμῆς&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;ex illo nomine Græco hic adjectivum&lt;/e2&gt; Hermeticus &lt;e3&gt;Latina inflexione compositum videtur, ita ut&lt;/e3&gt; hermeticus &lt;e4&gt;idem sit ac&lt;/e4&gt; mercurialis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T000640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035226Z">
        <seg>l &lt;e0&gt;Since Mercury is called&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;Ἑρμῆς&lt;/s1&gt; by the Greeks, &lt;e2&gt;the adjective&lt;/e2&gt; Hermeticus, &lt;e3&gt;formed from that Greek name with a Latin inflection, appears to have been composed in this way, so that&lt;/e3&gt; hermeticus &lt;e4&gt;is the same as&lt;/e4&gt; mercurial.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>l Chuquisaca &lt;e0&gt;eadem est cum urbe Argentea, vulgo&lt;/e0&gt; la Plata, &lt;e1&gt;de qua Baudrandus tomo 1 Geographiæ suæ pag. 90 sic scribit:&lt;/e1&gt; Argentea &amp; Argentina, &lt;b2&gt;la Plata&lt;/b2&gt;, urbs Americæ Meridionalis in Peruæ regno &amp; in præfectura Charcarum, cujus caput existit in montanis, &amp; prope fluvium Picolmaïum, archiepiscopali dignitate fulget, cui subsunt quatuor episcopi suffraganei.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040202Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040202Z">
        <seg>l Chuquisaca &lt;e0&gt;is the same as the city of Silver, commonly called&lt;/e0&gt; la Plata, &lt;e1&gt;of which Baudrandus in the first volume of his Geography on page. 90 writes as follows:&lt;/e1&gt; Silver and Argentina, &lt;b2&gt;la Plata&lt;/b2&gt;, a city of South America in the kingdom of Peru and in the province of Charcas, whose capital is situated in the mountains and near the Picolmai River, shines with the dignity of an archbishopric, to which four suffragan bishops are subject.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>l Gallao &lt;e0&gt;est idem celebris portus Peruviæ, qui&lt;/e0&gt; Callao, &lt;e1&gt;de quo supra mentionem fecimus&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035306Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035306Z">
        <seg>That same famous port of Peru is Callao, which we mentioned above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>l Pulluli &lt;e0&gt;inter alias significationes accipitur pro stolonibus, qui ex arborum radicibus enascuntur, a verbo&lt;/e0&gt; pullulare, &lt;e1&gt;quod est stolones aut germen emittere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054803Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054803Z">
        <seg>Suckers, among other meanings, is taken for the shoots that grow from the roots of trees, from the verb to sucker, which is to emit shoots or a sprout.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>la</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211659Z">
        <seg>The Latin</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>laborabat memoria e multis aliquam saltem præteritorum favorum imaginem menti adducere, nec poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045515Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045515Z">
        <seg>His memory struggled to bring to mind even a single image of past favors, and it could not.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>languebant artus, velut sub sarcina;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041907Z">
        <seg>The limbs languished, as if under a burden;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>laudabatque Patrem luminum, qui tanta, sapientibus ac prudentibus abscondita, humilibus, parvulis, indoctis revelabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050637Z">
        <seg>and praised the Father of lights, who revealed such great things, hidden from the wise and prudent, to the humble, little ones, and the uneducated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>laudetur Dominus in Sanctis suis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033412Z">
        <seg>Let the Lord be praised in his saints.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>lecta &amp; inspecta fuit petitio &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031400Z">
        <seg>The petition, etc., was read and examined.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>liberos, quos illi superna benedictio datura erat, optimis moribus imbuere, sancteque educare satageret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012823Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012823Z">
        <seg>He would strive to instill the finest morals in the children whom the divine blessing was to grant him, and to raise them up piously.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>liquescebat in dulci jubilo, novaque Tertiaria reputabat se plus, quam nomine, beatam&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203505Z">
        <seg>She was dissolved in sweet joy, and the new Tertiary considered herself blessed, not in name only, but in reality.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>liquescebat in dulci jubilo, novaque Tertiaria reputabat se plus, quam nomine, beatam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040230Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040230Z">
        <seg>She was dissolved in sweet joy, and the new Tertiary considered herself blessed, not in name only, but in reality.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>locum prædixit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062449Z">
        <seg>He predicted the place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>longius abest meta, ad quam propero, attingam demum, sed non modo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013133Z">
        <seg>The goal I rush toward is still far off; I will finally reach it, but not yet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>lucellum qualecumque illud matri afferebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035802Z">
        <seg>He brought whatever little he had to his mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>luctabatur, velut ingenti mole pressa, spirandi facultas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013347Z">
        <seg>His ability to breathe was struggling, as if pressed by a great weight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>lumina inusitata, excelsa, pretiosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044535Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044535Z">
        <seg>Unusual, lofty, precious lights;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>lætaberis, imo tuopte experimento probabis, quod dico”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062347Z">
        <seg>You will rejoice; indeed, you will prove for yourself what I say.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>lætumque Pæna canentes.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040441Z">
        <seg>and singing a joyful paean.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>m &lt;e0&gt;Biographus Deiparam hic vocat cælestem&lt;/e0&gt; anthophoram, &lt;e1&gt;id est&lt;/e1&gt;, floriferam &lt;e2&gt;vel&lt;/e2&gt; flores ferentem, &lt;e3&gt;ab&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;ἄνθος&lt;/s4&gt; flos, &lt;e5&gt;&amp; participio&lt;/e5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;φορὸς&lt;/s6&gt; ferens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054810Z">
        <seg>The biographer here calls the Virgin Mary a celestial flower-bearer, that is, flower-bearing or bearing flowers, from ἄνθος, flower, and the participle φορὸς, bearing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>m &lt;e0&gt;Cum nomen istius urbis&lt;/e0&gt; Orura &lt;e1&gt;diu apud geographos frustra quæsiverim, &amp; Lovaniensis editiohujus Vitæ mendis scateat, haud immerito vereor, ne nomen illud hoc loco luxatum sit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040208Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040208Z">
        <seg>Since I have long sought in vain the name of this city, Orura, among geographers, and the Louvain edition of this Life is riddled with errors, I am not without reason to fear that the name has been garbled in this passage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>m Cancrenam &lt;e0&gt;hoc loco poni pro&lt;/e0&gt; gangræna &lt;e1&gt;suspicor: nam&lt;/e1&gt; gangræna &lt;e2&gt;apud medicos est mortificatio vel putrefactio alicujus membri, sicut omnes passim norunt, quo sensu&lt;/e2&gt; cancrenum vulnus &lt;e3&gt;in Glossario barbaro Cangii pro vulnere gangræna infecto legitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035309Z">
        <seg>I suspect that Cancrenam is placed here for gangrene: for gangrene among physicians is the mortification or putrefaction of a limb, as everyone knows, in which sense a cancerous wound is read in Cangii's barbarous glossary for a gangrenous wound.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>m Phaseolus &lt;e0&gt;aut&lt;/e0&gt; faseolus, &lt;e1&gt;ac etiam&lt;/e1&gt; faselus &lt;e2&gt;est genus leguminis, quo lexicographi pisa vel fabas intelligi volunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T001407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035232Z">
        <seg>m Phaseolus, &lt;e0&gt;or&lt;/e0&gt; bean, &lt;e1&gt;and also&lt;/e1&gt; faselus, &lt;e2&gt;is a genus of legume which lexicographers understand to mean peas or beans.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>magister F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050020Z">
        <seg>Master F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>magistro F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044942Z">
        <seg>To Master F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>magna es, sublimis es, gloriosa, præfulgida, incomparabilis es in triumphali Sanctarum agmine.” Permulta similia ructabat identidem corde pleno, affectu suavissimo, at corpore prorsus immobili: nam intimus ille jubilus, ut linguam &amp; os sedentis laxaverat, ita reliqua membra ligarat, adeo quod nec valebat de sella surgere, nec brachia aut crura stupore obrigida movere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044600Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044600Z">
        <seg>You are great, you are sublime, glorious, resplendent, incomparable in the triumphal procession of the Saints." He repeatedly bellowed many similar things from a heart full of the sweetest feeling, yet his body remained completely motionless: for that innermost jubilation, having loosened the tongue and mouth of the seated man, had bound the rest of his limbs, so that he could neither rise from his seat nor move his arms or legs, which were stiffened by stupor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>male locari tot scholarum labores, tot noctes insomnes, tot disputationum clamorosas raucedines, si tantis parta sudoribus eruditio proximorum saluti, fideique propagationi non impendatur, si ad metam inertis otii, ad vanitatem plausumque inanis pompæ, vel in temporario laureatæ frontis honore prætumide conquiescat, oscitet, desideat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060456Z">
        <seg>It is a pity to squander so many scholars' labors, so many sleepless nights, the hoarse clamors of so many disputations, if the learning won by such sweat is not devoted to the salvation of others and the propagation of the faith; if it rests, puffed up, at the goal of idle leisure, in the vanity and applause of empty pomp, or in the temporary honor of a laurel on its brow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>maluisset frequentius;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055650Z">
        <seg>He would have preferred more often.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mandat, primo diluculo cœnobium adeat, Provincialem moribundum conveniat, ei denuntiet, ex hac quantumvis complorata infirmitate nequaquam ipsum e vivis abiturum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032101Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032101Z">
        <seg>He commands that at first light he go to the monastery, visit the dying Provincial, and announce to him that from this, however much to be lamented, infirmity he is by no means to depart from this life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mandibularum cardines, tamquam filo chalybæo arctissime strictos, ad omnem oris motum luxari a junctura;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013527Z">
        <seg>the hinges of the jaws, as if most tightly bound by a steel wire, to be dislocated from the joint by any movement of the mouth;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>manum aperiebat, claudebatque promptissime;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033335Z">
        <seg>He opened and closed his hand very readily.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>manum dexteram occupabat ramus, index nitidissimæ virginitatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031918Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031918Z">
        <seg>A branch occupied the right hand, the index of the most brilliant virginity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mappas &amp; pallas altarium, operimenta calicum bombycina, &amp; quidquid textile ad sacram hanc supellectilem spectare poterat, omni pietatis ingenio, arte, elegantia conficiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060112Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060112Z">
        <seg>Altar cloths and veils, silk covers for chalices, and anything that could pertain to this sacred furniture were made with all skill, art, and elegance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>matronam vero novo sibi beneficio implicuit, ipsamque tandem, non in lucrum unicæ per quot hebdomades diei, sed plene in omnium suorum exercitiorum amicam communionem, &amp; participium, arcano Dei impulsu stimulata, admisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062929Z">
        <seg>But he drew the matron into a new benefit for himself, and at last admitted her, not to the profit of a single day for a few weeks, but fully into the friendly communion and participation of all his exercises, stimulated by a secret impulse of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>maxime cum ingens illustrium personarum multitudo ad concionem (de qua fructus eximii sperabantur) fuisset invitata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053458Z">
        <seg>especially since a great multitude of distinguished persons had been invited to the assembly (from which exceptional results were hoped).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>maxime postquam is, qui in se ceperat exorare Madriti facultates regias novæ fundationi necessarias, Limam vacuus rediit, ne spem quidem permissionis umquam obtinendæ secum apportans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062718Z">
        <seg>especially after he, who had undertaken to solicit from Madrid the royal faculties necessary for the new foundation, returned to Lima empty-handed, not even bringing with him the hope of ever obtaining permission.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>maxime quod sciret, ipsum non jam obstinatione voluntaria, aut obedientiæ contemptu, sed sola infirmitate resistendi violento ac annoso vitio, sic obbrutuisse in absurditate intemperantiæ omnium vecordissimæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060800Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060800Z">
        <seg>especially since he knew that he had not fallen into this by willful obstinacy or contempt for obedience, but only through his weakness in resisting a violent and long-standing vice, and had thus been so brutalized by the most wanton intemperance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>maxime ubi tot dies noctesque plane insomnes, tot motuum vicissitudines, tanta occipitis inflammatio, ac temporum inquietudo certam fere caliginem ac judicii eclipsim minabantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025711Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025711Z">
        <seg>especially when so many days and nights were completely sleepless, so many changes of motion, such a inflammation of the occiput, and the restlessness of the times threatened a near certainty of darkness and an eclipse of judgment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>maxime, quod experimentis jam didicerat, vix aliter se importunas procorum molestias, parentum, fratrum, domesticorum adsultum, mundique immundi conspectum evadere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040035Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040035Z">
        <seg>Above all, she had already learned from experience that she could scarcely escape the importunate annoyances of suitors, the advances of parents, brothers, and household servants, and the sight of the filthy world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>me Rosam torquebat, imo torrebat indies vehementius.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025742Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025742Z">
        <seg>Rose was twisting me, no, roasting me more fiercely each day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>me infelicem, quæ illo nunc careo!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050941Z">
        <seg>Wretched that I am, for now I am without him!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>medullæ, instar cinerum, aruerunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013428Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013428Z">
        <seg>The marrow withered like ashes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>meminerat, de quibus ille promiserat Joan.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061919Z">
        <seg>He remembered the things about which he had promised Joan.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>memineris, a Domino te constitutum piscatorem hominum, robustius tibi laxandum est rete in capturam peccatorum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060558Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060558Z">
        <seg>Remember that you were appointed by the Lord as a fisherman of men; you must cast your net more vigorously to capture sinners.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mihi nolo impedimento sint panni luctaturæ ac morituræ pro Sacramento;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060226Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060226Z">
        <seg>I do not want the cloths of the wrestling and dying for the Sacrament to be a hindrance to me.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mirabantur omnes, &amp; præcipue chirurgi, qui pridie metuerant oleum &amp; operam perdi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054029Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054029Z">
        <seg>All were amazed, and especially the surgeons, who the day before had feared that the oil and their labor would be lost.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mirari se, cur Deus nondum horribili abysso demerserit infelicem mundum, qui tantam ferebat peccatricem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040537Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040537Z">
        <seg>He marvels that God has not yet plunged the wretched world, which bore so great a sinner, into a horrible abyss.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mirum, non succubuisse citius;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025621Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025621Z">
        <seg>It's a wonder it didn't collapse sooner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>miscebant secretissima invicem colloquia, hærebant in mutuis vultibus, tamquam rerum aliarum aut obliti, aut incurii;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051158Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051158Z">
        <seg>They exchanged the most secret conversations with one another, and stared into each other's faces, as if oblivious or indifferent to all other things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>molesto pruritui, ubi fortius urebat, applicuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034956Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034956Z">
        <seg>He applied it to an annoying itch, where it burned the most.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mortalitate hæreditaria cuncti putrescimus, nec est, qui suæ corruptionis non circumferat sentinam: itaque, mater, parum putato, quod tunicam obiter maculavi improvida: longe fœdius ipsum Redemptoris vultum rheumata, sputa, salivæ turpium carnificum, pro nostris sceleribus deformarunt”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061324Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061324Z">
        <seg>By hereditary mortality we all rot, and there is no one who does not carry about the stench of his own corruption: therefore, mother, do not think it a small thing that I inadvertently stained my tunic; far fouler still, the face of the Redeemer itself was disfigured by tears, spittle, and the salivations of vile executioners, on account of our sins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mox &amp; metropolitanum collegium, &amp; regius senatus cum omnibus magistratibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042945Z">
        <seg>soon &amp; the metropolitan college, &amp; the royal senate with all the magistrates.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mox deliquio abreptus, alvo in fluxum soluta, sudavit algidus, tremuit, distortisque oculis nil nisi mortis ultimos spirabat angores.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034336Z">
        <seg>Soon, seized by a fainting spell, his bowels loosened in a flow, he sweated, chilled, trembled, and with distorted eyes breathed nothing but the final agonies of death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mox fascias explicuit, palam clamitans, integerrime se curatam, publiceque in miraculi fidem robuste jactavit brachium gestu motuque expeditissimo, acsi numquam languisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043202Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043202Z">
        <seg>She soon unfastened her bandages, crying out aloud that she had been cared for most scrupulously, and publicly and forcefully proclaimed her faith in the miracle with a most agile gesture and movement of her arm, as if she had never been ill.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mox gaudio suo impar, in altissimam ecstasim&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; diriguit, attonitis, quotquot aderant, quod nihilominus apte expediteque responderet sacerdoti, qui de more, sacratissimam Hostiam manu tenens, interrogabat, quæ sub illo articulo solent interrogari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025928Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025928Z">
        <seg>Soon, overcome by his joy, he was lifted into the highest ecstasy, to the astonishment of all who were present, since he nevertheless answered the priest, who according to custom, holding the most sacred Host in his hand, was asking the questions that are usually asked at that point, fittingly and promptly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mox panno, ne quid in labiis residuum notari posset, os abstersit, ac pelvim elutam, defricatam, nitidam reportavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061413Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061413Z">
        <seg>He then wiped his mouth with a cloth so that no trace of residue might be noticed, and brought back the basin, washed, scrubbed, and shining.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mox pavide circumspiciens, advertit, non aliunde esse, quam a facie orantis in angulo Rosæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053021Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053021Z">
        <seg>Soon, looking around in fear, he noticed that it came from no other place than the face of the one praying in the corner of the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mox, nemine juvante, liberrimis passibus incedebat per conclave.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034928Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034928Z">
        <seg>Soon, with no one to help him, he was walking about the room with complete freedom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mulier, quamquam mordacissimo dolori impar, horis non minus viginti novem tormento subjacuit: verum ubi postridie ad eosdem cruciatus redeundum fuit, arrepta chartacea Rosæ imagine (ex iis, quæ Roma in Peruviam illatæ fuerant) dolenti plagæ apposuit, &amp; exinde quidquid acrimoniæ inspergebatur, omni doloris sensu caruit, tametsi pluribus adhuc diebus curæ illius acerbitas fuerit continuanda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040348Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040348Z">
        <seg>The woman, though unequal to the most excruciating pain, endured the torture for no fewer than twenty-nine hours: But when the next day she was to be subjected to the same torments, she seized a paper image of Rosa (one of those that had been brought from Rome to Peru), applied it to the painful wound, and from that moment, whatever acrimony was poured upon it, she felt no pain, even though the bitterness of that treatment had to be continued for several more days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>multis etiam lachrymæ fluebant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031537Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031537Z">
        <seg>Many were also shedding tears.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>multisque flexibus sinuosam absolvens oden, Virgini cedebat vicem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045310Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045310Z">
        <seg>Completing an ode winding through many bends, she yielded the turn to the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>n Cantaber, &lt;e0&gt;id est, natus in Cantabria, quæ jam communiter Biscaya nominatur, &amp; cujus incolæ inter Asturiam, Castellam veterem, Navarram &amp; Oceanum Cantabricum commorantur, &amp; idiomate proprio, a vicinis populis prorsus diverso, utuntur&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035316Z">
        <seg>Cantabrus, that is, born in Cantabria, which is now commonly called Biscay, and whose inhabitants dwell between Asturias, Old Castile, Navarre, and the Cantabrian Sea, and use their own idiom, entirely different from that of the neighboring peoples.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam &amp; ruboris incendium, &amp; doloris acrimoniam, crebra stillicidia tacite affirmabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043413Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043413Z">
        <seg>For both the blush of shame and the acrimony of pain were frequently affirmed by silent trickles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam Rosæ a frequenti Communione adeo increvit pascentis Sacramenti esuries, ut paulum adultiori sacra Synaxis ter in singulas hebdomadas fuerit permittenda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055659Z">
        <seg>For Rose's thirst for the Sacrament of Pastoral Care grew so great from frequent Communion that she was permitted the sacred Synaxis three times each week, once she was a little older.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam Virgo gliscentem sui nominis celebritatem aversata, dolebat ex candido indumento aliis feminis internosci in publico, eminus se venientem monstrari digitis, notari oculis, laudari a prætereuntibus, ipsique S. Catharinæ Senensi nonnumquam ab obviis comparari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040342Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040342Z">
        <seg>For the Virgin, averse to the growing fame of her name, was distressed that from her white habit she was recognized in public by other women, that from afar people would point their fingers at her as she approached, that she was noted by their eyes, praised by passersby, and that she herself was sometimes compared to St. Catherine of Siena by those she met.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam ad aræ crepidinem defixis humi genibus, &lt;s2&gt;[tandem amplectitur.]&lt;/s2&gt; inter orandum placidissimo animi deliquio sensim diriguit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040351Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040351Z">
        <seg>For, with his knees fixed on the ground before the altar, he finally embraced it. While praying, he was gradually overcome by a most peaceful faintness of mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam ad aræ crepidinem defixis humi genibus, [tandem amplectitur.] inter orandum placidissimo animi deliquio sensim diriguit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203511Z">
        <seg>For, with his knees fixed on the ground before the altar, he finally embraced it. While praying, he was gradually overcome by a most peaceful faintness of mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam aliunde quoque innotuit hujus prodigii conspicua frequentia, ut illud Virgo nec celare conata sit, nec negare potuerit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045205Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045205Z">
        <seg>For the conspicuous frequency of this prodigy became known from other sources as well, so that the Virgin neither tried to conceal it nor could deny it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam annis superioribus in palmaram distributione (utpote Filia Ordinis) præterita numquam fuerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044247Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044247Z">
        <seg>For in the previous years, in the distribution of palm branches (as a Daughter of the Order), she had never been omitted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam divinum est mysterium hoc, quod cernitis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041144Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041144Z">
        <seg>For what you see is a divine mystery.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam ex iis non ultra quinque sumebat sextis feriis, adjecto solo vervecini fellis haustu, ut hac lautitie quinque plagarum Christi acerbitatem præsentius ruminaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041318Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041318Z">
        <seg>For he would take no more than five of them on the six days of fasting, adding only a drink of kid's wool, so that by this austerity he might more keenly contemplate the bitterness of the five wounds of Christ.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam exigui temporis interlapsu, vir illis in ædibus numquam antea visus, nec aliunde cuiquam illic domesticorum notus, intravit, Rosæ matrem poposcit, eidem quadraginta libras argenteas pro novo pallio coëmendo porrexit, abiit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061131Z">
        <seg>For a short time later, a man, never before seen in those houses and unknown to any of the household, entered, asked for Rosa's mother, offered her forty silver pounds to buy a new cloak, and left.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam impeditus erat P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053833Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053833Z">
        <seg>for P. was impeded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam impeditus erat P. Martinez, &amp; tertia noctis hora imminebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011010Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011010Z">
        <seg>For P. Martinez was held up, and the third hour of the night was at hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam inprimis, Vidua Aloysii Nugnez Maria de Vera, profluvio, febri, dolore stomachi, &amp; lethali vomitu ad ipsum mortis deducta vestibulum, ultimis sacramentis ad emigrandum se paraverat, judicante medico in diem posterum minime supervicturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040239Z">
        <seg>For, first of all, the widow of Aloysius Nugnez Maria de Vera, brought to the very brink of death by a profuse discharge, fever, stomach pain, and a fatal vomiting, had prepared herself for her departure with the last sacraments, the physician judging that she would not survive until the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam integro fere decennio ante fundationem, quandoque per symbola &amp; figuras, alias manifeste &amp; in proprio typo non solum fuit illi divinitus demonstratum monasterium, quod Limæ sub S. Catharinæ Senensis patrocinio, numeroso sacrarum virginum collegio dicandum erat, sed etiam primam ibi futuram monialium præfectam, &amp; cœnobii fundatricem Luciam Guerram a Daga ex vultu agnovit, spiritu suo imbuit, &amp; osculo pacis quodammodo initiavit, dicens ei: “Euge mater ingenti suo te operi Deus reservavit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042614Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042614Z">
        <seg>for almost a full decade before the foundation, sometimes through symbols and figures, at other times manifestly and in its own proper form, the monastery was not only divinely shown to him, which was to be under S. under the patronage of St. Catherine of Siena, was to be dedicated to a numerous college of holy virgins, but also recognized in her face the first future abbess and foundress of the convent, Lucia Guerra da Daga, imbued her with his spirit, and in a manner initiated her with the kiss of peace, saying to her: "Well done, mother, God has reserved you for his great work."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam interea, dum tu miracula narrabas absentis imaginis de Atocha, hæc præsens edebat signa inusitatæ lætitiæ, movebat in nos columbinos suos ocellos, attollebatur novo corpulentiæ typo extra telæ superficiem, velut inde ad nos exitura cum sopito Filiolo, spargebat arcanas dulcedines, renitebat, radiabat augustior, nunc parvulo suo, nunc nobis materne adblandiens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054543Z">
        <seg>for in the meantime, while you were narrating the miracles of the absent image of Atocha, this one, present, was giving signs of unusual joy, moving her dovelike eyes toward us, rising on a new kind of corpulence beyond the surface of the canvas, as if about to come forth from it to us with her slumbering little Son, scattering secret sweetnesses, shining, radiating with greater majesty, now to her little one, now caressing us maternally.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam medici, vitæ horas nonnisi octo ei residuas esse, constanter asseverabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035443Z">
        <seg>For the doctors constantly asserted that he had no more than eight hours to live.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam nullus deinceps ibi culex Catharinam punxit aut suxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043912Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043912Z">
        <seg>For from then on, no mosquito there ever bit or sucked Catherine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam post decem &amp; novem menses (ut capite sequente narrabitur) exhumata Rosa, eumdem servabat ac præsentibus adhalabat odorem, quem quidem in die sepulturæ alii rosaceo simillimum, alii velut ex liliorum, rosarum, balsamorum, totiusque florilegii mixtura compositum perceperunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031134Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031134Z">
        <seg>For after nineteen months (as will be related in the following chapter), when the rose was exhumed, it retained and diffused the same fragrance that was present at its burial—a fragrance which on the day of burial some perceived as very like that of a rose, others as if composed of a mixture of lilies, roses, balsams, and the entire flower garden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam puer interdum continuis quindecim horis jacebat sine loquela &amp; sensu, spumans ore, brachiis ac tibiis immane palpitans, interdum &amp; occiput solo illidens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034708Z">
        <seg>For the boy would sometimes lie for fifteen straight hours, speechless and senseless, foaming at the mouth, his arms and shins throbbing violently, and at times even striking his back on the ground.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam quæ tanto suffragio dignabatur novitiam suam excelsa supra sidera, hic operabatur ut felix cæli porta.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054332Z">
        <seg>For she who was honored with such a vote, her high station above the stars, here served as a blessed gate of heaven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam sub iis fere motibus in alio sacello prodigiosæ imaginis, cui a Rosario nomen est, repente splendidus vultus micuit, spectantibus, qui propius adstiterant, universis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030638Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030638Z">
        <seg>For under those very motions, in another chapel of the miraculous image, which is called the Rosary, a splendid face suddenly shone upon all who were standing by.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam ubi ceteræ proles, pro garrulo rudis ætatis genio, aut sordes loquebantur, quas nondum intelligebant, aut cantilenas amatorias aliunde forsan auditas innoxie repetebant, plorabat Rosa, fugiebat, puerile flagitium deferebat matri, querebatur offendi Deum, quodque alia ratione castigare satis non poterat, in se ipsa lachrymis amarissimis vindicabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053325Z">
        <seg>for where were the other offspring, for the garrulous genius of a rude age, or they spoke of filth they did not yet understand, or innocently repeated amorous ditties perhaps overheard elsewhere, Rosa would weep, flee, report the childish disgrace to her mother, complain that God was offended, and whatever she could not otherwise punish sufficiently, she avenged on herself with the bitterest tears.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam ut Christus parte seriæ sextæ, toto Sabbatho, &amp; initio Dominicæ jacuerat in tumulo, sic Rosa parte anni MDCXVII, toto anno MDCXVIII, &amp; initio anni MDCXIX quieverat in sepulchro, in quo &amp; datum ei fuerat non videre corruptionem, &amp; corpus æque odoriferum inde prodiit, sicut intrarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031425Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031425Z">
        <seg>For just as Christ lay in the tomb during part of the sixth hour, the entire Sabbath, and at the beginning of the Lord's day, so Rose rested in the sepulchre during part of the year 1617, the whole of 1618, and at the beginning of 1619, in which she was granted neither to see corruption nor did her body come forth any less fragrant than when it entered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam ut particulam Francisci tunc fœde spumantis tremulo pectori imposuit, in ictu oculi conquievit puer, in se rediit, consedit in lectulo, haustum aquæ petiit, convaluit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034717Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034717Z">
        <seg>For as soon as he placed a drop of Francis's then foully foaming medicine on his trembling chest, the boy was immediately quieted, came to himself, sat up in his little bed, asked for a drink of water, and recovered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam videns novitiam Mariam jugiter affligi, ea ad chorum cum ceteris digressa, manum audacter admovit operi, &amp;, Rosæ opitulante effigie, perfecit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040915Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040915Z">
        <seg>For seeing the novice Mary constantly afflicted, she, having stepped aside from the choir with the others, boldly set to work on the piece and, with the help of the image of the Rose, completed it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam vix ambulare ac fari cœperat, cum quotidie, ob muliebrem aviæ, matrisque de nomine Rosæ controversiam, innocens Puella utriusque persecutioni se vidit miserrime implicitam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052448Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052448Z">
        <seg>For she had scarcely begun to walk and speak when, every day, because of a dispute between her grandmother and her mother over the name Rose, the innocent girl found herself most wretchedly entangled in the persecution of both.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>naturam ei omnino divinam esse, attamen bene se observasse, quod gratia res sit alia a Deo, tametsi Dei imaginem mirabiliter contineat, animamque constituat deiformem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052848Z">
        <seg>that its nature is altogether divine, yet he took good care to observe that grace is something other than God, even though it wonderfully contains the image of God and constitutes the soul deiform.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ne miremur lucis angelos umbræ speciem assumpsisse, dum gerebant officium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051828Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051828Z">
        <seg>Let us not be surprised that the angels of light assumed the appearance of shadows while carrying out their duty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ne parcas, quantumcumque pungas aut vellices, pro meo demerito parum est”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030105Z">
        <seg>Do not spare, however much you prick or pull, for it is too little for my deserts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec Limæ dumtaxat, sed toto regno invaluit usus non inutiliter;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040236Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040236Z">
        <seg>and the practice became widespread not only in Lima, but throughout the entire kingdom, and not without good effect.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec alio oblectari se posse, quam quod experimentali hac certitudine, sentiret intra se Deum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050309Z">
        <seg>and could find no other delight than that, by this certainty born of experience, he felt God within himself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec alio posthac nomine te compellabo”: quasi cum Præcursoris matre protestaretur his Ambrosii verbis: “Habet vocabulum suum, quod agnovimus, non quod elegimus”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034702Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034702Z">
        <seg>"nor will I henceforth call you by that name": as if he were protesting with the mother of the Forerunner with these words of Ambrose: "He has his own name, which we recognized, not which we chose."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec aliud dolebat, quam quod cæteri non æque, ut ipsa, totam monasterii imaginem in vivo radio præsentem habebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062740Z">
        <seg>She grieved for nothing else, but that the others did not, as she did, have the entire image of the monastery present in living color.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec aliud moratus, illico abscessit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062257Z">
        <seg>Without further delay, he left at once.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec alius, tam extraneorum, qui cum Antonio accesserant, quam inquilinorum sensus fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035833Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035833Z">
        <seg>Nor was there any other feeling among the strangers who had come with Antony than that of the tenants.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec de illo aliud audierat, quam significari mysterium impenetrabile, nec temere ulli mortalium perscrutandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061826Z">
        <seg>He had heard nothing else about it, except that it was a sign of an impenetrable mystery, not to be rashly probed by any mortal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec deerant, qui pio furto hinc limbum tunicæ, inde veli extremitates paratis forficibus modo clam, mox palam invaderent, ut brevi stipatoribus opus fuerit contra rapacem devotionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030406Z">
        <seg>Nor were there lacking those who, by a pious theft, would snatch from here the hem of a tunic, from there the ends of a veil, with scissors secretly at first, then openly, so that the attendants soon had to fend off this rapacious devotion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec deerant, quibus Rosa videretur, præ nimio in seraphicam matrem studio, insanire;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062715Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062715Z">
        <seg>Nor were there lacking those who thought that Rosa, from her excessive devotion to the seraphic mother, was insane.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec defuit strato tam nobili dignum cervical;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042623Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042623Z">
        <seg>Nor was a pillow worthy of such a noble bed lacking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec deinceps umquam hoc morbo tentatus fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034720Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034720Z">
        <seg>And he was never afflicted with this disease again.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec destitit pugna, donec ante ipsam sanctissimi Rosarii aram in placidissima ecstasi rapta, &amp; pallens, &amp; rubens, ac splendoribus fulgida apparens, &amp; post sensibus reddita, in jubilum grati animi obtentæ victoriæ erupit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041340Z">
        <seg>Nor did the struggle cease until, before the very altar of the Most Holy Rosary, she was rapt in the most peaceful ecstasy, pale and flushed and shining with splendors, and, upon regaining her senses, she broke out into a jubilant expression of gratitude for the victory she had won.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec enim tunc (uti hodie) Limæ amplitudo usque ad illa ulteriora trans fluvium spatia procreverat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061156Z">
        <seg>For at that time (as it is today), the size of Lima had not yet grown to those more distant spaces beyond the river.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec forte alius est, qui diuturnam infirmi patientiam aut citius abigat, aut voracius absumat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052631Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052631Z">
        <seg>Nor is there perhaps another who either more quickly drives away a long-standing patient's endurance or more voraciously consumes it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec gemitu ac levi suspirio cruciatus suos accusantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052640Z">
        <seg>nor accusing his torments with a groan and a faint sigh.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec in tota ipsius vita, vel semel ei obrepserit fœda aliqua cogitatio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044055Z">
        <seg>Nor in his entire life did a single foul thought ever creep into his mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec loco se movit, donec is procul abiisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033849Z">
        <seg>He did not move from the spot until he had gone far away.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec loco, ubi feria quinta genua fixerat, surgebat, donec postera die Parasceves divinus ille Thesaurus (cum quo semper erat cor suum) transferretur ritu solito ad tabernaculum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060044Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060044Z">
        <seg>Nor did he rise from the place where he had knelt on the Thursday, until the next day, Good Friday, that divine Treasure (with whom his heart was ever) was transferred in the usual rite to the tabernacle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec minus e ramo, quam de rosis continuo scintillabant densa gloriæ fulgetra.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031921Z">
        <seg>Nor did the branch shine any less than the roses; continuously, dense flashes of glory sparkled from it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec molli spernis adesse Rosæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053248Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053248Z">
        <seg>Nor do you spurn being soft to Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec mora, fructum exigit, memor fortasse ex Canticis, quod ait Sponsa: Fructus ejus dulcis gutturi meo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051019Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051019Z">
        <seg>And without delay, it demands its fruit, perhaps recalling from the Songs what the Bride says: "His fruit is sweet to my throat."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec opinandum, hanc Sponsi voluntatem esse, ut bis moriar”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013139Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013139Z">
        <seg>Nor should it be thought that this is the Spouse's will, that I should die twice."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec peregrina tot ænigmatum involucra cogitatu sat capaci adæquabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045701Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045701Z">
        <seg>Nor did the foreign wrappers of so many riddles match a mind sufficiently capable of thought.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec perfunctorie, sed ea solicitudine examinis, eo contritionis stimulo, eo gemituum ac lachrymarum apparatu, acsi per hebdomadas &amp; menses utroque Sacramento penitus caruisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055721Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055721Z">
        <seg>not perfunctorily, but with the earnestness of an examination, the spur of contrition, and a display of groans and tears, as if he had been entirely deprived of both Sacraments for weeks and months.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec quietem primam reparabat, donec ad pristinam abstinentiam læta redire permitteretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041145Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041145Z">
        <seg>She did not restore her first rest until she was allowed to return joyfully to her former abstinence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec satiari poterat his verbis tenerrimis: “Pater ignosce illis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030018Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030018Z">
        <seg>He could not be satisfied with these most tender words: "Father, forgive them."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec se illi sustinendo parem umquam fuisse, nisi quatenus prodigiose manu Omnipotentis conservabatur in vita.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045822Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045822Z">
        <seg>nor was he ever equal to sustaining himself, except insofar as he was miraculously preserved alive by the hand of the Almighty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec se, diutius solito ibi fortasse moraturam, evocaret ad cœnam, donec ultro ipsa prodiret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052042Z">
        <seg>nor would he summon her to dinner, perhaps staying there longer than usual, until she herself came out of her own accord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec segnius interea pergebat sui vultus roseum decorem jejuniis deterere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035939Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, she was no less diligent in using fasts to wear away the rosy beauty of her face.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec sibi videbatur totum seraphicæ magistræ induisse habitum, nisi &amp; spineam induisset illius coronam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042111Z">
        <seg>It did not seem to her that she had fully assumed the habit of the seraphic mistress, unless she had also taken up her crown of thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec solum istis, sed &amp; iis, quæ procul celebrabantur in dissita S. Augustini ecclesia, quæ non minus quatuor aut quinque platearum anfractu seu interjectu distabat a Rosæ domicilio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043736Z">
        <seg>not only those, but also those that were celebrated far off in the distant church of St. Augustine, which was no less than four or five blocks away by winding streets from Rose's home.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec solum terrere venerat, sed &amp; nocere, si posset: nam (ut vidit ab immota se despici) rabido morsu pertentabat virgineum illud corpusculum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051930Z">
        <seg>He had come not only to frighten, but also to harm, if he could: for (seeing that she looked down on him from her immobility) he gnawed at that virgin body with a rabid bite.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec tamen Rosæ nomen tunc ei impositum, sed Isabellæ sive Elizabeth;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034640Z">
        <seg>However, her name at that time was not Rose, but Isabella or Elizabeth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec tamen audebat humillimæ Virgini honorificum illud periculum aperire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030046Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030046Z">
        <seg>Yet he did not dare to reveal that honorable peril to the most humble Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec tamen impedimentum, quo lubens volens tenebatur, austeræ matri audebat detegere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040004Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040004Z">
        <seg>Yet she did not dare to reveal to her austere mother the impediment that held her against her will.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec tamen, in tanto humillimæ contritionis apparatu, sufficiens materia semper adfuit, in quam tuto proferri posset sacramentalis absolutionis sententia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040645Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040645Z">
        <seg>Yet even in such a display of the most humble contrition, there was not always sufficient matter in which the sentence of sacramental absolution could be safely pronounced.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec totam illius videri staturam, sed solo vultu, &amp; pectore tenus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050547Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050547Z">
        <seg>not his entire stature could be seen, but only his face and up to his chest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec toto cautissimæ suæ modestiæ velo sufficiebat intimos erga illam fervores abscondere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054118Z">
        <seg>Not even her most cautious veil of modesty was enough to hide her innermost passions for her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec ulla malignantis auræ intemperies, non horrida obscuritas, non frigora, non turbines ventorum, insuaves erant Virgini, undequaque suo corpusculo quidquid incommodorum poterat accersenti.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041646Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041646Z">
        <seg>Neither the malice of the air, nor the dreadful darkness, nor the cold, nor the whirlwinds of the winds were unpleasant to the Virgin, who from every side drew upon her little body whatever discomfort she could.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec umquam ei facundia defuit, qua sequelam virtutis, fugamque vitiorum efficaciter persuaderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060741Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060741Z">
        <seg>He was never at a loss for the eloquence to effectively persuade his audience of the pursuit of virtue and the avoidance of vice.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec umquam formidinis sensum expresserant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052150Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052150Z">
        <seg>They had never expressed a sense of fear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec verba erant, sed tonitrua: nam repente mens illa capacissima simul immensum concepit horrorem inferni, simul apprehendit vastitatem æternitatis, simul divinam attentius reverita majestatem ponderabat secum omnis peccati fœdam infelicitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035002Z">
        <seg>There were no words, but thunder: for suddenly that most capacious mind conceived at once the immense horror of hell, grasped the vastness of eternity, and, more attentively revering the divine majesty, weighed within itself the foul misfortune of all sin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec videbatur tantæ Virginis debitis honoribus satisfactum, quoadusque solemniores exsequiæ (quibus ipse prorex interesse poposcerat) nondum fuerant celebratæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031218Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031218Z">
        <seg>It did not seem that the due honors for so great a Virgin had been satisfied, until more solemn funeral rites (at which the pro-regent himself had requested to be present) had not yet been celebrated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec, nisi voti compos, humo se attollebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040518Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040518Z">
        <seg>He would not rise from the ground unless he had fulfilled his vow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec, quoad vixit, in his ullum pristini languoris vestigium notavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033341Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033341Z">
        <seg>Nor did he notice any trace of his former languor in them as long as he lived.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>necdum scio congrue te amare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053043Z">
        <seg>I don't yet know how to love you properly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>negat iste, se posse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033353Z">
        <seg>He denies that he can.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>negavit ægra se posse: verum, audito, quod ipse paterfamilias jusserat, se subditam recordata, paruit, sumpsit, exhausit, tenui voce adjiciens: Ite, nuntiate Gundisalvo domino meo, me, quod revera non poteram, ipso jubente potuisse, nec in ipsa mortis janua me fore immemorem, quid hero suo debeat subdita.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040717Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040717Z">
        <seg>The sick woman refused, saying she could not; but when she heard that her master himself had commanded, she remembered her duty and obeyed. She took it, drank it down, and in a faint voice added: "Go, tell my lord Gundisalvus that I, what I truly could not, was able to do at his command, and that even at the very gates of death I was not forgetful of what a subject owes her hero."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nemo dubitabat, intra pauculos dies e vivis abituram: domestici velut jam mortuam plangebant, cum subinde, num adhuc spiraret, ambigerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013118Z">
        <seg>No one doubted that she would be dead within a few days: her household mourned her as if she were already dead, while at the same time they were uncertain whether she was still breathing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe Regina SS. Rosarii supplici Rosæ gratiam (quam optabat) spoponderat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043556Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043556Z">
        <seg>For the Queen of the Holy Rosary had promised the supplicant Rose the grace (which she desired).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe approbavit proficiscendi consilium, commemoravit summam pecuniariam, quam femina cum marito in reliquæ vitæ sustentaculum pari arbitratu constituerant, dixitque, hanc ambobus (eo quod prole carerent) sat fore.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012148Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012148Z">
        <seg>He approved the plan to set out, mentioned the sum of money that the woman and her husband had agreed upon as a joint provision for the rest of their lives, and said that this would be enough for both of them, since they had no children.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe credebat, hoc deberi tum privato beneficio, tum communi lætitiæ illius diei;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040246Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040246Z">
        <seg>He certainly believed that this was owed both to a private benefit and to the common joy of that day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe duplicavit lanam, arreptoque bacillo, tantam ejus copiam non tam ingessit, quam impressit cervicali, ut hoc suberis tandem duritiem exæquarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042815Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042815Z">
        <seg>Indeed, she doubled the wool, and, seizing a rod, she pressed so much of it onto the collar that this cork eventually matched its hardness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe idipsum olim seraphica sua mater ac magistra optaverat, &amp; tandem inter hos ardores increpabat seipsam, quod non arderet satis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054150Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054150Z">
        <seg>For her seraphic mother and teacher had once desired the very same thing, and at last, amid these ardors, she would reproach herself for not burning enough.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe qui super alios clericos &amp; Ordinum Fratres supremum habuerunt in ordinandis &amp; instituendis Americanis ecclesiis &amp; monasteriis officialatum, jurisdictionem &amp; spiritualem principatum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054658Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054658Z">
        <seg>namely, those who held supreme authority over other clerics and brothers of the Orders in the ordaining and establishing of American churches and monasteries, with officialdom, jurisdiction, and spiritual primacy;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe tria ligna tortuosa, indolata, nodosa tabulis imposuit, ac inter commissuras tenuioribus paxillis firmavit sub stragulo, ne cederent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042549Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042549Z">
        <seg>He placed three winding, unseasoned, and knotty logs on the planks and, between the joints, secured them with thinner pegs under a blanket so they wouldn't give way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe ut interea sub hoc divino Sole fortius incalesceret devotum pectus, &amp; vivifica Sacramenti conjunctione vegetius foveretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055929Z">
        <seg>so that in the meantime the devoted heart might grow stronger under this divine Sun, and be more vigorously nourished by the life-giving union of the Sacrament.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe vario &amp; ipse examine scrutabatur Virginis spiritum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050702Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050702Z">
        <seg>Indeed, he too was scrutinizing the Virgin's spirit with a varied examination.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque animus illi deerat tentandi etiam his majora, ni prudentes confessarii vetuissent, qui identidem, interposito moderaminis præcepto, discretam virium rationem habendam occlamitabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041932Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041932Z">
        <seg>Nor was he lacking in the spirit to attempt even greater things, had not prudent confessors forbidden it, who repeatedly, with the precept of moderation interposed, cried out that a discreet use of one's strength should be made.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque converti ad corporea phantasmata, sed imaginibus vacuum, spiritualis formæ luminosa puritate imprægnari: hic Deum immediato, intimoque illapsu implere sacrarium fidelis animæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050324Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050324Z">
        <seg>nor be turned to corporeal phantasms, but be imbued with the luminous purity of spiritual form by images: here to fill the sanctuary of the faithful soul with God by an immediate and intimate influx;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque defuere ex iis, qui rogarint Dominum, ut ab hujus terroris atroci tormento ipsos demum immunes vellet, libenter se quodvis aliud pœnarum genus subituros”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050423Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050423Z">
        <seg>Nor were there wanting among those who had begged the Lord to grant them immunity from this atrocious terror, who readily declared themselves willing to undergo any other kind of punishment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque enim illa animi sui profunda demissio permittebat, quod usque ad singularem sponsæ dignitatem, tantæque celsitudinis titulum auderet ultro aspirare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044027Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044027Z">
        <seg>For his deep self-deprecation would not allow him to dare, even to the singular dignity of a bride and the title of such exalted rank, to aspire of his own accord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque humanitus quidquam horum Rosæ innotuisse poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012430Z">
        <seg>Nor could anything of these have been known to Rosa by human means.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque incassum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035818Z">
        <seg>Nor in vain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque jam Rosa privatim inter familiares, sed Jonas inter Ninivitas videretur reipsa ordiri pœnitentiæ concionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060543Z">
        <seg>Nor would Rosa now be seen privately among the faithful, but Jonas among the Ninevites, actually beginning a sermon of repentance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque me præteriit: siquidem &amp; mihi, ad pondus concessæ adversitatis, gazam inæstimabilem superfluentium gratiarum admensus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052810Z">
        <seg>Nor did he pass me by: for to me, in proportion to the weight of the adversity I was granted, he has measured out an inestimable supply of abundant grace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque mulier sat sui compos remanebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040704Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040704Z">
        <seg>Nor did the woman remain sufficiently composed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque putare se, ullum cujuscumque partis aut membri superesse cruciatum, quem ipsa non senserit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052705Z">
        <seg>nor to think that there is any suffering of any kind or part that she herself has not felt.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ni prompte strinxeris, elabuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051422Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051422Z">
        <seg>If you don't squeeze them promptly, they will slip away.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ni totum, quod exquiro, dixeris, nec ego te, nec tu me, quantum sat est, intelliges, &amp; figurarum (quas partim explicui, partim explicaturus sum) ænigmata sensumque integre non assequeris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050501Z">
        <seg>If you do not say everything that I seek, neither you will understand me, nor I you, as much as is sufficient, and you will not fully grasp the riddles and meaning of the figures (which I have partly explained, and partly shall explain).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nihil in moribus aut motibus apparuisse tetricum vel subtriste, quod acidam saperet austeritatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040921Z">
        <seg>nothing in his manners or movements appeared grim or gloomy, which would have a sour austerity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nihilominus cum aliquot secularibus Hispanis Indum secuti sunt, ut saltem (quando plus non poterant) agonizanti mulieri adsisterent, litanias, preces, commendationem animæ super migrantem recitarent, dæmonumque insultus aqua lustrali abigerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035808Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035808Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, they followed the Indian with several Spanish laymen, so that at least (when they could do no more) they might attend the agonizing woman, recite litanies, prayers, and the commendation of the soul over the dying woman, and drive away the assaults of demons with holy water.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nihilominus reperit solicita carnis suæ domitrix, quo &amp; illis propriam dispensaret cruciatuum portionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041916Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, the mistress of her own flesh found a way to dispense to them their own share of torments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nihilominus semper e cælo sibi adfuisse præsidium &amp; robur, quo universa imperturbate sustineret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052708Z">
        <seg>nevertheless, he always had a heavenly protection and strength to sustain all things unperturbed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nihilominus, qua licuit vocis tenuitate ac nutibus mandavit, indicari Rosæ discrimen suum, quidque ipsa de morbi hujus successu &amp; exitu sentiret, sibi liberrime renuntiare jussit, ut, si moriendum sit, continuo præparare se possit ad momentum, a quo pendet æternitas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012340Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, with all the gentleness of her voice and by her nods, she signaled to Rosa her own peril, and commanded her to tell her most freely what she herself felt about the course and outcome of this illness, so that, if she were to die, she might at once prepare herself for the moment from which eternity depends.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nil fore certius, quam ipsum febrilis enthusiasmi&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt;, aut delirii arguendum iri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032138Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032138Z">
        <seg>Nothing would be more certain than that it would itself be accused of feverish enthusiasm or delirium.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nimirum ab avia materna Isabella de Herrera, cui inter lustrales aquas videbatur is honor deberi humano more.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034643Z">
        <seg>namely, from his maternal grandmother Isabella de Herrera, to whom that honor seemed to be owed in the human manner amid the lustral waters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nimirum procul inter secretiores ædium porticus ambulabat Rosa, &amp; cum ipsa Jesus in statura octennis pueri, sed amictus jam lumine sicut vestimento.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051152Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051152Z">
        <seg>Indeed, Rosa was walking far off among the more secluded porticoes of the buildings, and with her was Jesus, in stature like an eight-year-old boy, but already his radiance shone like clothing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nimirum sentiens cor suum concalescere, dum illius aspectui inhærebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053654Z">
        <seg>Indeed, feeling his heart grow warm as he gazed upon her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nimirum sex ex Ordine Prædicatorum, quinque ex Societate Jesu, singulique seorsim (velut uno ore, ac sub fide juramenti) testati sunt, Virgineum Rosæ candorem ea semper nituisse puritate, ut neque veniali macula ullius lasciviæ umquam fuerit attaminata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044052Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044052Z">
        <seg>namely, six from the Order of Preachers, five from the Society of Jesus, and each one separately (as with one voice, and under oath) testified that the Virgin of the Rose had always shone with such purity that she was never stained by the venial spot of any lasciviousness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nitebat sana undique cuticula, densum fulgebat capillitium, videbatur puella non tam carere porrigine, quam semper caruisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040323Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040323Z">
        <seg>Her skin shone healthy and smooth all over, her thick hair gleamed, and the girl seemed not so much to lack a pimple as to have never had one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nobiscum ille habitare dignabitur, certus esto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054100Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054100Z">
        <seg>Rest assured, he will deign to live with us.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>non istud, sed illud factum aut dictum fuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053334Z">
        <seg>that it was not this, but that which was done or said.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>non languendum circa inutiles vocularum controversias;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060453Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060453Z">
        <seg>not to languish over useless controversies of words;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>non nimis hærendum esse in subtilitatum apicibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060450Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060450Z">
        <seg>one should not be too hung up on subtle points.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>non quod deessent nocendi vires: nam idem a Francisca impetu obliquo resiliens, aliam corripuit feminam, eique cum togæ laciniis intimam subuculam raptim succendit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055312Z">
        <seg>Not that he lacked the power to do harm: for the same blow, rebounding from Francisca's oblique thrust, struck another woman, and with the folds of his toga he swiftly set fire to the inner tunic beneath her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>non tardarunt manifesta experimenta conciliare dictis integram fidem: nam ab illo ipso momento, quo Christophorus loqui desierat, cœpit æger Provincialis morbi discessum lentis indiciis advertere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032144Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032144Z">
        <seg>They did not delay to bring manifest experiments to bear on the words to give them full credence: for from that very moment when Christopher had ceased to speak, the sick Provincial began to notice the disease's departure by gradual signs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>non unico ore, sed poris omnibus clamasse in imagine Redemptorem: “Redamate amantem vos”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053920Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053920Z">
        <seg>He cried out, not with a single mouth but with all his pores, in the image of the Redeemer: "Redeem the one who loves you."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nondum primæ infantiæ cunas reliquerat, quando rosarum speciem, nomen, spinas induit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052426Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052426Z">
        <seg>She had not yet left the cradle of her earliest infancy when she took on the appearance, the name, and the thorns of a rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>noster hic labor, &amp; cura esto”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055417Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055417Z">
        <seg>This is our labor, and take care of it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>notabat angulos, flexus confines;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062734Z">
        <seg>He noted the corners, the bends, the boundaries.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nova semper inveniens, quæ miraretur &amp; approbaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050625Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050625Z">
        <seg>always finding new things that she would marvel at and approve of.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>noverant puellam, ad suas tam atroces &amp; crebras injurias patientissimam, ea sola tolerare nequaquam posse, quibus vel tenuiter Deum offendi existimaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053319Z">
        <seg>They knew the girl, who was so patient with her own atrocious and frequent injuries, could by no means endure alone those by which she would have thought God was even slightly offended.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nulla re creata potuisse recreari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041752Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041752Z">
        <seg>that nothing created could be recreated;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>numerabat vices familia, &amp; nonnumquam quindecies cantasse brevi horarii guadrantis spatio, deprehendit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061556Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061556Z">
        <seg>He counted the family's turns and discovered that they had sometimes sung fifteen times in the short span of a quarter hour.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>numquam auditum sermonem jactantiæ, fastus, ostentationis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040918Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040918Z">
        <seg>never a word of boasting, arrogance, or ostentation;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>numquam sibi obrepsisse impatientiæ motum, quo vel latum pilum discesserit a resignata conformitate ac amussi divinæ voluntatis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052711Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052711Z">
        <seg>that he had never allowed a movement of impatience to creep in, by which even a hair's breadth would have departed from the resigned conformity and submission to the divine will.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nunc experiar;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055244Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055244Z">
        <seg>Now I will try.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nunc instare severius, ut saltem formosam (qua tunc rutilabat adhuc) cæsariem erigeret calamistris, ut bysso, fimbriis, cæteroque cultu nonnihil sese ampullaret, fuco ac linimentis roseam oris pulchritudinem gratiose accenderet, gestu, habitu, illecebris nativi decoris juvaret honestatem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035412Z">
        <seg>now to press more strictly, so that she would at least set her beautiful hair (which was still then in its natural curl) with curling-irons, so that with linen, fringes, and with the rest of her adornment would embellish herself somewhat, graciously set off the rosy beauty of her lips with rouge and ointments, and with her bearing, her dress, and the charms of her natural grace would enhance her dignity;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nunc obsecratione, nunc jurgio virum interpellans, ne ludibrium sibi ac familiæ ineptissima fallacis somnii narratione accerseret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032135Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032135Z">
        <seg>now with entreaty, now with reproach, interrupting the man, lest he bring ridicule upon himself and his family with the most inept account of a deceptive dream.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>o animæ æstuantis vincula tenacissima, nexus perjucundi, amplexus ineluctabiles!” Gundisalvus commiserantis &amp; gratulantis affectui medius, rogabat num placeret anulum (unde hæc lætitiæ tormenta erant) manumittere, ut illo sedilis &amp; gaudii se carcere expediret?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044644Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044644Z">
        <seg>"O bonds of a burning soul, most tenacious, most delightful ties, inescapable embraces!" Gundersalvus, caught between pity and congratulation, asked whether it would please him to release the ring (from which these torments of joy arose), so that he might free himself from its prison of repose and delight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>o deliciæ!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044641Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044641Z">
        <seg>Oh, what a delight!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>o felicem animam, quæ penes se illum nunc distinet!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050944Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050944Z">
        <seg>O happy soul, who now holds him with her!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>o festinate reverti ad hunc propitium Redemptorem, quem si nunc spernitis, nulla supererit in inferno redemptio”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060536Z">
        <seg>Oh, hurry back to this merciful Redeemer, for if you now despise him, there will be no redemption left for you in hell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>o ignes!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044638Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044638Z">
        <seg>Oh, fires!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>o quæ flammulæ!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044628Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044628Z">
        <seg>Oh, what little flames!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>obiisti, prout vixisti;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030350Z">
        <seg>You died as you lived.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>oblectabat nitidioris cultus fimbriata ostentatio, tenebat venusti capillitii femineus amor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012510Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012510Z">
        <seg>He delighted in the ostentatious display of more polished grooming and was held by a feminine love of charming hair.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>obliquavit opus, flores absolvit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035536Z">
        <seg>He twisted the work, and completed the flowers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>obtendunt solutionem germani fratres, quæritur apocha, sed nusquam apparet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033958Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033958Z">
        <seg>The German brothers offer a solution; a receipt is sought, but it appears nowhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>obtenebrabatur oculorum acies, pulsabant accensa tempora, videbatur ardere cælum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040701Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040701Z">
        <seg>The acuity of the eyes was darkened, the blazing times pulsed, the sky seemed to burn;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>oculisque cum gemitu in cælum elevatis, “O (inquit) bone Jesu!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060626Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060626Z">
        <seg>and, with eyes raised to heaven and a groan, said, "O good Jesus!"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>omni hominum ordini, summis, mediis, infimis charus &amp; admirandus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050200Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050200Z">
        <seg>Beloved and admired by all classes of people: the highest, the middle, and the lowest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>optabat sibi obtigisse sexum, facundiam, vires, quibus universo terrarum orbi solum Jesu Christi amorem persuaderet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054146Z">
        <seg>he wished that he had been granted sex, eloquence, and strength, by which he would persuade the entire world of the love of Jesus Christ alone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ore, naso, oculis ubertim fœda sanies profluebat, &amp; si qua parte leviuscule attingebatur, decoriabatur, lacera pelle manum sequente.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035712Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035712Z">
        <seg>From the mouth, nose, and eyes, a foul pus flowed abundantly, and if any part was even slightly touched, it would be disfigured, the torn skin clinging to the hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>os &amp; fauces interius hiantibus plagis cruente ulcerari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013533Z">
        <seg>to ulcerate the mouth and throat with bleeding wounds on the inside;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ossa mihi sensim resolvuntur in pulverem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013424Z">
        <seg>My bones are slowly dissolving into dust.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>parenti ad manum erat Rosæ cingulum, quo Virgo postremis vitæ annis tunicam adstrinxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035018Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035018Z">
        <seg>At hand was Rose's belt, with which the Virgin had fastened her tunic in the final years of her life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>parvoque labore grabatum, uti genitrix permiserat, absolvit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042533Z">
        <seg>And with little effort, he finished the bed, as his mother had permitted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>paulo post, ejusdem conjugem a septem mensibus gravidam, ideoque pharmacorum, juxta medicinæ canones, incapacem idem corripuerat malum cum frequenti vomitu;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040035Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040035Z">
        <seg>Shortly after, his wife, who was seven months pregnant and therefore, according to the rules of medicine, unable to take medicine, was also afflicted by the same malady with frequent vomiting.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>peculiare tamen quotannis, ad normam Dominici instituti, septem illos continuos menses observabat, qui festo exaltatæ S. Crucis, &amp; Paschati Resurrectionis interfluunt, Ordinis jejunio consecrati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041312Z">
        <seg>However, he observed annually, according to the rule of the Dominican institute, those seven consecutive months which lie between the Feast of the Exaltation of the Holy Cross and Easter of the Resurrection, consecrated to the Order's fast.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>per illa te obtestor, periclitanti Franciscæ salutem impetra”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055250Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055250Z">
        <seg>I implore you by these, to secure the safety of the endangered Francisca.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>per triginta &amp; sex horas idem perstiterat placidissimi oris decor, labiorum venustas, oculorum semipatentium vivax fulgor, &amp; manuum candor;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031112Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031112Z">
        <seg>For thirty-six hours he had maintained the same placid expression, the beauty of his lips, the lively gleam of his half-open eyes, and the whiteness of his hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>peractoque circuitu, recta sacellum Rosarianum (suum portum) repetiit: ibi ad pedes Deiparæ Virginis cor suum effudit per undantes lachrymas, seipsam reprehendens, si forte palmam benedictam, aut nimis ambitiose concupierat, aut per inertiam non quæsierat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044300Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044300Z">
        <seg>And having completed the circuit, she returned straight to the Rosary chapel (her haven): there at the feet of the Virgin Mother she poured out her heart through streaming tears, reproaching herself that she had either too ambitiously coveted the blessed palm, or through inertia had not sought it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>peractum pompa funebri altaris ministerium: temperavit sonoras confusi populi inquietudines dolorifica sacri cadaveris absentia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031239Z">
        <seg>The altar's funeral pomp was completed: it tempered the sonorous, confused restlessness of the people with the painful absence of the sacred corpse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>perdens vero, iterum doloribus cum patientia donata fuit, quæ dum matri obediens narraret, visa est vultu angelico radiare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041919Z">
        <seg>But when she was lost, she was once again endowed with patience amid her pains, and as she obediently recounted them to her mother, she seemed to radiate an angelic countenance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>perrexitque, ut ante, jactare in sublime rosas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062523Z">
        <seg>And he went on, as before, to toss roses high into the air.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>perseverarat eadem flexilium membrorum tractabilitas, eadem nivei vultus amœnitas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031125Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031125Z">
        <seg>The same tractability of supple limbs remained, the same pleasantness of a snowy face;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>pete, ut more suo iisdem nunc bonis te repleat, quibus solebat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055919Z">
        <seg>Ask that, in his usual way, he now fill you with the same good things he used to.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>piæ cuidam personæ sæpius (at per soporem) apparuerat felix Rosa, edocens, quam jucundo bonoque statu res suæ erant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032024Z">
        <seg>The blessed Rose had often appeared (but in a dream) to a certain pious person, showing how pleasant and good her affairs were.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>placuitque ipsius dulci bonitati, conferre mihi vires extraordinarias, ac mire vivaces, quibus acie irretorta liberrime &amp; prolixe intuerer magnificentissimum Regem meum in toto decore suo: non enim, uti alias, ex obliquo, aut solo capite ac pectore tenus, sed integrum, ac totum cernebam a vertice ad plantas usque, &amp; ex directo facie ad faciem”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052736Z">
        <seg>and it pleased His sweet goodness to grant me extraordinary and wonderfully vivid powers, by which, with an unturned gaze, I might most freely and at length behold my most magnificent King in all His splendor: for not, as at other times, from the side, or only from the head and chest down, but I saw him whole, and entire from the top of his head to the soles of his feet, &amp; face to face."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>plerumque animo libero &amp; quietissimo perrexisse in Deum, at interdum quoque cum imbecillitate corpusculi, cum somnolentia &amp; distractionibus luctandum fuisse, verum ab illo tempore in posterum expeditius se orare, eo quod inter initia sentiret mirabiliter a Deo introrsum attrahi totam animam cum singulis potentiis, gustuque indicibili intellectum, voluntatem, memoriam, divinæ pulchritudini sic intus affigi, ut nec sponte se phantasmatum intercursu divellere, nec occupationibus sensuum externorum distrahi possit a complexu, &amp; admiratione præsentis in anima dulcissimi Numinis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050253Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050253Z">
        <seg>that he had generally advanced toward God with a free and very quiet mind, but that at times he had also to struggle with the frailty of his little body, with drowsiness, and with distractions; yet from that time forward he prayed more expeditiously, because in the beginning he had felt himself wonderfully drawn inwardly by God, with his whole soul and all its powers, and with an unspeakable taste, he perceived that his intellect, will, and memory were so affixed to the divine beauty within that he could neither of his own accord tear himself away through the interposition of phantasms, nor be distracted from this union by the occupations of the external senses, but remained in the admiration of the most sweetest Deity present in the soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>post examen anxiam Mariam graves invasere scrupuli, ne forte aut per imperitiam respondendi, aut per linguæ memoriæve cespitationem plus vel minus, quam exacta veritas &amp; rigor illius tribunalis exegerant, dictitasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032231Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032231Z">
        <seg>After the examination, anxious scruples assailed Mary, lest she might have said more or less than the exact truth and rigor of that tribunal demanded, either through inexperience in answering or through a stumble of the tongue or memory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>postea irrogabunt præsenti Sponso meo”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060239Z">
        <seg>They will later ask my current sponsor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>postremum infallibilis ex alto succursus in quibusvis tum necessitatibus, tum repentinis periculis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061752Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061752Z">
        <seg>ultimately, infallible help from on high in whatever needs or sudden dangers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>potiones magis puero obfuerunt: nam inde spuebat ore sanguinem, dolebat costis, auribus, stomacho, jecinore.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040627Z">
        <seg>The potions did the boy more harm: for he spat blood from his mouth, and his ribs, ears, stomach, and liver ached.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>premebat magnarum opum vasta œconomia, nec tamen hæc religiosæ vocationis certitudinem in animo Luciæ seu convellere potuerunt, seu delibare;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011953Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011953Z">
        <seg>She was pressured by a vast economy of great wealth, yet these could neither shake nor diminish the certainty of her religious vocation in Lucia's mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>presbyter Cardin.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043655Z">
        <seg>Cardinal Priest</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>primo, quando venerat illius animum confortatura inter adversitates, quas quidem tunc patiebatur gravissimas: secundo, cum supra Virginis sepulchrum manifeste eamdem vidit in aëre, flexis poplitibus ritu supplicum intercedentem apud Deum pro Lima, dilecta patria sua.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032030Z">
        <seg>First, when she came to comfort his spirit amid the adversities he was then suffering most severely; second, when he saw her clearly in the air above the Virgin's tomb, kneeling with her knees bent in the manner of a supplicant, interceding with God for Lima, her beloved homeland.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>primum est, paucis a Virginis obitu diebus tantam fuisse ejuscemodi conversionum frequentiam, quod Limæ in publicis officinis, ubicumque tortilia disciplinarum flagella, setarum cingula, &amp; variæ sortis cilicia vendebantur, subito defecerint hæc mortificationis ac pœnitentiæ instrumenta ob emptorum multitudinem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032625Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032625Z">
        <seg>First, a few days after the Virgin's death, there was such a great frequency of these conversions that in Lima, the public shops where discipline whips, hair belts, and various kinds of hair shirts were sold, these instruments of mortification and penance suddenly ran out due to the great number of buyers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>primum hoc ordine evenit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040056Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040056Z">
        <seg>First, this happened in this order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>primum incruenti Sacrificii mystam nominatim expressit: quid multa?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062455Z">
        <seg>First, he explicitly named the initiate of the bloodless Sacrifice: what more?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>prius illi sub episcopali infula tantisper sudandum fore pro Dei gloria, antequam hujus mortalis vitæ molestiis exuatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032104Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032104Z">
        <seg>that he must first sweat for a time under the episcopal miter for the glory of God, before he is freed from the troubles of this mortal life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>prius tamen eorum numerum inire voluit, reperitque acuminata frustula parum abesse a trecentis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042902Z">
        <seg>However, he first wanted to count them, and found that the sharp fragments were just short of three hundred.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>procurrebant decuriatim, saltitabant, pugnabant, sursum deorsum per muros, armaria, tegulas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052020Z">
        <seg>they ran about in squads, leaped, fought, up and down over the walls, cupboards, and tiles;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>prodiit Virgo quam lassitudine anhela, tam vultu hilaris, ac ultra morem lætissima;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052141Z">
        <seg>The Virgin appeared, breathless with fatigue, yet her face was cheerful and she was exceedingly joyful, more so than is customary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proinde &amp; hoc inter alia complura condonandum esse ardori suorum affectuum, maxime si verum foret, quod aliunde obscura persuasione acceperat, ex sacris indulgentiarum thesauris aliquod repositum esse lucrum fidelibus, quicumque hoc simplicitatis religioso obsequio sanctæ cruci reverentiam exhiberent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054934Z">
        <seg>Therefore, this, like many other things, was to be forgiven on account of the fervor of his affections, especially if it were true, as he had received by some obscure persuasion, that from the sacred treasuries of indulgences a certain profit had been reserved for the faithful, whoever should show reverence to the holy cross in this simple religious observance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proinde cessa scrutari, nec inutili conatu me, teque lassaveris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040834Z">
        <seg>Therefore, stop scrutinizing, and do not weary yourself and me with a useless effort.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proinde cum quadam vice obtrusisset Filiæ gestandam calanticam aureo sericoque reticulo interfulgidam, una præceptum addidit, ut obediendi religionem injiceret obluctaturæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035424Z">
        <seg>So when she had once forced upon her daughter a calantike to be worn, glittering with gold and silk netting, she added a precept to instill in her a duty of obedience to counter her resistance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proinde huic orbæ ac derelictæ suam e cælo impenderet tutelam, quam tandem adoptaturus esset in filiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013303Z">
        <seg>Therefore, he would bestow upon this forsaken world his heavenly protection, which he would eventually adopt as his daughter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proinde in Rosam quotidie anxia respiciens, quærebat millies, verane sint, quæ illa prædixerat de confessario suo diutius supervicturo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012247Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012247Z">
        <seg>Therefore, looking anxiously at Rosa every day, she asked a thousand times whether the things she had predicted about her confessor outliving her were true.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proinde non alia hic, quam immedicabilis Jobi patientia sibi opus fore, ut quoquo modo singulos crucifixi Sponsi sui dolores membratim experiretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013456Z">
        <seg>Therefore, she realized that nothing other than Job's incurable patience would be required of her, so that she might in some way experience the individual pains of her crucified Spouse, one by one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proinde non dubitarent, brevi se consummandam iri, at prius amarum hunc passionis suæ calicem amore Sponsi ad imum usque fundum sibi exhauriendum fore.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025736Z">
        <seg>Therefore, they would not doubt that they would soon be consumed, but first this bitter cup of their passion would have to be drained to the very dregs by the love of the Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proinde novo jubilo subsultans, in dulcissimo prodigio ludentem Sponsi manum tacite adorabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044501Z">
        <seg>Thus, leaping with a new joy, she silently adored the hand of the Bridegroom playing in the sweetest wonder.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proinde si aliquando suspectum sibi fuerat Rosæ epitheton, jam illud in tuto esse, utpote divinitus approbatum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034804Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034804Z">
        <seg>Therefore, if the epithet Rosa had ever seemed suspect to him, it was now safe, being, as it were, divinely approved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>promissum monasterium citius in Utopia&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; aut spatiis imaginariis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011906Z">
        <seg>The promised monastery is more quickly in Utopia or imaginary spaces;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>promissum monasterium citius in Utopia&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; aut spatiis imaginariis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062818Z">
        <seg>the promised monastery, more quickly in Utopia or in imaginary spaces;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>propendebant viscera, nec nisi magno labore atque periculo retrudebantur introrsum, mox iterum proditura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033536Z">
        <seg>The entrails were hanging out, and could only be forced back in with great labor and danger, to come out again soon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proscenium istud solius fuisse divini Amoris, reciprocos hominum amores invitantis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053917Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053917Z">
        <seg>that this stage was the sole property of divine Love, inviting the reciprocal loves of men.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>prosiliit ferocissimus hostis gigantea mole, qua contremuit cella, nil tremente Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052113Z">
        <seg>The fiercest enemy leaped forth with a gigantic bulk, which made the cell tremble, but Rosa trembled not.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>præcipue ab obitu, quando in mortuali feretro Rosæ facies liberius videnda omnibus patuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051446Z">
        <seg>especially after her death, when in her coffin Rose's face was more freely exposed to all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>præcipue e molaribus unus cum crepuisset medius, nec chirurgis ad eum evellendum ars animusque sufficerent, interdiu noctuque vexabat miseram, ut nec dormire, nec cibum masticare potuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035939Z">
        <seg>Especially one of her molars, whose middle had split open, and since the surgeons lacked both the skill and the nerve to pull it, tormented the poor woman day and night, so that she could neither sleep nor chew her food.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>præsertim quod mira perspicuitate conceptus sublimes &amp; altissimos propriis terminis nosset explicare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050045Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050045Z">
        <seg>especially because, with remarkable clarity of conception, he could explain sublime and most lofty ideas in his own terms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>pudebat Crucifixi sponsam, vel per momentum extra crucem ac passionem reperiri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052556Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052556Z">
        <seg>The Spouse of the Crucified was ashamed to be found, even for a moment, apart from the cross and passion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>pudebat sub spinoso Capite se membrum videri delicatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042053Z">
        <seg>He was ashamed to appear a delicate member under the thorny Head.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>pulverem (quem olim, dum e sepulchro Rosæ collegerat, suavissime fragrare notarat) protraxit e scrinio, eamdemque tunc suaveolentiam denuo percipiens, in spem obtinendi auxilii fortior assurrexit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035520Z">
        <seg>He drew the powder (which he had once noted smelled most sweetly while he was gathering it from Rose's tomb) from the chest, and, perceiving that same fragrance again, he rose more hopeful of receiving help.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>pœna erat quicumque motus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041904Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041904Z">
        <seg>Any movement was a punishment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>q.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032454Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032454Z">
        <seg>question</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>qua gratanter accepta, subintulit: “Hæc Rosa tu es;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061906Z">
        <seg>Upon gratefully receiving it, she added: "This rose is you."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>qua lata, dimisit ancillam, præcipiens, ne cui domesticorum diceret, ubinam Rosa lateret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052038Z">
        <seg>She let the maid go, ordering her not to tell any of the household where Rosa was hiding.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam Limæ constituendum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062821Z">
        <seg>to be established in Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam chara sint tibi stigmata nostri Redemptoris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055247Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055247Z">
        <seg>How dear to you are the stigmata of our Redeemer;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam denique attenta ac sedula, ut domesticas illorum præverteret offensas, placaret animos, mitigaret afflictiones, singulæ propemodum testantur paginæ in jurato Processu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035737Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035737Z">
        <seg>how attentive and diligent she was, in order to forestall their domestic quarrels, soothe their spirits, and mitigate their afflictions, is testified by nearly every page of the sworn deposition.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam longa nimis est patientia tua!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060629Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060629Z">
        <seg>How exceedingly long your patience is!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam medullitus mecum tristabitur maritus meus, ut audierit filium nostrum illi Societati non fore aggregandum”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012609Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012609Z">
        <seg>How deeply my husband will grieve with me when he hears that our son will not be admitted to that Society.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam nec præco, nec templorum excelsa tintinnabula, sed sola cujusque singularis devotio invitarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030536Z">
        <seg>invited by neither a herald, nor the lofty bells of the temples, but by each person's own individual devotion alone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam officiosa, ut iisdem ex morbo decubuis ministraret, adsisteret, pharmaca, ceteraque necessaria compararet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035734Z">
        <seg>how officious she was, ministering to them from their sickbeds, staying by their side, and procuring medicines and other necessities;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam torrent suaviter, quam repunt tenere per medullas!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044635Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044635Z">
        <seg>How sweetly the torrent flows, how it rushes through the very marrow!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam ubi exorsa fuit postridie, domum rediens, invenit filium pedi attracto jam solide insistere, &amp; aliquantulum ambulare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034215Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034215Z">
        <seg>The next day, upon returning home from where she had set out, she found her son, with his foot drawn up, already standing firmly and able to walk a little.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quamquam diligens perquisitio utrobique, tum materna, tum quæstoris, in domo non defuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042948Z">
        <seg>Although a diligent search, both by the mother and by the quaestor, has not been lacking in the house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quando vel maxime humana prudentia (plerumque inidonea spirituum ponderatrix) importune Virginem ad alias vocationum semitas divertere conabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035929Z">
        <seg>when human prudence (often an inept judge of spirits) was most insistently trying to divert the Virgin to other paths of calling.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quare domestico necessitudinis jure audebat verecundam ultro compellare dicendo: “Hodie, o Rosa, ut video, rursum pluerunt favores”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054342Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054342Z">
        <seg>Therefore, by virtue of their domestic relationship, he dared to address the shy woman directly, saying, "Today, O Rosa, as I see it, the favors have rained down again."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quasi ad crucifixi Sponsi exemplum dixisset: “Ecce mater tua”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030152Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030152Z">
        <seg>as if, following the example of the crucified Spouse, he had said: "Behold your mother."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quem eo tandem reduxit, ut horis omnino duabus contentus esset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043020Z">
        <seg>which he eventually reduced to the point that he was content with only two hours altogether.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quem, ut sibi a D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044523Z">
        <seg>whom, as it were, from God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quem, ut sibi a D. Maria de Usategui porrectus fuerat, modicæ chartæ involutum stringebat intra pugnum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010124Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010124Z">
        <seg>which, as it had been handed to him by D. Maria de Usategui, he clutched in his fist, wrapped in a small piece of paper.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>qui R.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012126Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012126Z">
        <seg>who R.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>qui eum non diligunt, bonitatem ignorant”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053151Z">
        <seg>Those who do not love him, do not know goodness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>qui fervores!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044632Z">
        <seg>What fervor!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>qui vero ex Hispanis &amp; Americanis&lt;/e0&gt; Mestizi &lt;e1&gt;nominantur, ut in auctiore Cangii Glossario ad has voces fusius videre est&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061643Z">
        <seg>who are called Mestizos from the Spaniards and Americans, as can be seen more fully in the later edition of Cangii's Glossary under these words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quia donum consilii mirifice in tanto viro eminere, sentiebant omnes.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050206Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050206Z">
        <seg>For everyone could see that the gift of counsel shone wonderfully in such a man.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quia es qui es, &amp; quia te amo super omnia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053102Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053102Z">
        <seg>Because you are who you are, and because I love you above all things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quia inter lanam extimumque pannum, ubi facies decumbentis excipienda erat, tortiles juncos clam occuluit, quos veteris sportæ fragmento in hunc usum detraxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042750Z">
        <seg>because between the wool and the outer cloth, where the face of the recliner was to be supported, he secretly hid some rolled rushes, which he had taken from an old basket for this purpose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quia inter ludos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034938Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034938Z">
        <seg>because between games</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quia prototypo nulla aut devotior aut similior.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055027Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055027Z">
        <seg>For no prototype is more devoted or more similar.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quibus de quacumque re ad victum &amp; medicamen necessaria exacte providere studuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042421Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042421Z">
        <seg>He strove to provide them with everything necessary for sustenance and medicine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quibus incommodis dum solito molestius die quadam gravaretur, a Rosæ indumento remedium ingenti fide quæsivit, &amp; reperit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034615Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034615Z">
        <seg>When he was burdened by these troubles on a certain day more severely than usual, he sought a remedy in Rose's garment with great faith, and found it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quibus quidem notis distinguitur &lt;s6&gt;ἀπὸ&lt;/s6&gt; &lt;s7&gt;τῆς&lt;/s7&gt; &lt;s8&gt;μελικηριδος&lt;/s8&gt;, quæ rotundior, humilior, latior, molliorque est, &amp; facilius, ut digitis cedit, sic postea recurrit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055616Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055616Z">
        <seg>By which characteristics it is distinguished from the *melichridus*, which is rounder, lower, broader, and softer, and more easily, as it yields to the fingers, so it recurves afterward.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quid demum haberet mali?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013328Z">
        <seg>What, after all, could he have against it?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quid ultra imperem?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042837Z">
        <seg>What more can I command?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quidquid de saluberrimi exercitii facilitate jactabat Virgo, meras ipse hyperbolas, femineæque simplicitatis fabulas interpretabatur: nec tamen destitit Rosa docere fratrem, qua methodo ac ratione, inter medias seculi occupationes, animum teneret in oratione defixum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045106Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045106Z">
        <seg>Whatever the Virgin boasted about the ease of the most salutary exercise, he himself interpreted as mere hyperboles and fables of feminine simplicity; yet Rosa did not cease to teach her brother by what method and with what reasoning he should keep his mind fixed in prayer amid the occupations of the world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quietissime exspiravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042905Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042905Z">
        <seg>He expired very peacefully.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quin &amp; abditissimas suæmet conscientiæ latebras Virgini patuisse, evidenter agnovit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012157Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012157Z">
        <seg>He clearly recognized that even the most hidden recesses of his own conscience had been laid bare to the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quin &amp; aliis suadebat, clarum sereni firmamenti aspectum inter potiora, quæ spiritum elevant allectamenta, posse numerari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043717Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043717Z">
        <seg>Indeed, he also urged others that the clear sight of the serene firmament could be counted among the chief things that uplift the spirit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quin &amp; eo tandem processit ars atque industria, ut qui dormituræ lectulus, idem &amp; equuleus esset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042502Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042502Z">
        <seg>Indeed, art and industry have advanced to such a degree that the little bed for sleeping is also a pillory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quin &amp; postea ejusdem Seraphicæ virginis imitatione sibimet capillos ad vivam usque cutem, inscia matre, præscidit, ne quid superesset funium, quibus contra juratum virginitatis propositum aut traheret, aut traheretur ad nuptias: jam enim satis didicerat ex ludicris, luti inquinamenta suo capillitio quandoque etiam serio posse insidiari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035031Z">
        <seg>She even, later, by imitating that same Seraphic virgin, cut her hair down to the very skin, her mother unaware, so that no tuft would remain by which, contrary to her sworn vow of virginity, she might be either drawn or dragged to marriage: for she had already learned well enough from her playtime that the filth of mud could sometimes seriously threaten her tresses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quin continuo jacendum fuit in lectulo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034125Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034125Z">
        <seg>Rather, he had to lie down immediately on the little bed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quin flagitabat superaddi sputa, calces, verbera, nihilque omitti, quod extremi contemptus saperet vilitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040512Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040512Z">
        <seg>He was even demanding that spittle, kicks, and beatings be added, and that nothing be omitted which smacked of the vilest contempt.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quin potius majorem indies lumbricorum vim effundebat cum sanguine.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033750Z">
        <seg>Rather, it was pouring a greater force of worms with the blood each day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quinimo in ”cursum pruriunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033449Z">
        <seg>Rather, they are itching for the race.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quinimo te, o salus, o gaudium animæ meæ, te, inquam, opto amare quantum tu ipse, mi Deus, te amas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053114Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053114Z">
        <seg>Rather, I desire you, O health, O joy of my soul; you, I say, I desire to love as much as you yourself, my God, love yourself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quinimo, hac ipsa nocte, in honorem SS. Trinitatis una hæc virgula” (digito monstrabat surculum nulla fœcunditatis nota tunc usquam turgidum) “tria nobis caryophylla protrudet”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055141Z">
        <seg>"Rather, this very night, in honor of the Holy Trinity, this very little twig" (he pointed to a shoot, then entirely bare of any sign of fertility) "will produce three carobs for us."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quisnam aut Virginis languorem illi dominæ aut certum adventum emissi mancipii nuntiasset Rosæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051732Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051732Z">
        <seg>Who could have told Rosa of either the lady's fainting spell or the certain arrival of the freed slave?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quo &lt;b0&gt;Rosa a S. Maria&lt;/b0&gt; in posterum cognominaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T213212Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203449Z">
        <seg>by which &lt;b0&gt;Rose of St. Mary&lt;/b0&gt; would be nicknamed in the future.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quo &lt;b3&gt;Rosa a S. Maria&lt;/b3&gt; in posterum cognominaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034742Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034742Z">
        <seg>by which &lt;b3&gt;Rose of St. Mary&lt;/b3&gt; would be nicknamed in the future.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quo convalescente, torquebat Rosam febris non sua, nec tamen a sermone abesse voluit: febrilem enim ardorem facile superabat exæstuans divinæ gloriæ desiderium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042124Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042124Z">
        <seg>As he recovered, a fever that was not his own was tormenting Rosa, yet she did not wish to be absent from the conversation: for she easily overcame the feverish ardor, kindled by a desire for divine glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quo dicto innotuit, sui transitus terminum divina revelatione compertum habuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042710Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042710Z">
        <seg>Upon this, it became known that he had learned from divine revelation the appointed time of his passing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quo fit, quod illata corpora mox depulpet, ossiumque duritiem extemplo resolvat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035334Z">
        <seg>Thus it happens that it soon pulps the introduced bodies and at once dissolves the hardness of the bones.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quo loco templum, quo situ porta, officinæ, porticus, dormitoria forent disponenda;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062737Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062737Z">
        <seg>where the temple, and in what location the gate, workshops, portico, and dormitories were to be arranged.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quo sensu Persius&lt;/e0&gt; costam montis &lt;e1&gt;appellavit&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060908Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060908Z">
        <seg>In what sense Persius called the ridge of the mountain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quo ut pervenit, unico semper angulo immota consedit, nullo abrepta famosæ curiositatis spectaculo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035948Z">
        <seg>Wherever she arrived, she always sat motionless in a single corner, never carried away by the spectacle of the famed curiosity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod Gaspari narratum insomnium videbatur, donec Filia nummos, uti acceperat, adnumeravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042604Z">
        <seg>What had been told to Gaspar seemed a dream, until the daughter counted the coins as she had received them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod ad inutile pondus &amp; continuos tantummodo dolores gestabatur ab humero prædensis fasciis obvolutum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043156Z">
        <seg>which, as a useless weight, was carried on the shoulder, wrapped in the bandages of the Predensians, and caused nothing but constant pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod audisset, malignum [spiritum] aliquando per pupam (fortasse idolum fuerit) garritum edidisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043230Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043230Z">
        <seg>that he had heard an evil spirit at one time utter a cackle through a puppet (perhaps it was an idol).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod contra tot prudentum arbitria plus sola ipsa sapere velit, quam oporteat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062805Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062805Z">
        <seg>that it would want to be wiser than is fitting, against the judgment of so many prudent people, by relying on its own wisdom alone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod cœptum anno Domini MDCXXX, ob multitudinem testium, qui fuere numero centum octoginta tres, non potuit absolvi, nisi in mense Maio anno MDCXXXII.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043041Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043041Z">
        <seg>which, having been begun in the year of the Lord 1630, could not be completed because of the great number of witnesses, who numbered one hundred and eighty-three, until the month of May in the year 1632.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod mirabiliter re ipsa, ut Virgo prænuntiaverat, evenit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042412Z">
        <seg>which, wonderfully, came to pass in fact, just as the Virgin had foretold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod si licentia in experimentum virtutis negabatur, plerumque in aliud triduum fraudabat sitim, non prius bibitura quam mater annueret: imo &amp; questa est, ab ea sibi hanc veniam non crebrius denegari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035728Z">
        <seg>For if permission to test her strength was denied, she would usually stave off her thirst for another three days, not drinking until her mother gave her leave. Indeed, she complains that her mother does not deny her this permission very often.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod tamen ille nec dixerat, nec somniarat&lt;a1&gt;b&lt;/a1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203338Z">
        <seg>which, however, he had neither said nor dreamed of.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod tamen ille nec dixerat, nec somniarat&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;b&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041550Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041550Z">
        <seg>which, however, he had neither said nor dreamed of.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod tibi jam satis supra demonstravimus testibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054701Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054701Z">
        <seg>which we have already demonstrated to you sufficiently above with testimonies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod vero id ibidem aliis Rosa communicare potuerit, singulare fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043940Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043940Z">
        <seg>That she was able to share it with others there, Rosa, was a unique thing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod ægerrime paterentur domesticum hunc thesaurum quotidiano suo ac suorum conspectui, devotioni, gaudio subduci: præsertim, quia pluries a Rosa didicerant, gratiosam hanc Salvatoris effigiem toti domui salutarem esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054044Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054044Z">
        <seg>that they would suffer grievously to have this domestic treasure removed from their daily sight, devotion, and joy; especially since they had learned from Rosa on several occasions that this gracious image of the Savior was salutary for the entire household.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod, nisi immatura morte præventa fuisset, perfecisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042341Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042341Z">
        <seg>which, had it not been prevented by an untimely death, it would have accomplished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quodque interrogantibus responsum dabat, simul idioma erat mentis in Deum elevatæ: exempli gratia, si cum aliis feminis hortum intranti, operosius laudabatur viridarii amœnitas, respondebat comiter: “Venustus ac pulcher est hortus, Deus illi flores adaugeat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045006Z">
        <seg>She would answer everyone who asked, and at the same time her speech was a sign of a mind lifted up to God: for example, if, upon entering the garden with other women, the beauty of the green space was more lavishly praised, she would politely reply, "The garden is lovely and beautiful; may God increase its flowers."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quodque virgines in capite aliquam coronulam albam, martyres rubram, &amp; doctores viridem gestabunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032408Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032408Z">
        <seg>Each virgin will wear a small white crown on her head, the martyrs a red one, and the doctors a green one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quoniam etiam in diebus nostris non defuerunt misericordiæ ejus super nos: &amp; licet paterne, culpis nostris exigentibus, corripiat, non tamen in perpetuum obliviscitur, sed in angustiis nostris multiplici nos electorum, &amp; amicorum suorum præsidio custodire non desinit, qui meritorum, &amp; suffragiorum suorum patrociniis nos muniat &amp; defendat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041055Z">
        <seg>For even in our days His mercies have not failed us. And though He chastens us as a father, our faults demanding it, He does not forget us forever; but in our afflictions He never ceases to protect us with manifold aid through His elect and His friends, by whose merits and intercessions He arms and defends us.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quos considerabat omni humana ope tam indigos, quam destitutos;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032820Z">
        <seg>whom he considered, in every human respect, as needy as they were destitute.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quæ fugæ istius damnum, offendiculum, dedecus, unico simul confuso metu complexa, cum, post plurium dierum quæstionem, profuga nusquam reperiretur, ad Rosæ opem confugit, suamque illi commendans angustiam, monasterio perditum mancipium restitui flagitavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033936Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033936Z">
        <seg>She, embracing the damage, obstacle, and disgrace of this escape—all at once confused with fear—when, after several days of searching, the fugitive could not be found, fled to Rosa's aid, and, commending her distress to her, demanded that the lost servant be restored to the monastery.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quæ postea a consciis prodigii interrogata, quidnam putarit hoc aërio rosarum suppendiculo portendi, respondit candore impavido, his præsignificari futurum apud Limenses suos insigne sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis monasterium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062539Z">
        <seg>When later questioned by those aware of the prodigy as to what she thought this aerial rosary pendant portended, she replied with unflinching candor that it was a foretaste of the future, distinguished monastery of Saint Catherine of Siena among her fellow Limeños.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quæ, quod pridie dixerat, simplicissime confirmavit, totam rei gestæ seriem grato animo in suæ seraphicæ magistræ favorem, &amp; gratiam refundens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055445Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055445Z">
        <seg>She most simply confirmed what she had said the day before, gratefully giving thanks for the entire series of events in favor of her seraphic mistress.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quæque ut in ceteris, ita celebrius in his duobus nec primam similem visa est, nec habere sequentem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044033Z">
        <seg>And each, as in the others, so more famously in these two, seemed neither the first of its kind nor to have a successor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quærebat loco solito Rosam, asylum ac solatium suum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031723Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031723Z">
        <seg>She sought her usual refuge and solace, Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quærebat per ambulacra infirmas præ ceteris horridas, magisque porriginosas, his mira alacritate componebat lectulum, eluebat vasa, parabat edulia, neque tam vile, tetricum, abjectum obsequium commodari infirmis poterat, quod non ipsa ultro prensaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061302Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061302Z">
        <seg>She would seek out the sick along the corridors—those most dreadful and protracted of all—and with remarkable alacrity she would make their beds, wash their vessels, and prepare their food. Nor could she render them any mean, grim, or base service that she did not herself willingly undertake.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quærebat, pulsabat, gemebat spiritus contribulatus, nec audiebatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045630Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045630Z">
        <seg>he cried out, he knocked, he groaned with a broken spirit, and was not heard;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quæsitum ubique &amp; diu, geminatur cum quæstione anxietas, turbantur omnia, inventum nihil.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035644Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035644Z">
        <seg>Seeking everywhere and for a long time, anxiety doubles with the search, everything is disturbed, nothing is found.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quæst.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050836Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050836Z">
        <seg>question</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>recedite a viis pessimis, quibus pecudum instar ducimini ad macellum stygii lanionis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060527Z">
        <seg>Depart from the worst of paths, by which you are led like cattle to the slaughterhouse of a Stygian butcher;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>recipiuntur in domesticum contubernium octo inopes, mutatur e vestigio inofficiosum testamentum, consobrinæ cum consobrinis totius opulentiæ scribuntur ex asse hæredes;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032836Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032836Z">
        <seg>Eight needy people are taken into the household; an inofficious will is changed on the spot; cousins are written into the will as heirs to the entire estate;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>recursum ad solitum Roseæ virtutis emplastrum: &amp; applicitis cruri virgineis reliquiis, cessit inquietudo &amp; dolor, successit cum placidissimo somno perfecta &amp; stabilis valetudo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034850Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034850Z">
        <seg>resorting to the usual plaster of Rose's virtue: and with the virgin relics applied to the leg, the restlessness and pain subsided, and perfect and stable health followed with the most peaceful sleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>regularium claustra cruentis flagellorum ictibus undique pœnitentiam resonabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043103Z">
        <seg>The bars of the regulars resounded on all sides with penitence from the bloody blows of the whips.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>reliqua viscera torrebat lentus calor, ac præcipue caput, crebris accendebatur ardoribus, denique ex profunda melancholia contumax &amp; adusta gravedo, sensim vergebat in tabem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035548Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035548Z">
        <seg>A slow heat was scorching his remaining viscera, and especially his head, which was kindled by frequent ardors; finally, from a deep melancholy, a stubborn and seared heaviness was gradually turning into consumption.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>reliquosque domesticos officii, ac Christianæ pietatis admonuit, ut jam non femina, sed apostolus prædicare videretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042852Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042852Z">
        <seg>She admonished the other household servants and the Christian household to the duties of their office, so that she might be seen preaching, not as a woman, but as an apostle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>rem tam arduam validissimis obstaculis, remoris, ac difficultatibus haud carituram, verumtamen in ipso tempestatum summo furore ac motu, tranquillissime sperandum fore in superno auxilio, quod Rosa jam Sponso immortali conregnans, facillime sit exoratura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012030Z">
        <seg>that a task so arduous would not be without the strongest obstacles, delays, and difficulties; nevertheless, in the very height of the storm's fury and motion, it would be most peacefully hoped for that help would come from above, which the Rose, already co-reigning with the immortal Spouse, would most easily obtain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>remedium aliunde nullum supererat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035840Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035840Z">
        <seg>No other remedy remained.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>repente quæ plumbum fuerat, se plumam sensit, nemine adminiculante surrexit, domum repetiit, matri ingenue rem, ut erat, detexit, proque monastica aliud clausuræ genus (quod cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040140Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040140Z">
        <seg>Suddenly she who had been lead felt herself a feather; unassisted she rose, returned home, ingenuously revealed the matter to her mother just as it was, and in exchange for the monastic life chose another form of enclosure (which chapter…</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>reposcebat a sancta Catharina Senensi alteram hanc Limensem Catharinam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031726Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031726Z">
        <seg>She was emulating this other Catherine of Lima after Saint Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>resiccata valde tenuia, impense acria, calidaque.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043153Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043153Z">
        <seg>Very finely dried, intensely pungent, and hot.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>respondebat ad interrogata seriatim, apposite, ordinate: conversabatur expedite, disponebat agenda, agebat disposita, facilitate, attentione, promptitudine, qua solent, qui unice negotiis exterioribus intendunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044839Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044839Z">
        <seg>He answered questions in sequence, appropriately and in order; he conducted himself expeditiously, arranged his agenda, and carried out his plans with facility, attentiveness, and promptness—qualities typical of those who devote themselves exclusively to external affairs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>respondit illa, se brevi illo, quo coram imagine orarat, spatio sensisse reducem in articulos vigorem, illosque detumuisse statim violento spiraculo, ut solet aëre plena vesica, dum premitur, sicque una cum tumore omnem pariter evanuisse dolorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055439Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055439Z">
        <seg>She replied that in the short time she had been praying before the image, she felt the strength return to her joints, and that they immediately swelled up again with a violent puff, as a bladder full of air does when it is pressed, and that in this way all the pain vanished along with the swelling.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>restituor quasi numquam excidissem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050510Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050510Z">
        <seg>I am restored as if I had never fallen away.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>rigebant membra sine motu;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013319Z">
        <seg>Their limbs lay rigid and motionless.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>rogasti me, &amp; ecce ad solitas preces te voco: surge (inquam) Filiola mea, surge, jam hora sonuit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051400Z">
        <seg>You asked me, and behold I call you to the usual prayers: arise, my little daughter, arise, for the hour has already sounded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>rosis porro liliisque significari puras ac Deo dilectas Limensium virginum animas, quæ quidem hinc inde per urbem sine ordine sparsim habitabant, &amp; suo tempore in cælum claustrale fore congregandas, ubi tutissimæ sint ab omni secularis calcanei protritu, solique æterno Numini fragrent odorem suavitatis, donec inde translatæ in Sponsi paradisum, eidem nectantur in immarcescibilem gloriæ coronam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062601Z">
        <seg>that the roses and lilies signify the pure and God-beloved souls of the virgins of Lima, who indeed dwelt scattered here and there throughout the city without order, and were to be gathered in due time into the cloistered heaven, where they will be safest from all the wear and tear of the secular world, and will breathe for the eternal Deity alone the fragrance of sweetness, until, translated from there into the Paradise of the Spouse, they are bound to Him in an unfading crown of glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>rursum guttulis guttæ, rivis rivuli successere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053846Z">
        <seg>Again, drops succeeded drops, and rivulets succeeded rivulets.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>rursum patuerunt hiulcæ fistulæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035908Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035908Z">
        <seg>The hollow pipes opened again.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sanguinem, qui verbera sequeretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041504Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041504Z">
        <seg>blood, which would follow the beatings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sat erat in his, aut plane elinguem fieri, aut compendio protestari, nil esse jucunditatis, lætitiæ, jubili, in istoc mundo inferiori, quod vel umbratenus assimiletur gaudio istius pretiosi Convivii, ubi infinita animæ fames incidit in pastum Deo dignum, in cibum filiorum, in mamillam Verbi incarnati, quod solum esurientes replet bonis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055907Z">
        <seg>it was enough in these things, or to be made completely mute, or to protest in short that there is no sweetness, joy, or jubilation in this lower world that can even remotely be likened to the joy of that precious Banquet, where the infinite hunger of the soul falls upon a meal worthy of God, upon the food of the sons, upon the breast of the Incarnate Word, which alone fills the hungry with good things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sciant, absque pondere afflictionum ad gratiæ cumulos non perveniri: agnoscant, juxta ærumnarum incrementa augeri pariter charismatum mensuram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052820Z">
        <seg>Let them know that without the weight of afflictions one does not attain the fullness of grace; let them acknowledge that, as the hardships increase, the measure of charisms increases accordingly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sciebat hunc esse calicem, quem Sponsus biberat, &amp; ipsi, ut sponsæ miscuerat, ac tamdiu ante ei præmonstraverat: unde constanter &amp; jucunde, velut e manu Christi Domini porrectum totum ebibit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042750Z">
        <seg>She knew this was the chalice the Bridegroom had drunk, and which he had mixed for her, the bride, and had shown her so long before; from which she drank it all, steadfastly and joyfully, as if it had been offered to her by the hand of Christ the Lord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sciebat præterea, scrupuli sui morsus nemini præterquam sibi ac cælo notos fuisse: cumque maxime nocturnis horis soleret ad omnem peregrinum strepitum expavescere, sub voce præmemorata non tantum nihil expavit, sed plane refecta est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032259Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032259Z">
        <seg>She also knew that the pangs of her conscience were known to no one but herself and the heavens; and since she was in the habit of being startled by any unfamiliar sound, especially at night, at the aforementioned voice she not only was not startled at all, but was in fact completely reassured.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>scio quid ille mihi, tametsi indignæ, pollicitus sit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062024Z">
        <seg>I know what he promised me, even though it was unworthy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>se Rosæ ultimos spiritus trahenti adfuisse prope mortem, eaque occasione clam petiisse a moribunda, confestim ubi ad Sponsi cælestem thalamum pertigisset, satageret impetrare sibi a Numine gratuitum illud ac singulare donum, quod ipse tunc mente volebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032730Z">
        <seg>that he had been present as Rose drew her last breath, near death, and on that occasion had secretly asked of the dying woman that, as soon as he reached the Spouse's heavenly bridal chamber, he would strive to obtain for himself from the Divine that free and singular gift which he then desired in his mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>se quidem refloridis oblati Carmeli desertis mirifice &amp; capi &amp; affici, verumtamen solius divinæ, non humanæ inspirationis proprium id opus ac munus esse: Spiritum ubi vult spirare, aptam regularis status tum electionem, tum commutationem non esse volentis, neque currentis, sed miserentis Dei;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040317Z">
        <seg>that indeed the Carmelite deserts, offered to the reflowering, are wonderfully both to be taken and to be affected, yet that this work and office is proper to divine, not human, inspiration: that the Spirit blows where He wills, and that the apt choice and change of the regular state is not of the will of the one who wills, nor of the one who runs, but of the merciful God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>se vero de suopte nomine hoc insuper adjectum ei velle auctarium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034739Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034739Z">
        <seg>but he himself, of his own accord, wanted to add this further to his reward.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>secreti hujus beneficium, quia concessit Dominus, non auferat minister”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040837Z">
        <seg>"Let the minister not take away the benefit of this secret, because the Lord has granted it."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed &amp; illa insolubiliter videbatur adglutinata: conabatur semel iterumve fortunatus homo se excutere de sedili, disjungere tibias, aut brachia saltem attrahere, nec quidquam horum poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044610Z">
        <seg>but she too seemed fastened in an insoluble way: the lucky man tried again and again to wrench himself from the seat, to pull his thighs apart, or at least to draw in his arms, but he could do none of it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed (Numine sic disponente) tardarunt illæ, atque interea Rodericus clam parentibus cœnobium sancti Francisci adiit, [illum Ordini Franciscano adscribendum esse,] habitum petiit, admissionem obtinuit, monitisque, &amp; demum flexis ad consensum parentibus, octiduo post, solemni ritu in pauperem Fratrum Minorum Ordinem adlectus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012640Z">
        <seg>But (as Providence had ordained), they were delayed, and in the meantime Rodericus secretly went to the monastery of Saint Francis with his parents, requested the habit, obtained admission, and after receiving their counsel and finally winning his parents' consent, he was received by solemn rite into the poor Order of the Friars Minor eight days later.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035818Z">
        <seg>but Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed Parvuli incessus gravior erat, quam puerorum esse soleat, majestas inerat, &amp; quacumque fixisset pedem, subitaneo lumine resplendebat pavimentum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051201Z">
        <seg>But the Parvuli's gait was more stately than is usual for children; there was a majesty to it, and wherever he placed his foot, the floor would suddenly gleam with light.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed Rosa leniter accedens, dextrum jacentis brachium inter cubitum ac pugnum comiter arripuit, valide at successive strinxit, diuque sic attinuit, nec nisi post moras admodum prolixas placidissime dimisit, simulque disparuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032330Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032330Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, gently approaching, kindly seized the right arm of the reclining figure between the elbow and the fist, squeezed it firmly but gradually, and held it so for a long time, and only after a very long delay released it most peacefully, and at the same time vanished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed Rosa postridie, oratione præmissa, matrem adiit, librum oblatum expeditissime legit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012957Z">
        <seg>But the next day, after a prayer, Rosa went to her mother, and read the offered book as quickly as possible.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed Rosa præ tenuitate spiritus tunc respondere aliud non potuit, nisi quod verbis intercisis diceret, malum quidem se nullum habere, at ipsissimam mortem in intimis viscerum suorum penetralibus liberrime dominari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013332Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, for want of spirit, could at that moment answer nothing else, except that, her words broken, she said she had no evil indeed, but that death itself ruled most freely in the innermost recesses of her bowels.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed Rosam merito dici lampadem amoris non sat intellexit, qui primus dixit, Philostratus&lt;a1&gt;c&lt;/a1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032724Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032724Z">
        <seg>But he who first said that the lamp of love was fittingly called Rose did not fully understand it, Philostratus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed Rosam nec terreri, nec timidiuscule silere permittebat ipsa supernæ revelationis firmissima certitudo, adeo ut, quod verbis idoneis adstruere satis non poterat, expressis in assula notis architectonicis conaretur evidentius adumbrare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062725Z">
        <seg>But the very firmest certainty of the heavenly revelation did not permit Rosa to be either terrified or to fall silent in a timid manner, so much so that, since she could not adequately prove it with words, she attempted to more clearly outline it with the architectural marks inscribed on the tablet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed Virgini pro affectus magnitudine videbantur pauca: fascem cupierat, non fasciculum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044935Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044935Z">
        <seg>But to a virgin, the magnitude of the affection seemed small: she had desired a bundle, not a sheaf.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed addito jussu, ne injussa illas a se revelleret, aut quoquomodo submoveret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035508Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035508Z">
        <seg>but with the added order that he should not, without permission, remove them from himself or in any way move them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed aliter assueta præscriptum horæ punctum seu dormiendo seu evigilando attingere non potuit, idque sat fuit, ut vehementi scrupulo torqueretur, velut rea violatæ obedientiæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035715Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035715Z">
        <seg>But being accustomed to a different routine, she could not meet the appointed hour, whether by sleeping or by staying awake, and that alone was enough to torment her with a severe pang of conscience, as if she were guilty of disobedience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ambæ ad odoriferam Rosæ famam compunctæ, ejusque validis coram Altissimi throno precibus commendatæ, nodos impuritatis feliciter exuerunt, &amp; in calle virtutis constantem fixere pedem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032631Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032631Z">
        <seg>But both, moved by the fragrant fame of the Rose and commended by her powerful prayers before the throne of the Most High, happily cast off the knots of impurity and firmly fixed their foot in the path of virtue.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ancilla domestica Bernarda, in thaumaturgo Rosæ myrothecio efficax infanti malagma invenit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040332Z">
        <seg>but the domestic servant Bernarda, in the thaumaturgist Rose of Myrotheca, found an effective ointment for the infant.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed audita non fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045748Z">
        <seg>but it was not heard.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed blandum, candidum, ac pene in angelum lucis transfiguratum: nam species illi humana erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052156Z">
        <seg>but gentle, fair, and almost transfigured into an angel of light: for his appearance was human.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed cavebant singuli, ne Rosæ insiderent, ac velut ex condicto suæ parcebant hospitæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043801Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043801Z">
        <seg>But each one was careful not to step on the roses, and as if by arrangement, they spared their hostess.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed consopitus erat omnis affectuum calor, obtusa omnis ratio, omnem undique pietatis sensum profundissimus obsederat lethargus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045546Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045546Z">
        <seg>But all warmth of feeling was extinguished, all reason dulled; a profound lethargy had seized every sense of piety.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed continuo revolvebatur in eamdem parœmiam sobria liquescentis animulæ ebrietas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044359Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044359Z">
        <seg>but the sobriety of the melting little soul was continually turned into the same proverb: drunkenness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed contra evenit: nam ipsa miraculorum celebritas &amp; multitudo, indies ad invisendum Rosæ sepulchrum plures pluresque accersebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031336Z">
        <seg>But the opposite occurred: for the very fame and multitude of miracles drew ever more people day by day to visit Rose's tomb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed crevit admiratio, quando novitiarum magistra (quæ juxta in suo cubiculo, reliquis ad chorum euntibus, remanserat) affirmavit, se vigilem, sub tanti operis vicino molimine, strepitum ne minimum quidem percepisse in tam alto dormitorii silentio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040934Z">
        <seg>But amazement grew when the novice mistress (who had remained in her cell nearby while the others went to the choir) affirmed that she, being awake under the nearby pressure of such a work, had not perceived the slightest noise in the profound silence of the dormitory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed culicum insolentiam non ferens, unum, qui sanguine jam turgebat, manu occidit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043854Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043854Z">
        <seg>But, unable to bear the mosquito's insolence, he killed one that was already swollen with blood with his hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed cum, desperato omni alio medicamine, appressit pectori eamdem imaginem, abscessit dolor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040407Z">
        <seg>But when, having despaired of all other remedy, he pressed the same image to his breast, the pain subsided.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed de hoc infra.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042626Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042626Z">
        <seg>but more on this below.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed dum festinus instat operi, ferrum incautus sinistræ manui immersit ea profunditate, ut una cum cute lacera caro penderet a junctura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034816Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034816Z">
        <seg>But as he was hurrying to get the work done, he carelessly plunged the iron into his left hand so deeply that, along with the torn skin, the flesh hung from the joint.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ejusdem particulæ beneficio, post imploratum Rosæ auxilium, morbus uterque fugatus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034844Z">
        <seg>But by the benefit of that same particle, after imploring Rose's help, both illnesses were driven away.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed eo vespere nec spes residua fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055135Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055135Z">
        <seg>But that evening, there was no hope left.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed euntem occupavit cælitus immissa cogitatio, quæ deinceps omnem exarmavit formidinem, docuit fiduciam, infudit securitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062052Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062052Z">
        <seg>but a thought sent down from heaven seized him as he went, which thenceforth disarmed all fear, taught him confidence, and instilled security.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed explicare manum, implexosque exerere digitos nequaquam potuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044650Z">
        <seg>But he could by no means straighten his hand and stretch out his fingers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed formosa, decens, urbana.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052159Z">
        <seg>but beautiful, proper, and urbane.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed frustra fuere omnia, prævalente secularium clamore: tandem necesse fuit cantores choro egressos, ipsummet altare, ubi litabatur, circumdare, ut e propinquo vicissim audirent, &amp; audirentur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031032Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031032Z">
        <seg>But all was in vain, the clamor of the seculars prevailing; at last it became necessary to have the singers leave the choir and surround the very altar where the service was being held, so that they might in turn hear and be heard up close.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed frustra: nam sensim eo redibant, unde attractæ fuerunt, solamque oculorum medietatem operiebant: quasi Limenses suos etiam post mortem Virgo pupillis columbinis amaret dulciter intueri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030415Z">
        <seg>but in vain: for they slowly returned to where they had been drawn, covering only the middle of the eyes: as if the Virgin, even after her death, sweetly loved to gaze with her dove-like pupils at her Limeños.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed generosa Discipula pluris faciebat magistræ dolorosa stigmata, quam delicata solatia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052553Z">
        <seg>but the noble Disciple valued the painful stigmata of her teacher more than delicate consolations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed huic fere evenit, quod olim duobus aulicis vitam S. Antonii abbatis fortuita curiositate legentibus&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;: nam dum altera, quasi fallendo tempori lectitabat, hæc velut subjectis cælestium flammarum tædis succendebatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032705Z">
        <seg>But this almost happened to two courtiers who were reading the life of St. Anthony the Abbot out of mere curiosity: for while the one was reading, as it were, to while away the time, the other was being kindled as it were by the torches of heavenly flames;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed huic graviter respondit Rosa: “Nolim haberi tam delicata, ut pro Sponsi mei ornatu unicæ noctis labor aut nimius mihi sit aut molestus: quæ enim tam socors aut otiosa fuerit nupta, cui durum sit noctem impendere ornamento &amp; cultui, quo maritus eget, ut postridie decenter in publicum prodeat”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060130Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060130Z">
        <seg>But Rosa answered him sharply: "I do not wish to be so delicate that the labor of my groom's adornment for the one night should be too great or troublesome for me. For what bride could be so idle or unconcerned that she would find it hard to spend the night on the adornment and grooming her husband requires, so that the next day she might appear in public decently?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed hæc morbum irritando, nova indies pernicie non ægram, sed ipsam ægritudinem adjuvabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033738Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033738Z">
        <seg>But by irritating the disease, these new daily perils did not afflict the patient, but aided the very illness itself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ibidem dubitavimus, an eadem phrasis apud Hispanos in usu sit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041436Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041436Z">
        <seg>But there we also wondered whether the same phrase is in use among the Spaniards.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ille Marpesia caute durior, aut responsa denegabat, aut punctum, cui respondendum fuerat, obscuris inanium verborum ambagibus solertissime declinabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035951Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035951Z">
        <seg>But Marpesias, shrewdly more cautious, either refused to answer or most skillfully evaded the point that had been raised with obscure, empty circumlocutions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed illico ut cordi palpitabundo modicum illius terræ applicuit, utroque morbo se explicitam deprehendit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035601Z">
        <seg>But as soon as she applied a little of that earth to her palpitating heart, she found herself overtaken by both diseases.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed impeditus planxit foris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040447Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040447Z">
        <seg>But hindered, he wept outside.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed impleri debuit, quod cælitus prædictum erat, fore sepulturam illius gloriosam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030434Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030434Z">
        <seg>But it was to be fulfilled, as had been divinely foretold, that his burial would be glorious.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed incassum, quia contumax vitium, triginta &amp; trium annorum possessione ac mora, nimium invaluerat, nec videbatur aliud pereunti superesse remedium, nisi quod Rosa post tot aliorum hortamenta, consilia, invectivas, post tot Superiorum correptiones, minas, clamores, suam exereret ardentioris facundiæ mirabilem suadam, hominique loqueretur ad cor, &amp; ejus saltem animæ salutem omnipotenti Numinis dexteræ commendaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060753Z">
        <seg>but in vain, for the stubborn vice, by thirty-three years of possession and delay, had grown too strong, and no other remedy seemed to remain for the perishing man, except that Rosa, after so many exhortations, counsels, and invectives from others, after so many rebukes, threats, and clamors from her superiors, would exercise her marvelous, more ardent eloquence, speak to the man's heart, and commend at least the salvation of his soul to the hand of the Almighty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed incassum: vix enim dies perpauci effluxerant, cum crucifer cespes apud proreginam funditus exaruit, manifesto indicio, propriam sibi defuisse crucis &amp; Calvariæ hortulanam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054952Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054952Z">
        <seg>but in vain: for scarcely a few days had passed when the crusader's tuft of grass at the queen's prow withered up completely, a clear sign that its own gardener of the Cross and Calvary had been lacking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed insuper iidem in decantandis Deo laudibus strenue pro modulo suo me juvant”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043822Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043822Z">
        <seg>but moreover, the same people energetically help me, to the best of their ability, in praising God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed interim cavebat, &amp; (quoad potuit) obstabat, ne in talium epularum quantulamcumque communionem aut sympathiam admitteretur miserum corpus, quod soli passioni &amp; cruciatui destinarat: &amp;, si eam Rex introducebat in cellaria sua, ipsa suum corpus jubebat foris stare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041947Z">
        <seg>But in the meantime she was on her guard and, as far as she could, kept herself from being admitted to any partaking or sympathy in such feasting—her wretched body, which she had devoted solely to suffering and torment. And if the King brought her into his cellars, she herself ordered her body to remain outside.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ipsam confessarii jam spiritu grandem repererant, quando tam tenellæ suæ ætati permiserunt accubitum in sacro Convivio, quod præbet delicias regibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055644Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055644Z">
        <seg>But the confessors themselves had already found her great in spirit, when they permitted so tender an age to recline at the sacred Banquet, which provides delights for kings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed latius extenditur, nempe usque ad terras Australes, novam Guineam &amp; archipelagum sancti Lazari versus mare Indicum, sicut Baudrandus affirmat.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035108Z">
        <seg>but extends more broadly, namely to the Southern lands, New Guinea &amp; the Archipelago of St. Lazarus toward the Indian Ocean, as Baudrandus affirms.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed mater secundas in parturientis utero remansisse adverterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035740Z">
        <seg>But the mother had noticed that the afterbirth had remained in the woman's womb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed mater sonticum&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; morbum suspicata (præsertim quod eadem nonnullorum confessariorum erat seu crisis, seu imperita credulitas) medicum advocat, ejusque curæ inutili suam, tot undique tribulis vexatam, Rosam commendat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010338Z">
        <seg>But the mother, suspecting a contagious disease (especially since it was the same crisis or uninformed credulity of some confessors), calls for a doctor and entrusts her own daughter, Rose—already beset on all sides by troubles—to his care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed mater sonticum&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; morbum suspicata (præsertim quod eadem nonnullorum confessariorum erat seu crisis, seu imperita credulitas) medicum advocat, ejusque curæ inutili suam, tot undique tribulis vexatam, Rosam commendat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045741Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045741Z">
        <seg>But the mother, suspecting a contagious disease (especially since it was the same malady that had afflicted some of the confessors, whether a crisis or uninformed credulity), calls for a doctor and entrusts her own daughter, Rosa—who was being beset on all sides by troubles—to his useless care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed mater suspicans, Filiolam puerili more discendi tædium ac laborem defugere, induxit confessarium, ut coram se Juvenculam de neglectu redargueret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012950Z">
        <seg>But the mother, suspecting that her daughter was, in her childish way, shirking the tedium and labor of learning, brought in the confessor to rebuke the young woman before her for her neglect.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed medicus vi crescentis morbi perterritus mandarat pridie, ne infans ab ægra lac sugeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055222Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055222Z">
        <seg>But the physician, terrified by the increasing severity of the disease, had ordered the day before that the infant should not suckle from the sick woman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed membrana quidem ab essentia, tunica vero ab usu nuncupatur) totum ventrem infernum continens: huic enim toti &lt;s2&gt;περιτείνεται&lt;/s2&gt;, hoc est, circumtenditur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033700Z">
        <seg>but the membrane is indeed called the essence, the tunic, however, from its use) containing the entire lower belly: for it &lt;s2&gt;περιτείνεται&lt;/s2&gt;the whole, that is, it surrounds it;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed morbum comitialem, cui obnoxius erat, silentio presserat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012420Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012420Z">
        <seg>but he had kept silent about the epilepsy from which he suffered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed mox fatebatur, se actum agere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045803Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045803Z">
        <seg>But he soon admitted that he was putting on an act.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ne jubilæi lucrum amitteret, a Rosæ (quæ pridie sepulta fuerat) sarcophago, recta ad ædem metropolitanam sibi eundum fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034531Z">
        <seg>But so as not to lose the benefit of the jubilee, he had to go straight from the sarcophagus of Rosa (who had been buried the day before) to the cathedral.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed nec illa, nec istæ apparebant sub lineamento, quo solita analogia repræsentarent Creatorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045531Z">
        <seg>But neither those nor these appeared under the outline by which the usual analogy represented the Creator.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed neque illarum nec horum ulli afficiebatur pertinax femina, adeo ut neque dignaretur alloquio: quinimo, ubi testamentum suum condidit, perquam studiose cavit, ne quid ulli prædictorum (tametsi posteriores octo notorie indigerent) de suis facultatibus legaret, sed ne meminisset quidem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032804Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032804Z">
        <seg>But the stubborn woman was not swayed by any of them, so much so that she would not even deign to speak to them. Indeed, when she made her will, she took great care not to leave anything to any of the aforementioned (even though the latter eight were notoriously in need), but did not even mention them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed neque illis displicere merito queat meæ devotionis simplicitas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054921Z">
        <seg>But neither can they justly take offense at the simplicity of my devotion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed neutiquam Rosa, quæ die postero ab oratorio veniens, quæstorem jamjam domo exiturum, ac proregi sese stiturum, vultu alacri affata est: “Vade, (inquit) nec formides, lætior ad nos redibis e palatio, quam hinc exieris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012752Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012752Z">
        <seg>but by no means Rosa, who, coming from the oratory the next day, addressed the treasurer, who was just about to leave the house and was about to set out for the pro-king, with a cheerful face: "Go," she said, "and do not be afraid; you will return to us from the palace in a happier mood than you left us here."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed nihilo segnius itum in vota, &amp; dum ad aram sacerdos pio Numini commendabat defunctam, voce non impari sese populus commendabat Rosæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031243Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031243Z">
        <seg>But the vows were no less fervently made, and while the priest commended the deceased to the pious Deity at the altar, the people, in no less fitting a voice, commended themselves to Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed noluit Deus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041702Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041702Z">
        <seg>but God did not will it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed non tardavit festinus ad adjuvandum Dominus, quin cor illud contritum &amp; humiliatum opportune recrearet, blando affatu sic eam alloquens: “Filia, ego neminem condemno, nisi volentem condemnari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061810Z">
        <seg>But the Lord did not delay to hasten to help; rather, He opportunely comforted that contrite and humbled heart, speaking to her with gentle words: "Daughter, I condemn no one, unless one is willing to be condemned;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed numquam in talem incidit interpretem, qui melius ipsa intimam favi hujus dulcedinem aut experimento nosset, aut aptis indiciis explicaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061736Z">
        <seg>but he never came across an interpreter who could better know the innermost sweetness of this honey by experience, or explain it with fitting indications.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ob multitudinem testium (centum octoginta tres fuere) variosque morarum obices, usque in Maium anni MDCXXXII duravit perquisitio: cui de more ad complementum supererat visitatio tumbæ &amp; reliquiarum Famulæ Dei.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031642Z">
        <seg>But because of the multitude of witnesses (there were one hundred eighty-three) and various obstacles delaying the process, the inquiry lasted until May 1632; to which, as was customary, was added a visitation of the tomb and relics of the Servant of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ob reverentiam matris illic præsentis ad singula non descendit, contenta dixisse generatim, se a domesticis, ob vitæ singularitatem, varias molestias, insultus, vexationes tolerasse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050355Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050355Z">
        <seg>But out of respect for her mother, who was present, she did not go into specifics, content to say in general that she had endured various annoyances, insults, and vexations from her household staff because of the singularity of her life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed obstabat superstes maritus, &amp; numerosa familia, quos omnes paulo post Deus ab hac luce evocavit, necnon innumera &amp; potentissima alia fundationis post obitum virginis impedimenta sustulit, durosque animos olim aversos ad favorem pii operis flexit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042620Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042620Z">
        <seg>But the surviving husband and a numerous family stood in the way, whom God shortly after called from this light, and He also removed countless and very powerful other obstacles to the foundation after the virgin's death, and bent the hard hearts that had once been opposed to favoring the pious work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed occurrit eunti visibilis Christus, blandeque affatus Virginem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051223Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051223Z">
        <seg>but Christ appeared to the one who was going, and gently spoke to the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed opportune tumulum circumsederant cœnobii Patres dignitate ac senio graviores, tum ne thesaurus ille diriperetur, tum ut eidem decenti ordine admoverent debiles &amp; mancos, qui spe haud inani obtinendæ curationis accedebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030654Z">
        <seg>But the monks of the monastery, more distinguished by dignity and age, had opportunely surrounded the tomb, both to prevent that treasure from being plundered and to admit in an orderly fashion the weak and the lame who approached with no vain hope of receiving a cure.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed pampini, quia amarissimi: his decoctis vescebatur libentius, quod &amp; succi amaritudine, &amp; floris sui cognatione, aptius Parasceven, quam prandium repræsentarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041259Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041259Z">
        <seg>but the shoots, because they were most bitter: boiled, they were eaten more willingly, since both the bitterness of their juice and their kinship to their flower made them a more fitting representation of the Passion than of a mere meal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed passim avulsione opus fuit, quam cutis &amp; cruor sequebantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041743Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041743Z">
        <seg>but everywhere avulsion was necessary, which was followed by skin and blood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed paulatim resolvebatur, quia gloriosissimum martyrii genus ei erat lente &amp; articulatim mori.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042744Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042744Z">
        <seg>but it was gradually resolved, because the most glorious kind of martyrdom for him was to die slowly and in stages.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed pene in singulis hærebat verbis, causata vocabulorum inopiam, nec aliter de iis fari noverat, quam quod ineffabiles dictitaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055855Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055855Z">
        <seg>But he was at a loss for words in almost every sentence, caused by a scarcity of words, and he knew no other way to speak of them than to call them ineffable.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed perdifficilis ingestio os parvulum diductione cruciabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052435Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052435Z">
        <seg>But the extremely difficult feeding tormented the little one's mouth with dislocation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed prius infirmo capiti particulam ex Rosæ vestimentis imposuerat, subque hujus galeæ fiducia iter ingressa, postquam in summa æde nonnihil consederat, advertit, omni se capitis dolore penitus liberatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034537Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034537Z">
        <seg>But first she had placed a piece of Rose's clothing on her ailing head, and having entered the path under the protection of this helmet, after she had sat for a while in the main hall, she realized that she was completely free of all headache.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed properandum ad solidiora.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053257Z">
        <seg>But we must hurry to more solid matters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed re diligentius explorata, compertum fuit, nonnisi meros splendores esse, at insolitos, festivos, benignos, quibus Virgini ipsa Regina virginum publice applaudebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030648Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030648Z">
        <seg>But upon closer examination, it was discovered that they were nothing but mere splendors, though unusual, festive, and benign, to which the Virgin herself, Queen of Virgins, publicly applauded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed reipsa urebant, torrebant, assabant utramque manum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035350Z">
        <seg>But in reality, they burned, scorched, and roasted both hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed repente exanguis pallor in vividum colorem sub oculis spectantium transiit, omniumque animos hilaritatis suæ blando fulgore accendit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040304Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040304Z">
        <seg>But suddenly, the deathly pallor on his face gave way to a vivid color beneath the eyes of the onlookers, and he lit up everyone's spirits with his charming radiance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed sera nox erat, qua nec medicum advocare, nec domesticorum ullum ad opem importune lubebat accersere: agnoscebat electuario vel sorbitiuncula se indigere, qua utcumque stomacho succurreret, ac vires instauraret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051059Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051059Z">
        <seg>But it was late at night, when he could neither call for a doctor nor was he inclined to importunately summon any of his household for help: he knew he needed an electuary or a little drink, by which he could somehow soothe his stomach and restore his strength.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed simul difficillima taliter amanti, cui felicius videtur non esse, quam non amare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050730Z">
        <seg>but at the same time it is exceedingly difficult for one who loves so deeply, for whom it seems happier not to exist than not to love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed stitit præcipitem allata ex monasterio Rosæ effigies, quæ, ut juxta Aspitiam aspicienda in lectulo collocata fuit, eodem adnutu prostravit febres, erexit infirmam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040816Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040816Z">
        <seg>But the image of the Rose, brought from the monastery in haste, checked the raging fever; and when it was placed on the bed for Aspisia to gaze upon, it at that very glance laid low the fever and raised the sick woman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed tamen sexum inermem, qui cum Rosa in templo substiterat, sic perculit, ut propemodum exanimaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060205Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060205Z">
        <seg>But nevertheless, he struck the unarmed sex, who had remained in the temple with Rosa, so that he nearly killed her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed transeuntem juxta pascua vaccarum, circa locum Verastegui, improviso erumpens taurus, ferociter insecutus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033831Z">
        <seg>But as he was passing by the cattle pastures near the place of Verastegui, a bull suddenly burst forth and fiercely pursued him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed tunc festinus &amp; amans venerat, tamquam Virginem illic sponsam sibi quæsiturus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044145Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044145Z">
        <seg>but then he had come, hastening and in love, as though to seek the Virgin there as his bride.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ubi modicam ex Rosæ habitu laciniam affectæ parti applicuit, illomet instanti distillationem stitit, dolorem fugavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034550Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034550Z">
        <seg>But when she applied a small, rose-infused strip of cloth to the affected part, it immediately stopped the bleeding and relieved the pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ut extensum, frigidum, rigidum invenit, velut suæ jacturæ sat certa, sorori dixit: “Jam &amp; hunc mihi servum tam utilem &amp; necessarium Deus abstulit, sit nomen ejus benedictum”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033207Z">
        <seg>But when he found it extended, cold, and rigid, as if its loss were certain, he said to his sister, "Now God has also taken away from me this servant, who was so useful and necessary to me; blessed be his name."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ut vidit evadere se non posse, ingenue candideque fassa est (quantum per congruorum vocabulorum penuriam potuit) magnalia Dei, quæ sibi post tristissimam ærumnam obtingebant, ut in momento se videbat repositam in unione, qua exciderat, aut se excidisse putarat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045856Z">
        <seg>But when she saw that she could not escape, she ingenuously and candidly confessed (as best she could, for lack of fitting words) the great works of God that had befallen her after her most wretched suffering, seeing herself in an instant restored to the union from which she had fallen, or had thought she had fallen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed vivis, &amp; altissimis experimentis exacte didicerat, suisque principiis, regulis, &amp; capitibus methodice digestam, egregie callebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050055Z">
        <seg>but he was alive, and had learned it exactly through the most profound experiments, and was an expert in it, having digested it methodically according to his own principles, rules, and headings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed, una cum reluctante summa verecundia obstabat, quod determinate, quid in suis dictis corrigere teneretur, nesciebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032241Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032241Z">
        <seg>but, with the utmost reluctance and modesty, he was at a loss, not knowing specifically what he was required to correct in his own words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sentiebat afflicta totum a catena malum esse, velut quæ per extritam pellem hinc inde carni nervisque profunda incumbebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041711Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041711Z">
        <seg>She felt that the entire chain was afflicting her, like one that, through worn leather, pressed deeply here and there into the flesh and nerves.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sentiebat tam inusitatis, ac plane divinis ardoribus paulatim se imparem fieri;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032708Z">
        <seg>he felt that he was gradually becoming unfit for such unusual, and indeed divine, ardors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sentio avidos liberrimi amoris impetus, ut cum rapidi amnes disruptis obicibus, non fluendo, sed tuendo, sese evolvunt per cataractam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050513Z">
        <seg>I feel the ravenous impulses of the freest love, as when swift rivers, their banks broken, not by flowing but by holding back, rush through the cataract.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sentio tibias meas carere impedimento;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033446Z">
        <seg>I feel my shins are unencumbered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>septem vero mensium ex Ordinis sui præscripto jejunium a festo sanctæ Crucis exaltatæ usque ad Pascha sic observabat, ut nonnisi ad vesperam exigua panis vilioris buccella, haustuque frigidæ aquæ sedaret famem &amp; sitim.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041132Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041132Z">
        <seg>For seven months, however, he observed the fast prescribed by his Order from the feast of the Exaltation of the Holy Cross until Easter, so that he would not eat anything until evening, and then would relieve his hunger and thirst with only a small morsel of cheap bread and a drink of cold water.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>serpebant dulciter per omnes animæ suæ venas ignotæ flammulæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062942Z">
        <seg>Unknown little flames were sweetly creeping through all the veins of her soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sese nihilominus esse paratissimam suere ac dissuere, quæcumque juberet mater.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035548Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035548Z">
        <seg>She nevertheless declared herself most ready to sew or to undo whatever her mother commanded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>si qua transeuntem observasset, gestiebat animo illic os oculosque affricare, ubi Rosa vestigium fixerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050715Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050715Z">
        <seg>If she had observed a passerby, she would have been eager to press her mouth and eyes there, where Rosa had left her trace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>si quoquo modo sibi licuisset prædicatoris fungi officio, utique se opertam horrido cilicio, nudipedem, squallidam interdiu noctuque per omnes Limensium vicos, &amp; compita circumlaturam crucifixi Redemptoris imaginem, &amp; ubique per fora, per trivia, per angiportus identidem clamore lugubri vociferaturam: “Resipiscite, o resipiscite, peccatores;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060524Z">
        <seg>if it had in any way been permitted for her to fulfill the office of a preacher, she would surely have wrapped herself in a horrid hair-shirt, barefoot, filthy, by day and by night through all the streets of Limensium, carrying the image of the crucified Redeemer on a crossroads, and everywhere in the marketplaces, at the crossroads, and in the alleys she would repeatedly cry out with a lugubrious cry: "Repent, O repent, sinners;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>si residuæ vitæ meæ brevitas id permitteret, quam excelsa, quam pretiosa, jucunda, eximia tibi de Dei suavitate, de læta ipsius curia, de regione æternitatis narranda haberem!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030127Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030127Z">
        <seg>If the brevity of my remaining life would permit, what lofty, precious, delightful, and extraordinary things I would have to tell you about the sweetness of God, about His joyful court, about the region of eternity!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>si sternutatione exonerabatur miserum caput, aut alia fortius excreandi importuna necessitas vultum orisque habitum violentius commovebat?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042136Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042136Z">
        <seg>Was his wretched head relieved by a sneeze, or was some other, more urgent need to cough violently distorting his face and the expression of his mouth?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sibi denique alios superne impulsus non obtigisse hactenus, præter eos, quos habitu protestabatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040320Z">
        <seg>that he had not, in fact, received any other divine impulses so far, apart from those he professed by his conduct;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sibique videbatur plumbeo magis, quam cannabaceo stromate subuculata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041910Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041910Z">
        <seg>And she seemed to herself to be swathed in a leaden rather than a hemp mattress.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sic Rosam vel a prima infantia docuit solitarios angulos domi quærere, mundique commercia devitare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043218Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043218Z">
        <seg>Thus, from early infancy, she taught Rosa to seek solitary corners at home and to avoid worldly commerce.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sic tamen ut neutri, ad quid ea hora vocarentur, &lt;s2&gt;[severe examinarunt.]&lt;/s2&gt; indicaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053824Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053824Z">
        <seg>Yet so that neither of them would indicate what they were called for at that hour.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sic tamen ut neutri, ad quid ea hora vocarentur, [severe examinarunt.] indicaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011004Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011004Z">
        <seg>Yet they did not strictly examine either of them as to what they were called for at that hour.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sic visione ruminanda intentissime occupatus, vigili impatientia diem expectabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032129Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032129Z">
        <seg>Thus, deeply occupied with ruminating on the vision, he awaited the day with impatient vigilance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque &amp; iste fluctus, ne fluctuaret obedientia, sponte detumuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035437Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035437Z">
        <seg>And so this wave, so that obedience would not waver, voluntarily subsided.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque Virgo, cum sua saturrima inedia usque in vesperam, subinde in sequentem usque diem perseverabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055957Z">
        <seg>Thus the Virgin, with her most complete fast, persevered until evening, and often until the following day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque consummavit opus bis mille foliorum, quæ amodo pauca sibi videbantur præ admirabili scribendi facilitate.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032352Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032352Z">
        <seg>Thus he completed the work of two thousand pages, which now seemed to him few in comparison with the admirable ease of writing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque corallinum illud sponsionis vinculum ab exsoluta Matre in se transtulerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043602Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043602Z">
        <seg>Thus that coral pledge-bond had been transferred to it from the discharged Mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque fidens divinæ providentiæ, reperit, quod in singulos menses clam Joannæ adferret in exactam solutionem hospitii, pacta dumtaxat silentii fidem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061221Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061221Z">
        <seg>Thus, trusting in divine providence, he discovered that each month he had been secretly bringing to Joanna the exact payment for her lodging, having only agreed to keep faith with her silence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque munitus demum salutaribus Sacramentis, conceptaque ingenti salutis fiducia, tranquillum, placidum, serenum spiritum reddidit Creatori, pactus ante cum Petro, quod tam ipsum, quam Rosam a morte inviseret, si eam sibi facultatem Dominus indulgeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060849Z">
        <seg>Thus fortified at last with the salutary Sacraments, and filled with great confidence of salvation, he returned his spirit to the Creator, tranquil, placid, and serene, having previously made a pact with Peter that he would visit both himself and Rosa in death, if the Lord would grant him that opportunity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque videbatur purgatorii magis, quam inferni, ærumnam sustinere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045620Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045620Z">
        <seg>Thus it seemed to endure the torments of purgatory rather than of hell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque, vocato somno, exanguia membra deinceps composuit ad soporem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042914Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042914Z">
        <seg>And so, sleep calling, he laid his pale limbs down to rest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>simili ardore transversim a sinistro latere per meditullium cordis ignitus pugio me pervadit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013418Z">
        <seg>A dagger, kindled with a similar ardor, pierces me transversely from the left side through the center of my heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>simillimo torre sese transversim penetrari a latere ad latus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013521Z">
        <seg>to be penetrated transversely by a very similar tower from side to side;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>simulque ad aliquam hujus sacram imaginem (quæcumque illa esset toti regioni celebrior, ac de fama beneficiorum illustrior) publici refugii perennem tesseram communi voto collectam firmissime stabiliri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054252Z">
        <seg>and at the same time to establish most firmly a perpetual public refuge, collected by a common vow from some sacred image of it (whichever was most celebrated throughout the region and most renowned for the fame of its benefits).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>singula exhausti sui corpusculi membra proprium habitura equuleum, ut nuluum alteri aut succurrere possit, aut cruciatum naturali consensu partiri;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013232Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013232Z">
        <seg>Each exhausted member of his body would have its own little horse, so that it could neither help another nor share in its suffering by natural sympathy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>singularem, obstipam, ac sui ipsius carnificem proclamitans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041154Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041154Z">
        <seg>proclaiming a singular, speechless, and self-wounding one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>singulas juncturas singuli obsident cruciatus, quibus nec nomen, neque tormentum, cui eos comparem, invenio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013431Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013431Z">
        <seg>Each joint is beset by its own torments, for which I can find neither a name nor a torment to compare them to.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>siquidem illo mane, solemni totius urbis jubilo, in æde metropolitana publicabantur litteræ apostolicæ, quibus undique fideles ad testificandum de notis Rosæ prodigiis compellebantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040249Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040249Z">
        <seg>For on that morning, with solemn jubilation throughout the entire city, apostolic letters were being published in the metropolitan church, by which the faithful from everywhere were summoned to testify to the well-known miracles of the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>siquidem instabat propinquus dies, quo illi habendus erat solemnis ad populum sermo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053449Z">
        <seg>For the day was at hand on which he was to deliver his formal address to the people.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>siquidem intractabile viderant brachium, &amp; in omnem medelam contumax, ut non vererentur tamquam de incurabili ambigue mussitare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054032Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054032Z">
        <seg>For they had seen the arm as intractable and stubborn against all treatment, so that they did not hesitate to murmur ambiguously about an incurable case.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>siquidem ipsamet adjuvit Rosam instrumenta suæ mortificationis humiliter occultantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040856Z">
        <seg>For she herself helped Rosa to humbly conceal the instruments of her mortification.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>siquidem plus quam duodecies jacto reti nullum prendidit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033908Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033908Z">
        <seg>For indeed, having cast the net more than twelve times, he caught nothing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>siquidem ut illas, tamquam onerosas ac superfluas, honesta sæpius excusatione rogabat impediri, ita hæc magis magisque gaudebat cumulari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030059Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030059Z">
        <seg>For just as she would often, with a polite excuse, ask to have those things, as if they were burdensome and superfluous, kept at bay, so she took ever greater pleasure in having this one heaped upon her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>siquidem, Augustino teste, quod corpori est anima, hoc amanti animæ Deus, illique minor est pœna ab animando, quam ab amando desistere aut repelli.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045645Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045645Z">
        <seg>For, as Augustine testifies, as the soul is to the body, so is God to the soul that loves Him; and for that soul, it is a lesser punishment to cease to live than to cease to love or be repelled from love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sitim eamdem, quæ in cruce exanguem torsit Redemptorem, arida Rosæ viscera faucesque occupaturam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013235Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013235Z">
        <seg>the same thirst that wrung the Redeemer, bleeding on the cross, would seize the dry viscera and throat of the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sive ad Isabellæ nomen respondisset vocantibus, irata mater expediebat virgidemiam, cædebat immeritam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052454Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052454Z">
        <seg>Whether she answered to the name of Isabella when called, the angry mother would unleash her virginity, slaughtering the undeserving.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sive ad lentam curam migraret tantisper in civitatem, deerant peregrinæ &amp; inopi sumptus, quibus semestre hospitium conduceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061159Z">
        <seg>Whether he moved to the city for slow treatment, the funds were lacking for a six-month stay.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sive erecta stabat, capiti vertigo ponderosissima obtinniebat languidam somnolentiæ cantilenam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043038Z">
        <seg>Whether standing erect, a most ponderous dizziness held sway over her head, a languid lullaby of drowsiness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sive pansis brachiis procubuisset in terram, adgliscebat soporiferi corporis suave blandimentum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043041Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043041Z">
        <seg>Whether he had fallen to the ground with arms outstretched, he clung to the sweet caress of the soporific body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sola Rosa sentiebat (sed obscurius adhuc) clamantem eminus Sponsum: Præbe mibi cor tuum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044126Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044126Z">
        <seg>Only Rosa felt (but even more obscurely) the Spouse crying out from afar: Give me your heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sola Rosa, sui jam in paradiso secura, curam ægroti totam in se transtulit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032052Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032052Z">
        <seg>Rosa alone, now safe in paradise, took on the entire care of the sick man.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sola pectoris anhela palpitatio, &amp; languidæ vocis querula raucedo Virgini scintillam vitæ inesse arguebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013322Z">
        <seg>The solitary, panting flutter of her breast and the languid, querulous rasp of her voice argued that a spark of life remained in the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>soli tibi ac genitrici meæ hanc curam pro amore Dei assumito;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013217Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013217Z">
        <seg>For the love of God, only you and my mother, take on this care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>solum exspectabatur lucidum mentis intervallum, ut extremis communiretur morientium sacramentis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035440Z">
        <seg>Only a lucid interval of mind was awaited, so that he might receive the last sacraments of the dying.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>solum in beneficii memoriam conspicua remanserat cicatrix, quæ tamen etiam firmabat reparati peritonæi integritatem, ne amplius rumpi posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033552Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033552Z">
        <seg>Only a conspicuous scar remained as a reminder of the favor, which, however, also confirmed the integrity of the repaired peritoneum so that it could not be torn again.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>solæ plantæ ab his doloribus vacabant, quas tamen aut saxorum collisione, aut fornacis adustione a cruciatibus immunes esse non sinebat: &amp; quod fere singulare est in divinarum consolationum affluentia, quibus solet sanctitas etiam in terris abundare, &amp; piorum caro in Deum vivum jucundatur &amp; exultat, ab eis corpus suum alienum, &amp; jejunum esse cogebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041535Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041535Z">
        <seg>Only the soles of his feet were free from these pains, yet he would not allow them to be immune from suffering, either through the collision of rocks or the scorching of a furnace. And what is almost unique in the abundance of divine consolations—by which holiness is wont to abound even on earth, and the flesh of the pious rejoices and exults in the living God—he forced his body to be alien to them and to remain fasting.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sonoreque ac libere in prætereuntem jocati, “En (inquiunt) monialem opipare habitam!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040757Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040757Z">
        <seg>and loudly and freely, joking to a passerby, "Behold (they say) a nun richly dressed!"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sonuere in turribus æra campana, micuere luminaria, increbuere voces &amp; laudum acroamata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031258Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031258Z">
        <seg>The bells rang in the towers, the lights twinkled, and voices and hymns of praise resounded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>soporis &amp; quietis spes aberat, laxis passibus ibatur ad mortem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035514Z">
        <seg>Hope for sleep and quiet was gone; with slack steps, one walked toward death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sponsas esse ex nuptiali veste, &amp; fulgore deprehendit, suarum lachrymarum stillicidiis saxa ad incisionem emollientes, &amp; una cum illis consimili veste circumamicta novit, se ad tanti sponsalitii gradum per ardua præparari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041504Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041504Z">
        <seg>She perceived that the brides were clad in nuptial garments and gleaming with radiance, their tears softening the stones for carving, and she knew that, together with them, wrapped in similar attire, she was being prepared for the arduous rite of such a marriage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>statim Virgo præ ingentis fiduciæ alacritate se ipsa robustior, animose respondit: “Ego vero Sponsum habeo, qui prægrandia &amp; rara dare potest, neque pusilla aut mediocria dumtaxat per diffidentiam ab illo speranda sunt”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061841Z">
        <seg>Immediately the Virgin, animated by great confidence, responded boldly: "I, however, have a Spouse who can give things that are exceedingly great and rare, and not merely small or mediocre things should be expected from him through lack of faith."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>statimque suspicata id quod erat, Filiam interrogavit, cur eo loci id abscondisset, aut cui bono?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041222Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041222Z">
        <seg>Immediately suspecting what it was, she asked her daughter why she had hidden it there, or to what purpose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>statimque, velut substratis prunis insideret, tota fervidissime æstuavit, ac subito infra unicæ Salutationis angelicæ moram, enixa est calculum magnitudine nucis avellanæ, punctim undique cuspidatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034153Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034153Z">
        <seg>And immediately, as if seated on hot embers, she was seized with a burning heat throughout, and suddenly, in less time than it takes to say the Angelic Salutation, she gave birth to a calculus the size of a hazelnut, pointed on every side.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>stude cautius ambulare in semitis mandatorum Dei, ne pereas: en, quidquid coneris tegere, non latuit Sponsum meum intentio tua”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060700Z">
        <seg>Strive to walk more carefully in the paths of God's commandments, lest you perish: behold, whatever you try to cover up, your intention has not escaped my Spouse."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>suadebat enim postea quæstor Gundisalvus vir optimus, qui apud virginem Rosam sibi concreditam authoritate, &amp; domestico imperio plurimum valebat, reformatam excalceatarum virginum sacri Carmeli regulam in clauso cœnobio potius subire, quam inter postremas sancti Dominici tertiarias extra septa monasterii militare, se non tantum protectione, sed &amp; opera &amp; opibus adjumento futurum, pollicebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041331Z">
        <seg>For Gundisalvus, the treasurer, a most excellent man, who by virtue of his authority and domestic influence held great sway over the virgin Rose, to whom he had been entrusted, later advised that the reformed rule of the barefoot virgins of the holy Carmel be adopted in a cloistered convent, than to live outside the monastery walls as one of the last of Saint Dominic's tertiaries, he promised that he would be a help not only with protection, but also with labor and resources.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sub aëre tam humido, calido, &amp; ex concurrentium refluxibus pulverulento, nulla Rosæ vultui insederat fuligo, nulla genas tinxerat flavedo vel horridus pallor;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031118Z">
        <seg>Under air so humid, hot, and dusty from the reek of the competitors, no soot had settled on Rose's face, no sallowness or ghastly pallor had stained her cheeks.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sub gigantea mole alias hanc inexpugnabilem feminam, tremente cellula, corripuit, adeoque contorsit, ut ejus ossa comminuta, &amp; nervi distracti viderentur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042006Z">
        <seg>Under a gigantic mass, he seized this otherwise impregnable woman, her cell trembling, and twisted her so violently that her bones seemed crushed and her nerves torn apart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sub hoc Rosa effertur Gundisalvi domo ad tumulum, ut quæ viva seraphicam magistram ad vivum expresserat gestamine senticoso, eamdem &amp; mortua repræsentaret evidentius sub communi utrique corona.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042421Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042421Z">
        <seg>Under this rose, the body of Gundisalvi is carried from the house to the tomb, so that she who, while alive, had vividly portrayed the seraphic teacher in her thorny bearing might, even in death, represent her more clearly under the common crown.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>subitisque &amp; præcocibus incrementis, excelsa contemplationis peritia eo maturuit ac pertigit, ut circa duodecimum ætatis annum deprehensa fuerit jam conscendisse illum orationis gradum, quem unitivum mystica theologia appellat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044808Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044808Z">
        <seg>Through sudden and precocious growths, her lofty expertise in contemplation matured and reached such a point that, by about the twelfth year of her age, it was perceived to have already ascended to that degree of prayer which mystical theology calls the unitive.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>subjungebat tamen: “Vide, num amari dignus fit ille admirabilis mundi artifex;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045208Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045208Z">
        <seg>He nevertheless added: "See whether that admirable artisan of the world becomes worthy of being loved."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sublimi apud Deum gratia polleat necesse est: utinam animæ suæ tam lucidæ ac flagrantis intimum statum nosse liceat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055826Z">
        <seg>He must be endowed with sublime grace before God; may he be permitted to know the innermost state of his soul, so bright and burning.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>succedant alia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054323Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054323Z">
        <seg>Let others succeed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sufficere tot eventuum tragica specimina in testimonium promptæ ultra vires obedientiæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041126Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041126Z">
        <seg>to suffice as so many tragic examples of events in testimony to ready obedience beyond one's powers;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sufficiat hoc postremum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040710Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040710Z">
        <seg>Let this last one suffice.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sui sociis in Americam trajecisse, ibique barbaris Euangelium prædicasse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041420Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041420Z">
        <seg>He and his companions crossed over to America, and there preached the Gospel to the barbarians.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>suo idiomate GASPACHOS&lt;a0&gt;e&lt;/a0&gt; appellitabat hoc genus embammatis, quia frigidum sumebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203330Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203330Z">
        <seg>In its own dialect, it was called GASPACHOS, because it was eaten cold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>suo idiomate GASPACHOS&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;e&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; appellitabat hoc genus embammatis, quia frigidum sumebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041237Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041237Z">
        <seg>In its own dialect, it was called GASPACHOS, because it was eaten cold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>supernos attactus præpotentes, dulces, delicatos;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044532Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044532Z">
        <seg>The most powerful, sweet, and delicate touches above;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>superstes erat maritus, in plurimos (uti videbatur) annos victurus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011946Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011946Z">
        <seg>A husband was left, who, it seemed, was to live for many years.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>surge: nam instat hora”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051338Z">
        <seg>Arise: for the hour is at hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sæpe cum gemitu dicebat familiari D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060310Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060310Z">
        <seg>He often said with a groan to his familiar, "Sir."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sæpe cum gemitu dicebat familiari D. Franciscæ Urtado de Bustamante: “Utinam idonea ratio modusve occurreret adornandi fugam ad barbarorum provincias, ut illic ab idololatris pro Jesu Christo immanissime trucidemur”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011438Z">
        <seg>He often said with a groan to the servant of Don Francisco Urtado de Bustamante: "If only a suitable plan or method could be found for preparing an escape to the provinces of the barbarians, so that there we might be most horribly slaughtered by the idolaters for the sake of Jesus Christ."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sæpissime permolesta ischias utrique coxendici graviter incumbebat, non segnius interim costas vellicante pleuritide&lt;a3&gt;f&lt;/a3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T010809Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T010809Z">
        <seg>Very often, a severely troublesome sciatica pressed heavily on both hips, not less so at the same time a pleurisy was twitching the ribs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sæpissime permolesta ischias utrique coxendici graviter incumbebat, non segnius interim costas vellicante pleuritide&lt;s9&gt;&lt;a10&gt;f&lt;/a10&gt;&lt;/s9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052612Z">
        <seg>Very often, a severely troublesome sciatica pressed heavily on both hips, not less so, meanwhile, was the pleurisy tearing at the ribs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sæpius &amp; Virginem Matrem, decoram, amabilem, dulciterque familiarem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050538Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050538Z">
        <seg>often also the Virgin Mother, beautiful, lovely, and sweetly familiar.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tacuit tamen, donec post Rosæ obitum Lucia, pro more sacramentali confessione animam expiatura, accederet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012011Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012011Z">
        <seg>She remained silent, however, until after Rose's death, when Lucia would approach to purify her soul through the sacramental confession.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tandem ausu furibundo, quam discerpere non poterat, raptabat per solum, trahebat, versabat, ceu lacerum aliquod peripsema;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051936Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051936Z">
        <seg>At last, with a furious boldness, it snatched what it could not tear apart, dragging it across the ground, pulling it, turning it, as if it were some tattered rag.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tandem innuebat ancillæ, sine strepitu pondus auferret, ne forte superveniens mater furtivæ devotionis turbaret scenam, aditumque similia iterandi præcluderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041631Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041631Z">
        <seg>She finally motioned to the maidservant to remove the weight without a sound, lest the mother, arriving unexpectedly, disturb the secret devotion and block any chance to repeat it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tanta erat verborum proportio, gravitas, ac mensura, sine parergo, sine circuitu, sine confusione ac superfluitate.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050643Z">
        <seg>There was such a proportion, gravity, and measure to the words, without digression, without circumlocution, without confusion and superfluity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tantum interrogavit, unde id nosset: Respondit ille, se Lima Potosium usque, vallante angelica custodia, feliciter aut calcasse, aut declinasse obvia quæque pericula;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051856Z">
        <seg>He only asked how he knew this. He replied that he had, with the protection of an angelic guard, either successfully braved or successfully avoided every danger all the way from Lima to Potosí.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tantumque abfuit, quod ulla hos terrores mitigarit assuetudo, ut pene adauxerit: nam ubi postridie Virgo relabebatur in idem chaos, denuo sub eadem luridæ æternitatis larva recurrebant horridi pavores, nec mœrenti suggerebat ligata memoria, quod inde pridie emersisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045611Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045611Z">
        <seg>So far was it from any habit softening these terrors that it almost increased them: for when the next day the Virgin relapsed into the same chaos, the hideous terrors would again rush in under the same lurid mask of eternity, and the bound memory offered the dying woman no comfort that she had emerged from it the day before.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>taxare Virginem tamquam aut chimeris delusam, aut affectui pertinaciter indulgentem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062753Z">
        <seg>To judge the Virgin as either a fool for chimeras or one who stubbornly indulges her affections.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>te quidem videre ante meum obitum desideravi, idque solum dixi, nil ultra”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053350Z">
        <seg>I did indeed desire to see you before my death, and I said only that, nothing more.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tempestive aderunt vires, ut possit, tametsi non sine conditione alteri onerosa”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053508Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053508Z">
        <seg>Help will arrive in time, so that he can, though not without a condition burdensome to another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tibi (inquam) tibi, amabilissime Jesu, qui tanto humani generis amore bullis &amp; æstuas”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053728Z">
        <seg>to you (I say) to you, most loving Jesus, who with so much love for the human race boils and burns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tibi omnium affectuum sudent balsama;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053725Z">
        <seg>All affections sweat balsam for you.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tibi sese enervet omne pectus piæ violentia charitatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053722Z">
        <seg>May every breast be enervated in you by the pious violence of charity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043649Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043649Z">
        <seg>Title</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>toto hunc corpore successive depulit, eodemque simul affrictu, tam febrium, quam anginæ suæ incendia restinxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034959Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034959Z">
        <seg>He successively rubbed this all over his body, and with the same friction simultaneously extinguished the fevers and his sore throat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>totum denique contabulationis systema&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; contegebant phrygii operis peripetasmata&lt;a1&gt;c&lt;/a1&gt;, &amp; stragula auro turgido late fimbriata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031509Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031509Z">
        <seg>Finally, the entire system of flooring was covered with Phrygian tapestries and with rugs lavishly fringed with gilded braid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>totum hoc, quidquid fuerat miraculi, eo dumtaxat spectare ac tendere, ut Salvator, quam enixe cupiat amari ab omnibus, ipsomet sudorifero vultu, æstuque præter ordinem luculento manifestaret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053914Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053914Z">
        <seg>all of this, whatever miracle it was, served only to show that the Savior, who so earnestly desires to be loved by all, was revealed by his very own sweat-stained face and unusually brilliant radiance;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>totum suum levamen fuisse, sentire Deum in anima sua præsentem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041755Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041755Z">
        <seg>that his entire comfort was to feel God present in his soul;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>totumque subito discussum est periculum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034240Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034240Z">
        <seg>And the entire danger was suddenly dispelled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>triumphabat, sed in cruce: nimirum prostabat in Virginis cubiculo par humanæ staturæ crux lignea, e cujus brachiis utrimque prominebant clavi fortes ac solidi, ut sufficerent corpori sustinendo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043048Z">
        <seg>He triumphed, but on the cross: for in the Virgin's chamber lay a wooden cross of human stature, from whose arms on either side protruded strong, solid nails, sufficient to support a body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tu Domina, tu palma exaltata in Cades, me ramo ditabis immarcescibili”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044309Z">
        <seg>O Lady, you exalted palm in Cadiz, you will bless me with an unfading branch.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tu de reliquis, ut magis volueris, disponito”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042514Z">
        <seg>You may arrange the rest as you wish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tu mihi sponsa esto”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044343Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044343Z">
        <seg>Be my bride.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tu vero ea præfidentia loqueris, acsi in tantæ fabricæ sumptuosa impendia tibi in promptu jam essent bis centies mille libræ argenti”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062328Z">
        <seg>But you speak with such confidence, as if you already had two hundred thousand pounds of silver on hand for the lavish expenses of such a grand undertaking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tu vero, nobilis Vincenti, ignosce, &amp; locum da veritati, quam tibi dictura sum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060632Z">
        <seg>But you, noble Vincent, forgive me, and give a hearing to the truth I am about to tell you.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tua res agitur: si quid dissimulaveris, si quid celaveris in examine, Dei beneficium negasti;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050458Z">
        <seg>It is your own affair: if you have concealed anything, if you have hidden anything in the examination, you have denied God's benefit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tua sum, tuam me profiteor, tua ero”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044353Z">
        <seg>I am yours, I profess myself yours, I will be yours.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tum reditum centies ad idem scriniolum, chartæ omnes sigillatim excussæ, protractæ, repositæ: aderant omnia præter apocham.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034007Z">
        <seg>Then, a hundred times back to the same little desk, all the papers were examined, stretched out, and put back one by one: everything was there except the receipt.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tumbæ vero gradatim circumstabant in viginti novem argenteis candelabris totidem bilibres candelæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031506Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the tombs were gradually surrounded by twenty-nine silver candelabras, each holding as many two-pound candles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>turbanda alias, &amp; turbatura, si rescivisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045729Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045729Z">
        <seg>to be stirred up otherwise, and a commotion, if he had found out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>turgebant venæ, rubebat, ardebat tumida &amp; dire inflammata tibia, ut nec stragulum, nec lodicis tenue operimentum valuerit sustinere: &amp; quia insimul cruenta dysenteria exhauriebat miserum, extrema sacramenta petiit, medico annuente: nam actum esse de vita uterque persuasum habebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040553Z">
        <seg>His veins swelled, he blushed, and his swollen, terribly inflamed shin burned so fiercely that not even a blanket or a thin sheet could bear it: and because at the same time a bloody dysentery was wasting the poor man, he asked for the last rites with the doctor's consent, for both were convinced that his life was at an end.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tutelam, suffragium pollicerentur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031304Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031304Z">
        <seg>They would promise protection and the vote.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tuum erit dimovere obstacula, quibus me tam ineluctabiliter nosti implicitam”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011937Z">
        <seg>It will be your task to remove the obstacles, in which you know me so ineluctably entangled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tuus?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050430Z">
        <seg>yours?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ubi frigidum est, inde calor expellitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025832Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025832Z">
        <seg>Where it is cold, heat is driven out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ubique festivis applausibus ac publicæ lætitiæ signis novæ cælorum Civi acclamatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031249Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031249Z">
        <seg>Everywhere, the new Citizen of the Heavens was acclaimed with festive applause and public signs of joy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde &amp; nomen habet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033703Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033703Z">
        <seg>From which it also gets its name.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde &amp; pro modico aquæ haustu, flexis poplitibus, a Gundisalvo (si forte aderat) libandi facultatem sibi exorabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040704Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040704Z">
        <seg>From which, for a small drink of water, he would, with knees bent, beg Gundisalvo (if he happened to be there) for the opportunity to taste.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde aliquamdiu hanc secum eodem in lectulo accubare jussit per noctem, ut arte artem deluderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042511Z">
        <seg>So he ordered her to lie with him in the same little bed for a while at night, so that by art he might defeat the art.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde ancilla Mariana in opem sibi advocanda fuit: hæc vero post Virginis obitum rem totam, ut gesta fuerat, manifestavit, quam Rosæ humilitas (ut alia majora &amp; plurima) de more occultasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040729Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040729Z">
        <seg>Hence a Marian servant had to be summoned to her aid; she, however, after the Virgin's death, revealed the entire matter, as it had transpired, which Rose's humility (like many other greater things) had, by custom, concealed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde confessarius solemni Matutinarum officio domi interfuturus, parabat discedere ad cœnobium, postridie rediturus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030214Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030214Z">
        <seg>Therefore the confessor, who was to preside at the solemn Matins service at home, was preparing to depart for the monastery, to return the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde confessione expiata, sacroque munita Viatico, &amp; uncta Oleo, solam exspectabat horam, qua per tot ulcera emitteret animam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035719Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035719Z">
        <seg>Having confessed and been absolved, fortified with the sacred Viaticum and anointed with the Oil, she awaited only the hour when she would breathe her soul through so many wounds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde cuidam matronæ post annos complures familiariter interroganti, quomodo lectum adeo horribilem, [ac] arduum potuerit vel nocte unica tolerare, respondit cum lepore (uti solebat) &amp; gratia, talem se ex eo accubitu dolorem capere, quantus utique sufficere posset in satisfactionem pro quavis amicissima persona Deo offerendam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042645Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042645Z">
        <seg>When a certain matron, after several years, familiarly asked how she could have endured such a horrible and arduous bed even for a single night, she replied with her usual wit and grace that she derived from that couch a pain sufficient, in itself, to atone for any very dear friend offered to God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde cælitus admonita Ordinem Prædicatorum esse in sancta Ecclesia paradisum voluptatis divinæ, in quo ipsa velut rosa cælestis ceteris ibi liliorum, &amp; rosarum vernantibus plantis socianda, &amp; inserenda erat, quod postea ex intimis præcordiis optans petebat, &amp; voti compos facta, ante prædictam sibi summe gratissimam sanctissimi Rosarii aram, Alphonso Velasquez ejusdem Ordinis tunc confessionibus ipsius virginis dante, anno Domini sexto supra millesimum &amp; sexcentesimum in clara sancti Laurentii celebritate habitum suscepit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041324Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041324Z">
        <seg>whereby, divinely admonished, she understood that the Order of Preachers was in the holy Church a paradise of divine delight, into which she herself, like a heavenly rose, was to be joined and inserted among the other there budding lilies and roses, which she afterward, with the deepest longing of her heart, desired and sought, and, her vow accomplished, Before the aforementioned altar of the most holy Rosary, most gracious to her, she received the habit of the same Order from Alphonso Velasquez, then confessor to the virgin, in the year of our Lord one thousand six hundred and six, during the celebrated feast of Saint Lawrence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde digressis aliis, secreto extorsit a Virgine, cur interpellasset, utique mysterium subesse, quod rescire sua intererat, omnino se scire id velle.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054534Z">
        <seg>After the others had gone off, he secretly forced the Virgin to tell him why she had interrupted him, insisting that there must be a mystery behind it which he was determined to uncover.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde ex tunc constituit parens infantem placidissimam deinceps, cum exeundum foret, secum non exportare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034823Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034823Z">
        <seg>Henceforth, the parent resolved not to take the very placid infant with them when they had to go out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde factum est, quod sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis monasterium Limæ, ex eventu, apud consciam plebem sæpissime Rosæ monasterium (hoc est diu ante vaticinio Rosæ prænuntiatum) promiscue amet appellari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012045Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012045Z">
        <seg>Hence it happened that the monastery of St. Catherine of Siena in Lima, by chance, was most often called the Monastery of the Rose by the aware populace (this having been foretold long before by the prophecy of the Rose).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde humillima Rosæ modestia, grave, serium, perque intervalla repetitum examen suæ vocationis ac spiritus, recusare non præsumpsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050014Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050014Z">
        <seg>Hence, the most humble Rose did not presume to refuse the grave, serious, and repeated examination of her calling and spirit, at intervals.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde illomet vespere post cœnam cogitabundus ac tristis, angorem sui animi solis conjugi Rosæque aperuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012746Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012746Z">
        <seg>So that evening after supper, pensive and sad, he revealed the anguish of his soul to his wife and to Rosa alone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde in horto umbrosas platanos muro proximas conspicata, locum elegit, ubi auxiliante Ferdinando fratre, oratoriolum ab omni domesticorum prospectu semotum efformaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043243Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043243Z">
        <seg>Spotting some shady plane trees in the garden close to the wall, she chose a spot where, with the help of her brother Ferdinand, she could build a small oratory, hidden from all view of the household.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde initio grossioris panni incommodum volumen in cervical adhibuit, deinde paulatim crudum laterem, denique petram polygonam&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt; &amp; undique scabram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T002113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T002113Z">
        <seg>Thus, at first he applied a coarse cloth to the neck, then gradually a raw side, and finally a polygonal stone, rough on all sides.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde initio grossioris panni incommodum volumen in cervical adhibuit, deinde paulatim crudum laterem, denique petram polygonam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; &amp; undique scabram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042725Z">
        <seg>Thus, at first he applied a bulky roll of coarser cloth to the neck, then gradually a raw side, and finally a polygonal stone, rough on all sides.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde jugiter apud confessarios lamentabatur, ingratam se esse tam suavi Numinis in se bonitati, nescire se modum apte colendi tam excelsam amicitiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053440Z">
        <seg>Hence she constantly lamented to the confessors that she was unworthy of the sweetness of the Divine goodness in her, and did not know how to properly cultivate such an exalted friendship.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde nonnullis etiam de infirmæ salute paulo melius sperare collibuit, quod eam crederent pauxillum dormivisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025723Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025723Z">
        <seg>Hence, some even dared to hope a little better for the sick woman's health, because they believed she had slept a little.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde nunc prece, nunc lachrymis ambiebat matrem obsecrando, ne secum se abduceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043317Z">
        <seg>So she now besought her mother with prayer and with tears, not to take her away with her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde per totam deinceps vitam securissima erat, se jugiter in protectione Dei cæli commorari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061720Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061720Z">
        <seg>Hence, for the rest of her life, she was most secure, always dwelling in the protection of God of heaven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde placando huic jurgio nec annorum lustrum satis fuit, donec tandem controversiæ finem imposuit Sacramentale Chrisma, quo sanctissimus Limensium archipræsul Turibius&lt;a0&gt;i&lt;/a0&gt; in parochia loci Quivi, dum Rosæ illiniebat confirmationis signaculum, omisso baptismali nomine, Rosam pronuntiare maluit, cujus fortasse jam tunc fragrantiam senserat in spiritu, quo plenus erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T212109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203314Z">
        <seg>so that to settle this dispute not even a five-year period was sufficient, until at last the Sacramental Chrism put an end to the controversy, when the most holy Archbishop of Lima, Turibius&lt;a0&gt;i&lt;/a0&gt;, in the parish of Quivi, while anointing Rosa with the sign of confirmation, omitted her baptismal name, He preferred to pronounce Rosa, whose fragrance he had perhaps already then perceived in the spirit with which he was filled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde placando huic jurgio nec annorum lustrum satis fuit, donec tandem controversiæ finem imposuit Sacramentale Chrisma, quo sanctissimus Limensium archipræsul Turibius&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;i&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; in parochia loci Quivi, dum Rosæ illiniebat confirmationis signaculum, omisso baptismali nomine, Rosam pronuntiare maluit, cujus fortasse jam tunc fragrantiam senserat in spiritu, quo plenus erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034711Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034711Z">
        <seg>so that to settle this dispute not even a five-year period was sufficient, until at last the Sacramental Chrism put an end to the controversy, when the most holy Archbishop of Lima, Turibius&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;i&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, in the parish of Quivi, while anointing Rosa with the sign of confirmation, omitted her baptismal name, He preferred to pronounce Rosa, whose fragrance he had perhaps already then perceived in the spirit with which he was filled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde postremis vitæ annis, quos in Gundisalvi quæstoris domo exegit, in humili sella collecto corpusculo, sedentaria noctes suas transegit, capite paulisper reclinato ad columnam lecticæ, in qua Gundisalvi minorennes filiolæ acquiescebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042920Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042920Z">
        <seg>Thus, in the last years of her life, which she spent in the house of the treasurer Gundisalvi, she passed her nights in a humble chair, her small frame gathered in, her head for a moment resting against the column of the bed in which Gundisalvi's minor daughters lay.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde prodigiis opus fuit, quibus a longe præpararetur ad mysterium, erigeretur ad consensum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044104Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044104Z">
        <seg>Hence, prodigies were necessary, by which he would be prepared from afar for the mystery and raised to consent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde pœnaliter arescenti Virgini hoc etiam cum moriente Sponso tolerandum superfuit clamare: &lt;b0&gt;Sitio&lt;/b0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042759Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042759Z">
        <seg>Thus, the Virgin, who was withering away as if in punishment, still had to endure, even with her dying Bridegroom, the cry: "I thirst."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde renes &amp; latera ferri algor in atrocissimi doloris consensum attraxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041714Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041714Z">
        <seg>From which the cold of iron had drawn the kidneys and flanks into the consensus of the most atrocious pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde replicuit: [quæ cupiebat, ut filius Societati nostræ nomen daret,] “Ah!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012606Z">
        <seg>whereupon she replied: [what she wanted, was for her son to join our Society,] "Ah!"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde rogabat Sponsum, dignaretur potius ipsam deducere per vias ordinarias suorum electorum, nec ultra permitteret sic amantem dehiscere ad ima profundi, ubi non est substantia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045639Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045639Z">
        <seg>Therefore she begged the Bridegroom to deign rather to lead her along the ordinary paths of his elect, and not to permit the one so in love to fall into the depths of the abyss, where there is no substance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde sic demum patuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041705Z">
        <seg>Thus it was finally revealed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde stupore attonitus ad P. Antonium de la Vega Loarsa ejusdem Societatis virum integerrimum rem detulit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012123Z">
        <seg>whereupon, stunned with amazement, he reported the matter to Father Anthony de la Vega Loarsa, a most upright man of the same Society;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde tam victam esse, quam vicisse, par gaudium fuit miranti æternam Sapientiam, quæ ludens in orbe terrarum, protestatur delicias suas esse cum filiis hominum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051037Z">
        <seg>For the one who marvels at the eternal Wisdom, it was as great a joy to have been conquered as to have conquered, for she, playing on the face of the earth, proclaims that her delight is with the children of men.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde æger, susceptis Ecclesiæ sacramentis, ad extremum vitæ fugientis agonem perductus jacuit sine motu, ceslavit arteriarum pulsus, &amp; consona trium medicorum voce jam in faucibus mortis hærere&lt;a1&gt;*&lt;/a1&gt; conclamabatur: sed implorato beatæ Rosæ potente auxilio, modicum pulveris e sepulchro ejusdem Beatæ hausit e cochleari aqua resolutum, &amp; illico salutem cum pristino vigore integerrime recuperavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043243Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043243Z">
        <seg>whereupon the patient, having received the sacraments of the Church, was brought to the final agony of his fleeting life and lay motionless, the pulses of his arteries ceased, and by the unanimous voice of three physicians it was proclaimed that he was already in the throes of death: but having implored the powerful help of the blessed Rose, he took a little powder from her tomb, dissolved in water with a spoon, and immediately recovered his health and former strength in the most perfect manner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unica hæc, &amp; vera est scala paradisi, nec præter crucem alia superest, qua in cælum ascendatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052826Z">
        <seg>This alone is the true ladder of paradise, and there is no other way to ascend to heaven except through the cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unico totoque illo spatio, ubi sacrum istud cadaver immissum fuit, gleba mutavit indolem, ac fere pumicea soliditate in se coacta obduruit, ut solum ibi digitis levique manu teri aut frangi nequeat, quin ferro opus sit, ut excavetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035344Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035344Z">
        <seg>In that entire space where that sacred corpse had been placed, the soil changed its nature and hardened into a nearly pumice-like solidity, so that it can only be ground or broken by fingers and a light hand, and must be excavated with iron.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut denique Matris suavissimæ prævalidis gemitibus cesserat exarmata ira Filii;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054447Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054447Z">
        <seg>so that at last the Son's anger was disarmed by the most sweet Mother's most powerful groans.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut dum in me excarnificanda occupabuntur, differant saltem injurias, quas eheu!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060236Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060236Z">
        <seg>so that while they are busy dismembering me, they may at least postpone the injuries, which alas!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut in horreum Domini copiosos Indorum manipulos comportent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060443Z">
        <seg>to bring into the Lord's barn the abundant sheaves of the Indians;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut iste jam placatior, benigno arrisu Matrem, deinde &amp; Rosam indulgentissime respexerit: exinde gratiam in tuto jam esse, ne dubitaret confessarius;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054450Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054450Z">
        <seg>so that this one, now more placated, would look upon the Mother with a kindly smile, and then upon Rosa with the utmost indulgence; from that point on, the confessor should have no doubt that grace was now safely secure.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut jam neque digitorum ullus moveri, multo minus forficem, aut quidvis aliud tenere posset, vel apprehendere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055346Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055346Z">
        <seg>so that he could no longer move any of his fingers, much less hold a pair of scissors or anything else, or grasp it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut millenas animarum chiliades ab æternæ damnationis voragine eripiant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060446Z">
        <seg>to rescue thousands upon thousands of souls from the vortex of eternal damnation;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut nemo dubitarit, magnum aliquid inter illas tenebras gestum esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052144Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052144Z">
        <seg>So that no one would doubt that something great had taken place amid those darknesses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut pateret evidentius, uno utramque spiritu excelluisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060006Z">
        <seg>so that it would be more evident that he had excelled in both by a single spirit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut putasses cæcam esse, quando vel maxime aquila erat, acie irretorta suum admirans solem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044901Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044901Z">
        <seg>You would have thought it blind, when it was at its most eagle-eyed, gazing in wonder at its own sun.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut repente sentiebat totam simul animam collucere &amp; inflammari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045859Z">
        <seg>He suddenly felt his entire soul shining and aflame all at once.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut tremuerat afflictum cor suum, percipiendo alternos Matris Filiique affatus, quibus illa gratiam, hic justitiam ac pœnas urgebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054444Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054444Z">
        <seg>as his afflicted heart trembled, perceiving the alternating words of the Mother and the Son, by which she urged grace, and he justice and punishments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut tristissima domum redierat post insolitam hanc repulsam: deinde, ut postridie multis demum lachrymis Dei Matrem flexerat ad intercessionem, negante Filio placari se posse illi cœtui tam obstinate discordi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054441Z">
        <seg>having returned home in the deepest sorrow after this unusual rebuff; then, the next day, having at last moved the Mother of God to intercede with many tears, since the Son refused to be appeased toward that assembly so stubbornly at odds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utere prout lubet, fac inde, quæ vis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061038Z">
        <seg>Use it as you please; do whatever you want with it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>uterque stirpe non ignobili, sed opibus permodicis, nisi quod ingentis opulentiæ loco fuerit tali filiola cælitus ditari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T214719Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211811Z">
        <seg>both were of noble birth, but of very modest means, unless it was their great fortune to have such a little daughter blessed from heaven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utique experta, quam sint Sponsi ubera meliora vino, quam fragrantia unguentis optimis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051124Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051124Z">
        <seg>Indeed, she has experienced how the bridegroom's breasts are better than wine, and more fragrant than the finest ointments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utique numquam id affirmatura, nisi experimento suo comprobasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044957Z">
        <seg>She would certainly never have affirmed it, unless she had proven it by her own experiment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utpote secura, Deum non permissurum, quod consonis horum calculis evocaretur ab humilioris instituti electa semita, quam Seraphica magistra de Senis felicissime præcalcarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040307Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040307Z">
        <seg>being secure, she would not permit God to be drawn by the calculations of these men from the chosen path of a humbler institution, which the Seraphic Mother of Siena had most happily trodden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utpote, qui ex viris sui instituti primus adiit montana, quibus hodie nomen &lt;b0&gt;Sancta&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Crux de la Sierra&lt;/b1&gt;&lt;a2&gt;a&lt;/a2&gt;, ubi plurimas dæmoni animas eripuit, ac demum Limæ anno MDCXXVI sancto fine quievit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012306Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012306Z">
        <seg>Indeed, he was the first of the men of his order to go to the mountains, which today are called &lt;b0&gt;Santa&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Crux de la Sierra&lt;/b1&gt;&lt;a2&gt;a&lt;/a2&gt;, where he delivered many souls from the devil, and finally rested in Lima in the year 1626 in holy death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utque cruciatum cruciatu simul pelleret &amp; vindicaret, pugnum crebrius impegit capiti, ubi densioribus aculeis spinea corona (de qua infra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040611Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040611Z">
        <seg>And so, to drive out and avenge the torment with torment, he struck his head more frequently with his fist, where the crown of thorns was denser with its spikes (of which, below, chapter).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utque focum accenderet, ardentem aliunde titionem fuerat mutuata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045019Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045019Z">
        <seg>And so that she could light the fire, she had borrowed a burning brand from elsewhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utque talis rosa nulli alteri plantæ adhæreret, quam ei, qui est flos campi, &amp; lilium convallium&lt;a1&gt;b&lt;/a1&gt;, invenit basilicum, seu ozymum, quem studiosius colebat reservatum ut plantam regiam ipsi Regi seculorum, siccum &amp; eradicatum, cui de hoc dolenti ipse Christus apparens dixit, se suis manibus illum eradicasse, &amp; projecisse, ut ipse solus esset ejus verus basilicus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T041929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T041929Z">
        <seg>that such a rose should adhere to no other plant than to him who is the flower of the field, and the lily of the valleys&lt;a1&gt;b&lt;/a1&gt;, he found basil, or ozyma, which he more diligently cultivated, reserved as a royal plant for the King of ages himself, dry and uprooted, to whom Christ himself, appearing to this grieving one, said that he himself had uprooted and cast it away with his own hands, so that he alone might be its true basilic.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utque terrores duplicaret, fecit eam experiri contactu, quam pilosis, hispidis, ericinis lacertis esset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052119Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052119Z">
        <seg>And to double the terrors, he made her feel by touch how hairy, rough, and bristly the lizards were.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utrimque velut in vivo ac animato libro se responsa distincte legere multo significantius, quam si mutis characteribus descripta vel picta decerneret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054357Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054357Z">
        <seg>to read the answers distinctly for himself in a living and animated book on both sides, which was far more meaningful than if he were to discern them from characters that were merely written or painted;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>valeo, sanus sum: quod a quatuor annis circiter numquam potui, nunc possum: meis insisto talis: plantas, quæ terram nosse desueverant, humi nunc figo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033409Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033409Z">
        <seg>I am well, I am healthy: what I have not been able to do for about four years, I can now do: I am like this to my plants: I now plant in the ground those plants that had grown used to living without soil.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vaporabat habitaculum cordis sui pretiosis odoribus ignitarum aspirationum, totamque omnium suarum potentiarum ac virium familiam colligebat in obsequium chari hospitis postridie ad ipsam diversuri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055730Z">
        <seg>It exhaled the precious scents of its heart's ignited aspirations, and gathered the whole family of its powers and strengths into obedience to its dear guest, who was to take up lodging there the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>varios distinguere titulos, modos, stimulos hujus dilectionis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053201Z">
        <seg>to distinguish the various titles, modes, and incentives of this love;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vel unde in quæstoris domo procul hinc dissita innotuisse potuit, te subitanea debilitate languidam isto medicamine indigere?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051700Z">
        <seg>Or from where, in the questor's house so far away from here, could it have become known that you, stricken by a sudden weakness, needed that medicine?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vel, comparatione suorum scelerum, permodicum esse, quod patiebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052646Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052646Z">
        <seg>or, in comparison to his own crimes, what he was suffering was very slight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>veni delicata, veni tenellula, experire melius, disce plenius, num te deceat sic fastidire miseram &amp; coæqualem tuam, in qua non minus, quam in te ipsa, splendet imago tui Creatoris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061407Z">
        <seg>Come, delicate one, come, tender one, experience better, learn more fully, whether it is fitting for you to so disdain your wretched and fellow human being, in whom the image of your Creator shines no less than in yourself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>venienti exposuit, noctem se duxisse inquietissimam, tristem, insomnem, jugiter sibi ante oculos obversante suorum consobrinorum desolatione;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032817Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032817Z">
        <seg>He told his visitor that he had spent a most restless, sad, and sleepless night, with the desolation of his cousins constantly turning over in his mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>venit præfixa dies, &amp; rheda cum D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043355Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043355Z">
        <seg>The appointed day came, and the carriage with D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>venit præfixa dies, &amp; rheda cum D. Ludovica ejusque filia Isabella Alexia substitit ante fores.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T003551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T003551Z">
        <seg>The appointed day arrived, and the carriage with Lady Ludovica and her daughter Isabella Alexia stopped before the doors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verebantur censorium Rosæ adhuc parvulæ supercilium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053316Z">
        <seg>They feared Rosa's censor still rested on her little eyebrow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verebantur enim Rosæ adhuc parvulæ censorium supercilium Immenso gaudio perfundebatur, quoties Dei honorem insigni aliquo successu promoveri videbat, ut accidit in insigni peccatricis conversione.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042118Z">
        <seg>For they feared that the censor's brow was still overshadowed by the little rose, and he was filled with immense joy whenever he saw the honor of God promoted by some notable success, as happened in the remarkable conversion of the sinner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum &amp; hi velut in magnæ perplexitatis negotio videbantur trepidare, nec certi quidpiam resolvebant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042713Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042713Z">
        <seg>But they too seemed to be flustered as if in a matter of great perplexity, and they did not resolve on anything certain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum Potosio Truxillum eunti adversa fuisse omnia, ita ut aliud suspicari nequiverit, quam Rosam ab oratione promissa, &amp; angelum suum a tutela commissa interea destitisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051859Z">
        <seg>Indeed, everything was against Potosi as he went to Trujillo, so that he could not help but suspect that Rosa had gone back on her promise and had in the meantime abandoned her angel entrusted to his care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum circa illas (summi Pastoris mente in similibus jam percepta) nihil extra lineam sacrorum rituum audendum fore vel permittendum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031738Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031738Z">
        <seg>But concerning those (the mind of the Supreme Shepherd having already perceived the like), nothing outside the line of sacred rites is to be heard or permitted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum cordatiores his, altiorem, suaviorem, nobisque paradisi exulibus peregrinum, &amp; incognitum judicarunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031137Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031137Z">
        <seg>But the more sensible of these judged it to be higher, sweeter, and for us exiles of paradise, a stranger and an unknown.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum de hac nobis ad compendium sufficiat concors depositio confessariorum, quotquot generalem Virginis exomologesim exceperunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044046Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044046Z">
        <seg>But for this, the unanimous testimony of the confessors, as many as have received the general exomologesis of the Virgin, shall suffice for us in short.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum divina bonitas una cum acutissimis pœnitentiæ stimulis misericordiæ fiduciam ingerebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032551Z">
        <seg>But divine goodness, together with the keenest stings of penitence, instilled confidence in mercy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum dum exspectat, dum agoni se præparat, dum socias exemplo, gestu, hortatu invitat, nuntiatur hostem longius abire a litore;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060254Z">
        <seg>But while she waits, while she prepares herself for the struggle, while she invites her companions by example, gesture, and encouragement, word is brought that the enemy is moving farther from the shore.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum non nisi post Rosæ obitum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061937Z">
        <seg>But only after Rose's death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum populus, uni Virginis tumbæ iconibus ac rosariis contingendæ intentus, circa tumulum laborabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031500Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031500Z">
        <seg>But the people, intent on touching the icons and rosaries of the Virgin's tomb, were working around the tomb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum ubi beatum Virginis transitum percepit, totum repente impulsu altiori in communem gratulationis impetum efferbuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031255Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031255Z">
        <seg>But when he perceived the blessed Virgin's passing, he suddenly, with a loftier impulse, broke forth into a general outburst of congratulations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verumtamen altius mota, in certitudinis experimentum, cælitus arrham quasi pignoratitiam exspectavit &amp; obtinuit, quæ talis fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043516Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043516Z">
        <seg>However, moved to a higher level into the experiment of certainty, it awaited and obtained from heaven a kind of pledge-like down payment, which was as follows.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vicecancellarius.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043500Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043500Z">
        <seg>Vice-Chancellor</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vide, an laudari, coli, adorari mereatur a nobis illa æterna Majestas, cui terrenum hoc germen, rudes trunci, cuncta vegetabilia modo, quo possunt, laudis canticum adsusurrant, pronæ venerationis tributum pendunt”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045211Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045211Z">
        <seg>See if that eternal Majesty deserves to be praised, honored, and adored by us, to whom this earthly seed, the rudimentary trunks, and all plants, in whatever way they can, whisper a song of praise and pay homage with bowed heads.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>videbatur jam assecuta, dum in exangui facie, præter obscurum pallorem, &amp; maciem cavernosam nihil conspicuum apparebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040735Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040735Z">
        <seg>She seemed already to have regained consciousness, while on her bloodless face, apart from a dull pallor and a cavernous emaciation, nothing was visible.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>videbatur non femina, sed apostolus prædicare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030204Z">
        <seg>It seemed not a woman, but an apostle, preaching.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>videbatur nonnisi cum vita finiendum profluvium, adeo late pavimentum incessabilis cruor purpurarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035657Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035657Z">
        <seg>The profuse bleeding seemed to be stopped only by death, for the floor was so widely stained purple by the ceaseless blood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vidensque luctari infirmam, eo quod nesciret, unde incipiendum, aut quæ nomina danda morbo tam anonymo ac præternaturali, imperiosius per vitutem sanctæ obedientiæ adstrinxit Filiam eloqui secreta, quæ non noverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013514Z">
        <seg>And seeing her struggle, weak because she did not know where to begin or what names to give to a disease so anonymous and supernatural, he more imperiously, by the virtue of holy obedience, compelled the Daughter to reveal the secrets she did not know.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>videtur candentis ferri globus mihi per tempora adigi, &amp; a summo vertice usque ad infimam plantam dextri pedis ignitum veru transmitti, cujus me vehementia quodammodo in altum attollit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013415Z">
        <seg>It seems to me that a ball of white-hot iron is brought up through my limbs, and from the very crown of my head to the sole of my right foot a fiery spear is transmitted, whose force somehow lifts me up.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vilissimoque diabolo pudendam imbecillitatem exprobravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051958Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051958Z">
        <seg>and reproached the vilest devil with shameful weakness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vires omnium admiratione integerrime recuperavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012405Z">
        <seg>He recovered his strength with the utmost integrity, to the admiration of all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vis ingenue tibi dicam, ad quid veneris?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060639Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060639Z">
        <seg>Shall I tell you frankly what you've come for?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vis perspicacis ingenii &amp; alta judicii maturitas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050135Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050135Z">
        <seg>Sharpness of insight and deep maturity of judgment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vivendum sibi, moriendumque esse sub charo S. Catharinæ Senensis schemate ac magisterio, adfore tamen aliquando tempus, quo hujus nommis atque instituti novum Limæ exsurgat monasterium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040323Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040323Z">
        <seg>that they must live and die under the dear habit and teaching of St. Catherine of Siena, yet that a time will come when a new monastery of this name and institute will arise in Lima;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vix intrarant, cum ad solitum Rosæ invitatorium, vidit certatim incurvari arbores, adeo quod summitatum cacumina terram attingebant, lenteque verrebant solum, velut profundæ adorationis solemnissimo ritu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045159Z">
        <seg>They had scarcely entered when, at Rose's usual invitation, she saw the trees bend in a rush, so that their very tops touched the ground and slowly swept the earth, as in the most solemn rite of profound adoration.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vix temperatum a verbere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042802Z">
        <seg>Barely restrained from striking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vixque oratorium ingressi, ubi uxor cum Joanne de Benavides &amp; Petro Chiandro familiaribus taciti expectabant, viderunt, non sine ictu pietatis effigiem undequaque velut roris antelucani guttulis confertim madidam &amp; obductam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053759Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053759Z">
        <seg>and having scarcely entered the oratory, where his wife, with John de Benavides and Peter Chiandro, relatives, was waiting in silence, they saw, not without a pang of piety, the effigy, from every side, as if thickly drenched and coated with drops of pre-dawn dew.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vobis solis ultimum hoc pietatis in me officium supplex commendo”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013220Z">
        <seg>I humbly commend to you alone this final act of kindness for me.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>§ I.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030520Z">
        <seg>Section I.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>§ II.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T030936Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T030936Z">
        <seg>Section II.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>§ III.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031326Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031326Z">
        <seg>Section III.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>§ IV.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031611Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031611Z">
        <seg>Section IV.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>§ V.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034519Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034519Z">
        <seg>Section V.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>§ VI.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T035319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T035319Z">
        <seg>Section VI.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>§ VII.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T040224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T040224Z">
        <seg>Section Seven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ægerrime adquievit vis populi, hoc uno contenta, quod acceperat, Rosæ suæ reliquias in tuto esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T031742Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T031742Z">
        <seg>The will of the people was most reluctantly acquiesced to, content with this one thing: that the relics of his Rose were safe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ægra ex adverso, malum pudorem obstinatione pejori obarmans, contendebat pervicaciter, in Panama se sacro Baptismo tinctam fuisse, &amp; ut fidem conciliaret mendacio, fingebat nomina plurium, quos interfuisse jactabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012901Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012901Z">
        <seg>The sick woman, on the other hand, arming herself against shame with a worse obstinacy, contended stubbornly that she had been baptized in Panama, and to lend credence to her lie, she fabricated the names of several people whom she claimed had been present.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Æquanimiter hanc repulsæ asperitatem tulit mitissima Rosa, attamen subjunxit: “Videbitur decretum Altissimi diversum esse, vestroque fortius: nam novitius ille ad solemnem professionem rite pertinget, &amp; Ordinem, pietatis, ac religionis exemplo, insigniter illustrabit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T012451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T012451Z">
        <seg>The most gentle Rose bore this harsh rebuff with equanimity, yet she added, "It will appear that the decree of the Most High is different, and stronger than yours: for that novice will duly attain to solemn profession, and will notably illustrate the Order by his example of piety and religion."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Æquiores, fumantis melancholiæ, seu atræ bilis esse paroxysmos interpretabantur, quodque cerebrum inedia ac vigiliis attenuatum nimis, pateret horridis luridorum phantasmatum incursibus, ex adustione vitiati splenis, aut fœculenti sanguinis corruptione.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045713Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045713Z">
        <seg>They interpreted paroxysms as more moderate attacks of smoky melancholy, or black bile, and held that the brain, too greatly weakened by fasting and sleeplessness, lay open to the horrid incursions of lurid phantoms, arising from a scorched spleen or the corruption of foul blood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Æstuabat impar oneri, gemebat, obluctabatur, donec interdum victa premeretur usque ad solum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041637Z">
        <seg>It groaned under the unequal burden, it struggled, it resisted, until at times, overcome, it was pressed down to the ground.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Æstuabat, arctabatur, jam palmæ oblita Virgo, pro qua successerat Lilium convallium tam speciosum, tam pretiosum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044424Z">
        <seg>It was scorched and withered; the Virgin, long since forgotten, for whom the lily of the valleys—so beautiful, so precious—had taken its place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Æstuavit repente mulier, bulliit, arsit illo effectu, quem semper fugerat, &amp; si umquam timuerat de eo vel leviter cogitare, gravius nunc timebat, pii propositi exequendi opportunitatem sibi aut elabi aut retardari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032919Z">
        <seg>The woman was suddenly seized by a burning heat, she bubbled and burned with that effect which she had always shunned, and if she had ever feared even to think of it lightly, she now feared more grievously that the opportunity to carry out her pious purpose might slip away or be delayed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Æthiopi cuidam servo Didaci de Ayala ex nervorum attractione &amp; tenaci plica, brachium dextrum ac manus a pluribus annis denegebant officium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T043206Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T043206Z">
        <seg>A certain Ethiopian servant of Diego de Ayala, due to nerve traction and a tenacious fold, had had his right arm and hand refuse to function for many years.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>æque in rebus tum Ecclesiæ, tum reipublicæ versatissimus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050157Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050157Z">
        <seg>equally well-versed in the affairs of both the Church and the state.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Ad agonem (inquit) me succingo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060223Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060223Z">
        <seg>"I gird myself for the contest," he says.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Advertito (inquit) o Rosa, hic tergiversandi ac silendi locum non esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050454Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050454Z">
        <seg>"I tell you," he said, "O Rosa, there is no room here for equivocation and silence."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Ah, &lt;s2&gt;[venerabatur, ac etiam exprimebat in plantis,]&lt;/s2&gt; frater, nimio dolore crucior, dum video, contemptim jacere solo, &amp; calcaneis proteri qualemcumque rudissimum typum illius adorandæ crucis, in qua innocens Agnus Dei, suæmet vitæ inæstimabili pretio nos redemit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054915Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054915Z">
        <seg>Ah, brother, I am tormented by excessive pain as I see the most rudimentary form of that adored cross—the one on which the innocent Lamb of God redeemed us with the inestimable price of his own life—contemptuously lying on the ground and being trampled underfoot by my heels.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Ah, [venerabatur, ac etiam exprimebat in plantis,] frater, nimio dolore crucior, dum video, contemptim jacere solo, &amp; calcaneis proteri qualemcumque rudissimum typum illius adorandæ crucis, in qua innocens Agnus Dei, suæmet vitæ inæstimabili pretio nos redemit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T011205Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T011205Z">
        <seg>Ah, brother, I worship it and even imprint it on my feet, for I am tormented by overwhelming pain as I see it lying contemptuously on the ground and being trampled underfoot by my heels, that most rudimentary form of that adored cross on which the innocent Lamb of God redeemed us with the inestimable price of his own life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“An forte (inquiebat) conscia meæ instabilitatis, &amp; indolis, qua facile ab una civitate in aliam, de regno in regnum transcurrere soleo, constantiæ me admonuit, firmitatem imperavit”:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032337Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032337Z">
        <seg>"Or perhaps," he said, "aware of my instability and of my nature, by which I am wont to pass easily from one city to another, from kingdom to kingdom, she admonished me of constancy, she commanded firmness."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Apponis erga eum cor tuum” Job.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051552Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051552Z">
        <seg>"Lay your heart on him." Job.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Benedictus Deus!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T033406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T033406Z">
        <seg>Blessed be God!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Canta, (inquit) mi pulle, canta, ne moriaris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061541Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061541Z">
        <seg>"Sing, my little one," he said, "sing, lest you die."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Comperi (inquit) manifestis indiciis, spiritui meo donum illud ob Rosæ in cælis interventum fuisse mirabiliter impertitum”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T032736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T032736Z">
        <seg>"I discovered (he said) by clear indications that this gift had been miraculously bestowed upon my spirit through Rose's intercession in heaven."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Domine plus, plus: imple adorandum &amp; sanctum beneplacitum justissimæ voluntatis tuæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T025640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T025640Z">
        <seg>O Lord, more and more: fulfill the adored and holy good pleasure of Your most just will.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Dum in ima caliginosæ derelictionis voragine absorptam me puto &amp; lugeo, en repente, in ipso pristinæ unionis meridie, velut in Sponsi brachiis me deprehendo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050507Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050507Z">
        <seg>While I think and lament myself absorbed in the deepest vortex of gloomy desolation, behold, suddenly, at the very noon of our former union, I find myself as it were in the arms of the Bridegroom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Et habeo (inquit) efficax certumque cataplasma, quod illico mihi sanabit manum”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T034825Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T034825Z">
        <seg>"And I have," he said, "an effective and certain poultice that will immediately heal my hand."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Hæc sunt, (inquit) mater mea dulcissima, quæ tibi utcumque adumbrare potui, ut obedirem, cetera ne ruditer quidem possum, nec si possem, caperes”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T013539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T013539Z">
        <seg>"These are, my sweetest mother," he said, "the things I have been able in any way to outline for you so that I might obey; the rest I cannot even crudely, and even if I could, you would not accept them."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Imo (ait) mysterium suberat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054540Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054540Z">
        <seg>"Indeed," he said, "a mystery lay beneath."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Malebam (inquiebat) obsequi, debito adjungere lucrum explicitæ obedientiæ, &amp; materni respectus humile tributum una cum penso dependere”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035601Z">
        <seg>"I would rather," he said, "obey, add the profit of explicit obedience to my duty, and, together with my stipend, pay a humble tribute of filial respect."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Mihi (inquiens) tu deinceps Rosa diceris, &amp; eris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034659Z">
        <seg>"You shall be called Rosa from now on, and you shall be."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Ne quidquam dubites, ait, mi pater (nam ipsum &amp; uxorem parentum nomine honorare consueverat) ne, inquam, timueris: non enim hujuscemodi ostenta nobis indulsit benignus iste Salvator, tamquam propediem his ædibus abiturus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054057Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054057Z">
        <seg>"Do not doubt anything," he said, "my father" (for he was accustomed to honor his parents by calling them by their names), "do not be afraid, I say: for this kind Savior has not granted us such displays as if he were about to leave this house any day now."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Ne tradas bestiis animas confitentes tibi”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T042000Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T042000Z">
        <seg>Do not hand over the souls that confess to you to the beasts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Perge, (inquiens) o mater, perge de hoc argumento plura, quæ nosti, disserere”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054528Z">
        <seg>"Go on, mother, go on and discuss more about this matter that you know."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Promitto, inquit, tibi, me recta ad matrem hinc redituram, ædesque paternas, quoad jusseris, habituram pro asceterio”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040134Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040134Z">
        <seg>"I promise you," she said, "that I will return straight from here to my mother and, as long as you command, will regard the family home as my ascetic cell."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Putasne (inquit) decere Virginem, toties in publico ad levandas paleas explicare faciei operimentum?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054903Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054903Z">
        <seg>"Do you think," he said, "it is fitting for a virgin to uncover her face so often in public to relieve the burdens?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Quid doles?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051226Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051226Z">
        <seg>What do you ache?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Quid putas, ad ea mihi regereret seraphica mater mea?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052538Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052538Z">
        <seg>What do you think, would my seraphic mother have me return to those things?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Quid video?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053737Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053737Z">
        <seg>What do I see?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Quorsum (inquit) hæc tuimet incuria?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061311Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061311Z">
        <seg>"What is the reason for this negligence of yours?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Suspensa (inquit) in unitivo quietissimæ contemplationis lumine, vidi fulgorem admirabilis excellentiæ, decoris, ac majestatis, quem sine termino quaquaversus diffuderat omnipræsens ac subtilis immensitas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052724Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052724Z">
        <seg>"Suspended (he said) in the unitive light of the most tranquil contemplation, I saw the radiance of admirable excellence, beauty, and majesty, which the ever-present and subtle immensity had diffused without limit in every direction."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Teneritudo (inquit) robusti gaudii super omne gaudium imaginabile, cognatio filiationis divinæ, interior renovatio vetustatis in ipsa animæ essentia, impletio sinuum voluntatis, vita &amp; alacritas omnium affectuum, firma, sancta, lepida, &amp; demum undequaque ineffabilis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050606Z">
        <seg>"Tenderness," he says, "of a robust joy beyond all imaginable delight, kinship with divine sonship, an inner renewal of antiquity in the very essence of the soul, the fulfillment of the desires of the will, life and alacrity in all affections, firm, holy, graceful, and, in short, ineffable in every respect."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Tibi (inquit) Rosula videtur, quæ coram Deo Rosa est, ac grandis quidem?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050650Z">
        <seg>"Do you see Rosula," he said, "who before God is a Rose, and indeed a great one?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Vide (inquit) o Rosa, eximium favorem, quo te Filius meus dignatus est”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044414Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044414Z">
        <seg>"See (he said) O Rose, the extraordinary favor with which my Son has honored you."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>”Cumque amor poësim doceat, Rosa pariter has querelas familiari metro illigarat, hoc fere sensu:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050947Z">
        <seg>And since love teaches poetry, Rosa likewise had woven these complaints into a familiar meter, with this approximate meaning:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>…Sane gavisa est Rosa, ut se vidit circumamictam varietate;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040224Z">
        <seg>…Indeed, Rosa rejoiced to see herself surrounded by a variety of things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
